a
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>A-<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>the prep. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81">ā</a></em> shortened before double cons., as <em>akkosati (ā + kruś), akkhāti (ā + khyā), abbahati (ā + bṛh)</em></p><p>Best to be classed here is the <em>a-</em> we call expletive. It represents a reduction of <em>ā-</em> (mostly before liquids and nasals and with single consonant instead of double). Thus <em>anantaka</em> (for <em>ā-nantaka = nantaka</em>) Vv.80#7; <strong>amajjapa</strong> (for <em>ā-majjapa = majjapa</em>) Ja.vi.328; <em>amāpaya</em> (for <em>āmāpaya</em> = <em>māpaya</em>) Ja.vi.518; <em>apassato (= passantassa)</em> Ja.vi.552.</p><p>A-<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(<em>an-</em> before vowels) neg. part. prefixed to 1 nouns and adjectives; 2 verbal forms, used like (1) whether <em>part.</em>, <em>ger.</em>, <em>grd.</em> or <em>inf.</em>; 3 finite verbal forms. In compound. with words having originally two initial cons. the latter reappear in their assimilated form (e.g. appaṭicchavin). In meaning it equals <em>na-</em>, <em>nir-</em> and <em>vi-</em>. Often we find it opp. to <em>sa-</em>. Verbal negatives which occur in specific verb. function will be enumerated. separately, while examples of neg. forms of (1) &amp; (2) are given under their positive form unless the neg. involves a distinctly new concept, or if its form is likely to lead to confusion or misunderstanding</p><p>Concerning the combining &amp; contrasting (orig. neg.) <em>-a-(ā)</em> in redupl. formations like <em>bhavā-bhava</em> see *<a href="bword://%C4%81">ā</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>Vedic a-, an-; Idg. *n̊, gradation form to *ne (see *<a href="bword://na">na</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); Gr. ἀ, ἀν-; Lat. *en-, in-; Goth. Ohg. &amp; Ags. un-; Oir. an-, in-</p><p>A-<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>the augment (sign of action in the past), prefixed to the root in <em>pret., aor. &amp; cond.;</em> tenses; often omitted in ordinary prose. See forms under each verb; cp. also <em><a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a></em> Identical with this <em>a-</em> is the <em>a-</em> which functions as base of some pron. forms like <em>ato, attha, asu</em> etc. (q.v.).</p><p>Vedic a-; Idg. *e (loc. of pron. stem, cp. ayaṃ; orig. a deictic adv. with specific reference to the past, cp Sk sma); Gr. ἐ-; also in Gr. ἐκεϊ, Lat. equidem, enim.</p><p>A-<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>the sound a (<em>a-kāra</em>) Ja.vi.328, Ja.vi.552; Vv-a.279, Vv-a.307, Vv-a.311.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ā">ā</a>, amajjapa, <a href="bword://ā">ā</a>, <a href="bword://na">na</a>, <a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a></p>

ababa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A name given not to a special purgatory but to a period of time in Avīci.</p><p>One term of Ababa is equal to four hundred of Abbuda; an Abbuda being reckoned as the time taken to remove twenty Kosalan Khāris (equal to a cartload) of tila-seeds, taking one seed at the end of each century. Snp.p.126 SN.i.152</p><p>5hell</p>

ababa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the name of a hell, or place in Avīci, where one suffers for an <em>ababa</em> of years.</p>

ababa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Name of a certain Purgatory, enumerated with many other similar names at AN.v.173 = Snp.p.126 (cp. aṭaṭa, abbuda &amp; also Avs.i.4, Avs.i.10 &amp; see for further expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of term Snp-a.476 sq.</p><p>of uncertain origin, prob. onomatopoetic</p>

abaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>not tied, unbound, unfettered Snp.39 (variant reading and Nd ii.abandha; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d-<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by rajju-bandhan’ ādisu yena kenaci abaddha Snp-a.83).</p><p>a + baddha</p>

abala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not strong, weak, feeble Snp.1120 (= dubbala, appabala, appathāma Cnd.73); Dhp.29 (˚assa a weak horse = dubbalassa Dhp-a.i.262; opp. sīghassa a quick horse).</p><p>a + bala</p>

abalabala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>very foolish, very feeble; babbling (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abaḷabaḷa">abaḷabaḷa</a></p>

abandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noun adjective not tied to, not a follower or victim of Iti.56 (mārassa; variant reading abaddha).</p><p>a + bandha</p>

abandhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without fetters or bonds, unfettered, untrammelled Snp.948, cp. Mnd.433.</p><p>a + bandhana</p>

abandonment
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Abandonment:</strong> contemplation of: <em>patinissaggānupassanā</em> is one of the 18 chief kinds of insight; see: <em>vipassanā</em> further <em>ānāpānasati</em> 16.</p>

abaḷabaḷa|aba.laba.la|abalabala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aba.laba.la</span></p><p>very foolish, very feeble; babbling (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abalabala">abalabala</a></p>

abbahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(from abbahati) pulling out, drawing out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbuhana">abbuhana</a>, <a href="bword://abbūhana">abbūhana</a></p>

abbahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>tears up or off; pulls out, draws out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbuhati">abbuhati</a>, <a href="bword://abbūhati">abbūhati</a></p>

abbahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Abbahati &amp; abbuhati</p><p>to draw off, pull out (a sting or dart);<br />imper pres <em>abbaha</em> Thag.404; Ja.ii.95 (variant reading BB appuha = abbuha; C. explains by uddharatha)<br />aor <em>abbahi</em> Ja.v.198 (variant reading BB abbuhi), <em>abbahī</em> (metri causa) Ja.iii.390 (variant reading BB dhabbuḷi = abbuḷhi) = Pv.i.8#6 (which reads T. abbūḷha, but Pv-a.41 explains nīhari) = Dhp-a.i.30 (vv.ll. sabbahi, sabbamhi; gloss K. B abbūḷhaṃ) = Vv.83#9 (T. abbuḷhi; variant reading BB abbuḷhaṃ, SS avyahi; Vv-a.327 explains as uddhari), &amp; <em>abbuhi</em> AN.iii.55 (variant reading abbahi, C abbahī ti nīhari), see also vv.ll. under abbahi<br />ger <em>abbuyha</em> Snp.939 (= abbuhitvā uddharitvā Mnd.419; v.l SS abbuyhitvā; Snp-a.567 reads <em>avyuyha</em> &amp; explains by uddharitvā); SN.i.121 (taṇhaṃ); SN.iii.26 (id.; but spelt abbhuyha)<br />pp <em>abbuḷha</em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em>abbāheti</em> [Sk ābarhayati] to pull out, drag out Ja.iv.364 (satthaṃ abbāhayanti; variant reading abbhā˚); Dhp-a.ii.249 (asiṃ).<br />ger <em>abbāhitvā</em> (= ˚hetvā) Vin.ii.201 (bhisa-muḷālaṃ) with variant reading BB aggahetvā SS abbūhitvā, cp. Vin.i.214 (vv.ll. aggahitvā abbāhitvā).<br />pp <em><a href="bword://abb%C5%AB%E1%B8%B7hita">abbūḷhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>the first more freq. for pres., the second often in aor. forms; Sk. ābṛhati, ā + <em>bṛh<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, pp bṛḍha (see <em><a href="bword://abb%C5%AB%E1%B8%B7ha">abbūḷha</a></em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbūḷhita">abbūḷhita</a>, <a href="bword://abbūḷha">abbūḷha</a></p>

abbahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbahati">abbahati</a></p>

abbahitvā|abbahitvaa|abbahitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbahitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbahati">abbahati</a></p>

abbajati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes to, returns to.</p>

abbaje
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>T. reading at AN.ii.39, evidently interpreted by ed. as ā + vraje, pot. of ā + <em>vraj</em> to go to, come to (cp pabbajati), but is preferably with variant reading SS to be read <em>aṇḍaje</em> (corresponding with vihangama in prec. line).</p>

abbata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noun adjective</p><ol><li>(nt.) that which is not “vata” i.e. moral obligation, breaking of the moral obligation Snp.839 (asīlata +); Mnd.188 (variant reading SS abhabbata; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> again as a-vatta). Snp-a.545 (= dhutangavataṃ vinā</li><li>(adj.) one who offends against the moral obligation, lawless Dhp.264 (= sīlavatena ca dhutavatena ca virahita Dhp-a.iii.391; vv.ll. k. adhūta &amp; abhūta; B abbhuta, C. abbuta).</li></ol><p>a + vata, Sk. avrata</p>

abbaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in uday˚ at Mil.393 stands for <em><a href="bword://avyaya">avyaya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avyaya">avyaya</a></p>

abbaṇa|abba.na|abbana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abba.na</span></p><p>adjective without wounds Dhp.124.</p><p>a + vaṇa, Sk. avraṇa</p>

abbeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.iii.34 &amp; Ja.vi.17 is probably a mistake in MSS for <em><a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a></em>.</p><p>Trenckner, Notes 64 n. 19</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a></p>

abbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine n. \~ā, feminine a</i></p><p>cloud; a thundercloud.</p>

abbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>A (dense &amp; dark) cloud, a cloudy mass AN.ii.53; Vin.ii.295 = Mil.273 in list of to things that obscure moon- &amp; sunshine, viz.; <em>abbhaṃ mahikā</em> (mahiyā A) <em>dhūmarajo</em> (megho Miln), <em>Rāhu</em>. This list is referred to at Snp-a.487 &amp; Vv-a.134. SN.i.101 (˚sama pabbata a mountain like a thunder-cloud); Ja.vi.581 (abbhaṃ rajo acchādesi) Pv.iv.3#9 (nīl˚ = nīla-megha Pv-a.251). As f. <em>abbhā</em> at Dhs.617 &amp; Dhs-a.317 (used in sense of adj. “dull” Dhs-a explains by valāhaka); perhaps also in <em><a href="bword://abbh%C4%81matta">abbhāmatta</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%AB%E1%B9%ADa">kūṭa</a></em> the point or summit of a storm-cloud Thag.1064; Ja.vi.249, Ja.vi.250; Vv.1#1 (= valāhaka-sikhara Vv-a.12)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a></em> a mass of clouds, a thick cloud Iti.64; Snp.348 (cp. Snp-a.348).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADala">paṭala</a></em> a mass of clouds Dhs-a.239</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mutta">mutta</a></em> free from clouds Snp.687 (see [as abbhāmutta Dhp.382](bword://as abbhāmutta Dhp.382)).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83vil%C4%81pa">saṃvilāpa</a></em> thundering SN.iv.289.</li></ul><p>Vedic abhra nt. &amp; later Sk. abhra m. “dark cloud”; Idg. *m̊bhro, cp. Gr. ἀφρἀφρο scum, froth, Lat. imber rain; also Sk. ambha water, Gr. ο ̓́μβρος rain, Oir ambu water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhāmatta">abbhāmatta</a>, <a href="bword://kūṭa">kūṭa</a>, <a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a>, <a href="bword://paṭala">paṭala</a>, <a href="bword://mutta">mutta</a>, as abbhāmutta Dhp.382, <a href="bword://saṃvilāpa">saṃvilāpa</a></p>

abbhacchādita|abbhacchaadita|abbhacchadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhacchaadita</span></p><p>covered (with) Thag.1068.</p><p>pp. of abhi + ā + chādeti</p>

abbhaghana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i></p><p>mass of clouds; a thick cloud</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

abbhakkhāna|abbhakkhaana|abbhakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhakkhaana</span></p><p>false accusation; slander.</p>

abbhakkhāna|abbhakkhaana|abbhakkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhakkhaana</span></p><p>accusation, slander, calumny DN.iii.248, DN.iii.250; MN.i.130; MN.iii.207; AN.iii.290 sq. Dhp.139 (cp. Dhp-a.iii.70).</p><p>fr. abbhakkhāti</p>

abbhakkhāti|abbhakkhaati|abbhakkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhakkhaati</span></p><p>to speak against to accuse, slander DN.i.161 = AN.i.161 (an-abbhakkhātu-kāma); AN.iv.182 (id.); Ja.iv.377. Cp. Intens. <em><a href="bword://abbh%C4%81cikkhati">abbhācikkhati</a></em>.</p><p>abhi + ā + <em>khyā</em>, cp. Sk. ākhyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhācikkhati">abbhācikkhati</a></p>

abbhamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(or mfn.) (of) the size of a cloud</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhāmatta">abbhāmatta</a>, <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

abbhamātta|abbhamaatta|abbhamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhamaatta</span></p><p>(or mfn.) (of) the size of a cloud</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhamatta">abbhamatta</a>, <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

abbhantara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) interior, internal; being within, included in, among; belonging to one ‘s house, personal, intimate.</li><li>(n.)<ol><li>intermediate space, interval; the inside, interior.</li><li>a measure of length (= 28 hatthas).</li></ol></li></ol>

abbhantara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = antara, i.e. internal, inner, being within or between; nt. <em>-ṃ</em> the inner part, interior, interval (see <em>as ˚-</em>) Vin.i.111 (satt˚ with interval of seven); AN.iv.16 (opp. bāhira); Dhp.394 (id.); Thag.757 (˚âpassaya lying inside); Ja.iii.395 (˚amba the inside of the Mango); Mil.30 (˚e vāyo jivo), Mil.262, Mil.281 (bāhir-abbhantara dhana) Dhp-a.ii.74 (adj. c. gen. being among; variant reading abbhantare). Cases used adverbially: instr. <em>abbhantarena</em> in the meantime in between Dhp-a.ii.59. loc. <em>abbhantare</em> in the midst of, inside of, within (c. gen. or-˚) Ja.i.262 (rañño), Ja.i.280 (tuyhaṃ); Dhp-a.ii.64 (variant reading antare), Dhp-a.ii.92 (sattavass˚); Pv-a.48 (= anto).</p><p>abhi + antara; abhi here in directive function = towards the inside, in there, with-in, cp. abhi i.1 a</p><p>See also: as ˚\-</p>

abbhantarapassaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>resting inside, in the heart</p><p>See also: abbantara</p>

abbhantarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>intimate; trusted.</p>

abbhantarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun intimate friend, confidant, “chum” Ja.i.86 (+ ativissāsika), Ja.i.337 (“insider”, opp. bāhiraka).</p><p>fr. abbhantara, cp. Sk. abhyantara in same meaning</p>

abbhantarima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>being far within; inner, innermost; very intimate.</p>

abbhantarima
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective internal, inner (opp. <em><a href="bword://b%C4%81hirima">bāhirima</a></em>) Vin.iii.149; Ja.v.38.</p><p>superl. formation fr. abbhantara in contrasting function</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bāhirima">bāhirima</a></p>

abbhanujānāti|abbhanujaanaati|abbhanujanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhanujaanaati</span></p><ol start="3"><li>sg. concedes; agrees with (gen.) about (acc).</li></ol>

abbhanumodana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(&amp; ˚ā f.) being pleased, satisfaction, thanksgiving DN-a.i.227 Vv-a.52 (˚ānu˚); Sdhp.218</p><p>fr. abbhanumodati</p>

abbhanumodati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>accepts with thanks; approves, praises; assents to.</p>

abbhanumodati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be much pleased at to show great appreciation of Vin.i.196; DN.i.143, DN.i.190; SN.iv.224; Mil.29, Mil.210; Dhp-a.iv.102 (variant reading ˚ānu˚).</p><p>abhi + anu + modati</p>

abbhanumodi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhanumodati">abbhanumodati</a></p>

abbhanumodita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhanumoditar">abbhanumoditar</a></p><p>(from abbhanumodati) one who approves; one who prasies.</p>

abbhanumoditar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhanumoditar">abbhanumoditar</a></p><p>(from abbhanumodati) one who approves; one who prasies.</p>

abbhasama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>like a cloud; high as the sky</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

abbhasampilāpa|abbhasampilaapa|abbhasampilapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhasampilaapa</span></p><p>massing together of clouds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

abbhatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective brought (to), procured, got, Ja.vi.291.</p><p>ā + bhata + ika, <em>bhṛ</em>.</p>

abbhatikkanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who has thoroughly, left behind Ja.v.376.</p><p>pp. of abhi + ati + <em>kram</em>, cp. atikkanta</p>

abbhatireka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>excessive; very great.</p>

abbhattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= <em>attha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, only in phrase <em>abbhatthaṃ gacchati</em> “to go towards home”, i.e. setting; fig. to disappear, vanish MN.i.115, MN.i.119; MN.iii.25; AN.iv.32; Mil.305; pp. <em>abhhattaṅgata</em> “set”, gone, disappeared Dhs.1038 (atthangata +) Kv.576.</p><p>abhi + attha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in acc. abhi + atthaṃ, abhi in function of “towards” = homeward, as under abhi i.1 a; cp. Vedic abhi sadhasthaṃ to the seat R.V. ix. 21. 3</p>

abbhatthatā|abbhatthataa|abbhatthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhatthataa</span></p><p>“going towards setting”, disappearance, death Ja.v.469.</p><p>abstr. fr. abbhatta</p>

abbhatthaṃ gacchati|abbhattha.m gacchati|abbhattham gacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhattha.m gacchati</span></p><p>disappears, comes to an end</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhatthaṃ">abbhatthaṃ</a></p>

abbhatthaṃ|abbhattha.m|abbhattham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhattha.m</span></p><p>towards home, towards setting.</p>

abbhatthaṃ yāti|abbhattha.m yaati|abbhattham yati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhattha.m yaati</span></p><p>disappears, comes to an end</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhatthaṃ">abbhatthaṃ</a></p>

abbhatīta|abbhatiita|abbhatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhatiita</span></p><ol><li>(pass.) overstepped, transgressed; neglected; passed (by).</li><li>(act.)<ol><li>having elapsed; passed away.</li><li>past; dead.</li></ol></li></ol>

abbhatīta|abbhatiita|abbhatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhatiita</span></p><p>emphatic of atīta in all meanings, viz.</p><ol><li>passed, gone by SN.ii.183 (+ atikkanta); nt. <em>-ṃ</em> what is gone or over the past Ja.iii.169.</li><li>passed away, dead MN.i.465; SN.iv.398; Thag.242, Thag.1035.</li><li>transgressed, overstepped neglected Ja.iii.541 (saṃyama).</li></ol><p>pp. of abhi + ati + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, cp. atīta &amp; atikkanta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

abbhavalāhakā|abbhavalaahakaa|abbhavalahaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhavalaahakaa</span></p><p>One of the Cloud-group of devas. They are embodied in the thunder clouds (cumulus clouds), and when they wish to revel and delight themselves, thunder clouds make their appearance in the sky. SN.iii.256</p>

abbhañjana|abbha~njana|abbhanjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~njana</span></p><p>rubbing with oil, smearing, oiling, lubricating; unguent, oil.</p>

abbhañjana|abbha~njana|abbhanjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~njana</span></p><p>anointing, lubricating, oiling; unction, unguent Vin.i.205; Vin.iii.79; Mil.367 (akkhassa a.); Vism.264; Vv-a.295.</p><p>fr. abbhañjati</p>

abbhañjati|abbha~njati|abbhanjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~njati</span></p><p>anoints; smears; lubricates.</p>

abbhañjati|abbha~njati|abbhanjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~njati</span></p><p>to anoint; to oil, to lubricate MN.i.343 (sappi-telena); SN.iv.177; Pp.56; Dhp-a.iii.311; Vv-a.68 (sata-pāka-telena). Caus. <em>abbhañjeti</em> same Ja.i.438 (telena ˚etvā); Ja.v.376 (sata-pāka-telena ˚ayiṃsu); Caus. ii. <em>abbhanjāpeti</em> to cause to anoint Ja.iii.372.</p><p>abhi + añj</p>

abbhañjitvā|abbha~njitvaa|abbhanjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~njitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhañjati">abbhañjati</a></p>

abbhañjiṃsu|abbha~nji.msu|abbhanjimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~nji.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhañjati">abbhañjati</a></p>

abbhaññāsi|abbha~n~naasi|abbhannasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~n~naasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhijānāti">abhijānāti</a></p>

abbhaññāsuṃ|abbha~n~naasu.m|abbhannasum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbha~n~naasu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhijānāti">abhijānāti</a></p>

abbhenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbheti">abbheti</a></p>

abbhetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbheti">abbheti</a></p>

abbheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>summons; recalls, rehabilitates a <em>bhikkhu</em> who has been temporarily expelled.</p>

abbheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rehabilitate a bhikkhu who has been suspended for breach of rules Vin.ii.7 (abbhento), Vin.ii.33 (abbheyya); Vin.iii.112 (abbheti), Vin.iii.186 = Vin.iv.242 (abbhetabba)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://abbhita">abbhita</a></em> (q.v.). See also abbhāna.</p><p>abhi + ā + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhita">abbhita</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

abbhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>recalled, rehabilitated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbheti">abbheti</a></p>

abbhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>come back, rehabilitated, reinstated Vin.iii.186 = Vin.iv.242 (an˚).</li><li>uncertain reading at Pv.i.12#3 in sense of “called” (an˚ uncalled), where id p. at Ja.iii.165 reads anavhāta &amp; at Thig.129 ayācita.</li></ol><p>pp. of abbheti</p>

abbhiññāsi|abbhi~n~naasi|abbhinnasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhi~n~naasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhijānāti">abhijānāti</a></p>

abbhocchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(besides <em>abbocch˚</em>; q.v. under <em>abbokiṇṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>) not cut off, uninterrupted, continuous Ja.i.470 (variant reading abbo˚); Ja.vi.254, Ja.vi.373; Cp.i.6#3; Mil.72 Vism.362 (bb), Vism.391 (bb).</p><p>a + vi + ava + chinna</p>

abbhohārika|abbhohaarika|abbhoharika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhohaarika</span></p><p>see <em>abbo˚</em>.</p>

abbhokirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to sprinkle over, to cover, bedeck Vv.5#9 (= abhi-okirati abhippakirati), Vv.35#11 (variant reading abbhuk˚). Cp. <em>abbhukkirati &amp; abbhokkiraṇa;</em> pp. <em><a href="bword://abbhoki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">abbhokiṇṇa</a></em> see under <em><a href="bword://abboki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">abbokiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>abhi + ava + kirati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhokiṇṇa">abbhokiṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://abbokiṇṇa">abbokiṇṇa</a></p>

abbhokiṇṇa|abbhoki.n.na|abbhokinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhoki.n.na</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://abboki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">abbokiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of abbhokirati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbokiṇṇa">abbokiṇṇa</a></p>

abbhokkiraṇa|abbhokkira.na|abbhokkirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokkira.na</span></p><p>in naṭānaṃ a. “turnings of dancers” DN-a.i.84 in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of sobha-nagarakaṃ of DN.i.6.</p><p>fr. abbhokirati</p>

abbhokuṭika|abbhoku.tika|abbhokutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhoku.tika</span></p><p>spelling at Dhp-a.iv.8 for <em><a href="bword://abbh%C4%81ku%E1%B9%ADika">abbhākuṭika</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhākuṭika">abbhākuṭika</a></p>

abbhokāsa|abbhokaasa|abbhokasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasa</span></p><p>an open space, the open; (a place in) the open air:</p>

abbhokāsa|abbhokaasa|abbhokasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasa</span></p><p>the open air, an open &amp; unsheltered space DN.i.63 (= alagganatthena a. viya DN-a.i.180), DN.i.71 (= acchanna DN-a.i.210), DN.i.89; MN.iii.132; AN.ii.210; AN.iii.92; AN.iv.437, AN.v.65; Snp.p.139 (˚e nissinna sitting in the open) Ja.i.29, Ja.i.215; Pp.57.</p><p>abhi + avakāsa</p>

abbhokāsi|abbhokaasi|abbhokasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhokāsin">abbhokāsin</a></p><p>(an ascetic or bhikkhu) living in the open, in the open air.</p>

abbhokāsika|abbhokaasika|abbhokasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasika</span></p><p>(an ascetic or bhikkhu) living in the open, in the open air.</p>

abbhokāsika|abbhokaasika|abbhokasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the open air, one who lives in the open, the practice of certain ascetics. DN.i.167; MN.i.282; AN.iii.220; Vin.v.131, Vin.v.193; Ja.iv.8 (+ nesajjika); Pp.69; Mil.20, Mil.342. (One of the 13 Dhutaṃgas). See also Mnd.188; Cnd.587.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> the practice or system of the “campers-out” Mnd.558 (so read for abbhokāsi-kankhā, cp. Mnd.188).</li></ul><p>fr. abbhokāsa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a></p>

abbhokāsikanga|abbhokaasikanga|abbhokasikanga
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasikanga</span></p><p><strong>Abbhokāsik’anga:</strong> ‘living in the open air’, is one of the ascetic means to purification <em>dhutānga</em>.</p>

abbhokāsin|abbhokaasin|abbhokasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhokaasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhokāsin">abbhokāsin</a></p><p>(an ascetic or bhikkhu) living in the open, in the open air.</p>

abbhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhuṃ">abbhuṃ</a></p><p>an exclamation of horror or fright or aversion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhuṃ">abhuṃ</a></p>

abbhu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>unprofitableness, idleness, nonsense Ja.v.295 (= abhūti avaḍḍhi C.).</p><p>a + <em><a href="bword://bh%C5%AB">bhū</a></em> most likely = Vedic abhva and P. abbhuṃ, see also <em><a href="bword://abbh%C4%81matta">abbhāmatta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhū">bhū</a>, <a href="bword://abbhāmatta">abbhāmatta</a></p>

abbhucittajāta|abbhucittajaata|abbhucittajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhucittajaata</span></p><p>astonished</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuta">abbhuta</a></p>

abbhudayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>rises (as the sun); rises over</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudeti">abbhudeti</a></p>

abbhuddhunāti|abbhuddhunaati|abbhuddhunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuddhunaati</span></p><p>to shake very much Vv.64#9 (= adhikaṃ uddhunāti Vv-a.278).</p><p>abhi + ud + dhunāti</p>

abbhudeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>rises (as the sun); rises over</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudayati">abbhudayati</a></p>

abbhudeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go out over, to rise AN.ii.50, AN.ii.51 (opp. atthaṃ eti, of the sun)<br />ppr <em>abbhuddayaṃ</em> Vv.64#17 (= abhi-uggacchanto Vv-a.280; abbhusayaṃ ti pi pāṭho).</p><p>abhi + ud + eti</p>

abbhudāharati|abbhudaaharati|abbhudaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudaaharati</span></p><p>to bring towards, to fetch, to begin or introduce (a conversation) MN.ii.132.</p><p>abhi + ud + ā + harati</p>

abbhudāhasi|abbhudaahasi|abbhudahasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudaahasi</span></p><p>(he) uttered, introduced (a subject of conversation).</p>

abbhudīrayati|abbhudiirayati|abbhudirayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudiirayati</span></p><p>raises (the voice); utters</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudīreti">abbhudīreti</a></p>

abbhudīrayi|abbhudiirayi|abbhudirayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudiirayi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudīrayati">abbhudīrayati</a></p>

abbhudīresuṃ|abbhudiiresu.m|abbhudiresum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudiiresu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudīreti">abbhudīreti</a></p>

abbhudīreti|abbhudiireti|abbhudireti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudiireti</span></p><p>raises (the voice); utters</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudīrayati">abbhudīrayati</a></p>

abbhudīreti|abbhudiireti|abbhudireti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhudiireti</span></p><p>to raise the voice, to utter Thig.402; DN-a.i.61; Sdhp.514.</p><p>abhi + ud + īreti</p>

abbhuggacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes forth, goes out; goes out to meet; rises; spreads abroad.</p>

abbhuggacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go forth, go out, rise into DN.i.112, DN.i.127; AN.iii.252 (kitti-saddo a.) Pp.36. ger. <em>-gantvā</em> Ja.i.88 (ākāsaṃ), Ja.i.202; Dhp-a.iv.198 aor. <em>-gañchi</em> MN.i.126 (kittisaddo); Ja.i.93<br />pp <em><a href="bword://abbhuggata">abbhuggata</a></em>.</p><p>abhi + ud + gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggata">abbhuggata</a></p>

abbhuggacchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggacchissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggacchuṃ|abbhuggacchu.m|abbhuggacchum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuggacchu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggahetvā|abbhuggahetvaa|abbhuggahetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuggahetvaa</span></p><p>pulling up.</p>

abbhuggamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(nt<br />■ adj.) going out over, rising over (c. acc.) Pv-a.65 (candaṃ nabhaṃ abbhuggamanaṃ; so read for T. abbhuggamānaṃ).</p><p>fr. abbhuggacchati</p>

abbhuggantvā|abbhuggantvaa|abbhuggantva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuggantvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>gone forth; gone out to meet; risen; spread abroad (about, acc. or gen.), generally heard</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>gone forth, gone out, risen DN.i.88 (kitti-saddo a., cp. Dhp-a.i.146: sadevakaṃ lokaṃ ajjhottharitvā uggato), DN.i.107 (saddo); Snp.p.103 (kittisaddo).</p><p>pp. of abbhuggacchati</p>

abbhuggañchi|abbhugga~nchi|abbhugganchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhugga~nchi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggañchuṃ|abbhugga~nchu.m|abbhugganchum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhugga~nchu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhuggañchīti|abbhugga~nchiiti|abbhugganchiti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhugga~nchiiti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuggacchati">abbhuggacchati</a></p>

abbhujjalana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>breathing out fire, spitting fire.</p>

abbhujjalana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>breathing out fire, i.e. carrying fire in one’s month (by means of a charm) DN.i.11 (= mantena mukhato aggi-jala-nīharaṇaṃ DN-a.i.97).</p><p>abhi + ud + jalana, from <em>jval</em></p>

abbhukkirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>sprinkles; besprinkles; scatters up over.</p>

abbhukkirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to sprinkle over, to rinse (with water) DN.ii.172 (cakkaratanaṃ; neither with Morris J.P.T.S. 1886, 131 “give up”, nor with trsl. of Ja.ii.311 “roll along”); Ja.v.390; Pv-a.75. Cp. <em>abbhokkirati</em>.</p><p>abhi + ud + kirati</p>

abbhukkiraṇa|abbhukkira.na|abbhukkirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhukkira.na</span></p><p>drawing out, pulling, in daṇḍa-sattha˚ drawing a stick or sword Cnd.576#4 (cp abbhokkiraṇa). Or is it <em>abbhuttīraṇa</em> (cp. uttiṇṇa outlet).</p><p>abhi + ud + <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

abbhunnadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>resounding; made to resound.</p>

abbhunnadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>resounding, resonant Thag.1065).</p><p>pp. of abhi + ud + nadati</p>

abbhunnamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>rises; springs up.</p>

abbhunnamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to, spring up, burst forth DN.ii.164<br />pp <em><a href="bword://abbhu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87ata">abbhuṇṇata</a></em> (&amp; ˚unnata), q.v.; Caus. <em>abbhunnāmeti</em> to stiffen, straighten out, hold up erect DN.i.120 (kāyaṃ one’s body); AN.ii.245 (id.); DN.i.126 (patodalaṭṭhiṃ; opp. <em><a href="bword://apan%C4%81meti">apanāmeti</a></em> to bend down).</p><p>abhi, + ud + namati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuṇṇata">abbhuṇṇata</a>, <a href="bword://apanāmeti">apanāmeti</a></p>

abbhunnameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>holds up, raises; makes erect</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhunnāmeti">abbhunnāmeti</a>, <a href="bword://abbhunnamati">abbhunnamati</a></p>

abbhunnamitvā|abbhunnamitvaa|abbhunnamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhunnamitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhunnamati">abbhunnamati</a></p>

abbhunnāmeti|abbhunnaameti|abbhunnameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhunnaameti</span></p><p>holds up, raises; makes erect</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhunnameti">abbhunnameti</a>, <a href="bword://abbhunnamati">abbhunnamati</a></p>

abbhussahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>effort, exertion, endeavor; encouragement.</p>

abbhussahanatā|abbhussahanataa|abbhussahanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhussahanataa</span></p><p>instigation, incitement Vin.ii.88.</p><p>abstr. fr. abhi + *utsahana, cp. ussāha</p>

abbhussakati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Abbhussakati &amp; ˚usukkati</p><p>to go out over, rise above (acc.), ascend, freq. in phrase <em>ādicco nabhaṃ abbhussakkamāno</em> MN.i.317; SN.iii.156 = Iti.20<br />■ See also SN.i.65; SN.v.44; AN.i.242 (same simile); AN.v.22 (id.).</p><p>abhi + ud + <em>ṣvaṣk</em>, see <em><a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></p>

abbhussakkamāna|abbhussakkamaana|abbhussakkamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhussakkamaana</span></p><p>rising towards; ascending to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhussukkamāna">abbhussukkamāna</a></p>

abbhusseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rise; variant reading at Vv.64#17 according to Vv-a.280: abbhuddayaṃ (see <em><a href="bword://abbhudeti">abbhudeti</a></em> abbhussayan ti pi pāṭho.</p><p>abhi + ud + seti of <em>śī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhudeti">abbhudeti</a></p>

abbhussukkamāna|abbhussukkamaana|abbhussukkamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhussukkamaana</span></p><p>rising towards; ascending to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhussakkamāna">abbhussakkamāna</a></p>

abbhusukkati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Abbhussakati &amp; ˚usukkati</p><p>to go out over, rise above (acc.), ascend, freq. in phrase <em>ādicco nabhaṃ abbhussakkamāno</em> MN.i.317; SN.iii.156 = Iti.20<br />■ See also SN.i.65; SN.v.44; AN.i.242 (same simile); AN.v.22 (id.).</p><p>abhi + ud + <em>ṣvaṣk</em>, see <em><a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></p>

abbhusūyaka|abbhusuuyaka|abbhusuyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhusuuyaka</span></p><p>indignant at, impatient of.</p>

abbhusūyaka|abbhusuuyaka|abbhusuyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhusuuyaka</span></p><p>adjective zealous, showing zeal, endeavouring in (-˚) Pgdp.101. Abbhussakati &amp; usukkati;</p><p>abhi + usūyā + ka</p>

abbhuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) astonishing, wonderful; out of the ordinary, strange.</li><li>(n.) a wonderful thing, a marvel.</li></ol>

abbhuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Abbhuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adj. nt. terrifying, astonishing; strange, exceptional puzzling, extraordinary, marvellous, supernormal Described as a term of surprise &amp; consternation (vimhay;’ āvahass’ adhivacanaṃ DN-a.i.43 &amp; Vv-a.329) &amp; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “something that is not” or “has not been before”, viz abhūtaṃ Thag-a.233; abhūta-pubbatāya abbhutaṃ Vv-a.191 Vv-a.329; abhūta-pubbaṃ DN-a.i.43.</p><ol><li>(adj.) wonderful marvellous etc. Snp.681 (kiṃ ˚ṃ, comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with lomahaṃsana) Ja.iv.355 (id.); Thig.316 (abbhutaṃ vata vācaṃ bhāsasi = acchariyaṃ Thag-a.233); Vv.44#9 (˚dassaneyya); Sdhp.345, Sdhp.496.</li><li>(nt.) the wonderful, a wonder, marvel SN.iv.371, also in ˚dhamma (see Cpd.). Very freq. in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>acchariyaṃ</em> and a part. of exclamation, viz <em>acchariyaṃ bho abbhutaṃ bho</em> wonderful indeed &amp; beyond comprehension, strange &amp; stupefying DN.i.206; acch. vata bho abbh. <em>vata bho</em> DN.i.60; acch. bhante abbh. AN.ii.50 aho acch. aho abbh. Ja.i.88; acch. vata abbh. vata Vv.83#16<br />■ Thus also in phrase <em>acchariyā abbhutā dhammā</em> wonderful &amp; extraordinary signs or things MN.iii.118, MN.iii.125; AN.ii.130; AN.iv.198; Mil.8; and in <em>acchariya-abbhutacitta-jāta</em> dumbfounded &amp; surprised Ja.i.88; Dhp-a.iv.52; Pv-a.6, Pv-a.50.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> mysterious phenomenon, something wonderful supernormal; designation of one of the nine angas or divisions of the Buddhist Scriptures (see <em><a href="bword://nava">nava</a></em> B 2; Vin.iii.8; MN.i.133; AN.ii.103; AN.iii.86, AN.iii.177; Pp.43; Mil.344; Pv-a.2, etc.</li></ul><p>*Sk. adbhuta which appears to be constructed from the Pāli &amp; offers like its companion *āścarya (acchariya abbhuta see below) serious difficulties as to etym. The most probable solution is that P. abbhuta is a secondary adj<br />■ formation from abbhuṃ which in itself is nt. of abbha = Vedic abhva (see etym. under abbhāmatta and cp. abbhu, abbhuṃ &amp; J.P.T.S. 1889, 201). In meaning abbhuta is identical with Vedic abhva contrary to what usually happens, i.e. striking, abnormal, gruesome horrible etc.; &amp; that its significance as a + ; <em><a href="bword://bh%C5%AB">bhū</a></em> (“unreal?”) is felt in the background is also evident from the traditional etym. of the Pāli Commentators (see below) See also acchariya</p><p>Abbhuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a bet, a wager, only in phrase <em>abbhutaṃ karoti</em> (sahassena) to make a bet or to bet (a thousand i.e. kahāpaṇa’s or pieces of money) Vin.iii.138; Vin.iv.5; Ja.i.191; Ja.v.427; Ja.vi.192; Pv-a.151; &amp; in phrase pañcahi sahassehi abbhutaṃ hotu Ja.vi.193.</p><p>= abbhuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in the sense of invoking strange powers in gambling, thus being under direct spell of the “unknown”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://nava">nava</a>, <a href="bword://bhū">bhū</a></p>

abbhutadhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the name of one of the nine categories (<em>aṅgas</em>) of the scriptures</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuta">abbhuta</a></p>

abbhutaṃ karoti|abbhuta.m karoti|abbhutam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuta.m karoti</span></p><p>makes a wager; lays a bet.</p>

abbhutaṃ|abbhuta.m|abbhutam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuta.m</span></p><p>is used often as an exclamation (frequently combined with acchariyam): how wonderful! what an astonishing thing!</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuta">abbhuta</a></p>

abbhuyyāsi|abbhuyyaasi|abbhuyyasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuyyaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuyyāti">abbhuyyāti</a></p>

abbhuyyāta|abbhuyyaata|abbhuyyata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuyyaata</span></p><p>who has marched out (against, acc.)</p><p>See also: abhi\-uyyāta, <a href="bword://abbhuyyāti">abbhuyyāti</a></p>

abbhuyyāta|abbhuyyaata|abbhuyyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuyyaata</span></p><p>marched against, attacked Vin.i.342; MN.ii.124.</p><p>pp. of abbhuyyāti</p>

abbhuyyāti|abbhuyyaati|abbhuyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuyyaati</span></p><p>marches against.</p>

abbhuyyāti|abbhuyyaati|abbhuyyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhuyyaati</span></p><p>to go against, to go against, to march (an army) against, to attack SN.i.82 (aor ˚uyyāsi)<br />pp <em>abbhuy</em> <em>y</em> <em>āta</em> (q.v.).</p><p>abhi + up + yāti of <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: yā</p>

abbhuṃ|abbhu.m|abbhum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abbhuṃ">abbhuṃ</a></p><p>an exclamation of horror or fright or aversion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhuṃ">abhuṃ</a></p>

abbhuṃ|abbhu.m|abbhum
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.m</span></p><p>(interj.) alas, terrible, dreadful, awful (excl. of fright &amp; shock) Vin.ii.115 (Bdhgh. explains as “utrāsa-vacanam-etaṃ”); MN.i.448. See also <em>abbhu &amp; abbhuta</em>.</p><p>Vedic abhvaṃ, nt. of abhva, see expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> under abbhamatta. Not quite correct Morris J.P.T.S. 1889, 201: abbhuṃ = ā + bhuk; cp also abbhuta</p>

abbhuṇha|abbhu.nha|abbhunha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.nha</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>very hot Dhp-a.ii.87 (variant reading accuṇha).</li><li>quite hot, still warm (of milk) Dhp-a.ii.67.</li></ol><p>ahhi + uṇha</p>

abbhuṇṇata|abbhu.n.nata|abbhunnata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.n.nata</span></p><p>standing up, held up, erect Ja.v.156 (in <em>abbhuṇṇatatā</em> state of being erect. stiffness), Ja.v.197 (˚unnata; variant reading abbhantara, is reading correct?).</p><p>pp. of abbhunnamati</p>

abbhuṭṭhāsi|abbhu.t.thaasi|abbhutthasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.t.thaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuṭṭhāti">abbhuṭṭhāti</a></p>

abbhuṭṭhāti|abbhu.t.thaati|abbhutthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.t.thaati</span></p><p>rises to go towards; starts for.</p>

abbhuṭṭhāti|abbhu.t.thaati|abbhutthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhu.t.thaati</span></p><p>(˚ṭṭhahati) to get up to, proceed to, DN.i.105 (cankamaṃ).</p><p>abhi + ud + <em>sthā</em></p>

abbhācikkhanta|abbhaacikkhanta|abbhacikkhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaacikkhanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhācikkhati">abbhācikkhati</a></p>

abbhācikkhati|abbhaacikkhati|abbhacikkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaacikkhati</span></p><p>accuses (falsely); slanders; misrepresents.</p>

abbhācikkhati|abbhaacikkhati|abbhacikkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaacikkhati</span></p><p>to accuse, slander, calumniate DN.i.161; DN.iii.248, DN.iii.250; MN.i.130, MN.i.368, MN.i.482 MN.iii.207; AN.i.161.</p><p>Intens. of abbhākkhāti</p>

abbhācikkhi|abbhaacikkhi|abbhacikkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaacikkhi</span></p><ol start="2"><li>sg.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhācikkhati">abbhācikkhati</a></p>

abbhācikkhiṃ|abbhaacikkhi.m|abbhacikkhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaacikkhi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhācikkhati">abbhācikkhati</a></p>

abbhāgamana|abbhaagamana|abbhagamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaagamana</span></p><p>coming, arrival; approach.</p>

abbhāgamana|abbhaagamana|abbhagamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaagamana</span></p><p>coming arrival, approach Vin.iv.221.</p><p>abhi + ā + gamana; cp. Sk. abhyāgama</p>

abbhāgata|abbhaagata|abbhagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaagata</span></p><p>come; arrived (as a guest); an (uninvited) guest.</p>

abbhāgata|abbhaagata|abbhagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaagata</span></p><p>having arrived or come; (m.) a guest, stranger Vv.1#5 (= abhi-āgata, āgantuka Vv-a.24).</p><p>abhi + ā + gata</p>

abbhāghāta|abbhaaghaata|abbhaghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaaghaata</span></p><p>slaughtering-place Vin.iii.151 (+ āghāta).</p><p>abhi + āghāta</p>

abbhāghātanissita|abbhaaghaatanissita|abbhaghatanissita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaaghaatanissita</span></p><p>situated near a place of execution.</p>

abbhāhana|abbhaahana|abbhahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaahana</span></p><p>in <em>udaka˚</em>; the pulling up or drawing up of water Vin.ii.318 (Bdhgh on Cullavagga v.16, 2, corresponding to udaka-vāhana on p. 122).</p><p>either = abbāhana or āvāhana</p>

abbhāhata|abbhaahata|abbhahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaahata</span></p><p>struck; afflicted (with); impeded.</p>

abbhāhata|abbhaahata|abbhahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaahata</span></p><p>struck, attacked, afflicted SN.i.40 (maccunā); Thag.448; Snp.581; Ja.vi.26 Ja.vi.440; Vism.31, Vism.232; DN-a.i.140, DN-a.i.147; Dhp-a.iv.25.</p><p>abhi + ā + hata, pp. of <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

abbhākuṭika|abbhaaku.tika|abbhakutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaaku.tika</span></p><p>adjective not frowning, genial Vin.iii.181 (but here spelt bhākuṭikabhākuṭika); DN.i.116, cp. DN-a.i.287; Dhp-a.iv.8 (as variant reading T. has abbhokuṭika).</p><p>a + bhākuṭi + ka; Sk. bhrakuṭi frown</p>

abbhāmatta|abbhaamatta|abbhamatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaamatta</span></p><p>adjective monstrous dreadful, enormous, “of the size of a large cloud (thus C. on SN.i.205 &amp; Ja.iii.309) SN.i.205 = Thag.652 (variant reading abbha˚ &amp; abbhāmutta) = Ja.iii.309 (variant reading ˚mutta).</p><p>abbhā + matta (?) according to the Pāli Com.; but more likely = Vedic abhva huge, enormous monstrous, with ā metri causa. On abhva (a + <em><a href="bword://bh%C5%AB">bhū</a></em> what is contradictory to anything that is) cp. abbhuta abbhuṃ, and see Walde, Lat. Wtb. under dubius</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhū">bhū</a></p>

abbhāna|abbhaana|abbhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaana</span></p><p>the act of rehabilitation of a <em>bhikkhu</em> who has been temporarily expelled; re-admittance.</p>

abbhāna|abbhaana|abbhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbhaana</span></p><p>coming back, rehabilitation of a bhikkhu who has undergone a penance for an expiable offence Vin.i.49 (˚âraha), Vin.i.53 (id.), Vin.i.143 Vin.i.327; Vin.ii.33, Vin.ii.40, Vin.ii.162; AN.i.99<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://abbheti">abbheti</a></em>.</p><p>abhi + āyana of ā + <em>yā (i)</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbheti">abbheti</a></p>

abbocchinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>uninterrupted; unbroken.</p>

abbocchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://abboki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">abbokiṇṇa</a></em> 2 and <em>abbhochinna</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbokiṇṇa">abbokiṇṇa</a></p>

abbohārika|abbohaarika|abboharika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abbohaarika</span></p><p>adjective not of legal or conventional status, i.e</p><ol><li>negligible, not to be decided Vin.iii.91, Vin.iii.112 (see also <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 361 n. 4)</li><li>uncommon, extraordinary Ja.iii.309 (variant reading BB abbho˚); Ja.v.271, Ja.v.286 (Kern: ineffective).</li></ol><p>a + vi + ava + hārika of voharati</p>

abbokiṇṇa|abboki.n.na|abbokinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abboki.n.na</span></p><ol><li>filled MN.i.387 (paripuṇṇa +); Dhp-a.iv.182 (pañca jātisatāni a.).</li><li><a href="bword://abbocchinna">abbocchinna</a>*, a + vi + ava + chinna] uninterrupted, constant, as <em>-ṃ</em> adv. in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>satataṃ samitaṃ</em> AN.iv.13 = AN.iv.145; Kv.401 (variant reading abbhokiṇṇa), cp. also <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 231 n.1 (abbokiṇṇa undiluted?); Vb.320.</li><li>doubtful spelling at Vin.iii.271 (Bdhgh on Pārāj. iii.1, 3).</li></ol><p>= <em><a href="bword://abbhoki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">abbhokiṇṇa</a></em>, abhi + ava + kiṇṇa, cp. abhikiṇṇa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbocchinna">abbocchinna</a>, <a href="bword://abbhokiṇṇa">abbhokiṇṇa</a></p>

abbuda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A period of suffering in Avīci, the time taken to remove twenty cartloads of tila-seeds, taking one seed at the end of each century. Snp.p.126 SN.i.152</p><p>5hell</p>

abbuda
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>the foetus in the 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> months after conception the 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of the five prenatal stages of development, viz <em>kalala, abbuda, pesi, ghana, pasākha</em> Mnd.120; Mil.40 Vism.236.</li><li>a tumour, canker, sore Vin.iii.294, Vin.iii.307 (only in Samantapāsādikā; both times as <em>sāsanassa a)</em>.</li><li>a very high numeral, appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> exclusively to the denotation of a vast period of suffering in Purgatory; in this sense used as adj. of Niraya (abbudo nirayo the “vast-period hell, cp. nirabbuda). SN.i.149 = AN.ii.3 (chattiṃsati pañca ca abbudāni); SN.i.152 = AN.v.173 = Snp.p.126 (cp. Snp-a.476: abbudo nāma koci pacceka-nirayo n’ atthi, Avīcimhi yeva abbuda-gaṇanāya paccanokāso pana abbudo nirayo ti vutto; see also <em>Kindred Sayings</em> p. 190); Ja.iii.360 (sataṃ ninnahuta-sahassānaṃ ekaṃ abbudaṃ).</li><li>a term used for “hell” in the riddle SN.i.43 (kiṃsu lokasmiṃ abbudaṃ “who are they who make a hell on earth” Mrs Rh. D. The answer is “thieves”, so we can scarcely take it in meaning of 2 or 3. The C. has vināsa-karaṇaṃ.</li></ol><p>etym. unknown, orig. meaning “swelling”, the Sk. form arbuda seems to be a trsl. of P. abbuda</p>

abbudda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; m(fn).</i></p><ol><li><p>(n.) a swelling;</p><ol><li>the human embryo in the second week after conception.</li><li>(metaph.) a tumour, a cancer</li></ol></li><li><ol><li>(n.) a certain high numeral; a great number (of years)</li><li>(m.) the name of a hell, or place in Avīci, where one suffers for an abbuda of years.</li></ol></li></ol>

abbuhana
