<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.m</span></p><p>repeatedly, again and again; constantly.</p>

abhiṇhaṃ|abhi.nha.m|abhinham
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.m</span></p><p>adverb repeatedly, continuous, often MN.i.442 (˚āpattika a habitual offender), MN.i.446 (˚kāraṇa continuous practice); Snp.335 (˚saṃvāsa continuous living together); Ja.i.190; Pp.32; Dhp-a.ii.239; Vv-a.116 (= abhikkhaṇa), Vv-a.207, Vv-a.332; Pv-a.107 (= abhikkhaṇaṃ). Cp. <em><a href="bword://abhi%E1%B9%87haso">abhiṇhaso</a></em>.</p><p>contracted form of abhikkhaṇaṃ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhiṇhaso">abhiṇhaso</a></p>

abhiṇhaṃdassana|abhi.nha.mdassana|abhinhamdassana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.mdassana</span></p><p>frequently seeing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhiṇhadassana">abhiṇhadassana</a>, <a href="bword://abhiṇhaṃ">abhiṇhaṃ</a></p>

abhiṇhaṃkāraṇa|abhi.nha.mkaara.na|abhinhamkarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.mkaara.na</span></p><p>(or ~ā f.) continuous training</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhiṇhaṃ">abhiṇhaṃ</a></p>

abhiṇhaṃsannipāta|abhi.nha.msannipaata|abhinhamsannipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.msannipaata</span></p><p>holding frequent assemblies</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhiṇhasannipāta">abhiṇhasannipāta</a>, <a href="bword://abhiṇhaṃ">abhiṇhaṃ</a></p>

abhiṇhaṃāpattika|abhi.nha.maapattika|abhinhamapattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.nha.maapattika</span></p><p>repeatedly committing an offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhiṇhaṃ">abhiṇhaṃ</a></p>

abhiṭhāna|abhi.thaana|abhithana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.thaana</span></p><p>deadly crime (one of the six great crimes: <em>mātughāta</em>, <em>pitughāta</em>, <em>arahantaghāta</em>, <em>lohituppāda</em>, <em>saṅghabheda</em>, <em>aññasatthāruddesa</em>)</p>

abhiṭhāna|abhi.thaana|abhithana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhi.thaana</span></p><p>great or deadly crime Only at Snp.231 = Kp.vi.10 (quoted Kv.109). Six are there mentioned, &amp; are explained (Kp-a.189) as “matricide parricide, killing an Arahant, causing schisms, wounding a Buddha, following other teachers”. For other relations &amp; suggestions see Dhs trsl.267<br />■ See also ānantarika.</p><p>abhi + ṭhāna, cp. abhitiṭṭhati; lit. that which stands out above others</p>

abhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abhuṃ">abhuṃ</a></p><p>an exclamation of horror or fright or aversion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuṃ">abbhuṃ</a></p>

abhumma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective groundless, unfounded, unsubstantial, Ja.v.178; Ja.vi.495.</p><p>a + bhumma</p>

abhuṃ|abhu.m|abhum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhu.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://abhuṃ">abhuṃ</a></p><p>an exclamation of horror or fright or aversion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbhuṃ">abbhuṃ</a></p>

abhāsitvā|abhaasitvaa|abhasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhaasitvaa</span></p><p>uttering; having spoken.</p>

abhīruka|abhiiruka|abhiruka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhiiruka</span></p><p>adjective fearless DN-a.i.250.</p><p>a + bhīru + ka</p>

abhīta|abhiita|abhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhiita</span></p><p>adjective fearless Ja.vi.193. See also <em><a href="bword://abhida">abhida</a></em> 1.</p><p>a + bhīta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhida">abhida</a></p>

abhūta|abhuuta|abhuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">abhuuta</span></p><p>adjective not real, false, not true, usually as nt. ˚ṃ falsehood, lie, deceit Snp.387; Iti.37; instr <em>abhūtena</em> falsely DN.i.161.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81din">vādin</a></em> one who speaks falsely or tells lies Snp.661; Dhp.306 = Iti.42; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “ariy’ ûpavāda-vasena alika-vādin” Snp-a.478; as “tucchena paraṃ abhācikkhanto Dhp-a.iii.477.</li></ul><p>a + bhūta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vādin">vādin</a></p>

ability to acquire insight
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[ability to acquire insight]]Ability to acquire insight:</strong> cf. <em>ugghatitaññū,</em> <em>vipacitaññū</em></p>

abodes
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Abodes:</strong> <em>vihāra</em> The 4 Divine <strong>abodes:</strong> <em>brahma-vihāra</em> The 9 <strong>abodes</strong> of beings: <em>sattāvāsa</em></p>

absence
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Absence:</strong> natthi - Paccaya, is one of the 24 conditions paccaya,.</p>

absorption
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Absorption:</strong> see: <em>jhāna</em></p>

abstentions
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Abstentions:</strong> the 3: <em>virati</em></p>

aby
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Aby˚</p><p>see <em>avy˚</em>.</p>

acakkhuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without sight; blind; without insight, without understanding.</p>

acaṅkama|aca"nkama|acankama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aca"nkama</span></p><p>adjective not fit for walking, not level or even Thag.1174 (magga).</p><p>a + cankama</p>

acc-
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc</i></p><p>-</p><p>prefix is the usual form of the prefix <em>ati-</em> before vowels other than <em>ī</em>. In words of more than three syllables the vowel following <em>acc-</em> is often strengthened.</p>

acc
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Acc-</p><ol><li>a + c˚, e.g. accuta = a + cuta.</li><li>Assimilation group of<ol><li>ati + vowel.</li><li>c + cons. e.g. acci = arci.</li></ol></li></ol>

accagamā|accagamaa|accagama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accagamaa</span></p><p>overcame.</p>

accaguṃ|accagu.m|accagum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accagu.m</span></p><p>accaguṃaccagū</p><p>aor. 3 sg. surpassed, overcame.</p>

accagā|accagaa|accaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accagaa</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. pret. of ati-gacchati (q.v. for similar forms) he overcame, should or could overcome Snp.1040 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> wrongly as pp. = atikkanta at Cnd.10 and as atīta at Dhp-a.iv.494); Dhp.414.</p><p>ati + agā</p>

accahasi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor 3 sg.</i></p><p>of atiharati to bring over, to bring, to take Ja.iii.484 (= ativiya āhari C.).</p><p>fr. ati + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

accanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>beyond the (usual) end or limit; excessive, very great; perpetual; absolute, complete; most commonly <em>accantaṃ</em>, adv., excessively, exceedingly; perpetually. absolutely, completely.</p>

accanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective &amp; adverb ˚-</p><ol><li>uninterrupted, continuous, perpetual Ja.i.223; Mil.413; Vv-a.71; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.125, Pv-a.266; Sdhp.288.</li><li>final, absolute, complete; adv. thoroughly SN.i.130 (˚ṃ hataputtā’ mhi); SN.iii.13 = AN.i.291 sq.; AN.v.326 sq. (˚niṭṭha ˚yogakkhemin); Kv.586 (˚niyāmatā final assurance; cp Kvu trsl. 340).</li><li>(˚-) exceedingly, extremely, very much AN.i.145 (˚sukhumāla, extremely delicate), Mil.26 (id.); Snp.794 (˚suddhi = param ttha-accantasuddhi Snp-a.528); Thag.692 (˚ruci); Dhp.162 (˚dussīlya = ekanta Dhp-a.iii.153).</li></ol><p>ati + anta, lit. “up to the end”</p>

accantabrahmacāri|accantabrahmacaari|accantabrahmacari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accantabrahmacaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://accantabrahmacārin">accantabrahmacārin</a></p><p>living the holy or celibate life absolutely, constantly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantabrahmacārin|accantabrahmacaarin|accantabrahmacarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accantabrahmacaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://accantabrahmacārin">accantabrahmacārin</a></p><p>living the holy or celibate life absolutely, constantly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantadussīlya|accantadussiilya|accantadussilya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accantadussiilya</span></p><p>extremely bad conduct</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantaniṭṭha|accantani.t.tha|accantanittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accantani.t.tha</span></p><p>of absolute, constant perfection or firmness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantapariyosāna|accantapariyosaana|accantapariyosana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accantapariyosaana</span></p><p>having an absolute, constant ideal</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantaruci
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having superior splendor, surpassing in splendor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantayogakkhemi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://accantayogakkhemin">accantayogakkhemin</a></p><p>having absolute, constant peace from exertion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accantayogakkhemin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://accantayogakkhemin">accantayogakkhemin</a></p><p>having absolute, constant peace from exertion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accanta">accanta</a></p>

accarucī|accarucii|accaruci
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accarucii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atirocati">atirocati</a></p>

accasara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>going beyond the limits (of proper behaviour), too self-sure, overbearing, arrogant, proud SN.i.239 (variant reading accayasara caused by prolepsis of foll. accaya); Ja.iv.6 (atisara); Dhp-a.iv.230 (= expecting too much).</li><li>going beyond the limits (of understanding), beyond grasp, transcendental (of <em><a href="bword://pa%C3%B1ha">pañha</a></em> a question) MN.i.304; SN.v.218 (variant reading SS for BB reading ajjhapara). Cp. accasārin.</li></ol><p>a form. fr. aor. accasari (ati + <em>sṛ</em>;), influenced in meaning by analogy of ati + a + sara <em>(smṛ)</em> Not with Morris (J.P.T.S. 1889, 200) a corruption of accaya + sara <em>(smṛ)</em>, thus meaning “mindful of a fault”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pañha">pañha</a></p>

accasari
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor 3 sg.</i></p><p>of atisarati to go beyond the limit, to go astray Ja.v.70.</p><p>fr. ati + <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

accasarā|accasaraa|accasara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accasaraa</span></p><p>give offense.</p>

accasarā|accasaraa|accasara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accasaraa</span></p><p>overbearing, pride, selfsurity Vb.358 (+ māyā). <em>Note.</em> In id. p. at Pp.23 we read <em><a href="bword://acch%C4%81dan%C4%81">acchādanā</a></em> instead of accasarā.</p><p>abstr. to accasara</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādanā">acchādanā</a></p>

accasarī|accasarii|accasari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accasarii</span></p><p>gave offense.</p>

accasārin|accasaarin|accasarin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accasaarin</span></p><p>adjective = accasara 1., aspiring too high Snp.8 sq. (yo nâccasārī, opp. to na paccasārī; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Snp-a.21 by yo nâtidhāvi, opp. na ohiyyi).</p>

accatari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atitarati">atitarati</a></p>

accatari
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://atitarati">atitarati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atitarati">atitarati</a></p>

accati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to praise, honour, celebrate Dāvs v.66 (<em>accayittha</em>, pret.)-pp <em><a href="bword://accita">accita</a></em>, q.v.</p><p>Vedic arcati, <em>ṛc</em>, orig. meaning to be clear &amp; to sing i.e. to sound clear, cp. arci</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accita">accita</a></p>

accatāri|accataari|accatari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accataari</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atitarati">atitarati</a></p>

accaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m. instr.</i></p><ol><li><p>(instr.) <em>~ena</em>, and, more rarely, abl. <em>~ā</em>, loc. <em>~e</em> (+ gen. or ifc),</p><ol><li>after the lapse (of) at the end (of), after.</li><li>after the death (of).</li></ol></li><li><p>going beyond (the norm); transgression; offense.</p></li></ol>

accaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(temporal) lapse, passing; passing away, end, death. Usually as instr. <strong>accayena</strong> after the lapse of, at the end or death of, after Vin.i.25; DN.ii.127 (rattiyā a.), DN.ii.154 (mam˚ when I shall be dead); MN.i.438 (temās˚ after 3 months); SN.i.69; Snp.102 (catunnaṃ māsānaṃ), Snp.p.110 (rattiyā); Ja.i.253 (ekāha-dvīh˚), Ja.i.291 (katipāh˚ after a few days); Pv-a.47 (katipāh˚), Pv-a.82 (dasamās˚), Pv-a.145 (vassasatānaṃ)</li><li>(modal) passing or getting over, overcoming, conquering, only in phrase <em>dur-accaya</em> difficult to overcome, of kāmapanka Snp.945 (= dur-atikkamanīya Snp-a.568), of sanga Snp.948: taṇhā Dhp.336; sota Iti.95</li><li>(fig.) going beyond (the norm) transgression, offence Vin.i.133 (thull˚ a grave offence), Vin.i.167 (id.); Vin.ii.110, Vin.ii.170; esp. in foll. phrases: <em>accayo maṃ accagamā</em> a fault has overcome me, i.e. has been committed by me (in confession formula) DN.i.85 (= abhibhavitvā pavatto has overwhelmed me DN-a.i.236); AN.i.54; MN.i.438 (id.); <em>accayaṃ accayato passati</em> to recognise a breach of the regulation as such Vin.i.315; AN.i.103 AN.ii.146 sq.; <em>-ṃ deseti</em> to confess the transgression SN.i.239 <em>-ṃ accayato paṭigaṇhāti</em> to accept (the confession of) the fault, i.e. to pardon the transgression, in confessionformula at DN.i.85 = (Vin.ii.192; MN.i.438 etc.). In the same sense <em>accaya-paṭiggahaṇa</em> pardon, absolution Ja.v.380 <em>accayena desanaṃ paṭigaṇhāti</em> Ja.i.379; <em>accayaṃ khamati</em> to forgive Mil.420.</li></ol><p>from acceti, ati + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, going on or beyond; cp. Sk. atyaya</p><p>See also: accayena, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

accayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>passes (of time), elapses; passes by, eludes; passes by, neglects.</li><li>goes beyond; oversteps; overcomes; becomes free of.</li></ol>

accayato deseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>confesses to an offense (as being such)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayato passati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>recognizes an offense (as being such)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayato paṭigaṇhāti|accayato pa.tiga.nhaati|accayato patiganhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accayato pa.tiga.nhaati</span></p><p>accepts (the confession of) the fault, pardons the offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayaṃ deseti|accaya.m deseti|accayam deseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accaya.m deseti</span></p><p>confesses to an offense (as being such)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayaṃ passati|accaya.m passati|accayam passati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accaya.m passati</span></p><p>recognizes an offense (as being such)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayaṃ paṭigaṇhāti|accaya.m pa.tiga.nhaati|accayam patiganhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accaya.m pa.tiga.nhaati</span></p><p>accepts (the confession of) the fault, pardons the offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayo maṃ  accagamā|accayo ma.m  accagamaa|accayo mam  accagama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accayo ma.m  accagamaa</span></p><p>accayo maṃ (no) accagamā</p><p>(?) a fault has overcome me (us), i.e. has been committed by me (us)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accayo maṃ no accagamā|accayo ma.m no accagamaa|accayo mam no accagama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accayo ma.m no accagamaa</span></p><p>accayo maṃ (no) accagamā</p><p>(?) a fault has overcome me (us), i.e. has been committed by me (us)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></p>

accaṅkusa|acca"nkusa|accankusa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acca"nkusa</span></p><p>beyond the control of the goad.</p>

accaṅkusa|acca"nkusa|accankusa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acca"nkusa</span></p><p>adjective beyond the reach of the goad DN.ii.266 (nāga).</p><p>ati + ankusa</p>

acceka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>urgent, pressing; irregular.</p>

acceka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= accāyika, special; <em>-cīvara</em> a special robe Vin.iii.261; cp. Vin Texts i.29#3.</p>

accekacīvara|accekaciivara|accekacivara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accekaciivara</span></p><p>robe or material for a robe given not at the usual time (because of pressing reasons of the giver)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acceka">acceka</a></p>

accepts
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>acknowledges; is fit for is fitting, applies.</p>

access-concentration
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Access-concentration:</strong> see: <em>samādhi</em></p>

access
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Access:</strong> Moment of access into one-pointed concentration: see: <em>javana</em></p>

acceti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to pass (of time), to go by, to elapse Thag.145 (accayanti ahorattā).</li><li>to overcome to get over Mil.36 (dukkhaṃ)</li></ol><p>caus <em><a href="bword://acceti">acceti</a></em> to <em>make</em> go on (loc.), to put on Ja.vi.17 (sūlasmiṃ; C. āvuṇeti) but at this passage prob. to be read <em><a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ati + eti fr. <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acceti">acceti</a>, <a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

accha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. clear; pure, clean; pellucid; transparent.</p><p>accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a bear.</p>

accha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective clear, transparent Vin.i.206 (˚kañjika); DN.i.76 (maṇi = tanucchavi DN-a.i.221), DN.i.80 (udakapatta), DN.i.84 (udaka-rahada); MN.i.100; SN.ii.281 (˚patta); SN.iii.105 (id.); AN.i.9; Ja.ii.100 (udaka); Vv.79#10 (vāri); DN-a.i.113 (yāgu).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a></em> having clear water, with clear water (of lotus ponds) Vv.44#11; Vv.81#5; f.</li><li><em>-odikā</em> Vv.41#2 = Vv.60#2.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. accha, dial., to <em>ṛc</em> (see <em><a href="bword://accati">accati</a></em>), thus “shining”; cp. Sk. ṛkṣa bald, bare and Vedic ṛkvan bright Monier-Williams however takes it as a + cha fr. <em>chad</em> thus “not covered, not shaded”</p><p>Accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a bear Vin.i.200; AN.iii.101; Ja.v.197, Ja.v.406, Ja.v.416; Mil.23 Mil.149. At Ja.vi.507 accha figures as Name of an animal, but is in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> taken in the sense of accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (acchā nāma aghammigā C.). <em>Note.</em> Another peculiar form of accha is P. ikka (q.v.).</p><p>Vedic ṛkṣa = Gr. α ̓́ρκτος, Lat. ursus, Cymr. arth</p><p>Accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>= akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (a die) see <em><a href="bword://acci">acci</a></em>-bandha.</p><p>Accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective hurtful, painful, bad Dhp-a.iv.163 (˚ruja).</p><p>Ved. ṛkṣa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a>, <a href="bword://accati">accati</a>, <a href="bword://acci">acci</a></p>

acchaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Sloth Bear, also called <em><a href="bword://ikka">ikka</a></em>, <em>Melursus ursinus</em> Ja.vi.538. This medium-sized bear has a shaggy black coat, a white v-shaped mark on its breast and a long white snout. It has a slow lumbering gait but can attack swiftly and without provocation. Sloth bears sometimes attacked forest-dwelling monks A.iii.100. Monks were allowed to have a bear skin mat Vin.ii.174 and to use bear fat as a medicine Vin.i.200. Sloth bears have black hair and one cannot escape them even by climbing a tree Ja.vi.507. One Jātaka story describes villagers finding a bear near their village and attacking it with arrows, sticks and staffs to drive it away Ja.iv.327.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ikka">ikka</a></p>

acchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, a bear Ja.v.71.</p>

acchakañjīka|acchaka~njiika|acchakanjika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaka~njiika</span></p><p>rice gruel</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accha">accha</a></p>

acchambhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not frightened, undismayed, fearless Snp.42 (reading achambhin; Cnd.13 explains abhīru anutrāsi etc.); Ja.vi.322 (= nikkampa C.). See chambhin.</p><p>a + chambhin</p>

acchanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>clothed (with); covered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acchanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective covered with, clothed in, fig. steeped in (c. loe.) Ja jii.323 (lohite a. = nimugga C.) At DN.i.91 <em>nacchanna</em> is for na channa (see *<a href="bword://channa">channa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) not fair, not suitable or proper (paṭirūpa).</p><p>pp. of acchādeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://channa">channa</a></p>

acchara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>wonderful thing, a marvel; amazement</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchera">acchera</a></p>

accharika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(nt. or f.?) in <em>-ṃ vādeti</em> to make heavenly music (lit. the sounds of an accharā or heavenly nymph) AN.iv.265.</p><p>fr. accharā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

accharikā|accharikaa|accharika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accharikaa</span></p><p>snapping of the fingers.</p>

acchariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n. (m.)</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) wonderful, surprising, strange, marvelous; unusual, exceptional, of rare occurrence.</li><li>(n. (and m.)) a wonderful thing; a marvel.</li></ol>

acchariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun wonderful, surprising strange, marvellous DN.ii.155; MN.i.79; MN.iii.118, MN.iii.125, MN.iii.144 (an˚); SN.iv.371; AN.i.181; Mil.28, Mil.253; Dhp-a.iii.171; Pv-a.121; Vv-a.71 (an˚). As <em>nt</em>. often in exclamations how wonderful! what a marvel! Ja.i.223, Ja.i.279; Ja.iv.138 Ja.vi.94 (a. vata bho); Dhp-a.iv.51 (aho a.); Vv-a.103 (aho ti acchariyatthena nipāto). Thus freq. comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>abbhutaṃ</em> = how wonderful &amp; strange, marvellous, beyond comprehension, e.g. DN.i.2, DN.i.60, DN.i.206, DN.i.210; DN.ii.8; and in phrase <em>acchariyā abbhutā dhammā</em> strange &amp; wonderful things, i.e. wonderful signs, portents marvels, MN.iii.118 MN.iii.125; AN.iv.198; Mil.8; also as adj. in phrase <em>acchariyaabbhuta-(citta-)jātā</em> with their hearts full of wonder and surprise Dhp-a.iv.52; Pv-a.6, Pv-a.50<br />■ See also acchera accheraka.</p><p>cp. Sk. āścarya since Upanishads of uncertain etym<br />■ The conventional etym. of Pāli grammarians connects it with accharā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (which is prob. correct &amp; thus reduces Sk. āścarya to a Sanskritisation of acchariya) viz. Dhammapāla: anabhiṇha-ppavattitāya accharāpaharaṇa-yoggaṃ that which happens without a moment’s notice, at the snap of a finger; i.e. causally unconnected (cp. Goth. silda-leiks in similar meaning) Vv-a.329; and Buddhaghosa: accharā-yoggan ti acchariyaṃ accharaṃ paharituṃ yuttan ti attho DN-a.i.43</p>

acchariyamanussa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>wonderful exceptional man</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchariya">acchariya</a></p>

acchariyaṃ|acchariya.m|acchariyam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchariya.m</span></p><p>is used often as an exclamation (frequently combined with <em>abbhutaṃ</em>) how wonderful! what a wonderful thing! it’s a marvel!</p><p>See also: acchiraya</p>

accharā|accharaa|acchara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accharaa</span></p><p>celestial nymph, a female divinity.</p>

accharā|accharaa|acchara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accharaa</span></p><p>Accharā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine the snapping of the fingers, the bringing together of the finger-tips:</p><ol><li>(lit.) <em>accharaṃ paharati</em> to snap the fingers Ja.ii.447; Ja.iii.191; Ja.iv.124, Ja.iv.126 Ja.v.314; Ja.vi.366; Dhp-a.i.38, Dhp-a.i.424<br />■ As measure: as much as one may hold with the finger-tips, a pinch Ja.v.385; Dhp-a.ii.273 (˚gahaṇamattaṃ); cp. ekacchara-matta Dhp-a.ii.274.</li><li>(fig.) a finger’s snap, i.e. a short moment in <em>ek˚acchara-kkhaṇe</em> in one moment Mil.102, and in def. of acchariya (q.v.) at DN-a.i.43; Vv-a.329.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85gh%C4%81ta">saṅghāta</a></em> the snapping of the fingers as signifying a short duration of time, a moment, <em>˚matta</em> momentary only for one moment (cp. BSk. acchaṭāsanghāta Divy.142) AN.i.10, AN.i.34, AN.i.38; AN.iv.396; Thag.405; Thag.2, Thag.67 (expld at Thag-a.76 as ghaṭikāmattam pi khaṇaṃ angulipoṭhanamattam pi kālaṃ).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> the sound of the snapping of a finger Ja.iii.127.</li></ul><p>etym. uncertain, but certainly dialectical; Trenckner connects it with ācchurita (Notes 76); Childers compares Sk. akṣara (see <em><a href="bword://akkhara">akkhara</a></em>); there may be a connection with akkhaṇa in akkhaṇa-vedhin (cp. BSk. acchaṭā Divy.555), or possibly a relation to ā + <em>tsar</em>, thus meaning “stealthily”, although the primary meaning is “snapping, a quick sound”</p><p>Accharā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine a celestial nymph MN.i.253 (pl. accharāyo MN.ii.64; Thig.374 (= <strong>devaccharā</strong> Thag-a.252); Ja.v.152 sq (Alambusā a.) Vv.5#5 (= devakaññā Vv-a.37); Vv.17<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2;<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Vv.18#11 etc.; Dhp-a.iii.8, Dhp-a.iii.19; Pv-a.46 (<em>dev˚</em>); Mil.169 Sdhp.298.</p><p>Vedic apsaras = āpa, water + sarati, orig; water nymph</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://saṅghāta">saṅghāta</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://akkhara">akkhara</a>, devaccharā</p>

accharāsaṅghāta|accharaasa"nghaata|accharasanghata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accharaasa"nghaata</span></p><p>finger-snap; a moment.</p>

acchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to sit, to sit still Vin.i.289; AN.ii.15; Iti.120 (in set <em>carati tiṭṭhati a. sayati</em>, where otherwise nisinna stands for acchati) Vv.74#1 (= nisīdati Vv-a.298); Pv-a.4.</li><li>to stay remain, to leave alone Thag.936; Ja.iv.306.</li><li>to be behave, live Vin.ii.195; DN.i.102; SN.i.212; Vv.11#2; Pv.iii.3#1 (= nisīdati vasati Pv-a.188); Mil.88; Dhp-a.i.424 In this sense often pleonastic for finite verb, thus aggiṃ karitvā a. (= aggiṃ karoti) DN.i.102; aggiṃ paricaranto a (= aggiṃ paricarati) DN-a.i.270; tantaṃ pasārento a. (tantaṃ pasāreti) Dhp-a.i.424<br />pot <em>acche</em> Iti.110; aor <em><a href="bword://acchi">acchi</a></em> Vin.iv.308; Dhp-a.i.424.</li></ol><p>Vedic āsyati &amp; āste; <em>ās</em>; cp. Gr. η ̈ ̔σται</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchi">acchi</a></p>

acchecchi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of chindati “he has cut out or broken, has destroyed” (see also <em><a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></em> 3) in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>taṇhaṃ</em> MN.i.122; SN.i.12, SN.i.23, SN.i.127 (so read for acchejja); SN.iv.105, SN.iv.207. Iti.47; AN.iii.246, AN.iii.445; Dhp-a.iv.70 (gloss acchindi, for acchidda pret. of Dhp.351). The variant reading at all passages is <em>acchejji</em>, which is to be accounted for on graphological grounds, ch &amp; j being substituted in MSS. Kern (Toevoegselen s. v.) mistakes the form tries to explain acchejji as adj. = ati-ejin (ejā), acchecchi ati-icchin (icchā). The syntactical construction however clearly points to an aor.</p><p>Sk. acchaitsīt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></p>

acchedana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>robbing, plundering Ja.vi.544.</p><p>abstr. to acchindati</p>

acchejja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchejja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= a + chejja not to be destroyed, indestructible, see <em><a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></p>

acchera
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://acchariya">acchariya</a></em> wonderful, marvellous SN.i.181; Vv.84#13 (comp. accheratara); Pv.iii.5#1 (˚rūpa = acchariyasabhāva Pv-a.197); Sdhp.244, Sdhp.398.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchariya">acchariya</a></p>

accheraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://acchera">acchera</a></em> (acchariya) Ja.i.279; Bv.i.9 (pāṭihīraṃ).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchera">acchera</a></p>

accheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is; sits; remains, stays; continues (doing something); sits idle, sits doing nothing; (often used as an auxiliary with part, or absol.).</p>

acchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchati">acchati</a></p>

acchi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at SN.iv.290 is faulty spelling for acci (q.v.).</p>

acchibaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>laid out in squares or laid out in strips.</p>

acchidda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://chidda">chidda</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chidda">chidda</a></p>

acchijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchijja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(variant reading accheja) destroying (?) SN.i.127. Is the reading warranted? Cp. acchecchi.</p>

acchindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>tears off; removes, removes forcibly; tears away from; robs (someone, acc. or gen.); plunders; deprives (of).</p>

acchindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to remove forcibly, to take away, rob, plunder Vin.iv.247 (sayaṃ a. to appropriate); Ja.ii.422; Ja.iii.179; Ja.iv.343; Mil.20; Sdhp.122<br />ger <em>acchinditvā</em> Ja.ii.422; Dhp-a.i.349; Pv-a.241 (sayaṃ); &amp; <em>acchetvā</em> MN.i.434. Caus. ii. <em>acchindāpeti</em> to induce a person to theft Vin.iv.224, Vin.iv.247.</p><p>ā + chindati, lit. to break for oneself</p>

acchindi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchindimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchinditvā|acchinditvaa|acchinditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchinditvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchindāpesuṃ|acchindaapesu.m|acchindapesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchindaapesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindāpeti">acchindāpeti</a></p>

acchindāpeti|acchindaapeti|acchindapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchindaapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>removed forcibly, taken away; plundered.</li><li>robbed</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchindati">acchindati</a></p>

acchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective removed, taken away, stolen, robbed Vin.iv.278, Vin.iv.303; Ja.ii.78 Ja.iv.45; Ja.v.212.</p><p>ā + chinna, pp. of acchindati</p>

acchinnacīvara|acchinnaciivara|acchinnacivara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchinnaciivara</span></p><p>whose robe has been taken away or stolen</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchinna">acchinna</a></p>

acchinnacīvaraka|acchinnaciivaraka|acchinnacivaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchinnaciivaraka</span></p><p>(~-cīvarika)n. whose robe has been taken away or stolen</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchinnacīvara">acchinnacīvara</a></p>

acchisaṃ|acchisa.m|acchisam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchisa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchati">acchati</a></p>

acchitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp m(fn.) impers.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchati">acchati</a></p>

acchiva
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of plant Ja.vi.535.</p>

acchiva
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>certain species of tree (Hypanthera Moringa) Ja.vi.535.</p><p>*Sk. akṣiba and akṣība</p>

acchodaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having clear water; clear water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accha">accha</a></p>

acchodi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having clear water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accha">accha</a></p>

acchodika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having clear water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accha">accha</a></p>

acchupeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>inserts; puts on or in.</p>

acchupeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to procure or provide a hold, to insert, to put on or in Vin.i.290 (aggaḷaṃ), Vin.ii.112.</p><p>ā + chupeti, Caus. of chupati</p>

acchupīyati|acchupiiyati|acchupiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg (?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchupiiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchupeti">acchupeti</a></p>

acchādana|acchaadana|acchadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (mfn.?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadana</span></p><p>covering, clothing, cloth; protection.</p>

acchādana|acchaadana|acchadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadana</span></p><p>covering, clothing Thag.698; Mil.279<br />■ fig. protection, sheltering Ja.i.307.</p><p>fr. acchādeti</p>

acchādanā|acchaadanaa|acchadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadanaa</span></p><p>covering, hiding, concealment Pp.19, Pp.23<br />■ <em>Note.</em> In id. p. at Vb.358 we read <em><a href="bword://accasar%C4%81">accasarā</a></em> for acchādanā. Is the latter merely a gloss?</p><p>= prec.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accasarā">accasarā</a></p>

acchādayati|acchaadayati|acchadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadayati</span></p><p>covers; clothes or presents with (clothes, instr.); puts on (clothes, acc.).</p>

acchādesi|acchaadesi|acchadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acchādesuṃ|acchaadesu.m|acchadesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acchādeti|acchaadeti|acchadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadeti</span></p><p>to cover, to clothe, to put on DN.i.63 = Iti.75; Ja.i.254; Ja.iii.189; Ja.iv.318; Pp.57; Pv.i.10#5 (ger. acchādayitvāna); DN-a.i.181 (= paridahitvā); Pv-a.49, Pv-a.50<br />■ fig to envelop, to fill Ja.vi.581 (abbhaṃ rajo acchādesi dust filled the air)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://acchanna">acchanna</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + chādeti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, Caus. of <em>chad</em>, cp. BSk. ācchādayati jīvitena to keep alive Avs.i.300; Divy.136 Divy.137</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchanna">acchanna</a></p>

acchādetvā|acchaadetvaa|acchadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acchādita|acchaadita|acchadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadita</span></p><p>clothed (with)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acchādāpetvā|acchaadaapetvaa|acchadapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">acchaadaapetvaa</span></p><p>cause. absol. causing to put on; presenting; clothing (with)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchādeti">acchādeti</a></p>

acci-bandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Acci-bandha</p><p>adjective at Vin.i.287 is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh as caturassa-kedāra-baddha (“divided into short pieces” Vin Texts ii.207), i.e. with squares of irrigated fields. The vv.ll. are acca˚ and acchi˚, and we should prefer the conjecture <em>acchi-baddha</em> “in the shape of cubes or dice”, i.e. with square fields.</p><p>= accibaddha?</p>

acci
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">&amp;</i></p><p>(in verse); <em>accī</em> (f.) a ray of light, a beam, flame SN.iv.290 (spelt acchi), SN.iv.399; AN.iv.103; AN.v.9; Snp.1074 (vuccati jālasikhā Cnd.11); Ja.v.213; Mil.40; Thag-a.154 (dīp’) Sdhp.250.</p><p>Vedic arci m. &amp; arcis nt. &amp; f. to; <em>ṛc</em>, cp. accati</p>

accikā|accikaa|accika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accikaa</span></p><p>flame MN.i.74; SN.ii.99.</p><p>fr. acci</p>

accimant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective flaming, glowing, fiery; brilliant Thag.527; Ja.v.266; Ja.vi.248; Vv.38#8.</p><p>fr. acci, cp. Vedic arcimant &amp; arciṣmant</p>

accita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>honoured, praised, esteemed Ja.vi.180.</p><p>pp. of accati</p>

accodaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>too much water (opp. anodaka no water) Dhp-a.i.52.</p><p>ati + udaka</p>

accodara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>too much eating, greediness, lit. too much of a belly Ja.iv.279 (C. ati-udara).</p><p>ati + udara</p>

accodāta|accodaata|accodata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accodaata</span></p><p>too white, too pale.</p>

accogāḷha|accogaa.lha|accogalha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accogaa.lha</span></p><p>too extravagant.</p>

accogāḷha|accogaa.lha|accogalha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accogaa.lha</span></p><p>adjective too abundant, too plentiful (of riches), lit. plunged into AN.iv.282, AN.iv.287, AN.iv.323 sq.</p><p>ati + ogāḷha</p>

accokkaṭṭha|accokka.t.tha|accokkattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accokka.t.tha</span></p><p>(of a robe) pulled down too far.</p>

accuggacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rise out (of), ger. accuggamma DN.ii.38; AN.v.152 (in simile of lotus).</p><p>ati + uggacchati</p>

accuggamamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>rising out (of); being raised above.</p>

accuggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>risen out (of); raised high; high, tall; lofty; haughty.</p>

accuggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>very high or lofty Mil.346 (giri); Vv-a.197; Dhp-a.ii.65.</li><li>too high, i.e. too shrill or loud Ja.vi.133 (sadda), Ja.vi.516 (fig. = atikuddha very angry C.).</li></ol><p>ati + uggata</p>

accukkaṭṭha|accukka.t.tha|accukkattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">accukka.t.tha</span></p><p>(a a robe) pulled up too high.</p>

accumulation
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Accumulation:</strong> of Kamma see: <em>āyūhana</em></p>

accupati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.iv.250 read <em>accuppati</em>, aor. 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. of accuppatati to fall in between (lit. on to), to interfere (with two people quarelling). C. explains atigantvā uppati. There is no need for Kern’s corr. acchupati (Toev. s. v.).</p>

accupaṭṭhapeti|accupa.t.thapeti|accupatthapeti
