aggasavaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>chief disciple</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl.</i></p><p>before; in front; in the presence of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the state or condition of being the first, pre-eminence Pv-a.9, Pv-a.89.</p><p>abstr. of agga = Sk. agratvan</p>

aggatā|aggataa|aggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggataa</span></p><p>preeminence; chief position.</p>

aggatā|aggataa|aggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggataa</span></p><p>pre-eminence, prominence, superiority Kv.556 (˚ṃ gata); Dpvs.iv.1 (guṇaggataṃ gatā)<br />■ (adj.) <strong>mahaggata</strong> of great value or superiority DN.i.80; DN.iii.224.</p><p>abstr. of agga</p><p>See also: mahaggata</p>

aggava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aggavat">aggavat</a></p><p>of great eminence, excellent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective occupying the first place, of great eminence AN.i.70, AN.i.243.</p>

aggavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aggavat">aggavat</a></p><p>of great eminence, excellent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggavādi|aggavaadi|aggavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aggavādin">aggavādin</a></p><p>(one) who proclaims the highest, the best</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggavādin|aggavaadin|aggavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aggavādin">aggavādin</a></p><p>(one) who proclaims the highest, the best</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggañña|agga~n~na|agganna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga~n~na</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) chief, principal; primitive, original.</li><li>(n.) the beginning of things; origin</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggaḷa|agga.la|aggala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga.la</span></p><ol><li>a bolt or bar for fastening a door (or window); the doorpanel or door.</li><li>a patch</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggala">aggala</a></p>

aggaḷa|agga.la|aggala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga.la</span></p><p>Aggaḷa &amp; Aggaḷā</p><p>feminine (see <em>occasionally with l.</em>) a contrivance to fasten anything for security or obstruction:</p><ol><li>a bolt or cross-bar Vin.i.290; DN.i.89 (<em>-ṃ ākoteṭi</em> to knock upon the cross-bar; a. = kavāṭa DN-a.i.252); AN.iv.359 (id.) SN.iv.290; AN.i.101 = AN.i.137 = AN.iv.231. <em>(phusit˚</em>; with fastened bolts, securely shut Thag.385 (id.); Vin.iv.47; Ja.v.293 (<em>-ṃ uppīḷeti</em> to lift up the cross-bar.</li><li>a strip of cloth for strengthening a dress etc., a gusset Vin.i.290 (+ tunna), Vin.i.392 (Bdhgh on MV viii.21, 1); Ja.i.8 (+ tunna), Ja.vi.71 (<em>-ṃ datvā</em>); Vin.iv.121.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://d%C4%81na">dāna</a></em> putting in a gusset Ja.i.8.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a></em> the post or board, in which the cross-bar is fixed (cp. ˚vaṭṭi) MN.iii.95.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADi">vaṭṭi</a></em> = ˚phalaka Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.148.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C5%ABci">sūci</a></em> bolting pin MN.i.126.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. argala &amp; argalā to; <em>*areg</em> to protect, ward off, secure etc. as in Ags. reced house; <em>*aleg</em> in Sk. rakṣati to protect Gr. ἀλέςω id., Ags. ealh temple. Cp. also <em>*areq</em> in Gr.ἀρκέω = Lat. arceo, Orcus, Ohg rigil bolt.</p><p>See also: occasionally with l., <a href="bword://dāna">dāna</a>, <a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a>, <a href="bword://vaṭṭi">vaṭṭi</a>, <a href="bword://sūci">sūci</a></p>

aggaḷapura|agga.lapura|aggalapura
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga.lapura</span></p><p>A city where Revata went on his way from Soreyya to Sahajāti, prior to the Council of Vesālī. Vin.ii.300</p><p>???2town</p>

aggaḷā|agga.laa|aggala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga.laa</span></p><p>Aggaḷa &amp; Aggaḷā</p><p>feminine (see <em>occasionally with l.</em>) a contrivance to fasten anything for security or obstruction:</p><ol><li>a bolt or cross-bar Vin.i.290; DN.i.89 (<em>-ṃ ākoteṭi</em> to knock upon the cross-bar; a. = kavāṭa DN-a.i.252); AN.iv.359 (id.) SN.iv.290; AN.i.101 = AN.i.137 = AN.iv.231. <em>(phusit˚</em>; with fastened bolts, securely shut Thag.385 (id.); Vin.iv.47; Ja.v.293 (<em>-ṃ uppīḷeti</em> to lift up the cross-bar.</li><li>a strip of cloth for strengthening a dress etc., a gusset Vin.i.290 (+ tunna), Vin.i.392 (Bdhgh on MV viii.21, 1); Ja.i.8 (+ tunna), Ja.vi.71 (<em>-ṃ datvā</em>); Vin.iv.121.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://d%C4%81na">dāna</a></em> putting in a gusset Ja.i.8.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a></em> the post or board, in which the cross-bar is fixed (cp. ˚vaṭṭi) MN.iii.95.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADi">vaṭṭi</a></em> = ˚phalaka Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.148.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C5%ABci">sūci</a></em> bolting pin MN.i.126.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. argala &amp; argalā to; <em>*areg</em> to protect, ward off, secure etc. as in Ags. reced house; <em>*aleg</em> in Sk. rakṣati to protect Gr. ἀλέςω id., Ags. ealh temple. Cp. also <em>*areq</em> in Gr.ἀρκέω = Lat. arceo, Orcus, Ohg rigil bolt.</p><p>See also: occasionally with l., <a href="bword://dāna">dāna</a>, <a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a>, <a href="bword://vaṭṭi">vaṭṭi</a>, <a href="bword://sūci">sūci</a></p>

aggaṅkuraka|agga"nkuraka|aggankuraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agga"nkuraka</span></p><p>the top shoot</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>aggha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine price; worth.</p><p>aggha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter hospitality, respectful reception of a guest; an offering of water etc. to a guest.</p>

aggha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>price, value, worth, Mil.244; Mhvs.26, Mhvs.22; Mhvs.30, Mhvs.76; Vv-a.77<br />■ <strong>mahaggha</strong> (adj.) of great value Ja.iv.138; Ja.v.414; Ja.vi.209; Pv.ii.1#18. See also mahāraha. <em><a href="bword://appaggha">appaggha</a></em> (adj.) of little value Ja.iv.139; Ja.v.414 <em>-anaggha</em> (nt.) pricelessness, Ja.v.484; cattari anagghāni, the four priceless things, viz. setacchatta, nisīdanapallanka ādhāraka, pādapīṭhikā Dhp-a.iii.120, Dhp-a.iii.186. (adj.) priceless invaluable Ja.v.414; Mhvs.26, Mhvs.25; Dhp-a.iv.216<br />■ <em>agghena</em> (instr.) for the price of Vin.ii.52, cp. Bdhgh on p. 311, 312.</li><li>an oblation made to a guest DN.ii.240; Ja.iv.396 = Ja.iv.476.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81raka">kāraka</a></em> a valuator Ja.i.124.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pada">pada</a></em> valuableness Ja.v.473 (˚lakkhaṇaṃ nāma mantaṃ).</li></ul><p>see <em><a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></em></p><p>See also: mahaggha, <a href="bword://appaggha">appaggha</a>, <a href="bword://kāraka">kāraka</a>, <a href="bword://pada">pada</a>, <a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></p>

agghaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = aggha; worth, having the value of (-˚) Mhvs.30, Mhvs.77. <em>an˚</em>; priceless Mhvs.30, Mhvs.72.</p>

agghanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. having the value of, worth.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agghanikā">agghanikā</a></p>

agghanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) having the value of, equal to, worth Vin.iv.226; Ja.i.61 (satasahass˚), Ja.i.112; DN-a.i.80 (kahāpaṇ˚); Dhp-a.iii.120 (cuddasakoṭi˚); Mhvs.26, Mhvs.22; Mhvs.34, Mhvs.87<br />■ f. <em>-ikā</em> Ja.i.178 (satasahass˚).</p><p>fr. *agghana, abstr. to agghati</p>

agghanikā|agghanikaa|agghanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agghanikaa</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. having the value of, worth.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agghanaka">agghanaka</a></p>

agghaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective priceless, invaluable, beyond the reach of money Mil.192.</p><p>in function &amp; form grd. of agghati</p>

agghati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is worth, has the value of; deserves; frequently in phrase <em>kalaṃ na agghati (soḷasiṃ)</em>, is not worth a (sixteenth) part (of).</p>

agghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>intr. to be worth, to have the value of (acc.), to deserve Ja.i.112 (satasahassaṃ; aḍḍhamāsakaṃ); Ja.vi.174, Ja.vi.367 (padarajaṃ); Dhp-a.iii.35 (maṇin nâgghāma); Mhvs.32, Mhvs.28. Freq. in stock phrase <em>kalaṃ nāgghati (nāgghanti) soḷasiṃ</em> not to be worth the 16<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part of (cp. kalā) Vin.ii.156; SN.i.233; Dhp.70; Vv.20#7 (nânubhoti Vv-a.104), Vv.43#7; Ja.v.284<br />caus <em>agghāpeti</em> to value, to appraise, to have a price put on (acc.) Ja.i.124; Ja.iv.137, Ja.iv.278; Mil.192; Mhvs.27, Mhvs.23. Cp. agghāpanaka &amp; agghāpaniya.</p><p>Sk. arghati, <em>argh = arh</em> (see arhati), cp. Gr. ἀλφή reward, ἀλφάνω to deserve</p>

agghika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(-˚) an oblation, decoration or salutation in the form of garlands, flowers etc., therefore meaning “string, garland” (cp. Sinhalese ägä “festoon work”) Mhvs.19, Mhvs.38 (<em>pupph˚</em>;), Mhvs.34, Mhvs.73 (<em>ratan˚</em>;), Mhvs.34, Mhvs.76 (<em>dhaj˚</em>); Dāvs i.39 (<em>pupphamay˚</em>); v.51 (<em>kusum˚</em>;).</p><p>= agghiya</p>

agghiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) valuable, precious, worth Ja.vi.265 (maṇi); Dhp-a.ii.41 (ratan of jewel’s worth); Mhvs.30, Mhvs.92.</li><li>(nt.) a respectful oblation Ja.v.324 = Ja.vi.516; Dpvs.vi.65; vii.4.</li></ol><p>grd. form from agghati</p>

agghāpanaka|agghaapanaka|agghapanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agghaapanaka</span></p><p>valuator, appraiser Ja.i.124, Ja.i.125; Ja.v.276 (˚ika).</p><p>fr. agghāpana to agghāpeti, Caus. of agghati</p>

agghāpaniya|agghaapaniya|agghapaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agghaapaniya</span></p><p>adjective that which is to be valued, in <em>-kamma</em> the business of a valuator Ja.iv.137.</p><p>grd. of agghāpeti, see <em><a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></p>

agghāpenta|agghaapenta|agghapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agghaapenta</span></p><p>valuing; putting a price on</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></p>

agghāpesi|agghaapesi|agghapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agghaapesi</span></p><p>valued; put a price on</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agghati">agghati</a></p>

aggi-m-āsīna|aggi-m-aasiina|aggi-m-asina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggi-m-aasiina</span></p><p>aggi-m-āsīna</p><p>mfn. sitting near a fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>fire; flames; conflagration.</li><li>cremation fire; a pyre (as or deal or suicide).</li><li>the ritual fire, the sacrificial fire; (one of) the three fires of brahmanical ritual; the god Aggi Bhagavā.</li><li>(metaphorically) mental fire, fever.</li></ol>

aggi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>fire.</p><ol><li>fire, flames, sparks; conflagration Vin.ii.120 (fire in bathroom); MN.i.487 (anāhāro nibbuto f. gone out for lack of fuel); SN.iv.185, SN.iv.399 (sa-upādāno jalati provided with fuel blazes); Snp.62; Dhp.70 (= asaniaggi Dhp-a.iii.71); Ja.i.216 (sparks), Ja.i.294 (pyre); Ja.ii.102, Ja.iii.55; Ja.iv.139; Vv-a.20 (aggimhi tāpanaṃ + udake temanaṃ)<br />■ The var. phases of lighting and extinguishing the fire are given at AN.iv.45: aggiṃ ujjāleti (kindle, make burn), ajjhupekkhati (look after, keep up), nibbāpeti (extinguish, put out), nikkhipati (put down, lay). Other phrases are e.g. aggiṃ jāleti (kindle) Ja.ii.44; gaṇhāti (make or take) Ja.i.494 (cp. below b); deti (set light to Ja.i.294; nibbāpeti (put out) Iti.93; Sdhp.552. aggi nibbāyati the f. goes out SN.ii.85; MN.i.487; Ja.i.212 (udake through water); Mil.304. aggi nibbuto the f. is extinguished (cp. ˚nibbāna) Ja.i.61; Mil.304. agginā dahati to burn by means of fire, to set fire to AN.i.136, AN.i.199; Pv-a.20. <em>udar˚</em>; the fire supposed to regulate digestion Pv-a.33; cp. <em>Dial.</em> ii.208, note 2; <em>kapp˚uṭṭhān˚</em>; the universal conflagration Ja.iii.185; <em>dāv˚</em>; a wood or jungle fire Ja.i.212; <em>naḷ˚</em>; the burning of a reed Ja.vi.100; <em>padīp˚</em> fire of a lamp Mil.47.</li><li>the sacrificial fire: In one or two of the passages in the older texts this use of Aggi is ambiguous. It may possibly be intended to denote the personal Agni, the fire-god. But the commentators do not think so, and the Jātaka commentary, when it means Agni, has the phrase <em>Aggi Bhagavā</em> the Lord Agni e.g. at Ja.i.285, Ja.i.494; Ja.ii.44. The ancient ceremony of kindling a holy fire on the day the child is born and keeping it up throughout his life, is also referred to by that commentary e.g. Ja.i.285; Ja.ii.43. <em>Aggiṃ paricarati</em> (cp. ˚paricāriyā) to serve the sacred fire Vin.i.31 (jaṭilā aggī paricaritukāmā); AN.v.263, AN.v.266; Thig.143 (= aggihuttaṃ paric˚ Thag-a.136); Dhp.107; Ja.i.494; Dhp-a.ii.232 aggiṃ juhati (cp. ˚homa, ˚hutta) to sacrifice (in)to the fire AN.ii.207; often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with aggihuttaṃ paricarati e.g. SN.i.166; Snp.p.79. aggiṃ namati &amp; santappeti to worship the fire AN.v.235. aggissa (gen.) paricāriko Ja yi.207 (cp. below ˚paricārika); aggissa ādhānaṃ AN.iv.41.</li><li>(ethical, always-˚) the fire of burning, consuming, feverish sensations. Freq. in standard set of 3 fires, viz. <em>rāg˚, dos˚ moh˚</em>; or the fires of lust, anger and bewilderment. The number three may possibly have been chosen with reference to the three sacrificial fires of Vedic ritual. At SN.iv.19; AN.iv.41 sq. there are 7 fires, the 4 last of which are <em>āhuneyy˚, gahapat˚, dakkhiṇeyy˚, kaṭṭh˚</em>. But this trinity of cardinal sins lies at the basis of Buddhist ethics &amp; the fire simile was more probably suggested by the number. DN.iii.217; Iti.92, Vb.368. In late books are found others: <em>ind˚</em>; the fire of the senses Pv-a.56; <em>dukkh˚</em> the glow of suffering ib. 60; <em>bhavadukkh˚</em>; of the misery of becomings Sdhp.552; <em>vippaṭisār˚</em>; burning remorse Pv-a.60; <em>sok˚</em>; burning grief Pv-a.41.</li></ol><p><em>Note.</em> The form <em>aggini</em> occurs only at Snp.668 &amp; Snp.670 in the meaning of “pyre”, and in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with sama “like”, viz. aggini-samaṃ jalitaṃ Snp.668 (= samantato jali taṃ aggiṃ Snp-a.480); aggini-samāsu Snp.670 (= aggisamāsu Snp-a.481). The form <em>agginī</em> in phrase niccagginī can either be referred to gini (q.v.) or has to be taken as nom. of aggini (in adj. function with ī metri causa otherwise as adj. agginiṃ), meaning looking constantly after the fire, i.e. careful, observant, alert.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em> (agyâgāra) a heated room or hut with a fire Vin.i.24; Vin.iv.109; DN.i.101, DN.i.102 (as variant reading BB for agāra); MN.i.501; AN.v.234, AN.v.250. <em>-<a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></em> a great mass of fire, a huge fire, fire-brand SN.ii.85; AN.iv.128; Thig.351 (˚samākāmā); Ja.iv.139; Ja.vi.330; Pts.i.125; Dpvs.vi.37; Mil.304. <em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> having become (like) fire Mil.302<br />■ ja fire-born Ja.v.404 (C; text aggijāta). <em>-ṭṭha</em> fire-place Ja.v.155. <em>-ṭṭhāna</em> fire-place Vin.ii.120 (jantāghare, in bathroom). <em>-<a href="bword://da%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dha">daḍḍha</a></em> consumed by fire Dhp.136; Pv.i.7#4 <em>-<a href="bword://d%C4%81ha">dāha</a></em> (mahā˚) a holocaust AN.i.178. <em>-<a href="bword://nik%C4%81sin">nikāsin</a></em> like fire Ja.iii.320 (suriya). <em>-<a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> the extinction of fire Ja.i.212 <em>-<a href="bword://pajjota">pajjota</a></em> fire-light AN.ii.140 (one of the 4 lights, viz canda˚, suriya˚, a˚, paññā˚). <em>-<a href="bword://paricara%E1%B9%87a">paricaraṇa</a></em> (-ṭṭhāna) the place where the (sacrificial) fire is attended to Dhp-a.i.199 <em>-<a href="bword://paricariy%C4%81">paricariyā</a></em> fire-worship Dhp-a.ii.232; Snp-a.291 (pāri˚), Snp-a.456 <em>-<a href="bword://paric%C4%81rika">paricārika</a></em> one who worship the fire aN.v.263 (brāhmaṇa) <em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81l%C4%81">sālā</a></em> a heated hall or refectory Vin.i.25, Vin.i.49 = Vin.ii.210 Vin.i.139; Vin.ii.154. <em>-<a href="bword://sikh%C4%81">sikhā</a></em> the crest of the fire, the flame, in simile <em>˚ûpama</em>, like a flaming fire Snp.703; Dhp.308; Iti.43, Iti.90 (ayoguḷa). <em>-<a href="bword://hutta">hutta</a></em> (nt.) the sacrificial fire (see above 2), Vin.i.33, Vin.i.36 = Ja.i.83; Vin.i.246 = Snp.568 (˚mukha-yañña); SN.i.166; Dhp.392; Snp.249, Snp.p.79; Ja.iv.211; Ja.vi.525; Thag-a.136 (= aggi); Dhp-a.iv.151 (˚ṃ brāhmaṇo namati). <em>-huttaka</em> (nt.) fire-offering Ja.vi.522 (= aggi-jūhana C.). <em>-<a href="bword://hotta">hotta</a></em> = ˚hutta Snp-a.456 (variant reading BB ˚hutta). <em>-<a href="bword://homa">homa</a></em> fire-oblation (or perhaps sacrificing to Agni DN.i.9 (= aggi-jūhana DN-a.i.93).</p><p>Vedic agni = Lat. ignis. Besides the contracted form aggi we find the diaeretic forms gini (q.v.) and aggini (see below)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a>, <a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://daḍḍha">daḍḍha</a>, <a href="bword://dāha">dāha</a>, <a href="bword://nikāsin">nikāsin</a>, <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a>, <a href="bword://pajjota">pajjota</a>, <a href="bword://paricaraṇa">paricaraṇa</a>, <a href="bword://paricariyā">paricariyā</a>, <a href="bword://paricārika">paricārika</a>, <a href="bword://sālā">sālā</a>, <a href="bword://sikhā">sikhā</a>, <a href="bword://hutta">hutta</a>, <a href="bword://hotta">hotta</a>, <a href="bword://homa">homa</a></p>

aggidatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A brahmin of Khemavatī, father of the Buddha Kakusandha. His wife was named Visākhā. DN.ii.7</p>

aggihoma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>oblation in the fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggihutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the oblation to Agni, the rite of the fire oblation; the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggihuttamissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>various tools etc. for the fire-oblation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggihutta">aggihutta</a></p>

aggihuttamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>for which the fire-oblation is the chief or foremost</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggihutta">aggihutta</a></p>

aggika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>(from aggi) one who tends the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggiya">aggiya</a></p>

aggika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who worships the fire Vin.i.71 (jaṭilaka); DN.ii.339 sq. (jaṭila); SN.i.166 (brāhmaṇa).</p><p>aggi + ka</p>

aggikabhāradvāja|aggikabhaaradvaaja|aggikabharadvaja
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggikabhaaradvaaja</span></p><p>Aggikabhāradvāja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->AggikaBhāradvāja</p><p>A brahmin of Sāvatthī, of the Bhāradvāja clan. The Buddha, while on his rounds, sees him tending the fire and preparing oblations, and stands for alms in front of his house. The brahmin abuses him, calling him <em>muṇḍaka</em> and <em>vasala</em>. Thereupon the Buddha preaches to him the Vasala Sutta (or, as it is sometimes called, the Aggika Bhāradvāja Sutta), and wins him over to the faith. Snp.21–25</p><p>Aggikabhāradvāja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->AggikaBhāradvāja</p><p>A brahmin of Rājagaha, a fire-tender. He prepares a meal for sacrifice, and when the Buddha, out of compassion for him, appears before his house for alms, he says the meal is meant only for one who has the “threefold lore” (the three Vedas). The Buddha gives the brahmin another interpretation of the “threefold lore”;. The brahmin, thereupon, becomes a convert, enters the Order, and, in due course, attains arahantship. SN.i.166<em>f.</em></p>

aggikkhandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>great mass of fire; a huge blazing fire, a bonfire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

agginisama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>filled (to the brim) with fire; being a mass of fire; a mass of fire.</p>

aggipajjota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>light from a fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggiparicitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>damaged by fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggiparijita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(same as aggiparicitta)</p>

aggipāricariyā|aggipaaricariyaa|aggiparicariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggipaaricariyaa</span></p><p>attendance on, or care of, the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggipāricārika|aggipaaricaarika|aggiparicarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggipaaricaarika</span></p><p>who tends the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggisikhā|aggisikhaa|aggisikha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggisikhaa</span></p><p>flame; a crest of a fire.</p>

aggisādhārana|aggisaadhaarana|aggisadharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggisaadhaarana</span></p><p>subject to, vulnerable to, fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggisālā|aggisaalaa|aggisala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggisaalaa</span></p><p>fire-room (where water is heated), a heated hall; a room for the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggivessana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Probably the name of a brahmin clan, the Agnivesyāyanas, and the Ksatriyas who were so styled, took the name from their brahmin priests.</p><p>The name is used by the Buddha in addressing Saccaka Nigaṇṭhaputta, MN.i.229<em>f.</em> MN.i.237<em>f.</em> and also Dīghanakha Paribbājaka. MN.i.497<em>f.</em></p><p>In the Dantabhūmi Sutta MN.iii.128<em>f.</em> the novice Aciravata is thus addressed by Prince Jayasena, who visits him, and also by the Buddha.</p>

aggiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>(from aggi) one who tends the ritual fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggika">aggika</a></p>

aggiṭṭhāna|aggi.t.thaana|aggitthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggi.t.thaana</span></p><p>place for the fire; a fireplace, hearth</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></p>

aggregates or clusters
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[aggregates or clusters]]Aggregates or clusters:</strong> <em>khandha</em></p>

aggupaṭṭhāka|aggupa.t.thaaka|aggupatthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggupa.t.thaaka</span></p><p>the chief personal (male) attendant</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggāsana|aggaasana|aggasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggaasana</span></p><p>the first or chief seat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a></p>

aggāḷave cetiye|aggaa.lave cetiye|aggalave cetiye
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aggaa.lave cetiye</span></p><p>Aggāḷave CetiyeAggālavacetiya</p><p>The chief shrine at Āḷavī, originally a pagan place of worship, but later converted into a Buddhist <em>vihāra</em>. The Buddha stopped here on many occasions during his wanderings, and this was the scene of several Vinaya rules, e.g. against monks digging the ground Vin.iv.32 and cutting trees, Vin.iv.34 using unfiltered water for building purposes, Vin.iv.48 sleeping in the company of novices, Vin.iv.16 giving new buildings in hand. Vin.ii.172<em>f.</em></p><p>The Chabbaggiyā are censured here for a <em>nissaggiya</em> offence. Vin.iii.224 The Vaṅgīsa Sutta was preached there to Vaṅgīsa, on the occasion of the death of his preceptor, Nigrodhakappa. Snp.59<em>f.</em> In the early years of Vaṅgīsa’s novitiate he stayed at the shrine with his preceptor, and disaffection arose within him twice, once because of women, the second time because of his tutor’s solitary habits, SN.i.185–186 and later, again, through pride in his own powers of improvisation (<em>paṭibhāna</em>). SN.i.187 Here, again, the Buddha utters the praises of Hatthaka Āḷavaka, who visits him with a large following, whose fealty has been won (according to Hatthaka) by observing the four characteristics of sympathy (<em>saṅgahavatthūni</em>) learnt from the Buddha. AN.iv.216–220</p><p>26.06173,83.3807343shrine</p>

agha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter (&amp; m.) &amp; mfn.</p><ol><li>(n. m) wicked; pain, distress.</li><li>(mfn.) bad; dangerous.</li></ol><p>agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter (&amp; m. or mfn.?) (?) the air, the sky; space; (void, unrestricted?).</p>

agha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter evil, grief, pain, suffering, misfortune SN.i.22; MN.i.500 (<em>roga gaṇḍa salla agha</em>); AN.ii.128 (id.); Ja.v.100; Thig.491; Sdhp.51<br />■ adj. painful, bringing pain Ja.vi.507 (agha-m-miga = aghakara m. C.). <em>-<a href="bword://bh%C5%ABta">bhūta</a></em> a source of pain SN.iii.189 (+ agha &amp; salla).</p><p>cp. Sk. agha, of uncertain etym.</p><p>Agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter the sky, orig. the dark sky, dark space, the abyss of space DN.ii.12; SN.v.45; Vv.16#1 (aghasi gama, loc. vehāsaṃ gama Vv-a.78); Ja.iv.154; Dhs.638 (+ aghagata) Vb.84 (id.).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> going through or being in the sky or atmosphere Dhs.638, Dhs.722; Vb.84.</li><li><em>-gāmin</em> moving through the atmosphere or space i.e. a planet SN.i.67 = Mil.242 (ādicco seṭṭho aghagāminaṃ).</li></ul><p>the etym. suggested by Morris J.P.T.S. 1889, 200 (with ref. to MN.i.500, which belongs under agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) is untenable (to Sk. kha, as a-kha = agha, cp Jain Prk. khaha). Neither does the pop. etym. of Bdhgh offer any clue (= a + gha from <em>ghan</em> that which does not strike or aghaṭṭaniya is not strikeable Dhs-a.326 cp. Dhs. trsl. 194 &amp; Ja.iv.154 aghe ṭhitā = appaṭighe ākāse ṭhitā the air which does not offer any resistance) On the other hand the primary meaning is <em>darkness</em>, as seen from the phrase <em>lokantarikā aghā asaṃvutā andhakārā</em> DN.ii.12; SN.v.454, and BSk. <em>aghasaṃvṛta</em> Mvu.i.240, adj. dark Mvu.i.41; Mvu.ii.162; Lal.552</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhūta">bhūta</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a></p>

aghagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>aghagata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter wicked; distress</p><p>aghagata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter sky; space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a>, <a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a></p>

aghagāmi|aghagaami|aghagami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aghagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aghagāmin">aghagāmin</a></p><p>moving through the sky, through the space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a></p>

aghagāmin|aghagaamin|aghagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aghagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aghagāmin">aghagāmin</a></p><p>moving through the sky, through the space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a></p>

aghammiga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>sort of wild animal Ja.vi.247 (= aghāvaha miga), Ja.vi.507 (= aghakara). Cp. BSk. agharika Divy.475.</p><p>to agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->?</p>

aghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Thag.321 may be read as agha-gata or (preferably) with variant reading as aggha-gataṃ, or (with Neumann) as agghaṃ agghatānaṃ. See also Mrs. Rh. D, <em>Psalms of the Brethren</em>, p. 191.</p>

aghavin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective suffering pain, being in misery Snp.694 (= dukkhita Snp-a.489).</p><p>to agha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

aghāvi|aghaavi|aghavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aghaavi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aghāvin">aghāvin</a></p><p>being in misery; suffering pain.</p>

aghāvin|aghaavin|aghavin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aghaavin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aghāvin">aghāvin</a></p><p>being in misery; suffering pain.</p>

agiha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>homeless, without a house.</p>

agility
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Agility:</strong> <em>Lahutā</em></p>

agyantarāya|agyantaraaya|agyantaraya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agyantaraaya</span></p><p>danger from fire.</p>

agyāgāra|agyaagaara|agyagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agyaagaara</span></p><p>hut for the (ritual) fire(s).</p>

agāmaka|agaamaka|agamaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaamaka</span></p><p>where there are no villages; without habitation, uninhabited.</p>

agāra|agaara|agara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaara</span></p><p>house; a dwelling place</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agārā">agārā</a></p>

agāra|agaara|agara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaara</span></p><ol><li><p>house or hut, usually implying the comforts of living at home as opp. to <em>anagāra</em> homelessness or the state of a homeless wanderer (mendicant). See <em>anagāriyā</em><br />■ Thus freq. in two phrases contrasting the state of a householder (or layman, cp <em><a href="bword://gihin">gihin</a></em>), with that of a religious wanderer <em>(pabbajita)</em> viz.</p><ol><li><em>kesamassuṃ ohāretvā kāsāyāni vatthāni acchādetvā agārasmā anagāriyaṃ pabbajati</em> “to shave off hair &amp; beard, put on the yellow robes, and wander forth out of the home into the homeless state” DN.i.60 etc. cp. Cnd.172#ii. See also SN.i.185 (<em>agārasmā anagāriyaṃ nikkhanta</em>); MN.ii.55 <em>(agāraṃ ajjhāvasatā)</em>; Snp.274, Snp.805 <em>(˚ṃ āvasati)</em>, and with <em><a href="bword://pabbajita">pabbajita</a></em> DN.i.89, DN.i.115, DN.i.202, DN.i.230; Pv.ii.13#17</li><li>of a “rājā cakkavattin” compared with a “sambuddha”: <em>sace agāraṃ āvasati vijeyya paṭhaviṃ imaṃ adaṇḍena asatthena… sace ca so pabbajati agārā anagāriyaṃ vivaṭacchado sambuddho arahā bhavissati</em> “he will become the greatest king when he stays at home, but the greatest saint when he takes up the homeless life”, the prophesy made for the infant Gotama DN.ii.16; Snp.1002, Snp.1003<br />■ Further passages for <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em> e.g. Vin.i.15; DN.i.102 (BB. has variant reading <em>agyāgāra</em>, but DN-a.i.270 expl. as <em>dānāgāra</em>); AN.i.156, AN.i.281; AN.ii.52 sq. Dhp.14, Dhp.140; Ja.i.51, Ja.i.56; Ja.iii.392; Dpvs.i.36.</li></ol></li><li><p><em>anagāra</em> (adj.) houseless, homeless; a mendicant (opp. <em><a href="bword://gaha%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">gahaṭṭha</a></em> Snp.628 = Dhp.404; Snp.639, Snp.640 (+ <em>paribbaje</em>); Pv.ii.2#5 (= <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81v%C4%81sa">anāvāsa</a></em> Pv-a.80)<br />■ (nt.) the homeless state (= <em>anagāriyā</em>) Snp.376. See also <em>agga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p></li><li><p><em>-āgāra</em>: Owing to freq. occurrence of <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em> at the end of compounds of which the first word ends in <em><a href="bword://a">a</a></em>, we have a dozen quite familiar words ending apparently in <em>āgāra</em>. This form has been considered therefore as a proper doublet of <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em>. This however is wrong. The long <em><a href="bword://%C4%81">ā</a></em> is simply a contraction of the short <em><a href="bword://a">a</a></em> at the end of the first part of the cpd. with the short <em><a href="bword://a">a</a></em> at the beginning of <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em> Of the compounds the most common are: <em>āgantuk˚</em>; reception hall for strangers or guests SN.iv.219; SN.v.21<br />■ <em>itth˚</em> lady’s bower SN.i.58, SN.i.89<br />■ <em>kūṭ˚</em>; a house with a peaked roof, or with gables SN.ii.103 SN.ii.263; SN.iii.156; SN.iv.186; SN.v.43; AN.i.230; AN.iii.10, AN.iii.364; AN.iv.231; AN.v.21. <em>-koṭṭh˚</em>; storehouse granary DN.i.134 (cp. DN-a.i.295); SN.i.89. <em>-tiṇ˚</em>; a house covered with grass SN.iv.185; AN.i.101. <em>-bhus˚</em>; threshing shed, barn AN.i.241. <em>-santh˚</em>; a council hall DN.i.91; DN.ii.147; SN.iv.182; SN.v.453; AN.ii.207; AN.iv.179 sq. <em>-suññ˚</em>; an uninhabited shed; solitude SN.v.89, SN.v.157, SN.v.310 sq., SN.v.329 sq. AN.i.241 (variant reading for bhusâgāra); AN.iii.353; AN.iv.139, AN.iv.392, AN.iv.437, AN.v.88, AN.v.109, AN.v.323 sq.</p></li></ol><p>cp. Sk. agāra, probably with the a-of communion; Gr. ἀγεἱρω to collect, ἀγορά market. Cp. in meaning &amp; etym. gaha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gihin">gihin</a>, <a href="bword://pabbajita">pabbajita</a>, <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a>, <a href="bword://gahaṭṭha">gahaṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://anāvāsa">anāvāsa</a>, <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a>, <a href="bword://ā">ā</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a></p>

agāraka|agaaraka|agaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaaraka</span></p><p>small house, a cottage MN.i.450; Ja.vi.81.</p><p>fr. agāra</p>

agārasappa|agaarasappa|agarasappa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaarasappa</span></p><p>Common Wolf Snake, <em>Lycodon aulicus</em>. The Pāḷi name means “house snake”. The color and markings of this snake are variable but it is usually light to dark-brown with yellow crossbars and it can grow up to 765 mm. Of all Indian snakes this is the one most commonly found in human habitation, whether in rural areas or crowded cities. It hides during the day and comes out at night to hunt geckos, skinks, mice, cockroaches and birds nesting in roofs. Although non-venomous, the wolf snake closely resembles the very dangerous krait and is often mistaken for it. Its its discovery in a house usually causes consternation. The Jātaka describes a man carrying an unlucky robe on a stick “as if he were carrying a house snake” Ja.i.372.</p>

agāravasa|agaaravasa|agaravasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaaravasa</span></p><p>household life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a></p>

agārika|agaarika|agarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaarika</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>having a house, in <em>eka˚, dva˚</em>; etc. DN.i.166 = AN.i.295 = AN.ii.206.</li><li>a householder, layman Vin.i.17. f. <em>agārikā</em> a housewife Vin.i.272. See also <em><a href="bword://%C4%81g%C4%81rika">āgārika</a></em>.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgārika">āgārika</a></p>

agārikabhūta|agaarikabhuuta|agarikabhuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaarikabhuuta</span></p><p>(while) being a householder, leading a householder’s life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agārika">agārika</a></p>

agārin|agaarin|agarin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaarin</span></p><p>adjective one who has or inhabits a house, a householder Snp.376, Thag.1009; Ja.iii.234<br />■ f. <em>agārinī</em> a housewife Vv.52#7 (= <em>gehassāmmī</em> Vv-a.225) Pv.iii.4#3 (id. Pv-a.194). Agariya = agarika</p><p>fr. agāra</p>

agāriya|agaariya|agariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; m. feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaariya</span></p><p>belonging to a house; living in a house; a householder; a housewife; a lay person</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agārika">agārika</a></p>

agāriya|agaariya|agariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaariya</span></p><p>AgāriyaAgārika</p><p>a layman MN.i.504 (˚bhūta)<br />■ Usually in neg. <em>anagāriyā</em> (f.) the homeless state (= <em>anagāraṃ</em> as opp. to <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em> (q.v.) in formula <em>agārasmā anagāriyaṃ pabbajita</em> (gone out from the house into the homeless state) Vin.i.15; MN.i.16; MN.ii.55, MN.ii.75; AN.i.49; DN.iii.30 sq., DN.iii.145 sq.; Snp.274, Snp.1003; Pv.ii.13#16; DN-a.i.112.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a></p>

agārā|agaaraa|agara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">agaaraa</span></p><p>house; a dwelling place</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a></p>

aha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>day (followed by an other word in compounds it takes the form aho, as in ahoratta)</p>

aha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Aha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable exclamation of surprise, consternation, pain etc “ch! alas! woe!”. Perhaps to be seen in cpd. <em>-kāmā</em> miserable pleasures lit. “woe to these pleasures!”) gloss at Thag-a.292 for T. kāmakāmā of Thig.506 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as “ahā ti lāmaka-pariyāyo”). See also <em><a href="bword://ahaha">ahaha</a></em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. aha &amp; P. aho; Germ. aha; Lat. ehem etc.</p><p>Aha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(-˚) &amp; <em>Aho</em> (˚-) neuter a day. (</p><ol><li><em>-aha</em> only in foll. compounds &amp; cases: <em>instr.</em> ekâhena in one day Ja.vi.366; <em>loc.</em> tadahe on that (same) day Pv-a.46; <em>acc.</em> katipâhaṃ (for) some or several days Ja.i.152 etc (kattpâha); sattāhaṃ seven days, a week Vin.i.1; DN.ii.14; Ja.iv.2, and freq.; anvahaṃ daily Dāvs iv.8<br />■ The initial <em><a href="bword://a">a</a></em> of <em><a href="bword://aha%E1%B9%83">ahaṃ</a></em> <em>(acc.)</em> is elided after i, which often appears lengthened: kati ’haṃ how many days? SN.i.7 ekâha-dvī ’haṃ one or two days Ja.i.292; dvīha-tī ’han two or three days Ja.ii.103; Vv-a.45; ekâha-dvī ’h’ accayena after the lapse of one or two days Ja.i.253<br />■ A doublet of aha is <em><a href="bword://anha">anha</a></em> (through metathesis from ahan), which only occurs in phrases <em>pubbanho &amp; sāyanha;</em> (q.v.); an adj. der. fr. aha is <em>-ahika</em>: see pañcâhika (consisting of 5 days)</li><li><em>aho˚</em>; in cpd. <em><a href="bword://ahoratta">ahoratta</a></em> (m. &amp; nt.) [cp BSk. ahorātraṃ Avs.i.209] &amp; <em>ahoratti</em> (f.) day &amp; night occurring mostly in oblique cases and adverbially in; <em>acc.</em> <em>ahorattaṃ</em>: MN.i.417 (˚ânusikkhin); Dhp.226 (id.; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by divā ca rattiñ ca tisso sikkhā sikkhamāna Dhp-a.iii.324); Thag.145 (ahorattā accayanti); Ja.iv.108 (˚ānaṃ accaye); Pv.ii.13#1 (˚ṃ); Mil.82 (ena)<br />■ <em>ahorattiṃ</em> Dhp.387; Ja.vi.313 (variant reading BB for T. aho va rattiṃ).</li></ol><p>Vedic ahan &amp; ahas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahaha">ahaha</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://ahaṃ">ahaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://anha">anha</a>, <a href="bword://ahoratta">ahoratta</a></p>

ahaha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>One of the purgatories mentioned in the Sutta-Nipāta list. Snp.p.126 It is the name given to a period of suffering in Avīci and is equivalent in duration to twenty Ababā. SN.i.152</p><p>5hell</p>

ahaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the name of a hell, or place in Avīci, where one suffers for an <em>ahaha</em> of years.</p>

ahaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>exclamation of woe Ja.iii.450 (ahahā in metre).</li><li>neuter Name of a certain division of Purgatory (Niraya), lit. oh woe! AN.v.173 = Snp.p.126.</li></ol><p>onomat. after exclamation ahahā: see *<a href="bword://aha">aha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aha">aha</a></p>

ahaṃ|aha.m|aham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pron.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aha.m</span></p><p>1 pers. (sg.) I, myself; the ego; (pl.) we.</p>

ahaṃ|aha.m|aham
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aha.m</span></p><p>pronoun pronoun of 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> person “I”. nom. sg. <em><a href="bword://aha%E1%B9%83">ahaṃ</a></em> SN.iii.235; AN.iv.53; Dhp.222, Dhp.320; Snp.172, Snp.192, Snp.685, Snp.989, Snp.1054, Snp.1143; Ja.i.61; Ja.ii.159<br />■ In pregnant sense (my ego, myself, I as the one &amp; only i.e. egotistically) in foll. phrases: yaṃ vadanti mama. na te ahaṃ SN.i.116, SN.i.123; ahaṃ asmi “I am” (cp. ahaṃkāra below) SN.i.129; SN.iii.46, SN.iii.128 sq.; SN.iv.203; AN.ii.212 AN.ii.215 sq.; Vism.13; ahaṃ pure ti “I am the first” Vv.84#50 (= ahamahaṃkārā ti Vv-a.351)<br />■ gen. dat. <em>mayhaṃ</em> Snp.431, Snp.479; Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.160, <em><a href="bword://mama">mama</a></em> SN.i.115; Snp.22, Snp.23 Snp.341, Snp.997; Ja.ii.159, &amp; <em>mamaṃ</em> SN.i.116; Snp.253 (= mama C.), Snp.694, Snp.982<br />■ instr. <em>mayā</em> Snp.135, Snp.336, Snp.557, Snp.982; Ja.i.222, Ja.i.279<br />■ acc. <em>maṃ</em> Snp.356, Snp.366, Snp.425, Snp.936; Ja.ii.159; Ja.iii.26, &amp; <em>mamaṃ</em> Ja.iii.55, Ja.iii.394<br />■ loc. <em>mayi</em> Snp.559; Ja.iii.188. The enclitic form in the sg. is me, &amp; functions in diff. cases, as gen. (Snp.983; Ja.ii.159), acc. (Snp.982), instr. (Ja.i.138, Ja.i.222), &amp; abl<br />■ Pl. nom.; <em><a href="bword://maya%E1%B9%83">mayaṃ</a></em> (we) Snp.31, Snp.91, Snp.167, Snp.999; Ja.ii.159; Ja.vi.365, <em>amhe</em> Ja.ii.129, &amp; <em><a href="bword://vaya%E1%B9%83">vayaṃ</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ gen. <em>amhākaṃ</em> Ja.i.221; Ja.ii.159 &amp; <em>asmākaṃ</em> Snp.p.106<br />■ acc. <em>amhe</em> Ja.i.222; Ja.ii.415; <em>asme</em> Ja.iii.359<br />■ instr. <em>amhehi</em> Ja.i.150; Ja.ii.417 &amp; <em>asmābhi</em> Thag-a.153 (Tha-ap.132)<br />■ loc. <em>amhesu</em> Ja.i.222. The enclitic form for the pl. is <em><a href="bword://no">no</a></em> (for acc. dat &amp; gen.) see under; <em><a href="bword://vaya%E1%B9%83">vayaṃ</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> selfishness, egotism, arrogance (see also mamaṃkāra) MN.iii.18, MN.iii.32; SN.ii.253; SN.iii.80, SN.iii.136, SN.iii.169 sq.; SN.iv.41 SN.iv.197, SN.iv.202; AN.i.132 sq.; AN.iii.444; Ud.70; Ne.127, and freq. passim.</li></ul><p>Vedic ahaṃ = Av. azəm; Gr. ἐγώ(ν); Lat. ego; Goth. ik, Ags. ic, Ohg. ih etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahaṃ">ahaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://mama">mama</a>, <a href="bword://mayaṃ">mayaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://vayaṃ">vayaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://no">no</a>, <a href="bword://vayaṃ">vayaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a></p>

ahaṇkāra|aha.nkaara|ahankara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aha.nkaara</span></p><ol><li>the (false) conception of one’s individuality; pride, conceit.</li><li>the theory of self-creation</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahaṃ">ahaṃ</a></p>

ahaṇkārapasuta|aha.nkaarapasuta|ahankarapasuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aha.nkaarapasuta</span></p><p>devoted to the theory of self-creation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahaṇkāra">ahaṇkāra</a></p>

ahe
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable exclamation of surprise or bewilderment: alas! woe etc., perhaps in cpd. <em>ahevana</em> a dense forest (lit. oh! this forest, alas! the forest (i.e. how big it is) Ja.v.63 (uttamāhevanandaho, if reading is correct, which is not beyond doubt. C. on p. 64 explains as “ahevanaṃ vuccati vanasaṇḍo”).</p><p>= aho, cp. aha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ahetu-patisandhika
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ahetu-patisandhika:</strong> see: <em>patisandhi</em></p>

ahetuka-citta
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ahetuka-citta:</strong> see: <em>hetu</em></p>

ahetuka-ditthi
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ahetuka-ditthi:</strong> View or opinion of uncausedness or randomness of existence &amp; phenomena; see: <em>ditthi</em></p>

ahetuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without a cause.</p>

ahetukavāda|ahetukavaada|ahetukavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahetukavaada</span></p><p>holding a theory of non-causality</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahetuka">ahetuka</a></p>

ahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>snake.</p>

ahi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>snake Vin.ii.109; DN.i.77; SN.iv.198; AN.iii.306 sq.; AN.iv.320; AN.v.289; Mnd.484; Vism.345 (+ kukkura etc.); Vv-a.100; Pv-a.144.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://ku%E1%B9%87apa">kuṇapa</a></em> the carcase of a snake Vin.iii.68 = MN.i.73; AN.iv.377.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81ha">gāha</a></em> a snake catcher or trainer Ja.vi.192</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhika">guṇṭhika</a></em> (? reading uncertain, we find as vv.ll. ˚guṇḍika, ˚guṇṭika &amp; ˚kuṇḍika; the BSk. paraphrase is ˚tuṇḍika Divy.497. In view of this uncertainty we are unable to pronounce a safe etymology; it is in all probability a dialectical; may be Non-Aryan, word. See also under kuṇḍika &amp; guṇṭhika &amp; cp. Morris in; J.P.T.S. 1886, 153 a snake charmer Ja.i.370 (˚guṇḍ˚); Ja.ii.267; Ja.iii.348 (˚guṇḍ˚), Ja.iv.456 (T. ˚guṇṭ; variant reading BB ˚kuṇḍ˚), Ja.iv.308 (T. ˚kuṇḍ˚, variant reading SS ˚guṇṭh˚), Ja.iv.456 (T. ˚guṇṭ˚; variant reading BB ˚kuṇḍ); Ja.vi.171 (T. ˚guṇḍ˚; variant reading BB ˚kuṇḍ˚); Mil.23, Mil.305.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://chattaka">chattaka</a></em> (nt.) “a snake’s parasol”, a mushroom DN.iii.87; Ja.ii.95; Ud.81 (C. on viii.5, 1).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://tu%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Dika">tuṇḍika</a></em> = ˚guṇṭhika Vism.304 Vism.500.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://peta">peta</a></em> a Peta in form of a snake Dhp-a.ii.63.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mekhal%C4%81">mekhalā</a></em> “snake-girdle”, i.e. outfit or appearance of a snake Dhp-a.i.139.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81taka">vātaka</a></em> (-roga) Name of a certain disease (“snakewind-sickness”) Vin.i.78; Ja.ii.79; Ja.iv.200; Dhp-a.i.169 Dhp-a.i.187, Dhp-a.i.231; Dhp-a.iii.437.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vijj%C4%81">vijjā</a></em> “snake-craft”, i.e. fortune-telling or sorcery by means of snakes DN.i.9 (= sappa-daṭṭhatikicchana-vijjā c’ eva sapp’ avhāyana-vijjā ea “the art of healing snake bites as well as the invocation of snakes (for magic purposes)” DN-a.i.93).</li></ul><p>Vedic ahi, with Av. aži perhaps to Lat. anguis etc., see Walde <em>Lat. Wtb.</em> s. v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuṇapa">kuṇapa</a>, <a href="bword://gāha">gāha</a>, <a href="bword://guṇṭhika">guṇṭhika</a>, <a href="bword://chattaka">chattaka</a>, <a href="bword://tuṇḍika">tuṇḍika</a>, <a href="bword://peta">peta</a>, <a href="bword://mekhalā">mekhalā</a>, <a href="bword://vātaka">vātaka</a>, <a href="bword://vijjā">vijjā</a></p>

ahicchattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter a</i></p><p>mushroom</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahi">ahi</a></p>

ahihattaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Mushrooms and toadstools, sometimes also <em>ahicchattaka</em>, fungi of the classes Hymenomycetes and Gasteromycetes. The Pāḷi names mean “snake umbrellas” Ja.ii.95 as does the Hindi for mushrooms, <em>sarpchatr</em>. There are over 50 species of edible mushrooms in India, some of them growing in the north. They were, according to the legend, the first plants to appear after the formation of the earth D.iii.87. See <em>Bhūmipappaṭaka</em>.</p><p>See also: Bhūmipappaṭaka</p>

ahimekhalikā|ahimekhalikaa|ahimekhalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahimekhalikaa</span></p><p>snake-girdle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahi">ahi</a></p>

ahimsā|ahimsaa|ahimsa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahimsaa</span></p><p><strong>Ahimsā:</strong> see: <em>avihimsā</em></p>

ahirika-anottappa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ahirika-anottappa:</strong> ‘lack of moral shame and fear of wongdoing’, are two of the 4 disadvantageous factors associated with all kammically disadvantageous states of consciousness, the two others being restlessness <em>uddhacca</em> and confusion <em>moha</em>. Cf. Tab. II.</p><p>The Buddha pointed that: There are two evil things, namely, lack of moral shame and lack of fear of wongdoing A. II, 6. Not to be ashamed of what one should be ashamed of; not to be ashamed of evil, disadvantageous states: this is called lack of moral shame Pug. 59. Not to fear what one should feared… this is called lack of Fear of Wrongdoing Pug. 60.</p>

ahirika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) shameless.</li><li>(n.) shamelessness</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahirīka">ahirīka</a></p>

ahirika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ahirika &amp; Ahirīka</p><p>adjective shameless, unscrupulous DN.iii.212, DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; AN.ii.219; Dhp.244; Snp.133 (˚īka); Iti.27 (˚īka); Pp.19 (see <em>nt. unscrupulousness</em>) Dhs.365; Ne.39, Ne.126; Dhp-a.iii.352.</p><p>fr. a + hirī</p><p>See also: nt. unscrupulousness</p>

ahirīka|ahiriika|ahirika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahiriika</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) shameless.</li><li>(n.) shamelessness</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahirika">ahirika</a></p>

ahirīka|ahiriika|ahirika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahiriika</span></p><p>Ahirika &amp; Ahirīka</p><p>adjective shameless, unscrupulous DN.iii.212, DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; AN.ii.219; Dhp.244; Snp.133 (˚īka); Iti.27 (˚īka); Pp.19 (see <em>nt. unscrupulousness</em>) Dhs.365; Ne.39, Ne.126; Dhp-a.iii.352.</p><p>fr. a + hirī</p><p>See also: nt. unscrupulousness</p>

ahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun not good or friendly, harmful, bad; unkindliness DN.iii.246; Dhp.163; Snp.665, Snp.692; Mil.199 (˚kāma). Ahirika &amp; Ahirika;</p><p>a + hita</p>

ahivijjā|ahivijjaa|ahivijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahivijjaa</span></p><p>serpent-lore</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahi">ahi</a></p>

ahivātakaroga|ahivaatakaroga|ahivatakaroga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahivaatakaroga</span></p><p>certain dangerous contagious disease</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahi">ahi</a></p>

ahiṃsaka|ahi.msaka|ahimsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahi.msaka</span></p><p>adjective not injuring others, harmless, humane, SN.i.165; Thag.879; Dhp.225; Ja.iv.447.</p><p>fr. ahiṃsā</p>

ahiṃsakabhāradvāja|ahi.msakabhaaradvaaja|ahimsakabharadvaja
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahi.msakabhaaradvaaja</span></p><p>AhiṃsakabhāradvājaAhiṃsakaBhāradvāja</p><p>One of the Bhāradvāja brothers. He came to the Buddha at Sāvatthī and the Buddha suggested to him the desirability of living up to his name by practising ahiṃsā. It is said that later he became an arahant. SN.i.164</p>

ahiṃsā|ahi.msaa|ahimsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahi.msaa</span></p><p>not hurting, humanity, kindness DN.iii.147; AN.i.151; Dhp.261, Dhp.270; Ja.iv.71; Mil.402.</p><p>a + hiṃsā</p>

aho
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>an exclamation oh!, expressing;</p><ol><li>a wish or a hope; oh that… !, would that … !.</li><li>astonishment or joy; admiration (sometimes ironic): oh! oh indeed!</li><li>dismay, painful surprise; compassion, regret: oh! alas!.</li><li>blame, reproach.</li><li>a summons: hey!</li></ol>

aho
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable exclamation of surprise, astonishment or consternation: yea, indeed well; I say! for sure! Vv-a.103 (aho ti acchariy’ atthena nipāto); Ja.i.88 (aho acchariyaṃ aho abbhutaṃ), Ja.i.140. Usually comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with similar emphatic particles, e.g. aho vata Dhp-a.ii.85; Pv-a.131 (= sādhu vata); aho vata re DN.i.107; Pv.ii.9<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4 5<!-- raw HTML omitted -->. Cp. <em><a href="bword://ahe">ahe</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. aho, for etym. see *<a href="bword://aha">aha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahe">ahe</a>, <a href="bword://aha">aha</a></p>

ahogaṅgā|ahoga"ngaa|ahoganga
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahoga"ngaa</span></p><p>A mountain in North India, on the Upper Ganges. There, for some time, lived the Thera Sambhūta Sānavāsi, and it was there that Yasa Kākandakaputta saw him. The meeting of arahants to discuss what measures should be taken against the Vesālī monks was also held there, and at the meeting were present monks from the Western country and from Avanti-Dakkhiṇāpatha. Vin.ii.298–289</p><p>???2hill</p>

ahoratta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>day and night.</p>

ahorattanusikkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ahorattanusikkhin">ahorattanusikkhin</a></p><p>continually studying; learning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahoratta">ahoratta</a></p>

ahorattanusikkhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ahorattanusikkhin">ahorattanusikkhin</a></p><p>continually studying; learning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahoratta">ahoratta</a></p>

ahorattaṃ|ahoratta.m|ahorattam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahoratta.m</span></p><p>day and night; continually</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ahoratta">ahoratta</a></p>

ahoratti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i></p><p>day and a night.</p>

ahosi-kamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ahosi-kamma:</strong> ‘ineffective kamma’; see: <em>kamma.</em></p>

ahosi-kamma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ahosi-kamma</p><p>neuter an act or thought whose kamma has no longer any potential force: <em>Cpd.</em> 145. At p. 45 ahosikakamma is said to be a kamma inhibited by a more powerful one. See Buddhaghosa in Vism. Chap. xix.</p>

ahuhāliya|ahuhaaliya|ahuhaliya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahuhaaliya</span></p><p>hoarse &amp; loud laugh Ja.iii.223 (= danta-vidaṃsaka-mahā-hasita C.).</p><p>onomat.</p>

ahuvāsiṃ|ahuvaasi.m|ahuvasim
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahuvaasi.m</span></p><p>1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. pret. of hotī (q.v.) I was Vv.82#6 (= ahosiṃ Vv-a.321).</p>

ahāsa|ahaasa|ahasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahaasa</span></p><p>absence of exultancy, modesty Ja.iii.466 (= an-ubbillāvitattaṃ C.).</p><p>a + hāsa, cp. Sk. ahāsa &amp; aharṣa</p>

ahāsi|ahaasi|ahasi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahaasi</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of <em><a href="bword://harati">harati</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://harati">harati</a></p>

ahīnindriya|ahiinindriya|ahinindriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ahiinindriya</span></p><p>see discussed under <em><a href="bword://abhinindriya">abhinindriya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abhinindriya">abhinindriya</a></p>

aja
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Domestic Goat, also <em>ajaka</em> and <em>ajiya</em>, Hindi <em>ajgar</em>, <em>Capra hircus</em> It.36 Ja.ii.278 Ja.v.241 M.i.344 M.iii.167 M.iv. 420. Goats are medium-sized mammals related to sheep with backward-sloping horns and short tails. They were kept for their milk, meat and wool. We read of goats being slaughtered at sacrifices, of long-haired goat getting caught in thorn bushes Ja.426 Ja.437 S.ii.228 and of a man keeping goats, living on the milk and making smoke to protect them from mosquitoes Ja.iii.401. Saddles were sometimes tipped with goats” horns Vin.i.196. There is an unusual Jātaka story in which an abandoned child is suckled by a goat Ja.v.429.</p>

aja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>goat; (m.) a male goat, a billy-goat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajā">ajā</a></p>

aja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>he-goat, a ram DN.i.6, DN.i.127; AN.ii.207; Ja.i.241; Ja.iii.278 sq. Ja.v.241; Pp.56; Pv-a.80.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://e%E1%B8%B7aka">eḷaka</a></em> [Sk. ajaiḍaka] goats &amp; sheep DN.i.5, DN.i.141; AN.ii.42 sq., AN.ii.209; Ja.i.166; Ja.vi.110; Pp.58. As pl. <em>-ā</em> SN.i.76; Iti.36; Ja.iv.363.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pada">pada</a></em> goat-footed MN.i.134.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81la">pāla</a></em> goatherd in <em>˚nigrodharukkha</em> (Npl.) “goatherds’ Nigrodha-tree” Vin.i.2 sq. Dpvs.i.29 (cp. Mhvs.iii.302).</li><li><em>-pālikā</em> a woman goatherd Vin.iii.38.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://lakkha%E1%B9%87a">lakkhaṇa</a></em> “goat-sign”, i.e. prophesying from signs on a goat etc. DN.i.9 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> DN-a.i.94 as “evarūpānaṃ ajānaṃ mansaṃ khāditabbaṃ evarūpānaṃ na khāditabban ti”).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://la%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Dik%C4%81">laṇḍikā</a></em> (pl.) goats’ dung, in phrase nāḷimattā a. a cup full of goats’ dung (which is put down a bad minister’s throat as punishment) Ja.i.419; Dhp-a.ii.70; Pv-a.282.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vata">vata</a></em> “goats’ habit”, a practice of certain ascetics (to live after the fashion of goats) Ja.iv.318.</li></ul><p>aja-pada refers to a stick cloven like a goat’s hoof; so also at Vism.161.</p><p>Vedic aja fr. <em>aj</em> (Lat. ago to drive), cp. ajina</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a>, <a href="bword://pada">pada</a>, <a href="bword://pāla">pāla</a>, <a href="bword://lakkhaṇa">lakkhaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://laṇḍikā">laṇḍikā</a>, <a href="bword://vata">vata</a></p>

ajacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective of low birth Ja.iii.19; Ja.vi.100.</p><p>a + jacca</p>

ajaddhuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>or ~ā feminine abstinence (from eating).</p>

ajaddhuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajaddhuka &amp; Ajaddhumāra</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://jaddhu">jaddhu</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jaddhu">jaddhu</a></p>

ajaddhumāra|ajaddhumaara|ajaddhumara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaddhumaara</span></p><p>Ajaddhuka &amp; Ajaddhumāra</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://jaddhu">jaddhu</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jaddhu">jaddhu</a></p>

ajaddhumārikā|ajaddhumaarikaa|ajaddhumarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaddhumaarikaa</span></p><p>death by starvation.</p>

ajagara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>large snake (rock-snake?), Boa Constrictor Ja.vi.507; Mil.23, Mil.303, Mil.364, Mil.406; Dhp-a.iii.60. Also as <em><a href="bword://ajakara">ajakara</a></em> at Ja.iii.484 (cp. Trenckner, Notes p. 64).</p><p>aja + gara = gala fr. <em>*gel</em> to devour, thus “goateater”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajakara">ajakara</a></p>

ajajjara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://jajjara">jajjara</a></em>. Ajaddhuka &amp; Ajaddhumara;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jajjara">jajjara</a></p>

ajaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(&amp; ~ā f.?) a young goat.</p>

ajaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>goat, pl. goats Vin.ii.154<br />■ f. <em>ajikā</em> Ja.iii.278 &amp; <em>ajiyā</em> Ja.v.241.</p>

ajakalāpaka|ajakalaapaka|ajakalapaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajakalaapaka</span></p><p>A Yakkha who tried to frighten the Buddha, but who, later, became his disciple. Ud.4–5</p>

ajakalāpakacetiya|ajakalaapakacetiya|ajakalapakacetiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajakalaapakacetiya</span></p><p>A shrine at Pāvā at which sacrifices were offered to Ajakalāpaka. Ud.4</p><p>26.683878,84.0551931shrine</p>

ajakara
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Indian Python, sometimes <em>ajagara</em>, Hindi, <em>ajgar</em>, <em>Python molurus</em> Ja.iii.484. This powerful constrictor is marked with distinctive irregular brown saddles over yellow or grey and can grow up to 5 meters in length. The Pāḷi name means “goat-eater” and indeed the Indian Python can grow big enough to kill and swallow goats, calves or small deer. The comment was made that “pythons are not poisonous but they are very strong. They wrap their coils around any man or animal who comes near and crush them” Ja.vi.507.</p>

ajakaraṇī|ajakara.nii|ajakarani
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajakara.nii</span></p><p>The river on whose banks was the Lonagiri (or Lena°) <em>vihāra</em> where lived the Thera Sabbaka (Sappaka). Thag.307–310</p><p>Here also, in a cave, dwelt the Thera Bhūta. Thag.518<em>f.</em></p><p>This river was probably a branch of the Aciravatī.</p><p>Gandak2river</p>

ajalakkhaṇa|ajalakkha.na|ajalakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajalakkha.na</span></p><p>signs of health or omen on a goat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajamoja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cummin-seed Vv-a.186.</p><p>Sk. ajamoda, cp. Sk. ajājī</p>

ajapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>“goat-footed” cloven like a goat’s hoof</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajapālaka|ajapaalaka|ajapalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajapaalaka</span></p><p>goatherd</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajapālanigrodha|ajapaalanigrodha|ajapalanigrodha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajapaalanigrodha</span></p><p>A banyan tree in Uruvelā, on the banks of the Nerañjara, near the Bodhi tree. A week after the Enlightenment the Buddha went there and spent a week cross-legged at the foot of the tree. There he met the Huhuṅkajātika Brahmin. Vin.i.2–3 Two weeks later he went there again from the Rajāyatana. Vin.i.4 It was then that the Brahma Sahampati appeared to him and persuaded him to preach the doctrine, in spite of the difficulty of the task. Vin.i.5–7 When the Buddha wishes to have someone as his teacher, Sahampati appears again and suggests to him that the Dhamma be considered his teacher. AN.ii.20<em>f.</em> SN.i.138<em>f.</em></p><p>By Ajapāla-nigrodha it was, too, that, immediately after the Enlightenment, Māra tried to persuade the Buddha to die at once. DN.ii.112 Several other conversations held here with Māra are recorded in the Saṃyutta. SN.i.103<em>f.</em></p><p>Here, also, the Buddha spent some time before the Enlightenment. DN.ii.267</p><p>The Brahmā Sutta SN.v.167 and the Magga Sutta, SN.v.185 both on the four satipaṭṭhāna, and another Brahmā Sutta SN.v.232<em>f.</em> on the five <em>indriyāni</em>, were concerning thoughts that occurred to the Buddha on various occasions at the foot of this tree, when he sat there soon after the Enlightenment. On all these occasions Brahma Sahampati appeared to him and confirmed his thoughts. Several old brahmins, advanced in years, visited the Buddha during this period and questioned him as to whether it were true that he did not pay respect to age. To them he preached the four Thera-karaṇā dhamma. AN.ii.22</p><p>24.695102, 84.9912751shrine</p>

ajavisāṇavaddhikā|ajavisaa.navaddhikaa|ajavisanavaddhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajavisaa.navaddhikaa</span></p><p>with points like goats’ horns</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajeyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajeyya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Ajjeyya;</p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>not to be taken by force Kp.viii.8 (cp. Kp-a.223).</li><li>not to be overpowered, invincible Snp.288; Ja.v.509.</li></ol><p>a + jeyya, grd. of jayati, q.v.</p><p>Ajeyya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective not decaying, not growing old, permanent Ja.vi.323. Ajja &amp; Ajja</p><p>a + jeyya, grd. of jīyati, q.v.</p>

ajeḷakā|aje.lakaa|ajelaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aje.lakaa</span></p><p>goats and sheep; (perhaps two kinds of goats?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajikakkhāyita|ajikakkhaayita|ajikakkhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajikakkhaayita</span></p><p>chewed by goats</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajikā">ajikā</a></p>

ajikā|ajikaa|ajika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajikaa</span></p><p>(and ajiyā?) a young she-goat.</p>

ajikā|ajikaa|ajika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajikaa</span></p><p>Ajiya = ajikā</p><p>(see <em><a href="bword://ajaka">ajaka</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajaka">ajaka</a></p>

ajikākhīra|ajikaakhiira|ajikakhira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajikaakhiira</span></p><p>goat’s milk</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajikā">ajikā</a></p>

ajina
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of deer or possibly an alternative name for the Blackbuck, the hide of which was often used by ascetics as a cloak Dhp.394 Ja.v.407 Vin.i.192. See <em>Miga</em>.</p><p>See also: Miga</p>

ajina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the hairy skin of an animal; esp. the skin of the black antelope (worn as a garment and used as a covering by ascetics).</p>

ajina
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the hide of the black antelope, worn as a garment by ascetics DN.i.167; Snp.1027; Ja.i.12, Ja.i.53; Ja.iv.387; Ja.v.407. kharājina a rough skin (as garment) MN.i.343; SN.iv.118; AN.ii.207; Snp.249 (= kharāni a˚-cammāni Snp-a.291). <em>dantājina</em> ivory (q.v.).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://khipa">khipa</a></em> a cloak made of a network of strips of a black antelope’s hide DN.i.167; SN.i.117; AN.i.240, AN.i.295; AN.ii.206; Vin.i.306; Vin.iii.34; Ja.vi.569.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pave%E1%B9%87i">paveṇi</a></em> a cloth of the size of a couch made from pieces of ant. skin sewn together Vin.i.192; DN.i.7 (= ajina-cammehi mañcappamāṇena sibbitvā katā paveṇi DN-a.i.87); AN.i.181.</li><li><em>-sāṭī</em> a garment of skins (= ajina-camma-sāṭī Dhp-a.iv.156) Dhp.394 = Ja.i.481 = Ja.iii.85.</li></ul><p>Vedic ajina, to aja, orig. goats’ skin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khipa">khipa</a>, <a href="bword://paveṇi">paveṇi</a></p>

ajinakkhipa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>or neuter a cloak of antelope skin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajina">ajina</a></p>

ajinappaveṇī|ajinappave.nii|ajinappaveni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajinappave.nii</span></p><p>covering or rug made from pieces of antelope skin sewn together</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajina">ajina</a></p>

ajinasāṭī|ajinasaa.tii|ajinasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajinasaa.tii</span></p><p>garment made of skins</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajina">ajina</a></p>

ajini
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor</i></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. jayati, q.v. Ajiya = ajika</p>

ajira
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>court, a yard Mhvs.35, Mhvs.3.</p><p>Vedic ajira to <em>aj</em>, cp. Gr. ἀγρός, Lat. ager, Goth. akrs = Ger. Acker, = E. acre</p>

ajita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Ajita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk. He devoted his time to explaining the Pātimokkha rules to the monks. At the time of the Second Council he was a monk of ten years’ standing and was appointed to assign seats to the Theras. Vin.ii.305</p><p>Ajita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A <em>paribbājaka</em> who visited the Buddha, and at whose instigation the Buddha preached to the Bhikkhus on the difference between dhamma and adhamma. AN.v.229<em>ff.</em></p><p>Ajita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>General of the Licchavis and follower of the Buddha. Immediately after his death he was born in Tāvatiṃsā; he visited the Buddha to refute a statement made about him by the naked ascetic Pātikaputta to the effect that he had been born in the Mahāniraya as a result of having followed the teaching of the Buddha. DN.iii.15–16</p><p>Ajita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Ajina</p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verses speak of how sometimes the wise are unknown, while the fool is honored. Thag.129–130</p>

ajitakesakambala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>AjitakesakambalaKesakambalaAjita</p><p>Head of one of the six heretical sects mentioned in the Pitakas as being contemporaneous with the Buddha. He is described as a Titthaka (non-Buddhist teacher), leader of a large following, virtuous and held in esteem by the people. SN.i.68</p><p>According to the Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN.i.55 where Ajātasattu describes a visit paid to Ajita, he taught the doctrine of “cutting off,” i.e. annihilation at death. He was a nihilist who believed in neither good nor evil. The answer Ajita gave to Ajātasattu is given elsewhere SN.iii.207 MN.i.515 as being the view of a typical sophist. His name is often introduced into the stereotyped list of the six teachers even where the views they are alleged to have expressed do not coincide with those attributed to Ajita in the Sāmaññaphala Sutta. In the Saṃyutta SN.iv.398 he is represented as talking about the rebirths of his adherents—he who denied rebirth. In the Aṅguttara AN.i.286 he seems to have been confused with Makkhali Gosāla. He was called Kesakambali because he wore a blanket of human hair, which is described as being the most miserable garment. It was cold in cold weather, hot in the hot, evil-smelling and uncouth.</p><p>Ajita was evidently much older in years than the Buddha, for we find Pasenadi, in the early years of his friendship with the Buddha, telling him that he was a young novice compared with Ajita. SN.i.68</p><p>References to ascetics wearing hair garments are found in several passages of the Pali canon. DN.i.167 MN.i.77 MN.i.238 AN.i.240</p>

ajitamāṇava|ajitamaa.nava|ajitamanava
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajitamaa.nava</span></p><p>AjitamāṇavaAjita</p><p>One of the disciples of Bāvarī who visited the Buddha at the request of their teacher.</p><p>He was the first to question the Buddha, and the questions asked by him form the Ajitamāṇavapucchā of the Parāyana Vagga of the Sutta Nipāta. Snp.1221–1228</p><p>A verse attributed to Ajita-māṇava is found in the Theragāthā. Thag.20</p><p>The Ajita-pucchā are referred to in the Saṃyutta, SN.ii.47<em>f.</em> where they are expounded by the Buddha to Sāriputta.</p>

ajiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajiya = ajikā</p><p>(see <em><a href="bword://ajaka">ajaka</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajaka">ajaka</a></p>

ajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>today; now; just now; at the present time.</p>

ajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajja &amp; Ajjā</p><p>adverb to-day, now Snp.75 Snp.153, Snp.158, Snp.970, Snp.998; Dhp.326; Ja.i.279; Ja.iii.425 (read bahutaṃ ajjā; not with Kern, Toev. s. v. as “food”); Pv.i.11#7 (= idāni Pv-a.59); Pv-a.6, Pv-a.23; Mhvs.15, Mhvs.64. Freq. in phrase <em><a href="bword://ajjatagge">ajjatagge</a></em> (= ajjato + agge(?) or ajja-tagge, see *<a href="bword://agga">agga</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) from this day onward, henceforth Vin.i.18; DN.i.85; DN-a.i.235.</p><ul><li><em>-kālaṃ</em> (adv.) this morning Ja.vi.180;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://divasa">divasa</a></em> the present day Mhvs.32, Mhvs.23.</li></ul><p>Vedic adya &amp; adyā, a + dyā, a˚ being base of demonstr. pron. (see *<a href="bword://a">a</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) and dyā an old loc. of dyaus (see <em><a href="bword://diva">diva</a></em>), thus “on this day”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjatagge">ajjatagge</a>, <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://divasa">divasa</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://diva">diva</a></p>

ajjadagge
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>from this day onward.</p>

ajjaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p><em>Ocimum canum</em> Vin.iv.35, a slender erect herb with a four-angled stem and elliptic leaves covered with fine hairs.</p>

ajjasaṭṭhim|ajjasa.t.thim|ajjasatthim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjasa.t.thim</span></p><p>since six days ago, for six days</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a></p>

ajjatagge
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>henceforth; from this day forth.</p>

ajjatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) referring or belonging to today; modern; present.</li><li>(m.) the period of the current day; this day, today.</li></ol>

ajjatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective referring to the day, today’s, present, modern (opp. <em><a href="bword://por%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">porāṇa</a></em>) Thag.552; Dhp.227; Ja.ii.409<br />■ dat. <em>ajjatanāya</em> for today Vin.i.17; Pv-a.171 &amp; passim.</p><p>cp. Sk. adyatana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://porāṇa">porāṇa</a></p>

ajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to get, procure, obtain Ja.iii.263 (?). pp. ajjita (q.v.).</p><p>Vedic arjati, <em>ṛj</em>, a variant of <em>arh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://arahati">arahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://arahati">arahati</a></p>

ajjatā|ajjataa|ajjata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjataa</span></p><p>the present time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a></p>

ajjatā|ajjataa|ajjata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjataa</span></p><p>the present time, in <em>ajjatañ ca</em> this very day SN.i.83 (variant reading ajjeva).</p><p>abstr. fr. ajja</p>

ajjava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun straight, upright (usually comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://maddava">maddava</a></em> gentle, soft) DN.iii.213; AN.i.94; AN.ii.113; AN.iii.248; Snp.250 (+ maddava), Snp.292 (id.); Ja.iii.274; Dhs.1339; Vb.359 (an˚); Snp-a.292 (= ujubhāva), Snp-a.317 (id.).</p><p>cp. Sk. ārjava, to <em>ṛju</em>, see <em><a href="bword://uju">uju</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://maddava">maddava</a>, <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ajjavatā|ajjavataa|ajjavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjavataa</span></p><p>straight forwardness, rectitude, uprightness Dhs.1339. (+ ajimhatā &amp; avankatā).</p><p>fr. prec.</p>

ajjh-
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ajjh-</p><p>(?) is the form of the preverb <em>adhi-</em> before vowels except <em>ī</em>.</p>

ajjh
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajjh-</p><p>Assimilation group of adhi + vowel.</p>

ajjhabhavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></p>

ajjhabhavi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of <em><a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></em> to conquer, overpower, overcome SN.i.240 (prohib. mā vo kodho ajjhabhavi); Ja.ii.336. Cp. ajjhabhu &amp; ajjhobhavati.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></p>

ajjhabhu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to overcome, conquer Iti.76 (dujjayaṃ a. he conquered him who is hard to conquer; variant reading ajjhabhi for ajjhabhavi). Cp. ajjhabhavi.</p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of <em><a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></em> (q.v.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></p>

ajjhabhāsi|ajjhabhaasi|ajjhabhasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhabhaasi</span></p><p>addressed.</p>

ajjhabhāsi|ajjhabhaasi|ajjhabhasi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhabhaasi</span></p><p>to address SN.iv.117 (gāthāhi); Kp v. = Snp.p.46 (gāthāya); Pv-a.56, Pv-a.90.</p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of <em>adhibhāseti</em></p>

ajjhabhāsiṃ|ajjhabhaasi.m|ajjhabhasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhabhaasi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhabhāsi">ajjhabhāsi</a></p>

ajjhabhāsiṃsu|ajjhabhaasi.msu|ajjhabhasimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhabhaasi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhabhāsi">ajjhabhāsi</a></p>

ajjhabhū|ajjhabhuu|ajjhabhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhabhuu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhibhavati">adhibhavati</a></p>

ajjhagamaṃ|ajjhagama.m|ajjhagamam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagama.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagamaṃsu|ajjhagama.msu|ajjhagamamsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagama.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagamiṃ|ajjhagami.m|ajjhagamim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagami.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagamuṃ|ajjhagamu.m|ajjhagamum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagamu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagamā|ajjhagamaa|ajjhagama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagamaa</span></p><p>ajjhagamā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg.</p><p>ajjhagamā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a>, <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagaṃ|ajjhaga.m|ajjhagam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaga.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigā">adhigā</a></p>

ajjhagaṃsu|ajjhaga.msu|ajjhagamsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaga.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigā">adhigā</a></p>

ajjhagā|ajjhagaa|ajjhaga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagaa</span></p><p>ajjhagā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg.</p><p>ajjhagā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigā">adhigā</a>, <a href="bword://adhigā">adhigā</a></p>

ajjhagā|ajjhagaa|ajjhaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhagaa</span></p><p>he came to, got to, found, obtained, experienced SN.i.12 (vimānaṃ); Snp.225 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Kp-a.180 by vindi paṭilabhi), Snp.956 (ratiṃ; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Mnd.457 by adhigacchi); Iti.69 (jātimaraṇaṃ); Dhp.154 (taṇhānaṃ khayaṃ); Vv.32#7 (visesaṃ attained distinction; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.135 by adhigata); Vv.50#21 (amataṃ santiṃ; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Vv-a.215 by variant reading SS adhigañchi, T. adhigacchati).</p><p>adhi + agā 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. pret. of <em><a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></em> (q.v. for similar forms)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigacchati">adhigacchati</a></p>

ajjhagū|ajjhaguu|ajjhagu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaguu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhigā">adhigā</a></p>

ajjhapara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>SN.v.218: substitute variant reading <em><a href="bword://accasara">accasara</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accasara">accasara</a></p>

ajjhapatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajjhappatta &amp; Ajjhapatta</p><ol><li>having reached, approached, coming near to Ja.ii.450 Ja.vi.566 (p; C. attano santikaṃ patta).</li><li>having fallen upon, attacked Ja.ii.59; Ja.v.198 (p; C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>)</li><li>attained, found, got Snp.1134 (= adhigacchi Cnd); Ja.iii.296 (p. C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>); Ja.v.158 (ajjhāpatta; C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>).</li></ol><p>adhi + ā + *prāpta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a>, <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a>, <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></p>

ajjhapattā|ajjhapattaa|ajjhapatta
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhapattaa</span></p><p>Black Kite, <em>Milvus migrans</em> Ja.ii.59 Ja.iv.57 S.v.146. A large brown-colored bird with a unique forked tail, the black kite is India’s most common hawk. It quite happily lives in towns and cities and can be seen swooping down to pick up edible garbage from the street with remarkable adroitness. It has even been known to snatch food from people’s hands. In the Jātaka we read of kites snatching meat from butchers’shops Ja.iii.100 Ja.vi.334.</p>

ajjhappatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ajjhappatta &amp; Ajjhapatta</p><ol><li>having reached, approached, coming near to Ja.ii.450 Ja.vi.566 (p; C. attano santikaṃ patta).</li><li>having fallen upon, attacked Ja.ii.59; Ja.v.198 (p; C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>)</li><li>attained, found, got Snp.1134 (= adhigacchi Cnd); Ja.iii.296 (p. C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>); Ja.v.158 (ajjhāpatta; C. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em>).</li></ol><p>adhi + ā + *prāpta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a>, <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a>, <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></p>

ajjhappattā|ajjhappattaa|ajjhappatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhappattaa</span></p><p>fallen upon or rushed at.</p>

ajjhatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>(from ajjhattaṃ) inner; subjective; within oneself; what is internal; oneself.</p>

ajjhatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun that which is personal, subjective, arises from within (in contrast to anything outside, objective or impersonal); as adv. &amp; ˚interior, personal, inwardly (opp.; <em>bahiddhā bāhira</em> etc outward, outwardly); Cp. ajjhattika &amp; see Dhs. trsl. 272. DN.i.37 (subjective, inward, of the peace of the 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> jhāna), DN.i.70 = AN.ii.210; AN.v.206 (inward happiness. a. sukkhaṃ niyakajjhattaṃ attano santāne ti attho DN-a.i.183 cp. Dhs-a.169, Dhs-a.338, Dhs-a.361); SN.i.70, SN.i.169; SN.ii.27 (kathaṃ kathī hoti is in inward doubt), SN.ii.40 (sukhaṃ dukkhaṃ); SN.iii.180 (id.), SN.iv.1 sq. (āyatanāni), SN.iv.139, SN.iv.196; SN.v.74 (<em>ṭhitaṃ cittaṃ ajjhattaṃ</em> susaṇṭhitaṃ suvimuttaṃ a mind firm, inwardly well planted, quite set free), SN.v.110, SN.v.143, SN.v.263, SN.v.297, SN.v.390; AN.i.40 (rūpasaññī), AN.i.272 (kāmacchanda etc.); AN.ii.158. (sukhadukkhaṃ), AN.ii.211; AN.ii.86 (cetosamatha), AN.iii.92 (vūpasantacitta); AN.iv.32 (sankhittaṃ), AN.iv.57(itthindriyaṃ), AN.iv.299 (cittaṃ), AN.iv.305 (rūpasaññī), AN.iv.360 (cetosamatha), AN.iv.437 (vūpasantacitta); AN.v.79 sq. AN.v.335 sq. (sati); Iti.39 (cetosamatha inward peace), Iti.80, Iti.82, Iti.94; Ja.i.045 (chātajjhatta with hungry insides); Ja.v.338 (id.) Pts.i.76 (cakkhu etc.); Dhs.161 (= attano jātaṃ Dhs-a.169), Dhs.204, Dhs.1044; Pp.59; Vb.1 sq. (khandhā), Vb.228 (sati), Vb.327 (paññā), Vb.342 (arū’pasaññī)<br />■ adv. <em>-ṃ</em> inwardly personally (in contrast-pair <em>ajjhattaṃ vā bahiddhā vā</em>; see also cpd. ˚bahiddhā) AN.i.284; AN.ii.171; AN.iv.305; AN.v.61; Snp.917 (= upajjhayassa vā ā ācariyassa vā te guṇā assū ti Mnd.350).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81ramma%E1%B9%87a">ārammaṇa</a></em> a subjective object of thought Dhs.1047</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cintin">cintin</a></em> thought occupied with internal things Snp.174, Snp.388</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bahiddh%C4%81">bahiddhā</a></em> inside &amp; outside, personal-external, mutual interacting SN.ii.252 sq.; SN.iii.47; SN.iv.382; Cnd.15; Dhs.1049 etc. (see also <em><a href="bword://bahiddh%C4%81">bahiddhā</a></em>).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rata">rata</a></em> with inward joy DN.ii.107; SN.v.263 = Dhp.362 = Ud.64 (+ samāhita); Thag.981; AN.iv.312; Dhp-a.iv.90 (= gocar’ ajjhatta-sankhātāya kammaṭṭhāna-bhāvanāya rata).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em> one’s own or inner form Vin.iii.113 (opp. bahiddhā-rūpa &amp; ajjh˚-bah˚ r.).;</li><li><em>-saññojana</em> an inner fetter, inward bond AN.i.63 sq.; Pp.22 Vb.361.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://santi">santi</a></em> inner peace Snp.837 (= ajjhattānaṃ rāgādīnaṃ santibhāva Snp-a.545; cp. Mnd.185).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://samu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81na">samuṭṭhāna</a></em> originating from within Ja.i.207 (of hiri; opp. bahiddhā˚).</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. adhyātma, cp. attā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārammaṇa">ārammaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://cintin">cintin</a>, <a href="bword://bahiddhā">bahiddhā</a>, <a href="bword://bahiddhā">bahiddhā</a>, <a href="bword://rata">rata</a>, <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a>, <a href="bword://santi">santi</a>, <a href="bword://samuṭṭhāna">samuṭṭhāna</a></p>

ajjhattabahiddhā|ajjhattabahiddhaa|ajjhattabahiddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhattabahiddhaa</span></p><p>inwardly and outwardly; in regard to oneself and others</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhattaṃ">ajjhattaṃ</a></p>

ajjhattaṃ|ajjhatta.m|ajjhattam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhatta.m</span></p><p>concerning oneself in oneself; inwardly, personally; in regard to oneself (opp. bahiddhā).</p>

ajjhattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>personal; internal, inward; belonging to oneself (opp. bāhirā); what is personal, what belongs to oneself.</p>

ajjhattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective personal, inward (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 207 &amp; Mnd.346: ajjhattikaṃ vuccati cittaṃ) opp. <em><a href="bword://b%C4%81hira">bāhira</a></em> outward (q.v.). See also āyatana<br />■ MN.i.62 SN.i.73 (˚ā rakkhā na bāhirā); SN.iv.7 sq. (āyatanāni); SN.v.101 (anga); AN.i.16 (anga); AN.ii.164 (dhātuyo); AN.iii.400 (āyatanāni); AN.v.52 (id.); Iti.114 (id.), Iti.9 (anga); Kp iv. (= Kp-a.82); Ja.iv.402 (bāhira-vatthuṃ ayācitvā ajjhattikassa nāmaṃ gaṇhati); Dhs.673, Dhs.751; Vb.13, Vb.67, Vb.82 sq., Vb.119, Vb.131, Vb.392 sq.</p><p>ajjhatta + ika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bāhira">bāhira</a></p>

ajjhavodahi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. aor. of <em>ajjhodahati</em> to put down Ja.v.365 (= odahi, ṭhapesi C.). Kern, Toev s. v. proposes reading ajjhavādahi (= Sk. avādhāt).</p><p>Sk. adhyavadhāti</p>

ajjhayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>study (learning by heart) of the Vedas Mil.225. See also ajjhena.</p><p>adhi + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

ajjhena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>learning, study (esp. of the Vedas); recitation.</p>

ajjhena
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>study (esp. of the Vedas) MN.iii.1; Ja.ii.327 (as variant reading to be preferred to ajjhesanā); Ja.iii.114 (= japa); Ja.v.10 (pl. = vede), Ja.vi.201 = Ja.vi.207; Vb.353; Snp-a.314 (mant’).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kujja">kujja</a></em> (˚kūta variant reading?) a hypocrite, a pharisee Snp.242 cp. Snp-a.286.</li></ul><p>Sk. adhyayana, see also <em><a href="bword://ajjhayana">ajjhayana</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kujja">kujja</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhayana">ajjhayana</a></p>

ajjhesana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>request, entreaty (esp. to expound or recite the dhamma).</p>

ajjhesanā|ajjhesanaa|ajjhesana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhesanaa</span></p><p>request, entreaty Vin.i.6 = DN.ii.38 = SN.i.138; Ja.ii.327 (better variant reading ajjhena).</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></p>

ajjhesati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>requests; asks (esp for instruction or recitation of the dhamma).</p>

ajjhesati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to request, ask, bid Dhp-a.iv.18; aor. <em>ajjhesi</em> Vin.ii.200; pp <em>ajjhiṭṭha &amp; ajjhesita;</em> (q.v.), with which cp. pariyiṭṭha &amp; ˚esita.</p><p>adhi + <em>iṣ</em>; cp. BSk. adhyeṣate Divy.160</p>

ajjhesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></p>

ajjhesita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>requested, asked, bidden Cnd.16 (= ajjhiṭṭha).</p><p>pp. of ajjhesati; cp. ajjhiṭṭha</p>

ajjhesitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></p>

ajjheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cares for, longs for, broods.</p>

ajjheti
