ajjhohata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>having swallowed Sdhp.610 (balisaṃ maccho viya: like a fish the fishhook).</p><p>pp. of ajjhoharati</p>

ajjhohāra|ajjhohaara|ajjhohara
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhohaara</span></p><p>A large and probably fantastic marine creature Ja.v.462. See <em>Timi</em>.</p><p>See also: Timi</p>

ajjhohāra|ajjhohaara|ajjhohara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhohaara</span></p><p>taking food; swallowing.</p>

ajjhohāra|ajjhohaara|ajjhohara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhohaara</span></p><ol><li>taking food, swallowing, eating &amp; drinking Vin.iv.233; Mil.176, Mil.366.</li><li>N of a fabulous fish (swallower”; cp. timingala) Ja.v.462.</li></ol><p>Sk. abhyavahāra</p>

ajjhokirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>bestrews; scatters down on.</p>

ajjhokāsa|ajjhokaasa|ajjhokasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhokaasa</span></p><p>(from ajjhokāse) the open air.</p>

ajjhokāsa|ajjhokaasa|ajjhokasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhokaasa</span></p><p>the open air, only in loc. <em>ajjhokāse</em> in the open Vin.i.15; SN.i.212; Dhp-a.iv.100.</p><p>adhi + okāsa</p>

ajjhokāsagata|ajjhokaasagata|ajjhokasagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhokaasagata</span></p><p>being in the open air</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhokāsa">ajjhokāsa</a></p>

ajjhokāse|ajjhokaase|ajjhokase
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhokaase</span></p><p>in the open air, in the open.</p>

ajjholambati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>hangs down (over).</p>

ajjholambati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to hang or hold on to (acc.), to cling to SN.iii.137; MN.iii.164 = Ne.179 cp. Sdhp.284 &amp; Sdhp.296.</p><p>adhi + ava + <em>lamb</em></p>

ajjhomaddati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>treads upon, crushes down.</p>

ajjhomaddati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to crush down AN.iv.191, AN.iv.193.</p><p>adhi + ava + <em>mṛd</em></p>

ajjhomucchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>swooning; clinging to (in longing).</p>

ajjhomucchita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>stiffened out (in a swoon), lying in a faint (?) AN.iii.57 sq (variant reading ajjhomuñcïta or ˚muccita better: sarīre attached to her body, clinging to her b.).</p><p>pp. adhi + ava + <em>mūrch</em>, cp. adhimuccita</p>

ajjhopanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>addicted to, enthralled.</p>

ajjhopanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>? only found in one stock phrase, viz. gathita (q.v.) mucchita ajjhopanna with ref. to selfishness, greed bonds of craving. The reading <em><a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a></em> is the lectio difficilior, but the accredited reading <em><a href="bword://ajjhos%C4%81na">ajjhosāna</a></em> seems to be clearer and to harmonize better with the cognate ajjhosita &amp; ajjhosāna (n.) in the same context. The confusion between the two is old-standing and hard to be accounted for. Trenckner under variant reading to MN.i.162 on p. 543 gives ajjhopanna as BB (= adhi-opanna). The MSS. of Nd ii.clearly show ajjhopanna as inferior reading, which may well be attributable to the very frequent SS substitution of p for s (see Nd ii.Introd. xix.). Besides this mixture of vv.ll. with s and p there is another confusion between the vv.ll. <em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81panna">ajjhāpanna</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a></em> which adds to the complication of the case. However since the evidence of a better reading between these two preponderates for ajjhopanna we may consider the o as established and, with a little more clearness to be desired, may in the end decide for <em><a href="bword://ajjhos%C4%81na">ajjhosāna</a></em> (q.v.), which in this case would have been liable to change through analogy with ajjhāpanna, from which it took the ā and p. Cp. also ajjhosita. The foll. is a synopsis of readings as preferred or confused by the Ed. of the var. texts.</p><ol><li><em><a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a></em> as T. reading: MN.i.162, MN.i.173, MN.i.369; AN.i.74; AN.ii.28 AN.iii.68, AN.iii.242; Mnd 75, Mnd.76; DN-a.i.59; <em>as variant reading</em>: DN.i.245.</li><li><em>ajjhosāna as variant reading</em>: AN.i.74 (C. explains ajjhosāya gilitvā ṭhita); Nd ii.under nissita &amp; passim; Ud.75, Ud.76 (ajjhosanna); DN-a.i.59 (id.).</li><li><em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81panna">ajjhāpanna</a></em> as T. reading DN.i.245; DN.iii.43, DN.iii.46; SN.ii.194, SN.ii.270: SN.iv.332 (ajjhapaṇṇa) AN.v.178, AN.v.181; Nd ii.under nissita; Mil.401; <em>as variant reading</em> MN.i.162; AN.iii.242; Ud.75, Ud.76.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhosāna">ajjhosāna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhāpanna">ajjhāpanna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhosāna">ajjhosāna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhāpanna">ajjhāpanna</a></p>

ajjhosa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosati">ajjhosati</a></p>

ajjhosa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= ajjhosāya, in verse only as <em>ajjhosa tiṭṭhati</em> to cleave or cling to SN.iv.73; Thag.98, Thag.794.</p>

ajjhosati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>grasps, clings to; relishes.</p>

ajjhosati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be bound to, to be attached, bent on; to desire, cleave to, indulge in. Fut. <em>ajjhosissati</em> (does it belong here?) MN.i.328 (c. acc. paṭhaviṃ, better as ajjhesati). grd. <em>ajjhositabha</em> MN.i.109 (+ abhinanditabba, variant reading ˚etabba); Dhs-a.5 (id.); ger. <em><a href="bword://ajjhos%C4%81ya">ajjhosāya</a></em> (q.v.) pp. <em><a href="bword://ajjhosita">ajjhosita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>adhi + ava + sayati, <strong>sā</strong>, to bind, pp. sita: see <em><a href="bword://ajjhosita">ajjhosita</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosāya">ajjhosāya</a>, <a href="bword://ajjhosita">ajjhosita</a>, sā, <a href="bword://ajjhosita">ajjhosita</a></p>

ajjhosita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(act.) attached to, grasping; being bent on.</li><li>(pass.) grasped, caught</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosati">ajjhosati</a></p>

ajjhosita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>hanging on, cleaving to, being bent on, (c. loc.) SN.ii.94 (+ mamāyita); AN.ii.25 (diṭṭha suta muta +); Mnd.75 Mnd.106, Mnd.163 = Nd ii under nissita; Thig.470 (asāre = taṇhāvasena abhiniviṭṭha Thag-a.284); Pv.iv.8#4 (mayhaṃ ghare taṇhābhinivisena abhiniviṭṭha Pv-a.267; variant reading BB ajjhesita SS ajjhāsita). <em>-an˚</em>; SN.iv.213; SN.v.319; Mnd.411; Mil.74 (pabbajita).</p><p>cp. Sk. adhyavasita, from adhi + ava + <strong>sā</strong>; but sita is liable to confusion with sita = Sk. śrita, also through likeness of meaning with esita; see <em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81sita">ajjhāsita</a></em> &amp; ajjhesita</p><p>See also: sā, <a href="bword://ajjhāsita">ajjhāsita</a></p>

ajjhositabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosita">ajjhosita</a></p>

ajjhosāna|ajjhosaana|ajjhosana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhosaana</span></p><p>(&amp; m.?) grasping, clinging to; relishing.</p>

ajjhosāna|ajjhosaana|ajjhosana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhosaana</span></p><p>cleaving to (earthly joys), attachment, DN.ii.58 sq.; DN.iii.289; MN.i.498 (+ abhinandana); SN.iii.187; AN.i.66; AN.ii.11 (diṭṭhi˚, kāma˚ + taṇhā). In comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with (icchā) and mucchā at Nd ii.under chanda &amp; nissita and taṇhā (see also <em><a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a></em>), and at Dhs.1059 of lābha (the expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Dhs-a.363, Dhs-a.370, from as to eat, is popular etym.) Ne.23 sq. (of taṇhā).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhopanna">ajjhopanna</a></p>

ajjhosāya|ajjhosaaya|ajjhosaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhosaaya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosati">ajjhosati</a></p>

ajjhosāya|ajjhosaaya|ajjhosaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhosaaya</span></p><p>being tied to, hanging on, attached to only in phrase <em>a. tiṭṭhati</em> (+ abhinandati, same in Divy MN.i.266; SN.iv.36 sq.; SN.iv.60, SN.iv.71 sq.; Mil.69. See also <em><a href="bword://ajjhosa">ajjhosa</a></em>.</p><p>ger. of ajjhosati, cp. BSk. adhyavasāya tiṣṭhati Divy.37, Divy.534</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhosa">ajjhosa</a></p>

ajjhotharīyati|ajjhothariiyati|ajjhothariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhothariiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhottharati">ajjhottharati</a></p>

ajjhottharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>covers, spreads over; overwhelms, overpowers; threatens.</p>

ajjhottharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cover over, spread out, spread over, cover; to submerge, flood Vin.i.111; Ja.i.61, Ja.i.72, Ja.i.73; Mil.296, Mil.336; Dhp.i.264; Pass. <em>-tthariyati</em> to be overrun with (instr.), to be smothered, to be flooded AN.iii.92 = Pp.67; aor. <strong>ajjhotthari</strong> Vv-a.48 (gāmapadeso: was flooded). pp. <em><a href="bword://ajjhotthata">ajjhotthata</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>adhi + ava + <em>stṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: ajjhotthari, <a href="bword://ajjhotthata">ajjhotthata</a></p>

ajjhottharatitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhottharati">ajjhottharati</a></p>

ajjhotthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>spread over; covered, filled; overcome, crushed, overpowered Ja.i.363 (ajjhottaṭa) Ja.i.410; Ja.v.91 (= adhipanna); Dhp-a.i.278; Pv-a.55; Dāvs v.5.</p><p>pp. of ajjhottharati</p>

ajjhoṭhapeti|ajjho.thapeti|ajjhothapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjho.thapeti</span></p><p>to bring to Pv-a.148 (gāmaṃ), where we should read ˚ṭṭhapeti.</p><p>adhi + ava + ṭhapeti, Caus. of <em>sthā</em></p>

ajjhupagacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes to, reaches, enters (a state); consents to, agrees.</p>

ajjhupagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to come to, to reach, obtain; to consent to, agree, submit Thig.474 (= sampaṭicchati Thag-a.285); Ja.ii.403; Mil.300; pp. <em><a href="bword://ajjhupagata">ajjhupagata</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>adhi + upa + <em>gam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupagata">ajjhupagata</a></p>

ajjhupagamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(from ajjhupagacchati) consent, agreeing to.</p>

ajjhupagamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>consent, agreement, justification Vin.ii.97, Vin.ii.104.</p><p>adhi + upa + <em>gam</em></p>

ajjhupagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>come to, obtained, reached AN.v.87, cp. AN.v.210; AN.v.187 sq.</p><p>pp. of ajjhupagacchati</p>

ajjhupaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take (food) to oneself Ja.ii.293 (aor. ajjhupāhari = ajjhohari C.).</p><p>adhi + upa + <em>hṛ</em>; cp. upaharati</p>

ajjhupekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhat">ajjhupekkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhati">ajjhupekkhati</a></p>

ajjhupekkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><em>-ā</em> (f.) care, diligence, attention Pts.i.16; Pts.ii.119; Vb.230 sq. Dhp-a.iv.3.</p><p>abstr. from ajjhupekkhati</p>

ajjhupekkhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhat">ajjhupekkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhati">ajjhupekkhati</a></p>

ajjhupekkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to look on AN.i.257; Mil.275.</li><li>to look on intently or with care, to oversee, to take care of AN.iv.45 (kaṭṭh’aggi, has to be looked after); Pv-a.149 (sisaṃ colaṃ vā).</li><li>to look on indifferently to be indifferent to neglect Vin.ii.78 = Vin.iii.162, cp. Ja.i.147; MN.i.155 MN.ii.223; AN.iii.194, AN.iii.435; Ja.v.229; Dhp-a.iv.125.</li></ol><p>adhi + upa + <em>ikṣ</em>; cp. BSk. adhyupekṣati</p>

ajjhupekkhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhitar">ajjhupekkhitar</a></p><p>(from ajjhupekkhati) one who looks on with disinterestedness, detachment.</p>

ajjhupekkhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhati">ajjhupekkhati</a></p>

ajjhupekkhitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhitar">ajjhupekkhitar</a></p><p>(from ajjhupekkhati) one who looks on with disinterestedness, detachment.</p>

ajjhupekkhitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who looks on (carefully), one who takes care or controls, an overseer caretaker SN.v.69 (sādhukaṃ), SN.v.324 (id.), SN.v.331 sq. Vb.227.</p><p>n. ag. to ajjhupekkhati</p>

ajjhupekkhitvā|ajjhupekkhitvaa|ajjhupekkhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhupekkhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhati">ajjhupekkhati</a></p>

ajjhupekkhiṃsu|ajjhupekkhi.msu|ajjhupekkhimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhupekkhi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupekkhati">ajjhupekkhati</a></p>

ajjhupesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupeti">ajjhupeti</a></p>

ajjhupeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes to; approaches; goes to meet.</p>

ajjhupeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go to meet, to receive Ja.iv.440.</p><p>cp. Sk. abhyupeti; adhi + upa + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

ajjhupeyyaṃ|ajjhupeyya.m|ajjhupeyyam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhupeyya.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupeti">ajjhupeti</a></p>

ajjhābhava|ajjhaabhava|ajjhabhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaabhava</span></p><p>excessive power, predominance Ja.ii.357.</p><p>cp. Sk. adhyābhava</p>

ajjhābhavati|ajjhaabhavati|ajjhabhavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaabhavati</span></p><p>to predominate Ja.ii.357.</p><p>adhi + ā + <em><a href="bword://bh%C5%AB">bhū</a></em>, in meaning of abhi + <em>bhu</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhū">bhū</a></p>

ajjhācarati|ajjhaacarati|ajjhacarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaacarati</span></p><p>practices, commits (an offense).</p>

ajjhācarati|ajjhaacarati|ajjhacarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaacarati</span></p><ol><li>to conduct oneself according to Vin.ii.301; MN.i.523; Mil.266.</li><li>to flirt with (perhaps to embrace) Ja.iv.231 (aññam-aññaṃ) pp. <em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81ci%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">ajjhāciṇṇa</a></em>. See also accāvadati &amp; aticarati.</li></ol><p>adhi (or ati?) + ā + <em>car</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāciṇṇa">ajjhāciṇṇa</a></p>

ajjhācare|ajjhaacare|ajjhacare
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaacare</span></p><p>as regards conduct.</p>

ajjhāciṇṇa|ajjhaaci.n.na|ajjhacinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaci.n.na</span></p><p>much practiced; habitually done</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhācarati">ajjhācarati</a></p>

ajjhāciṇṇa|ajjhaaci.n.na|ajjhacinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaci.n.na</span></p><p>habitually done Vin.ii.80 sq., Vin.ii.301.</p><p>pp. of ajjhācarati</p>

ajjhācāra|ajjhaacaara|ajjhacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaacaara</span></p><p>misconduct, transgression (esp. sexual).</p>

ajjhācāra|ajjhaacaara|ajjhacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaacaara</span></p><ol><li>minor conduct (conduct of a bhikkhu as to those minor rules not included in the Pārājikas or Saṃghādisesas) Vin.i.63 (see note in <em>Vin. Texts</em>, i.184.</li><li>flirtation Vin.iii.128 (in the Old Cy as expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of avabhāsati).</li><li>sexual intercourse Ja.i.396; Ja.v.327 (˚cara variant reading for ajjhāvara); Mil.127 (an˚).</li></ol><p>to adhi (ati?) + ā + <em>car</em></p>

ajjhāgamuṃ|ajjhaagamu.m|ajjhagamum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaagamu.m</span></p><p>they found.</p>

ajjhāgāre|ajjhaagaare|ajjhagare
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaagaare</span></p><p>at home, in one’s house.</p>

ajjhāgāre|ajjhaagaare|ajjhagare
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaagaare</span></p><p>adverb at home, in one’s own house AN.i.132 = Iti.109; AN.ii.70.</p><p>adhi + agāre, loc. of agāra</p>

ajjhājapajjanta|ajjhaajapajjanta|ajjhajapajjanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaajapajjanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n. committing an offense; incurring; guilty of.</p>

ajjhājapatti|ajjhaajapatti|ajjhajapatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaajapatti</span></p><p>transgression; committing of (an offense).</p>

ajjhājīva|ajjhaajiiva|ajjhajiva
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaajiiva</span></p><p>too rigorous or strenuous a livelihood MN.ii.245 (+ adhipāṭimokkha).</p><p>adhi (ati?) + ā + <em>jīv</em></p>

ajjhājīve|ajjhaajiive|ajjhajive
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaajiive</span></p><p>as regards way of life.</p>

ajjhāpajjati|ajjhaapajjati|ajjhapajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapajjati</span></p><p>to commit an offence, to incur, to become guilty of (acc.) Vin.iv.237. pp. <em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81panna">ajjhāpanna</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>adhi + ā + <em>pad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāpanna">ajjhāpanna</a></p>

ajjhāpana|ajjhaapana|ajjhapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapana</span></p><p>Ajjhāpana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter teaching of the sacred writ, instruction Mil.225.</p><p>fr. Caus. ii. of ajjheti</p><p>Ajjhāpana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter burning, conflagration Ja.vi.311.</p><p>ā + jhāpana fr. <em>kṣā</em></p>

ajjhāpanna|ajjhaapanna|ajjhapanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapanna</span></p><ol><li>(act.) who has committed, become guilty of (an offense).</li><li>(pass.) committed?.</li></ol>

ajjhāpanna|ajjhaapanna|ajjhapanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapanna</span></p><p>become guilty of offence DN.i.245; DN.iii.43; SN.ii.270; AN.iv.277, AN.iv.280; AN.v.178, AN.v.181 <em>an˚</em>; guiltless, innocent Vin.i.103; DN.iii.46; SN.ii.194, SN.ii.269; AN.v.181; Mil.401. For all passages except AN.iv.277 AN.iv.280, cp. ajjhopanna.</p><p>pp. of adhi + āpajjati</p>

ajjhāpannaka|ajjhaapannaka|ajjhapannaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapannaka</span></p><p>one who has committed an offense.</p>

ajjhāpatti|ajjhaapatti|ajjhapatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapatti</span></p><p>incurring guilt Dhs.299 (an˚).</p><p>abstr. to ajjhāpajjati</p>

ajjhāpīḷita|ajjhaapii.lita|ajjhapilita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaapii.lita</span></p><p>harassed, overpowered, tormented Pv-a.180 (khuppipāsāya by hunger &amp; thirst).</p><p>adhi + ā + pīḷita</p>

ajjhāruha|ajjhaaruha|ajjharuha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaruha</span></p><p>(&amp; <em>-rūha</em>) adjective growing up over, overwhelming AN.iii.63 sq. = SN.v.96; Ja.iii.399.</p><p>to adhi + ā + <em>ruh</em></p>

ajjhāruḷha|ajjhaaru.lha|ajjharulha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn. (pass.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaru.lha</span></p><p>grown over, grown upon; spread over, dominated</p><p>See also: ajjhāruhati</p>

ajjhārāma|ajjhaaraama|ajjharama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaraama</span></p><p>the territory, grounds of a park.</p>

ajjhārāme|ajjhaaraame|ajjharame
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaraame</span></p><p>within a park or monastery.</p>

ajjhārūha|ajjhaaruuha|ajjharuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaruuha</span></p><p>overgrowing, dominating, parasitical.</p>

ajjhārūhati|ajjhaaruuhati|ajjharuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaruuhati</span></p><p>grows in and up over (as a parasite); spreads over, dominates.</p>

ajjhārūhati|ajjhaaruuhati|ajjharuhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaruuhati</span></p><p>to rise into the air, to climb over, spread over SN.i.221 = Ne.173 (ajjhottharati SA; cp. Mrs. Rh. D. <em>Kindred Sayings</em> i.285).</p><p>adhi + ārohati cp. atyārohati</p>

ajjhārūḷha|ajjhaaruu.lha|ajjharulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaaruu.lha</span></p><p>adjective grown up or high over Ja.iii.399.</p><p>pp. of adhi + ā + <em>ruh</em></p>

ajjhāsaya|ajjhaasaya|ajjhasaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaasaya</span></p><p>intention, desire, wish, disposition, bent DN.ii.224 (adj.: intent on, practising); Ja.i.88, Ja.i.90; Ja.ii.352; Ja.v.382; Dhs-a.314, Dhs-a.334; Pv-a.88, Pv-a.116, Pv-a.133 (adj. dān˚ intent on on giving alms), Pv-a.168 Sdhp.219 Sdhp.518.</p><p>Frequently in phrase <em>ajjhāsayānurūpa</em> according to his wish, as he wanted Pv-a.61 Pv-a.106 Pv-a.128</p><p>fr. adhi + ā + <em>śri</em>, orig. hanging on, leaning on, BSk. however adhyāśaya Divy.586</p>

ajjhāsayatā|ajjhaasayataa|ajjhasayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaasayataa</span></p><p>desire, longing Pv-a.127 (uḷār˚ great desire for c. loc.).</p><p>abstr. to ajjhāsaya</p>

ajjhāsayaṃ|ajjhaasaya.m|ajjhasayam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaasaya.m</span></p><p>as to the intention, state of mind.</p>

ajjhāsita|ajjhaasita|ajjhasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaasita</span></p><p>intent on, bent on Mil.361 (jhān˚). Cp. ajjhosita &amp; nissita.</p><p>pp. of adhi + ā + <em>śri</em></p>

ajjhāvadati|ajjhaavadati|ajjhavadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavadati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://acc%C4%81vadati">accāvadati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accāvadati">accāvadati</a></p>

ajjhāvara|ajjhaavara|ajjhavara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavara</span></p><p>surrounding; waiting on, service, retinue Ja.v.322, Ja.v.324, Ja.v.326, Ja.v.327 (explained at all passages by <em><a href="bword://paris%C4%81">parisā</a></em>). Should we read ajjhācara? Cp. ajjhācāra.</p><p>fr. adhi + ā + <em>var</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parisā">parisā</a></p>

ajjhāvasa|ajjhaavasa|ajjhavasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasat">ajjhāvasat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvasanta|ajjhaavasanta|ajjhavasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvasat|ajjhaavasat|ajjhavasat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasat">ajjhāvasat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvasatar|ajjhaavasatar|ajjhavasatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasatar</span></p><p>one who inhabits DN.i.63 (agāraṃ).</p><p>n. ag. to ajjhāvasati</p>

ajjhāvasatha|ajjhaavasatha|ajjhavasatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasatha</span></p><p>the inside or environs of a house or dwelling.</p>

ajjhāvasathe|ajjhaavasathe|ajjhavasathe
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasathe</span></p><p>within a house or dwelling.</p>

ajjhāvasati|ajjhaavasati|ajjhavasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasati</span></p><p>inhabits; dwells in, lives on (as ruler or owner); esp. <em>agāram ~ati</em>, lives the settled life of a householder.</p>

ajjhāvasati|ajjhaavasati|ajjhavasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasati</span></p><p>to inhabit (agāraṃ a house; i.e. to be settled or live the settled life of a householder DN.ii.16; MN.i.353; Vin.iv.224; Ja.i.50; Pp.57; Mil.348<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ajjh%C4%81vuttha">ajjhāvuttha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>adhi + ā + <em>vas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvuttha">ajjhāvuttha</a></p>

ajjhāvasaṃ|ajjhaavasa.m|ajjhavasam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvasī|ajjhaavasii|ajjhavasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavasii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvuttha|ajjhaavuttha|ajjhavuttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavuttha</span></p><p>lived in, inhabited, occupied</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhāvasati">ajjhāvasati</a></p>

ajjhāvuttha|ajjhaavuttha|ajjhavuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaavuttha</span></p><p>inhabited, occupied (of a house) Vin.ii.210; Ja.i.145; Ja.ii.333; Pv-a.24 (˚ghara); fig. (not) occupied by Snp-a.566 (= anosita).</p><p>cp. Sk. adhyuṣita; pp. of ajjhāvasati</p>

ajjhāyaka|ajjhaayaka|ajjhayaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaayaka</span></p><p>one who studies (the Vedas); a studious, learned person, a scholar.</p>

ajjhāyaka|ajjhaayaka|ajjhayaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhaayaka</span></p><p>(a brahmin) engaged in learning the Veda (<strong>mantajjhāyaka</strong> Ja.vi.209; Snp-a.192), a scholar of the brahmanic texts, a studious learned person DN.i.88, DN.i.120; DN.iii.94; AN.i.163; AN.iii.223; Snp.140 (˚kula: thus for ajjhāyakula Fsb.); Thag.1171; Ja.i.3 Ja.vi.201, Ja.vi.498; DN-a.i.247.</p><p>cp. Sk. adhyāyaka, cp. ajjhayana</p><p>See also: mantajjhāyaka</p>

ajjhūpagata|ajjhuupagata|ajjhupagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. (act.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhuupagata</span></p><p>who has come to, reached; entered upon; agreed to; gone over to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhupagacchati">ajjhupagacchati</a></p>

ajjhūpekkhati|ajjhuupekkhati|ajjhupekkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjhuupekkhati</span></p><p>looks on without interfering; views with impartiality or disinterestedness; tolerates, condones.</p>

ajjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>obtained Sdhp.98.</p><p>pp. of ajjati</p>

ajjuka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of plant Vin.vi.34.</p>

ajjuka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk of Vesālī. In settling a dispute regarding the estate of his lay-supporter, he was accused of partisanship by one of the parties concerned and was reported to Ānanda. The case went up before Upāli, who decided in favour of Ajjuka. Vin.iii.66–67</p>

ajjuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>name of a plant, perhaps white basil.</p>

ajjuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Name of a plant, Ocimum Gratissimum Vin.iv.35; DN-a.i.81 (all MSS. have ajjaka).</p><p>*Sk. arjaka</p>

ajjukanna
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of tree Ja.vi.535.</p>

ajjukaṇṇa|ajjuka.n.na|ajjukanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjuka.n.na</span></p><p>Name of a tree Pentaptera Tomentosa Ja.vi.535 (nn).</p><p>*Sk. arjakarṇa</p>

ajjuna
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Sometimes also <em><a href="bword://kakudha">kakudha</a></em>, Hindi <em>arjun</em>, <em>Terminalia arjuna</em>, a large tree sometimes attaining a girth of 3.6 m and a height of 30 m and commonly found growing on the banks of rivers Ja.vi.518. It has pale greenish or grey bark, long inclined branches and white flowers. The bark is sometimes boiled in water to make a tonic. After washing his robe the Buddha climbed out of the Nerañjara River by grasping a branch of a <em><a href="bword://kakudha">kakudha</a></em> tree Vin.i.28. The <em>Mahāvastu</em> gives the name as <em>kakubha</em> and describes the tree as being beautiful and having wide-spreading branches Mvu.iii.302. The past Buddha Anomadassī attained enlightenment under an <em>ajjuna</em> tree Bv.8.23.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kakudha">kakudha</a>, <a href="bword://kakudha">kakudha</a></p>

ajjuna
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse describes understanding the Dhamma as like drawing oneself from water to dry land. Thag.88</p>

ajjuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the tree Pentaptera Arjuna Ja.vi.535; Dhp-a.i.105 (˚rukkha).</p><p>Vedic arjuna, to <em>raj</em>; cp. Gr. ἀργός white, α ̓́ργυρος silver, Lat. argentum</p>

ajjuṇho|ajju.nho|ajjunho
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajju.nho</span></p><p>this day; the rest of this day and night.</p>

ajjuṇho|ajju.nho|ajjunho
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajju.nho</span></p><p>adverb this moonlight night Vin.i.25; Vin.iv.80.</p><p>haplology fr. ajja-juṇho; see <em><a href="bword://ju%E1%B9%87h%C4%81">juṇhā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juṇhā">juṇhā</a></p>

ajjā|ajjaa|ajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajjaa</span></p><p>Ajja &amp; Ajjā</p><p>adverb to-day, now Snp.75 Snp.153, Snp.158, Snp.970, Snp.998; Dhp.326; Ja.i.279; Ja.iii.425 (read bahutaṃ ajjā; not with Kern, Toev. s. v. as “food”); Pv.i.11#7 (= idāni Pv-a.59); Pv-a.6, Pv-a.23; Mhvs.15, Mhvs.64. Freq. in phrase <em><a href="bword://ajjatagge">ajjatagge</a></em> (= ajjato + agge(?) or ajja-tagge, see *<a href="bword://agga">agga</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) from this day onward, henceforth Vin.i.18; DN.i.85; DN-a.i.235.</p><ul><li><em>-kālaṃ</em> (adv.) this morning Ja.vi.180;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://divasa">divasa</a></em> the present day Mhvs.32, Mhvs.23.</li></ul><p>Vedic adya &amp; adyā, a + dyā, a˚ being base of demonstr. pron. (see *<a href="bword://a">a</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) and dyā an old loc. of dyaus (see <em><a href="bword://diva">diva</a></em>), thus “on this day”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjatagge">ajjatagge</a>, <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://divasa">divasa</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a>, <a href="bword://diva">diva</a></p>

ajā|ajaa|aja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaa</span></p><p>female goat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aja">aja</a></p>

ajā|ajaa|aja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaa</span></p><p>she-goat Ja.iii.125; Ja.iv.251.</p>

ajānana|ajaanana|ajanana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaanana</span></p><p>˚- neuter not knowing, ignorance (of) Ja.v.199 (˚bhāva); Ja.vi.177 (˚kāla).</p><p>a + jānana</p>

ajātasattu|ajaatasattu|ajatasattu
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajaatasattu</span></p><p>AjātasattuVedehiputta</p><p>Son of Bimbisāra, King of Māgadha, and therefore half-brother to Abhayarājakumāra. He murdered his father to gain the throne, and conspired with Devadatta to kill the Buddha, but was later converted. He had a son Udāyibhadda. DN.i.50</p><p>Ajātasattu was greatly impressed by Devadatta’s powers of <em>iddhi</em> and became his devoted follower. Vin.ii.185 He built for him a monastery at Gayāsīsa and waited upon him morning and evening carrying food for him, sometimes as much as five hundred cartloads in five hundred cooking pans. SN.ii.242</p><p>Devadatta incited him to seize the throne, killing his father if necessary. When Bimbisāra learnt of the prince’s intentions he abdicated in his favour. But Devadatta was not satisfied till Bimbisāra, who was one of the Buddha’s foremost supporters, was killed.</p><p>Ajātasattu helped Devadatta in several of the latter’s attempts to kill the Buddha.</p><p>Later he was filled with remorse for these past misdeeds as he confesses himself; DN.i.85 but evidently, for very shame, he refrained from visiting the Buddha till he was won over by the persuasions of his physician Jīvaka Komārabhacca. And when in the end he did go to the Buddha, it was in great fear and trembling; so nervous was he that he imagined conspirators in the very silence surrounding the Buddha where he dwelt in the monastery, in Jīvaka’s Mango grove at Rājagaha. DN.i.49–50</p><p>It was on the occasion of this visit that the Sāmaññaphala Sutta was preached. The king admits that he had been to various teachers before, but had failed to find satisfaction in their teachings. It is noteworthy that the Buddha greets the king cordially on his arrival and makes no mention whatever of the king’s impiety. Instead, when Ajātasattu expresses his repentance at the end of the discourse, the Buddha accepts his confession and lets him off almost too lightly. But after the king had departed the Buddha tells the monks how the king’s misdeeds had wrought his undoing both in this world and the next, for if he had not been guilty of them, the Eye of Truth would have been opened for him on the occasion of this sermon. DN.i.85–86</p><p>Henceforth the king became a loyal adherent of the Buddha’s faith, though, as far as we know, he never waited again either upon the Buddha or upon any member of the Order for the discussion of ethical matters. He was so full of love and respect for the Buddha that when he heard of Upaka Maṇḍikāputta having spoken rather impolitely to the Buddha, he at once flew into a rage. AN.ii.182</p><p>Bimbisāra had married a sister of Pasenadi, and when he was killed she died of grief.</p><p>The revenue of a Kāsī village had been given to her by her father, Mahākosala, as part of her dowry, but after Bimbisāra’s murder, Pasenadi refused to continue it. Thereupon Ajātasattu declared war on his uncle. Before this, uncle and nephew seem to have been on very friendly terms. Once Ajātasattu sent Pasenadi a wonderful piece of foreign fabric, sixteen cubits long and eight broad, mounted on a pole to serve as a canopy. This Pasenadi gave to Ānanda. MN.ii.116</p><p>At first he was victorious in three battles, but, later, he was defeated by Pasenadi, who followed the military advice of an old monk, the Elder Dhanuggahatissa; Ajātasattu was taken captive with his army. On giving an undertaking not to resort to violence again, he was released, and to seal the friendship, Pasenadi gave him his daughter Vajirā as wife, and the revenue of the disputed village was gifted to her as bath-money. SN.i.82–85</p><p>Later, when through the treachery of Pasenadi’s minister, Dīgha Kārāyana, his son Viḍūḍabha usurped the throne, Pasenadi, finding himself deserted, went towards Rājagaha to seek Ajātasattu’s help, but on the way he died of exposure and Ajātasattu gave him burial.</p><p>About a year before the Buddha’s death, Ajātasattu sent his chief minister and confidant, the brahmin Vassakāra, to the Buddha to intimate to him his desire to make war on the Vajjians and to find out what prediction the Buddha would make regarding his chances of victory. The Buddha informed the brahmin that the Vajjians practised the seven conditions of welfare which they had learnt from him, and that they were therefore invincible. DN.ii.72<em>f.</em> The Saṃyutta Nikāya mentions the Buddha as saying that the time would come when the Vajjians would relinquish their strenuous mode of living and that then would come Ajātasattu’s chance. SN.ii.268</p><p>Rumours are mentioned of King Candappajjota making preparations for a war on Ajātasattu to avenge the death of his friend Bimbisāra, but no mention is made of actual fighting. MN.iii.7</p><p>It was he who built the fortress of Pāṭāliputta, which later became the capital of Māgadha.</p>

ajīraka|ajiiraka|ajiraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ajiiraka</span></p><p>indigestion Ja.i.404; Ja.ii.181, Ja.ii.291; Ja.iii.213, Ja.iii.225.</p><p>a + jīraka</p>

akaddama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from mud; pure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaddama">kaddama</a></p>

akakkasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not harsh; not rough; smooth</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kakkasa">kakkasa</a></p>

akalaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>absence of quarreling; harmony</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kalaha">kalaha</a></p>

akalla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>unhealthy; unfit; low in spirits; not sound</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akalya">akalya</a>, <a href="bword://kalla">kalla</a></p>

akallarūpa|akallaruupa|akallarupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akallaruupa</span></p><p>in very poor spirits; unwell</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kallarūpa">kallarūpa</a></p>

akalu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(cp. <em><a href="bword://agalu">agalu</a></em>) an ointment Ja.iv.440 (<em>akaluñ candanañ</em> ca, variant reading BB <em>aggaluṃ</em>; C. explains as <em>kālākaluñ ca rattacandanañ ca</em>, thus implying a blacking or dark ointment); Ja.vi.144 (<em>-candana-vilitta</em>; variant reading BB <em>aggalu˚</em>); Mil.338 (<em>-tagara-tālīsaka-lohita-candana</em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agalu">agalu</a></p>

akalya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>unhealthy; unfit; low in spirits; not sound</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akalla">akalla</a>, <a href="bword://kalya">kalya</a></p>

akalyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>ill; not fit.</p>

akamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>lack of action; not working; non-activity; not a proper or valid act of the <em>saṅgha</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

akammaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i></p><p>not ready or fit for any act or use</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kammaniya">kammaniya</a></p>

akammañña|akamma~n~na|akammanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akamma~n~na</span></p><p>not ready or fit for any act or use</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kammañña">kammañña</a></p>

akammāsa|akammaasa|akammasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akammaasa</span></p><p>spotless; without blemish</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kammāsa">kammāsa</a></p>

akampi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akampin">akampin</a></p><p>(from kampati), not shaking, not trembling.</p>

akampin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akampin">akampin</a></p><p>(from kampati), not shaking, not trembling.</p>

akampita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>not trembling; not shaken</p><p>See also: kampita</p>

akampittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kampati">kampati</a></p>

akampiyatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the condition of not being shaken, stableness Mil.354.</p><p>abstr. fr. akampiya, grd. of a + kampati</p>

akanittha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Akanittha:</strong> the ‘Great or Non-junior Ones’, i.e. ‘Highest Gods’, are the inhabitants of the 5th and highest heaven of the Pure Abodes <em>suddhāvāsa</em>, cf. <em>avacara</em> deva II <em>Anāgāmī</em></p>

akaniṭṭha|akani.t.tha|akanittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">\~ā m. plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akani.t.tha</span></p><p>the name of one of the highest classes of devas, the fifth class of <em>suddhāvāsas</em>.</p>

akaniṭṭhagāmi|akani.t.thagaami|akanitthagami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akani.t.thagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akaniṭṭhagāmin">akaniṭṭhagāmin</a></p><p>going to the <em>akaniṭṭha</em> world.</p>

akaniṭṭhagāmin|akani.t.thagaamin|akanitthagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akani.t.thagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akaniṭṭhagāmin">akaniṭṭhagāmin</a></p><p>going to the <em>akaniṭṭha</em> world.</p>

akaniṭṭhā|akani.t.thaa|akanittha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akani.t.thaa</span></p><p>A class of devas, living in the highest of the five Suddhāvasā (Pure Abodes). DN.iii.237</p><p>In the Mahāpadāna Sutta DN.ii.52<em>f.</em> the Buddha mentions that he visited their abode and conversed with beings who were born there as a result of the holy lives they had lived under various Buddhas.</p><p>In the Sakkapañha Sutta. DN.ii.286 Sakka speaks of them as the highest devas, and expresses his satisfaction that he, too, will be born among them in his last life.</p>

akanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not desired or wished; unpleasing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kanta">kanta</a></p>

akantehi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>unpleasantly; in an undesired way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kantehi">kantehi</a></p>

akantena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>unpleasantly; in an undesired way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kantena">kantena</a></p>

akantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>unpleasant</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kantika">kantika</a></p>

akantiya
