akicchalābhi|akicchalaabhi|akicchalabhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akicchalaabhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akicchalābhin">akicchalābhin</a></p><p>obtaining easily</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiccha">kiccha</a></p>

akicchalābhin|akicchalaabhin|akicchalabhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akicchalaabhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akicchalābhin">akicchalābhin</a></p><p>obtaining easily</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiccha">kiccha</a></p>

akicchena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>with ease, without trouble</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kicchena">kicchena</a></p>

akilanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kilanta">kilanta</a></p>

akilāsu|akilaasu|akilasu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akilaasu</span></p><p>untiring, unwearied; making every effort</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kilāsu">kilāsu</a></p>

akilāsu|akilaasu|akilasu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akilaasu</span></p><p>adjective not lazy, diligent, active, untiring SN.i.47; SN.v.162; Ja.i.109; Mil.382.</p><p>a + kilāsu</p>

akiriya-ditthi
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Akiriya-ditthi:</strong> The false view or opinion of the inefficacy of action: That neither moral good nor moral evil action have any delayed consequences for anyone. This wrong view w as taught by Pūrana-Kassapa; see: <em>ditthi</em></p>

akiriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not practical, unwise, foolish Ja.iii.530 (<em>-rūpa = akattabba-rūpa</em> C.); Mil.250.</p><p>a + kiriya</p>

akiriyavāda|akiriyavaada|akiriyavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akiriyavaada</span></p><p>professing the doctrine that activity is not morally significant or effective; advocating abstention</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiriyavāda">kiriyavāda</a></p>

akiriyavādi|akiriyavaadi|akiriyavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akiriyavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akiriyavādin">akiriyavādin</a></p><p>professing the doctrine that activity is not morally significant or effective; advocating abstention</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiriyavādin">kiriyavādin</a></p>

akiriyavādin|akiriyavaadin|akiriyavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akiriyavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akiriyavādin">akiriyavādin</a></p><p>professing the doctrine that activity is not morally significant or effective; advocating abstention</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiriyavādin">kiriyavādin</a></p>

akiriyā|akiriyaa|akiriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akiriyaa</span></p><ol><li>(f.) non-activity; non-performance; abstention (from); failure to act.</li><li>(f.) the absence of independent activity; the absence of morally significant or morally effective activity</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiriyā">kiriyā</a></p>

akissava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. (?)</i></p><p>(according to commentaries) without wisdom.</p>

akissava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at SN.i.149 is probably faulty reading for <em><a href="bword://aki%C3%B1cana">akiñcana</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akiñcana">akiñcana</a></p>

akittayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kittayati">kittayati</a></p>

akitti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>ill fame; reproach</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kitti">kitti</a></p>

akittisañjanana|akittisa~njanana|akittisanjanana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akittisa~njanana</span></p><p>(-ī)n. producing ill fame</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akittisañjananī">akittisañjananī</a>, <a href="bword://kitti">kitti</a></p>

akittisañjananī|akittisa~njananii|akittisanjanani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akittisa~njananii</span></p><p>producing ill fame</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akittisañjanana">akittisañjanana</a>, <a href="bword://kitti">kitti</a></p>

akiñcana|aki~ncana|akincana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aki~ncana</span></p><p>who or which has nothing; without possessions, poor; without responsibilities; (according to commentaries) without impediments, without defilements</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiñcana">kiñcana</a></p>

akka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Swallow Wort, Hindi <em>akaron</em>, <em>Calotropis gigantea</em> Vin.i.306, a small stout shrub with a beautiful reddish or sometimes light purple flower. The juice of the leaves have medical properties, floss from the seeds is used as stuffing and in ancient times fiber from the stem was used to make bow strings M.i.429.</p>

akka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the name of a plant, Calotropis gigantea; swallow wort plant?</p>

akka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Name of a plant: Calotropis Gigantea, swallow-wort MN.i.429 (<em>-assa jiyā</em> bowstrings made from that plant).</p><ul><li><em>-nāla</em> a kind of dress material Vin.i.306 (vv.ll. agga &amp; akkha˚);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81%E1%B9%ADa">vāṭa</a></em> a kind of gate to a plantation, a movable fence made of the akka plant Vin.ii.154 (cp. akkha-vāṭa).</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. arka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vāṭa">vāṭa</a></p>

akkamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going near, approaching, stepping upon, walking to Ja.i.62.</p><p>cp. BSk. ākramaṇa Jtm.31#58</p>

akkamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>steps on or treads upon; presses upon.</p>

akkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tread upon, to approach, attack Ja.i.7, Ja.i.279; Thag-a.9<br />■ to rise Vin.iii.38<br />ger <strong>akkamma</strong> Cp.iii.7#2<br />pp <em><a href="bword://akkanta">akkanta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + kamati, <em>kram</em></p><p>g417</p><p>See also: akkamma, <a href="bword://akkanta">akkanta</a></p>

akkamitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>will tread on</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkamati">akkamati</a></p>

akkamitvā|akkamitvaa|akkamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkamitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkamati">akkamati</a></p>

akkamiṃsu|akkami.msu|akkamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkamati">akkamati</a></p>

akkandati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lament, wail, cry SN.iv.206.</p><p>ā + kandati, <em>krand</em></p>

akkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(pass.) stepped on; trodden upon; pressed upon.</li><li>(act.) who has stepped on, trodden upon; who has stepped on to a shoe or sandal</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkamati">akkamati</a></p>

akkanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>stepped upon, mounted on AN.i.8; Ja.i.71; Mil.152; Dhp-a.i.200.</p><p>pp. of akkamati</p>

akkanāla|akkanaala|akkanala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkanaala</span></p><p>the stalk of the akka plant.</p>

akkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter</p><ol><li>an axle, an axle-tree.</li><li>the collarbone.</li></ol><p>akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a die; dice.</p>

akkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>the axle of a wheel DN.ii.96; SN.v.6; AN.i.112; Ja.i.109, Ja.i.192; Ja.v.155 (<em>akkhassa phalakaṃ yathā</em>; C.: <em>suvaṇṇaphalakaṃ viya</em>, i.e. shiny, like the polished surface of an axle); Mil.27 (+ <em>īsā + cakka)</em>, Mil.277 <em>(atibhārena sakaṭassa akkho bhijjati</em>: the axle of the cart breaks when the load is too heavy); Pv-a.277 <em>-akkhaṃ abbhañjati</em> to lubricate the axle SN.iv.177; Mil.367.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://chinna">chinna</a></em> one whose axle is broken; with broken axle SN.i.57; Mil.67.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhagga">bhagga</a></em> with a broken axle Ja.v.433</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bha%C3%B1jana">bhañjana</a></em> the breaking of the axle Dhp-a.i.375; Pv-a.277.</li></ul><p>Vedic akṣa; Av. aša; Gr. α ̓́ςων α ̔́μαςα ohariot with <em>one</em> axle); Lat. axis; Ohg. etc. ahsa, E. axle, to root of Lat. ago, Sk. <em>aj</em></p><p>Akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a die DN.i.6 (but explained at DN-a.i.86 as ball-game: <em>guḷakīḷa</em>); SN.i.149 = AN.v.171 = Snp.659 (<em>appamatto ayaṃ kali yo akkhesu dhanaparājayo</em>); Ja.i.379 (<em>kūṭ˚</em>; a false player, sharper, cheat) <em>anakkha</em> one who is not a gambler Ja.v.116 (C.: <em>ajūtakara</em>). Cp. also <em>accha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassa">dassa</a></em> (cp. Sk. akṣadarśaka) one who looks at (i.e. examines) the dice, an umpire, a judge Vin.iii.47; Mil.114, Mil.327, Mil.343 (dhamma-nagare).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhutta">dhutta</a></em> one who has the vice of gambling DN.ii.348; DN.iii.183; MN.iii.170; Snp.106 (+ itthidhutta &amp; surādhutta).;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81%E1%B9%ADa">vāṭa</a></em> fence round an arena for wrestling Ja.iv.81. (? read akka-).</li></ul><p>Vedic akṣa, prob. to akṣi &amp; Lat. oculus, “that which has eyes” i.e. a die; cp. also Lat. ālea game at dice (fr.* asclea?)</p><p>Akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective (-˚) having eyes, with eyes Pv-a.39 (BB. <strong>rattakkha</strong> with eyes red from weeping, gloss on <em><a href="bword://assumukha">assumukha</a></em>). Prob. <em><a href="bword://akkha%E1%B9%87a">akkhaṇa</a></em> is connected with <em><a href="bword://akkha">akkha</a></em>.</p><p>to akkhi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chinna">chinna</a>, <a href="bword://bhagga">bhagga</a>, <a href="bword://bhañjana">bhañjana</a>, <a href="bword://dassa">dassa</a>, <a href="bword://dhutta">dhutta</a>, <a href="bword://vāṭa">vāṭa</a>, rattakkha, <a href="bword://assumukha">assumukha</a>, <a href="bword://akkhaṇa">akkhaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://akkha">akkha</a></p>

akkhadassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>judge; a magistrate.</p>

akkhadhutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>gamble; one addicted to dicing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkha">akkha</a></p>

akkhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the collar-bone Vin.iv.213 (<em>adhakkhakaṃ</em>); Vin.v.216.</p><p>akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + ka</p>

akkhama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>impatient; not able to put up with; not amenable</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khama">khama</a></p>

akkhambiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp neg. mfn.</i></p><p>not to be made stiff; not be obstructed or hindered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khambheti">khambheti</a></p>

akkhantī|akkhantii|akkhanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhantii</span></p><p>impatience, intolerance; irascibility; unwillingness to forgive</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khantī">khantī</a></p>

akkhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine f. &amp; n.; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(m. f. &amp; n.) a sound; a syllable.</li><li>(n.) an expression, a word.</li></ol>

akkhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective constant, durable, lasting DN.iii.86. As tt. for one of 4 branches of Vedic learning (DN.i.88) it is Phonetics which probably included Grammar, and is explained by <em><a href="bword://sikkh%C4%81">sikkhā</a></em> (DN-a.i.247 = Snp-a.477) pl. nt. <em>akkharāni</em> sounds, tones, words. <em>citt’akkhara</em> of a discourse (<strong>suttanta</strong>) having variety &amp; beauty of words or sounds (opposed to beauty of thought) AN.i.72 = AN.iii.107 = SN.ii.267. <em>Akkharāni</em> are the sauce, flavour (<em><a href="bword://vya%C3%B1jana">vyañjana</a></em> of poetry SN.i.38. To know the context of the a˚ the words of the texts, is characteristic of an Arahant Dhp.352 (C. is ambiguous Dhp-a.iv.70). Later: <em>akkharaṃ</em> a syllable or sound Pv-a.280 (called <em><a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> in next line) <em>akkharāni</em> an inscription Ja.ii.90; Ja.iv.7 (<em>likhitāni</em> written), Ja.iv.489; Ja.vi.390, Ja.vi.407. In Grammar: a letter Kacc. 1.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://cintaka">cintaka</a></em> a grammarian or versifier Kp-a.17; Snp-a.16 Snp-a.23, Snp-a.321. cp. Snp-a.466; Pv-a.120.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pabheda">pabheda</a></em> in phrase sakkharappabheda phonology &amp; etymology DN.i.88 (akkharappabhedo ti sikkhā ca nirutti ca Snp-a.447 = DN-a.i.247 = AN.iii.223 = Snp.p.105.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pi%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">piṇḍa</a></em> “word-ball”, i.e. sequence of words or sounds Dhp-a.iv.70 (= akkharānaṃ sannipāto Dhp.352).</li></ul><p>Vedic akṣara</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sikkhā">sikkhā</a>, suttanta, <a href="bword://vyañjana">vyañjana</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://cintaka">cintaka</a>, <a href="bword://pabheda">pabheda</a>, <a href="bword://piṇḍa">piṇḍa</a></p>

akkharappabheda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(&amp; n.?) phonology and etymology</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhara">akkhara</a></p>

akkharikā|akkharikaa|akkharika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkharikaa</span></p><p>(from akkhara) a letter-game.</p>

akkharikā|akkharikaa|akkharika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkharikaa</span></p><p>game (recognising syllables written in the air or on one’s back). DN.i.7; Vin.ii.10; Vin.iii.180. So explained at DN-a.i.86. It may be translated “letter game”; but all Indian letters of that date were syllables.</p>

akkhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>unhurt, uninjured; unimpaired, whole, perfect</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khata">khata</a></p>

akkhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unhurt, without fault Mhvs.19, Mhvs.56 (C. <em><a href="bword://niddosa">niddosa</a></em>)<br />■ acc. <em>akkhataṃ</em> (adv.) in safety, unhurt. Only in one phrase Vv.84#52 (<em>paccāgamuṃ Pāṭaliputtaṃ akkhataṃ</em>) &amp; Pv.iv.11#1 (<em>nessāmi taṃ Pāṭaliputtaṃ akkhataṃ</em>); see Vv-a.351; Pv-a.272.</p><p>pp. of a + <em>kṣan</em>, cp. parikkhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niddosa">niddosa</a></p>

akkhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not decaying, in <em>akkhayapaṭibhāna</em>, of unfailing skill in exposition Mil.3, Mil.21.</p><p>a + khaya, <em>kṣi</em></p>

akkhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khayita">khayita</a></p>

akkhaṇa|akkha.na|akkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.na</span></p><p>the wrong time, the wrong moment; esp. an inopportune moment to be born as a man, birth in unfavorable circumstances (when one cannot take advantage of a Buddha being in the world)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khaṇa">khaṇa</a></p>

akkhaṇa|akkha.na|akkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.na</span></p><p>wrong time, bad luck, misadventure, misfortune. There are 9 enumerated at DN.iii.263; the usual set consists of 8; thus DN.iii.287; Vv-a.193; Sdhp.4 sq. See also <em><a href="bword://kha%E1%B9%87a">khaṇa</a>-<a href="bword://vedhin">vedhin</a></em> (adj. n.) a skilled archer, one who shoots on the moment, i.e. without losing time, explained as one who shoots without missing (the target) or as quickly as lightning <em>(akkhaṇa = vijju)</em>. In var. combinations; mostly as <em>durepātin</em> a. AN.i.284 (+ <em>mahato kāyassa padāletā</em>), AN.ii.170 sq. (id.), AN.i.202; AN.iv.423, AN.iv.425; Ja.ii.91 (explained as either <em>avirādhita-vedhī</em> or <em>akkhaṇaṃ vuccati vijju</em>: one who takes and shoots his arrows as fast as lightning), Ja.iii.322; Ja.iv.494 (C. explains <em>aviraddha-vedhin vijju-ālokena vijjhana-samattha</em> p. 497). In other combination at Ja.i.58 <em>(akkhaṇavedhin + vālavedhin)</em>; Ja.v.129 (the 4 kinds of archers: <em>a., vālavedhin</em>, <em>saddavedhin</em> &amp; <em>saravedhin)</em>.</p><p>In BSk. we find akṣuṇṇavedha (a Sanskritised Pāli form cp. Mathurā kṣuṇa = Sk. kṣaṇa) at Divy.58, Divy.100, Divy.442 (always with dūrevedha), where MSS. however read akṣuṇa˚; also at Lal.178. See Divy Index, where translation is given as “an act of throwing the spear so as to graze the mark” (Schiefner gives “Streifschuss”).</p><p><em>Note.</em> The explanations are not satisfactory. We should expect either an etym. bearing on the meaning “hitting the centre of the target” (i.e. its “eye”) (cp. E. bull’s eye), in which case a direct relation to akkha = akkhi eye would not seem improbable (cp. formation ikkhana or an etym. like “hitting without mishap”, in which case the expression would be derived directly from ak khaṇa (see prec.) with the omission of the neg. an-akkhaṇa in the meaning of “lightning” (Ja-a.ii.91) is not supported by literary evidence.</p><p>a + khaṇa, BSk. akṣaṇa Avs.i.291 = Avs.i.332</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khaṇa">khaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://vedhin">vedhin</a></p>

akkhaṇavedhi|akkha.navedhi|akkhanavedhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.navedhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhaṇavedhin">akkhaṇavedhin</a></p><p>(probably) piercing the target.</p>

akkhaṇavedhin|akkha.navedhin|akkhanavedhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.navedhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhaṇavedhin">akkhaṇavedhin</a></p><p>(probably) piercing the target.</p>

akkhaṇdakāri|akkha.ndakaari|akkhandakari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.ndakaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhaṇdakārin">akkhaṇdakārin</a></p><p>making whole; complete</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khaṇḍakārin">khaṇḍakārin</a></p>

akkhaṇdakārin|akkha.ndakaarin|akkhandakarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.ndakaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhaṇdakārin">akkhaṇdakārin</a></p><p>making whole; complete</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khaṇḍakārin">khaṇḍakārin</a></p>

akkhaṇḍa|akkha.n.da|akkhanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkha.n.da</span></p><p>with no gaps, whole, entire, complete</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akhaṇḍa">akhaṇḍa</a>, <a href="bword://khaṇḍa">khaṇḍa</a></p>

akkheyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>to be named; to be told, to be expressed; what can be expressed or described, a nameable object</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the eye.</p>

akkhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the eye MN.i.383 (<em>ubbhatehi akkhīhi</em>); Snp.197, Snp.608; Ja.i.223, Ja.i.279; Ja.v.77; Ja.vi.336; Pv.ii.9#26 (<em>akkhīni paggharanti</em>: shed tears, cp. Pv-a.123); Vv-a.65 (<em>-īni bhamanti</em>, my eyes swim) cp. akkhīni me dhūmāyanti Dhp-a.i.475; Dhp-a.ii.26; Dhp-a.iii.196 (<em>-īni ummīletvā</em> opening the eyes); Sdhp.103, Sdhp.380<br />■ In combination with <em>sa-</em> as <em>sacchi &amp; sakkhi;</em> (q.v.). As adj. (-˚) <em>akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%C3%B1jana">añjana</a></em> eye ointment, collyrium Dhp-a.iii.354.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%ABpa">kūpa</a></em> the socket of the eye Ja.iv.407.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">gaṇḍa</a></em> eye-protuberance, i.e. eye-brow (?) Ja.vi.504 (for pamukha T.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C5%ABtha">gūtha</a></em> secretion from the eye Pv-a.198.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C5%ABthaka">gūthaka</a></em> id. Snp.197 (= dvīhi akkhicchiddehi apanīta-ttaca-maṃsasadiso a˚-gūthako Snp-a.248).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://chidda">chidda</a></em> the eye-hole Snp-a.248.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dala">dala</a></em> the eye-lid DN-a.i.194; Thag-a.259; Dhs-a.378.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81ta">pāta</a></em> “fall of the eye” i.e. a look, in mand˚ of soft looks (adj.) Pv-a.57.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C5%ABra">pūra</a></em> an eye-full, in akkhipūraṃ assuṃ (assu?) an eye full of tears Ja.vi.191.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mala">mala</a></em> dirt from the eye Pv.iii.5#3 (˚gūtha C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://roga">roga</a></em> eye disease Dhp-a.i.9.</li></ul><p>to <em>*oks</em>, an enlarged form of <em>*oq<!-- raw HTML omitted -->u<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, cp. Sk. īkṣate, kṣaṇa, pratīka, anīka; Gr. ο ̓́σσε, ω ̓́ψ (*Κύκλωψ), ὀφχαλμός, πρόσωπον; Lat. oculus, Ags. ēowan (= E eye wind-ow); Goth. augō. See also cakkhu &amp; cp. akkha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> ikkhaṇika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjana">añjana</a>, <a href="bword://kūpa">kūpa</a>, <a href="bword://gaṇḍa">gaṇḍa</a>, <a href="bword://gūtha">gūtha</a>, <a href="bword://gūthaka">gūthaka</a>, <a href="bword://chidda">chidda</a>, <a href="bword://dala">dala</a>, <a href="bword://pāta">pāta</a>, <a href="bword://pūra">pūra</a>, <a href="bword://mala">mala</a>, <a href="bword://roga">roga</a></p>

akkhigūthaka|akkhiguuthaka|akkhiguthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhiguuthaka</span></p><p>an excretion from the eye</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a></p>

akkhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Akkhika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective (-˚) having eyes, with eyes Thag.960 (<em>añjan˚</em>; with eyes anointed); Dhp-a.iv.98 (<em>aḍḍh˚</em>; with half an eye, i.e. stealthily); Sdhp.286 (<em>tamb˚</em>; red-eyed) <em>-an˚</em>; having no eyes Dhp-a.i.11.</p><p>Akkhika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter the mesh of a net Ja.i.208. <em>-<a href="bword://h%C4%81raka">hāraka</a></em> one who takes up a mesh (?) MN.i.383 (corresp with <em>aṇḍahāraka</em>).</p><p>cp. Sk. akṣa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://hāraka">hāraka</a></p>

akkhikahāraka|akkhikahaaraka|akkhikaharaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhikahaaraka</span></p><p>akkhikahāraka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine one who removes eyes.</p><p>akkhikahāraka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine one who collects seeds.</p>

akkhikūpa|akkhikuupa|akkhikupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhikuupa</span></p><p>the eye-socket</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a></p>

akkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://akkhika">akkhika</a></em> Ja.iii.190 (<em>mand˚</em>; softeyed); Vv.32#3 (<em>tamb˚</em> red-eyed); Dhp-a.i.11.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhika">akkhika</a></p>

akkhissaṃ|akkhissa.m|akkhissam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhissa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>not reviled; not criticized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khitta">khitta</a></p>

akkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Akkhitta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://khitta">khitta</a></em>.</p><p>Akkhitta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective hit, struck, thrown Ja.iii.255 (= <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ka%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dhita">ākaḍḍhita</a></em> C.).</p><p>BSk ākṣipta Divy.363, pp. of ā + <em>kṣip</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khitta">khitta</a>, <a href="bword://ākaḍḍhita">ākaḍḍhita</a></p>

akkhittacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khittacitta">khittacitta</a></p>

akkhitārakā|akkhitaarakaa|akkhitaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhitaarakaa</span></p><p>pupil of the eye; the eye</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a></p>

akkhobbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be shaken, imperturbable Mil.21.</p><p>a + <em>kṣubh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://khobha">khobha</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khobha">khobha</a></p>

akkhobhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://akkhobbha">akkhobbha</a></em> Ja.v.322 (= <em>khobhetun na sakkhā</em> C.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhobbha">akkhobbha</a></p>

akkhohiṇī|akkhohi.nii|akkhohini
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhohi.nii</span></p><p>one of the highest numerals (1 followed by 42 ciphers, Childers) Ja.v.319; Ja.vi.395.</p><p>= akkhobhiṇī</p>

akkhudda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not small or mean; considerable, important</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khudda">khudda</a></p>

akkhuddāvakāsa|akkhuddaavakaasa|akkhuddavakasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhuddaavakaasa</span></p><p>being a considerable occasion (for …) (?)); of no mean appearance (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhudda">akkhudda</a></p>

akkhāhata|akkhaahata|akkhahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaahata</span></p><p>fixed on an axle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkha">akkha</a></p>

akkhāna|akkhaana|akkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaana</span></p><p>telling, announcing, teaching; a tale, a recitation, a legend.</p>

akkhāna|akkhaana|akkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaana</span></p><p>telling stories, recitation; tale, legend DN.i.6 (= DN-a.i.84: <em>Bhārata-Rāmāyanādi</em>); DN.iii.183; MN.i.503; MN.iii.167; Sdhp.237<br />■ preaching, teaching Mnd.91 (<em>dhamm˚</em>;). The 5<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Veda Ja.v.450. (<em>vedam akkhānapañcamaṃ</em>; C: <em>itihāsapañcamaṃ vedacatukkaṃ</em>)<br />■ The spelling <em>ākhyāna</em> also occurs (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. ākhyāna</p>

akkhāsi|akkhaasi|akkhasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhāta|akkhaata|akkhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaata</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhātar">akkhātar</a></p><p>one who tells; one who names; a preacher, a teacher.</p>

akkhāta|akkhaata|akkhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaata</span></p><p>adjective announced, proclaimed, told, shown AN.i.34 (<em>dur˚</em>); AN.ii.195; AN.iv.285, AN.iv.322; AN.v.265 AN.v.283; Snp.172, Snp.276, Snp.595, Snp.718.</p><p>pp. of akkhāti</p>

akkhātar|akkhaatar|akkhatar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaatar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhātar">akkhātar</a></p><p>one who tells; one who names; a preacher, a teacher.</p>

akkhātar|akkhaatar|akkhatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaatar</span></p><p>one who relates, a speaker, preacher, story-teller SN.i.11, SN.i.191; SN.iii.66; Snp.167.</p>

akkhāti|akkhaati|akkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaati</span></p><p>announces; tells; declares; tells about; teaches.</p>

akkhāti|akkhaati|akkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaati</span></p><p>to declare, announce, tell Snp.87 Snp.172;<br />imper <em>akkhāhi</em> Snp.988, Snp.1085;<br />aor <em><a href="bword://akkh%C4%81si">akkhāsi</a></em> Snp.251, Snp.504, Snp.1131 (= ācikkhi etc. Cnd.465);<br />fut <em>akkhissati</em> Pv.iv.1#63;<br />■ cond. <em>akkhissaṃ</em> Snp.997; Ja.vi.523<br />pass <em>akkhāyati</em> to be proclaimed, in phrase <em>aggaṃ a</em> to be deemed chief or superior, to be first, to excel Mil.118 Mil.182 (see [in BSk. agram ākhyāyate Mvu.iii.390](bword://in BSk. agram ākhyāyate Mvu.iii.390));<br />ger <em>akkheyya</em> to be pronounced SN.i.11; Iti.53<br />pp <em><a href="bword://akkh%C4%81ta">akkhāta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ Intensive or Frequentative is <em><a href="bword://%C4%81cikkhati">ācikkhati</a></em>.</p><p>ā + <em>khyā</em>, Idg. *seq<!-- raw HTML omitted -->u<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; cp. Sk. ākhyāti, Lat. inquam, Gr. ἐννέπω, Goth. saihvan, Ger. sehen etc. See also akkhi &amp; cakkhu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāsi">akkhāsi</a>, in BSk. agram ākhyāyate Mvu.iii.390, <a href="bword://akkhāta">akkhāta</a>, <a href="bword://ācikkhati">ācikkhati</a></p>

akkhātā|akkhaataa|akkhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaataa</span></p><p>told, declared, taught; called, named</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhāya|akkhaaya|akkhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaaya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhāyati|akkhaayati|akkhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaayati</span></p><p>akkhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg.</p><p>akkhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pass. pr. 3 sg. is declared; is called, is stated to be; is named; esp. <em>aggam ~ayati</em>, is called the most excellent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a>, <a href="bword://akkhāti">akkhāti</a></p>

akkhāyika|akkhaayika|akkhayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaayika</span></p><p>adjective relating, narrating Ja.iii.535; <em>lokakkhāyikā kathā</em> talk about nature-lore DN.i.8; Mil.316.</p>

akkhāyin|akkhaayin|akkhayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaayin</span></p><p>adjective telling, relating, announcing SN.ii.35; SN.iii.7; Ja.iii.105.</p>

akkhāyita|akkhaayita|akkhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhaayita</span></p><p>not eaten</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khāyita">khāyita</a></p>

akkhīṇāsava|akkhii.naasava|akkhinasava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkhii.naasava</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkhīṇāsava">akkhīṇāsava</a></p><p>in whom the <em>āsavas</em> have not perished, who is not an <em>arhat</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khīṇāsava">khīṇāsava</a></p>

akkocchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></p>

akkodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(m.) freedom from anger; forbearance.</li><li>(mfn.) free from anger; forbearing</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kodha">kodha</a></p>

akkodhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from anger; not easily angered. forbearing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kodhana">kodhana</a></p>

akkodhupāyāsi|akkodhupaayaasi|akkodhupayasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkodhupaayaasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkodhupāyāsin">akkodhupāyāsin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kodhupāyāsin">kodhupāyāsin</a></p>

akkodhupāyāsin|akkodhupaayaasin|akkodhupayasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkodhupaayaasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akkodhupāyāsin">akkodhupāyāsin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kodhupāyāsin">kodhupāyāsin</a></p>

akkosa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>shouting at; abuse; reviling.</p>

akkosa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>shouting at, abuse, insult, reproach, reviling Snp.623; Mil.8 (+ <em><a href="bword://paribh%C4%81sa">paribhāsa</a></em>); Snp-a.492 Thag-a.256; Pv-a.243; Dhp-a.ii.61.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></em> always as dasa a˚-vatthūni 10 bases of abuse 10 expressions of cursing Ja.i.191; Snp-a.364, Snp-a.467; Dhp-a.i.212; Dhp-a.iv.2.</li></ul><p>ā + <em>kruś = kruñc</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ku%C3%B1ca">kuñca</a></em> &amp; koñca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; to sound, root kr̥, see note on gala</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paribhāsa">paribhāsa</a>, <a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a>, <a href="bword://kuñca">kuñca</a></p>

akkosaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. &amp; masculine (one) who abuses, scolds, reviles.</p>

akkosaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who abuses, scolds or reviles, + <em><a href="bword://paribh%C4%81saka">paribhāsaka</a></em> AN.ii.58; AN.iii.252; AN.iv.156; AN.v.317; Pv-a.251.</p><p>from last</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paribhāsaka">paribhāsaka</a></p>

akkosakaparibhāsika|akkosakaparibhaasika|akkosakaparibhasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkosakaparibhaasika</span></p><p>(one) who abuses and insults.</p>

akkosanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkosati">akkosati</a></p>

akkosati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to scold, swear at, abuse, revile Ja.i.191; Ja.ii.416; Ja.iii.27; Dhp-a.i.211; Dhp-a.ii.44. Often combined with <em><a href="bword://paribh%C4%81sati">paribhāsati</a></em>, e.g. Vin.ii.296; Dhp-a.iv.2; Pv-a.10<br />aor <em><a href="bword://akkocchi">akkocchi</a></em> Dhp.3; Ja.iii.212 (= <strong>akkosi</strong> Dhp-a.i.43. Der. wrongly fr. <em>krudh</em> by Kacc. vi.417 cp. Franke, Einh. Pāli-gramm. 37, and Geiger, P. Gr. § 164)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://akku%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">akkuṭṭha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>to <em>krus</em> see <em><a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paribhāsati">paribhāsati</a>, <a href="bword://akkocchi">akkocchi</a>, akkosi, <a href="bword://akkuṭṭha">akkuṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></p>

akkosavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>basis, ground for abuse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></p>

akkoseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>akkosetiakkosayati</p><p>pr. 3 sg. scolds, abuses, curses, reviles.</p>

akkositabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: akkosita</p>

akkosiyamāna|akkosiyamaana|akkosiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akkosiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></p>

akkudhasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>unprovoked and calm, mild and un-angered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuddha">kuddha</a></p>

akkula
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective confused, perplexed, agitated, frightened Ud.5 <em>(akkulopakkula</em> and <em>akkulapakkulika)</em> See <em><a href="bword://%C4%81kula">ākula</a></em>.</p><p>= ākula</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākula">ākula</a></p>

akkulo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>an exclamation (designed to terrify)</p>

akkuṭṭha|akku.t.tha|akkuttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akku.t.tha</span></p><p>reviled; scolded, railed at</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkosa">akkosa</a></p>

akkuṭṭha|akku.t.tha|akkuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akku.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) being reviled, scolded, railed at Snp.366 (= dasahi akkosavatthūhi abhisatto Snp-a.364); Ja.vi.187.</li><li>(nt.) reviling, scolding, swearing at; in combination <em>akkuṭṭha-vandita</em> Snp.702 (= <em>akkosa-vandana</em> Snp-a.492) Thig.388 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Thag-a.256 as above).</li></ol><p>pp. of akkosati</p>

akkuṭṭhasanta|akku.t.thasanta|akkutthasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akku.t.thasanta</span></p><p>being reviled; or calm when reviled.</p>

akkuṭṭhavandita|akku.t.thavandita|akkutthavandita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akku.t.thavandita</span></p><p>reviling and respect.</p>

akopa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without agitation or anger</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kopa">kopa</a></p>

akopiyamāna|akopiyamaana|akopiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akopiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kopiyamāna">kopiyamāna</a></p>

akotūhalamaṅgalika|akotuuhalama"ngalika|akotuhalamangalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akotuuhalama"ngalika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kotūhalamaṅgalika">kotūhalamaṅgalika</a></p>

akovida
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not experienced; not knowledgeable about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kovida">kovida</a></p>

akriyā|akriyaa|akriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akriyaa</span></p><ol><li>(f.) non-activity; non-performance; abstention (from); failure to act.</li><li>(f.) the absence of independent activity; the absence of morally significant or morally effective activity</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kriyā">kriyā</a></p>

akubba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akubbat">akubbat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kubbati">kubbati</a></p>

akubbamāna|akubbamaana|akubbamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akubbamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kubbati">kubbati</a></p>

akubbat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akubbat">akubbat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kubbati">kubbati</a></p>

akuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from deceit; not fraudulent; genuine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuha">kuha</a></p>

akuhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not deceitful, not fraudulent; genuine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuhaka">kuhaka</a></p>

akujjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>not creaking, not making a noise</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akūjana">akūjana</a></p>

akukkucca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from anxiety; free from feelings of guilt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kukkucca">kukkucca</a></p>

akukkuccaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not causing anxiety (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kukkuccaka">kukkuccaka</a></p>

akukkuccakajāta|akukkuccakajaata|akukkuccakajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akukkuccakajaata</span></p><p>not causing anxiety</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akukkuccaka">akukkuccaka</a></p>

akukkukajāta|akukkukajaata|akukkukajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. (?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akukkukajaata</span></p><p>without shoots?</p>

akukkukata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(what is) not temporary; give an unlimited time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kukkukata">kukkukata</a></p>

akula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>non-family, a family that is destroyed or disgraced</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kula">kula</a></p>

akulaputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>not of good family</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kulaputta">kulaputta</a></p>

akuppa-dhamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Akuppa-dhamma:</strong> The unshakeable state; is that of one who has attained full mastery over the absorptions <em>jhāna</em>. In Pug. 4 it is said: What person is unshakable? If a person gains the meditative attainments of the fine-material and formless sphere <em>rūpāvacara</em> - <em>arūpāvacara;</em> and he gains them at his wish, without any problem or strain with free choice of place, object and duration, and enters them and emerges from them effortlessly, then it is impossible that in such a person the attainments may become shaken through negligence. This person is unshakeable.</p>

akuppa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) not able to be disturbed; stable; immovable; not open to challenge or valid objection.</li><li>(n.) what is not able to be disturbed; (a state of) stability.</li><li>lack of agitation; (the state of) not being provoked or angered, forbearance</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuppa">kuppa</a></p>

akuppa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be shaken, immovable; sure, steadfast safe Vin.i.11 (akuppā me ceto-vimutti) = SN.ii.239; Vin.ii.69; Vin.iv.214; DN.iii.273; MN.i.205, MN.i.298; SN.ii.171; AN.iii.119, AN.iii.198; Mil.361.</p><p>a + kuppa, grd. of <em>kup</em>, cp. BSk. akopya Mvu.iii.200</p>

akuppadhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not not vulnerable to disturbance; whose essential nature is stability or unchangability</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akuppa">akuppa</a></p>

akuppamāna|akuppamaana|akuppamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akuppamaana</span></p><p>is not steadfast; is not unshakable</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuppati">kuppati</a></p>

akuppatā|akuppataa|akuppata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akuppataa</span></p><p>“state of not being shaken”, surety, safety; epithet of Nibbāna Thag.364.</p><p>abstr. fr. last</p>

akuppā-ceto-vimutti|akuppaa-ceto-vimutti|akuppa-ceto-vimutti
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akuppaa-ceto-vimutti</span></p><p><strong>Akuppā-ceto-vimutti:</strong> Unshakeable release of mind; see <em>ceto-vimutti</em></p>

akusala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li><ol><li>(mfn.) not good, not right; evil, harmful, not conducing to well-being; evil, acting wrongly.</li><li>(n. (and m.?)) what is bad or evil; wrong or unsuitable action or behavior; what leads to suffering or is harmful.</li></ol></li><li><p>(mfn.) not good at, incompetent; not skilled, not expert; not knowledgeable about or conversant with</p></li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></p>

akusala
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Akusala:</strong> Disadvantageous, are all those kammic intentions <em>kamma</em> - <em>cetanā</em> see: <em>cetanā</em> and all consciousness and mental properties associated therewith, which are accompanied either by greed <em>lobha</em>, hate <em>dosa</em> or confusion <em>moha</em> or derivatives thereof. All these mental states are causing disadvantageous kamma-results and contain the initiating seeds of unhappy &amp; painful future, destiny and rebirth. Cf. kamma, <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em> 1, Tab. II.</p>

akusala-sādhārana-cetasika|akusala-saadhaarana-cetasika|akusala-sadharana-cetasika
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akusala-saadhaarana-cetasika</span></p><p><strong>Akusala-sādhārana-cetasika:</strong> Universal, general or primary disadvantageous mental properties associated with all disadvantageous intentions: These are four; 1: Lack of moral shame <em>ahirika,</em> 2: Lack of fear of wrongdoing <em>anottappa</em>, 3: Restlessness <em>uddhacca</em>, 4: Confusion <em>moha</em>. For 1 and 2 see: <em>ahirika-anottappa;</em> for 3 see: <em>nīvarana;</em> for 4 see: <em>mūla</em> see also the Appendix. The corresponding opposite term designating advantageous (beautiful) mental property is <em>sobhana-sādhārana-cetasika</em> see: <em>sobhana</em>.</p>

akusala-vitakka
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Akusala-vitakka:</strong> Disadvantageous thoughts as defined under <em>akusala</em> In M. 20, five methods of overcoming them are given: by changing the object, thinking of the evil results, paying no attention to them, analyzing them, suppressing them.</p><blockquote><p>M. 20 is translated in: The Removal of Distracting Thoughts WHEEL 21.<br />See also: How to remove recurring Disadvantageous Distracting Thoughts?</p></blockquote>

akusalabhāgiya|akusalabhaagiya|akusalabhagiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akusalabhaagiya</span></p><p>associated to what is wrong, with evil or harm</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kusalabhāgiya">kusalabhāgiya</a></p>

akusalapakkhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>belonging to the group of bad qualities</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kusalapakkhika">kusalapakkhika</a></p>

akusalā dhammā|akusalaa dhammaa|akusala dhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akusalaa dhammaa</span></p><p>wrong practices or actions; evil conduct; harmful factors</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kusalā dhammā">kusalā dhammā</a></p>

akusīta|akusiita|akusita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akusiita</span></p><p>not slothful; conscientious</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kusīta">kusīta</a></p>

akutobhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having nothing to fear from anywhere, from any quarter. secure; where there is nothing to fear from any quarter.</p>

akutobhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective see *<a href="bword://ku">ku</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ku">ku</a></p>

akāca|akaaca|akaca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaaca</span></p><p>without impurity, without flaw; pure, clear; smooth.</p>

akāca|akaaca|akaca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaaca</span></p><p>adjective pure, flawless, clear DN.ii.244; Snp.476; Ja.v.203.</p><p>a + kāca</p>

akācin|akaacin|akacin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaacin</span></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://ak%C4%81ca">akāca</a></em> Vv.60#1. Kern (Toevoegselen s. v.) proposes reading <em>akkācin</em> (= Sk. arka-arcin shining as the sun), but Vv-a.253 explains by <em><a href="bword://niddosa">niddosa</a></em>, and there is no variant reading to warrant a misreading.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akāca">akāca</a>, <a href="bword://niddosa">niddosa</a></p>

akāla|akaala|akala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaala</span></p><p>not the right time, not a suitable time (sometimes: nighttime)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāla">kāla</a></p>

akālacāri|akaalacaari|akalacari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalacaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akālacārin">akālacārin</a></p><p>traveling or going for alms at the wrong time, an an unsuitable time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālacārin">kālacārin</a></p>

akālacārin|akaalacaarin|akalacarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalacaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akālacārin">akālacārin</a></p><p>traveling or going for alms at the wrong time, an an unsuitable time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālacārin">kālacārin</a></p>

akālacīvara|akaalaciivara|akalacivara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalaciivara</span></p><p>robe material given at a time other than the appointed period, or given during the appointed period to a specific <em>bhikkhu</em> or group of <em>bhikkhus</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālacīvara">kālacīvara</a></p>

akālamegha|akaalamegha|akalamegha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalamegha</span></p><p>an unseasonable raincloud; unseasonable rainy weather</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akāla">akāla</a></p>

akālapuppha|akaalapuppha|akalapuppha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalapuppha</span></p><p>flower appearing out of season; an unseasonable flower</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akāla">akāla</a></p>

akālavādi|akaalavaadi|akalavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akālavādin">akālavādin</a></p><p>speaking at the wrong time, at an unsuitable time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālavādin">kālavādin</a></p>

akālavādin|akaalavaadin|akalavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akālavādin">akālavādin</a></p><p>speaking at the wrong time, at an unsuitable time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālavādin">kālavādin</a></p>

akālaññu|akaala~n~nu|akalannu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaala~n~nu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālaññu">kālaññu</a></p>

akāle|akaale|akale
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">loc.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaale</span></p><p>not at the right time, at an unsuitable time (sometimes: at night); out of season</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāle">kāle</a></p>

akālena|akaalena|akalena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalena</span></p><p>at the wrong time, at an unsuitable time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālena">kālena</a></p>

akālika|akaalika|akalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaalika</span></p><p>not dependent on time; not limited to a particular time; immediate, immediately effective</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kālika">kālika</a></p>

akāma|akaama|akama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaama</span></p><ol><li>not so wishing; unwilling, reluctant.</li><li>without sensual desire</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāma">kāma</a></p>

akāmaka|akaamaka|akamaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaamaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. unwilling; not wishing (to)</p>

akāmakāmi|akaamakaami|akamakami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaamakaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akāmakāmin">akāmakāmin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāmakāmin">kāmakāmin</a></p>

akāmakāmin|akaamakaamin|akamakamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaamakaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://akāmakāmin">akāmakāmin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāmakāmin">kāmakāmin</a></p>

akāmā|akaamaa|akama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaamaa</span></p><p>against one; s will; whether one wishes it or not; without (previous) intention</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akāma">akāma</a></p>

akāpurisasevita|akaapurisasevita|akapurisasevita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">akaapurisasevita</span></p><p>practiced or cultivated by superior men; not practiced or cultivated by worthless men</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāpurisa">kāpurisa</a></p>

akāraka|akaaraka|akaraka
