aneḷa|ane.la|anela
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ane.la</span></p><p>adjective faultless, pure; only in foll. compounds <em>-gala</em> free from the dripping or oozing of impurity (thus expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at DN-a.i.282, viz. elagalana-virahita), but more likely in lit. meaning “having a pure or clear throat or, of <em><a href="bword://v%C4%81c%C4%81">vācā</a></em> speech: “clearly enunciated” (thus Mrs. Rh. D at <em>Kindred Sayings</em> i.241) Vin.i.197 = DN.i.114 = SN.i.189; AN.ii.51, AN.ii.97; AN.iii.114, AN.iii.195. Cp. also Mvu.iii.322<br />■ <em>-mūga</em> same as prec. “having a clear throat” i.e. not dumb, fig. clever, skilled DN.iii.265; Snp.70 (alālāmukha Snp-a.124), cp. Cnd.259.</p><p>an + eḷa = ena, see <em><a href="bword://ne%E1%B8%B7a">neḷa</a></em> &amp; cp. BSk. eḍa (mūka); Vedic anena</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vācā">vācā</a>, <a href="bword://neḷa">neḷa</a></p>

aneḷagala|ane.lagala|anelagala
