<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ani.t.tha"ngata</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81">niṭṭhā</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhā">niṭṭhā</a></p>

aniṭṭhita|ani.t.thita|anitthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ani.t.thita</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">niṭṭhita</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhita">niṭṭhita</a></p>

anna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>food (esp. boiled rice)</p>

anna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>“eating”, food, esp. boiled rice, but includes all that is eaten as food, viz. odana, kummāsa, sattu, maccha, maṃsa (rice gruel, flour, fish, meat) Mnd.372 = Mnd.495. Anna is spelt <em><a href="bword://a%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">aṇṇa</a></em> in combinations <em>apar’ aṇṇa</em> and <em>pubb’ aṇṇa</em>. Under dhañña (Cnd.314) are distinguished 2 kinds, viz. raw, natural cereals (pubb’ aṇṇaṃ: sāli, vīhi, yava, godhūma, kangu varaka, kudrūsaka) and boiled, prepared food (apar’ aṇṇaṃ sūpeyya curry). Snp-a.378 (on Snp.403) explains anna by yāgubhattâdi<br />■ DN.i.7; AN.i.107, AN.i.132; AN.ii.70, AN.ii.85, AN.ii.203; Snp.82, Snp.240, Snp.403, Snp.924; Ja.iii.190; Pp.51; Sdhp.106, Sdhp.214.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81pa">āpa</a></em> food &amp; water Sdhp.100;</li><li><em>-da</em> giving food Snp.297;</li><li><em>-pāna</em> food &amp; water, eating &amp; drinking, to eat &amp; to drink Snp.485 Snp.487 Pv.i.5#2 Pv.i.8#2; Kp-a.207, Kp-a.209 Pv-a.7 Pv-a.8 Pv-a.30 Pv-a.31 Pv-a.43</li></ul><p>Vedic anna, orig. pp. of adati to eat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṇṇa">aṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

annabhāra|annabhaara|annabhara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">annabhaara</span></p><p>A well-known <em>paribbājaka</em> who lived in the Paribbājakārāma on the banks of the River Sappinī near Rājagaha. He is mentioned as staying with the well-known <em>paribbājakas</em>, Varadhara and Sakuludāyi. The Buddha visits them and talks about the four factors of Dhamma (<em>dhammapadāni</em>) which are held in esteem by everyone: not-coveting, not-malice, right-mindfulness, right-concentration. AN.ii.29–31</p><p>On another occasion they discuss the “brahmin truth.” The Buddha visits them and tells them what he considers to be the brahmin truths (<em>brāhmaṇasaccāni</em>): that no creatures are to be harmed; all sense-delights are impermanent, painful and changing; all becomings are impermanent, etc.; a brahmin is one who has no part in or attachment to anything any more. AN.ii.176–177</p>

annabhāra|annabhaara|annabhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">annabhaara</span></p><p>food carrier; a grass-seller(?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anna">anna</a></p>

annabhāranesādā|annabhaaranesaadaa|annabharanesada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">annabhaaranesaadaa</span></p><p>grass sellers and fowlers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://annabhāra">annabhāra</a></p>

annada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>giving food</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anna">anna</a></p>

annaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in dur˚ see <em><a href="bword://anvaya">anvaya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anvaya">anvaya</a></p>

ano
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ano-</p><p>is a frequent form of comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>an-ava</em>, see <em>ava</em>.</p>

anodaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anudaka">anudaka</a>, <a href="bword://anūdaka">anūdaka</a>, <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

anodaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without water, dry Ja.i.307; Dhp-a.i.52; Sdhp.443.</p><p>an + udaka</p>

anodarikatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>freedom from gluttony; moderation in eating</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odarikatta">odarikatta</a></p>

anodhijina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who has not overcome the limits, who has not gone beyond what is limited or limits; or not knowing as far as the furthest extent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhi">odhi</a></p>

anodhikatvā|anodhikatvaa|anodhikatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anodhikatvaa</span></p><p>not limiting, without limitation; without discrimination, without distinction</p><p>See also: odhita</p>

anodhiṃ karitvā|anodhi.m karitvaa|anodhim karitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anodhi.m karitvaa</span></p><p>not limiting, without limitation; without discrimination, without distinction</p><p>See also: odhita</p>

anodissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>without reference (to a specific person); in general; universally</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odissa">odissa</a></p>

anodissaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unrestricted, without exception, general, universal; only in cpd. <em>-vasena</em> universally thoroughly (with ref. to mettā) Ja.i.81; Ja.ii.146; Vv-a.97 (in general; opp. <em>odissaka-vasena</em>). See also Mrs. Rh. D. <em>Psalms of the Brethren</em> p. 5 n. 1.</p><p>an + odissa + ka</p>

anoggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oggata">oggata</a></p>

anogha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in anogha-tiṇṇa see <em><a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></em>. Anojaka = anoja</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></p>

anohitasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohitasota">ohitasota</a></p>

anojagghati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>laughs at; mocks</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anujagghati">anujagghati</a></p>

anojagghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at DN.i.91 is variant reading for <em><a href="bword://anujagghati">anujagghati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anujagghati">anujagghati</a></p>

anojaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Anojaka = anojā</p><p>Vv.35#4 (= Vv-a.161, where classed with yodhikā bandhujīvakā).</p>

anojava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anojavat">anojavat</a></p><p>lacking strength or vitality; lacking nutriment; not juicy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojavat">ojavat</a></p>

anojavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anojavat">anojavat</a></p><p>lacking strength or vitality; lacking nutriment; not juicy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojavat">ojavat</a></p>

anojā|anojaa|anoja
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anojaa</span></p><p>A type of plant with red flowers Ja.i.9 Ja.vi.536.</p>

anojā FIXME double|anojaa FIXME double|anoja FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anojaa FIXME double</span></p><p>Anojā</p><p>feminine a kind of shrub or tree with red flowers Ja.vi.536 (korandaka +); usually in cpd. <em>anojapuppha</em> the a. flower, used for wreaths etc. Ja.i.9 (˚dāma a garland of a flowers); Ja.vi.227 (id.); Dhp-a.ii.116 (˚cangoṭaka).</p><p>*Sk. anujā</p>

anojā|anojaa|anoja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anojaa</span></p><p>Anojaka = anojā</p><p>Vv.35#4 (= Vv-a.161, where classed with yodhikā bandhujīvakā).</p>

anoka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) without a home; independent.</li><li>(n.) homelessness; independence</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

anoka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>houselessness, a houseless state, fig freedom from worldliness or attachment to life, singleness SN.v.24 = AN.v.232 = Dhp.87 (okā anokaṃ āgamma). adj. homeless, free from attachment SN.i.176; Dhp.87 (<em><a href="bword://an%C4%81laya">anālaya</a></em> Dhp-a.ii.162); Snp.966 (adj.; explained at Mnd.487 by abhisankhāra-sahagatassa viññāṇassa okāsaṃ na karoti &amp; at Snp-a.573 by abhisankhāra-viññān;’ ādīnaṃ anokāsabhūta).</p><p><em><a href="bword://s%C4%81rin">sārin</a></em> living in a houseless state, fig. being free from worldly attachment SN.iii.10 = Mnd.197; Snp.628 (= <em>anālaya-cārin</em> Snp-a.468); Ud.32; Dhp.404 (variant reading anokka˚) Dhp-a.iv.174 (= <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81laya">anālaya</a></em> -cārin); Mil.386.</p><p>an + oka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anālaya">anālaya</a>, <a href="bword://sārin">sārin</a>, <a href="bword://anālaya">anālaya</a></p>

anokasāri|anokasaari|anokasari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anokasaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anokasārin">anokasārin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okasārin">okasārin</a></p>

anokasārin|anokasaarin|anokasarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anokasaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anokasārin">anokasārin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okasārin">okasārin</a></p>

anokāsaṃ kata|anokaasa.m kata|anokasam kata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anokaasa.m kata</span></p><p>ho has not given the opportunity; who has not consented or given permission</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okāsaṃ karoti">okāsaṃ karoti</a></p>

anokāsaṃ kārāpetvā|anokaasa.m kaaraapetvaa|anokasam karapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anokaasa.m kaaraapetvaa</span></p><p>without having consent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okāsaṃ kārāpeti">okāsaṃ kārāpeti</a></p>

anoma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (only ˚-) not inferior, superior, perfect, supreme, in foll. compounds</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87a">guṇa</a></em> supreme virtue DN-a.i.288.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassika">dassika</a></em> of superior beauty Vv.20#7, Vv-a.103 (both as variant reading; T. anuma˚); Vv.43#7.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a></em> one who has supreme knowledge; of unexcelled wisdom (Name of a Buddha) Ja.i.228.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81ma">nāma</a></em> of perfect name SN.i.33 (“by name the Peerless” Mrs. Rh. D.) SN.i.235; Snp.153, Snp.177 (cp. Snp-a.200).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nikkama">nikkama</a></em> of perfect energy Vv.64#27 (= paripuṇṇa-viriyatāya a. Vv-a.284)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1%C3%B1a">pañña</a></em> of lofty or supreme wisdom (Ep. of the Buddha Snp.343, Snp.352 (= mahāpañña Snp-a.347); Thig.522 (paripuṇṇa-pañña Thag-a.296), Dhp-a.i.31.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">vaṇṇa</a></em> of excellent colour Snp.686; Ja.vi.202.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a></em> of supreme exertion or energy Snp.353.</li></ul><p>an + oma</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://guṇa">guṇa</a>, <a href="bword://dassika">dassika</a>, <a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a>, <a href="bword://nāma">nāma</a>, <a href="bword://nikkama">nikkama</a>, <a href="bword://pañña">pañña</a>, <a href="bword://vaṇṇa">vaṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a></p>

anomajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anomajjat">anomajjat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anomajjati">anomajjati</a></p>

anomajjanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anomajjati">anomajjati</a></p>

anomajjat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anomajjat">anomajjat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anomajjati">anomajjati</a></p>

anomajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>rubs down along; strokes (one’s limbs) again and again.</p>

anomajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rub along over, to stroke, only in phrase <em>gattāni pāṇinā a</em>. to rub over one’s limbs with the hand MN.i.80, MN.i.509; SN.v.216.</p><p>anu + ava + majjati, <em>mṛj</em></p>

anomananta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>not bowing; not stooping; not sinking.</p>

anomanikkama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of superior or excellent energy and exertion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oma">oma</a></p>

anomanāma|anomanaama|anomanama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anomanaama</span></p><p>perfectly named; or named for one’s perfection or superior virtues</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oma">oma</a></p>

anonamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>not to bend, to be inflexible, in foll. expressions: <em>anonamaka</em> (nt.) not stooping Dhp-a.ii.136; <em>auonamanto</em> (ppr.) not bending DN.ii.17 = DN.iii.143; <em>anonami-daṇḍa</em> (for anonamiya˚) an inflexible stick Mil.238 (anoṇami˚ T, but anonami˚ vv.ll., see Mil.427).</p><p>an + onamati</p>

anopa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://an%C5%ABpa">anūpa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anūpa">anūpa</a></p>

anopama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>incomparable, peerless; excellent, best</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anūpama">anūpama</a>, <a href="bword://anupama">anupama</a>, <a href="bword://upamā">upamā</a></p>

anopamā|anopamaa|anopama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anopamaa</span></p><p>Daughter of the Treasurer Majjha of Sāketa. She was so called (’Peerless”) because of her beauty. When she grew up, all sorts of eminent men sought her hand with rich gifts, but she was unwilling to marry. She heard the Buddha preach and, meditating on his sermon, attained the Third Fruit of the Path. Later she entered the Order, and on the seventh day thereafter became an arahant. Thig.151–156</p>

anopārambha|anopaarambha|anoparambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anopaarambha</span></p><p>not open to criticism, not censurable</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opārambha">opārambha</a></p>

anoramati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>not to stop, to continue Ja.iii.487; Dhp-a.iii.9 (ger. <em>-itvā</em> continually).</p><p>an + ava + <em>ram</em></p>

anorapāra|anorapaara|anorapara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anorapaara</span></p><p>adjective having (a shore) neither on this side nor beyond Mil.319.</p><p>an + ora + pāra</p>

anosita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not inhabited, not occupied.</p>

anosita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not inhabited (by), not accessible (to) Snp.937 (= anajjhositaṃ Mnd.441 jarādīhi anajjhāvutthaṃ ṭhānaṃ Snp-a.566).</p><p>an + ava + sita, pp. of <strong>sā</strong></p><p>See also: sā</p>

anosārita|anosaarita|anosarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anosaarita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osārita">osārita</a></p>

anotatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A semi-mythical lake, said to be in the Himalayas. Thag.430</p><p>Himalaya5lake</p>

anotiṇṇa|anoti.n.na|anotinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anoti.n.na</span></p><p>who has not gone into, has not penetrated; not having entered; not appeared; not under consideration.</p>

anottappa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.a</i></p><p>shamelessness; failure to avoid wrong-doing or blame</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappa">ottappa</a></p>

anottappa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Anottappa:</strong> see: <em>ahirika</em></p>

anottappa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>recklessness, hardness DN.iii.212; Iti.34 (ahirika +); Pp.20; Dhs.365. Cp. anottāpin. Anottapin &amp; Anottappin;</p><p>an + ottappa</p>

anottappi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anottappin">anottappin</a></p><p>shameless, not fearful (of doing wrong)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappin">ottappin</a></p>

anottappin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anottappin">anottappin</a></p><p>shameless, not fearful (of doing wrong)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappin">ottappin</a></p>

anottappin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Anottāpin &amp; Anottappin</p><p>adjective not afraid of sin, bold, reckless, shameless DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282 (pp; ahirika); Snp.133 (p; ahirika +); Iti.27, Iti.115 (anatāpin anottappin vv.ll. anottāpin); Pp.20, Pp.24.</p><p>fr. anottappa</p>

anottāpi|anottaapi|anottapi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anottaapi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anottāpin">anottāpin</a></p><p>shameless, not fearful (of doing wrong)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottāpin">ottāpin</a></p>

anottāpin|anottaapin|anottapin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anottaapin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anottāpin">anottāpin</a></p><p>shameless, not fearful (of doing wrong)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottāpin">ottāpin</a></p>

anottāpin|anottaapin|anottapin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anottaapin</span></p><p>Anottāpin &amp; Anottappin</p><p>adjective not afraid of sin, bold, reckless, shameless DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282 (pp; ahirika); Snp.133 (p; ahirika +); Iti.27, Iti.115 (anatāpin anottappin vv.ll. anottāpin); Pp.20, Pp.24.</p><p>fr. anottappa</p>

anovadiyamāna|anovadiyamaana|anovadiyamana
