anta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine end, limit, conclusion; death, destruction; edge, boundary; extreme, opposing principle.</p><p>anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. last, ultimate; worst, mean, contemptible.</p><p>anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter (sg. &amp; pl.) the bowels, intestines.</p>

anta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>end, finish, goal SN.iv.368 (of Nibbāna); Snp.467; Ja.ii.159. <em>antaṃ karoti</em> to make an end (of) Snp.283, Snp.512; Dhp.275, cp. antakara, ˚kiriyā<br />■ loc. <em>ante</em> at the end of, immediately after Ja.i.203 (vijay˚).</li><li>limit, border edge Vin.i.47; Dhp.305 (van˚); Ja.iii.188.</li><li>side see <em><a href="bword://ekamanta%E1%B9%83">ekamantaṃ</a></em> (on one side, aside).</li><li>opposite side, opposite, counterpart; pl. parts, contrasts, extremes; thus also used as “constituent, principle” (in <em>tayo &amp; cattāro antā;</em> or does it belong to anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 2 in this meaning Cp. ekantaṃ extremely, under anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->): <em><a href="bword://dve">dve</a></em> antā (two extremes) Vin.i.10; SN.ii.17; SN.iii.135. <em><a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></em> antā (both sides) Vin.i.10; SN.ii.17; Ja.i.8; Mnd.109. <em>eko, dutiyo</em> anto (contrasts) Mnd.52. As tayo antā or principles(?) viz. sakkāya, s- samudaya, s- nirodha DN.iii.216, cp. AN.iii.401; as <em>cattāro</em>, viz. the 3 mentioned plus s- nirodhagāmini-paṭipadā at SN.iii.157. Interpreted by Morris as “goal” (J.P.T.S. 1894, 70)</li></ol><p>Often pleonastically, to be explained as a “pars pro toto” figure, like <em><a href="bword://kammanta">kammanta</a></em> (q.v.) the end of the work, i.e. the whole work (cp. E sea-<em>side</em>, country-<em>side</em>); <em>vananta</em> the border of the wood the woods Dhp.305; Pv.ii.3#10 (explained by vana Pv-a.86 same use in BSk., vanânta e.g. at Jtm.vi.21; cp. also grāmânta Avs.i.210); <strong>suttanta</strong> (q.v.), etc. Cp. ākāsanta Ja.vi.89 &amp; the pleonastic use of patha.; <em>-<a href="bword://ananta">ananta</a></em> (n.) no end, infinitude; (adj.) endless, corresponds either to Sk anta or antya, see *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p><em>-ānanta</em> end &amp; no end, or finite and endless, DN.i.22; DN-a.i.115. <em>-ānantika</em> (holding views of, or talking about finiteness and infinitude DN.i.22 (see expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at DN-a.i.115) SN.iii.214, SN.iii.258 sq.; Pts.i.155. <em>-<a href="bword://kara">kara</a></em> putting an end to (n.) a deliverer, saviour; usually in phrase dukkhass’a (of the Buddha) MN.i.48, MN.i.531; AN.ii.2; AN.iii.400 sq.; Thag.195; Iti.18; Snp.32, Snp.337, Snp.539; Pp.71. In other comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> AN.ii.163 (vijjāy’); Snp.1148 (pañhān’)<br />■ <em>-<a href="bword://kiriy%C4%81">kiriyā</a></em> putting an end to, ending, relief, extirpation; always used with ref. to dukkha SN.iv.93; Iti.89; Snp.454, Snp.725; Dhp-a.iv.45 <em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> = antagū Cnd.436 (+ koṭigata). <em>-gāhikā</em> (f.), viz diṭṭhi, is an attribute of micchādiṭṭhi, i.e. heretical doctrine The meaning of anta in this comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is not quite clear: either “holding (wrong) principles (goals, Morris)” viz. the 3 as specified above 4 under tayo antā (thus Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 70), or “taking extreme sides, i.e. extremist”, or “wrong, opposite (= antya, see *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) (thus Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v.) Vin.i.172; DN.iii.45, DN.iii.48 (an˚) SN.i.154; AN.i.154; AN.ii.240; AN.iii.130; Pts.i.151 sq. <em>-<a href="bword://g%C5%AB">gū</a></em> one who has gone to the end, one who has gone through or overcome (dukkha) AN.iv.254, AN.iv.258, AN.iv.262; Snp.401 (= vaṭṭadukkhassa antagata); Snp.539. <em>-ruddhi</em> at Ja.vi.8 is doubtful reading (antaruci?). <em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADi">vaṭṭi</a></em> rimmed circumference Ja.iii.159 <em>-<a href="bword://sa%C3%B1%C3%B1in">saññin</a></em> being conscious of an end (of the world) DN.i.22 cp. DN-a.i.115.</p><p>Vedic anta; Goth. andeis = Ohg. anti = E. end; cp. also Lat. antiae forehead (: E. antler), and the prep anti opposite, antika near = Lat. ante; Gr. ἀντί &amp; α ̓́ντα opposite; Goth., Ags. and; Ger. ant-; orig. the opposite (i.e. what stands against or faces the starting-point)</p><p>Anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>having an end, belonging to the end; only in neg. <em><a href="bword://ananta">ananta</a></em> endless, infinite, boundless (opp. <em><a href="bword://antavant">antavant</a></em>); which may be taken as equal to anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (corresp. with Sk. anta (adj.) or antya; also in doublet anañca, see ākās’ânañca and viññāṇ’ânañca); DN.i.23, DN.i.34 = DN.iii.224, DN.iii.262 sq.; Snp.468 (˚pañña); Dhp.179, Dhp.180 (˚gocara having an unlimited range of mental vision, cp Dhp-a.iii.197); Ja.i.178.</li><li>extreme, last, worst Ja.ii.440 (C. hīna, lāmaka); see also *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 4<br />■ acc. as adv. in <em><a href="bword://ekanta%E1%B9%83">ekantaṃ</a></em> extremely, very much, “utterly” Dhp.228 etc See eka.</li></ol><p>Vedic antya</p><p>Anta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter the lower intestine, bowels mesentery Iti.89; Ja.i.66, Ja.i.260 (˚vaddhi-maṃsa etc.); Vism.258; Dhp-a.i.80.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhi">gaṇṭhi</a></em> twisting of the bowels, lit. “a knot in the intestines” Vin.i.275 (˚ābādha).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87a">guṇa</a></em> <a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87a">guṇa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = gula<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> the intestinal tract, the bowels SN.ii.270; AN.iv.132; Kp iii. = Mil.26; Vism.42; Kp-a.57.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> the anus Ja.iv.402.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADi">vaṭṭi</a></em> = ˚guṇa Vism.258.</li></ul><p>Vedic āntra, contr. fr. antara inner = Lat. interus, Gr. ε ̓́ντερα intestines</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ekamantaṃ">ekamantaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://dve">dve</a>, <a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a>, <a href="bword://kammanta">kammanta</a>, suttanta, <a href="bword://ananta">ananta</a>, <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://kara">kara</a>, <a href="bword://kiriyā">kiriyā</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://gū">gū</a>, <a href="bword://vaṭṭi">vaṭṭi</a>, <a href="bword://saññin">saññin</a>, <a href="bword://ananta">ananta</a>, <a href="bword://antavant">antavant</a>, <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://ekantaṃ">ekantaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://gaṇṭhi">gaṇṭhi</a>, <a href="bword://guṇa">guṇa</a>, <a href="bword://guṇa">guṇa</a>, <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a>, <a href="bword://vaṭṭi">vaṭṭi</a></p>

