anupubbakāraṇa|anupubbakaara.na|anupubbakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbakaara.na</span></p><p>gradual training.</p>

anupubbanirodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>successive cessation.</p>

anupubbapaṭipadā|anupubbapa.tipadaa|anupubbapatipada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbapa.tipadaa</span></p><p>regular or gradual progress.</p>

anupubbasikkhā|anupubbasikkhaa|anupubbasikkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbasikkhaa</span></p><p>regular or gradual training.</p>

anupubbaso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i></p><p>in regular order.</p>

anupubbata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>acting in turn, gradation, succession Vv.64#14 (= anukūla kiriyā i.e. as it pleases Vv-a.280) cp. ānupubbatā. Anupubbi-katha</p><p>fr. anupubba</p>

anupubbavihāra|anupubbavihaara|anupubbavihara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbavihaara</span></p><p>gradual or successive stage (in meditation).</p>

anupubbavihāri|anupubbavihaari|anupubbavihari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbavihaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupubbavihārin">anupubbavihārin</a></p><p>moving through successive stages.</p>

anupubbavihārin|anupubbavihaarin|anupubbaviharin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbavihaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupubbavihārin">anupubbavihārin</a></p><p>moving through successive stages.</p>

anupubbaṃ|anupubba.m|anupubbam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubba.m</span></p><p>in regular order, by turn; gradually.</p>

anupubbena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>gradually; successively, in due course.</p>

anupubbi-kathā|anupubbi-kathaa|anupubbi-katha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupubbi-kathaa</span></p><p>Anupubbi-kathā</p><p>feminine a gradual instruction, graduated sermon regulated exposition of the ever higher values of four subjects (dāna-kathā, sīla˚, sagga˚, magga˚) i.e. charity righteousness, the heavens, and the Path. Bdhgh. explains the term as anupubbikathā nāma dānânantaraṃ sīlaṃ sīlânantaro saggo saggânantaro maggo ti etesaṃ dīpana-kathā (DN-a.i.277). Vin.i.15, Vin.i.18; Vin.ii.156, Vin.ii.192; DN.i.110; DN.ii.41; MN.i.379; Ja.i.8; Vv-a.66, Vv-a.197, Vv-a.208; DN-a.i.308; Dhp-a.i.6; Mil.228<br />■ The spelling is frequently <em><a href="bword://%C4%81nupubbikath%C4%81">ānupubbikathā</a></em> (as to lengthening of anu see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> Note<br />a), e.g. at DN.i.110; DN.ii.41; MN.i.379; Ja.i.8; Mil.228.</p><p>anupubba + kathā, formation like dhammi-kathā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānupubbikathā">ānupubbikathā</a>, <a href="bword://anu">anu</a></p>

anupucchamāna|anupucchamaana|anupucchamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupucchamaana</span></p><p>inquiring after.</p>

anupucchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to ask or inquire after (c. acc.) Snp.432, Snp.1113<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anupu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">anupuṭṭha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + pucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupuṭṭha">anupuṭṭha</a></p>

anupuṭṭha|anupu.t.tha|anuputtha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupu.t.tha</span></p><p>asked.</p>

anupuṭṭha|anupu.t.tha|anuputtha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupu.t.tha</span></p><p>asked Snp.782 (= pucchita Snp-a.521).</p><p>pp. of anupucchati</p>

anupādaṃ|anupaada.m|anupadam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaada.m</span></p><p>adverb at the foot Vism.182 (opp. <em>anusīsaṃ</em> at the head).</p><p>anu + pāda</p>

anupādi|anupaadi|anupadi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadi</span></p><p>Anupādāna &amp; Anupādi</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81d%C4%81na">upādāna</a></em> &amp; <em>upādi</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādāna">upādāna</a></p>

anupādinna|anupaadinna|anupadinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadinna</span></p><p>not evolved or derived, primary; not evolved by the influence of kamma; inanimate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādiṇṇa">anupādiṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://upādinna">upādinna</a></p>

anupādisesa|anupaadisesa|anupadisesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadisesa</span></p><ol><li>(of <em>nibbāna</em>, at death) without any remnant of material support (the result of previous kamma), absolute.</li><li>(of <em>arhats</em>) without any remnant of material support.</li><li>without any trace or infection remaining; without leaving; any trace or infection</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādisesa">upādisesa</a></p>

anupādisesa-nibbāna|anupaadisesa-nibbaana|anupadisesa-nibbana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadisesa-nibbaana</span></p><p><strong>Anupādisesa-nibbāna:</strong> see <em>Nibbāna</em> <em>&amp; upādi</em></p>

anupādiya|anupaadiya|anupadiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadiya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupādiyat">anupādiyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyat">upādiyat</a></p>

anupādiyamāna|anupaadiyamaana|anupadiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyamāna">upādiyamāna</a></p>

anupādiyat|anupaadiyat|anupadiyat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadiyat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupādiyat">anupādiyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyat">upādiyat</a></p>

anupādiṇṇa|anupaadi.n.na|anupadinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadi.n.na</span></p><p>not evolved or derived, primary; not evolved by the influence of kamma; inanimate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādinna">anupādinna</a>, upadiṇṇa</p>

anupādā|anupaadaa|anupada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadaa</span></p><p>not taking as one’s own, not holding on to; without (material) support, without dependence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādāya">anupādāya</a></p>

anupādā|anupaadaa|anupada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadaa</span></p><p><em>anupādāniya, anupādāya, anupādiyāna, anupādiyiṭvā</em> see <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81diyati">upādiyati</a></em>. Anupadana &amp; Anupadi;</p><p>ger. of an + upādiyati = anupādāya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

anupādāna|anupaadaana|anupadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadaana</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) without taking as one’s own, without holding on to; without (material) support, without fuel.</li><li>(n.) not holding; on to; absence of support or fuel</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādāna">upādāna</a></p>

anupādāna|anupaadaana|anupadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadaana</span></p><p>Anupādāna &amp; Anupādi</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81d%C4%81na">upādāna</a></em> &amp; <em>upādi</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādāna">upādāna</a></p>

anupādāya|anupaadaaya|anupadaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaadaaya</span></p><p>not taking as one ‘s own, not holding on to, not grasping; without support, without dependence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādāya">anupādāya</a></p>

anupāhana|anupaahana|anupahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaahana</span></p><p>adjective without shoes Ja.vi.552.</p><p>an + upāhana</p>

anupāhanā|anupaahanaa|anupahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaahanaa</span></p><p>without sandals; barefoot</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāhanā">upāhanā</a></p>

anupāhāra|anupaahaara|anupahara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaahaara</span></p><p>not bringing; non-supply.</p>

anupālaka|anupaalaka|anupalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaalaka</span></p><p>adjective guarding, preserving Sdhp.474.</p><p>anu + pālaka</p>

anupālana|anupaalana|anupalana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaalana</span></p><p>maintenance, guarding, keeping Dpvs.iii.2.</p><p>fr. anupāleti</p>

anupāleti|anupaaleti|anupaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaaleti</span></p><p>to safeguard, warrant, maintain Mil.160 (santatiṃ).</p><p>anu + pāleti</p>

anupāpeti|anupaapeti|anupapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaapeti</span></p><p>to make reach or attain, to lead to, to give or make find Ja.vi.88; Cp. xi. 4 (aor <em>anupāpayi</em>); Mil.276<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anup%C4%81pita">anupāpita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Caus. of anupāpuṇāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupāpita">anupāpita</a></p>

anupāpita|anupaapita|anupapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaapita</span></p><p>having been lead to or made to reach, attained, found Mil.252.</p><p>pp. of anupāpeti</p>

anupāpuṇitvā|anupaapu.nitvaa|anupapunitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaapu.nitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupāpuṇāti">anupāpuṇāti</a></p>

anupāpuṇāti|anupaapu.naati|anupapunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaapu.naati</span></p><p>reaches, attains; falls to one’s lot.</p>

anupāpuṇāti|anupaapu.naati|anupapunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaapu.naati</span></p><p>(anuppā˚) to reach, attain, get to, find SN.i.105; ger. <em>anuppatvāna</em> Pv.ii.9#24 (˚pāpuṇitvā Pv-a.123)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anupatta">anupatta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://anup%C4%81peti">anupāpeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + pāpuṇāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupatta">anupatta</a>, <a href="bword://anupāpeti">anupāpeti</a></p>

anupāsikā|anupaasikaa|anupasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaasikaa</span></p><p>one who is not a female lay-disciple</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāsikā">upāsikā</a></p>

anupāsita|anupaasita|anupasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaasita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāsita">upāsita</a></p>

anupāta|anupaata|anupata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaata</span></p><p>attack in speech, contest, reproach AN.i.161 (vāda˚).</p><p>of anupatati</p>

anupāti|anupaati|anupati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaati</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupātin">anupātin</a></p><p>following; attacking.</p>

anupātin|anupaatin|anupatin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaatin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anupātin">anupātin</a></p><p>following; attacking.</p>

anupātin|anupaatin|anupatin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaatin</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>following, indulging in Ja.iii.523 (khaṇa˚).</li><li>attacking, hurting Ja.v.399.</li></ol><p>fr. anupāta</p>

anupāya|anupaaya|anupaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaaya</span></p><p>not going near; free from involvement</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāya">upāya</a></p>

anupāya|anupaaya|anupaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaaya</span></p><p>wrong means Ja.i.256; Sdhp.405.</p><p>an + upāya</p>

anupāyāsa|anupaayaasa|anupayasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaayaasa</span></p><ol><li>trouble-free; not bringing annoyance.</li><li>not irritated, at peace; free from trouble or annoyance</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāyāsa">upāyāsa</a></p>

anupāyāsa|anupaayaasa|anupayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupaayaasa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81y%C4%81sa">upāyāsa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāyāsa">upāyāsa</a></p>

anupīḷaṃ|anupii.la.m|anupilam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anupii.la.m</span></p><p>at Pv-a.161 is to be read <em>anuppīḷan</em> (q.v.).</p>

anuraho
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>in secret, in private.</p>

anuraho
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb in secret, face to face, private MN.i.27.</p><p>anu + raho</p>

anurakkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anurakkhat">anurakkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhati">anurakkhati</a></p>

anurakkhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective preserving, keeping up Ja.iv.192 (<em>vaṃsa˚</em>); Ja.vi.1 (id.).</p><p>fr. anurakkhati, cp. ˚rakkhin</p>

anurakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i></p><p>guarding, preservation, taking care of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhaṇa">anurakkhaṇa</a></p>

anurakkhana-padhāna|anurakkhana-padhaana|anurakkhana-padhana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkhana-padhaana</span></p><p><strong>Anurakkhana-padhāna:</strong> the ‘effort to maintain’ advantageous states; see: <em>padhāna</em></p>

anurakkhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhati">anurakkhati</a></p>

anurakkhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anurakkhat">anurakkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhati">anurakkhati</a></p>

anurakkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>protects, guards; preserves; takes care of.</p>

anurakkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to guard, watch over (acc.), preserve, protect, shield Snp.149; Dhp.327; Ja.i.46; Pp.12<br />ppr med˚ <em>rakkhamāna(ka)</em> as adj. Sdhp.621.</p><p>anu + rakkhati</p>

anurakkhaṇa|anurakkha.na|anurakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkha.na</span></p><p>guarding, preservation, taking care of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhana">anurakkhana</a></p>

anurakkhaṇa|anurakkha.na|anurakkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkha.na</span></p><p><em>-ā</em> (f.) guarding, protection, preservation DN.iii.225 sq.; AN.ii.16 sq.; Ja.i.133 Pp.12; Dpvs.iv.24 (adj.); Vv-a.32 (citta˚); Sdhp.449.</p><p>abstr. fr. anurakkhati</p>

anurakkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective guarding, preserving, keeping Ja.v.24.</p><p>fr. anurakkhati</p>

anurakkhissāmi|anurakkhissaami|anurakkhissami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkhissaami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhati">anurakkhati</a></p>

anurakkhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurakkhati">anurakkhati</a></p>

anurakkhiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective in <em>dur˚</em>; difficult to guard Vin.iii.149.</p><p>f. anurakkhati</p>

anurakkhā|anurakkhaa|anurakkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkhaa</span></p><p>guarding; preservation.</p>

anurakkhā|anurakkhaa|anurakkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anurakkhaa</span></p><p>guarding, protection, preservation SN.iv.323 (anuddayā a. anukampā).</p><p>= anurakkhaṇā</p>

anuratta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>attached or devoted to; fond of.</p>

anuratta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective pp. of anu + <em>rañj</em> ] attached or devoted to, fond of, faithful Thig.446 (bhattāraṃ); Ja.i.297; Mil.146.</p>

anuravanā|anuravanaa|anuravana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuravanaa</span></p><p>lingering of the sound, resounding Mil.63.</p><p>abstr. fr. anuravati</p>

anuravati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to resound, to sound after, linger (of sound) Mil.63.</p><p>anu + ravati</p>

anurañjita|anura~njita|anuranjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anura~njita</span></p><p>illumined, brighterted, beautified Bv.i.45 (byāmapabhā˚ by the shine of the halo); Vv-a.4 (sañjhātapa˚ for sañjhāpabhā˚).</p><p>pp. of anu + rañjeti, Caus. of <em>rañj</em></p>

anurodati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cry after, cry for Ja.iii.166 = Pv.i.12#7 (dārako candaṃ a.).</p><p>anu + rodati</p>

anurodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>compliance, consideration, liking.</p>

anurodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>compliance, consideration satisfaction (opp. <em><a href="bword://virodha">virodha</a></em>) SN.i.111; SN.iv.210; Snp.362; Dhs.1059; Vb.145; Dhs-a.362.</p><p>fr. anu + <em>rudh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://virodha">virodha</a></p>

anurodhavirodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>compliance, and opposition; liking and dislike</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurodha">anurodha</a></p>

anuruddha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>First cousin of the Buddha and one of his most eminent disciples. He was the son of the Sākyan Amitodana and brother of Mahānāma. When members of other Sākyan families had joined the Order of their distinguished kinsman, Mahānāma was grieved that none had gone forth from his own. He therefore suggested to his brother that one of them should leave household life. Anuruddha was at first reluctant to agree, for he had been reared most delicately and luxuriously, dwelling in a different house for each season, surrounded by dancers and mimes. But on hearing from Mahānāma of the endless round of household cares he agreed to go. He could not, however, get his mother’s consent until he persuaded his cousin Bhaddiya to go with him. Together they went with Ānanda, Bhagu, Kimbila, Devadatta and their barber Upāli, to the Blessed One at the Anupiya Mango Grove and were ordained. Before the rainy season was over Anuruddha acquired the divine eye. Vin.ii.180–183 He was later ranked foremost among those who had obtained this attainment. AN.i.23</p><p>He then received from Sāriputta, as topic of meditation, the eight thoughts of a great man. AN.iv.228<em>ff.</em> Another conversation he had with Sāriputta before becoming an arahant is reported. AN.i.281–282 He went into the Pācīnavaṃsadāya in the Ceti country to practise these. He mastered seven, but could not learn the eighth. The Buddha, being aware of this, visited him and taught it to him. Thereupon Anuruddha developed insight and realised arahantship in the highest grade. AN.iv.228<em>ff.</em> Thag.901</p><p>When the Buddha, disgusted with the quarrels of the Kosambī monks, went away to seek more congenial surroundings, it was to Pācīnavaṃsadāya that he repaired, where were Anuruddha, Nandiya and Kimbila. The Upakkilesa Sutta, MN.iii.153<em>f.</em> on the sweets of concord and freedom from blemish, seems to have been preached specially to Anuruddha on that occasion, for we are told at the end that he was pleased to have heard it, no mention being made of the other two. And again in the Naḷakapāna Sutta, MN.i.462<em>ff.</em> though a large number of distinguished monks are present, it is to Anuruddha that the Buddha directly addresses his questions, and it is Anuruddha who answers on behalf of them all. See also the Cūḷa—and the Mahā-Gosiṅga Suttas.</p><p>Anuruddha was present when the Buddha died at Kusinārā, and knew the exact moment of his death; the verse he uttered on that occasion is thoughtful and shows philosophic calm, in contrast, for example, with that of Ānanda. DN.ii.156–157</p><p>Anuruddha was foremost in consoling the monks and admonishing them as to their future course of action. It was Anuruddha again that the Mallas of Kusinārā consulted regarding the Buddha’s last obsequies. DN.ii.160<em>f.</em></p><p>In one of the verses ascribed to Anuruddha in the Theragāthā Thag.904 it is said that for twenty-five years he did not sleep at all, and that for the last thirty years of his life he slept only during the last watch of the night.</p><p>The Anuruddha Saṃyutta SN.v.294 gives an account of a series of questions asked by Moggallāna on the <em>satipaṭṭhāna</em>, their extent, etc. Anuruddha evidently laid great emphasis on the cultivation of the <em>satipaṭṭhāna</em>, for we find mention of them occurring over and over again in his discourses. He attributes all his powers to their development, and admonishes his hearers to practise them. SN.v.299–306 He himself considered the divine eye as the highest attainment. Thus in the Mahāgosiṅga Sutta MN.i.213 he declares it to be more worthy than knowledge of the doctrine, meditation, forest-life, discourse on the abhidhamma or self-mastery.</p><p>Once he lay grievously ill in the Andhavana in Sāvatthī, but the pain made no impression on his mind, because, he says, his mind was well grounded in the <em>satipaṭṭhāna</em>. SN.v.302 Apart from his teaching of the <em>satipaṭṭhāna</em>, he does not seem to have found fame as a teacher. He was of a retiring disposition and never interfered in any of the monks’ quarrels.</p><p>Mention is often made of Anuruddha’s <em>iddhi</em>-powers. Thus, he was one of those who went to the Brahma-world to curb the pride of the Brahma who had thought that no ascetic could reach his world. SN.i.145 (The others being Moggallāna, Mahākassapa and Mahākappina.) The mother of the Yakkha Piyaṅkara, while wandering in search of food, heard him at night reciting some verses from the Dhammapada and stood spellbound listening. SN.i.209</p><p>His <em>iddhi</em>, however, does not seem to have enabled him to prevent his fellow-dweller Abhiñjika from talking too much, SN.ii.203–204 nor his other fellow-dweller Bāhiya from attempting to create dissension in the Order. AN.ii.239 Among the Vajjians he seems to have been held particularly in esteem, together with Nandiya and Kimbila. A <em>yakkha</em> named Dīgha tells the Buddha how the Vajjians are envied by the inhabitants of the deva and brahma worlds on account of the presence of these distinguished monks in their country. MN.i.210</p>

anuruddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>compliant; pleased with, approving</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anurujjhati">anurujjhati</a></p>

anuruddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>enggaged in, devoted to; compliant or complied with, pleased SN.iv.71, (anānuruddha).</p><p>pp. of anurujjhati</p>

anuruddhapaṭiviruddha|anuruddhapa.tiviruddha|anuruddhapativiruddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuruddhapa.tiviruddha</span></p><p>approving and rejecting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuruddha">anuruddha</a></p>

anurujjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>approves (of); is pleased or content.</p>

anurujjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to conform oneself to, have a regard for, approve, to be pleased AN.iv.158; Dhs-a.362<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anuruddha">anuruddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. anurudhyate, Pass. of anu + <em>rudh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuruddha">anuruddha</a></p>

anurādha|anuraadha|anuradha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuraadha</span></p><p>An Elder. Once when he was staying in a forest hut in the Mahāvana in Vesāli, near to where the Buddha was, certain wandering ascetics came to him and asked him whether or not a Tathāgata exists after death; dissatisfied with his answer they called him “fool” and went away. Thereupon Anurādha sought advice from the Buddha, who asked him “How, inasmuch as it cannot be said of a Tathāgata even in this very life that he really exists, can anything be said regarding him after death?” SN.iii.116–119 SN.iv.380–386</p>

anurūpa|anuruupa|anurupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuruupa</span></p><p>adjective suitable, adequate, seeming, fit, worthy; adapted to, corresponding, conform with (-˚ Ja.i.91; Ja.vi.366 (tad˚); Pv-a.61 (ajjhāsaya˚ according to his wish), Pv-a.128 (id.), Pv-a.78, Pv-a.122, Pv-a.130, Pv-a.155; etc. Cp. also <em><a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADir%C5%ABpa">paṭirūpa</a></em> in same meaning.</p><p>anu + rūpa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paṭirūpa">paṭirūpa</a></p>

anurūpikā|anuruupikaa|anurupika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuruupikaa</span></p><p>conforming; suitable.</p>

anusahagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having a residuum, accompanied by a minimum of… SN.iii.130; Kv.81, see *<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%87u">aṇu</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṇu">aṇu</a></p>

anusajjhāyati|anusajjhaayati|anusajjhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusajjhaayati</span></p><p>recites again; repeats.</p>

anusampavaṅkatā|anusampava"nkataa|anusampavankata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusampava"nkataa</span></p><p>being inclined towards; intimacy.</p>

anusampavaṅkatā|anusampava"nkataa|anusampavankata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusampava"nkataa</span></p><p>disputing, quarrelling(?) Vin.ii.88 (under anuvādâdhikaraṇa).</p><p>anu + saṃ + pavankatā; is reading correct?</p>

anusandahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>applies to, connects with, fixes on; aims at; conforms to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusandhati">anusandhati</a>, anusandheti</p>

anusandahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to direct upon, to apply to AN.iv.47 sq. (cittaṃ samāpattiyā; so to be read with variant reading for anusandati) Mil.63 (but here prob. to be read as <em><a href="bword://anusandati">anusandati</a></em>, q.v.).</p><p>anu + saṃ + <em>dhā</em>, cf. Vedic abhi + saṃ + dhā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusandati">anusandati</a></p>

anusandati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>flows, flows along; moves along, moves to; moves after.</p>

anusandati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to stream along after, to follow, to be connected with. Thus to be read at Mil.63 for anusandahati (anuravati + ; of sound) while at AN.iv.47 the reading is to be corrected to <em><a href="bword://anusandahati">anusandahati</a></em>.</p><p>Vedic anusyandati, anu + <em>syad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusandahati">anusandahati</a></p>

anusandeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>applies to, connects with, fixes on; aims at; conforms to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusandhati">anusandhati</a>, anusandheti</p>

anusandhanatā|anusandhanataa|anusandhanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusandhanataa</span></p><p>application, adjusting Dhs.8 (cittassa).</p><p>= anusandhi</p>

anusandhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>applies to, connects with, fixes on; aims at; conforms to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusandhati">anusandhati</a>, anusandheti</p>

anusandhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>connection, (logical) conclusion, application DN-a.i.122 (where 3 kinds are enumerated, viz. <em>pucchā˚, ajjhāsayā˚, yathā˚</em>); Ne.14 (pucchato; Hard., in Index “complete cessation”?!). Esp. freq in (Jātaka) phrase <em>anusandhiṃ ghaṭeti</em> “to form the connection”, to draw the conclusion, to show the application of the story or point out its maxim Ja.i.106; Ja.i.308; Dhp-a.ii.40, Dhp-a.ii.47; etc.</p><p>fr. anu + saṃ + <em>dhā</em></p>

anusantata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>extended; continued.</p>

anusantatavutti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>continually practicing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusantata">anusantata</a></p>

anusarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>follows, pursues; conforms oneself to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anusarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to follow, conform oneself to SN.iv.303 (phalaṃ anusarissati BB, but balaṃ anupadassati SS perhaps to be preferred)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://anus%C4%81reti">anusāreti</a></em> to bring together with, to send up to or against Mil.36 (aññamaññaṃ a. anupeseti).</p><p>anu + <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāreti">anusāreti</a></p>

anusati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>recollection, remembrance, calling to mind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussati">anussati</a></p>

anusatthar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>instructor, adviser Ja.iv.178 (ācariya +). Cp anusāsaka.</p><p>n. ag. to anu + <em>sās</em>, cf. Sk. anuśāsitṛ &amp; P. satthar</p>

anusatthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>admonition, rule, instruction Ja.i.241; Mil.98, Mil.172, Mil.186 (dhamma˚), Mil.225, Mil.227, Mil.347.</p><p>Sk. anuśāsti, anu + <em>śās</em>, cp. anusāsana</p>

anusavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>flows in; flows upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussavati">anussavati</a></p>

anusavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at SN.ii.54 (āsavā na a.; variant reading anusayanti) &amp; iv. 188 (akusalā dhammā na a.; variant reading anusenti) should preferably be read anusayati: see <em><a href="bword://anuseti">anuseti</a></em> 2.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuseti">anuseti</a></p>

anusaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(latent) disposition, tendency, propensity, inherence.</p>

anusaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Anusaya:</strong> The 7 ‘latent tendencies’, hidden inclinations, or latent liabilities are:</p><p>1: The latent tendency to sense-greed <em>kāma-rāga</em> <em>samyojana</em>,<br />2: The latent tendency to aversion <em>patigha</em>,<br />3: The latent tendency to speculative opinion <em>ditthi</em>,<br />4: The latent tendency to skeptical doubt <em>vicikicchā,</em><br />5: The latent tendency to conceit &amp; pride <em>māna</em>,<br />6: The latent tendency to craving for continued existence <em>bhava-rāga,</em><br />7: The latent tendency to ignorance <em>avijjā</em> D. 33; A. VII, 11, 12.</p><p>These things are called ‘latent tendencies’ since, in consequence of their endurance, they ever and again - life after life- tend to become the conditions for the arising of ever new sense-greed, etc. Vis.M XXII, 60.</p><p>Yam. VII, first determines in which beings such and such latent tendencies exist, and which latent tendencies, and with regard to what, and in which sphere of existence. Thereafter it gives an explanation concerning their overcoming, their penetration, etc. Cf. Guide VI vii. According to Kath. several ancient Buddhist schools erroneously held the opinion that the <em>anusayas</em> as such, meant merely latent, hence kammically neutral qualities, which however contradicts the Theravāda conception. Cf. Guide V, 88, 108, 139.</p>

anusaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(see Kvu trsl. 234 n. 2 and Cpd. 172 n. 2). Bent, bias proclivity, the persistance of a dormant or latent disposition predisposition, tendency. Always in bad sense. In the oldest texts the word usually occurs absolutely, without mention of the cause or direction of the bias. So Sn.14 = 369, 545; MN.iii.31; SN.iii.130, SN.iv.33, SN.v.28 SN.v.236; AN.i.44; AN.ii.157; AN.iii.74, AN.iii.246, AN.iii.443. Or in the triplet obstinacy, prejudice and bias (<em>adhiṭṭhānābhinivesānusayā</em>) SN.ii.17; SN.iii.10, SN.iii.135, SN.iii.161; AN v.iii. Occasionally a source of the bias is mentioned. Thus pride at SN.i.188 SN.ii.252 ff., SN.ii.275; SN.iii.80, SN.iii.103, SN.iii.169, SN.iii.253; SN.iv.41, SN.iv.197; AN.i.132 AN.iv.70 doubt at MN.i.486-ignorance lust and hatred at SN.iv.205, MN.iii.285. At DN.iii.254, DN.iii.282; SN.v.60; and AN.iv.9. we have a list of seven anusaya’s, the above five and delusion and craving for rebirth. Hence-forward these lists govern the connotation of the word; but it would be wrong to put that connotation back into the earlier passages. Later references are Pts.i.26, Pts.i.70 ff., Pts.i.123 Pts.i.130, Pts.i.195; Pts.ii.36, Pts.ii.84, Pts.ii.94, Pts.ii.158; Pp.21; Vb.340, Vb.383 Vb.356; Kv.405 ff. Dpvs.i.42.</p><p>anu + <em>śī</em>, seti Sk. anuśaya has a diff. meaning</p>

anusayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusayin">anusayin</a></p><p>latent; persistent.</p>

anusayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusayin">anusayin</a></p><p>latent; persistent.</p>

anusayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective DN.ii.283 (me dīgharatta˚), “for me, so long obsessed (with doubts)”. The reading is uncertain.</p><p>fr. anusaya</p>

anusayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>inherent, persistent, latent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuseti">anuseti</a></p>

anusayita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>dormant, only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>dīgharatta˚</em>; latent so long Thag.768; Snp.355 Snp.649. Cp. anusaya &amp; anusayin.</p><p>pp. of anuseti, anu + <em>śī</em></p>

anusañcaranta|anusa~ncaranta|anusancaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~ncaranta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusañcarati">anusañcarati</a></p>

anusañcarati|anusa~ncarati|anusancarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~ncarati</span></p><p>goes around, walks about (with); walks along, traverses; considers.</p>

anusañcarati|anusa~ncarati|anusancarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~ncarati</span></p><p>to walk along, to go round about, to visit MN.i.279; SN.v.53, SN.v.301; Ja.i.202 Ja.iii.502; Pv-a.279 (nagaraṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anusa%C3%B1carita">anusañcarita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + saṃ + carati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusañcarita">anusañcarita</a></p>

anusañcarita|anusa~ncarita|anusancarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~ncarita</span></p><p>frequented, visited, resorted to Mil.387.</p><p>pp. of anusañcarati</p>

anusañceteti|anusa~nceteti|anusanceteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~nceteti</span></p><p>to set ones mind on, concentrate, think over, meditate Pp.12.</p><p>anu + saṃ + ceteti</p>

anusaññāti|anusa~n~naati|anusannati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~n~naati</span></p><p>to go to, to visit, inspect, control<br />ppr med <em>-saññāyamāna</em> Vin.iii.43 (kammante);<br />inf <em>-saññātuṃ</em> AN.i.68. (janapade).</p><p>either anu + saṃ + <em>jñā</em> (jānāti) or (preferably) = anusaṃyāti as short form of anusaṃyāyati, like anuyāti → anuyāyati of anu + saṃ + <strong>yā</strong>, cf. Sk. anusaṃyāti in same meaning</p><p>See also: yā</p>

anusaññāyati|anusa~n~naayati|anusannayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~n~naayati</span></p><p>goes through, travels about; inspects, supervises; goes along with, escorts</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaṃyāyati">anusaṃyāyati</a></p>

anusaññāyatuṃ|anusa~n~naayatu.m|anusannayatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa~n~naayatu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaññāyati">anusaññāyati</a></p>

anusaṃsāvetvā|anusa.msaavetvaa|anusamsavetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.msaavetvaa</span></p><p>having attended; having escorted.</p>

anusaṃvacchara|anusa.mvacchara|anusamvacchara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.mvacchara</span></p><p>adjective yearly Dhp-a.i.388 (nakkhattaṃ). Usually nt. <em>-ṃ</em> as adv. yearly, every year Ja.i.68; Ja.v.99. On use of anu in this comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> A a.</p><p>anu + saṃv˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anu">anu</a></p>

anusaṃvaccharaṃ|anusa.mvacchara.m|anusamvaccharam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.mvacchara.m</span></p><p>yearly; every year.</p>

anusaṃyāyamāna|anusa.myaayamaana|anusamyayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.myaayamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaṃyāyati">anusaṃyāyati</a></p>

anusaṃyāyati|anusa.myaayati|anusamyayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.myaayati</span></p><p>goes through, travels about; inspects, supervises; goes along with, escorts</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaññāyati">anusaññāyati</a></p>

anusaṃyāyati|anusa.myaayati|anusamyayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.myaayati</span></p><p>to traverse; to go up to, surround, visit (acc.) MN.i.209 (Bhagavantaṃ ˚itvā) Ja.iv.214 (variant reading anuyāyitvā). See also <em><a href="bword://anuy%C4%81ti">anuyāti</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://anusa%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81ti">anusaññāti</a></em>.</p><p>anu + saṃ + yāyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāti">anuyāti</a>, <a href="bword://anusaññāti">anusaññāti</a></p>

anusaṃyāyitvā|anusa.myaayitvaa|anusamyayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.myaayitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaṃyāyati">anusaṃyāyati</a></p>

anusaṭa|anusa.ta|anusata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusa.ta</span></p><p>sprinkled with (-˚), bestrewn, scattered Vv.5#3 (paduma˚ magga = vippakiṇṇa Vv-a.36).</p><p>Sk. anusṛta, pp. of anu + <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

anuseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>clings to, is occupied with.</li><li>lies latent; inheres; recurs persistently.</li></ol>

anuseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to “lie down with”, i.e.</p><ol><li>trs. to dwell on, harp on (an idea) SN.ii.65; SN.iii.36; SN.iv.208.</li><li>(of the idea) to obsess, to fill the mind persistently, to lie dormant &amp; be continually cropping up. MN.i.40, MN.i.108, MN.i.433; SN.ii.54 (so read with SS for <em>anusavanti</em>), SN.iv.188; AN.i.283; AN.iii.246 Pp.32, Pp.48</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anusayita">anusayita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + seti. cp. Sk. anuśayate or˚ śete, from <em>śī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusayita">anusayita</a></p>

anuseṭṭhi|anuse.t.thi|anusetthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuse.t.thi</span></p><ol><li>an under-seṭṭhi (banker, merchant) Ja.v.384 (see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> B iii. a.).</li><li>in redupl. cpd <em>seṭṭhānuseṭṭhi</em> (see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> B iv) “bankers &amp; lesser bankers” i.e. all kinds of well-to-do families Ja.vi.331.</li></ol><p>anu + seṭṭhi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anu">anu</a>, <a href="bword://anu">anu</a></p>

anusibbati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to interweave Vin.iii.336 (introd. to Sam. Pās.).</p><p>anu + sibbati, <em>siv</em> to sew</p>

anusikkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusikkhat">anusikkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusikkhati">anusikkhati</a></p>

anusikkhamāna|anusikkhamaana|anusikkhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusikkhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusikkhati">anusikkhati</a></p>

anusikkhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusikkhati">anusikkhati</a></p>

anusikkhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusikkhat">anusikkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusikkhati">anusikkhati</a></p>

anusikkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>learns, practises, studies; esp. practises (something, acc.) in imitation of (somebody, gen.), follows someone’s example in; follows, imitates (someone, usually gen., occasionally acc.).</p>

anusikkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to learn of somebody (gen.); to follow one’s example, to imitate Vin.ii.201 (ppr. med. ˚amāna); SN.i.235; AN.iv.282, AN.iv.286, AN.iv.323; Snp.294 (vattaṃ, cp. RV iii.59, 2: vratena śikṣati), Snp.934; Ja.i.89; Ja.ii.98; Ja.iii.315; Ja.v.334; Ja.vi.62; Thag.963; Mil.61<br />caus <em>anusikkhāpeti</em> to teach [= Sk anuśikṣayati] Mil.352.</p><p>Vedic anuśikṣati; anu + Desid. of <em>śak</em></p>

anusikkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusikkhin">anusikkhin</a></p><p>(from anusikkhati) studying, learning; following, imitating.</p>

anusikkhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusikkhin">anusikkhin</a></p><p>(from anusikkhati) studying, learning; following, imitating.</p>

anusikkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective studying, learning MN.i.100; Dhp.226 (ahoratta˚ = divā ca rattiñ ca tisso sikkhā sikkhamāna Dhp-a.iii.324).</p><p>fr. anusikhati</p>

anusiṭṭha|anusi.t.tha|anusittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusi.t.tha</span></p><p>instructed, taught, advised</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusiṭṭha|anusi.t.tha|anusittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusi.t.tha</span></p><p>instructed, admonished, advised; ordered, commanded MN.ii.96; Ja.i.226; Pv.ii.8#11; Mil.284, Mil.349.</p><p>Vedic anuśiṣṭa, pp. of anusāsati</p>

anusiṭṭhi|anusi.t.thi|anusitthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusi.t.thi</span></p><p>instruction; admonition; ordering.</p>

anusocana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>mourning; sorrow.</p>

anusocana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>bewailing, mourning Pv-a.65.</p><p>abstr. fr. anusocati</p>

anusocati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>bewails, mourns over, regrets.</p>

anusocati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to mourn for, to bewail Snp.851 (atītaṃ na a.; cp. Mnd.222); Pv.i.12#7; Pv.ii.6#8; Pv-a.95.</p><p>anu + socati</p>

anusota
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Anusota˚</p><p>in <em><a href="bword://anusota%E1%B9%83">anusotaṃ</a></em> (adv.) along the stream or current down-stream AN.ii.12; Ja.i.70 (opp. <em><a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADisota%E1%B9%83">paṭisotaṃ</a></em> against the stream); Pv-a.169 (Gangāya a. āgacchanto).</p><ul><li><em>-gāmin</em> “one who follows the stream”, i.e. giving way to ones inclinations, following ones will AN.ii.5 AN.ii.6 (opp paṭi˚) Snp.319 (= sotaṁ anugacchanto Snp-a.330) Pp.62</li></ul><p>anu + sota, in ˚ṃ as adv. or acc. to expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> under anu A a.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusotaṃ">anusotaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://paṭisotaṃ">paṭisotaṃ</a></p>

anusotagāmi|anusotagaami|anusotagami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusotagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusotagāmin">anusotagāmin</a></p><p>following the stream; going with the current</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusotaṃ">anusotaṃ</a></p>

anusotagāmin|anusotagaamin|anusotagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusotagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusotagāmin">anusotagāmin</a></p><p>following the stream; going with the current</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusotaṃ">anusotaṃ</a></p>

anusotapaṭisotaṃ|anusotapa.tisota.m|anusotapatisotam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusotapa.tisota.m</span></p><p>up and down-stream</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusotaṃ">anusotaṃ</a></p>

anusotaṃ|anusota.m|anusotam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusota.m</span></p><p>with the stream or current, downstream.</p>

anussada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without excesses, without obstrusiveness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussada">ussada</a></p>

anussada
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective <a href="bword://anussuta">anussuta</a>*; v.l K.B. anussada; Dhp-a.iv.165 explains with taṇhā-ussāvâbhāva vv.ll. ˚ussada˚); Iti.97 (vv.ll. anussata &amp; anussara).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussuta">anussuta</a></p>

anussadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not overflowing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussadaka">ussadaka</a></p>

anussara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussarat">anussarat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anussaramāna|anussaramaana|anussaramana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaramaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anussaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anussarat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussarat">anussarat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anussarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>anussarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. follows, pursues; conforms oneself to</p><p>anussarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. remembers, recollects, calls to mind; reflects upon.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusarati">anusarati</a></p>

anussarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to remember, recollect, have memory of (acc.), bear in mind; be aware of DN.ii.8, DN.ii.53, DN.ii.54 (jātito etc.); SN.iii.86 sq. (pubbenivāsaṃ), SN.v.67 (dhammaṃ a. anuvitakketi), 303 (kappasahassaṃ) AN.i.25, AN.i.164 (pubbenivāsaṃ), AN.i.207 (tathāgataṃ, dhammaṃ etc.); AN.iii.285 (id.), AN.iii.323 (nivāsaṃ), AN.iii.418; AN.v.34, AN.v.38, AN.v.132 199, 336 (kalyāṇamitte); Iti.82 (dhammaṃ), Iti.98 (pubbenivāsaṃ); Ja.i.167; Ja.ii.111; Dhp.364; Pv.i.5#9; Pp.60; Sdhp.580, Sdhp.587; DN-a.i.257; Kp-a.213; Dhp-a.ii.84; Dhp-a.iv.95; Pv-a.29, Pv-a.53, Pv-a.69, Pv-a.79, Pv-a.107<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anussarita">anussarita</a></em> (see <em><a href="bword://anussaritar">anussaritar</a></em>)-Caus <em>anussarāpeti</em> to remind someone, to call to mind Ja.ii.147.</p><p>Vedic anusmarati, anu + <em>smṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarita">anussarita</a>, <a href="bword://anussaritar">anussaritar</a></p>

anussaraṃ|anussara.m|anussaram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussara.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussarati">anussarati</a></p>

anussaraṇa|anussara.na|anussarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussara.na</span></p><p>remembrance; repeated recollection.</p>

anussaraṇa|anussara.na|anussarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussara.na</span></p><p>remembrance, memory, recollection Iti.107 (= anussati at id. p. SN.v.67) Pv-a.25, Pv-a.29.</p><p>abstr. to anussarati</p>

anussaraṇaka|anussara.naka|anussaranaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussara.naka</span></p><p>who remembers.</p>

anussarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.r</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussaritar">anussaritar</a></p><p>one who remembers or recollects.</p>

anussaritabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>will remember; will recollect.</p>

anussaritar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.r</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussaritar">anussaritar</a></p><p>one who remembers or recollects.</p>

anussaritar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who recollects or remembers SN.v.197, SN.v.225 (saritar +) AN.v.25, AN.v.28.</p><p>n. ag. to anussarita, pp. of anussarati</p>

anussati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>recollection, remembrance, calling to mind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusati">anusati</a></p>

anussati
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Anussati:</strong> ‘recollection’, reflection, meditation, contemplation. The six recollections often described in the Suttas e.g. A. VI, 10, 25; D. 33 are:</p><p>1: Recollection of the Buddha, <em>buddhānussati</em><br />2: Recollection of his Doctrine, The Dhamma, <em>dhammānussati</em><br />3: Recollection of his Sangha Community of Noble Disciples, <em>sanghānussati</em><br />4: Recollection of Morality, <em>sīlānussati</em><br />5: Recollection of Generosity, <em>cāgānussati</em><br />6: Recollection of divine beings, <em>devatānussati.</em></p><blockquote><p>1: The Noble Disciple, Mahānāma, recollects thus: Worthy, honourable and perfectly self-Enlightened is the <em>Buddha</em> ! Consummated in knowledge and behaviour, totally transcended, expert in all dimensions, knower of all worlds, unsurpassable trainer of those who can be tamed, both teacher and guide of gods as well as of humans, blessed, exalted, awakened and enlightened is the Buddha !!!</p><p>2: Perfectly formulated is this Buddha- <em>Dhamma</em>, visible right here and now, immediately effective, timeless, inviting each and everyone to come and see for themselves, inspect, examine and verify. Leading each and everyone through progress towards perfection. Directly observable, experiencable and realizable by each intelligence…</p><p>3: Perfectly training is this Noble <em>S</em> <em>angha</em> community of the Buddha’s disciples; the right way, the true way, the good way, the direct way! Therefore do these eight kinds of individuals, the four Noble pairs, deserve both gifts, self-sacrifice, offerings, hospitality and reverential salutation with joined palms, since this Noble Sangha community of the Buddha’s Noble Disciples, is an unsurpassable and forever unsurpassed field of merit, in the world, for the world, to honour, and give to…</p><p>4: The Noble Disciple further recollects his own morality <em>sīla,</em> which is unbroken, without any breach, unspotted, untarnished, conducive to liberation, praised by the wise, independent of craving &amp; opinion, leading to concentration.</p><p>5: The Noble Disciple further recollects his own generosity <em>cāga</em> thus: Blessed truly am I, highly blessed am I who, amongst beings defiled with the filth of stinginess, live with heart free from stinginess, liberal, open-handed, rejoicing in giving, ready to give anything asked for, glad to give and share with others.</p><p>6: The Noble Disciple further recollects the divine beings <em>devatā:</em> There are the divine beings of the retinue of the Four Great Kings, the divine beings of the World of the Thirty-Three, the Yāma-devas… and there are divine beings besides see: <em>deva</em>. Such faith, such morality, such knowledge, such generosity, such insight, possessed of which those divine beings, after vanishing from here, are reborn in those worlds, such things are also found in me. A. III,70; VI,10; XI,12.</p></blockquote><p>At the time when the Noble Disciple recollects the Perfect One… at such a time his mind is neither possessed of greed, nor of hate, nor of confusion. Quite upright at such a time is his mind owing to the Perfect One… With upright mind the Noble Disciple attains understanding of the meaning, understanding of the law of Dhamma, and attains to joy through the law of Dhamma. In the joyous one rapture arises. With heart enraptured, his whole being becomes stilled. Stilled within his being, he feels happiness; and the mind of the happy one becomes firm. Of this Noble Disciple it is said that amongst those gone astray, he walks on the right path, among those suffering he abides free from suffering. Thus having reached the stream of the law, he develops and expands this recollection of the Enlightened One. A. VI, 10.</p><p>In A. I, 21 PTS: I, xvi and A. I, 27 PTS: xx. 2 another 4 recollections are added:</p><p>7: Recollection of death <em>maranānussati,</em><br />8: Awareness of the body <em>kāyagatā</em> - <em>sati,</em><br />9: Awareness of breathing <em>ānāpāna-sati</em>,<br />10: Recollection of Peace <em>upasamānussati</em>.</p><p>The first six recollections are fully explained in Vis.M VII, the latter four in Vis.M VIII.</p>

anussati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>remembrance, recollection, thinking of, mindfulness. A late list of subjects to be kept in mind comprises <em>six</em> <em>anussati-ṭṭhānāni</em>, viz. <em>Buddha˚, Dhamma˚, Saṅgha˚ sīla˚, cāga˚, devatā˚</em>; i.e. proper attention to the Buddha the Doctrines, the Church, to morality, charity, the gods Thus at DN.iii.250, DN.iii.280 (cp. AN.i.211); AN.iii.284, AN.iii.312 sq. AN.iii.452; AN.v.329 sq.; Pts.i.28. Expanded to 10 subjects (the above plus <em>ānāpāna-sati, maraṇa-sati, kāyagatā-sati upasamānussati</em>) at AN.i.30, AN.i.42 (cp. Lal.34). For other references see DN.i.81; SN.v.67 = Iti.107 (anussaraṇa at latter pass.); AN.iii.284, AN.iii.325, AN.iii.452. Pts.i.48, Pts.i.95, Pts.i.186 Pp.25, Pp.60; Dhs.14, Dhs.23, Dhs.1350 (anussati here to be corr to asati, see <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> 351); Sdhp.225, 231, 482. See also anuttariya (anussat-ânuttariya).</p><p>Sk. anusmṛti, fr. anu + <em>smṛ</em>; cp. sati</p>

anussatiṭṭhāna|anussati.t.thaana|anussatitthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussati.t.thaana</span></p><p>subject of recollection</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussati">anussati</a></p>

anussatānuttariya|anussataanuttariya|anussatanuttariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussataanuttariya</span></p><p>the supremacy of recollection</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussati">anussati</a></p>

anussava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>what has been heard or repeated; hearsay; tradition.</p>

anussava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>hearsay, report, tradition MN.i.520; MN.ii.211; SN.ii.115; SN.iv.138; AN.i.26; Ja.i.158 (with ref. to part. <em><a href="bword://kira">kira</a></em> = annussav’atthe nipāto; so also at Vv-a.322, cf. anussavana); ii.396, 430 (id.); iv.441; instr. <em>-ena</em> from hearsay, by report AN.ii.191 (cf. itihītihaṃ).</p><p>anu + sava fr. <em>śru</em>, cp. Vedic śravas nt.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kira">kira</a></p>

anussavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= anussava Pv-a.103 (<em><a href="bword://kira">kira</a></em> -saddo anussavane, from hearsay).</p><p>anu + savana fr. <em>śru</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kira">kira</a></p>

anussavappasanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who believes from hearsay or report</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussava">anussava</a></p>

anussavasacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>depending on tradition for one’s truths; accepting tradition as true</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussava">anussava</a></p>

anussavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>flows in; flows upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusavati">anusavati</a></p>

anussavika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “belonging to hearsay”, traditional; one who is familiar with tradition or who learns from hearsay MN.i.520; MN.ii.211. Cp. anussutika.</p><p>fr. anussava</p>

anussaṅki|anussa"nki|anussanki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussa"nki</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussaṅkin">anussaṅkin</a></p><p>without apprehension, not fearful</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussaṅkin">ussaṅkin</a></p>

anussaṅkin|anussa"nkin|anussankin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussa"nkin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anussaṅkin">anussaṅkin</a></p><p>without apprehension, not fearful</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussaṅkin">ussaṅkin</a></p>

anussita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not exalted, humble</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussita">ussita</a></p>

anussuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not busy, quiet, unconcerned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussuka">ussuka</a></p>

anussuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from greed Dhp.199; cf. anussukin variant reading DN.iii.47, also anissukin and apalāsin.</p><p>an + ussuka</p>

anussukita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Vv-a.74 &amp; <em>anussukin</em> Pp.23 = <em><a href="bword://anussuka">anussuka</a></em>.</p><p>an + ussuk˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussuka">anussuka</a></p>

anussuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp.</i></p><p>free from lust or defilement.</p>

anussuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Anussuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective free from lust Dhp.400 (= ussāvâvena anussuta C.). See also <em><a href="bword://anussada">anussada</a></em>.</p><p>an + ussuta, ud + <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>Anussuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>heard of; only in cpd. <em>ananussuta</em> unheard of SN.ii.9; Pp.14.</p><p>anu + suta, pp. of <em>śru</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussada">anussada</a></p>

anussutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective according to tradition or report, one who goes by or learns from hearsay DN-a.i.106, DN-a.i.107.</p><p>fr. anu + <em>śru</em>, cp. anussavika</p>

anussuyyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://anusuyyaka">anusuyyaka</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusuyyaka">anusuyyaka</a></p>

anussādetvā|anussaadetvaa|anussadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol. neg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a></p>

anussāha|anussaaha|anussaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaaha</span></p><p>want of exertion or determination</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāha">ussāha</a></p>

anussāvaka|anussaavaka|anussavaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavaka</span></p><p>one who proclaims (the kammavācā); one who presents for upasampadā.</p>

anussāvaka|anussaavaka|anussavaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavaka</span></p><p>one who proclaims or announces, a speaker (of a kammavācā) Vin.i.74.</p><p>fr. anussāveti</p>

anussāvana|anussaavana|anussavana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavana</span></p><p>-ā, feminine</p><ol><li>causing to be heard, sounding; proclamation (esp. of a kammavācā).</li><li>canvassing, speaking repeatedly (in order to win support)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāvana">anusāvana</a></p>

anussāvana|anussaavana|anussavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavana</span></p><p><em>-ā</em> (f.) a proclamation Vin.i.317, Vin.i.340; Vin.v.170, Vin.v.186, Vin.v.202 sq.</p><p>fr. anussāveti</p>

anussāvayati|anussaavayati|anussavayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavayati</span></p><p>causes to be heard; speaks out; proclaims (esp. a kammavācā); presents (someone) for upasampadā; speaks repeatedly (in order to win support)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a>, <a href="bword://anusāveti">anusāveti</a></p>

anussāvesi|anussaavesi|anussavesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a></p>

anussāvesuṃ|anussaavesu.m|anussavesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a></p>

anussāveti|anussaaveti|anussaveti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaaveti</span></p><p>causes to be heard; speaks out; proclaims (esp. a kammavācā); presents (someone) for upasampadā; speaks repeatedly (in order to win support)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāveti">anusāveti</a>, <a href="bword://anussāvayati">anussāvayati</a></p>

anussāveti|anussaaveti|anussaveti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaaveti</span></p><p>to cause to be heard or sound; to proclaim utter, speak out Vin.i.103 (˚ssāviyamāna ppr. Pass.), Vin.ii.48 (saddaṃ a.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anuss%C4%81vita">anussāvita</a></em>.</p><p>anu + sāveti, Caus. of <em>śru</em>, cp. B.Sk. anuśrāvayati “to proclaim aloud the guilt of a criminal” Avs.i.102; Avs.ii.182</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāvita">anussāvita</a></p>

anussāvita|anussaavita|anussavita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavita</span></p><p>announced; proclaimed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a></p>

anussāvita|anussaavita|anussavita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaavita</span></p><p>proclaimed, announced Vin.i.103.</p><p>pp. of anussāveti</p>

anussāviyamāna|anussaaviyamaana|anussaviyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anussaaviyamaana</span></p><p>being proclaimed; being announced</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a></p>

anusumbhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to adorn, embellish, prepare Ja.vi.76.</p><p>anu + sumbhati (sobhati); <em>śubh</em> or (Vedic) <em>śumbh</em></p>

anusuyyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. not envious; free from jealousy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusūyaka">anusūyaka</a>, <a href="bword://usuyyaka">usuyyaka</a></p>

anusuyyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not envious, not jealous Snp.325 (= usuyyāvigamena a. Snp-a.332); Ja.ii.192 (variant reading anussuyyaka); Ja.v.112.</p><p>an + usuyyaka</p>

anusuyyaṃ|anusuyya.m|anusuyyam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusuyya.m</span></p><p>reading at Ja.iii.27, see <em><a href="bword://anasuyya%E1%B9%83">anasuyyaṃ</a></em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. anasūyaṃ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anasuyyaṃ">anasuyyaṃ</a></p>

anusuyyāyamāna|anusuyyaayamaana|anusuyyayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusuyyaayamaana</span></p><p>not envious, not discontent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusūyāyamāna">anusūyāyamāna</a></p>

anusuṇāti|anusu.naati|anusunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusu.naati</span></p><p>to hear; pret. <em><a href="bword://anassu%E1%B9%83">anassuṃ</a></em> [Sk. anvaśruvaṃ] I heard MN.i.333.</p><p>anu + <em>śru</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anassuṃ">anassuṃ</a></p>

anusāra|anusaara|anusara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaara</span></p><p>“going along with”, following, conformity. Only in obl. eases (-˚) <strong>anusārena</strong> (instr. in consequence of, in accordance with, according to Ja.i.8; Pv-a.187 (tad), Pv-a.227; and <em>anusārato</em> (abl.) id. Sdhp.91.</p><p>fr. anu + <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: anusārena</p>

anusāreti|anusaareti|anusareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaareti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://anusarati">anusarati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusarati">anusarati</a></p>

anusārin|anusaarin|anusarin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaarin</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective following, striving after, acting in accordance with, living up to or after Freq. in formula <em>dhammānusārin saddhānusārin</em> living in conformity with the Norm &amp; the Faith DN.iii.254; MN.i.142, MN.i.479; SN.iii.225; SN.v.200 sq.; AN.i.74; AN.iv.10; Pp.15<br />■ Cp. also SN.i.15 (bhavasota˚); SN.iv.128 (id.); Ja.vi.444 (paṇḍitassa˚ = veyyāvaccakara C.); Sdhp.528 (attha˚).</p><p>fr. anu + sarati</p>

anusārī|anusaarii|anusari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaarii</span></p><p>fragrant substance.</p>

anusāsa|anusaasa|anusasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasa</span></p><p>(someone) for upasampadä; speaks repeatedly (in).</p>

anusāsaka|anusaasaka|anusasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasaka</span></p><p>order to win support).</p>

anusāsaka|anusaasaka|anusasaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasaka</span></p><p>adviser, instructor, counsellor Ja.ii.105; Mil.186, Mil.217, Mil.264. Cp. anusatthar.</p><p>fr. anusāsati</p>

anusāsana|anusaasana|anusasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasana</span></p><p>advice; instruction.</p>

anusāsana|anusaasana|anusasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasana</span></p><p>advice, instruction, admonition DN.iii.107; AN.i.292 (˚pāṭihāriya cp. anusāsanī); Mil.359.</p><p>Vedic anuśāsana, fr. anu + <em>śās</em></p>

anusāsanī|anusaasanii|anusasani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasanii</span></p><p>advice; instruction; admonition.</p>

anusāsanī|anusaasanii|anusasani
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasanii</span></p><p>instruction, teaching, commandment, order SN.v.108; AN.ii.147; AN.iii.87 AN.v.24 sq., AN.v.49, AN.v.338; Ja.v.113; Thig.172, Thig.180; Pv.iii.7#6 Thag-a.162; Vv-a.19, Vv-a.80, Vv-a.81.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81%E1%B9%ADih%C4%81riya">pāṭihāriya</a></em> (anusāsani˚) the miracle of teaching, the wonder worked by the commandments (of the Buddha Vin.ii.200; DN.i.212, DN.i.214; DN.iii.220; AN.i.170; AN.v.327; Ja.iii.323; Pts.ii.227 sq.</li></ul><p>fr. anusāsati, cp. anusāsana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pāṭihāriya">pāṭihāriya</a></p>

anusāsanīpāṭiharīya|anusaasaniipaa.tihariiya|anusasanipatihariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasaniipaa.tihariiya</span></p><p>the miracle of teaching (one of the three marvels of the Buddha).</p>

anusāsanīya|anusaasaniiya|anusasaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasaniiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsat|anusaasat|anusasat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anusāsat">anusāsat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsati|anusaasati|anusasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasati</span></p><p>teaches, instructs; advises, exhorts; guides, leads (a group); governs, administers.</p>

anusāsati|anusaasati|anusasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasati</span></p><ol><li>to advise, admonish, instruct in or give advice upon (c. acc.) to exhort to Vin.i.83; DN.i.135; DN.ii.154; Dhp.77, Dhp.159 (aññaṃ); Ja.vi.368; Cp.i.10#3; Pv.ii.6#8; Pv-a.148<br />grd <em>anusāsiya</em> Vin.i.59; and <em>-sāsitabba</em> Dhp-a.iii.99<br />pass <em>-sāsiyati</em> Vin.ii.200; Mil.186.</li><li>to rule, govern (acc.) administer to (dat.) SN.i.236 = Snp.1002 (paṭhaviṃ dhammenam-anusāsati, of a Cakkavattin); Ja.ii.2; Ja.vi.517 (rajjassa rajjaṃ C., i.e. take care of) DN-a.i.246 (read ˚sāsantena) Pv-a.161 (rajjaṃ)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anusi%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">anusiṭṭha</a></em> (q.v.); cp. anusatthar anusatthi &amp; ovadati.</p><p>Vedic anuśāsati, anu + <em>sās</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusiṭṭha">anusiṭṭha</a></p>

anusāsayiṃ|anusaasayi.m|anusasayim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasayi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsi|anusaasi|anusasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsikā|anusaasikaa|anusasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasikaa</span></p><p>who advises; an instructor, adviser; a preceptor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsaka">anusāsaka</a></p>

anusāsita|anusaasita|anusasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsitabba|anusaasitabba|anusasitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsitvā|anusaasitvaa|anusasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsiya|anusaasiya|anusasiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāsiyamāna|anusaasiyamaana|anusasiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaasiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusāsati">anusāsati</a></p>

anusāvana|anusaavana|anusavana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaavana</span></p><p>-ā, feminine</p><ol><li>causing to be heard, sounding; proclamation (esp. of a kammavācā).</li><li>canvassing, speaking repeatedly (in order to win support)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāvana">anussāvana</a></p>

anusāveti|anusaaveti|anusaveti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaaveti</span></p><p>causes to be heard; speaks out; proclaims (esp. a kammavācā); presents (someone) for upasampadā; speaks repeatedly (in order to win support)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anussāveti">anussāveti</a>, <a href="bword://anussāvayati">anussāvayati</a></p>

anusāyika|anusaayika|anusayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaayika</span></p><p>inherent, persistent.</p>

anusāyika|anusaayika|anusayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusaayika</span></p><p>adjective attached to one, i.e. inherent, chronic (of disease) MN.ii.70 (ābādha, variant reading BB anussāyika) Dhp-a.i.431 (roga).</p><p>fr. anusaya</p>

anusūyaka|anusuuyaka|anusuyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusuuyaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. not envious; free from jealousy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusuyyaka">anusuyyaka</a>, <a href="bword://usūyaka">usūyaka</a></p>

anusūyāyamāna|anusuuyaayamaana|anusuyayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anusuuyaayamaana</span></p><p>not envious, not discontent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusuyyāyamāna">anusuyyāyamāna</a></p>

anutappa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>to be regretted; to be sorrowed over</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutappati">anutappati</a></p>

anutappati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>vexes; causes remorse (afterwards).</li><li>, suffers remorse; regrets, repents (afterwards); sorrows over.</li></ol>

anutappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be sorry for, to regret, repent, feel remorse Ja.i.113; Ja.iv.358; Ja.v.492 (ppr. an-anutappaṃ); Dhp.67, Dhp.314; Pv.ii.9#42; Dhp-a.ii.40. grd. <em>anutappa</em> to be regretted AN.i.22, AN.i.77; AN.iii.294, and <em><a href="bword://anut%C4%81piya">anutāpiya</a></em> AN.iii.46 (an˚).</p><p>anu + tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; Sk. anutapyate, Pass. of anutapati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutāpiya">anutāpiya</a></p>

anutappissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutappati">anutappati</a></p>

anuthera
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an inferior Thera, one who comes next to the elder Vin.ii.212 (<em>therānutherā</em> Th. &amp; next in age).</p><p>anu + thera</p>

anutiṭṭha|anuti.t.tha|anutittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuti.t.tha</span></p><p>practiced, effected, done.</p>

anutiṭṭhati|anuti.t.thati|anutitthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuti.t.thati</span></p><p>to look after, to manage, carry on Ja.v.113 (= anugacchati) Pv-a.78.</p><p>anu + tiṭṭhati see also <em><a href="bword://anu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhahati">anuṭṭhahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuṭṭhahati">anuṭṭhahati</a></p>

anutrasta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://utrasta">utrasta</a></p>

anutrāsa|anutraasa|anutrasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutraasa</span></p><p>free from fear; unafraid</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://utrāsa">utrāsa</a></p>

anutrāsin|anutraasin|anutrasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutraasin</span></p><p>adjective not terrified, at ease Thag.864.</p><p>an + utrāsin</p>

anuttara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having no superior; supreme, best</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttara">uttara</a></p>

anuttara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “nothing higher”, without a superior, incomparable, second to none, unsurpassed, excellent, preeminent Snp.234 (= adhikassa kassaci abhāvato Kp-a.193), 1003; Dhp.23, Dhp.55 (= asadisa appaṭibhāga Dhp-a.i.423); Pv.iv.3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5 2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (dhamma); Dhs.1294; DN-a.i.129; Pv-a.1, Pv-a.5, Pv-a.6, Pv-a.18, etc.</p><p>an + uttara</p>

anuttariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>incomparable, excellent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānuttariya">ānuttariya</a></p>

anuttariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>preeminence, superiority, excellency; highest ideal, greatest good. They are mentioned as sets of 3 (viz. <em>dassana˚, paṭipadā˚, vimutti˚</em>;) at DN.iii.219, or of 6 (viz. <em>dassana˚, savana˚ lābha˚, sikkhā˚, pāricariyā˚, anussata˚</em>;) at DN.iii.250 DN.iii.281; AN.i.22; AN.iii.284, AN.iii.325 sq., AN.iii.452; Pts.i.5. Cp. MN.i.235; AN.v.37. See also ānuttariya.</p><p>abstr. fr. anuttara</p>

anutthavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>praises; speaks of repeatedly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a></p>

anutthuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anutthunat">anutthunat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a></p>

anutthunanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>anutthunanta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>part. pr. mfn.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a></p>

anutthunat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anutthunat">anutthunat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a></p>

anutthunā|anutthunaa|anutthuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutthunaa</span></p><p>wailing, crying, lamenting Mnd.167 (= vācāpalāpa vippalāpa etc.).</p><p>fr. anutthunāti</p>

anutthunāti|anutthunaati|anutthunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutthunaati</span></p><p>anutthunāti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. wails; deplores; laments (for)</p><p>anutthunāti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. praises; speaks of repeatedly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthuṇāti">anutthuṇāti</a>, <a href="bword://anutthuṇāti">anutthuṇāti</a></p>

anutthunāti|anutthunaati|anutthunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutthunaati</span></p><p>to wail, moan, deplore, lament, bewail DN.iii.86; Snp.827 (cp. Mnd.167); Dhp.156; Ja.iii.115; Ja.v.346, Ja.v.479; Dhp-a.iii.133; Pv-a.60 (wrongly applied for ghāyati, of the fire of conscience).</p><p>anu + thunati (thunāti); anu + <em>stan</em></p>

anutthuṇāti|anutthu.naati|anutthunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutthu.naati</span></p><p>anutthuṇāti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. wails; deplores; laments (for)</p><p>anutthuṇāti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. praises; speaks of repeatedly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a>, <a href="bword://anutthunāti">anutthunāti</a></p>

anuttiṇṇa|anutti.n.na|anuttinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutti.n.na</span></p><p>who has not crossed over; not come out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttiṇṇa">uttiṇṇa</a></p>

anuttāna|anuttaana|anuttana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuttaana</span></p><p>adjective not (lying) open, not exposed; fig. unexplained, unclear Ja.vi.247.</p><p>an + uttāna</p>

anuttānīkata|anuttaaniikata|anuttanikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuttaaniikata</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttānīkata">uttānīkata</a></p>

anutunī|anutunii|anutuni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutunii</span></p><p>not menstruating; not in the fertile period</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://utunī">utunī</a></p>

anutāpa|anutaapa|anutapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaapa</span></p><p>anguish, remorse, conscience Vv.40#5 (= vippaṭisāra Vv-a.180); Dhs-a.384.</p><p>fr. anu + <em>tāpa</em></p>

anutāpi|anutaapi|anutapi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaapi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anutāpin">anutāpin</a></p><p>repenting; regretting.</p>

anutāpin|anutaapin|anutapin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaapin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anutāpin">anutāpin</a></p><p>repenting; regretting.</p>

anutāpin|anutaapin|anutapin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaapin</span></p><p>adjective repenting, regretting Thig.57, Thig.190; Vv.21; Vv-a.115.</p><p>fr. anutāpa</p>

anutāpiya|anutaapiya|anutapiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaapiya</span></p><p>grd. of <em><a href="bword://anutappati">anutappati</a></em>, q.v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anutappati">anutappati</a></p>

anutāḷeti|anutaa.leti|anutaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutaa.leti</span></p><p>to beat Ja.ii.280.</p><p>anu + taḷeti</p>

anutīre|anutiire|anutire
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutiire</span></p><p>alongside or near the bank (of a river)</p>

anutīre|anutiire|anutire
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anutiire</span></p><p>adverb along side or near the bank (of a river) Snp.18 (= tīra-samīpe Snp-a.28) Cp. anu A b.</p><p>anu + tīre, loc. of tīra</p>

anuvadamāna|anuvadamaana|anuvadamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvadamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvadati">anuvadati</a></p>

anuvadanā|anuvadanaa|anuvadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvadanaa</span></p><p>approval; support.</p>

anuvadanā|anuvadanaa|anuvadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvadanaa</span></p><p>blaming, blame, censure Vin.ii.88 (anuvāda +).</p><p>fr. anuvadati</p>

anuvadati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>repeats the words of; approves; agrees with.</li><li>criticizes, censures.</li></ol>

anuvadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to blame, censure, reproach Vin.ii.80, Vin.ii.88<br />grd <em><a href="bword://anuvajja">anuvajja</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. ava˚; anu + vadati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvajja">anuvajja</a></p>

anuvajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>to be censured; a fault</p><p>See also: anudavita</p>

anuvajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective to be blamed, censurable, worthy of reproach Snp.p.78 (an˚ = anuvādavimutta Snp-a.396).</p><p>grd. of anu + vadati, cp. anuvāda &amp; Sk. avavadya</p>

anuvasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>inhabits; dwells; stays.</p>

anuvasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to live with somebody, to dwell, inhabit Ja.ii.421. Caus. <em>-vāseti</em> to pass, spend (time) Ja.vi.296<br />pp <em>-vuttha</em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + vasati</p>

anuvasitvā|anuvasitvaa|anuvasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvasati">anuvasati</a></p>

anuvassaṃ|anuvassa.m|anuvassam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvassa.m</span></p><p>annually; every rainy season.</p>

anuvassaṃ|anuvassa.m|anuvassam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvassa.m</span></p><p>adverb for one rainy season; every rainy season or year, i.e. annually C. on Thag.24.</p><p>anu + vassa</p>

anuvassika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>one who has kept one vassa residence; who has passed one rainy season.</p>

anuvassika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who has (just) passed one rainy season Thag.24 (“scarce have the rains gone by” Mrs. Rh. D.; see <em>trsl.</em> p. 29 n. 2).</p><p>fr. anuvassaṃ</p>

anuvattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. following, attending, faithful (to); agreeing with; a follower, partisan, adherent.</p>

anuvattaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>= <em><a href="bword://anupavattaka">anupavattaka</a></em> (q.v.) Thag.1014 (cakka˚).</li><li>following, siding with (-˚) Vin.iv.218 (ukkhittânuvattikā f.).</li></ol><p>fr. anuvatteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupavattaka">anupavattaka</a></p>

anuvattana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>complying with, conformity with (-˚), compliance, observance, obedience Ja.i.367 (dhamma˚); Ja.v.78.</p><p>abstr. fr. anuvattati</p>

anuvattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>follows, attends, sides with; conforms with, practices; imitates.</p>

anuvattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to follow, imitate, follow one’s example (c. acc.), to be obedient DN.ii.244; Vin.ii.309 (bdhgh.); Vin.iv.218; Ja.i.125, Ja.i.300; DN-a.i.288; Pv-a.19.</li><li>to practice, execute Pv.iv.7#12</li></ol><p>caus <em>-vatteti</em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. anuvartati, anu + vattati</p>

anuvattayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>causes to go on after, keeps in motion; maintains, continues</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvatteti">anuvatteti</a>, <a href="bword://anuvattati">anuvattati</a></p>

anuvatteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>causes to go on after, keeps in motion; maintains, continues</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvattayati">anuvattayati</a>, <a href="bword://anuvattati">anuvattati</a></p>

anuvatteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://anupavatteti">anupavatteti</a></em> (q.v.) Thag.826 (dhammacakkaṃ: “after his example turn the wheel Mrs. Rh. D.).</p><p>anu + vatteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupavatteti">anupavatteti</a></p>

anuvatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvattati">anuvattati</a></p>

anuvattin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective following, acting according to or in conformity with (-˚), obedient Ja.ii.348 (f. ˚inī), Ja.iii.319 (id.); Dhp.86 (dhamma˚); Vv.15#5 (vasa˚ = anukūlabhāvena vattana sīla Vv-a.71); Dhp-a.ii.161.</p><p>fr. anuvattati</p>

anuvattitabbal
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; impers.</i></p><p>to be followed; to be conformed to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvattati">anuvattati</a></p>

anuvattiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvattati">anuvattati</a></p>

anuvattāpana|anuvattaapana|anuvattapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvattaapana</span></p><p>making conform to.</p>

anuvedha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>further or second stab.</p>

anuvejja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective in an˚ see <em><a href="bword://anuvijjati">anuvijjati</a></em>. Anuvyanjana &amp; anubyanjana;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvijjati">anuvijjati</a></p>

anuvicaramāna|anuvicaramaana|anuvicaramana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaramaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>wanders about or through; explores.</li><li>thinks of, ponders.</li></ol>

anuvicarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to wander about, stroll roam through, explore DN.i.235; Ja.ii.128; Ja.iii.188; Pv-a.189 (= anupariyāti)<br />caus <em>-vicāreti</em> to think over (lit. to make one’s mind wander over), to meditate ponder (cp. anuvicinteti); always comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a></em> (q.v.) AN.i.264 (cetasā), AN.i.iii.178 (dhammaṃ cetasā a.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anuvicarita">anuvicarita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + vi + carati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a>, <a href="bword://anuvicarita">anuvicarita</a></p>

anuvicarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>gone through, explored; scrutinized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>reflected, pondered over, thought out SN.iii.203 (manasā); DN-a.i.106 (= <em><a href="bword://anucarita">anucarita</a></em>).</p><p>pp. of anuvicāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anucarita">anucarita</a></p>

anuvicarāpeti|anuvicaraapeti|anuvicarapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaraapeti</span></p><p>guides (someone) round</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>testing, examining; discerning, finding out.</p>

anuvicca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>having known or found out, knowing well or thoroughly, testing, finding out MN.i.301, MN.i.361 (variant reading <em>-vijja</em>); AN.ii.3, AN.ii.84; AN.v.88; Dhp.229 (= jānitvā Dhp-a.iii.329); Snp.530 (= anuviditvā Snp-a.431) Ja.i.459 (= jānitvā C.); Ja.iii.426; Pp.49.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> a thorough investigation, examination, test Vin.i.236 (here spelt <em>anuvijja</em>) = MN.i.379 (= ˚viditvā C.) AN.iv.185.</li></ul><p>ger. of anuvijjati, for the regular from <em>anuvijja</em> prob. through influence of anu + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em> (anu-v-icca for anvicca) cf. anveti &amp; adhicca; &amp; see <em><a href="bword://anuvijjati">anuvijjati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a>, <a href="bword://anuvijjati">anuvijjati</a></p>

anuviccakāra|anuviccakaara|anuviccakara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuviccakaara</span></p><p>considered action; action undertaken after scrutiny</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicca">anuvicca</a></p>

anuvicinaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who examines, an examiner Mil.365.</p><p>fr. anu + vicināti</p>

anuvicinanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicināti">anuvicināti</a></p>

anuvicintayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>thinks or ponders over; meditates upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicinteti">anuvicinteti</a></p>

anuvicinteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>thinks or ponders over; meditates upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicintayati">anuvicintayati</a></p>

anuvicinteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to think or ponder over, to meditate DN.ii.203; SN.i.203 (yoniso ˚cintaya, imper “marshall thy thoughts in ordered governance” Mrs. Rh. DN variant reading anucintaya); Thag.747; Dhp.364; Iti.82 (dhammaṃ ˚ayaṃ); Ja.iii.396; Ja.iv.227; Ja.v.223 (dhammaṃ ˚cintayanto).</p><p>anu + vi + cinteti</p>

anuvicintetvā|anuvicintetvaa|anuvicintetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicintetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicinteti">anuvicinteti</a></p>

anuvicināti|anuvicinaati|anuvicinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicinaati</span></p><p>investigates; examines.</p>

anuvicāra|anuvicaara|anuvicara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaara</span></p><p>meditation, reflexion, thought Dhs.85 (= <em><a href="bword://vic%C4%81ra">vicāra</a></em>).</p><p>anu + vicāra, cf. anuvicāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vicāra">vicāra</a></p>

anuvicāraya|anuvicaaraya|anuvicaraya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaaraya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvicārayat">anuvicārayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicārayati">anuvicārayati</a></p>

anuvicārayat|anuvicaarayat|anuvicarayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaarayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvicārayat">anuvicārayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicārayati">anuvicārayati</a></p>

anuvicārayati|anuvicaarayati|anuvicarayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaarayati</span></p><p>gone through, explored; scrutinized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicāreti">anuvicāreti</a>, <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicāreti|anuvicaareti|anuvicareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaareti</span></p><p>gone through, explored; scrutinized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicārayati">anuvicārayati</a>, <a href="bword://anuvicarati">anuvicarati</a></p>

anuvicāretvā|anuvicaaretvaa|anuvicaretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvicaaretvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvicāreti">anuvicāreti</a></p>

anuviddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective pierced, intertwined or set with (-˚) Vv-a.278.</p><p>pp. of anuvijjhati</p>

anuvidhiyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>acts in conformity with; follows the rules of, follows</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvidhīyati">anuvidhīyati</a></p>

anuvidhāyaka|anuvidhaayaka|anuvidhayaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhaayaka</span></p><p>acting in conformity with.</p>

anuvidhīyanta|anuvidhiiyanta|anuvidhiyanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhiiyanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvidhīyati">anuvidhīyati</a></p>

anuvidhīyanā|anuvidhiiyanaa|anuvidhiyana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhiiyanaa</span></p><p>acting in accordance with; acting in conformity.</p>

anuvidhīyanā|anuvidhiiyanaa|anuvidhiyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhiiyanaa</span></p><p>acting according to, conformity with MN.i.43.</p><p>abstr. fr. anuvidhīyati</p>

anuvidhīyati|anuvidhiiyati|anuvidhiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhiiyati</span></p><p>acts in conformity with; follows the rules of, follows</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvidhiyyati">anuvidhiyyati</a></p>

anuvidhīyati|anuvidhiiyati|anuvidhiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvidhiiyati</span></p><p>to act in conformity with, to follow (instruction) MN.ii.105 = Thag.875; SN.iv.199; Ja.ii.98; Ja.iii.357.</p><p>cf. Sk. anuvidhīyate &amp; adj. anuvidhāyin; Pass. of anu + vi + <em>dhā</em>, cf. vidahati</p>

anuvidita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>thoroughly understanding; well informed.</p>

anuvidita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>found out, recognised; one who has found out or knows well Snp.528, Snp.530 (= anubuddha Snp-a.431). Same in B.Sk., e.g. Mvu.iii.398.</p><p>pp. of anuvijjati</p>

anuvigaṇeti|anuviga.neti|anuviganeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuviga.neti</span></p><p>considers, regards; takes notice of, takes account of.</p>

anuvigaṇeti|anuviga.neti|anuviganeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuviga.neti</span></p><p>to take care of, regard, heed, consider Thag.109.</p><p>anu + vi + gaṇeti</p>

anuvijjaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an examiner; an adjucator.</p>

anuvijjaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who finds out, an examiner Vin.v.161.</p><p>fr. anuvijja, ger. of anuvijjati</p>

anuvijjamāna|anuvijjamaana|anuvijjamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvijjamaana</span></p><p>searching out, investigating; examining.</p>

anuvijjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to know thoroughly, to find out, to trace, to come to know; inf <em>-vijjitiṃ</em> Ja.iii.506; ger. <em>-viditvā</em> Snp-a.431, also <em>-vijja vicca;</em> (see both under anuvicca); grd. <em>ananuvejja</em> not to be known, unfathomable, unknowable MN.i.140 (Tathāgato ananuvejjo)<br />caus <em>anuvijjāpeti</em> to make some one find out Ja.v.162<br />pp <em><a href="bword://anuvidita">anuvidita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + vid, with fusion of Vedic vetti to know, and Pass. of vindati to find (= vidyate)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvidita">anuvidita</a></p>

anuvijjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to pierce or be pierced, to be struck or hurt with (instr.) Ja.vi.439</li><li>to be affected with, to fall into, to incur Dhp-a.iii.380 (aparādhaṃ)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anuviddha">anuviddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>anu + <em>vyadh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuviddha">anuviddha</a></p>

anuvijjiyamāna|anuvijjiyamaana|anuvijjiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvijjiyamaana</span></p><p>is searched out, investigated; is examined.</p>

anuvijjā|anuvijjaa|anuvijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvijjaa</span></p><p>examining.</p>

anuvikkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(further) dispersed, distracted.</p>

anuvikkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective dispersed over SN.v.277 sq. (+ anuvisaṭa).</p><p>anu + vi + khitta, pp. of anu + vikkhipati</p>

anuvilokaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvilokayat">anuvilokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvilokayati">anuvilokayati</a></p>

anuvilokayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvilokayat">anuvilokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvilokayati">anuvilokayati</a></p>

anuvilokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks round at; surveys</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuviloketi">anuviloketi</a></p>

anuvilokesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuviloketi">anuviloketi</a></p>

anuviloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuviloketi">anuviloketi</a></p>

anuviloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks round at; surveys</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvilokayati">anuvilokayati</a></p>

anuviloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to look round at, look over, survey, muster MN.i.339; Snp.p.140; Ja.i.53; Mil.7 (lakaṃ), Mil.21 (parisaṃ), Mil.230.</p><p>anu + vi + loketi; B.Sk. anuvilokayati</p>

anuvisaṭa|anuvisa.ta|anuvisata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvisa.ta</span></p><p>dispersed; spread; distracted.</p>

anuvisaṭa|anuvisa.ta|anuvisata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvisa.ta</span></p><p>(anu + visaṭa, pp. of anu + vi + <em>sṛ</em>; ] spread over SN.v.277 sq.; Ja.iv.102.</p>

anuvitakkaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvitakkayat">anuvitakkayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a></p>

anuvitakkayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvitakkayat">anuvitakkayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a></p>

anuvitakkenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a></p>

anuvitakketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>reflects upon, ponders over.</p>

anuvitakketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to reflect, think, ponder over, usually combined with <em>anuvicāreti</em> DN.i.119; DN.iii.242; SN.v.67 = Iti.107 (anussarati +); AN.iii.383.</p><p>anu + vi + takketi</p>

anuvitakketvā|anuvitakketvaa|anuvitakketva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvitakketvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvitakketi">anuvitakketi</a></p>

anuvivaṭṭa|anuviva.t.ta|anuvivatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuviva.t.ta</span></p><p>side-piece of a bhikkhu’s robe.</p>

anuvivaṭṭa|anuviva.t.ta|anuvivatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuviva.t.ta</span></p><p>an “after-evolution”, devolution; as part of a bhikkhu’s dress: a sub-vivaṭṭa (q.v.) Vin.i.287 (<em><a href="bword://viva%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADa">vivaṭṭa</a></em> +).</p><p>anu + vivaṭṭa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vivaṭṭa">vivaṭṭa</a></p>

anuvuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>living with, staying, dwelling Ja.ii.42 (cira˚); Ja.v.445 (id.).</p><p>pp. of anuvasati, cf. Sk. anūṣita</p>

anuvyañjana|anuvya~njana|anuvyanjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvya~njana</span></p><p>Anuvyañjana &amp; anubyañjana</p><p>(e.g. Vin.iv.15; Ja.i.12) neuter accompanying (i.e. secondary attribute, minor or inferior characteristic, supplementary or additional sign or mark (cf. mahāpurisa-lakkhaṇa) Vin.i.65 (abl. anuvyañjanaso “in detail”); MN.iii.126; SN.iv.168; AN.iv.279 (abl.); AN.v.73 sq.; Pp.24, Pp.58; Mil.339; Vv-a.315; Dhs-a.400.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81hin">gāhin</a></em> taking up or occupying oneself with details, taken up with lesser or inferior marks DN.i.70 (cf. Mvu.iii.52); DN.iii.225; SN.iv.104; AN.i.113; AN.ii.16, AN.ii.152 sq.; Dhs.1345 (cf. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 351).</li></ul><p>anu + vyañjana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāhin">gāhin</a></p>

anuvyañjanaggāhi|anuvya~njanaggaahi|anuvyanjanaggahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvya~njanaggaahi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvyañjanaggāhin">anuvyañjanaggāhin</a></p><p>occupying oneself with details or minor characteristics.</p>

anuvyañjanaggāhin|anuvya~njanaggaahin|anuvyanjanaggahin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvya~njanaggaahin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuvyañjanaggāhin">anuvyañjanaggāhin</a></p><p>occupying oneself with details or minor characteristics.</p>

anuvyañjanaso|anuvya~njanaso|anuvyanjanaso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvya~njanaso</span></p><ol><li>as to detail; in detail.</li><li>as to the letter, the accurate text (?)</li></ol>

anuvyañjanaṃ|anuvya~njana.m|anuvyanjanam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvya~njana.m</span></p><p>letter by letter.</p>

anuvāceti|anuvaaceti|anuvaceti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaaceti</span></p><p>recites after or again; teaches what has been taught.</p>

anuvāceti|anuvaaceti|anuvaceti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaaceti</span></p><p>to say after, to repeat (words), to recite or make recite after or again DN.i.104 (= tehi aññesaṃ vācitaṃ anuvācenti DN-a.i.273); Mil.345 Cp. anubhāseti.</p><p>anu + Caus. of <em>vac</em></p>

anuvāda|anuvaada|anuvada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaada</span></p><ol><li>repetition, further explanation; approval, support; faction-forming.</li><li>criticism; blame; sensure.</li></ol>

anuvāda|anuvaada|anuvada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaada</span></p><ol><li>blaming, censure, admonition Vin.ii.5 Vin.ii.32; AN.ii.121 (atta˚, para˚); Vb.376.</li><li>in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>vādānuvāda</em>: talk and lesser or additional talk, i.e. “small talk” (see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> B iv.) DN.i.161; MN.i.368.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://adhikara%E1%B9%87a">adhikaraṇa</a></em> a question or case of censure Vin.ii.88 sq.; Vin.iii.164 (one of the 4 adhikaraṇāni, q.v.).</li></ul><p>fr. anuvadatī, cp. Sk. anuvāda in meaning of “repetition”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anu">anu</a>, <a href="bword://adhikaraṇa">adhikaraṇa</a></p>

anuvādadhikaraṇa|anuvaadadhikara.na|anuvadadhikarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaadadhikara.na</span></p><p>legal question or case concerning support, or concerning a faction, or a legal question or case arising from censure or criticism</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuvāda">anuvāda</a></p>

anuvāsana|anuvaasana|anuvasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaasana</span></p><p>an oily enema, an injection Mil.353.</p><p>fr. anuvāseti</p>

anuvāseti|anuvaaseti|anuvaseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaaseti</span></p><p>to treat with fragrant oil, i.e. to make an injection or give an enema of salubrious oil Mil.169; grd. <em>-vāsanīya</em> ibid.; pp. <em>-vāsita</em> Mil.214.</p><p>anu + vāseti, Caus. of vāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> odour, perfume</p>

anuvāta|anuvaata|anuvata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaata</span></p><p>facing or border (on a bhikkhu’s robe)</p>

anuvāta|anuvaata|anuvata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaata</span></p><p>Anuvāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a forward wind, the wind that blows from behind, a favourable wind; <em>-ṃ</em> adv. with the wind, in the direction of the wind (opp. <em><a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADiv%C4%81ta%E1%B9%83">paṭivātaṃ</a></em>) AN.i.226 (˚paṭivātaṃ); Sdhp.425 (paṭivāta˚). In <em>anuvāte</em> (anu + vāte) at Ja.ii.382 “with the wind, facing the w. in front of the wind” anu is to be taken as prep. c. loc &amp; to be separated from vāte (see <em><a href="bword://anu">anu</a></em> A b.).</p><p>anu + <em><a href="bword://v%C4%81">vā</a></em> to blow</p><p>Anuvāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>only in connection with the making of the bhikkhus’ garments <em>(cīvara)</em> “weaving on, supplementary weaving, or along the seam”, i.e. hem, seam, binding Vin.i.254, Vin.i.297; Vin.ii.177; Vin.iv.121 (aggala +); Pv-a.73 (anuvāte appabhonte since the binding was insufficient).</p><p>anu + <em><a href="bword://v%C4%81">vā</a></em> to weave (?) in analogy to vāta from <em><a href="bword://v%C4%81">vā</a></em> to blow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paṭivātaṃ">paṭivātaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://anu">anu</a>, <a href="bword://vā">vā</a>, <a href="bword://vā">vā</a>, <a href="bword://vā">vā</a></p>

anuvātaṃ|anuvaata.m|anuvatam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuvaata.m</span></p><p>with the wind; downwind.</p>

anuyanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. mfn &amp; masculine</i></p><p>following; a follower, a vassal</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāti">anuyāti</a></p>

anuyanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at AN.v.22 is doubtful reading (variant reading <em><a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a></em>). The meaning is either “inferior to, dependent on, a subject of, a vassal” or “attending on”. The explanation may compare Sk. anuyātaṃ attendance, in which latter case anu-yantṛ would be “an inferior ruler” and P. yanta would represent the n. a.g. yantā as a-stem. The variant reading is perhaps preferable as long as other passages with anuyanta are not found (see <em><a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a></em> 2).</p><p>anu + <strong>yā</strong>, cp. anuyāyin or Sk. yantṛ ruler [ <em>yam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a>, <a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a>, yā</p>

anuyoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>application; devotion (to).</p>

anuyoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>application, devotion to (-˚), execution, practice of (-˚); often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with anuyutta in phrase <em>-anuyogaṃ anuyutta = practisiṅg</em> e.g. Vin.i.190 (maṇḍan’ ânuyogaṃ anuyutta); DN.iii.113 (attakilamath’ ânuyogaṃ a.); AN.ii.205 (attaparitāpan’ ânuyogaṃ a.)<br />■ As adj. (-˚) doing, given to, practising (cp. anuyutta). DN.i.5; DN.iii.107; MN.i.385; SN.i.182; SN.iii.239 SN.iv.330; SN.v.320; AN.i.14; AN.iii.249; AN.iv.460 sq.; AN.v.17 sq. AN.v.205; Ja.i.90 (padhān’ ânuyogakiccaṃ); Vv.84#38 (dhamma˚) Mil.348; DN-a.i.78, DN-a.i.104.</li><li>invitation, appeal, question (cp. anuyuñjati 2) Mil.10 (ācariyassa ˚ṃ datvā).</li></ol><p>Sk. anuyoga, fr. anu + <em>yuj</em></p>

anuyogabhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>fear of interrogation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyoga">anuyoga</a></p>

anuyogakkhama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>which can stand scrutiny; who is fit to be questioned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyoga">anuyoga</a></p>

anuyogavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective applying oneself to, full of application or zeal, devoted Pv-a.207.</p><p>anuyoga + vant</p>

anuyogavatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>procedure in examination</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyoga">anuyoga</a></p>

anuyogaṃ dadāti|anuyoga.m dadaati|anuyogam dadati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyoga.m dadaati</span></p><p>answers the question (satisfactorily), passes an examination</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyoga">anuyoga</a></p>

anuyogi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyogin">anuyogin</a></p><p>applying oneself to; devoted to.</p>

anuyogin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyogin">anuyogin</a></p><p>applying oneself to; devoted to.</p>

anuyogin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective applying oneself to, devoted to (-˚) Dhp.209 (atta˚ given to oneself, self-concentrated).</p><p>fr. anuyoga</p>

anuyutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.)applying oneself to, practicing; intent upon.</li><li>(mfn. and m.) following, attending on; subject to; a vassal or subordinate king</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjati">anuyuñjati</a></p>

anuyutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>applying oneself to, dealing with, practising, given to, intent upon DN.i.166, DN.i.167; DN.iii.232 = AN.ii.205 (attaparitāpan’ ânuyogaṃ a.); SN.iii.153 SN.iv.104; Snp.663 (lobhaguṇe), Snp.814 (methunaṃ = <em>samāyutta</em> Snp-a.536), 972 (jhān˚); Pp.55; Pv-a.163 (jāgariya˚) 206.</li><li>following, attending on; an attendant, inferior vassal, in expression <em><a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a></em> or <em>rājā anuyutta</em> a prince royal or a smaller king (see <em><a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a></em> 3 b) AN.v.22 (v l for T. anuyanta, q.v.); Snp.553 (= <em>anugāmin, sevaka</em> Snp-a.453).</li></ol><p>pp. of anuyuñjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a>, <a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a></p>

anuyuñja|anuyu~nja|anuyunja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~nja</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyuñjat">anuyuñjat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjati">anuyuñjati</a></p>

anuyuñjanā|anuyu~njanaa|anuyunjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njanaa</span></p><p>(&amp; <em>-yuñjana</em> nt.) application or devotion to (-˚) Mil.178; Vv-a.346 (anuyujjanaṃ wrong spelling?)</p><p>abstr. fr. anuyuñjati</p>

anuyuñjat|anuyu~njat|anuyunjat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyuñjat">anuyuñjat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjati">anuyuñjati</a></p>

anuyuñjati|anuyu~njati|anuyunjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njati</span></p><ol><li>practises, gives oneself up to.</li><li>questions, examines; calls to account</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjiyati">anuyuñjiyati</a></p>

anuyuñjati|anuyu~njati|anuyunjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njati</span></p><ol><li>to practice, give oneself up to (acc.), attend, pursue SN.i.25, SN.i.122 (˚yuñjan “in loving self-devotion” mrs. rh. D.); SN.iii.154; SN.iv.104, SN.iv.175; Dhp.26 (pamādaṃ = <em><a href="bword://pavatteti">pavatteti</a></em> Dhp-a.i.257), 247 (surāmeraya-pānaṃ = <em><a href="bword://sevati">sevati</a></em> bahulīkaroti Dhp-a.iii.356); Pv-a.61 (kammaṭṭhānaṃ).</li><li>to ask a question, to call to account take to task Vin.ii.79; Vv.33#5; ppr. Pass. <em>-yuñjiyamāna</em> Pv-a.192</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em>anuyojeti</em> “to put to”, to address, admonish, exhort Dhp-a.iv.20.</p><p>anu + yuñjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pavatteti">pavatteti</a>, <a href="bword://sevati">sevati</a>, <a href="bword://anuyutta">anuyutta</a></p>

anuyuñjisaṃ|anuyu~njisa.m|anuyunjisam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njisa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjiyati">anuyuñjiyati</a></p>

anuyuñjiyamāna|anuyu~njiyamaana|anuyunjiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njiyamaana</span></p><p>being questioned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjiyati">anuyuñjiyati</a></p>

anuyuñjiyati|anuyu~njiyati|anuyunjiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~njiyati</span></p><ol><li>practises, gives oneself up to.</li><li>questions, examines; calls to account</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjati">anuyuñjati</a></p>

anuyuñjiṃsu|anuyu~nji.msu|anuyunjimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyu~nji.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyuñjiyati">anuyuñjiyati</a></p>

anuyyutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uyyutta">uyyutta</a></p>

anuyāgi|anuyaagi|anuyagi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaagi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyāgin">anuyāgin</a></p><p>offering after the example of another.</p>

anuyāgin|anuyaagin|anuyagin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaagin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anuyāgin">anuyāgin</a></p><p>offering after the example of another.</p>

anuyāgin|anuyaagin|anuyagin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaagin</span></p><p>adjective offering after the example of another DN.i.142.</p><p>fr. anu + <em>yaj</em></p>

anuyāta|anuyaata|anuyata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaata</span></p><p>followed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāti">anuyāti</a></p>

anuyāta|anuyaata|anuyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaata</span></p><p>gone through or after, followed, pursued SN.ii.105 (magga); AN.v.236; Iti.29; Mil.217.</p><p>pp. of anuyāti</p>

anuyāti|anuyaati|anuyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaati</span></p><p>goes after, follows; goes along through</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāyati">anuyāyati</a></p>

anuyāti|anuyaati|anuyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaati</span></p><p>Anuyāti &amp; anuyāyati</p><ol><li>to go after, to follow Ja.vi.49 (fut. ˚yissati), Ja.vi.499 (yāyantaṃ anuyāyati = anugacchati C).</li><li>to go along by, to go over, to visit Mil.391 (˚yāyati)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anuy%C4%81ta">anuyāta</a></em> (q.v.). See also anusaṃyāyati</p><p>anu + <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāta">anuyāta</a>, yā</p>

anuyāyati|anuyaayati|anuyayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaayati</span></p><p>goes after, follows; goes along through</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāti">anuyāti</a></p>

anuyāyati|anuyaayati|anuyayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaayati</span></p><p>Anuyāti &amp; anuyāyati</p><ol><li>to go after, to follow Ja.vi.49 (fut. ˚yissati), Ja.vi.499 (yāyantaṃ anuyāyati = anugacchati C).</li><li>to go along by, to go over, to visit Mil.391 (˚yāyati)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://anuy%C4%81ta">anuyāta</a></em> (q.v.). See also anusaṃyāyati</p><p>anu + <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuyāta">anuyāta</a>, yā</p>

anuyāyin|anuyaayin|anuyayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuyaayin</span></p><p>adjective going after, following, subject to (gen.) Snp.1017 (anânuyāyin); Ja.vi.309; Mil.284.</p><p>cp. Sk. anuyāyin, anu + <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: yā</p>

