<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavattidhamma</span></p><p>not subject to return (to this world); not liable to turn back or change.</p>

anāvattin|anaavattin|anavattin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavattin</span></p><p>adjective noun one who does not return, almost syn. with anāgāmin in phrase <em>anāvatti-dhamma</em> one who is not destined to shift or return from one birth to another, DN.i.156 (cp. DN-a.i.313); DN-a.iii.132; Pp.16 sq., Pp.62.</p><p>an + āvattin</p>

anāvayha|anaavayha|anavayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavayha</span></p><p>not to be take in marriage (as a wife)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvahati">āvahati</a></p>

anāvaṭa|anaava.ta|anavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaava.ta</span></p><p>not obstructed; not shut off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭa">āvaṭa</a></p>

anāvaṭa|anaava.ta|anavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaava.ta</span></p><p>˚- not shut; in <em>-dvāratā</em> (f.) not closing the door againṡt another, accessibility, openhand edness DN.iii.191.</p><p>an + āvaṭa</p>

anāvaṭṭi|anaava.t.ti|anavatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaava.t.ti</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anāvaṭṭin">anāvaṭṭin</a></p><p>not turning to; not seduced, unaffected.</p>

anāvaṭṭin|anaava.t.tin|anavattin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaava.t.tin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anāvaṭṭin">anāvaṭṭin</a></p><p>not turning to; not seduced, unaffected.</p>

anāvikata|anaavikata|anavikata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavikata</span></p><p>etc. see <em>āvikata</em>.</p>

anāvikatvā|anaavikatvaa|anavikatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavikatvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikatvā">āvikatvā</a></p>

anāvila|anaavila|anavila
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavila</span></p><p>clear; pure; undisturbed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvila">āvila</a></p>

anāvila|anaavila|anavila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavila</span></p><p>adjective undisturbed, unstained, clean, pure DN.i.84 (= nikkaddama DN-a.i.226); DN-a.iii.269, 270; Snp.637 (= nikkilesa Snp-a.469 = Dhp-a.iv.192); Thig.369 (āvilacitta +); Dhp.82, Dhp.413; Thag-a.251; Sdhp.479.</p><p>an + āvila</p>

anāvuta|anaavuta|anavuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavuta</span></p><p>not obstructed; not shut off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuta">āvuta</a></p>

anāvuttha|anaavuttha|anavuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavuttha</span></p><p>adjective not dwelt in DN.ii.50.</p><p>an + āvuttha, pp. of āvasati</p>

anāvāsa|anaavaasa|anavasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaavaasa</span></p><p>adjective noun uninhabited, an uninhabited place Vin.ii.22, Vin.ii.33; Ja.ii.77.</p><p>an + āvāsa</p>

anāyasa|anaayasa|anayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayasa</span></p><p>adjective void of means, unlucky, unfortunate Vv.84#5 (= natthi ettha āyo sukhan ti anāyasaṃ Vv-a.335).</p><p>an + āya + sa, or should we read anāyāsa?</p>

anāyatana|anaayatana|anayatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayatana</span></p><p>nonexertion, not exerting oneself, sluggishness, indolence Ja.v.121 (˚sīla = dussīla C.).</p><p>an + āyatana</p>

anāyatanaso|anaayatanaso|anayatanaso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayatanaso</span></p><p>in the wrong sphere or place; unsuitably</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyatanaso">āyatanaso</a></p>

anāyussa|anaayussa|anayussa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayussa</span></p><p>not giving long life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyussa">āyussa</a></p>

anāyāsa|anaayaasa|anayasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayaasa</span></p><p>free from trouble; without distress</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyāsa">āyāsa</a></p>

anāyāsa|anaayaasa|anayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayaasa</span></p><p>adjective free from trouble or sorrow, peaceful Thag.1008.</p><p>an + āyāsa</p>

anāyūha|anaayuuha|anayuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayuuha</span></p><p>without thrusting, without struggling</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyūha">āyūha</a></p>

anāyūhat|anaayuuhat|anayuhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaayuuhat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anāyūhat">anāyūhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyūhati">āyūhati</a></p>

anāññāta|anaa~n~naata|anannata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaa~n~naata</span></p><p>not understood, not known</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāta">āññāta</a></p>

anāḷhika|anaa.lhika|analhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaa.lhika</span></p><p>not rich; poor; destitute.</p>

anāḷhika|anaa.lhika|analhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaa.lhika</span></p><p>Anāḷhiya &amp; Anāḷhika</p><p>adjective not rich, poor, miserable, destitute, usually comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with daḷidda MN.i.450; MN.ii.178 (variant reading BB. anāḷiya) AN.iii.352 sq. (vv.ll. BB. anāḷhika), 384; Ja.v.96.</p><p>an + ālhiya, Sk. āḍhya, see also *<a href="bword://addha">addha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://addha">addha</a></p>

anāḷhiya|anaa.lhiya|analhiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaa.lhiya</span></p><p>not rich; poor; destitute.</p>

anāḷhiya|anaa.lhiya|analhiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anaa.lhiya</span></p><p>Anāḷhiya &amp; Anāḷhika</p><p>adjective not rich, poor, miserable, destitute, usually comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with daḷidda MN.i.450; MN.ii.178 (variant reading BB. anāḷiya) AN.iii.352 sq. (vv.ll. BB. anāḷhika), 384; Ja.v.96.</p><p>an + ālhiya, Sk. āḍhya, see also *<a href="bword://addha">addha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://addha">addha</a></p>

anīgha|aniigha|anigha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniigha</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha; occurs in a list of Pacceka Buddhas. MN.iii.70</p>

anīgha|aniigha|anigha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniigha</span></p><p>free from affliction, from misery; free from trouble.</p>

anīgha|aniigha|anigha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniigha</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://nigha">nigha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> and cp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%ABgha">īgha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nigha">nigha</a>, <a href="bword://īgha">īgha</a></p>

anīka|aniika|anika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m.)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniika</span></p><p>an army; troops.</p>

anīka|aniika|anika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniika</span></p><p>army, array, troops (orig. “front”, i.e. of the battle-array) Vin.iv.107 (where expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in detail); Snp.623 (bala˚ strong in arms, with strong array i.e. of khanti which precedes; cp. Snp-a.467).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> a splendid army Snp.421 (= balakāya senāmukha Snp-a.384).</li><li><em>-ṭṭha</em> a sentinel, royal guard DN.iii.64, DN.iii.148; Ja.v.100; Ja.vi.15 (“men on horseback”, horseguard); Mil.234, Mil.264.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassana">dassana</a></em> troop-inspection DN.i.6 (aṇīka˚ at DN-a.i.85, q.v. interpretation); Vin.iv.107 (senābyūha +).</li></ul><p>Ved. anīka face, front, army to Idg. <em>*ogu̯</em> (see), cp. Gr. ο ̓́μμα eye, Lat. oculus, see also Sk. pratīka and P. akkhi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://dassana">dassana</a></p>

anīkadassana|aniikadassana|anikadassana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniikadassana</span></p><p>troop inspection, a review of soldiers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīka">anīka</a></p>

anīkagga|aniikagga|anikagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniikagga</span></p><p>the front, van of an army</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīka">anīka</a></p>

anīkaṭṭha|aniika.t.tha|anikattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniika.t.tha</span></p><p>soldier, esp. a royal guard or bodyguard</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīka">anīka</a></p>

anīti|aniiti|aniti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniiti</span></p><p>freedom from calamity; health, safety</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īti">īti</a></p>

anīti|aniiti|aniti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniiti</span></p><p>safety, soundness, sound condition, health AN.iv.238; Mil.323 (abl. ˚ito).</p><p>an + īti</p>

anītiha|aniitiha|anitiha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniitiha</span></p><p>not received through tradition; not based on hearsay.</p>

anītiha|aniitiha|anitiha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniitiha</span></p><p>adjective not such and such, not based on hearsay (itiha), not guesswork or (mere) talk AN.ii.26; Thag.331 (cp. MN.i.520); Snp.1053 (= Cnd.49, Cnd.151); Ja.i.456; Ne.166 (cp. Iti.28).</p><p>an + ītīha, the latter a cpd. der. fr. iti + ha = saying so and so, cp. itihāsa &amp; itihītihaṃ</p>

anītika|aniitika|anitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniitika</span></p><p>free from calamity or distress.</p>

anītika|aniitika|anitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aniitika</span></p><p>adjective free from injury or harm, healthy, secure Vin.ii.79 = Vin.ii.124 (+ anupaddava); Vin.iii.162; SN.iv.371; Snp.1137 (ītī vuccanti kilesā etc. Cnd.48); Mil.304.</p><p>fr. anīti</p>

anīḷaka|anii.laka|anilaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anii.laka</span></p><p>(of honey) pure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anelaka">anelaka</a>, <a href="bword://aniḷaka">aniḷaka</a>, <a href="bword://aneḷaka">aneḷaka</a></p>

anūdaka|anuudaka|anudaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuudaka</span></p><p>without water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anudaka">anudaka</a>, <a href="bword://anodaka">anodaka</a>, <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

anūhata|anuuhata|anuhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuuhata</span></p><p>not removed; not extracted, not rooted out.</p>

anūhata|anuuhata|anuhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuuhata</span></p><p>adjective not rooted out, not removed or destroyed Thag.223 = Cnd.97#4; Dhp.338 (= asamucchinna Dhp-a.iv.48).</p><p>pp. of an + ūhaññati, ud + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

anūna FIXME double|anuuna FIXME double|anuna FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuuna FIXME double</span></p><p>Anūnaka = anūna</p><p>Dpvs.iv.34.</p>

anūna|anuuna|anuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuuna</span></p><p>not less; not deficient; complete; full</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a></p>

anūna|anuuna|anuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuuna</span></p><p>adjective not lacking, entire, complete, without deficiency Ja.vi.273; Dpvs.v.52; Mil.226; DN-a.i.248 (+ paripūra, explained by anavaya). Anunaka = anuna</p><p>Vedic anūna, an + ūna</p>

anūnaka|anuunaka|anunaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuunaka</span></p><p>not deficient, complete; having no lack (of, instr.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnaka">ūnaka</a></p>

anūnaka|anuunaka|anunaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuunaka</span></p><p>Anūnaka = anūna</p><p>Dpvs.iv.34.</p>

anūnatā|anuunataa|anunata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuunataa</span></p><p>completeness Cp.iii.6#11.</p><p>abstr. fr. anūna</p>

anūpa|anuupa|anupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupa</span></p><p>adjective watery, moist; watery land, lowland Ja.iv.358 (anopa T; anupa C. p. 359), 381 (˚khetta); Mil.129 (˚khetta).</p><p>Vedic anūpa, anu + ap: see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81pa">āpa</a></em>, orig. alongside of water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

anūpadhika|anuupadhika|anupadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupadhika</span></p><p>for anu˚ in metre Snp.1057, see <em><a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></p>

anūpadhīka|anuupadhiika|anupadhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupadhiika</span></p><p>free from attachment; free from acquisitions (which lead to rebirth)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anūpadhīka">anūpadhīka</a></p>

anūpaghāta|anuupaghaata|anupaghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupaghaata</span></p><p>not killing, not murdering. Dhp.185 (= anupahananañ c’eva anupaghātanañ ca Dhp-a.iii.238).</p><p>metrically for anupa˚</p>

anūpahata|anuupahata|anupahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupahata</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupahata">anupahata</a>, <a href="bword://upahata">upahata</a></p>

anūpakhajja|anuupakhajja|anupakhajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupakhajja</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupakhajja">anupakhajja</a></p>

anūpalitta|anuupalitta|anupalitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupalitta</span></p><p>not attached to; not sticking to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupalitta">anupalitta</a>, <a href="bword://upalitta">upalitta</a></p>

anūpalitta|anuupalitta|anupalitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupalitta</span></p><p>adjective free from taint, unstained, unsmeared Snp.211, Snp.392, Snp.468, Snp.790 Snp.845; Dhp.353; cf. Mnd.90 and Dhp-a.iv.72.</p><p>an + upalitta, with ū in metre</p>

anūpama|anuupama|anupama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupama</span></p><p>A monk whose verses are recorded in the Theragāthā. Thag.213–214</p>

anūpama|anuupama|anupama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupama</span></p><p>incomparable, peerless; excellent, best</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupama">anupama</a>, <a href="bword://anopama">anopama</a>, <a href="bword://upamā">upamā</a></p>

anūpama|anuupama|anupama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupama</span></p><p>at Iti.122 is metric reading for <em>anupama</em> (see <em><a href="bword://upama">upama</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upama">upama</a></p>

anūpanāhi|anuupanaahi|anupanahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupanaahi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anūpanāhin">anūpanāhin</a></p><p>(also <em>anupanāhi(n)</em>) not resentful; without rancor.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāhin">upanāhin</a></p>

anūpanāhin|anuupanaahin|anupanahin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupanaahin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://anūpanāhin">anūpanāhin</a></p><p>(also <em>anupanāhi(n)</em>) not resentful; without rancor.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāhin">upanāhin</a></p>

anūpanāhin|anuupanaahin|anupanahin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupanaahin</span></p><p>adjective not bearing ill-will, not angry with Ja.iv.463.</p><p>an + upanāhin, with ū metri causa</p>

anūpanīta|anuupaniita|anupanita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupaniita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupanīta">anupanīta</a>, <a href="bword://upanīta">upanīta</a></p>

anūpavajja|anuupavajja|anupavajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupavajja</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupavajja">anupavajja</a>, <a href="bword://upavajja">upavajja</a></p>

anūpavāda|anuupavaada|anupavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupavaada</span></p><p>lack of abuse; not blaming or criticizing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupavāda">anupavāda</a>, <a href="bword://upavāda">upavāda</a></p>

anūpavāda|anuupavaada|anupavada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupavaada</span></p><p>not grumbling, not finding fault Dhp.185 (= anupavādanañ c’ eva anupavadāpanañ ca Dhp-a.iii.238).</p><p>an + upavāda, with metrically lengthened u</p>

anūpaya|anuupaya|anupaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuupaya</span></p><p>not going near (to); free from involvement</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupaya">anupaya</a>, <a href="bword://upaya">upaya</a></p>

anūsara|anuusara|anusara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">anuusara</span></p><p>not having saline soil</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūsara">ūsara</a></p>

apa-iti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>apa-iti</p><p>pr. 3 sg. goes away; departs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apeti">apeti</a>, <a href="bword://apāyati">apāyati</a></p>

apa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apa˚</p><p>Well-defined directional prefix, meaning “away from, off” Usually as base-prefix (except with ā), &amp; very seldom in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with other modifying prefixes (like sam, abhi etc.).</p><ol><li><em>apa</em> = Vedic <em>apa</em> (Idg. *apo): apeti to go away = Gr. α ̓́πειμι, Lat. abeo, Goth. afiddja; apeta gone away rid; ˚kaḍḍhati to draw away, remove; ˚kamati walk away ˚gacchati go away; ˚nidhāti put away (= ἀποτιχημι abdo); ˚nudati push away; ˚neti lead away; ˚vattati turn away (= āverto); ˚sakkati step aside; ˚harati take away.</li><li><em>apa</em> = Vedic <em>ava</em> (Idg. *aue; see <em>ava</em> for details) There exists a widespread confusion between the two preps. apa &amp; ava, favoured both by semantic (apa = away, ava = down, cp. E. off) &amp; phonetic affinity (p softened to b, esp. in BB Mss., &amp; then to v, as b → v is frequent, e.g. bya˚ → vya˚ etc.). Thus we find in Pāli apa where Vedic and later literary Sk. have ava in the foll. instances: apakanti, ˚kassati, ˚kirati, ˚gata, ˚cāra ˚jhāyati, ˚thaṭa, ˚dāna, ˚dhāreti, ˚nata, ˚nāmeti, ˚nīta ˚lekhana, ˚loketi, ˚vadati.</li></ol><p>Vedic apa; Idg. *apo = Gr. ἀπό, Av. apa, Lat. ab from *ap (cp. aperio); Goth. af, Ger. ab, Ags. E. of. A compar. form fr. apa is apara “further away”</p>

apabbūhati|apabbuuhati|apabbuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabbuuhati</span></p><p>pushes aside, removes;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabyūhati">apabyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apaviyūhati">apaviyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apabyūheti">apabyūheti</a></p>

apabbūhati|apabbuuhati|apabbuhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabbuuhati</span></p><p>Apabbūhati &amp; Apabyūhati</p><p>to push off, remove, scrape away AN.iii.187 (apaviyūhitvā, vv.ll. ˚bbūhitvā); Ja.i.265 (<em>paṃsuṃ</em>)<br />caus <em>-byūhāpeti</em> to make remove or brush Ja.iv.349 (<em>paṃsuṃ</em>).</p><p>apa + vi + <em>ūh</em></p>

apabbūhitvā|apabbuuhitvaa|apabbuhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabbuuhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabbūhati">apabbūhati</a></p>

apabodhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>wards off; keeps clear of, avoids.</p>

apabyāma|apabyaama|apabyama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabyaama</span></p><p>to the left; placed on the left</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavyāma">apavyāma</a></p>

apabyāma|apabyaama|apabyama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabyaama</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://apavy%C4%81ma">apavyāma</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavyāma">apavyāma</a></p>

apabyūhati|apabyuuhati|apabyuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabyuuhati</span></p><p>pushes aside, removes;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabbūhati">apabbūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apaviyūhati">apaviyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apabyūheti">apabyūheti</a></p>

apabyūhati|apabyuuhati|apabyuhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabyuuhati</span></p><p>Apabbūhati &amp; Apabyūhati</p><p>to push off, remove, scrape away AN.iii.187 (apaviyūhitvā, vv.ll. ˚bbūhitvā); Ja.i.265 (<em>paṃsuṃ</em>)<br />caus <em>-byūhāpeti</em> to make remove or brush Ja.iv.349 (<em>paṃsuṃ</em>).</p><p>apa + vi + <em>ūh</em></p>

apabyūheti|apabyuuheti|apabyuheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apabyuuheti</span></p><p>pushes aside, removes;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabyūhati">apabyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apaviyūhati">apaviyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apabbūhati">apabbūhati</a></p>

apaca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>“not cooking”, homeless.</p>

apacaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>diminution, decrease; loss, decay; esp. diminution of kamma, decrease of the possibility of rebirth.</p>

apacaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>falling off, diminution (opp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81caya">ācaya</a></em> gathering, heaping up), unmaking, esp. loss (of wordliness) decrease (of possibility of rebirth Vin.ii.2 = Vin.iii.21 = Vin.iv.213 cp. Ja.iii.342; SN.ii.95 (kāyassa ācayo pi apacayo pi); AN.iv.280 = Vin.ii.259 (opp. ācaya); Ja.iii.342 (sekho ˚ena na tappati); Vb.106, Vb.319, Vb.326, Vb.330.</p><ul><li><em>-gāmin</em> going towards decrease, “making for the undoing of rebirth” (<em>Dhs trsl.</em> 82) AN.v.243, AN.v.277; Dhs.277 Dhs.339, Dhs.505, Dhs.1014; Vb.12, Vb.16 sq.; Ne.87 (cp. Kv.156).</li></ul><p>fr. apa + <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ācaya">ācaya</a>, <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

apacayagāmin|apacayagaamin|apacayagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacayagaamin</span></p><p>leading to diminution</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacaya">apacaya</a></p>

apacayārāma|apacayaaraama|apacayarama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacayaaraama</span></p><p>finding delight in diminution</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacaya">apacaya</a></p>

apacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter masculine</i></p><p>offspring, descendant.</p>

apacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>offspring, child DN.i.90 (bandhupāda˚ cp. muṇḍaka), DN.i.103 (id.); SN.i.69 (an˚) Snp.991; DN-a.i.254.</p><p>Vedic apatya nt.; der. fr. apa</p>

apaccakkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unseen; in instr. f. <em>apaccakkhāya</em> as adv. without being seen, not by direct evidence Mil.46 sq.</p><p>a + paṭi + akkha</p>

apacchapurima
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “neither after nor before”, i.e. at the same time, simultaneous Ja.iii.295.</p><p>a + paccha + purima</p>

apaccupekkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>disregarding; neglecting.</p>

apacetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be honored; to be treated with respect.</p>

apaceyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be honored; deserving respect.</p>

apacina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (?)</i></p><p>not collecting; or watching over.</p>

apacinat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apacinat">apacinat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacinati">apacinati</a></p>

apacinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>apacinati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. diminishes, makes less; dismantles, does away with</p><p>apacināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. pays attention to; respects</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacināti">apacināti</a>, <a href="bword://apacinati">apacinati</a></p>

apacineyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be respected; deserving honor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaciniyya">apaciniyya</a></p>

apacinitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: a apacinati</p>

apaciniyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be respected; deserving honor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacineyya">apacineyya</a></p>

apacināti|apacinaati|apacinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacinaati</span></p><p>apacināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. diminishes, makes less; dismantles, does away with</p><p>apacināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. pays attention to; respects</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacinati">apacinati</a>, <a href="bword://apacinati">apacinati</a></p>

apacināti|apacinaati|apacinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacinaati</span></p><ol><li>[in meaning of Sk. apacīyate cp. P. upaciyyati Pass. of upacināti] to get rid of, do away with, (cp. apacaya), diminish, make less SN.iii.89 (opp. ācināti); Thag.807; Ja.iv.172 (apacineth’ eva kāmāni = viddhaṃseyyatha C.). Here belong prob. aor 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. <em>apaciyiṃsu</em> (to be read for upacciṃsu) at Ja.vi.187 (akkhīni a. “the eyes gave out”) and Pot. pres. <em>apace</em> Thag-a.72 (on Thag.40).</li><li>[= apacayati] to honour, esteem; observe, guard Vin.i.264 (apacinayamāna cīvaraṃ (?) variant reading apacitiyamāna; trsl. guarding his claim is, <em>Vin Texts);</em> MN.i.324 (see detail under apaviṇāti) Thag.186 (grd. apacineyya to be honoured); Ja.v.339 (anapacinanto for T. anupacinanto, variant reading anapavinati)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://apacita">apacita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>apa + cināti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacita">apacita</a></p>

apacita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>honored; esteemed.</p>

apacita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>honoured, worshipped, esteemed Thag.186; Ja.ii.169; Ja.iv.75; Vv.5#10 (= pūjita Vv-a.39); Vv.35#11 (cp. Vv-a.164); Mil.21.</p><p>pp. of apacayati or apacināti</p>

apaciti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>honoring; respecting; homage.</p>

apaciti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>honour, respect, esteem, reverence Thag.589; Ja.i.220 Ja.ii.435; Ja.iii.82; Ja.iv.308; Ja.vi.88; Mil.180, Mil.234 (˚ṃ karoti), Mil.377 (pūjana +); Snp-a.332 (˚karaṇa). Cp. apacāyana.</p><p>Vedic apaciti in diff. meaning, viz. expiation</p>

apacāra|apacaara|apacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaara</span></p><p>falling off, fault, wrong doing Ja.vi.375.</p><p>fr. apa + <em>car</em>, cp. Sk. apa &amp; abhi-carati</p>

apacāyamāna|apacaayamaana|apacayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacāyati">apacāyati</a></p>

apacāyana|apacaayana|apacayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayana</span></p><p>honouring, honour, worship, reverence Ja.i.220; Ja.v.326; DN-a.i.256 (˚kamma); Vv-a.24 (˚ṃ karoti = añjalikaṃ karoti); Pv-a.104 (˚kara, adj.), Pv-a.128 (+ paricariya).</p><p>abstr. fr. apa + <em>cāy</em>, which is itself a der. fr. <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em>, cināti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

apacāyati|apacaayati|apacayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayati</span></p><p>honors; respects.</p>

apacāyati|apacaayati|apacayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayati</span></p><p>to honour respect, pay reverence DN.i.91 (pūjeti +); Ja.iii.82. Pot. <em>apace</em> (for apaceyya, may be taken to apacināti 2; AN.iv.245; Thag-a.72 (here to apacināti 1)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apacita">apacita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>fr. apa- <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em>, cp. cināti &amp; cayati, with diff. meaning in Sk.; better expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> perhaps as denom. fr. *apacāya in meaning of apacāyana, cp. apacita</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apacita">apacita</a>, <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

apacāyika|apacaayika|apacayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayika</span></p><p>honoring; respecting.</p>

apacāyika|apacaayika|apacayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayika</span></p><p>adjective honouring, respecting Ja.iv.94 (vaddha˚ cp. vaddhâpacāyin); Pv.ii.7 <!-- raw HTML omitted -->8<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (jeṭṭha˚); Pv.iv.3#24 (id.). In B.Sk. the corresp. phrase is jyeṣṭhâpacayaka.</p><p>fr. *apacāya, cp. B.Sk. apacāyaka Mvu.i.198; Divy.293</p>

apacāyin|apacaayin|apacayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apacaayin</span></p><p>adjective honouring, paying homage, revering Snp.325 (vaddha˚ = vaddhānaṃ apaciti karaṇena Snp-a.332) = Dhp.109; Ja.i.47, Ja.i.132, Ja.i.201 Ja.ii.299; Ja.v.325; Mil.206; Sdhp.549.</p><p>fr. *apacāya; cp. apacāyika</p>

apadesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>reason, cause, argument MN.i.287 (an˚).</li><li>statement, designation Pv-a.8.</li><li>pretext Ja.iii.60; Ja.iv.13; Pv-a.154. Thus also <em>apadesaka</em> Ja.vi.179.</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. apadeśa</p>

apadhāreti|apadhaareti|apadhareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apadhaareti</span></p><p>to observe, request ask Thag-a.16.</p><p>Caus. of apa + <em>dhṛ</em>; cp. Sk. ava-dhārayati, but also BSk. apadhārayati Divy.231</p>

apadisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>reference, testimony, witness Dhp-a.ii.39.</p><p>fr apa + <em>diś</em></p>

apadisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>indicates, designates; refers to (as a witness); uses as a pretext.</p>

apadisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to call to witness, to refer to, to quote Vin.iii.159; Ja.i.215; Ja.iii.234; Ja.iv.203; Mil.270; Dhp-a.ii.39; Ne.93.</p><p>apa + disati</p>

apadisitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apadisati">apadisati</a></p>

apadissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apadisati">apadisati</a></p>

apadāna|apadaana|apadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apadaana</span></p><p>cutting; reaping; harvest.</p>

apadāna|apadaana|apadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apadaana</span></p><ol><li>removing, breaking off, DN.iii.88.</li><li>[= Sk. avadāna cp. ovāda] advice, admonition, instruction, morals Vin.ii.4 (an˚ not taking advice), Vin.ii.7 (id.) MN.i.96; AN.v.337 sq. (saddhā˚) Thag.47.</li><li>legend, life history. In the title Mahāpadāna suttanta it refers to the 7 Buddhas. In the title Apadānaṃ, that is ʻthe stories’, it refers almost exclusively to Arahants. The other, (older), connotation seems to have afterwards died out. See <em>Dialogues</em> ii.3<br />■ Cp. also pariyāpadāna.</li></ol><p>= Sk. apadāna</p>

apadānasobhana|apadaanasobhana|apadanasobhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apadaanasobhana</span></p><p>(~ī)n. made clear or manifest in actions or by its results</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apadāna">apadāna</a></p>

apagabbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>interpreted by the Buddha as apa + gabbha, describing one who has destroyed the possibility of rebirth) timid, lacking boldness; ? of low birth?.</p>

apagabbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not entering another womb, i.e. not destined to another rebirth Vin.iii.3.</p><p>a + pa + gabbha</p>

apagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go away, turn aside Dhp-a.i.401 (˚gantvā)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>apa + <em>gam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apagama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>going away, disappearance Sdhp.508.</p><p>Sk. apagama</p>

apagata-kāḷaka|apagata-kaa.laka|apagata-kalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apagata-kaa.laka</span></p><p>apagata-kāḷaka</p><p>mfn. without speck or stain; without defect</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) gone, gone away (from); departed.</li><li>(n.) error; transgression.</li></ol>

apagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>gone, gone away from (c. abl.), removed; deceased, departed Iti.112; Pv-a.39 Pv-a.63 (= peta), Pv-a.64 (= gata).</li><li>(˚-) freq. as prefix meaning without, lit. having lost, removed from; free from Vin.ii.129 (˚gabbhā having lost her foetus, having a miscarriage); Ja.i.61 (˚vattha without clothes); Pv-a.38 (˚soka free from grief), Pv-a.47 (˚lajja not shy), Pv-a.219 (˚viññāṇa without feeling)<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://apakata">apakata</a></em>.</li></ol><p>pp. of apagacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakata">apakata</a></p>

apagatagabbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i></p><p>without a fetus, having miscarried</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apagatajimha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>without a twist, without crookedness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apagataphegguka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with the accessory wood gone</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apagatavaṅka|apagatava"nka|apagatavanka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apagatava"nka</span></p><p>without bend or crookedness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apagata">apagata</a></p>

apahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>taking away, stealing, robbing Ja.ii.34.</p><p>Sk. apahāra, fr. apaharati</p>

apaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take away, remove, captivate, rob Ja.iii.315 (aor. apahārayiṃ); Mil.413; DN-a.i.38.</p><p>apa + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

apaharaṇa|apahara.na|apaharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apahara.na</span></p><p>= apahara Mil.195.</p>

apahari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apaharin">apaharin</a></p><p>taking away; stealing; a thief.</p>

apaharin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apaharin">apaharin</a></p><p>taking away; stealing; a thief.</p>

apahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>destroyed.</p>

apahatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apahattar">apahattar</a></p><p>one who takes away or removes.</p>

apahattar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apahattar">apahattar</a></p><p>one who takes away or removes.</p>

apahattar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who takes away or removes, destroyer MN.i.447 = Kv.528.</p><p>n. ag. to apaharati</p>

apajaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not giving up; grasping, greedy (?).</p>

apajaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not giving up, greedy, miserly AN.iii.76 (variant reading apānuta; Commentary explains (a)vaḍḍhinissita mānatthaddha)</p><p>a + pajaha</p>

apajita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>or neuter conquered, lost; or a loss, a defeat.</p>

apajita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>defeat Dhp.105.</p><p>pp. of apa + <em>ji</em></p>

apajjhāyati|apajjhaayati|apajjhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apajjhaayati</span></p><p>is intent; is absorbed (in thought); broods.</p>

apajjhāyati|apajjhaayati|apajjhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apajjhaayati</span></p><p>to muse, meditate, ponder, consider MN.i.334 (nijjhāyati +); MN.iii.14 (id.).</p><p>apa + jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; cp. Sk. abhi-dhyāyati</p>

apakantati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cuts off.</p>

apakantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cut off Thig.217 (gale = gīvaṃ chindati Thag-a.178; Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>corrects to kabale a.).</p><p>apa + kantati, Sk. ava + kṛntati</p>

apakaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to throw away, put off; hurt, offend slight; possibly in reading T. <em><a href="bword://apakirit%C5%ABna">apakiritūna</a></em> at Thig.447 (q.v.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apakata">apakata</a></em> (q.v.). Cp. apakāra.</p><p>apa + karoti, cp. Sk. apakaroti &amp; apakṛta in same meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakiritūna">apakiritūna</a>, <a href="bword://apakata">apakata</a></p>

apakassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

apakassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>draws away; removes; draws back.</li><li>draws back, secedes; alienates oneself</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakāsati">apakāsati</a></p>

apakassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to throw away, remove Snp.281 (variant reading BB &amp; Snp-a ava˚; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by niddhamati &amp; nikkaḍḍhati Snp-a.311)<br />ger <em><a href="bword://apakassa">apakassa</a></em> Sn.ii.198 = Mil.389. See also <em><a href="bword://apak%C4%81sati">apakāsati</a></em>. Apakara &amp; ka;</p><p>Sk. apa- &amp; ava-kaṛṣati, cp. apakaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassa">apakassa</a>, <a href="bword://apakāsati">apakāsati</a></p>

apakata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>put off, done away, in ājīvik <em>āpakata</em> being without a living MN.i.463 (the usual phrase being ˚apagata); Mil.279 (id.). At Iti.89 the reading of same phrase is ājīvikā pakatā (variant reading ā˚ vakatā).</p><p>pp. of apakaroti</p>

apakataññu|apakata~n~nu|apakatannu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakata~n~nu</span></p><p>adjective ungrateful Vin.ii.199.</p><p>a + pa + kataññu</p>

apakaḍḍhanta|apaka.d.dhanta|apakaddhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaka.d.dhanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakaḍḍhati">apakaḍḍhati</a></p>

apakaḍḍhati|apaka.d.dhati|apakaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>draws away; takes off; removes; extracts; picks out.</p>

apakaḍḍhati|apaka.d.dhati|apakaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to draw away, take off, remove DN.i.180; DN.iii.127; Dhp-a.ii.86. Caus. <em>apakaḍḍhāpeti</em> Ja.i.342; Ja.iv.415; Mil.34<br />■ Cp <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em>; &amp; see <em><a href="bword://pakattheti">pakattheti</a></em>.</p><p>apa + kaḍḍhati, cp. Sk. apa-karṣati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a>, <a href="bword://pakattheti">pakattheti</a></p>

apakaḍḍhi|apaka.d.dhi|apakaddhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaka.d.dhi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakaḍḍhati">apakaḍḍhati</a></p>

apakaṭṭha|apaka.t.tha|apakattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaka.t.tha</span></p><p>drawn away, removed (from); loose</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkaṭṭha">apakkaṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

apakiritūna|apakirituuna|apakirituna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakirituuna</span></p><p>throwing away; rejecting.</p>

apakiritūna|apakirituuna|apakirituna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakirituuna</span></p><p>at Thig.447 T (reading of C. is abhi˚) is explained Thag-a.271 to mean apakiritvā chaḍḍetvā throwing away, slighting, offending. The correct etym = Sk. avakirati (ava + <em>kṛ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to strew, cast out) in sense “to cast off, reject”, to which also belongs kirāta in meaning “cast off” i.e. man of a so-called low tribe. See also avakirati 2.</p>

apakkamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>departs; goes away; leaves; retires, turns aside.</p>

apakkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go away, depart, go to one side Ja.iii.27; Sdhp.294<br />aor <em><a href="bword://apakkami">apakkami</a></em> Pv.iv.7#5; ger. <em>apakkamitvā</em> Pv-a.43, Pv-a.124 &amp; <em><a href="bword://apakkamma">apakkamma</a></em> Pv.ii.9#28.</p><p>cp. Sk. apakramati, apa + <em>kram</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkami">apakkami</a>, <a href="bword://apakkamma">apakkamma</a></p>

apakkami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkamati">apakkamati</a></p>

apakkamiṃsu|apakkami.msu|apakkamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakkami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkamati">apakkamati</a></p>

apakkamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkamati">apakkamati</a></p>

apakkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>gone away, departed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakkamati">apakkamati</a></p>

apakkaṭṭha|apakka.t.tha|apakkattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakka.t.tha</span></p><p>drawn away, removed (from); loose</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakaṭṭha">apakaṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

apakāka|apakaaka|apakaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakaaka</span></p><p>Apakāra &amp; ˚ka</p><p>injury, mischief; one who injures or offends Dhp-a.iii.63; Sdhp.283.</p><p>cf. Sk. apakāra &amp; apakaroti</p>

apakāra|apakaara|apakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakaara</span></p><p>Apakāra &amp; ˚ka</p><p>injury, mischief; one who injures or offends Dhp-a.iii.63; Sdhp.283.</p><p>cf. Sk. apakāra &amp; apakaroti</p>

apakāsati|apakaasati|apakasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakaasati</span></p><ol><li>draws away; removes; draws back.</li><li>draws back, secedes; alienates oneself</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

apakāsati|apakaasati|apakasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apakaasati</span></p><p>at Vin.ii.204 is to be read as <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em> and interpreted as “draw away, distract, bring about a split or dissension (of the Sangha)”. The variant reading on p. Vin.ii.325 justifies the correction (apakassati) as well as Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “parisaṃ ākaḍḍhanti”<br />■ Cp. AN.iii.145 &amp; see; <em><a href="bword://avapak%C4%81sati">avapakāsati</a></em> The reading at the id. p. at AN.v.74 is <em><a href="bword://avakassati">avakassati</a></em> (comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> w. vavakassati, where Vin.ii.204 has avapakāsati) which is much to be preferred (see <em><a href="bword://vavakassati">vavakassati</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a>, <a href="bword://avapakāsati">avapakāsati</a>, <a href="bword://avakassati">avakassati</a>, <a href="bword://vavakassati">vavakassati</a></p>

apalekhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>scraping off.</p>

apalekhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>licking off, in cpd. <strong>hatthāpalekhana</strong> “hand-licking” (i.e. licking one’s hand after a meal, the practice of certain ascetics) MN.i.77 (with variant reading hatthâvalekhana MN.i.535; Trenckner compares BSk. hastapralehaka Lal.312 &amp; hastâvalehaka Lal.323), MN.i.412; Pp.55 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Pp-a.231 as hatthe piṇḍamhe niṭṭhite jivhāya hatthaṃ apalekhati).</p><p>apa + lekhana from <em>likh</em> in meaning of <em>lih</em>, corresponding to Sk. ava-lehana</p><p>See also: hatthāpalekhana</p>

apalekhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lick off Pp-a.231 (hatthaṃ).</p><p>apa + lekhati in meaning of Sk. avalihati</p>

apalepa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>lack of plaster.</p>

apalepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in “so ’palepa patito jarāgharo” at Thig.270 is to be read as “so palepa˚”. Morris’s interpret. J.P.T.S. 1886, 126 therefore superfluous.</p>

apalepapatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>falling down through the loss of plaster</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalepa">apalepa</a></p>

apalibodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apalibuddha &amp; Apalibodha</p><p>unobstructed, unhindered, free Ja.iii.381 (˚bodha); Mil.388; Dhp-a.iii.198.</p><p>a + palibuddha, pp. of pari + <em>bṛh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://palibujjhati">palibujjhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://palibujjhati">palibujjhati</a></p>

apalibuddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apalibuddha &amp; Apalibodha</p><p>unobstructed, unhindered, free Ja.iii.381 (˚bodha); Mil.388; Dhp-a.iii.198.</p><p>a + palibuddha, pp. of pari + <em>bṛh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://palibujjhati">palibujjhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://palibujjhati">palibujjhati</a></p>

apalokana-kamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>apalokana-kamma</p><p>neuter a (formal) act (of the <em>saṅgha</em>) giving permission or approval.</p>

apalokana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>~ā. feminine looking for approval or permission.</p>

apalokana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>permission, leave, in ˚kamma proposal of a resolution, obtaining leave (see <em><a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> i.3; Vin.ii.89; Vin.iv.152.</p><p>fr. apaloketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

apalokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>looks towards; looks back; looks to, considers.</li><li>looks to for approval, consults; asks permission; asks or takes leave</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apalokenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apalokesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apaloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apaloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>looks towards; looks back; looks to, considers.</li><li>looks to for approval, consults; asks permission; asks or takes leave</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalokayati">apalokayati</a></p>

apaloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to look ahead, to look before, to be cautious, to look after MN.i.557 (variant reading for apaciṇāti, where Ja.v.339 C. has avaloketi); Mil.398.</li><li>to look up to, to obtain permission from (acc.), to get leave, to give notice of Vin.iii.10, Vin.iii.11; Vin.iv.226 (anapaloketvā = anāpucchā), Vin.iv.267 (+ āpucchitvā); MN.i.337; SN.iii.95 (bhikkhusanghaṃ anapaloketvā without informing the Sangha); Ja.vi.298 (vājānaṃ); Dhp-a.i.67</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://apalokita">apalokita</a></em> (q.v.). See also apalokana &amp; ˚lokin.</p><p>BSk. ava-lokayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalokita">apalokita</a></p>

apaloketvā|apaloketvaa|apaloketva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaloketvaa</span></p><p>looking towards; considering; asking permission; taking leave; consulting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apalokin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “looking before oneself”, looking at, cautious Mil.398.</p><p>Sk. avalokin</p>

apalokita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>apalokita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. asked for leave; consulted</p><p>apalokita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. not liable to decay, not subject to destruction.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaloketi">apaloketi</a></p>

apalokita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>asked permission, consulted SN.iii.5.</li><li>(nt.) permission, consent MN.i.337 (Nāgâpalokitaṃ apalokesi).</li><li>(nt.) an epithet of Nibbāna SN.iv.370.</li></ol><p>pp. of apaloketi; Sk. avalokita</p>

apalāletabba|apalaaletabba|apalaletabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaaletabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalāḷeti">apalāḷeti</a></p>

apalāpin|apalaapin|apalapin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaapin</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://apal%C4%81sin">apalāsin</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. apalāpin “denying, concealing” different</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalāsin">apalāsin</a></p>

apalāsin|apalaasin|apalasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaasin</span></p><p>adjective either “not neglectful, pure, clean” (= apalāpin fr. palāsa chaff cp. apalāyin at Ja.v.4), or “not selfish, not hard, generous (as inferred from comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with amakkhin &amp; amaccharin) or “brave, fearless, energetic” (= apalāyin) DN.iii.47 cp. Pp.22. See <em><a href="bword://pal%C4%81sin">palāsin</a></em>.</p><p><em>apaḷāsin</em>; but spelling altogether uncertain. There seems to exist a confusion between the forms apalāyin apalāpin &amp; apalāsin, owing to freq. miswriting of s, y, p in MSS. (cp. Cnd introd. p. xix.). We should be inclined to give apalāsin, as the lectio difficilior, the preference. The expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Pp.22 as “yassa puggalassa ayaṃ paḷāso pahīno ayaṃ vuccati puggalo apaḷāsī” does not help us to clear up the etym. nor the vv.ll.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://palāsin">palāsin</a></p>

apalāyin|apalaayin|apalayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaayin</span></p><p>adjective not running away, steadfast, brave, fearless Cnd.13 (abhīru anutrāsin apalāyin as expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of acchambhin and vīra); Ja.iv.296; Ja.v.4 (where C gives variant “<em>apalāpinī</em> ti pi pāṭho”, which latter has variant reading <em>apalāsinī</em> &amp; is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as palāpa-rahite anavajjasarīre p. 5). See also apalāsin.</p><p>a + palāyin</p>

apalāḷana|apalaa.lana|apalalana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaa.lana</span></p><p>enticing, luring away.</p>

apalāḷeti|apalaa.leti|apalaleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaa.leti</span></p><p>entices, lures away.</p>

apalāḷeti|apalaa.leti|apalaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apalaa.leti</span></p><p>to draw over to Vin.i.85.</p><p>apa + lāḷeti</p>

apamāra|apamaara|apamara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apamaara</span></p><p>epilepsy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasmāra">apasmāra</a></p>

apamāra|apamaara|apamara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apamaara</span></p><p>epilepsy Vin.i.93. Cp. <em><a href="bword://apasm%C4%81ra">apasmāra</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. apasmāra</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasmāra">apasmāra</a></p>

apamārika|apamaarika|apamarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apamaarika</span></p><p>epileptic</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apamāriya">apamāriya</a></p>

apamārika|apamaarika|apamarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apamaarika</span></p><p>adjective epileptic Vin.iv.8, Vin.iv.10, Vin.iv.11.</p><p>cp. Sk. apasmārin</p>

apamāriya|apamaariya|apamariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apamaariya</span></p><p>epileptic</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apamārika">apamārika</a></p>

apanaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>tied back; (or untied).</p>

apanaddhakalāpa|apanaddhakalaapa|apanaddhakalapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanaddhakalaapa</span></p><p>with quiver tied behind; (or “with the hair-knot untied).</p>

apanamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>bends away; bends down; goes away.</p>

apanamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go away Snp.1102 (apanamissati, variant reading apalām˚ &amp; apagam˚; explained at Cnd.60 by vajissati pakkhamissati etc<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apanata">apanata</a></em> (q.v.) Caus. <em><a href="bword://apan%C4%81meti">apanāmeti</a></em>.</p><p>semantically doubtful</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanata">apanata</a>, <a href="bword://apanāmeti">apanāmeti</a></p>

apanata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i></p><p>bent away; disinclined, averse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanamati">apanamati</a></p>

apanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“bent away”, drawn aside, in ster. comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>abhinata + apanata</em> (“strained forth &amp; strained aside” Mrs Rh. D. <em>Kindred S.</em> p. 39) MN.i.386; SN.i.28.</p><p>pp. of apanamati</p>

apandara
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>See <em>Kāka</em>.</p><p>See also: Kāka</p>

apaneti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>leads away; removes; excepts, excludes; denies.</p>

apaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lead away, take or put away, remove Ja.i.62, Ja.i.138; Ja.ii.4, Ja.ii.155 (aor. apānayi), Ja.iii.26; Mil.188, Mil.259, Mil.413; Pv-a.41, Pv-a.74, Pv-a.198 (= harati) Sdhp.63 Pass. <em>apanīyati</em> SN.i.176<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apan%C4%ABta">apanīta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>apa + <em>nī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanīta">apanīta</a></p>

apanetvā|apanetvaa|apanetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apaneyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apanidahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apanidahati &amp; apanidheti</p><p>to hide, conceal Vin.iv.123 (˚dheti ˚dheyya, ˚dhessati); Pv-a.215 (˚dhāya ger.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apanihita">apanihita</a></em><br />caus <em>apanidhāpeti</em> to induce somebody to conceal Vin.iv.123.</p><p>apa + ni + <em>dhā</em>, cp. Vedic apadhā hiding-place; Sk. apadadhāti = Gr. ἀποτἱχημι = Lat. abdo “do away”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanihita">apanihita</a></p>

apanidha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>concealing.</p>

apanidhenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanidheti">apanidheti</a></p>

apanidhesuṃ|apanidhesu.m|apanidhesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanidhesu.m</span></p><ol start="3"><li>sg.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanidheti">apanidheti</a></p>

apanidheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr.</i></p><ol start="3"><li>sg places aside; hides, conceals.</li></ol>

apanidheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apanidahati &amp; apanidheti</p><p>to hide, conceal Vin.iv.123 (˚dheti ˚dheyya, ˚dhessati); Pv-a.215 (˚dhāya ger.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apanihita">apanihita</a></em><br />caus <em>apanidhāpeti</em> to induce somebody to conceal Vin.iv.123.</p><p>apa + ni + <em>dhā</em>, cp. Vedic apadhā hiding-place; Sk. apadadhāti = Gr. ἀποτἱχημι = Lat. abdo “do away”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanihita">apanihita</a></p>

apanidhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>removed hidden concealed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanidheti">apanidheti</a></p>

apanidhāpeti|apanidhaapeti|apanidhapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanidhaapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanidheti">apanidheti</a></p>

apanihita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>concealed, in abstr. <em>-ttaṃ</em> (nt.) hiding, concealing, theft Pv-a.216.</p><p>pp. of apanidahati</p>

apanudana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apanudana &amp; Apanūdana</p><p>neuter taking or driving away, removal Vin.ii.148; Ja.i.94 (<em>dukkha˚</em>); Snp.252 (id.); Pv-a.114 (id.).</p><p>Sk. apanodana, fr. apanudati</p>

apanudati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apanudati &amp; Apanudeti</p><p>to push or drive away, remove dispel; pres. <em>apanudeti</em> Mil.38. aor. <em>apānudi</em> Pv.i.8#6 (= apanesi Pv-a.41); Pv.ii.3#14 (= avahari aggahesi Pv-a.86) Dāvs i.8. ger. <em>apanujja</em> DN.ii.223. See also der. <em><a href="bword://apanudana">apanudana</a></em>. Apanudana &amp; Apanudana;</p><p>apa + <em>nud</em>, cp. Vedic apanudati &amp; Caus. Sk. apanodayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanudana">apanudana</a></p>

apanudeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apanudati &amp; Apanudeti</p><p>to push or drive away, remove dispel; pres. <em>apanudeti</em> Mil.38. aor. <em>apānudi</em> Pv.i.8#6 (= apanesi Pv-a.41); Pv.ii.3#14 (= avahari aggahesi Pv-a.86) Dāvs i.8. ger. <em>apanujja</em> DN.ii.223. See also der. <em><a href="bword://apanudana">apanudana</a></em>. Apanudana &amp; Apanudana;</p><p>apa + <em>nud</em>, cp. Vedic apanudati &amp; Caus. Sk. apanodayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanudana">apanudana</a></p>

apanudita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apanuditar">apanuditar</a></p><p>one who removes.</p>

apanuditar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apanuditar">apanuditar</a></p><p>one who removes.</p>

apanuditar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>remover, dispeller DN.iii.148.</p><p>n. ag. fr. apanudati, Sk. apanoditṛ</p>

apanujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>having driven away; having removed.</p>

apanāmesi|apanaamesi|apanamesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanaamesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanamati">apanamati</a></p>

apanāmeti|apanaameti|apanameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanaameti</span></p><p>makes bend down, lowers; turns away (trans.); takes away, removes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanamati">apanamati</a></p>

apanāmeti|apanaameti|apanameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanaameti</span></p><ol><li>to take away, remove MN.i.96 = AN.i.198 (kathaṃ bahiddhā a. carry outside) Kp.viii.4 (= aññaṃ ṭhānaṃ gameti Kp-a.220).</li><li>[= Sk. ava-namati] to bend down, lower, put down Vin.ii.208 (chattaṃ); SN.i.226 (id.); Ja.ii.287 (id., variant reading apanetvā) DN.i.126 (hatthaṃ, for salute).</li></ol><p>Caus. fr. apanamati</p>

apanāmetvā|apanaametvaa|apanametva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanaametvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanamati">apanamati</a></p>

apanīta|apaniita|apanita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaniita</span></p><p>taken away, removed; dispelled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apanīta|apaniita|apanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaniita</span></p><p>taken away or off; removed, dispelled Pv-a.39.</p><p>Sk. apanīta, pp. of apa + nī, see <em><a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></em> &amp; cp. also onīta = apanīta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apanīyati|apaniiyati|apaniyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaniiyati</span></p><p>See also: apaniyyati, <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apanīyyati|apaniiyyati|apaniyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaniiyyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanīyati">apanīyati</a>, <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

apanūdana|apanuudana|apanudana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apanuudana</span></p><p>Apanudana &amp; Apanūdana</p><p>neuter taking or driving away, removal Vin.ii.148; Ja.i.94 (<em>dukkha˚</em>); Snp.252 (id.); Pv-a.114 (id.).</p><p>Sk. apanodana, fr. apanudati</p>

apanṇṇakatā|apan.n.nakataa|apannnakata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apan.n.nakataa</span></p><p>certainty, absoluteness SN.iv.351 sq.</p><p>abstr. of apaṇṇaka</p>

apapibati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to drink from something Ja.ii.126 (aor. apāpāsi). Apabbuhati &amp; Apabyuhati;</p><p>apa + pibati</p>

apara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>another, a further, one more; following, subsequent; later; western.</p>

apara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective another, i.e. additional, following next, second (with pron. inflexion, i.e. nom. pl apare) DN.iii.190 (˚pajā another, i.e. future generation) Snp.791, Snp.1089 (n’); Ja.i.59 (aparaṃ divasaṃ on some day following); Ja.iii.51 (apare tayo sahāyā “other friends three” i.e. three friends, cp. similarly Fr. nous autres Franc˚ais), Ja.iv.3 (dīpa); Pv-a.81 (˚divase on another day), Pv-a.226; with other part. like <em>aparo pi</em> DN.iii.128<br />■ nt. <em>aparaṃ</em> what follows i.e. future state, consequence; future Vin.i.35 (nâparaṃ nothing more); Snp.1092 (much the same as punabbhava, cp. Cnd.61). Cases adverbially; <em>aparaṃ</em> (acc. further, besides, also Ja.i.256; Ja.iii.278; often with other part like <em>athāparaṃ</em> &amp; further, moreover Snp.974; and <em>puna c’ aparaṃ</em> Iti.100; Mil.418 (so read for puna ca paraṃ and passim; <em>aparam pi</em> Vism.9<br />■ <em>aparena</em> in future DN.iii.201<br />■ Repeated (reduplicative formation) <em>aparāparaṃ</em> (local) to &amp; fro Ja.i.265, Ja.i.278; Pv-a.198; (temporal) again and again, off &amp; on Ja.ii.377; Mil.132; Vv-a.271; Pv-a.176 (= punappunaṃ).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://anta">anta</a></em> (aparanta) = aparaṃ, with anta in same function as in compounds vananta (see *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 5): a further away, westward Ja.v.471; Mil.292 (janapada). b future DN.i.30 (˚kappika, cp. DN-a.i.118); MN.ii.228 (˚ânudiṭṭhi-thought of the future); SN.iii.46 (id.).</li><li><em>-āpariya</em> (fr. aparâpara ever-following, successive, continuous, everlasting; used with ref. to kamma Ja.v.106; Mil.108.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81ga">bhāga</a></em> the future lit. a later part of time, only in loc. aparabhāge at a future date, later on Ja.i.34, Ja.i.262; Ja.iv.1; Vv-a.66.</li></ul><p>Vedic apara, der. fr. apa with compar. suffix-ra = Idg. *aporos “further away, second”; cp. Gr. ἀπωτέρω farther, Lat. aprilis the second month (after March, i.e. April). Goth. afar = after</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://bhāga">bhāga</a></p>

aparaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>aparaddha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. destroyed, disappeared.</p><p>aparaddha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn.</p><ol><li>(act.) having missed (the mark); having fallen short of (acc.); having done wrong (to) or offended against (loc.).</li><li>(pass.) missed, misunderstood; done wrongly; a failing, mistake, offense</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajjhati">aparajjhati</a></p>

aparaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>missed (c. acc.), gone wrong, failed, sinned (against = loc.) DN.i.91, DN.i.103, DN.i.180; SN.i.103 (suddhimaggaṃ); Thag.78; Snp.891 (suddhiṃ viraddha khalita Mnd.300); Pv-a.195.</p><p>pp. of aparajjhati</p>

aparaddhuṃ|aparaddhu.m|aparaddhum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaddhu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajjhati">aparajjhati</a></p>

aparagoyāna|aparagoyaana|aparagoyana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparagoyaana</span></p><p>One of the four great continents into which the earth is divided. According to the Aṅguttara Nikāya, AN.i.227 AN.v.59 each <em>cakkavāla</em> (world-system) has an Aparagoyāna.</p><p>5continent</p>

aparajjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>misses (one’s aim), fails; wrongs or offends against (+acc., gen/dat. or loc).</p>

aparajjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to sin or offend against (c. loc.) Vin.ii.78 = Vin.iii.161; Ja.v.68; Ja.vi.367; Mil.189; Pv-a.263<br />pp <em>aparaddha &amp; aparādhita;</em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. aparādhyate, apa + <em>rādh</em></p>

aparajjhaṃ|aparajjha.m|aparajjham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparajjha.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajjhati">aparajjhati</a></p>

aparajju
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>on the following day.</p>

aparajju
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb on the foll. day Vin.ii.167; SN.i.186; Mil.48.</p><p>Sk. apare-dyus</p>

aparajjugata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>which occurred on the previous day</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajju">aparajju</a></p>

aparanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the future; a future or final state</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparantakappika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>who theorizes about the future</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparanta">aparanta</a></p>

aparantanudiṭṭhi|aparantanudi.t.thi|aparantanuditthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparantanudi.t.thi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparantanudiṭṭhin">aparantanudiṭṭhin</a></p><p>holding (false) views concerning the future</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparanta">aparanta</a></p>

aparantanudiṭṭhin|aparantanudi.t.thin|aparantanuditthin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparantanudi.t.thin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparantanudiṭṭhin">aparantanudiṭṭhin</a></p><p>holding (false) views concerning the future</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparanta">aparanta</a></p>

aparapaccaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not dependent or relying on others Vin.i.12 (vesārajja-ppatta +); DN.i.110 (id.); MN.ii.41; MN.i.491; SN.iii.83; DN-a.i.278 (nâssa paro paccayo).</p><p>a + para + paccaya</p>

aparapajā|aparapajaa|aparapaja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparapajaa</span></p><p>grandchildren and descendants</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparapara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>one after another; various; following</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparaparaṃ|aparapara.m|aparaparam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparapara.m</span></p><p>to and fro; again and again; more and more</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparaṃ|apara.m|aparam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apara.m</span></p><p>again, further, moreover</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparaṇṇa|apara.n.na|aparanna
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apara.n.na</span></p><p>A general term for raw cereals Vin.iii.50. See <em>Dhañña</em>.</p><p>See also: Dhañña</p>

aparaṇṇa|apara.n.na|aparanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apara.n.na</span></p><p>vegetables.</p>

aparaṇṇa|apara.n.na|aparanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apara.n.na</span></p><p>“the other kind of cereal”, prepared or cooked cereals, pulse etc. Opp. to pubbaṇṇa the unprepared or raw corn (= āmakadhañña Vin.iv.265; Vin.iii.151 (pubb˚ +); Vin.iv.265, Vin.iv.267; AN.iv.108, AN.iv.112 (tila-mugga-māsā˚; opp. sāli-yavaka etc.); Cnd.314 (aparaṇṇaṃ nāma sūpeyyaṃ); Ja.v.406 (˚jā = hareṇukā pea); Mil.106 (pubbaṇṇa˚). See also dhañña &amp; harita.</p><p>apara + aṇṇa = anna</p>

aparena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i></p><ol><li>after, beyond; later on, afterwards.</li><li>to the west</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://apara">apara</a></p>

aparighaṃsanta|aparigha.msanta|aparighamsanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparigha.msanta</span></p><p>not rubbing or scraping.</p>

aparihāna-dhamma|aparihaana-dhamma|aparihana-dhamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparihaana-dhamma</span></p><p><strong>Aparihāna-dhamma:</strong> ‘incapable of relapse’, or ‘of falling away’, namely, with regard to deliverance from some or all mental chains of existence see: <em>samyojana</em>. Thus all Noble Disciples are called, i.e. all those who have attained any of the 4 noble paths to Nobility see: <em>ariya-puggala.</em> With regard to the absorptions <em>jhāna</em>, anyone is called ‘unrelapsable’ who has attained full mastery over the absorptions. See A. VI, 62; Pug. 6. Cf. <em>akuppa-dhamma</em></p>

aparihāniya-dhamma|aparihaaniya-dhamma|aparihaniya-dhamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparihaaniya-dhamma</span></p><p><strong>Aparihāniya-dhamma:</strong> ‘conditions of welfare’ lit. of non-decline, for a nation. Seven such conditions are mentioned in the Mahā-Parinibbāna Sutta D. 16. They are followed by five sets of 7, and one set of 6 conditions, conducive to the welfare of the Community of Bhikkhus, the Sangha. Identical texts at A. VII, 20-25. To be distinguished from the preceding term.</p>

aparissāvanaka|aparissaavanaka|aparissavanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparissaavanaka</span></p><p>(one who has no water-strainer.</p>

aparitassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>freedom from fear or worry.</p>

aparitassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparitassin">aparitassin</a></p><p>not trembling; without fear.</p>

aparitassin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparitassin">aparitassin</a></p><p>not trembling; without fear.</p>

aparādha|aparaadha|aparadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadha</span></p><p>sin, fault, offence, guilt Ja.i.264 (nir˚); Ja.iii.394; Ja.iv.495; Vv-a.69; Pv-a.87, Pv-a.116.</p><p>fr. apa + <em>rādh</em></p>

aparādhenta|aparaadhenta|aparadhenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparādheti">aparādheti</a></p>

aparādheti|aparaadheti|aparadheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadheti</span></p><p>lets (something) go wrong or fail; neglects</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajjhati">aparajjhati</a></p>

aparādhi|aparaadhi|aparadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparādhin">aparādhin</a></p><p>offending; criminal.</p>

aparādhika|aparaadhika|aparadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhika</span></p><p>adjective guilty, offending, criminal Ja.ii.117 (vāja˚); Mil.149 (issara˚), Mil.189 (aparādhikatā).</p><p>fr. aparādha, cp. Sk. aparādhin</p>

aparādhin|aparaadhin|aparadhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aparādhin">aparādhin</a></p><p>offending; criminal.</p>

aparādhita|aparaadhita|aparadhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhita</span></p><p>missed; done wrongly; neglected</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparajjhati">aparajjhati</a></p>

aparādhita|aparaadhita|aparadhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadhita</span></p><p>transgressed, sinned, failing Ja.v.26 (so read for aparadh’ ito).</p><p>pp. of aparādheti, Caus. of apa + <em>rādh</em>; cp. aparaddha</p>

aparādika|aparaadika|aparadika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaadika</span></p><p>(one) who has offended or committed a fault, who has fallen short (of).</p>

aparājita|aparaajita|aparajita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaajita</span></p><p>One of the Pacceka Buddhas mentioned in the Isigili Sutta. MN.iii.70</p>

aparājita|aparaajita|aparajita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaajita</span></p><p>adjective unconquered Snp.269; Ja.i.71, Ja.i.165.</p><p>Vedic aparājita; a + parājita</p>

aparāpariya-vedanīya-kamma|aparaapariya-vedaniiya-kamma|aparapariya-vedaniya-kamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaapariya-vedaniiya-kamma</span></p><p><strong>Aparāpariya-vedanīya-kamma:</strong> ‘kamma bearing fruits in later births’; see: <em>kamma.</em></p>

aparāyin|aparaayin|aparayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aparaayin</span></p><p>adjective having no support Ja.iii.386 (f. <em>-ī</em>; C. appatiṭṭhā appaṭisaraṇā).</p><p>a + parāyin, cp. parāyana</p>

apasabya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to the left, placed on the left</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasavya">apasavya</a></p>

apasakkati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes away or aside.</p>

apasakkati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go away, to go aside Ja.iv.347 (variant reading for apavattati); Vv-a.101; Pv-a.265 (aor ˚sakki = apakkami).</p><p>apa + sakkati</p>

apasammajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>sweeps away; winnows.</p>

apasavya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to the left, placed on the left</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasabya">apasabya</a></p>

apasavya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective right (i.e. not left), contrary Ud.50 (T. has niṭṭhubhitvā abyāmato karitvā; vv ll. are apabhyāmāto, abhyāmato &amp; C. apasabyāmato) where C. explains apasabyāmato karitvā by apasabyaṃ katvā “which latter corresponds in form but not in meaning to Sk. apasavyaṃ karoti to go on the right side” (Morris J.P.T.S. 1886, 127)<br />■ See <em><a href="bword://apavy%C4%81ma">apavyāma</a></em>.</p><p>apa + savya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavyāma">apavyāma</a></p>

apasavyato karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>goes round (something or someone) keeping one’s left side towards it or them (a mark of disrespect)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasavyaṃ karoti">apasavyaṃ karoti</a>, <a href="bword://apasavya">apasavya</a></p>

apasavyaṃ karoti|apasavya.m karoti|apasavyam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasavya.m karoti</span></p><p>goes round (something or someone) keeping one’s left side towards it or them (a mark of disrespect)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasavyato karoti">apasavyato karoti</a>, <a href="bword://apasavya">apasavya</a></p>

apasmāra|apasmaara|apasmara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasmaara</span></p><p>epilepsy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apamāra">apamāra</a></p>

apasmāra|apasmaara|apasmara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasmaara</span></p><p>epilepsy, convulsion, fit Ja.iv.84. Cp. apamāra.</p><p>Sk. apasmāra, lit. want of memory, apa + <em>smṛ</em>.</p>

apassanto
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>etc. see <em><a href="bword://passati">passati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://passati">passati</a></p>

apassaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>support, rest Thag-a.258.</li><li>bed, bolster, mattress, in <em>kaṇṭak˚</em>; a mattress of thorns, a bolster filled with thorns (as cushion for asceties) MN.i.78; Ja.i.493; Ja.iii.235. <em>-sāppassaya</em> with a head rest Ja.iv.299.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%AB%E1%B9%ADhaka">pīṭhaka</a></em> a chair with a head-rest Ja.iii.235.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. apāśraya, fr. apasseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pīṭhaka">pīṭhaka</a></p>

apassayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>leans against; has a support in; depends upon.</p>

apassayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective reclining on, in <em>kaṇṭaka˚</em>; one who lies on a bed of thorns (see <em><a href="bword://ka%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADaka">kaṇṭaka</a></em>) MN.i.78; Ja.iv.299 (v. l, kaṇḍikesayika); Pp.55.</p><p>fr. apassaya; cp. Sk. apāśrayin-˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaṇṭaka">kaṇṭaka</a></p>

apassena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>something to lean on or rely on; rest, s upport.</p>

apassena
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>rest, support, dependence MN.iii.127 (˚ka); DN.iii.224 (cattāri apassenāni); as adj <em>caturāpassena</em> one who has the fourfold support viz sankhāy’ ekaṃ paṭisevati, adhivāseti, parivajjeti, vinodeti AN.v.30.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a></em> (cp. Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 71) a bolsterslab head-rest Vin.i.48; Vin.ii.175, Vin.ii.209.</li></ul><p>fr. apasseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://phalaka">phalaka</a></p>

apassenaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>rest, support.</p>

apassenaphalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>reclining board (to protect a wall)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apassena">apassena</a></p>

apasseta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apassetar">apassetar</a></p><p>one who leans against.</p>

apassetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apassayati">apassayati</a></p>

apassetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apassetar">apassetar</a></p><p>one who leans against.</p>

apasseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lean against, have a support in (acc.), to depend on.</p><ol><li>(lit.) lean against Vin.ii.175 (bhitti apassetabbo the wall to be used as a head-rest).</li><li>(fig.) mostly in ger. <em>apassāya</em> dependent upon, depending on, trusting in (loc. or acc or-˚) Vin.iii.38; Ja.i.214; Pv-a.189</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://apassita">apassita</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ See also avasseti.</p><p>Sk. apāśrayati, apa + ā + <em>sri</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apassita">apassita</a></p>

apassita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>leaning against Ja.ii.69 (tālamūlaṃ = nissāya ṭhita C.).</li><li>depending on, trusting in (c. acc. or loc.) Vv.10#1 (parâgāraṃ = nissita Vv-a.101); Ja.iv.25 (balamhi = balanissita). See also avassita.</li></ol><p>pp. of apasseti</p>

apassāya|apassaaya|apassaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apassaaya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apassayati">apassayati</a></p>

apasāda|apasaada|apasada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaada</span></p><p>putting down, blame, disparagement MN.iii.230.</p><p>fr. apa + <em>sad</em></p>

apasādana|apasaadana|apasadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadana</span></p><p>reproach; disparagement.</p>

apasādayati|apasaadayati|apasadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadayati</span></p><p>dismisses; rebukes, reproaches; disparages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasādesi|apasaadesi|apasadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasādeta|apasaadeta|apasadeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apasādetar">apasādetar</a></p><p>(from apasādeti) one who rebukes; one who disparages.</p>

apasādetabba|apasaadetabba|apasadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasādetar|apasaadetar|apasadetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apasādetar">apasādetar</a></p><p>(from apasādeti) one who rebukes; one who disparages.</p>

apasādeti|apasaadeti|apasadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadeti</span></p><p>dismisses; rebukes, reproaches; disparages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādayati">apasādayati</a></p>

apasādeti|apasaadeti|apasadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to refuse, decline Vin.iv.213, Vin.iv.263; Ja.v.417 (= uyyojeti).</li><li>to depreciate blame, disparage Vin.iii.101; MN.iii.230 (opp. <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81deti">ussādeti</a></em>) DN-a.i.160</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://apas%C4%81dita">apasādita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Caus. of apa + <em>sad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a>, <a href="bword://apasādita">apasādita</a></p>

apasādetvā|apasaadetvaa|apasadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasādita|apasaadita|apasadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadita</span></p><p>dismissed; rebuked; disparaged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasādita|apasaadita|apasadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadita</span></p><p>blamed, reproached, disparaged SN.ii.219; Snp-a.541.</p><p>pp. of apasādeti</p>

apasādiyamāna|apasaadiyamaana|apasadiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr.mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaadiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apasādeti">apasādeti</a></p>

apasāraṇa|apasaara.na|apasarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apasaara.na</span></p><p>removing; taking (a ship) out from land (?):</p>

apatacchika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in <em>khārāpatācch˚</em>; (q.v.) a kind of torture.</p>

apattacīvaraka|apattaciivaraka|apattacivaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apattaciivaraka</span></p><p>(one) who has no bowl or (bhikkhu’s) robe.</p>

apattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>thrown away.</p>

apattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apattha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective thrown away Dhp.149 (= chaḍḍita Dhp-a.iii.112).</p><p>Sk. apāsta, pp. of apa + <em>as<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em></p><p>Apattha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. pret. of pāpunāti (q.v.).</p>

apatthaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>relying on (loc.)</p>

apatthaṭa|apattha.ta|apatthata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apattha.ta</span></p><p>strewn (with), covered.</p>

apatthaṭa|apattha.ta|apatthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apattha.ta</span></p><p>= avatthaṭa covered Thag.759.</p>

apatthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apatthita &amp; Apatthiya</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://pattheti">pattheti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pattheti">pattheti</a></p>

apatthiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Apatthita &amp; Apatthiya</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://pattheti">pattheti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pattheti">pattheti</a></p>

apavadati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>reviles; denies; rejects.</p>

apavadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to reproach, reprove, reject, despise DN.i.122 (= paṭikkhipati DN-a.i.290); SN.v.118 (+ paṭikkosati).</p><p>apa + vadati</p>

apavagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>completion, end, final delivery, Nibbāna; in phrase <em>saggāpavagga</em> Dāvs ii.62; Dāvs iii.75.</p><p>Sk. apavarga</p>

apavahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>carries off or away.</p>

apavahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to carry or drive away; Caus. <em>apavāheti</em> to remove, give up Mil.324 (kaddamaṃ).</p><p>apa + vahati</p>

apavattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to turn away or aside, to go away Ja.iv.347 (variant reading apasakkati).</p><p>apa + <em>vṛt</em>, cp. Lat. āverto</p>

apaviddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>thrown away; rejected, abandoned (often a dead body).</p>

apaviddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>thrown away, rejected, discarded, removed SN.i.202; SN.iii.143; Snp.200 (susānasmiṃ = <em><a href="bword://cha%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dita">chaḍḍita</a></em> Snp-a.250); Thag.635; Dhp.292 (= <em><a href="bword://cha%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dita">chaḍḍita</a></em> Dhp-a.iii.452); Pv.iii.8#2 (susānasmiṃ; so read for T. apaviṭṭha); Ja.i.255; Ja.iii.426; Ja yi.90 (= <em><a href="bword://cha%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dita">chaḍḍita</a></em> C.). Sdhp.366.</p><p>pp. of apavijjhati, Vedic apa + <em>vyadh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chaḍḍita">chaḍḍita</a>, <a href="bword://chaḍḍita">chaḍḍita</a>, <a href="bword://chaḍḍita">chaḍḍita</a></p>

apaviyūhati|apaviyuuhati|apaviyuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaviyuuhati</span></p><p>pushes aside, removes;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabyūhati">apabyūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apabbūhati">apabbūhati</a>, <a href="bword://apabyūheti">apabyūheti</a></p>

apaviyūhati|apaviyuuhati|apaviyuhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaviyuuhati</span></p><p>see <em>appabbūhati</em>.</p>

apaviyūhitvā|apaviyuuhitvaa|apaviyuhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apaviyuuhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaviyūhati">apaviyūhati</a></p>

apaviṇāti|apavi.naati|apavinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavi.naati</span></p><p>is probably misreading for <em>apaciṇāti</em> (see apac˚ 2). As variant reading at Ja.v.339 (anapavinanto) for T. anupacinanto (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by avaloketi C.). Other vv.ll. are anuvi˚ &amp; apavī˚ meaning “not paying attention”. The positive form we find as <em><a href="bword://apav%C4%AB%E1%B9%87ati">apavīṇati</a></em> “to take care of, to pay attention to (c. acc.) at MN.i.324, where Trenckner unwarrantedly assumes a special root <em>veṇ</em> (see Notes p. 781), but the vv.ll. to this passage (see MN.i.557) with apavīṇāti and apacinati confirm the reading apaciṇāti, as does the gloss apaloketi.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavīṇati">apavīṇati</a></p>

apaviṭṭha|apavi.t.tha|apavittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavi.t.tha</span></p><p>at Pv.iii.8#2 is to be read <em><a href="bword://apaviddha">apaviddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaviddha">apaviddha</a></p>

apavyāma|apavyaama|apavyama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavyaama</span></p><p>to the left; placed on the left</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apabyāma">apabyāma</a></p>

apavyāma|apavyaama|apavyama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavyaama</span></p><p>disrespect, neglect, in phrase <em>apayvāmato</em> (apaby˚) <em><a href="bword://karoti">karoti</a></em> to treat disrespectfully, to insult, defile SN.i.226 (variant reading abyāmato; C. explains apabyāmato karitvā abyāmato katvā); Kv.472 (vv.ll. asabyākato abyāto, apabyāto; <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 270 n.1 remarks: “B trsl.: abyāsakato. The Burmese scholar U. Pandi, suggests we should read apabyākato, by which he understands blasphemously”; it is here comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></em>, as at Dhp-a.ii.36); Dhp-a.ii.36 (“want of forbearance” Ed. doubtful reading; vv.ll. appabyāyakamma &amp; apasāma) For further detail see; <em><a href="bword://apasavya">apasavya</a></em>.</p><p>apa + vyāma</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://karoti">karoti</a>, <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a>, <a href="bword://apasavya">apasavya</a></p>

apavyāmato karoti|apavyaamato karoti|apavyamato karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavyaamato karoti</span></p><p>goes round (something or someone) keeping one’s left side towards it or them (a mark of disrespect)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavyāmaṃ karoti">apavyāmaṃ karoti</a>, <a href="bword://apavyāma">apavyāma</a></p>

apavyāmaṃ karoti|apavyaama.m karoti|apavyamam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavyaama.m karoti</span></p><p>goes round (something or someone) keeping one’s left side towards it or them (a mark of disrespect)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apavyāmato karoti">apavyāmato karoti</a>, <a href="bword://apavyāma">apavyāma</a></p>

apavīṇati|apavii.nati|apavinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavii.nati</span></p><p>observes attentively; watches; watches over.</p>

apavīṇati|apavii.nati|apavinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apavii.nati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://apavi%E1%B9%87%C4%81ti">apaviṇāti</a></em> (= apaciṇāti).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaviṇāti">apaviṇāti</a></p>

apayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n. is going away; is retreating.</p>

apayiṃsu|apayi.msu|apayimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayi.msu</span></p><p>had gone away; had retreated.</p>

apayāna|apayaana|apayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayaana</span></p><p>going away, retreat.</p>

apayāna|apayaana|apayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayaana</span></p><p>going away, retreat DN.i.9 (opp. upa˚); DN-a.i.95.</p><p>Sk. apayāna, fr. apayāti</p>

apayāpetabba|apayaapetabba|apayapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayaapetabba</span></p><p>going away; retreating.</p>

apayāpeti|apayaapeti|apayapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayaapeti</span></p><p>makes go, makes retreat; dismisses.</p>

apayāti|apayaati|apayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apayaati</span></p><p>to go away Ja.vi.183 (apāyāti metri causa; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as apagacchati palāyati)<br />caus <em>apayāpeti</em> [Sk. apayāpayati] to make go drive away, dismiss MN.iii.176; SN.ii.119.</p><p>Sk. apayāti, apa + <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: yā</p>

apañjasa|apa~njasa|apanjasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa~njasa</span></p><p>going off one’s correct course.</p>

apaññaka|apa~n~naka|apannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa~n~naka</span></p><p>adjective = apañña, ignorant Dpvs.vi.29.</p>

apaṅga|apa"nga|apanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa"nga</span></p><p>mark or circle drawn on the forehead or at the outer corner of the eye</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāṅga">apāṅga</a>, <a href="bword://avaṅga">avaṅga</a></p>

apaṅga|apa"nga|apanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa"nga</span></p><p>(apānga) the outer corner of the eye Ja.iii.419 (asitâpangin black-eyed); Ja.iv.219 (bahi˚). Spelt <em><a href="bword://ava%E1%B9%85ga">avaṅga</a></em> at Vin.ii.267, where the phrase avangaṃ karoti i.e. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh. ibid p. 327 as “avangadese adhomukhaṃ lekhaṃ karonti”. According to Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>20 Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is not quite correct, since avanga stands here in the meaning of “a coloured mark upon the body (cp. PW. apānga).</p><p>Sk. apānga</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṅga">avaṅga</a></p>

apaṇata|apa.nata|apanata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.nata</span></p><p>bent away; disinclined, averse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apanamati">apanamati</a></p>

apaṇṇaka|apa.n.naka|apannaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.n.naka</span></p><ol><li>not doubtful, certain, sure; having a certain outcome.</li><li>(of a die 1) true; straight-sided, cubical.</li></ol>

apaṇṇaka|apa.n.naka|apannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.n.naka</span></p><p>adjective <a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87aka">paṇṇaka</a>*; Weber Ind. Str. iii.150 &amp; Kuhn, Beitr. p. 53 take it as *a-praśna-ka certain, true, absolute MN.i.401, MN.i.411; AN.v.85, AN.v.294, AN.v.296; Ja.i.104 (where explained as <em>ekaṃsika aviruddha niyyānika</em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paṇṇaka">paṇṇaka</a></p>

apaṇṇakata|apa.n.nakata|apannakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.n.nakata</span></p><p>sure, safe, certain; having a certain outcome.</p>

apaṇṇakaṃ|apa.n.naka.m|apannakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.n.naka.m</span></p><p>surely; certainly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaṇṇaka">apaṇṇaka</a></p>

apaṭṭhapeti|apa.t.thapeti|apatthapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apa.t.thapeti</span></p><p>to put aside, leave out, neglect Ja.iv.308; Ja.v.236.</p><p>Caus. fr. apa-tiṭṭhati, cp. Sk. apa + <em>sthā</em> to stand aloof</p>

apekhamāna|apekhamaana|apekhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekhati">apekhati</a></p>

apekhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks for, longs for, pays regard to (ace. or loc); has reference to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhati">apekkhati</a></p>

apekhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekhin">apekhin</a></p><p>regardful of; looking for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhin">apekkhin</a></p>

apekhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekhin">apekhin</a></p><p>regardful of; looking for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhin">apekkhin</a></p>

apekhā|apekhaa|apekha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekhaa</span></p><p>regard, attention, longing for (loc)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhā">apekkhā</a></p>

apekhā|apekhaa|apekha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekhaa</span></p><p>Apekkhā &amp; Apekhā</p><p>feminine attention, regard, affection for (loc.); desire longing for (c. loc.) SN.i.77; SN.iii.132; SN.v.409 (mātā-pitusu) Vin.iv.214; Snp.38 (= vuccati taṇhā etc. Cnd.65; = taṇhā sineha Snp-a.76); Ja.i.9, Ja.i.141; Thag.558; Dhp.345 (puttesu dāresu ca = taṇhā Dhp-a.iv.56); Dhs.1059, Dhs.1136 (= ālayakaraṇa-vasena apekkhatī ti apekkhā Dhs-a.365, cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 279). Freq. as adj. (-˚or in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>sa˚</em>; and <em>an˚</em>;), viz. Vin.iii.90 (visuddha˚); SN.i.122 (otara˚); <em>sa˚</em> AN.iii.258, AN.iii.433; AN.iv.60 sq.; <em>an˚</em>; without consideration regardless, indifferent SN.v.164; AN.iii.252, AN.iii.347, AN.iii.434; Snp.200 (anapekkhā honti ñātayo); Ja.i.9. Cp. anapekkhin apekkhavant; also B.Sk. avekṣatā.</p><p>Sk. apekṣā, fr. apa + <em>īkṣ</em>. The spelling is either kkh or kh, they are both used promiscuously a tendency towards kh prevailing, as in upekhā, sekha</p>

apekkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective waiting for, looking for SN.i.122 (otāra˚).</p><p>= apekkhā</p>

apekkhamāna|apekkhamaana|apekkhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekkhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhati">apekkhati</a></p>

apekkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks for, longs for, pays regard to (ace. or loc); has reference to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekhati">apekhati</a></p>

apekkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to desire, long for, look for, expect Snp.435 (kāme n’âpekkhate cittaṃ), Snp.773 (ppr. apekkhamāna); Ja.iv.226 (id.); Dhs-a.365. anapekkhamāna paying no attention to (acc.) Snp.59; Ja.v.359.</li><li>[Sk. avīkṣate, ava + <em>īkṣ</em>; see avekkḥati] to consider refer to, look at, ger. apekkhitvā (cp. Sk. avīkṣya) with reference to Vv-a.13</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://apekkhita">apekkhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. apīkṣate, apa + <em>īkṣ</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhita">apekkhita</a></p>

apekkhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekkhavat">apekkhavat</a></p><p>full of longing or desire (for, loc)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhā">apekkhā</a></p>

apekkhavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective full of longing or desire, longing, craving Vin.iv.214; SN.iii.16; Thag.558; Ja.v.453 (= sataṇha); Snp-a.76. Apekkha &amp; Apekha</p><p>fr. apekkhā</p>

apekkhavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekkhavat">apekkhavat</a></p><p>full of longing or desire (for, loc)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekkhā">apekkhā</a></p>

apekkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekkhin">apekkhin</a></p><p>regardful of; looking for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekhin">apekhin</a></p>

apekkhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://apekkhin">apekkhin</a></p><p>regardful of; looking for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekhin">apekhin</a></p>

apekkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective considering, regarding, expecting looking for; usually neg. <em>an˚</em>; indifferent (against) = loc. SN.i.16, SN.i.77; SN.ii.281; SN.iii.19, SN.iii.87; Snp.166 (kāmesu), Snp.823 (id.), Snp.857; Dhp.346. Cp. apekkhavant.</p><p>Sk. apekṣin, but B.Sk. avekṣin, e.g. Jtm.215; fr. apa + <em>īkṣ</em></p>

apekkhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>taken care of, looked after, considered Ja.vi.142, Ja.vi.149 (= olokita C.).</p><p>pp. of apekkhati</p>

apekkhā|apekkhaa|apekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekkhaa</span></p><p>regard, attention, longing for (loc)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apekhā">apekhā</a></p>

apekkhā|apekkhaa|apekkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apekkhaa</span></p><p>Apekkhā &amp; Apekhā</p><p>feminine attention, regard, affection for (loc.); desire longing for (c. loc.) SN.i.77; SN.iii.132; SN.v.409 (mātā-pitusu) Vin.iv.214; Snp.38 (= vuccati taṇhā etc. Cnd.65; = taṇhā sineha Snp-a.76); Ja.i.9, Ja.i.141; Thag.558; Dhp.345 (puttesu dāresu ca = taṇhā Dhp-a.iv.56); Dhs.1059, Dhs.1136 (= ālayakaraṇa-vasena apekkhatī ti apekkhā Dhs-a.365, cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 279). Freq. as adj. (-˚or in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>sa˚</em>; and <em>an˚</em>;), viz. Vin.iii.90 (visuddha˚); SN.i.122 (otara˚); <em>sa˚</em> AN.iii.258, AN.iii.433; AN.iv.60 sq.; <em>an˚</em>; without consideration regardless, indifferent SN.v.164; AN.iii.252, AN.iii.347, AN.iii.434; Snp.200 (anapekkhā honti ñātayo); Ja.i.9. Cp. anapekkhin apekkhavant; also B.Sk. avekṣatā.</p><p>Sk. apekṣā, fr. apa + <em>īkṣ</em>. The spelling is either kkh or kh, they are both used promiscuously a tendency towards kh prevailing, as in upekhā, sekha</p>

apesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i></p><p>kind of gate or hurdle.</p>

apesiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>means of barring a door Vin.ii.154 (Bdhgh. explains on p. 321: apesī ti dīghadārumhi khāṇuke pavesetvā kaṇḍaka-sākhāhi vinandhitvā kataṃ dvāra-tthakanakaṃ).</p><p>? of uncertain origin</p>

apesiyamāna|apesiyamaana|apesiyamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apesiyamaana</span></p><p>adjective not being in service Vin.ii.177.</p><p>ppr. fr. a + peseti (q.v.)</p>

apeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>gone away, departed (from); rid of; deprived of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apeti">apeti</a></p>

apeta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective gone away; (med.) freed of, rid of, deprived of (instr., abl. or ˚-) Dhp.9 (damasaccena); Pv-a.35 (dukkhato); usually ˚-in sense of “without, -less”, e.g. apeta-kaddama free from mud stainless Dhp.95; ˚vattha without dress Ja.v.16; ˚viññāṇa without feeling, senseless Dhp.41; Thig.468; ˚viññāṇattaṃ senselessness, lack of feeling Pv-a.63.</p><p>pp. of apeti</p>

apetalomahaṃsa|apetalomaha.msa|apetalomahamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apetalomaha.msa</span></p><p>with excitement gone</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apeta">apeta</a></p>

apetatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>absence (of) Pv-a.92.</p><p>abstr. to apeta</p>

apetaviññāṇa|apetavi~n~naa.na|apetavinnana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apetavi~n~naa.na</span></p><p>without consciouness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apeta">apeta</a></p>

apeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes away; departs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāyati">apāyati</a>, <a href="bword://apa-iti">apa-iti</a></p>

apeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go away, to disappear DN.i.180 (upeti pi apeti pi) Ja.i.292; Snp.1143 (= n’ apagacchanti na vijahanti Cnd.66)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://apeta">apeta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>apa + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, cp. Gr. α ̓́πειμι, Lat. abeo, Goth. af-iddja</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apeta">apeta</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

apetteyyatā|apetteyyataa|apetteyyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apetteyyataa</span></p><p>in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://amatteyyat%C4%81">amatteyyatā</a></em> irreverence against father and mother DN.iii.70 (cp. Dhp.332 &amp; Dhp-a.iv.34).</p><p>a + petteyyatā, abstr. fr. *paitṛya fatherly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://amatteyyatā">amatteyyatā</a></p>

apeyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be drunk, not drinkable Ja.vi.205 (sāgara).</p><p>a + peyya, grd. of <em>pā</em></p>

aphegguka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not weak, i.e. strong Ja.iii.318.</p><p>a + pheggu + ka</p>

aphusa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>not to be touched Mil.157 (trsl. unchangeable by other circumstances; Tr. on p. 425 remarks “aphusāni kiriyāni seems wrong, at any rate it is unintelligible to me”).</p><p>Sk. *aspṛśya, a + grd. of phusati to touch</p>

api ca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>and yet, nevertheless; but, however; moreover</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api ce
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>even if</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api hi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>although</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><ol><li><p>even, also; even though, although;</p><ol><li>prothetic (at the beginning of a sentence or clause)</li><li>enclitic</li></ol></li><li><p>but, and (at the beginning of a clause)</p></li><li><p>as interrogative particle (usually at the beginning of a sentence or clause)</p></li><li><p>as emphasis to optative: perhaps, maybe; please may…; if only; I wish that…</p></li><li><p>indeed, surely; yes (at the beginning of a sentence).</p></li></ol>

api nu kho
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>interrogative particle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api nu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>interrogative particle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api nūna|api nuuna|api nuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api nuuna</span></p><p>interrogative particle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable both prep. &amp; conj., orig meaning “close by”, then as prep. “towards, to, on to on” and as adv. “later, and, moreover”.</p><ol><li><p>(prep. pref.)</p><ol><li>prep. c. loc.: <em>api ratte</em> later on in the night (q.v.)-</li><li>pref.: <em><a href="bword://apidh%C4%81na">apidhāna</a></em> putting on to; <em>apiḷahati</em> bind on to, apihita (= Gr. ἐπιχετός, epithet) put on to (q.v.).</li></ol></li><li><p>(conj. &amp; part.).</p><ol><li><p>in affirmative sentences meaning primarily “moreover, further, and then, even”</p><ol><li><p>(single) <em>prothetic:</em> api dibbesu kāmesu even in heavenly joys Dhp.187; ko disvā na pasīdeyya api kaṇhâbhijātiko even an unfortunate-born Snp.563 api yojanāni gacchāma, even for leagues we go Pv.iv.10#7 (= anekāni yojanāni pi g. Pv-a.270. <em>Epithetic</em> (more freq. in the form <em><a href="bword://pi">pi</a></em>): muhuttam api even a little while Dhp.106, Dhp.107 aham api daṭṭhukāmo I also wish to see Snp.685. Out of prothetic use (= even = even if) develops the conditional meaning of “if”, as in api sakkuṇemu (and then we may = if we may) Ja.v.24 (c. = api nāma sakkuṇeyyāma see further under β app’eva nāma)<br />■ <em>api-api</em> in correlation corresponds to Lat. et-et Sk ca-ca, meaning both… and, and… as well as, &amp; is esp. freq. in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>app’ ekacce… app’ ekacce</em> (and) some… and others i.e. some… others [<em>not</em> with Kern</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. to <em>appa!</em> ] e.g. at DN.i.118; Thig.216; Vv-a.208, etc. <em>-app’ ekadā</em> “morever once” = sometimes Vin.iv.178; SN.i.162; SN.iv.111; Ja.i.67; Dhp-a.iii.303, etc</p></li><li><p>(in combination with other emphatic or executive particles) <em>[api ca](bword://api ca)</em> further, and also moreover DN.i.96; Mil.25, Mil.47. <em>-<em>api ca kho</em></em> moreover and yet, still, all the same Iti.89 (+ pana variant reading); Mil.20, Mil.239. <em>-api ca kho pana</em> all the same, never mind nevertheless Ja.i.253. <em>-api ssu</em> so much so Vin.ii.76 <em>-app’ eva nāma</em> (with pot.) (either) surely, indeed, yes I reckon, (or) I presume, it is likely that, perhaps Vin.i.16 (surely); Vin.ii.85 (id.); cp. pi DN.i.205 (sve pi upasaṃkameyyāma tomorrow I shall surely come along), DN.i.226 (siyā thus shall it be); MN.i.460 = Iti.89 (moreover, indeed) Ja.i.168 (surely) Vin.ii.262 (perhaps) Ja.v.421 (id., piyavācaṃ labheyyāma)</p></li></ol></li><li><p>in interrog<br />■ dubit. sentences as part. of interrog. (w. indic. or pot.) corresponding to Lat. nonne, i e. awaiting an affirmative answer (“not, not then”): api Yasaṃ kulaputtaṃ passeyya do you not see… Vin.i.16; api samaṇa balivadde addasā have you not then seen… SN.i.115; api kiñci labhāmase shall we then not get anything? Ja.iii.26; api me pitaraṃ passatha do you then not see my father? Pv-a.38<br />■ Also comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with other interr. part. e.g. <em>[api nu](bword://api nu)</em> Ja.ii.415.</p></li></ol></li></ol><p>Sk. api &amp; pi; Idg. *epi *pi *opi; cp. Gr. ε ̓́πι on to, ο ̓́πι (ο ̓́πιχεν behind, ὀπίσσα back = close at one’s heels); Lat. ob. in certain functions; Goth. iftuma. The assimil. form before vowels is <em>app˚</em>; (= Sk. apy˚) See further details under <em><a href="bword://pi">pi</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apidhāna">apidhāna</a>, <a href="bword://pi">pi</a>, <a href="bword://api ca">api ca</a>, api ca kho, <a href="bword://api nu">api nu</a>, <a href="bword://pi">pi</a></p>

api ssu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>and further, moreover</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

api ssudaṃ|api ssuda.m|api ssudam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api ssuda.m</span></p><p>and further, moreover</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

apidahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to put on (see <em><a href="bword://api">api</a></em> 1b), to cover up, obstruct, Ja.v.60 (inf. apidhetuṃ) pp. <em><a href="bword://apihita">apihita</a></em>, Pass. <em><a href="bword://apith%C4%AByati">apithīyati</a></em>, Der. apidhāna (q.v.).</p><p>api + <em>dhā</em>, cp. Gr. ἐπιτἱχημι</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a>, <a href="bword://apihita">apihita</a>, <a href="bword://apithīyati">apithīyati</a></p>

apidhāna|apidhaana|apidhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apidhaana</span></p><p>cover; a lid.</p>

apidhāna|apidhaana|apidhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apidhaana</span></p><p>cover, lid Vin.i.203, Vin.i.204; Vin.ii.122. See apidahati.</p><p>Vedic apidhāna in same meaning</p>

apiha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “unhankering (Mrs Rh. D.) SN.i.181 (+ akankha; variant reading BB asita).</p><p>apihālu? a + piha, uncertain origin, see next. Morris J<em>P.I.S.</em> 1886 takes it as a + spṛha</p>

apihita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>covered Ja.iv.4.</p><p>pp. of apidahati</p>

apihālu|apihaalu|apihalu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apihaalu</span></p><p>without desire; not envious.</p>

apihālu|apihaalu|apihalu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apihaalu</span></p><p>adjective not hankering, free from craving, not greedy SN.i.187 = Thag.1218 (akuhako nipako apihālu); Snp.852 (+ amaccharin, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Snp-a.549 as apihana-sīlo, patthanātanhāya rahito ti vuttaṃ hoti, thus perhaps taking it as a + pi (= api) + hana (fr. <em>dhā</em>, cp. pidahati &amp; pihita) cp. also Cnd.227).</p><p>a + pihālu, analysed by Fausböll Sn. Gloss. p. 229 as a-spṛhayālu, but Bdhgh evidently different (see below)</p>

apilāpana|apilaapana|apilapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apilaapana</span></p><p>counting up, repetition [Kern, <em>Toev</em>, s.v. gives der. fr. a + plāvana] Ne.15, Ne.28 Ne.54; Mil.37.</p><p>fr. api + <em>lap</em></p>

apilāpanatā|apilaapanataa|apilapanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apilaapanataa</span></p><p>in the pass. at Dhs.14 = Cnd.628 is evidently meant to be taken as a + pilāpana + tā (fr pilavati, <em>plu</em>), but whether the der. &amp; interpret. of Dhs A is correct, we are unable to say. On general principles it looks like popular etym. Mrs. Rh. D. translates (p. 16 “opposite of superficiality” (lit “not floating”); see her detailed note <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 16.</p>

apilāpati|apilaapati|apilapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apilaapati</span></p><p>recites.</p>

apilāpeti|apilaapeti|apilapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apilaapeti</span></p><p>“to talk close by”, i.e. to count up, recite, or: talk idly, boast of Mil.37 (sāpatheyyaṃ).</p><p>api + <em>lap</em></p>

apiratte
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>later in the night Ja.vi.560.</p><p>read api ratte, see <em><a href="bword://api">api</a></em> 1a</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://api">api</a></p>

apithīyati|apithiiyati|apithiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apithiiyati</span></p><p>Pass. of <em><a href="bword://apidahati">apidahati</a></em> to be obstructed, covered, barred, obscured Ja.ii.158. See also <strong>pithīyati</strong>.</p><p>for apidhīyati; api + <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apidahati">apidahati</a>, pithīyati</p>

apitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective fatherless Ja.v.251.</p><p>a + pitika</p>

apiḷahati|api.lahati|apilahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api.lahati</span></p><p>Apiḷahati &amp; Apiḷandhati</p><p>to tie on, fasten, bind together; to adorn oneself with (acc.) Ja.v.400 (ger. apiḷayha = piḷandhitvā C.)-Cp. apiḷandhana &amp; pp apiladdha.</p><p>Sk. apinahyati, on n: ḷ see note on gala, &amp; cp. guṇa: guḷa, veṇu: veḷu etc. On ndh for yh see avanandhati</p>

apiḷandha|api.landha|apilandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api.landha</span></p><p>adjective at Vv.36#1 should be read as <em>apiḷaddha</em> (= Sk. apinaddha) pp. of apiḷandhati (apiḷandhati) “adorned with”, or (with variant reading SS) as <em><a href="bword://api%E1%B8%B7andhana">apiḷandhana</a></em>; Vv-a.167 explains by analankata, mistaking the <em><a href="bword://a">a</a></em> of api for a negation.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apiḷandhana">apiḷandhana</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a></p>

apiḷandhana|api.landhana|apilandhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api.landhana</span></p><p>that which is tied on, i.e band, ornament, apparel, parure Vv.64#10, Vv.64#18 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> inacurately at Vv-a.279 by; a-kāro nipātamattaṃ, pilandhanaṃ = ābhāraṇaṃ); Ja.vi.472 (c. pilandhituṃ pi ayuttaṃ?). Apilahati &amp; Apilandhati;</p><p>fr. apiḷandhati, also in shorter (&amp; more usual) form; <em>piḷandhana</em>, q.v.</p>

apiḷandhati|api.landhati|apilandhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">api.landhati</span></p><p>Apiḷahati &amp; Apiḷandhati</p><p>to tie on, fasten, bind together; to adorn oneself with (acc.) Ja.v.400 (ger. apiḷayha = piḷandhitvā C.)-Cp. apiḷandhana &amp; pp apiladdha.</p><p>Sk. apinahyati, on n: ḷ see note on gala, &amp; cp. guṇa: guḷa, veṇu: veḷu etc. On ndh for yh see avanandhati</p>

appa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) small, little; trifling; few; rare; iic: very little, next to nothing (almost equivalent to a negative).</li><li>(n.) a little, a small amount; a trifling thing.</li></ol>

appa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective small, little, insignificant, often in the sense of “very little = (next to) nothing” (so in most compounds); thus expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.334 as equivalent to a <em>negative</em> part. (see <em><a href="bword://appodaka">appodaka</a></em>) DN.i.61 (opp. mahant, DN-a.i.170 parittaka); Snp.713, Snp.775, Snp.805, Snp.896 (= appaka, omaka thoka, lamaka, jatukka, parittaka Mnd.306); Dhp.174; Ja.i.262; Pp.39<br />■ nt. <em>appaṃ</em> a little, a small portion, a trifle; pl. appāni small things, trifles AN.ii.26 = Iti.102; AN.ii.138; Dhp.20 (= thokaṃ eka-vagga-dvi-vagga-mattam pi Dhp-a.i.158), Dhp.224 (˚smiṃ yācito asked for little), Dhp.259.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a></em> of little value (opp. mahaggha priceless) Ja.i.9 Pp.33; Dhp-a.iv.184.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ass%C4%81da">assāda</a></em> [BSk. alpâsvāda, cp. Divy.224 = Dhp.186; alpa + ā + <em>svād</em> ] of little taste or enjoyment affording little pleasure (always used of kāmā Vin.ii.25 = MN.i.130 = AN.iii.97 = Cnd.71; Snp.61; Dhp.186 (= supina-sadisatāya paritta-sukha Dhp-a.iii.240); Thig.358 (= Thag-a.244); Ja.ii.313; Vism.124.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81ta%E1%B9%85ka">ātaṅka</a></em> little (or no illness, freedom from illness, good health (= appābādha with which often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) <a href="bword://%C4%81b%C4%81dha">ābādha</a>* same as appātanka (q.v.) DN.i.204; DN.iii.166, DN.iii.237; MN.ii.125; AN.i.25; AN.ii.88; AN.iii.30, AN.iii.65 sq., AN.iii.103, AN.iii.153; Pv.iv.1#44;</li><li><em>-ābādhatā</em> id. <a href="bword://%C4%81yuka">āyuka</a>* short lived DN.i.18; Pv-a.103, Also as <em>-āyukin</em> Vv.41#6.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81h%C4%81ra">āhāra</a></em> taking little or no food, fasting MN.ii.5; Snp.165 (= ekāsana-bhojitāya ca parimita-bhojitāya ca Snp-a.207), Also as <em>-āhāratā</em> MN.i.245; MN.ii.5.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a></em> having little or no water, dry Snp.777 (macche va appodake khīṇasote = parittodake Mnd.50); Vv.84#3 (+ appabhakkha expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.334 as “appa-saddo h’ ettha abhāvattho appiccho appanigghoso ti ādisu viya”); Ja.i.70; Dhp-a.iv.12</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kasira">kasira</a></em> in instr. ˚kasirena with little or no difficulty DN.i.251; SN.v.51; Thag.16.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kicca">kicca</a></em> having few duties, free from obligations, free from care Snp.144 (= appaṃ kiccaṃ assā ti Kp-a.241).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gandha">gandha</a></em> not smelling or having a bad smell Mil.252 (opp. sugandha).</li><li><em>-ṭṭha</em> “standing in little” i.e. connected with little trouble DN.i.143; AN.i.169.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://th%C4%81maka">thāmaka</a></em> having little or no strength, weak SN.iv.206.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassa">dassa</a></em> having little knowledge or wisdom Snp.1134 (see Cnd.69 expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by paritta-pañña Snp-a.605).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nigghosa">nigghosa</a></em> with little sound, quiet, still, soundless (cp. Vv-a.334, as quoted above under ˚odaka) AN.v.15 (+ appasadda); Snp.338; Mnd.377; Mil.371.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1%C3%B1a">pañña</a></em>, of little wisdom Ja.ii.166; Ja.iii.223 Ja.iii.263.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pu%C3%B1%C3%B1a">puñña</a></em> of little merit MN.ii.5.</li><li><em>-puññatā</em> having little merit, unworthiness Pv.iv.10#7.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phalat%C4%81">phalatā</a></em> bringing little fruit Pv-a.139.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhakkha">bhakkha</a></em> having little or nothing to eat Vv.84#3.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhoga">bhoga</a></em> having little wealth, i.e. poor, indigent Snp.114 (= sannicitānaṃ ca bhogānaṃ āyamukhassa ca abhāvato Snp-a.173).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ma%C3%B1%C3%B1ati">maññati</a></em> to consider as small, to underrate: see separately.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://matta">matta</a></em> little, slight, mean (usually as ˚ka; not to be confounded with appamatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> AN.iii.275; Ja.i.242; also meaning “contented with little (of the bhikkhu) Iti.103 = AN.ii.27; f.</li><li><em>-ā</em> trifle, smallness însignificance DN.i.91; DN-a.i.55.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mattaka">mattaka</a></em> small, insignificant trifling, nt. a trifle (cp. ˚matta) Vin.1, Vin.213; Vin.ii.177 (˚vissajjaka the distributor of little things, cp. AN.iii.275 &amp; Vin.iv.38, Vin.iv.155); DN.i.3 (= appamattā etassā ti appamattakaṃ DN-a.i.55); Ja.i.167; Ja.iii.12 (= aṇu); Pv-a.262</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://middha">middha</a></em> “little slothful”, i.e. diligent, alert Mil.412</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rajakkha">rajakkha</a></em> having little or no obtuseness DN.ii.37; MN.i.169 Sdhp.519.</li><li><em>-ssaka</em> having little of one’s own, possessing little AN.i.261; AN.ii.203.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a></em> having few or no companious lonely, alone Dhp.123.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> free from noise quiet MN.ii.2, MN.ii.23, MN.ii.30; AN.v.15; Snp.925 (= appanigghosa Mnd.377); Pp.35; Mil.371.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://siddhika">siddhika</a></em> bringing little success or welfare, dangerous Ja.iv.4 (= mandasiddhi vināsabahula C.); Ja.vi.34 (samuddo a. bahu-antarāyiko)</li><li><em>-ssuta</em> possessing small knowledge, ignorant, uneducated DN.i.93 (opp. bahussuta); DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; SN.iv.242; Iti.59; Dhp.152; Pp.20, Pp.62; Dhs.1327.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://harita">harita</a></em> having little or no grass SN.i.169; Snp.p.15 (= paritta-harita-tiṇa Snp-a.154).</li></ul><p>Vedic alpa, cp. Gr. ἀλαπάζω (λαπάζω) to empty (to make little), ἀλαπαδνός weak; Lith. alpnas weak alpstù to faint</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appodaka">appodaka</a>, <a href="bword://aggha">aggha</a>, <a href="bword://assāda">assāda</a>, <a href="bword://ātaṅka">ātaṅka</a>, <a href="bword://ābādha">ābādha</a>, <a href="bword://āyuka">āyuka</a>, <a href="bword://āhāra">āhāra</a>, <a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a>, <a href="bword://kasira">kasira</a>, <a href="bword://kicca">kicca</a>, <a href="bword://gandha">gandha</a>, <a href="bword://thāmaka">thāmaka</a>, <a href="bword://dassa">dassa</a>, <a href="bword://nigghosa">nigghosa</a>, <a href="bword://pañña">pañña</a>, <a href="bword://puñña">puñña</a>, <a href="bword://phalatā">phalatā</a>, <a href="bword://bhakkha">bhakkha</a>, <a href="bword://bhoga">bhoga</a>, <a href="bword://maññati">maññati</a>, <a href="bword://matta">matta</a>, <a href="bword://mattaka">mattaka</a>, <a href="bword://middha">middha</a>, <a href="bword://rajakkha">rajakkha</a>, <a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://siddhika">siddhika</a>, <a href="bword://harita">harita</a></p>

appabhassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>talking little</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appabhikkhuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with few <em>bhikkhus</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appabhoti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p><em>(Appahoti)</em> see <em><a href="bword://pahoti">pahoti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pahoti">pahoti</a></p>

appaccaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(n.) discontent, dissatisfaction, dejection, sulkiness DN.i.3 (= appatītā honti tena atuṭṭhā asomanassitā ti appacayo; domanass’ etaṃ adhivacanaṃ DN-a.i.52); DN.iii.159; MN.i.442; AN.i.79, AN.i.124, AN.i.187; AN.ii.203 AN.iii.181 sq.; AN.iv.168, AN.iv.193; Ja.ii.277; Snp.p.92 (kapa dosa + appacaya); Vv.83#31 (= domanassaṃ Vv-a.343) Snp-a.423 (= appatītaṃ domanassaṃ).</li><li>(adj.) unconditioned Dhs.1084, Dhs.1437.</li></ol><p>a + paccaya</p>

appadassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of little vision; of little knowledge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appadhaṃsa|appadha.msa|appadhamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msa</span></p><p>adjective not to be destroyed Ja.iv.344 (variant reading duppadhaṃsa).</p><p>= appadhaṃsiya, Sk. apradhvaṃsya</p>

appadhaṃsika|appadha.msika|appadhamsika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msika</span></p><p>no to be destroyed; invincible</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appadhaṃsiya">appadhaṃsiya</a></p>

appadhaṃsika|appadha.msika|appadhamsika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msika</span></p><p>Appadhaṃsika &amp; ˚iya</p><p>(adj.) not to be violated or destroyed, inconquerable, indestructible DN.iii.175 (˚ika, variant reading ˚iya); Ja.iii.159 (˚iya); Vv-a.208 (˚iya); Pv-a.117 (˚iya). Cp. <em><a href="bword://appadha%E1%B9%83sa">appadhaṃsa</a></em>.</p><p>grd. of a + padhaṃseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appadhaṃsa">appadhaṃsa</a></p>

appadhaṃsita|appadha.msita|appadhamsita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msita</span></p><p>adjective not violated, unhurt, not offended Vin.iv.229.</p><p>pp. of a + padhaṃseti</p>

appadhaṃsiya|appadha.msiya|appadhamsiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msiya</span></p><p>no to be destroyed; invincible</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appadhaṃsika">appadhaṃsika</a></p>

appadhaṃsiya|appadha.msiya|appadhamsiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadha.msiya</span></p><p>Appadhaṃsika &amp; ˚iya</p><p>(adj.) not to be violated or destroyed, inconquerable, indestructible DN.iii.175 (˚ika, variant reading ˚iya); Ja.iii.159 (˚iya); Vv-a.208 (˚iya); Pv-a.117 (˚iya). Cp. <em><a href="bword://appadha%E1%B9%83sa">appadhaṃsa</a></em>.</p><p>grd. of a + padhaṃseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appadhaṃsa">appadhaṃsa</a></p>

appaduṭṭha|appadu.t.tha|appaduttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appadu.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective not corrupt, faultless, of good behaviour Snp.662 (= padosâbhāvena a. Snp-a.478) Dhp.137 (= niraparādha Dhp-a.iii.70).</p><p>a + paduṭṭha</p>

appagabbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unobtrusive, free from boldness, modest SN.ii.198 = Mil.389, Snp.144, Snp.852 (cp Mnd.228 &amp; Kp-a.232); Dhp.245.</p><p>a + pagabbha</p>

appaggha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of little value or price</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appaharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) with few or no green plants.</li><li>(n.) a place where no green plants grow</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appahīna|appahiina|appahina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appahiina</span></p><p>adjective not given up, not renounced MN.i.386; Iti.56, Iti.57; Cnd.70<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Pp.12, Pp.18.</p><p>a + pahīna, pp. of pahāyati</p>

appaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā &amp; -ikā)n. &amp; neuter</p><ol><li>(mfn.) little, small, insignificant; rare; (pl.) few.</li><li>(n.) a little; a small amount.</li></ol>

appaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective little, small, trifling; pl. few. nt. <em>-ṃ</em> adv. a little DN.ii.4; AN.v.232 sq., AN.v.253 sq.; Snp.909 (opp. bahu); Dhp.85 (appakā = thokā na bahū Dhp-a.ii.160); Pv.i.10#2 (= paritta Pv-a.48); Pv.ii.9#39; Pp.62; Pv-a.6, Pv-a.60 (= paritta). f. <em><a href="bword://appik%C4%81">appikā</a></em> Ja.i.228<br />■ instr. <em>appakena</em> by little, i.e. easily DN-a.i.256. <em>-<a href="bword://anappaka">anappaka</a></em> not little, i.e. much, considerable, great; pl. many SN.iv.46; Dhp.144; Pv.i.11#7 (= bahū Pv-a.58); Pv-a.24, Pv-a.25 (read anappake pi for T. ˚appakeci; so also Kp-a.208).</p><p>appa + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appikā">appikā</a>, <a href="bword://anappaka">anappaka</a></p>

appakasirena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>with little difficulty</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appakicca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with few duties or cares; involving few duties or tasks</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appakiṇṇa|appaki.n.na|appakinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appaki.n.na</span></p><p>not crowded.</p>

appakiṇṇa|appaki.n.na|appakinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appaki.n.na</span></p><p>little or not crowded, not overheaped AN.v.15 (C. anākiṇṇa).</p><p>appa + kiṇṇa, although in formation also = a + pakiṇṇa</p>

appakāra|appakaara|appakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appakaara</span></p><p>adjective not of natural form, of bad appearance, ugly, deformed Ja.v.69 (= sarīrappakāra-rahita dussaṇṭhāna C.). Cp. apākatika.</p><p>a + pakāra</p>

appalābha|appalaabha|appalabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appalaabha</span></p><p>receiving little gain or profit</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of small measure, little, insignificant</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Appamatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective see <em><a href="bword://appa">appa</a></em>.</p><p>appa + matta</p><p>Appamatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective not negligent, i.e. diligent, careful, heedful, vigilant, alert zealous MN.i.391–MN.i.92; SN.i.4; Snp.223 (cp. Kp-a.169), Snp.507 Snp.779 (cp. Mnd.59); Dhp.22 (cp. Dhp-a.i.229); Thig.338 = upaṭṭhitasati Thag-a.239).</p><p>a + pamatta, pp. of pamadati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā &amp; mattikā1)n. &amp; neuter</p><ol><li>(mfn.) little, slight, insignificant.</li><li>(n.) a trifle; a little thing</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with little clay</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamattā|appamattaa|appamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamattaa</span></p><p>small matter; a trifle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appamaññati|appama~n~nati|appamannati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appama~n~nati</span></p><p>thinks little of; underrates, despises.</p>

appamaññati|appama~n~nati|appamannati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appama~n~nati</span></p><p>to think little of, to underrate, despise Dhp.121 (= avajānāti Dhp-a.iii.16; variant reading avapamaññati).</p><p>appa + maññati</p>

appamaññā|appama~n~naa|appamanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appama~n~naa</span></p><p>boundlessness, infinitude (as name for the brahmavihāras)</p>

appamaññā|appama~n~naa|appamanna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appama~n~naa</span></p><p><strong>Appamaññā:</strong> The 4 ‘Infinite States’, identical with the 4 <em>brahma-vihāra</em></p>

appamaññā|appama~n~naa|appamanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appama~n~naa</span></p><p>boundlessness, infinitude, as psych. t. t. appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in later books to the four varieties of philanthropy, viz <em>mettā karuṃā muditā upekkhā</em> i.e. love, pity, sympathy desinterestedness, and as such enumerated at DN.iii.223 (q. v for detailed ref. as to var. passages); Pts.i.84; Vb.272 sq.; Dhs-a.195. By itself at Snp.507 (= mettajjhānasankhātā a. Snp-a.417). See for further expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --><em>Dhs trsl.</em> p. 66 and mettā.</p><p>a + pamaññā, abstr. fr. pamāṇa = Sk. *pramānya</p>

appameyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>immeasurable; infinite, boundless.</p>

appameyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective immeasurable, infinite, boundless MN.i.386; SN.v.400; AN.i.266; Thag.1089 (an˚); Pp.35; Mil.331 Sdhp.338.</p><p>a + pameyya = Sk. aprameya, grd. of a + pra + <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

appamāda|appamaada|appamada
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaada</span></p><p><strong>Appamāda:</strong> Alertness, attentiveness, carefulness, non-laxity, earnestness, diligence, vigilance, is considered as the foundation of all advantageous progress.</p><p>Just as all the footprints of living beings are surpassed by the footprint of the elephant, and the footprint of the elephant is considered as the mightiest amongst them, just so have all the meritorious qualities alertness as their foundation, and alertness is considered as the mightiest of these qualities A. X, 15.</p><p>Cf. the Chapter on alertness Appamāda Vagga in Dhp., and the Buddha’s last exhortation: Transient are all constructions. Be Alert &amp; Train! <em>appamādenasampādetha</em> D. 16 - In the commentaries, it is often explained as the presence lit. ‘non-absence’ of awareness <em>satiyāavippavāsa.</em></p>

appamāda|appamaada|appamada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaada</span></p><p>thoughtfulness, carefulness, conscientiousness, watchfulness, vigilance, earnestness, zeal DN.i.13 (: a. vuccati satiyā avippavāso DN-a.i.104); DN.iii.30 DN.iii.104 sq., DN.iii.112, DN.iii.244, DN.iii.248, DN.iii.272; MN.i.477 (˚phala); SN.i.25 SN.i.86, SN.i.158, SN.i.214; SN.ii.29, SN.ii.132; SN.iv.78 (˚vihārin), SN.iv.97, SN.iv.125, SN.iv.252 sq.; SN.v.30 sq. (˚sampadā), SN.v.41 sq., SN.v.91, SN.v.135, SN.v.240, SN.v.250, SN.v.308 SN.v.350; AN.i.16, AN.i.50. (˚adhigata); AN.iii.330, AN.iii.364, AN.iii.449; AN.iv.28 (˚gāravatā), AN.iv.120 (˚ṃ garu-karoti); AN.v.21, AN.v.126 (kusalesu dhammesu); Snp.184, Snp.264, Snp.334 (= sati-avippavāsa-sankhāta a. Snp-a.339); Iti.16 (˚ṃ pasaṃsanti puññakiriyāsu paṇḍitā), Iti.74 (˚vihārin); Dhp.57 (˚vihārin, cp. Dhp-a.i.434); Dhp.327 (˚rata = satiyā avippavāse abhirata Dhp-a.iv.26); Dāvs ii.35; Kp-a.142.</p><p>a + pamāda</p>

appamāna-ceto-vimutti|appamaana-ceto-vimutti|appamana-ceto-vimutti
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaana-ceto-vimutti</span></p><p><strong>Appamāna-ceto-vimutti:</strong> Infinite mental release see: <em>ceto-vimutti</em></p><p><strong>Appamāna-subha:</strong> a kind of divine being: see: <em>deva</em> II.</p>

appamānābha|appamaanaabha|appamanabha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaanaabha</span></p><p><strong>Appamānābha:</strong> a kind of divine being; see: <em>deva</em> II.</p>

appamāṇa|appamaa.na|appamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaa.na</span></p><p>(freq. spelled <em>appamāna</em>) adjective</p><ol><li>“without measure”, immeasurable, endless, boundless unlimited, unrestricted all-permeating SN.iv.186 (˚cetaso) AN.ii.73; AN.v.63; Snp.507 (mettaṃ cittaṃ bhāvayaṃ appāmāṇaṃ = anavasesa-pharaṇena Snp-a.417; cp. appamaññā) Iti.21 (mettā), Iti.78; Ja.ii.61; Pts.ii.126 sq.; Vb.16, Vb.24 Vb.49, Vb.62, Vb.326 sq.; Dhs.182, Dhs.1021, Dhs.1024, Dhs.1405; Dhs-a.45 Dhs-a.196 (˚gocara, cp. anantagocara). See also on term <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 60.</li><li>“without difference”, irrelevant, in general (in commentary style) Ja.i.165; Ja.ii.323.</li></ol><p>a + pamāṇa</p>

appamāṇasubhā|appamaa.nasubhaa|appamanasubha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaa.nasubhaa</span></p><p>A class of devas of the Rupaloka belonging to the plane of third <em>jhāna</em>.</p><p>Beings are born there who are possessed of faith, virtue, learning, munificence and wisdom. MN.iii.102</p>

appamāṇābhā|appamaa.naabhaa|appamanabha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appamaa.naabhaa</span></p><p>A class of devas of the Rūpaloka, belonging to the plane of second <em>jhāna</em>.</p><p>Beings are born there who have absorbed the idea of boundless brilliancy, MN.iii.147 or who are possessed of faith, virtue, learning, munificence and wisdom. MN.iii.102</p>

appanihita-vimokkha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Appanihita-vimokkha:</strong> D esireless mental release see: *vimokkha</p><p>_ <strong>Appanihitānupassanā:</strong> Contemplation of desirelessness see: _vipassanā*</p>

appanā|appanaa|appana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appanaa</span></p><p>determining, fixing; esp. the fixing of thought on an object, complete concentration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appaṇā">appaṇā</a></p>

appanā|appanaa|appana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appanaa</span></p><p><a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a>* application (of mind), ecstasy,fixing of thought on an object conception (as psychol. t. t.) Ja.ii.61 (˚patta); Mil.62 (of vitakka); Dhs.7, Dhs.21, Dhs.298; Vism.144 (˚samādhi); Dhs-a.55, Dhs-a.142 (def. by Bdhg. as “ekaggaṃ cittaṃ ārammaṇe appeti”), Dhs-a.214 (˚jhāna). See on term <em>Cpd.</em> pp. 56 sq., 68 129, 215; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> xxviii.10, 53, 82, 347.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appeti">appeti</a></p>

appanā-samādhi|appanaa-samaadhi|appana-samadhi
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appanaa-samaadhi</span></p><p><strong>Appanā-samādhi:</strong> Absorption concentration or full concentration from <em>apeti</em> to fix, is the concentration existing during absorption <em>jhāna</em>, whilst the neighbourhood or access-concentration <em>upacāra</em> <em>samādhi</em> only approaches the 1st absorption without attaining it; see: <em>samādhi</em></p>

appapurisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i></p><p>with few men, with few males</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appapurisaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i></p><p>with few men, with few males</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

apparajakkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with little or no passion or impurity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

apparajakkhajātika|apparajakkhajaatika|apparajakkhajatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">apparajakkhajaatika</span></p><p>being by nature free from passion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apparajakkha">apparajakkha</a></p>

appasacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>little learning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appasadda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>appasadda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn.</p><ol><li>free from noise quiet.</li><li>making little or no noise quiet.</li></ol><p>appasadda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter quiet</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appasattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with a small caravan</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appassaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having little property</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appassuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>appassuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. of little learning, uneducated.</p><p>appassuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter little learning, ignorance</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appasuta">appasuta</a>, <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appassāda|appassaada|appassada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">appassaada</span></p><p>of little enjoyment; affording little pleasure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

appassāda|appassaada|appassada
