avaca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective low, only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>uccāvacā</em> (pl.) high and low, see <em><a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></em>. Kvu-a.38.</p><p>der. fr. ava after the analogy of ucca → ut</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

avacana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>“non-word”, i.e. the wrong word or expression Ja.i.410.</p><p>a + vacana</p>

avacara
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Avacara:</strong> S phere, realm, level or dimension. The 3 levels of existence are: the sense-level <em>kāmāvacara</em>, the fine-material level <em>rūpāvacara</em>, the formless level <em>arūpāvacara</em>. Which things are of the sense-level <em>kāmāvacara</em>? Whatever things exist within the interval bounded beneath by the <em>Avīci</em> hell and above by the <em>paranimmitavasavatti</em> heaven (see: <em>deva</em> ), being therein included, to wit: the groups of existence, the elements, sources (see: <em>khandha</em> <em>dhātu</em> <em>āyatana )</em> #āyatana, form, feeling, perception, mental constructions and consciousness, all these things are of the sense-level. But which things are then of the fine material level <em>rūpāvacara</em>? Whatever things exist within the interval bounded beneath by the Brahma-world and above by the <em>akanittha</em> world (see: <em>deva</em> ), having therein their level, and being therein included… and also consciousness and mental properties in one who has entered the fine-material absorptions, or who has been reborn at that level, or who already during his life-time is living in happiness of the absorptions, all these things are of the fine-material level. Which things are of the formless level <em>arūpāvacara</em>? Consciousness and mental properties arising within the interval bounded beneath by the beings reborn in the level of unbounded space and above by the beings reborn at the level of neither-perception-nor-non-perception (see: <em>jhāna</em> 5-8), and the consciousness and mental properties in one who has entered the formless absorptions, or who has been reborn at that level, or who already during his lifetime is living in happiness of the formless absorptions, all these things are of the formless level. Cf. Dhs. se e: 1280, 1282, 1284; Vibh. XVIII. App.</p>

avacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) neuter adjective</p><ol><li>(adj.) living in or with, moving in DN.i.206 (santika˚ one who stays near, a companion); fig. dealing or familiar with, at home in AN.ii.189 (atakka˚); AN.iv.314 (parisā˚); Ja.i.60 (tāḷa˚ one conversant with music, a musician see *<a href="bword://t%C4%81%E1%B8%B7a">tāḷa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); Ja.ii.95 (sangāma˚); Mil.44 (id. and yoga˚)</li><li>(n.) sphere (of moving or activity), realm, plane (of temporal existence); only as t.t. in <em>kāmāvacara rupāvacara arūpāvacara</em> or the 3 realms of sense-desires form and non-form: <em>kāma˚</em>; DN.i.34 (˚deva); Dhs.431 (as adj.); <em>rūpa˚</em>; Pp.37; <em>arūpa˚</em>; Pp.38; Pts.i.83, Pts.i.84, Pts.i.101; Dhs-a.387; Pv-a.138, Pv-a.163; to be omitted in Dhs.1268, Dhs.1278.</li></ol><p>ava + <em>car</em>, also BSk. avacara in same sense, e.g. antaḥpurâvacarā the inmates of the harem Jtm.210</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāḷa">tāḷa</a></p>

avacaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avacaraka &amp; ocaraka</p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>only in cpd. kāmâvacarika as adj. to kāmâvacara, belonging to the sphere of sense experiences, Sdhp.254.</li><li>Late form of ocaraka, spy, only in C. on Thag.315 ff. quoted in <em>Brethren</em> 189, n</li><li>Occurs in BSk (Divy.127).</li></ol><p>fr. avacara</p>

avacaraṇa|avacara.na|avacarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avacara.na</span></p><p>being familiar with, dealing with, occupation Ja.ii.95.</p><p>fr. avacarati 1</p>

avacchedaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective cutting off, as nt. <em>-ṃ</em> adv. in phrase kabaḷâvacchedakaṃ after the manner of cutting off mouthfuls (of food) Vin.ii.214 Vin.iv.196; cp. āsāvacchedika whose hope or longing has been cut off or destroyed Vin.i.259.</p><p>ava + cheda + ka</p>

avacchidda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>cut, torn asunder; full of holes.</p>

avacchidda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective perforated, only in redupl. (intensive) cpd. <em>chiddāvacchidda</em> perforated all over, nothing but holes Ja.iii.491; Dhp-a.i.122. Dhp-a.284 Dhp-a.319. Cp. chidda-vicchidda.</p><p>ava + chidda</p>

avacuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>second pret. of <em>vac</em>, in prohib form mā evaṃ avacuttha do not speak thus Ja.vi.72; Dhp-a.iv.228.</p>

avadehaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective in the idiom <em>udarāvadehakaṃ bhuñjati</em>, to eat one’s fill MN.i.102; Thag.935. Vism.33 has udarāvadehaka-bhojana, a heavy meal.</p><p>ava + deha + ka but more likely direct fr. ava + <em>dih</em></p>

avadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 sg. aor.</i></p><p>of <em><a href="bword://vadhati">vadhati</a></em><br />■ At Dhp-a.ii.73 avadhi = odhi.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vadhati">vadhati</a></p>

avadhāraṇa|avadhaara.na|avadharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadhaara.na</span></p><p>calling attention to, affirmation, emphasis; as t.t. used by C’s in explanation of <em><a href="bword://eva%E1%B9%83">evaṃ</a></em> at DN-a.i.27; and of <em><a href="bword://kho">kho</a></em> at Pv-a.11, Pv-a.18.</p><p>Cp. Sk. avadhāraṇa, fr. ava + <em>dhṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a>, <a href="bword://kho">kho</a></p>

avadāna|avadaana|avadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaana</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://apad%C4%81na">apadāna</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apadāna">apadāna</a></p>

avadāniya|avadaaniya|avadaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaaniya</span></p><p>illiberal, niggardly.</p>

avadāniya|avadaaniya|avadaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaaniya</span></p><p>adjective stingy, niggardly Snp.774 (= Mnd.36 which explains as follows: avaṃ gacchanti ti pi avadāniyā; maccharino pi vuccanti avadāniyā; buddhānaṃ vacanaṃ n’âdiyantī ti avadāniyā. Snp-a.516 condenses this expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> into the foll. avangamanatāya maccharitāya buddhâdīnaṃ vacanaṃ anādiyanatāya ca avadāniyā).</p><p>fr. avadāna cutting off; ava + <em>dā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to cut</p>

avadāpana|avadaapana|avadapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaapana</span></p><p>(cleansing): see <em><a href="bword://vod%C4%81pana">vodāpana</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vodāpana">vodāpana</a></p>

avadāpeti|avadaapeti|avadapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaapeti</span></p><p>(to deal out) only BSk pary˚ Divy.202.</p>

avadāta|avadaata|avadata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaata</span></p><p>(= odāta) Dāvs iii.14 (metri causa).</p>

avadātaka|avadaataka|avadataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaataka</span></p><p>or neuter white; or a white garment.</p>

avadāyati|avadaayati|avadayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadaayati</span></p><p>to have pity on, to feel sorry for Ja.iv.178 (bhūtānaṃ nâvadāyissaṃ, gloss n’ânukampiyaṃ).</p><p>denom. fr. avadā in same meaning as anuddā, to <em>dā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>: see *<a href="bword://dayati">dayati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dayati">dayati</a></p>

avadīyati|avadiiyati|avadiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avadiiyati</span></p><p>to burst, split open Ja.vi.183 (= bhijjati C. see also <em><a href="bword://udd%C4%AByati">uddīyati</a></em>,</p><p>Sk. avadīryati, ava + <em>ḍr<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, ḍrṇāti, see etym. under darī</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uddīyati">uddīyati</a></p>

avagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to come to, approach, visit (cp. Vedic avagacchati) Pv-a.87. Avaganda (-karaka)</p><p>ava + gacchati</p>

avagamaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>making known, expressing.</p>

avagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Pv-a.222 is uncertain reading; the meaning is “known, understood” (aññāta Pv.iv.1#11); perhaps we should read āvikata or adhigata (so variant reading BB). Avagahati &amp; ogahati;</p><p>pp. of avagacchati</p>

avagaṇḍa|avaga.n.da|avaganda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaga.n.da</span></p><p>Avagaṇḍa (-kāraka)</p><p>adjective “making a swelling”, i.e. puffing out the cheeks, stuffing the cheeks full (when eating); only nt. <em>-ṃ</em> as adv. after the manner or in the way of stuffing etc. Vin.ii.214; Vin.iv.196.</p><p>ava + gaṇḍa˚</p>

avagaṇḍakāraka|avaga.n.dakaaraka|avagandakaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaga.n.dakaaraka</span></p><p>Avagaṇḍa (-kāraka)</p><p>adjective “making a swelling”, i.e. puffing out the cheeks, stuffing the cheeks full (when eating); only nt. <em>-ṃ</em> as adv. after the manner or in the way of stuffing etc. Vin.ii.214; Vin.iv.196.</p><p>ava + gaṇḍa˚</p>

avagaṇḍakāraṃ|avaga.n.dakaara.m|avagandakaram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaga.n.dakaara.m</span></p><p>stuffing the cheeks full; so as to make the cheeks swell.</p>

avaggaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>hindrance, impediment, used at DN-a.i.95 as syn. for drought (dubuṭṭhikā).</p><p>Sk. avagraha</p>

avaghaṭṭana|avagha.t.tana|avaghattana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avagha.t.tana</span></p><p>pushing, hitting.</p>

avaguṇṭhana|avagu.n.thana|avagunthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avagu.n.thana</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) covering Sdhp.314.</p><p>fr. oguṇṭheti</p>

avagāha|avagaaha|avagaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avagaaha</span></p><p>plunging into; going deep into.</p>

avagāhati|avagaahati|avagahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avagaahati</span></p><p>Avagāhati &amp; ogāhati</p><p>to plunge or enter into, to be absorbed in (acc. &amp; loc.) Vism.678 (vipassanāvīthiṃ); Sdhp.370, Sdhp.383.</p><p>ava + gāhati</p>

avaharamāna|avaharamaana|avaharamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaharamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avaharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>takes down, puts down; takes away; steals.</p>

avaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avaharati &amp; oharati</p><p>to steal Ja.i.384; Pv-a.47 (avahari vatthaṃ), Pv-a.86 (id., = apānudi)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avaha%E1%B9%ADa">avahaṭa</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ava + <em>hṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avahaṭa">avahaṭa</a></p>

avaharaṇa|avahara.na|avaharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahara.na</span></p><p>(-˚) taking away, removal; theft Pv-a.47 (sāṭaka˚), Pv-a.92 (soka˚).</p><p>fr. avaharati in both meanings</p>

avaharaṇacitta|avahara.nacitta|avaharanacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahara.nacitta</span></p><p>(&amp; n.?) with the intention of stealing; (an intention to steal; ?)</p>

avahari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avaharituṃ|avaharitu.m|avaharitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaharitu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avaharitvā|avaharitvaa|avaharitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaharitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avahariṃsu|avahari.msu|avaharimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahari.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avaharuṃ|avaharu.m|avaharum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaharu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avahasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to laugh at, deride, mock Ja.v.111 (aññamaññaṃ); Pv-a.178<br />aor <strong>avahasi</strong> Ja.iv.413.</p><p>ava + <em>has</em></p><p>See also: avahasi</p>

avahaṭa|avaha.ta|avahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaha.ta</span></p><ol><li>(pass.) taken away; stolen.</li><li>(act.) taking away; having stolen</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avahaṭa|avaha.ta|avahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaha.ta</span></p><p>taken away, stolen Mil.46.</p><p>pp. of avaharati</p>

avahiyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is left behind; is found wanting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avahīyati">avahīyati</a></p>

avahāra|avahaara|avahara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahaara</span></p><p>taking away; theft.</p>

avahāra|avahaara|avahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahaara</span></p><p>taking, acquiring, acquisition Vin.v.129 (pañca avahārā, viz. theyya˚, pasayha˚, parikappa˚ paṭicchanna˚, kusa˚).</p><p>fr. avaharati</p>

avahāraka|avahaaraka|avaharaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahaaraka</span></p><p>(one) who takes away; a thief.</p>

avahāri|avahaari|avahari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahaari</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avahāsi|avahaasi|avahasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaharati">avaharati</a></p>

avahīyati|avahiiyati|avahiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahiiyati</span></p><p>is left behind; is found wanting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avahiyyati">avahiyyati</a></p>

avahīyati|avahiiyati|avahiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avahiiyati</span></p><p>to be left behind, to stay behind Ja.v.340.</p><p>for ohīyati</p>

avajaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>defeat Dhp-a.ii.228 (variant reading for T. ajaya).</p><p>ava + jaya, cp. apajita</p>

avajhāyati|avajhaayati|avajhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajhaayati</span></p><p>broods, mourns</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajjhāyati">avajjhāyati</a></p>

avajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>what is blameable; imperfection, a fault.</p>

avajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective low, inferior, blamable bad, deprecable Dhp.318, Dhp.319; Dhs.1160. More fig. in neg. form <em>anavajja</em> blameless, faultless DN.i.70 (= anindita DN-a.i.183); AN.ii.26 = Iti.102; Snp.47 (˚bhojin carrying on a blameless mode of livelihood, see Cnd.39), Snp.263 (= anindita agarahita Kp-a.140): Pts.ii.116, Pts.ii.170; Pp.30, Pp.41, Pp.58; Sdhp.436. Opp. <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81vajja">sāvajja</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. avadya, seemigly a + vadya, but in reality a der. fr. ava. According to Childers = Sk. avarjya from <em>vraj</em>, thus meaning “not to be shunned, not forbidden” This interpret<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is justified by context of Dhp.318, Dhp.319. The P. commentator refers it to ava + <em>vad</em> (for *ava-vadya in sense of to blame, cp. apavadati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sāvajja">sāvajja</a></p>

avajjatā|avajjataa|avajjata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajjataa</span></p><p>only neg. <em>an˚</em>; blamelessness, faultlessness Pp.25, Pp.41; Dhs.1349.</p><p>abstr. to prec.</p>

avajjha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be killed or destroyed, inviolable Snp.288; Ja.v.69; Ja.vi.132.</p><p>grd of a + vadhati, Sk. vadhya, <em>vadh</em></p>

avajjhāyati|avajjhaayati|avajjhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajjhaayati</span></p><p>broods, mourns</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajhāyati">avajhāyati</a></p>

avajānanta|avajaananta|avajananta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaananta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajānati|avajaanati|avajanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaanati</span></p><ol><li>despises; underrates.</li><li>denies, repudiates.</li></ol>

avajāneyya|avajaaneyya|avajaneyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaaneyya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajāni|avajaani|avajani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaani</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajānitabba|avajaanitabba|avajanitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaanitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajānitvā|avajaanitvaa|avajanitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaanitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajāniya|avajaaniya|avajaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaaniya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avajānāti|avajaanaati|avajanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaanaati</span></p><ol><li>to deny Vin.ii.85; AN.iii.164 = Pp.65.</li><li>(later) to despise Dhp-a.iii.16; Pv-a.175 (grd ˚jānitabba)-Of short stem-form <em>ñā</em> are found the foll grd. avaññeyya Pv-a.175, and with o˚: grd. oñātabba Pv-a.195; pp. <em>avañāta</em>, besides <em><a href="bword://ava%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81ta">avaññāta</a></em>.</li></ol><p>ava + <em>jñā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaññāta">avaññāta</a></p>

avajāta|avajaata|avajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaata</span></p><p>of low or base birth; of lower character than one’s parents; an unworthy child.</p>

avajāta|avajaata|avajata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajaata</span></p><p>adjective low-born, of low or base birth, fig of low character (opp. abhijāta) Snp.664 (= buddhassa avajātaputta Snp-a.479); Iti.63; Mil.359.</p><p>ava + jāta; cp. B.Sk. avajāta in meaning misborn, miscarriage</p>

avajīyati|avajiiyati|avajiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajiiyati</span></p><p>is conquered; is lost.</p>

avajīyati|avajiiyati|avajiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avajiiyati</span></p><p>to be diminished, to be lost, be undone Ja.i.313 (jitaṃ a; variant reading avajījy˚); Dhp.179 (jitaṃ a = dujjitaṃ hoti Dhp-a.iii.197).</p><p>ava + jīyati; Sk. avajiryate</p>

avakanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cut, cut open, cut off Ja.iv.251 (galak’ âvakantaṃ).</p><p>for *avakatta, Sk. avakṛtta; pp. of avakantati, see *<a href="bword://kanta">kanta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kanta">kanta</a></p>

avakantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakantati &amp; okantati</p><p>(okk˚) to cut off, cut out, cut away carve-(<em>ava</em>:) Ja.iv.155<br />pp <em>avakanta &amp; avakantita</em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. avakṛntati, ava + kantati, cp. also apakantati</p>

avakantita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cut out Pv-a.213. Avakappana &amp; okappana</p><p>pp. of avakantati</p>

avakappanā|avakappanaa|avakappana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakappanaa</span></p><p>Avakappanā &amp; okappanā</p><p>feminine preparation, fixing up, esp. harnessing Ja.vi.408.</p><p>ava + kappanā</p>

avakaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“to put down”, to despise, throw away; only in der, avakāra &amp; avakārin.; pp. <em><a href="bword://avakata">avakata</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ See also avākaroti &amp; cp. avakirati 2.</p><p>Sk. apakaroti, cp. P. apa˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakata">avakata</a></p>

avakassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>removes; draws away; secedes.</p>

avakassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakassati &amp; okassati</p><p>to drag down, to draw or pull away, distract, remove<br />■ AN.v.74 = Vin.ii.204 (+ vavakassati).</p><p>cp. Sk. avakarṣati, ava + kṛṣ; see also <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em> &amp; avakaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

avakata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= apakata, variant reading at Iti.89.</p>

avakaḍḍhana|avaka.d.dhana|avakaddhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka.d.dhana</span></p><p>removing.</p>

avakaḍḍhati|avaka.d.dhati|avakaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>Ne.4 (avakaḍḍhayitvā). Pass.; <em><a href="bword://avaka%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dhati">avakaḍḍhati</a></em> Ja.iv.415 (hadayaṃ me a. my heart is weighed down = sokena avakaḍḍhīyati C; variant reading avakassati)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avaka%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dhita">avakaḍḍhita</a></em>.</p><p>ava + kaḍḍhati, cp. avakassati &amp; apakassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakaḍḍhati">avakaḍḍhati</a>, <a href="bword://avakaḍḍhita">avakaḍḍhita</a></p>

avakaḍḍhita|avaka.d.dhita|avakaddhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka.d.dhita</span></p><p>pulled down, dragged away Dhp-a.iii.195.</p><p>pp. of avakaḍḍhati</p>

avakaṃsa|avaka.msa|avakamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka.msa</span></p><p>dragging down, detraction, abasement, in cpd. <em>ukkaṃsāvak˚</em>; lifting up &amp; pulling down, raising and lowering rise &amp; fall DN.i.54.</p><p>fr. ava-karṣati; on ṃs: *rṣ cp. haṃsati: harṣati</p>

avakaṅkhati|avaka"nkhati|avakankhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka"nkhati</span></p><p>desires, longs for.</p>

avakaṅkhati|avaka"nkhati|avakankhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka"nkhati</span></p><p>(-˚) to wish for, strive after SN.iv.57 (n’); Ja.iv.371 (n’); Ja.v.340 (n’), Ja.iv.348 (n’ = na pattheti C).</p><p>ava + kankhati; cp. Sk. anu-kānkṣati</p>

avakaṇṇaka|avaka.n.naka|avakannaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaka.n.naka</span></p><p>Given in the Pācittiya rules’ as an example of a low name (<em>hīnanāma</em>). Vin.iv.6<em>ff.</em></p>

avakirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakirati &amp; okirati</p><ol><li>to pour down on, to pour out over; aor. avakiri Pv-a.86; ger. ˚kiritvā Ja.v.144.</li><li>to cast out, reject, throw out; aor. avākiri Vv.30#5 = Vv.48#5 (variant reading ˚kari; Vv-a.126 explains by chaḍḍesi vināsesi)</li></ol><p>pass <em>avakirīyati</em> Pv.iii.1#10 (= chaḍḍīyati Pv-a.174); grd. ˚kiriya (see sep.). See also apakiritūna. pp <em><a href="bword://oki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">okiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>ava + kirati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okiṇṇa">okiṇṇa</a></p>

avakiriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be cast out or thrown away; rejectable, low, contemptible Ja.v.143 (taken by C. as ger. = avakiritvā).</p><p>grd of avakirati</p>

avakkama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>entering, appearance Ja.v.330 (gabbhassa).</p><p>fr. avakkamati</p>

avakkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkamati &amp; okkamati</p><p>to approach. to enter, go into or near to, to fall into, appear in, only in ger. (poetically) avakamma Ja.iii.480 (variant reading apa˚).</p><p>ava + kamati fr. <em>kram</em></p>

avakkanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) entered by, beset with, overwhelmed by (instr.) SN.iii.69 (dukkha˚, sukha˚ and an˚).</p><p>pp. ofnext</p>

avakkanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>coming down (into); entering a womb, conception; appearance.</p>

avakkanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>entry, appearance, coming down into, opportunity for rebirth SN.ii.66 (nāmarūpassa), SN.iii.46 (pañcannaṃ indriyānaṃ); Pp.13 (= okkanti nibbatti pātubhāvo Pp-a 184); Kv.142 (nāmarūpassa); Mil.123 (gabbhassa).</p><p>fr. avakkamati</p>

avakkhalita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>washed off, taken away from, detracted DN-a.i.66 (variant reading apa˚).</p><p>pp. of avakkhaleti, Caus. of <em>kṣal</em></p>

avakkhipana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>throwing down, putting down Ja.i.163.</p><p>fr. avakkhipati</p>

avakkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkhipati &amp; okkhipati</p><p>to throw down or out, cast down, drop; fig usually applied to the eyes = to cast down, hence transferred to the other senses and used in meaning of “to keep under, to restrain, to have control over” (cp. also avakkhāyati), aor. ˚khipi DN-a.i.268 (bhusaṃ, variant reading avakkhasi).</p><p>ava + khipati; cp. Sk. avakṣipati</p>

avakkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>cast down, thrown down.</p>

avakkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkhitta &amp; okkhitta</p><ol><li>[= Sk. avakṣipta] thrown down, flung down, cast down, dropped thrown out, rejected. (<em>ava</em>:) MN.i.296 (ujjhita +); DN-a.i.281 (an˚), DN-a.i.289 (pinḍa); Pv-a.174 (piṇḍa).</li><li>[= Sk. utkṣipta?] thrown off, gained, produced, got (cp. uppādita), in phrase <em>sed’ âvakkhitta</em> gained by sweat AN.ii.67; AN.iii.45.</li></ol><p>pp. of avakkhipati</p>

avakkāra|avakkaara|avakkara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakkaara</span></p><p>leftovers or surplus from a meal.</p>

avakkāra|avakkaara|avakkara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakkaara</span></p><p>throwing away, refuse, sweepings; only in cpd. <em>-pātī</em> a bowl for refuse, slop basin, ash-bin Vin.i.157, Vin.i.352; Vin.ii.216; MN.i.207; Dhp-a.i.305.</p><p>Sk. avaskara faeces, fr. avaṃ + karoti</p>

avakkārapāti|avakkaarapaati|avakkarapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakkaarapaati</span></p><p>dish for the left overs or surplus</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakkāra">avakkāra</a></p>

avakujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with the face downward, prone; upset.</p>

avakujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective face downward, head first, prone, bent over (opp. ukkujja &amp; uttāna) Ja.i.13 Bv.ii.52; Ja.v.295; Ja.vi.40; Pv.iv.10#8; Pv-a.178.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1%C3%B1a">pañña</a></em> (adj.) one whose reason is turned upside down (like an upturned pot, i.e. empty) AN.i.130; Pp.31 (= adhomukha-pañña Pp-a.214).</li></ul><p>ava + kujja, cp. B.Sk. avakubja Mvu.i.29, avakubjaka ibid. Mvu.i.213; Mvu.ii.412</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pañña">pañña</a></p>

avakujjapañña|avakujjapa~n~na|avakujjapanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakujjapa~n~na</span></p><p>whose intellect is upside down, not retentive (like an upturned jar); who cannot learn or remember</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakujja">avakujja</a></p>

avakārakaṃ|avakaaraka.m|avakarakam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakaaraka.m</span></p><p>adverb throwing away, scattering about Vin.ii.214.</p><p>fr. avakāra</p>

avakārin|avakaarin|avakarin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakaarin</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) despising, degrading, neglecting Vb.393 sq. (an˚). Avakasa &amp; okasa;</p><p>fr. avakāra</p>

avakāsa|avakaasa|avakasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avakaasa</span></p><p>Avakāsa &amp; okāsa</p><ol><li>“appearance”: akkhuddâvakāso dassanāya not little (or inferior) to behold (of appearance) DN.i.114; ariyāvakāsa appearing noble or having the app. of an Aryan Ja.v.87; katâvakāsa put into appearance Vv.22#9.</li><li>“opportunity”: kata˚ given leave DN.i.276; Snp.1030; anavakāsakārin not giving occasion Mil.383<br />■ <em>anavakāsa</em> not having a chance or opportunity (to happen), impossible; always in ster. phrase aṭṭhānaṃ etaṃ anavakāso Vin.ii.199; AN.i.26; AN.v.169; Pp.11, Pp.12; Pv-a.28.</li></ol><p>ava + <em>kāś</em> to shine, cp. Sk. avakāśa</p>

avalambati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Olambati &amp; avalambati</p><p>to hang down, hang on, to be supported by, rest on. The form in o is the older. Pres. avalambare Pv.ii.1#18 (= olambamānā tiṭṭhanti Pv-a.77); Pv.ii.10#2 (= olambanti Pv-a.142); olambati MN.iii.164 (+ ajjholambati); Ja.i.194; Pv-a.46<br />ger <em>avalamba</em> (for ˚bya) Pv.iii.3#5 (= olambitva Pv-a.189 &amp; <em>olambetvā</em> Ja.iii.218. See also <em><a href="bword://olubbha">olubbha</a></em>.</p><p>ava + <em>lamb</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olubbha">olubbha</a></p>

avalambati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Only in late verse. To hang down. Pv.ii.1#18; Pv.ii.10#2. Ger. avalamba (for ˚bya) Pv.iii.3#5 cp. olubbha.</p><p>= <em><a href="bword://olambati">olambati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olambati">olambati</a></p>

avalekhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avalekhana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter</p><ol><li>scraping, scraping off Vin.ii.141 (˚pidhara), Vin.ii.221 (˚kaṭṭha).</li><li>scratching in writing down Ja.iv.402, (˚sattha a chisel for engraving letters).</li></ol><p>fr. avalekhati</p><p>Avalekhana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter variant reading for <em><a href="bword://apalekhana">apalekhana</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apalekhana">apalekhana</a></p>

avalekhanakaṭṭha|avalekhanaka.t.tha|avalekhanakattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avalekhanaka.t.tha</span></p><p>scraping stick.</p>

avalekhanapiṭhara|avalekhanapi.thara|avalekhanapithara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avalekhanapi.thara</span></p><p>receptacle for scrapers.</p>

avalekhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>scrapes off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avalikhati">avalikhati</a></p>

avalekhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to scrape off Vin.ii.221 (variant reading apa˚).</p><p>ava + lekhati, <em>likh</em>, Sk. avalikhati</p>

avalekhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avalekhati">avalekhati</a></p>

avalepana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) neuter smearing, daubing, plastering MN.i.385 (pīta˚); Snp.194 (kāyo taca-maṃs’ âvalepano the body plastered with skin &amp; flesh).</p><p>fr. ava + <em>lip</em></p>

avalikhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>scrapes off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avalekhati">avalekhati</a></p>

avalitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>smeared; plastered; smeared onto.</p>

avalitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) besmeared; in cpd. <em>ullittāvalitta</em> “smeared up &amp; down” i.e. plastered inside &amp; outside AN.i.101.</p><p>Sk. avalipta, pp. of ava-limpati</p>

avalokayamāna|avalokayamaana|avalokayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avalokayamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avalokayati">avalokayati</a></p>

avalokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks at, regards; looks back</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaloketi">avaloketi</a></p>

avalokenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaloketi">avaloketi</a></p>

avaloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks at, regards; looks back</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avalokayati">avalokayati</a></p>

avamata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>despised.</p>

avamaññatha|avama~n~natha|avamannatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avama~n~natha</span></p><p>they slighted; they despised.</p>

avamaññati|avama~n~nati|avamannati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avama~n~nati</span></p><p>to slight, to disregard, despise Dhp-a.i.170; Pv-a.37, Pv-a.175; Sdhp.271<br />pp Caus <strong>avamānita</strong>.</p><p>Sk. avamanyate</p><p>See also: avamānita</p>

avamaṅgala|avama"ngala|avamangala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avama"ngala</span></p><p>adjective of bad omen, unlucky, infaustus (opp. abhimangala); nt. bad luck, ill omen Ja.i.372, Ja.i.402; Ja.ii.197; Ja.vi.10 Ja.vi.424; Dhp-a.iii.123; Pv-a.261. Cf. next.</p><p>ava + mangala, ava here in privative function</p>

avamaṅgalla|avama"ngalla|avamangalla
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avama"ngalla</span></p><p>adjective of bad omen, nt. anything importune, unlucky Ja.i.446. Avamana &amp; omana;</p><p>fr. avamangala</p>

avamāna|avamaana|avamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avamaana</span></p><p>Avamāna &amp; omāna</p><p>disregard, disrespect, contempt Ja.ii.386; Ja.iii.423; Ja.v.384. Cp. next.</p><p>fr. ava + <em>man</em>, think</p>

avamānana|avamaanana|avamanana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avamaanana</span></p><p>= avamāna Ja.i.22.</p><p>fr. avamāna</p>

avamāneti|avamaaneti|avamaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avamaaneti</span></p><p>to despise Ja.v.246. - pp. <strong>avamānita</strong> Pv-a.36.</p><p>Caus. of avamaññati</p><p>See also: avamānita</p>

avanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://o%E1%B9%87ata">oṇata</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇata">oṇata</a></p>

avanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) feminine stooping, bending, bowing down, humiliation Mil.387 (unnat’âvanati).</p><p>fr. avanamati</p>

avani
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>bed or course of a river; earth, ground Dāvs iv.5.</p><p>Vedic avani</p>

avanti
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>One of the four great monarchies in the time of the Buddha, the other three being Māgadha, Kosala and Vaṃsa (or Vatsa). Avanti is also mentioned among the sixteen Great Nations. AN.i.213 AN.iv.252 AN.iv.256 AN.iv.260</p><p>Its capital was Ujjenī. But according to another account, DN.ii.235 Māhissati is mentioned as having been, at least for some time, the capital of Avanti. It is quite likely that ancient Avanti was divided into two parts, the northern part having its capital at Ujjenī and the southern part at Māhissati (Māhismatī).</p><p>The King of Avanti was Pajjota, a man of violent temper Vin.i.277 and therefore known as Caṇḍa Pajjota. He wished to conquer the neighbouring kingdom of Kosambī, of which Udena was king, but his plans did not work out as he had anticipated.</p><p>The kingdom of Assaka is invariably mentioned in connection with Avanti. Even in the Buddha’s life-time, Avanti became a centre of Buddhism. Among eminent monks and nuns who were either born or resided there, are to be found.</p><ul><li>Mahā Kaccāna</li><li>Nanda Kumāraputta</li><li>Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</li><li>Dhammapāla</li><li>Abhayarājakumāra</li><li>Isidatta and Isidāsī</li></ul><p>Mahā Kaccāna seems to have spent a good deal of his time in Avanti, dwelling in the city of Kuraraghara in the Papāta Pabbata. SN.iii.9 SN.iii.12 SN.iv.115–16 AN.v.46 He experienced great difficulty in collecting ten monks, in order that Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa might receive the higher Ordination; in fact it was not until three years had elapsed that he succeeded. Vin.i.195 Later, when Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa visited the Buddha at Sāvatthī, he conveyed to the Buddha Mahā Kaccāna’s request that special rules might be laid down for the convenience of the monks of Avanti Dakkhiṇāpatha and of other border countries. Vin.i.197–198 The Buddha agreed, and among the rules so laid down were the following.</p><ol><li>The higher Ordination could be given with only four monks and a Vinayadhara.</li><li>Monks are allowed the use of shoes with thick linings (because in Avanti the soil is black on the surface, rough and trampled by cattle).</li><li>Monks are enjoined to bathe frequently.</li><li>Sheepskins, goatskins, etc., could be used as coverlets.</li><li>Robes could be accepted on behalf of a monk who has left the district, and the ten days’ rule with regard to such a gift will not begin until the robes have actually reached the monk’s hands. Vin.iii.195–196</li></ol><p>By the time of the Vesāli Council, however, Avanti had become one of the important centres of the orthodox school, for we find Yasa Kākandakaputta sending messengers to Avanti to call representatives to the Council, and we are told that eighty-eight arahants obeyed the summons. Vin.ii.298–299</p><p>Even in the Buddha’s day there were rumours of the King of Avanti making preparations to attack Māgadha, but we are not told that he ever did so. MN.iii.7 Subsequently, however, before the time of Candagupta, Avanti became incorporated with Māgadha.</p><p>Avanti is now identified with the country north of the Vindhaya Mountains and north-east of Bombay, roughly corresponding to modern Mālwa, Nimār and adjoining parts of the Central Provinces.</p><p>22.67484, 75.624381janapada</p><p>See also: called Avanti Dakkhiṇāpatha</p>

avantiputta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>King of Madhurā. He once went in royal state to visit Mahā Kaccāna who was staying in the Gundā Grove in Madhurā.</p><p>Their discussion is recorded in the Madhura Sutta. MN.ii.83–90</p><p>It is said that after the interview Avantiputta became a follower of the Buddha’s teaching.</p>

avapakassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>draws back, secedes; alienates oneself</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avapakāsati">avapakāsati</a></p>

avapakāsati|avapakaasati|avapakasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avapakaasati</span></p><p>draws back, secedes; alienates oneself</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avapakāsati">avapakāsati</a></p>

avapakāsati|avapakaasati|avapakasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avapakaasati</span></p><p>is a doubtful comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of kassati, the comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> ava + pa occurring only in this word. In all likelihood it is a distortion of <em><a href="bword://vavakassati">vavakassati</a></em> (vi + ava + kassati), supplementing the ordinary <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em>. See meaning &amp; further discussion under apakāsati- Vin.ii.204 (apakāsati + ; variant reading avapakassati Bdhgh. in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> on p. 325 has apapakāsati which seems to imply (a)vavakassati); AN.iii.145 sq. (avapakāsituṃ).</p><p>ava + pa + kāsati = kassati, fr. <em>kṛṣ</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vavakassati">vavakassati</a>, <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

avapatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>fallen, fallen down.</p>

avapatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://opatta">opatta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opatta">opatta</a></p>

avapivati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to drink from Ja.i.163.</p><p>ava + <em>pā</em>, cp. apapibati</p>

avapāyin|avapaayin|avapayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avapaayin</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective coming for a drink, drinking Ja.i.163.</p><p>cp. avapivati</p>

avarajjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>misses, neglects, fails (of); offends, commits a fault.</p>

avarajjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) to neglect, fail, spurn Thag.167; Ja.iv.428 (variant reading ˚rujjh˚).</p><p>ava + rajjhati of <em>rādh</em>, cp. Sk. avarādhyate</p>

avarapure
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">loc.</i></p><p>to the west of the city.</p>

avaruddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>opposed (to); hostile; rebellious.</p>

avaruddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>Doubtful reading at Vin.iv.181, apparently meaning ʻin revolt, out of hand’ (of slaves)</li><li>[late form of oruddha] restrained Sdhp.592.</li></ol><p>fr. avarundhati</p>

avaruddhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>subdued, expelled, banished Ja.vi.575; Dpvs.i.21 (Np).</p><p>avruddha + ka</p>

avaruddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to expel, remove, banish Ja.vi.505 (= nīharati C.), Ja.vi.515 See also <em><a href="bword://avarundhati">avarundhati</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. aparundhati; ava + ruddhati of <em>rudh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avarundhati">avarundhati</a></p>

avarundhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to put under restraint to put into one’s harem as subsidiary wife.</p><p>ava + rundhati. Only referred to by Dhp. in his Cy (Thag-a.271) on oruddha</p>

avasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective powerless Sdhp.290. Avasata &amp; Osata;</p><p>a + vasa</p>

avasajjetvā|avasajjetvaa|avasajjetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasajjetvaa</span></p><p>throwing down (on); putting into; (or hanging on, suspending)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassajjetvā">avassajjetvā</a></p>

avasarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes down (to); moves away (to).</p>

avasarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go down, to go away (to) Snp.685 (variant reading BB. T. avaṃsari).</p><p>ava + <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

avasari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasarati">avasarati</a></p>

avasariṃ|avasari.m|avasarim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasari.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasarati">avasarati</a></p>

avasaṭa|avasa.ta|avasata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasa.ta</span></p><p>gone away; withdrawn</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasarati">avasarati</a></p>

avasaṭa|avasa.ta|avasata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasa.ta</span></p><p>Avasaṭa &amp; Osaṭa</p><p>withdrawn, gone away; one who has left a community &amp; gone over to another sect, a renegade Vin.iv.216, Vin.iv.217 (= titthāyatanaṃ saṃkata).</p><p>Sk. apasṛta, cp. also samavasṛta, pp. of ava + <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

avasesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(n.) the remainder, the rest; what is left.</li><li>(mfn.) remaining, left; other; the other…, the rest (of).</li></ol>

avasesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avasesa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>remainder, remaining part; only in compounds <em>an˚</em>; (adj.) without any remainder i.e. fully, completely MN.i.220 = AN.v.347 (˚dohin); AN.i.20 sq., AN.i.88; Snp.146; Pp.17; Dhs.363 Dhs.553; Snp-a.417 (˚pharaṇa); Pv-a.71 (˚ato, adv. altogether not leaving anything out); &amp; <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81vasesa">sāvasesa</a></em> leaving something over, having something left AN.i.20 sq., AN.i.88; Pv.iii.5#5 (jīvita˚ having still a little life left).</p><p>Sk. avaśeṣa, fr. ava + <em>śiṣ</em>, cp. avasissati</p><p>Avasesa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective remaining, left Snp.694 (āyu avaseso); Ja.iii.19; Vb.107 (taṇhā ca avasesā ca kilesā) Pv-a.19 (avasesā ca ñātakā the rest of the relatives), Pv-a.21 (avasesā parisā), Pv-a.201 (aṭṭhi-tacamatt’ âvasesa-sarīra with a body on which nothing but skin &amp; bones were left), Pv-a.206 (aṭṭhi-sanghātamatt;’ âvasesa-sarīra)<br />■ nt. (as pred.) <em>-ṃ</em> what is left Pv-a.52 (app’ avasesaṃ); Kp-a.245 (n’ atthi tesaṃ avasesaṃ).</p><p>see prec.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sāvasesa">sāvasesa</a></p>

avasesaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective being left, overflowing, additional, more Ja.i.400 (an˚); Dpvs.iv.45.</p><p>fr. avasesa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

avasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun not having control over oneself, DN.ii.275.</p><p>a + vasin fr. <em>vaś</em></p>

avasissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is left over; is left as a remnant; remains.</p>

avasissati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be left over to remain, in phrase yaṃ pamāṇa-kataṃ kammaṃ na taṃ tatrâvasissati DN.i.251; AN.v.299 = SN.iv.322; Ja.ii.61 (see expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> on p. 62). Also in the phrases <em>taco ca nahārū ca aṭṭhi ca avasissatu sarīre upasussatu maṃsa-lohitaṃ</em> MN.i.481; AN.i.50; SN.ii.28, and <em>sarīrāni avasissanti</em> SN.ii.83. With the latter phrases cp. <em><a href="bword://avasussati">avasussati</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. avaśiṣyate; Pass. of ava + <em>śis</em>; but expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. as fut of avasīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasussati">avasussati</a></p>

avasissi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasissati">avasissati</a></p>

avasissiṃsu|avasissi.msu|avasissimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasissi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasissati">avasissati</a></p>

avasitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) besprinkled, anointed, consecrated, only in phrase <em>rājā khattiyo muddhāvasitto</em> of a properly consecrated king (see also <em><a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a></em>) DN.i.69; DN.ii.227; DN.iii.64; Pp.56; DN-a.i.182 (T. muddhâvassita variant reading ˚abhisitta); etc<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://abhisitta">abhisitta</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of osiñcati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khattiya">khattiya</a>, <a href="bword://abhisitta">abhisitta</a></p>

avasiñcanaka|avasi~ncanaka|avasincanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasi~ncanaka</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective pouring over (act. &amp; med.), overflowing Ja.i.400 (an˚).</p><p>fr. osiñcati</p>

avasiṭṭha|avasi.t.tha|avasittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasi.t.tha</span></p><p>left over, remaining; the rest (of)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasissati">avasissati</a></p>

avasiṭṭha|avasi.t.tha|avasittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasi.t.tha</span></p><p>(sic &amp; not osiṭṭha) left, remaining, over SN.ii.133; Ja.i.138; Ja.v.339; Vv-a.66 pl. avasiṭṭhā all who are left, the others Pv-a.165 (janā).</p><p>pp. of avasissati, Sk. avaśiṣṭa</p>

avasiṭṭhaka|avasi.t.thaka|avasitthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasi.t.thaka</span></p><p>adjective remaining, left Ja.iii.311.</p><p>fr. avasiṭṭha</p>

avassa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective against one’s will, inevitable Ja.i.19 (˚bhāvin); Ja.v.319 (˚gāmitā). Usually as nt. <em>-ṃ</em> adv inevitably (cp. BSk. avaśyaṃ Divy.347; Avs.i.209 etc. Ja.iii.271; DN-a.i.263; Sdhp.293.</p><p>a + <em>vaś</em></p>

avassajati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avassajati &amp; ossajati</p><p>to let loose, let go, send off, give up, dismiss, release (ava) Ja.iv.425; Ja.v.487 (aor. avassaji read for avissaji).</p><p>ava + <em>sṛj</em>, perhaps ud + <em>sṛj</em> = Sk. utsṛjati, although the usual Vedic form is avasṛjati The form ossajati puzzled the BSk. writers in their sanskritisation apotsṛjati = apa + ut + <em>sṛj</em> Divy.203</p>

avassajiṃ|avassaji.m|avassajim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avassaji.m</span></p><p>let loose; gave up; dismissed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassajī">avassajī</a></p>

avassajjetvā|avassajjetvaa|avassajjetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avassajjetvaa</span></p><p>throwing down (on); putting into; (or hanging on, suspending)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasajjetvā">avasajjetvā</a></p>

avassajī|avassajii|avassaji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avassajii</span></p><p>avassajī<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg. let loose; gave up; dismissed</p><p>avassajī<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassajiṃ">avassajiṃ</a>, <a href="bword://avassajī">avassajī</a></p>

avassakaṃ|avassaka.m|avassakam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avassaka.m</span></p><p>adverb inevitably Dpvs.ix.13.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://avassa">avassa</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassa">avassa</a></p>

avassana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>not bleating Ja.iv.251.</p><p>a + vassana, Sk. vāsana of <em>vāś</em> to bleat</p>

avassava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>outflow, effect, only neg. <em><a href="bword://anassava">anassava</a></em> no further effect Vin.ii.89; MN.i.93; MN.ii.246; AN.iii.334 sp.</p><p>ava + sava, Sk. ˚srava fr. <em>sru</em> to flow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anassava">anassava</a></p>

avassaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>support, help, protection, refuge Ja.i.211; Ja.ii.197; Ja.iv.167; Mil.160; Dhp-a.ii.267; Dhp-a.iv.198; Pv-a.5, Pv-a.113.</p><p>Sk. *avāśraya for the usual apāśraya, see P. apassaya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

avasseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lean against, to depend on, find shelter in (loc.) Ja.ii.80 (aor. avassayiṃ = vāsaṃ kappesiṃ C.). pp. <em><a href="bword://avassita">avassita</a></em>.</p><p>ava + ā + <em>śri</em>, for the usual *apāśrayati; see <em><a href="bword://apasseti">apasseti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassita">avassita</a>, <a href="bword://apasseti">apasseti</a></p>

avassita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>depending on, dealing with Ja.v.375. See apassita.</p><p>for apassita, Sk. apaśrita</p>

avassuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>leaking; dripping, wet; foul, rotten; lustful, sexually excited.</p>

avassuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>(lit.) flowing out or down, oozing, leaking Ja.iv.20.</li><li>(fig.) (cp. <em>anvāssava</em> &amp; āsava) filled with desire lustful (opp.; <em>anavassuta</em>, q.v.) Vin.ii.236; SN.iv.70, SN.iv.184 (an˚); AN.i.261, AN.i.262 (an˚); AN.ii.240; AN.iv.128, AN.iv.201; Snp.63 (an˚); Pp.27, Pp.36; Dpvs.ii.5 (T. reads <em><a href="bword://avassita">avassita</a></em>).</li></ol><p>■ Neg. <em>anavassuta</em>: 1 not leaking, without a leak Ja.iv.20 (nāvā = udaka-pavesan’ âbhāvena a. C.). 2 free from leakage, i.e. from lust or moral intoxication Dhp.39 (˚citta) Snp.63 (see expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in detail at Cnd.40); Snp-a.116 (kilesa-anvāssava-virahita).</p><p>Sk. *avasruta, pp. of ava + <em>sru</em>, cp. <em><a href="bword://avassava">avassava</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassita">avassita</a>, <a href="bword://avassava">avassava</a></p>

avassāvana|avassaavana|avassavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avassaavana</span></p><p>straining, filtering (?) Ja.ii.288.</p><p>fr. ava + Caus. of <em>sru</em> to flow</p>

avasussati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to dry up, to wither; in later quotations of the old <em>kāmaṃ taco ca nahāru ca aṭṭhi ca avasussatu</em> (upasussatu sarīre maṃsalohitaṃ Ja.i.71, Ja.i.110; Sdhp.46. It is a later spelling for the older avasissatu see Trenckner (MN.i.569)<br />fut <em>avasucchati</em> (= Sk. *˚śokṣyati, fut. of Intens.) Ja.vi.550 (variant reading BB ˚sussati; C. avasucchissati).</p><p>Sk. *ava-suṣyati of <em>śuṣ</em></p>

avasādayati|avasaadayati|avasadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaadayati</span></p><p>makes sink down; disheartens, discourages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasādeti">avasādeti</a></p>

avasādeti|avasaadeti|avasadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaadeti</span></p><p>makes sink down; disheartens, discourages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasādayati">avasādayati</a></p>

avasāna|avasaana|avasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaana</span></p><p>(-˚) neuter stopping ceasing; end, finish, conclusion Ja.i.87 (bhattakicc-âvasāne at the end of the meal); Pv-a.76 (id.).</p><p>for <em><a href="bword://os%C4%81na">osāna</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāna">osāna</a></p>

avasāya|avasaaya|avasaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaaya</span></p><p>stopping, end, finish Thig.12 (= avasānaṃ niṭṭhānaṃ Thag-a.19). But the id. p. at Dhp.218 has anakkhāte.</p><p>fr. avaseti</p>

avasāyi|avasaayi|avasayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avasāyin">avasāyin</a></p><p>determined; reaching the end.</p>

avasāyin|avasaayin|avasayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasaayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avasāyin">avasāyin</a></p><p>determined; reaching the end.</p>

avasī|avasii|avasi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasii</span></p><p>metri causa for avasi, a + vasi, aor. of <em>vas<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to stop, stay, rest Ja.v.66 (mā avasī).</p>

avasūra|avasuura|avasura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avasuura</span></p><p>sundown, sunset, acc. <em>-ṃ</em> as adv. at or with sundown Ja.v.56 (anāvasūraṃ metrically).</p><p>ava + sūra; ava here in function of *avaṃs see <em>ava</em> ii</p>

avatata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avatata &amp; otata</p><p>stretched over, covered, spread over with Vv.64#3 (-˚); Vv-a.276 (chādita).</p><p>ava + tata, pp. of <em>tan</em></p>

avataṃsa|avata.msa|avatamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avata.msa</span></p><p>see <em>vataṃsaka</em>.</p>

avatiṇṇa|avati.n.na|avatinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avati.n.na</span></p><p>Avatiṇṇa &amp; otiṇṇa</p><p>fallen into, affected with (-˚), as <strong>ava˚</strong>; rare late or poetical form of <em>o˚</em>; e.g. Ja.v.98 (issâ˚). See <em><a href="bword://oti%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">otiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of otarati</p><p>See also: ava˚, <a href="bword://otiṇṇa">otiṇṇa</a></p>

avatiṭṭhati|avati.t.thati|avatitthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avati.t.thati</span></p><p>takes one’s stand; remains standing; continues, lingers; falls down on or into.</p>

avatiṭṭhati|avati.t.thati|avatitthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avati.t.thati</span></p><p>to abide, linger, stand still. DN.i.251 = SN.iv.322 = AN.v.299 (tatra˚); SN.i.25 (v.l otiṭṭhati); Thag.1, Thag.21; Ja.ii.62; Ja.iv.208 (aor. avaṭṭhāsi). pp. <em><a href="bword://ava%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">avaṭṭhita</a></em> (q.v.). Avatinna &amp; otinna;</p><p>ava + tiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṭṭhita">avaṭṭhita</a></p>

avattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avattha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aimless (of cārikā, a bhikkhu’s wandering, going on tour) AN.iii.171 (C. avavatthika).</p><p>der. uncertain</p><p>Avattha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>thrown away Ja.v.302 (= chaḍḍita C.).</p><p>Sk. apāsta, apa + āsta, pp. of <em>as<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em></p>

avattharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to strew, cover over or up Ja.i.74 (˚amāna ppr.), Ja.i.255 (˚itvā ger.); Ja.iv.84; Dāvs i.38<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ottha%E1%B9%ADa">otthaṭa</a></em> Cp. pariy˚.</p><p>ava + tharati, <em>stṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otthaṭa">otthaṭa</a></p>

avattharaṇa|avatthara.na|avattharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avatthara.na</span></p><p>setting in array, deploying (of an army) Ja.ii.104 (of a robber-band), Ja.ii.336.</p><p>fr. avattharati</p>

avatthasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avatiṭṭhati">avatiṭṭhati</a></p>

avatthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>standing firmly; placed; steady; continuing; lasting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṭṭhita">avaṭṭhita</a>, <a href="bword://avatiṭṭhati">avatiṭṭhati</a></p>

avatthu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avatthu (&amp; ˚ka)</p><p>adjective groundless, unfounded (fig) Vin.ii.241; Ja.i.440 (˚kaṃ vacanaṃ). For lit meaning see <em><a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></em>.</p><p>a + vatthu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></p>

avatthuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avatthu (&amp; ˚ka)</p><p>adjective groundless, unfounded (fig) Vin.ii.241; Ja.i.440 (˚kaṃ vacanaṃ). For lit meaning see <em><a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></em>.</p><p>a + vatthu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></p>

avatthāraṇa|avatthaara.na|avattharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avatthaara.na</span></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://avatthara%E1%B9%87a">avattharaṇa</a></em> DN-a.i.274. Avatthu (&amp; ka);</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avattharaṇa">avattharaṇa</a></p>

avaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in neg. <em><a href="bword://anavaya">anavaya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anavaya">anavaya</a></p>

avayava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>limb, member, constituent, part Vv-a.53 (sarīra˚ = gattā). Vv-a.168 Vv-a.201, Vv-a.276; Pv-a.211 (sarīra˚ = gattā), Pv-a.251 (mūl˚ the fibres of the root). As t. t. g. at Snp-a.397. In the commentaries <em><a href="bword://avayava">avayava</a></em> is often used where <em><a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> would have been used in the older texts.</p><p>Der<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> uncertain. Cp. mediaeval Sk. avayava</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avayava">avayava</a>, <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a></p>

avañcana|ava~ncana|avancana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~ncana</span></p><p>adjective not (even) tottering, i.e. unfit for any motion (esp. walking), said of crippled feet Ja.i.214 = Cp.iii.9#10.</p><p>a + vañcana from <em>vañc</em></p>

avañña|ava~n~na|avanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~n~na</span></p><p>adjective despised, despicable Pv.iii.1#13 (= avaññeyya avajānitabba Pv-a.175).</p><p>to avaññā</p>

avaññatti|ava~n~natti|avannatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~n~natti</span></p><p>only as neg. <em>an˚</em>; the fact of not being despised inferior or surpassed, egotism, pride, arrogance Iti.72 Vb.350, Vb.356; <em>-kāma</em> (adj.) wishing not to be surpassed unvilling to be second, wanting to be praised AN.ii.240 AN.iv.1 sq.</p><p>ava + ñatti = Sk. *avajñapti, fr. ava + <em>jñā</em></p>

avaññā|ava~n~naa|avanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~n~naa</span></p><p>contempt, disregard, disrespect Ja.i.257 (˚ya).</p><p>Sk. avajñā, fr. ava + <em>jñā</em></p>

avaññāta|ava~n~naata|avannata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~n~naata</span></p><p>despised; underrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avañāta">avañāta</a>, <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avaññāta|ava~n~naata|avannata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~n~naata</span></p><p>adjective despised, treated with contempt Pv-a.135 (an˚); Sdhp.88, Sdhp.90.</p><p>pp. of avajānāti</p>

avañāta|ava~naata|avanata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava~naata</span></p><p>despised; underrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaññāta">avaññāta</a>, <a href="bword://avajānati">avajānati</a></p>

avaḍḍhi|ava.d.dhi|avaddhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.d.dhi</span></p><p>“non-growth”, decay Dhp-a.iii.335; C on AN.iii.76 (cp. apajaha).</p><p>a + vaḍḍhi</p>

avaṃ|ava.m|avam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.m</span></p><p>downwards.</p>

avaṃ|ava.m|avam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.m</span></p><p>adverb the prep. ava in adv. use, down, downward; in C. often expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by <em><a href="bword://adho">adho</a></em>. Rarely absolute, the only passage found so far being Snp.685 (avaṃ sari he went down, variant reading avasari, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by otari Snp-a.486). Opp. uddhaṃ (above, up high). Freq. in cpd <em><a href="bword://ava%E1%B9%83sira">avaṃsira</a></em> (adj.) head downward (+ uddhaṃpāda feet up) a position characteristic of beings in Niraya (Purgatory) e.g. SN.i.48; Snp.248 (patanti sattā nirayaṃ avaṃsirā adhogata-sīsā Snp-a.290); Vv.52#25 (of Revatī, + uddhaṃpāda); Pv.iv.1#46; Ja.i.233 (+ uddhapāda); Ja.iv.103 (nirayaṃ vajanti yathā adhammo patito avaṃsiro); Mnd.404 (uddhaṃpāda +); Dhp-a.iv.153 (gloss adhosira)<br />■ On avaṃ˚ cp further avakkāra, avākaroti, avekkhipati.</p><p>Vedic avāk &amp; avāṃ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adho">adho</a>, <a href="bword://avaṃsira">avaṃsira</a></p>

avaṃsira|ava.msira|avamsira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.msira</span></p><p>with the head downwards; headlong, headfirst</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṃ">avaṃ</a></p>

avaṅga|ava"nga|avanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava"nga</span></p><p>mark or circle drawn on the forehead or at the outer corner of the eye</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaṅga">apaṅga</a>, <a href="bword://apāṅga">apāṅga</a></p>

avaṅga|ava"nga|avanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava"nga</span></p><p>see <em>apanga</em>.</p>

avaṇṇa|ava.n.na|avanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.n.na</span></p><p>blame, reproach, fault DN.i.1 (= dosā nindā DN-a.i.37); Iti.67; Pp.48, Pp.59.</p><p>a + vaṇṇa</p>

avaṇṇanīya|ava.n.naniiya|avannaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.n.naniiya</span></p><p>adjective indescribable Ja.v.282.</p><p>grd. of a + vaṇṇeti</p>

avaṇṭa|ava.n.ta|avanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.n.ta</span></p><p>adjective without a stalk Ja.v.155.</p><p>a + vaṇṭa</p>

avaṭaṃsaka|ava.ta.msaka|avatamsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.ta.msaka</span></p><p>(= vaṭ˚) see <em>Vin Texts</em> ii.347.</p>

avaṭhiti|ava.thiti|avathiti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.thiti</span></p><p>firm position; steadiness, stability</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṭṭhiti">avaṭṭhiti</a></p>

avaṭṭhasi|ava.t.thasi|avatthasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avatiṭṭhati">avatiṭṭhati</a></p>

avaṭṭhita|ava.t.thita|avatthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thita</span></p><p>standing firmly; placed; steady; continuing; lasting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avatthita">avatthita</a>, <a href="bword://avatiṭṭhati">avatiṭṭhati</a></p>

avaṭṭhita|ava.t.thita|avatthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thita</span></p><p>adjective “standing down” = standing up, firm, fixed, settled, lasting Thag.1140 Usually neg. <em>an˚</em>; unsettled, unsteady; not lasting, changeable Dhp.38 (˚citta; cp. Dhp-a.i.308 cittaṃ thāvaraṃ natthi); Pv-a.87 (= na sassata not lasting for ever).</p><p>Sk. avasthita, ava + thita</p>

avaṭṭhitatā|ava.t.thitataa|avatthitata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thitataa</span></p><p>steadiness, only as neg. <em>an˚</em>; unsteadiness, fickleness Thag-a.259.</p><p>abstr. fr. prec.</p>

avaṭṭhiti|ava.t.thiti|avatthiti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thiti</span></p><p>firm position; steadiness, stability</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṭhiti">avaṭhiti</a></p>

avaṭṭhiti|ava.t.thiti|avatthiti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thiti</span></p><p>(firm) position, posture, steadfastness SN.v.228; Dhs.11, Dhs.570.</p><p>Sk. avasthiti</p>

avaṭṭhāna|ava.t.thaana|avatthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ava.t.thaana</span></p><p>position, standing place Ja.i.508; Pv-a.286.</p><p>Sk. avasthāna</p>

avebhaṅgika|avebha"ngika|avebhangika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avebha"ngika</span></p><p>adjective not to be divided or distributed Vin.i.305. Cp. next.</p><p>fr. a + vi + bhanga</p>

avebhaṅgiya|avebha"ngiya|avebhangiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avebha"ngiya</span></p><p>that which is not to be divided, an inalienable possession; 5 such objects enumerated at Vin.ii.171, which are the same as under <em><a href="bword://avissajjiya">avissajjiya</a></em> (q.v.); Vin.v.129.</p><p>= avebhangika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avissajjiya">avissajjiya</a></p>

avecca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>understanding, having penetrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aveti">aveti</a></p>

avecca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb certainly, definitely, absolutely, perfectly, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh. as acala (on DN.ii.217), or as paññāya ajjhogahetvā (on Snp.229); by Dhp. as apara-paccaya-bhāvena (on Pv.iv.1#25)<br />■ Usually in phrase <em>Buddhe Dhamme Saṅghe avecca-pasādo</em> perfect faith in the B., the Dhamma &amp; the Sangha, e.g. at MN.i.47; SN.ii.69; SN.iv.271 sq., SN.iv.304, SN.v.344, SN.v.405; AN.i.222; AN.ii.56; AN.iii.212, AN.iii.332, AN.iii.451; AN.iv.406 AN.v.183; further at Pts.i.161 (˚pasanna); Snp.229 (yo ariyasaccāni avecca passati); Pv.iv.1#25.</p><p>Usually taken as ava + ger. of <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em> (*itya), cp. adhicca &amp; abhisamecca, but by P. grammarians as a vecca. The form is not sufficiently clear semantically B.Sk. avetya, e.g. Jtm. 210, is a Sanskritisation of the P. form</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

aveccapasanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aveccappasanna">aveccappasanna</a></p><p>having a trust founded in knowledge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avecca">avecca</a></p>

aveccapasāda|aveccapasaada|aveccapasada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aveccapasaada</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aveccappasāda">aveccappasāda</a></p><p>trust founded in knowledge, intelligent faith.</p>

aveccappasanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aveccappasanna">aveccappasanna</a></p><p>having a trust founded in knowledge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avecca">avecca</a></p>

aveccappasāda|aveccappasaada|aveccappasada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aveccappasaada</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aveccappasāda">aveccappasāda</a></p><p>trust founded in knowledge, intelligent faith.</p>

avedha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be hurt or disturbed, inviolable unshakable, imperturbable Snp.322 (˚dhamma = akampanasabhāva Snp-a.331).</p><p>a + vedha, grd. of <em>vidh (vyadh)</em> to pierce, Sk. avedhya</p>

avedhadamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not subject to trembling; unshakeable.</p>

avedhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avedhin">avedhin</a></p><p>not trembling.</p>

avedhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avedhin">avedhin</a></p><p>not trembling.</p>

avekalla
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(˚-) adjective without deficiency, in <em>-buddhi</em> complete knowledge Ja.vi.297.</p><p>a + vekalla</p>

avekkhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avekkhati">avekkhati</a></p>

avekkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks down on; looks upon; considers.</p>

avekkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to look at, to consider, to see Iti.33 (variant reading ap˚); Dhp.28, Dhp.50, Ja.iv.6; Dhp-a.i.259 (= passati).</p><p>B.Sk. avīkṣate. The regular Pāli form however is apekkhati, to which the BSk. av˚ corresponds</p>

avekkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avekkhiṃ">avekkhiṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avekkhati">avekkhati</a></p>

avekkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to jump, hop lit. to throw (a foot) down Ja.iv.251 (= pacchimapāde khipati C.).</p><p>avaṃ + khipati, <em><a href="bword://ava%E1%B9%83">avaṃ</a></em> here in form <em>ave</em> corresp. to avaḥ, cp. pure for puraḥ etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avaṃ">avaṃ</a></p>

avekkhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>watched</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avekkhati">avekkhati</a></p>

avekkhitācāra|avekkhitaacaara|avekkhitacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avekkhitaacaara</span></p><p>who watches his behavior</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avekkhita">avekkhita</a></p>

avekkhiṃ|avekkhi.m|avekkhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avekkhi.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avekkhiṃ">avekkhiṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avekkhati">avekkhati</a></p>

avera
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective peaceable, mild, friendly Snp.150 (= veravirahita Kp-a.248); Sdhp.338<br />■ ˚ṃ (nt.) friendliness kindness DN.i.247 (˚citta); Dhp.5 (= khantimetta Dhp-a.i.51).</p><p>a + vera</p>

averin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun = avera Dhp.197, Dhp.258.</p>

aversion
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Aversion:</strong> Towards all existence, contemplation of: see: <em>vipassanā</em> 4.</p>

aveti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>understands; knows.</p>

avhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>calling, name; adj. (-˚) called, having the name of Snp.684 (isi˚), Snp.686 (Asit˚), Snp.689 (kanhasiri˚), Snp.1133 (Sace˚, cp. Cnd.624).</p><p>fr. avhayati; cp. Sk. āhvaya “betting”</p>

avhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>calls; summons; invokes; challenges; calls back</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avheti">avheti</a></p>

avhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avhayati &amp; Avheti</p><ol><li>to call upon, invoke, appeal to DN.i.244 (avhayāma imper.) Pv-a.164.</li><li>to call, call up, summon MN.i.17; Ja.ii.10 Ja.ii.252 (= pakkosati); Ja.v.220 (avhayesi); Ja.vi.18, Ja.vi.192, Ja.vi.273 (avhettha pret.); Vv.33#1 (avheti).</li><li>to give a name to call, to address Snp-a.487 (= āmanteti ālapati)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avh%C4%81ta">avhāta</a></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><p>Sk. āhvayati, ā + <em>hū</em> or <em>hvā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avhāta">avhāta</a></p>

avheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>calls; summons; invokes; challenges; calls back</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avhayati">avhayati</a></p>

avheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avhayati &amp; Avheti</p><ol><li>to call upon, invoke, appeal to DN.i.244 (avhayāma imper.) Pv-a.164.</li><li>to call, call up, summon MN.i.17; Ja.ii.10 Ja.ii.252 (= pakkosati); Ja.v.220 (avhayesi); Ja.vi.18, Ja.vi.192, Ja.vi.273 (avhettha pret.); Vv.33#1 (avheti).</li><li>to give a name to call, to address Snp-a.487 (= āmanteti ālapati)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avh%C4%81ta">avhāta</a></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><p>Sk. āhvayati, ā + <em>hū</em> or <em>hvā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avhāta">avhāta</a></p>

avheyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avheti">avheti</a></p>

avhāna|avhaana|avhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaana</span></p><p>summoning, invitation; invocation; naming.</p>

avhāna|avhaana|avhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaana</span></p><ol><li>begging, calling, asking Snp.710; Vism.68 (˚ânabhinandanā).</li><li>addressing, naming Snp-a.605 (= nāma).</li></ol><p>fr. avhayati, Sk. āhvāna in diff. meaning</p>

avhāta|avhaata|avhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaata</span></p><p>called, summoned Ja.iii.165 = (an˚ = anāhuta ayāctia) = Pv.i.12#3, cp. Pv-a.64. The id p. at Thig.129 reads <em>ayācita</em>.</p><p>pp. of avhayati</p>

avhāyana|avhaayana|avhayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaayana</span></p><p>summoning, invitation; invocation; name.</p>

avhāyana|avhaayana|avhayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaayana</span></p><p>calling to, asking, invocation, imploration DN.i.11 (Sir-avhāyane, variant reading avhayana expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at DN-a.i.97 with reading Sirivhāyana as “ehi Siri mayhaṃ sire patiṭṭhāhī ti evaṃ sire Siriyā avhayanaṃ”), DN.i.244, DN.i.245 (variant reading avhāna).</p><p>cp. Sk. āhvayana</p>

avhāyika|avhaayika|avhayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avhaayika</span></p><p>adjective calling, giving a name; masculine one who gives a name Ja.i.401 = Ja.iii.234</p><p>fr. avhaya</p>

aviccaṃ|avicca.m|aviccam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avicca.m</span></p><p>at Ja.v.434 read <em>aviviccaṃ</em> i.e. not secretly, openly.</p><p>a + viviccaṃ</p>

avicāreti|avicaareti|avicareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avicaareti</span></p><p>not to examine Vv-a.336.</p><p>a + vicāreti</p>

aviddasu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective ignorant, foolish Snp.762 (= bāla Snp-a.509); Dhp.268 = Cnd.514 (= aviññū Dhp-a.iii.395); Pv-a.18 (so read for avindasu). Avinasaka (ika)</p><p>a + viddasu</p>

avidūra|aviduura|avidura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aviduura</span></p><p>adjective not far, near; usually in loc. ˚e as adv. near Snp.147.</p><p>a + vidūra</p>

avigata-paccaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Avigata-paccaya:</strong> Non-disappearance, is one of the 24 conditional relations <em>paccaya</em>.</p>

aviha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(or m.) and ~ā m.pl.</p><ol><li>(m.pl.) the name of one of the highest classes o/devas, the first class of suddhāvāsas.</li><li>(n.) the world of the Aviha devas.</li></ol>

aviha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Aviha:</strong> Non-falling, immovable yet derivation is uncertain; Sanskrit <em>avrha</em> is one of the five Pure Abodes <em>suddhāvāsa</em> in the fine-material level. For details, see: under <em>Anāgāmī</em></p>

aviha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the world of the Aviha’s, i.e. the 12th of the 16 Brahmā-words, cp. <em>Kindred Sayings</em> 48 n. 3; <em>Cpd.</em> 139<br />■ SN.i.35, SN.i.60; AN.i.279; Pp.17. Avihimsa (Avihesa)</p><p>of uncertain etym.</p>

avihesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avihiṃsa (Avihesa)</p><p>feminine absence of cruelty, mercy, humanity, friendliness, love DN.iii.213, DN.iii.215, DN.iii.240 (avihesā); Snp.292 (= sakaruṇabhāva Snp-a.318); Iti.82 (˚vitakka).</p><p>a + vihiṃsā</p>

aviheṭhaka|avihe.thaka|avihethaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avihe.thaka</span></p><p>adjective not harassing, not hurting DN.iii.166 (but cp. Snp-a.318 <em>avihesaka</em> in same context) Mil.219.</p><p>a + viheṭhaka</p>

avihimsā|avihimsaa|avihimsa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avihimsaa</span></p><p><strong>Avihimsā,</strong> <em>ahimsā,</em> <em>avihesā:</em> harmlessness, nonviolence, absence of cruelty. The thought of harmlessness or non-cruelty; <em>avihimsā</em> - <em>vitakka</em> is one of the three constituents of right motivation <em>sammā-sankappa,</em> i.e. the 2nd factor of the 8-fold path see: <em>magga</em>. In the several lists of elements <em>dhātu</em> appears also an element of harmlessness <em>avihesā</em> - <em>dhātu</em>, in the sense of an irreducible elementary quality of Noble thought, speech &amp; behaviour. See Dhp. 225, 261, 270, 300.</p>

avihiṃsa|avihi.msa|avihimsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avihi.msa</span></p><p>Avihiṃsa (Avihesa)</p><p>feminine absence of cruelty, mercy, humanity, friendliness, love DN.iii.213, DN.iii.215, DN.iii.240 (avihesā); Snp.292 (= sakaruṇabhāva Snp-a.318); Iti.82 (˚vitakka).</p><p>a + vihiṃsā</p>

avihā|avihaa|aviha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avihaa</span></p><p>A class of devas. Their world ranks among the five foremost of the rupa-worlds, the Suddhāvāsā.DN.ii.52 DN.iii.237 DN.iii.237 Anāgāmīs are born in Avihā and there attain arahantship. The Buddha once visited Avihā. DN.ii.50–51</p>

avijjā|avijjaa|avijja
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avijjaa</span></p><p><strong>Avijjā:</strong> I gnorance, nescience, the blindness of not knowing, is synonymous with confusion <em>moha</em> (see <em>mūla</em> ), is the primary &amp; deepest root of all evil and suffering in the world, veiling man’s mental eyes and preventing him from seeing the true nature of things. It is the confusion that fools beings by making life appear to them as permanent, happy, substantial and beautiful and preventing them from seeing that everything in reality is impermanent, liable to suffering, void of ‘I’ and ‘mine’, and basically impure see: <em>vipallāsa</em>. Ignorance is defined as not knowing the Four Noble Truths, namely, suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the way to its ceasing see: S. XII, 4.</p><p>As ignorance is the foundation of all life-maintaining actions, and the root of all evil and suffering, it therefore stands first in the formula of Dependent Origination <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em>. But for that reason, says Vis.M XVII, 36f ignorance should not be regarded as the causeless root-cause of the world, since is not causeless. The cause of it is stated thus: With the arising of mental fermentations <em>āsava</em> there is the arising of ignorance M. 9. But there is a figurative way in which it can be treated as a root-cause; namely, when it is made to serve as a starting point in an exposition of the Round of Existence… As it is said: No first beginning of ignorance can be perceived, Bhikkhus, before which ignorance was not, and after which it came to be. But it can be perceived that ignorance has its specific causal condition <em>idappaccaya</em> A. X, 61. The same statement is made A. X, 62 about the craving for existence <em>bhava-tanhā</em> (see <em>tanhā</em> ). Craving and ignorance are called the outstanding causes or creators of the kamma that lead to unhappy and happy destinies Vis.M XVII, 38.</p><p>As ignorance still exists though in a very refined way until the attainment of Arahatship, it is counted as the last of the 10 mental chains <em>samyojana,</em> which bind beings to the cycle of rebirths. As the first two roots of evil, greed and hate (see: <em>mūla</em> ), are on their part rooted in ignorance, consequently all disadvantageous states of mind are inseparably bound up with ignorance. Ignorance or confusion is the most obstinate, dense, deep, subtle, hidden and fearsome of the three roots of evil.</p><p>Ignorance is one of the fermentations <em>āsava</em> and latent tendencies <em>anusaya</em>. It is often called a hindrance <em>nīvarana</em> e.g. in S. XV, 3; A. X, 61 but does not appear together with the usual list of five hindrances. It is however immanent in them all, yet especially dominant in doubt &amp; uncertainty <em>vicikicchā.</em></p>

avijjā|avijjaa|avijja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avijjaa</span></p><p>ignorance; the main root of evil and of continual rebirth (see <em><a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADicca">paṭicca</a></em>-samuppāda, cp. SN.ii.6, SN.ii.9, SN.ii.12; Snp.p.141 &amp; many other passages) See on term; <em>Cpd.</em> 83 n. 3, 187 sq, 262 sq. &amp; for further detail; <em><a href="bword://vijj%C4%81">vijjā</a></em>. avijjā is termed an <em><a href="bword://anusaya">anusaya</a></em> (DN.iii.254, DN.iii.282; SN.iv.205, SN.iv.208 sq., SN.iv.212); it is one of the <em>āsavā</em> (Vin.iii.4; DN.i.84; DN.iii.216; Iti.49; Dhs.1100, Dhs.1109), of the <em>oghā</em> (DN.iii.230, DN.iii.276; Dhs.390, Dhs.1061, Dhs.1162), of the <em>nīvaraṇāni</em> (SN.ii.23; AN.i.223; Iti.8; Dhs.1162, Dhs.1486), of the <em>saṃyojanāni</em> (DN.iii.254; Dhs.1131, Dhs.1460). See for various characterisatons the foll. passages: Vin.i.1; Vin.iii.3; DN.iii.212 DN.iii.230, DN.iii.234, DN.iii.274; MN.i.54, MN.i.67, MN.i.144; SN.ii.4, SN.ii.26, SN.ii.263; SN.iii.47 SN.iii.162; SN.iv.256; SN.v.52; AN.i.8, AN.i.285; AN.ii.132, AN.ii.158, AN.ii.247; AN.iii.84 sq. AN.iii.414; AN.iv.228; Iti.34 (yā kāc’ imā duggatiyo asmiṃ loke paramhi ca avijjāmūlakā sabbā icchā-lobha-sammussayā), Iti.57, Iti.81; Snp.199, Snp.277, Snp.729 (jāti-maraṇa-saṃsāraṃ ye vajanti punappunaṃ… avijjāy’eva sā gati), Snp.730, Snp.1026, Snp.1033 (avijjāya nivuto loko); Dhp.243; Cnd.99; Pp.21; Dhs.390 Dhs.1061, Dhs.1162; Dhp-a.iii.350; Dhp-a.iv.161 (˚paligha).</p><p>Sk. avidyā; fr. a + <em>vid</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paṭicca">paṭicca</a>, <a href="bword://vijjā">vijjā</a>, <a href="bword://anusaya">anusaya</a></p>

avijānaṃ|avijaana.m|avijanam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avijaana.m</span></p><p>not knowing, ignorant Dhp.38, Dhp.60; Iti.103.</p><p>a + vijānaṃ</p>

avikampamāna|avikampamaana|avikampamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avikampamaana</span></p><p>adjective not hesitating, not wavering, not doubting Ja.iv.310 (= anosakkamāna C.; Kern takes it at this passage as a + vikalpamāna, see</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s.v., but unnecessarily), Ja.vi.176 (= nirāsanka C.); Ja.vi.273.</p><p>a + vi + kampamāna, ppr. med. of <em>kamp</em></p>

avikampi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avikampin">avikampin</a></p><p>not shaking; intrepid, confident.</p>

avikampin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avikampin">avikampin</a></p><p>not shaking; intrepid, confident.</p>

avikampin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unmoved, not shaking, steady Vv.50#22 (= acala Vv-a.215).</p><p>fr. a + vi + <em>kamp</em></p>

avikkhepa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Avikkhepa:</strong> Undistractedness, is a synonym of concentration <em>samādhi</em>, one-pointedness of mind <em>citt’ekaggatā</em> and calm tranquillity <em>samatha</em>, further see: <em>samatha</em> - <em>vipassanā.</em></p>

avikkhepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>calmness, balance, equanimity DN.iii.213; AN.i.83; Pts.i.94; Pts.ii.228; Dhs.11, Dhs.15, Dhs.570.</p><p>a + vikkhepa</p>

avikopin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not agitated, not moving, unshaken, undisturbed Ja.vi.226 (acchejja +).</p><p>a + vikopin; fr. vi + <em>kup</em></p>

avinibbhoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be distinguished, indistinct Ja.iii.428 (˚sadda).</p><p>a + vinibbhoga</p>

avinibbhujaṃ|avinibbhuja.m|avinibbhujam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avinibbhuja.m</span></p><p>adjective unable to distinguish or to know Ja.v.121 (= atīrento C.).</p><p>ppr. of a + vinibbhujati</p>

avinicchayaññū|avinicchaya~n~nuu|avinicchayannu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avinicchaya~n~nuu</span></p><p>adjective not knowing how to decide Ja.v.367.</p><p>a + vinicchaya + ñū</p>

avināsaka|avinaasaka|avinasaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avinaasaka</span></p><p>Avināsaka (˚ika)</p><p>adjective not causing destruction AN.iii.38 (˚ika); Ja.v.116 (= anāsaka C.).</p><p>a + vināsa + ka</p>

avināsana|avinaasana|avinasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avinaasana</span></p><p>adjective imperishable Dpvs.iv.16.</p><p>a + vināsana</p>

avināsika|avinaasika|avinasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avinaasika</span></p><p>Avināsaka (˚ika)</p><p>adjective not causing destruction AN.iii.38 (˚ika); Ja.v.116 (= anāsaka C.).</p><p>a + vināsa + ka</p>

avipakkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not gone away, not dispersing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avippakkanta">avippakkanta</a></p>

avipariṇāma|avipari.naama|aviparinama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avipari.naama</span></p><p>absence of change, steadfastness, endurance DN.i.18; DN.iii.31, DN.iii.33 (˚dhamma); DN-a.i.113 (= jarā-vasena vipariṇāmassa abhāvato).</p><p>a + viparināma</p>

avippakkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>not gone away, not dispersing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avipakkanta">avipakkanta</a></p>

avippavāsa|avippavaasa|avippavasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avippavaasa</span></p><p>adjective noun thoughtfulness, mindfulness, attention; adj. not neglectful, mindful, attentive eager Vin.v.216; Snp.1142 (cp. Cnd.101: anussatiyā bhāvento); DN-a.i.104 (appamādo vuccati satiyā avippavāso) Dhp-a.iv.26 (appamāda = satiyā avippavāsa).</p><p>a + vippavāsa</p>

avippaṭisāra|avippa.tisaara|avippatisara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avippa.tisaara</span></p><p>absence of regret or remorse AN.iii.46.</p><p>a + vippaṭisāra</p>

avirodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>absence of obstruction, gentleness MN.ii.105 = Thag.875.</p><p>a + virodha</p>

avirodhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= avirodha Ja.iii.320, Ja.iii.412; Ja.v.378.</p>

aviruddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not contrary, unobstructed, free, without difficulties Dhp.406; Snp.365, Snp.704, Snp.854.</p><p>a + viruddha</p>

avirūḷhi|aviruu.lhi|avirulhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aviruu.lhi</span></p><p>absence or cesssation of growth Snp.235; Dhp-a.i.245 (˚dhamma).</p><p>a + virūḷhi</p>

avisaggatā|avisaggataa|avisaggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisaggataa</span></p><p>state of being undisturbed harmony, balance Ja.vi.224 (C. avisaggata). Cp. <em><a href="bword://avyagga">avyagga</a></em>.</p><p>a + visaggatā, variant reading viy˚, thus as a + viyagga, Sk. vyagra = ākula</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avyagga">avyagga</a></p>

avisare
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.v.117 according to Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s.v. corrupted from <em>avisaye</em>, i.e. towards a wrong or unworthy object, C. differently: avisare = avisaritvā atikkamitva; variant reading <em>adhisare</em>.</p><p>a + visaya, loc</p>

avisaṃvādaka|avisa.mvaadaka|avisamvadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisa.mvaadaka</span></p><p>adjective not deceiving, not lying DN.i.4; DN.iii.170; Pp.57; DN-a.i.73.</p><p>a + visaṃvada + ka</p>

avisaṃvādanatā|avisa.mvaadanataa|avisamvadanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisa.mvaadanataa</span></p><p>honesty, faithfulness, uprightness DN.iii.190.</p><p>abstr. fr. a + visaṃvāda</p>

avisaṃvādeti|avisa.mvaadeti|avisamvadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisa.mvaadeti</span></p><p>to keep one’s word, to be honest, to be true Ja.v.124.</p><p>a + visaṃ + Caus. of <em>vad</em></p>

avissaji
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.vi.79 is with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. better to be read <em>avassaji</em> (see <em>avassajati</em>).</p>

avissajjiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not to be given away, inalienable (cp. avebhangiya) Vin.i.305 (˚ika for ˚iya); Vin.ii.170 (five such objects in detail); Vin.v.216 (+ avebh˚) Ja.vi.568.</p><p>grd. of a + vissajjati</p>

avissāsaniya|avissaasaniya|avissasaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avissaasaniya</span></p><p>adjective not to be trusted, untrustworthy Ja.iii.474.</p><p>a + visāsana + iya, ika</p>

avisāhaṭa|avisaaha.ta|avisahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisaaha.ta</span></p><p>adjective imperturbed Dhs.15, Dhs.24, Dhs.287, Dhs.570. (˚mānasata).</p><p>a + visāhaṭa</p>

avisāri|avisaari|avisari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avisārin">avisārin</a></p><p>not diffuse.</p>

avisārin|avisaarin|avisarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avisaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://avisārin">avisārin</a></p><p>not diffuse.</p>

avitakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from thought DN.iii.219, DN.iii.274; Thig.75 (“where reasonings cease” trsl.); Dhs.161 (“free from the working of conception” trsl.), Dhs.504 etc.</p><p>a + vitakka</p>

avivayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i></p><p>not marriageable; not suitable for marriage.</p>

avivāda|avivaada|avivada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avivaada</span></p><p>absence of contesting or disputing, agreement, harmony DN.iii.245; Snp.896 (˚bhūma Snp-a.557 or ˚bhumma Mnd.308, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as Nibbāna).</p><p>a + vivāda</p>

aviññāṇaka|avi~n~naa.naka|avinnanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avi~n~naa.naka</span></p><p>adjective senscless, without feeling or consciousness, unfeeling Dhp-a.i.6 (saviññāṇaka +).</p><p>a + viññāṇa + ka</p>

aviññū|avi~n~nuu|avinnu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avi~n~nuu</span></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://aviddasu">aviddasu</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aviddasu">aviddasu</a></p>

avoidance
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Avoidance:</strong> see: <em>Cāritta-sīla</em> - The effort to avoid all evil states, see: <em>padhāna</em> 1.</p>

avosita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in neg. <em>an˚</em>; unfulfilled, undone Thag.101.</p><p>reading uncertain, cp. avyosita</p>

avyagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not bewildered, not confused SN.v.66. Cp. <em><a href="bword://avisaggat%C4%81">avisaggatā</a></em>.</p><p>a + vyagga, Sc. vyagra</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avisaggatā">avisaggatā</a></p>

avyatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not miserable, fortunate Ja.iii.466 (= akilamāna C.).</p><p>a + vyatha, cp. Sk. vyathā misfortune</p>

avyattatā|avyattataa|avyattata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyattataa</span></p><p>state or condition of not being manifest or visible, concealment, hiding Dhp-a.ii.38.</p><p>abstr. fr. avyatta</p>

avyaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>absence of loss or change, safety DN.i.72 (instr. ˚ena safely); Mil.393 (as abbaya T.).</p><p>a + vyaya</p>

avyayaggamanasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with non-distracted mind; with mind focused</p><p>See also: avyaggamānasa</p>

avyayaggamānasa|avyayaggamaanasa|avyayaggamanasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyayaggamaanasa</span></p><p>with non-distracted mind; with mind focused</p><p>See also: avyaggamanasa</p>

avyosita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not having reached perfection, imperfect Thag.784 (aby˚).</p><p>a + vyosita, Sk. vyavasita</p>

avyāharati|avyaaharati|avyaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaaharati</span></p><p>not to bring or procure Ja.v.80.</p><p>a + vy + āharati</p>

avyākata|avyaakata|avyakata
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaakata</span></p><p><strong>Avyākata:</strong> Indeterminable - i.e. neither determined as kammically advantageous nor as disadvantageous - are the kammically neutral or inert, i.e. amoral, states of consciousness and mental properties. They are either 1: Mere kamma-results <em>vipāka</em>, as e.g. all the sense perceptions and the mental properties associated therewith, or they are 2: Kammically independent &amp; inert functions <em>kiriya</em> - <em>citta</em>, i.e. neither kammic nor kamma-resultant. See Tab. I. App.</p>

avyāpajjha|avyaapajjha|avyapajjha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaapajjha</span></p><p>Avyāpajjha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(abyābajjha) neuter (act.) kindness of heart; (pass.) freedom from suffering (Ep. of Nibbāna Vin.i.183 (avyāpajjh˚âdhimutta); Iti.31 (abyābajjh’ārāma).</p><p>a + vyapajjha or bajjha, a confusion between the roots <em>bādh</em> or <em>pad</em></p><p>Avyāpajjha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(abyābajjha) adj.) free from oppression or injury not hurting, kind DN.ii.242 (avera +), DN.ii.276; MN.i.90; Iti.16 = Iti.52 (sukhaṃ); Mil.410 (avera +).</p><p>either a + *vyāpadya or more likely a + *vyābādhya</p>

avyāpanna|avyaapanna|avyapanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaapanna</span></p><p>adjective free from desire to injure, free from malice, friendly, benevolent DN.iii.82–DN.iii.83 (˚citta) AN.ii.220 (id.); Pp.68 (id.)<br />■ Same in B.Sk. e.g. Divy.105, Divy.302.</p><p>a + vyāpanna</p>

avyāpāda|avyaapaada|avyapada
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaapaada</span></p><p><strong>Avyāpāda:</strong> Hatelessness, non-ill-will, good-will, amity, goodness; is one of the three kinds of right motivation (see: <em>sacca</em> IV. 2), or advantageous thoughts <em>vitakka</em> and is the 9th of the 10 advantageous courses of actions <em>kamma-patha</em> II. The most frequently used synonyms are <em>adosa</em> (see: <em>mūla</em> ) and <em>mettā</em> (see: <em>brahma-vihāra</em> ).</p>

avyāpāda|avyaapaada|avyapada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaapaada</span></p><p>absence of desire to injure, freedom from malice DN.iii.215, DN.iii.229, DN.iii.240; Iti.82 (all MSS have aby˚); Dhs.33, Dhs.36, Dhs.277, Dhs.313, Dhs.1056.</p><p>a + vyāpāda</p>

avyāseka|avyaaseka|avyaseka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaaseka</span></p><p>adjective untouched, unimpaired DN.i.182 (˚sukha = kilesa vyāseka-virahitattā avyāseka DN-a.i.183); Pp.59.</p><p>a + vy + āseka</p>

avyāvaṭa|avyaava.ta|avyavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaava.ta</span></p><p>adjective not occupied, i.e. careless, neglectful, not worrying Vin.iii.136; Cnd.72 (abyāvaṭa for appossukka Snp.43); Ja.iii.65; Ja.vi.188. Mil.177 (abyā˚).</p><p>a + vyāvaṭa = Sk vyāpṛta</p>

avyāyata|avyaayata|avyayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaayata</span></p><p>adjective at random, without discrimination, careless Ja.i.496 (= avyatta C.).</p><p>a + vyāyata of <em>yam</em></p>

avyāyika|avyaayika|avyayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaayika</span></p><p>imperishable; lasting.</p>

avyāyika|avyaayika|avyayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avyaayika</span></p><p>adjective not liable to loss or change, imperishable Ja.v.508 (= avigacchanaka C.).</p><p>fr. avyaya</p>

avāgata|avaagata|avagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaagata</span></p><p>only in phrase dhammā avāgat-amhā, we are fallen from righteousness, Ja.v.82 (C. explains apāgata).</p><p>ava + ā + gacchati</p>

avāka|avaaka|avaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaaka</span></p><p><em>Blyxa oryzetorum</em>, a short, tufted aquatic plant often found growing in rice fields Ja.iii.522.</p>

avākaroti|avaakaroti|avakaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaakaroti</span></p><ol><li>to revoke, undo, rescind, not fulfill, spoil, destroy Ja.iii.339 (avākayirā = avakareyya chindeyya C.); Ja.v.495, Ja.v.500; Ja.vi.280.</li><li>to give back, restore Ja.vi.577 (= deti C.).</li></ol><p>either ava + ā + karoti or avaṃ + karoti, the latter more probable. It is not necessary to take it with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. as Sk. apākṛṇoti, apa + ā + <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

avākirati|avaakirati|avakirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaakirati</span></p><p>wrong by Hardy Vv-a Index for <em><a href="bword://avakirati">avakirati</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakirati">avakirati</a></p>

avāpurati|avaapurati|avapurati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaapurati</span></p><p>opens ( a door)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāpurati">apāpurati</a></p>

avāpurati|avaapurati|avapurati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaapurati</span></p><p>to open (a door) Ja.i.63; Ja.vi.373.</p><p>same as apāpurati</p>

avāpuraṇa|avaapura.na|avapurana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaapura.na</span></p><p>(from apāpurati)a key</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāpuraṇa">apāpuraṇa</a></p>

avāpuraṇa|avaapura.na|avapurana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaapura.na</span></p><p>key SN.iii.132; AN.iv.374.</p><p>same as apāpuraṇa</p>

avāpurīyati|avaapuriiyati|avapuriyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaapuriiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avāpurati">avāpurati</a></p>

avāvaṭa|avaava.ta|avavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaava.ta</span></p><p>adjective unobstructed, unhindered, free. Of a woman, not married Ja.v.213 (= apetâvaraṇā, which read for ˚bharaṇā, apariggahitā C.).</p><p>a + vāvaṭa</p>

avāyima|avaayima|avayima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaayima</span></p><p>not made by weaving; not woven.</p>

avāṭuka|avaa.tuka|avatuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avaa.tuka</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ap%C4%81%E1%B9%ADuka">apāṭuka</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāṭuka">apāṭuka</a></p>

avī|avii|avi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">avii</span></p><p>Avī˚</p><p>in general see <em>vī˚</em>.</p>

avīci|aviici|avici
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aviici</span></p><p>Hell. Descriptions of it are to be found in several places in the four Nikāyas MN.iii.183 AN.i.141–142 It is a quadrangular space, one hundred leagues each way, four-doored, walled all round and above with steel and with floor of incandescent molten steel.</p><p>It is noteworthy that the word Avīci occurs only once in the four Nikāyas—namely, in a passage in the Cakkavatti-Sīhanāda Sutta of the Dīgha Nikāya, DN.iii.75 AN.i.159 but in this context there is no indication that the name refers to a purgatory. The word is not found in a list of purgatories given in the Sutta Nipāta Snp.126–131 and in the Saṃyutta. SN.i.152 It is, however, found in a poem in the Itivuttaka It89 which recurs in the Vinaya, Vin.ii.203 and there it is specifically called a niraya.</p><p>In the Dīgha passage mentioned above, the reference to Avīci is in connection with a tremendous growth of population which will occur in Jambudīpa in a future age. Houses will be so close that a cock could fly from any one to the next, and one would think it Avīci.</p><p>5hell</p>

avīci|aviici|avici
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aviici</span></p><p><strong>Avīci:</strong> is the name of one of the most frightful hells <em>niraya</em>.</p>

avīci|aviici|avici
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aviici</span></p><ol><li><em>avīciniraya</em>, one of the (great) hells (see <em><a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a></em>), described in vivid colours at many passages of the Pāli canon, e.g at Vin.ii.203 = Iti.86; Mnd.18, Mnd.347, Mnd.405 = Cnd.304 iiid; Pts.i.83; Dhs.1281; Ja.i.71, Ja.i.96; Ja.iii.182; Ja.iv.159; Dhp-a.i.148; Pv-a.52; Snp-a.290; Sdhp.37, Sdhp.194; Pgdp.5 sq. etc etc.</li><li>disintegration, decay Vism.449 (a. jarā nāma).</li></ol><p>B.Sk. avīci a + vīci (?) no intermission, or no pleasure (?), unknown, but very likely popular etym.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a></p>

awakenment
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Awakening:</strong> see: <em>Bodhi</em></p>

aya FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ayo &amp; Aya</p><p>neuter iron. The <em>nom.</em> <em><a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a></em> found only in set of 5 metals forming an alloy of gold (jātarūpa), viz <em>ayo, loha</em> (copper), <em><a href="bword://tipu">tipu</a></em> (tin), <em><a href="bword://s%C4%ABsa">sīsa</a></em> (lead), <em><a href="bword://sajjha">sajjha</a></em> (silver AN.iii.16 = SN.v.92; of obl. cases only the <em>instr.</em> <em>ayasā</em> occurs Dhp.240 (= ayato Dhp-a.iii.344); Pv.i.10#13 (paṭikujjita, of Niraya)<br />■ Iron is the material used κα ̓τἐςοξήν in the outfit &amp; construction of Purgatory or Niraya (see <em><a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a></em> &amp; Avīci &amp; cp. Vism.56 sq.)<br />■ In comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> both <em>ayo˚</em> &amp; <em>aya˚</em> occur as bases.</p><ol><li><p>ayo˚</p><ul><li><em>-kapāla</em> an iron pot AN.iv.70 (variant reading ˚guhala) Cnd.304#iii. d.2 (of Niraya).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%AB%E1%B9%ADa">kūṭa</a></em> an iron hammer Pv-a.284.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kh%C4%ABla">khīla</a></em> an iron stake SN.v.444; MN.iii.183 = Cnd.304#iii. c; Snp-a.479.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gu%E1%B8%B7a">guḷa</a></em> an iron ball SN.v.283; Dhp.308; Iti.43 = Iti.90; Thig.489; DN-a.i.84.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a></em> an iron club Ud.93; Vv-a.20.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a></em> an iron house Ja.iv.492.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADala">paṭala</a></em> an iron roof or ceiling (of Niraya) Pv-a.52.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81k%C4%81ra">pākāra</a></em> an iron fence Pv.i.10#13 = Cnd.304#iii. d.1.</li><li><em>-maya made</em> of iron Snp.669 (kūṭa); Ja.iv.492 (nāvā); Pv.i.10#14 (bhūmi of N.); Pv-a.43, Pv-a.52.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://muggara">muggara</a></em> an iron club Pv-a.55</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85ku">saṅku</a></em> an iron spike SN.iv.168; Snp.667.</li></ul></li><li><p>aya˚</p><ul><li><em>-kapāla</em> = ayo˚ Dhp-a.i.148 (variant reading ayo˚).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> a worker in iron Mil.331.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%AB%E1%B9%ADa">kūṭa</a></em> = ayo˚ Ja.i.108; Dhp-a.ii.69 (variant reading).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://na%E1%B9%85gala">naṅgala</a></em> an iron plough Dhp-a.i.223; Dhp-a.iii.67</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADaka">paṭṭaka</a></em> an iron plate or sheet (cp. loha˚) Ja.v.359</li><li><em>-paṭhavi</em> an iron floor (of Avīci) Dhp-a.i.148.</li><li><em>-saṅghāṭaka</em> an iron (door) post Dhp-a.iv.104.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C5%ABla">sūla</a></em> an iron stake Snp.667; Dhp-a.i.148.</li></ul></li></ol><p>Sk. ayaḥ nt. iron &amp; ore, Idg. *ajes-, cp. Av. ayah, Lat. aes, Goth. aiz, Ohg. ēr (= Ger. Erz.) Ags. ār (= E. ore).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a>, <a href="bword://tipu">tipu</a>, <a href="bword://sīsa">sīsa</a>, <a href="bword://sajjha">sajjha</a>, <a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a>, <a href="bword://kūṭa">kūṭa</a>, <a href="bword://khīla">khīla</a>, <a href="bword://guḷa">guḷa</a>, <a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a>, <a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a>, <a href="bword://paṭala">paṭala</a>, <a href="bword://pākāra">pākāra</a>, <a href="bword://muggara">muggara</a>, <a href="bword://saṅku">saṅku</a>, <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://kūṭa">kūṭa</a>, <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a>, <a href="bword://paṭṭaka">paṭṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://sūla">sūla</a></p>

aya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a></p><p>(nom. <em>ayo</em>; acc. <em>ayo</em>, <em>ayaṃ</em>; instr. <em>ayasā</em>, <em>ayena</em>; abl. <em>ayasā</em>, <em>ayato</em>) iron; metal.</p>

aya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Aya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a></em>.</p><p>Aya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(fr. <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, go)</p><ol><li>income, in <em>aya-potthaka</em> receipt book Ja.i.2.</li><li>inlet (for water, <em>aya-mukha</em>) DN.i.74; AN.ii.166, AN.iv.287.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

ayaguḷa|ayagu.la|ayagula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayagu.la</span></p><p>an iron ball</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayaguḷa">ayaguḷa</a></p>

ayakapāla|ayakapaala|ayakapala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayakapaala</span></p><p>an iron pot</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayokapāla">ayokapāla</a></p>

ayakaṭāha|ayaka.taaha|ayakataha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayaka.taaha</span></p><p>an iron pot or pan</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, ayokaṭaha</p>

ayakūṭa|ayakuu.ta|ayakuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayakuu.ta</span></p><p>iron hammer.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayokūṭa">ayokūṭa</a></p>

ayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>“going”, road</li><li>going to, goal SN.v.167 (ekāyano maggo leading to one goal, a direct way), SN.v.185 (id.); DN-a.i.313; Dāvs iv.40. See also eka˚.</li></ol><p>Vedic ayana, fr. <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

ayapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an iron bowl</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayopatta">ayopatta</a></p>

ayapaṭṭa|ayapa.t.ta|ayapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayapa.t.ta</span></p><p>an iron strip or band</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayopaṭṭa">ayopaṭṭa</a></p>

ayas
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a></p><p>(nom. <em>ayo</em>; acc. <em>ayo</em>, <em>ayaṃ</em>; instr. <em>ayasā</em>, <em>ayena</em>; abl. <em>ayasā</em>, <em>ayato</em>) iron; metal.</p>

ayasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>made of iron</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyasa">āyasa</a></p>

ayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>ill repute, disgrace Mil.139, Mil.272; Dāvs i.8.</p><p>a + yasa, cp. Sk. ayaśaḥ</p>

ayasiṅghātaka|ayasi"nghaataka|ayasinghataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayasi"nghaataka</span></p><p>an iron bowl</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayosiṅghātaka">ayosiṅghātaka</a></p>

ayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes; goes towards; reaches, obtains; reaches (a state); is involved (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a>, <a href="bword://iti">iti</a></p>

ayaṃ|aya.m|ayam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aya.m</span></p><p>pronoun demonstr. pron. “this, he”; f. ayaṃ; nt. idaṃ &amp; imaṃ “this, it” etc. This pron. combines in its inflection two stems, viz. <em>as˚</em>; (ayaṃ in nom. m. &amp; f.) &amp; <em>im˚</em>; (id in nom. nt.).</p><h3>I. Forms.</h3><ol><li>■ (sg.) nom. m. ayaṃ Snp.235; Ja.i.168, Ja.i.279<br />■ f. ayaṃ [Sk. iyaṃ] Kp.vii.12; Ja.ii.128, Ja.ii.133;<br />■ nt. idaṃ Snp.224; Ja.iii.53; &amp; imaṃ Mil.46.;<br />■ <em>acc.</em> m. imaṃ Ja.ii.160<br />■ f. imaṃ [Sk. īmāṃ] Snp.545, Snp.1002; Ja.i.280.<br />■ <em>gen. dat.</em> m imassa Ja.i.222, Ja.i.279 &amp; assa Snp.234, Snp.1100; Kp.vii.12 (dat.); Ja.ii.158;<br />■ f. imissā Ja.i.179 &amp; assā [Sk. asyāḥ] Ja.i.290; Dhp-a.iii.172.<br />■ <em>instr.</em> m. nt. iminā Ja.i.279; Pv-a.80 &amp; (peculiarly or perhaps for amunā) aminā Snp.137;<br />■ f imāya [Sk. anayā] Ja.i.267. The instr. anena [Sk. anena is not proved in Pāli.]<br />■ <em>abl.</em> <strong>asmā</strong> Snp.185; Dhp.220; imasmā (not proved).;<br />■ <em>loc.</em> m. nt. imasmiṃ Kp iii.; Ja.ii.159 &amp; asmiṃ Snp.634; Dhp.242;<br />■ f. imissā Pv-a.79 (or imissaṃ?) &amp; imāyaṃ (no ref.)</li><li>■ <em>(pl.) nom.</em> m. ime Ja.i.221; Pv.i.8#3;<br />■ f. imā [Sk. imāḥ] Snp.897 &amp; imāyo Snp.1122;<br />■ nt. imāni [= Sk.] Vin.i.84.<br />■ <em>acc.</em> m. ime [Sk. imān] Ja.i.266; Ja.ii.416;<br />■ f. imā [Sk. imāḥ] Snp.429; Ja.ii.160.<br />■ <em>gen.</em> imesaṃ Ja.ii.160 &amp; esaṃ [Sk. eṣāṃ] MN.ii.86, &amp; esānaṃ MN.ii.154; MN.iii.259;<br />■ f. also āsaṃ Ja.i.302 (= etāsaṃ C.) &amp; imāsaṃ;<br />■ <em>instr.</em> m. nt imehi Ja.vi.364;<br />■ f. imāhi. <em>loc.</em><br />■ m. nt imesu [Sk. eṣu] Ja.i.307.</li></ol><h3>II. <em>Meanings</em></h3><ol><li><em><a href="bword://aya%E1%B9%83">ayaṃ</a></em> refers to <em>what is immediately in front</em> of the speaker (the subject in question) or before his eyes or in his present time &amp; situation, thus often to be translated by “before our eyes”, “the present” “this here”, “just this” (&amp; not the other) (opp. para) viz. atthi imasmiṃ kāye “in this our visible body” Kp iii. yath’ âyaṃ padīpo “like this lamp here” Snp.235; ayaṃ dakkhiṇā dinnā “the gift which is just given before our eyes” Kp.vii.12; ime pādā imaṃ sīsaṃ ayaṃ kāyo Pv.i.8#3 asmiṃ loke paramhi ca “in this world &amp; the other” Snp.634, asmā lokā paraṃ lokaṃ kathaṃ pecca na socati Snp.185; cp. also Dhp.220, Dhp.410; Ja.i.168; Ja.iii.53</li><li>It refers to <em>what immediately precedes</em> the present of the speaker, or to what has just been mentioned in the sentence; viz. yaṃ kiñci vittaṃ… idam pi Buddhe ratanaṃ “whatever… that” Snp.224; ime divase these days (just gone) Ja.ii.416; cp. also Vin.i.84; Snp.429; Ja.ii.128 Ja.ii.160</li><li>It refers to <em>what immediately follows</em> either in time or in thought or in connection: dve ime antā “these are the two extremes, viz.” Vin.i.10; ayaṃ eva ariyo maggo “this then is the way” ibid.; cp. Ja.i.280.</li><li>With a touch of (often sarcastic) characterisation it establishes a <em>closer personal relation</em> between the speaker &amp; the object in question &amp; is to be translated by “like that such (like), that there, yonder, yon”, e.g. imassa vānarindassa “of that fellow, the monkey” Ja.i.279; cp. Ja.i.222, Ja.i.307; Ja.ii.160 (imesaṃ sattānaṃ “creatures like us”) So also repeated as ayañ ca ayañ ca “this and this”, “so and so” Ja.ii.3; idañ c’ idañ ca “such &amp; such a thing Ja.ii.5</li><li>In combination with a pron. rel. it expresses either a <em>generalisation</em> (whoever, whatever) or a <em>specialisation</em> (= that is to say, what there is of, i.e. Ger. und zwar), e.g. yâyaṃ taṇhā Vin.i.10; yo ca ayaṃ… yo ca ayaṃ “I mean this… and I mean” ibid.; ye kec’ ime Snp.381; yadidaṃ “i.e.” Mil.25; yatha-y-idaṃ “in order that” (w. pot.) Snp.1092. See also seyyathīdaṃ</li><li>The gen. of all genders functions in general as a <em>possessive pron.</em> of the 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = his, her, its (lit. of him etc.) and thus resembles the use of tassa, e.g. āsava’ ssa na vijjanti “his are no intoxications” Snp.1100; sīlaṃ assā bhindāpessāmi “I shall cause her character to be defamed” Ja.i.290; assa bhariyā “his wife” Ja.ii.158 etc. freq.</li></ol><p>Sk. ayaṃ etc., pron. base Idg. *i (cp. Sk. iha), f. *ī. Cp. Gr. ἰν, μιν; Lat. is (f. ea, nt. id); Goth is, nt. ita; Ohg. er (= he), nt. ez (= it); Lith. jìs (he) f. jì (she).</p><p>See also: asmā, <a href="bword://ayaṃ">ayaṃ</a></p>

ayira
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ayira &amp; Ayyira</p><p>n-adj. n. ariyan nobleman, gentleman (opp. servant); (adj.) arīyan, well-born, belonging to the ruling race, noble, aristocratic gentlemanly Ja.v.257; Vv.39#6<br />■ f. <em>ayirā</em> lady, mistress (of a servant) Ja.ii.349 (variant reading oyyakā); voc. ayire my lady Ja.v.138 (= ayye C.).</p><p>Vedic ārya, Metathesis for ariya as diaeretic form of ārya, of which the contracted (assimilation) form is ayya. See also ariya</p>

ayiraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>master; lord.</p>

ayiraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= ayira; cp. ariyaka &amp; ayyaka; DN.iii.190 (variant reading BB yy); Ja.ii.313.</p>

ayo
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ayo &amp; Aya</p><p>neuter iron. The <em>nom.</em> <em><a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a></em> found only in set of 5 metals forming an alloy of gold (jātarūpa), viz <em>ayo, loha</em> (copper), <em><a href="bword://tipu">tipu</a></em> (tin), <em><a href="bword://s%C4%ABsa">sīsa</a></em> (lead), <em><a href="bword://sajjha">sajjha</a></em> (silver AN.iii.16 = SN.v.92; of obl. cases only the <em>instr.</em> <em>ayasā</em> occurs Dhp.240 (= ayato Dhp-a.iii.344); Pv.i.10#13 (paṭikujjita, of Niraya)<br />■ Iron is the material used κα ̓τἐςοξήν in the outfit &amp; construction of Purgatory or Niraya (see <em><a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a></em> &amp; Avīci &amp; cp. Vism.56 sq.)<br />■ In comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> both <em>ayo˚</em> &amp; <em>aya˚</em> occur as bases.</p><ol><li><p>ayo˚</p><ul><li><em>-kapāla</em> an iron pot AN.iv.70 (variant reading ˚guhala) Cnd.304#iii. d.2 (of Niraya).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%AB%E1%B9%ADa">kūṭa</a></em> an iron hammer Pv-a.284.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kh%C4%ABla">khīla</a></em> an iron stake SN.v.444; MN.iii.183 = Cnd.304#iii. c; Snp-a.479.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gu%E1%B8%B7a">guḷa</a></em> an iron ball SN.v.283; Dhp.308; Iti.43 = Iti.90; Thig.489; DN-a.i.84.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a></em> an iron club Ud.93; Vv-a.20.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a></em> an iron house Ja.iv.492.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADala">paṭala</a></em> an iron roof or ceiling (of Niraya) Pv-a.52.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81k%C4%81ra">pākāra</a></em> an iron fence Pv.i.10#13 = Cnd.304#iii. d.1.</li><li><em>-maya made</em> of iron Snp.669 (kūṭa); Ja.iv.492 (nāvā); Pv.i.10#14 (bhūmi of N.); Pv-a.43, Pv-a.52.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://muggara">muggara</a></em> an iron club Pv-a.55</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85ku">saṅku</a></em> an iron spike SN.iv.168; Snp.667.</li></ul></li><li><p>aya˚</p><ul><li><em>-kapāla</em> = ayo˚ Dhp-a.i.148 (variant reading ayo˚).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> a worker in iron Mil.331.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C5%AB%E1%B9%ADa">kūṭa</a></em> = ayo˚ Ja.i.108; Dhp-a.ii.69 (variant reading).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://na%E1%B9%85gala">naṅgala</a></em> an iron plough Dhp-a.i.223; Dhp-a.iii.67</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADaka">paṭṭaka</a></em> an iron plate or sheet (cp. loha˚) Ja.v.359</li><li><em>-paṭhavi</em> an iron floor (of Avīci) Dhp-a.i.148.</li><li><em>-saṅghāṭaka</em> an iron (door) post Dhp-a.iv.104.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C5%ABla">sūla</a></em> an iron stake Snp.667; Dhp-a.i.148.</li></ul></li></ol><p>Sk. ayaḥ nt. iron &amp; ore, Idg. *ajes-, cp. Av. ayah, Lat. aes, Goth. aiz, Ohg. ēr (= Ger. Erz.) Ags. ār (= E. ore).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayo">ayo</a>, <a href="bword://tipu">tipu</a>, <a href="bword://sīsa">sīsa</a>, <a href="bword://sajjha">sajjha</a>, <a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a>, <a href="bword://kūṭa">kūṭa</a>, <a href="bword://khīla">khīla</a>, <a href="bword://guḷa">guḷa</a>, <a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a>, <a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a>, <a href="bword://paṭala">paṭala</a>, <a href="bword://pākāra">pākāra</a>, <a href="bword://muggara">muggara</a>, <a href="bword://saṅku">saṅku</a>, <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://kūṭa">kūṭa</a>, <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a>, <a href="bword://paṭṭaka">paṭṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://sūla">sūla</a></p>

ayoguḷa|ayogu.la|ayogula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayogu.la</span></p><p>an iron ball</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayaguḷa">ayaguḷa</a></p>

ayojjha
