ayopatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an iron bowl</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayapatta">ayapatta</a></p>

ayopaṭṭa|ayopa.t.ta|ayopatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayopa.t.ta</span></p><p>an iron strip or band</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayapaṭṭa">ayapaṭṭa</a></p>

ayosiṅghātaka|ayosi"nghaataka|ayosinghataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayosi"nghaataka</span></p><p>an iron bowl</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayas">ayas</a>, <a href="bword://ayasiṅghātaka">ayasiṅghātaka</a></p>

ayujjhā|ayujjhaa|ayujjha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayujjhaa</span></p><p>AyujjhāAyojjhā</p><p>Two visits of the Buddha to this city are recorded in the Canon; on one occasion he preached the Phena Sutta SN.iii.140<em>ff.</em> and on the other the Dārukkhandha Sutta, although the latter is said in some manuscripts to have taken place in Kosambī. SN.iv.179<em>f.</em> In both these references the city is said to be on the Ganges; the town usually called Ayojjha (Ayodhyā) is, rather, on the Sarayū, adjacent to or an alternative name for Sāketa. However, in the Pali texts <em>gaṅgā</em> is sometimes used for any river, and that would seem to be the case here.</p><p>25.614286,83.6718751town</p>

ayya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.f.</i></p><ol><li>(m.) a lord, master; a worthy person; the venerable sir, (f.) a mistress; a noble lady; the venerable lady.</li><li>(mfn.) well-born; worthy, venerable.</li></ol>

ayya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noun adjective</p><ol><li>(n.) gentleman, sire, lord master Ja.iii.167 = Pv-a.65; Dhp-a.i.8 (ayyā pl. the worthy gentlemen, the worthies), Dhp-a.i.13 (amhākaṃ ayyo our worthy sir); Dhp-a.ii.95</li><li>(adj.) worthy, gentlemanly, honourable Vin.ii.191; Dhp-a.ii.94 sq<br />■ The <em>voc.</em> is used as a polite form of address (cp. Ger. “Sie” and E. address “Esq.” like E. Sir, milord or simply “you” with the implication of a pluralis majestatis; thus voc. proper <em><a href="bword://ayya">ayya</a></em> Ja.i.221 Ja.i.279, Ja.i.308; pl. nom. as voc. <strong>ayyā</strong> in addressing several Ja.ii.128, Ja.ii.415; nom. sg. as voc. (for all genders &amp; numbers); <em>ayyo</em> Vin.ii.215; Ja.iii.126, Ja.iii.127<br />■ f. <strong>ayyā</strong> lady mistress MN.ii.96 (= mother of a prince); Dhp-a.i.398 voc. <em>ayye</em> my lady Ja.v.138.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://putta">putta</a></em> lit. son of an Ariyan, i.e. an aristocratic (young man gentleman (cp. in meaning kulaputta); thus a son of my master (lit.) said by a servant Ja.iii.167. b lord master, “governor” Ja.i.62 (by a servant); DN-a.i.257 (sāmi, opp. dāsi-putta); Pv-a.145 (by a wife to her husband); Dhp-a.ii.110. c prince (see <em>W.Z.K.M.</em> xii., 1898 75 sq. &amp; <em>Epigraphia Indica</em> iii.137 sq.) Ja.vi.146.</li></ul><p>contracted form for the diaeretic ariya (q.v. for etym.). See also ayira</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayya">ayya</a>, ayyā, ayyā, <a href="bword://putta">putta</a></p>

ayyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>a grandfather; an ancestor.</li><li>a master</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayyika">ayyika</a></p>

ayyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>grandfather, (so also BSk., e.g. Mvu.ii.426; Mvu.iii.264) Ja.iii.155; Ja.iv.146; Ja.vi.196; Pv.i.84; Mil.284. ayyaka-payyakā grandfather &amp; great grandfather; forefathers, ancestors Ja.i.2; Pv-a.107 (= pitāmahā)<br />■ f <em><a href="bword://ayyak%C4%81">ayyakā</a></em> grandmother, granny Vin.ii.169; SN.i.97; Ja.ii.349 (here used for “lady”, as variant reading BB); &amp; <em><a href="bword://ayyik%C4%81">ayyikā</a></em> Thig.159 Vism.379.</p><p>demin. of ayya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayyakā">ayyakā</a>, <a href="bword://ayyikā">ayyikā</a></p>

ayyakā|ayyakaa|ayyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayyakaa</span></p><p>grandmother</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayyikā">ayyikā</a></p>

ayyaputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>young master, the son of the house; a (young) lord; a husband</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayya">ayya</a></p>

ayyaputtaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>young lord, a husband</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayya">ayya</a></p>

ayyavosāṭitaka|ayyavosaa.titaka|ayyavosatitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayyavosaa.titaka</span></p><p>(food) offered to the ancestors</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayya">ayya</a></p>

ayyika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>a grandfather; an ancestor.</li><li>a master</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayyaka">ayyaka</a></p>

ayyikā|ayyikaa|ayyika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ayyikaa</span></p><p>grandmother</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayyakā">ayyakā</a></p>

ayyira
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ayira &amp; Ayyira</p><p>n-adj. n. ariyan nobleman, gentleman (opp. servant); (adj.) arīyan, well-born, belonging to the ruling race, noble, aristocratic gentlemanly Ja.v.257; Vv.39#6<br />■ f. <em>ayirā</em> lady, mistress (of a servant) Ja.ii.349 (variant reading oyyakā); voc. ayire my lady Ja.v.138 (= ayye C.).</p><p>Vedic ārya, Metathesis for ariya as diaeretic form of ārya, of which the contracted (assimilation) form is ayya. See also ariya</p>

añcati|a~ncati|ancati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~ncati</span></p><p>Ja.i.417, read añchati (see next).</p>

añchati|a~nchati|anchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~nchati</span></p><p>stretches; pulls, drags, turns (on a lathe).</p>

añchati|a~nchati|anchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~nchati</span></p><p>to pull, drag, pull along to turn on a lathe DN.ii.291 (bhamakāro dīghaṃ a., where K has note: añjanto ti pi acchanto ti pi pātho) = MN.i.56 (vv.ll. MN.i.532 acch˚ &amp; añj˚); Thag.750 (añcāmi T., v.l aññāmi). Añchati should also be read at Ja.i.417 for <em>udakaṃ añcanti</em> (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of udañcanī pulling the water up from a well, q.v.), where it corresponds to <em>udakaṃ ākkaḍḍhati</em> in the same sentence.</p><p>in meaning = ākaḍḍhati, which latter is also the Sk. gloss (ākārṣayati) to the Jain Prk. aṃchāvei = añchati see Morris, J. P. T. S. 1893, 60</p>

añhamāna|a~nhamaana|anhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~nhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://asnāti">asnāti</a></p>

añhamāna|a~nhamaana|anhamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~nhamaana</span></p><p>eating, taking food; enjoying: only SS at Snp.240; all MSS at Snp.239 have asamāna. Snp-a.284 explains by āhārayamāna.</p><p>Sk. aśnāna, ppr. med. of aśnāti, <em>aś</em> to eat</p>

añja|a~nja|anja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~nja</span></p><p>adverb suddenly, lit. with a pull or jerk pull on! go on! gee up! Ja.i.192.</p><p>orig. imper. of añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; cp. Sk. anjasā (instr.) quickly, Goth. anaks</p>

añjali|a~njali|anjali
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njali</span></p><p>extending, stretching forth, gesture of lifting up the hands as a token of reverence (cp. E. to “tender” one’s respect), putting the ten fingers together and raising them to the head (Vv-a.7: dasanakha-samodhāna-samujjalaṃ añjaliṃ paggayha). Only in stock phrases</p><ol><li><em>añjaliṃ paṇāmeti</em> to bend forth the outstretched hands Vin.ii.188; DN.i.118; Snp.352; Snp.p.79</li><li><em>-ṃ paggaṇhāti</em> to perform the a. salutation Ja.i.54; Dhp-a.iv.212; Vv-a.7, Vv-a.312 (<em>sirasmiṃ</em> on one’s head) Pv-a.93.</li><li><em>-ṃ karoti</em> id. Pv-a.178; cp. <em>katañjali</em> (adj. with raised hands Snp.1023; Ja.i.17; Pv-a.50, and <em>añjalikata</em> id. Pv.ii.12#20. Cp. pañjali</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> respectful salutation, as above AN.i.123; AN.ii.180 AN.iv.130; Vv.78#8, Vv.83#16; Dhp-a.i.32.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87%C4%ABya">karaṇīya</a></em> (adj.) that is worthy of being thus honoured DN.iii.5; AN.ii.34; AN.iii.36 AN.iv.13 sq.; Iti.88.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. añjali, fr. añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a>, <a href="bword://karaṇīya">karaṇīya</a></p>

añjalika|a~njalika|anjalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njalika</span></p><p>with cupped hands raised.</p>

añjalikamma|a~njalikamma|anjalikamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njalikamma</span></p><p>respectful salutation with cupped hands</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjalī">añjalī</a></p>

añjalikaraṇīya|a~njalikara.niiya|anjalikaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njalikara.niiya</span></p><p>worthy of respectful salutations</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjalī">añjalī</a></p>

añjalikā|a~njalikaa|anjalika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njalikaa</span></p><p>the raising of the hands as a sign of respectful salutation Vv.1#5 (explained at Vv-a.24 as dasanakha-samodhāna samujjalaṃ añjaliṃ sirasi paggaṇhantī guṇa-visiṭṭhānaṃ apacayānaṃ akāsiṃ).</p><p>= añjali</p>

añjalī|a~njalii|anjali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; f.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njalii</span></p><p>the cupped hands; esp. as raised to the head (as a gesture of respect or supplication).</p>

añjana|a~njana|anjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njana</span></p><ol><li>smearing, anointing (?).</li><li>ointment, esp. a collyrium for the eyes, used for decoration or as a medication; a pigment.</li></ol>

añjana|a~njana|anjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njana</span></p><p>ointment, esp. a collyrium for the eyes, made of antimony, adj. anointed, smeary glossy, black (cp. kaṇha ii. and kāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> note).</p><ol><li>Vin.i.203 (five kinds viz. kāḷ˚, ras˚, sot˚, geruka, kapalla) DN.i.7, DN.i.12; DN-a.i.98 (khār˚); DN-a.i.284; Dhp-a.iii.354 (akkhi eye-salve).</li><li>glossy, jet-black Ja.i.194; Ja.ii.369; Ja.v.416. The reading añjana at AN.iv.468 is wrong, it should be corrected into thanamajjanamattaṃ. See also pacc˚. In meaning collyrium box at Thig.413 (= añjana-nāḷi Thag-a.267); Dhp-a.ii.25.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-akkhiha</em> with anointed eyes Thag.960.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://upapisana">upapisana</a></em> perfume to mix with ointment Vin.i.203; Vin.ii.112.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">cuṇṇa</a></em> aromatic powder Dhs-a.13.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81%E1%B8%B7i">nāḷi</a></em> an ointment tube, collyrium box Thag-a.267.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a></em> Name of a tree (“black tree) Ja.i.331.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">vaṇṇa</a></em> of the colour of collyrium, i.e. shiny, glossy, dark, black DN.ii.18 (lomāni); Ja.i.138 (kesā) Ja.i.194; Ja.ii.369; Pv-a.258 (vana).</li></ul><p>from añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapisana">upapisana</a>, <a href="bword://cuṇṇa">cuṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://nāḷi">nāḷi</a>, <a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a>, <a href="bword://vaṇṇa">vaṇṇa</a></p>

añjanakkhika|a~njanakkhika|anjanakkhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanakkhika</span></p><p>with eyes painted with collyrium</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjana">añjana</a></p>

añjanarukkha|a~njanarukkha|anjanarukkha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanarukkha</span></p><p>A type of tree Ja.i.331. The name means “black tree”.</p>

añjanavana|a~njanavana|anjanavana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanavana</span></p><p>AñjanavanaAñcanavana</p><p>A garden at Sāketa. In it was a deer park where the Buddha used to stay. On one such occasion Kakudha came to see him, SN.i.54 and also the wanderer Kuṇḍaliya, SN.v.73 who lived near by. Here were preached the Sāketa Sutta and the Jarā Sutta.</p><p>When Ānanda was staying there a nun of the Jaṭila persuasion visited him and questioned him on the use of samādhi. AN.iv.427–428</p><p>There Sujātā met the Buddha, and having listened to his discourse became an arahant. Thig.145–150</p><p>26.8, 82.21park</p>

añjanisalākā|a~njanisalaakaa|anjanisalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanisalaakaa</span></p><p>stick for applying ointment</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjanī">añjanī</a></p>

añjanisalākā|a~njanisalaakaa|anjanisalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanisalaakaa</span></p><p>stick to put the ointment on with Vin.i.203; Vin.ii.135; Ja.iii.419.</p>

añjanithavikā|a~njanithavikaa|anjanithavika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanithavikaa</span></p><p>bag for the collyrium pot</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjanī">añjanī</a></p>

añjanāvaniya|a~njanaavaniya|anjanavaniya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanaavaniya</span></p><p>He dwelt in the Añjanavana in a small hut, and there he became Awakened. Thag.55</p>

añjanī|a~njanii|anjani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanii</span></p><p>box for ointments, a collyrium pot.</p>

añjanī|a~njanii|anjani
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanii</span></p><p>box for ointment, a collyrium pot Vin.i.203, Vin.i.204; Vin.ii.135; Vin.iv.168; MN.ii.65 = Thag.773.</p><p>fr. añjana</p>

añjanūpapiṃsana|a~njanuupapi.msana|anjanupapimsana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njanuupapi.msana</span></p><p>perfume to mix with ointment</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjana">añjana</a></p>

añjasa|a~njasa|anjasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njasa</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) straight; direct (of a road); upright (in morals).</li><li>(n. (&amp; m.?)) the direct road or way.</li></ol>

añjasa|a~njasa|anjasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njasa</span></p><p>straight, straightforward (of a road) DN.i.235; Ja.i.5; Thig.99; Vv.50#20 (cp. Vv-a.215); Vv-a.84 (akuṭila); Mhvs.25, Mhvs.5; Mil.217; Sdhp.328, Sdhp.595. Cp pañjasa.</p><p>Sk. āñjasa (?). Cp. ārjava = P. ajjava, see *<a href="bword://a%C3%B1jati">añjati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; añjaya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjati">añjati</a></p>

añjasayana|a~njasayana|anjasayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njasayana</span></p><p>the straight course</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjasa">añjasa</a></p>

añjati|a~njati|anjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njati</span></p><ol><li>smears, anoints; paints; makes clear.</li><li>goes.</li></ol>

añjati|a~njati|anjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njati</span></p><p>Añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See añja, añjaya, añjali, añjasa.</p><p>= Sk. ṛñjati, ṛjyati to stretch, pull along, draw out, erect; cp. Sk. ṛju straight, caus. irajyati; Gr. ὀρέγω Lat. rego, rectus = erect. See also P. uju, añchati, ajjita ānañja-ānejja</p><p>Añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Añjeti</p><p>to smear, anoint, paint SN.ii.281; Ja.iv.219 (akkhīni añjetvā, variant reading BB añcitvā). Caus. ii. <em>añjāpeti</em> Dhp-a.i.21. pp. <em><a href="bword://a%C3%B1jita">añjita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>= Sk. añjayati, Caus. of anakti to smear etc.; cp. Sk. añji ointment, ājya butter; Lat. unguo to anoint, unguentum ointment; Ohg. ancho = Ger. Anke butter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añjita">añjita</a></p>

añjaya|a~njaya|anjaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njaya</span></p><p>adjective straight Ja.iii.12 (vv.ll. ajjava &amp; and ājjava better?) expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as ujuka, akuṭila. See also ajjava. Should we assume misreading for añjasa?</p><p>from añjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

añjita|a~njita|anjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~njita</span></p><p>smeared, anointed Ja.i.77 (su-añjitāni akkhīni); Ja.iv.421 (añjit’akkha).</p><p>Sk. ankta &amp; añjayita, pp. of añjeti</p>

añña-d-attha|a~n~na-d-attha|anna-d-attha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na-d-attha</span></p><p>añña-d-attha</p><p>masculine another object or aim</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

añña-d-atthika|a~n~na-d-atthika|anna-d-atthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na-d-atthika</span></p><p>añña-d-atthika</p><p>mfn. having another object or purpose</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

añña-m-añña|a~n~na-m-a~n~na|anna-m-anna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na-m-a~n~na</span></p><p>añña-m-añña</p><p>mfn. pron.</p><ol><li>one another; each other.</li><li>one after the other; now one… now another…; various, different.</li></ol>

añña-m-aññakhādikā|a~n~na-m-a~n~nakhaadikaa|anna-m-annakhadika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na-m-a~n~nakhaadikaa</span></p><p>añña-m-aññakhādikā</p><p>feminine the eating of one another</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña-m-añña">añña-m-añña</a></p>

añña-m-aññaṃ|a~n~na-m-a~n~na.m|anna-m-annam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na-m-a~n~na.m</span></p><p>añña-m-aññaṃ</p><p>adv. towards one another; mutually, reciprocally</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña-m-añña">añña-m-añña</a></p>

añña|a~n~na|anna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. pron.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na</span></p><p>other; different; other than (+ abl.); another, a further, one more; somebody, something else; anything else; pl. (the) others, the rest.</p><p>añña…añña</p><p>(?)</p><ol><li>one… another…; some… some.</li><li>one … another (different) …; one thing … another thing …</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

añña|a~n~na|anna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na</span></p><p><strong>Añña:</strong> Other, being of the opposite category.</p>

añña|a~n~na|anna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~na</span></p><p>pronoun another etc</p><h3>A. <em>By itself</em></h3><ol><li>other not the same, different, another, somebody else (opp oneself) Vin.iii.144 (aññena, scil. maggena, gacchati to take a different route); Snp.459, Snp.789, Snp.904; Dhp.158 (opp attānaṃ), Dhp.165; Ja.i.151 (opp. attano); Ja.ii.333 (aññaṃ vyākaroti give a diff. answer).</li><li>another one, a second nt. else, further Snp.1052 (= uttariṃ nt. Cnd.17); else Ja.i.294. aññaṃ kiñci (indef.) anything else Ja.i.151. yo añño every other, whoever else Ja.i.256.</li><li><em>aññe</em> (pl. (the) others, the rest Snp.189, Snp.663, Snp.911; Dhp.43, Dhp.252 Dhp.355; Ja.i.254</li></ol><h3>B. del. <em>in correlation</em></h3><ol><li><p><em>copulative.</em> añña. añña the one … the other (… the third etc.); this, that &amp; the other; some … some Vin.i.15; Mil.40; etc.</p></li><li><p><em>reciprocative</em> <em>añño aññaṃ, aññamaññaṃ, aññoññaṃ</em> one another, each other, mutually, reciprocally (in ordinary construction &amp; declension of a noun or adj. in; <em>sg.;</em> cp Gr. ἀλλήλων, αλλήλους in <em>pl.</em>).</p><ol><li><em>añño aññaṃ</em> Dhp.165</li><li><strong>aññamañña</strong> (cp. BSk. añyamañya Mvu.ii.436) as <em>pron.:</em> n’ālaṃ aññamaññassa sukhāya vā dukkhāya vā DN.i.56 = SN.iii.211. n’aññamaññassa dukkhaṃ iccheyya do not wish evil to each other Snp.148. daṇḍehi aññamaññaṃ upakkamanti (approach each other) MN.i.86 = Cnd.199 ˚ṃ agāravo viharati AN.iii.247. dve janā ˚ṃ ghātayiṃsu (slew each other) Ja.i.254. aññamaññaṃ hasanti Ja.v.111 ˚ṃ musale hantvā Ja.v.267. ˚ṃ daṇḍâbhigāṭena Pv-a.58 or <em>adj.:</em> aññamaññaṃ veraṃ bandhiṃsu (established mutual enmity) Ja.ii.353; ˚ṃ piyasaṃvāsaṃ vasiṃsu Ja.ii.153; aññamaññaṃ accayaṃ desetvā (their mutual mistake) Dhp-a.i.57 or <em>adv.</em> dve pi aññamaññaṃ paṭibaddha citta ahesuṃ (in love with each other) Ja.iii.188; or ˚- aññamañña-paccaya mutually dependent, interrelated Pts.ii.49, Pts.ii.58.</li><li><em><a href="bword://a%C3%B1%C3%B1o%C3%B1%C3%B1a">aññoñña</a></em> (˚-) Ja.v.251 (˚nissita); Dāvs v.45 (˚bhinna)</li></ol></li><li><p><em>disjunctive</em> añña… añña one… the other, this one… that one, different, different from aññaṃ jīvaṃ. aññaṃ sarīraṃ one is the soul… the other is the body i.e. the soul is different from the body DN.i.157; MN.i.430; AN.v.193; aññā va saññā bhavissati añño attā DN.i.187 Thus also in phrase <em>aññena aññaṃ</em> opposite, the contrary differently, contradictory (lit. other from that which is other) Vin.ii.85 (paṭicarati make counter-charges); DN.i.57 (vyākāsi gave the opposite or contradictory reply) Mil.171 (aññaṃ kayiramānaṃ aññena sambharati).</p></li></ol><p><em>anañña</em> 1 not another, i.e. the same, self-same, identical MN.i.256 (= ayaṃ) 2 not another, i.e. alone, by oneself, oneself only Snp.65 (˚posin; opp. paraṃ) = Nd.4 cp. Cnd.36 3 not another, i.e. no more, only, alone Snp.p.106 (dve va gatiyo bhavanti anaññā: and no other or no more, only two). See also under compounds.</p><ul><li><em>-ādisa</em> different Ja.vi.212, ˚tā difference Pv-a.243</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://khantika">khantika</a></em> acquiescing in diff. views, following another faith (see <em><a href="bword://khantika">khantika</a></em>) DN.i.187; MN.i.487.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://titthiya">titthiya</a></em> an adherent of another sect, a non-Buddhist.; DN.iii.115; MN.i.494, MN.i.512; SN.ii.21, SN.ii.32 sq., SN.ii.119; SN.iii.116 sq.; SN.iv.51, SN.iv.228 SN.v.6, SN.v.27 sq.; AN.i.65, AN.i.240; AN.ii.176; AN.iv.35 sq.; Vin.i.60; Ja.i.93; Ja.ii.415.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhika">diṭṭhika</a></em> having diff. views (comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with añña-khantika) DN.i.187; MN.i.487.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a></em> (an˚) not to be guided by somebody else, i.e. independent in one’s views, having attained the right knowledge by oneself (opp. para˚) Snp.55, Snp.213, Snp.364.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mano">mano</a></em> (an˚) (adj.) not setting one’s heart upon others Vv.11#5 (see Vv-a.58)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> holding other views, an˚ (adj.) Dpvs.iv.24.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81daka">vādaka</a></em> one who gives a diff. account of things, one who distorts a matter, a prevaricator Vin.iv.36.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vihita">vihita</a></em> being occupied with something else, distracted, absent-minded Vin.iv.269; Dhp-a.iii.352, Dhp-a.iii.381; ˚tā distraction, absentmindedness Dhp-a.i.181.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sara%E1%B9%87a">saraṇa</a></em> (an˚) not betaking oneself to others for refuge, i.e. of independent, sure knowledge SN.iii.42 = SN.v.154.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sita">sita</a></em> dependent or relying on others Snp.825.</li></ul><p>Vedic anya, with compar. suff. ya; Goth. anpar; Ohg. andar; formation with n analagous to those with l in Gr. α ̓́λλος (α ̓́λjος), Lat. alius (cp. alter) Goth. aljis Ags. elles = E. else. From demonstr. base *eno, see *<a href="bword://na">na</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> and cp. a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: aññamañña, <a href="bword://aññoñña">aññoñña</a>, <a href="bword://khantika">khantika</a>, <a href="bword://khantika">khantika</a>, <a href="bword://titthiya">titthiya</a>, <a href="bword://diṭṭhika">diṭṭhika</a>, <a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a>, <a href="bword://mano">mano</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a>, <a href="bword://vādaka">vādaka</a>, <a href="bword://vihita">vihita</a>, <a href="bword://saraṇa">saraṇa</a>, <a href="bword://sita">sita</a>, <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

aññacetāpana|a~n~nacetaapana|annacetapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nacetaapana</span></p><p>asking for something else; exchanging for something else (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññadatthu|a~n~nadatthu|annadatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadatthu</span></p><p>only, exclusively, absolutely; merely; rather, on the contrary.</p>

aññadatthu|a~n~nadatthu|annadatthu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadatthu</span></p><p>adverb part. of affirmation = surely, all-round absolutely (ekaṃsa-vacane nipāto DN-a.i.111) only, at any rate DN.i.91; DN.ii.284; Snp.828 (na h’ aññadatth’ atthi pasaṃsa-lābhā, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Snp-a.541 as na hi ettha pasaṃsa-lābhato añño attho atthi, cp. also Mnd.168); Mil.133; Vv-a.58; Pv-a.97, Pv-a.114.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a></em> sure-seeing, seeing everything, all pervading DN.i.18; DN.iii.135, DN.iii.185; AN.ii.24; AN.iii.202; AN.iv.89, AN.iv.105; Iti.15.</li></ul><p>lit. aññad atthu let there be anything else, i.e. be it what it will, there is nothing else, all everything, surely</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a></p>

aññadatthudasa|a~n~nadatthudasa|annadatthudasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadatthudasa</span></p><p>seeing absolutelyl the only seer ( of Brahmā or Tathāgata)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññadatthu">aññadatthu</a></p>

aññadatthuhara|a~n~nadatthuhara|annadatthuhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadatthuhara</span></p><p>who merely, only takes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññadatthu">aññadatthu</a></p>

aññadatthuṃ|a~n~nadatthu.m|annadatthum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadatthu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññadatthu">aññadatthu</a></p>

aññadiṭṭhika|a~n~nadi.t.thika|annaditthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadi.t.thika</span></p><p>having different views</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññadā|a~n~nadaa|annada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadaa</span></p><p>at another time, at other times.</p>

aññadā|a~n~nadaa|annada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nadaa</span></p><p>adverb at another time, else, once SN.iv.285; Ja.v.12; Dhp-a.iv.125.</p><p>añña + dā, cp. kadā, tadā, yadā</p>

aññakanthika|a~n~nakanthika|annakanthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nakanthika</span></p><p>acquiescing in different views</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññamokkha|a~n~namokkha|annamokkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~namokkha</span></p><p>to be released by another</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññapekha|a~n~napekha|annapekha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~napekha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññapekkha">aññapekkha</a></p><p>wishing for knowledge, wanting to know</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññā">aññā</a></p>

aññapekkha|a~n~napekkha|annapekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~napekkha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññapekkha">aññapekkha</a></p><p>wishing for knowledge, wanting to know</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññā">aññā</a></p>

aññatama|a~n~natama|annatama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natama</span></p><p>one out of many; one or other of.</p>

aññatama|a~n~natama|annatama
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pron.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natama</span></p><p>adj. one out of many, the one or the other of, a certain, any Mhvs.38, Mhvs.14.</p><p>añña + superl. suff. tama; see also <em><a href="bword://a%C3%B1%C3%B1atara">aññatara</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññatara">aññatara</a></p>

aññatara|a~n~natara|annatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. pron.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natara</span></p><ol><li>one of two; one of several; a certain… (often used as indefinite article: ‘a’); somebody, some….</li><li>other, another; different.</li></ol>

aññatara|a~n~natara|annatara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pron.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natara</span></p><p>adj. one of a certain number, a certain, somebody, some; often used (like <em><a href="bword://eka">eka</a></em>) as indef. article “a”. Very frequent, e.g. Snp.35, Snp.210; Iti.103; Dhp.137, Dhp.157; Ja.i.221, Ja.i.253; Ja.ii.132 etc. devaññatara a certain god, i.e. any kind of god SN.iv.180 = AN.iv.461.</p><p>Sk. anyatara, añña + compar. suff. tara, cp. Lat. alter, Goth. anpar etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

aññataraññatara|a~n~natara~n~natara|annatarannatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natara~n~natara</span></p><p>one or other; all kinds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññatara">aññatara</a></p>

aññathatta|a~n~nathatta|annathatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathatta</span></p><p>change, alteration; change of mind; doubt, anxiety</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññathatta|a~n~nathatta|annathatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathatta</span></p><ol><li>change, alteration SN.iii.37; SN.iv.40; AN.i.153; AN.iii.66; Kv.227 (= jarā C, cp <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 55 n. 2); Mil.209.</li><li>difference Ja.i.147; Iti.11.</li><li>erroneous supposition, mistake Vin.ii.2; SN.iii.91; SN.iv.329.</li><li>fickleness, change of mind, doubt wavering, MN.i.448, MN.i.457 (+ domanassa); Ja.i.33 (cittaṃ) Pv-a.195 (cittassa).</li></ol><p>aññathā + tta</p>

aññathā|a~n~nathaa|annatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaa</span></p><p>in a different manner; otherwise (than, abl.); in the wrong way, falsely, erroneously; not so.</p><p>aññathā … aññathā …</p><p>(?) in one way … in another way …; like this … different…</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññathā|a~n~nathaa|annatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaa</span></p><p>adverb in a different manner, otherwise, differently SN.i.24; Snp.588, Snp.757; Dhs-a.163; Pv-a.125, Pv-a.133. <em>anaññathā</em> without mistake Vv.44#18; <em>anaññatha</em> (nt.) certainty, truth Pts.ii.104 (= tatha).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> 1 a different existence AN.ii.10; Iti.9 = Iti.94; Snp.729, Snp.740, Snp.752; 2 a state of difference; i.e. change alteration, unstableness DN.i.36; SN.ii.274; SN.iii.8, SN.iii.16, SN.iii.42 Vb.379.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81vin">bhāvin</a></em> based on difference SN.iii.225 sq. SN.iv.23 sq., SN.iv.66 sq.; an˚ free from difference Vin.i.36.</li></ul><p>añña + thā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://bhāvin">bhāvin</a></p>

aññathābhava|a~n~nathaabhava|annathabhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaabhava</span></p><p>becoming different; change, alteration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññathābhāvi|a~n~nathaabhaavi|annathabhavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaabhaavi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññathābhāvin">aññathābhāvin</a></p><p>of changeable nature, inevitably changing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññathābhāvin|a~n~nathaabhaavin|annathabhavin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaabhaavin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññathābhāvin">aññathābhāvin</a></p><p>of changeable nature, inevitably changing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññathācariyaka|a~n~nathaacariyaka|annathacariyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nathaacariyaka</span></p><p>following a different teaching</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññathā">aññathā</a></p>

aññatitthiya|a~n~natitthiya|annatitthiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natitthiya</span></p><p>belonging to another sec; a non-Buddhist</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññato|a~n~nato|annato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nato</span></p><ol><li>from another, from somebody else; elsewhere; from elsewhere.</li><li>as another (thing), as different.</li></ol>

aññatra|a~n~natra|annatra
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natra</span></p><ol><li>elsewhere, somewhere else; in another place.</li><li>but for, besides, except, apart from; without (with abl. or instr. (and acc.?)).</li></ol><p>aññatra … aññatra …</p><p>(?) in one place … in another …</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññatra">aññatra</a></p>

aññatra|a~n~natra|annatra
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natra</span></p><p>adverb elsewhere, somewhere else Ja.v.252; Pv.iv.1#62. In comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> also añña˚, e.g. aññatra-yoga (adj.) following another discipline DN.i.187; MN.i.487<br />■ As prep. c. abl. (and instr.) but besides, except, e.g. a. iminā tapo-pakkamena DN.i.168 kiṃ karaṇīyaṃ a. dhammacariyāya SN.i.101; ko nu aññatram-ariyehi who else but the Nobles Snp.886 (= ṭhapetvā saññā-mattena Snp-a.555). <em>-kiṃ aññatra</em> what but, i.e. what else is the cause but, or: this is due to; but for DN.i.90 (vusitavā-mānī k. a. avusitattā); SN.i.29 (k. k. a adassanā except from blindness); Snp.206 (id.).</p><p>anya + tra, see also <em><a href="bword://a%C3%B1%C3%B1attha">aññattha</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññattha">aññattha</a></p>

aññatrayoga|a~n~natrayoga|annatrayoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natrayoga</span></p><p>following another discipline or religious practice</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññatra">aññatra</a></p>

aññatrācariyaka|a~n~natraacariyaka|annatracariyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natraacariyaka</span></p><p>following a different teaching</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññatra">aññatra</a></p>

aññattha|a~n~nattha|annattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nattha</span></p><p>somewhere else, anywhere else, elsewhere.</p>

aññattha|a~n~nattha|annattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~nattha</span></p><p>adverb somewhere or anywhere else, elsewhere (either place where or whereto) Ja.i.291; Ja.ii.154; Dhs-a.163; Dhp-a.i.212; Dhp-a.iii.351; Pv-a.45; Mhvs.4, Mhvs.37; Mhvs.22, Mhvs.14.</p><p>from añña = aññatra, adv. of place, cp. kattha, ettha</p>

aññatthācariyaka|a~n~natthaacariyaka|annatthacariyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~natthaacariyaka</span></p><p>following a different teaching</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññattha">aññattha</a></p>

aññavadaka|a~n~navadaka|annavadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~navadaka</span></p><ol><li>(m.) one who speaks about something else, a prevaricator.</li><li>(n.) the offense of prevaricating</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññavadī|a~n~navadii|annavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~navadii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññavadīn">aññavadīn</a></p><p>professing another doctrine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

