aññā|a~n~naa|anna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naa</span></p><p>knowledge, philoso phic insight; perfect knowledge i.e. arahantship.</p>

aññā|a~n~naa|anna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naa</span></p><p><strong>Aññā:</strong> ‘highest knowledge’, gnosis, refers to the perfect knowledge of the Arahat Saint; see: <em>ariya-puggala</em>. The following passage occurs frequently in the Suttas, when a Bhikkhu indicates his attainment of Nobility <em>arahatta:</em> He makes known the highest knowledge <em>aññam</em> <em>vyākaroti,</em> in exactly this way: ‘Rebirth has ceased, fulfilled is this Noble life, done is what should be done, and there is no more of this to come.’</p><p>The ‘ability of highest knowledge’ <em>aññ</em> <em>indriya</em> <em>=</em> <em>aññā</em> - <em>indriya</em>; see: <em>indriya</em>, however, is present in six of the eight stages of Nobility, that is, beginning with the fruition of Stream-Winning <em>sotāpatti</em> - <em>phala</em> up to the path of Nobility <em>arahatta</em> - <em>magga</em>. See Dhs. PTS 362-364, 505, 553; Indriya Vibhanga; path 162.</p>

aññā|a~n~naa|anna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naa</span></p><p>knowledge, recognition, perfect knowledge, philosophic insight, knowledge par excellence, viz. Arahantship, saving knowledge gnosis (cp. on term <em>Compend.</em> 176 n. 3 and <em>Psalms of Brethren</em> introd. xxxiii.) MN.i.445; SN.i.4 (sammad˚), SN.i.24 (aññāya nibbuta); SN.ii.221; SN.v.69, SN.v.129 (diṭṭh’eva dhamme), SN.v.133, SN.v.237; AN.iii.82, AN.iii.143, AN.iii.192; AN.v.108; Iti.39 sq., Iti.53, Iti.104; Dhp.75, Dhp.96; Kp.vii.11; Mil.334<br />■ <em>aññaṃ vyākaroti</em> to manifest ones Arahantship (by a discourse or by mere exclamation) Vin.i.183; SN.ii.51 sq., SN.ii.120; SN.iv.139; SN.v.222; Ja.i.140; Ja.ii.333. See also arahatta.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://atthika">atthika</a></em> desirous of higher knowledge Pv.iv.1#14.</li><li><em>-ārādhana</em> the attainment of full insight MN.i.479.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a></em> the faculty of perfect knowledge or of knowledge made perfect DN.iii.219; SN.v.204; Iti.53; Pp.2; Dhs.362 Dhs.505, Dhs.552; Ne.15, Ne.54, Ne.60.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://citta">citta</a></em> the thought of gnosis the intention of gaining Arahantship SN.ii.267; AN.iii.437</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADivedha">paṭivedha</a></em> comprehension of insight Vin.ii.238.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vimokkha">vimokkha</a></em> deliverance by the highest insight Snp.1105, Snp.1107 (Cnd.19: vuccati arahatta-vimokkho).</li></ul><p>Sk. ājñā, = ā + <em>jñā</em>, cp. ājānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atthika">atthika</a>, <a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a>, <a href="bword://citta">citta</a>, <a href="bword://paṭivedha">paṭivedha</a>, <a href="bword://vimokkha">vimokkha</a></p>

aññācitta|a~n~naacitta|annacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naacitta</span></p><p>thought for knowledge, the intention of gaining perfect knowledge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aññā">aññā</a></p>

aññādisa|a~n~naadisa|annadisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naadisa</span></p><p>of another kind; different</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://añña">añña</a></p>

aññāmañña-paccaya|a~n~naama~n~na-paccaya|annamanna-paccaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naama~n~na-paccaya</span></p><p><strong>Aññāmañña-paccaya:</strong> M utuality-condition, is one of the 24 conditions <em>paccaya</em>.</p>

aññāta|a~n~naata|annata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naata</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññātar">aññātar</a></p><p>one who knows, who understands; a knower.</p>

aññāta|a~n~naata|annata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naata</span></p><p>Aññāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>known, recognised Snp.699. <em>an˚</em>; what is not known, in phrase anaññāta-ññassāmī’ t’ indriya the faculty of him (who believes): “I shall know what is not known (yet)” DN.iii.219; SN.v.204; Iti.53 Pp.2; Dhs.296 (cp. Dhs trsl.86); Ne.15, Ne.54, Ne.60, Ne.191.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C4%81nin">mānin</a></em> one who prides himself in having perfect knowledge one who imagines to be in possession of right insight AN.iii.175 sq.; Thag.953.</li></ul><p>pp. of ājānāti, q.v.</p><p>Aññāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>unknown, see <em><a href="bword://%C3%B1%C4%81ta">ñāta</a></em>.</p><p>a + ñāta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mānin">mānin</a>, <a href="bword://ñāta">ñāta</a></p>

aññātaka|a~n~naataka|annataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naataka</span></p><p>unknown; unfamiliar.</p>

aññātaka|a~n~naataka|annataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naataka</span></p><p>Aññātaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>he who is not a kinsman Dhp-a.i.222.</p><p>a + ñātaka, cp. Sk. ajñāti</p><p>Aññātaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective unknown, unrecognisable, only in phrase <em>-vesena</em> in unknown form, in disguise Ja.i.14; Ja.iii.116; Ja.v.102.</p><p>Demin. of aññāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

aññātakavesena|a~n~naatakavesena|annatakavesena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naatakavesena</span></p><p>in disguise; not in one owns form.</p>

aññātakoṇḍañña|a~n~naatako.n.da~n~na|annatakondanna
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naatako.n.da~n~na</span></p><p>AññātakoṇḍaññaKoṇḍañña</p><p>One of the five ascetics known as the Pañcavaggiyā. When, after the Enlightenment, the Buddha visited them at Isipatana and preached the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, Koṇḍañña won the Fruit of the First Path. As he was the first among humans to realise the Dhamma the Buddha praised him saying “<em>aññāsi vata bho Kondañño</em>” twice; hence he came to be known as Aññata Koṇḍañña. Vin.i.12</p><p>Five days later when the Anattalakhana Sutta was preached he became arahant. Vin.i.13–14 He was the first to be ordained with the formula “<em>ehi, bhikkhu</em>” and the first to receive higher ordination. Later the Buddha declared him to be the best of those who first comprehended the Dhamma. He was also declared to be pre-eminent among disciples of long-standing (<em>rattaññūnam</em>). AN.i.23</p><p>Several verses attributed to Koṇḍañña are given in the Theragāthā, admonishing fellow celibates to lead the higher life, because everything is impermanent, bound to ill and void of soul. Thag.674–688</p><p>Vaṅgīsa once extolled his virtues in the presence of the Buddha. Thag.673</p>

aññātar|a~n~naatar|annatar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naatar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññātar">aññātar</a></p><p>one who knows, who understands; a knower.</p>

aññātar|a~n~naatar|annatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naatar</span></p><p>one who knows, a knower of DN.ii.286; MN.i.169; SN.i.106 (dhammassa); Kv.561.</p><p>n. ag. to ājānāti</p>

aññātukāma|a~n~naatukaama|annatukama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naatukaama</span></p><p>adjective desirous of gaining right knowledge AN.iii.192. See ājānāti.</p><p>ā + jñātuṃ + kāma</p>

aññātāvi|a~n~naataavi|annatavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a~n~naataavi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://aññātāvin">aññātāvin</a></p><p>who has complete insight; (completely understood?).</p>

