<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thadhaa</span></p><p>in eight parts</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhadoṇa|a.t.thado.na|atthadona
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thado.na</span></p><p>num. divided into eight measures</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhaguṇaṃ|a.t.thagu.na.m|atthagunam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thagu.na.m</span></p><p>eight times; eight times as much</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhaha|a.t.thaha|atthaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thaha</span></p><p>or masculine a period of eight days</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhaka|a.t.thaka|atthaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thaka</span></p><p>Aṭṭhaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A celebrated sage, composer and reciter of sacred runes, mentioned together with nine others (Vāmaka, Vāmadeva, Vessāmitta, Yamataggi, Aṅgīrasa, Bhāradvāja, Vāseṭṭha and Bhagu), Vin.i.245 DN.i.104 as the ancient sages of the brahmins. They abstained from food at unseasonable times. They were the first teachers of the Tevijja Brahmins DN.i.238 and great sacrifices were conducted by them. AN.iv.61</p><p>Various teachings are attributed to them, e.g. that they recognised five kinds of Brahmins—<em>brahmasama</em>, <em>devasama</em>, <em>mariyāda</em>, <em>sambhinnamariyāda</em>, and <em>brāhmaṇacaṇḍāla</em>. AN.iii.224<em>ff.</em> These sages did not claim to have discerned and realised the five qualities-truth, austerities, chastity, study and munificence—specified by the brahmins for the attainment of merit and the achievement of what is right, MN.ii.199–200 though their followers behaved as if they did. Nor did they claim that they personally saw and knew that “here alone resides the truth and everything else is vain.”. MN.ii.169</p><p>Aṭṭhaka may be identified with Aṣṭaka mentioned as the author of Ṛg Veda 10.104 or else with Atri, author of Ṛg Veda 5.5.</p><p>Aṭṭhaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha mentioned in a nominal list. MN.iii.70</p>

aṭṭhaka|a.t.thaka|atthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thaka</span></p><p>group of eight; a whole consisting of eight parts; a section consisting of groups of eights.</p>

aṭṭhaka|a.t.thaka|atthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thaka</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>eightfold Vin.i.196 = Ud.59 (˚vaggikāni); Vv-a.75 = Dhp-a.iii.104 (˚bhatta).</li><li>˚ā (f.) the eight day of the lunar month (cp. aṭṭhamī), in phrase <em>rattīsu antar’aṭṭhakāsu</em> in the nights between the eighths, i.e. the 8<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> day before and after the full moon Vin.i.31, Vin.i.288 (see <em>Vin Texts</em> i.130<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); MN.i.79; AN.i.136; Mil.396; Ja.i.390.</li><li>˚ṃ (nt.) an octad Vv.67#2 (aṭṭh eight octads = 64); Vv-a.289, Vv-a.290. On sabbaṭṭhaka see <em><a href="bword://a%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">aṭṭha</a></em> B 1 a. See also antara.</li></ol><p>Sk. aṣṭaka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhakanāgara|a.t.thakanaagara|atthakanagara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thakanaagara</span></p><p>A city from which came the householder Dasama who, while on a visit to Pāṭaliputta on business, went to see Ānanda at Beluvagāma and questioned him. MN.i.349<em>f.</em> AN.v.342–347</p><p>4town</p>

aṭṭhakavaggiya|a.t.thakavaggiya|atthakavaggiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thakavaggiya</span></p><p>belonging to the Aṭṭhakavagga of the Suttanipāta; (Snp 766–975).</p>

aṭṭhakā|a.t.thakaa|atthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thakaa</span></p><p>the eighth day after the full moon in the two winter months; the festival held at that time.</p>

aṭṭhama|a.t.thama|atthama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thama</span></p><p>Pacceka Buddha, one of the names given in a list of such. MN.iii.70</p>

aṭṭhama|a.t.thama|atthama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thama</span></p><p>(~ā &amp; ~ī) n. &amp; ~ī feminine</p><ol><li>(mfn.) the eighth.</li><li>(f., scil. <em>ratti</em>) the eighth day or night; esp. the eight day or night of the lunar half-month.</li></ol>

aṭṭhama|a.t.thama|atthama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thama</span></p><p>ordinal number the eighth Snp.107, Snp.230 (cp. Kp-a.187), Snp.437<br />■ f. ˚ī the eighth day of the lunar half month (cp. aṭṭhakā) AN.i.144; Snp.402; Vv.16#6 (in all three pass. as pakkhassa cātuddasī pañcadasī ca aṭṭhamī); AN.i.142; Snp.570 (ito atthami scil. divase, loc.).</p><p>Sk. aṣṭama, see *<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">aṭṭha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭha">aṭṭha</a></p>

aṭṭhamaka|a.t.thamaka|atthamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">a.t.thamaka</span></p><p>= aṭṭhama the eighth.</p><ol><li>lit. Mil.291 (att˚ self-eighth).</li><li>as tt. the eighth of eight persons who strive after the highest perfection, reckoned from the first or Arahant. Hence the eighth is he who stands on the lowest step of the Path and is called a sotāpanna (q.v. Kv.243–Kv.251 (cp. <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 146 sq.); Ne.19, Ne.49, Ne.50; Pts.ii.193 (+ sotāpanna).</li></ol>

