<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cetaapita</span></p><p>asked for; exchanged; bought.</p>

ceṭa|ce.ta|ceta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ce.ta</span></p><p>servant, a boy Ja.iii.478. See next.</p>

ceṭaka|ce.taka|cetaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ce.taka</span></p><p>servant; a slave.</p>

ceṭaka|ce.taka|cetaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ce.taka</span></p><p>servant, a slave, a (bad) fellow Vin.iv.66; ] Vin.ii.176 = Dhp-a.iv.92 (duṭṭha˚ miserable fellow); Dhp-a.iii.281, Dhp-a.iv.82 (bhātika-ceṭakā rascals of brothers); Dhp-a.v.385; Mil.222.</p>

cha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>num. six.</p>

cha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Cha &amp; Chaḷ</p><p>(cha in composition effects gemination of consonant, e.g. chabbīsati = cha + vīsati, chabbaṇṇa cha + vaṇṇa, chaḷ only before vowels in compound chaḷanga, chaḷ-abhiññā) the number six.</p><p><em>Cases:</em> nom. cha, gen. channaṃ, instr. chahi (chambhī (?) Ja.iv.310, which should be chambhi &amp; prob chabbhi = ṣaḍbhiḥ; see also chambhī), loc. chasu (chassu), num. ord. chaṭṭha the sixth. Cp. also saṭṭhi (60) soḷasa (16). Six is applied whenever a “major set is concerned (see 2), as in the foll.: 6 munis are distinguished at Cnd.514 (in pairs of 3: see <em><a href="bword://muni">muni</a></em>); Cnd.6 bhikkhus as a “clique” (see chabaggiya, cp. the Vestal virgins in Rome, 6 in number); 6 are the sciences of the Veda (see chaḷanga); there are 6 buddha-dhammā (Cnd.466), 6 viññāṇakāyā (see <em><a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></em>); 6 senses &amp; sense-organs (see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a></em>)-cha dānasālā Ja.i.282; oraṃ chahi māsehi kālakiriyā bhavissati (l shall die in 6 months, i.e. not just yet, but very soon, after the “next” moon) Pv.iv.3#35. Six bodily faults Ja.i.394 (viz. too long, too short too thin, too fat, too black, too white). Six thousand Gandhabbas Ja.ii.334.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%83sa">aṃsa</a></em> six-cornered Dhs.617.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> the set of six Vedāngas, disciplines of Vedic science, viz. 1 kappa 2 vyākaraṇā, 3 nirutti, 4 sikkhā, 5 chando (viciti) 6 jotisattha (thus enumerated at Vv-a.265; at Pv-a.97 in sequence 4, 1, 3, 2, 6, 5): DN.iii.269; Vv.63#16; Pv.ii.6#13 Mil.178, Mil.236. With ref. to the upekkhās, one is called the “one of six parts” (chaḷ-ang’ upekkhā) Vism.160</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://abhi%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81">abhiññā</a></em> the 6 branches of higher knowledge Vin.ii.161 Pp.14. See abhiññā.</li><li><em>-āsīti</em> eighty-six [i.e. twice that many in all directions: psychologically 6 × 8 = 6 × (4 × 2)<!-- raw HTML omitted -->10<!-- raw HTML omitted -->], of people: an immense number, millions Pv.ii.13#7: of Petas Pv-a.212; of sufferings in Niraya Pv.iii.10#6.</li><li><em>-āhaṃ</em> for six days Ja.iii.471.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ka%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">kaṇṇa</a></em> heard by six ears, i.e. public (opp. catukaṇṇa) Ja.vi.392</li><li><em>-tiṃsa(ti)</em> thirty-six AN.ii.3; Iti.15; Dhp.339; Dhp-a.iii.211 Dhp-a.iii.224 (˚yojana-parimaṇḍala); Dhp-a.iv.48.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://danta">danta</a></em> having six</li></ul><p>tusks, in <em>˚daha</em> Name of one of the Great Lakes of the Himavant (satta-mahā-sarā), lit. lake of the elephant with 6 tusks. cp. cha-visāṇa Vism.416. <em>-<a href="bword://dv%C4%81rika">dvārika</a></em> entering through six doors (i.e. the senses) Dhp-a.iv.221 (taṇhā). <em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tura">dhātura</a></em> (= dhātuya) consisting of six elements MN.iii.239. <em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1ca">pañca</a></em> (chappañca) six or five Mil.292. <em>-phass’ āyatana</em> having six seats of contact (i.e. the outer senses) MN.iii.239; Thag.755; Pv-a.52 cp. Snp.169. <em>-baṇṇa</em> (= vaṇṇa) consisting of six colours (of raṃsi, rays) Ja.v.40; Dhp-a.i.249; Dhp-a.ii.41; Dhp-a.iv.99. <em>-baggiya</em> (= vaggiya) forming a group of six, a set of (sinful Bhikkhus taken as exemplification of trespassing the rules of the Vinaya (cp. Oldenberg, <em>Buddha</em> <!-- raw HTML omitted -->7<!-- raw HTML omitted -->384) Their names are Assaji, Punabhasu, Paṇḍuka, Lohitaka, Mettiya, Bhummajaka Vin.ii.1, Vin.ii.77, and passim Ja.ii.387; Dhp-a.iii.330. <em>-bassāni</em> (= vassāni) six years Ja.i.85; Dhp-a.iii.195. <em>-bidha</em> (= vidha) sixfold Vism.184 <em>-bisāṇa</em> (= visāṇa) having six (i.e. a “major set”) of tusks (of pre-eminent elephants) Ja.v.42 (Nāgarājā), Ja.v.48 (kuñjara), cp. chaddanta<br />■ <em>bīsati</em> (= vīsati) twenty-six Dhp-a.iv.233 (devalokā). Chakana &amp; Chakana;</p><p>Vedic ṣaṣ &amp; ṣaṭ (ṣaḍ = chaḷ) Gr.; ε ̔́ς, Lat. sex, Goth, saihs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://muni">muni</a>, <a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a>, <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a>, <a href="bword://aṃsa">aṃsa</a>, <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://abhiññā">abhiññā</a>, <a href="bword://kaṇṇa">kaṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://danta">danta</a>, <a href="bword://dvārika">dvārika</a>, <a href="bword://dhātura">dhātura</a>, <a href="bword://pañca">pañca</a></p>

chab
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Chab˚</p><p>see under <em><a href="bword://cha">cha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cha">cha</a></p>

chabbaggiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>belonging to the group of six ( <em>bhikkhus</em> or <em>bhikkhunīs</em> who are represented as continually violating vinaya rules or expected behavior)</p>

chabbaggiyā|chabbaggiyaa|chabbaggiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chabbaggiyaa</span></p><p>A group of six monks, contemporary with the Buddha, frequently mentioned as being guilty of various Vinaya offences. Vin.i.84<em>f.</em> Vin.i.104 Vin.i.106 Vin.i.111 Vin.i.113 Vin.i.114 Vin.i.138 Vin.i.160 Vin.i.170 Vin.i.185 Vin.i.189 Vin.i.192 Vin.i.194 Vin.i.203<em>f.</em> Vin.i.216 Vin.i.285 Vin.i.306 Vin.i.316 Vin.ii.73 Vin.ii.105<em>ff.</em> Vin.ii.145<em>ff.</em> Vin.ii.213<em>ff.</em> Vin.ii.241 Vin.ii.262</p><p>The six leaders were Assaji, Punabbasu, Panduka, Lohitaka, Mettiya and Bhummaja hence their name.</p><p>There were also nuns in their following, who likewise violated the Vinaya rules in various ways. Vin.ii.262 Vin.ii.266 Vin.ii.269 Vin.ii.271 Vin.ii.276</p>

chabbyāputtā|chabbyaaputtaa|chabbyaputta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chabbyaaputtaa</span></p><p>A royal clan of Nāgas. Vin.ii.110 AN.ii.72</p>

chabbīsa|chabbiisa|chabbisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chabbiisa</span></p><p>num. twenty six.</p>

chabbīsati|chabbiisati|chabbisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chabbiisati</span></p><p>num. twenty six.</p>

chacattārīsā|chacattaariisaa|chacattarisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chacattaariisaa</span></p><p>num. 46</p>

chacattāḷīsa|chacattaa.liisa|chacattalisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chacattaa.liisa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chacattāḷīsaṃ">chacattāḷīsaṃ</a></p><p>num. 46</p>

chacattāḷīsaṃ|chacattaa.liisa.m|chacattalisam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chacattaa.liisa.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chacattāḷīsaṃ">chacattāḷīsaṃ</a></p><p>num. 46</p>

chada
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>anything that covers, protects or hides, viz. a cover, an awning DN.i.7≈ (sa-uttara˚ but ˚chadana at DN.ii.194)<br />■ a veil in phrase <strong>vivaṭacchada</strong> “with the veil lifted” thus spelt only at Cnd.242, Cnd.593, Dhp-a.i.106 (vivattha˚ variant reading vaṭṭa˚) &amp; DN-a.i.251 (vivatta˚), otherwise <em>-chadda</em> -shelter, clothing in phrase ghāsacchada Pp.51 (see <em><a href="bword://gh%C4%81sa">ghāsa</a></em> &amp; cp. chāda)<br />■ a hedge Ja.vi.60<br />■ a wing Thig.108 (citra˚).</p><p>cp. chādeti <em>chad</em> = saṃvaraṇe Dhtp.586</p><p>See also: vivaṭacchada, <a href="bword://ghāsa">ghāsa</a></p>

chadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>a thatch; a roof; a cover.</li><li>a leaf, foliage; hair.</li><li>an external (false) covering; a disguise; a pretense.</li></ol>

chadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= chada, viz. <em>lit.</em></p><ol><li>a cover, covering Ja.i.376; Ja.v.241.</li><li>a thatch, a roof Vin.ii.154 (various kinds), Vin.ii.195; Ja.ii.281; Dhp-a.ii.65 (˚piṭṭha), Dhp-a.iv.104 (˚assa udaka-patana-ṭṭhāna), Dhp-a.ii.178; Pv-a.55.</li><li>a leaf, foliage Ja.i.87; Thag.527.</li><li>hair Ja.v.202-<em>fig.</em> pretence, camouflage, counterfeiting Snp.89 (= paṭirūpaṃ katvā Snp-a.164); Dhs.1059 = Vb.361 Cnd.271#ii. Dhs reads chandanaṃ &amp; Vb chādanaṃ.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://i%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhik%C4%81">iṭṭhikā</a></em> a tile Dhp-a.iv.203.</li></ul><p>Vedic <em>chad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://iṭṭhikā">iṭṭhikā</a></p>

chadda
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= chada, only in phrase vivattacchadda (or vivaṭa˚) DN.i.89; Snp.372, Snp.378, Snp.1003, Snp.1147; DN-a.i.251 Nd ii.however &amp; DA read ˚chada expl. by vivaṭa-rāgadosamoha-chadana Snp-a.365.</p><p>Dhtp.590 &amp; Dhtm.820 expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> a root <em>chadd</em> by “vamane,” thus evidently taking it as an equivalent of <em>chaḍḍ</em></p>

chaddantā|chaddantaa|chaddanta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaddantaa</span></p><p>One of the seven great lakes of the Himālaya region. AN.iv.101</p><p>Himalaya3lake</p>

chaddhā|chaddhaa|chaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaddhaa</span></p><p>sixfold Mil.2.</p><p>Sk. ṣaṭśaḥ</p>

chaddisā|chaddisaa|chaddisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaddisaa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisā">chaddisā</a></p><p>adverb towards the six directions.</p>

chaddisāpaṭicchādi|chaddisaapa.ticchaadi|chaddisapaticchadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaddisaapa.ticchaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisāpaṭicchādin">chaddisāpaṭicchādin</a></p><p>covering, protecting, in the six directions.</p>

chaddisāpaṭicchādin|chaddisaapa.ticchaadin|chaddisapaticchadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaddisaapa.ticchaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisāpaṭicchādin">chaddisāpaṭicchādin</a></p><p>covering, protecting, in the six directions.</p>

chadhātura|chadhaatura|chadhatura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chadhaatura</span></p><p>consisting of six elements.</p>

chadisā|chadisaa|chadisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chadisaa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisā">chaddisā</a></p><p>adverb towards the six directions.</p>

chadisāpaṭicchādi|chadisaapa.ticchaadi|chadisapaticchadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chadisaapa.ticchaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisāpaṭicchādin">chaddisāpaṭicchādin</a></p><p>covering, protecting, in the six directions.</p>

chadisāpaṭicchādin|chadisaapa.ticchaadin|chadisapaticchadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chadisaapa.ticchaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chaddisāpaṭicchādin">chaddisāpaṭicchādin</a></p><p>covering, protecting, in the six directions.</p>

chakala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>he-goat Ja.vi.237; <em>-ka</em> ibid. &amp; Vin.iii.166<br />■ f. <em>chakalī</em> Ja.vi.559.</p><p>cp. Sk. chagala, from chāga heifer</p>

chakalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>male goat.</p>

chakana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dung.</p>

chakana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Chakana &amp; Chakaṇa</p><p>neuter the dung of animals Vin.i.202; Ja.iii.386 (ṇ); Ja.v.286; Ja.vi.392 (ṇ).</p><p>Vedic śakṛt &amp; śakan; Gr. κόπρος; Sk. chagana is later, see Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 62 n. 16</p>

chakaṇa|chaka.na|chakana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaka.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chakana">chakana</a></p>

chakaṇa|chaka.na|chakana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaka.na</span></p><p>Chakana &amp; Chakaṇa</p><p>neuter the dung of animals Vin.i.202; Ja.iii.386 (ṇ); Ja.v.286; Ja.vi.392 (ṇ).</p><p>Vedic śakṛt &amp; śakan; Gr. κόπρος; Sk. chagana is later, see Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 62 n. 16</p>

chakaṇati|chaka.nati|chakanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaka.nati</span></p><p>dung.</p>

chakaṇatī|chaka.natii|chakanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chaka.natii</span></p><p>= chakana Cnd.199.</p>

chakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (pl.</i></p><p>nom, ~ā, anī) a set of six; a section of sets of six.</p>

chakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>set of six Vism.242 (meda˚ &amp; mutta˚).</p><p>from cha</p>

chakkanipāta|chakkanipaata|chakkanipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chakkanipaata</span></p><p>section consisting of sets of six.</p>

chakkhattuṃ|chakkhattu.m|chakkhattum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chakkhattu.m</span></p><p>indeclinable six times.</p>

chakkhattuṃ|chakkhattu.m|chakkhattum
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chakkhattu.m</span></p><p>adverb six times DN.ii.198; Dhp-a.iii.196.</p><p>Sk. ṣaṭkṛtvas</p>

chal-abhiñño|chal-abhi~n~no|chal-abhinno
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chal-abhi~n~no</span></p><p><strong>Chal-abhiñño:</strong> an Arahat who is a ‘possessor of the 6 higher powers’ see: <em>abhiññā</em>.</p>

chal-abhiññā|chal-abhi~n~naa|chal-abhinna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">chal-abhi~n~naa</span></p><p><strong>Chal-abhiññā:</strong> the 6 ‘higher powers’; see: <em>abhiññā</em></p>

challi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bark, bast Dhp-a.ii.165; Bdhgh on MV. viii.29.</p><p>Sk. challi</p>

chambhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>becomes stiff or rigid; is paralyzed (with fear); (but apparently understood also as shakes)</p>

chambhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be frightened Dhp-a.iv.52 (+ vedhati).</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://chambheti">chambheti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chambheti">chambheti</a></p>

chambheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be firm or rigid, fig. to be stiff with fear, paralysed: see <em><a href="bword://chambhin">chambhin</a></em> &amp; chambhitatta, Cp. ūrukhambha (under khambha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->).</p><p>cp. Sk. skabhnāti &amp; stabhnāti; <em>skambh</em>, and P. khambha, thambha &amp; khambheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chambhin">chambhin</a></p>

chambhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chambhin">chambhin</a></p><p>stiff, rigid (with fear); (but apparently understood also as shakes)</p>

chambhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://chambhin">chambhin</a></p><p>stiff, rigid (with fear); (but apparently understood also as shakes)</p>

chambhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective immovable, rigid; terrified, paralysed with fear SN.i.219; MN.i.19; Ja.iv.310 (variant reading jambhī, here with ref. to one who is bound (stiff with ropes (pāsasatehi chambhī) which is however taken by com. as instr. of cha &amp; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by chasu ṭhānesu, viz on 4 limbs, body &amp; neck; cp. cha)<br />■ <em><a href="bword://acchambhin">acchambhin</a></em> firm steady, undismayed SN.i.220; Snp.42; Ja.i.71<br />■ See chambheti &amp; chambhita.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://chambheti">chambheti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acchambhin">acchambhin</a>, <a href="bword://chambheti">chambheti</a></p>

chambhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>stiffened, benumbed, paralyzed (with fear); (but apparently understood also as) shaking.</p>

chambhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Only in der. chambhitatta (nt.) the state of being stiff, paralysis, stupefaction consternation, always combined with other expressions of fear, viz. uttāsa SN.v.386; bhaya Ja.i.345 (where spelled chambhittaṃ); Ja.ii.336 (where wrongly explained by sarīracalanaṃ), freq. in phrase <em><a href="bword://bhaya">bhaya</a></em> ch. <em><a href="bword://lomaha%E1%B9%83sa">lomahaṃsa</a></em> (fear stupefaction &amp; horripilation (“gooseflesh”) Vin.ii.156; SN.i.104; SN.i.118; SN.i.219; DN.i.49 (explained at DN-a.i.50 wrongly by sakala-sarīra calanaṃ); Cnd.470; Mil.23; Vb.367 Vism.187<br />■ In other connections at Cnd.1 (= Dhs.425 Dhs.1118, where thambhitatta instead of ch˚); Dhs.965 (on which see <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 242).</p><p>pp. of chambheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhaya">bhaya</a>, <a href="bword://lomahaṃsa">lomahaṃsa</a></p>

chambhitatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>stiffness, paralysis, rigidity (through fear)</p>

chammāsa|chammaasa|chammasa
