cittarūpaṃ|cittaruupa.m|cittarupam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaruupa.m</span></p><p>indeclinable as one’s intentions; in accordance with one’s thoughts; satisfactorily; ?</p>

cittasamuṭṭhāna|cittasamu.t.thaana|cittasamutthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittasamu.t.thaana</span></p><p>having its origin in thoughts or consciousness; arising from the mind or consciousness.</p>

cittasamādhāna|cittasamaadhaana|cittasamadhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittasamaadhaana</span></p><p>fixing; stilling of thoughts; profound meditation.</p>

cittasaṅkhara|cittasa"nkhara|cittasankhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittasa"nkhara</span></p><p>mental motive force; mental activity.</p>

cittasena
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Gandhabba present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.258 He is elsewhere DN.iii.204 mentioned as a Yakkha chieftain who should be invoked by the Buddha’s followers when troubled by evil spirits.</p>

cittass'ekaggatā|cittass'ekaggataa|cittass'ekaggata
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittass'ekaggataa</span></p><p><strong>[[cittass’ekaggatā]]Cittass’ekaggatā:</strong> ‘one-pointedness of mind’, is a synonym of concentration, or <em>samādhi</em></p>

cittatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>more varied or diversified.</p>

cittatara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">compar.</i></p><p>of citta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, more various, more varied. SN.iii.151 sq<br />■ a punning passage, thus: by the procedure (caraṇa) of mind (in the past) the present mind (citta) is still more varied. Cp. SA in loco: Asl.66 Expositor 88.</p>

cittatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noun = cittatā SN.v.158.</p>

cittatā|cittataa|cittata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittataa</span></p><p>SA on SN.iii.151 sq. (bhūmicittatāya dvāracittatāya ārammaṇacittatāya kammanānatta).</p><p>f. abstr. to citta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Cittatā</p><p>“being of such a heart or mind,” state of mind, character SN.iii.152; SN.iv.142 (vimutta˚); SN.v.158 (id.); AN.v.145 sq. (upārambha˚) Vb.372 (id.); Vb.359 (amudu˚); Pv-a.13 (visuddhi˚ noble character); paṭibaddha˚ (in love with) Pv-a.145 Pv-a.147, Pv-a.270. In SN.iii.152 <em>l</em> cittitā q.v.</p><p>f. abstr. to citta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

cittavikkhepa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>distraction; derangement of thoughts.</p>

cittavipallāssa|cittavipallaassa|cittavipallassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittavipallaassa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cittavipariyesa">cittavipariyesa</a></p>

cittavipariyesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>delusion, error of thinking.</p>

cittavipariyāsakata|cittavipariyaasakata|cittavipariyasakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittavipariyaasakata</span></p><p>afflicted with delusion of thought.</p>

cittekagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>concentration of thought.</p>

citti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>“giving thought or heart” only in combination with <em>kar</em>: cittikaroti to honour, to esteem. <em>cittikatvā</em>. MN.iii.24 AN.iii.172 Pv.ii.9#55 (cittiṁ k. = pūjetvā Pv-a.135); Dīpavaṁsa.i.2<br />■ acittikatvā MN.iii.22; AN.iv.392<br />pp <em>cittikata</em> thought (much) of Vin.iv.6 (&amp; a˚); Vb.2.</p><p>fr. <em>cit</em>, cp. citta, cintā, cinteti, formation like mutti → muc, sitti → sic</p>

cittika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(from citta) a painting; a picture.</p>

cittikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cittīkata">cittīkata</a></p>

cittikāra|cittikaara|cittikara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittikaara</span></p><p>respect, consideration Vv-a.178 (garu˚), Vv-a.242; Pv-a.26; Vb.371 (a˚); Vism.123 (cittī˚), Vism.188.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://citti">citti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://citti">citti</a></p>

cittita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i></p><p>made variegated, made diverse; decorated; painted.</p>

cittita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>painted, variegated, varied, coloured or resplendent with (-˚) SN.iii.152 (<em>sic l.</em> for cittatā) So SN-a, which, on p. 151, reads citten’ eva cittitaṃ for cintitaṃ. Thag.736; Thag.2, Thag.390 (su˚); Vv.36#7; Vv.40#2.</p><p>pp. of citteti, Denom. fr. citta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

cittiṃkaritvā|citti.mkaritvaa|cittimkaritva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">citti.mkaritvaa</span></p><p>respect; consideration; values.</p>

cittiṃkaritvānā|citti.mkaritvaanaa|cittimkaritvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">citti.mkaritvaanaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cittiṃkaritvā">cittiṃkaritvā</a></p>

cittā|cittaa|citta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaa</span></p><p>A nun whose Therīgāthāspeak of how in her old age she went to Gijjhakūṭa and there, after meditation, she attained arahantship. Thig.27–28</p>

cittāgāra|cittaagaara|cittagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaagaara</span></p><p>decorated pleasure house.</p>

cittālaṅkāra|cittaala"nkaara|cittalankara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaala"nkaara</span></p><p>which adorns or improves (makes fit?)</p>

cittālaṅkāracittaparikkhāra|cittaala"nkaaracittaparikkhaara|cittalankaracittaparikkhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaala"nkaaracittaparikkhaara</span></p><p>adornment or improvement (making fit?) and equipping of thinking?</p>

cittānupassanā|cittaanupassanaa|cittanupassana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaanupassanaa</span></p><p><strong>Cittānupassanā:</strong> ‘contemplation of consciousness’, is one of the 4 foundations of awareness or mindfulness <em>satipatthāna</em></p>

cittānupassi|cittaanupassi|cittanupassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaanupassi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://cittānupassin">cittānupassin</a></p><p>considering; contemplating the thinking or thoughts.</p>

cittānupassin|cittaanupassin|cittanupassin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaanupassin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://cittānupassin">cittānupassin</a></p><p>considering; contemplating the thinking or thoughts.</p>

cittānurakkhi|cittaanurakkhi|cittanurakkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaanurakkhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://cittānurakkhin">cittānurakkhin</a></p><p>who guards the thoughts.</p>

cittānurakkhin|cittaanurakkhin|cittanurakkhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittaanurakkhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://cittānurakkhin">cittānurakkhin</a></p><p>who guards the thoughts.</p>

cittīka|cittiika|cittika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cittika">cittika</a></p>

cittīkata|cittiikata|cittikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiikata</span></p><p>thought highly of; respected, honored; valued.</p>

cittīkataṃ|cittiikata.m|cittikatam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiikata.m</span></p><p>adverb with respect; showing respect.</p>

cittīkatvā|cittiikatvaa|cittikatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiikatvaa</span></p><p>maving shown respect; thought highly of; valued.</p>

cittīkatvānā|cittiikatvaanaa|cittikatvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiikatvaanaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cittīkatvā">cittīkatvā</a></p>

cittīkāra|cittiikaara|cittikara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cittiikaara</span></p><p>respect; consideration.</p>

civarapativisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cīvarapaṭiviṃsa">cīvarapaṭiviṃsa</a></p>

ciñcā|ci~ncaa|cinca
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci~ncaa</span></p><p>Tamarind, Hindi <em>tinti</em>, <em>Tamarindus indica</em> Ja.v.38, a large attractive semi-deciduous tree with small oblong leaves and a yellow flower splashed with red. The tamarind bears large irregularly curved pods containing a tart-tasting pulp in which are embedded large shiny seeds. The pulp is eaten and used in food preparation. The timber of the tamarind is hard and termite-resistant and the wood ash is used for tanning while the leaves, flowers and pods are used in dyeing.</p>

ciñcā|ci~ncaa|cinca
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci~ncaa</span></p><p>the tamarind tree Ja.v.38 (˚vana); Snp-a.78. Citi-citi</p><p>Sk. ciñcā &amp; tintiḍikā</p>

ciṅgulaka|ci"ngulaka|cingulaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulaka</span></p><p>A type of grain Sn.239.</p>

ciṅgulaka|ci"ngulaka|cingulaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulaka</span></p><p>toy wind mill.</p>

ciṅgulaka|ci"ngulaka|cingulaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulaka</span></p><p>(&amp; ˚ika) masculine neuter</p><ol><li>a kind of plant Snp.239 (= kaṇavīra-pupphasaṇṭhāna-sīsa Snp-a.283).</li><li>a toy windmill, made of palm-leaves, etc. (DN-a.i.86: tālapaṇṇādīhi kataṃ vātappahārena paribbhamana-cakkaṃ Vin.ii.10; DN.i.6; MN.i.266; AN.v.203; Mil.229.</li></ol>

ciṅgulayitvā|ci"ngulayitvaa|cingulayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulayitvaa</span></p><p>having turned or whirled around.</p>

ciṅgulika|ci"ngulika|cingulika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ciṅgulaka">ciṅgulaka</a></p>

ciṅgulāyati|ci"ngulaayati|cingulayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulaayati</span></p><p>to twirl round, to revolve like a windmill AN.i.112.</p><p>Denom. fr. cingula</p>

ciṅgulāyitvā|ci"ngulaayitvaa|cingulayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci"ngulaayitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ciṅgulayitvā">ciṅgulayitvā</a></p>

ciṇṇa|ci.n.na|cinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.n.na</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) journeyed through; traveled or roamed (over); lived in, experienced; (n. impers.) a journey was made; the was going about; practiced; observed; habitually engaged in; done; (of a penance) undergone.</li><li>(n.) practicing observance; conduct; habitual conduct or practice.</li></ol>

ciṇṇa|ci.n.na|cinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.n.na</span></p><p>travelled over, resorted to, made a habit of; done, performed, practised Ja.iii.541; Mil.360-su˚ well performed, accomplished SN.i.42 = SN.i.214 = Snp.181; Pv.iii.5#6<br />■ Cp. ā˚, pari˚, vi˚.</p><ul><li><em>-ṭṭhāna</em> the place where one is wont to go Ja.ii.159</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C4%81natta">mānatta</a></em> one who performs the Mānatta Vin.iv.242</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vasin">vasin</a></em> one who has reached mastership in (c. loc.) Thag-a.74; Vism.154, Vism.158, Vism.164, Vism.169, Vism.331 sq., Vism.376; der.</li><li><em>-vāsibhāva</em> Dhs-a.167 (read vasī˚).</li></ul><p>pp. of carati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mānatta">mānatta</a>, <a href="bword://vasin">vasin</a></p>

ciṇṇamānatta|ci.n.namaanatta|cinnamanatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.n.namaanatta</span></p><p>who has gone the mānatta penance.</p>

ciṇṇatta|ci.n.natta|cinnatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.n.natta</span></p><p>custom, habit Mil.57, Mil.105.</p><p>Der. fr. ciṇṇa</p>

ciṭi-ciṭi|ci.ti-ci.ti|citi-citi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.ti-ci.ti</span></p><p>Ciṭi-ciṭi</p><p>fizz DN-a.i.137.</p><p>redupl. interj.</p>

ciṭiciṭāyati|ci.tici.taayati|citicitayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.tici.taayati</span></p><p>makes the sound “citi-citi”; sputters, makes a sputtering sound.</p>

ciṭiciṭāyati|ci.tici.taayati|citicitayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ci.tici.taayati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://cicci%E1%B9%AD%C4%81yati">cicciṭāyati</a></em>; Vin.i.225; cp. Divy.606.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cicciṭāyati">cicciṭāyati</a></p>

clarity of consciousness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[clarity of consciousness]]Clarity of consciousness:</strong> <em>sampajañña</em></p>

clinging
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Clinging:</strong> the 4 kinds of: <em>upādāna</em></p>

co-nascence
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Co-nascence:</strong> <em>sahajāta-paccaya</em> is one of the 24 conditions <em>paccaya</em>.</p>

coca
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A fruit from which drinks were made Ja.v.420 Vin.i.246. In other ancient Indian literature, the name is used for the fruit of <em><a href="bword://kadal%C4%AB">kadalī</a></em>, <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81%E1%B8%B7ikera">nāḷikera</a></em>, <em><a href="bword://panasa">panasa</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://t%C4%81la">tāla</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kadalī">kadalī</a>, <a href="bword://nāḷikera">nāḷikera</a>, <a href="bword://panasa">panasa</a>, <a href="bword://tāla">tāla</a></p>

coca
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the cocoa-nut or banana, or cinnamon Ja.v.420 (˚vana); <em>-pāna</em> a sweet drink of banana or cocoa-nut milk Vin.i.246.</p><p>Both derivation &amp; meaning uncertain. The word is certainly not Aryan. See the note at; <em>Vinaya Texts</em> ii.132</p>

cocapāna|cocapaana|cocapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">cocapaana</span></p><p>drink made from plantains.</p>

codaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><p>(one) who reproves, or exhorts to action; (one) who accuses (of an offense)</p>

codaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who rebukes; exhorting, reproving Vin.i.173; Vin.ii.248 sq.; Vin.v.158, Vin.v.159 etc.; SN.i.63; MN.i.95 sq.; DN.iii.236; AN.i.53; AN.iii.196; AN.iv.193 sq. DN-a.i.40.</p><p>to codeti</p>

codana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codanā">codanā</a></p>

codanā|codanaa|codana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codanaa</span></p><ol><li>exhorting to action; admonition, reproof, criticism.</li><li>pressing (for payment of a debt or promise); accusation (of an offense)</li></ol>

codanā|codanaa|codana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codanaa</span></p><p>reproof, exhortation DN.i.230; DN.iii.218; AN.iii.352; Vin V.158, 159; Vism.276<br />■ As ttg in <em>codan’ atthe nipāto</em> an exhortative particle Ja.vi.211 (for ingha); Vv-a.237 (id.); Pv-a.88 variant reading (for handa).</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codanāvatthu|codanaavatthu|codanavatthu
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codanaavatthu</span></p><p>A valley (?) near Rājagaha, visited by the Buddha in the course of his wanderings. Here he laid down a rule, allowing monks to recite the <em>pātimokkha</em> under a learned monk if the leader of their own company should be inefficient. Vin.i.116<em>f.</em></p><p>near Rājagaha3feature</p>

codanāvatthu|codanaavatthu|codanavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codanaavatthu</span></p><p>ground for criticism or accusation.</p>

codayamāna|codayamaana|codayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codayamaana</span></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codayitvā|codayitvaa|codayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codayitvaa</span></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~entī)n. of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg</i></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codesuṃ)|codesu.m)|codesum)
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codesu.m)</span></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://codetar">codetar</a></p><p>(from codeti) one who admonishes; one who accuses (of an offense)</p>

codetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; impers.</i></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://codetar">codetar</a></p><p>(from codeti) one who admonishes; one who accuses (of an offense)</p>

codetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who reproves, one who exacts blame, etc. Vin.v.184.</p><p>n. ag. to codeti</p>

codeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>incites, causes to move quickly, drives on; exhorts to action.</li><li>importunes; presses (for a payment of a debt or promise)</li><li>criticizes, reproves, and admonishes.</li><li>accuses (of an offense, instr.); makes an accusation.</li></ol>

codeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor</i></p><p><em>acodayi</em> (verse 112),<br />inf codetuṃ,<br />grd codetabba;<br />pass <em>cujjati</em> &amp; codiyati;<br />pp cudita &amp; codita (q.v.):<br />caus <em>codāpeti</em> (Vin.iii.165)</p><p>to urge, incite, exhort; to reprove, reprimand, to call forth, to question; in spec. sense to demand payment of a debt (Ja.vi.69 iṇaṃ codetvā Ja.vi.245; Snp.120 iṇaṃ cujjamāna being pressed to pay up Pv-a.3 iṇayikehi codiyamāna) DN.i.230; Vin.i.43 (āpattiyā c. to reprove for an offence), Vin.i.114, Vin.i.170 sq. Vin.i.322 sq.; Vin.ii.2 sq., Vin.ii.80 sq.; Vin.iii.164, etc.; Ja.v.112; Dhp.379; Pv-a.39, Pv-a.74.</p><p>Vedic codati &amp; codayati, from; <em>cud</em></p>

codetvā|codetvaa|codetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">codetvaa</span></p><p>of codeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://codeti">codeti</a></p>

codita
