dhāreta|dhaareta|dhareta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaareta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dhāretar">dhāretar</a></p><p>one who memorizes; keeps in mind.</p>

dhāretabba|dhaaretabba|dharetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaretabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāreti">dhāreti</a></p>

dhāretar|dhaaretar|dharetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaretar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dhāretar">dhāretar</a></p><p>one who memorizes; keeps in mind.</p>

dhāretar|dhaaretar|dharetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaretar</span></p><p>one who causes others to remember, an instructor, teacher (cp. dhāraṇaka) AN.iv.196 (sotā sāvetā uggahetā dh.).</p><p>n. ag. to dhāreti 3</p>

dhāreti|dhaareti|dhareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaareti</span></p><ol><li>bears; holds, carries; preserves, keeps; upholds, maintains; uses, wears.</li><li>holds back, restrains.</li><li>holds as, considers; accepts as.</li><li>bears in mind; remembers; memorizes.</li><li>owes.</li></ol>

dhāreti|dhaareti|dhareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaareti</span></p><p>to hold, viz.</p><ol><li>to carry, bear, wear, possess; to put on, to bring give DN.i.166≈(chavadussāni etc.); Vin.i.16 = DN.i.110 (telapajjotaṃ); DN.ii.19 (chattaṃ to hold a sunshade over a person); Pv-a.47 (id.); dehaṃ dh. to “wear,” i.e. to have a body Iti.50, Iti.53 (antimaṃ d.); Ja.iv.3 (padumaṃ) Ja.vi.136; Pv.i.3#1 (vaṇṇaṃ dh. = vahasi Pv-a.14); tassa kahāpaṇaṃ daṇḍaṃ dh. “to inflict a fine of a k. on him Mil.171.</li><li>to hold back, restrain Vin.iv.261 (kathaṃ dhāretha how do you suppress or conceal pregnancy?); Dhp.222 (kodhaṃ).</li><li>to bear in mind know by heart, understand: dhammaṃ to know the Dhamma AN.iii.176; tipiṭakaṃ buddhavacanaṃ to know the 3 Piṭakas Mil.18<br />■ DN.ii.2; Pp.41 (suṇāti bhaṇati, dh. = remember). Cp. upadhāreti<br />■ With double acc.: to receive as, to take = believe, to take for consider as, call: upāsakaṃ maṃ dhāretu Bhagavā “call me your disciple” Vin.i.16 &amp; passim; atthajālan ti pi naṃ dhārehi (call it…) DN.i.46; yathā pañhaṃ Bhagavā vyākaroti tathā naṃ dhareyyāsi (believe it) DN.i.222; yathā no (atthaṃ) Gotamo vyākarissati tathā naṃ dhāressāma DN.i.236; evaṃ maṃ dhārehi adhimuttacittaṃ (consider as) Snp.1149 (= upalakkhehi Cnd.323)</li><li>to admit, allow, allow for, take up, support (a cause); to give, to owe DN.i.125 (may allow), DN.i.126; AN.ii.69 (na kassa kiñci dh. pays no tribute); Mil.47 (atthaṃ).</li></ol><p>Caus. of dharati, q.v. for etym.</p>

dhāretvā|dhaaretvaa|dharetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaretvaa</span></p><p>of dhāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāreti">dhāreti</a></p>

dhāreyya|dhaareyya|dhareyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaareyya</span></p><p>the ceremony of being carried away, i.e. the marriage ceremony, marriage (cp. dhareyya under dharati) Thig.472 (text has vāreyya but Thag-a.285 explains dhāreyya = vivāha).</p><p>orig. grd. of dhāreti</p>

dhārin|dhaarin|dharin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaarin</span></p><p>adjective -˚ holding, wearing, keeping; often in phrase antimadeha˚ “wearing the last body” (of an Arahant SN.i.14; Snp.471; Iti.32, Iti.40<br />■ Ja.i.47 (virūpa-vesa˚) Dāvs v.15<br />■ f. ˚inī Pv.i.10#8 (kāsikuttama˚).</p><p>Sk. dhārin, see <em><a href="bword://dh%C4%81reti">dhāreti</a></em> &amp; cp. ˚dhara, ˚dhāra</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāreti">dhāreti</a></p>

dhārita|dhaarita|dharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaarita</span></p><p>carried, worn, used; memorized.</p>

dhāritabba|dhaaritabba|dharitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaritabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāretabba">dhāretabba</a></p>

dhāriyamāna|dhaariyamaana|dhariyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaariyamaana</span></p><ol><li>being held; being held over, standing over, sheltering.</li><li>with something held over one; sheltered (?)</li></ol>

dhāropa|dhaaropa|dharopa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaropa</span></p><p>(a dialect word for) a bowl or a dish.</p>

dhārā|dhaaraa|dhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaraa</span></p><p>dhārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine a stream of water; a drop of liquid; a stream, a shower.</p><p>dhārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine the sharp edge of a knife or a sword etc.; a blade.</p>

dhārā|dhaaraa|dhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaraa</span></p><p>Dhārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine torrent, stream, flow, shower DN.i.74 (sammā˚ an even or seasonable shower; DN-a.i.218 = vuṭṭhi); DN.ii.15 (udakassa, streams) Ja.i.31; Pts.i.125 (udaka˚); Pv.ii.9#70 (sammā˚); Vv-a.4 (hingulika˚); Pv-a.139; Dhp-a.iv.15 (assu˚); Sdhp.595 (vassa˚).</p><p>Sk. dhārā, from dhāvati 1</p><p>Dhārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine the edge of a weapon Ja.i.455; Ja.vi.449; Dhp-a.317; DN-a.i.37<br />■ (adj. (-˚) having a (sharp) edge Ja.i.414 (khura˚) Mil.105 (sukhuma˚); ekato˚-ubhato˚ single- &amp; double-edged Ja.i.73 (asi); Ja.iv.12 (sattha).</p><p>Sk. dhārā, from dhāvati 2.</p>

dhārīyati|dhaariiyati|dhariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaariiyati</span></p><p>of dhareti is carried; is held</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhareti">dhareti</a></p>

dhāta|dhaata|dhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaata</span></p><p>satisfied; replete.</p>

dhāta|dhaata|dhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaata</span></p><p>fed, satiated; satisfied, appeased Vin.i.222; Ja.i.185; Ja.ii.247, Ja.ii.446; Ja.v.73; Ja.vi.555; Pv.i.11#8 (so read for dāta) = Pv-a.59 (: suhita titta); Mil.238, Mil.249<br />■ f abstr. <em>dhātatā</em> satiation, fulness, satisfaction, in ati Ja.ii.293.</p><p>Sk. *dhāyita of dhayati to suck, nourish, pp. dhīta</p>

dhātar|dhaatar|dhatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatar</span></p><p>upholder Ja.v.225.</p><p>n. ag. fr. <em>dhṛ</em>.</p>

dhāti|dhaati|dhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhātī">dhātī</a></p>

dhāticela|dhaaticela|dhaticela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaaticela</span></p><p>nurse’s garment.</p>

dhātu|dhaatu|dhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatu</span></p><ol><li><p>the basic material element; a primary element.</p></li><li><p>a principle (?)</p></li><li><ol><li>one of the 18 constituent elements of (sensory) experience.</li><li>an element, a conditioned constituent, of experience.</li></ol></li><li><p>a domain of experience or existence; sometimes a very general abstraction: ~hood; ~ness.</p></li><li><p>a psychological element; a disposition; constitution.</p></li></ol>

dhātu|dhaatu|dhatu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatu</span></p><p>element. Closely related to dhamma in meaning B 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, only implying a closer relation to physical substance. As to its gen. connotation cp. <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 198.</p><ol><li><p>a primary element, of which the usual set comprises the four paṭhavī, āpo, tejo, vāyo (earth water, fire, wind), otherwise termed cattāro mahābhūtā(ni): DN.i.215; DN.ii.294; DN.iii.228; SN.i.15; SN.ii.169 sq. SN.ii.224; SN.iv.175, SN.iv.195; AN.ii.165; AN.iii.243; Vb.14, Vb.72; Ne.73. See discussed at <em>Cpd.</em> 254 sq<br />■ A def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of dhātu is to be found at Vism.485<br />■ Singly or in other combinations paṭhavī˚ SN.ii.174; tejo˚ SN.i.144; DN.iii.227; the four plus ākāsa SN.iii.227, plus viññāna SN.ii.248; SN.iii.231; see below 2 b.</p></li><li><ol><li>natural condition, property, disposition; factor, item, principle, form. In this meaning in var. combinations &amp; applications, esp. closely related to khandha. Thus mentioned with; <em>khandha &amp; āyatana;</em> (sensory element &amp; element of sense-perception) as bodily or physical element, factor (see <em><a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></em> B 1 d &amp; cp. Cnd under dhātu) Thig.472. As such (physical substratum) it constitutes one of the lokā or forms of being (khandha˚ dhātu˚ āyatana˚ Cnd.550). Freq also in combination kāma-dhātu, rūpa˚ arūpa˚ “the elements or properties of k. etc.” as preceding &amp; conditioning bhava in the respective category (Cnd s. v.). See under d<br />■ As “set of conditions or state of being (-˚) in the foll.: <em>loka˚</em>; a world, of which 10 are usually mentioned (equalling 10,000: Pv-a.138) SN.i.26; SN.v.424; Pv.ii.9#61; Vb.336; Pv-a.138; <em>KS</em> ii.101, <em>n.</em> 1; - <em>nibbāna˚</em>; the state of N. SN.v.8; AN.ii.120; AN.iv.202; Ja.i.55; Iti.38 (dve: see under <em>Nibbāna</em>); Mil.312. Also in the foll. connections: amata˚ Iti.62; bhū˚ the verbal root bhū DN-a.i.229; ṭhapitāya dhātuyā “while the bodily element, i.e. vitality lasts” Mil.125; vaṇṇa˚ form beauty SN.i.131; Pv.i.3#1. In these cases it is so far weakened in meaning, that it simply corresponds to E abstr. suffix-hood or-ity (cp. ˚hood = origin. “form” see <em><a href="bword://ketu">ketu</a></em>), so perhaps in Nibbāna˚ = Nibbāna-dom. Cp dhātuka</li><li>elements in sense-consciousness: referring to the 6 ajjhattikāni &amp; 6 bāhirāni āyatanāni SN.ii.140 sq. Of these sep. sota˚ DN.i.79; DN.iii.38; Vb.334 dibbasota˚ SN.ii.121, SN.ii.212; SN.v.265, SN.v.304; AN.i.255; AN.iii.17 AN.iii.280; AN.v.199; cakkhu˚ Vb.71 sq.; mano˚ Vb.175, Vb.182 Vb.301; mano-viññāṇa˚ Vb.87, Vb.89, Vb.175, Vb.182 sq.</li><li>various: aneka˚ AN.i.22; AN.iii.325; AN.v.33; akusala˚ Vb.363; avijjā˚ SN.ii.132; ābhā˚ SN.ii.150; ārambha˚ SN.v.66 SN.v.104 sq.; AN.i.4; AN.ii.338; ṭhiti˚ SN.ii.175; SN.iii.231; AN.iii.338 dhamma˚ SN.ii.56; nekkhamma˚ SN.ii.151; AN.iii.447 nissāraṇiyā dhātuyo (5) DN.iii.239; AN.iii.245, AN.iii.290. See further SN.i.134, SN.i.196; SN.ii.153, SN.ii.248 (aniccā); SN.iii.231 (nirodha); SN.iv.67; AN.i.176; AN.ii.164; AN.iv.385; Dhs.58, Dhs.67, Dhs.121 Ne.57, Ne.64 sq.; Thag-a.20, Thag-a.49, Thag-a.285, -</li><li>Different sets and enumerations: as 3 under kāma˚, rūpa˚, arūpa AN.i.223 AN.iii.447; Pts.i.137; Vb.86, Vb.363, Vb.404 sq.; under rūpa˚ arūpa˚, nirodha˚ Iti.45<br />■ as 6 (pathavī etc. + ākāsa &amp; viññāṇa˚): DN.iii.247; AN.i.175 sq.; MN.iii.31, MN.iii.62, MN.iii.240; Pts.i.136; Vb.82 sq<br />■ as 7 (ābhā subha etc.): SN.ii.150<br />■ as 18: Pts.i.101, Pts.i.137; Pts.ii.230, Dhs.1333; Vb.87 sq. Vb.401 sq.; Vism.484 sq.</li></ol></li><li><p>a humour or affection of the body DN-a.i.253 (dhātusamatā).</p></li><li><p>the remains of the body after cremation Pv-a.76; a relic Vv-a.165 (sarīra˚, bodily relic); Dāvs v.3 (dasana˚ the toothrelic)</p></li></ol><p>■ abl. dhātuso according to one’s nature SN.ii.154 sq. (sattā sattehi saddhiṃ saṃsandanti etc.) Iti.70 (id.); SN.iii.65.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kath%C4%81">kathā</a></em> Name of 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> book of the Abhidhamma Vism.96</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kucchi">kucchi</a></em> womb Mil.176;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></em> skilled in the elements MN.iii.62; ˚kusalatā proficiency in the (18) elements DN.iii.212; Dhs.1333;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a></em> “house for a relic,” a dagoba Snp-a.194.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cetiya">cetiya</a></em> a shrine over a relic Dhp-a.iii.29;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81natta">nānatta</a></em> diversity of specific experience DN.iii.289; SN.ii.143; SN.iv.113 sq., SN.iv.284;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vibh%C4%81ga">vibhāga</a></em> distribution of relics Vv-a.297; Pv-a.212.</li></ul><p>Sk. dhātu to dadhāti, Idg. <em>*dhé</em>, cp. Gr. τίχημι, ἀνά χημα, Sk. dhāman, dhāṭr (= Lat. conditor); Goth gadēds; Ohg. tāt, tuom (in meaning-˚ = dhātu, cp. E serf-dom “condition of…”) tuon = E. to do; &amp; with k-suffix Lat. facio, Gr. (ε ̓́)χηκ(α), Sk. dhāka; see also <em><a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a>, <a href="bword://ketu">ketu</a>, <a href="bword://kathā">kathā</a>, <a href="bword://kucchi">kucchi</a>, <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a>, <a href="bword://ghara">ghara</a>, <a href="bword://cetiya">cetiya</a>, <a href="bword://nānatta">nānatta</a>, <a href="bword://vibhāga">vibhāga</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></p>

dhātuka|dhaatuka|dhatuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatuka</span></p><p>adjective (only-˚) having the nature, by nature, affected with, -like (cp. ˚dhamma B 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); often simply first part of cpd. (cp. E. friend-like = friendly = friend Ja.i.438 (kiliṭṭha˚ miserable), Ja.ii.31 (sama˚), Ja.ii.63 (badhira deaf), Ja.ii.102 (paṇḍuroga˚ having jaundice), Ja.ii.114 (dhuttika˚); Ja.iv.137 (vāmanaka˚ deformed), Ja.iv.391 (muddhā˚), Ja.v.197 (āvāṭa˚); Dhp-a.i.89 (anattamana˚).</p>

dhātura|dhaatura|dhatura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatura</span></p><p>adj. -˚ in cha˚ consisting of six elements (purisa) MN.iii.239 (where āpodhātu omitted by mistake). See dhātu 2 c.</p><p>= *dhātuya</p>

dhātī|dhaatii|dhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatii</span></p><p>nurse; a wet-nurse.</p>

dhātī|dhaatii|dhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaatii</span></p><p>wet nurse, foster-mother DN.ii.19; MN.i.395 MN.ii.97; Ja.i.57; Ja.iii.391; Pv-a.16, Pv-a.176. In compounds <em>dhāti˚</em>; viz. <em>-<a href="bword://cela">cela</a></em> swaddling cloth, baby’s napkin SN.i.205; Ja.iii.309.</p><p>Sk. dhātrī = Gr. τιχήνη wet nurse, to dhayati suck, suckle; Idg. <em>*dhēi</em> as in Gr. χ ̈ησχαι to milk, χ ̈ηλυς feeding χηλή female breast; cp. Lat. felare, femina (“giving suck”), filius (“suckling”); Old Irish dīnu lamb; Goth daddjan; Old High German tila breast ‣See also dadhi, dhītā dhenu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cela">cela</a></p>

dhāva|dhaava|dhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaava</span></p><p>running, racing.</p>

dhāva|dhaava|dhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaava</span></p><p>running, racing MN.i.446.</p><p>Sk. dhāva</p>

dhāvana|dhaavana|dhavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavana</span></p><p>running, galloping Ja.ii.431; Mil.351.</p><p>Sk. dhāvana</p>

dhāvanta|dhaavanta|dhavanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of dhāvati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāvati">dhāvati</a></p>

dhāvat|dhaavat|dhavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dhāvat">dhāvat</a></p><p>of dhāvati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāvati">dhāvati</a></p>

dhāvati|dhaavati|dhavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavati</span></p><p>runs; streams; rushes; runs around.</p>

dhāvati|dhaavati|dhavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavati</span></p><ol><li>to run run away, run quickly Snp.939 (cp. Mnd.419); Dhp.344; Ja.i.308; Ja.vi.332; Mnd.405 = Cnd.304#iii.; Pv.iv.16#1 = palāyati Pv-a.284#2; Dhp-a.i.389 (opp. gacchati); Pv-a.4; Sdhp.378.</li><li>to clean etc.: see <em><a href="bword://dhovati">dhovati</a></em>; cp dhavala &amp; dhārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</li></ol><p>Sk. dhāvati &amp; dhāvate 1 to flow, run etc.; cp. Gr. χέω (both meanings); Ags. déaw = E. dew; Ohg tou = Ger. tau; cp. also dhārā &amp; dhunāti 2 to clean (by running water) etc. = P. <em><a href="bword://dhovati">dhovati</a></em>, q.v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhovati">dhovati</a>, <a href="bword://dhovati">dhovati</a></p>

dhāvin|dhaavin|dhavin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhaavin</span></p><p>see <em>pa</em>˚.</p>

dhī|dhii|dhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a></p>

dhīna|dhiina|dhina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiina</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://adh%C4%ABna">adhīna</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adhīna">adhīna</a></p>

dhīra|dhiira|dhira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiira</span></p><p>intelligent, wise; steadfast, firm, resolute.</p>

dhīra|dhiira|dhira
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiira</span></p><p>adjective constant, firm, self-relying of character; wise, possessing the knowledge of the Dhamma, often = paṇḍita &amp; epithet of an Arahant DN.ii.128; SN.i.24 (lokapariyāyaṃ aññāya nibbutā dh.), SN.i.122, SN.i.221; Snp.45, Snp.235 (nibbanti dhīrā), Snp.913 (vippamutto diṭṭhigatehi dh.), Snp.1052; Iti.68 (˚upasevanā, opp. bāla), Iti.122 (dh. sabbaganthapamocano); Dhp.23, Dhp.28, Dhp.177 (opp bāla); Thag.4; Thag.2, Thag.7 (dhammā = tejussadehi ariyamaggadhammehi Thag-a.13); Ja.iii.396; Ja.v.116; Pv.ii.1#6 Pv.ii.9#45; Mnd.44, Mnd.55, Mnd.482; Cnd.324 (= jutimant); Mil.342; Kp-a.194, Kp-a.224, Kp-a.230; Dhp-a.iii.189 (= paṇḍita).</p><p>combining in meaning 1 Sk. dhīra “firm” fr. dhārayati (see <em><a href="bword://dharati">dharati</a></em> &amp; dhiti); 2 Vedic. dhīra “wise” fr. dīdheti (see *<a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->). The fluctuation of connotation is also seen in the explains of Com<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s<!-- raw HTML omitted --> which always give the foll. three conventional etymologies, viz. dhikkitapāpa dhiti-sampanna, dhiyā (= paññāya) samannāgata Mnd.44 ≈ (see *<a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dharati">dharati</a>, <a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a>, <a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a></p>

dhīrā|dhiiraa|dhira
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiiraa</span></p><p>Dhīrā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A bhikkhunī whose Therīgāthā verse speaks of the wise one who attains Nibbana. Thig.6</p><p>Dhīrā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Vīrā</p><p>A bhikkhunī whose Therīgāthā verse speaks of how the heroic one overcomes Māra. Thig.7</p>

dhīrūpasevanā|dhiiruupasevanaa|dhirupasevana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiiruupasevanaa</span></p><p>association with the wise.</p>

dhīta|dhiita|dhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dhītar">dhītar</a></p><p>daughter.</p>

dhītalikā|dhiitalikaa|dhitalika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitalikaa</span></p><p>doll Vin.iii.36, Vin.iii.126 (dāru˚); Dhs-a.321; Pv-a.16.</p><p>Dimin. of dhītā; cp. dhītikā &amp; potthalikā</p>

dhītar|dhiitar|dhitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dhītar">dhītar</a></p><p>daughter.</p>

dhītar|dhiitar|dhitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitar</span></p><p>Dhītar &amp; Dhītā</p><p>masculine &amp; feminine daughter Thig.336 (in faith); Ja.i.152, Ja.i.253 Ja.vi.366; Pv.i.11#5; Dhp-a.iii.171, Dhp-a.iii.176; Pv-a.16, Pv-a.21, Pv-a.61, Pv-a.105 deva˚ a female deva (see <em><a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em>) Vv-a.137 etc.; nattu˚ a granddaughter Pv-a.17; mātula˚ a niece Pv-a.55; rāja a princess Ja.i.207; Pv-a.74. In compound <em>dhītu</em>.</p><ul><li><em>-kkama</em> one who is desirous of a daughter Ja.vi.307 (= dhītu atthāya vicarati Com.; variant reading dhītu-kāma)</li><li><em>-dhītā</em> granddaughter Pv-a.16.</li></ul><p>Sk. dhītā, orig. pp. of dhayati to suck (cp. Lat. filia): see <em><a href="bword://dh%C4%81ta">dhāta</a></em> &amp; dhātī, inflūenced in inflection by Sk. duhitṛ, although etymologically different</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://deva">deva</a>, <a href="bword://dhāta">dhāta</a></p>

dhītikā|dhiitikaa|dhitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitikaa</span></p><p>doll.</p>

dhītikā|dhiitikaa|dhitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitikaa</span></p><p>doll Thig.374 (= dhītalikā Thag-a.252).</p><p>cp. dhītalikā</p>

dhītumaraṇa|dhiitumara.na|dhitumarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitumara.na</span></p><p>the death of a daughter or of daughters.</p>

dhītā|dhiitaa|dhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhītar">dhītar</a></p>

dhītā|dhiitaa|dhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitaa</span></p><p>Dhītar &amp; Dhītā</p><p>masculine &amp; feminine daughter Thig.336 (in faith); Ja.i.152, Ja.i.253 Ja.vi.366; Pv.i.11#5; Dhp-a.iii.171, Dhp-a.iii.176; Pv-a.16, Pv-a.21, Pv-a.61, Pv-a.105 deva˚ a female deva (see <em><a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em>) Vv-a.137 etc.; nattu˚ a granddaughter Pv-a.17; mātula˚ a niece Pv-a.55; rāja a princess Ja.i.207; Pv-a.74. In compound <em>dhītu</em>.</p><ul><li><em>-kkama</em> one who is desirous of a daughter Ja.vi.307 (= dhītu atthāya vicarati Com.; variant reading dhītu-kāma)</li><li><em>-dhītā</em> granddaughter Pv-a.16.</li></ul><p>Sk. dhītā, orig. pp. of dhayati to suck (cp. Lat. filia): see <em><a href="bword://dh%C4%81ta">dhāta</a></em> &amp; dhātī, inflūenced in inflection by Sk. duhitṛ, although etymologically different</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://deva">deva</a>, <a href="bword://dhāta">dhāta</a></p>

dhītī|dhiitii|dhiti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiitii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhiti">dhiti</a></p>

dhīyati|dhiiyati|dhiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhiiyati</span></p><p>to be contained Thag-a.13 (so read for dhiyati); Pv-a.71.</p><p>Sk. dhīyate, Pass. to dahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

dhūma|dhuuma|dhuma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuuma</span></p><p>smoke.</p>

dhūma|dhuuma|dhuma
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuuma</span></p><p>smoke, fumes Vin.i.204 (aroma of drugs) MN.i.220 (dh ˚ṃ kattā); AN.v.352 (id.); AN.ii.53; AN.iv.72 sq. AN.v.347 sq.; Ja.iii.401, Ja.iii.422 (tumhākaṃ dh-kāle at the time when you will end in smoke, i.e. at your cremation) Dhp-a.i.370 (eka˚ one mass of smoke); Vv-a.173 (for dhūpa, in gandhapuppha˚); Pv-a.230 (micchā-vitakka in expl. of vidhūma).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://andha">andha</a></em> blind with smoke Ja.i.216;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81lika">kālika</a></em> (cp. above dh- kāle) lasting till a person’s cremation Vin.ii.172 Vin.ii.288;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ketu">ketu</a></em> fire (lit. whose sign is smoke) Ja.iv.26; Ja.v.63</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://j%C4%81la">jāla</a></em> a mass of smoke Ja.v.497;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://netta">netta</a></em> a smoke-tube, i.e. a surgical instrument for sniffing up the smoke of medical drugs Vin.i.204; Vin.ii.120; Ja.iv.363; Thag-a.14</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sikh%C4%81">sikhā</a></em> fire (Ep. of Agni; lit. smoke-crested) Vv.35#2 (sikha) = Vv-a.161; Vism.416; also as sikhin Ja.vi.206. Dhumayati &amp; Dhumayati;</li></ul><p>Vedic dhūma = Lat. fumus; Gr. χυμός (mood, mind), χυμιάω (fumigate); Ohg. toum etc. Idg. <em>*dhu</em> cp. Gr. χύω (burn incense), χύος (incense). See also dhunāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://andha">andha</a>, <a href="bword://kālika">kālika</a>, <a href="bword://ketu">ketu</a>, <a href="bword://jāla">jāla</a>, <a href="bword://netta">netta</a>, <a href="bword://sikhā">sikhā</a></p>

dhūmaggi|dhuumaggi|dhumaggi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaggi</span></p><p>smoky fire (?); flames enveloped in smoke (?)</p>

dhūmakālikaṃ|dhuumakaalika.m|dhumakalikam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumakaalika.m</span></p><p>adverb until death or cremation (or mfn., lasting until cremation (?)</p>

dhūmanetta|dhuumanetta|dhumanetta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumanetta</span></p><ol><li>a pipe for (inhaling or ingesting) smoke.</li><li>an outlet or chimney.</li></ol>

dhūmayati|dhuumayati|dhumayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumayati</span></p><p>Dhūmāyati &amp; Dhūmayati</p><p>to smoke, to smoulder, choke; to be obscured to cloud over MN.i.142 (variant reading dhūpāyati); Pv.i.6#4 (pariḍayhati + dh. hadayaṃ); Dhp-a.i.425 (akkhīni me dh. I see almost nothing). pp. dhūmāyita.</p><p>Sk. dhūmayati, Denom. fr. dhūma</p>

dhūmāyanā|dhuumaayanaa|dhumayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayanaa</span></p><p>smoking, smoldering; pondering.</p>

dhūmāyanā|dhuumaayanaa|dhumayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayanaa</span></p><p>smoking, smouldering MN.i.143; Ne.24 (as variant reading to dhūpāyanā).</p>

dhūmāyati|dhuumaayati|dhumayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayati</span></p><ol><li>smokes; smolders; clouds over.</li><li>burns; ponders; is vexed.</li></ol>

dhūmāyati|dhuumaayati|dhumayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayati</span></p><p>Dhūmāyati &amp; Dhūmayati</p><p>to smoke, to smoulder, choke; to be obscured to cloud over MN.i.142 (variant reading dhūpāyati); Pv.i.6#4 (pariḍayhati + dh. hadayaṃ); Dhp-a.i.425 (akkhīni me dh. I see almost nothing). pp. dhūmāyita.</p><p>Sk. dhūmayati, Denom. fr. dhūma</p>

dhūmāyita|dhuumaayita|dhumayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayita</span></p><p>obscured, darkened (as if by smoke); hot, suffering.</p>

dhūmāyitatta|dhuumaayitatta|dhumayitatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayitatta</span></p><p>smoky darkness.</p>

dhūmāyitatta|dhuumaayitatta|dhumayitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayitatta</span></p><p>becoming like smoke, clouding over, obscuration SN.iii.124 (+ timirāyitattaṃ).</p><p>abstr. to dhūmāyati</p>

dhūmāyitvā|dhuumaayitvaa|dhumayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuumaayitvaa</span></p><p>of dhūmāyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhūmāyati">dhūmāyati</a></p>

dhūpa|dhuupa|dhupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupa</span></p><p>incense Ja.i.51, Ja.i.64, Ja.i.290 (gandha˚, dvandva cpd.); Ja.iii.144; Ja.vi.42; Pv-a.141 (gandhap̄uppha˚) dh˚ṃ dadāti to incense (a room) Ja.i.399. Sometimes misspelt <em><a href="bword://dh%C5%ABma">dhūma</a></em>, e.g. Vv-a.173 (gandhapuppha˚).</p><p>Sk. dhūpa of Idg. <em>*dhūp</em>, enlarged fr. <em>*dhū</em> in dhunāti (q.v.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhūma">dhūma</a></p>

dhūpana|dhuupana|dhupana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupana</span></p><p>incensing, fumigation; perfume, incense, spice Ja.iii.144; Ja.iv.236; Pv.iii.5#3 (sāsapa˚). Dhupayati &amp; Dhupayati;</p><p>Sk. dhūpana</p>

dhūpayati|dhuupayati|dhupayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupayati</span></p><p>Dhūpāyati &amp; Dhūpayati</p><p>to fumigate, make fragrant, perfume Vin.i.180; SN.i.40 (dhūpāyita) = Thag.448; AN.ii.214 sq.; Ja.i.73; Mil.333 (sīlagandhena lokaṃ dh.); Dhp-a.i.370 (aor. dhūpāyi) Dhp-a.iii.38 (ppr. dhūpayamāna)<br />pp dhūpita.</p><p>Sk. dhūpayati; caus. fr. dhūpa</p>

dhūpita|dhuupita|dhupita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupita</span></p><p>fumigated, flavoured Vv.43#5 (tela˚ flavoured with oil). Cp. pa˚.</p><p>pp. of dhūpāyati</p>

dhūpāyanā|dhuupaayanaa|dhupayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupaayanaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhūmāyanā">dhūmāyanā</a></p>

dhūpāyati|dhuupaayati|dhupayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupaayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhūmāyati">dhūmāyati</a></p>

dhūpāyati|dhuupaayati|dhupayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupaayati</span></p><p>Dhūpāyati &amp; Dhūpayati</p><p>to fumigate, make fragrant, perfume Vin.i.180; SN.i.40 (dhūpāyita) = Thag.448; AN.ii.214 sq.; Ja.i.73; Mil.333 (sīlagandhena lokaṃ dh.); Dhp-a.i.370 (aor. dhūpāyi) Dhp-a.iii.38 (ppr. dhūpayamāna)<br />pp dhūpita.</p><p>Sk. dhūpayati; caus. fr. dhūpa</p>

dhūpāyita|dhuupaayita|dhupayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuupaayita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhūmāyita">dhūmāyita</a></p>

dhūsara|dhuusara|dhusara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuusara</span></p><p>adjective dust-coloured Vv-a.335.</p><p>Sk. dhūsara, Ags. dust = E. dust &amp; dusk, Ger. dust; see dhvaṃsati &amp; dhunoti &amp; cp. Walde, <em>Lat Wtb.</em> under furo</p>

dhūta|dhuuta|dhuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuuta</span></p><p>Dhūta &amp; Dhūtaṅga</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://dhuta">dhuta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhuta">dhuta</a></p>

dhūtaṅga|dhuuta"nga|dhutanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dhuuta"nga</span></p><p>Dhūta &amp; Dhūtaṅga</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://dhuta">dhuta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhuta">dhuta</a></p>

di
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Di˚</p><p>secondary base of numeral “2,” contracted fr. dvi: see under <em><a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></em> B i.4.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></p>

dibba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>divine; heavenly; belonging to gods; supernatural; magical, marvelous.</p>

dibba
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective of the next world, divine, heavenly, celestial, superb magnificent, fit for exalted beings higher than man (devas, heroes, manes etc.), superhuman, opp. mānusaka human. Freq. qualifying the foll. “summa bona” <em><a href="bword://cakkhu">cakkhu</a></em> the deva-eye, i.e. the faculty of clairvoyance attr. in a marked degree to the Buddha &amp; other perfect beings (see <em><a href="bword://cakkhumant">cakkhumant</a></em>) DN.i.82, DN.i.162; DN.ii.20 (yena sudaṃ samantā yojanaṃ passati divā c’ eva rattiñ ca); DN.iii.219; SN.i.196; SN.ii.55 sq.; MN.ii.21; Iti.52; Thig.70; Pts.i.114 Pts.ii.175; Vism.434; Sdhp.482; Pv-a.5 (of Moggallāna) Tikp.278; Duka-pa.54. <em><a href="bword://sota">sota</a></em> the d. ear, matching the d. eye DN.i.79, DN.i.154; Ja.v.456; also as <em>sotadhātu</em> AN.i.255; MN.ii.19; DN.iii.38, DN.iii.281; Vism.430. <em><a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em> DN.i.153. <em>Āyu, vaṇṇa</em> etc. (see <em><a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a></em> ṭhānāni) AN.i.115; AN.iii.33; AN.iv.242; Pv-a.9 Pv-a.89. <em>kāmā</em> Snp.361; Dhp.187; Iti.94; also as kāmaguṇā AN.v.273. Of food, drink, dress &amp; other commodities AN.i.182; Ja.i.50, Ja.i.202; Ja.iii.189; Pv-a.23, Pv-a.50, Pv-a.70, Pv-a.76 etc. Def. as devaloke sambhūta DN-a.i.120; divibhavattā dibba Kp-a.227; divibhāvaṃ devattabhāva-pariyāpanna Pv-a.14<br />■ See further e.g. SN.i.105; DN.iii.146; Snp.176, Snp.641; Dhp.236, Dhp.417; Pp.60; Vism.407 (def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) Vism.423.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://osadha">osadha</a></em> magical drugs Mil.283;</li><li><em>-kāmā</em> (pl.) heavenly joys (see above) Ja.i.138 (opp. mānusakā);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cakkhuka">cakkhuka</a></em> endowed with the superhuman eye SN.ii.156; AN.i.23 AN.i.25;</li><li><em>-<em>paṇṇākāra</em></em> (dasavidha˚) the (tenfold) heavenly gift (viz. āyu, vaṇṇa etc.: see <em><a href="bword://%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81na">ṭhāna</a></em>) Dhp-a.iii.292</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> divine condition or state Pv-a.110;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://yoga">yoga</a></em> union with the gods SN.i.60;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vih%C4%81ra">vihāra</a></em> supreme condition of heart Mil.225;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sampatti">sampatti</a></em> heavenly bliss Ja.iv.3; Dhp-a.iii.292; Pv-a.16, Pv-a.30.</li></ul><p>Ved. divya = P. divya in verse (q.v.), Gr. δϊος (*Διvιος), Lat. dīus (*divios) = divine. Cp. deva</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cakkhu">cakkhu</a>, <a href="bword://cakkhumant">cakkhumant</a>, <a href="bword://sota">sota</a>, <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a>, <a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a>, <a href="bword://osadha">osadha</a>, <a href="bword://cakkhuka">cakkhuka</a>, paṇṇākāra, <a href="bword://ṭhāna">ṭhāna</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://yoga">yoga</a>, <a href="bword://vihāra">vihāra</a>, <a href="bword://sampatti">sampatti</a></p>

dibbacakkhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the divine eye; supernatural sight.</p>

dibbacakkhuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā ~ī)n. who possesses supernatural sight.</p>

dibbasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>supernatural hearing.</p>

dibbasotadhātu|dibbasotadhaatu|dibbasotadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dibbasotadhaatu</span></p><p>supernatural hearing.</p>

dibbati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>sports, amuses oneself; plays (with dice), gambles; is bright, shines; (goes; praises; is able)</p>

dibbati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to sport, to amuse oneself Vv-a.18 (in expl. of devī); to play at dice MN.ii.106 (akkhehi).</p><p>Sk. dīvyati, pp. dyūta see <em><a href="bword://j%C5%ABta">jūta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jūta">jūta</a></p>

dibbaṃ cakkhu|dibba.m cakkhu|dibbam cakkhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dibba.m cakkhu</span></p><p>the divine eye; supernatural sight.</p>

dibbā sotadhātu|dibbaa sotadhaatu|dibba sotadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dibbaa sotadhaatu</span></p><p>godlike or supernatural hearing.</p>

dibya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></p>

dicchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>wishes to give; gives.</p>

dicchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to wish to give, to be desirous of giving. SN.i.18 SN.i.20 (dicchare 3rd plural) Ja.iv.64</p><p>Sk. ditsati, Desid. fr. dadāti, base 4, q.v.</p>

diddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>smeared; poisoned.</p>

diddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>smeared Ja.v.425 sq.; esp. smeared with poison, poisoned Ja.iv.435 (sara, a poisoned arrow); perhaps to be read at Iti.68 for duṭṭha (scil. sara) and at SN.ii.230 for diṭṭha Cp. san˚.</p><p>Sk. digdha to <em>dih</em>, see <em><a href="bword://deha">deha</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://deha">deha</a></p>

diddhagada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diddhagata">diddhagata</a></p>

diddhagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>poisoned?; (smeared with poison?)</p>

dighacchā|dighacchaa|dighaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dighacchaa</span></p><p>hunger AN.ii.117.</p><p>= jighacchā</p>

dighañña|digha~n~na|dighanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">digha~n~na</span></p><p>adjective inferior, low, last, hindmost (i.e. westward) Ja.v.24 (where the Com. seems to imply a reading jighacchaṃ with meaning of 1st sg. pot. intens. of ghas, but d. is evidently the right reading), Ja.v.402, Ja.v.403 (˚rattiṃ at the end of the night).</p><p>for jighañña = Sk. jaghanya fr. janghā</p>

digucchā|digucchaa|diguccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">digucchaa</span></p><p>disgust Dhs-a.210 (asuci˚).</p><p>= jigucchā; Sk. jugupsā</p>

diguṇa|digu.na|diguna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">digu.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dviguṇa">dviguṇa</a></p>

dija
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvija">dvija</a></p>

dija
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see under <em><a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></em> B i.4.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></p>

dikkhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>initiated, consecrated, <em>cira˚</em> initiated long since SN.i.226 = Ja.v.138, Ja.v.139 (where dakkhita q.v.; Com. cira-pabbājita).</p><p>Sk. dīkṣita “having commenced the preparatory rites for sacrifice”</p>

dina
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>day Sdhp.239 <em>-duddinaṃ</em> darkness Dāvs v.50 (d. sudinaṃ ahosi, cp.i.49, cp.i.51); also as f. <em>duddinī</em> Vin.i.3.</p><p>Sk. dina; Lat. nun-dinae (*noven-dinom); Oir. denus; Goth. sin-teins; cp. divasa</p>

dindibha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>One or another of the four species of lapwing found in northern India, probably the Red-wattled Lapwing, Hindi <em>titeeri</em> or <em>titi</em>, <em>Vanellus indicus</em> Ja.vi.538, known as <em>tiṭṭibha</em> in Sanskrit. This large plover is brown above, white below, with black breast, head and neck and a white band running from the eyes down the side of the neck to the breast. It also has a bright red wattle in front of each eye. The lapwing is commonly seen in fields, open country and near water, running in short bursts as it hunts for food. At the slightest intrusion or sign of danger it makes a loud piercing “did he do it” call becoming increasingly frantic until the danger or the intruder passes. See Daṇḍamānavaka.</p>

dindibha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>kind of bird Ja.vi.538.</p><p>cp. Sk. ṭiṭṭibha?</p>

dindima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍiṇḍima">ḍiṇḍima</a></p>

dindima
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>musical instrument, a small drum Ja.vi.580; Bv.i.32. See also deṇḍima.</p><p>Sk. ḍiṇḍima, cp. dundubhi</p>

dinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) given; applied, what is given.</li><li>(n.) giving; the being given.</li></ol>

dinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>given, granted, presented etc., in all meanings of dadāti q.v.; esp. of giving alms. Pv.iv.3#26 (= mahādāna Pv-a.253) &amp; in phrase adinn’ādāna taking what is not given, i.e. stealing, adj adinnâdāyin stealing, refraining from which constitutes the 2nd sīla. ‣See under <a href="bword://s%C4%ABla">sīla</a><br />■ <em><a href="bword://dinna">dinna</a></em>: DN.i.55≈(n’ atthi dinnaṃ the heretic view of the uselessness of almsgiving) Ja.i.291 Ja.ii.128 Snp.191 Snp.227 Snp.240 Dhp.356 Pv-a.68 (given in marriage). Used as finite tense frequently e.g. Ja.i.151 Ja.i.152 Ja.vi.366<br />■ <em><a href="bword://adinna">adinna</a></em>: MN.i.39, MN.i.404; Snp.119 (theyyā adinnaṃ ādiyati), Snp.156, Snp.395, Snp.400, Snp.633; Pv-a.33 etc.</p><ul><li><em>-ādāyin</em> taking (only) what is given DN.i.4; DN-a.i.72</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://d%C4%81na">dāna</a></em> almsgiving Ja.iii.52 Dhp-a.i.396;</li><li><em>-dāyin</em> giving alms, liberal, munificent DN.iii.191</li></ul><p>Sk. dinna, pp. of dadāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sīla">sīla</a>, <a href="bword://dinna">dinna</a>, <a href="bword://adinna">adinna</a>, <a href="bword://dāna">dāna</a></p>

dinnaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an adopted son, in enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of four kinds of sons (atraja, khettaja, antevāsika, d.) Cnd.448; Ja.i.135 (= posāvanatthāya dinna).</p>

dinnapāṭikaṅkhi|dinnapaa.tika"nkhi|dinnapatikankhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dinnapaa.tika"nkhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dinnapāṭikaṅkhin">dinnapāṭikaṅkhin</a></p><p>wishing for only what is given.</p>

dinnapāṭikaṅkhin|dinnapaa.tika"nkhin|dinnapatikankhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dinnapaa.tika"nkhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dinnapāṭikaṅkhin">dinnapāṭikaṅkhin</a></p><p>wishing for only what is given.</p>

dinnādāyī|dinnaadaayii|dinnadayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dinnaadaayii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dinnādāyīn">dinnādāyīn</a></p><p>taking (only) what is given.</p>

dinnādāyīn|dinnaadaayiin|dinnadayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dinnaadaayiin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dinnādāyīn">dinnādāyīn</a></p><p>taking (only) what is given.</p>

dipada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvipada">dvipada</a></p>

dippati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>dippati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. shines, blazes; is manifest; appears clearly.</p><p>dippati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pass pr. 3 sg. of dīpeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīpeti">dīpeti</a></p>

dippati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shine, to shine forth, to be illustrious Vin.ii.285. Cp. pa˚.</p><p>Sk. dīpyate, see under *<a href="bword://d%C4%ABpa">dīpa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; cp. jotati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīpa">dīpa</a></p>

dipādaka|dipaadaka|dipadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dipaadaka</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvipādaka">dvipādaka</a></p>

dirasaññu|dirasa~n~nu|dirasannu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dirasa~n~nu</span></p><p>adjective one who has little common-sense Ja.vi.206, Ja.vi.207 Ja.vi.213, Ja.vi.214. Com. explains wrongly on p. Ja.vi.209 with “one who possesses two tongues” (of Agni), but has equivalent nippañña on p. Ja.vi.217 (text Ja.vi.214: appapañña +).</p><p>Sk. dara-saṃjña? See Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 118</p>

disa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an enemy.</p>

disa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an enemy Dhp.42, Dhp.162; Ja.iii.357; Ja.iv.217; Ja.v.453; Thag.874–Thag.6; cp. <em>Pss. Breth.</em>, 323, <em>n.</em> i.</p><p>Sk. dviṣant &amp; dviṣa (-˚); dveṣṭi &amp; dviśati to hate; cp. Gr. δεινός (corynthic δvεινία, hom. δέδvιμεν fearful; Lat. dīrus = E. dire</p>

disampati
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A king of long ago. His son was Reṇu and his chief stewards were firstly Govinda and later Jotipāla. DN.ii.230<em>f.</em></p>

disati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>grants; points out; shows.</p>

disati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to point, show; to grant, bestow etc. Usually in combination with pref. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81">ā</a></em>, or in Caus. <em><a href="bword://deseti">deseti</a></em> (q.v.). As simplex only at SN.i.217 (varaṃ disā to be read for disaṃ cp. Sk. adiśat). See also upa˚.</p><p>Ved. diśati, <em>*deik</em> to show, point towards; cp. Gr. δείκνυμι (δίκη = diśā), Lat. dico (indico, index = pointer judex), Goth. gateihan = Ger. zeigen, Ags. taecan = E token</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ā">ā</a>, <a href="bword://deseti">deseti</a></p>

disatā|disataa|disata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disataa</span></p><p>Disatā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine direction, quarter, region, part of the world Ja.iv.359; Pv.ii.9#21 (kiṃ disataṃ gato “where in the world has he gone?”); Vv.ii.3#2 (sādisatā the circle of the 6 directions, cp. Vv-a.102).</p><p>Sk. diśatā, see <em><a href="bword://dis%C4%81">disā</a></em></p><p>Disatā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine state of being an enemy, a host of enemies Ja.iv.295 (= disasamūha, variant reading as gloss: verasamoha).</p><p>Sk. *dviṣatā, see <em><a href="bword://disa">disa</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://disā">disā</a>, <a href="bword://disa">disa</a></p>

disaṅgamika|disa"ngamika|disangamika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disa"ngamika</span></p><p>intending to go elsewhere, to go to another place.</p>

disaṅgamiya|disa"ngamiya|disangamiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disa"ngamiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://disaṅgamika">disaṅgamika</a></p>

disaṅgata|disa"ngata|disangata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disa"ngata</span></p><p>who has gone elsewhere, to another place.</p>

disodisaṃ|disodisa.m|disodisam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disodisa.m</span></p><p>indeclinable hither and thither; in various locations.</p>

dissamāna|dissamaana|dissamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dissamaana</span></p><p>of dissati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dissati">dissati</a></p>

dissate
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dissati">dissati</a></p>

dissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is seen; is visible; appears; seems.</p>

dissati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Pass. of *dassati, q.v.</p>

disvā|disvaa|disva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disvaa</span></p><p>of dakkhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dakkhati">dakkhati</a></p>

disvāna|disvaana|disvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disvaana</span></p><p>of dakkhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dakkhati">dakkhati</a></p>

disvānaṃ|disvaana.m|disvanam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disvaana.m</span></p><p>of dakkhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dakkhati">dakkhati</a></p>

disā|disaa|disa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaa</span></p><p>A slave-woman of Okkāka. She was the mother of Kaṇha, ancestor of the Kaṇhāyanā-gotta. DN.i.93</p>

disā|disaa|disa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaa</span></p><p>disā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>f., pl.</p><ol><li>(f.) a direction, a cardinal point; a quarter of the sky; a region, area.</li><li>(pl.) another region, another country; abroad; elsewhere.</li></ol><p>disā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adverb in the four directions.</p>

disā|disaa|disa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaa</span></p><p>point of the compass, region quarter, direction, bearings. The 4 principal points usualy enumerated are <em>puratthimā</em> (E) <em>pacchimā</em> (W) <em><a href="bword://dakkhi%E1%B9%87%C4%81">dakkhiṇā</a></em> (S) <em><a href="bword://uttar%C4%81">uttarā</a></em> (N), in changing order. Thus at SN.i.101, SN.i.145; SN.ii.103; SN.iii.84; SN.iv.185, SN.iv.296; Cnd.302; Pv.ii.12#6 (caturo d.); Pv-a.52 (catūsu disāsu nirayo catūhi dvārehi yutto), and passim<br />■ To these are often added the two locations “above &amp; below” as <em>uparimā &amp; heṭṭhimā</em> disā (see [as uddhaṃ adho SN.iii.124 e.g. also called paṭidisā DN.iii.176](bword://as uddhaṃ adho SN.iii.124 e.g. also called paṭidisā DN.iii.176)), making in all 6 directions: DN.iii.188 sq. As a rule, however, the circle is completed by the 4 <em><a href="bword://anudis%C4%81">anudisā</a></em> (intermediate points; sometimes as vidisā: SN.i.224; SN.iii.239; DN.iii.176 etc.), making a round of 10 (dasa disā) to denote completeness, wide range &amp; all pervading comprehensiveness of states activities or other happening: Snp.719, Snp.1122 (disā catasso vidisā catasso uddhaṃ adho: dasa disā imāyo) Thig.487; Pts.ii.131; Cnd.239 (see also cātuddisa in this sense); Pv.i.11#1; Pv.ii.1#10; Vism.408. <em>sabbā</em> (all) is often substituted for 10: SN.i.75; DN.ii.15; Pv.i.2#1; Vv-a.184; Pv-a.71<br />■ <em><a href="bword://anudis%C4%81">anudisā</a></em> (sg.) is often used collectively for the 4 points in the sense of “in between,” so that the circle always implies the 10 points. Thus at SN.i.122 SN.iii.124. In other combinations as 6 abbreviated for 10 four disā plus uddhaṃ &amp; anudisaṃ at DN.i.222 = AN.iii.368 four d. + uddhaṃ adho &amp; anudisaṃ at SN.i.122; SN.iii.124; AN.iv.167. In phrase “mettāsahagatena cetasā ekaṃ disaṃ pharitvā viharati” (etc. up to 4th) the all-comprehending range of universal goodwill is further denoted by <em>uddhaṃ adho tiriyaṃ</em> etc., e.g. DN.i.250 Vb.272; see <em><a href="bword://mett%C4%81">mettā</a></em><br />■ As a set of 4 or 8 disā is also used allegorically (“set, circle”) for var. combinations viz. the 8 states of jhāna at MN.iii.222; the 4 satipaṭṭhānā etc. at Ne.121; the 4 āhārā etc. at Ne.117. See also in other applications Vin.i.50 (in meaning of “foreign country”); Vin.ii.217; SN.i.33 (abhayā), SN.i.234 (puthu˚); SN.iii.106; SN.v.216; DN.iii.197 sq.; Iti.103; Thag.874; Vv.41#6 (disāsu vissutā)<br />■ disaṃ kurute to run away Ja.v.340. <em>diso disaṃ</em> (often spelt disodisaṃ) in all directions (lit. from region to region) DN.iii.200; Ja.iii.491 Thag.615; Bv.ii.50; Pv.iii.1#6; Mil.398. But at Dhp.42 to disa (enemy), cp. Dhp-a.i.324 = coro coraṃ. See also <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1884, 82 on abl. diso = diśatah. Cp. <em><a href="bword://vidis%C4%81">vidisā</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ka">kāka</a></em> a compass-crow, i.e. a crow kept on board ship in order to search for land (cp. Fick, <em>Soc. Gl.</em> p. 173 E. Hardy, <em>Buddha</em> p. 18) Ja.iii.126, Ja.iii.267;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></em> one who knows the directions Vin.ii.217;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cakkhuka">cakkhuka</a></em> “seeing” (i.e. wise) in all directions Ja.iii.344;</li><li><em>-ḍāha</em> “sky-glow,” unusual redness of the horizon as if on fire, polar light (?) or zodiacal light (?) DN.i.10; Ja.i.374 Ja.vi.476; Mil.178; DN-a.i.95; cp. BSk. diśodāha Avs.ii.198;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pati">pati</a></em> (disampati) a king SN.i.86; Ja.vi.45;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81mokkha">pāmokkha</a></em> world-famed Ja.i.166;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81ga">bhāga</a></em> Sk. digbhāga direction, quarter Vin.ii.217;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C5%AB%E1%B8%B7ha">mūḷha</a></em> Sk. diṅmūḍha one who has lost his bearings Dpvs.ix.15;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81sika">vāsika</a></em> living in a foreign country Dhp-a.iii.176.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81sin">vāsin</a></em> = ˚vāsika Dhp-a.iv.27.</li></ul><p>Ved. diś &amp; diśā, to diśati “pointing out,” point; cp. Gr. δίκη = diśā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dakkhiṇā">dakkhiṇā</a>, <a href="bword://uttarā">uttarā</a>, as uddhaṃ adho SN.iii.124 e.g. also called paṭidisā DN.iii.176, <a href="bword://anudisā">anudisā</a>, <a href="bword://anudisā">anudisā</a>, <a href="bword://mettā">mettā</a>, <a href="bword://vidisā">vidisā</a>, <a href="bword://kāka">kāka</a>, <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a>, <a href="bword://cakkhuka">cakkhuka</a>, <a href="bword://pati">pati</a>, <a href="bword://pāmokkha">pāmokkha</a>, <a href="bword://bhāga">bhāga</a>, <a href="bword://mūḷha">mūḷha</a>, <a href="bword://vāsika">vāsika</a>, <a href="bword://vāsin">vāsin</a></p>

disādāha|disaadaaha|disadaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaadaaha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://disāḍāha">disāḍāha</a></p>

disāpāmokkha|disaapaamokkha|disapamokkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaapaamokkha</span></p><p>preeminent in the world; famed far and wide.</p>

disāḍāha|disaa.daaha|disadaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">disaa.daaha</span></p><p>glow or burning in the sky.</p>

ditta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://datta">datta</a></p>

ditta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ditta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>blazing. Dāvs v.32. Usually in cpd. āditta.</p><p>Sk. dīpta, <em>dīp</em>; cp. dīpa</p><p>Ditta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>proud, arrogant, insolent; wanton Thag.198; Ja.ii.432; Ja.iii.256 = Ja.iii.485; Ja.v.17, Ja.v.232 Ja.vi.90, Ja.vi.114.</p><p>Sk. dṛpta; cp. dappa</p>

diva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>heaven; the sky.</p>

diva
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>heaven Ja.iv.134 (˚ṃ agā); Ja.v.123 (˚ṃ patta); Pv-a.74 (˚ṃ gata)</li><li>day Snp.507 (rattindivaṃ night day); Vv-a.247 (rattindiva one night &amp; one day, i.e. 24 hrs.); Dhp-a.ii.8 (divā-divassa so early in the day) Also in divaṃ-kara, daymaker, = sun, Vv-a.307; usually as <strong>divākara</strong> (q.v.). Cp. devasika; see also <em><a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a></em>.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://santatta">santatta</a></em> heated for a whole day Ja.iv.118 (cp. divasa˚)</li></ul><p>Sk. diva (nt.), weak base diṷ (div) of strong form di̯ē (see <em><a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em>) to <em>*dei̯eṷo</em> to shine; cp. Sk. dyo heaven divā adv. by day; Lat. biduum (bi-divom) two days</p><p>See also: divākara, <a href="bword://ajja">ajja</a>, <a href="bword://santatta">santatta</a>, <a href="bword://deva">deva</a></p>

divasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i></p><p>day; day.</p>

divasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m;</i></p><p>nt. only in expression <em>satta divasāni</em> 7 days or a week Ja.iv.139; Mil.15) a day AN.i.206 (˚ṃ atināmeti); Ja.iii.52 (uposatha˚); Pv-a.31 (yāva sattadivasā a week long), Pv-a.74 (sattamo divaso) Usually in oblique cases adverbially, viz. acc. <em>divasaṃ</em> (during) one day, for one day, one day long AN.iii.304 AN.iv.317; Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.2; Dhp-a.iii.173 (taṃ d. that day) eka˚ one day Ja.i.58; Ja.iii.26; Pv-a.33, Pv-a.67<br />■ gen. <em>divasassa</em> (day) by day SN.ii.95 (rattiyā ca d. ca); Ja.v.162; DN-a.i.133<br />■ instr. <em>divasā</em> day by day Ja.iv.310; <em>divasena</em> (eka˚) on the same day Ja.i.59; sudivasena on a lucky day Ja.iv.210<br />■ loc. <em>divase</em> on a day: eka Ja.iii.391; jāta˚ on his birth-day Ja.iii.391; Ja.iv.138 dutiya˚ the next day Pv-a.12, Pv-a.13, Pv-a.17, Pv-a.31, Pv-a.80 etc.; puna id. Ja.i.278; Pv-a.19, Pv-a.38; sattame d. on the 7th day Snp.983; Mil.15; Pv-a.6; ussava˚ on the festive d. Vv-a.109; apara˚ on another day Pv-a.81. Also repeated <em>divase divase</em> day after day, every day Ja.i.87; Pv-a.3. abl. <em>divasato</em> from the day (-˚) Ja.i.50; DN-a.i.140.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara">kara</a></em> the “day-maker,” i.e. the sun (cp. divākara Vv-a.169, Vv-a.271;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81ga">bhāga</a></em> the day-part (opp. ratti˚ the night-part), day-time Mil.18 (˚ena); Pv-a.152 (˚ṃ), Pv-a.206 (˚e = divā);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://santatta">santatta</a></em> heated the livelong day SN.i.169; MN.i.453; AN.iv.70, cp. Vin.i.225; Mil.325; cp diva˚</li></ul><p>Sk. divasa; see <em><a href="bword://diva">diva</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kara">kara</a>, <a href="bword://bhāga">bhāga</a>, <a href="bword://santatta">santatta</a>, <a href="bword://diva">diva</a></p>

divasaṃ|divasa.m|divasam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">divasa.m</span></p><p>during the day.</p>

divaḍḍha|diva.d.dha|divaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diva.d.dha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diyaḍḍha">diyaḍḍha</a></p>

divi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Divi˚</p><p>an abstraction fr. divya constructed for etym. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of <em><a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></em> as <em>divi-bhava</em> (˚bhāva) of divine existence or character, a divine being, in “divi-bhavāni divyāni ettha atthī ti divyā” Snp-a.219; “divi-bhavattā dibbā ti” Kp-a.227; “divibhāvaṃ devattabhāvapariyāpanno ti dibbo” Pv-a.14.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></p>

divilla
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>musical instrument Dpvs.xvi.14.</p>

diviya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>divine; heavenly; belonging to gods; supernatural; magical, marvelous</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></p>

divya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></p>

divya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective divine Snp.153 (cp. Snp-a.219 under divi˚), Snp.524 (+ mānusaka); Ja.vi.172<br />■ (nt.) the divinity a divine being (= devatā) Ja.vi.150; Snp-a.219.</p><p>Sk. divya; the verse-form for the prose-form dibba (q.v.)</p>

divā|divaa|diva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">divaa</span></p><p>adverb &amp; feminine</p><ol><li>by day; in the day.</li><li>in the middle of the day; rather late (after the midday meal)</li></ol>

divā|divaa|diva
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">divaa</span></p><p>adverb by day SN.i.183; MN.i.125; Dhp.387; DN-a.i.251; Pv-a.43, Pv-a.142, Pv-a.206 (= divasa-bhāge) Often combined &amp; contrasted with <em>rattiṃ</em> (or ratto) by night; e.g. <em>divārattiṃ</em> by day &amp; by night SN.i.47; divā c’ eva rattiñ ca DN.ii.20; rattim pi divā pi Ja.ii.133 <em>divā ca ratto ca</em> SN.i.33; Snp.223; Dhp.296; Vv.31#4; Vv-a.128<br />■ <em>divātaraṃ</em> (compar. adv.) later on in the day MN.i.125; Ja.iii.48, Ja.iii.498<br />■ <em><a href="bword://atidiv%C4%81">atidivā</a></em> too late SN.i.200; AN.iii.117.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara">kara</a></em> (= divaṃ kara) the day-maker, the sun Thag-a.70 (Ap.v.16); Pv-a.155;</li><li><em>-divassa</em> (adv.) early in the day, at sunrise, at an early hour Vin.ii.190; SN.i.89, SN.i.91 SN.i.97; AN.v.185; MN.ii.100, MN.ii.112; Ja.ii.1; Ja.vi.31; Dhp-a.ii.8; Vv-a.239, Vv-a.242;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vih%C4%81ra">vihāra</a></em> the day-rest, i.e. rest during the heat of the day Vin.i.28, SN.i.129, SN.i.132, SN.i.146, SN.i.193 = Thag.1241; Snp.679;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81">saññā</a></em> consciousness by day, daily c DN.iii.223 = AN.ii.45;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://seyy%C4%81">seyyā</a></em> = ˚vihāra DN.i.112.</li></ul><p>Ved. divā, cp. diva</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atidivā">atidivā</a>, <a href="bword://kara">kara</a>, <a href="bword://vihāra">vihāra</a>, <a href="bword://saññā">saññā</a>, <a href="bword://seyyā">seyyā</a></p>

divādivassa|divaadivassa|divadivassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">divaadivassa</span></p><p>indeclinable in the middle of the day; rather late in the day.</p>

divārattiṃ|divaaratti.m|divarattim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">divaaratti.m</span></p><p>indeclinable by night and day.</p>

divāseyyā|divaaseyyaa|divaseyya
