dussa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>cloth; clothes.</p>

dussa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Dussa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter woven material, cloth, turban cloth; (upper) garment, clothes Vin.i.290 Vin.ii.128, Vin.ii.174; Vin.iv.159. DN.i.103; SN.v.71; MN.i.215; MN.ii.92; AN.v.347; Snp.679; Pv.i.10#3 (= uttarīyaṃ sāṭakaṃ Pv-a.49); Pv.ii.3#14; Pp.55; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.75<br />■ cīvara˚, q.v. chava˚ a miserable garment DN.i.166; AN.i.295; AN.ii.206; MN.i.78, MN.i.308.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Daka">karaṇḍaka</a></em> a clothes-chest SN.v.71 = MN.i.215; AN.iv.230</li><li><em>-koṭṭhagāra</em> a store-room for cloth or clothes Dhp-a.i.220 Dhp-a.i.393;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gaha%E1%B9%87a">gahaṇa</a></em> (-mangala) (the ceremony of) putting on a garment Dhp-a.ii.87;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81lan%C4%AB">cālanī</a></em> a cloth sieve Vin.i.202</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADa">paṭṭa</a></em> turban cloth Vin.ii.266 (= setavattha-paṭṭa Bdhgh.); SN.ii.102;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phala">phala</a></em> having clothes as fruit (of magic trees, cp. kapparukkha) Vv.46#2 (cp. Vv-a.199)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://maya">maya</a></em> consisting in clothes Vv.46#7 (cp. Vv-a.199)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://yuga">yuga</a></em> a suit of garments Vin.i.278; MN.i.215 = SN.v.71; Mil.31 (cp. Mhvs.i.61); Dhp-a.iv.11;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ratana">ratana</a></em> “a pearl of a garment,” a fine garment Mil.262.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADi">vaṭṭi</a></em> fringed cotton cloth Vin.ii.266.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ve%E1%B9%87i">veṇi</a></em> plaited cotton cloth Vin.ii.266.</li></ul><p>Sk. dūrśa &amp; dūṣya</p><p>Dussa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>at Ja.iii.54 is usually taken as = amussa (cp. amuka). C. explains as “near,” &amp; adds “asammussa.” Or is it Sk. dūṣya easily spoilt? See on this passage Andersen <em>Pali Reader</em> ii.124.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://karaṇḍaka">karaṇḍaka</a>, <a href="bword://gahaṇa">gahaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://cālanī">cālanī</a>, <a href="bword://paṭṭa">paṭṭa</a>, <a href="bword://phala">phala</a>, <a href="bword://maya">maya</a>, <a href="bword://yuga">yuga</a>, <a href="bword://ratana">ratana</a>, <a href="bword://vaṭṭi">vaṭṭi</a>, <a href="bword://veṇi">veṇi</a></p>

dussacālanī|dussacaalanii|dussacalani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dussacaalanii</span></p><p>cloth sieve.</p>

dussaddhāpaya|dussaddhaapaya|dussaddhapaya
