dvīhika|dviihika|dvihika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dviihika</span></p><p>adjective every other day MN.i.78</p>

dvīhitika|dviihitika|dvihitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dviihitika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhitika">duhitika</a></p>

dvīhitika|dviihitika|dvihitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">dviihitika</span></p><p>adjective to be gained or procured with difficulty (i.e. a livelihood which is hardly procurable), only in phrase <em>“dubbhikkhā d setaṭṭhikā salākavuttā,”</em> of a famine Vin.iii.6, Vin.iii.15, Vin.iii.87 Vin.iv.23; SN.iv.323. On the term &amp; its expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh (at Vin.iii.268: dujjīvikā īhī tī… dukkhena īhitaṃ ettha pavattatī ti) see Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>i.122<br />■ <em>Note.</em> Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is highly speculative, &amp; leaves the problem still unsolved. The case of du<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> appearing as du-(and not as dur-) before a vowel is most peculiar there may be a connection with <em>druh</em> (see <em><a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a></em>), which is even suggested by vv.ll. at SN.iv.223 as dūhitika duhitika (q.v.). Dve &amp; Dve;</p><p>du-īhitika, of du<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + īhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a></p>

dāha|daaha|daha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍāha">ḍāha</a></p>

dāha|daaha|daha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaha</span></p><p>see <em>ḍāha</em>.</p>

