dāruṇa|daaru.na|daruna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaru.na</span></p><p>(~ā ~ī)n. hard, severe; cruel, pitiless; dreadful, frightful, terrible.</p>

dāruṇa|daaru.na|daruna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaru.na</span></p><p>adjective strong, firm, severe harsh, cruel, pitiless SN.i.101; SN.ii.226; Snp.244; Dhp.139; Ja.iii.34; Pv.iv.3#6 (= ghora Pv-a.251); Mil.117 (vāta) Pv-a.24, Pv-a.52 (= ghora), Pv-a.159 (sapatha a terrible oath ghora), Pv-a.181 (= kurūrin), Pv-a.221 (˚kāraṇa); Sdhp.5, Sdhp.78, Sdhp.286.</p><p>Ved. dāruṇa, to dāru (“strong as a tree”), cp. Gr. δροόν = ἰσξυρόν Hesych; Lat. dūrus; Oir. dron (firm), Mir. dūr (hard) Ags. trum</p>

dārā|daaraa|dara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaraa</span></p><p>Dāra &amp; Dārā</p><p>feminine a young woman, esp. married woman, wife. As; <em>dārā</em> f. at Cnd.295 (d. vuccati bhariyā &amp; Iti.36; f. also <em>dārī</em> maiden, young girl Pv.i.11#5. Otherwise as <em>dāra</em> (coll-masc.): Dhp.345; Ja.i.120; Ja.ii.248 Ja.iv.7; Ja.v.104, Ja.v.288; Vv-a.299 (˚paṭiggaha)<br />■ <em>putta-dārā</em> (pl.) wife &amp; children Snp.108, Snp.262; Ja.i.262; cp. saputtadāra with w. &amp; ch. Pv.iv.3#47; putta ca dārā ca Snp.38 Snp.123. Freq. in definition of sīla No. 3 (kāmesu micchācārin or abrahmacariyā, adultery) as sakena dārena santuṭṭha AN.iii.348; AN.v.138; Snp.108 (a˚); Pv.1#2, etc- <strong>paradāra</strong> the wife of another MN.i.404 sq.; Dhp.246 Dhp.309; Snp.396 (parassa d.) Pv-a.261.</p><p>Sk. dāra (m.) &amp; dārā (f.), more freq. dārā (m.pl.); instr. sg. dārena Ja.iv.7; Pv.iv.1#77, etc. instr, pl. dārehi Snp.108 (sehi d. asantuṭṭho not satisfied with his own wife), loc. pl. dāresu Snp.38 (puttesu dāresu apekkhā), orig. “wives, womenfolk,” female members of the household = Gr. δοϋλος (slave; Hesychius δουλος = ἡ οἰκία; cp. also origin of Germ. frauenzimmer &amp; E. womanhood). Remnants of pl. use are seen in above passage. fr. Sn.</p><p>See also: paradāra</p>

dārābharaṇa|daaraabhara.na|darabharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaraabhara.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dārabharaṇa">dārabharaṇa</a></p>

dāsa|daasa|dasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasa</span></p><p>slave.</p>

dāsa|daasa|dasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasa</span></p><p>slave, often combined w. f. dāsī. Def. by Bdhgh as “antojāto” (DN-a.i.300), or as “antojātadhanakkīta-karamarânīta-sāmaṃ dāsabyaṃ upagatānaṃ aññataro” (ibid. DN-a.i.168)<br />■ In phrase <em>dāsā ca kammakarā</em> “slaves &amp; labourers” Vin.i.243, Vin.i.272; Vin.ii.154; as dāso kammakaro “a slave-servant” DN.i.60 (cp. d. kammakara)<br />■ Vin.i.72, Vin.i.76 (dāso na pabbājetabbo the slave cannot become a bhikkhu); DN.i.72; MN.ii.68 (fig. taṇhā˚); Ja.i.200, Ja.i.223; Ja.iii.343 (bought for 700 kahāpaṇas), Ja.iii.347; Pp.56; Pv-a.112.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kammakara">kammakara</a></em> (porisa) a slave-servant, an unpaid labourer, a serf Vin.i.240; AN.i.206; DN.iii.189; Dhp-a.iv.1;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87a">gaṇa</a></em> a troop of slaves Pv.iv.1#41;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://purisa">purisa</a></em> a servant Ja.i.385;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://porisa">porisa</a></em> a servant, slave Snp.769 (cp. Mnd.11 where 4 kinds of d. are mentioned);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://lakkha%E1%B9%87a">lakkhaṇa</a></em> fortunetelling from (the condition of) slaves DN.i.9.</li></ul><p>Ved. dāsa; orig. adj. meaning “non-Aryan,” i.e. slave (cp. Gr. βάρβαρος, Ger. sklave = slave); Av. dāha a Scythian tribe. Also connected w. dasyu (see dassukhīla)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kammakara">kammakara</a>, <a href="bword://gaṇa">gaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://purisa">purisa</a>, <a href="bword://porisa">porisa</a>, <a href="bword://lakkhaṇa">lakkhaṇa</a></p>

dāsabya|daasabya|dasabya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasabya</span></p><p>Dāsavya &amp; Dāsabya</p><p>neuter the condition of a slave, slavery, serfdom DN.i.73; MN.i.275 (b) Ja.i.226; DN-a.i.168 (b), DN-a.i.213; Dhp-a.iii.35; Pv-a.112, Pv-a.152.</p><p>cp. Sk. dāsya</p>

dāsabyatā|daasabyataa|dasabyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasabyataa</span></p><p>= dāsavya Sdhp.498. Dasavya &amp; Dasabya;</p>

dāsaka|daasaka|dasaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasaka</span></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse says that one who eats much and is lazy will keep being reborn. Thag.17</p><p>Perhaps it is this same Dāsaka who is mentioned in the Saṃyutta Nikāya SN.iii.127<em>ff.</em> as having been sent by the monks of Kosambī to Khemaka, carrying messages to and fro till he had walked up and down over two yojanas.</p>

dāsaka|daasaka|dasaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasaka</span></p><p>= dāsa in <em>-putta</em> a slave, of the sons of the slaves, mentioned as one of the sipp’ āyatanas at DN.i.51 (expl. by Bdhgh as balavasinehā-gharadāsa-yodhā DN-a.i.157)<br />■ <em>sadāsaka</em> with slaves, followed by slaves Vv.32#4<br />■ f. <em>dāsikā</em> a female slave (= dāsī) MN.i.126; Ja.vi.554.</p>

dāsakammakarā|daasakammakaraa|dasakammakara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasakammakaraa</span></p><p>slaves and servants.</p>

dāsakaputta|daasakaputta|dasakaputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasakaputta</span></p><p>retainer, a vassal.</p>

dāsaporisa|daasaporisa|dasaporisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasaporisa</span></p><p>slaves and serving-men.</p>

dāsaviya|daasaviya|dasaviya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasaviya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāsavya">dāsavya</a></p>

dāsavya|daasavya|dasavya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasavya</span></p><p>slavery; servitude; the condition of being a slave.</p>

dāsavya|daasavya|dasavya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasavya</span></p><p>Dāsavya &amp; Dāsabya</p><p>neuter the condition of a slave, slavery, serfdom DN.i.73; MN.i.275 (b) Ja.i.226; DN-a.i.168 (b), DN-a.i.213; Dhp-a.iii.35; Pv-a.112, Pv-a.152.</p><p>cp. Sk. dāsya</p>

dāsi|daasi|dasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasi</span></p><p>female slave.</p>

dāsidāsa|daasidaasa|dasidasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasidaasa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāsīdāsa">dāsīdāsa</a></p>

dāsima|daasima|dasima
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasima</span></p><p>A type of plant Ja.vi.536.</p>

dāsima|daasima|dasima
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasima</span></p><p>species of tree Ja.vi.536.</p>

dāsiputta|daasiputta|dasiputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasiputta</span></p><p>the son of a female slave.</p>

dāsitta|daasitta|dasitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasitta</span></p><p>the status of a (female) slave Mil.158.</p><p>Sk. dāsītva</p>

dāsiyā|daasiyaa|dasiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasiyaa</span></p><p>= dāsikā, a female slave Ja.vi.554.</p>

dāsī|daasii|dasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasii</span></p><p>female slave; a concubine.</p>

dāsī|daasii|dasi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasii</span></p><p>female servant, a handmaiden a slave-girl Vin.i.217, Vin.i.269, Vin.i.291; Vin.ii.10 (kula˚), Vin.ii.78, Vin.iii.161; MN.i.125; MN.ii.62 (ñāti˚); Pv.ii.3#21 (ghara˚); Pv-a.46, Pv-a.61, Pv-a.65<br />■ Cp. kumbha˚.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87a">gaṇa</a></em> a troop of slave-girls Ja.ii.127;</li><li><em>-dāsā</em> (pl.) maid&amp; man-servants Dhp-a.i.187; freq. to cpd. d-d-<em>paṭiggahaṇa</em> slave-trading DN.i.5≈(cp. DN-a.i.78);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://putta">putta</a></em> the son of a slave, an abusive term (gharadāsiyā va putto Dhp.i.257; cp. Sk. dāsīsuta) DN.i.93 (˚vāda);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhoga">bhoga</a></em> the possessions of a slave Vin.iii.136.</li></ul><p>Sk. dāsī, cp. dāsa. Nom. pl. dasso for dāsiyo Ja.iv.53; in compounds dāsi˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gaṇa">gaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://putta">putta</a>, <a href="bword://bhoga">bhoga</a></p>

dāsīdāsa|daasiidaasa|dasidasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daasiidaasa</span></p><p>male and female slaves.</p>

dāta|daata|data
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daata</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dātar">dātar</a></p><p>one who gives, a giver (+ gen. or acc.)</p>

dātabba|daatabba|databba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatabba</span></p><p>to be given; to be imposed; one must give; there must be giving.</p>

dātar|daatar|datar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dātar">dātar</a></p><p>one who gives, a giver (+ gen. or acc.)</p>

dātar|daatar|datar
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatar</span></p><p>giver, a generous person Pgdp.50<br />■ <em>adātā</em> one who does not give, a miser Pv.ii.8#2; otherwise as na dātā (hoti) AN.ii.203; Iti.65.</p><p>Sk. dāṭr, n. ag. of dadāti to give; cp. Gr. δώτωρ &amp; δοτήρ</p>

dātave|daatave|datave
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatave</span></p><p>of dassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dassati">dassati</a></p>

dātta|daatta|datta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatta</span></p><p>sickle, scythe Mil.33.</p><p>Sk. dātra, to <em>dā</em>, Sk. dāti, dyati to cut, divide, deal out; cp. Gr. δατέομαι, δαίομαι &amp; see <em><a href="bword://d%C4%81na">dāna</a></em>, dāpeti dāyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāna">dāna</a></p>

dātuye|daatuye|datuye
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatuye</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dātave">dātave</a></p>

dātuṃ|daatu.m|datum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daatu.m</span></p><p>of dassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dassati">dassati</a></p>

dāva|daava|dava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daava</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāya">dāya</a></p>

dāva|daava|dava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daava</span></p><p>in <em>-aggi</em> a jungle-fire Ja.i.213; Ja.iii.140; Vism.470; Dhp-a.i.281.</p><p>Sk. dāva, see *<a href="bword://dava">dava</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; daya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dava">dava</a></p>

dāvika|daavika|davika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daavika</span></p><p>adjective in piṇḍa˚, a cert. rank in the army (variant reading piṇḍa-dāyika) DN.i.51 = Mil.331 (DN-a.i.156: sāhasikamahāyodhā etc., with popular expl. of the terms piṇḍa &amp; davayati).</p>

dāya|daaya|daya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaya</span></p><p>forest; a park.</p>

dāya|daaya|daya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daaya</span></p><p>Dāya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>wood jungle, forest; a grove Vin.i.10 (miga˚), Vin.i.15, Vin.i.350; Vin.ii.138; SN.ii.152 (tiṇa˚); SN.iv.189 (bahukaṇṭaka d. = jungle) AN.v.337 (tiṇa˚); Ja.iii.274; Ja.vi.278. See also dāva.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81la">pāla</a></em> a grove keeper Vin.i.350; MN.i.205.</li></ul><p>Sk. dāva, conflagration of a forest; wood = easily inflammable substance; to dunoti (to burn) caus dāvayati, cp. Gr. δαίω (to burn) &amp; P. dava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Dāya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a gift, donation; share, fee DN.i.87≈(in phrase rājadāya brahmadeyya, a king’s grant, cp. rājadattiya); Ja.iv.138; Ja.v.363; Ja.vi.346. Cp dāyāda &amp; brahmadeyya.</p><p>Sk. dāya, to dadāti, etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pāla">pāla</a></p>

dāyajja|daayajja|dayajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayajja</span></p><ol><li>(n.) inheritance.</li><li>(m.) an heir.</li></ol>

dāyajja|daayajja|dayajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayajja</span></p><p>inheritance Vin.i.82; DN.iii.189; AN.iii.43; Ja.i.91; Vism.43 sq.; dowry Ja.iii.8<br />■ (adj.) one who inherits Vin.iii.66 (pituno of the father).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://upasampad%C4%81">upasampadā</a></em>, lit. the Upasampadā by way of inh., a particular form of ordination conferred on Sumana Sopāka, both novices seven yrs. old Dhp-a.iv.137.</li></ul><p>Sk. dāyādya; see <em><a href="bword://d%C4%81y%C4%81da">dāyāda</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampadā">upasampadā</a>, <a href="bword://dāyāda">dāyāda</a></p>

dāyaka|daayaka|dayaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. &amp; m.f. who gives, bestows; a donor, a benefactor.</p>

dāyaka|daayaka|dayaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaka</span></p><p>Dāyaka &amp; Dāyikā</p><p>adjective &amp; feminine giving, bestowing, distributing, providing (usually—˚); (n.) a donor, benefactor; a munificent person MN.i.236<em>f.</em> AN.i.26 AN.i.161 AN.ii.64 AN.ii.80 AN.iii.32 AN.iii.336 AN.iv.81 Snp.p. 87 Iti.19 (ito cutā manussattā saggaṁ gacchanti dāyakā) Ja.v.129 (kaṇḍa˚) Pv.i.1#1<em>f.</em>; Pv.i.1#2; Pv.i.4#2; Pv.i.5#5 DN-a.i.298 Pv-a.113 (= dada) Mil.258 (˚ānaṁ dakkhiṇā); Sdhp.276<br />■ feminine*<a href="bword://d%C4%81yik%C4%81">dāyikā</a>* Vin.ii.216 (bhikkhā˚), Vin.ii.289 (khīrassa)<br />■ <em>adāyaka</em> a stingy person, one who neglects almsgiving (cp. adānasīla) Pv.i.11#9; ˚ikā Pv.i.9#3</p><p>Sk. dāyaka, <em>dā</em> as in dadāti &amp; dāna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāyikā">dāyikā</a></p>

dāyana|daayana|dayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayana</span></p><p>cutting; <em>-agga</em> the first of what has been cut (on fields) Dhp-a.i.98; <em>-atthaṃ</em> for the purpose of mowing Dhp-a.iii.285.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://dayati">dayati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dayati">dayati</a></p>

dāyapāla|daayapaala|dayapala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayapaala</span></p><p>the keeper or guardian of a park.</p>

dāyati|daayati|dayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayati</span></p><p>to cut, mow, reap, caus. dāyāpeti to cause to be cut or mowed Dhp-a.iii.285.</p><p>Sk. dāti &amp; dyāti; <em>(dā)</em> to cut, divide, etc.; cp. dayati, dātta, dāna</p>

dāyika|daayika|dayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayika</span></p><p>adjective = dāyaka Pv-a.157; Sdhp.211, Sdhp.229. -Dayin</p>

dāyikā|daayikaa|dayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayikaa</span></p><p>Dāyaka &amp; Dāyikā</p><p>adjective &amp; feminine giving, bestowing, distributing, providing (usually—˚); (n.) a donor, benefactor; a munificent person MN.i.236<em>f.</em> AN.i.26 AN.i.161 AN.ii.64 AN.ii.80 AN.iii.32 AN.iii.336 AN.iv.81 Snp.p. 87 Iti.19 (ito cutā manussattā saggaṁ gacchanti dāyakā) Ja.v.129 (kaṇḍa˚) Pv.i.1#1<em>f.</em>; Pv.i.1#2; Pv.i.4#2; Pv.i.5#5 DN-a.i.298 Pv-a.113 (= dada) Mil.258 (˚ānaṁ dakkhiṇā); Sdhp.276<br />■ feminine*<a href="bword://d%C4%81yik%C4%81">dāyikā</a>* Vin.ii.216 (bhikkhā˚), Vin.ii.289 (khīrassa)<br />■ <em>adāyaka</em> a stingy person, one who neglects almsgiving (cp. adānasīla) Pv.i.11#9; ˚ikā Pv.i.9#3</p><p>Sk. dāyaka, <em>dā</em> as in dadāti &amp; dāna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāyikā">dāyikā</a></p>

dāyin|daayin|dayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayin</span></p><p>-Dāyin</p><p>adjective giving, granting, bestowing. Pv-a.121 (icchit’ icchita˚), Pv-a.157 (= kāma dada); Sdhp.214 (dānagga˚).</p><p>Sk. dāyin, of dadāti</p>

dāyāda|daayaada|dayada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaada</span></p><p>one who inherits; an heir.</p>

dāyāda|daayaada|dayada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaada</span></p><p>heir MN.i.86 = Cnd.199; SN.i.69, SN.i.90; SN.iv.72; AN.iii.72 sq.; Ja.iii.181; Ja.vi.151; Kp.viii.5. Often fig. with <em>kamma˚</em>; one who inherits his own deeds (see <em><a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> 3 A <em>b</em> &amp; compounds): MN.i.390 sq.; AN.v.289; &amp; as; <em>dhamma˚</em>; (spiritual heir) opposed to āmisa (material h.): MN.i.12; Iti.101; also as dhamma˚ DN.iii.84 as <em>brahma˚</em>; MN.ii.84; DN.iii.83<br />■ <em>adāyāda</em> not having an heir SN.i.69; Ja.v.267. See dāyajja &amp; dāyādaka.</p><p>Sk. dāyāda = dāya + ā-da receiving the (son’s) portion, same formation on ground of sam̊e idea as Lat heres = *ghero + ē-do receiver of what is left: see Brugmann <em>Album Kern</em> p. 29 sq.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

dāyādaka|daayaadaka|dayadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaadaka</span></p><p>an heir.</p>

dāyādaka|daayaadaka|dayadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaadaka</span></p><p>heir MN.ii.73; Thag.781, Thag.1142; f. <em>-ikā</em> Thig.327 (= dāyajjarahā Thag-a.234).</p><p>= dāyāda</p>

dāyādikā|daayaadikaa|dayadika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daayaadikaa</span></p><p>an heiress.</p>

dāḍima|daa.dima|dadima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.dima</span></p><p>the pomegranate tree; saf: punica granatum l.</p>

dāḷiddiya|daa.liddiya|daliddiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.liddiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāliddiya">dāliddiya</a></p>

dāḷiddiya|daa.liddiya|daliddiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.liddiya</span></p><p>Dāḷiddiya &amp; daḷiddiya</p><p>(nt.) poverty DN.iii.65, DN.iii.66; AN.iii.351 sq.; Ja.i.228; Dāvs ii.60; Sdhp.78.</p><p>Sk. *dāridrya</p>

dāṭha|daa.tha|datha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.tha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāṭhā">dāṭhā</a></p>

dāṭhabala|daa.thabala|dathabala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thabala</span></p><p>strong-toothed; whose strength is his teeth.</p>

dāṭhi|daa.thi|dathi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dāṭhin">dāṭhin</a></p><p>having large teeth or fangs; having tusks.</p>

dāṭhikā|daa.thikaa|dathika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thikaa</span></p><p>whiskers; mustache.</p>

dāṭhikā|daa.thikaa|dathika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thikaa</span></p><p>beard, whiskers Vin.ii.134 (na d. ṭhapetabbā, of the bhikkhus) Ja.i.305; Ja.v.42 (tamba˚), Ja.v.217 (mahā˚ having great whiskers); DN-a.i.263 (parūḷha-massu˚ with beard whiskers grown long).</p><p>Sk. *dāḍhikā = Prk. for daṃṣṭrikā</p>

dāṭhin|daa.thin|dathin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dāṭhin">dāṭhin</a></p><p>having large teeth or fangs; having tusks.</p>

dāṭhin|daa.thin|dathin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thin</span></p><p>adjective having tusks Ja.ii.245; Ja.iv.348; Thag p.1; Sdhp.286.</p><p>cp. Sk. daṃṣṭrin</p>

dāṭhā|daa.thaa|datha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thaa</span></p><p>fang; the canine tooth.</p>

dāṭhā|daa.thaa|datha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thaa</span></p><p>large tooth, fang, tusk; as adj. (-˚) having tusks or fangs DN.ii.18 (susukkha˚); Ja.i.505 (uddhaṭa-dāṭho viya sappo); Ja.iv.245 (nikkhanta˚); Dhp-a.i.215; Pv-a.152 (kaṭhina˚); Sdhp.286.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81vudha">āvudha</a></em> [Sk. daṃṣṭrāyudha] using a tusk as his weapon Ja.v.172;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://danta">danta</a></em> a canine tooth Kp-a.44;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://balin">balin</a></em> one whose strength lies in his teeth (of a lion) Snp.72.</li></ul><p>Sk. daṃṣṭrā to ḍasati (q.v.), cp. also daṭṭha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvudha">āvudha</a>, <a href="bword://danta">danta</a>, <a href="bword://balin">balin</a></p>

dāṭhābal|daa.thaabal|dathabal
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thaabal</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dāṭhābaln">dāṭhābaln</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāṭhabala">dāṭhabala</a></p>

dāṭhābaln|daa.thaabaln|dathabaln
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">daa.thaabaln</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dāṭhābaln">dāṭhābaln</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāṭhabala">dāṭhabala</a></p>

dīgha|diigha|digha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigha</span></p><p>long (in space or time)</p>

dīgha|diigha|digha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigha</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) long DN.i.17; MN.i.429; SN.i.104 (˚ṃ addhānaṃ); Snp.146, Snp.633 (opp. rassa); Dhp.60 Dhp.409; Pv.i.10#11 (˚ṃ antaraṃ all the time); Pv.ii.9#55 (id.) Thag.646 (˚m-antare); Dhs.617; Kp-a.245; Pv-a.27, Pv-a.28 Pv-a.33, Pv-a.46. See def. at Vism.272<br />■ dīghato lengthways Ja.vi.185; dīghaso in length Vin.iv.279; atidīgha too long Vin.iv.7, Vin.iv.8.</li><li>(m.) a snake (cp. Mvu.ii.45 dīrghaka) Ja.i.324; Ja.ii.145; Ja.iv.330.</li><li>Name of the Dīgha Nikāya (“the long collection”) Vism.96.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-aṅgulin</em> having long fingers (the 4th of the marks of a Mahāpurisa) DN.ii.17; DN.iii.143, DN.iii.150;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://antara">antara</a></em> corridor Ja.vi.349.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81yu">āyu</a></em> long-lived (opp. app’ āyu) DN.i.18; Ja.v.71. Also as <em>-ka</em> DN.iii.150; DN-a.i.135; Sdhp.511</li><li><em>-āvu</em> = ˚āyu in the meaning of āyasmant (q.v.) Ja.v.120</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://j%C4%81ti">jāti</a></em> (f.) a being of the snake kind, a snake Dhp-a.iii.322 also as <em>˚ka</em> at Ja.ii.145; Ja.iii.250; Ja.iv.333; Ja.v.449; DN-a.i.252;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a></em> having long fringes DN.i.7;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a></em> [Sk dīrghadarśin] far-seeing (= sabba-dassāvin) Pv-a.196</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81sika">nāsika</a></em> having a long nose Vism.283.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81%E1%B9%87aka">bhāṇaka</a></em> a repeater or expounder of the Dīgha Nikāya Ja.i.59 Vism.36, Vism.266, Vism.286; DN-a.i.15, DN-a.i.131;</li><li><em>-rattaṃ</em> (adv.) [Sk *dīrgharātraṃ, see Indexes to Avs; Divy &amp; Lal. V. otherwise dīrgha-kālaṃ] a long time DN.i.17, DN.i.206; AN.v.194; Snp.649; Iti.8; Ja.i.12, Ja.i.72; Pv.i.4#4; Pv.ii.13#11 (˚rattāya = ˚rattaṃ Pv-a.165); Pp.15; Dhp-a.iv.24;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://loma">loma</a></em> long-haired Vin.iii.129; also as <em>-ka</em> at Ja.i.484, f. ˚ikā SN.ii.228;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sotthiya">sotthiya</a></em> (nt.) long welfare or prosperity Dhp-a.ii.227.</li></ul><p>Ved. dīrgha, cp. Caus. drāghayati to lengthen, <em>*dlāgh</em> as in Gr. δολιξός (shaft), ἐνδελεξής (lasting etc.; cp. E. entelechy); Lat. indulges; Goth tulgus (enduring)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://antara">antara</a>, <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a>, <a href="bword://jāti">jāti</a>, <a href="bword://dasa">dasa</a>, <a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a>, <a href="bword://nāsika">nāsika</a>, <a href="bword://bhāṇaka">bhāṇaka</a>, <a href="bword://loma">loma</a>, <a href="bword://sotthiya">sotthiya</a></p>

dīghajāṇu|diighajaa.nu|dighajanu
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighajaa.nu</span></p><p>A Koliyan, inhabitant of Kakkarapatta. There he visited the Buddha and asked for a teaching which would bring happiness both in this world and the next. The Buddha explained to him the four conditions of temporal welfare and the four conditions of spiritual welfare. In this discourse the Buddha addresses him as Byagghapajja. AN.iv.281<em>ff.</em></p>

dīghakārāyana|diighakaaraayana|dighakarayana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighakaaraayana</span></p><p>Nephew of Bandhula, commander-in-chief of Pasenadi. After Bandhula’s death, Dīgha was appointed in his place and accompanied the king. Once, during their travels, they came to Nangaraka and, hearing that the Buddha was at Medatalumpa, went to see him, but Dīgha stayed outside while the Buddha talked to Pasenadi. MN.ii.118<em>f.</em></p>

dīghalaṭṭhi|diighala.t.thi|dighalatthi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighala.t.thi</span></p><p>A <em>devaputta</em> who once visited the Buddha at the Kalandakanivāpa in Veḷuvana and spoke a verse. SN.i.52</p>

dīghalomaka|diighalomaka|dighalomaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighalomaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. long haired.</p>

dīghanakha|diighanakha|dighanakha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighanakha</span></p><p>A wanderer. He visited the Buddha at Sūkarakhatalena and the Buddha preached to him the Dīghanakha Sutta, at the end of which he became a sotāpanna. In the sutta he is addressed as Aggivessana. MN.i.497<em>f.</em></p>

dīghanikāya|diighanikaaya|dighanikaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighanikaaya</span></p><p>the group of long discourses (one of the divisions of the Suttapiṭaka of the Pāḷi canon)</p>

dīghapiṭṭhika|diighapi.t.thika|dighapitthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine (mfn.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighapi.t.thika</span></p><p>long backed; (m.) snake.</p>

dīgharattassa|diigharattassa|digharattassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigharattassa</span></p><p>adverb for a long time.</p>

dīgharattaṃ|diigharatta.m|digharattam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigharatta.m</span></p><p>adverb for a long time.</p>

dīgharattāya|diigharattaaya|digharattaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigharattaaya</span></p><p>adverb for a long time.</p>

dīghaso|diighaso|dighaso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaso</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīghato">dīghato</a></p>

dīghatapassī|diighatapassii|dighatapassi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighatapassii</span></p><p>A Nigaṇṭha, follower of Nāṭaputta. He once visited the Buddha at Nāḷandā, and there followed a discussion on actions and their efficiency. Dīghatapassī reported this discussion to Nāṭaputta and Upāli, who was listening, went, against the advice of Dīghatapassī, to the Buddha and challenged him to a discussion, at the end of which Upāli was converted. MN.i.371<em>ff.</em></p>

dīghato|diighato|dighato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighato</span></p><p>in length; lengthways.</p>

dīghatta|diighatta|dighatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighatta</span></p><p>length; the being long.</p>

dīghatta|diighatta|dighatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighatta</span></p><p>length AN.i.54.</p><p>Sk. dīrghatvaṃ</p>

dīghaṃ|diigha.m|digham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigha.m</span></p><p>long.</p>

dīghaṅguli|diigha"nguli|dighanguli
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diigha"nguli</span></p><p>having long fingers (one of the 32 characteristics of a mahapurisa)</p>

dīghānusayi|diighaanusayi|dighanusayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaanusayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dīghānusayin">dīghānusayin</a></p><p>persistent for a long time.</p>

dīghānusayin|diighaanusayin|dighanusayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaanusayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dīghānusayin">dīghānusayin</a></p><p>persistent for a long time.</p>

dīghānusayita|diighaanusayita|dighanusayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaanusayita</span></p><p>persistent for a long time.</p>

dīghāyu|diighaayu|dighayu
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaayu</span></p><p>DīghāyuDīghāvu</p><p>Son of Dīghīti, king of Kosala. After his parents had been cruelly murdered by Brahmadatta, king of Kāsi, he became the attendant of this kind; in order to avenge their death, but when the occasion arose he made peace with Brahmadatta. His father’s kingdom was restored to him, and he married Brahmadatta’s daughter. Vin.i.343<em>ff.</em></p>

dīghāyuka|diighaayuka|dighayuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighaayuka</span></p><p>( ~ī)n. long lived.</p>

dīghīti|diighiiti|dighiti
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diighiiti</span></p><p>DīghītiDīghati</p><p>A king of Kosala. His kingdom was captured by Brahmadatta, king of Kāsi, and he and his wife fled to Benares, where they lived in disguise in the house of a potter. His wife bore a son named Dīghāyu. Before his birth, his mother had a craving to see the army drawn up in battle array, its swords tempered. Dīghīti appeased her craving with the help of his friend, the chaplain of Brahmadatta. Dīghāyu was sent away from the city lest harm should befall him, Later, Dīghīti’s place of refuge was discovered and he and his wife were being led to the place of execution when Dīghāyu, who was on a visit to the city, saw them. Dīghīti, recognising his son, called to him his dying advice “look not too far nor too near”. Dīghāyu heard and understood; he entered the service of Brahmadatta, first as an elephant-trainer, then as a musician, and finally as his personal servant. He wished to avenge the death of his parents, but when the occasion arose, he remembered his father’s instructions and desisted. Vin.i.342<em>ff.</em></p>

dīna|diina|dina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diina</span></p><p>adjective poor, miserable, wretched; base, mean, low DN.ii.202 (?) (˚māna; variant reading ninnamāna) Ja.v.448; Ja.vi.375; Pv.ii.8#2 (= adānajjhāsaya Pv-a.107), Pv.iv.8#1; Mil.406; Pv-a.120 (= kapaṇa), Pv-a.260 (id.), Pv-a.153 Sdhp.188, Sdhp.324.</p><p>Sk. dīna</p>

dīnamana|diinamana|dinamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diinamana</span></p><p>distressed in mind; dejected.</p>

dīnatta|diinatta|dinatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diinatta</span></p><p>wretchedness, miserable state Sdhp.78.</p><p>Sk. *dīnatvaṃ</p>

dīpa|diipa|dipa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipa</span></p><p>dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a lamp, a light.</p><p>dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter</p><ol><li>an island; a shelter, a place of refuge.</li><li>a division of the terrestrial world, one of the four continents.</li><li>the island of Sri Laṅkā.</li></ol>

dīpa|diipa|dipa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipa</span></p><p>Dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a lamp Ja.ii.104 (˚ṃ jāleti to light a l.); Dhp-a.ii.49 (id.), Dhp-a.ii.94 (id.)</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://acci">acci</a></em> the flame of a lamp Thag-a.154;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81loka">āloka</a></em> light of a l. Ja.i.266; Ja.vi.391; Dhp-a.i.359; Vv-a.51;</li><li><em>(-ṃ)kara</em> making light, shining, illuminating Cnd.399 (= pabhaṃ kara Snp.1136; but cp. Dhp.236 under dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); Vism.203</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://tittira">tittira</a></em> a decoy partridge (cp. dīpaka˚) Ja.iii.64;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a></em> lit. lamp-tree, the stand of a lamp, candlestick Dhp-a.iv.120;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sikh%C4%81">sikhā</a></em> the flame (lit. crest) of a l. Vism.171; Dhp-a.ii.49.</li></ul><p>Ved. dīpa to Ved. <em>dī</em>, dīpyate; Idg. *dei̯ā to shine (see <em><a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a></em>, deva); cp. Gr. δίαλος, δ ̈ηλος; see also <em><a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a></em></p><p>Dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine &amp; neuter an island continent (mahā˚, always as 4); terra firma, solid foundation, resting-place, shelter, refuge (in this sense freq. combined w. tāṇa lena &amp; saraṇa &amp; expl. in Com by patiṭṭhā)-</p><ol><li>lit. island: SN.v.219; Ja.iii.187; Vv-a.19; Mhvs.vii.7, Mhvs.vii.41<br />■ continent: cattāro mahādīpā SN.v.343; Vv.20#10 (= Vv-a.104); Vv-a.19; Pv-a.74 etc. Opp. the 2000 paritta-dīpā the smaller islands Kp-a.133</li><li>fig. shelter, salvation etc. (see also <em><a href="bword://t%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">tāṇa</a></em>): SN.iii.42 (atta˚ + attasaraṇa etc., not with S Index to dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); SN.v.154, SN.v.162 (id.), SN.iv.315 (maṃ˚, not to dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), SN.v.372; AN.i.55 sq. (+ tāṇa etc.); Snp.501 (atta˚ selfreliant self-supported, not with Fausböll to dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), Snp.1092, Snp.1094, Snp.1145 (= Satthā); Cnd.303; Dhp.236 (˚ṃ karohi = patiṭṭhā Pv-a.87); Pv.iii.1#9 (id. Pv-a.174) Ja.v.501 = Ja.vi.375 (dīpañ ca parāyaṇaṃ); Mil.84, Mil.257 (dhamma-dīpa, Arahantship).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81laya">ālaya</a></em> resting place Ja.vi.432;</li><li><em>-gabbhaka</em> same Ja.vi.459, Ja.vi.460.</li></ul><p>Ved. dvīpa = dvi + ap (*sp.) of āpa water, lit. “double-watered,” between (two) waters</p><p>Dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a car covered with a panther’s skin Ja.i.259; Ja.v.259 = Ja.vi.48.</p><p>cp. Sk. dvīpa tiger’s skin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://acci">acci</a>, <a href="bword://āloka">āloka</a>, <a href="bword://tittira">tittira</a>, <a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a>, <a href="bword://sikhā">sikhā</a>, <a href="bword://dibba">dibba</a>, <a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a>, <a href="bword://tāṇa">tāṇa</a>, <a href="bword://ālaya">ālaya</a></p>

dīpaka|diipaka|dipaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipaka</span></p><p>Dīpaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(= dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->)</p><ol><li>f. <em>dīpikā</em> a lamp, in daṇḍa˚ a torch Dhp-a.i.220, Dhp-a.i.399, -</li><li>(˚-) an image of, having the appearance of, sham etc.; in <em>-<a href="bword://kakkara">kakkara</a></em> a decoy partridge Ja.ii.161; <em>-<a href="bword://tittira">tittira</a></em> same Ja.iii.358; <em>-<a href="bword://pakkhin">pakkhin</a></em> a decoy bird Ja.v.376; <em>-<a href="bword://miga">miga</a></em> a d. antelope Ja.v.376.</li></ol><p>Dīpaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(= dīpa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) a (little) island Ja.i.278, Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.160.</p><p>Dīpaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>in vaṇidīpaka Pv-a.120 for vanibbaka (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kakkara">kakkara</a>, <a href="bword://tittira">tittira</a>, <a href="bword://pakkhin">pakkhin</a>, <a href="bword://miga">miga</a></p>

dīpana|diipana|dipana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipana</span></p><p>adjective illustrating, explaining; f. <em>-ī</em> explanation, commentary, Name of several Commentaries, e.g. the Paramattha-dīpanī of Dhammapāla on Th 2; Pv Vv<br />■ Cp. jotikā &amp; uddīpanā.</p>

dīpayati|diipayati|dipayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīpeti">dīpeti</a></p>

dīpenta|diipenta|dipenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipenta</span></p><p>(~entī)n. of dipeti</p><p>See also: dipeti</p>

dīpeti|diipeti|dipeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipeti</span></p><p>reveals, makes manifest; declares, proclaims; explains, makes clear; illustrates.</p>

dīpeti|diipeti|dipeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipeti</span></p><p>to make light, to kindle, to emit light, to be bright; to illustrate, explain AN.v.73 sq.; Dhp.363; Mil.40; Pv-a.94, Pv-a.95, Pv-a.102, Pv-a.104 etc.; Sdhp.49, Sdhp.349. Cp. ā˚.</p><p>Sk. dīpayati, Caus. to <em>dīp</em>, see *<a href="bword://d%C4%ABpa">dīpa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; cp. dippati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīpa">dīpa</a></p>

dīpi|diipi|dipi
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipi</span></p><p>Leopard, sometimes also <em>dīpika</em> or <em><a href="bword://sadd%C5%ABla">saddūla</a></em>, Hindi <em>sadul</em>, Gujarati <em>dipdo</em>, <em>Panthera pardus</em> A.ii.193 A.iii.101 Ja.i.132 Ja.ii.44 Ja.iv.475. This large cat has a yellow coat marked with black rosettes and a white underside. Nocturnal and solitary by habit, leopards live in thick jungle and lurk around villages where they prey on livestock and sometimes kill and even eat humans. Black leopards, called panthers, are sometimes born in the same litter with the normal spotted ones. The monk Tāḷaputa described himself as living in a forest grove “resounding with the cries of peacocks and herons and favored by leopards and tigers” Th.1113. Royal chariots were upholstered with leopard or tiger skins Ja.vi.503. Leopards are now almost extinct in northern India. See <em>Kālasīha</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://saddūla">saddūla</a>, Kālasīha</p>

dīpi|diipi|dipi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dīpin">dīpin</a></p><p>panther; a leopard.</p>

dīpika|diipika|dipika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipika</span></p><p>panther Ja.iii.480.</p><p>fr. dīpin</p>

dīpikā|diipikaa|dipika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipikaa</span></p><p>covered in panther or leopard skin.</p>

dīpin|diipin|dipin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dīpin">dīpin</a></p><p>panther; a leopard.</p>

dīpin|diipin|dipin
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipin</span></p><p>panther, leopard, tiger Vin.i.186 dīpicamma a leopard skin = Sk. dvīpicarman); AN.iii.101; Ja.i.342; Ja.ii.44, Ja.ii.110; Ja.iv.475; Ja.v.408; Ja.vi.538. dīpi-rājā king of the panthers Vism.270<br />■ f. <strong>dīpinī</strong> Mil.363 Mil.368; Dhp-a.i.48.</p><p>Sk. dvīpin</p><p>See also: dīpinī</p>

dīpita|diipita|dipita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipita</span></p><p>explained Vism.33.</p><p>pp. of dīpeti</p>

dīpitar|diipitar|dipitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diipitar</span></p><p>one who illumines Vism.211.</p><p>n. ag. fr. dīpeti</p>

dīyamāna|diiyamaana|diyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diiyamaana</span></p><p>of dīyati being given</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīyati">dīyati</a></p>

dīyati|diiyati|diyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diiyati</span></p><p>gives; makes a gift; yields; applies.</p>

dīyittha|diiyittha|diyittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">diiyittha</span></p><p>of dīyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīyati">dīyati</a></p>

dūbha FIXME double|duubha FIXME double|dubha FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubha FIXME double</span></p><p>Dūbha</p><p>adjective deceiving, see <em><a href="bword://dubbha">dubbha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dubbha">dubbha</a></p>

dūbha|duubha|dubha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dubbha">dubbha</a></p>

dūbha|duubha|dubha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubha</span></p><p>Dubbha &amp; Dūbha</p><p>adjective deceiving, hurting, trying to injure Vin.ii.203 (= Iti.86 where dubbhe); Pv.ii.9#3 (mitta˚). <em>adubbha</em> one who does not do harm, harmless Pv.ii.9#8 (˚pāṇin = ahiṃsakahattha). As nt. harmlessness, frankness, friendliness, good-will Vin.i.347 (adrūbhāya, but cp. vv.ll. p. Vin.i.395 adubbhaya &amp; adrabbhāvāya); SN.i.225 (adubbhāya trustily); Ja.i.180 (id. as adūbhāya); spelt wrongly <em><a href="bword://adr%C5%ABbhaka">adrūbhaka</a></em> (for adubbhaka, with variant reading adrabhaka in expl. of adubbha-pāṇin) at Ja.vi.311. <em>Note:</em> dabhāya (dat.) is also used in Sk. in sense of an adv. or infinitive which confirms the etymology of the word. Cp. dobha.</p><p>Sk. dambha, see <em><a href="bword://dubbhati">dubbhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://adrūbhaka">adrūbhaka</a>, <a href="bword://dubbhati">dubbhati</a></p>

dūbhaka|duubhaka|dubhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubhaka</span></p><p>Dūbhaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective deceiving, treacherous, harmful Snp-a.287 (mitta˚); f. <em>-ikā</em> Ja.ii.297.</p><p>Sk. dambhaka</p><p>Dūbhaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a diamond Ja.i.363 = Ja.iii.207.</p><p>Sk. dambha, cp. dambholi</p>

dūbhana|duubhana|dubhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubhana</span></p><p>deceiving, pillaging, robbing etc. at Ja.ii.388 is to be read as (pantha-) <em><a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a></p>

dūbhati|duubhati|dubhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubhati</span></p><p>Dubbhati &amp; dūbhati</p><p>to injure, hurt, deceive; to be hostile to, plot or sin against (either w. dat. Ja.v.245; Ja.vi.491, or w. loc Ja.i.267; Ja.iii.212) SN.i.85 (ppr. adubbhanto), SN.i.225; Iti.86 (dubbhe = dusseyya Com.) = Vin.ii.203 (where dubbho) Thag.1129; Ja.ii.125; Ja.iv.261; Ja.v.487, Ja.v.503<br />ppr also dūbhato Ja.iv.261; ger. dubbhitvā Ja.iv.79; grd. dubbheyya (variant reading dūbheyya) to be punished Ja.v.71. Cp. pa˚.</p><p>Sk. dabhnoti cp.; <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1889, 204: <em>dabh</em> (dambh), pp. dabdha; idg. <em>*dhebh</em>, cp. Gr. ἀτέμβω to deceive. Cp. also Sk <em>druh</em> (so Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 11 s. v. padubbhati). See also dahara &amp; dūbha, dūbhaka dūbhi</p>

dūbhin|duubhin|dubhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubhin</span></p><p>adjective -dubbhin Ja.ii.180 (vv.ll. dūbha &amp; dubbhi), Ja.ii.327; Ja.iv.257; Dhp-a.ii.23.</p>

dūbhī|duubhii|dubhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duubhii</span></p><p>perfidy, treachery, Ja.i.412; Ja.iv.57 (variant reading <em>dubhī</em>); Ja.vi.59 (= <em><a href="bword://apar%C4%81dha">aparādha</a></em>).</p><p>cp. Sk. dambha, see <em><a href="bword://dubbhati">dubbhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aparādha">aparādha</a>, <a href="bword://dubbhati">dubbhati</a></p>

dūhana|duuhana|duhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuhana</span></p><p>Dūhana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter infesting, polluting, defaming; robbing, only in <em>pantha˚</em>; (with variant reading duhana) waylaying Ja.ii.281, Ja.ii.388; Tikp.280.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a></em></p><p>Dūhana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter milking (-˚), in <em>kumbha˚</em>; filling the pails with milk, i.e. giving much milk (gāvo; cp. Sanskrit droṇadughā a cow which yields much milk) Snp.309</p><p>Sk. dohana, see <em><a href="bword://dohati">dohati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhana">duhana</a>, <a href="bword://dohati">dohati</a></p>

dūhara|duuhara|duhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuhara</span></p><p>hard to be taken away.</p>

dūhati|duuhati|duhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuhati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhati">duhati</a></p>

dūhitika|duuhitika|duhitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuhitika</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://duhitika">duhitika</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://duhitika">duhitika</a></p>

dūpadhārita|duupadhaarita|dupadharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duupadhaarita</span></p><p>wrongly considered, wrongly understood</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhārita">upadhārita</a></p>

dūpaṭṭhāka|duupa.t.thaaka|dupatthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duupa.t.thaaka</span></p><p>difficult to attend or nurse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dupaṭṭhāka">dupaṭṭhāka</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāka">upaṭṭhāka</a></p>

dūra|duura|dura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duura</span></p><p>adjective far distant, remote, <em>opp.</em> <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sanna">āsanna</a></em> (Ja.ii.154) or <em><a href="bword://santika">santika</a></em> (Dhs.677; Vism.402)<br />■ Pv-a.117. Often in compounds (see below), also as <em>dūri˚</em>; e.g. dūri-bhāva distance Vism.71 Vism.377; Dhs-a.76<br />■ Cases mostly used adverbially, viz acc. <em>dūraṃ</em> far Ja.ii.154; Dhp-a.i.192<br />■ abl. <em><a href="bword://d%C5%ABrato">dūrato</a></em> from afar, aloof Vin.i.15; Vin.ii.195; SN.i.212; Snp.511; Dhp.219; Ja.v.78 (dūra-dūrato); Mil.23; Pv-a.107. dūrato karoti to keep aloof from Pv-a.17<br />■ loc. <em>dūre</em> at a distance also as prep. away from, far from (c. abl.), e.g. Snp.468; Ja.ii.155, Ja.ii.449 (= ārā); Ja.iii.189<br />■ Snp.772; Dhp.304; Ja.vi.364; Dhs.677<br />■ <em>dūre-pātin</em> one who shoots far [cp. Sk. dūra-pātin] AN.i.284; AN.ii.170, AN.ii.202. Ja.iv.494 See also akkhaṇavedhin<br />■ <em>atidūre</em> too far Vin.ii.215.</p><ul><li><em>-<em>kantana</em></em> at Thag.1123: the correct reading seems to be the variant reading durākantana, see <em>ākantana</em>;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> gone far away Pv.ii.13#4 (= paralokagata Pv-a.164); Dhp-a.iii.377 (durā˚).</li><li><em>-(ṃ)gama</em> far-going, going here there Dhp.37 (cp. Dhp-a.i.304); Pv.ii.9#10;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">ghuṭṭha</a></em> farrenowned Pv.ii.8#2;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vih%C4%81ra">vihāra</a></em> (-vuttin) living far away Snp.220.</li></ul><p>Sk. dūra, Ved. duva (stirring, urging on), compar. davīyān, Av. dūrō (far), <em>*dāu</em>; cp. Ohg zawen, Goth. taujan = E. do. Another form is <em>*deṷā</em> far in respect to time, as in Gr. δήν, δηρόν, Lat. dū-dum (cp. dū-rare = en-dure). See also dutiya &amp; dūta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsanna">āsanna</a>, <a href="bword://santika">santika</a>, <a href="bword://dūrato">dūrato</a>, kantana, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://ghuṭṭha">ghuṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://vihāra">vihāra</a></p>

dūrakantana|duurakantana|durakantana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurakantana</span></p><p>cutting far off; banishment (?)</p>

dūrakkha|duurakkha|durakkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurakkha</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://rakkha">rakkha</a></em> &amp; cp. du<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>du<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + rakkha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://rakkha">rakkha</a></p>

dūrama|duurama|durama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurama</span></p><p>or neuter difficulty of enjoyment in; it is difficult to enjoy.</p>

dūratara|duuratara|duratara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuratara</span></p><p>, compar. further away; more distant.</p>

dūrato|duurato|durato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurato</span></p><p>from afar; at a distance.</p>

dūratta|duuratta|duratta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuratta</span></p><p>adjective reddish MN.i.36 (˚vaṇṇa).</p><p>du<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + ratta</p>

dūraṃ|duura.m|duram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duura.m</span></p><p>adverb a long way; far.</p>

dūraṅgama|duura"ngama|durangama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duura"ngama</span></p><p>going a long way; far reaching.</p>

dūre|duure|dure
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">loc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duure</span></p><p>far off; at a distance.</p>

dūrepāti|duurepaati|durepati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurepaati</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dūrepātin">dūrepātin</a></p><p>shooting at a distance; hitting from far off.</p>

dūrepātin|duurepaatin|durepatin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duurepaatin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dūrepātin">dūrepātin</a></p><p>shooting at a distance; hitting from far off.</p>

dūrā|duuraa|dura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuraa</span></p><p>from afar; at a distance.</p>

dūsaka|duusaka|dusaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. (one) who dirties or defiles; who spoils or harms; a violator; an offender, a malefactor.</p>

dūsaka|duusaka|dusaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusaka</span></p><p>adjective noun corrupting, disgracing, one who defiles or defames; a robber, rebel AN.v.71 (bhikkhunī˚); Ja.ii.270; Ja.iv.495; Snp.89 (kula˚ one who spoils the reputation of the clan); Dhp-a.ii.23 (kuṭi˚ an incendiary); Mil.20 (pantha˚). As <em><a href="bword://dussaka">dussaka</a></em> at Ja.v.113 (kamma˚); Snp-a.287 (mitta˚, variant reading B. for dūbhaka)-panthadūsaka a highwayman Mil.290<br />■ f. <em>dūsikā</em> Ja.iii.179 (also as <em>dūsiyā</em> = dosakārikā); <em>a˚</em>; harmless Snp.312 (see *<a href="bword://a">a</a>*˚).</p><p>Sk. dūṣaka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dussaka">dussaka</a>, <a href="bword://a">a</a></p>

dūsana|duusana|dusana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusana</span></p><p>spoiling, defiling Ja.ii.270; Sdhp.453.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://d%C5%ABseti">dūseti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūseti">dūseti</a></p>

dūsanīya|duusaniiya|dusaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusaniiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dosanīya">dosanīya</a></p>

dūsayati|duusayati|dusayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūseti">dūseti</a></p>

dūsesi|duusesi|dusesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusesi</span></p><p>of dūseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūseti">dūseti</a></p>

dūsesuṃ|duusesu.m|dusesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusesu.m</span></p><p>of dūseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūseti">dūseti</a></p>

dūseti|duuseti|duseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuseti</span></p><p>dirties, defiles; violates; spoils, damages.</p>

dūseti|duuseti|duseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuseti</span></p><p>to spoil, ruin; to injure, hurt; to defile, pollute, defame Vin.i.79, Vin.i.85, Vin.i.86; Vin.iv.212 (maṃ so dūsetukāmo, said by a bhikkhunī), Vin.iv.316 (dūsetuṃ) AN.iv.169 sq.; Ja.i.454; Ja.ii.270; Dhp-a.ii.22 (kuṭiṃ, damage destroy)<br />aor <em>dūsayi</em> Ja.ii.110 (fared ill)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://d%C5%ABsita">dūsita</a></em>. Cp. pa˚, pari˚.</p><p>Sk. dūṣayati, caus. of dussati (q.v.). Also as <em>dusseti</em> Pv-a.82</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūsita">dūsita</a></p>

dūsi|duusi|dusi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dūsin">dūsin</a></p><ol><li>defiling; spoiling; violating.</li><li>(m.) the name of mārā.</li></ol>

dūsin|duusin|dusin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://dūsin">dūsin</a></p><ol><li>defiling; spoiling; violating.</li><li>(m.) the name of mārā.</li></ol>

dūsin|duusin|dusin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusin</span></p><p>adjective noun = dūsạka, in <em>magga˚</em>; (cp. pantha-dusaka) a highway robber Snp.84 sq</p><p>Sk. dūṣin</p>

dūsita|duusita|dusita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusita</span></p><p>of dūseti violated; defiled, spoiled, harmed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūseti">dūseti</a></p>

dūsita|duusita|dusita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusita</span></p><p>depraved, sinful, evil Pv-a.226 (˚citta).</p><p>Sk. dūṣita, pp. of dūseti</p>

dūsī|duusii|dusi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duusii</span></p><p>Name of Māra in the time of Kakusandha Buddha. He was, in that birth, son of Kāḷī, sister of Moggallāna. He first incited the brahmin householders to revile Kakusandha’s monks, chief of whom were Vidhura and Sañjīva; when that effort failed owing to the thoughts of goodwill, pity, sympathy and equanimity, developed by the monks, he next incited the brahmins to show great honour to the monks, hoping, in that way, to tempt them. But, owing to the intervention of Kakusandha, Dūsī’s attempts failed and he was born in the Mahā Niraya. The story is given in the Māratajjanīya Sutta MN.i.333<em>ff.</em> Thag.1187–1191</p>

dūta|duuta|duta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuta</span></p><p>messenger; envoy; a proxy.</p>

dūta|duuta|duta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuta</span></p><p>Dūta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a messenger, envoy Vin.i.16 Vin.ii.32, Vin.ii.277; DN.i.150; SN.iv.194; Snp.411 (rāja˚), Snp.417. deva˚ Yama’s envoy, Death’s messenger AN.i.138, AN.i.142; MN.ii.75 sq.; Ja.i.138<br />■ -˚ṃ pāheti to send a messenger Mil.18, Pv-a.133.</p><p>Ved. dūta, prob. to dūra (q.v.) as “one who is sent (far) away,” also perhaps Gr. δουλος slave. See Walde <em>Lat. Wtb.</em> under dudum</p><p>Dūta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter play, gaming, gambling Ja.iv.248.</p><p>Sk. dyūta, see <em><a href="bword://j%C5%ABta">jūta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jūta">jūta</a></p>

dūtaka|duutaka|dutaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duutaka</span></p><p>messenger.</p>

dūtenūpasamapadā|duutenuupasamapadaa|dutenupasamapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duutenuupasamapadaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūtopasampadā">dūtopasampadā</a></p>

dūteyya|duuteyya|duteyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuteyya</span></p><p>errand; commission; carrying of messages; a message; messages.</p>

dūteyya|duuteyya|duteyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duuteyya</span></p><p>errand, commission, messages AN.iv.196; Ja.iii.134; DN-a.i.78. ˚ṃ gacchati to go on an errand Vin.ii.202; ˚ṃ harati to obtain a commission Vin.iii.87; Vin.iv.23.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> doing a messenger’s duty Vin.i.359;</li><li><em>-<em>pahiṇagamana</em></em> sending &amp; going on messages DN.i.5 = MN.iii.34; AN.ii.209; MN.i.180.</li></ul><p>Sk. dūtya, but varying in meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a>, pahiṇagamana</p>

dūtopasampadā|duutopasampadaa|dutopasampada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duutopasampadaa</span></p><p>ordination by means of a messenger or proxy.</p>

dūtūpasampadā|duutuupasampadaa|dutupasampada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">duutuupasampadaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūtopasampadā">dūtopasampadā</a></p>

earnestness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Earnestness:</strong> <em>appamāda</em></p>

earth-element
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Earth-element:</strong> see: <em>dhātu</em> I.</p>

eating
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Eating:</strong> knowing the measure in <em>bhojane</em> <em>mattaññutā.</em></p>

edha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>growing, prospering.</p>

edha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>fuel, fire etc. Only in adj. neg. an˚ without fuel Ja.iv.26.</p><p>Sk. edhaḥ, cp. idhma, inddhe; Gr. α ̓ϊχος, ἀίχω, Lat. aedes, Ohg. eit, Ags. ād funeral pile, etc. See idhuma iṭṭhaka</p>

edhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>prospers, grows strong; becomes happy.</li><li>finds; obtains.</li></ol>

edhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to prosper, succeed in, increase SN.i.217 (sukhaṃ); Snp.298; Dhp.193; Ja.i.223; Ja.iii.151. sukh˚edhita at Vin.iii.13 is better read as sukhe ṭhita as at Ja.vi.219.</p><p><em>edh</em>, cp. iddhati</p>

edhittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://edhati">edhati</a></p>

edisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā &amp; ~ī)n. like this, like so; such a …; of such a kind; such a thing, something like this.</p>

edisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective such like, such Vv.37#3; Pv-a.69, Pv-a.243.</p><p>Sk. īdṛśa</p>

edisaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.a</i></p><p>like this, like so; of such a kind; such a thing.</p>

edisaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= edisa Snp.313.</p>

effort
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Effort:</strong> the 4 right <strong>e.:</strong> <em>samma</em> - <em>ppadhāna</em> see: <em>padhāna</em> Right <strong>e.</strong> see: <em>sacca</em> IV 6, <em>magga</em> 6. - 5 elements of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>padhāniyanga</em></p>

ego-belief
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ego-belief:</strong> see: <em>ditthi</em>, <em>sakkāya-ditthi</em>, <em>vipallāsa</em>.</p>

ego-entity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ego-entity:</strong> <em>attā</em></p>

ego-idea
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ego-idea or Ego-perception:</strong> see: <em>vipallāsa</em></p>

egolessness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Egolessness:</strong> <em>anattā</em></p>

ehi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>imperat. used especially as introductory particles of exhortation, encouragement or summons (followed usually by an imperat. or fut.): come! come on!</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etha">etha</a>, <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

ehi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>come, come here Snp.165; Ja.ii.159; Ja.vi.367); Dhp-a.i.49. In the later language part. of exhortation = Gr. α ̓́γε, Lat. age, “come on” Dhp-a.ii.91; Pv-a.201 (+ tāva = α ̓́γεδή). <em><a href="bword://ehipassika">ehipassika</a></em> (adj.) [ehi + passa ika] of the Dhamma, that which invites every man to come to see for himself, open to all, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vism.216 as “ehi, passa imaṃ dhamman ti evaṃ pavattaṃ ehi-passavidhaṃ arahatī ti”, DN.ii.217; DN.iii.5, DN.iii.227; SN.i.9; SN.iv.41, SN.iv.272 SN.v.343; AN.i.158; AN.ii.198. <em>ehibhadantika</em> one who accepts an invitation DN.i.166; MN.i.342; MN.ii.161; AN.i.295; AN.ii.206 <em>ehi bhikkhu</em> “come bhikkhu!” the oldest formula of admission to the order Vin.i.12; Vin.iii.24; Dhp-a.i.87; Ja.i.82 f. <em>ehi bhikkhunī</em> Vin.iv.214 pl. etha bhikkhavo Dhp-a.i.95. ehibhikkhu-pabbajjā initiation into Bhikkhuship Snp-a.456. ehibhikkhubhāva-state of being invited to join the Sangha, admission to the Order Ja.i.82, Ja.i.86; Dhp-a.ii.32; Snp-a.456. <em>ehisāgata-(&amp; svāgata-)vādin;</em> a man of courtesy (lit. one who habitually says: “come you are welcome” DN.i.116; Vin.ii.11; Vin.iii.181.</p><p>imper. of eti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehipassika">ehipassika</a></p>

ehibhadantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>(one) who accepts an invitation (to take alms).</p>

ehibhikkhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(the oldest formula of admission to the order) “come o monk”; one ordained by the formula “ehi bhikkhu”.</p>

ehibhikkhunī|ehibhikkhunii|ehibhikkhuni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ehibhikkhunii</span></p><p>one ordained by the formula “<em>ehi bhikkhunī</em>”; (according to commentaries no woman was ordained but see Thig 109)</p>

ehipassika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>suitable to come and see, fitting to be shown.</p>

ehisi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ehisi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.</p><p>ehisi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a>, <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

ehisvāgatavādi|ehisvaagatavaadi|ehisvagatavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ehisvaagatavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ehisvāgatavādin">ehisvāgatavādin</a></p><p>welcoming: saying “come, you are welcome”.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehisāgatavādin">ehisāgatavādin</a></p>

ehisvāgatavādin|ehisvaagatavaadin|ehisvagatavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ehisvaagatavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ehisvāgatavādin">ehisvāgatavādin</a></p><p>welcoming: saying “come, you are welcome”.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehisāgatavādin">ehisāgatavādin</a></p>

ehisāgatavādi|ehisaagatavaadi|ehisagatavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ehisaagatavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ehisāgatavādin">ehisāgatavādin</a></p><p>welcoming: saying “come, you are welcome”.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehisvāgatavādin">ehisvāgatavādin</a></p>

ehisāgatavādin|ehisaagatavaadin|ehisagatavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ehisaagatavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ehisāgatavādin">ehisāgatavādin</a></p><p>welcoming: saying “come, you are welcome”.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehisvāgatavādin">ehisvāgatavādin</a></p>

eightfold path
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[eightfold path]]Eightfold Path:</strong> see: <em>magga</em>.</p>

eja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>or neuter disturbance, emotion.</p>

ejā|ejaa|eja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ejaa</span></p><p>motion, disturbance, agitation; emotion.</p>

ejā|ejaa|eja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ejaa</span></p><p>motion, turbulence, distraction seduction, craving SN.iv.64; Snp.791; Iti.91; Mnd.91, Mnd.353; Dhs.1059 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->u.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 277); Vv-a.232<br />■ <em><a href="bword://aneja">aneja</a></em> (adj. unmoved, undisturbed, calm, passionless SN.i.27, SN.i.141, SN.i.159 SN.iii.83; SN.iv.64; AN.ii.15; Mnd.353; Vv-a.107.</p><p>to <em>iñj</em>, q.v. and see <em>ānejja</em>. There is also a Sk. root <em>ej</em> to stir, move</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aneja">aneja</a></p>

ejānuga|ejaanuga|ejanuga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ejaanuga</span></p><p>following, under the influence of, emotion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ejā">ejā</a></p>

eka-bījī|eka-biijii|eka-biji
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eka-biijii</span></p><p><strong>Eka-bījī:</strong> ‘germinating only once more’, is the name for one of the 3 kinds of Stream-winners: see: <em>sotāpanna.</em></p>

eka-m-antaṃ|eka-m-anta.m|eka-m-antam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eka-m-anta.m</span></p><p>eka-m-antaṃ</p><p>adv. (or ekam antaṃ)</p><ol><li>to one side (showing respect); at a respectful distance.</li><li>on one side, aside; out of the way; a little apart.</li><li>to one side, apart; at a distance; in private, on one’s own.</li><li>in one place; in a single place</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

eka-m-ante
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>eka-m-ante</p><p>adv. apart, at a distance; in private, on one’s own; in a solitary place</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

eka-m-eka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>eka-m-eka</p><p>pron. &amp; mfn.</p><ol><li>each (one), each separate (one); one by one; (repeated:) each… one each….</li><li>any one (of several), one or the other; just one; a single one</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

eka-m-ekaṃ|eka-m-eka.m|eka-m-ekam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eka-m-eka.m</span></p><p>eka-m-ekaṃ</p><p>acc. adv. one by one; singly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

eka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>num. &amp; mfn.</p><ol><li><ol><li>one; only one; a single; one of two or many.</li><li>one only, one and the same.</li><li>(indefinite:) one, a certain; a, an.</li></ol></li><li><p>alone; solitary.</p></li><li><p>one and only, unique; preeminent.</p></li><li><p>all one; nothing but.</p></li><li><p>(pl.) some; some people; some authorities.</p></li></ol><p>eka … apara …</p><p>(?) one … another …; one … the other …</p><p>eka … eka …</p><p>(?) one … another …; one … the other …</p><p>eka … dutiya …</p><p>(?) one … another …; one … the other …</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a>, <a href="bword://eka">eka</a>, <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

eka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun masculine one. Eka follows the pron. declension i.e. nom. pl. is <em>eke</em> (e.g. Snp.43, Snp.294, Snp.780 etc.)</p><ol><li>“one” as number, either with or without contrast to two or more; often also “single” opp. to nānā various, many (q.v.). Very frequent by itself as well as with other numerals, ekangula one thumb Mhvs.29, Mhvs.11; Dhp-a.iii.127; ekapasse in one quarter Dhp-a.ii.52; ekamaccha a single fish Ja.i.222. In enumeration: eka dve pañca dasa Dhp-a.i.24. With other numerals: eka-tiṃsa (31) DN.ii.2 ˚saṭṭhi (61) Vin.i.20; ˚navuti (91) Dhp-a.i.97; ˚sata (101; Dhp-a.ii.14. Cp. use of “one less” in ekūna (see under compounds &amp; ūna).</li><li>(as predicative and adj.) one, by oneself, one only, alone, solitary AN.iii.67 (ek-uddesa) Ja.i.59 (ekadivasena on the one day only, i.e. on the same day); Dhp.395; Snp.35, Snp.1136 (see Cnd.172#a), ekaṃ ekaṃ one by one SN.i.104 (devo ekaṃ ekaṃ phusāyati rains drop by drop), cp. ekameka.</li><li>a certain one, some one, some; adj. in function of an indefinite article = a one (definite or indefinite): ekasmiṃ samaye once upon a time Ja.i.306; ekena upāyena by some means Ja.iii.393 ekaṃ kulaṃ gantuṃ to a certain clan (corresp. with asuka Dhp-a.i.45; ekadivasaṃ one day Ja.i.58; Ja.iii.26; Pv-a.67 Cp. Snp.1069 (see Cnd.172#b)<br />■ All these three categories are found represented in freq. compounds, of which the foll. are but a small selection.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a></em> see ˚*<a href="bword://pokkhara">pokkhara</a>*.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> calm, tranquil (of persons just converted), collected [cp. Buddh. Sk. ekāgra Jtm.31#70] SN.iv.125; AN.i.70, AN.i.266; AN.ii.14, AN.ii.29; AN.iii.175 (˚citta), AN.iii.391; Snp.341; Ja.i.88; Ne.28, cp. Mil.139</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://aggat%C4%81">aggatā</a></em> concentration; capacity to individualise; contemplation, tranquillity of mind (see on term <em>Cpd.</em> 16, 178<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 237, 240) SN.v.21, SN.v.197, SN.v.269 (cittassa); AN.i.36; AN.iv.40; Dhs.11 (cittassa); Vism.84.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> a part, division, something belonging to Ja.iii.308; Ud.69.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga%E1%B9%87a">aṅgaṇa</a></em> one (clear space Ja.ii.357.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81g%C4%81rika">āgārika</a></em> a thief, robber DN.i.52, DN.i.166; AN.i.154, AN.i.295; AN.ii.206; AN.iii.129; Mnd.416; Cnd.304#iii.a. DN-a.i.159 (= ekam eva gharaṃ parivāretvā vilumpanaṃ DN-a.i.159).</li><li><em>-āyana</em> leading to one goal, direct way or “leading to the goal as the one &amp; only way (magga MN.i.63; SN.v.167, SN.v.185.</li><li><em>-ārakkha</em> having one protector or guardian DN.iii.269; AN.v.29 sq.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81lopika">ālopika</a></em> = ekāgārika DN.i.166; AN.i.295; AN.ii.206.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81sana">āsana</a></em> sitting or living alone MN.i.437; Snp.718; Dhp.305; Ja.v.397; Mil.342; Vism.60 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with reference to eating, viz. ekāsane bhojanaṃ ekāsanaṃ, perhaps comparing āsana with asana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->. The foll ˚āsanika is ibid. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “taṃ sīlam assā ti ekāsaniko”)</li><li><em>-āsanika</em> one who keeps to himself Mil.20, Mil.216; Vism.69.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81ha">āha</a></em> one day MN.i.88; usually in cpd. ekâhadvîhaṃ one or two days Ja.i.255; Dhp-a.i.391.</li><li><em>-āhika</em> of or for one day DN.i.166.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://uttarika">uttarika</a></em> (-nikāya) is another title for Anguttarika-nikāya Mil.392.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C5%ABna">ūna</a></em> one less, minus one usually as 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part of a numeral cpd., like <em>˚vīsati</em> (21 − 1 = 19) Dhp-a.i.4; <em>˚paññāsa</em> (49) Ja.iii.220; <em>˚saṭṭhi</em> (59; Dhp-a.iii.412; <em>˚pañcasatā</em> (499) Dhp-a.ii.204. See <em>ūna-eka</em> one by one, each, severally, one to each DN.ii.18 (˚loma); DN.iii.144 (id.), DN.iii.157; Ja.i.222; Dhp-a.i.101 (ekekassa no ekekaṃ māsaṃ one month for each of us); Dhp-a.ii.114; Vv-a.256; Pv-a.42, Pv-a.43.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a></em> compact, solid, hard Dhp.81.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cara">cara</a></em> wandering or living alone, solitary SN.i.16; Snp.166, Snp.451; Dhp.37.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cariy%C4%81">cariyā</a></em> walking alone, solitude Dhp.61; Snp.820.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81rin">cārin</a></em> = ˚cara Mil.105.</li><li><em>-cittakkhaṇika</em> of the duration of <em>one</em> thought Vism.138.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cintin">cintin</a></em> “thinking one thing (only)”, simple Mil.92.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://th%C5%ABpa">thūpa</a></em> (all in one heap, mixed up, together Ja.v.17 (= sūkarapotakā viya C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://do%E1%B9%87ik%C4%81">doṇikā</a></em> (-nāvā) a trough-shaped canoe with an outrigger Ja.vi.305.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADalika">paṭalika</a></em> having a single sole (of sandals, upāhanā) Vism.125.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADa">paṭṭa</a></em> single cloth (cp. dupaṭṭa) Vism.109.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://padika">padika</a></em> (-magga) a small (lit. for one foot) foot-path Ja.i.315; Ja.v.491.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pala">pala</a></em> one carat worth (see <em><a href="bword://pala">pala</a></em>) Vism.339.</li><li><em>-passayika</em> is to be read ek’apassayika (see under <em>apa</em>˚).</li><li><em>-pahārena</em> all at once Vism.418; Dhs-a.333.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pi%E1%B9%ADaka">piṭaka</a></em> knowing <em>one</em> Piṭaka Vism.62</li><li><em>-puttika</em> having only one son Kp-a.237.</li><li><em>-purisika</em> (itthi (a woman) true to one man Ja.i.290.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pokkhara">pokkhara</a></em> a sort of drum Ja.vi.21, Ja.vi.580 (C. explains by ek-akkhi-bherī)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://b%C4%ABjin">bījin</a></em> having only one (more) seed, i.e. destined to be reborn only once SN.v.205; AN.i.233; AN.iv.380; Ne.189</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhattika">bhattika</a></em> having one meal a day AN.i.212; AN.iii.216; Ja.i.91</li><li><em>-bhattakinī</em> a woman true to one husband Ja.iii.63.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rajja">rajja</a></em> sole sovereignty Dhp.178; Pv-a.74.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C4%81j%C4%81">rājā</a></em> universal king Ja.i.47 (of the Sun).</li><li><em>-vāciya</em> a single remark or objection Ja.ii.353.</li><li><em>-vāraṃ</em> once Ja.i.292;</li><li><em>-vārena</em> id. Dhp-a.i.10.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sadisa">sadisa</a></em> fully alike or resembling, identical Ja.i.291</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sama">sama</a></em> equal Ja.vi.261.</li><li><em>-sāṭa &amp; sāṭaka;</em> having a single vestment, a “one-rober” SN.i.78 (˚ka); Ud.65.</li></ul><p>Vedic eka, i.e. e-ka to Idg. *oi as in Av. aēva, Gr. ο ̓ϊος one, alone; and also with diff. suffix in Lat. ū-nus, cp. Gr. οἰνός (one on the dice), Goth. etc ains = E. one</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a>, <a href="bword://pokkhara">pokkhara</a>, <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://aggatā">aggatā</a>, <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://aṅgaṇa">aṅgaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://āgārika">āgārika</a>, <a href="bword://ālopika">ālopika</a>, <a href="bword://āsana">āsana</a>, <a href="bword://āha">āha</a>, <a href="bword://uttarika">uttarika</a>, <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a>, <a href="bword://ghana">ghana</a>, <a href="bword://cara">cara</a>, <a href="bword://cariyā">cariyā</a>, <a href="bword://cārin">cārin</a>, <a href="bword://cintin">cintin</a>, <a href="bword://thūpa">thūpa</a>, <a href="bword://doṇikā">doṇikā</a>, <a href="bword://paṭalika">paṭalika</a>, <a href="bword://paṭṭa">paṭṭa</a>, <a href="bword://padika">padika</a>, <a href="bword://pala">pala</a>, <a href="bword://pala">pala</a>, <a href="bword://piṭaka">piṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://pokkhara">pokkhara</a>, <a href="bword://bījin">bījin</a>, <a href="bword://bhattika">bhattika</a>, <a href="bword://rajja">rajja</a>, <a href="bword://rājā">rājā</a>, <a href="bword://sadisa">sadisa</a>, <a href="bword://sama">sama</a></p>

eka-vokāra-bhava|eka-vokaara-bhava|eka-vokara-bhava
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eka-vokaara-bhava</span></p><p><strong>Eka-vokāra-bhava:</strong> one-group existence, is the existence of the unconscious beings <em>asañña-satta</em> as they possess only the materiality-group. Cf. <em>catu-vokāra-bhava</em> <em>pañca-vokāra-bhava</em>.</p>

ekabhattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>eating only one meal a day; eating only before noon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

ekabīji|ekabiiji|ekabiji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ekabiiji</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ekabījin">ekabījin</a></p><p>‘having one seed’, the most advanced type of sotāpanna, who will become an arahat in his next life as a man</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a></p>

