ela
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>salt(?) or water(?) in <em>elambiya</em> (= el˚ambu-ja) born in (salt) water Snp.845 (= ela-saññaka ambumhi jāta); Mnd.202 (elaṃ vuccati udakaṃ).</p><p>?</p>

elagalavāca|elagalavaaca|elagalavaca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">elagalavaaca</span></p><p>whose speech is accompanied by the dripping of saliva.</p>

elakapādaka|elakapaadaka|elakapadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">elakapaadaka</span></p><p>supported on a block (like a threshold stone) or with feet shape like those of a ram.</p>

elambaraka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of creeper or vine. The perfume of the flower would last for seven days Ja.vi.536.</p>

elambaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Name of a creeping vine Ja.vi.536. Elaluka (Elaluka)</p><p>?</p>

elamūga|elamuuga|elamuga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">elamuuga</span></p><p>unintelligent, stupid (as a sheep); (perhaps with association with impaired speech)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷamūga">eḷamūga</a></p>

elanda
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of plant M.i.124. It is described as inhibiting the growth of sal trees so it may have been a type of vine or creeper.</p>

elasticity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Elasticity:</strong> of materiality, mental properties or consciousness: <em>mudutā</em> see: <em>khandha</em> Materiality I.B. and Tab. II.</p>

elaṇḍa|ela.n.da|elanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ela.n.da</span></p><p>the castor oil plant; (or perhaps a kind of creeper)</p>

elaṇḍa|ela.n.da|elanda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ela.n.da</span></p><p>= eraṇḍa (?) MN.i.124.</p>

elders
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Elders:</strong> the teaching of the: <em>theravāda</em></p>

elements
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Elements:</strong> <em>dhātu</em> - Analysis of the 4 <strong>e.:</strong> <em>dhātu-vavatthāna</em></p>

elāluka|elaaluka|elaluka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">elaaluka</span></p><p>See <em>Kakkārika</em>.</p><p>See also: Kakkārika</p>

elāluka|elaaluka|elaluka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">elaaluka</span></p><p>Elāluka (Eḷāluka)</p><p>neuter a kind of cucumber(?) Vv.33#29; Ja.i.205; Ja.v.37; Dhp-a.i.278.</p><p>etym.?</p>

em
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>so; in this way.</p>

emotion
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Emotion:</strong> 8 sources of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>samvega-vatthu</em>. The 4 places rousing emotion; <em>samvejanīya-tthāna</em></p>

emptiness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Emptiness:</strong> <em>Suññatā</em> - Contemplation of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>suññatānupassanā</em> - For emptiness of self, pertaining to the 4 truths, see: <em>sacca</em>.</p>

ena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. pron.</i></p><p>him, her, it (referring to someone or something already mentioned); this, that; this one.</p>

ena
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>pronoun [fr. pron. base *ē̆, cp. e-ka; to this cp. in form &amp; meaning Lat. ūnus, Gr.; οἰνός, Ohg. ein, Oir. ōin only used in acc. enaṃ (taṃ enaṃ) “him, this one, the same” Snp.583, Snp.981, Snp.1114; Dhp.118, Dhp.313; Ja.iii.395; Cnd.304#iii.b. See also naṃ.</p>

ends
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Ends:</strong> ‘attaining two ends simultaneously’; <em>sama</em> - <em>sīsī.</em></p>

energy
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Energy:</strong> <em>viriya</em>, further see: <em>bojjhanga, bala, pāramī</em></p>

enlightened one
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[enlightened one]]Enlightened one:</strong> the: Buddha; see: <em>sammā</em> - <em>sambuddha.</em></p>

enlightenment
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Enlightenment:</strong> <em>bodhi</em> - The 7 elements of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>bojjhanga</em> - A being destined for <strong>e.:</strong> <em>Bodhisatta</em>.</p>

enthusiasm
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Enthusiasm:</strong> <em>pīti</em></p>

envy
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Envy:</strong> <em>Issā</em></p>

enījaṅgha|eniija"ngha|enijangha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eniija"ngha</span></p><p>with legs like the black antelope’s (one of the 32 characteristics of a mahāpurisa).</p>

equality-conceit
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Equality-conceit:</strong> see: <em>māna</em>.</p>

equanimity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Equanimity:</strong> <em>upekkhā</em> = <em>tatra</em> - <em>majjhattatā.</em> - Knowledge consisting in <strong>e.</strong> with regard to all constructions, see: <em>visuddhi</em> VI, 8. - Indulging in <strong>e.,</strong> see: <em>manopavicāra</em></p>

equilibrium of mental faculties
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[equilibrium of mental faculties]]Equilibrium of mental abilities:</strong> <em>indriya-samatta</em>.</p>

eragu
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of coarse grass which could be used for making coverings Vin.i.196.</p>

eragu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of grass.</p>

eraka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of plant Ja.iv.88.</p>

eraka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A verse admonishing this monk is found in the Theragāthā. Thag.93</p>

eraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Eraka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective driving away, moving Ja.iv.20 (˚vāta); <em>-vattika</em> a certain kind of torture MN.i.87 = AN.i.47 = AN.ii.122 = Cnd.604 = Mil.197.</p><p>fr. ereti</p><p>Eraka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter Typha-grass Ja.iv.88. As <em><a href="bword://eragu">eragu</a></em> (?) a kind of grass used for making coverlets Vin.i.196 (eraka Bdhgh. on DN.i.166).</p><p>fr. ereti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eragu">eragu</a></p>

erakavattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of torture.</p>

erayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes move, stirs; makes sound; raises one’s voice</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ereti">ereti</a>, <a href="bword://iriyati">iriyati</a></p>

erayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>spoken, uttered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://erayati">erayati</a></p>

eraṇdakatela|era.ndakatela|erandakatela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">era.ndakatela</span></p><p>castor oil.</p>

eraṇdatela|era.ndatela|erandatela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">era.ndatela</span></p><p>castor oil.</p>

eraṇḍa|era.n.da|eranda
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">era.n.da</span></p><p>Castor Oil Tree, sometimes <em><a href="bword://elanda">elanda</a></em>, Hindi <em>erandi</em>, <em>Ricinus communis</em> Ja.iv.205 M.ii.152, a tall annual bush with large blue-green leaves and which produces spiky pods containing seeds from which a colorless oil can be extracted. Castor oil is used as a lubricant, in lamps and in various medicines. The castor oil tree was considered the lowest of all trees Ja.ii.440. According to the Jātaka, the Bodhisattva was once reborn as a spirit in a castor oil tree Ja.i.423.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://elanda">elanda</a></p>

eraṇḍa|era.n.da|eranda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">era.n.da</span></p><p>the castor oil plant Cnd.680#ii.; Ja.ii.440. Cp. <em><a href="bword://ela%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">elaṇḍa</a></em>.</p><p>dial.?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://elaṇḍa">elaṇḍa</a></p>

ereti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes move, stirs; makes sound; raises one’s voice</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://erayati">erayati</a>, <a href="bword://iriyati">iriyati</a></p>

ereti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to move, set into motion, raise (one’s voice) MN.i.21; Snp.350 (eraya imper.); Thag.209 (eraye); Ja.iv.478<br />pp <em><a href="bword://erita">erita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>= īreti (q.v.) Caus. of <em>īr</em>, Sk. īrayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://erita">erita</a></p>

erita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>moved, stirred</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ereti">ereti</a></p>

erita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>moved, shaken, driven Ja.iv.424; Vv.39#4, Vv.42#4 ; Thag.104, Pv.ii.12#3; Vism.172 (+ samerita), Vism.342 (vāt˚ moved by the wind). Cp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%ABrita">īrita</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of ereti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īrita">īrita</a></p>

erāpatha|eraapatha|erapatha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eraapatha</span></p><p>A royal family of Nāgas, mentioned together with Virūpakkhas, Chabbyāputtas, and Kaṇhāgotamakas. For their own protection, monks are advised to fill their hearts with amity for these four classes of Nāgas. AN.ii.72 Vin.ii.109<em>f.</em></p>

erāvaṇa|eraava.na|eravana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eraava.na</span></p><p>In the Dhammika Sutta. Snp.379 Erāvaṇa is mentioned among the devas who visited the Buddha to pay him homage. He is also mentioned among the Nāgas present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.258</p>

erāvaṇa|eraava.na|eravana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eraava.na</span></p><p>Name of Indra’s elephant Snp.379; Vv.44#13; Vv-a.15.</p>

esa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>seeking; searching for.</p>

esa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Esa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://etad">etad</a></em>.</p><p>Esa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://esin">esin</a></em> Snp.286.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a>, <a href="bword://esin">esin</a></p>

esabha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) a by-form of <em><a href="bword://usabha">usabha</a></em> (q.v.), in cpd. rathesabha.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usabha">usabha</a></p>

esana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>esana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>seeking, seeking to obtain; search (esp. for food or means of living).</li><li>probing</li></ol><p>esana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. seeking, searching for.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esanā">esanā</a></p>

esanā|esanaa|esana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esanaa</span></p><ol><li>seeking, seeking to obtain; search (esp. for food or means of living).</li><li>probing</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://esana">esana</a></p>

esanā|esanaa|esana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esanaa</span></p><p>desire, longing, wish DN.iii.216, DN.iii.270; MN.i.79; SN.v.54, SN.v.139; AN.i.93; AN.ii.41; AN.v.31; Vv-a.83; Pv-a.98, Pv-a.163, Pv-a.265. See also <em>anesanā, isi &amp; pariy˚</em>.</p><p>fr. esati</p>

esanī|esanii|esani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esanii</span></p><p>probe.</p>

esanī|esanii|esani
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esanii</span></p><p>surgeon’s probe MN.ii.256.</p><p>fr. <em>iṣ</em></p>

esat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://esat">esat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esati">esati</a></p>

esati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>seeks, seeks to obtain; searches for.</p>

esati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to seek, search, strive for Snp.592 (esāno ppr. med.), Snp.919; Dhp.131.</p><p>ā + <em>iṣ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> with confusion of <em>iṣ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> and <em>iṣ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, icchati, see also <em><a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></em>, anvesati, pariyesati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajjhesati">ajjhesati</a></p>

escape
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Escape:</strong> <em>nissarana</em> see: <em>pahāna</em>.</p>

esi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esati">esati</a></p>

esika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><em>Esikā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (f.) a pillar, post AN.iv.106, AN.iv.109. Freq. in cpd. <em>-ṭṭhāyin</em> as stable as a pillar DN.i.14; SN.iii.202, SN.iii.211, SN.iii.217; DN-a.i.105.</p><p>a by-form of isīkā</p><p>Esikā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>desire, see <em><a href="bword://abb%C5%AB%E1%B8%B7ha">abbūḷha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbūḷha">abbūḷha</a></p>

esikaṭṭhāyiṭṭhita|esika.t.thaayi.t.thita|esikatthayitthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esika.t.thaayi.t.thita</span></p><p>as firm or enduring as a pillar</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esikā">esikā</a></p>

esikā|esikaa|esika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine \~a, neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esikaa</span></p><p>post, a pillar (usually placed at the city gate;)</p>

esin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective seeking, wishing, desiring SN.ii.11 (sambhav˚); Ja.i.87 (phal˚); Ja.iv.26 (dukkham˚); Pv.ii.9#28 (gharam); Pv-a.132.</p><p>Sk. eṣin, of <em>iṣ</em></p>

esitvā|esitvaa|esitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esati">esati</a></p>

essanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

essati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

essāmi|essaami|essami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">essaami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

esukārī|esukaarii|esukari
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esukaarii</span></p><p>A brahmin who visits the Buddha at Jetavana and asks him various questions on castes and their distinctions, from the point of view of their functions. The Buddha replies that birth’s invidious bar has been laid down by the brahmins, without consulting anybody else; all four castes alike can live the good life, which is the true service, and follow the Dhamma, which is the true wealth. At the end of the discourse Esukārī declares himself to be a follower of the Buddha. MN.ii.177<em>ff.</em></p>

esāna|esaana|esana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">esaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://esati">esati</a></p>

eta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p><p>demonstr. pronoun. this (referring to what is nearest in space or in thought; or to what immediately precedes or follows); often pointing out someone or something: here is …, there is ….</p>

etad
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p><p>demonstr. pronoun. this (referring to what is nearest in space or in thought; or to what immediately precedes or follows); often pointing out someone or something: here is …, there is ….</p>

etad
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pron.</i></p><p>adj. demonstr. pron. “this”, with on the whole the same meaning and function as <em>tad</em> only more definite and emphatic. Declined like <em>tad</em>.</p><h3>Cases</h3><p>■ nt. sg. <em><a href="bword://etad">etad</a></em> (poetical-archaic form) AN.ii.17; Snp.274, Snp.430, Snp.822, Snp.1087; Ja.i.61, Ja.i.279; &amp; <em>etaṃ</em> (the usual form) Snp.51, Snp.207, Snp.1036, Snp.1115; Ja.ii.159; pl. <em>etāni</em> Snp.52; Ja.ii.159<br />■ m. sg. <em><a href="bword://esa">esa</a></em> Snp.81, Snp.416, Snp.1052; Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.159; Mil.18; Dhp-a.i.18; &amp; <em>eso</em> Snp.61, Snp.312, Snp.393; Ja.vi.336; pl. <em>ete</em> Snp.188, Snp.760; Ja.i.223<br />■ f. sg. <em>esā</em> Snp.80 Snp.451; Ja.i.307; pl. <em>etā</em> Snp.297, Snp.897; Ja.ii.129<br />Oblique cases:<br />■ gen. dat. <em>etassa</em> Ja.ii.159; f. <em>etissā</em> Ja.iii.280;<br />■ instr <em>etena</em> Snp.655; Ja.i.222;<br />■ pl. loc. <em>etesu</em> Snp.248, Snp.339, Snp.1055 f. <em>etāsu</em> Snp.607.<br />■ Other cases regular &amp; frequent.</p><p>Vedic etad, of pron. base *e; see Walde, <em>Lat. Wtb.</em> under equidem</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a>, <a href="bword://esa">esa</a></p>

etadantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having this as an end, having an end here; ending thus</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p>

etadatthiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having this purpose; useful for this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p>

etadohi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>this as limit; to this limit, as far as this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p>

etageha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having longings for this, desiring this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p>

etaparama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having this (which usually follows) as their highest limit, i.e. only as good as this, no better than this; only as great as this, no more than this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etad">etad</a></p>

etaparamaṃ|etaparama.m|etaparamam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaparama.m</span></p><p>as well as, only as well as, on as much as</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etaparama">etaparama</a></p>

etarahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>now; a this time; nowadays, at the present time; esp. (a) in relation to the past and/or future; parallel (in direct speech) to tena kho pana samayena (+ historic present).</p>

etarahi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb now, at present DN.i.29, DN.i.151, DN.i.179, DN.i.200; DN.ii.3; Ja.i.215 (opp. tadā), Ja.iii.82; Ja.vi.364 (instead of paccuppanna).</p><p>Sk. etarhi, cp. tarahi &amp; carahi</p>

etase
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

etave
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

etena upāyena|etena upaayena|etena upayena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etena upaayena</span></p><p>in this way, thus; by this means</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāya">upāya</a></p>

eternity-belief
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Eternity-belief:</strong> <em>sassata-ditthi</em> see: <em>ditthi</em></p>

eternity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Eternity:</strong> cf. <em>kappa</em></p>

etha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>imperat. used especially as introductory particles of exhortation, encouragement or summons (followed usually by an imperat. or fut.): come! come on!</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehi">ehi</a>, <a href="bword://eti">eti</a></p>

eti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>eti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. goes; goes towards; reaches, obtains; reaches (a state); is involved (in)</p><p>eti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. comes, comes to; approaches; comes back, returns.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayati">ayati</a>, <a href="bword://iti">iti</a></p>

eti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go, go to, reach; often (= ā + eti) to come back, return Snp.364, Snp.376, Snp.666 (come); Ja.vi.365 (return); ppr. <em>ento</em> Ja.iii.433 (acc. suriyaṃ atthaṃ entaṃ the setting sun); imper 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. <em><a href="bword://ehi">ehi</a></em> only in meaning “come” (see separately) 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>etu</em> DN.i.60; 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. <em>etha</em> DN.i.211; Snp.997; Ja.ii.129; Dhp-a.i.95 (in admission formula “etha bhikkhavo” come ye [and be] bhikkhus! See ehi bhikkhu)<br />fut <em><a href="bword://essati">essati</a></em> Ja.vi.190, Ja.vi.365, &amp; <em>ehiti</em> Ja.ii.153; 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. <em>ehisi</em> Dhp.236 Dhp.369<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ita">ita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>P. eti represents Sk. eti as well as ā-eti, i.e. to go and to come (here); with Sk. eti cp. Av. aeiti, Gr. ε ̓ϊσι Lat. eo, it; Goth. iddja went, Obulg. iti, Oir. etha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ehi">ehi</a>, <a href="bword://essati">essati</a>, <a href="bword://ita">ita</a></p>

etta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>so much, just so much.</p>

etta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb there, here Pv.i.5#6 (sic; cp. Kp-a.254 note).</p><p>= Sk. atra, see also <em><a href="bword://ettha">ettha</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ettha">ettha</a></p>

ettaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā &amp; ettikā)n. such; so much, so great; so many; this much (expressing a total); just so much, only so much (and no more).</p>

ettaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective so much, this much, according to context referring either to deficiency or abundance, thus developing 2 meanings, viz.</p><ol><li>just as much (&amp; no more), only so little, all this, just this, such a small number, a little pl. so few, just so many DN.i.117 (opp. aparimāṇa), DN.i.124; AN.iv.114; Cnd.304#iii. (ettakena na tussati is not satisfied with this much); Vv.79#12 (cp. Vv-a.307); Mil.10, Mil.18 (alaṃ ettakena enough of this much); Dhp-a.i.90 (enough this much), Dhp-a.i.93, Dhp-a.i.399 (pl. ettakā); Dhp-a.ii.54 (only one), Dhp-a.ii.174 sq. Vv-a.233 (a little), Vv-a.323<br />■ ettakaṃ kālaṃ a short time (but see also under 2) Ja.i.34; Dhp-a.ii.20</li><li>ever so much (and not less), so much, pl. so many, ever so many, so &amp; so many, such a lot AN.iii.337; Ja.i.207 (pl ettakā), Ja.i.375 (nt. ettakaṃ); Ja.iii.80 (id.), Ja.iii.94 (˚ṃ dhanaṃ such great wealth); Mil.37 (pl.); Dhp-a.i.392, Dhp-a.i.396 (pl f. ettikā), Dhp-a.i.397, Dhp-a.i.398; Dhp-a.ii.14, Dhp-a.ii.89 (pl.), Dhp-a.ii.241 (pl. so many) Vv-a.65 (dhanaṃ)<br />■ ettakaṃ kālaṃ for some time, such a long time (see also above, under 1) Dhp-a.ii.62, Dhp-a.ii.81 Dhp-a.iii.318; Vv-a.330.</li></ol><p>etta + ka, contrasting-comparative function, cp. tattaka</p>

ettakaṃ|ettaka.m|ettakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ettaka.m</span></p><p>so much; so long</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ettaka">ettaka</a></p>

ettakena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i></p><p>by so much; to such an extent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ettaka">ettaka</a></p>

ettato
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb from here, therefore SN.i.185.</p><p>with double suffix for *atra-taḥ</p>

ettha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>here; there; (functioning as loc. of eta(d) in this, in these; in regard to this or these; in this respect; in this case, in that case; in these circumstances.</p>

ettha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb here, in this place; also temporal “now”, &amp; modal “in this case, in this matter” DN.ii.12; SN.v.375; Dhp.174; Snp.61, Snp.171, Snp.424 Snp.441, Snp.502, Snp.1037, &amp; freq. passim.</p><p>= Sk. atra, cp. etta</p>

etthantare
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>in this interval (of time or space); in the time between these; in the area between these; in the meantime</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ettha">ettha</a></p>

etto
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>from here, from there; than this; that way.</p>

etto
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb orig. abl. of etad; from this, from it, thence, hence, out of here Snp.448, Snp.875; Ja.i.223 (opp. ito), Ja.v.498; Pv.i.1#1 Pv.ii.10#4; Dhp-a.ii.80 (ito vā etto vā here &amp; there); Pv-a.103.</p><p>in analogy to ito fr. *et˚, as ito fr. *it˚</p>

ettāvatā|ettaavataa|ettavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ettaavataa</span></p><p>by just so much, with this much; to such an extent; so far, to that extent; thus.</p>

ettāvatā|ettaavataa|ettavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ettaavataa</span></p><p>adverb so far, to that extent, even by this much DN.i.205, DN.i.207; SN.ii.17; Snp.478; Vv.55#6 (cp. Vv-a.248); Pv.iv.1#67 Mil.14; DN-a.i.80; Snp-a.4; Pv-a.243.</p><p>fr. etta = ettaka, cp. kittāvatā: kittaka</p>

etādisa|etaadisa|etadisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaadisa</span></p><p>(~ā &amp; ~ī)n. such (a), of such a kind; such as (with or without correlative).</p>

etādisa|etaadisa|etadisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaadisa</span></p><p>adjective such, such like, of this kind DN.ii.157; Snp.588, Snp.681, Snp.836; Pv.i.9#4; Pv.iv.1#86 (= edisa yathā-vutta-rūpa Pv-a.243); Pv-a.ii.71.</p><p>etad + disa, of <em>dṛś</em>, cp. Sk. etādṛśa</p>

etādisikā|etaadisikaa|etadisika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaadisikaa</span></p><p>such (a), of such a kind.</p>

etāva|etaava|etava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaava</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://etāvat">etāvat</a></p><p>so great, of such extent.</p>

etāvat|etaavat|etavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaavat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://etāvat">etāvat</a></p><p>so great, of such extent.</p>

etāvataparama|etaavataparama|etavataparama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaavataparama</span></p><p>having such as its highest limit, i.e. only as good as such, only so great</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etāvat">etāvat</a></p>

etāvataparamaṃ|etaavataparama.m|etavataparamam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">etaavataparama.m</span></p><p>only to such an extent, only as greatly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://etāvataparama">etāvataparama</a></p>

eva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>eva<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>ind.</p><ol><li>that very…, the same …; exactly that…; just ….</li><li>only, merely, just.</li><li>indeed, really; certainly; absolutely; even, still.</li><li>eva is especially common in combination with other particles, e.g.: c’eva… ca …, both … and ….</li></ol><p>eva<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>ind. like, as; as it were.</p><p>n’eva … na</p><p>(?) neither … nor …</p><p>n’eva … no</p><p>(?) neither … nor …</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eva">eva</a>, <a href="bword://eva">eva</a></p>

eva
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb emphatic part “so, even, just”; very freq. in all contexts &amp; comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->ns.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li><em><a href="bword://eva">eva</a></em> Ja.i.61 (ajj’eva this veryday), Ja.i.278 (tath’eva likewise); Ja.ii.113 (ahaṃ e just I), Ja.ii.154 (ekam e. just one), Ja.ii.160 (attano e. his very own).</li><li>eva often appears with prothetic (sandhi-)y as <em><a href="bword://yeva">yeva</a></em>, most frequently after i and e, but also after the other vowels and ṃ, cp. Ja.i.293, Ja.i.307; Ja.ii.110, Ja.ii.128, Ja.ii.129 Ja.ii.159; Ja.iv.3; Ja.vi.363.</li><li>After ṃ eva also takes the form of <em>ñeva</em>, mostly with assimilation of ṃ to ñ, viz. tañ ñeva Ja.i.223; tasmiñ ñeva Ja.i.139; ahañ ñeva Mil.40.</li><li>After long vowels eva is often shortened to <em><a href="bword://va">va</a></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em> 1 such, like that Snp.279, Snp.280; Iti.108; Ja.ii.352, etc. 2 of such form, beauty or virtue Ja.i.294 Ja.iii.128, etc.</li></ul><p>Vedic eva</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eva">eva</a>, <a href="bword://yeva">yeva</a>, <a href="bword://va">va</a>, <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a></p>

evadassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evadassin">evadassin</a></p><p>seeing in this way; having such insight.</p>

evadassin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evadassin">evadassin</a></p><p>seeing in this way; having such insight.</p>

evam eva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>just so, just like that</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eva">eva</a></p>

evarūpa|evaruupa|evarupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">evaruupa</span></p><p>(~ā &amp; ~ī)n. &amp; neuter of such a kind, like this; such a thing; usually</p><ol><li>referring to something just mentioned or summing up a description just given: such a…; like that; such a thing; or</li><li>introducing a description or definition or list: as follows; such as the following; like this….</li></ol>

evaṃ|eva.m|evam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.m</span></p><ol><li>so in this way; in such a way; like this.</li><li>referring to what follows.</li><li>expressing assent.</li><li>introducing the second part of a comparison (often as evam eva or evam evam).</li></ol>

evaṃ|eva.m|evam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.m</span></p><p>adverb so, thus, in this way, either referring to what precedes or what follows, e.g.</p><ol><li>thus (as mentioned, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vism.528 as “niddiṭṭha-nayanidassana”) DN.i.193 (evaṃ sante this being being so), DN.i.195 (id.); Vin.ii.194 (evaṃ bhante, yes); Ja.i.222; Pv.ii.13#12 evaṃ etaṃ, just so)</li><li>thus (as follows) MN.i.483 (evaṃ me sutaṃ “thus have I heard”)<br />■ Often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with similar emphatic part., as evam eva kho “in just the same way” (in final conclusions) DN.i.104, DN.i.199 DN.i.228, DN.i.237, DN.i.239; in older form evaṃ byā kho (= evam iva kho) Vin.ii.26; Vin.iv.134 = DN-a.i.27; evam evaṃ “just so” DN.i.51; Snp.1115; evaṃ kho DN.i.113; evam pi Snp.1134; evaṃ su DN.i.104; etc. etc.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhin">diṭṭhin</a></em> holding such a view MN.i.484.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81ma">nāma</a></em> having that name MN.i.429.</li></ul><p>Vedic evaṃ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diṭṭhin">diṭṭhin</a>, <a href="bword://nāma">nāma</a></p>

evaṃbhāvi|eva.mbhaavi|evambhavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mbhaavi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃbhāvin">evaṃbhāvin</a></p><p>certain to become like this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃbhāvin|eva.mbhaavin|evambhavin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mbhaavin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃbhāvin">evaṃbhāvin</a></p><p>certain to become like this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃdiṭṭhi|eva.mdi.t.thi|evamditthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mdi.t.thi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃdiṭṭhin">evaṃdiṭṭhin</a></p><p>holding such a belief, having such a theory</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃdiṭṭhika|eva.mdi.t.thika|evamditthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mdi.t.thika</span></p><p>holding such a belief, having such a theory</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃdiṭṭhin|eva.mdi.t.thin|evamditthin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mdi.t.thin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃdiṭṭhin">evaṃdiṭṭhin</a></p><p>holding such a belief, having such a theory</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃgata|eva.mgata|evamgata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mgata</span></p><p>in such a state; of such a kind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃgatika|eva.mgatika|evamgatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mgatika</span></p><p>producing or having a future course of this kind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃjacca|eva.mjacca|evamjacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mjacca</span></p><p>of such and such a class; of this kind of family</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃkāri|eva.mkaari|evamkari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mkaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃkārin">evaṃkārin</a></p><p>acting this way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃkārin|eva.mkaarin|evamkarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mkaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃkārin">evaṃkārin</a></p><p>acting this way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃrūpa|eva.mruupa|evamrupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mruupa</span></p><p>having such a physical form or appearance</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃsaddha|eva.msaddha|evamsaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.msaddha</span></p><p>having such a reputation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃsaññi|eva.msa~n~ni|evamsanni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.msa~n~ni</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃsaññin">evaṃsaññin</a></p><p>perceiving in this way; of such awareness or perception</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃsaññin|eva.msa~n~nin|evamsannin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.msa~n~nin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃsaññin">evaṃsaññin</a></p><p>perceiving in this way; of such awareness or perception</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃvihitaka|eva.mvihitaka|evamvihitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mvihitaka</span></p><p>determined, effected in this way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃvihāri|eva.mvihaari|evamvihari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mvihaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃvihārin">evaṃvihārin</a></p><p>living in such a state or way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃvihārin|eva.mvihaarin|evamviharin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mvihaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃvihārin">evaṃvihārin</a></p><p>living in such a state or way</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃvādi|eva.mvaadi|evamvadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mvaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃvādin">evaṃvādin</a></p><p>professing such a doctrine; declaring a view like this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evaṃvādin|eva.mvaadin|evamvadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">eva.mvaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://evaṃvādin">evaṃvādin</a></p><p>professing such a doctrine; declaring a view like this</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://evaṃ">evaṃ</a></p>

evācāra|evaacaara|evacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">evaacaara</span></p><p>of such behavior, of such conduct.</p>

exertion
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Exertion:</strong> see <em>padhāna</em> <em>viriya</em> <em>magga</em> 6. - Reaching <em>Nibbāna</em> with or without <strong>e.;</strong> see: <em>anāgāmi</em></p>

existence
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Existence:</strong> <em>bhava</em> - The 5 groups of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>khandha</em> - The 4 substrata of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>upadhi</em> - Courses of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>gati</em> - Wheel of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>samsāra</em>. - Craving for <strong>e.:</strong> <em>bhava-tanhā</em> see: <em>tanhā</em> - The 3 characteristics of <strong>e.:</strong> <em>ti-lakkhana</em></p>

expression
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Expression:</strong> bodily and verbal: see: <em>viññatti</em></p>

extremes
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Extremes:</strong> the two <strong>e.</strong> and the middle path; see: <em>majjhima</em> - <em>patipadā.</em></p>

eye-consciousness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>visual-consciousness:</strong> <em>cakkhu-viññāna</em> see: <em>dhātu</em>, <em>khandha</em></p>

eye
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Eye:</strong> 5 kinds, see: <em>cakkhu</em> - Visual organ, see: <em>āyatana</em>.</p>

eye-organ
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Eye-organ:</strong> see: <em>āyatana</em></p>

eḷa|e.la|ela
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.la</span></p><p>in <em><a href="bword://e%E1%B8%B7am%C5%ABga">eḷamūga</a></em> deaf &amp; dumb AN.ii.252; AN.iii.436; AN.iv.226; Mil.20, Mil.251 (cp. <em>Mil trsl.</em> ii.71). A rather strange use and expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of eḷamūga (with ref. to a snake “spitting”) we find at Ja.iii.347, where it is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “eḷa-paggharantena mukhena eḷamūgaṃ” i.e. called eḷamūga because of the saliva (foam?) dripping from its mouth, variant reading elamukha<br />■ Cp. <em>neḷa &amp; aneḷa</em>.</p><p>Sk. enas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷamūga">eḷamūga</a></p>

eḷagala|e.lagala|elagala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lagala</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ane%E1%B8%B7a">aneḷa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aneḷa">aneḷa</a></p>

eḷagalā|e.lagalaa|elagala
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lagalaa</span></p><p>A type of plant S.iii.6.</p>

eḷagalā|e.lagalaa|elagala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lagalaa</span></p><p>the plant Cassia Tora (cp. Sk. eḍagaja the ringworm-shrub, Cassia Alata, after Halāyudha), Ja.iii.222 (= kambojī C.).</p><p>dial.?</p>

eḷagalāgumba|e.lagalaagumba|elagalagumba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lagalaagumba</span></p><p>an eḷagala thicket.</p>

eḷagga|e.lagga|elagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lagga</span></p><p>in kāmāmis˚ at Pv-a.107 is to be read kāmāmise lagga˚.</p>

eḷaka|e.laka|elaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.laka</span></p><p>Domestic Sheep, sometimes <em>eḷahā</em>, <em><a href="bword://e%E1%B8%B7aka">eḷaka</a></em> or <em>elikā</em> A.i.251 D.i.9 Ja.iii.480 S.ii.228, hollow-horned ruminants of the order Ovis, of which several species and breeds are found in northern India. Ewes were called <em>urāṇi</em> Ja.v.241 and rams <em>meṇḍa</em> or <em>urabbha</em>. Sheep were known for their long fleece which was used to make cloth and carpets S.ii.228 M.i.228. Mutton must have been eaten too because there is mention of sheep butchers M.i.343 S.ii.256. Bows were sometimes made out of ram’s horns Ja.ii.61 Ja.vi.68. The Buddha considered sheep to be gentle harmless creatures, like cows Sn.309. A group of monks who spent the three months of the rainy season without doing any spiritual practice other than maintaining strict silence were rebuked by the Buddha as being like dumb sheep Vin.i.159.In the Jātaka, there is a story of a proud brahmin who was flattered when a ram lowered its head to him only to find that it butted him rather than paid him reverence. See <em>Uṇṇa</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a>, Uṇṇa</p>

eḷaka-m-antaraṃ|e.laka-m-antara.m|elaka-m-antaram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.laka-m-antara.m</span></p><p>eḷaka-m-antaraṃ</p><p>ind. within the threshold; across the threshold.</p>

eḷaka|e.laka|elaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.laka</span></p><p>ram; a male goat, a billy goat.</p>

eḷaka|e.laka|elaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.laka</span></p><p>Eḷaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a threshold (see Morris, J.P.T.S. 1887, 146) Vin.ii.149 (˚pādaka-pītha, why not “having feet resembling those of a ram”? Cp. <em>Vin Texts</em> iii.165 “a chair raised on a pedestal”); DN.i.166; AN.i.295; AN.ii.206. The word its meaning seems uncertain.</p><p>?</p><p>Eḷaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a ram, a wild goat Snp.309; Vism.500 (in simile); Ja.i.166; Pp-a.233 (= urabbha)<br />■ f. <em>eḷakā</em> SN.ii.228, <em>eḷakī</em> Thig.438, <em>eḷikī</em> Ja.iii.481.</p><p>Sk. eḍaka</p>

eḷakacamma|e.lakacamma|elakacamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakacamma</span></p><p>sheep’s skin; goat’s skin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a></p>

eḷakaloma|e.lakaloma|elakaloma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakaloma</span></p><p>sheep’s hair; goat’s hair</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a></p>

eḷakasamāna|e.lakasamaana|elakasamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakasamaana</span></p><p>like sheep or goats</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a></p>

eḷakasaṃvāsa|e.lakasa.mvaasa|elakasamvasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakasa.mvaasa</span></p><p>living like a sheep; a sheep’s life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷaka">eḷaka</a></p>

eḷakā|e.lakaa|elaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakaa</span></p><p>an ewe; a female goat; a nanny-goat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷakī">eḷakī</a></p>

eḷakī|e.lakii|elaki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lakii</span></p><p>an ewe; a female goat; a nanny-goat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eḷakā">eḷakā</a></p>

eḷamūga|e.lamuuga|elamuga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.lamuuga</span></p><p>unintelligent, stupid (as a sheep); (perhaps with association with impaired speech)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://elamūga">elamūga</a></p>

eḷeyya|e.leyya|eleyya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.leyya</span></p><p>A rājā, probably of Māgadha. He was a devout follower of Uddaka-Rāmaputta. In his retinue were Yamaka, Moggalla, Ugga, Nāvindakī, Gandhabba, and Aggivessa, all of whom were also followers of the same teacher. AN.ii.180<em>f.</em></p>

eḷāluka|e.laaluka|elaluka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.laaluka</span></p><p>Elāluka (Eḷāluka)</p><p>neuter a kind of cucumber(?) Vv.33#29; Ja.i.205; Ja.v.37; Dhp-a.i.278.</p><p>etym.?</p>

eṇeyya|e.neyya|eneyya
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.neyya</span></p><p>Sometimes also <em>eṇeyyaka</em> or <em>eṇimiga</em>. Uncertain but perhaps another name for the Blackbuck doe, sometimes also <em><a href="bword://e%E1%B9%87i">eṇi</a></em> or <em>eṇimiga</em> A.i.48 D.iii.157 J.v.416 Thi.1135. See <em>Miga</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eṇi">eṇi</a>, Miga</p>

eṇeyya|e.neyya|eneyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.neyya</span></p><p>DN.iii.157; Ja.vi.537 sq., &amp; <em>Eṇeyyaka</em> AN.i.48; AN.ii.122; Ja.v.155 Cnd.604 = eṇi.</p>

eṇeyyajaṅgha|e.neyyaja"ngha|eneyyajangha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.neyyaja"ngha</span></p><p>with legs like the black antelope’s.</p>

eṇeyyaka|e.neyyaka|eneyyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.neyyaka</span></p><p>kind of torture.</p>

eṇi|e.ni|eni
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.ni</span></p><p>The Blackbuck doe. According to Pāṇinī <em>eṇi</em> is a term for a female deer. One of the 32 special characteristics of a Mahāpurisa is that he has legs like an <em>eṇi</em> D.iii.143. See <em>Miga</em>.</p><p>See also: Miga</p>

eṇi|e.ni|eni
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.ni</span></p><p>kind of antelope, only two foll. compounds: <em>-jaṅgha</em> “limbed like the antelope” (one of the physical characteristics of the Superman) DN.ii.17; DN.iii.143 DN.iii.156; MN.ii.136; SN.i.16; Snp.165; <em>-miga</em> the eṇi deer Ja.v.416; Snp-a.207, Snp-a.217.</p><p>etym.? dial.</p>

eṇikūla|e.nikuula|enikula
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.nikuula</span></p><p>A town on the river Eṇi. One of Baka’s good deeds which brought him rebirth in the Brahma-world was that of having set free the inhabitants when the village was raided. SN.i.143</p><p>4town</p>

eṭṭha|e.t.tha|ettha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.t.tha</span></p><p>see pariy˚; do. ˚eṭṭhi.</p><p>pp. of ā + <em>iṣ</em></p>

eṭṭhi|e.t.thi|etthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">e.t.thi</span></p><p>desire, wish, in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with gaveṭṭhi pariyeṭṭhi etc. Vb.353 = Vism.23, Vism.29 etc.</p><p>fr. eṭṭha, ā + <em>iṣ</em>, cp. Sk. eṣṭi</p>

factors:
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Factors:</strong> mental: see: <em>cetasika</em> - <strong>F.</strong> of absorption, see: <em>jhāna</em> - <strong>F.</strong> of enlightenment, see: <em>bojjhanga</em>.</p><p><strong>abilities = Abilities:</strong> <em>indriya</em>, see also <em>paccaya</em> 16.</p>

fading away
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[fading away]]Fading away (of craving) = disillusion:</strong> see: <em>virāga</em>.</p>

faith-devotee
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Faith-devotee:</strong> and <strong>Faith-liberated one:</strong> see: <em>ariya-puggala</em> B.</p>

faith
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Faith:</strong> <em>Saddhā</em></p>

faithful-natured
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Faithful-natured:</strong> <em>saddhā</em> - <em>carita</em> see: <em>carita</em></p>

fatalism
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Fatalism:</strong> see: <em>ditthi</em></p>

favour
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Favour:</strong> 4 ways of showing <em>sangaha-vatthu</em></p>

feeling
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Feeling:</strong> <em>vedanā</em>, further see: <em>khandha</em> - Contemplation of <strong>f.:</strong> <em>vedanānupassanā</em> see: <em>satipatthāna</em>.</p>

femininity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Femininity:</strong> s <em>bhāva</em> <em>indriya</em></p><p><strong>fetters = Mental chains:</strong> The 10 <strong>f.</strong> binding to existence; see: <em>samyojana</em></p>

fermentations
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Fermentations:</strong> see: <em>āsava</em>.</p>

few wishes
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[few wishes]]Few wishes:</strong> see: <em>appicchatā</em>.</p>

fine-material sphere
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[fine-material sphere]]Fine-material sphere:</strong> or <strong>[[fine-material sphere]]Fine-material world:</strong> see: <em>avacara</em> <em>loka</em> Absorptions of the: <em>rūpa-jjhāna</em> see: <em>jhāna</em></p>

fire-element
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Fire-element:</strong> see: <em>dhātu</em> I.</p>

five-group existence
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[five-group existence]]Five-group existence:</strong> <em>pañca-vokāra-bhava</em></p>

fivefold sense-door
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[fivefold sense-door]]Fivefold sense-door:</strong> Directing to the: <em>pañcadvārāvajjana</em> see: <em>viññāna-kicca</em>.</p>

fixed destiny
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[fixed destiny]]Fixed destiny:</strong> see: <em>niyata</em> - <em>micchā</em> - <em>ditthi</em>, <em>niyata-puggala</em>.</p>

fixity
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Fixity:</strong> see: <em>niyama</em> <em>tathatā</em> <em>dhamma-tthiti-ñāna</em></p>

floods
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Floods:</strong> the 4: <em>ogha</em> are identical with the 4 fermentations <em>āsava</em>.</p>

food
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Food:</strong> material: is one of the 4 nutriments <em>āhāra</em>. Foodproduced materiality, see: <em>samutthāna</em> - Refusing all further <strong>f.,</strong> see: <em>dhutānga</em> - Loathsomeness of <strong>f.</strong> see: <em>āhārepatikkūla</em> - <em>saññā</em></p>

foolish babble
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[foolish babble]]Foolish babble:</strong> <em>sampha</em> - <em>ppalāpa</em> see: kamma, <em>kamma -pat ha</em> I; cf. <em>tiracchāna</em> - <em>kathā.</em></p>

forbearance
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Forbearance:</strong> <em>khanti</em></p>

forest-dweller
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Forest-dweller:</strong> the ascetic practice for the: see: <em>dhutānga</em></p><p><strong>formation = Construction:</strong> <em>sankhāra</em></p>

foundation
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Foundation:</strong> <em>nissaya</em> one of the 24 conditions <em>paccaya</em>,. Wrong <strong>f.</strong> of morality, see: <em>nissaya</em> <strong>f.</strong> of sympathy: <em>sangaha-vatthu</em> <strong>f. f</strong> orming absorptions: <em>pādaka-jjhāna</em>, - <strong>f.</strong> of an Arahat’s mentality: see: <em>adhitthāna</em></p>

foundations of awareness or mindfulness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[foundations of awareness or mindfulness]]Foundations of awareness or mindfulness:</strong> the 4: <em>satipatthāna</em>.</p>

four-group existence
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[four-group existence]]Four-group existence:</strong> <em>catu-vokāra-bhava</em></p>

freedom of will
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[freedom of will]]Freedom of will:</strong> problem of the: cf. <em>paticcasamuppāda</em> X.</p>

friend
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Friend:</strong> noble: <em>kalyāna-mitta</em></p>

frivolous talk
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[frivolous talk]]Frivolous talk:</strong> cf. <em>tiracchāna</em> - <em>kathā</em> <em>kamma-patha</em> I, 7, kamma.</p>

fruition
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Fruition:</strong> result of supra-mundane path: <em>phala</em> see: <em>ariya-puggala</em> A.</p>

fruits of monk-life
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[fruits of monk-life]]Fruits of monk-life:</strong> <em>sāmañña-phala</em></p>

full comprehension
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[full comprehension]]Full comprehension:</strong> <em>pariññā</em></p>

functional consciousness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[functional consciousness]]Functional consciousness:</strong> or consciousness functioning independently of kamma: <em>kiriya</em> - <em>citta</em>, see <em>viññāna-kicca</em></p>

functions of consciousness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[functions of consciousness]]Functions of consciousness:</strong> <em>viññāna-kicca</em>.</p>

ga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>˚Ga</p><p>adj., only as ending: going. See e.g. atiga, anuga, antalikkha˚, ura˚, pāra˚, majjha˚, samīpa˚ hattha˚. It also appears as <em>˚gu</em>, e.g. in addha˚, anta˚ paṭṭha˚, pāra˚, veda˚<br />■ dugga (m. &amp; nt.) a difficult road Dhp.327 = Mil.379; Pv.ii.7#8 (= duggamana-ṭṭhāna Pv-a.102); Pv.ii.9#25; Ja.ii.385.</p><p>fr. <em>gam</em></p>

gabbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>womb of humans and animals.</li><li>the fetus.</li><li>an inner room or an apartment; (in a vihara) a separate room or cell.</li></ol>

gabbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>interior, cavity (loc. gabbhe in the midst of: angāra˚ Ja.iii.55); an inner room, private chamber, bedroom, cell. Of a Vihāra Vin.ii.303; Vin.iii.119; Vin.iv.45; Vv-a.188; Vv-a.220<br />■ Ja.i.90 (siri˚ royal chamber); Ja.iii.276; Vv.78#5 (= ovaraka Vv-a.304); Dhp-a.i.397; Mil.10, Mil.295. See also anto˚.</li><li>the swelling of the (pregnant) womb, the womb (cp kucchi). ˚ṃ upeti to be born Dhp.325 = Thag.17 Ne.34, Ne.129; ˚ṃ upapajjati to be born again Dhp.126 gabbhā gabbhaṃ… dukkaṃ nigacchanti from womb to womb (i.e. from birth to birth) Snp.278; gabbhato paṭṭhāya from the time of birth Ja.i.290, Ja.i.293. As a symbol of defilement g. is an epithet of kāma AN.iv.289, etc</li><li>the contents of the womb, i.e. the embryo foetus: dasa māse ˚ṃ kucchinā pariharitvā having nourished the foetus in the womb for 10 months DN.ii.14 dibbā gabbhā DN.i.229; on g. as contained in kucchi foetus in utero, see Ja.i.50 (kucchimhi patiṭṭhito), Ja.i.134, Ja.ii.2; Ja.iv.482; MN.i.265; Mil.123 (gabbhassa avakkanti); Dhp-a.i.3, Dhp-a.i.47; Dhp-a.ii.261<br />■ Pv.i.6#7; Pv-a.31; gabbho vuṭṭhāsi the child was delivered Vin.ii.278; itthi-gabbho &amp; purisa˚ female &amp; male child Ja.i.51; gabbhaṃ pāteti to destroy the foetus Vin.ii.268; apagatagabbhā (adj. having had a miscarriage Vin.ii.129; mūḷha-gabbhā id. MN.ii.102 (+ visatā˚); paripuṇṇa-gabbhā ready to be delivered Ja.i.52; Pv-a.86; saññi˚ a conscious foetus DN.i.54 = MN.i.518 = SN.iii.212; sannisinna-gabbhā having conceived Vin.ii.278.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://avakkanti">avakkanti</a></em> (gabbhe okkanti Cnd.304#1) conception DN.iii.103, DN.iii.231; Vism.499, Vism.500 (˚okkanti); this is followed by gabbhe ṭhiti &amp; gabbhe vuṭṭhāna, see Cnd;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81saya">āsaya</a></em> the impurities of childbirth Pv.iii.5#3 (= ˚mala)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87a">karaṇa</a></em> effecting a conception Snp.927;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> leaving the womb, in putte gabbhagate when the child was born Pv-a.112;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dv%C4%81ra">dvāra</a></em> the door of the bed-chamber Ja.i.62;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://parihara%E1%B9%87a">pariharaṇa</a></em> = next Vism.500;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://parih%C4%81ra">parihāra</a></em> “the protection of the embryo,” a ceremony performed when a woman became pregnant Ja.ii.2; Dhp-a.i.4;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81tana">pātana</a></em> the destruction of the embryo, abortion, an abortive preparation Vin.iii.83 sq.; Pv.i.6#6 (akariṃ); Pv-a.31 (dāpesi); Dhp-a.i.47 (˚bhesajja);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mala">mala</a></em> the uncleanness of delivery, i.e. all accompanying dirty matter Pv-a.80, Pv-a.173 (as food for Petas), Pv-a.198; Dhp-a.iv.215;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://avakkanti">avakkanti</a></em> (gabbhe okkanti Cnd.304#1) conception DN.iii.103, DN.iii.231; Vism.499, Vism.500 (˚okkanti); this is followed by gabbhe ṭhiti &amp; gabbhe vuṭṭhāna, see Cnd;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81na">vuṭṭhāna</a></em> (nt.) childbirth delivery Ja.i.52; Dhp-a.i.399; Dhp-a.ii.261;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://seyy%C4%81">seyyā</a></em> (f.) the womb; only in expressions relating to reincarnation as: na punar eti (or upeti) gabbhaseyyaṃ “he does not go into another womb,” of an Arahant Snp.29, Snp.152 Snp.535; Vv.53#24; and gabbhaseyyaka (adj.) one who enters another womb Vb.413 sq.; Vism.272, Vism.559, Vism.560 Bdhd 77, 78.</li></ul><p>Vedic garbha, either to <em>*gelbh</em>, as in Lat. galba, Goth. kalbo, Ohg. kalba, E. calf, or <em>*gṷe bh</em>, as in Gr.δελφύς womb, αδελφός sharing the womb, brother δέλφας young pig; cp. <em>*gelt</em> in Goth. kilpei womb. Ags cild, Ger. kind, E. child. Meaning: a cavity, a hollow or, seen from its outside, a swelling</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakkanti">avakkanti</a>, <a href="bword://āsaya">āsaya</a>, <a href="bword://karaṇa">karaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://dvāra">dvāra</a>, <a href="bword://pariharaṇa">pariharaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://parihāra">parihāra</a>, <a href="bword://pātana">pātana</a>, <a href="bword://mala">mala</a>, <a href="bword://avakkanti">avakkanti</a>, <a href="bword://vuṭṭhāna">vuṭṭhāna</a>, <a href="bword://seyyā">seyyā</a></p>

gabbhakaraṇa|gabbhakara.na|gabbhakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbhakara.na</span></p><p>(the art of) causing or aiding conception.</p>

gabbhapātana|gabbhapaatana|gabbhapatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbhapaatana</span></p><p>causing a miscarriage; a means of causing a miscarriage, esp. a concoction.</p>

gabbhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>cavern Snp.416 (giri˚); Vv.63#5 (giri˚).</p><p>Derivation uncertain. Cp. Sk. gahvara</p>

gabbhaseyyā|gabbhaseyyaa|gabbhaseyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbhaseyyaa</span></p><p>the womb.</p>

gabbhavokkanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>coming into being of the fetus in the womb; conception.</p>

gabbhaṃ gaṇhāti|gabbha.m ga.nhaati|gabbham ganhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbha.m ga.nhaati</span></p><p>conceives, becomes pregnant.</p>

gabbhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine (mfn.)</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://gabbhin">gabbhin</a></p><p>pregnant; filled; swelling; (f.) a pregnant woman.</p>

gabbhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine (mfn.)</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://gabbhin">gabbhin</a></p><p>pregnant; filled; swelling; (f.) a pregnant woman.</p>

gabbhinī|gabbhinii|gabbhini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbhinii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gabbhin">gabbhin</a></p>

gabbhinī|gabbhinii|gabbhini
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gabbhinii</span></p><p>adjective feminine pregnant, enceinte Vin.ii.268; SN.iii.202; Ja.i.151, Ja.i.290; Ja.iv.37; Pv.i.6#6; Pv-a.31, Pv-a.82: Vv-a.110 (-bhāva); in combination g˚ pāyamānā purisantaragatā (pregnant, lactating &amp; having had sex. intercourse AN.i.295 = AN.ii.206 = MN.i.77, MN.i.238, MN.i.307, MN.i.342 = Pp.55; with utunī anutunī (menstruating &amp; having ceased to menstruate) AN.iii.226 sq.</p>

gabbita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective proud, arrogant Ja.ii.340 (˚bhāva = issariya); Ja.iii.264 (˚sabhāva = dittasabhāva); Sum. V. on DN.iii.153 (= avamata).</p>

gaccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>small tree; a bush.</p>

gaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>shrub, a bush, usually together with <em><a href="bword://lat%C4%81">latā</a></em>, creeper; <em><a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a></em>, tree, e.g. Cnd.235, i<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; Ja.i.73; Mil.268; Vism.182 (described on p. Vism.183). With dāya, wood AN.iv.74 puppha˚ a flowering shrub Ja.i.120; khuddaka˚-vana a wood of small shrubs Ja.v.37<br />■ Pv-a.274; Vv-a.301 (-gumba, brushwood, underwood); Dhp-a.i.171 (-pothana-ṭṭhāna); Dhp-a.iv.78 (-mūla).</p><p>not = Sk. kaccha, grass-land, as Morris, <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1893, 16. The passage Ja.iii.287 stands with gaccha variant reading kaccha for gaccha at AN.iv.74; g˚ for k˚ at Snp.20</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://latā">latā</a>, <a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a></p>

gacchamāna|gacchamaana|gacchamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gacchamaana</span></p><p>going; moving; walking.</p>

gacchanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n. of gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></p>

gacchanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i></p><p>of gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></p>

gacchasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i></p><p>of gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></p>

gacchat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://gacchat">gacchat</a></p><p>of gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></p>

gacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li><ol><li>(of people, animals, rivers, roads etc) goes; moves, walks; goes away, leaves; goes to (+ acc. or santike/santikaṃ etc); often with absol., e.g. ādāya ~ati, gahetvā ~ati, goes with, takes; pahāya ~ati, goes off without, leaves behind.</li><li>goes to another existence, another birth etc.</li><li>follows a course; follows a future course.</li><li>goes to an activity; goes to do something.</li><li>goes to in a sexual intercourse; has intercourse with.</li></ol></li><li><p>goes to a state of condition; undergoes, reaches; obtains.</p></li><li><p>relies on.</p></li><li><p>the first person present is not rarely used to express an immediate or near future sense: I am going; I am going to go; we are about to go.</p></li></ol>

gacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>The three formations (described below in the etymology) are represented in Pāli as follows.</p><ol><li><p>gacch˚, in<br />pres gacchati;<br />imper gaccha &amp; gacchāhi;<br />pot gacche (Dhp.46, Dhp.224) &amp; gaccheyya;<br />ppr gacchanto,<br />med gacchamāna;<br />fut (second) gacchissati;<br />aor agacchi (Vv-a.307 variant reading agañchi)</p></li><li><p>gam˚ in three variations; viz</p><ol><li><em>gam˚</em>, in<br />pres. caus gameti;<br />fut gamissati;<br />aor 3 sg. agamā (Snp.408, Snp.976; Vv.79#7; Mhvs.vii.9), agamāsi &amp; gami (Pv.ii.8#6) 1 pl. agamiṃhase (Pv.ii.3#10), pl agamuṃ (Snp.290), agamaṃsu &amp; gamiṃsu; prohib. mā gami;<br />ger gamya (verse 31);<br />grd gamanīya (Kp-a.223)<br />■ See also der. gama, gamana, gāmika, gāmin</li><li><em>gan˚</em>, in<br />aor agañchi (on this form see Trenckner <em>Notes</em>, p. 71 sq)<br />■ In n’āgañchi Ja.iii.190 it belongs to ā + gam); pres<br />aor gañchisi (Snp.665);<br />inf gantuṃ<br />ger gantvā;<br />grd gantabba.<br />■ See also der. gantar.</li><li><em>ga˚</em>, in<br />pp gata.<br />■ See also ga, gati, gatta.</li></ol></li><li><p>gā˚ in<br />pret agā (Pv.ii.3#22),<br />aor 3rd pl. agū (= Sk. ˚uḥ), in ajjhagū, anvagū (q.v.)</p></li></ol><h3>Meanings and Use</h3><ol><li><p>to go, to be in motion, to move, to go on (opp. to stand still, tiṭṭhati). Freq in combination with tiṭṭhati nisīdati seyyaṃ kappeti “to go to stand, sit down &amp; lie down,” to denote all positions and every kind of behaviour; Cnd. s.v. gacchati. evaṃ kāle gacchante, as time went on Ja.iii.52, or evaṃ g˚ kāle (Pv-a.54, Pv-a.75) or gacchante gacchante kāle Dhp-a.i.319; gacchati = paleti Pv-a.56; vemakoṭi gantvā pahari (whilst moving) Dhp-a.iii.176.</p></li><li><p>to go, to walk (opp. to run, dhāvati) Dhp-a.i.389.</p></li><li><p>to go away, to go out, to go forth (opp. to stay, or to come āgacchati): agamāsi he went Pv.ii.8#6; yo maṃ icchati anvetu yo vā n’ icchati gacchatu “who wants me may come, who does not may go” Snp.564; āgacchantānañ ca gacchantānañ ca pamāṇaṃ n’ atthi “there was no end of all who came &amp; went” Ja.ii.133; gacchāma “let us go” Ja.i.263; gaccha dāni go away now! Ja.ii.160 gaccha re muṇḍaka Vism.343; gacchāhi go then Ja.i.151, Ja.i.222; mā gami do not go away! Ja.iv.2; pl. mā gamittha Ja.i.263; gacchanto on his way Ja.i.255, Ja.i.278 agamaṃsu they went away Ja.iv.3; gantukāma anxious to go Ja.i.222, Ja.i.292; kattha gamissasi where are you going? (opp. agacchasi) Dhp-a.iii.173; kahaṃ gacchissatha id. Ja.ii.128; kuhiṃ gamissati where is he going Snp.411, Snp.412.</p></li><li><p>with acc. or substitute: to go to, to have access to, to arrive or get at (with the aim of the movement or the object of the intention); hence fig to come to know, to experience, to realize</p><ol><li>with <em>acc.</em> of direction: Rājagahaṃ gami he went to R Pv.ii.8#6; Devadaha-nagaraṃ gantuṃ Ja.i.52; gacchām ahaṃ Kusināraṃ I shall go to K. DN.ii.128; Suvaṇṇabhūmiṃ gacchanti they intended to go (“were going” to S. Ja.iii.188; migavaṃ g. to go hunting Ja.i.149; janapadaṃ gamissāma Ja.ii.129; paradāraṃ g. to approach another man’s wife Dhp.246</li><li>with <em>adverbs</em> of direction or purpose (atthāya): santikaṃ (or santike) gacchati to go near a person (in gen.), pitu s. gacchāma Dhp-a.iii.172; devāna santike gacche Dhp.224 santikaṃ also Ja.i.152: Ja.ii.159, etc. Kathaṃ tattha gamissāmi how shall I get there? Ja.i.159; Ja.ii.159; tattha agamāsi he went there Ja.ii.160. dukkhānubhavanatthāya gacchamānā “going away for the purpose of undergoing suffering” Ja.iv.3; vohāratthāya gacchāmi I am going out (= fut.) on business Ja.ii.133<br />■ Similarly (fig.) in foll. expressions (op. “to go to Heaven,” etc. = to live or experience a heavenly life, op. next); Nirayaṃ gamissati Ja.vi.368; saggaṃ lokaṃ g. Ja.i.152; gacche pāram apārato Snp.1129, in this sense interpreted at Cnd.223 as adhigacchati phusati sacchikaroti, to experience-Sometimes with <em>double acc.:</em> Bhagavantaṃ saraṇaṃ gacchāmi “I entrust myself to Bh.” Vin.i.16-Cp. also phrases as atthangacchati to go home, to set, to disappear; antarā-gacchati to come between, to obstruct.</li></ol></li><li><p><em>to go</em> as a stronger expression for <em>to be</em>, i.e. to behave, to have existence, to fare (cp. Ger. es geht gut, Fr. cela <em>va</em> bien = it <em>is</em> good). Here belongs gati “existence,” as mode of existing, element, sphere of being, and out of this use is developed the periphrastic use of <em>gam˚</em>, which places it on the same level with the verb “to be” (see b)</p><ol><li>sugatiṃ gamissasi you will go to the state of well-being, i.e. Heaven Vin.ii.195; Iti.77; opp. duggatiṃ gacchanti Dhp.317–Dhp.319 maggaṃ na jānanti yena gacchanti subbatā (which will fall to their share) Snp.441; gamissanti yattha gantvā na socare “they will go where one sorrows not” Snp.445; Vv.51#4; yan ca karoti… tañ ca ādāya gacchati “whatever a man does that he will take with him SN.i.93</li><li><em>periphrastic</em> (w. ger. of governing verb) nagaraṃ pattharitvā gaccheyya “would spread through the town” Ja.i.62; pariṇāmaṃ gaccheyya “could be digested” DN.ii.127; sīhacammaṃ ādāya agamaṃsu “they took the lion’s skin away with them” Ja.ii.110 itthiṃ pahāya gamissati shall leave the woman alone Ja.vi.348; sve gahetvā gamissāmi “I shall come for it tomorrow” Mil.48.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Vedic gacchati, a desiderative (future) formation from <em>*gṷem</em> “I am intent upon going,” i.e. I go, with the foll. bases 1 Future-present *gṷemskéti → *gaścati → Sk. gacchati = Gr. βάσκω (to βαινω). In meaning cp. i, Sk. emi, Gr. εἰμι “I shall go” &amp; in form also Sk. pṛcchati = Lat. porsco “I want to know,” Vedic icchati “to desire.” 2 Present *gṷemi̯o = Sk. gamati = Gr. βαίνω, Lat. venio, Goth. qiman, Ohg koman, E. come; and non-present formations as Osk kúmbened, Sk. gata = Lat. ventus; gantu = (ad) ventus 3 *gṷā, which is correlated to *stā, in Pret. Sk ágām, Gr. ε ̓́βην, cp. β ̈ημα</p>

