gopītakaṃ|gopiitaka.m|gopitakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gopiitaka.m</span></p><p>adverb drinking like a cow; as a cow drinks.</p>

gorakkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn.) a</i></p><p>cowherd.</p>

gorasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>dairy product, milk, and its derivatives.</p>

goravaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>mooing; lowing.</p>

gosamita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>like cattle, equal to cattle.</p>

gosiṅgasālavanadāya|gosi"ngasaalavanadaaya|gosingasalavanadaya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gosi"ngasaalavanadaaya</span></p><p>Gosiṅgasālavanadāya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A forest tract near Nādikā. Once, when Anuruddha, Nandiya and Kimbila were living there, they were visited by the Buddha, who came from Giñjakāvasatha. The result of the visit was the preaching of the Cūḷagosiṅga Sutta. MN.i.205<em>f.</em> On another occasion, when the Buddha was staying there with many eminent monks, among whom were Sāriputta, Moggallāna and others, their conversation led to the preaching of the Mahāgosiṅga Sutta. MN.i.212<em>f.</em></p><p>25.78382,85.271763monasteryGosiṅgasālavanadāya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A forest tract near Vesāli. When the Buddha was living in the Kūṭāgārasāla, in the Mahāvana, he was visited by many people and there was great disturbance. In order to find quiet and solitude, certain monks, among whom were Cāla, Upacāla, Kakkaṭa, Kaḷimbha, Nikata and Kaṭissaha, retired into this forest. AN.v.133</p><p>25.9815,85.190733park</p>

gosāla |gosaala |gosala 
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gosaala </span></p><p>Gosāla</p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse speaks of how after eating honey and rice he returns to his hillside to meditate. Thag.23</p>

gosīla|gosiila|gosila
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gosiila</span></p><p>the practice of living or behaving like a cow.</p>

gotama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Gotama<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->BuddhaSiddhatthaSakyamunīTathāgataAṅgīrasa</p><p>He was a Sākiyan, son of Suddhodana and of Mahā Māyā, Suddhodana’s chief consort, and he belonged to the Gotama-gotta. At age 29 he departed from his home to seek Awakening. Six years later he realized this goal. He spent the remaining 45 years of his life teaching, and died at the age of eighty in Kusināra.</p>

gotama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>belonging to the gotama clan.</p>

gotamadvāra|gotamadvaara|gotamadvara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotamadvaara</span></p><p>The gate by which the Buddha left Pāṭaligāma, after having eaten there at the invitation of Sunidha and Vassakāra. Vin.i.230 Ud.viii.6 DN.ii.89</p><p>Pāṭaliputta2building</p>

gotamaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A class of ascetics, enumerated in a list of such classes. AN.iii.276</p>

gotamakacetiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>GotamakacetiyaGotamaka Cetiya</p><p>A shrine near and to the south of Vesāli. DN.iii.9 It was considered one of the beautiful spots of that town DN.ii.102 DN.ii.118 and the Buddha stayed there several times. During one such stay, he laid down the rule which allowed monks the use of three robes; he himself felt cold during the night and had to wear extra clothing. Vin.i.288 Vin.iii.195</p><p>25.920467,85.1206972shrine</p>

gotamatittha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>The ford by which the Buddha crossed the Ganges, after leaving Pāṭaligāma. Vin.i.230 Ud.viii.6 DN.ii.89</p><p>25.62171,85.186612ford</p>

gotamī|gotamii|gotami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotamii</span></p><p>woman of the gotama clan.</p>

gotrabhū|gotrabhuu|gotrabhu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotrabhuu</span></p><p>or m. neuter destroying the lineage.</p>

gotrabhū|gotrabhuu|gotrabhu
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotrabhuu</span></p><p><strong>Gotrabhū:</strong> lit. ‘who has entered the lineage of the Noble Ones’, i.e. the Matured One.</p><p>I. ‘change-of-lineage-Moment’ <em>gotrabhū</em> - <em>citta</em> is the last of the 4 impulse moments <em>javana</em> cf. <em>viññāna-kicca</em> immediately preceding the entering into an absorption <em>jhāna</em> or into one of the supra-mundane paths see: <em>ariya-puggala</em> A.. Cf. <em>visuddhi</em> VII.</p><p>II. The ‘Matured One’.;He who is endowed with those things, immediately upon which follows the entrance into the noble path <em>ariya-magga</em>, this person is called a ‘Matured One’.; Pug 10. In the Com. to this passage it is said:;He who through perceiving Nibbāna, leaves behind the whole multitude of worldlings <em>puthujjana</em>, the family of worldlings, the circle of worldlings, the designation of a worldling and enters into the multitude of the Noble Ones, the family of the Noble Ones, the circle of the Noble Ones, and obtains the designation of a Noble One, such a being is called a Matured One.; By this state of consciousness is meant the lightning-like transitional stage between the state of a worldling and that of a <em>sotāpanna</em> see: <em>ariya-puggala</em> - Gotrabhū is mentioned in this sense, i.e. as 9th <em>ariya-puggala,</em> in A. IX, 10; X, 16.</p>

gotrabhū|gotrabhuu|gotrabhu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotrabhuu</span></p><p>“become of the lineage”; a technical term used from the end of the Nikāya period to designate one, whether layman or bhikkhu, who, as converted, was no longer of the worldlings (puthujjanā), but of the Ariyas, having Nibbāna as his aim. It occurs in a supplementary Sutta in the Majjhima (MN.iii.256), and in another found in two versions, at the end of the Anguttara (AN.iv.373 and AN.v.23). Defined at Pp.12, Pp.13 &amp; Vism.138 amplified at Pts.i.66–Pts.i.68, frequent in P (Tika-p. 154 sq. 165, 324 etc.), mentioned at Vv-a.155. On the use of <em><a href="bword://gotrabh%C5%AB">gotrabhū</a></em> in medieval psychology see Aung, in <em>Compendium</em> 66–68. Compare the use of upanissaya at Ja.i.235-˚ñāṇa, Pp-a.184; Vism.673. Ā˚ Vism.683.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gotrabhū">gotrabhū</a></p>

gotrabhū-ñāna|gotrabhuu-~naana|gotrabhu-nana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gotrabhuu-~naana</span></p><p><strong>Gotrabhū-ñāna:</strong> ‘change-of-lineage-knowledge’; see: prec. and <em>visuddhi</em> VII</p>

gotta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; masculine plural</i></p><ol><li>(n.) exogamous kinship group, claiming descent from acommon ancestor (often a vedic ṛṣi); a sept, a clan; the clan name.</li><li>a related group or class; a group of similar individuals or concepts; a religious group.</li><li>(m.pl.) the (members) of the kinship group.</li></ol>

gotta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>ancestry, lineage. There is no word in English for <em><a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a></em>. It includes all those descended, or supposed to be descended, from a common ancestor. A <em><a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a></em> name is always distinguished from the personal name, the name drawn from place of origin or residence, or from occupation, and lastly from the nick-name. It probably means agnate rather than cognate. About a score of <em><a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a></em> names are known They are all assigned to the Buddha’s time. See also Rh. D. <em>Dialogues</em> i.27, 195 sq<br />■ jāti gotta lakkhaṇa Snp.1004; gotta salakkhaṇa Snp.1018; Ādiccā nāma gottena, Sākiyā nāma jātiyā Snp.423; jāti gotta kula Ja.ii.3; jātiyā gottena bhogena sadisa “equal in rank lineage &amp; wealth” Dhp-a.ii.218<br />■ evaṃ-gotta (adj. belonging to such &amp; such an ancestry MN.i.429; MN.ii.20, MN.ii.33 kathaṃ˚ of what lineage, or: what is your family name DN.i.92; nānā˚ (pl.) of various families Pv.ii.9#16<br />■ With nāma (name &amp; lineage, or nomen et cognomen): nāmagottaṃ Vin.i.93; Vin.ii.239; DN.i.92 (expl. at DN-a.i.257 paññatti-vasena nāmaṃ paveṇi-vasena gottaṃ: the name for recognition, the surname for lineage); Snp.648; Vv.84#45 (with nāma &amp; nāmadheyya; expl. at Vv-a.348 Vv-a.349: nāmadheyya, as Tisso, Phusso, etc.; gotta, as Bhaggavo Bhāradvājo, etc.)<br />■ <em>gottena</em> by the ancestral name: Vin.i.93; DN.ii.154; Snp.1019; Dhp.393; <em>gottato</em> same Ja.i.56. Examples: Ambaṭṭha Kaṇhāyana-gottena DN.i.92; Vipassī Koṇḍañño g˚; Kakusandho Kassapo g˚ Bhagavā Gotamo g˚ DN.ii.3; Nāgito Kassapo g˚ DN-a.i.310 Vasudevo Kaṇho g˚ Pv-a.94.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://thaddha">thaddha</a></em> conceited as regards descent (+ jāti˚ dhana˚) Snp.104;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1ha">pañha</a></em> question after one’s family name Snp.456;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADis%C4%81rin">paṭisārin</a></em> (adj.) relying on lineage DN.i.99 (cp. <em>Dialogues</em> i.122); AN.v.327 sq.;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bandhava">bandhava</a></em> connected by family ties (ñāti˚ +) Cnd.455;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rakkhita">rakkhita</a></em> protected by a (good) name Snp.315; Vv-a.72;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> talk over lineage, boasting as regards descent DN.i.99.</li></ul><p>Vedic gotra, to go</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>, <a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>, <a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>, <a href="bword://thaddha">thaddha</a>, <a href="bword://pañha">pañha</a>, <a href="bword://paṭisārin">paṭisārin</a>, <a href="bword://bandhava">bandhava</a>, <a href="bword://rakkhita">rakkhita</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a></p>

gottapaṭisarī|gottapa.tisarii|gottapatisari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gottapa.tisarii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://gottapaṭisarīn">gottapaṭisarīn</a></p><p>referring to, relying on lineage.</p>

gottapaṭisarīn|gottapa.tisariin|gottapatisarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gottapa.tisariin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://gottapaṭisarīn">gottapaṭisarīn</a></p><p>referring to, relying on lineage.</p>

gottā|gottaa|gotta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gottaa</span></p><p>gottī protectress Ja.v.329.</p><p>n. ag. to gopeti = Sk. goptṛ</p>

govata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the “cow practice”, behavior as of a cow (a practice of certain ascetics)</p>

govatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>observing the “cow practice”, one who lives or behaves like a cow.</p>

govatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://govata">govata</a></p>

govattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://govatika">govatika</a></p>

govikantana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>butcher’s knife.</p>

govinda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>The steward or treasurer of King Disampati. He had a son, Jotipāla, who succeeded him after his death and came to be known as Mahā Govinda. DN.ii.230<em>f.</em></p><p>“Govinda” was evidently a title and not a name.</p>

govindiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Evidently the title given to the High Treasurer. DN.ii.232</p>

goyogapilakkha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A spot near Benares, visited by the Buddha on his begging rounds. AN.i.280</p><p>25.21736,83.106072town</p>

goḷaka|go.laka|golaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.laka</span></p><p>ball Thag-a.255 (kīḷā˚).</p>

goḷocī|go.locii|goloci
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.locii</span></p><p>See Putī.</p>

goṇa|go.na|gona
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.na</span></p><p>an ox; a bull.</p>

goṇa|go.na|gona
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.na</span></p><p>Goṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>an ox, a bullock SN.iv.195 sq.; Ja.i.194 Ja.iv.67; Pv.i.8#2; Pv-a.39, Pv-a.40; Vv-a.63 (for ploughing) DN-a.i.163; Dhp-a.iii.60. <em>-sira</em> wild ox Ja.vi.538(= araññagoṇaka).</p><p>The Sanskrit goṇa, according to B. R., is derived from the Pali</p><p>Goṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>= goṇaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, in <em>-santhata</em> (of a pallanka), covered with a woollen rug Vv.81#8; Pv.iii.1#17; (text saṇṭhita variant reading BB goṇakatthata, cp. next).</p>

goṇaka|go.naka|gonaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.naka</span></p><p>long haired woolen rug or cover.</p>

goṇaka|go.naka|gonaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.naka</span></p><p>Goṇaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a kind of ox, a wild bull Ja.vi.538 <em>(arañña˚)</em>.</p><p>goṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Goṇaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a woollen cover with long fleece (DN-a.i.86: dīghalomako mahākojavo; caturangulādhikāni kira tassa lomāni) DN.i.7≈; SN.iii.144; Ja.v.506; Pv.ii.12#8 Thig.378 (+ tūlika); Thag-a.253 (= dīgha-lomakāḷakojava). <em>-atthata</em> spread w. a goṇaka-cover AN.i.137 AN.iii.50 = AN.iv.394; cp. AN.iv.94, AN.iv.231 (always of a <em>pallaṅka)</em> See also goṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>Sk. BSk. goṇika, cp. Pischel, <em>Beitr.</em> iii.236; also spelled gonaka</p>

goṇakatthata|go.nakatthata|gonakatthata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.nakatthata</span></p><p>spread with a woolen cover.</p>

goṇasirā|go.nasiraa|gonasira
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.nasiraa</span></p><p>Probably another name for the wild buffalo Ja.vi.538. See Mahasi.</p>

goṇisādika|go.nisaadika|gonisadika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.nisaadika</span></p><p>an ox-stall Vin.i.240; cp. <em>Vin. Texts</em> ii.121. As gonisādi Vin.iii.46.</p>

goṭaviya|go.taviya|gotaviya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.taviya</span></p><p>(goṭavisa Text) variant reading Ja.vi.225, part of a boat, the poop (expl. ib. p. Ja.vi.226 by nāvāya pacchimabandho).</p>

goṭhaphala|go.thaphala|gothaphala
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.thaphala</span></p><p>Very uncertain. It may refer to the fruit of <em>Solanum torvum</em>, Bengali <em>gota</em>, a small erect shrub, the yellow berries of which are used as a medicine. Alternatively, it may be the Towel Gourd, <em>Luffa aegyptiaca</em>, a climber with tough, smooth, angled stems and a cylindrical fruit about 15 to 46 cm long with dark colored stripes. The fruit of the <em>goṭha</em> was used as a medicine Vin.i.201.</p>

goṭhaphala|go.thaphala|gothaphala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.thaphala</span></p><p>medicinal seed Vin.i.201.</p><p>Sk. gotravṛkṣa? Kern</p>

goṭṭha|go.t.tha|gottha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">go.t.tha</span></p><p>cow pen; a cattle-fold.</p>

gradual instruction
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[gradual instruction]]Gradual instruction:</strong> <em>Ānupubbīkathā</em></p>

grasping
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Grasping:</strong> cf. <em>parāmāsa</em>, <em>upādāna</em>.</p>

great man
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[great man]]Great man:</strong> the 8 thoughts of a: <em>mahāpurisa-vitakka</em>.</p>

greed
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Greed:</strong> <em>lobha</em></p>

greedy consciousness
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[greedy consciousness]]Greedy consciousness:</strong> see: Tab, I, III. 22-29.</p>

greedy-natured
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Greedy-natured:</strong> <em>rāga-carita</em> see: <em>carita</em></p>

grief
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Grief:</strong> <em>domanassa</em> - Indulging in <strong>g.</strong> see: <em>manopavicāra</em>.</p>

groups
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Groups:</strong> of existence, see: <em>khandha</em> material groups, see: <em>rūpa-kalāpa</em> materiality-group, see: <em>rūpa-kāya</em> mind-group, see: <em>nāma-kāya</em></p>

growth
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Growth:</strong> bodily: <em>rūpassa</em> <em>upacaya</em> see: <em>khandha</em> I.</p>

grudge
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Grudge:</strong> see: <em>patigha</em></p>

gucch
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Gucch˚</p><p>in jigucchati (Des. of <em>gup</em> = Sk. jugupsate) to detest, see <em>s</em>. v.</p>

guggula
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A plant mentioned together with others which either are particularly fragrant or from which perfume is made Ja.vi.537. This possibly refers to <em>Commiphora caudata</em>, Hindi <em>guggul</em>, or <em>Ailanthus triphysa</em>, known in Maharastra as <em>gugguladhup</em>, although it is not clear whether this second plant grows in northern India. Both trees have a fragrant gum used in perfumes and incense. The first of these plants is a small deciduous shrub with greenish papery bark and yellowish flowers.</p>

guggula
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>kind of perfume Ja.vi.537.</p><p>?</p>

guhanā|guhanaa|guhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(f. abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guhanaa</span></p><p>to gūhati) hiding, concealing, keeping secret Vb.358 (+ pariguhanā). Also as gūhanā, q.v.</p>

guhā|guhaa|guha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guhaa</span></p><p>an enclosed (hiding) place or space; a cave; a cavern.</p>

guhā|guhaa|guha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guhaa</span></p><p>hiding place, a cave, cavern (cp kandara &amp; see giriguhā); fig. the heart (in ˚āsaya) According to Bdhgh. (on Vin.i.58, see <em>Vin. Texts</em> i.174 “a hut of bricks, or in a rock, or of wood.” Vin.i.58 Vin.i.96, Vin.i.107, Vin.i.239, Vin.i.284; Vin.ii.146; Vin.iii.155; Vin.iv.48 (cp. sattapaṇṇi-guhā); Snp.772, Snp.958; Ja.ii.418; Ja.vi.574; Vv.50#16.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81saya">āsaya</a></em> hiding in the heart; or the shelter of the heart AN.iv.98 (maccupāso +); Ja.v.367 (id.); Dhp.37 (cittaṃ see Dhp-a.i.304).</li></ul><p>Vedic guhā, <em>guh</em>, gūhati to hide (q.v.) Dhtp.337: saṃvaraṇa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsaya">āsaya</a></p>

guhāsaya|guhaasaya|guhasaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guhaasaya</span></p><p>being in the body.</p>

gulasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i></p><p>kind of plant.</p>

gulhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gūḷhaka">gūḷhaka</a></p>

gulissāni|gulissaani|gulissani
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gulissaani</span></p><p>A monk living in the wilds, who once came on some business to see the monks at the Kalandakanivāpa. It was on his account that the Gulissāni Sutta was preached. MN.i.469</p>

gumba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>troop of soldiers; a guard.</p>

gumba
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a troop, a heap, cluster, swarm. Of soldiers Vin.i.345; of fish (maccha˚) DN.i.84 = MN.i.279 = MN.ii.22; AN.i.9.</li><li>a thicket, a bush, jungle; the lair of an animal in a thicket (sayana˚ Ja.iv.256) SN.iii.6 (eḷagalā˚) Ja.iii.52 (nivāsa˚, vasana˚); Vv-a.301 (gaccha˚ underwood); Ja.i.149, Ja.i.167; Ja.ii.19; Ja.iii.55; Ja.iv.438; Vv-a.63, Vv-a.66 Cp. pagumba = gumba, in vana˚ Snp.233 (see Kp-a.192) veḷu˚ Thag.919</li></ol><p>■ Acc. gumbaṃ (adv.) thickly, in masses balled together Mil.117 (of clouds).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://antara">antara</a></em> thicket Vv-a.233.</li></ul><p>Sk. gulma, <em>*glem</em> to <em>*gel</em>, to be thick, to conglomerate, cp. Lat. glomus (ball), globus, etc. See guḷa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://antara">antara</a></p>

gumbiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>one of a troop of soldiers; a guard.</p>

gumbiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one of the troop (of soldiers) Vin.i.345.</p><p>fr. gumba</p>

gumpha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ogumpheti">ogumpheti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogumpheti">ogumpheti</a></p>

gundā|gundaa|gunda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gundaa</span></p><p>the rush nut, cyperus rotundus.</p>

gundāvana|gundaavana|gundavana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gundaavana</span></p><p>GundāvanaKundāvanaGuṇḍāvanaKuṇḍāvana</p><p>A forest in Madhurā. Mahā Kaccāna stayed there and was visited by Avantiputta, king of Madhurā, to whom he preached the Madhurā Sutta, MN.ii.83<em>f.</em> and by the brahmin Kandarāyana. AN.i.67<em>f.</em></p><p>27.45466, 77.656863park</p>

guru
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun venerable, reverend, a teacher Vv-a.229, Vv-a.230 (˚dakkhiṇā a teacher’s fee); Pv-a.3 (˚janā venerable persons) Sdhp.227 (˚ûpadesa), Sdhp.417.</p><p>a younger form of garu (q.v.); Sk. guru</p>

gustatory organ
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>[[gustatory organ]]Gustatory organ:</strong> see: <em>āyatana</em>.</p>

gutijjita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, whose name occurs in a nominal list. MN.iii.70</p>

gutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>guarded; protected; watchful; protector; defender; preserver.</p>

gutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li><p>as pp. guarded, protected</p><ol><li>lit nagaraṃ guttaṃ a well-guarded city Dhp.315 = Thag.653 Thag.1005; Devinda˚ protected by the Lord of gods Vv.30#8-</li><li>fig. (med.) guarded, watchful, constrained guarded in, watchful as regards… (with loc. SN.iv.70 (agutta &amp; sugutta, with danta, rakkhita) AN.iii.6 (atta˚ self-controlled); Snp.250 (sotesu gutto vijitindriyo), Snp.971 (id. + yatacārin); Dhp.36 (cittaṃ).</li></ol></li><li><p>as n. agent (= Sk. goptṛ, cp. kata in kāla-kata kāḷaṃ kartṛ) one who guards or observes, a guardian in Dhammassa gutta Dhp.257, observer of the Norm (expl. Dhp-a.iii.282: dhammojapaññāya samannāgata) cp. dhammagutta SN.i.222.</p></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a></em> one whose senses are guarded; with well-guarded senses Snp.63 (+ rakkhita-mānasāno; expl Snp-a: chassu indriyesu gopitindriyo); Cnd.230; Vv.50#15; Pv.iv.1#32;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dv%C4%81ra">dvāra</a></em> “with guarded doors” always in combination with indriyesu g-d. having the doors of the senses guarded, practising. self-control DN.i.63≈(explained DN-a.i.182 by pihita-dvāro), DN.i.70; SN.ii.218; SN.iv.103, SN.iv.112 SN.iv.119 sq., SN.iv.175; Snp.413 (+ susaṃvuta); Pp.24. Cp. foll.</li><li><em>-dvāratā</em> (f. abstr. to prec.) in indriyesu g˚ self constraint, control over (the doors of) one’s senses, always combined with bhojane mattaññutā (moderation in taking food) DN.iii.213; Iti.24; Pp.20, Pp.24; Dhs.1347; Pv-a.163 Opp. <em>a˚</em>; lack of sense-control DN.iii.213; Iti.23; Dhs.1345.</li></ul><p>Sk. gupta, pp. of <em>gup</em> in med. pass. sense, cp. gopeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a>, <a href="bword://dvāra">dvāra</a></p>

guttadvāra|guttadvaara|guttadvara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guttadvaara</span></p><p>with means of access (of the sense-faculties) guarded; defended; controlled.</p>

gutti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>protection; guarding; watchfulness; controlling.</p>

gutti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>protection, defence, guard; watchfulness</p><ol><li>lit. of a city AN.iv.106 sq</li><li>fig. of the senses in indriyānaṃ gutti Dhp.375; Pp.24 (+ gopanā) Dhs.1348; Sdhp.341 (agutti); Vin.iv.305; AN.ii.72 (atta˚) also in pl.: guttīsu ussuka keen in the practice of watchfulness DN.iii.148.</li></ol><p>Vedic gupti</p>

guttika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>guardian, one who keeps watch over, in <em>nagara˚</em>; the town-watchman, the chief-constable Pv-a.4; Mil.345.</p><p>fr. last</p>

guttā |guttaa |gutta 
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">guttaa </span></p><p>Guttā</p><p>A bhikkhunī to whom several Therīgāthā verses are addressed, encourageing her to overcome the defliments that have long kept her trapped in suffering. Thig.163–168</p>

guyha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>what is to be covered or concealed; the parts of the body which to be covered, esp. the private parts; what is to be kept secret, secret; private; a secret.</p>

guyha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>adj. to be hidden, hidden in ˚bhaṇḍaka the hidden part (of the body Dhp-a.iv.197.</li><li>(nt.) that which is hidden; lit. in <em>vattha˚</em>; hidden by the dress, i.e. the pudendum DN.i.106; Snp.1022, etc. (see <em><a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a></em>), fig. a secret Mil.92 guyhaṃ pariguyhati to keep a secret AN.iv.31; Cnd.510.</li></ol><p>ger. of <em>guh</em> = Vedic guhya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a></p>

guyhamanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>secret or private discussion or plan.</p>

guñja|gu~nja|gunja
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu~nja</span></p><p>Indian Liquorice, sometimes also <em><a href="bword://ji%C3%B1juka">jiñjuka</a></em>, Hindi <em>gumchi</em>, Maharastra <em><a href="bword://gu%C3%B1ja">guñja</a></em>, <em>Abrus precatorius</em> Ja.iv.333, an attractive twining slender shrub, its leaves shiny on top and silky below and pinkish-white flowers. The smooth shiny seeds are scarlet with a black eye and in the Tipiṭaka are said to resemble a peacock’s eye Ja.iv.334. Liquorice seeds, called <em>raktikā</em> in Sanskrit, were used as the basic unit of weight in ancient India. The seed weighs 109 milligrams. It is also powdered and used in snuff, taken to relieve headache or used as a poison. The root is used in the same way as the liquorice root.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jiñjuka">jiñjuka</a>, <a href="bword://guñja">guñja</a></p>

guñjā|gu~njaa|gunja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu~njaa</span></p><p>plant (Abrus precatorius); the redness of its berries is referred to in similes; Dhp-a.iv.133 (˚vaṇṇāni akkhīni). See also jiñjuka.</p>

guḷa|gu.la|gula
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.la</span></p><p>A Yakkha chief who should be invoked when unbelieving Yakkhas molest any follower of the Buddha. DN.iii.204</p>

guḷa|gu.la|gula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.a a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.la</span></p><p>type of sugar; in lumps or liquid; treacle; molasses.</p>

guḷa|gu.la|gula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.la</span></p><p>Guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to make into a ball, to conglomerate. Cp Sk. glauḥ ball; Gr. γλουτός; Ohg. chliuwa; Ger. kugel kloss; E. clot, cleat; also <em>*gel</em> with same meaning Sk. gulma tumour, gilāyu glandular swelling; cp. Lat glomus, globus; Ger. klamm; E. clamp, clump. A root <em>guḷ</em> is given by Dhtp.576,77 in meaning of “mokkha” a ball, in compounds sutta˚ a ball of string (= Ohg. chliuwa DN.i.54 = ; MN.iii.95; Pv-a.145; ayo˚ an iron globe Dhp.308; DN-a.i.84; loha˚ of copper Dhp.371; sela˚ a rockball i.e. a heavy stone-ball Ja.i.147.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%AB%E1%B8%B7%C4%81">kīḷā</a></em> play at ball Dhp-a.i.178; Dhp-a.iii.455; Dhp-a.iv.124.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://parima%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Dala">parimaṇḍala</a></em> the circumference of a ball, or (adj.) round globular, like a ball Pv-a.253.</li></ul><p>Sk. guḍa and gulī ball, guṭikā pill, guṇikā tumour; to <em>*gleu</em>.</p><p>Guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>sugar, molasses Vin.i.210, Vin.i.224 sq., Vin.i.245<br />■ <em><a href="bword://sagu%E1%B8%B7a">saguḷa</a></em> sugared, sweet, or “with molasses Ja.vi.324 (saguḷāni, i.e. saguḷa-pūve pancakes).</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81sava">āsava</a></em> sugar-juice Vv-a.73. <em>-<a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a></em> s<br />■ water Vin.i.226. <em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87a">karaṇa</a></em> a sugar factory ibid. Vin.i.210. <em>-pūvaka</em> sweet cake Mhvs.10–Mhvs.13 <em>-<a href="bword://ph%C4%81%E1%B9%87ita">phāṇita</a></em> molasses Vv-a.179.</p><p>Non-Aryan?</p><p>Guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a cluster, a chain (?), in <em>maṇi˚</em>; a cluster of jewels, always in simile with ref. to sparkling eyes “maniguḷa-sadisāni akkhīni” Ja.i.149; Ja.iii.126, Ja.iii.184 (variant reading BB ˚guḷika); Ja.iv.256 (variant reading id.); <em>mālā˚</em>; a cluster, a chain of flowers, a garland Ja.i.73, Ja.i.54; <em>puppha˚</em>; id. Dhp.172 Dhp.233.</p><p>for guṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, due to distance dissimilation in maṇiguṇa and mālāguṇa → maṇigula and mālāgula; cp similarly in meaning and form Ohg. chliuwa → Ger knäuel</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kīḷā">kīḷā</a>, <a href="bword://parimaṇḍala">parimaṇḍala</a>, <a href="bword://saguḷa">saguḷa</a>, <a href="bword://āsava">āsava</a>, <a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a>, <a href="bword://karaṇa">karaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://phāṇita">phāṇita</a></p>

guḷapiṇḍa|gu.lapi.n.da|gulapinda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.lapi.n.da</span></p><p>lump of sugar.</p>

guḷavākala|gu.lavaakala|gulavakala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.lavaakala</span></p><p>with a thick (or knotted? or matted?) fringe.</p>

guḷika|gu.lika|gulika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.lika</span></p><p>adjective like a chain, or having a chain, (nt. &amp; f.) a cluster, a chain in maṇi a string of jewels, a pearl necklace Ja.iii.184 (variant reading BB for ˚guḷa); Ja.iv.256; Vism.285 (+ muttā-guḷikā).</p><p>to guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = guṇa, cp. also guṇaka</p>

guḷikā|gu.likaa|gulika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.likaa</span></p><p>pill; a small ball or globe; a globule.</p>

guḷikā|gu.likaa|gulika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.likaa</span></p><p>little ball SN.v.462 (satta-kolaṭṭhi-mattiyo guḷikā, pl.) Thig.498 (kolaṭṭhimatta g˚ balls of the size of a jujube) cp. Thag-a.289.</p><p>to guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; cp. Sk. guṭikā pill, guṇikā tumour</p>

guḷā|gu.laa|gula
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.laa</span></p><p>swelling, pimple, pustule, blight, in cpd. <em>guḷā-guṇṭhika-jāta</em> DN.ii.55, which is also to be read at AN.ii.211 (in spite of Morris, prelim. remarks to AN.ii.4 whose trsln. is otherwise correct) = guḷā-gunṭḥita covered with swellings (i.e. blight); cp. similar expression at Dhp-a.iii.297 gaṇḍāgaṇḍa (-jāta) “having become covered all over with pustules (i.e. rash).” All readings at corresp. passages are to be corrected accordingly, viz. SN.ii.92 (guḷigandhika˚); SN.iv.158 (guṇaguṇika˚); the reading at Dpvs.xii.32, also variant reading SS at AN.ii.211, is as quoted above and the whole phrase runs: tantākulajātā guḷāguṇṭhikajātā “entangled like a ball of string and covered with blight.”</p><p>to guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

guḷāguṇdikajāta|gu.laagu.ndikajaata|gulagundikajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.laagu.ndikajaata</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://guḷāguṇṭhikajāta">guḷāguṇṭhikajāta</a></p>

guḷāguṇṭhikajāta|gu.laagu.n.thikajaata|gulagunthikajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.laagu.n.thikajaata</span></p><p>become enveloped in a tangled ball; knotted in ball; in a tangle of threads.</p>

guḷāsava|gu.laasava|gulasava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.laasava</span></p><p>spirit distilled from sugar or molasses.</p>

guṇa|gu.na|guna
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.na</span></p><p>One or another of the two dozen or more wood boring beetles that live in northern India. These beetles lay their eggs under the bark of trees and the larva burrow into the wood. The <em>guṇa</em> could eat fig wood but not that of harder trees Ja.iii.431.</p>

guṇa|gu.na|guna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.na</span></p><ol><li>a secondary element; a quality, an attribute; esp. a good quality.</li><li>a strand, a subdivision; a tie; a string; bow string; the string of a musical instrument.</li></ol>

guṇa|gu.na|guna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.na</span></p><p>Guṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li><p>a string, a cord-</p><ol><li>of a robe, etc., in (kāya-bandhanaṃ) saguṇaṃ katvā to make tight by tying with a knot Vin.i.46 (<em>Vin. Texts:</em> “laying the garments on top of each other,” wrongly construed), Vin.ii.213 (trsln. “folding his garments”); cp. guṇaka.</li><li>of musical instruments Vin.i.182 = AN.iii.375 (vīṇā)</li><li>of a bow, in aguṇa stringless Ja.v.433 (dhanu).</li></ol></li><li><p>(a strand of a rope as) constituent part ingredient, component, element; with numerals it equals -fold, e.g. pañca kāmaguṇā the 5 strands of kāma, or 5-fold craving (see <em><a href="bword://k%C4%81ma">kāma</a></em>); ekaguṇaṃ once, diguṇaṃ twice Snp.714; diguṇaṃ nivāpaṃ pacitvā cooking a double meal Vv-a.63; catugguṇa fourfold, of a sanghāti DN.ii.128; SN.ii.221, cp. Rhys Davids, <em>Dialogues</em> ii.145 aṭṭhaguṇa (hirañña) Thig.153; aneka-bhāgena guṇena seyyo many times or infinitely better Pv.iv.1#9; sataguṇena sahassa˚ 100 and 1,000 times Pv-a.41; asankheyyena guṇena infinitely, inconceivably Mil.106 sataguṇaṃ sahassaguṇaṃ Vism.126.</p></li><li><p>(a part as quality, esp.) good quality, advantage, merit Ja.i.266 Ja.ii.112; Ja.iii.55, Ja.iii.82<br />■ lobha˚ Snp.663; sādhu˚ Snp.678 sīla˚ Ja.i.213; Ja.ii.112; Buddha˚ Ja.ii.111; pabbajita Ja.i.59.</p></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://aggat%C4%81">aggatā</a></em> state of having the best qualities, superiority Dpvs.iv.1.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dha">aḍḍha</a></em> rich in virtue Sdhp.312, Sdhp.561</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://upeta">upeta</a></em> in khuppipāsāhi guṇûpeto as Pv-a.10 is to be read khuppipās’ âbhibhūto peto.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kath%C4%81">kathā</a></em> “tale of virtue,” praise Ja.i.307; Ja.ii.2.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kittana">kittana</a></em> telling one’s praises Pv-a.107, Pv-a.120.</li><li><em>-guṇika</em> in phrase tantākulajāta g-g-jāta at SN.iv.158, see under <em><a href="bword://gu%E1%B8%B7%C4%81">guḷā</a></em>-guṇṭhika.</li></ul><p>Non-Aryan?</p><p>Guṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a ball, a cluster, a chain (?), in anta˚ the intestines; MN.i.185, Kp.11, cp. Kp-a.57 for expln<br />■ mālāguṇa a garland or chain (cluster of flowers Dhp.53 (but ˚guḷa at Ja.i.73, Ja.i.74). See guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>for which often guḷa with common substitution of ḷ for ṇ, partly due to dissimilation, as mālāguḷa → mālāguṇa; cp. Sk. guṇikā tumour: guḷa and gaḷa veḷu: veṇu, and note on gala</p><p>Guṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a woodworm Ja.iii.431 (˚pāṇaka).</p><p>Derivation unknown. Cp. Sk. ghuna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāma">kāma</a>, <a href="bword://aggatā">aggatā</a>, <a href="bword://aḍḍha">aḍḍha</a>, <a href="bword://upeta">upeta</a>, <a href="bword://kathā">kathā</a>, <a href="bword://kittana">kittana</a>, <a href="bword://guḷā">guḷā</a></p>

guṇaka|gu.naka|gunaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.naka</span></p><p>(or m.) stitching to strengthen the edge of a belt.</p>

guṇaka|gu.naka|gunaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.naka</span></p><p>adjective having a knot at the end, thickened at the top (with ref. to kāyabandha, see <em><a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87a">guṇa</a></em> 1a) Vin.ii.136, cp. <em>Vin. Texts</em> ii.143.</p><p>to guḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, cp. guḷika?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://guṇa">guṇa</a></p>

guṇava|gu.nava|gunava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.nava</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://guṇavat">guṇavat</a></p><p>possessed of good qualities or virtues; excellent, estimable.</p>

guṇavant|gu.navant|gunavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.navant</span></p><p>adjective possessed of good qualities, virtuous Pv.ii.9#71 (= jhān’ ādiguṇa-yutta); Pv-a.62 (mahā˚).</p><p>to guṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

guṇavat|gu.navat|gunavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.navat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://guṇavat">guṇavat</a></p><p>possessed of good qualities or virtues; excellent, estimable.</p>

guṇi|gu.ni|guni
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.ni</span></p><p>kind of armour Ja.vi.449 (g. vuccate kavacaṃ C.); see Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 132.</p><p>of adj. guṇin, having guṇas or guḷas, i.e. strings or knots</p>

guṇḍika|gu.n.dika|gundika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.n.dika</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://gu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhika">guṇṭhika</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://guṇṭhika">guṇṭhika</a></p>

guṇṭheti|gu.n.theti|guntheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.n.theti</span></p><p>to cover to veil, to hide; pp. <strong>guṇṭhita</strong> in paṃsu˚ covered with dust Pv.ii.3#5 (in Hardy’s conjecture for kuṇṭhita, q.v.) Also in cpd. <em><a href="bword://paligu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhita">paliguṇṭhita</a></em> obstructed, entangled Snp.131 (mohena) where variant reading BB kuṇṭhita. Cp. o˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. guṇṭhayati Dhtp (563) &amp; Dhtm (793) give both roots; <em>guṇṭh &amp; guṇḍ</em> as syn. of <em>veṭh</em></p><p>See also: guṇṭhita, <a href="bword://paliguṇṭhita">paliguṇṭhita</a></p>

guṇṭhika|gu.n.thika|gunthika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.n.thika</span></p><p>(in meaning = guṇṭhita) one who is covered with or wrapped up in, only in <em>ahi˚</em>; a snake-trainer (like a Laocoon). See details under ahi or Ja.ii.267; Ja.iii.348 (text ˚guṇḍika); Ja.iv.308 (ahi-kuṇḍika, variant reading SS guṇṭhika), Ja.iv.456 (text ˚guṇṭika; variant reading BB ˚kuṇḍika). Also in guḷā-guṇṭhika (q.v.).</p>

guṇṭhima|gu.n.thima|gunthima
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.n.thima</span></p><p>covered over (?), see *<a href="bword://p%C4%81li">pāli</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pāli">pāli</a></p>

guṭṭha|gu.t.tha|guttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gu.t.tha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://goṭṭha">goṭṭha</a></p>

gādha|gaadha|gadha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaadha</span></p><p>gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter firm footing (in water); firm ground.</p><p>gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine or neuter a hole; a refuge.</p>

gādha|gaadha|gadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaadha</span></p><p>Gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>depth; a hole, a dugout AN.ii.107 = Pp.43 (cp. Pp-a 225); Sdhp.394 (˚ṃ khaṇati). Cp. gāḷha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>Sk. gāḷha pp. of <em>gāh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://g%C4%81hati">gāhati</a></em></p><p>Gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adj. passable, fordable in a˚ unfathomable, deep Pv-a.77 (= gambhīra). nt. a ford, a firm stand, firm ground, a safe place: gambhīre ˚ṃ vindati AN.v.202. ˚ṃ esati to seek the terra firma SN.i.127; similarly: ˚ṃ labhati to gain firm footing SN.i.47; ˚ṃ ajjhagā SN.iv.206; ˚ṃ labhate Ja.vi.440 (= patiṭṭhā). Cp. o˚, paṭi˚.</p><p>Sk. gāḷha firm Dhtp.167 “paṭiṭṭhāyaṃ” cp. also Sk. gādha, fordable &amp; see *<a href="bword://g%C4%81%E1%B8%B7ha">gāḷha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāhati">gāhati</a>, <a href="bword://gāḷha">gāḷha</a></p>

gādhati|gaadhati|gadhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaadhati</span></p><p>stands firm; has a firm footing.</p>

gādhati|gaadhati|gadhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaadhati</span></p><p>to stand fast, to be on firm ground, to have a firm footing: āpo ca paṭhavī ca tejo vāyo na gādhati “the four elements have no footing DN.i.223 = SN.i.15<br />■ Dhamma-Vinaye gādhati “to stand fast in the Doctrine &amp; Discipline” SN.iii.59 sq.</p><p>v. der. fr. gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

gāha|gaaha|gaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaaha</span></p><p>gāha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a hiding place; retreat; or diving in, penetrating.</p><p>gāha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. &amp; m. neuter</p><ol><li>(mfn.) seizing.</li><li>(mfn.) seizing; seizure; taking hold of; taking up; receiving.</li></ol>

gāha|gaaha|gaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaaha</span></p><ol><li>(n.) seizing, seizure, grip (cp. gaha): canda˚ suriya˚ an eclipse (lit. the moon, etc., being seized by a demon) DN.i.10 (= DN-a.i.95: Rāhu candaṃ gaṇhāti). Esp. applied to the sphere of the mind obsession, being possessed (by a thought), an idea opinion, view, usually as a preconceived idea, a wrong view, misconception. So in def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of diṭṭhi (wrong views) with paṭiggāha &amp; abhinivesa Cnd.271#iii (on lepa); Pp.22; Dhs.381 (= obsession like the grip of a crocodile Dhs-a.253), Dhs.1003; Vb.145, Vb.358. In the same formula as vipariyesa ggāha (wrong view), cp viparīta˚ Vv-a.331 (see <em><a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhi">diṭṭhi</a></em>). As doubt &amp; error in anekaṃ sa + g˚ in def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of kankhā &amp; vicikicchā Cnd.1, Vb.168; ekaṃsa˚ &amp; apaṇṇaka˚ certainty, right thought Ja.i.97<br />■ gāhaṃ vissajjeti to give up a preconceived idea Ja.ii.387.</li><li><ol><li>(adj.) act. holding: rasmi˚ holding the reins Dhp.222; dabbi˚ holding the spoons Pv.ii.9#53 (= gāhaka Pv-a.135)</li><li>(med-pass.) taken: jīvagāha taken alive, in ˚ṃ gaheti to take (prisoner) alive SN.i.84 karamaragāhaṃ gaheti same Ja.iii.361 (see <em><a href="bword://kara">kara</a></em>).</li></ol></li></ol><p>fr. gaṇhāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diṭṭhi">diṭṭhi</a>, <a href="bword://kara">kara</a></p>

gāhaka|gaahaka|gahaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaka</span></p><p>(one) who receives, a recipient; one who holds.</p>

gāhaka|gaahaka|gahaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaka</span></p><p>adjective f. gāhikā holding (-˚) chatta˚ Snp.688; Dāvs ii.119; katacchu˚ Pv-a.135; cāmarī˚ Ja.vi.218 Cp. saṃ˚.</p>

gāhana|gaahana|gahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahana</span></p><p>submersion, see avagahana, avagāhati &amp; avagāhana.</p><p>fr. last</p>

gāhati|gaahati|gahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahati</span></p><p>to immerse, to penetrate, to plunge into: see <em><a href="bword://g%C4%81dha">gādha</a></em> &amp; gāḷha; cp also avagadha ajjhogāhati, ogāhati, pagāhati.</p><p>Sk. gāhate but Dhtp.349 = viloḷana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gādha">gādha</a></p>

gāhavant|gaahavant|gahavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahavant</span></p><p>in ekaṃsa-gāhavatī nibbici kicchā “doubtlessness consisting in certainty” Vv-a.85 in explained of ekaṃsika.</p>

gāhayati|gaahayati|gahayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahayati</span></p><p>of gāheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāhenta|gaahenta|gahenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahenta</span></p><p>of gāheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāhesi|gaahesi|gahesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahesi</span></p><p>of gāheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāhetabba|gaahetabba|gahetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāheti|gaaheti|gaheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaaheti</span></p><p>causes to take ( or lay hold of); causes to be taken or seized; hands over, distributes, allots (to, dat./gen.); causes to learn, teaches.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gaṇhāti">gaṇhāti</a></p>

gāheti|gaaheti|gaheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaaheti</span></p><p>to understand, to account for DN-a.i.117.</p><p>v. denom. fr. gāha</p>

gāhetuṃ|gaahetu.m|gahetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahetu.m</span></p><p>of gāheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāhetvā|gaahetvaa|gahetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahetvaa</span></p><p>of gāheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāheyati|gaaheyati|gaheyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaaheyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāheti">gāheti</a></p>

gāhi|gaahi|gahi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahi</span></p><p>Imper. pres. of gāyati Ja.iii.507.</p>

gāhika|gaahika|gahika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahika</span></p><p>(-˚) = gahin, see *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anta">anta</a></p>

gāhikā|gaahikaa|gahika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahikaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāhaka">gāhaka</a></p>

gāhin|gaahin|gahin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahin</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) grasping, taking up, striving after, ādhāna˚ DN.iii.247; udaka˚ Ja.i.5; piya˚ Dhp.209; nimitta˚ anubyañjana˚ etc.</p>

gāhāpaka|gaahaapaka|gahapaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaapaka</span></p><p>one who is made to take up, a receiver Vin.ii.177 (patta˚).</p><p>fr. gāhāpeti</p>

gāhāpayati|gaahaapayati|gahapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaapayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāhāpeti">gāhāpeti</a></p>

gāhāpeti|gaahaapeti|gahapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaapeti</span></p><p>causes to take ( or lay hold of); causes to be taken or seized; hands over, distributes, allots (to, dat./gen.); causes to learn, teaches</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gaṇhāti">gaṇhāti</a></p>

gāhāpeti|gaahaapeti|gahapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaapeti</span></p><p>to cause to take; to cause to be seized or fetched; to remove. Aor, gāhāpesi Ja.i.53; Ja.ii.37; gāhāpayi Pv.iv.1#42<br />ger gāhāpetvā Ja.i.166; Ja.ii.127; Ja.iii.281; Dhp-a.i.62 (patta-cīvaraṃ) With double acc. mahājanaṃ kathaṃ g˚ made people believe your words Ja.ii.416; cetake kasā g. made the servants seize their whips Ja.iii.281. Cp. gaṇhāpeti.</p><p>caus. of gaṇhāti</p>

gāhāpettaba|gaahaapettaba|gahapettaba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">gaahaapettaba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāhāpeti">gāhāpeti</a></p>

