<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ikkaasa</span></p><p>See <em>Jatu</em>.</p><p>See also: Jatu</p>

ikkāsa|ikkaasa|ikkasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ikkaasa</span></p><p>or neuter a sticky substance used to bind pigment.</p>

ikkāsa|ikkaasa|ikkasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ikkaasa</span></p><p>? at Vin.ii.151 (+ kasāva) is trsl. by “slime of trees”, according to Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> on p. 321 (to C. V. vi.3, 1), who however reads <em><a href="bword://nikk%C4%81sa">nikkāsa</a></em>.</p><p>uncertain as regard meaning &amp; etym.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkāsa">nikkāsa</a></p>

ill-humour
