issati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>envies, resents, is jealous (of, dat./gen.)</p>

issati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bear ill-will, to be angry, to envy Ja.iii.7; ppr. med. issamānaka Sdhp.89, f. ˚ikā AN.ii.203<br />pp <em><a href="bword://issita">issita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>denom. fr. issā. Av. areṣyeiti to be jealous, Gr. ε ̓́ραται to desire; connected also with Sk. arṣati fr. <em>ṛṣ</em> to flow, Lat. erro; &amp; Sk. irasyati to be angry = Gr.; *ἄρης God of war, ἀρἀρη; Ags. eorsian to be angry</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://issita">issita</a></p>

issattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.)</i></p><p>archery.</p>

issattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(nt. m.)</p><ol><li>(nt.) archery (as means of livelihood &amp; occupation) MN.i.85; MN.iii.1; SN.i.100 (so read with variant reading; T has issatta, C. explains by usu-sippaṃ <em>K. S.</em> p. 318); Snp.617 (˚ṃ upajīvati = āvudha jīvikaṃ Snp-a.466); Ja.vi.81 Sdhp.390.</li><li>(m.) an archer Mil.250, Mil.305, Mil.352, Mil.418.</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. iṣvastra nt. bow, fr. iṣu (= P. usu) an arrow + <em>as</em> to throw. Cp. P. issāsa<br />■ Bdhgh in a strange way dissects it as “usuñ ca satthañ cā ti vuttaṃ hoti” (i.e. usu arrow + <em><a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a></em> sword, knife) Snp-a.466</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a></p>

issatthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an archer Mil.419.</p><p>issattha + ka</p>

issita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>being envied or scolded, giving offence or causing anger. Ja.v.44</p><p>pp. of <em>īrṣ</em> (see <em><a href="bword://issati">issati</a></em>); Sk. īrṣita</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://issati">issati</a></p>

