<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jhaayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jhāyin">jhāyin</a></p><p>meditative, self-concentrated, engaged in jhāna; who habitually practices jhāna.</p>

jhāyin|jhaayin|jhayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jhaayin</span></p><p>adjective pondering over (c. acc.) intent on: meditative, self-concentrated, engaged in jhāna-practice Vin.ii.75; SN.i.46 = SN.i.52; SN.ii.284; MN.i.334; AN.i.24; AN.iii.355; AN.iv.426; AN.v.156, AN.v.325 sq.; Snp.85 (magga˚), Snp.638, Snp.719, Snp.1009, Snp.1105; Iti.71, Iti.74, Iti.112; Ja.iv.7; Dhp.23 Dhp.110, Dhp.387 (reminding of jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, cp. Dhp-a.iv.144) Cnd.264; Vv.5#8; Pv.iv.1#32; Vb.342. Mnd.226 Cnd.342#2 = Vism.26 (āpādaka˚).</p><p>see *<a href="bword://jh%C4%81yati">jhāyati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; jhāna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jhāyati">jhāyati</a></p>

ji
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>-Ji</p><p>adjective suffix winning, victorious: sangāma˚ victorious in fight, in sangāmaj uttama “greatest of conquerors” Dhp.103; sabba SN.iv.83.</p><p>From jayati to conquer</p>

jigacchā|jigacchaa|jigaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigacchaa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://jighacch%C4%81">jighacchā</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jighacchā">jighacchā</a></p>

jighaccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jighacchat">jighacchat</a></p><p>is hungry.</p>

jighacchat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jighacchat">jighacchat</a></p><p>is hungry.</p>

jighacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to have a desire to eat, to be hungry DN.ii.266; pp. <em><a href="bword://jighacchita">jighacchita</a></em> Dhp-a.ii.145.</p><p>Desid. to ghasati, eat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jighacchita">jighacchita</a></p>

jighacchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>hungry; wanting to eat.</p>

jighacchā|jighacchaa|jighaccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jighacchaa</span></p><p>hunger.</p>

jighacchā|jighacchaa|jighaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jighacchaa</span></p><p>appetite, hunger, often combined with <em><a href="bword://pip%C4%81s%C4%81">pipāsā</a></em>, desire to drink, thirst, e.g. SN.i.18; AN.ii.143, AN.ii.153; Mil.304<br />■ MN.i.13, MN.i.114; MN.i.364; MN.iii.97, MN.iii.136; AN.iii.163; Dhp.203 (j. paramā rogā); Ja.ii.445; Ja.iii.19 (˚abhibhūta = chāta); Mil.204, Mil.304; Sdhp.118, Sdhp.388 Cp. khudā &amp; chāta.; <em>Note.</em> A diff. spelling as dighacchā occurs at AN.ii.117.</p><p>from jighacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pipāsā">pipāsā</a></p>

jigiṃsa|jigi.msa|jigimsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jigiṃsat">jigiṃsat</a></p><p>of jigiṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigiṃsati">jigiṃsati</a></p>

jigiṃsaka|jigi.msaka|jigimsaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msaka</span></p><p>who wishes to gain; desirous of; pursuing.</p>

jigiṃsaka|jigi.msaka|jigimsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msaka</span></p><p>adjective one who wishes to gain, desirous of, pursuing Snp.690.</p><p>see next</p>

jigiṃsamāna|jigi.msamaana|jigimsamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msamaana</span></p><p>of jigiṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigiṃsati">jigiṃsati</a></p>

jigiṃsanatā|jigi.msanataa|jigimsanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msanataa</span></p><p>desire for, covetousness Vb.353 (variant reading BB. nijigīsanatā); cp. Vism.29</p><p>n. abstr. fr. jigiṃsati</p>

jigiṃsanā|jigi.msanaa|jigimsana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msanaa</span></p><p>of jigiṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigiṃsati">jigiṃsati</a></p>

jigiṃsat|jigi.msat|jigimsat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jigiṃsat">jigiṃsat</a></p><p>of jigiṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigiṃsati">jigiṃsati</a></p>

jigiṃsati|jigi.msati|jigimsati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msati</span></p><p>wishes to win or obtain; desires; covets.</p>

jigiṃsati|jigi.msati|jigimsati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigi.msati</span></p><p>to desire, to wish to acquire, to covet Snp.700; Ja.ii.285; Ja.iii.172 (variant reading BB. jigissaṃ); Ja.iv.406 (variant reading SS. jihiṃ˚, BB. jigī˚); Ja.v.372; Ja.vi.268. As <em>jigīsati</em> Thag.1110.</p><p>Desid. of <em>ji</em>, jayati. On etym. see also Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 44</p>

jigucchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who dislikes or disapproves of MN.i.327 (paṭhavī˚, āpa˚ etc.) Mil.343.</p>

jigucchamāna|jigucchamaana|jigucchamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigucchamaana</span></p><p>of jigucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigucchati">jigucchati</a></p>

jigucchana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dislike, contempt, disgust Vism.159; Pv-a.120.</p>

jigucchanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n. of jigucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigucchati">jigucchati</a></p>

jigucchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>shuns, avoids; loathes, detest; is disgusted with or horrified at.</p>

jigucchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shun, avoid, loathe, detest, to be disgusted with or horrified at (c. instr.) DN.i.213 (iddhi-pāṭihāriyena aṭṭiyāmi harāyāmi j.): AN.iv.174 (kāyaduccaritena); Snp.215 (kammehi pāpakehi; Snp-a.266 = hiriyati); Ja.ii.287; Pp.36<br />ppr jigucchamāna Iti.43; grd. jigucchitabba AN.i.126; pp. jigucchita Snp.901<br />■ See also jeguccha, jegucchin.</p><p>Desid. of <em>gup</em></p>

jigucchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) disliked; detested.</li><li>(n.) avoidance; abstention.</li></ol>

jigucchitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n.</i></p><p>of jigucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigucchati">jigucchati</a></p>

jigucchitvā|jigucchitvaa|jigucchitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigucchitvaa</span></p><p>of jigucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigucchati">jigucchati</a></p>

jigucchā|jigucchaa|jiguccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigucchaa</span></p><p>disgust for, detestation, avoidance, shunning: tapo˚ (detesting asceticism) DN.i.174; SN.i.67; AN.ii.200 jigucchabībhaccha-dassana detestable &amp; fearful-looking Pv-a.56.; <em>Note.</em> A diff. spelling, digucchā, occurs at Dhs-a.210.</p>

jigīsaka|jigiisaka|jigisaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jigiisaka</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jigiṃsaka">jigiṃsaka</a></p>

jimha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>oblique; slanting; not straight, not on the right line; dishonest, false; (what is not straight; ?)</p>

jimha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective crooked, oblique, slant, fig. dishonest, false (cp. vanka, opp. uju | MN.i.31 (+ vanka) AN.v.289, AN.v.290; Ja.i.290 (spelled jima); Ja.iii.111 = Ja.v.222 Ja.vi.66; Vism.219 (ajimha = uju); Pv-a.51 (citta˚ vanka… ; opp. uju). Cp. kuṭila.</p><p>Vedic jihma</p>

jimhatā|jimhataa|jimhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jimhataa</span></p><p>crookedness, deceit (opp. ujutā) Dhs.50, Dhs.51 (+ vankatā); Vb.359.</p><p>n. abstr. to jimha</p>

jimheyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(from jimha) the not being straight; deceit, fraud.</p>

jimheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>crookedness, deceit, fraud MN.i.340 (sāṭheyyāni kūṭeyyāni vankeyyāni j.˚); AN.iv.189 (id.), AN.v.167.</p><p>from jimha</p>

jina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>jina<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine for Buddhists a designation usually only of a buddha; rarely of a paccekabuddha.</p><p>jina<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. beaten, conquered; or deprived (of property)</p>

jina
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>conquering, victorious, often of the Buddha, “Victor”: jitā me pāpakā dhammā tasmâhaṃ Upaka jino ti Vin.i.8 = MN.i.171; Vin.v.217; Snp.379, Snp.697, Snp.989, Snp.996. magga˚ conqueror of the Path Snp.84 sq.; saṃsuddha˚ (id.) Snp.372. Cp khetta˚. In other connections: Pv.iv.3#33; Thig.419 (jin’ amhase rūpinaṃ Lacchiṃ explained at Thag-a.268 as jinā amhase jinā vat’ amha rūpavatiṃ Siriṃ).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://cakka">cakka</a></em> the Buddha’s reign, rule, authority Ja.iv.100</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://putta">putta</a></em> disciple of the B. Mil.177;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C5%ABmi">bhūmi</a></em> the ground or footing of a conqueror Pv-a.254;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81sana">sāsana</a></em> the doctrine of the B. Dpvs.iv.3, Dpvs.iv.10.</li></ul><p>pp. med. of jayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cakka">cakka</a>, <a href="bword://putta">putta</a>, <a href="bword://bhūmi">bhūmi</a>, <a href="bword://sāsana">sāsana</a></p>

jinadattā|jinadattaa|jinadatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jinadattaa</span></p><p>A bhikkhunī mentioned in the Therīgāthā verses of Isidāsi, who describes Jinadattā as a learned and virtuous expert in the Vinaya. Thig.427</p>

jinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>jinati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3. sg wins, is victorious; conquers, defeats; wins.</p><p>jinati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. oppresses; deprives someone of property, confiscates someone’s property; fines.</p>

jine
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt.</i></p><ol start="3"><li>sg. of jināti</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://jināti">jināti</a></p>

jini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor.</i></p><ol start="3"><li>sg. of jināti</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://jināti">jināti</a></p>

jinitvā|jinitvaa|jinitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jinitvaa</span></p><p>of jayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jayati">jayati</a></p>

jiniṃsu|jini.msu|jinimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jini.msu</span></p><p>of jināti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jināti">jināti</a></p>

jināti|jinaati|jinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jinaati</span></p><p>= jayati (jeti). See also vi˚.</p>

jirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jīrati">jīrati</a></p>

jirīyati|jiriiyati|jiriyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jiriiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jīrīyati">jīrīyati</a></p>

jita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha. MN.iii.70</p>

jita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li><p>(mfn.)</p><ol><li>beaten, defeated; conquered, won.</li><li>who has lost something (acc.) to someone (in dicing or gambling)</li></ol></li><li><p>(n.) what has been won; conquest.</p></li></ol>

jita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>conquered, subdued, mastered: (nt.) victory. jitā me pāpakā dhammā Vin.i.8; Dhp.40, Dhp.104 (attā jitaṃ seyyo for attā jito seyyo see Dhp-a.ii.228), Vism.105, Vism.179; Vv.64#27 (jitindriya one whose senses are mastered, cp. guttindriya)<br />■ Cp. vi˚.</p><p>pp. of jayati, conquer</p>

jitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>mastery, conquest Vv-a.284.</p><p>n. abstr. of jita</p>

jitvā|jitvaa|jitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jitvaa</span></p><p>having conquered; having subdued.</p>

jivhadhātu|jivhadhaatu|jivhadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhadhaatu</span></p><p>the constituent element that is the tongue (as sense organ)</p>

jivhagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the tip of the tongue.</p>

jivhanicchākaraṃ|jivhanicchaakara.m|jivhanicchakaram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhanicchaakara.m</span></p><p>indeclinable sticking out the tongue.</p>

jivhaviññeyya|jivhavi~n~neyya|jivhavinneyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhavi~n~neyya</span></p><p>cognizable by the sense-organ that is the tongue.</p>

jivhindriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the sense of taste.</p>

jivhā|jivhaa|jivha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhaa</span></p><p>the tongue; the organ of taste.</p>

jivhā|jivhaa|jivha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhaa</span></p><p>the tongue.</p><ol><li>physically: Vin.i.34; AN.iv.131; Snp.673, Snp.716; Dhp.65 Dhp.360; Ja.ii.306; Pv-a.99 (of Petas: visukkha-kanthaṭṭha j.), Pv-a.152<br />■ Of the tongue of the mahāpurusha which could touch his ears &amp; cover his forehead: Snp.1022; Snp.p.108; &amp; pahūta-jivhatā the characteristic of possessing a prominent tongue (as the 27th of the 32 Mahāpurisa-lakkhaṇāni) DN.i.106 = Snp.p.107; DN.ii.18 <em>-dujjivha</em> (adj.) having a bad tongue (of a poisonous snake) AN.iii.260</li><li>psychologically: the sense of taste. It follows after ghāna (smell) as the 4th sense in the enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of sense-organs (jivhāya rasaṃ sāyati Nd.ii under rūpa; jivhā-viññeyya rasa DN.i.245; DN.ii.281; MN.ii.42) Vin.i.34; DN.iii.102, DN.iii.226; MN.i.191; Vism.444.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> the tip of the tongue AN.iii.109; AN.iv.137; Dhp-a.ii.33.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a></em> the organ of taste DN.iii.243, DN.iii.280, DN.iii.290; Dhs.585, Dhs.609, Dhs.653;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a></em> the sense of taste DN.iii.239; Dhs.585, Dhs.609, Dhs.972;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nittaddana">nittaddana</a></em> corr. to <em>-nitthaddhana</em> tying the tongue by means of a spell DN.i.11 (cp. DN-a.i.96)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vi%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">viññāṇa</a></em> the cognition of taste MN.i.112; DN.iii.243; Dhs.556, Dhs.612, Dhs.632;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://samphassa">samphassa</a></em> contact with the sense of taste SN.i.115; DN.iii.243; Dhs.585, Dhs.632, Dhs.787.</li></ul><p>Vedic jihvā, cp. Lat. lingua (older dingua); Goth. tuggo; Ohg. zunga; E. tongue</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a>, <a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a>, <a href="bword://nittaddana">nittaddana</a>, <a href="bword://viññāṇa">viññāṇa</a>, <a href="bword://samphassa">samphassa</a></p>

jivhānittaddana|jivhaanittaddana|jivhanittaddana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhaanittaddana</span></p><p>paralyzing the tongue by means of a spell.</p>

jivhāyatana|jivhaayatana|jivhayatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jivhaayatana</span></p><p>the sphere of perception, the sense organ that is the tongue.</p>

jiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>jiyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pass. pr. 3 sg. is defeated, loses (something, acc., in dicing or gambling)</p><p>jiyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. grows old; decays, wears out; perishes.</p>

jiyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>jiyyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pass. pr. 3 sg.</p><p>jiyyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jīyati">jīyati</a>, <a href="bword://jīyati">jīyati</a></p>

jiyā|jiyaa|jiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jiyaa</span></p><p>bow string.</p>

jiyā|jiyaa|jiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jiyaa</span></p><p>bow string MN.i.429 (five kinds); Ja.ii.88 Ja.iii.323; Vism.150; DN-a.i.207. <em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> bowstring-maker Mil.331.</p><p>Vedic jyā = Gr. βιός bow, cp. also Lat. filum thread</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a></p>

jiñjuka|ji~njuka|jinjuka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji~njuka</span></p><p>See <em>Guñja</em>.</p><p>See also: Guñja</p>

jiñjuka|ji~njuka|jinjuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji~njuka</span></p><p>the Gunja shrub (Abrus precatorius) Ja.iv.333 (akkhīni j. ˚phalasadisāni, cp. in same application guñjā); Ja.v.156 (j. ˚phalasannibha); Dhp-a.i.177 (˚gumba).</p>

jiṇṇa|ji.n.na|jinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji.n.na</span></p><p>old; worn out; an old man.</p>

jiṇṇa|ji.n.na|jinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji.n.na</span></p><ol><li>decayed, broken up, frail, decrepit, old: vuḍḍha mahallaka andhagata vayo-anupatta Cnd.261; jarājiṇṇatāya jiṇṇa DN-a.i.283<br />■ Vin.ii.189; DN.i.114; MN.ii.48 sq., MN.ii.66; AN.ii.249; AN.iv.173; Snp.1 (urago va jiṇṇaṃ tacaṃ jahāti); Pv.i.12#1 (same simile); Snp.1120 Snp.1144; Ja.i.58; Ja.iii.22 (-pilotikā worn-out rags); Dhp.155 Dhp.260; Pv.ii.11#4 (jarājiṇṇa Pv-a.147); Pp.33; Vism.119 (˚vihārā), Vism.356 (˚sandamānikā), Vism.357 (˚koṭṭha); Thag-a.213 (-ghara a tumble-down house); Pv-a.40 (-goṇa = jaraggava), Pv-a.55 (of a roof). Cp. ˚tara Ja.iv.108.</li><li>digested Ja.ii.362</li></ol><p>pp. of jarati</p>

jiṇṇaka|ji.n.naka|jinnaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji.n.naka</span></p><p>old; rather old; an old man.</p>

jiṇṇaka|ji.n.naka|jinnaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji.n.naka</span></p><p>adjective = jiṇṇa Snp.98, Snp.124; Ja.iv.178, Ja.iv.366; Sdhp.299 (sālā).</p>

jiṇṇatā|ji.n.nataa|jinnata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ji.n.nataa</span></p><p>decrepitude DN-a.i.283 (jarā˚).</p><p>cp. jiṇṇa, jaratā &amp; jīraṇatā</p>

jotaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective illuminating, making light; explaining Ja.ii.420; Dpvs.xiv.50; Mil.343 (= lamp-lighter)<br />■ f. <em>-ikā</em> explanation, commentary, Name of several Commentaries, e.g. the Paramatthajotikā on the Sutta Nipāta (Kp-a.11); cp. the similar expression dīpanī (Paramatthadīpanī on Th 2; Vv &amp; Pv.).; <em>Jotika</em> Np. Dhp-a.i.385 (Jotiya); Vism.233, Vism.382.</p><p>from juti</p>

jotana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><strong>jotanā</strong> (f.) illumination, explanation Ja.vi.542; Pts.ii.112; Vv-a.17 (˚nā).</p><p>cp. Sk. dyotana</p><p>See also: jotanā</p>

jotati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>shines; is bright; is splendid.</p>

jotati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shine, be splendid Ja.i.53; Ja.vi.100, Ja.vi.509; Pv-a.71 (<em>jotantī = obhāsentī</em>).</p><p>Sk. dyotate to shine, <em>*dei̯ā</em>; cp. Gr. δέαται shine, δ ̈ηλος clear; also Sk. <em>dī</em> in dīpyate; Lat. dies. Dhtp.120 gives <em>jut</em> in meaning “ditti,” i.e. light</p>

jotayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://joteti">joteti</a></p>

joteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>illuminate, makes bright; elucidates; makes appear, makes manifest; expresses.</p>

joteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>trs. to cause to shine, illuminate, make clear, explain AN.ii.51 = Ja.v.509 (bhāsaye jotaye dhammaṃ; Gloss Ja.v.510 katheyya for joteyya = jotaye Iti.108; Ja.ii.208; Pv-a.18</li><li>intrs. to shine Dhp-a.ii.163 (ñāṇajutiyā jotetvā); pp. jotita resplendent Pv-a.53.</li></ol><p>Caus. of jotati</p>

joti
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A class of gods, present at the Mahāsamaya. DN.ii.261</p>

joti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>light, splendor, radiance.</li><li>fire.</li></ol>

joti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><ol><li>light, splendour, radiance SN.i.93; AN.ii.85; Vv.16#2.</li><li>a star: see compounds</li><li>fire SN.i.169; Thag.415; Ja.iv.206; sajotibhūta set on fire SN.ii.260; AN.iii.407 sq.; Ja.i.232.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://par%C4%81ya%E1%B9%87a">parāyaṇa</a></em> (adj.) attaining to light or glory SN.i.93; AN.ii.85; DN.iii.233; Pp.51;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81vaka">pāvaka</a></em> a brilliant fire Vv.16#2 (expl. Vv-a.79: candima-suriya-nakkhatta tāraka-rūpānaṃ sādhāraṇa-nāmaṃ);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81s%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">pāsāṇa</a></em> a burning glass made of a crystal Dhp-a.iv.209;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C4%81lik%C4%81">mālikā</a></em> a certain torture (setting the body on fire: making a fiery garland MN.i.87 = AN.i.47 = AN.ii.122 = Mnd.154 = Cnd.604 = Mil.197</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a></em> a certain jewel (wishing stone) Vv-a.111, Vv-a.339; Dhp-a.i.198; Mil.118;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a></em> the science of the stars astronomy: one of the 6 Vedic disciplines: see chaḷanga cp. jotisā.</li></ul><p>Sk. jyotis (cp. dyuti) nt. to dyotate, see <em><a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parāyaṇa">parāyaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://pāvaka">pāvaka</a>, <a href="bword://pāsāṇa">pāsāṇa</a>, <a href="bword://mālikā">mālikā</a>, <a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a>, <a href="bword://sattha">sattha</a>, <a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a></p>

jotidāsa|jotidaasa|jotidasa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jotidaasa</span></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verses speak of kamma. Thag.143–144</p>

jotika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A householder of Rājagaha and father of Dīghāvu. When Dīghāvu fell ill he sent Jotika to tell the Buddha. SN.v.344<em>f.</em></p>

jotika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>flint, a tinder box.</p>

jotima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jotimat">jotimat</a></p><p>endowed with light or splendor.</p>

jotimant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective luminous, endowed with light or splendour, bright, excellent (in knowledge) Snp.348 (= paññājoti-sampanna Snp-a.348).</p><p>joti + mant, cp. also P. jutimant</p>

jotimat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jotimat">jotimat</a></p><p>endowed with light or splendor.</p>

jotimālika|jotimaalika|jotimalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jotimaalika</span></p><p>garland of fire, a certain torture.</p>

jotiparāyana|jotiparaayana|jotiparayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jotiparaayana</span></p><p>attaining to light or splendor.</p>

jotipāla|jotipaala|jotipala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jotipaala</span></p><p>Jotipāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>The Bodhisatta born as a brahmin of Vehaliṅga in the time of Kassapa Buddha. Ghaṭīkāra was his friend and invited Jotipāla to accompany him to the Buddha, but Jotipāla refused to go, saying that a “shaveling recluse” could be of no use to him. But Ghaṭīkāra was very insistent, and one day, after they had bathed together in the river, seized Jotipāla by the hair and made a final appeal. This boldness on the part of an inferior caused Jotipāla to realise his extreme earnestness and he agreed to go. After hearing the Buddha preach, Jotipāla became a monk. MN.ii.46ff.;S.i.34<em>f.</em></p><p>Jotipāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A brahmin, son of Govinda, chaplain of Disampati. Jotipāla was a friend of Disampati’s son, Reṇu, who had six other nobles as companions. On the death of Govinda, Jotipāla became chaplain to Disampati. He inspired Reṇu’s six companions to wait on Reṇu and make him promise to share the kingdom with them when he should come to the throne. This promise Reṇu kept when he succeeded his father and appointed Jotipāla to carry out the division of the kingdom, which the latter duly did. All the kings wished Jotipāla to be their chaplain, and he instructed them in the art of government, teaching the mantras also to seven eminent brahmins and to seven hundred young graduates. Jotipāla himself came to be known as Mahā Govinda.</p><p>After some time, Jotipāla took leave of the seven kings, his disciples and his wives, and spent the four months of the rainy season in a retired spot outside the city, developing <em>jhāna</em> in order to see Brahmā face to face. At the end of the four months, Brahmā Sanaṅkumāra appeared before him and gave him a boon. Jotipāla asked to be taught the way to reach the Brahma-world, and, having listened to Sanaṅkumāra’s exposition, decided to leave the world. The kings and all the others did their best to make him desist from this course, but finding their efforts of no avail they went with him into the homeless life, where all of them profited thereby.</p><p>Jotipāla was the Buddha in a previous life. DN.ii.232–251 He is twice mentioned in the Aṅguttara Nikāya AN.iii.372 AN.iv.135 in a list of ancient teachers with very large followings.</p>

jotisā|jotisaa|jotisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jotisaa</span></p><p>astronomy Mil.3.</p><p>= Sk. jyotiṣa (nt.)</p>

joy
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Joy:</strong> <em>somanassa</em> - Altruistic <strong>j.</strong> = <em>muditā</em> see: <em>brahma-vihāra</em>.</p>

juha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://juhat">juhat</a></p><p>of juhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>sacrifice; an offering.</p>

juhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>offering, sacrifice DN.i.12, Ja.ii.43.</p><p>fr. juhati</p>

juhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of juhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://juhat">juhat</a></p><p>of juhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>sacrifices, offers (to or in); gives, dedicates.</p>

juhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to pour (into the fire), to sacrifice, offer; to give, dedicate AN.ii.207 (aggiṃ) Snp.1046 (= Cnd.263 deti cīvaraṃ, etc.); Snp.428 (aggihuttaṃ jūhato), Snp.p.79 (aggiṃ); Pp.56; fut. juhissati SN.i.166 (aggiṃ); caus. hāpeti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pp. huta; see also <em><a href="bword://hava">hava</a></em>, havi, homa.</p><p>Sk. juhoti, <em>*gheu (d)</em>; cp. Gr. ξέω, ξύτρα, ξϋλος; Lat. fundo; Goth. giutan, Ohg. giozan</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://hava">hava</a></p>

juhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhitvā|juhitvaa|juhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">juhitvaa</span></p><p>of juhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhiṃ|juhi.m|juhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">juhi.m</span></p><p>of juhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juhāti|juhaati|juhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">juhaati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juhati">juhati</a></p>

juti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>effulgence; brightness; splendor.</p>

juti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>splendour, brightness, effulgence, light Ja.ii.353; Pv-a.122, Pv-a.137, Pv-a.198. The spelling juti at MN.i.328 (in combination gati + juti) seems to be faulty for cuti (so as variant reading given on p. 557).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhara">dhara</a></em> (jutin˚) carrying or showing light, shining resplendent, brilliant SN.i.121; Ja.ii.353; Dhp-a.i.432.</li></ul><p>Sk. jyuti &amp; dyuti, to dyotate, see <em><a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhara">dhara</a>, <a href="bword://jotati">jotati</a></p>

jutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) having light, in <em>mahā˚</em>; of great splendour DN.ii.272; AN.i.206; AN.iv.248.</p>

jutima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jutimat">jutimat</a></p><p>brilliant; bright; distinguished.</p>

jutimant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective brilliant, bright; usually fig. as prominent in wisdom: “bright.” distinguished, a great light (in this sense often as variant reading to jātimant) DN.ii.256 (ī); SN.v.24; Dhp.89 (= Dhp-a.ii.163 ñāṇajutiyā jotetvā) Snp.508; Pv.iv.1#35 (= Pv-a.230 ñāṇajutiyā jutimā).</p><p>fr. juti</p>

jutimanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jutimat">jutimat</a></p>

jutimantatā|jutimantataa|jutimantata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jutimantataa</span></p><p>splendour Snp-a.453.</p><p>fr. jutimant</p>

jutimat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jutimat">jutimat</a></p><p>brilliant; bright; distinguished.</p>

jutimatā|jutimataa|jutimata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jutimataa</span></p><p>splendour, brightness, prominence Ja.14; Ja.v.405</p><p>fr. jutimant</p>

jutindhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>resplendent; brilliant; effulgent.</p>

jutīma|jutiima|jutima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jutiima</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jutīmat">jutīmat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jutimat">jutimat</a></p>

jutīmat|jutiimat|jutimat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jutiimat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jutīmat">jutīmat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jutimat">jutimat</a></p>

juṇha|ju.nha|junha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ju.nha</span></p><p>juṇha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine the light half of a month.</p><p>juṇha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>f of juṇhā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://juṇhā">juṇhā</a></p>

juṇhā|ju.nhaa|junha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ju.nhaa</span></p><p>moonlight; a moonlit night.</p>

juṇhā|ju.nhaa|junha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ju.nhaa</span></p><p>[Sk. jyotsnā, see also P. dosinā) moonlight, a moonlit night, the bright fortnight of the month (opp kālapakkha) Vin.i.138, Vin.i.176; Ja.i.165; Ja.iv.498 (˚pakkha).</p>

jyā|jyaa|jya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jyaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jiyā">jiyā</a></p>

jāgara|jaagara|jagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagara</span></p><p>awake; vigilant; watchful.</p>

jāgara|jaagara|jagara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagara</span></p><p>adjective waking, watchful, careful, vigilant SN.i.3; AN.ii.13 = Iti.116; MN.ii.31; Iti.41; Mil.300<br />■ <em>bahu˚</em>; wide awake, well aware, cautious Snp.972 (cp. rakkhita-mānasāno in same context v. 63) Dhp.29.</p><p>fr. jāgarti</p>

jāgaranta|jaagaranta|jagaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagaranta</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of jāgarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāgarati">jāgarati</a></p>

jāgarat|jaagarat|jagarat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jāgarat">jāgarat</a></p><p>of jāgarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāgarati">jāgarati</a></p>

jāgarati|jaagarati|jagarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarati</span></p><p>is awake; is watchful.</p>

jāgarati|jaagarati|jagarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarati</span></p><p>to be awake, to be watchful, to be on the alert (cp. guttadvāra) Dhp.60 (dīghā jāgarato rattī), Dhp.226; Iti.41; Mil.300<br />pp <em><a href="bword://j%C4%81garita">jāgarita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. jāgarti to be awake (redupl. perf. for jājarti) <em>*ger &amp; gerēi;</em> cp. Lat. expergiscor (*exprogrīscor); Gr.ἐγείρω, perf. ἐγρήγορα (for *ἐγήγορα). Def. at Dhtp.254 by niddā-khaya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāgarita">jāgarita</a></p>

jāgaratā|jaagarataa|jagarata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarataa</span></p><p>watchfulness, vigilance SN.i.3.</p><p>cp. Sk. jāgaraṇa</p>

jāgaraṇa|jaagara.na|jagarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagara.na</span></p><p>means for waking or keeping awake Mil.301.</p><p>der. fr. jāgara</p>

jāgarita|jaagarita|jagarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp (mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarita</span></p><p>being awake; watchfulness.</p>

jāgarita|jaagarita|jagarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagarita</span></p><p>waking, vigil Iti.41; Pp.59.</p><p>pp. of jāgarti</p>

jāgariya|jaagariya|jagariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagariya</span></p><p>, ~a, neuter wakefulness, watchfulness.</p>

jāgariyā|jaagariyaa|jagariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagariyaa</span></p><p>keeping awake, watchfulness, vigilance, esp. in the sense of being cautious of the dangers that are likely to befall one who strives after perfection. Therefore freq. in combination “indriyesu guttadvāro bhojane mattaññū jāgariyaṃ anuyutto” (anuyuñjati: to apply oneself to or being devoted to vigilance), e.g. SN.ii.218; MN.i.32, MN.i.273, MN.i.354 sq. MN.i.471; AN.i.113 sq.; AN.ii.40<br />■ Also in <em>-ṃ bhajati</em> to pursue watchfulness (bhajetha keep vigil) Iti.42; Snp.926 (niddaṃ na bahulīkareyya j˚ṃ bhajeyya ātāpī)<br />■ SN.iv.104; MN.i.273, MN.i.355; Mil.388.</p><ul><li><em>-ānuyoga</em> application or practice of watchfulness Mnd.484.</li></ul><p>BSk. M Vastu jāgarikā</p>

jāgariyānuyoga|jaagariyaanuyoga|jagariyanuyoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaagariyaanuyoga</span></p><p>application, devotion to wakefulness.</p>

jāla|jaala|jala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaala</span></p><p>net; a network; a web.</p>

jāla|jaala|jala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaala</span></p><p>Jāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a net; netting, entanglement (lit. or fig.): snare, deceptíon (= māyā)</p><h3>A. <em>Lit</em></h3><p>Cnd.260 (= suttajāla, a plaiting of threads); Snp-a.115 Snp-a.263 (= suttamaya) DN.i.45 (anto-jālikata caught in a net); Snp.62, Snp.71, Snp.213, Snp.669; Ja.i.52; Ja.vi.139<br />■ kinkiṇika a row of bells DN.ii.183; muttā˚ a net of pearls Ja.i.9; Vv-a.40; loha˚ Pv-a.153; hema˚ Vv.35; a fowler’s net Dhp.174; a spider’s web Dhp.347; nets for hair Ja.vi.188 pabbata˚ a chain of mountains Ja.ii.399; sirā˚ network of veins Ja.v.69; Pv-a.68<br />■ Freq. in similes: see <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1907, 90</p><h3>B. <em>Fig</em></h3><p>Very often applied to the snares of Māra: SN.i.48 (maccuno); Snp.357 (id.); Dhp-a.iii.175 (Māra˚); Snp.527 (deception); taṇhā˚ the snare of worldly thirst (cp. ˚tanhā) MN.i.271; Thag.306; Snp-a.351 kāma˚ Thag.355; moha˚ SN.iii.83; mohasama Dhp.251 diṭṭhi˚ the fallacies of heresy DN.i.46; Ja.vi.220; ñāṇa the net of knowledge Vv-a.63; Dhp-a.iii.171. bhumma (vijjā) “earthly net,” i.e. gift of clearsight extending over the earth Snp-a.353.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a></em> a mesh of a net Ja.i.208:</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ta%E1%B9%87h%C4%81">taṇhā</a></em> the net of thirst Dhs.1059, Dhs.1136; Dhs-a.367;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C5%ABpa">pūpa</a></em> a “netcake”? Dhp-a.i.319;</li><li><em>-hatthapāda</em> (adj.) having net-like hands &amp; feet (one of the 32 marks of a Mahāpurisa prob. with reference to long nails DN.ii.17 (see <em>Dial.</em> ii.14, note 3), cp. jālitambanakhehi Vv.81#16 (explained at Vv-a.315: jālavantehi abhilohita-nakkehi. Tena jāli (variant reading jāla-) hatthataṃ mahāpurisa-lakkhaṇaṃ tambanakhataṃ anuvyañ anañ ca dasseti).</li></ul><p>Vedic jāla, prob. from <em>jaṭ</em>; to plait, make a tangle cp. jaṭita &amp; jaṭā; on l:ṭ cp. phulla: sphuṭa cāru: cāṭu; cela: ceṭa</p><p>Jāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>glow, blaze Ja.v.326; Pv-a.52 (= tejas), Pv-a.154 (raṃsi˚); Mil.357; Vism.419 (kappavināsaka˚).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://roruva">roruva</a></em> Name of one of the two Roruva hells (“blazes”) Ja.v.271;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sikh%C4%81">sikhā</a></em> a glowing crest i.e. a flame Cnd.11 (= accī).</li></ul><p>Sk. jvāla, from jalati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://akkhi">akkhi</a>, <a href="bword://taṇhā">taṇhā</a>, <a href="bword://pūpa">pūpa</a>, <a href="bword://roruva">roruva</a>, <a href="bword://sikhā">sikhā</a></p>

jālaka|jaalaka|jalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalaka</span></p><ol><li>a net Ja.vi.536; Dāvs v.51.</li><li>a bud AN.iv.117 sq. (˚jāta in bud)</li></ol><p>■ f. <strong>jālikā</strong> chain armour Mil.199.</p><p>jāla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + ka</p><p>See also: jālikā</p>

jālakajāta|jaalakajaata|jalakajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalakajaata</span></p><p>covered in small buds?</p>

jālapacchanna|jaalapacchanna|jalapacchanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalapacchanna</span></p><p>covered by a net.</p>

jālayati|jaalayati|jalayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalayati</span></p><p>of jāleti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāleti">jāleti</a></p>

jāleti|jaaleti|jaleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaleti</span></p><p>set lights to; lights.</p>

jāleti|jaaleti|jaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaleti</span></p><p>to cause to burn, to light, kindle Ja.ii.104; Ja.iv.290; Ja.v.32.</p><p>caus. of jalati. See also jaleti</p>

jāletuṃ|jaaletu.m|jaletum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaletu.m</span></p><p>of jāleti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāleti">jāleti</a></p>

jāli|jaali|jali
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaali</span></p><p>JāliJāḷi</p><p>The name of two Pacceka Buddhas, occurring in a nominal list. MN.iii.70</p>

jāli|jaali|jali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaali</span></p><p>Also written as: jālin<sup>1</sup></p><ol><li><p>(mfn.)</p><ol><li>having a net, ensnaring (esp. an epithet of taṇhā)</li><li>marked with a network of lines?</li></ol></li><li><p>(taṇhā) the ensnarer, desire.</p></li></ol><p>jāli(n)<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine &amp; neuter possessing flames.</p>

jālin|jaalin|jalin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalin</span></p><p>Also written as: jālin<sup>1</sup></p><ol><li><p>(mfn.)</p><ol><li>having a net, ensnaring (esp. an epithet of taṇhā)</li><li>marked with a network of lines?</li></ol></li><li><p>(taṇhā) the ensnarer, desire.</p></li></ol><p>jāli(n)<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. possessing flames.</p>

jālin|jaalin|jalin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalin</span></p><p>adjective noun “having a net,” ensnaring, deceptive:</p><ol><li>lit. a fisherman Ja.ii.178</li><li>fig. usually in f <em>-inī</em> of tanhā (ensnarer, witch) SN.i.107 = Dhp.180; AN.ii.211 Thag.162, Thag.908; Dhs.1059; Vism.1; Dhs-a.363; cp Mhvs.i.166; Mhvs.iii.92.</li></ol>

jālina|jaalina|jalina
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalina</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha. MN.iii.70</p>

jālinī|jaalinii|jalini
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalinii</span></p><p>A goddess of Tāvatiṃsā, a former wife of Anuruddha. Once seeing him old and feeble, she appeared before him in Kosala and bade him aspire to rebirth among the gods. Anuruddha told her there would be no rebirth for him. SN.i.200 Thag.908</p>

jālinī|jaalinii|jalini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalinii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jālin">jālin</a></p>

jālituṃ|jaalitu.m|jalitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalitu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāletuṃ">jāletuṃ</a></p>

jāliya|jaaliya|jaliya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaliya</span></p><p>A wanderer who, with his friend Mandissa, visited the Buddha at the Ghositārāma. DN.i.159 The Buddha preached to them the Jāliya Sutta. According to the Pātika Sutta, when Jāliya heard that Pātika could not come to hold a discussion with the Buddha at Vesāli, he went to the Tindukkhāna-paribbājakārāma and tried to get Pātikaputta to come. But the latter was unable to come, being fixed in his seat. Jāliya thereupon spoke insultingly to him, calling him boaster, etc. DN.iii.22<em>ff.</em></p>

jālā|jaalaa|jala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalaa</span></p><p>flame Ja.i.216, Ja.i.322; Mil.148, Mil.357.</p><p>see *<a href="bword://j%C4%81la">jāla</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jāla">jāla</a></p>

jālāhatthapāda|jaalaahatthapaada|jalahatthapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalaahatthapaada</span></p><p>with “netted” hands and feet, prob. with hands and feet marked with a network of lines (one of the 32 marks of a mahapurisa)</p>

jālāsaṅkhāta|jaalaasa"nkhaata|jalasankhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalaasa"nkhaata</span></p><p>(that) which is called a net.</p>

jālāvātapāna|jaalaavaatapaana|jalavatapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaalaavaatapaana</span></p><p>lattice window or shutter.</p>

jāmāta|jaamaata|jamata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaamaata</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jāmātar">jāmātar</a></p><p>son-in-law.</p>

jāmātar|jaamaatar|jamatar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaamaatar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jāmātar">jāmātar</a></p><p>son-in-law.</p>

jāmātar|jaamaatar|jamatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaamaatar</span></p><p>(&amp; <em>jāmāta</em> Ja.iv.219) daughter’s husband, son-inlaw Thig.422 (= Thag-a.269 duhitu pati); Ja.ii.63; Ja.v.442.</p><p>Vedic jāmātar. Deriv. uncertain. BR. take it as jā + mātar, the builder up of the family, supposing the case where there is no son and the husband goes to live in the wife’s family, a bīna marriage. More likely fr. ldg <em>*gem</em>, to marry. Cp. Gr.γαμέω· γαμβρός, Lat. gener</p>

jāmātara|jaamaatara|jamatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaamaatara</span></p><p>son-in-law.</p>

jāna|jaana|jana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaana</span></p><p>(one) who knows, who has an understanding.</p>

jāna|jaana|jana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaana</span></p><p>adjective knowing or knowable, understandable Ja.iii.24 (= jānamāna). <em><a href="bword://dujj%C4%81na">dujjāna</a></em> difficult to understand DN.i.170, DN.i.187; MN.i.487; MN.ii.43. <em>su˚</em> recognizable, intelligible Pv.iv.1#35 (= suviññeyya Pv-a.230). Cp. ājāna.</p><p>to <em>jñā</em>, see <em><a href="bword://j%C4%81n%C4%81ti">jānāti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dujjāna">dujjāna</a>, <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānamana|jaanamana|janamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanamana</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānana|jaanana|janana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanana</span></p><p>knowledge, cognizance, recognition; intelligence, learning, skill Ja.i.145 (attānaṃ-˚kālato paṭṭhāya from the time of self-recognition), Ja.i.200 (-˚manta knowledge of a spell, a spell known by tumhākaṃ), Ja.ii.221; Snp-a.330; Dhp-a.ii.73 (˚sabhāva ñatta); DN-a.i.86 (akkhara˚); Vism.391 (˚atthāya in order to know), Vism.436 (= pajānana). Cp. ājānana. <em><a href="bword://aj%C4%81nana">ajānana</a></em> not knowing (˚-) Ja.v.199; Ja.vi.177; not known Ja.i.32 (˚sippa).</p><p>fr. <em>jñā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ajānana">ajānana</a></p>

jānanaka|jaananaka|jananaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaananaka</span></p><p>adjective knowing Dhs-a.394.</p><p>Sk. *jñānaka, cp. jānana &amp; Sk. jānaka (c. gen.) expert Avs.ii.119, 120, as n. ib. i.216</p>

jānanatā|jaananataa|jananata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaananataa</span></p><p>the fact of knowing, knowledge Kp-a.144.</p><p>abstr. fr. jānana</p>

jānanta|jaananta|jananta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaananta</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānapada|jaanapada|janapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; m.f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanapada</span></p><p>belonging to or living in a country or district; belonging to the country (as opposed to the town); a country man or a woman; rustic.</p>

jānapada|jaanapada|janapada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanapada</span></p><p>adjective noun belonging to the country, living in the c.; pl. country-folk (opp. negamā townsfolk) DN.i.136, DN.i.142; MN.ii.74; Ja.ii.287, Ja.ii.388; DN-a.i.297 (= janapada-vāsin).</p><p>fr. janapada</p>

jānapaṭicchanna|jaanapa.ticchanna|janapaticchanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanapa.ticchanna</span></p><p>(~antī)n. know and concealed.</p>

jānat|jaanat|janat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jānat">jānat</a></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānaṃ|jaana.m|janam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaana.m</span></p><p>adverb of also jānāti knowingly; with knowledge</p><p>See also: also jānāti</p>

jānemu|jaanemu|janemu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanemu</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jāneyya|jaaneyya|janeyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaneyya</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jāneyyuṃ|jaaneyyu.m|janeyyum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaneyyu.m</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jāneyyāma|jaaneyyaama|janeyyama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaneyyaama</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jāneyyāsi|jaaneyyaasi|janeyyasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaaneyyaasi</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jāni|jaani|jani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaani</span></p><p>jāni<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine a wife.</p><p>jāni<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine loss, deprivation; fine, confiscation.</p>

jāni|jaani|jani
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaani</span></p><p>Jāni<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine deprivation, loss, confiscation of property; plundering, robbery; using force, ill-treatment DN.i.135 = AN.i.201 (vadhena vā bandhena vā jāniyā vā); SN.i.66 (hatajānisu), Ja.i.55 (variant reading jāti), Ja.i.212 (mahājānikara a great robber); Ja.iv.72 (dhana,˚ variant reading hāni); Dhp.138 (= Dhp-a.iii.70 dhanassa jāni, variant reading hāni).</p><p>from jahati, confused in meaning with jayati. See jahati &amp; cp. janti</p><p>Jāni<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine wife, in jānipatayo (pl.) wife &amp; husband (cp. jāyā(m)pati) AN.ii.59 sq.</p>

jānimha|jaanimha|janimha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanimha</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānipatayo|jaanipatayo|janipatayo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanipatayo</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānipatī">jānipatī</a></p>

jānipatī|jaanipatii|janipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanipatii</span></p><p>the wife and husband.</p>

jānipita|jaanipita|janipita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanipita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jānipitar">jānipitar</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://janīpitar">janīpitar</a></p>

jānipitar|jaanipitar|janipitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanipitar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://jānipitar">jānipitar</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://janīpitar">janīpitar</a></p>

jānissami|jaanissami|janissami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">jaanissami</span></p><p>of jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

jānissati|jaanissati|janissati
