katuṃ|katu.m|katum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">katu.m</span></p><p>doing, acting.</p>

katvā|katvaa|katva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">katvaa</span></p><p>having done or made.</p>

katvāna|katvaana|katvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">katvaana</span></p><p>having done or made.</p>

katākata|kataakata|katakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kataakata</span></p><p>what is) done and not done; (what has been) done and (what) not (yet) done; manufactured and not manufactured</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kata">kata</a></p>

katāvakāsa|kataavakaasa|katavakasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kataavakaasa</span></p><p>having been given the opportunity; having been given consent; having obtained a chance or scope</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kata">kata</a></p>

katāvi|kataavi|katavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kataavi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://katāvin">katāvin</a></p><p>one who has done (what was to be done); skilled, accomplished.</p>

katāvin|kataavin|katavin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kataavin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://katāvin">katāvin</a></p><p>one who has done (what was to be done); skilled, accomplished.</p>

katāvin|kataavin|katavin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kataavin</span></p><p>adjective noun one who has done (what could be done), used like <strong>katakicca</strong> to denote one who has attained Arahantship SN.i.14; Mil.264.</p><p>secondary formation fr. kata</p><p>See also: katakicca</p>

kavaca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>armor; a coat of mail.</p>

kavaca
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>mail, a coat of mail, armour DN.ii.107 = Ud.64 (applied to existence); Thag.614 (of sīla); Ja.iv.92, Ja.iv.296; Mil.199, Mil.257; Vism.73.</p><ul><li><em>-<em>jālikā</em></em> a mail-coat Mil.199.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. kavaca</p><p>See also: jālikā</p>

kavandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter a</i></p><p>headless trunk of the body (usually alive, or at least capable of movement)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kabandha">kabandha</a></p>

kavandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><ol><li>the (headless) trunk of the body, endowed with the power of motion Vin.iii.107; cp. SN.ii.260 (asīsaka˚); Mil.292; Dhp-a.i.314.</li><li>a headless dwarf, whose head has been crushed down into his body Ja.v.424, Ja.v.427 (cp. the story of Dhanu, the Rākṣasa who was punished by having his head and thighs forced into his body, Raghuvaṃsa xii.57).</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. kavandha &amp; kabandha</p>

kavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>thinker; a poet; a maker of verse.</p>

kavi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>poet SN.i.38; SN.ii.267; Dāvs i.10; four classes enumerated at AN.ii.230 &amp; DN-a.i.95, viz. 1 cintā˚ an original 2 suta˚ one who puts into verse what he has heard 3 attha˚ a didactic 4 paṭibhāṇa˚ an improvisor</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kata">kata</a></em> composed by poets SN.ii.267; AN.i.72.</li></ul><p>Vedic kavi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kata">kata</a></p>

kaviṭṭha|kavi.t.tha|kavittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavi.t.tha</span></p><p>the elephant-apple tree, Feronia elephantum Ja.v.38 (˚vana).</p><p>cp. kapittha</p>

kavya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>poetry; ballad, ode (cp. kabba) Ja.vi.213, Ja.vi.216.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> a poet Ja.vi.216.</li></ul><p>cp. Vedic kavya wise; sacrificer</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a></p>

kavāṭa|kavaa.ta|kavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavaa.ta</span></p><p>the leaf or panel of a door or shutter; a door or shutter.</p>

kavāṭa|kavaa.ta|kavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavaa.ta</span></p><ol><li>the panels of the door, the door proper, not the aperture Vin.ii.114, Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.207, Vin.ii.208 (see Vin.ii.148 for the description of a door), Vin.iv.269, Vin.iv.304 (˚baddha = āvasatha); Ja.i.19; Cnd.235#1d; Vism.28 (˚koṇa door-corner).</li><li><em>dvāra˚</em>; a door-post Ja.i.63; Ja.ii.334; Pv-a.280.</li><li>a window Mhvs.ix.17<br />■ ˚ṃ paṇāmeti to open the door Vin.ii.114, Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.207; ˚ṃ ākoṭeti to knock at the door DN.i.88 (= DN-a.i.252); Vin.ii.208<br />■ <em>akavāṭaka</em> (adj.) having no doors, doorless Vin.ii.148, Vin.ii.154 (variant reading for akkavāta Text).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pi%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">piṭṭha</a></em> the panels and posts of a door; the door and the door-posts Vin.i.47, Vin.i.48 = Vin.ii.208, Vin.ii.218;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://baddha">baddha</a></em> “door-bound,” closed, secure Vin.iv.292 (see also above).</li></ul><p>See also: <a href="bword://piṭṭha">piṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://baddha">baddha</a></p>

kavāṭabaddha|kavaa.tabaddha|kavatabaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavaa.tabaddha</span></p><p>closed or fastened with a door; fitted with a door</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kavāṭa">kavāṭa</a></p>

kavāṭaka|kavaa.taka|kavataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavaa.taka</span></p><p>= kavāṭa Vin.ii.148; DN-a.i.62 (nīvaraṇa˚).</p>

kavāṭapiṭṭha|kavaa.tapi.t.tha|kavatapittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">kavaa.tapi.t.tha</span></p><p>door panel and post</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kavāṭa">kavāṭa</a></p>

kaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>buying, purchase; purchase price.</p>

kaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>purchase, buying AN.iii.226 (+ vi˚).</p><p><em>-(a)kkaya</em>, buying &amp; selling Pv.i.5#6 (see also Kp.vii.6 and note). <em>-<a href="bword://vikkaya">vikkaya</a></em> (kraya vikraya) buying selling, trade in ˚paṭivirata DN.i.5 = AN.ii.209 = AN.v.205 Pp.58; DN.i.64; SN.v.473; Snp.929; Ja.v.243; Khus.114; Dhp-a.i.78; Pv-a.29 (= Kp-a.212).</p><p>fr. <em>kri</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vikkaya">vikkaya</a></p>

kayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to buy; Inf. ketuṃ Ja.iii.282; cp. kiṇāti.</p><p><em>krī</em>, perhaps connected with <em>kṛ</em></p>

kayavikkaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>trading; buying and selling</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaya">kaya</a></p>

kayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>buyer, trader, dealer Mil.334.</p><p>fr. <em>krī</em>, cp. BSk. krayika Divy.505</p>

kayin
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>buyer Ja.vi.110.</p>

