namassat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://namassat">namassat</a></p><p>of namassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassati">namassati</a></p>

namassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>pays homage to; reverences; prays to.</p>

namassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to pay honour to, to venerate, honour, do homage to (often with pañjalika &amp; añjaliṃ katvā) Snp.236, Snp.485, Snp.598, Snp.1058 Snp.1063; Cnd.334; Ja.iii.83; Pv.ii.12#20; Kp-a.196; pot namasseyya Iti.110; Dhp.392, 1st pl. namassemu Snp.995 ppr. namassaṃ Snp.334, Snp.934; namassanto Snp-a.565, (usually) namassamāna Snp.192, Snp.1142; Mnd.400; Ja.ii.73; Vv-a.7<br />aor <em>namassiṃsu</em> Snp.287<br />ger <em><a href="bword://namassitv%C4%81">namassitvā</a></em> Ja.i.1<br />grd (as adj.) <em>namassaniya</em> (venerable) Mil.278.</p><p>Ved. namasyati, Denom. fr. namo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassitvā">namassitvā</a></p>

namassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://namassiṃ">namassiṃ</a></p><p>of namassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassati">namassati</a></p>

namassitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassati">namassati</a></p>

namassitvā|namassitvaa|namassitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">namassitvaa</span></p><p>of namassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassati">namassati</a></p>

namassiyā|namassiyaa|namassiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">namassiyaa</span></p><p>(namassā) feminine worship, veneration Mil.140.</p><p>Sk. namasyā</p>

namassiṃ|namassi.m|namassim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">namassi.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://namassiṃ">namassiṃ</a></p><p>of namassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namassati">namassati</a></p>

namataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>felt covering for a knife.</p>

namataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>piece of cloth Vin.ii.115 (satthaka), Vin.ii.123, Vin.ii.267 (˚ṃ dhāreti).</p><p>word &amp; etym. doubtful; cp. nantaka &amp; Bdhgh. Vin.ii.317: matakan (sic) ti satthakavedhanakaṃ (= veṭhanakaṃ) pilotikakhaṇḍaṃ</p>

namati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>bends; bows; inclines; turns towards.</p>

namati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bend, bend down (trs. &amp; instr.) direct, apply SN.i.137 (cittaṃ) Snp.806; Ja.i.61 (aor. nami, cittaṃ)<br />caus <em>nameti</em> (<em>not</em> nāmeti, Fsb. to Snp.1143 nāmenti, which is to be corrected to n’ âpenti) to bend, to wield Dhp.80 = Dhp.145 (namayati). As <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81meti">nāmeti</a></em> at Ja.vi.349. pp. <em><a href="bword://namita">namita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Ved. namati, Idg. <em>*nem</em> to bend; also to share out, cp. Gr. νέμω, Goth. niman = Ger. nehmen. See cognates in Walde loc. cit. under nemus</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāmeti">nāmeti</a>, <a href="bword://namita">namita</a></p>

namayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāmeti">nāmeti</a></p>

nametabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāmetabba">nāmetabba</a></p>

nameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāmeti">nāmeti</a></p>

nami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of namati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namati">namati</a></p>

namita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bent on, disposed to (-˚), able or capable of Ja.iii.392 (pabbajjāya-namita-citta); Mil.308 (phalabhāra˚).</p><p>pp. nameti</p>

namo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>be my adoration to.</p>

namo
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><em>Nama</em> (nt.) nomage, veneration esp. used as an exclamation of adoration at the beginning of a book (namo tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sammāsambuddhassa) Snp.540, Snp.544; Pv-a.1, Pv-a.67.</p><p>Ved. namas, cp. Av. nəmo prayer; Gr. νέμος, Lat. nemus (see <em><a href="bword://namati">namati</a></em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namati">namati</a></p>

namuci
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(Np.) a name of Māra.</p>

namucī|namucii|namuci
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">namucii</span></p><p>the name of an asura; the name of māra.</p>

nanandar
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>husband’s sister Ja.v.269 (= sāmikassa bhaginī p. Ja.v.275).</p><p>Sk. nanāndṛ &amp; nanāndā, to nanā “mother”</p>

nanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Vaccha</p><p>Mentioned in a list of well known leaders of the Ajivakas, the others being Kisa Saṅkicca and Makkhali Gosāla. MN.i.524 They were declared by Purāṇa Kassapa, in his classification of the six classes, to be of the pure white class. AN.iii.384</p><p>The austerities practised by Nanda Vaccha are detailed in the Mahā Saccaka Sutta. MN.i.238</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>The Buddha’s half-brother. He became a monk, but was dissatisifed due to longing for his beautiful former partner. The Buddha shows him the bauty of the celestial nymphs, beside whom his beloved looked like a scalded she-monkey with nose and ears cut off. The Buddha promised Nanda 500 such nymphs if he were to stay as a monk. He remained in robes, but later became ashamed of his purpose and practiced sincerely, attaining arahantship. Ud.iii.2 His Theragāthā verses speak of his struggle with desire. Thag.157<em>f.</em></p><p>Later, on seeing how eminently Nanda was trained in self control, the Buddha declared him chief among his disciples in that respect. AN.i.25</p><p>Nanda was very beautiful, and was only four inches shorter than the Buddha. He once wore a robe made according to the dimensions of the Buddha’s robe. Discovering this, the Buddha chided him for his presumption. Vin.iv.173 Another time Nanda is said to have donned a robe which was pressed on both sides, painted his face, and gone to see the Buddha, carrying a bright bowl. The Buddha chided him, and Nanda thereupon became a forest dweller and a rag-robe-man. SN.ii.281</p><p>The Aṅguttara Nikāya AN.iv.166<em>f.</em> contains a discourse in which the Buddha discusses Nanda’s claim to have achieved self control in all things.</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Nandamāṇava</p><p>One of the chief disciples of Bāvarī. His conversation with the Buddha is recorded in the Nanda māṇavaapucchā. Snp.1007 Snp.1124</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Nandagopālaka</p><p>He was a cowherd of Kosambi. One day he heard the Buddha preach to the monks, using as simile a log of wood how, in certain circumstances, it finds its way direct to the sea and how, similarly, a monk may reach nibbina. Nanda asked permission to join the Order. But the Buddha insisted that he should first return the cattle, for which he was responsible, to their owners. Nanda did so, and was then ordained, becoming an arahant soon after. SN.iv.181</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A <em>devaputta</em> who visited the Buddha and had a conversation with him. S.i.62.</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->6<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Kumāputta</p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse extolls the virtues of the homeless life. Thag.36</p><p>Nanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->7<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>The Isigili Sutta mentions four Pacceka Buddhas of this name. MN.iii.70</p>

nanda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Pv.ii.6#7 used either as interj. (= nanu, q.v.) or as voc. in the sense of “dear”; the first expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to be preferred &amp; n. probably to be read as nanu (variant reading nuna or; <em><a href="bword://handa">handa</a></em> (in which case nanu would be gloss).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://handa">handa</a></p>

nandaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nandaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Once, at the Buddha’s request, he preached a sermon to the nuns; on the first day they became sotāpannas, and, on the second, five hundred of them attained arahantship. MN.iii.270 AN.iv.358<em>ff.</em> From that time the Buddha declared him foremost among exhorters of the nuns. AN.i.24</p><p>The Theragāthā Thag.279–282 contains several verses uttered by him to a woman to whom he was once married. She met him begging alms in Savatthi and smiled to him with sinful heart.</p><p>The Aṅguttara Nikāya attributes two discourses to Nandaka. The first AN.i.193<em>f.</em> was preached at the Migāramātupāsāda and takes the form of a discussion with Sāḷha, Migāra’s grandson, and Rohana, Pekkhuniya’s grandson on greed, covetousness, malice and delusion, and the benefits following their destruction. The second discourse is a sermon addressed to the monks at the waiting hall at Jetavana. It is said that the Buddha was attracted to the spot by the sound of Nandaka’s preaching, and, finding the door locked, stood for a long time outside, listening. AN.iv.358<em>ff.</em> When his back began to ache he knocked at the door, and, having entered, told Nandaka that he had been waiting until the end of his discourse to speak to him. Nandaka expressed. his regret that he should have kept the Buddha waiting and pleaded ignorance of his presence. The Buddha, conscious of Nandaka’s remorse, went on to praise his sermon, and said that the preaching of such sermons was the duty of all pious monks. When the Buddha left, Nandaka resumed his sermon, and told his audience of the five results of listening to the Dhamma in due season.</p><p>Nandaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verses speak of how a horse might stumble but then recovers and stands firm. Thag.173<em>f.</em></p><p>Nandaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A minister of the Licchavis who visited the Buddha at the Kūṭāgārasālā in Vesali. The Buddha tells him that the Ariyan disciple, possessed of unwavering loyalty to the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Saṅgha., and having Ariyan virtues, is assured of enlightenment and happiness. During the conversation, a man comes to tell Nandaka that his bath is ready. Nandaka sends him away, saying that the inner washing loyalty to the Buddha is far more important. SN.v.389</p>

nandaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective giving pleasure, pleasing, full of joy; (f.) <em>nandikā</em> Ja.iv.396 (+ khiḍḍā), either as adj or f. abstract pleasure, rejoicing (= abhindandanā Commentary)</p><p>Sk. <em>nandikā</em></p>

nandamāna|nandamaana|nandamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandamaana</span></p><p>of nandati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nandati">nandati</a></p>

nandana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nandana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A <em>devaputta</em> who visited the Buddha at Sāvatthī and asked him questions regarding virtue, wisdom, etc. The Buddha answered them, and he went away satisfied. SN.i.62</p><p>Nandana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Nandanavana</p><p>The chief of the parks in Tāvatiṃsā, where the inhabitants of Tāvatiṃsā, headed by Indra, go for their amusement. AN.iii.40 Cakkavatti kings are born in Tāvatiṃsā after death and spend their time in Nandanavana. SN.v.342</p><p>5park</p>

nandana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.f.</i></p><p>(from nandati) delight, pleasure, enjoyment.</p>

nandanā|nandanaa|nandana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandanaa</span></p><p>rejoicing, delight, pleasure SN.i.6 = Snp.33.</p><p>Sk. nandanā</p>

nandati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is pleased; is glad; enjoys.</p>

nandati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be glad, to rejoice, find delight in, be proud of (c. instr.) SN.i.110; AN.iv.94 sq.; Snp.33; Dhp.18<br />caus <em>nandeti</em> to please, to do a favour Ja.iv.107 (nandaya = tosehi Com.); Pv-a.139 (= toseti)-ppr. nandayanto Ja.vi.588<br />■ Cp. ānandati.</p><p>Ved. nandati, <em>nand = nad</em> (cp. vind → vid etc.) orig. to utter sounds of joy</p>

nandavatī|nandavatii|nandavati
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandavatii</span></p><p>A nun, sister of Thullanandā. Her other two sisters were Nandā and Sundarīnandā. Vin.iv.211 Vin.iv.259</p>

nandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see *<a href="bword://yuga">yuga</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://yuga">yuga</a></p>

nandhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>meaning not so much “to bind as “to cover”: see apiḷandhati, upanandhati, onandhati pariyonandhati.</p><p>for nayhati, der. fr. naddha after analogy of baddha → bandhati</p>

nandhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naddhi">naddhi</a></p>

nandhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>(usually spelt nandi) a strap, thong Ja.i.175 (rathassa cammañ ca nandiñ ca); Snp.622 = Dhp.398 (+ varatta) Snp-a.400; Dhp-a.i.44, Dhp-a.iv.160.</p><p>Sk. naddhrī to naddha, pp. of <em>nah</em> to bind</p>

nandi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><ol><li>joy, pleasure, delight.</li><li>a drum announcing or accompanying proclamations.</li></ol>

nandi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nandi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Nandī</p><p>masculine &amp; feminine</p><ol><li>joy, enjoyment, pleasure, delight in (c loc.) SN.i.16, SN.i.39, SN.i.54; SN.ii.101 sq. (āhāre); SN.iii.14 (= upādāna), SN.iv.36 sq.; AN.ii.10 (kāma˚, bhava˚, diṭṭhi˚), AN.iii.246, AN.iv.423 sq. (dhamma˚); Snp.1055 (+ nivesana); Cnd.330 (= taṇhā); Pp.57; Dhs.1059≈(in def. of taṇhā) Vb.145, Vb.356, Vb.361; Dhs-a.363; Thag-a.65, Thag-a.167<br />■ For nandī at Mil.289 read tandī.</li><li>a musical instrument: joy-drum [Sk. nandī] Vin.iii.108 (= vijayabheri) Cp. ā˚.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-(y)āvatta</em> “turning auspiciously” (i.e. turning to the right: see dakkhiṇāvatta), auspicious, good Ne.2 Ne.4, Ne.7, Ne.113 (always attr. of naya);</li><li><em>-ūpasecana</em> (rāgasalla sprinkled over with joy, having joy as its sauce Ne.116, Ne.117; cp. maṃsûpasecana (odana) Ja.iii.144 = Ja.vi.24</li><li><em>-kkhaya</em> the destruction of (finding) delight SN.iii.51</li><li><em>-(ṃ)jaha</em> giving up or abandoning joy Snp.1101 (+ okañjaha &amp; kappañjaha) Cnd.331;</li><li><em>-bhava</em> existence of joy, being full of joy, in <em>˚parikkhīṇa</em> one in whom joy is extinct (i.e. an Arahant), explained however by Com as one who has rid himself of the craving for rebirth (tīsu bhavesu parikkhīnataṇha Dhp-a.iv.192 = Snp-a.469 SN.i.2 SN.i.53 Snp.175 Snp.637 = Dhp.413;</li><li><em>- mukhī</em> (adj. f.) “joyfaced,” showing a merry face, epithet of the night (esp the eve of the uposatha) Vin.i.288 (ratti); Vin.ii.236 (id.)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C4%81ga">rāga</a></em> pleasure &amp; lust, passionate delight SN.ii.227 SN.iii.51; SN.iv.142, SN.iv.174, SN.iv.180; MN.i.145; Dhs.1059 ≈ Dhs.1136 esp. as attr. of taṇhā in phrase n-r-sahagata-taṇhā (cp Mvu.iii.332: nandīrāgasahagatā tr̥ṣṇā) Vin.i.10; SN.iii.158; SN.v.425 sq.; Pts.ii.137; Ne.72;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83yojana">saṃyojana</a></em> the fetter of finding delight in anything Snp.1109, Snp.1115 Cnd.332;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a></em> the rise or origin of delight MN.iii.267.</li></ul><p>Sk. nandi, but cp. BSk. nandī Divy.37</p><p>Nandi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>= nandhi.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://rāga">rāga</a>, <a href="bword://saṃyojana">saṃyojana</a>, <a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a></p>

nandimukhī|nandimukhii|nandimukhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandimukhii</span></p><p>‘just before the beating of the (morning) drum’, bright-faced (?)</p>

nandimukhīratti|nandimukhiiratti|nandimukhiratti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n). (?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandimukhiiratti</span></p><p>daybreak.</p>

nandin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nandin">nandin</a></p><p>taking delight; enjoying.</p>

nandin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective finding or giving delight, delighting in, pleasurable, gladdening. SN.ii.53 (vedanā) AN.ii.59 AN.ii.61 Iti.112</p><p>Sk. nandin</p>

nandissaṃ|nandissa.m|nandissam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandissa.m</span></p><p>of nandati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nandati">nandati</a></p>

nandivisāla|nandivisaala|nandivisala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandivisaala</span></p><p>A deva who visits the Buddha and converses with him on the nature of the body and its riddance. SN.i.63 SN.i.15</p>

nandiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nandiya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>He belonged to a Sākiyan family of Kapilavatthu, and was called Nandiya because his birth brought bliss. He left the world at the same time as Anuruddha, Kimbila and the others, and he soon attained arahantship. Thereafter he dwelt with his companions in the Pācīnavaṃsamigadāya. Vin.i.350<em>f.</em> It was to them that the Upakkilesa Sutta was preached. MN.iii.155 Later, they seem to have lived in the Gosiṅgasālavana. MN.i.205 His Theragāthā verse is a warning to the Dark Lord not to attack a bhikkhu such as he. Thag.25</p><p>Nandiya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Sakyan layman. He visited the Buddha at the Nigrodhārāma in Kapilavatthu and had a discussion with him on the different kinds of Ariyan disciple, the one who dwells in remissness and the one who is earnest.SN.v.397<em>ff.</em> SN.v.403 Later, when the Buddha returned to Sāvatthī for the rainy season, Nandiya also went there, finding some business to do, and from time to time he visited the Buddha. At the end of the rains, when the Buddha and the monks were about to start on tour, Nandiya went to the Buddha and was taught the eleven conditions which lead to the destruction of evil. AN.v.334<em>ff.</em></p><p>Nandiya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Paribbājaka who visits the Buddha at Jetavana and asks him the conditions for the attainment of nibbana. The Buddha teaches him the Noble Eightfold Path. SN.v.11</p>

nandiṃjaha|nandi.mjaha|nandimjaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandi.mjaha</span></p><p>abandoning enjoying.</p>

nanduttarā|nanduttaraa|nanduttara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nanduttaraa</span></p><p>A bhikkhunī whose Therīgāthā verses speak of her former useless practice of austerities and her current state of Awakening. Thig.87–91</p>

nandā|nandaa|nanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandaa</span></p><p>Nandā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A nun, sister of Thullanandā. Her other two sisters were Nandavatī and Sundarinandā. Vin.iv.211 Vin.iv.259</p><p>Nandā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Declared by the Buddha AN.i.25 to be foremost among nuns in <em>jhāna</em>. She is evidently to be identified with Sundarīnandā.</p><p>Nandā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A nun mentioned as having died at Nātika and having been reborn spontaneously in the Suddhāvāsā, there to pass away, never to return. DN.ii.91 SN.v.356<em>f.</em></p>

nandī|nandii|nandi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nandii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nandi">nandi</a></p>

nandūpasecana|nanduupasecana|nandupasecana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nanduupasecana</span></p><p>with a sauce or infusion of enjoying.</p>

nanikāma|nanikaama|nanikama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nanikaama</span></p><p>adjective disagreeable, unpleasant Dhp.309 (˚seyyā an uncomfortable bed).</p><p>na + nikāma = anikāma</p>

nantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dirty or ragged cloth; a rag.</p>

nantaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>shred rag, worn-out cloth, usually explained by jiṇṇapilotika (Ja.iii.22) or khaṇḍabhūtā pilotikā (Pv-a.185) or pilotika only (Vv-a.311)<br />■ SN.v.342; AN.iii.187; AN.iv.376 (˚vāsin as variant reading; text has nantikavāsin); Vv.80#7 (anantaka) Pv.iii.2#14; Ja.iii.22 (˚vāsin clad in rags).</p><p>a contamination of namataka (Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 169), maybe Sk. naktaka “cover for nakedness (Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 81<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), unless it be non-Aryan</p>

nantakavāsi|nantakavaasi|nantakavasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nantakavaasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nantakavāsin">nantakavāsin</a></p><p>wearing rags.</p>

nantakavāsin|nantakavaasin|nantakavasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nantakavaasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nantakavāsin">nantakavāsin</a></p><p>wearing rags.</p>

nanu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable (particle of affirmation) not; is it not? certain; surely.</p>

nanu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li>part. of affirmation (cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->): surely, certainly Pv.ii.6#7 (so to be read for nanda? variant reading BB nuna); Manor. Pūr. on AN.v.194 (Andersen P. R. 91)</li><li>part. of interrogation (= Lat. nonne) “is it not (cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->): Ja.i.151; Ja.iii.393; Dhp-a.i.33.</li></ol><p>Ved. nanu</p>

napuṃsaka|napu.msaka|napumsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">napu.msaka</span></p><p>adjective of no sex; lit. Vism.548, Vism.553; Thag-a.260; Vb.417; in gram. of the neuter gender Kacc. 50; Pv-a.266 (is reading correct?)</p><p>Ved. napuṃsaka = na + puṃs “notmale”</p>

nara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>man; a person; pl. men; people.</p>

nara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>man (in poetry esp. a brave, strong heroic man), pl. either “men” or “people” (the latter e.g. at Snp.776, Snp.1082; Pv.i.11#12)<br />■ AN.i.130 AN.ii.5; AN.iii.53; Snp.39, Snp.96, Snp.116, Snp.329, Snp.591, Snp.676, Snp.865 etc. Dhp.47, Dhp.48, Dhp.262, Dhp.309, Dhp.341; Ja.iii.295; Mnd.12 = Cnd.335 (definition); Vv-a.42 (popular etymology: narati netī ti naro puriso, i.e. a “leading” man); Pv-a.116 = Dhp.125.</p><ul><li><em>-ādhama</em> vilest of men Snp.246;</li><li><em>-āsabha</em> “man-bull”, i.e. lord of men Snp.684, Snp.996;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://inda">inda</a></em> “man-lord”, i.e. king Snp.836; Ja.i.151;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></em> best of men (Ep. of the Buddha) SN.i.23; DN.iii.147; Snp.1021;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em> god-man or man-god (pl.), gods, also epithet of the B. “king of men” SN.i.5; Pv.iv.3#50;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81r%C4%AB">nārī</a></em> (pl.) men &amp; women, appl. to male &amp; female angelic servants (of the Yakkhas) Vv.32#4, Vv.33#7 Vv.53#8; Pv.ii.11#2;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%ABra">vīra</a></em> a hero (?), a skilled man (?) Thag.736 (<em>naravīrakata</em> “by human skill &amp; wit” Mrs. Rh D.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%ABha">sīha</a></em> lion of men Ja.i.89.</li></ul><p>Ved. nara, cp. nṛtu; Idg. <em>*ner</em> to be strong or valiant = Gr. ἀνήρ, ἀγ ήνωρ (valiant), δρώψ (*νρώψ); Lat neriosus (muscuḷar), Nero (Sabinian, cp. Oscan ner Lat. vir); Oir. nert</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://inda">inda</a>, <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a>, <a href="bword://deva">deva</a>, <a href="bword://nārī">nārī</a>, <a href="bword://vīra">vīra</a>, <a href="bword://sīha">sīha</a></p>

narada
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Spikenard, sometimes also <em><a href="bword://ma%E1%B9%83si">maṃsi</a></em>, Hindi <em>nalab</em>, <em>Nardostachys grandiflora</em> Ja.vi.537 Ja.vi.535, an erect perennial herb with elongated spatulate leaves and a rosy-pink or sometimes blue flower. The whole plant has a distinct lingering smell but the stout black-colored rhizome in particular gives off a sweet spicy fragrance. The Buddha referred to the root as <em>kāḷānusārī</em> and considered it to be the sweetest of all root fragrances A.v.22 and also to be a useful medicine Vin.i.203. An amber or sometimes greenish oil is extracted from the root by steam distillation. Both the whole root and the oil are widely used in medicines, perfumes and to make incense. Spikenard grows on the southern side of the Himalayas between 3500 and 4500 meters and is usually collected from the wild, although occasionally cultivated. Historical records show that spikenard made its way by trade as far as Egypt in ancient times.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Narada</em></p><p>Root: as an aromatic, bitter tonic, stimulant, antiseptic, for convulsions, inhaled (with other plants) for ulcers of nose and palate, dysentery, constipation, bronchitis (with other plants), as a laxative and to improve urination.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://maṃsi">maṃsi</a></p>

narada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>nard, ointment Ja.vi.537. Nala &amp; Nala;</p><p>Sk. nalada, Gr. νάρδος, of Semitic origin, cp. Hebr. nīrd</p>

naraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i></p><p>pit; a cleft.</p>

naraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a pit DN.i.234; Thag.869; Ja.iv.268 (˚āvāṭa Pv-a.225).</li><li>a name for Niraya, i.e. purgatory; a place of torment for the deceased (see <em><a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a></em> &amp; cp. list of narakas at Divy.67) SN.i.209; Snp.706; Pv-a.52; Sdhp.492 (saṃsāraghora˚), Sdhp.612.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85g%C4%81ra">aṅgāra</a></em> the ashes of purgatory Sdhp.32.</li></ul><p>Sk. naraka; etym. doubtful, problematic whether to Gr. νέρτερος (= inferus), Ags. nord = north as region of the underworld</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niraya">niraya</a>, <a href="bword://aṅgāra">aṅgāra</a></p>

naravasabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://narāsabha">narāsabha</a></p>

narāsabha|naraasabha|narasabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naraasabha</span></p><p>leader of men.</p>

nassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nassana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>disappearance, loss, destruction AN.iii.54 (˚dhamma adj. doomed to perish).</p><p>cp. Sk. naśana</p>

nassanadhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>liable to perish, to disappear.</p>

nassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is lost; is lost from sight; perishes; disappears, goes away; comes to nothing; is lost from the memory, is forgotten.</p>

nassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(v. intr.) to perish, to be lost or destroyed, to disappear, come to an end Snp.666 (na hi nassati kassaci kammaṃ); Iti.90; Ja.i.81, Ja.i.116, Ja.i.150; pret. <em>nassaṃ</em> (prohib.) Snp.1120, pl anassāma MN.i.177; aor. <em><a href="bword://nassi">nassi</a></em> AN.iii.54 (mā nassi prohib.) Ja.iv.137 (cakkhūni ˚iṃsu: the eyes failed); fut. <em>nassisati</em> Ja.i.5; cond. <em>nassissa</em> Ja.ii.112<br />caus <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81seti">nāseti</a></em> (q.v.) See also pa˚.</p><p>Ved. naś; naśyati &amp; naśati, cp. Gr.; νέκυς, νεκρός (corpse), νέκταρ (“overcoming death = nec + tr̥, cp. tarati); Lat. neco, noceo, noxius</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassi">nassi</a>, <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nassaṃ|nassa.m|nassam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nassa.m</span></p><p>of nassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nassāsiṃ|nassaasi.m|nassasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nassaasi.m</span></p><p>of nassati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of namati bent; inclined; turned towards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://namati">namati</a></p>

nata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bent (on) SN.i.186 (a˚); Snp.1143; Cnd.327.</p><p>Sk. nata, pp. of namati, q.v.</p>

nati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>bending; inclination; bent.</p>

nati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>bending, bent, inclination SN.ii.67; SN.iv.59; MN.i.115. Kalya-rupa</p><p>Sk. nati of <em>nam</em></p>

natta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine a</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nattar">nattar</a></p><p>grandchild.</p>

natta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>night, acc. <em>nattaṃ</em> by night, in <em>nattam-ahaṃ</em> by day &amp; by night Snp.1070 (variant reading BB and Cnd rattamahaṃ).</p><p>Sk. nakta, see <em><a href="bword://nakkhatta">nakkhatta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nakkhatta">nakkhatta</a></p>

nattamahaṃ|nattamaha.m|nattamaham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nattamaha.m</span></p><p>by day and night.</p>

nattamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>one who is like a grandson, who is the age or status of one’s grandson</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nattar">nattar</a></p>

nattamāla|nattamaala|nattamala
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nattamaala</span></p><p>Fever Nut, Hindi <em>naktamala</em>, <em>Caesalpina crista</em> Vin.i.201, an extensive climbing shrub covered with prickles, compound leaves and pale yellow flowers. The bark and leaves and the oil extracted from seed all have medicinal value. The fever nut is often grown as a hedge.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Nattamāla</em></p><p>Bark and leaves: as febrifuge, emmenagogue, anthemintic. Seeds: as tonic, laxative, antipyretic, antiperiodic, for malaria and colic. Seed oil: as emollient, for rheumatism, skin diseases, in ears to stop discharge.</p>

nattamāla|nattamaala|nattamala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nattamaala</span></p><p>tree; the indian beech; the tree pongamia pinnata.</p>

nattar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine a</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nattar">nattar</a></p><p>grandchild.</p>

nattar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>grandson Ja.i.60 (nattu, gen.), Ja.i.88; Ud.91 Ud.92; Pv-a.17 (nattu-dhītā great-grand-daughter), Pv-a.25 (nattā nom.).</p><p>Sk. naptṛ, analogy-formation after mātṛ etc. from Ved. napāt; cp. Lat. nepos; Ags. nefa = E. nephew Ohg. nevo</p>

natthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable it is not; there is not.</p>

natthibhāva|natthibhaava|natthibhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">natthibhaava</span></p><p>non-existence Dhp-a.iii.324.</p><p>n’ atthi-bhāva</p>

natthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>an unbeliever (in the existence or efficacy of good conduct, in the existence of another world, etc); a skeptic.</p>

natthika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun one who professes the motto of “natthi,” a sceptic, nihilist SN.i.96; usually in compounds</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhi">diṭṭhi</a></em> scepticism, nihilistic view, heresy Snp.243 (= micchāditthi Com.); Vv-a.342; Pv-a.244;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> one who professes a nihilistic doctrine SN.iii.73; MN.i.403; AN.ii.31; Pv-a.215 (+ micchādiṭṭhika).</li></ul><p>Sk. nāstika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diṭṭhi">diṭṭhi</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a></p>

natthikavāda|natthikavaada|natthikavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">natthikavaada</span></p><p>professing the doctrine of an unbeliever.</p>

natthitā|natthitaa|natthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">natthitaa</span></p><p>non-existence; absence.</p>

natthitā|natthitaa|natthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">natthitaa</span></p><p>nihilism SN.ii.17; Ja.v.110.</p><p>Sk. nāstitā, fr. n’ atthi</p>

natthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>into the nose.</li><li>an agent or substance that causes sneezing (?)</li></ol>

natthu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>the nose Ja.v.166 (= nāsā Com.).</li><li>= ˚kamma medical treatment through the nose Vin.iii.83 (deti).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> nose-treatment, consisting in the application of hot oil (DN-a.i.98: telaṃ yojetvā n-karaṇaṃ) DN.i.12; Vin.i.204; MN.i.511; Dhp-a.i.12;</li><li><em>-karaṇī</em> a pocket handkerchief Vin.i.204.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. nas f. &amp; nasta, see etym. under nāsā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

natthukamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>treatment through the nose.</p>

natthukaraṇī|natthukara.nii|natthukarani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">natthukara.nii</span></p><p>nasal-dropper.</p>

nattumatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nattamatta">nattamatta</a></p>

nava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn.</p><ol><li>new, fresh; recent; young; (iic also: newly, recently)</li><li>new, having recently entered the <em>saṅgha</em>; a novice.</li></ol><p>nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>num. nine.</p>

nava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(num.) number nine. gen<br />■ dat. navannaṃ (Snp.p.87) instr<br />■ abl. navahi (Vv-a.76), loc. navasu.</p><p><em>Meaning and Application:</em> The primitive-Aryan importance of the “mystic” nine is not found in Buddhism and can only be traced in Pali in folkloristic undercurrents (as fairy tales) &amp; stereotype traditions in which 9 appears as a number implying a higher trinity = 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>navabhūmaka pāsāda (a palace 9 stories high more freq. satta˚, 7) Ja.i.58; nava-hiraññakoṭīhi (w. 9 koṭis of gold) Vv-a.188; nava yojana Dhp-a.ii.65.</li><li>navangabuddhasāsana “the 9 fold teaching of Buddha,” i.e. the 9 divisions of the Buddh. Scriptures according to their form or style, viz. suttaṃ geyyaṃ veyyākaraṇaṃ gāthā udānaṃ itivuttakaṃ jātakaṃ abbhutadhammaṃ vedallaṃ MN.i.133; AN.ii.103, AN.ii.178; AN.iii.86 sq., AN.iii.177 sq.; Pp.43; Mil.344; Dpvs.iv.15; Pv-a.2. Cp. chaḷanga<br />■ nava sattāvāsā “9 abodes of beings” Kp iv. (in exemplifying No. 9), viz. (see DN.iii.263 = Kp-a.86, Kp-a.87 cp. also AN.iv.39 sq.) 1 manussā, devā, vinipātikā; 2 Brahmakāyikā devā; 3 Ābhassarā; 4 Subhakiṇhā; 5 Asaññasattā; 6 Ākāsanañcâyatana-upagā; 7 Viññāṇanañcâyatana˚; 8 Ākiñcaññāyatana˚; 9 Nevasaññâsaññâyatana˚-nava sotā (Snp.197) or nava dvārā (Vv-a.76; variant reading mukhā), 9 openings of the body, viz (Snp-a.248), 2 eyes, ears, nostrils, mouth, anus &amp; urethra (cp.; <em>S.B.E.</em> 39, 180; 40, 259 sq.)<br />■ nava vitakkā 9 thoughts Cnd.269 (q.v.).</li><li>a trace of the week of 9 days is to be found in the expression “navuti-vassasatasahass-āyukā” giving the age of a divinity as 9 million years (= a divine week) Vv-a.345<br />■ Cp navuti.</li></ol><p>Ved. navan, Idg. *neṷn̊, cp. Lat. novem (*noven), Gr. ἐννέα, Goth. niun, Oir. nōin, E. nine Connection with nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> likely because in counting by tetrads (octo = 8 is a dual!) a <em>new</em> series begins with No. 9</p><p>Nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>new, fresh; unsoiled, clean of late, lately acquired or practised (opp. pubba purāṇa). Often syn. with taruṇa. Snp.28, Snp.235 (opp purāṇaṃ), Snp.944 (id.), Snp.913 (opp. pubba); Pv.i.9#2 (of clothes = costly); Ja.iv.201 (opp. purāṇa); Mil.132 (salila fresh water).</li><li>young, unexperienced, newly initiated; a novice Vin.i.47 (navā bhikkhū the younger bhs., opp. therā); SN.i.9 (+ acira-pabbajita); SN.ii.218 Snp.p.93 (Gotamo navo pabbajjāya “a novice in the Wanderer’s life”); Dhp-a.i.92 (bhikkhu).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> building new, making repairs, “doing up, mending Vin.ii.119, Vin.ii.159; Vin.iii.81; Ja.i.92; Ja.iv.378; Cnd.385;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kammika">kammika</a></em> an expert in making repairs or in building, a builder (cp. vaḍḍhaki) Vin.ii.15; Vin.iv.211;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ghata">ghata</a></em> fresh ghee Ja.ii.433 (variant reading ˚sappi).</li></ul><p>Ved. nava, Idg. *neṷn̊ (cp. nava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) = Lat. novus, Gr. νέος (*νέvος), Lith. navas; Goth. niujis etc. = E. new; also Sk. <em>navya</em> = Gr. νεϊος, Lat. Novius May be related to na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a>, <a href="bword://kammika">kammika</a>, <a href="bword://ghata">ghata</a></p>

navaggaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>freshly or recently caught</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>period of nine days</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navahapaṭicchanna|navahapa.ticchanna|navahapaticchanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navahapa.ticchanna</span></p><p>concealed for nine days</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navahā|navahaa|navaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navahaa</span></p><p>within nine days</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>navaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine new; having recently entered the <em>saṅgha</em>; a novice.</p><p>navaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine &amp; neuter a set of nine; (being) a section consisting of a set or sets of nine.</p>

navaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun young; a young man, a newly ordained bhikkhu (opp. thera), novice (cp. Divy.404) Ja.i.33 (sangha˚); Pv-a.76 (id.)<br />■ Freq. in compar <em><a href="bword://navakatara">navakatara</a></em> a younger one, or the youngest (opp. theratara) DN.ii.154; Ja.i.218; Mil.24.</p><p>Sk. navaka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://navakatara">navakatara</a></p>

navakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>building work; repairs.</li><li>fresh actions, present acts</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navakammika bhāradvāja|navakammika bhaaradvaaja|navakammika bharadvaja
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navakammika bhaaradvaaja</span></p><p>Navakammika Bhāradvāja</p><p>One of the Bhāradvājas. Once, when the Buddha was staying in a forest in Kosala, Navakammika, who was there seeing about some timber work, saw the Buddha at the foot of a tree and asked him what pleasure he found in his contemplations. When the Buddha explained to him how he had found liberty, the Brahmin was pleased and accepted the Buddha as his teacher. SN.i.179</p>

navakammika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>(one) who is in charge of building work; a builder. Carpenter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navakanipāta|navakanipaata|navakanipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navakanipaata</span></p><p>section consisting of sets of nine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://navaka">navaka</a></p>

navakatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>, compar. of navaka newer; younger (in the <em>saṅgha</em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://navaka">navaka</a></p>

navama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>the ninth.</p>

navama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>ordinal number the ninth Snp.109; f. ˚ī Vv-a.72.</p><p>Sk. navama = Oir. nōmad; cp. Lat. nonus; Gr. ε ̓́νατος, Goth. niunda with diff. superl suffixes</p>

navamūlaka|navamuulaka|navamulaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navamuulaka</span></p><p>with nine bases, with nine roots</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navanava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>ever new; quite new; one new after another</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navanavutiyo
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>NavanavutiyoNavanavatiya</p><p>A city in Uttarakuru. DN.iii.201</p><p>5town</p>

navanivesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>new residence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navanīta|navaniita|navanita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navaniita</span></p><p>fresh butter.</p>

navanīta|navaniita|navanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navaniita</span></p><p><em><a href="bword://non%C4%ABta">nonīta</a></em> fresh butter Vin.i.244 (cp. gorasa); DN.i.201; MN.iii.141; Pv.iii.5#5 (nonīta); Pp.69, Pp.70; Mil.41, Dhs.646, Dhs.740; Dhp-a.i.417; Pv-a.199.</p><p>cp. Ved. navanīta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nonīta">nonīta</a></p>

navappāya|navappaaya|navappaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navappaaya</span></p><p>being mainly novices</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navaratta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>newly dyed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navasassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the first-fruits of the harvest</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with nine apertures</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

navaṭṭhānika|nava.t.thaanika|navatthanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nava.t.thaanika</span></p><p>connected with nine occasions or matters</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

naviya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective praiseworthy Mil.389.</p><p>Sk. navya, either grd. of navate to praise; or = nava, q.v.</p>

navuti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i></p><p>, num ninety.</p>

navuti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>number number ninety Vv-a.345 &amp; in compound <em>eka˚</em>; 91; DN.ii.2 (i.e. 92 minus 1; in expr. ekanavuto kappo, variant reading ekanavuti kappe); <em>dvā˚</em>; 92 (see <em><a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></em> AN II. &amp; B III.); Pv-a.19, Pv-a.21; <em>aṭṭhā˚</em>; 98; Snp.311 (diseases sprung fr. orig. 3).</p><p>Ved. navati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvi">dvi</a></p>

navutika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāvutika">nāvutika</a></p>

navutiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective worth ninety Ja.v.485. Cp. nāvutika.</p>

navutiṃ|navuti.m|navutim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navuti.m</span></p><p>, num ninety.</p>

navāvuta|navaavuta|navavuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">navaavuta</span></p><p>newly woven</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nava">nava</a></p>

naya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>way, way of proceeding; (right) method; method of interpretation; inference.</p>

naya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun “leading”; usually m: way (fig.), method, plan, manner; inference; sense, meaning (in grammar); behaviour, conduct AN.ii.193 = Cnd.151 (˚hetu through inference); Ne.2 (method), Ne.4 (id.), Ne.7, Ne.113; Mil.316 (nayena = nayahetu); Kp-a.74; Vv-a.112 (sense, context, sentence) Pv-a.1 (ways or conduct), Pv-a.117 (meaning), Pv-a.126 (id.), Pv-a.136, Pv-a.280<br />■ <em>nayaṃ neti</em> to draw a conclusion, apply an inference, judge, behave SN.ii.58 = Vb.329; Ja.iv.241 (anayaṃ nayati dummedho: draws a wrong conclusion) Pv-a.227 (+ anumināti)<br />■ With <em>-ādi˚</em>; N. has the function of continuing or completing the context “and similarly,” e.g. ˚ādinaya-pavatta dealing with this &amp; the following Vv-a.2;… ti ādinā nayena thus &amp; similarly, &amp; so forth Ja.i.81; Pv-a.30<br />■ Instr. <em>nayena</em> (-˚) as adv. in the way of, as, according(ly): āgata according to what has been shown or said in… Ja.i.59; Vv-a.3; Pv-a.280; purima˚ as before Ja.i.59 Ja.iv.140; vutta˚ as said (above) (cp. vutta-niyāmena Pv-a.13, Pv-a.29, Pv-a.36, Pv-a.71, Pv-a.92 etc<br />■ <em>sunaya</em> a sound judgment Ja.iv.241; <em>dunnaya</em> a wrong principle, method or judgment, or as adj.: wrongly inferred, hard to be understood, unintelligible AN.iii.178 = Ne.21; Ja.iv.241.</p><p>from nayati, to lead, see <em><a href="bword://neti">neti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://neti">neti</a></p>

nayamāna|nayamaana|nayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nayamaana</span></p><p>of nayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

nayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the eye.</p>

nayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the eye Thig.381; Vv.35#3; Dhs.597 Vb.71 sq.; Mil.365; Thag-a.255; Vv-a.161 (= cakkhu) Pv-a.40 (nettāni nayanāni), Pv-a.152; Sdhp.448, Sdhp.621.</p><p>Sk. nayana, to nayati = the leader cp. also netra = P. netta</p>

nayati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Neti &amp; nayati</p><p>to lead, guide, conduct; to take, carry (away); fig. to draw a conclusion, to understand, to take as Dhp.80, Dhp.145, Dhp.240, Dhp.257; Ja.i.228 Ja.iv.241 (nayaṃ n. to draw a proper conclusion); Vv-a.42 (narati = nayati);<br />imper <em><a href="bword://naya">naya</a></em> Pv.ii.11#3, &amp; <em>nehi</em> Ja.ii.160; Pv-a.147; poetic imper. <em>nayāhi</em> see in paṭi˚;<br />pot <em>naye</em> Dhp.256 (to lead a cause = vinicchineyya Dhp-a.iii.381)<br />fut <em>nessāmi</em> Ja.ii.159; Pv.ii.4#5;<br />aor <strong>nayi</strong> Ja.iv.137.<br />ger <em>netvā</em> Pv-a.5, Pv-a.6, etc.<br />inf <em>netuṃ</em> Pv-a.123, Pv-a.145 <em>(˚kāma) &amp; netave;</em> Ja.i.79 = Dhp.180.<br />grd <em><a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a></em> (see sep.),<br />pp <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABta">nīta</a></em>.<br />pass <em><a href="bword://n%C4%AByati">nīyati</a></em> (q.v.).<br />■ Cp. naya, nīti, netta etc. also ā˚, upa˚, paṭi˚, vi˚.</p><p>Vedic nayati, <em>nī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naya">naya</a>, nayi, <a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a>, <a href="bword://nīta">nīta</a>, <a href="bword://nīyati">nīyati</a></p>

nayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>leads, guides, conducts; takes; takes away; takes away in marriage; carries off.</li><li>finds out, ascertains; decides; determines.</li></ol>

nayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://neti">neti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://neti">neti</a></p>

nayhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>tying, binding; bond, fetter Dhp-a.iv.161.</p><p>Sk. nahana</p>

nayhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tie, bind; only in comp. with prep. as upanayhati (cp. upāhanā sandal), pilandhati etc<br />pp <em><a href="bword://naddha">naddha</a></em> (q.v.). See also nandhi, nāha; onayhati unnahanā, piḷayhati.</p><p>Ved. nahyati, Idg. <em>*nedh</em> as in Lat. nodus &amp; Ved. nahu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naddha">naddha</a></p>

nayiṃsu|nayi.msu|nayimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nayi.msu</span></p><p>of nayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

naḷa|na.la|nala
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.la</span></p><p>A general term for reeds and rushes, grasses that grow in or near water D.iii.75. Numerous types of reeds and rushes grow in northern India but few of them can be identified with the ones mentioned in the Tipiṭaka. A special caste of people harvested certain reeds and rushes D.i.51 S.iii.155. They would cut the stalks, grasp them, shake them up and down, throw them aside and then cut more S.iii.155. After this the stalks would be tied into sheaves S.ii.113. The Buddha said: “By hankering for the future and pining for the past, fools dry up and wither away like a green reed that has been cut” S.i.5. Numerous household articles were made out of reeds and rushes; sandals and stools Vin.iv.39, baskets and mats S.i.5. They were also used to make small huts S.i.156 S.iv.185, to thatch the roofs of houses and in the wattle-and-daub construction of walls Ja.iv.318. In the Jātaka, there is a story about merchants who sail to a sea called Nāḷamala which looked like a vast expanse of reeds Ja.iv.140. This almost certainly refers to the Shatt-al-arab below the confluence of the Tigris and Euphrates Rivers in Iraq. <strong>Isikā</strong> D.i.77 M.ii.170, <em><a href="bword://ka%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADaka">kaṭṭaka</a></em> Dhp.164 and <em><a href="bword://pabbaja">pabbaja</a></em> Th.27 Th.233 Vin.i.190 S.i.77 were all different types of reeds. <strong>Santha</strong> was a reed or cane used to make bow strings M.i.429 and <em><a href="bword://saravana">saravana</a></em> a reed with sharp leaves used to make arrow shafts S.iv.198.</p><p>See also: Isikā, <a href="bword://kaṭṭaka">kaṭṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://pabbaja">pabbaja</a>, Santha, <a href="bword://saravana">saravana</a></p>

naḷa|na.la|nala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.la</span></p><p>A Gandhabba chieftain DN.ii.258 to be invoked by followers of the Buddha in time of need. DN.iii.204</p>

naḷa|na.la|nala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.la</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nala">nala</a></p>

naḷa|na.la|nala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.la</span></p><p>Nala &amp; Naḷa</p><p>a species of reed; reed in general Vin.iv.35; AN.ii.73; Dhp.337; Cnd.680#ii; Ja.i.223; Ja.iv.141 Ja.iv.396 (n. va chinno); Pv.i.11#6 (id.); Dhp-a.iii.156; Dhp-a.iv.43 See also nāḷa, nāḷī &amp; nāḷikā.</p><ul><li><em>-āgāra</em> a house built of reeds SN.i.156; SN.iv.185 (+ tiṇāgāra); AN.i.101 (+ tiṇāgāra); Cnd.40#d (id.), Mil.245 cp. Avs Index ii.228 (naḍāgāra);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></em> a fire of reeds Ja.vi.100 (˚vaṇṇaṃ pabbataṃ);</li><li><em>-<em>kalāpī</em></em> a bundle of r SN.ii.114;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> a worker in reeds, basket-maker DN.i.51 (+ pesakāra &amp; kumbhakāra); Ja.v.291; Thag-a.28; Pv-a.175 (+ vilīvakāra); Dhp-a.i.177;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://da%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Daka">daṇḍaka</a></em> a shaft of r. Ja.i.170;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://maya">maya</a></em> made of r. Vin.ii.115;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vana">vana</a></em> a thicket of reeds Ja.iv.140; Mil.342;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sannibha">sannibha</a></em> reedcoloured Ja.vi.537 (Com.: naḷa-puppha-vaṇṇa rukkhasunakha);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://setu">setu</a></em> a bridge of reeds Snp.4.</li></ul><p>Ved. naḍa &amp; Sk. naḷa, with dial. ḍ (ḷ) for *narda, cp. Gr.; νάρδης</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a>, kalāpī, <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://daṇḍaka">daṇḍaka</a>, <a href="bword://maya">maya</a>, <a href="bword://vana">vana</a>, <a href="bword://sannibha">sannibha</a>, <a href="bword://setu">setu</a></p>

naḷaka|na.laka|nalaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.laka</span></p><p>Hindi <em>nal</em>, <em>Phragmites karka</em>, a tall grass with close-jointed stems and which commonly grow along river banks. The stems were used as needle cases Ja.i.170 and are now widely used to make stools, mats, baskets, winnowing baskets and fish traps.</p>

naḷakapāna|na.lakapaana|nalakapana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.lakapaana</span></p><p>A village in Kosala, where the Buddha once stayed and preached the Naḷakapāna Sutta. MN.i.462 There were two groves near the village, the Ketakavana and the Palāsavana; in the latter, Sāriputta preached two sermons at the request of the Buddha. AN.v.122<em>f.</em> AN.v.125<em>f.</em></p><p>Kosala3town</p>

naḷapi|na.lapi|nalapi
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.lapi</span></p><p>A type of aquatic animal Ja.vi.537.</p>

naḷapin|na.lapin|nalapin
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.lapin</span></p><p>water-animal Ja.vi.537.</p>

naḷerupucimanda|na.lerupucimanda|nalerupucimanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.lerupucimanda</span></p><p>NaḷerupucimandaNaḷerupucimandaNalerupucimaṇḍa</p><p>A grove near Verañja where the Buddha spent part of his time on his visit to Nerañjā. Vin.iii.1 AN.iv.172 AN.iv.197</p><p>26.88294,78.813713park</p>

naḷāgāra|na.laagaara|nalagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.laagaara</span></p><p>hut made of reeds; a hut roofed with reeds or rushes.</p>

naḷāṭa|na.laa.ta|nalata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.laa.ta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nalāṭa">nalāṭa</a></p>

naṃ|na.m|nam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.m</span></p><p>naṃ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>sg. acc of na</p><p>naṃ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable empathic particle: indeed; certainly.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

naṅgala|na"ngala|nangala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngala</span></p><p>naṅgala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a plow.</p><p>naṅgala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a tail.</p>

naṅgala|na"ngala|nangala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngala</span></p><p>plough SN.i.115; SN.iii.155; AN.iii.64; Snp.77 (yuga˚ yoke &amp; plough); Snp.p.13; Ja.i.57; Thig.441 (= sīra Thag-a.270); Snp-a.146; Vv-a.63 Vv-a.65; Pv-a.133 (dun˚ hard to plough); Dhp-a.i.223 (aya˚), Dhp-a.iii.67 (id.).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%ABs%C4%81">īsā</a></em> the beam of a plough SN.i.104 (of an elephant’s trunk);</li><li><em>-kaṭṭhakaraṇa</em> ploughing SN.v.146 = Ja.ii.59</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ph%C4%81la">phāla</a></em> [mod. Ind. phār] ploughshare (to be understood as Dvandva) Dhp-a.i.395.</li></ul><p>Ved. lāngala; nangala by dissimilation through subsequent nasal, cp. Milinda → Menandros Etym. unknown, prob. dialectical (already in RV iv. 57<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), because unconnected with other Aryan words for plough. Cp. Balūčī nangār</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īsā">īsā</a>, <a href="bword://phāla">phāla</a></p>

naṅgalakaṭṭhakaraṇa|na"ngalaka.t.thakara.na|nangalakatthakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalaka.t.thakara.na</span></p><p>or mfn. of naṅgala (a place) where there has been plowing (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgalamukha|na"ngalamukha|nangalamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalamukha</span></p><p>the opening of a conduit.</p>

naṅgalin|na"ngalin|nangalin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalin</span></p><p>adjective noun having or using a plough, ploughman, in mukha˚ “using the mouth as plough” Thag.101 (maulvergnügt, Neumann) (Mrs. Rh. D. harsh of speech).</p>

naṅgalāvatta|na"ngalaavatta|nangalavatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalaavatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://naṅgalāvattan">naṅgalāvattan</a></p><p>the turning track of the plow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgalāvattan|na"ngalaavattan|nangalavattan
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalaavattan</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://naṅgalāvattan">naṅgalāvattan</a></p><p>the turning track of the plow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgalāvattani|na"ngalaavattani|nangalavattani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalaavattani</span></p><p>Also written as: naṅgalāvattanin<sup>1</sup></p><p>turning the plow</p><p>naṅgalāvattani(n)<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. swishing its tail</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a>, <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgalāvattanin|na"ngalaavattanin|nangalavattanin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngalaavattanin</span></p><p>Also written as: naṅgalāvattanin<sup>1</sup></p><p>turning the plow</p><p>naṅgalāvattani(n)<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. swishing its tail</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a>, <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgalīsā|na"ngaliisaa|nangalisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngaliisaa</span></p><p>the pole of a plow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naṅgala">naṅgala</a></p>

naṅgula|na"ngula|nangula
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngula</span></p><p>tail Thag.113 = Thag.601 (go˚).</p><p>Sk. lāngū̆la to langa &amp; lagati (q.v.). cp. Gr.; λαγγάζω, Lat. langueo</p>

naṅguṭṭha|na"ngu.t.tha|nanguttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngu.t.tha</span></p><p>tail.</p>

naṅguṭṭha|na"ngu.t.tha|nanguttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na"ngu.t.tha</span></p><p>= nangula AN.ii.245; Ja.i.194 (of a bull); Ja.ii.19 (of an elephant); Ja.iii.16 (sūci˚), Ja.iii.480 (panther); Ja.iv.256 (of a deer); Dhp-a.i.275 (of a fish); Dhp-a.ii.64.</p><p>dial. for *nangūlya → *nanguḷhya?</p>

naṭa|na.ta|nata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.ta</span></p><p>dancer; an acrobat; an entertainer.</p>

naṭa|na.ta|nata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.ta</span></p><p>dancer, player, mimic, actor Vin.iv.285; SN.iv.306 sq. Dhp-a.iv.60 (˚dhītā), Dhp-a.iv.65 (˚karaka), Dhp-a.iv.224 (˚kīḷā); Mil.359 (˚naccaka); Sdhp.380<br />■ Cp. naṭaka &amp; nāṭaka.</p><p>Sk. naṭa dial. ṭ, cp. Prk. naḍa, of <em>nṛt</em>, see <em><a href="bword://naccati">naccati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naccati">naccati</a></p>

naṭaka|na.taka|nataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.taka</span></p><p>dancer; an acrobat; an entertainer.</p>

naṭaka|na.taka|nataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.taka</span></p><p>= naṭa Vin.iv.285; Mil.331; Pv-a.3<br />■ f. <em>naṭikā</em> DN-a.i.239.</p><p>Sk. naṭaka</p>

naṭati|na.tati|natati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.tati</span></p><p>to dance, play Vv-a.210 (= naccati).</p><p>Sk. naṭati, of <em>nṛt</em>, with dial. ṭ, cp. naccati</p>

naṭṭaki|na.t.taki|nattaki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.taki</span></p><p>dancer; an acrobat; an entertainer.</p>

naṭṭha|na.t.tha|nattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.tha</span></p><p>lost, lost to sight; disappeared; perished; spoiled; come to nothing, fruitless.</p>

naṭṭha|na.t.tha|nattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.tha</span></p><p>perished, destroyed; lost AN.ii.249; Ja.i.74; Ja.i.267.</p><p>Sk. naṣṭha, pp. of nassati (naśyati), q.v.</p>

naṭṭhacīvarikā|na.t.thaciivarikaa|natthacivarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.thaciivarikaa</span></p><p>whose robe is lost.</p>

naṭṭhana|na.t.thana|natthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.thana</span></p><p>destruction Mil.180, Mil.237.</p><p>Der. fr. naṭṭha</p>

naṭṭhāyika|na.t.thaayika|natthayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">na.t.thaayika</span></p><p>bankrupt Mil.131, Mil.201.</p><p>cp. Sk. naṣṭhārtha, i.e. naṣṭha + artha</p>

ne
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>of na</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

ne
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ne, Nesan</p><p>see *<a href="bword://na">na</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

necayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; \~a, masculine plural</i></p><p>(one) who accumulates, who stores up.</p>

necayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective rich, wealthy DN.i.136, DN.i.142 (read nevāsika cp. naivasika Mvu.iii.38); AN.v.149 (variant reading BB nerayika, Com. nevāsiko ti nivāsakaro).</p><p>fr. nicaya</p>

neccenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of nacceti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nacceti">nacceti</a></p>

negama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(one) who lives in a small town; a townsman; a (leading) trader or merchant.</li><li>the inhabitants of a small town; the townspeople.</li></ol>

negama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun the inhabitant of a (small) town; citizen; also collect. = jana, people Vin.i.268, Vin.i.273; DN.i.136, DN.i.139; Ja.iv.121; Ja.vi.493; Dāvs iii.3; DN-a.i.297 Often combined with <em>-jānapadā</em> (pl.) “townsmen &amp; countryfolk” SN.i.89; DN.iii.148, DN.iii.172; Ja.149.</p><p>fr. nigama</p>

negamajanapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://negamajānapada">negamajānapada</a></p>

negamajānapada|negamajaanapada|negamajanapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; \~a, masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">negamajaanapada</span></p><p>townspeople and country people.</p>

neka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pl. acc (pl. gen.</i></p><p><em>~ānaṃ</em>, <em>~esaṃ</em>), not one, more than one; many; various.</p>

neka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not one, several, many Snp.308; Vv.53#6 (˚citta variegated = nānāvidhacitta Vv-a.236), Vv.64#1 (id. = anekacitta Vv-a.275); Tikp.366.</p><p>Sk. naika = na eka, cp. aneka</p>

nekada
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= anekadā (frequently).</p>

nekadhā|nekadhaa|nekadha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nekadhaa</span></p><p>in many ways; many ways</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://neka">neka</a></p>

nekatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>dishonest; practicing fraud.</p>

nekatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective deceitful, fraudulent; a cheat DN.iii.183; Thag.940; Mil.290; Pv-a.209; Ja.iv.184.</p><p>fr. nikati</p>

nekavassagaṇika|nekavassaga.nika|nekavassaganika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nekavassaga.nika</span></p><p>adverb of neka in existence for many years; produced over many years</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://neka">neka</a></p>

nekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>made of gold; gold; a coin or ornament made of gold.</p>

nekkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>golden ornament, a certain coin of gold SN.i.65; AN.i.181; AN.ii.8, AN.ii.29; Dhp.230 (= Dhp-a.iii.329 jambonada nikkha); Vism.48; variant reading at Vv.20#8, Vv.43#8.</p><p>Vedic niṣka; cp. nikkha</p>

nekkhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>departure from worldly life; the renunciation of worldly things and values; whatever is the opposite of or rejection of all worldly, sensual experience and desires.</p>

nekkhamma
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>giving up the world leading a holy life, renunciation of, or emancipation from worldliness, freedom from lust, craving &amp; desires, dispassionateness self-abnegation, Nibbāna. Vin.i.18 (˚e ānisaṁsa) DN.i.110 (identical), DN.iii.239, DN.iii.275, DN.iii.283 MN.iii.129 AN.i.147 (= khema, i.e. nibbāna); AN.iii.245; AN.iv.186 (ānisaṁsa), AN.iv.439 sq. Snp.424 (˚ṁ daṭṭhu khemato) Dhp.181 Pts.i.107 sq.; Pts.ii.169 sq. Cnd.370; Vism.116, Vism.325 Ja.i.19 Ja.i.137 Vv.84<!-- raw HTML omitted -->42<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (= nibbāna Vv-a.348); Netti.53, Netti.87 Netti.106 sq. Mil.285 (˚ṁ abhinikkhanta) Dhp-a.iii.227 Thag-a.266</p><ul><li><em>-ādhimutta</em> bent on self-abnegation (enumerated with 5 other ideals of Arahantship: paviveka, avyāpajjha upādānakkhaya, taṇhakkhaya, asammoha) Vin.i.183; AN.iii.376;</li><li><em>-ābhirata</em> fond of renunciation AN.iv.224 AN.v.175; Pts.ii.173;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the sphere or element of dispassionateness SN.ii.152; Vb.86; Ne.97; Vism.487</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ninna">ninna</a></em> merging into or bent on a holy life SN.iii.233</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vitakka">vitakka</a></em> a thought of self-abnegation SN.ii.152; AN.i.275 AN.ii.252; Iti.82;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85kappa">saṅkappa</a></em> = prec. SN.ii.152; AN.iii.146 Vb.104, Vb.235;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sita">sita</a></em> based or bent on a holy life (opp <em>geha˚</em>; q.v.) SN.iv.232;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sukha">sukha</a></em> the joy or happiness of Arahantship MN.iii.110; AN.i.80; Dhp.267, Dhp.272; Dhp-a.iii.400.</li></ul><p>formally a derivation fr. nikkhamma (ger. of nikkhamati) = Sk. *naiṣkramya, as shown also by its semantic affinity to <em><a href="bword://nikkhanta">nikkhanta</a></em>, in which the metaphorical sense has entirely superseded the literal one. On the other hand, it may be a bastard derivation fr. nikkāma = Sk. *naiṣkāmya, although the adj. nikkāma does not show the prevailing meaning &amp; the wide range of nikkhanta, moreover formally we should expect nekkamma. In any case the connection with; <em><a href="bword://k%C4%81ma">kāma</a></em> is pre-eminently felt in the connotation of n., as shown by var. passages where a play of word exists between n &amp; kāma (cp. kāmānaṃ nissaraṇaṃ yad idaṃ nekkhammaṃ Iti.61, cp. Vin.i.104; AN.iii.245; also MN.i.115). The use of the similar term abhinikkhamana further warrants its derivation fr. nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a>, <a href="bword://ninna">ninna</a>, <a href="bword://vitakka">vitakka</a>, <a href="bword://saṅkappa">saṅkappa</a>, <a href="bword://sita">sita</a>, <a href="bword://sukha">sukha</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhanta">nikkhanta</a>, <a href="bword://kāma">kāma</a></p>

nekkhammadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the domain of renunciation; renunciation.</p>

nekkhammasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>connected with, attached to, rooted in, the of renunciation; appropriate (only) to the life of renunciation.</p>

nekāyika|nekaayika|nekayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nekaayika</span></p><p>adjective versed in the 4 (or 5) Nikāyas Mil.22; cp. Cunningham, <em>Stupa of Bharhut</em> 142, 52.</p><p>fr. nikāya</p>

nela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; m.f.</i></p><p>(according to commentaries) without fault; pure.</p>

nela
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Neḷa &amp; Nela</p><p>(adj.)</p><ol><li>without fault or sin blameless, faultless; not hurting, humane, gentle merciful, innocuous DN.i.4 (Bdhgh explains: elaṃ vuccati doso; n’ assā (i.e. vācāya) elan ti nelā; niddosā ti attho. “Nelango setapacchādo” ti ettha vuttanelaṃ viya; DN-a.i.75); AN.ii.209; AN.v.205; Ja.v.156; Vv.50#18, Vv.63#6 (= niddosa Vv-a.262); Pp.29, Pp.57; Dhs.1343 (vācā) = niddosa Dhs-a.397.</li><li>(somewhat doubtful “clean,” with ref. to big cats (mahā-biḷārā nelamaṇḍalaṃ vuccati), whereas young ones are called “elephants, cubs” (something like “pigs”) (taruṇā bhinka-cchāpamaṇḍalaṃ) Ja.v.418.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> of faultless limbs or parts, of a chariot (ratha = running perfectly SN.iv.291 = Ud.76 (nelagga text nelanga variant reading) = DN-a.i.75 = Dhs-a.397.</li><li><em>-patī</em> (f.) = neḷavatī (of vācā) humane, gentle Ja.vi.558 (na elapatī elapāta-rahitā madhurā Com.).</li></ul><p>na + eḷa = Sk. anenas, of enas fault, sin. The other negated form, also in meaning “pure clean,” is <em><a href="bword://ane%E1%B8%B7a">aneḷa</a></em> (&amp; aneḷaka), q.v. On ḷ: n. cp. lāngala nangala; tulā: tūṇa etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://aneḷa">aneḷa</a></p>

nelaṅga|nela"nga|nelanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nela"nga</span></p><p>nelaṅga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. whose parts are faultless; whose frame is smooth or polished; ?</p><p>nelaṅga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. the interior part or seat of a carriage; having an interior part or seat; ?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nela">nela</a></p>

nema
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>the lower, buried, half.</p>

nema
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>edge, point; root SN.v.445; AN.iv.404; <em>gambhīra˚</em>; (adj.) with deeply rooted point, firmly established SN.v.444; AN.iv.106.</p><p>cp. nemi</p>

nemantanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nemantanika">nemantanika</a></p>

nemantaṇika|nemanta.nika|nemantanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nemanta.nika</span></p><p>(from nimantana) (one) who accepts invitations (to meals)</p>

nemantaṇika|nemanta.nika|nemantanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nemanta.nika</span></p><p>adjective one who lives by invitations MN.i.31.</p><p>fr. nimantana</p>

nemi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nemi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A servitor of Kuvera. DN.iii.201</p><p>Nemi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, perhaps the same as Nimi. MN.iii.70</p>

nemi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>the rim of a wheel; a rim.</p>

nemi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>the circumference of a wheel, circumference, rim, edge (cp. nema AN.i.112; Vv.64#5; Mil.238, Mil.285; Vism.198 (fig. jarāmaraṇa˚ the rim of old age &amp; death, which belongs to the wheel of Saṃsāra of the chariot of existence, bhavaratha); Dhp-a.ii.124 (˚vaṭṭi); Vv-a.277.</p><p>Vedic nemi, perhaps to namati</p>

nemitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>(one) who interprets signs or omens.</p>

nemitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>fortune-teller, astrologer DN.ii.16, DN.ii.19; AN.iii.243.</p><p>Sk. naimitta, fr. nimitti</p>

nemittaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nemittika">nemittika</a></p>

nemittaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nemittaka &amp; Nemittika</p><p>an astrologer, fortune-teller, soothsayer DN.i.8 (i) = DN-a.i.91; AN.iii.111; Ja.iv.124; Mil.19 (i), Mil.229; Vism.210 (i); Dhp-a.ii.241 (a)</p><p>Sk. naimittika, fr. nimitta</p>

nemittika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>one who drops hints (for gain)</p>

nemittika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nemittaka &amp; Nemittika</p><p>an astrologer, fortune-teller, soothsayer DN.i.8 (i) = DN-a.i.91; AN.iii.111; Ja.iv.124; Mil.19 (i), Mil.229; Vism.210 (i); Dhp-a.ii.241 (a)</p><p>Sk. naimittika, fr. nimitta</p>

nemittikatā|nemittikataa|nemittikata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nemittikataa</span></p><p>= nimitta-kammaṃ, i.e. prognostication; inquisitiveness, insinuation Vb.352 = Vism.23; explained at Vism.28.</p><p>abstr. fr. nemittika</p>

nemiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) having a circumference etc. Ja.vi.252.</p><p>= nemika</p>

nemī|nemii|nemi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nemii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nemi">nemi</a></p>

nepakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>prudence, discrimination, carefulness; usually as <em>sati˚</em>; SN.v.197 sq.; MN.i.356; AN.iii.11 AN.iv.15; Cnd.629 B; Vb.244, Vb.249; Vism.3 (= paññā) Dhp-a.iv.29.</p><p>fr. nipaka</p>

nepuñña|nepu~n~na|nepunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nepu~n~na</span></p><p>experience, skill, cleverness Pp.25, Pp.35; Dhs.16, Dhs.292; Dhs-a.147.</p><p>fr. nipuṇa</p>

nerayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(someone) living, suffering in hell.</li><li>(one) doomed to suffer in niraya.</li></ol>

nerayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective belonging to niraya or purgatory, hellish; one doomed to suffering in purgatory (n. satta = inhabitant of n. Vin.ii.205 (āpāyiko n. kappaṭṭho); Vin.iv.7; DN.iii.6, DN.iii.9, DN.iii.12; AN.i.265; AN.ii.231 (vedanaṃ vediyati… seyyathā pi sattā nerayikā); AN.iii.402 sq.; Snp.664; Mnd.97 (gati) Vv.52#1, Ja.iv.3 (sattā); Pp.51; Vb.412 sq.; Vism.415 (˚sattā), Vism.424; Mil.148 (sattā); Pv-a.27 (id.), Pv-a.52 (˚bhāva) Pv-a.255; Vv-a.23; Sdhp.193, Sdhp.198.</p><p>fr. niraya, cp. BSk. nairayika Divy.165</p>

nerañjarāya|nera~njaraaya|neranjaraya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nera~njaraaya</span></p><p>NerañjarāyaNerañjara</p><p>A river. After the Enlightenment, the Buddha lived under the Ajapāla Nigrodha at Uruvelā, on the banks of this river. There Māra tempted him, and, later, Brahmā persuaded him to preach the Dhamma. Vin.i.1<em>ff.</em> Snp.425<em>ff.</em> SN.i.103<em>f.</em> SN.i.122 SN.i.136<em>ff.</em> SN.v.167 SN.v.185 SN.v.232 Ud.i.1–4 Ud.ii.1 Ud.iii.10 AN.ii.20<em>f.</em> DN.ii.267</p><p>24.72157, 85.005681river</p>

nerutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun based on etymology; an etymologist or philologist Thag-a.153; Ne.8, Ne.9, Ne.32, Ne.33.</p><p>fr. nirutti</p>

nesajjika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">gen.</i></p><p>/dat. (an ascetic) who does not use a bed; (an ascetic or bhikkhu) who rests or sleeps only in a sitting position; requiring sitting only (not lying); a <em>bhikkhu</em> who has undertaken the dhutanga of not lying down.</p>

nesajjika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective being &amp; remaining in a sitting position (as an ascetic practice) AN.iii.220; Thag.904 Thag.1120; Cnd.587; Ja.iv.8; Pp.69; Vism.79; Mil.20, Mil.342. The <em>n-˚aṅga</em> is one of the dhūtanga-precepts, enjoining the sitting posture also for sleeping, see Vin.v.193, Vism.61, &amp; dhūtanga.</p><p>fr. nisajjā</p>

nesan
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ne, Nesan</p><p>see *<a href="bword://na">na</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

nesaṃ|nesa.m|nesam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nesa.m</span></p><p>of na</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

nesuṃ|nesu.m|nesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

nesāda|nesaada|nesada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nesaada</span></p><p>man of a particular low caste (of hunters); a fowler, a hunter.</p>

nesāda|nesaada|nesada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nesaada</span></p><p>hunter; also a low caste Vin.iv.7 (+ veṇa &amp; rathakāra); SN.i.93 (˚kula); AN.i.107; AN.ii.85; Ja.ii.36 Ja.iii.330; Ja.iv.397, Ja.iv.413; Ja.v.110, Ja.v.337; Ja.vi.71; Pp.51 (˚kula) Mil.311; Dhp-a.iii.24; Pv-a.176.</p><p>fr. nisāda; cp. Sk. niṣāda &amp; naiṣāda = one who lies in wait</p>

nesādī|nesaadii|nesadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nesaadii</span></p><p>woman of a particular low caste (of hunters); a fowler, a huntress.</p>

neta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://netar">netar</a></p><p>one who takes, who leads; a leader; a guide.</p>

netabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be led or brought; to be carried on; to be brought forward; to be adduced</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

netar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://netar">netar</a></p><p>one who takes, who leads; a leader; a guide.</p>

netar
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>leader, guide, forerunner Snp.86, Snp.213; Mnd.446.</p><p>Vedic netṛ, n. ag. of neti</p>

netaṃ ṭhānaṃ vijjati|neta.m .thaana.m vijjati|netam thanam vijjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">neta.m .thaana.m vijjati</span></p><p>it is (not) the case; it is (not) possible</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ṭhāna">ṭhāna</a></p>

neti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

neti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Neti &amp; nayati</p><p>to lead, guide, conduct; to take, carry (away); fig. to draw a conclusion, to understand, to take as Dhp.80, Dhp.145, Dhp.240, Dhp.257; Ja.i.228 Ja.iv.241 (nayaṃ n. to draw a proper conclusion); Vv-a.42 (narati = nayati);<br />imper <em><a href="bword://naya">naya</a></em> Pv.ii.11#3, &amp; <em>nehi</em> Ja.ii.160; Pv-a.147; poetic imper. <em>nayāhi</em> see in paṭi˚;<br />pot <em>naye</em> Dhp.256 (to lead a cause = vinicchineyya Dhp-a.iii.381)<br />fut <em>nessāmi</em> Ja.ii.159; Pv.ii.4#5;<br />aor <strong>nayi</strong> Ja.iv.137.<br />ger <em>netvā</em> Pv-a.5, Pv-a.6, etc.<br />inf <em>netuṃ</em> Pv-a.123, Pv-a.145 <em>(˚kāma) &amp; netave;</em> Ja.i.79 = Dhp.180.<br />grd <em><a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a></em> (see sep.),<br />pp <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABta">nīta</a></em>.<br />pass <em><a href="bword://n%C4%AByati">nīyati</a></em> (q.v.).<br />■ Cp. naya, nīti, netta etc. also ā˚, upa˚, paṭi˚, vi˚.</p><p>Vedic nayati, <em>nī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naya">naya</a>, nayi, <a href="bword://neyya">neyya</a>, <a href="bword://nīta">nīta</a>, <a href="bword://nīyati">nīyati</a></p>

netra
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://netta">netta</a></p>

netta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>a leader.</li><li>the eye.</li><li>a leading rein.</li></ol>

netta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Netta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a guide Ja.iii.111; Ne.130.</p><p>Sk. netra, fr. neti</p><p>Netta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter guidance, anything that guides, a conductor, fig. the eye. SN.i.26 (sārathī nettāni gahetvā = the reins); Vin.i.204 (dhūma˚ for smoke); Ja.iv.363 (id.); DN.i.12 (˚tappana, set t. &amp; cp. DN-a.i.98); Snp.550 (pasanna˚), Snp.1120; Cnd.371 (= cakkhu), Cnd.669; Ja.vi.290 (tamba˚ with red eyes); Pv.i.8#3 (eyes = nayanāni Com.) Dhs.597; Vb.71 sq.</p><p>Sk. netra</p>

nettar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nettar">nettar</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://netta">netta</a></p>

nettatappana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(applying) eye-salve, eye-ointment.</p>

netthar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in phrase <em>netthāraṃ vattati</em> to behave in such a way as to get rid of blame or fault Vin.ii.5; Vin.iii.183; MN.i.442<br />■ Bdhgh on Vin.ii.5 (p. 309) explains: nittharantānaṃ etan ti netthāraṃ yena sakkā nissāraṇā nittharituṃ taṃ aṭṭhārasa-vidhaṃ sammāvattuṃ vattantī ti attho.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></em>; does any connection exist with Vedic neṣṭṛ?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></p>

netthāra|netthaara|netthara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">netthaara</span></p><p>crossing; release; acquittance.</p>

netti
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Yakkha chieftain. DN.iii.204</p>

netti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i></p><p>guide, conductor; support (= nettika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) Iti.37 (āhāra˚-pabhava), Iti.38 (bhava˚), Iti.94 (netticchinna bhikkhu = Arahant). Cp. nettika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> dhamma˚, bhava˚.</p><p>Vedic netrī, f. to netṛ</p>

nettika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>one who makes conduits for irrigation.</p>

nettika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>having as guide or forerunner, in Bhagavaṃ˚’ dhamma MN.i.310; AN.i.199 AN.iv.158, AN.iv.351; AN.v.355.</li><li>a conduit for irrigation; one who makes conduits for watering Dhp.80 (= udakaṃ nenti nettikā), Dhp.145; fig. that which supplies with food or water, in <em>bhava˚</em>; (“the roots of existence, clinging to existence”) DN.i.46 (ucchinna˚ with the roots of existence cut); <em>sanettika</em> clinging to existence, a bad man AN.ii.54. Cp. netti.</li></ol><p>netta + ika</p>

nettiṃsa|netti.msa|nettimsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">netti.msa</span></p><p>sword Ja.ii.77 (˚vara-dhārin; C. nettiṃsā vuccanti khaggā); Ja.iv.118 (C. gives it as adj. = nikkaruṇa merciless; &amp; says “khaggassa nāmaṃ”); Ja.vi.188 (˚varadhārin).</p><p>cp. Sk. nistriṃśa, Halāyudha 2, 317; very doubtful, whether nis + triṃśa (thirty), prob. a dial distortion</p>

nettucchinna
