<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nijjareti">nijjareti</a></p>

nijjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unvanquished Mil.192 (˚kammasūrā), Mil.332 (˚vijita-sangāma); Sdhp.360.</p><p>Sk. nirjita, nis + jita</p>

nijjiṇṇa|nijji.n.na|nijjinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nijji.n.na</span></p><p>of nijjareti worn away; destroyed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nijjareti">nijjareti</a></p>

nijjiṇṇa|nijji.n.na|nijjinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nijji.n.na</span></p><p>adjective destroyed, overcome, exhausted, finished, dead DN.i.96; MN.ii.217; AN.i.221 (vedanākkhayā sabbaṃ dukkhaṃ n. bhavissati) MN.i.93; AN.v.215 sq.; Ne.51.</p><p>Sk. nirjīrṇa, nis + jiṇṇa</p>

nijjāleti|nijjaaleti|nijjaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nijjaaleti</span></p><p>to make an end to a blaze, to extinguish, to put out Ja.vi.495 (aggiṃ).</p><p>nis + jāleti</p>

nijjīvata|nijjiivata|nijjivata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nijjiivata</span></p><p>adjective lifeless, soulless Dhs-a.38; Mil.413.</p><p>Sk. nirjīvita, nis + jīva<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nikacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nikaroti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikaroti">nikaroti</a></p>

nikacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nikati">nikati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikati">nikati</a></p>

nikanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>cut; scraped.</p>

nikanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective cut, (ab-)razed MN.i.364 (of a fleshless bone).</p><p>Sk. nikṛtta &amp; nikṛntita (cp. Divy.537, Divy.539), ni + kantati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nikantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cut down, to cut up, cut off Pv-a.210 (piṭṭhi-maṃsāni the flesh of the back, variant reading SS for ukkant˚); Pgdp.29.</p><p>Sk. ni-kṛṇtati, see *<a href="bword://kantati">kantati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kantati">kantati</a></p>

nikanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>wanting, longing; attachment, fondness.</p>

nikanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>desire, craving, longing for, wish Thag.20; Pts.ii.72, Pts.ii.101; Dhs.1059 Dhs.1136; Vism.239, Vism.580; Dhs-a.369; Dhp-a.iv.63; DN-a.i.110 Dāvs iii.40.</p><p>Sk. nikānti, ni + kamati</p>

nikara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>multitude Dāvs v.25 (jātipuppha˚).</p><p>Sk. nikara, ni + karoti</p>

nikaraṇā|nikara.naa|nikarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikara.naa</span></p><p>or is it ˚aṃ?) = nikati (fraud) Pp.19, Pp.23 (as syn. of māyā).</p>

nikaroti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>humiliates; deceives; cheats.</p>

nikaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bring down, humiliate, to deceive, cheat Snp.138 (nikubbetha Pot. = vañceyya Kp-a.247). pp. nikata (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. nikaroti, ni + karoti</p>

nikasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>whetstone Dāvs iii.87 (˚opala).</p><p>Sk. nikasa, ni + kasati</p>

nikasāva|nikasaava|nikasava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikasaava</span></p><p>adjective free from impurity Vin.i.3; opp. anikkasāva (q.v. Dhp.9≈.</p><p>Sk. niṣkaṣāya nis + kasāva see <em><a href="bword://kas%C4%81ya">kasāya</a></em> 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasāya">kasāya</a></p>

nikata
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Nikata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>An upāsaka of Ñātikā. After death he was born in the Suddhāvāsā, there to pass away. SN.v.358<em>f.</em> DN.ii.91<em>f.</em></p><p>Nikata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>One of several eminent theras mentioned as staying in the Kūṭāgārasāla in Vesāli. When the Buddha came there, Licchavis crowded out the place with all their retinues, and Nikata and his coleagues, desiring solitude, retired to the Gosiṅgasālavana. AN.v.133<em>f.</em></p>

nikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>deceived; cheated.</p>

nikata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective deceived, cheated MN.i.511 (+ vañcita paladdha); SN.iv.307 (+ vañcita paluddha).</p><p>Sk. nikṛta, ni + karoti “done down”</p>

nikati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>fraud; cheating; deceit.</p>

nikati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>fraud, deceit, cheating DN.i.5 (= DN-a.i.80 paṭirūpakena vañcanaṃ); DN.iii.176; Snp.242 (= nirāsaṃ-karaṇaṃ Snp-a.286); Ja.i.223; Pv.iii.9#5 (+ vañcana); Pp.19, Pp.23, Pp.58; Vv-a.114; Pv-a.211 (paṭirūpadassanena paresaṃ vikāro)<br />■ instr. <em>nikatiyā</em> (metri causa) Ja.i.223, <em>nikatyā</em> Ja.ii.183, <em><a href="bword://nikacca">nikacca</a></em> SN.i.24. Cp nekatika.</p><p>Sk. nikṛti, see prec.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikacca">nikacca</a></p>

nikaṇṇika|nika.n.nika|nikannika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.n.nika</span></p><p>adjective under (4) ears, secret, cp. catukkaṇṇa Ja.iii.124; nt. adv. secretly Vin.iv.270, Vin.iv.271.</p>

nikaṇṇikāṃ|nika.n.nikaa.m|nikannikam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.n.nikaa.m</span></p><p>in the ear; in whispers.</p>

nikaṭṭha|nika.t.tha|nikattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.t.tha</span></p><p>brought down; controlled (?)</p>

nikaṭṭha|nika.t.tha|nikattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective brought down, debased, low. As one kind of puggala (n-kāya + ncitta) AN.ii.137. loc. <em>nikaṭṭhe</em> (adv.) near Ja.iii.438 Thag-a.105 (v. 33) (= santike Ja.iii.438).</p><p>cp. Sk. nikṛṣṭa, ni + kasati</p>

nikaṭṭhacitta|nika.t.thacitta|nikatthacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.t.thacitta</span></p><p>mentally controlled.</p>

nikaṭṭhakāya|nika.t.thakaaya|nikatthakaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nika.t.thakaaya</span></p><p>physically controlled.</p>

niketa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i></p><p>home; a (temporary) habitation; where someone or something stays or settles or is attached.</p>

niketa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>house, abode Dhp.91 (= ālaya Dhp-a.ii.170).</li><li>(fig.) company association. (In this sense it seems to be interpreted as belonging to ketu “sign, characteristic, mark,” and niketa-sārin would have to be taken as “following the banner or flag of…,” i.e. belonging or attached to i.e. a follower of, one who is devoted to.) <em>a˚</em>; not living in company, having no house Snp.207; Mil.244 (+ nirālaya).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81sin">vāsin</a></em> (a˚) not living in a house, not associating with anybody Mil.201;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sayana">sayana</a></em> = ˚vāsin Mil.361;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81rin">sārin</a></em> (a˚) “wandering homeless” or “not living in company, i.e. not associating with, not a follower of… SN.iii.9 sq. = Mnd.198; Snp.844 = SN.iii.9; Snp-a.255 = SN.iii.10; Snp.970 (= Mnd.494 q.v.).</li></ul><p>Sk. niketa settlement, ni + cināti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vāsin">vāsin</a>, <a href="bword://sayana">sayana</a>, <a href="bword://sārin">sārin</a></p>

niketacārika|niketacaarika|niketacarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niketacaarika</span></p><p>ranging from its home (in)</p>

niketasāri|niketasaari|niketasari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niketasaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketasārin">niketasārin</a></p><p>keeping to a home; clinging; attached.</p>

niketasārin|niketasaarin|niketasarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niketasaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketasārin">niketasārin</a></p><p>keeping to a home; clinging; attached.</p>

niketava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketavat">niketavat</a></p><p>having a dwelling.</p>

niketavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective parting company with Mil.288 (kamma˚).</p><p>to niketa</p>

niketavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketavat">niketavat</a></p><p>having a dwelling.</p>

niketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketin">niketin</a></p><p>having a home; (one) who has a long connection with.</p>

niketin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niketin">niketin</a></p><p>having a home; (one) who has a long connection with.</p>

niketin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having an abode, being housed, living in Snp.422 (kosalesu); Ja.iii.432 (duma-sākhā-niketinī f.).</p>

nikhanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikhanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nikhanati &amp; Nikhaṇati</p><p>to dig into, to bury, to erect, to cover up Vin.ii.116; Vin.iii.78 (akkhiṃ = cover the eye, as a sign); Ja.v.434 = Dhp-a.iv.197 (id.); DN.ii.127 (ṇ); Ja.i.264; Snp-a.519 (ṇ, to bury)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nikh%C4%81ta">nikhāta</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. nikhanati, ni + khanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhāta">nikhāta</a></p>

nikhaṇati|nikha.nati|nikhanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikha.nati</span></p><p>nikhaṇati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. closes (the eye); winks.</p><p>nikhaṇati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. digs (something) into (the ground), buries; implants, erects.</p>

nikhaṇati|nikha.nati|nikhanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikha.nati</span></p><p>Nikhanati &amp; Nikhaṇati</p><p>to dig into, to bury, to erect, to cover up Vin.ii.116; Vin.iii.78 (akkhiṃ = cover the eye, as a sign); Ja.v.434 = Dhp-a.iv.197 (id.); DN.ii.127 (ṇ); Ja.i.264; Snp-a.519 (ṇ, to bury)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nikh%C4%81ta">nikhāta</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. nikhanati, ni + khanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhāta">nikhāta</a></p>

nikhaṇitabba|nikha.nitabba|nikhanitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikha.nitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikhaṇitvā|nikha.nitvaa|nikhanitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikha.nitvaa</span></p><p>of nikhaṇati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikhaṇiyamāna|nikha.niyamaana|nikhaniyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikha.niyamaana</span></p><p>of nikhaṇati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikhila
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective all, entire, whole Dāvs v.40 (˚loka variant reading sakala˚).</p><p>Sk. nikhila cp. khila</p>

nikhādana|nikhaadana|nikhadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikhaadana</span></p><p>sharp carpenter’s tool, perhaps a chisel.</p>

nikhādana|nikhaadana|nikhadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikhaadana</span></p><p>“eating down,” a sharp instrument, a spade or (acc. to Morris, <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1884, 83) a chisel Vin.iii.149 Vin.iv.211; Ja.ii.405 (so read for khādana); Ja.iv.344; Ja.v.45.</p><p>Sk. *nikhādana, ni + khādati, cp. khādana</p>

nikhāta|nikhaata|nikhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikhaata</span></p><p>of nikhaṇati dug in; buried; fixed in the ground</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikhāta|nikhaata|nikhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikhaata</span></p><ol><li>dug, dug out (of a hole), buried (of a body) Snp-a.519.</li><li>dug in, erected (of a post) Snp.28; Dhp-a.ii.181 (nagara-dvāre n. indakhīla) See also a˚.</li></ol><p>pp. of nikhaṇati</p>

nikhīṇa|nikhii.na|nikhina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikhii.na</span></p><p>adjective having or being lost Ja.vi.499 (˚patta without wings, deprived of its wings).</p><p>nis + khīṇa</p>

nikiḷita|niki.lita|nikilita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niki.lita</span></p><p>adjective engrossed in play Ja.vi.313.</p><p>Sk. *nikrīḍita, pp. of nikrīḍayati, ni + kīḷati</p>

nikiṇṇa|niki.n.na|nikinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niki.n.na</span></p><p>thrown down; put down; placed.</p>

nikiṇṇa|niki.n.na|nikinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niki.n.na</span></p><p>adjective “strewn down into,” hidden away, sheltered Ja.iii.529.</p><p>Sk. *nikīrṇa, pp. ni + kirati, cp. kiraṇa</p>

nikkaddama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective unstained, not dirty, free from impunity DN-a.i.226.</p><p>nis + kaddama</p>

nikkama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noun adjective exertion, strength, endurance. The orig. meaning of “going forth” is quite obliterated by the fig. meaning (cp nikkhamati &amp; nekkhamma) AN.i.4; AN.iii.214; Vv.18#7 (= viriya Vv-a.96); Dhs.13, Dhs.22, Dhs.219, Dhs.571; Vism.132; Mil.244 (+ ārambha)<br />■ (adj.) strong in (-˚), enduring exerting oneself SN.i.194 (tibba˚); SN.v.66, SN.v.104 sq.; Snp.68 (daḷha˚, cp. Nd ii.under padhānavā), Snp.542 (sacca˚).</p><p>Sk. niṣkrama; nis + kama</p>

nikkamadhātu|nikkamadhaatu|nikkamadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkamadhaatu</span></p><p>exertion; endurance.</p>

nikkamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>exerts oneself.</p>

nikkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go out, to go forth; in fig meaning: to leave behind lust, evil &amp; the world, to get rid of “kāma” (craving), to show right exertion, strength Mil.245 (+ arabhati) + SN.i.156 (kkh).</p><p>Sk. niṣkramati, nis + kamati, see also <em><a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></em> &amp; nekkhamma</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikanta">nikanta</a></p>

nikkarunatā|nikkarunataa|nikkarunata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkarunataa</span></p><p>= following Vism.314.</p>

nikkaruṇa|nikkaru.na|nikkaruna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaru.na</span></p><p>adjective without compassion, heartless Snp.244 (= sattānaṃ anatthakāma); Sdhp.508.</p><p>nis + karuṇa, adj. of karuṇā</p>

nikkaruṇā|nikkaru.naa|nikkaruna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaru.naa</span></p><p>without compassion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://karuṇā">karuṇā</a></p>

nikkaruṇā|nikkaru.naa|nikkaruna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaru.naa</span></p><p>heartlessness Pv-a.55.</p><p>Sk. niṣkaruṇatā; nis + karuṇā</p>

nikkasava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from defect or impurity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasāva">kasāva</a></p>

nikkasāva|nikkasaava|nikkasava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkasaava</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nikas%C4%81va">nikasāva</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikasāva">nikasāva</a></p>

nikkaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“buying off,” redemption Ja.vi.577.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣkraya, nis + kaya cp. nikkiṇāti</p>

nikkaḍḍhana|nikka.d.dhana|nikkaddhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhana</span></p><p>, ~ā, feminine dragging out; throwing out, ejecting.</p>

nikkaḍḍhanta|nikka.d.dhanta|nikkaddhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhanā|nikka.d.dhanaa|nikkaddhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhanaa</span></p><p>throwing out, ejection Ja.iii.22 (a˚); Ja.v.234. (= niddhamanā).</p>

nikkaḍḍhapesi|nikka.d.dhapesi|nikkaddhapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhapesi</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhapeti|nikka.d.dhapeti|nikkaddhapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhapeti</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhati|nikka.d.dhati|nikkaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhati</span></p><p>drags out; draws out; throws out, ejects.</p>

nikkaḍḍhati|nikka.d.dhati|nikkaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to throw out Vin.iv.274 (Caus. nikkaḍḍhāpeti ibid.) Ja.i.116; Ja.ii.440; Snp-a.192. pp. nikkaḍḍhita.</p><p>Sk. niṣkarṣati, nis + kasati, cp. kaḍḍhati</p>

nikkaḍḍhenta|nikka.d.dhenta|nikkaddhenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhenta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhanta">nikkaḍḍhanta</a></p>

nikkaḍḍheti|nikka.d.dheti|nikkaddheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dheti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhi|nikka.d.dhi|nikkaddhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhi</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhita|nikka.d.dhita|nikkaddhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhita</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhita thrown or dragged out; expelled; ejected</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhita">nikkaḍḍhita</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhita|nikka.d.dhita|nikkaddhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhita</span></p><p>adjective thrown out Ja.ii.103 (gehā); Pv-a.179 (read ḍḍh for ḍḍ).</p><p>Sk. *niṣkarṣita see <em><a href="bword://nikka%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhitabba|nikka.d.dhitabba|nikkaddhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhita">nikkaḍḍhita</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhitvā|nikka.d.dhitvaa|nikkaddhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhitvaa</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhiyamāna|nikka.d.dhiyamaana|nikkaddhiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhiyamaana</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaḍḍhiṃsu|nikka.d.dhi.msu|nikkaddhimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.d.dhi.msu</span></p><p>of nikkaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkaḍḍhati">nikkaḍḍhati</a></p>

nikkaṅkha|nikka"nkha|nikkankha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka"nkha</span></p><p>adjective not afraid, fearless, not doubting confident, sure Ja.i.58. Cp. nissaṃsaya.</p><p>Sk. niḥśanka, nis + kankha, adj. of kankhā, cp. kankhin</p>

nikkaṅkhā|nikka"nkhaa|nikkankha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka"nkhaa</span></p><p>fearlessness, state of confidence, trust (cp. nibbicikicchā) SN.v.221.</p><p>Sk. niḥśankā, nis + kankhā</p>

nikkaṇṭaka|nikka.n.taka|nikkantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.n.taka</span></p><p>free from obstacles; free from sedition</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaṇṭaka">kaṇṭaka</a></p>

nikkaṇṭaka|nikka.n.taka|nikkantaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.n.taka</span></p><p>adjective free from thorns or enemies Mil.250; cp. akaṇṭaka.</p><p>Sk. niṣkaṇṭaka, nis + kaṇṭaka</p>

nikkaṭṭha|nikka.t.tha|nikkattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.t.tha</span></p><p>drawn away; withdrawn.</p>

nikkaṭṭhacitta|nikka.t.thacitta|nikkatthacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.t.thacitta</span></p><p>mentally withdrawn or apart.</p>

nikkaṭṭhakāya|nikka.t.thakaaya|nikkatthakaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikka.t.thakaaya</span></p><p>physically withdrawn or apart.</p>

nikkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>a golden ornament for neck or breast, a ring Ja.ii.444; Ja.vi.577.</li><li>(already Vedic) a golden coin or a weight of gold (cp. a “pound sterling”) equal to 15 suvaṇṇas (Vv-a.104 = suvaṇṇassa pañcadasa-dharaṇaṃ nikkhan ti vadanti) SN.ii.234 (suvaṇṇa &amp; singi˚); Ja.i.84 (id.); AN.iv.120 (suvaṇṇa˚); Vv.20#8 Vv.43#8 (variant reading SS nekkha) Ja.vi.180; Mil.284. suvaṇṇanikkha-sataṃ (100 gold pieces) Ja.i.376; Ja.iv.97; Ja.v.58 ˚sahassaṃ (1000) Ja.v.67; Dhp-a.i.393<br />■ See also nekkha.</li></ol><p>Vedic niṣka; cp. Oir. nasc (ring), Ohg. nusca (bracelet)</p>

nikkhama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective going out from Pv-a.80 (nāsikāya n<br />■ mala). <em>dun˚</em>; at Thag.72 is to be read dunnikkhaya, as indicated by vv.ll. See the latter.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣkrama</p>

nikkhamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>going out, departing Ja.ii.153; Vv-a.71 (opp. pavesana); fig renunciation Kp-a.184 (kata˚ as adj. = nikkāmin). See also abhi˚. Nikkhameti &amp; Nikkhameti;</p><p>BSk. niṣkramaṇa, to nikkhamati</p>

nikkhamanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n. of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes or comes out (esp. out of a town or village); comes forth (to fight etc); emerges; gets out; escapes; leaves; esp. leaves the household life; sticks out, protrudes.</p>

nikkhamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go forth from, to come out of (c. abl.), to get out, issue forth depart, fig. to leave the household life behind (<em>agārā</em> n.), to retire from the world (cp. abhinikkhamati etc.) or to give up evil desire</p><ol><li>lit. (often with bahi outside, out; opp. pavisati to enter into: AN.v.195) DN.ii.14 (mātu kucchismā); Ja.i.52 (mātukucchito)<br />imper <em><a href="bword://nikkhama">nikkhama</a></em> Pv.i.10#3;<br />ppr <em>nikkhamanto</em> Ja.i.52 Ja.ii.153; Ja.iii.26 (mukhato); Pv-a.90;<br />aor <em><a href="bword://nikkhami">nikkhami</a></em> Ja.ii.154; Ja.iii.188;<br />fut <em>-issati</em> Ja.ii.154;<br />ger <em><a href="bword://nikkhamma">nikkhamma</a></em> Ja.i.51, Ja.i.61 (fig.) &amp; <em><a href="bword://nikkhamitv%C4%81">nikkhamitvā</a></em> Ja.i.16, Ja.i.138 (fig.), Ja.i.265, Ja.iii.26; Ja.iv.449 (n. pabbajissāmi); Pv-a.14, Pv-a.19 (fig.), Pv-a.67 (gāmato), Pv-a.74 (id.);<br />inf <strong>nikkhamituṃ</strong> Ja.i.61 (fig.); Ja.ii.104; Pv.i.10#2 (bahi n.);<br />grd <em>nikkhamitabba</em> Vin.i.47.</li><li>fig. (see also <em><a href="bword://nikkamati">nikkamati</a></em>, &amp; cp. nekkhamma &amp; BSk niṣkramati in same meaning, e.g. Divy.68 etc.) SN.i.156 (ārabbhati +) = Mil.245 (where nikkamati); Ja.i.51 (agārā), Ja.i.61 (mahābhinikkhamanaṃ “the great renunciation”), Pv-a.19 (id.)</li></ol><p>pp nikkhanta; caus. nikkhameti (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. niṣkramati, nis + kamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhama">nikkhama</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhami">nikkhami</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhamma">nikkhamma</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhamitvā">nikkhamitvā</a>, nikkhamituṃ, <a href="bword://nikkamati">nikkamati</a></p>

nikkhameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhāmeti">nikkhāmeti</a></p>

nikkhameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nikkhameti &amp; Nikkhāmeti</p><p>to make go out or away, to bring out or forth SN.ii.128; Ja.i.264 Ja.ii.112<br />pp <em>nikkhāmita</em> Ja.iii.99 (+ nicchuddha thrown out, in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of nibbāpita; variant reading BB. nikaḍhāpita).</p><p>Caus. of nikkhamati</p>

nikkhami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp n. impers.</i></p><p>of nikkhamati one must go out; one must leave</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamitvā|nikkhamitvaa|nikkhamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhamitvaa</span></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamitvāna|nikkhamitvaana|nikkhamitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhamitvaana</span></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamitūna|nikkhamituuna|nikkhamituna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhamituuna</span></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhamiṃ|nikkhami.m|nikkhamim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhami.m</span></p><p>of nikkhāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhāmeti">nikkhāmeti</a></p>

nikkhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikkhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; n.</i></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><ol><li>(mfn.) (who has) gone out, left; gone; sticking out, protruding; outside, apart from.</li><li>(n.) going out, leaving, departure</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective gone out, departed from (c. abl.), gone away; also med going out, giving up, figuratively leaving behind, resigning renouncing (fusing in meaning with kanta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of kāmyati = desireless). SN.i.185 (agārasmā anagāriyaṁ) Snp.991 (Kapilavatthumhā n. lokanāyako) Ja.i.149 Ja.ii.153 iv.364 (˚bhikkhā, in sense of nikkhāmita˚, variant reading nikkhitta˚ perhaps preferable, explained p. 366 nibaddha˚ designed for, given to) Snp-a.605 (figuratively; as variant reading for nikkāma) Dhp-a.ii.39 Pv-a.61 (bahi); Cnd under nissita Cnd.107 (free, unobstructed)</p><p>pp. of nis + kamati, see <em><a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>getting out (from); emerging.</p>

nikkhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective liable to destruction, able to be destroyed, in <em>dun˚</em>; hard to destroy Ja.iv.449 (= dun-nikkaḍḍhiya Com.); also to be read (variant reading) at Thag.72 for dunnikkhama. Cp. nikhīṇa.</p><p>Sk. *niḥkṣaya, nis + khaya</p>

nikkhaṇati|nikkha.nati|nikkhanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkha.nati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikhaṇati">nikhaṇati</a></p>

nikkhepa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>the putting down, setting down, laying down; a deposit, pledge.</li><li>the trace, prints, of the setting down.</li></ol>

nikkhepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>putting down, laying down; casting off, discarding, elimination, giving up, renunciation; abstract or summary treatment. Dhs-a.6 344 ‣See under <a href="bword://m%C4%81tik%C4%81">mātikā</a>;<br />■in grammar pada˚ the setting of the verse; i.e. rules of composition (Mil.381).<br />■Vin.i.16 pādukānaṁ = the putting down of the slippers i.e. the slippers as they were, put down<br />■Ja.iii.243 dhura˚ giving up one’s office or charge<br />■Ja.i.236 sarīra ˚ṁ kāresi had the body laid out; Dīpavaṁsa.xvii.109 (identical). Vism 618 (= cuti) Dhp-a.ii.98 (sarīra˚) DN-a.i.50 (sutta˚) Dhs-a.344 Mil.91</p><p>Sk. nikṣepa, see <em><a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mātikā">mātikā</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhepana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>putting down, setting down; putting into; a place for putting down or into.</p>

nikkhepana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= nikkhepa SN.iii.26 (bhāra˚ getting rid of the load, opp. bhārâdānaṃ); Mil.356 (= comparison) Vism.236 (deha˚). Nikhanati &amp; Nikhanati;</p>

nikkhipanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>throws or puts or lays down; puts into; places, sets down; sets out; lays (eggs)</li><li>lays aside; sets aside; puts away, stores; deposits; entrusts.</li></ol>

nikkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to lay down (carefully), to put down, to lay (an egg) Vin.ii.114; Iti.13, Iti.14 (Pot. nikkhipeyya); Pp.34; Ja.i.49 (aṇḍakaṃ)</li><li>to lay aside, to put away Vin.i.46 (patta-cīvaraṃ) AN.i.206 (daṇḍaṃ to discard the weapon; see <em><a href="bword://da%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">daṇḍa</a></em>) Mhvs.14, Mhvs.10 (dhanu-saraṃ).</li><li>to eliminate, get rid of, give up Pv.ii.6#15 (dehaṃ to get rid of the body) Dhs-a.344 (vitthāra-desanaṃ).</li><li>to give in charge to deposit, entrust, save Pp.26; Vv-a.33 (sahassathavikaṃ)<br />aor <em><a href="bword://nikkhipi">nikkhipi</a></em> DN.ii.161 (Bhagavato sarīraṃ Ja.ii.104, Ja.ii.111, Ja.ii.416; <em>fut. ˚issati</em> DN.ii.157 (samussayaṃ) ger. <em>-itvā</em> MN.iii.156 (cittaṃ); Ja.ii.416; Ja.vi.366; grd <em>-itabba</em> Vin.i.46</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://nikkhitta">nikkhitta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em>nikkhipāpeti</em> to cause to be laid down, to order to be put down etc. Pv-a.215 (gosīsaṭṭhiṃ). Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. nikṣipati, ni + khipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://daṇḍa">daṇḍa</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhipi">nikkhipi</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhitta">nikkhitta</a></p>

nikkhipi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhipita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>laid or set down; thrown down.</p>

nikkhipitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipita">nikkhipita</a></p>

nikkhipitvā|nikkhipitvaa|nikkhipitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhipitvaa</span></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhipitvāna|nikkhipitvaana|nikkhipitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhipitvaana</span></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhipiṃsu|nikkhipi.msu|nikkhipimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhipi.msu</span></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhippa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nikkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhippagāmi|nikkhippagaami|nikkhippagami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhippagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikkhippagāmin">nikkhippagāmin</a></p><p>laid down when one goes.</p>

nikkhippagāmin|nikkhippagaamin|nikkhippagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhippagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikkhippagāmin">nikkhippagāmin</a></p><p>laid down when one goes.</p>

nikkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nikkhipati cast down; put down, laid down; set down, set out, set forth; set aside, laid aside; deposited; installed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective laid down, lying; put down into, set in, arranged; in compounds (˚-having laid down = freed of, rid of DN.ii.14 (maṇi-ratanaṃ vatthe n. set into); Iti.13 (sagge: put into heaven) Ja.i.53, Ja.i.266; Pv.iii.6#8; Mil.343 (<em>agga˚</em>; put down as the highest, i.e. of the highest praise; cp. BSk. agranikṣipta Lal.167); Pv-a.148 (dhana n. = collected variant reading SS. nikkita). nikkhitta- <em><a href="bword://da%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">daṇḍa</a></em> (adj.) not using a weapon (cp. daṇḍa) SN.i.141 etc.; nikkhitta- <em><a href="bword://dhura">dhura</a></em> unyoked freed of the yoke AN.i.71; AN.iii.108; cp. Dhs-a.145; - <em>su˚</em>; well set, well arranged AN.ii.147 sq. (˚assa pada-vyañjanassa attho sunnayo hoti); opp. <em>dun˚</em> AN.i.59; Ne.21.</p><p>Sk. nikṣipta, see <em><a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://daṇḍa">daṇḍa</a>, <a href="bword://dhura">dhura</a>, <a href="bword://nikkhipati">nikkhipati</a></p>

nikkhittaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun one to whose charge something has been committed Dpvs.iv.5 (<em>agga˚</em>; thera original depositary of the Faith).</p><p>fr. nikkhitta</p>

nikkhāmenta|nikkhaamenta|nikkhamenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhaamenta</span></p><p>of nikkhāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhāmeti">nikkhāmeti</a></p>

nikkhāmeti|nikkhaameti|nikkhameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhaameti</span></p><p>of nikkhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhamati">nikkhamati</a></p>

nikkhāmeti|nikkhaameti|nikkhameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhaameti</span></p><p>Nikkhameti &amp; Nikkhāmeti</p><p>to make go out or away, to bring out or forth SN.ii.128; Ja.i.264 Ja.ii.112<br />pp <em>nikkhāmita</em> Ja.iii.99 (+ nicchuddha thrown out, in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of nibbāpita; variant reading BB. nikaḍhāpita).</p><p>Caus. of nikkhamati</p>

nikkhāmetvā|nikkhaametvaa|nikkhametva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhaametvaa</span></p><p>of nikkhāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhāmeti">nikkhāmeti</a></p>

nikkhāmita|nikkhaamita|nikkhamita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkhaamita</span></p><p>of nikkhāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkhāmeti">nikkhāmeti</a></p>

nikkilesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>freedom fr. moral blemish Mnd.340 = Nd ii.under pucchā Cnd.185; as adj. pure, unstained Dhp-a.iv.192 = Snp-a.469 (= anāvila).</p><p>nis + kilesa</p>

nikkiṇati|nikki.nati|nikkinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikki.nati</span></p><p>to buy back, to redeem Ja.vi.576, Ja.vi.585; Mil.284.</p><p>Sk. niṣkriṇāti, nis + kiṇāti</p>

nikkiṇṇa|nikki.n.na|nikkinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikki.n.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikiṇṇa">nikiṇṇa</a></p>

nikkiṇṇa|nikki.n.na|nikkinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikki.n.na</span></p><p>adjective spread out, spread before, ready (for eating) Ja.vi.182 (= ṭhapita Com.).</p><p>Sk. niṣkīrṇa, nis + kiṇṇa, see <em><a href="bword://kira%E1%B9%87a">kiraṇa</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kiraṇa">kiraṇa</a></p>

nikkodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without anger, free from anger Ja.iv.22.</p><p>nis + kodha</p>

nikkuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from deceit</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kuha">kuha</a></p>

nikkuha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without deceit, not false AN.ii.26 = Iti.113; Snp.56; Cnd.342.</p><p>nis + kuha</p>

nikkujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujja">nikujja</a></p>

nikkujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective bent down, i.e. head forward, lying on one’s face; upset, thrown over AN.i.130; SN.v.48; Pv.iv.7#7 (k); Pp.31. Opp. ukkujja.</p><p>ni + kubja, better spelling is nikujja see <em><a href="bword://nikkujjati">nikkujjati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkujjati">nikkujjati</a></p>

nikkujjanā|nikkujjanaa|nikkujjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkujjanaa</span></p><p>, ~a, n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjanā">nikujjanā</a></p>

nikkujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikkujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to turn upside down, to upset Vin.ii.113; AN.iv.344 (pattaṃ). pp. <em><a href="bword://nikkujjita">nikkujjita</a></em>.</p><p>for nikujjati (q.v.) through analogy with opp. ukkujjati. Etym. perhaps to kujja humpback, Sk kubja, but better with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>1 p. 175 = Sk. nyubjati influenced by kubja with regard to k.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkujjita">nikkujjita</a></p>

nikkujjeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkujjati">nikkujjati</a></p>

nikkujjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjita">nikujjita</a></p>

nikkujjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective lying face downward, overturned, upset fallen over, stumbled Vin.i.16; DN.i.85, DN.i.110; DN.i.147, MN.i.24 (k.); AN.i.173; AN.iii.238; Thig.28, Thig.30 (k.); Ja.iii.277; Snp-a.155 (= adhomukha-ṭhapita); DN-a.i.228.</p><p>pp. of nikkujjati; often (rightly) spelt nikujjita, q.v.</p>

nikkujjitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjita">nikujjita</a></p>

nikkujjitvā|nikkujjitvaa|nikkujjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkujjitvaa</span></p><p>of nikujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikkāma|nikkaama|nikkama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaama</span></p><p>without sensual desire; without sensual pleasure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāma">kāma</a></p>

nikkāma|nikkaama|nikkama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaama</span></p><p>adjective without craving or lust, desireless Snp.1131 (= akāmakāmin Cnd.340 pahīnakāma Snp-a.605 with variant reading: nikkāma). Cp. next.</p><p>Sk. niṣkāma, nis + kāma</p>

nikkāmi|nikkaami|nikkami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikkāmin">nikkāmin</a></p><p>without desires; without sensual pleasures.</p>

nikkāmin|nikkaamin|nikkamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikkāmin">nikkāmin</a></p><p>without desires; without sensual pleasures.</p>

nikkāmin|nikkaamin|nikkamin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaamin</span></p><p>adjective = nikkāma Snp.228 (= katanikkhamana Kp-a.184).</p><p>nis + kāmin</p>

nikkāraṇa|nikkaara.na|nikkarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaara.na</span></p><p>without motive; disinterested</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāraṇa">kāraṇa</a></p>

nikkāraṇā|nikkaara.naa|nikkarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(abl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaara.naa</span></p><p>= adv.) without reason, without cause or purpose Snp.75 (= akāraṇā ahetu Cnd.341).</p><p>Sk. niṣkāraṇā, nis + kāraṇaṃ</p>

nikkāsa|nikkaasa|nikkasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikkaasa</span></p><p>is Bdhgh’s reading for <em><a href="bword://ikk%C4%81sa">ikkāsa</a></em> (q.v.) Vin.ii.151, with C. on p. 321.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ikkāsa">ikkāsa</a></p>

nikubbati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr.</i></p><ol start="3"><li>sg.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikaroti">nikaroti</a></p>

nikujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>‘with the (hollow side of the) curve facing down’; face downwards, upside down.</p>

nikujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nikkujja">nikkujja</a></em>, q.v. also for <em>nikujjita</em> which is more correctly spelt <em>k</em> than <em>kk</em> (cp. Trenckner, Preface to Majjhima Nikāya &amp; see <em>ni</em>˚ A 1).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikkujja">nikkujja</a></p>

nikujjanā|nikujjanaa|nikujjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikujjanaa</span></p><p>, ~a, neuter placing (the bowl of a layman) face downwards; over turning.</p>

nikujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>nikujjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. tums upside down, overtums; places face downwards; esp. tums (a layman’s bowl) upside down, refuses alms (with a formal act of the <em>saṅgha</em>)</p><p>nikujjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. rejects; ? puts aside, stores.</p>

nikujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be bent down on, i.e. to attach importance to, to lay weight on DN.i.53 (as vv.ll. to be preferred to text reading nikkujj˚ cp. nikujja); DN-a.i.160 (nikk˚).</p><p>ni + kujjati, see <em><a href="bword://kujja">kujja</a></em> &amp; cp. nikkujja</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kujja">kujja</a></p>

nikujjeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikujjetvā|nikujjetvaa|nikujjetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikujjetvaa</span></p><p>of nikujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikujjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nikujjitati turned upside down, overturned; face downwards</p><p>See also: nikujjitati</p>

nikujjitvā|nikujjitvaa|nikujjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikujjitvaa</span></p><p>of nikujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikujjiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nikujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikujjati">nikujjati</a></p>

nikuñja|niku~nja|nikunja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niku~nja</span></p><p>hollow down, a glen, thicket Dāvs iv.32.</p><p>Sk. nikuñja, ni + kuñja</p>

nikāma|nikaama|nikama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaama</span></p><p>desire, pleasure, longing: only in compounds; see <em><a href="bword://nanik%C4%81ma">nanikāma</a></em>.</p><ul><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> read by Kern (</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><ol start="174"><li>at Thag.1271 for na kāmakāra but unjustified (see Snp-a on Snp.351);</li></ol></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://l%C4%81bhin">lābhin</a></em> gaining pleasure SN.ii.278; MN.i.354; MN.iii.110; AN.ii.23, AN.ii.36 Pp.11, Pp.12; Vb.332.</p></li></ul><p>Vedic nikāma, ni + kāma</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nanikāma">nanikāma</a>, <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://lābhin">lābhin</a></p>

nikāmalābhī|nikaamalaabhii|nikamalabhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaamalaabhii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikāmalābhīn">nikāmalābhīn</a></p><p>obtaining to one’s wish, at will.</p>

nikāmalābhīn|nikaamalaabhiin|nikamalabhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaamalaabhiin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nikāmalābhīn">nikāmalābhīn</a></p><p>obtaining to one’s wish, at will.</p>

nikāmanā|nikaamanaa|nikamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaamanaa</span></p><p>= nikanti, Dhs.1059.</p>

nikāmayamāna|nikaamayamaana|nikamayamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaamayamaana</span></p><p>of nikāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikāmeti">nikāmeti</a></p>

nikāmayati|nikaamayati|nikamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaamayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikāmeti">nikāmeti</a></p>

nikāmeti|nikaameti|nikameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaameti</span></p><p>desires, wants more of; takes pleasure in, enjoys.</p>

nikāmeti|nikaameti|nikameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaameti</span></p><p>to crave, desire, strive after, ppr nikāmayaṃ SN.i.122, &amp; nikāmayamāna Vin.ii.108. Cp. nikanta &amp; nikanti.</p><p>Sk. ni-kāmayati, ni + kāmeti</p>

nikāra|nikaara|nikara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaara</span></p><p>service, humility Ja.iii.120 (nikāra-pakāra, prob. to be read nipaccākāra, q.v.).</p><p>Sk. nikāra in diff. meaning, ni + kāra</p>

nikāsa|nikaasa|nikasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaasa</span></p><p>noun adjective appearance; adj. of appearance, like Ja.v.87 (-˚), corresp. to ˚avakāsa.</p><p>ni + <em>kaś</em></p>

nikāsin|nikaasin|nikasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaasin</span></p><p>adjective “shining,” resembling, like Ja.iii.320 (aggi-nikāsinā suriyena).</p><p>cp. Sk. nikāśin; fr. ni + kāsati</p>

nikāya|nikaaya|nikaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaaya</span></p><p>group; sect; a collection; esp. a group of discourses, one of the five collections of the Suttapiṭaka.</p>

nikāya|nikaaya|nikaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikaaya</span></p><p>collection (“body”) assemblage, class, group.</p><ol><li><em>generally</em> (always-˚): <em>eka˚</em>; one class of beings Dhs-a.66; <em>tiracchāna˚</em>; the animal kingdom SN.iii.152; <em>deva˚</em>; the assembly of the gods, the gods DN.ii.261 (60); MN.i.102; SN.iv.180; AN.iii.249; AN.iv.461; Pv-a.136; <em>satta˚</em>; the world of beings, the animate creation, a class of living beings SN.ii.2, SN.ii.42, SN.ii.44; MN.i.49 (tesaṃ tesaṃ sattānaṃ tamhi tamhi s<br />■ nikāye of all beings in each class); Vb.137; Pv-a.134.</li><li><em>especially</em> the coll. of Buddhist Suttas, as the 5 sections of the Suttanta Piṭaka, viz. Dīgha˚, Majjhima˚, Saṃyutta˚ Anguttara˚ (referred to as D.M.S.A. in Dictionaryquotations), Khuddaka˚; enumerated Pv-a.2; Anvs p. 35; Dhp-a.ii.95 (dhammāsanaṃ āruyha pañcahi nikāyehi atthañ ca kāraṇañ ca ākaḍḍhitvā). The <em>five</em> Nikāyas are enumerated also at Vism.711; <em>one</em> is referred to at Snp-a.195 (pariyāpuṇāti master by heart). See further details under piṭaka. Cp. nekāyika.</li></ol><p>Sk. nikāya, ni + kāya</p>

nikīḷitāvin|nikii.litaavin|nikilitavin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikii.litaavin</span></p><p>adjective playful, playing or dallying with (c. loc.), finding enjoyment in SN.i.9 (a˚ kāmesu), SN.iv.110 (id.).</p><p>fr. ni-kīḷati</p>

nikūjati|nikuujati|nikujati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikuujati</span></p><p>makes an inarticulate noise; calls; sings.</p>

nikūjati|nikuujati|nikujati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikuujati</span></p><ol><li>to chirp, warble, hum Thag.1270 (nikūjaṃ); Thag-a.211 (nikūji).</li><li>to twang, jingle, rustle Ja.iii.323</li></ol><p>pp nikūjita<br />■ Cp abhi˚.</p><p>ni + kūjati “to sing on”</p>

nikūjita|nikuujita|nikujita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikuujita</span></p><p>sung forth, warbled out Thig.261.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nik%C5%ABjati">nikūjati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nikūjati">nikūjati</a></p>

nikūṭa|nikuu.ta|nikuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikuu.ta</span></p><p>projection; end, culmination.</p>

nikūṭa|nikuu.ta|nikuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nikuu.ta</span></p><p>corner, top, climax Ja.i.278 (arahatta˚, where usually arahattena kūṭaṃ etc.) DN-a.i.307 (id.).</p><p>ni + kūṭa to kūṭa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nilacchesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nillaccheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillaccheti">nillaccheti</a></p>

nilaccheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>castrates; marks.</p>

nilacchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nillaccheti castrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillaccheti">nillaccheti</a></p>

nilavāsi|nilavaasi|nilavasi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nilavaasi</span></p><p>A Thera mentioned as staying at the Kukkuṭārāma in Pāṭaliputta. Vin.i.300</p>

nilaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>dwelling, habitation, lair, nest Ja.iii.454.</p><p>fr. ni + <em>lī</em></p>

nilañchaka|nila~nchaka|nilanchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nila~nchaka</span></p><p>Also written as: Nillañchaka</p><p>adjective noun one who marks cattle, i.e. one who castrates or deprives of virility Ja.iv.364 (spelt <em><a href="bword://tila%C3%B1chaka">tilañchaka</a></em> in text, but right in variant reading), explained as “tisūlâdi-ankakaraṇena lañchakā ca lakkhaṇakārakā ti attho” (p 366). cp. nillacchita.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirlāñchana, of nirlāñchayati = nis + laccheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tilañchaka">tilañchaka</a></p>

nilenaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>settling place, hiding-place, refuge Ja.v.102 (so read for <em>nillenaka</em> explained by nilīyanaṭṭhāna p. 103).</p><p>cp. Sk. nilayana, fr. ni + <em>lī</em></p>

nilicchita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nillacchita">nillacchita</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillacchita">nillacchita</a></p>

nillacchesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nilacchesi">nilacchesi</a></p>

nillaccheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillaccheti">nillaccheti</a></p>

nillaccheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to deprive of the marks or characteristics (of virility), to castrate Thig.437 (= purisa-bhāvassa lacchana-bhūtāni bījakāni nillacchesi nīhari Thag-a.270). See also nillañchaka &amp; nillacchita.</p><p>nis + laccheti of <em>lāñch</em>, cp. lakkhaṇa</p>

nillacchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nillaccheti castrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillaccheti">nillaccheti</a></p>

nillacchita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective castrated Thig.440; written as <em><a href="bword://nilicchita">nilicchita</a></em> at Ja.vi.238 (variant reading BB as gloss niluñcita). explained by “vacchakakāla… nibbījako kato, uddhaṭabījo (p. 239).</p><p>Sk. *nirlāñchita, nis + lacchita of nillaccheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nilicchita">nilicchita</a></p>

nillajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective shameless Sdhp.382. Ni(l)lanchaka</p><p>nis + lajjā</p>

nillapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without deceit, free from slander AN.ii.26 = Iti.113. Nillaleti &amp; Nilloleti;</p><p>nis + lapa</p>

nillañchaka|nilla~nchaka|nillanchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nilla~nchaka</span></p><p>Also written as: Nillañchaka</p><p>adjective noun one who marks cattle, i.e. one who castrates or deprives of virility Ja.iv.364 (spelt <em><a href="bword://tila%C3%B1chaka">tilañchaka</a></em> in text, but right in variant reading), explained as “tisūlâdi-ankakaraṇena lañchakā ca lakkhaṇakārakā ti attho” (p 366). cp. nillacchita.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirlāñchana, of nirlāñchayati = nis + laccheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tilañchaka">tilañchaka</a></p>

nillehitvā|nillehitvaa|nillehitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillehitvaa</span></p><p>having licked, licked of; having took up with the finger.</p>

nillekha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without scratches, without edges (?) Vin.ii.123 (of jantāghara).</p><p>nis + lekha</p>

nillobha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from greed Ja.iv.10.</p><p>nis + lobha</p>

nillokana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun watching out; watchful, careful Ja.v.43, Ja.v.86 (˚sīla).</p><p>nis + lokana</p>

nilloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nilloketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nilloketi">nilloketi</a></p>

nilloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>observes; looks at carefully; examines.</p>

nilloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to watch out, keep guard, watch, observe Vin.ii.208.</p><p>nis + loketi</p>

nillolupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: Nilloluppa</p><p>adjective free from greed or desires Snp.56 (= Cnd.362 nittaṇha); Ja.v.358.</p><p>nis + loluppā</p>

nilloluppa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: Nilloluppa</p><p>adjective free from greed or desires Snp.56 (= Cnd.362 nittaṇha); Ja.v.358.</p><p>nis + loluppā</p>

nillopa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>plunder.</p>

nillopa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>plundering, plunder DN.i.52; AN.i.154; Mnd.144 (˚ṃ harati); Cnd.199#7; Tikp.167, Kp.280; DN-a.i.159.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirlopa, nis + <em>lup</em></p>

nilloḷeti|nillo.leti|nilloleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillo.leti</span></p><p>Nillāḷeti &amp; Nilloḷeti</p><p>to move (the tongue) up &amp; down SN.i.118; MN.i.109; DN-a.i.42 (pp. nillāḷita-jivhā); Dhp-a.iv.197 (jivhaṃ nilloleti; variant reading nillāleti &amp; lilāḷeti) = Ja.v.434 (variant reading nillelati for ˚lo˚).</p><p>nis + <em>lul</em>, cp. Sk. laḍayati &amp; loḍayati</p>

nillāletvā|nillaaletvaa|nillaletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillaaletvaa</span></p><p>having wagged (the tongue); having moved the tongue to and fro.</p>

nillāḷeti|nillaa.leti|nillaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillaa.leti</span></p><p>Nillāḷeti &amp; Nilloḷeti</p><p>to move (the tongue) up &amp; down SN.i.118; MN.i.109; DN-a.i.42 (pp. nillāḷita-jivhā); Dhp-a.iv.197 (jivhaṃ nilloleti; variant reading nillāleti &amp; lilāḷeti) = Ja.v.434 (variant reading nillelati for ˚lo˚).</p><p>nis + <em>lul</em>, cp. Sk. laḍayati &amp; loḍayati</p>

nillāḷetvā|nillaa.letvaa|nillaletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillaa.letvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillāḷetvā">nillāḷetvā</a></p>

nillī|nillii|nilli
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nillii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīlī">nīlī</a></p>

niluñchita|nilu~nchita|nilunchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nilu~nchita</span></p><p>of nillaccheti castrated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nillaccheti">nillaccheti</a></p>

nilīna|niliina|nilina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niliina</span></p><p>who has alighted on, settled on; perched; keeping apart; hidden; lurking, lying in wait.</p>

nilīna|niliina|nilina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niliina</span></p><p>adjective sitting on (c. loc.), perched; hidden, concealed, lying in wait Ja.i.135, Ja.i.293; Ja.iii.26; Vv-a.230.</p><p>pp. of nilāyati</p>

nilīyana|niliiyana|niliyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niliiyana</span></p><p>hiding Ja.v.103 (˚ṭṭhāna hiding-place).</p><p>abstr. fr. nilīyati, cp. Sk. nilayana</p>

nilīyati|niliiyati|niliyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niliiyati</span></p><p>to sit down (esp. for the purpose of hiding), to settle, alight; to keep oneself hidden, to lurk, hide Ja.i.222, Ja.i.292; Mil.257; Pv-a.178. aor nilīyi Ja.i.158; Ja.iii.26; Dhp-a.ii.56; Pv-a.274<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nil%C4%ABna">nilīna</a></em>. Caus. ii. <em>nilīyāpeti</em> to conceal, hide (trs.) Ja.i.292.</p><p>ni + līyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nilīna">nilīna</a></p>

nimajjhima
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective the middle one Ja.v.371.</p>

nimantaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun one who invites Mil.205.</p>

nimantana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>invitation; a meal to which one is invited.</p>

nimantana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>invitation Vin.i.58 = Vin.ii.175; DN.i.166; MN.i.77; AN.i.295; Ja.i.116 (ṇ), Ja.i.412 Pp.55.</p><p>to nimanteti</p>

nimantanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective inviting; (nt.) Name of a Suttanta MN.i.331; quoted at Vism.393.</p>

nimantayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimantayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nimanteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimantesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nimanteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimantetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of nimantita</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimantita">nimantita</a></p>

nimanteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>calls, summons; invites (someone, acc.) to (instr.); offers, asks (someone) to accept (instr.)</p>

nimanteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to send a message, to call, summon, invite, coax (to = c. instr. Snp.981 (nimantayi aor., āsanena asked him to sit down) Ja.vi.365; Cnd.342; Dhp-a.iii.171 (˚ayiṃsu); DN-a.i.169; Vv-a.47 (pānīyena invite to a drink); Pv-a.75, Pv-a.95. pp. <em><a href="bword://nimantita">nimantita</a></em>, q.v<br />■ Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. nimantrayati, ni + manteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimantita">nimantita</a></p>

nimantetvā|nimantetvaa|nimantetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimantetvaa</span></p><p>of nimanteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimantita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nimanteti invited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimantita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>invited Snp.p.104; Pv-a.22 (bhattena to the meal), Pv-a.86 (= āmantita), Pv-a.141.</p><p>pp. of nimanteti</p>

nimantiyamāna|nimantiyamaana|nimantiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimantiyamaana</span></p><p>of nimanteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimanteti">nimanteti</a></p>

nimba
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Neem Tree, sometimes <em><a href="bword://pucimanda">pucimanda</a></em>, Hindi <em>neem</em>, <em>Azadirachta indica</em> A.i.32 A.v.212 Ja.iv.205, a large deciduous tree the leaves of which have an extremely bitter taste and medicinal and insecticidal properties. It was believed that a mango tree growing next to a neem would give bitter fruit as a result Ja.ii.105. Neem produces a hard durable timber and stakes made from the wood were used to impale criminals Ja.iii.34. An extract from the leaves was used as medicine Vin.i.201.</p><p>In India today, country folk use neem twigs to clean their teeth in the morning, chewing the twig until it frays and then rubbing it up and down over their teeth. The Buddha recommended using a tooth stick because it keeps the eyes clear, purifies the sinuses, sweetens the breath, cleans the taste buds, prevents phlegm and mucus getting in the food and makes food more tasty A.iii.249. However, he does not say what type of wood the tooth stick should be made from. See <em>Tambūla</em>.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Nimba</em></p><p>Root: as tonic, antiperiodic, strangulation of intestine. Stems: Bitter astringent, antiperiodic, cholera, vermifuge, demulcent, stimulant, cholera. As a contraceptive, 100 grams of bark from the stem is soaked in 1 liter of water and this infusion is taken by men daily for a month. Leaves: For boils, anthelmintic, on skin diseases, young leaves eaten for heart disease and tuberculosis, diarrhea, dysentery, promotes lactation. Flowers: Tonic, stomachic, stimulant. Fruit and seeds: Purgative, emollient, anthelmintic, local stimulant, insecticide, antiseptic, astringent, for leprosy, piles, heart and urinary diseases and tuberculosis.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pucimanda">pucimanda</a>, Tambūla</p>

nimba
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the Nimb tree (Azadirachta Indica), bearing a bitter leaf, &amp; noted for its hard wood Vin.i.152 (˚kosa), Vin.i.284 (id.), Vin.i.201 (˚kasāva); AN.i.32 AN.v.212; Vv.33#36 (˚muṭṭhi, a handful of N. leaves); Ja.ii.105, Ja.ii.106; Dhp-a.i.52 (˚kosa); Dhs-a.320 (˚paṇṇa, the leaf of the N. as example of tittaka, bitter taste); Vv-a.142 (˚palāsa); Pv-a.220 (˚rukkhassa daṇḍena katasūla).</p><p>Sk. nimba, non-Aryan</p>

nimbabīja|nimbabiija|nimbabija
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimbabiija</span></p><p>seed of the neem tree.</p>

nimbapaṇṇa|nimbapa.n.na|nimbapanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimbapa.n.na</span></p><p>leaf of the neem tree.</p>

nimesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>winking Mil.194.</p><p>= nimisa, cp. Vedic nimesa</p>

nimināti|niminaati|niminati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niminaati</span></p><p>to turn round, change to barter, exchange for (c. instr.): pres. imper. <em>niminā</em> Ja.v.343 (= parivattehi Com.); pres. 1st pl. <em>nimimhase</em> Ja.ii.369, pot. <em>nimineyya</em> Ja.iii.63; fut. <em>nimissati</em> Ja.v.271 Ja.v.453 (devatāhi nirayaṃ); aor. <em>nimmini</em> Ja.iii.63; ger <em>niminitvā</em> Mil.279.</p><p>Sk. niminoti in diff. meaning, the P. meaning being influenced by <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em>; ni + mināti, <em>mi</em> to fix, measure cp. Sk. nimaya barter, change</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

nimisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>winking, shutting the eyes; <em><a href="bword://animisa">animisa</a></em> not winking Dāvs v.26. See also <em><a href="bword://nimesa">nimesa</a></em>.</p><p>cp. Vedic nimiṣ f. &amp; nimiṣa nt.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://animisa">animisa</a>, <a href="bword://nimesa">nimesa</a></p>

nimisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to wink DN.ii.20 (animisanto, not winking; variant reading BB animm˚; Ja.iii.96 (ummisati +). Cp. nimisatā.</p><p>Sk. nimiṣati, ni + misati</p>

nimisatā|nimisataa|nimisata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimisataa</span></p><p>winking Ja.vi.336 (a˚).</p><p>abstr. to nimisati</p>

nimissaṃ|nimissa.m|nimissam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimissa.m</span></p><p>barter; exchange for.</p>

nimitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li><ol><li>a sign or mark by which something or someone is recognized or identified or known or defined; a distinguishing mark or appearance; a perceived (enduring) attribute, predicate (especially that of permanence); an attribution.</li><li>the organ of generation (of either sex), the pudenda.</li></ol></li><li><p>an object or appearance or happening which is significant, which expresses more than itself;</p><ol><li>a sign, a significant appearance; an omen, a portent.</li><li>an indication, a hint.</li></ol></li><li><ol><li>what one notes or marks; an object of thought or meditation or concentration; an image.</li><li>an internal/ appearance or total awareness; a mental impression (appearing as an early stage of jhāna, a sign of progress)</li></ol></li><li><p>a ground, a cause, a reason.</p></li></ol>

nimitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>sign, omen, portent, prognostication DN.i.9 (study of omens = n. satthaṃ DN-a.i.92, q.v. for detailed expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); Ja.i.11 (caturo nimitte nâddasaṃ); Mil.79, Mil.178 Esp. as <em>pubba˚</em>; signs preceding an event, portents, warnings, foreshadowings SN.v.154, SN.v.278, SN.v.442; Iti.76 (cp. Divy.193, of the waning of a god); Ja.i.48, Ja.i.50 (32 signs before birth, some at DN-a.i.61), Ja.i.59; Mil.298; Vism.577.</li><li>outward appearance, mark, characteristic, attribute phenomenon (opp. essence) DN.iii.249; AN.i.256; AN.iii.319 AN.iii.375 sq.; AN.iv.33, AN.iv.418 sq.; Ja.i.420; Pts.i.60, Pts.i.91 sq., Pts.i.164, Pts.i.170, Pts.ii.39, Pts.ii.64; Vb.193 sq<br />■ Mental reflex, image (with ref to jhāna) Vism.123, cp. Dhs-a.167<br />■ Specified e.g. as foll.: oḷārika SN.v.259; pasādaniya SN.v.156; paccavekkhana˚ DN.iii.278; Vb.334; bahiddhā-sankhārā Pts.i.66 sq.; bāla˚ (opp. paṇḍita˚) MN.iii.163; AN.i.102 mukha˚ (= face) DN.i.80; SN.iii.103; SN.v.121; AN.v.92, AN.v.97 sq., AN.v.103; rūpa˚, sadda˚ etc. SN.iii.10; MN.i.296; Pts.i.92 Pts.i.112; samatha˚ DN.iii.213; samādhi˚ etc. AN.i.256 sq. subha˚ (&amp; asubha˚) SN.v.64, SN.v.103 sq.; AN.i.3 sq., AN.i.87, AN.i.200, AN.v.134; Vism.178 sq. <em>nimittaṃ gaṇhāti</em> to make something the object of a thought, to catch up a theme for reflection Vin.i.183, cp. SN.v.150 sq. (˚ṃ uggaṇhāti) MN.i.119 (= five sorts of mental images); Cnd.659; Dhs-a.53 (= ākāra). See below n-gāhin &amp; animitta.; nimittaṃ parivajjeti to discard the phenomenal SN.i.188; Snp.341.</li><li>mark, aim: in nimittaṃ karoti to pick out the aim, to mark out Ja.v.436; Cnd.235, Cnd.1#d; Mil.418.</li><li>sexual organ (cp. lakkhaṇa) Vin.iii.129 (n. &amp; a˚, as term of abuse); see also kāṭa &amp; koṭacikā.</li><li>ground reason, condition, in; <em>nimittena</em> (instr.) and <em>nimittaṃ</em> (acc.) as adv. = by means of, on account of Dhp-a.iii.175 (instr.) Pv-a.8, Pv-a.97 (jāti-nimittaṃ), Pv-a.106 (kiṃ n˚ṃ = kissa hetu), Pv-a.242 (yaṃ n˚ṃ = yato nidānaṃ). gahita-nimittena “by means of being caught” Vism.144 = Dhs-a.116 (read translation 154 accordingly!). adj. <em><a href="bword://nimitta">nimitta</a></em> (-˚ caused by, referring to Pv-a.64 (maraṇa-nimittaṃ rodanaṃ)</li></ol><p>■ <em>animitta</em> free from marks or attributes not contaminated by outward signs or appearance undefiled, ụnaffected, unconditioned (opp. sa˚) SN.i.188 SN.iv.225 (phassa), SN.iv.268, SN.iv.360 (samādhi); MN.i.296 (cetovimutti); AN.i.82; AN.iii.292; AN.iv.78; Vin.iii.129; Thag.92; DN.iii.219, DN.iii.249; Dhp.92; Snp.342; Pts.i.60, Pts.i.91; Pts.ii.36, Pts.ii.59 sq (vimokha), 65 sq., 99; Dhs.530 (read a˚ for appa˚) Vism.236; Dhs-a.223 (absence of the 3 lakkhaṇas) Mil.333, Mil.413; Dhp-a.ii.172; Thag-a.50. See also <em>Cpd.</em> 199 211<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5<!-- raw HTML omitted -->. <em>sanimitta</em> SN.v.213 sq.; AN.i.82.</p><ul><li><em>-ānusārin</em> following outward signs (= ˚gāhin) AN.iii.292; Ne.25;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> prognostication, prophecy Vin.v.172; Vb.353;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87a">karaṇa</a></em> = gāhin SN.iv.297;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81hin">gāhin</a></em> “taking signs,” enticed or led away by outward signs entranced with the general appearance, sensuously attracted DN.i.70 (cp. <em>dialogues</em> i.80); DN.iii.225; SN.iv.104 SN.iv.168; AN.ii.16; AN.iii.99; AN.v.348; Pp.20, Pp.24, Pp.58; Dhs.1345; Mil.367, Mil.403. Cp. Vism.151, Vism.209.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. nimitta, to <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em>, although etym. uncertain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimitta">nimitta</a>, <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a>, <a href="bword://karaṇa">karaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://gāhin">gāhin</a>, <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

nimittaggāha|nimittaggaaha|nimittaggaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittaggaaha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāha">nimittaggāha</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics; exclusive concentration (on, loc.); being completely taken up (by, loc.)</p>

nimittaggāhī|nimittaggaahii|nimittaggahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittaggaahii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāhīn">nimittaggāhīn</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics.</p>

nimittaggāhīn|nimittaggaahiin|nimittaggahin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittaggaahiin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāhīn">nimittaggāhīn</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics.</p>

nimittagāha|nimittagaaha|nimittagaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittagaaha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāha">nimittaggāha</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics; exclusive concentration (on, loc.); being completely taken up (by, loc.)</p>

nimittagāhī|nimittagaahii|nimittagahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittagaahii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāhīn">nimittaggāhīn</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics.</p>

nimittagāhīn|nimittagaahiin|nimittagahin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittagaahiin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittaggāhīn">nimittaggāhīn</a></p><p>apprehending, responding emotionally to, an object or appearance; grasping, occupying oneself with, external features or characteristics.</p>

nimittanusāri|nimittanusaari|nimittanusari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittanusaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittanusārin">nimittanusārin</a></p><p>following attributes.</p>

nimittanusārin|nimittanusaarin|nimittanusarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimittanusaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nimittanusārin">nimittanusārin</a></p><p>following attributes.</p>

nimittaṃ gaṇhāti|nimitta.m ga.nhaati|nimittam ganhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimitta.m ga.nhaati</span></p><p>marks, apprehends the characteristic features; apprehends an object or appearance (as distinguished in various ways); distinguishes, identifies an object or appearance.</p>

nimittaṃ karoti|nimitta.m karoti|nimittam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimitta.m karoti</span></p><p>makes a sign; marks; indicates; distinguishes.</p>

nimmadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>touching, touch, crushing, subduing AN.ii.34 (mada-nimmadana, crushing out pride; may, however, be taken as nis + mada of <em>mad</em> “de-priding,” lit. disintoxication); Bv.i.81; Vism.293.</p><p>to nimmādeti</p>

nimmadaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective suppressible DN.ii.243.</p><p>Sk. nirmṛdya, grd. of nimmadeti</p>

nimmadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmadeti">nimmadeti</a></p>

nimmaddana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>touching, crushing Mil.270 (na vāto hattha-gahaṇaṃ vā nimmaddanaṃ vā upeti the wind cannot be grasped).</p><p>nis + <em>mṛd</em></p>

nimmadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>humbles; subdues.</p>

nimmajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nimmajjana (Nimmiñjana?)</p><p>a kind of (oil-)cake Vv.33#38 (nimmajjani = tilapiññāka Vv-a.147); Pv.i.10#10 (˚miñjana, variant reading BB ˚majjani); Pv-a.47 (doṇi˚).</p><p>*mṛd-yana? perhaps nonAryan</p>

nimmajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>rubs or wipes off.</p>

nimmakkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without egotism, not false, not slandering Snp.56 (cp Cnd.356 makkha = niṭṭhuriya; see also Snp-a.108; paraguṇa-vināsana-lakkhaṇo makkho).</p><p>nis + makkha, cp. Sk. nirmatsara</p>

nimmakkhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from flies Ja.i.262; Dhp-a.i.59. Nimmajjana (Nimminjana?)</p><p>Sk. nirmakṣika</p>

nimmala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from impurity, stainless, clean, pure AN.iv.340; Dhp.243; Cnd.586; Vism.58 Sdhp.250. Nimmata-pitika</p><p>nis + mala</p>

nimmanussa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>void of men, absence of men Ja.iii.148.</p><p>nis + manussa + ya</p>

nimmathana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>crushing Ja.iii.252; Vism.234 (sattu˚); Dhp-a.iii.404; Vv-a.284.</p><p>nis + mathana</p>

nimmatheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to crush out, suppress, destroy Ja.i.340. Cp. abhimatthati.</p><p>nis + matheti</p>

nimmaṃsa|nimma.msa|nimmamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimma.msa</span></p><p>adjective fleshless MN.i.58, MN.i.364; Pv-a.68.</p><p>nis + maṃsa</p>

nimminitvā|nimminitvaa|nimminitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimminitvaa</span></p><p>produces, forms, creates (by magic or supernatural power)</p>

nimminitvāna|nimminitvaana|nimminitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimminitvaana</span></p><p>of nimmināti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmināti">nimmināti</a></p>

nimmināti|nimminaati|nimminati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn &amp; m.n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimminaati</span></p><p>produces, forms, creates (by magic or supernatural power)</p>

nimmināti|nimminaati|nimminati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimminaati</span></p><p>to measure out, fashion, build, construct form; make by miracle, create, compose produce, lay out, plan, aor. <em>nimmini</em> Ja.i.232; Pv-a.245; Dhp-a.iv.67; ger <em>nimminitvā</em> Ja.i.32; Vv-a.80, &amp; <strong>nimmāya</strong> Vv.16#3<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nimmita">nimmita</a></em> See also nimmātar and nimmāna. Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirmimīti &amp; nirmāti, nis + mināti; <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em>; cp. nimināti</p><p>See also: nimmāya, <a href="bword://nimmita">nimmita</a>, <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

nimmita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nimmināti formed, produced, created; (a form) created (by magic or supernatural power); a magical creation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmināti">nimmināti</a></p>

nimmita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective past participle</p><p>measured out, planned, laid out; created (by supernatural power iddhi); measured, stately DN.i.18, DN.i.56 (iddhiyā pi DN-a.i.167), DN.i.219 (Su˚ devaputta. Np.), ibid. (Paranimmitavasavattī devā a class of devas, lit. “created by others, but also possessed of great power: Vv-a.79, Vv-a.80); also one of the 5, or the 3 spheres (kāmûpapattiyo) in the kāmaloka, viz. paccupaṭṭhita-kāmā, nimmānarati˚ (or nimmita˚), paranimmita˚. Iti.94; Dhs.1280 (cp. kāma) DN.iii.218; Ja.i.59, Ja.i.146 (kāyo n’ eva deva˚ na brahma˚), Ja.i.232, Cnd.202#a, also under pucchā; Pv.ii.1#19 (su˚, well constructed i.e. symmetrical); Vism.228 (Mārena nimmitaṃ Buddharūpaṃ); Vv-a.36 (= mitaṃ gacchati vāraṇo), Vv-a.79 Thag-a.69, Thag-a.70; Mil.1, Mil.242. See also abhinimmita.</p><p>pp. of nimmināti</p>

nimmiñjana|nimmi~njana|nimminjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmi~njana</span></p><p>Nimmajjana (Nimmiñjana?)</p><p>a kind of (oil-)cake Vv.33#38 (nimmajjani = tilapiññāka Vv-a.147); Pv.i.10#10 (˚miñjana, variant reading BB ˚majjani); Pv-a.47 (doṇi˚).</p><p>*mṛd-yana? perhaps nonAryan</p>

nimmoka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the slough or castoff skin of a snake Pv-a.63.</p><p>Sk. nirmoka fr. nis + moceti</p>

nimmugga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimugga">nimugga</a></p>

nimmujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimujjati">nimujjati</a></p>

nimmujjā|nimmujjaa|nimmujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmujjaa</span></p><p>Nimujjā (nimmujjā)</p><p>diving, immersion, in cpd. ummujja-nimujja(ṃ karoti) DN.i.78. See ummujjā</p><p>Sk. *nimajj-yā</p>

nimmādesi|nimmaadesi|nimmadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaadesi</span></p><p>of nimmadeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmadeti">nimmadeti</a></p>

nimmādeti|nimmaadeti|nimmadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaadeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmadeti">nimmadeti</a></p>

nimmādeti|nimmaadeti|nimmadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaadeti</span></p><p>to crush, subdue humiliate; insult DN.i.92 (variant reading <em>-maddeti</em>; = DN-a.i.257 nimmadati nimmāne karoti), Dhp.93, Dhp.96.</p><p>either = Sk. nirmṛdayati (<em>mṛd</em>) or *nirmādayati to nirmada. free from pride = nirmāna</p>

nimmāna|nimmaana|nimmana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaana</span></p><p>Nimmāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter measuring; production, creation, work; issara-n-hetu caused by God MN.ii.122; AN.i.173; Vb.367. <em>N- ratī</em> devā a class of devas, e.g. at DN.i.218; Iti.94; Vism.225; DN-a.i.114 Thag-a.169; Vv-a.149. Cp. (para-) nimmita.</p><p>Sk. nirmāṇa, see <em><a href="bword://nimmin%C4%81ti">nimmināti</a></em></p><p>Nimmāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective free from pride, humble DN-a.i.257.</p><p>Sk. nirmāna, nis + māna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmināti">nimmināti</a></p>

nimmānarati|nimmaanarati|nimmanarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanarati</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) belonging to the nimmānarati devas.</li><li>(f.) the world of the nimmānarati devas.</li></ol>

nimmānaratino|nimmaanaratino|nimmanaratino
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanaratino</span></p><p>the name of a group of devas.</p>

nimmānaratī|nimmaanaratii|nimmanarati
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanaratii</span></p><p>A class of devas, inhabiting the fifth of the six deva-worlds. DN.i.218 MN.i.289 SN.i.133 AN.i.210</p>

nimmānarāti|nimmaanaraati|nimmanarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanaraati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmānarati">nimmānarati</a></p>

nimmānayati|nimmaanayati|nimmanayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmānāyati">nimmānāyati</a></p>

nimmāniyati|nimmaaniyati|nimmaniyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaaniyati</span></p><p>to be abased, to be mocked Vin.ii.183.</p><p>Pass. to nimmāna, of nis + māna</p>

nimmānāyati|nimmaanaayati|nimmanayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaanaayati</span></p><p>becomes humble, subdued; is humbled.</p>

nimmāta|nimmaata|nimmata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaata</span></p><p>of nimmināti created</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimmināti">nimmināti</a></p>

nimmāta-pitika|nimmaata-pitika|nimmata-pitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaata-pitika</span></p><p>Nimmāta-pitika</p><p>adjective one who has neither mother nor father, an orphan Dhp-a.ii.72.</p><p>nis + māta-pitika</p>

nimmātar|nimmaatar|nimmatar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaatar</span></p><p>the creator; maker; builder.</p>

nimmātar|nimmaatar|nimmatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmaatar</span></p><p>maker, builder, creator DN.i.18, DN.i.56 (in formula: brahmā… kattā nimmātā…).</p><p>Sk. nirmātṛ, n. ag. of nimmināti</p>

nimmīlati|nimmiilati|nimmilati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmiilati</span></p><p>Nimīlati &amp; Nimmīlati</p><p>to shut, close (the eyes) Ja.i.279; Dhp-a.ii.6 (akkhīni nimmīlituṃ nâsakkhi) Caus. <em>nim(m)īl-eti</em> id. MN.i.120; Dhp-a.ii.28 (paralokaṃ opp. ummīleti); Ja.i.279; Vism.292 (akkhīni ni˚).</p><p>ni + mīlati</p>

nimmīleti|nimmiileti|nimmileti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmiileti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimīleti">nimīleti</a></p>

nimmīleti|nimmiileti|nimmileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmiileti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nim%C4%ABlati">nimīlati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimīlati">nimīlati</a></p>

nimmīletvā|nimmiiletvaa|nimmiletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmiiletvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimīletvā">nimīletvā</a></p>

nimmūla|nimmuula|nimmula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimmuula</span></p><p>adjective without root, rootless Ja.vi.177.</p><p>nis + mūla</p>

nimokkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>release, deliverance.</p>

nimokkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= vimokkha SN.i.2 (variant reading SS vi˚, preferable).</p>

nimugga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nimujjati sunk down; plunged, immersed in; submerged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimujjati">nimujjati</a></p>

nimugga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective plunged, immersed in, sunk down or fallen into (-˚) (c. loc. Vin.iii.106 (gūthakūpe sasīsakaṃ n.); DN.i.75; Ja.i.4 Ja.iii.393 (gūthakalale), Ja.iii.415; Mnd.26; Pp.71; Mil.262 Sdhp.573. Nimujja (nimmujja)</p><p>cp. Sk, nimagna, pp. of nimujjati</p>

nimujjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>diving, ducking; bathing Pv-a.47.</p><p>Sk. nimajjana</p>

nimujjanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nimujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimujjati">nimujjati</a></p>

nimujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>sinks down; dives into, immerses oneself in; is submerged.</p>

nimujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to sink down, plunge into (with loc.), dive in, be immersed AN.iv.11; Pp.74; Ja.i.66, Ja.i.70; Ja.iii.163, Ja.iii.393 (kāmakalale); Ja.iv.139; aor nimujji Ja.ii.293; Pv-a.47 (udake)<br />caus <em>nimujjeti</em> (so read for nimujjati Ja.v.268) &amp; <em>nimujjāpeti</em> to cause to sink or dive, to drown Ja.iii.133; Ja.iv.142 (nāvaṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nimugga">nimugga</a></em> q.v.</p><p>Sk. nimajjati, ni + mujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimugga">nimugga</a></p>

nimujjituṃ|nimujjitu.m|nimujjitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimujjitu.m</span></p><p>of nimujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimujjati">nimujjati</a></p>

nimujjitvā|nimujjitvaa|nimujjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimujjitvaa</span></p><p>of nimujjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimujjati">nimujjati</a></p>

nimujjā|nimujjaa|nimujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimujjaa</span></p><p>Nimujjā (nimmujjā)</p><p>diving, immersion, in cpd. ummujja-nimujja(ṃ karoti) DN.i.78. See ummujjā</p><p>Sk. *nimajj-yā</p>

nimīlati|nimiilati|nimilati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimiilati</span></p><p>Nimīlati &amp; Nimmīlati</p><p>to shut, close (the eyes) Ja.i.279; Dhp-a.ii.6 (akkhīni nimmīlituṃ nâsakkhi) Caus. <em>nim(m)īl-eti</em> id. MN.i.120; Dhp-a.ii.28 (paralokaṃ opp. ummīleti); Ja.i.279; Vism.292 (akkhīni ni˚).</p><p>ni + mīlati</p>

nimīlayati|nimiilayati|nimilayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimiilayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimīleti">nimīleti</a></p>

nimīleti|nimiileti|nimileti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimiileti</span></p><p>closes; closes one’s eyes.</p>

nimīletvā|nimiiletvaa|nimiletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nimiiletvaa</span></p><p>of nimīleti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimīleti">nimīleti</a></p>

ninahāta|ninahaata|ninahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninahaata</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninhāta">ninhāta</a></p>

nindana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>blaming, reviling, finding fault Dhp-a.iii.328.</p><p>abstr. fr. nindati</p>

nindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>blames; criticizes; censures.</p>

nindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to blame, find fault with, censure AN.ii.3; AN.v.171 AN.v.174; Snp.658; Ja.vi.63; Dhp.227;<br />inf. nindituṃ Dhp.230 grd nindanīya Snp-a.477.<br />pp nindita (q.v.); cp. also nindiya.</p><p>Sk. nindati, <em>nid</em> as in Gr. ο ̓́νειδος (blame), Lith. naids (hatred), Goth. naitjan (to rail or blaspheme) Ohg. neizzan (to plague); cp. Goth. neip = Ohg. nīd (envy)</p>

nindita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter id.</i></p><p>of nindati blamed; criticized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nindati">nindati</a></p>

nindita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective blamed, reproved, reviled; faulty, blameworthy Dhp.228; Pv.ii.3#34 (a˚ blameless agarahita pasaṃsa Pv-a.89); Sdhp.254, Sdhp.361<br />■ <em><a href="bword://anindita">anindita</a></em> Ja.iv.106 (˚angin).</p><p>pp. of nindati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anindita">anindita</a></p>

nindiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nindati</p><ol><li>(n.) low ground; a hollow, a depression.</li><li>(mfn.)<ol><li>low, low-lying, sunken; deep.</li><li>bending towards; inclined towards</li></ol></li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nindati">nindati</a></p>

nindiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective blameable, faulty, blameworthy Snp.658 (= nindanīya Snp-a.477) Ne.132. pi nindiyā at Pv-a.23 is to be read as pīṇitindriyā</p><p>Sk. nindya, orig. grd. of nindati</p>

nindā|nindaa|ninda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaa</span></p><p>blame, criticism; fault-finding.</p>

nindā|nindaa|ninda
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaa</span></p><p>blame, reproach, fault-finding, fault, disgrace SN.iii.73; AN.ii.188; AN.iv.157 sq.; MN.i.362; Snp.213 (+ pasaṃsā blame &amp; praise) Dhp.81 (id.); Snp.826, Snp.895, Snp.928; Dhp.143, Dhp.309; Mnd.165 Mnd.306, Mnd.384; Dhp-a.ii.148<br />■ In compound <em>nindi˚</em>; see <em>anindi</em>˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. nindā, to nindati</p>

nindārosa|nindaarosa|nindarosa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaarosa</span></p><p>fault-finding and offending.</p>

nindārosi|nindaarosi|nindarosi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaarosi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nindārosin">nindārosin</a></p><p>fault-finding and offensive.</p>

nindārosin|nindaarosin|nindarosin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaarosin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nindārosin">nindārosin</a></p><p>fault-finding and offensive.</p>

nindāvyārosa-upārambhabhaya|nindaavyaarosa-upaarambhabhaya|nindavyarosa-uparambhabhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaavyaarosa-upaarambhabhaya</span></p><p>nindāvyārosa-upārambhabhaya</p><p>mfn. fear of fault-finding, insult and criticism.</p>

nindāvyārosana-upārambhabhaya|nindaavyaarosana-upaarambhabhaya|nindavyarosana-uparambhabhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nindaavyaarosana-upaarambhabhaya</span></p><p>nindāvyārosana-upārambhabhaya</p><p>neuter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nindāvyārosa-upārambhabhaya">nindāvyārosa-upārambhabhaya</a></p>

ninhāta|ninhaata|ninhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninhaata</span></p><p>washed, cleansed.</p>

ninhāta|ninhaata|ninhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninhaata</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%87h%C4%81ta">niṇhāta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṇhāta">niṇhāta</a></p>

ninhātapāpaka|ninhaatapaapaka|ninhatapapaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninhaatapaapaka</span></p><p>cleansed off evil.</p>

ninhāya|ninhaaya|ninhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninhaaya</span></p><p>washing; cleansing.</p>

ninna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>low ground; a hollow, a depression</p><p>mfn.</p><ol><li>low, low-lying, sunken; deep.</li><li>bending towards; inclined towards.</li></ol>

ninna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) bent down (cp ninnata), low-lying, deep, low, sunken Ja.ii.3 (magga) Pv-a.29 (bhūmibhāga), Pv-a.132 (ṭhāṇa); esp. freq. as-˚ bent on, inclining to, leading to, aiming at, flowing into etc. Often combined with similar expressions in chain <em>taccarita tabbahula taggaruka tanninna tappoṇa tappabbhāra tadādhimutta</em> (with variation nibbāna˚ viveka˚ etc. for tad˚): Nd ii.under tad; Ja.ii.15; Pts.ii.197- Vin.ii.237 = AN.iv.198 (samuddo anupubba˚ etc.) AN.iv.224 (viveka˚); AN.v.175 (id.); MN.i.493 (Nibbāna˚) Similarly: samudda˚ Gangā MN.i.493; nekkhamma Ja.i.45 (verse 258); samādhi˚ Mil.38.</li><li>(acc. as adv. downward: ninnaṃ pavattati to flow downward MN.i.117; Pv.i.5#7; ninnagata running down Mil.259 (udaka); ninnaga Dāvs iv.28.</li><li>(nt.) low land, low ground, plain (opp. thala elevation, plateau): usually with ref. to a raincloud flooding the low country Snp.30 (mahamegho ˚ṃ pūrayaṃto); Snp-a.42 (= pallala); Iti.66 (megho ˚ṃ pūreti); Pv.ii.9#45 (megho ˚ṃ paripūrayanto).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://unnata">unnata</a></em> low lying &amp; elevated Mil.349 (desabhāga).</li></ul><p>Vedic nimna, der. fr. ni down, prob. combined with <em>-na</em> of <em>nam</em> to bend, thus meaning “bent down,” cp. unna &amp; panna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnata">unnata</a></p>

ninnata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective bent down, bent upon, in <em>ninnatattā</em> (fem. abstr.) aim, purpose (?) Dhs-a.39 (is the reading correct?).</p><p>ni + nata</p>

ninneta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ninnetar">ninnetar</a></p><p>one who ascertains, settles; a guide.</p>

ninnetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninneti">ninneti</a></p>

ninnetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ninnetar">ninnetar</a></p><p>one who ascertains, settles; a guide.</p>

ninnetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who leads down to, one who disposes of (c. gen.) bringer of, giver, usually in phrase atthassa n. (bringer of good: “Heilbringer”) of the Buddha SN.iv.94; MN.i.111; AN.v.226 sq., AN.v.256 sq.; Pts.ii.194.</p><p>n. ag. to ni-nayati = Sk. *ninayitṛ, cp. netar</p>

ninneti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>leads or takes away; takes out; drains; ascertains.</p>

ninneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lead down, lead away; drain, (udakaṃ), desiccate Vin.ii.180<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ninn%C4%ABta">ninnīta</a></em>, q.v.</p><p>Sk. ninayati, ni + nayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninnīta">ninnīta</a></p>

ninnetvā|ninnetvaa|ninnetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnetvaa</span></p><p>leads or takes away; takes out; drains; ascertains.</p>

ninnāda|ninnaada|ninnada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaada</span></p><p>(&amp; <em>Nināda</em> Miln, Dāvs) sounding forth, sound, tune, melody AN.ii.117 (˚sadda) Ja.vi.43; Vv-a.161; Mil.148; Dāvs v.31.</p><p>Sk. <em>nināda</em>, ni + nāda</p>

ninnādi|ninnaadi|ninnadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ninnādin">ninnādin</a></p><p>resounding, resonant; of deeper timber.</p>

ninnādin|ninnaadin|ninnadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ninnādin">ninnādin</a></p><p>resounding, resonant; of deeper timber.</p>

ninnādin|ninnaadin|ninnadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaadin</span></p><p>adjective sounding (loud), resonant (of a beautiful voice) DN.ii.211 (cp. aṭṭhanga brahmassara &amp; bindu).</p><p>fr. ninnāda</p>

ninnāmayati|ninnaamayati|ninnamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaamayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninnāmeti">ninnāmeti</a></p>

ninnāmeti|ninnaameti|ninnameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaameti</span></p><p>puts out (the tongue)</p>

ninnāmeti|ninnaameti|ninnameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaameti</span></p><p>to bend down, put out (the tongue) DN.i.106 (jivhaṃ = nīharati DN-a.i.276) Ja.i.163, Ja.i.164; cp. Divy.7, Divy.71 (nirṇāmayati).</p><p>Caus. of ni + namati</p>

ninnāmetvā|ninnaametvaa|ninnametva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaametvaa</span></p><p>of ninnāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninnāmeti">ninnāmeti</a></p>

ninnāmin|ninnaamin|ninnamin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninnaamin</span></p><p>adjective bending downwards, descending AN.iv.237.</p><p>fr. ni + <em>nam</em></p>

ninnīta|ninniita|ninnita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninniita</span></p><p>washed off; cleaned off.</p>

ninnīta|ninniita|ninnita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninniita</span></p><p>adjective lead down, lead away; drained, purified, free from (˚-) AN.i.254 (ninnīta-kasāva of gold: free fr. dross).</p><p>pp. of ninneti</p>

ninnītakasāva|ninniitakasaava|ninnitakasava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ninniitakasaava</span></p><p>with impurities cleaned off.</p>

nipa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.v.6 read as <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABpa">nīpa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīpa">nīpa</a></p>

nipacc-ākāra|nipacc-aakaara|nipacc-akara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipacc-aakaara</span></p><p>Nipacc-ākāra</p><p>obedience, humbleness, service SN.i.178; SN.v.233; AN.v.66; Ja.i.232 Ja.iv.133; Vv-a.22, Vv-a.320; Pv-a.12. Nipacca-vadin</p><p>nipacca, ger. of nipatati + ākāra</p>

nipacca-vādin|nipacca-vaadin|nipacca-vadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipacca-vaadin</span></p><p>Nipacca-vādin</p><p>adjective speaking hurtfully Snp.217 (= dāyakaṃ nipātetvā appiyavacanāni vattā Snp-a.272).</p><p>nipacca, ger. of nipāteti + vādin</p>

nipaccakāra|nipaccakaara|nipaccakara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaccakaara</span></p><p>humbleness; obedience; respect.</p>

nipaccavādi|nipaccavaadi|nipaccavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaccavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipaccavādin">nipaccavādin</a></p><p>speaking humbly; speaking hurtfully?</p>

nipaccavādin|nipaccavaadin|nipaccavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaccavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipaccavādin">nipaccavādin</a></p><p>speaking humbly; speaking hurtfully?</p>

nipaccākāra|nipaccaakaara|nipaccakara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaccaakaara</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipaccakāra">nipaccakāra</a></p>

nipadāmase|nipadaamase|nipadamase
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipadaamase</span></p><p>at Ja.iii.120 is an old misreading &amp; is to be corrected into; <em>nipatāmase</em> (= let us gather, bring together = dedicate), unless it be read as <em>nipphadāmase</em> (= do, set forth, prepare, give), in spite of Com. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> p. 121: nikārapakārā (= nipaccakārā?) upasaggā (upasajja?) dāmase (<em>dā</em>) ti attho; endorsed by Müller, <em>P.G.</em> p. 97 &amp; Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p.175. It cannot be ni + pa dāmase, since ni is <em>never</em> used as secondary (modifying verb-component (see <em>ni</em>˚ A 2), &amp; Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is popular etym. Cp. nipatāmase at Ja.iv.361 (see <em><a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i></p><p>lies down.</p>

nipajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lie down (to sleep) DN.i.246; AN.iv.332; Ja.i.150; Dhp-a.i.40; Pv-a.280; aor <em><a href="bword://nipajji">nipajji</a></em> Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.154; Ja.iii.83; Vv-a.75, Vv-a.76; Pv-a.74, Pv-a.75 Pv-a.93; ger. <strong>nipajja</strong> Ja.i.7 (Ja.v.44: ˚ṭṭhānacankama)<br />caus <strong>nipajjāpeti</strong> to lay down, deposit Ja.i.50, Ja.i.253, Ja.i.267 Ja.iii.26, Ja.iii.188; Dhp-a.i.50; Vv-a.76 (˚etvā rakkhāpetha) Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. nipadyate, ni + pajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajji">nipajji</a>, nipajja, nipajjāpeti</p>

nipajjaṃ|nipajja.m|nipajjam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajja.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nipajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipajjisāmi|nipajjisaami|nipajjisami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajjisaami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjaṃ">nipajjaṃ</a></p>

nipajjitvā|nipajjitvaa|nipajjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajjitvaa</span></p><p>of nipajjati having laid down; having slept</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipajjiṃsu|nipajji.msu|nipajjimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajji.msu</span></p><p>of nipajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipajjā|nipajjaa|nipajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajjaa</span></p><p>lying down.</p>

nipajjāpetvā|nipajjaapetvaa|nipajjapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipajjaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable intelligent; clever; adept.</p>

nipaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective intelligent, clever, prudent, wise SN.i.13, SN.i.52, SN.i.187; MN.i.339; AN.i.165 (+ jhāyin); AN.iii.24, AN.iii.138; Snp.45≈ Dhp.328≈ Dhp-a.i.62; Snp.283, Snp.962, Snp.1038; Cnd.349 (= jātimā = Mnd.478; Bv.i.49; Vb.426; Mil.34, Mil.342, Mil.411 Vism.3 (def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->).</p><p>cp. BSk. nipaka chief, fr. Sk. nipa, chief, master</p>

nipakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Vin.i.200 read <em><a href="bword://nippakka">nippakka</a></em>. Nipacc-akara</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippakka">nippakka</a></p>

nipalāvita|nipalaavita|nipalavita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipalaavita</span></p><p>past participle (Com. reading for vipalāvita text) made to swim, immersed, thrown into water Ja.i.326.</p><p>Sk. viplāvita, see plavati</p>

nipanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nipajjati lying down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipajjati">nipajjati</a></p>

nipanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective lying down Ja.i.151, Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.103; Ja.iii.276 (˚kāle while he was asleep), Ja.iv.167; Pv-a.43, Pv-a.75, Pv-a.265 (spelt nippanna, opp. nikujja).</p><p>pp. of nipajjati</p>

nipannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = nipanna Pts.ii.209; Ja.i.151.</p>

nipannokasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>the place where (someone) lies or is lying down.</p>

nipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipatat">nipatat</a></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipatanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipatat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipatat">nipatat</a></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipatati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><ol><li>falls down or off; falls down into; sinks; falls upon; falls down before.</li><li>flies down; alights; comes down; settles; sits down; lies down.</li><li>comes upon; invites, entertains.</li></ol>

nipatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(<em>intrs</em>.) to fall down, fly down, descend, go out Vin.ii.192 (Bhagavato pādesu sirasā n. bending his head at the feet of Bh.); Pv-a.60 (id.); Ja.i.278; Ja.v.467 (nippatissāmi = nikkhamissāmi Com.) Pv.ii.8#9 (variant reading BB parivisayitvā) = nikkhamitvā Pv-a.109 (cp. nippatati).</li><li>(trs.) to bring together to convene, in nipatāmase (pres. subj.) “shall we convene” Ja.iv.361. See also nipadāmase<br />■ Cp. abhi˚ san˚.</li></ol><p>Sk. nipatati, ni + patati</p>

nipatitvā|nipatitvaa|nipatitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipatitvaa</span></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipatī|nipatii|nipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipatii</span></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nippabha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without splendour Ja.ii.415; Mil.102.</p><p>nis + prabhā</p>

nippacati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>cooks; infuses.</p>

nippaci
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cooks; infuses.</p>

nippadesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in instr. &amp; abl. = separately Dhs-a.2, Dhs-a.30, Dhs-a.37, Dhs-a.297.</p><p>Sk. *niṣpradesa, nis + padesa</p>

nippadā|nippadaa|nippada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippadaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphadā">nipphadā</a></p>

nippadā|nippadaa|nippada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippadaa</span></p><p>? at SN.i.225 read nipphādā (q.v.).</p>

nippajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nippajjati &amp; Nipphajjati</p><p>to be produced, be accomplished, spring forth, ripen result, happen Dhp-a.ii.4 (pph); Pv-a.19 (= upakappati), Pv-a.71 (phalaṃ ijjhati n.), Pv-a.120 (id.). pp. <em><a href="bword://nipphanna">nipphanna</a></em>. See also nipphādeti &amp; nipphatti etc.; cp. also abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. niṣpadyate, nis + pajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphanna">nipphanna</a></p>

nippakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>cooked</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippacati">nippacati</a></p>

nippakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective boiled, infused Vin.i.200.</p><p>nis + pakka</p>

nippakāra|nippakaara|nippakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippakaara</span></p><p>adjective of no flavour, tasteless, useless Ja.i.340.</p><p>nis + pakāra 2</p>

nippalibodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without hindrances, unobstructed Mil.11.</p><p>nis + palibodha</p>

nippalāpa|nippalaapa|nippalapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippalaapa</span></p><p>adjective free from prattle or talk, not talking AN.ii.183 (apalāpa + ; variant reading ˚palāsa).</p><p>nis + palāpa</p>

nippanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nipanna">nipanna</a></em> &amp; nipphanna.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipanna">nipanna</a></p>

nippapañca|nippapa~nca|nippapanca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippapa~nca</span></p><p>adjective free from diffuseness SN.iv.370; Dhp.254 (Tathāgata); <em>-ārāma</em> not fond of delay MN.i.65 (Neumann trsl. i.119: “dem keine Sonderheit behagt”); AN.iii.431; AN.iv.229 sq.; Mil.262.</p><p>nis + papañca</p>

nippariyāya|nippariyaaya|nippariyaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippariyaaya</span></p><ol><li>without distinction or difference, absence of explanation or demonstration Dhs-a.317 (˚ena not figuratively), Dhs-a.403 (˚desanā); Vv-a.320.</li><li>unchangeable, not to be turned Mil.113, Mil.123 Mil.212.</li></ol><p>nis + pariyāya</p>

nippatati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>flies out, rushes out; falls out; departs, hastens away.</p>

nippatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nippatati &amp; Nipphatati</p><p>to fall out; rush out, come forth, go out from (c. abl.) Vin.ii.151 (nipphaṭati variant reading nippaṭati); Ja.v.467 (= nikkhamati Com.; or is it nipatati?)<br />ger <em>nippacca</em> (cp. BSk. nirpatya Avs.i.209).</p><p>nis + patati</p>

nippati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nippatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippatati">nippatati</a></p>

nippatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nippatati flown out; hastened away</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippatati">nippatati</a></p>

nippatitvā|nippatitvaa|nippatitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippatitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippatati">nippatati</a></p>

nippatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>without wings, plucked (of a bird) Vin.iv.259.</li><li>without leaves Ja.iii.496 (= patita-patta); Snp-a.117 (˚puppha)<br />■ <em>Note</em> nippatta at Dhs.1035 is to be read as <em><a href="bword://nibbatta">nibbatta</a></em>.</li></ol><p>nis + patta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbatta">nibbatta</a></p>

nippatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nipphatti">nipphatti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphatti">nipphatti</a></p>

nippañña|nippa~n~na|nippanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippa~n~na</span></p><p>adjective unwise, foolish Pv-a.40, Pv-a.41 (= dummati).</p><p>nis + pañña</p>

nippaṭati|nippa.tati|nippatati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippa.tati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphaṭati">nipphaṭati</a></p>

nippesika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>one who solicits gifts in an inappropriate way; (according to commentaries : one who grinds or scrapes away, who belittles or denies the good qualities of others for his own advantage)</p>

nippesika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who performs jugglery, a juggler DN.i.8 (= nippeso sīlaṃ etesan ti DA.i.91); AN.iii.111.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣpeṣa clashing against, bounce, shock, niṣ + <em>piṣ</em></p>

nippesikatā|nippesikataa|nippesikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippesikataa</span></p><p>soliciting gifts in an inappropriate way; (according to commentaries : by grinding away, belittling or denying others’ good qualities)</p>

nippesikatā|nippesikataa|nippesikata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippesikataa</span></p><p>jugglery, trickery (cp. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 176) Vb.353 (explained at Vism.29); Mil.383.</p><p>abstr. fr. prec.</p>

nipphadā|nipphadaa|nipphada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphadaa</span></p><p>, abstr. accomplishment; completion.</p>

nipphajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(or ˚nā f.?) resulting, procedure, achievement, plot Ja.iv.83.</p><p>n. abstr. fr. nipp(h)ajjati</p>

nipphajjati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nipphajjati</p><p>see <em>nippajjati</em>.</p>

nipphajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nippajjati &amp; Nipphajjati</p><p>to be produced, be accomplished, spring forth, ripen result, happen Dhp-a.ii.4 (pph); Pv-a.19 (= upakappati), Pv-a.71 (phalaṃ ijjhati n.), Pv-a.120 (id.). pp. <em><a href="bword://nipphanna">nipphanna</a></em>. See also nipphādeti &amp; nipphatti etc.; cp. also abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. niṣpadyate, nis + pajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphanna">nipphanna</a></p>

nipphala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without fruit, barren in <em>a˚</em>; not without fruit, i.e. amply rewarded (dāyaka, the giver of good gifts) Pv.i.4#2; Pv.i.5#5, Pv-a.194; Sdhp.504.</p><p>nis + phala</p>

nipphalita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective broken out, split open Ja.i.493 (lasī = nikkhantā Com.; variant reading nipphaḷita).</p><p>Sk. niṣphārita, pp. of nipphaleti, nis + phaleti</p>

nipphanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective accomplished, perfected, trained SN.i.215 (˚sobhin, spelt nippanna) Ja.iv.39 (˚sippa master of the art, M.A.); Dhp-a.iii.285 (sasse); Dhs-a.316; in phil. determined, conditioned Kvu xi.7; xxiii.5; Vism.450; <em>Pts. of Controversy</em>, 395 Cp. abhi˚, pari˚. See also <em>Cpd.</em> 156, 157.</p><p>pp. of nippajjati</p>

nipphannasobhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>splendid when accomplished.</p>

nipphannasobhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipphannasobhin">nipphannasobhin</a></p><p>splendid when accomplished.</p>

nipphannasobhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nipphannasobhin">nipphannasobhin</a></p><p>splendid when accomplished.</p>

nipphatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nippatati &amp; Nipphatati</p><p>to fall out; rush out, come forth, go out from (c. abl.) Vin.ii.151 (nipphaṭati variant reading nippaṭati); Ja.v.467 (= nikkhamati Com.; or is it nipatati?)<br />ger <em>nippacca</em> (cp. BSk. nirpatya Avs.i.209).</p><p>nis + patati</p>

nipphatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>result, accomplishment, effect, end, completion, perfection Ja.i.56, Ja.i.335 (of dreams), Ja.i.343, Ja.i.456; Ja.iv.137 (sippe); Ja.vi.36; Vv-a.138 (sippa˚); Dhp-a.ii.6 (import, meaning, of a vision); Dhs-a.354; Pv-a.122, Pv-a.282 (sippe); Ne.54. Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣpatti</p>

nipphattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having a result Ja.iii.166 (evaṃ˚ of such consequence).</p><p>fr. nipphatti</p>

nipphaṭati|nippha.tati|nipphatati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippha.tati</span></p><p>moves from its place; falls.</p>

nipphotayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>shakes; shakes to and fro; (beats; ?)</p>

nipphotenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphoṭeti">nipphoṭeti</a></p>

nipphoteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>dashes down; crushes.</p>

nipphoṭana|nippho.tana|nipphotana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippho.tana</span></p><p>beating SN.iv.300 (variant reading ṭh.). Cp. nippothana.</p><p>nis + pothanā</p>

nipphoṭenta|nippho.tenta|nipphotenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippho.tenta</span></p><p>nipphoṭenta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>part. pr. mfn. of nipphoṭeti</p><p>nipphoṭenta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>part. pr. mfn. of nipphoṭeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphoṭeti">nipphoṭeti</a>, <a href="bword://nipphoṭeti">nipphoṭeti</a></p>

nipphoṭeti|nippho.teti|nipphoteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippho.teti</span></p><p>shakes; shakes to and fro.</p>

nipphoṭeti|nippho.teti|nipphoteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippho.teti</span></p><p>to beat down, smother, crush SN.i.101, SN.i.102.</p><p>nis + potheti</p>

nipphādaka|nipphaadaka|nipphadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaadaka</span></p><p>adjective producing, accomplishing Dhs-a.47; Pv-a.147 (sukha-˚ṃ puññaṃ).</p><p>fr. nipphādeti</p>

nipphādana|nipphaadana|nipphadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaadana</span></p><p>accomplishment Mil.356; DN-a.i.195.</p><p>Sk. niṣpādana, to nipphādeti</p>

nipphādar|nipphaadar|nipphadar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaadar</span></p><p>one who produces or gains SN.i.225 (atthassa; read nipphādā nom. for nippadā).</p><p>n. ag. = Sk. niṣpādayitṛ cp. nipphāditar</p>

nipphādeti|nipphaadeti|nipphadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaadeti</span></p><p>to bring forth, produce; accomplish, perform Ja.i.185 (lābhasakkāraṃ); Ja.v.81; Mil.299; Vv-a.32, Vv-a.72 (grd. nipphādetabba, Name of ablative case); Sdhp.319, Sdhp.426<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nipph%C4%81dita">nipphādita</a></em>. Cp abhinipphādeti.</p><p>Caus. of nippajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphādita">nipphādita</a></p>

nipphādita|nipphaadita|nipphadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaadita</span></p><p>(having) produced, producing (perhaps = nipphāditar) Vv-a.113.</p><p>pp. of nipphādeti</p>

nipphāditar|nipphaaditar|nipphaditar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaaditar</span></p><p>one who produces or accomplishes Pv-a.8 (read “so nipphāditā” for sā nipphādikā). Cp. <em><a href="bword://nipph%C4%81dita">nipphādita</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://nipph%C4%81daka">nipphādaka</a></em>.</p><p>n. ag. to nipphādeti, cp. nipphādar</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphādita">nipphādita</a>, <a href="bword://nipphādaka">nipphādaka</a></p>

nipphāṇitatta|nipphaa.nitatta|nipphanitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipphaa.nitatta</span></p><p>state of being free from sugar or molasses Ja.iii.409.</p><p>nis + phāṇita + tva</p>

nippipāsa|nippipaasa|nippipasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippipaasa</span></p><p>adjective without thirst or desire Snp.56; Cnd.351.</p><p>nis + pipāsā</p>

nippitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective a bastard Ja.i.133 (variant reading nippītika q.v.).</p><p>Sk. *niṣpaitṛka = fatherless or *niṣprītika?</p>

nippothana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>crushing, beating, destroying Snp-a.390.</p><p>nis + pothana of <em>puth</em> to crush</p>

nippoṭheti|nippo.theti|nippotheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippo.theti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphoṭeti">nipphoṭeti</a></p>

nippurisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>without men Pv-a.177.</li><li>without men, executed by females (female devas only (of turiyā = a female orchestra) Vin.i.15; DN.ii.21; Ja.v.506. Cp. Mvu.iii.165 (niṣpuruṣena nāṭakena &amp; Avs.i.321 (niṣpuruṣena tūryeṇa; see also note in Index p. 229), whereas Divy.3 (see Index) has niṣparuṣa (soft), with variant reading niṣpuruṣa.</li></ol><p>nis + purisa</p>

nippādeti|nippaadeti|nippadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippaadeti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nipph%C4%81deti">nipphādeti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipphādeti">nipphādeti</a></p>

nippāpa|nippaapa|nippapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippaapa</span></p><p>adjective free from sin Snp.257 = Dhp.205.</p><p>nis + pāpa</p>

nippātesi|nippaatesi|nippatesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippaatesi</span></p><p>of nippatita</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippatita">nippatita</a></p>

nippātita|nippaatita|nippatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippaatita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippatita">nippatita</a></p>

nippītika|nippiitika|nippitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippiitika</span></p><p>not involving or accompanied by joy.</p>

nippītika|nippiitika|nippitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippiitika</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>free from (feelings of) enjoyment (characteristic of 3rd jhāna, q.v.) DN.i.75; AN.i.81.</li><li>being unloved, a foster child etc. (?) see <em><a href="bword://nippitika">nippitika</a></em>.</li></ol><p>nis + pīti + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippitika">nippitika</a></p>

nippīḷana|nippii.lana|nippilana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippii.lana</span></p><p>squeezing; pressing; pressing against.</p>

nippīḷana|nippii.lana|nippilana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippii.lana</span></p><p>squeezing, pressing; a blow Ja.iii.160. Cp. abhinippīḷanā.</p><p>nis + pīḷana</p>

nippīḷanā|nippii.lanaa|nippilana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippii.lanaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippīḷana">nippīḷana</a></p>

nippīḷeti|nippii.leti|nippileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippii.leti</span></p><p>to squeeze, press, clench, urge Ja.i.63, Ja.i.223. Pass. <em>nippīḷiyati</em>, only in ppr. <em>nippīḷiyamāna</em> being urged Vin.ii.303; Vv-a.138; Pv-a.31, Pv-a.192. Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>nis + pīḷeti</p>

nippīḷiyamāna|nippii.liyamaana|nippiliyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nippii.liyamaana</span></p><p>presses; squeezes.</p>

nipuṇa|nipu.na|nipuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipu.na</span></p><ol><li>clever; skillful; subtle; acute.</li><li>(what is) subtle, abstruse.</li></ol>

nipuṇa|nipu.na|nipuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipu.na</span></p><p>adjective clever, skilful, accomplished; fine, subtle, abstruse DN.i.26≈(n. gambhīra dhamma), DN.i.162 (paṇḍita +); MN.i.487 (dhamma); SN.i.33; SN.iv.369; AN.iii.78; Snp.1126 (= gambhīra duddasa etc. Cnd.350); Vb.426; Mil.233, Mil.276; DN-a.i.117; Vv-a.73 (ariyasaccesu kusala +) Vv-a.232; Pv-a.1, Pv-a.16. Cp. abhinipuṇa.</p><p>Sk. nipuṇa, dial. for nipṛṇa, to pṛṇoti, <em>pṛ</em>.</p>

nipāka|nipaaka|nipaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaaka</span></p><p>adjective full grown, fully developed, in full strength Ja.vi.327 (of a tree).</p><p>Sk. nipāka, ni + pāka (pacati)</p>

nipāta|nipaata|nipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaata</span></p><ol><li>fall, falling; descent; settling.</li><li>a section of a book.</li></ol>

nipāta|nipaata|nipata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaata</span></p><ol><li>falling down Dhp.121 (udabindu˚); Vv-a.279 (diṭṭhi˚, a glance) Pv-a.45 (asa˚).</li><li>descending MN.i.453.</li><li>a particle the gram. term for adverbs, conjunctions &amp; interjections Ja.v.243 (assu); Pv-a.11 (mā), Pv-a.26 (vo), Pv-a.40 (taṃ), Pv-a.50 (ca).</li><li>a section of a book (see next). Cp. vi˚ san˚.</li></ol><p>Sk. nipāta, ni + pāta, of nipatati</p>

nipātaka|nipaataka|nipataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaataka</span></p><p>adjective divided into sections or chapters Dpvs.iv.16.</p><p>to nipāta</p>

nipātana|nipaatana|nipatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatana</span></p><ol><li>falling upon Dhp-a.i.295.</li><li>going to bed Vv-a.71 (pacchā˚ opp. pubbuṭṭhāna) Cp. nipātin.</li></ol><p>to nipatati</p>

nipātayati|nipaatayati|nipatayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipāteti">nipāteti</a></p>

nipātenta|nipaatenta|nipatenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatenta</span></p><p>of nipāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipāteti">nipāteti</a></p>

nipātesi|nipaatesi|nipatesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatesi</span></p><p>of nipāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipāteti">nipāteti</a></p>

nipātesuṃ|nipaatesu.m|nipatesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatesu.m</span></p><p>of nipāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipāteti">nipāteti</a></p>

nipāteti|nipaateti|nipateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaateti</span></p><ol><li>makes fall down (on), makes lie down; lowers; throws down; fixes, fixes (the teeth) in; casts upon, imputes; (brings together; ?)</li><li>inlays, embosses.</li><li>sets down as a special or irregular form.</li></ol>

nipāteti|nipaateti|nipateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaateti</span></p><p>to let fall, throw down into (c. loc.); bring to fall, injure; fig. cast upon, charge with DN.i.91; MN.i.453 (ayokaṭāhe); Ja.iii.359; Snp-a.272; Pv-a.152 (bhūmiyaṃ). pp. <strong>nipātita</strong> corrupt, evil wicked Vin.ii.182 (caṇḍa + ; text nippātita, variant reading nipphātita).</p><p>ni + Caus. of patati</p><p>See also: nipātita</p>

nipātetvā|nipaatetvaa|nipatetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatetvaa</span></p><p>of nipatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nipatati">nipatati</a></p>

nipātin|nipaatin|nipatin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipaatin</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>falling or flying down, chancing upon Dhp.35, Dhp.36 (yatthakāma˚ cittaṃ = yattha yattha icchati tattha tatth’ eva nipatati Dhp-a.i.295).</li><li>going to bed DN.i.60 (pacchā˚ going to bed late). Cp. abhi˚.</li></ol><p>to nipatati</p>

nipīlana|nipiilana|nipilana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nipiilana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nippīḷana">nippīḷana</a></p>

nirabbuda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A hell, or a period of suffering. Equal to twenty Abbudas. Snp.p.126 SN.i.149</p><p>40.25264,58.439415hell</p>

nirabbuda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from (latent) disposition.</p>

nirabbuda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nirabbuda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter a vast number; also Name of a hell SN.i.149 = AN.ii.3 = AN.v.171 (explained at AN.v.173 as “seyyathā pi vīsati abbudā nirayā evam eko nirabbudo nirayo”); Ja.iii.360 (Com.: vīsati abbudāni ekaṃ nirabbudaṃ).</p><p>cp. BSk. nirarbuda &amp; abbuda 3</p><p>Nirabbuda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective free from boils or tumours, healthy (see <em>fig.</em>) Vin.iii.18 (of the Sangha).</p><p>nis + abbuda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: fig.</p>

nirabbudda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. (metaph.)</i></p><p>free com cancers, healthy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbudda">abbudda</a></p>

niragamā|niragamaa|niragama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niragamaa</span></p><p>went out; left; came out, emerged, appeared.</p>

niraggala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(niraggaḷa) adjective unobstructed, free, rich in result SN.i.76 = Iti.21; AN.ii.43; AN.iv.151; MN.i.139; Snp.303; Cnd.284 C<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; Vv.64#31 (= Vv-a.285).</p><p>nis + aggala</p>

niraggaḷa|niragga.la|niraggala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niragga.la</span></p><p>A sacrifice in which alms are given with wide open, boltless doors.</p>

niraggaḷa|niragga.la|niraggala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niragga.la</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) unbarred, unimpeded; free.</li><li>(mfn. &amp; n.) unlimited (of a gift or sacrifice); the name of a brahmanical sacrifice.</li></ol>

niraggika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without fire Mil.324 (˚okāsa).</p><p>nis + aggi + ka</p>

nirajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be thrown out, to be expelled, to lose (with abl.) Ja.vi.502, Ja.vi.503 (raṭṭhā); variant reading BB nirajhati Com. ni(g)gacchati; Thig.93 (aor. nirajji ‘haṃ = na jānim ahaṃ Thag-a.90. Kern (wrongly) proposes reading virajjhi).</p><p>Pass. of nirajati, nis + ajati, Vedic nirajati to drive out cattle</p>

nirantara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having no interval, continuous, uninterrupted Pv-a.135. Usually in nt. as adv nirantaraṃ always, incessantly, constantly; immediately at once Dhs-a.168; Pv-a.52, Pv-a.80, Pv-a.107, Pv-a.110 (= satataṃ) Pv-a.120; Dhp-a.i.13.</p><p>nis + antara</p>

niranusaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>adverb free from (latent) disposition.</p>

niraparādha|niraparaadha|niraparadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraparaadha</span></p><p>adjective without offence, guiltless, innocent Ja.i.264.</p><p>nis + aparādha</p>

nirapekkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective not heeding, unsuspecting, disregarding, indifferent, reckless Vv-a.27, Vv-a.47 (jīvitaṃ); Pv-a.62; DN-a.i.177; Mil.343 (jīvitaṃ).</p><p>nis + apa + <em>īkṣ</em></p>

nirasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without food or subsistence, poor Ja.iv.128.</p><p>nis + asana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nirasassāda|nirasassaada|nirasassada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirasassaada</span></p><p>not bringing enjoyment; without relish; listless.</p>

nirassajati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>lets go.</p>

nirassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws off; lets go; drives away.</p>

nirassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to throw off, despise, neglect Snp.785, Snp.954; Mnd.76 (so read for nidassati, variant reading SS nir˚), Mnd.444; Snp-a.522<br />pp <em>niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirasyati, nis + assati, <em>as</em> to throw</p>

nirassāda|nirassaada|nirassada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirassaada</span></p><p>adjective without taste, insipid, dull Vism.135. Cp. nirāsāda.</p><p>nis + assāda</p>

nirata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>delighting in; attached, devoted to; intent upon.</p>

nirata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective fond of, attached to (-˚) SN.i.133; DN-a.i.250; Pv-a.5 (duccarita˚), Pv-a.89, Pv-a.161 (hitakaraṇa˚).</p><p>pp. of niramati</p>

niratta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. the lack of an enduring self; ? the letting go (of an enduring self); ?</p><p>niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter thrown off; let go.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirassati">nirassati</a></p>

niratta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective noun soulless; view of soullessness or unsubstantiality; thus interpreted (in preference to niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) by Com. on Snp.787 Snp.858, Snp.919. See foll.</p><p>Sk. *nirātman, nis + attan</p><p>Niratta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective rejected, thrown off, given up Snp.1098; Cnd.359. <em>Note.</em> At Snp.787, Snp.858, Snp.919 the interpretation of Mnd.82 Mnd.248 = Mnd.352 and also Bdhgh assume a cpd. of nis + attan (= nirātman): see *<a href="bword://niratta">niratta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>Sk. nirasta, pp. of nirasyati, see <em><a href="bword://nirassati">nirassati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niratta">niratta</a>, <a href="bword://nirassati">nirassati</a></p>

nirattan
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nirattan">nirattan</a></p><p>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niratta">niratta</a></p>

nirattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>unprofitable, useless</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://attha">attha</a></p>

nirattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective useless, groundless, unproficient, vain (opp. sāttha profitable) Snp.582 (nt. as adv.), Snp.585 (niratthā paridevanā); Dhp.41; Ja.iii.26; Pv-a.18 (˚bhāva uselessness), Pv-a.83 (= duḥ).</p><p>nis + attha</p>

niratthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>unprofitable; useless; meaningless.</p>

niratthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = nirattha; Vv-a.324; Pv-a.18, Pv-a.40, Pv-a.63, Pv-a.102 etc<br />■ f. <em>-ikā</em> Thag-a.258; Mil.20; Sdhp.68.</p>

niratthakaṃ|niratthaka.m|niratthakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niratthaka.m</span></p><p>(~ā, ~ikā)n. useless; to no purpose.</p>

niravasesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without remainder, complete, inclusive Ne.14, Ne.15, cp. Mil.91, Mil.182.</p><p>nis + avasesa</p>

niraya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Hell. The Saṃyutta and Aṅguttara Nikāyas and the Sutta Nipāta contain a list of hells: Abbuda, Nirabbuda, Ababa, Aṭaṭa, Ahaha, Kumuda, Sogandhika, Uppala, Puṇḍarīka, Paduma. SN.i.149 AN.v.173 Snp.p.126</p><p>The Devadūta Sutta MN.iii.185 of the Majjhima Nikāya contains another list: Gūtha, Kukkuḷa, Simbalivana, Asipattavana and Khārodakanadī.</p><p>40.25264,58.439415hell</p>

niraya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.m. a</i></p><p>place or state of punishment and torture (after death); hell, a hell.</p>

niraya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>purgatory, hell, a place of punishment &amp; torture, where sin is atoned (i.e. kamma ripens = paccati, is literally boiled) by terrible ordeals (kāraṇāni) similar to &amp; partly identical with those of Hades &amp; Tartarus. There are a great number of hells, of which the most fearful is the; <em>Avīcimahāniraya</em> (see <em>Avīci</em>). Names of other purgatories occur frequently in the <em>Jātaka</em> collection, e.g. Kākola Ja.vi.247; Khuradhāra Ja.v.269 sq.; Dhūma-roruva Ja.v.271 Patāpana Ja.v.266, Ja.v.271, Ja.v.453; Paduma Ja.iv.245; Roruva Ja.iii.299; Ja.v.266; Ja.vi.237; Sanghāta Ja.v.266; Sañjīva ibid. Sataporisa Ja.v.269; Sattisūla Ja.v.143. As the principal one n. is often mentioned with the other apāyas (states of suffering), viz. tiracchānayoni (animal world) &amp; pittivisaya (the; <em>manes</em>), e.g. at Mnd.489; Cnd.517, Cnd.550; Pv.iv.11; Thag-a.282; Pv-a.27 sq. (see <em><a href="bword://ap%C4%81ya">apāya</a></em>)<br />■ There is a great variety of qualifying adjectives connected with niraya, all of which abound in notions of fearful pain, awful misery &amp; continuous suffering, e.g. kaṭuka ghora, dāruṇa, bhayānaka, mahābhitāpa, sattussada etc<br />■ Descriptions of N. in glowing terms of frightfulness are freq. found from the earliest books down to the late Peta-Vatthu, Pañcagati-dīpana &amp; Saddhammopāyana Of these the foll. may be quoted as characteristic: SN.i.152 (10 nirayas); MN.iii.183; AN.i.141 Snp.p.126 = AN.v.173; Mnd.404 sq. = Cnd.304#iii.c; Ja.iv.4 (Mittavindaka); Vv.52 (Revatī); Pv.i.10; Pv.iii.10; Pv.iv.1 Pv.iv.7; Dhp-a.i.148<br />■ See on the whole subject, esp. L Scherman, <em>Materialen zur indischen Visionsliteratur</em>, Leipzig 1792; &amp; W. Stede, <em>Die Gespenstergeschichten des Peta Vatthu</em>, Leipzig 1914, pp. 33–⁠39<br />■ <em>References:</em> Vin.i.227 (apāya duggati vinipāta niraya); DN.i.82, DN.i.107 (id.); Vin.ii.198 (yo kho sanghaṃ bhindati kappaṃ nirayamhi paccati), Vin.ii.204; Vin.ii.203 = Iti.86; DN.i.228 (+ tiracchānayoni), DN.i.54 (read nirayasate for niriyasate); DN.iii.111; SN.iv.126; SN.v.356, SN.v.450; MN.i.73, MN.i.285, MN.i.308, MN.i.334; MN.ii.86, MN.ii.149 MN.ii.186; MN.iii.166, MN.iii.203, MN.iii.209; AN.iv.405; AN.v.76, AN.v.182, AN.v.184; Snp.248 (patanti sattā nirayaṃ avaṃsirā), Snp.333, Snp.660 sq., Snp.677 sq. Dhp.126, Dhp.140, Dhp.306, Dhp.311, Dhp.315; Thag.304 (adhammo nirayaṃ neti dhammo pāpeti suggatiṃ) = Dhs-a.38 = DN-a.i.99 = Dhp-a.i.22; Thig.456; Iti.12; Ja.iv.463; Pp.60; Pts.i.83 (Avīci˚); Vb.86, Vb.337; Vism.102; Mil.148; Dhp-a.i.22; Dhp-a.iii.71; Sdhp.7, Sdhp.285<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://nerayika">nerayika</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-gāmin</em> (adj.) leading to purgatory (magga) Snp.277</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dukkha">dukkha</a></em> the pain of H. Snp.531;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81la">pāla</a></em> a guardian of P. a devil AN.i.138, AN.i.141; MN.iii.179; Mnd.404; Vv-a.226 Names of guardians (after their complexion) e.g. Kāḷa (black) &amp; Upakāḷa (blackish) Ja.vi.248.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhaya">bhaya</a></em> the fear of P. Ja.i.168; Vism.392;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83vattanika">saṃvattanika</a></em> conducive to P. Mnd.489.</li></ul><p>BSk. niraya, nis + aya of <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em> = to go asunder, to go to destruction, to die, cp. in meaning Vedic nirṛti. The popular etym. given by Dhammapāla at Pv-a.53 is “n’ atthi ettha ayo sukhan ti” = there is no good; that given by Bdhgh at Vism.427 “n’ atthi ettha assādasaññito ayo” (no refreshment)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apāya">apāya</a>, <a href="bword://nerayika">nerayika</a>, <a href="bword://dukkha">dukkha</a>, <a href="bword://pāla">pāla</a>, <a href="bword://bhaya">bhaya</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvattanika">saṃvattanika</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

nirayagāmanīya|nirayagaamaniiya|nirayagamaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirayagaamaniiya</span></p><p>leading to hell.</p>

nirayagāmi|nirayagaami|nirayagami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirayagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nirayagāmin">nirayagāmin</a></p><p>going to hell.</p>

nirayagāmin|nirayagaamin|nirayagamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirayagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nirayagāmin">nirayagāmin</a></p><p>going to hell.</p>

nirayapāla|nirayapaala|nirayapala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirayapaala</span></p><p>guard, a torturer in hell.</p>

niraṃkaroti|nira.mkaroti|niramkaroti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nira.mkaroti</span></p><p>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niraṅkaroti">niraṅkaroti</a></p>

niraṅkaroti|nira"nkaroti|nirankaroti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nira"nkaroti</span></p><p>separates oneself from; repudiates; rejects; removes.</p>

niraṅkaroti|nira"nkaroti|nirankaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nira"nkaroti</span></p><p>Niraṅkaroti &amp; nirākaroti</p><p>to think little of, despise, neglect, disregard, repudiate throw away, ruin, destroy Thag.478; Iti.83 (nirākare) Ja.iii.280 = Ja.v.498; Ja.iv.302; Pv.iii.9#6 (= chaḍḍeti pajahati Pv-a.211); Vv-a.109<br />pp <em>(a)nirākata</em> Iti.39.</p><p>Sk. nirākaroti, nis + ā <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

niraṅkata|nira"nkata|nirankata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nira"nkata</span></p><p>of niraṅkaroti repudiated, rejected; removed.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niraṅkaroti">niraṅkaroti</a></p>

nirindhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without fuel (of fire), Thag-a.148 (aggi); Dhp-a.i.44 (jātaveda). Niriha(ka)</p><p>nis + indhana</p>

niriñjana|niri~njana|nirinjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niri~njana</span></p><p>adjective not moving, stable, unshaken Vism.377 (= acala, āneñja).</p><p>nis + iñjanā, fr. iñjati</p>

nirodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>ceasing, cessation; the being no more; stopping, shutting off.</li><li>(for saññāvedayitanirodha) the cessation of conception and feeling.</li></ol>

nirodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>oppression, suppression; destruction, cessation annihilation (of senses, consciousness, feeling being in general: sankhārā). Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of the word is: “ni-saddo abhāvaṃ, rodha-saddo ca cārakaṃ dīpeti Vism.495<br />■ N. in many cases is synonymous with nibbāna &amp; parinibbāna; it may be said to be even a stronger expression as far as the <em>active</em> destruction of the causes of life is concerned. Therefore frequently combined with <em><a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> in formula “sabbasankhāra-samatho… virāgo nirodho nibbānaṃ,” e.g. SN.i.136; Iti.88. Nd ii.s. nibbāna (see <em><a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> iii.6). Also in combination with <em><a href="bword://nibbid%C4%81">nibbidā</a></em>, e.g. SN.iii.48, SN.iii.223; SN.iii.163 sq. SN.v.438<br />■ The opposite of nirodha is <em><a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a></em>, cp formula “yaṃ kiñci samudaya-dhammaṃ sabban taṃ nirodha-dhammaṃ” e.g. Nd ii.under sankhārā &amp; passim</p><ol><li>Vin.i.1, Vin.i.10; DN.ii.33, DN.ii.41, DN.ii.57 sq., DN.ii.112; DN.iii.130 sq. DN.iii.136 sq., DN.iii.226 sq.; Ja.i.133; Ja.ii.9 sq., Ja.ii.223; Ja.iii.59 sq., Ja.iii.163, Ja.v.438; MN.i.140, MN.i.263, MN.i.410; AN.i.299; AN.iv.456 (= āsavānaṃ parikkhaya); Thig.6 (= kilesanirodha Thag-a.13), Thag-a.158; Iti.46 = Snp.755 (nirodhe ye vimuccanti te janā maccuhāyino); Iti.62 = Snp.754; Snp.731, Snp.1037; Pts.i.192; Pts.ii.44 sq. Pts.ii.221; Pp.68; Vb.99 sq., Vb.229; Ne.14, Ne.16 sq.; Vism.372; Vv-a.63; Pv-a.220 (jīvitassa)</li><li>(as-˚): anupubba DN.iii.266; AN.iv.409, AN.iv.456; abhisaññā˚ DN.i.180; asesavirāga˚ SN.ii.4, SN.ii.12; SN.iv.86; SN.v.421 sq.; AN.i.177; AN.ii.158, AN.ii.161 upādāna˚ SN.iii.14; kāma˚ AN.iii.410 sq.; jāti˚ SN.iv.86 taṇhā˚ DN.iii.216; dukkha˚ DN.iii.136; SN.iii.32, SN.iii.60 SN.iv.4 sq., SN.iv.14, SN.iv.384; AN.i.177; nandi˚ SN.iii.14; SN.iv.36; bhava (= nibbāna) SN.ii.117; SN.iii.14; AN.v.9; Pts.i.159; sakkāya DN.iii.240; SN.v.410; AN.ii.165 sq.; AN.iii.246, AN.iii.325 sq. AN.v.238 sq.; saññāvedayita˚ DN.iii.262, DN.iii.266; SN.iv.217, SN.iv.293 sq.; SN.v.213) sq.; AN.i.41; AN.iii.192; AN.iv.306; AN.v.209.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> subject to destruction, able to be destroyed destructible (usually in formula of samudaya-dhamma see above) Vin.i.11; DN.i.110; SN.iv.47, SN.iv.107, SN.iv.214; MN.iii.280; AN.v.143 sq.;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhammat%C4%81">dhammatā</a></em> liability to destruction SN.iv.217</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the element or condition of annihilation, one of the 3 dhātus, viz. rūpa, arūpa˚ n˚. DN.iii.215; Iti.45 Ne.97;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81">saññā</a></em> perception or consciousness of annihilation DN.iii.251 sq., DN.iii.283; AN.iii.334;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sam%C4%81patti">samāpatti</a></em> attainment of annihilation Pts.i.97, Pts.i.100; Mil.300 Vism.702.</li></ul><p>BSk. nirodha, to nirundhati, cp. nirujjhati &amp; niruddha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a>, <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a>, <a href="bword://nibbidā">nibbidā</a>, <a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://dhammatā">dhammatā</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a>, <a href="bword://saññā">saññā</a>, <a href="bword://samāpatti">samāpatti</a></p>

nirodhadhamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>liable to cessation; inevitably ceasing.</p>

nirodhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>cessation; obstructing, making cease.</p>

nirodhetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>destroying; dissolving; annihilating.</p>

nirodheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to oppress, destroy Vism.288 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of passambheti).</p><p>Denom. fr. nirodha</p>

nirodhetvā|nirodhetvaa|nirodhetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp (mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirodhetvaa</span></p><p>having destroyed; having dissolved; having annihilated.</p>

nirodhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective obstructing, destroying Iti.82 (paññā˚), cp. MN.i.115.</p><p>fr. nirodha</p>

niroga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABroga">nīroga</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīroga">nīroga</a></p>

niroja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective tasteless, insipid Ja.ii.304; Ja.iii.94; Ja.vi.561.</p><p>nis + oja</p>

nirudaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without water, waterless MN.i.543; Cnd.630.</p><p>nis + udaka</p>

niruddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>ceased; no more</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirujjhati">nirujjhati</a></p>

niruddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>past participle expelled, destroyed; vanished, ceased SN.iii.112; Dhs.1038.</p><p>pp. of nirundhati, cp. nirujjhati</p>

nirujjhamāna|nirujjhamaana|nirujjhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirujjhamaana</span></p><p>of nirujjhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirujjhati">nirujjhati</a></p>

nirujjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirāsasa">nirāsasa</a></p>

nirujjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be broken up, to be dissolved, to be destroyed, to cease, die Vin.i.1; DN.i.180 sq., DN.i.215; DN.ii.157; SN.iii.93 (aparisesaṃ); SN.iv.36 sq., SN.iv.60, SN.iv.98, SN.iv.184 sq.; SN.iv.294, SN.iv.402; SN.v.213 sq.; AN.iii.165 sq. (aparisesaṃ); AN.v.139 sq. Ja.i.180; Pp.64; Sdhp.606<br />pp <em><a href="bword://niruddha">niruddha</a></em>. Cp nirodha.</p><p>Pass. of nirundhati (nirodhati) ni + rundhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niruddha">niruddha</a></p>

nirumbhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to suppress, hush, silence Ja.i.62 (text nirumhitvā, variant reading SS nirumbhitvā, cp. san-nirumhitvā Vv-a.217).</p><p>Sk.? Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> p. 59 ni + <em>rudh</em> (?)</p>

nirundhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nirujjhati">nirujjhati</a></em>, niruddha, nirodha &amp; nirodheti. Cp. parirundhati.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirujjhati">nirujjhati</a></p>

nirupaddava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without affliction or mishap, harmless, secure, happy Ja.iv.139; Pv-a.262 (sotthi).</p><p>nis + upaddava</p>

nirupadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>free from belongings; from free from attachment</p><p>See also: nirūpadhi, <a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></p>

nirupadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (in verse always <em>nirūpadhi</em>) free from passions or attachment, desireless controlled Vin.ii.156; SN.i.194 (vippamutta +), SN.iv.158; AN.i.80, AN.i.138 (sītibhūta +); Dhp.418 (id.); Thag.1250; Thag.2, Thag.320 (vippamutta + ; explained by niddukkha Thag-a.233); Iti.46, Iti.50, Iti.58, Iti.62; Snp.33, Snp.34, Snp.642 (sītibhūta +) Pv.iv.1#34; Dhp-a.iv.225 (= nirupakkilesa); Pv-a.230.</p><p>nis + upadhi, cp. upadhīka</p>

nirupadhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>free from attachments; free from belongings.</p>

nirupaghāta|nirupaghaata|nirupaghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirupaghaata</span></p><p>adjective not hurt, not injured or set back Mil.130.</p><p>nis + upaghāta</p>

nirupakāra|nirupakaara|nirupakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirupakaara</span></p><p>adjective useless Ja.ii.103.</p><p>nis + upakāra</p>

nirupama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective without comparison, incomparable Snp-a.455 (= atitula).</p><p>nis + upama</p>

nirupatāpa|nirupataapa|nirupatapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirupataapa</span></p><p>without pain, without trouble.</p>

nirupatāpa|nirupataapa|nirupatapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirupataapa</span></p><p>adjective not harassed (burnt) or afflicted (by pain or harm) Thig.512.</p><p>nis + upatāpa</p>

nirussukka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective , careless, unconcerned, indifferent to (c. loc.) Thag-a.282.</p><p>nis + ussukka</p>

nirussāsa|nirussaasa|nirussasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nirussaasa</span></p><p>adjective breathless Ja.iii.416; Ja.iv.121, cp. Ja.vi.197; Ja.vi.82.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirucchvāsa, nis + ussāsa</p>

niruttara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective making no reply Pv-a.117.</p><p>nis + uttara</p>

nirutti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>an expression; a form of words; a way of speaking; an alternative terminology, a gloss.</p>

nirutti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>one of the Vedāngas (see chaḷanga), expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of words, grammatical analysis etymological interpretation; pronunciation, dialect way of speaking, expression Vin.ii.139 (pabbajitā… sakāya niruttiyā Buddhavacanaṃ dūsenti); DN.i.202 (loka˚, expression); MN.iii.237 (janapada˚); SN.iii.71 (tayo n-pathā); AN.ii.160 (˚paṭisambhidā); AN.iii.201; Dhp.352 (˚padakovida = niruttiyañ ca sesapadesu cā ti catūsu pi paṭisambhidāsu cheko ti attho Dhp-a.iv.70 i.e. skilled in the dialect or the original language of the holy Scriptures); Pts.i.88 sq.; Pts.ii.150 (˚paṭisambhidā) Cnd.563; Dhs.1307; Ne.4, Ne.8, Ne.33, Ne.105; Mil.22 Vism.441; Snp-a.358; Pv-a.97.</p><p>Sk. nirukti, nis + <em>vac</em></p>

niruttipatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>, ~a, neuter a way of speaking; an expression; (merely using an expression; ?)</p>

niruttipaṭisambhidā|niruttipa.tisambhidaa|niruttipatisambhida
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niruttipa.tisambhidaa</span></p><p>analytic knowledge or understanding of the form of words, of expressions.</p>

niruḷha|niru.lha|nirulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niru.lha</span></p><p>adjective grown, risen; usual, customary, common Vv-a.108.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirūḍha, pp. of niruhati</p>

nirādīnava|niraadiinava|niradinava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraadiinava</span></p><p>free from danger</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ādīnava">ādīnava</a></p>

nirādīnava|niraadiinava|niradinava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraadiinava</span></p><p>adjective not beset with dangers, not in danger, unimperilled Vin.iii.19.</p><p>nis + ādīnava</p>

nirāhāra|niraahaara|nirahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraahaara</span></p><p>adjective without food, not eating, fasting Ja.iv.225; Sdhp.389.</p><p>nis + āhāra</p>

nirākaroti FIXME double|niraakaroti FIXME double|nirakaroti FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraakaroti FIXME double</span></p><p>Nirākaroti</p><p>see <em>nirankaroti</em>.</p>

nirākaroti|niraakaroti|nirakaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraakaroti</span></p><p>Niraṅkaroti &amp; nirākaroti</p><p>to think little of, despise, neglect, disregard, repudiate throw away, ruin, destroy Thag.478; Iti.83 (nirākare) Ja.iii.280 = Ja.v.498; Ja.iv.302; Pv.iii.9#6 (= chaḍḍeti pajahati Pv-a.211); Vv-a.109<br />pp <em>(a)nirākata</em> Iti.39.</p><p>Sk. nirākaroti, nis + ā <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

nirākata|niraakata|nirakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraakata</span></p><p>of niraṅkata</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niraṅkata">niraṅkata</a></p>

nirākula|niraakula|nirakula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraakula</span></p><p>adjective unconfused, clear, calm, undisturbed Ja.i.17 (v. 94).</p><p>nis + ākula</p>

nirālamba|niraalamba|niralamba
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraalamba</span></p><p>adjective unsupported Mil.295 (ākāsa).</p><p>nis + ālamba</p>

nirālaya|niraalaya|niralaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraalaya</span></p><p>adjective houseless, homeless Mil.244 (= aniketa). At Dhp-a.iv.31 as expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of appossukka. f. abstr. <em>nirālayatā</em> homelessness Mil.162, Mil.276, Mil.420.</p><p>nis + ālaya</p>

nirāma|niraama|nirama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraama</span></p><p>adjective healthy, undepraved, without sin, virtuous Snp.251, Snp.252 (˚gandha nikkilesayoga Snp-a.293), Snp.717 (id. = nikkilesa Snp-a.499).</p><p>nis + āma, cp. nirāmaya</p>

nirāmagandha|niraamagandha|niramagandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraamagandha</span></p><p>free from corruption, free from taint</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmagandha">āmagandha</a></p>

nirāmaya|niraamaya|niramaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraamaya</span></p><p>adjective not ill, healthy, good, without fault Pv-a.164.</p><p>nis + āmaya</p>

nirāmisa|niraamisa|niramisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraamisa</span></p><p>not worldly, not physical or material; pure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

nirāmisa|niraamisa|niramisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraamisa</span></p><p>adjective having no meat or prey; free from sensual desires, disinterested, not material SN.i.35, SN.i.60; SN.iv.219, SN.iv.235; SN.v.68, SN.v.332; AN.iii.412; DN.iii.278 Vb.195; Vism.71; Sdhp.475, Sdhp.477.</p><p>nis + āmisa</p>

nirārambha|niraarambha|nirarambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraarambha</span></p><p>without killing; free from slaughter.</p>

nirārambha|niraarambha|nirarambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraarambha</span></p><p>adjective without objects (for the purpose of sacrificing), i.e. without the killing of animals (of yañña) SN.i.76; AN.ii.42 sq.</p><p>nis + ārambha</p>

nirāsa|niraasa|nirasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasa</span></p><p>without desire; without hope or expectation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsā">āsā</a></p>

nirāsa|niraasa|nirasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasa</span></p><p>adjective not hungry, not longing for anything, desireless SN.i.12, SN.i.23, SN.i.141; AN.i.107 sq.; Snp.1048 (anigha +), Snp.1078 (id.); Cnd.360; Pp.27; Pv.iv.1#33 (= nittaṇha Pv-a.230). See also amama.</p><p>nis + āsā</p>

nirāsasa|niraasasa|nirasasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasasa</span></p><p>without desire; without expectation.</p>

nirāsatti|niraasatti|nirasatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasatti</span></p><p>free from attachment</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsatti">āsatti</a></p>

nirāsattin|niraasattin|nirasattin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasattin</span></p><p>adjective not hanging on to, not clinging or attached to (c. loc.) Snp.851 (= nittaṇha Snp-a.549); Mnd.221.</p><p>adj. to pp. āsatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with nis</p>

nirāsava|niraasava|nirasava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasava</span></p><p>adjective without intoxication, undefiled, sinless Thag-a.148.</p><p>nis + āsava</p>

nirāsaya|niraasaya|nirasaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasaya</span></p><p>without a resting place or resort; without desires; without (wrong) intentions or dispositions</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsaya">āsaya</a></p>

nirāsaya|niraasaya|nirasaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasaya</span></p><p>adjective without (outward) support, not relying on (outward) things, without (sinful) inclinations Snp.56 (Cnd.360 b reads nirāsasa), Snp.369, Snp.634, Snp.1090 (Cnd.361 reads nirāsaṃsa); Dhp.410; Dhp-a.iv.185 (variant reading BB nirāsāsa; explained by nittaṇha).</p><p>nis + āsaya, fr. <em>śri</em></p>

nirāsaṃsa|niraasa.msa|nirasamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasa.msa</span></p><p>adjective without wishes, expectations or desires, desireless Snp.1090 (Nd ii.reading for nirāsaya); Cnd.361 (cp. Dhp-a.iv.185 nirāsāsa *nirāsaṃsa, variant reading for nirāsaya).</p><p>nis + āsaṃsa, <em>śaṃs</em></p>

nirāsaṅka|niraasa"nka|nirasanka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasa"nka</span></p><p>adjective without apprehension, unsuspicious, not doubting Ja.i.264; Vism.180.</p><p>nis + āsankā</p>

nirāsaṅkatā|niraasa"nkataa|nirasankata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasa"nkataa</span></p><p>the not hesitating Ja.vi.337.</p><p>abstr. fr. nirāsanka</p>

nirāsāda|niraasaada|nirasada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasaada</span></p><p>adjective tasteless, yielding no enjoyment Thag.710. Cp. nirassāda.</p><p>nis + assāda</p>

nirāsāsa|niraasaasa|nirasasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraasaasa</span></p><p>without desire; without expecatation.</p>

nirātaṅka|niraata"nka|niratanka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niraata"nka</span></p><p>adjective healthy Mil.251 (of paddy).</p><p>nis + ātanka</p>

nirīha|niriiha|niriha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niriiha</span></p><p>Nirīha(ka)</p><p>adjective inactive, motionless, without impulse Thag-a.148 (˚ka); Mil.413 (+ nijjīvata); Vism.484, Vism.594 sq.</p><p>nis + īha</p>

nirīhaka|niriihaka|nirihaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niriihaka</span></p><p>Nirīha(ka)</p><p>adjective inactive, motionless, without impulse Thag-a.148 (˚ka); Mil.413 (+ nijjīvata); Vism.484, Vism.594 sq.</p><p>nis + īha</p>

nisabha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verses speak of giving up desire and patiently awaiting one’s time. Thag.195–196</p>

nisabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i></p><p>bull; a leader; the best or most excellent (of a group)</p>

nisabha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“bull among men,” i.e. prince, leader; “princeps,” best of men epithet of the Buddha SN.i.28, SN.i.48, SN.i.91; MN.i.386; Ja.v.70 Ja.vi.526; Vv.16#7 (isi˚), cp. Vv-a.83 for expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->; Vv.63#7 (isi˚ = ājānīya Vv-a.262).</p><p>Sk. nṛ + ṛṣabha, cp. usabha. On relation of usabha: vasabha: nisabha see Snp-a.40</p>

nisabhavatā|nisabhavataa|nisabhavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisabhavataa</span></p><p>the being (like) a bull?</p>

nisada
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nisada &amp; Nisadā</p><p>feminine a grindstone, esp. the understone of a millstone Vin.i.201; (˚pota id.); Mil.149; Vism.252 (˚pota, where Kp-a at id. p. reads ˚putta). Cp. ā˚.</p><p>Sk. dṛṣad f.; for n: d cp. P. nijjuha = Sk. dātyūha etc.</p>

nisadā|nisadaa|nisada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisadaa</span></p><p>stone for grinding, a (lower) millstone.</p>

nisadā|nisadaa|nisada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisadaa</span></p><p>Nisada &amp; Nisadā</p><p>feminine a grindstone, esp. the understone of a millstone Vin.i.201; (˚pota id.); Mil.149; Vism.252 (˚pota, where Kp-a at id. p. reads ˚putta). Cp. ā˚.</p><p>Sk. dṛṣad f.; for n: d cp. P. nijjuha = Sk. dātyūha etc.</p>

nisadāpota|nisadaapota|nisadapota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisadaapota</span></p><p>, ~a, neuter a stone for grinding; an (upper, smaller) millstone or stone roller.</p>

nisadāpotaka|nisadaapotaka|nisadapotaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisadaapotaka</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisadāpotaka">nisadāpotaka</a></p>

nisagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nisagga &amp; Nissagga</p><p>giving forth, bestowing; natural state, nature SN.i.54 (˚ss˚). cp. <em>nisaṭṭha</em></p><p>ni or nis + <em>sṛj</em></p>

nisajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisajjeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to spend, bestow, give, give up Pv-a.105 (dānûpakaraṇā nisajjesi read better as ˚karaṇǡni sajjesi). See also <em><a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></em>.</p><p>sic MSS. for niss˚; Sk. niḥsarjayati, nis + sajjeti, Caus. of <em>sṛj</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nisajjā|nisajjaa|nisajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisajjaa</span></p><p>(the act of) sitting, sitting down; a session.</p>

nisajjā|nisajjaa|nisajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisajjaa</span></p><p>sitting down, opportunity for sitting, seat Pv.iv.1#2 (seyyā +); Ja.i.217; Pv-a.24 (˚ādipaṭikkhepa-ṭṭhāna), Pv-a.219 (pallankâbhujanādi-lakkhaṇā nisajjā). Cp. nesajjika.</p><p>Sk. *niṣadyā of ni <em>sad</em></p>

nisamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisāmeti">nisāmeti</a></p>

nisamma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb carefully, considerately, observing Snp.54; Cnd.367 Cnd.481 b (= sutvā). Esp. in phrase <em>n- kārin</em> acting considerately Dhp.24 (= Dhp-a.i.238); Ja.iii.106; Ja.vi.375; Mil.3; cp. n. kiriyāya Mil.59. Cp. nisanti.</p><p>orig. ger. of nisāmeti, Sk. niśamya, <em>śam</em></p>

nisammakāri|nisammakaari|nisammakari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisammakaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nisammakārin">nisammakārin</a></p><p>of nisamma acting after careful consideration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisamma">nisamma</a></p>

nisammakārin|nisammakaarin|nisammakarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisammakaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nisammakārin">nisammakārin</a></p><p>of nisamma acting after careful consideration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisamma">nisamma</a></p>

nisanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(close) observation; careful attention.</p>

nisanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>careful attention or observation AN.ii.97; AN.iii.201; AN.iv.15 (dhamma˚), AN.iv.36 (id.) AN.iv.296; AN.v.166 (dhamma˚); Dpvs.i.53 (˚kāra). Cp. nisamma &amp; nisāmeti.</p><p>Sk. *niśānti, ni + <em>śam</em></p>

nisaṅkhati|nisa"nkhati|nisankhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisa"nkhati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisaṅkhiti">nisaṅkhiti</a></p>

nisaṅkhiti|nisa"nkhiti|nisankhiti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisa"nkhiti</span></p><p>formation; accumulation.</p>

nisaṅkhiti|nisa"nkhiti|nisankhiti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisa"nkhiti</span></p><p>deposit (of merit or demerit), accumulation, effect (of kamma Snp.953 (= Mnd.442 abhisankhārā).</p><p>Sk. ni-saṃskṛti, ni + saṃ + <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

nisaṭṭha|nisa.t.tha|nisattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisa.t.tha</span></p><p>past participle given up, spent, lost Thig.484 (variant reading ˚ss˚); Thag-a.286 (= pariccatta). Cp nisajjeti &amp; nisagga.; Nisada &amp; Nisada</p><p>nis + saṭṭha of <em>sṛj</em></p>

nisedha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>restraint; prevention, prohibition; contradiction, denial.</p>

nisedha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun holding back, restraining; prevention, prohibition Dhp.389; Dhp-a.iv.148; <em>hirī˚</em> restrained by shame SN.i.168 = Snp.462; Dhp.143.</p><p>fr. ni + <em>sedh</em></p>

nisedhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective prohibiting, restraining; one who prohibits, an obstructer Ja.ii.220.</p><p>fr. nisedha</p>

nisedhanatā|nisedhanataa|nisedhanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisedhanataa</span></p><p>refusing, refusal, prohibition Mil.180 (a˚).</p><p>abstr. to nisedheti</p>

nisedhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>prevents; prohibits; suppresses.</p>

nisedhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisedheti">nisedheti</a></p>

nisedhesuṃ|nisedhesu.m|nisedhesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisedhesu.m</span></p><p>of nisedheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisedheti">nisedheti</a></p>

nisedhetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nisedheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisedheti">nisedheti</a></p>

nisedheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>prevents; prohibits; keeps off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisedhati">nisedhati</a></p>

nisedheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to keep off, restrain, prohibit, prevent SN.i.121 (nisedha, imper.); Ja.iii.83, Ja.iii.442; Thag-a.250; Vv-a.105 (nirayûpapattiṃ)<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://nisedha">nisedha</a></em>.</p><p>Caus. of ni + <em>sedh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisedha">nisedha</a></p>

nisevana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>, also -ā feminine practising, enjoying; pursuit Pp.20, Pp.24; Sdhp.406.</p><p>Sk. niṣevana, cp. nisevati</p>

nisevati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i></p><p>frequents, inhabits, resorts to; serves, waits upon; practices, performs; indulges in.</p>

nisevati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to resort to, practise, pursue, follow, indulge in Ja.ii.106; Snp.821 (= Mnd.157); Pv.ii.3#19 (= karoti Pv-a.87); Mil.359<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nisevita">nisevita</a></em>.</p><p>ni + <em>sev</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisevita">nisevita</a></p>

nisevita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nisevati</p><ol><li>(mfn,) visited, frequented; practiced, studied; waited upon.</li><li>(n.)<ol><li>resorting to; practice.</li><li>(a sign of) frequenting; a trace?</li></ol></li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisevati">nisevati</a></p>

nisevita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective frequented, practised, enjoyed, indulged in MN.i.178; Sdhp.373.</p><p>pp. of nisevati</p>

nisinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nisīdati sitting down; (being) seated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective sitting down, seated Ja.i.50, Ja.i.255; Ja.iii.126; Kp-a.250; Pv-a.11, Pv-a.16, Pv-a.39 passim<br />■ Often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; contrasted with; <em>tiṭṭhaṃ</em> (standing), <em>caraṃ</em> (walking) &amp; <em><a href="bword://saya%E1%B9%83">sayaṃ</a></em> (sayāna; lying down), e.g. at Snp.151, Snp.193; Iti.82.</p><p>Sk. niṣanna, pp. of nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sayaṃ">sayaṃ</a></p>

nisinnaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) sitting; seated.</li><li>(n.) the act of sitting.</li></ol>

nisinnaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = nisinna; MN.i.333; Ja.i.163; Dhp-a.iii.175.</p>

nisita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective sharp MN.i.281 (āvudhajāta pīta˚?); Ja.iv.118 (su˚); Vv-a.233; Pv-a.155, Pv-a.192, Pv-a.213.</p><p>Sk. niśita, ni + pp. of <em>śā</em> to whet</p>

nisiñcati|nisi~ncati|nisincati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisi~ncati</span></p><p>to besprinkle Mhvs.vii.8.</p><p>ni + siñcati</p>

nissadda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noiseless, soundless, silent Ja.i.17 (verse 94); Dhp-a.iii.173.</p><p>nis + sadda</p>

nissagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nisagga &amp; Nissagga</p><p>giving forth, bestowing; natural state, nature SN.i.54 (˚ss˚). cp. <em>nisaṭṭha</em></p><p>ni or nis + <em>sṛj</em></p>

nissaggiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>subject to forfeiture; required to be surrendered; (an offense) involving surrendering, involving forfeiture.</li><li>something (to be) discharged or thrown.</li></ol>

nissaggiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective to be given up, what ought to be rejected or abandoned Vin.i.196, Vin.i.254; Vin.iii.195 sq.</p><p>Sk. *niḥsārgya grd. of nis + sajjeti, not = Sk. naisargika</p>

nissajita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjiṃ">nissajjiṃ</a></p>

nissajitvā|nissajitvaa|nissajitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissajitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjitvā">nissajjitvā</a></p>

nissajitvāna|nissajitvaana|nissajitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissajitvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjitvāna">nissajjitvāna</a></p>

nissajiṃ|nissaji.m|nissajim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaji.m</span></p><p>nissajjati</p><p>absol. lets go; gives up; hands over; surrenders; lets fly, throws.</p>

nissajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>lets go; gives up; hands over; surrenders; lets fly, throws.</p>

nissajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to let loose, give up, hand over, give, pour out Vin.ii.188 ger. <strong>nissajja</strong> [Sk. niḥsṛjya] Snp.839 (variant reading nisajja); Mnd.189 (id.); Snp-a.545. pp. <em>nisaṭṭha &amp; nissaṭṭha;</em> (q.v.) Cp. nissaggiya &amp; paṭi˚.</p><p>nis + sajjati, <em>sṛj</em>. See also <em><a href="bword://nisajjeti">nisajjeti</a></em></p><p>See also: nissajja, <a href="bword://nisajjeti">nisajjeti</a></p>

nissajjeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>to be given up, renounced?</p>

nissajjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lets go; gives up; hands over; surrenders; lets fly, throws.</p>

nissajjitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nissajjitvā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nissajjitvā|nissajjitvaa|nissajjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissajjitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nissajjitvāna|nissajjitvaana|nissajjitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissajjitvaana</span></p><p>of nissajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nissajjiṃ|nissajji.m|nissajjim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissajji.m</span></p><p>nissajjati</p><p>absol. lets go; gives up; hands over; surrenders; lets fly, throws.</p>

nissakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“going out from,” ttg. a name of the <em>ablative</em> case Ja.v.498; Vv-a.152, Vv-a.154, Vv-a.180 Vv-a.311; Pv-a.147, Pv-a.221.</p><p>fr. nis + sakkati = <em>sakk</em></p>

nissakkana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going out, creeping out; only in <em>biḷāra˚</em>; at DN.ii.83 (variant reading BB as gloss nikkhamana) + SN.iv.194; AN.v.195.</p><p>Sk. *niḥsarpana, nis + <em>sakk</em>, confused with <em>sṛp</em>, see Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> p. 60 &amp; cp. apassakkati o˚, pari˚</p>

nissama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>exertion, endeavour Ja.v.243.</p><p>ni + sama</p>

nissanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>result; outcome.</li><li>discharge; trickling down.</li></ol>

nissanda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>flowing or trickling down; discharge, dropping issue; result, outcome, esp. effect of Kamma AN.iii.32; Ja.i.31, Ja.i.205, Ja.i.426 (sarīra˚); Dhp-a.i.395; Dhp-a.ii.36, Dhp-a.ii.86; Vv-a.14 (puñña-kammassa n-phala); Pv-a.47 (puññakammassa), Pv-a.58 (id.); Mil.20. Mil.117; Pgdp.102.</p><p>Sk. nisyanda &amp; niṣyanda, ni + ; <em>syand</em> (syad), see <em><a href="bword://sandati">sandati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sandati">sandati</a></p>

nissantāpa|nissantaapa|nissantapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissantaapa</span></p><p>adjective without grief or selfmortification Pv-a.62.</p><p>nis + santāpa</p>

nissarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>goes out, departs; escapes from; is rid of, is separated from.</p>

nissarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to depart, escape from, be freed from (c. abl.) AN.i.260 (yasmā atthi loke nissaraṇaṃ tasmā sattā lokamhā nissaranti)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nissa%E1%B9%ADa">nissaṭa</a></em>, grd <em><a href="bword://nissara%E1%B9%87%C4%ABya">nissaraṇīya</a></em> (q.v.); cp. also nissaraṇa &amp; paṭi˚.</p><p>nis + sarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissaṭa">nissaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://nissaraṇīya">nissaraṇīya</a></p>

nissaraṇa|nissara.na|nissarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissara.na</span></p><p>going out, departure; escape; separation (from), riddance; means, remedy to get rid of or counteract something.</p>

nissaraṇa|nissara.na|nissarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissara.na</span></p><p>going out, departure; issue, outcome, result; giving up, leaving behind being freed, escape (from saṁsāra), salvation. Vin.i.104 DN.iii.240 DN.iii.248<em>f.</em> SN.i.128 SN.i.142 SN.ii.5 SN.iii.170 (catunnaṁ dhātūnaṁ); SN.iv.7 sq. (identical); SN.v.121 sq. AN.i.258 AN.i.260 AN.ii.10 (kāmānaṁ etc.); AN.iii.245 sq.; AN.iv.76 (uttariṁ); AN.v.188 MN.i.87 (kāmānaṁ), MN.i.326 (uttariṁ); MN.iii.25 Iti.37 Iti.1 Pts.ii.180, Pts.244 Vb.247 Vism 116 Thag-a.233 Dhs-a.164 Sdhp.579.<br />■cp. nissaṭa &amp; nissaraṇīya</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a></em> wise in knowing results, prescient, able to find a way to salvation SN.iv.205;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1%C3%B1a">pañña</a></em> (adj.) = ˚dassin DN.i.245 (a˚); DN.iii.46; SN.ii.194; SN.iv.332; AN.v.178 (a˚), AN.v.181 sq.; Mil.401.</li></ul><p>Sk. niḥsaraṇa, to nis + sarati, cp. BSk. nissaraṇa nissaraṇa giving up (?) Avs.ii.193</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dassin">dassin</a>, <a href="bword://pañña">pañña</a></p>

nissaraṇīya|nissara.niiya|nissaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissara.niiya</span></p><p>connected with, conducive to, separation, getting rid of, escape.</p>

nissaraṇīya|nissara.niiya|nissaraniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissara.niiya</span></p><p>adjective connected with deliverance, leading to salvation able to be freed. The 3 n. dhātuyo (elements of deliverance) are <em><a href="bword://nekkhamma">nekkhamma</a></em> (escape from cravings) <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ruppa">āruppa</a></em> (from existence with form), <em><a href="bword://nirodha">nirodha</a></em> (from all existence), in detail at Iti.61 (kāmānaṃ n. nekkhammaṃ rūpānaṃ n. āruppaṃ, yaṃ kiñci bhūtaṃ sankhataṃ n nirodho). The 5 n-dh. are escape fr. kāma, vyāpāda vihesā, rūpa, sakkāya: AN.iii.245; cp. AN.i.99 AN.iii.290.</p><p><em>Note.</em> The spelling is often <em>nissāraṇīya</em>, thus at Vin.iv.225; DN.iii.239 (the five n-dhātuyo), DN.iii.247, DN.iii.275.</p><p>grd. of nissarati, with relation to nissaraṇa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nekkhamma">nekkhamma</a>, <a href="bword://āruppa">āruppa</a>, <a href="bword://nirodha">nirodha</a></p>

nissatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective powerless, unsubstantial; f. abstr. <em>-tā</em> absence of essence, unsubstantiality (see <em><a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> A) Dhs-a.38, Dhs-a.139, Dhs-a.263; cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> pp. xxxiii.&amp; 26.</p><p>Sk. *niḥsattva, nis + satta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></p>

nissaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>support, what one depends or relies on; refuge, shelter; dependence; reliance.</li><li>support, resource; requisite.</li><li>(a relation of) dependence and guidance and supervision (under an upajjhāya or ācariya)</li></ol>

nissaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>that on which anything depends, support help, protection; endowment, resource, requisite, supply foundation, reliance on (acc. or-˚) Vin.i.58 (the four resources of bhikkhu, viz. piṇḍiyālopa-bhojanaṃ, paṃsukūla-cīvaraṃ, rukkhamūla-senāsanaṃ, pūtimuttabhesajjaṃ); Vin.ii.274, Vin.ii.278; DN.iii.137, DN.iii.141; AN.i.117; AN.iii.271 AN.iv.353; AN.v.73; Snp.753, Snp.877; Mnd.108 (two n.: taṇhā˚ diṭṭhi˚), Mnd.190, cp. Cnd s. v.; Cnd.397#A (the requisites of a bhikkhu in diff. enumeration); Pts.ii.49 sq., Pts.ii.58 sq. Pts.ii.73 sq.; Pts.ii.220; Ne.7, Ne.65; Vism.12, Vism.535. <em>nissayaṃ karoti</em> to rely on, to be founded on to take one’s stand in Snp.800<br />■ Cp. nissāya &amp; nissita.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> giving assistance or help, an (ecclesiastical act of help or protection Vin.i.49 Vin.i.143, Vin.i.325; Vin.ii.226 AN.i.99 Pv.iv.1#1 (so to be read at the 2 latter passages for niyassa˚);</li><li><em>-sampanna</em> finding one’s strength in AN.iv.353</li></ul><p>Sk. niśraya, of ni + <em>śri</em>, corresp. in meaning to Sk. āśraya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

nissayakaraṇīya|nissayakara.niiya|nissayakaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissayakara.niiya</span></p><p>requiring (formally acquired) supervision, requiring a mentor.</p>

nissayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>(from nissayati) depending on, relying on; dependence.</p>

nissayasampanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.</i></p><p>, ~ā, feminine having a support; having supporters or mentors.</p>

nissayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nissayat">nissayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissayati">nissayati</a></p>

nissayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>leans on; relies on, depends on; resorts to.</p>

nissayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lean on, a foundation on, rely on, trust, pursue Snp.798 (sīlabbataṃ; Snp-a.530 = abhinivisati); Vv-a.83 (katapuññaṃ). Pass. <em>nissīyati</em> Vv-a.83. pp. <em><a href="bword://nissita">nissita</a></em> ger. <em><a href="bword://niss%C4%81ya">nissāya</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. niśrayati, but in meaning = āśrayati, ni + <em>śri</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissita">nissita</a>, <a href="bword://nissāya">nissāya</a></p>

nissayatā|nissayataa|nissayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissayataa</span></p><p>dependence, requirement, resource Snp.856; Mnd.245.</p><p>abstr. to nissaya</p>

nissaṃsaya|nissa.msaya|nissamsaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa.msaya</span></p><p>adjective having no doubt, free from doubt Mil.237<br />■ acc. as <em>adv.</em> without doubt undoubtedly Pv.iv.8#1; Dhp-a.i.106; Pv-a.95.</p><p>nis + saṃsaya</p>

nissaṅga|nissa"nga|nissanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa"nga</span></p><p>adjective unattached, unobstructed, disinterested, unselfish Sdhp.371, Sdhp.398, Sdhp.411 etc.; Tikp.10 f. abstr. <em>-tā</em> disinterestedness Ja.i.46.</p><p>nis + sanga</p>

nissaṭa|nissa.ta|nissata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa.ta</span></p><p>(who has) gone out, left; separated (from); rid of, free from</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissarati">nissarati</a></p>

nissaṭa|nissa.ta|nissata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa.ta</span></p><p>adjective flown or come out from, appeared; let loose, free, escaped from SN.iii.31 SN.iv.11 sq.; AN.i.260; AN.iv.430 (a˚); AN.v.151 sq.; Ja.iii.530 Ja.vi.269; Nd ii.under nissita; Pts.ii.10 sq.; Mil.95, Mil.225 (bhava˚). See also nissaraṇa. Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>pp. of nis + sarati, <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

nissaṭṭha|nissa.t.tha|nissattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa.t.tha</span></p><p>of nissajjati let go; set free; given up; handed over, surrendered; let fly, Thrown</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nissaṭṭha|nissa.t.tha|nissattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissa.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective dismissed, given up, left, granted, handed over, given Vin.iii.197 (˚cīvara) MN.i.295; MN.ii.203; Vv-a.341. See also <em><a href="bword://nisa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">nisaṭṭha</a></em> &amp; paṭi˚.</p><p>pp. of nissajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisaṭṭha">nisaṭṭha</a></p>

nissenī|nissenii|nisseni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissenii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisseṇī">nisseṇī</a></p>

nissesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective whole, entire; nt. acc. as adv. <strong>nissesaṃ</strong> entirely, completely Cnd.533.</p><p>nis + sesa</p><p>See also: nissesaṃ</p>

nisseṇi|nisse.ni|nisseni
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisse.ni</span></p><p>ladder, a flight of stairs DN.i.194, DN.i.198; Ja.i.53; Ja.ii.315; Ja.iii.505; Mil.263; Vism.244, Vism.340 (in simile); Dhp-a.i.259.</p><p>fr. nis + <em>śri</em>, orig. that which leans against, or leads to something, cp. Sk. śreṇī a row</p>

nisseṇī|nisse.nii|nisseni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisse.nii</span></p><p>ladder; a flight of steps.</p>

nissinnokasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n.</i></p><p>of nisinna the place where (someone) sits or is sitting or has sat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisinna">nisinna</a></p>

nissirīka|nissiriika|nissirika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissiriika</span></p><p>adjective having lost his (or its) splendour or prosperity Ja.vi.225 (ājīvika), Ja.vi.456 (rājabhavana).</p><p>nis + sirī</p>

nissita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nissayati resorting to, inhabiting; dependent (on); leaning on; attached to, supported by; close to, connected to; resorted to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissayati">nissayati</a></p>

nissita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective hanging on, dependent on, inhabiting; attached to, supported by, living by means of, relying on, being founded or rooted in, bent on As-˚ often in sense of a prep. = by means of, on account of, through, esp. with pron. kiṃ˚ (= why, through what Snp.458; taṃ˚ (therefore, on acct. of this) SN.iv.102. For combination with var. synonyms see Cnd. s.v. &amp; cp. Mnd.75, Mnd.106<br />■ SN.ii.17 (dvayaṃ; cp. SN.iii.134); SN.iv.59, SN.iv.365, SN.v.2 sq., SN.v.63 sq.; AN.iii.128; Dhp.339 (rāga˚); Snp.752, Snp.798 Snp.910; Ja.i.145; Mnd.283; Pv.i.8#6 (sokaṃ hadaya˚ lying in), Pv.ii.6#6 (paṭhavi˚ supported by); Vb.229; Ne.39 (˚citta) Mil.314 (inhabiting); Pv-a.86 (māna˚)<br />■ <em>anissita</em> unsupported not attached, free, emancipated Snp.66, Snp.363 Snp.753, Snp.849, Snp.1069 (unaided); Ja.i.158; Mil.320, Mil.351<br />■ Cp apassita.</p><p>Sk. niśrita, pp. of nissayati, corresp. in meaning to Sk. āśrita</p>

nissitajappi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nissitajappin">nissitajappin</a></p><p>speaking only what others say, relying on others’ opinions.</p>

nissitajappin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nissitajappin">nissitajappin</a></p><p>speaking only what others say, relying on others’ opinions.</p>

nissitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun adherent, supporter (orig. one who is supported by), pupil Ja.i.142, Ja.i.186; Dhp-a.i.54.</p><p>fr. prec.</p>

nissitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dependence on, i.e. interference by, being too near, nearness Vism.118 (pantha˚). Cp san˚.</p><p>fr. nissita</p>

nissoka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from sorrow, without grief, not mourning Pv-a.62; Kp-a.153.</p><p>nis + soka</p>

nisssajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nissajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissajjati">nissajjati</a></p>

nissuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>flowed out or away, vanished.</p>

nissuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective flown out or away, vanished, disappeared MN.i.280.</p><p>fr. nis + <em>sru</em>, see <em><a href="bword://savati">savati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://savati">savati</a></p>

nissāra|nissaara|nissara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaara</span></p><p>adjective sapless, worthless, unsubstantial Ja.i.393; Sdhp.51, Sdhp.608, Sdhp.612.</p><p>nis + sāra</p>

nissārajja|nissaarajja|nissarajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaarajja</span></p><p>adjective without diffidence, not diffident, confident Ja.i.274 (+ nibbhaya).</p><p>Sk. niḥ + śārada + ya</p>

nissāraṇa|nissaara.na|nissarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaara.na</span></p><p>indeclinable (temporary) expulsion; suspension.</p>

nissāraṇa|nissaara.na|nissarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaara.na</span></p><p>going or driving out, expulsion Mil.344 (osāraṇa-n- paṭisāraṇa), Mil.357.</p><p>fr. nissarati</p>

nissāraṇīya|nissaara.niiya|nissaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaara.niiya</span></p><p>, ~a, neuter</p><ol><li>(from nissāraṇa)</li><li>involving suspension; (the formal act) connected with suspension.</li><li>to be got rid of?</li></ol>

nissāreti|nissaareti|nissareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaareti</span></p><p>of nissarati expels (temporarily from the <em>saṅgha</em>), suspends</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissarati">nissarati</a></p>

nissārita|nissaarita|nissarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaarita</span></p><p>of nissarati expelled; suspended</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissarati">nissarati</a></p>

nissārīyati|nissaariiyati|nissariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaariiyati</span></p><p>of nissarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nissarati">nissarati</a></p>

nissāya|nissaaya|nissaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaaya</span></p><ol><li><ol><li>depending on, relying on; using as one’s support.</li><li>because of; for the sake of.</li></ol></li><li><p>in (formal) dependence, with support; having as mentor (+ gen. or acc.)</p></li><li><p>leaning against; beside; near.</p></li></ol>

nissāya|nissaaya|nissaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissaaya</span></p><p>preposition accusative leaning on (in all fig. meanings) Cnd.368 (= upanissāya, ārammaṇaṃ ālambanaṃ karitvā).</p><ol><li>near, near by, on, at Ja.i.167 (pāsānapiṭṭhaṃ), Ja.i.221 (padumasaraṃ); Pv-a.24 (bāhā), Pv-a.134 (taṃ = with him).</li><li>by means of, through, by one’s support, by way of Ja.i.140 (rājānaṃ: under the patronage of the k.); Ja.iv.137 (id.); Ja.ii.154 (tumhe); Mil.40 (kāyaṃ), Mil.253 (id.); Pv-a.27 (ye = yesaṃ hetu), Pv-a.154 (nadī˚ alongside of).</li><li>because of, on account of, by reason of, for the sake of Ja.i.203 (amhe), Ja.i.255 (dhanaṃ), Ja.i.263 (maṃ); Pv-a.17 (kiṃ), Pv-a.67 (namaṃ), Pv-a.130 (taṃ)<br />■ Cp. nissaya, nissita.</li></ol><p>ger. of nissayati, Sk. *niśrāya, BSk niśritya, ni + <em>śri</em></p>

nissīma|nissiima|nissima
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nissiima</span></p><p>adjective outside the boundary Vin.i.255 (˚ṭṭha), Vin.i.298 (˚ṃ gantuṃ); Vin.ii.167 (˚e ṭhito).</p><p>cp. Sk. niḥsīman with diff. meanings (“boundless”), nis + sīma</p>

nisumbhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>knocks down; treads down.</p>

nisumbhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to knock down Thig.302 (= pāteti Thag-a.227).</p><p>ni + <em>sumbh</em> (subhnāti)</p>

nisā|nisaa|nisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaa</span></p><p>night Vv.35#2 (loc. nise); Vv-a.161 (loc. nisati, variant reading nisi = rattiyaṃ); Mil.388 (loc. nisāya) Dāvs ii.6; Dāvs v.2 (nisāyaṃ). See also nisītha.</p><p>Sk. niś &amp; niśā, prob. with niśītha (midnight) to ni + ; <em>śi</em> = lying down</p>

nisāda|nisaada|nisada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaada</span></p><p>robber Ja.iv.364. Cp. nesāda.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣāda, a Non-Aryan or barbarian</p>

nisādana|nisaadana|nisadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaadana</span></p><p>grinding Dhp-a.i.308.</p><p>= ni + śātana</p>

nisādika|nisaadika|nisadika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaadika</span></p><p>adjective fit for lying down, suitable for resting Vin.i.239 (go˚).</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣādin, ni + <em>sad</em></p>

nisādin|nisaadin|nisadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaadin</span></p><p>adjective lying down DN.iii.44, DN.iii.47.</p><p>fr. ni + <em>sad</em></p>

nisāmaka|nisaamaka|nisamaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamaka</span></p><p>closely observing; attending carefully to.</p>

nisāmaka|nisaamaka|nisamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamaka</span></p><p>adjective observant, listening to, attending to, careful of AN.v.166, AN.v.168 (dhammānaṃ).</p><p>cp. Sk. niśāmana</p>

nisāmakajātika|nisaamakajaatika|nisamakajatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamakajaatika</span></p><p>naturally disposed to attending carefully; given to closely observing.</p>

nisāmakajātiya|nisaamakajaatiya|nisamakajatiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamakajaatiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisāmakajātiya">nisāmakajātiya</a></p>

nisāmayati|nisaamayati|nisamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisāmeti">nisāmeti</a></p>

nisāmayi|nisaamayi|nisamayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamayi</span></p><p>of nisāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisāmeti">nisāmeti</a></p>

nisāmayitvā|nisaamayitvaa|nisamayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaamayitvaa</span></p><p>of nisāmeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisāmeti">nisāmeti</a></p>

nisāmeti|nisaameti|nisameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaameti</span></p><p>hears; observes (closely); attends carefully (to)</p>

nisāmeti|nisaameti|nisameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaameti</span></p><p>to attend to, listen to, observe, be careful of, mind Ja.iv.29 (anisāmetvā by not being careful); Ja.v.486; Dhp-a.i.239 (+ upadhāneti); Pv-a.1 (imper nisāmayatha). Cp. nisanti, nisamma.</p><p>ni + sāmeti</p>

nisāna|nisaana|nisana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaana</span></p><p>hone on which to sharpen a knife Mil.282.</p><p>ni + <em>śā</em> to sharpen, to whet, cp. nisita</p>

nisāra|nisaara|nisara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaara</span></p><p>adjective noun full of sap, excellent, strong (of a tree) Vv.63#1 (= niratisaya sārassa nisiṭṭhasārassa rukkhassa Vv-a.261).</p><p>ni + sāra</p>

nisātaka|nisaataka|nisataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaataka</span></p><p>in <em>koka˚</em>; Ja.vi.538, a certain wild animal; the meaning is not clear, etymologically it is to be derived fr. Sk. niśātayati to strike, to fell. See Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>1p. 152, s. v. koka. The variant reading is ˚nisādaka, evidently influenced by nisāda.</p>

nisātakā|nisaatakaa|nisataka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisaatakaa</span></p><p>A type of animal Ja.vi.538.</p>

nisīdana|nisiidana|nisidana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidana</span></p><p>something to sit on; a rug or mat for sitting on.</p>

nisīdana|nisiidana|nisidana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidana</span></p><p>sitting down, occasion or opportunity to sit, a mat to sit on Vin.i.295 Vin.ii.123 (˚ena vippavasati); SN.v.259 (˚ṃ gaṇhāti). <em>-paccattharaṇa</em> a mat for sitting on Vin.i.47, Vin.i.295; Vin.ii.209 Vin.ii.218.</p><p>Sk. niṣadana, fr. nisīdati</p>

nisīdanta|nisiidanta|nisidanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīdati|nisiidati|nisidati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidati</span></p><p>sits down; is seated, sits; sits up.</p>

nisīdati|nisiidati|nisidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidati</span></p><p>to sit down, to be seated, to sit, to dwell Cnd.433; Ja.iii.392; Ja.vi.367; Pv.ii.9#3 (nisīdeyya Pot.); Pv-a.74. aor. nisīdi Vin.i.1; Ja.ii.153; Pv-a.5, Pv-a.23, Pv-a.44; 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. nisīdiṃsu (Ja.i.307) nisīdisuṃ (Mhvs.vii.40); ger. <em><a href="bword://nis%C4%ABditv%C4%81">nisīditvā</a></em> (Ja.ii.160; Pv-a.5 Pv-a.74), <em><a href="bword://nisajja">nisajja</a></em> DN.ii.127) and <em>nisīditvāna</em> (Snp.1031); grd <em><a href="bword://nis%C4%ABditabba">nisīditabba</a></em> Vin.i.47. pp. <em><a href="bword://nisinna">nisinna</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus 2 <em><a href="bword://nis%C4%ABd%C4%81peti">nisīdāpeti</a></em> [cp. Sk. niṣādayati] to cause to sit down, to make one be seated, to invite to a seat Ja.iii.392; Ja.vi.367; Pv-a.17, Pv-a.35 (there āsane); Mil.20. Cp. abhi˚, san˚.</p><p>Sk. niṣīdati, ni + sīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīditvā">nisīditvā</a>, <a href="bword://nisajja">nisajja</a>, <a href="bword://nisīditabba">nisīditabba</a>, <a href="bword://nisinna">nisinna</a>, <a href="bword://nisīdāpeti">nisīdāpeti</a></p>

nisīdi|nisiidi|nisidi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidi</span></p><p>ca.ger of nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīdita|nisiidita|nisidita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nisīditar">nisīditar</a></p><p>(from nisīdati) one who sits.</p>

nisīditabba|nisiiditabba|nisiditabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiiditabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīditar|nisiiditar|nisiditar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiiditar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nisīditar">nisīditar</a></p><p>(from nisīdati) one who sits.</p>

nisīditvā|nisiiditvaa|nisiditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiiditvaa</span></p><p>of nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīditvāna|nisiiditvaana|nisiditvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiiditvaana</span></p><p>of nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīdiṃsu|nisiidi.msu|nisidimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidi.msu</span></p><p>of nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīdāpeti|nisiidaapeti|nisidapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidaapeti</span></p><p>of nisīdati causes to sit down, seats; invites to sit; makes sit up; lets stay; places; makes kneel; forces down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisīdati">nisīdati</a></p>

nisīdāpetvā|nisiidaapetvaa|nisidapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidaapetvaa</span></p><p>of nisīdapeti</p><p>See also: nisīdapeti</p>

nisīdāpita|nisiidaapita|nisidapita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidaapita</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of nisīdapeti</p><p>See also: nisīdapeti</p>

nisīdāpiyati|nisiidaapiyati|nisidapiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiidaapiyati</span></p><p>of nisīdapeti</p><p>See also: nisīdapeti</p>

nisītha|nisiitha|nisitha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiitha</span></p><p>midnight, night.</p>

nisītha|nisiitha|nisitha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisiitha</span></p><p>midnight, night Thag.3 (aggi yathā pajjalito nisīthe; variant reading BB nisive), Thag.524 (variant reading nisive); Ja.iv.432; Ja.v.330, Ja.v.331 (variant reading BB nisive), Ja.v.506 (= rattibhāga Com.).</p><p>Sk. niśītha, see <em><a href="bword://nis%C4%81">nisā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nisā">nisā</a></p>

nisūdana|nisuudana|nisudana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nisuudana</span></p><p>destroying, slaughtering Mil.242.</p><p>ni + <em>sūd</em></p>

nitamba
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the ridge of a mountain or a glen, gully DN-a.i.209.</p><p>Sk. nitamba; etym. unknown</p>

nitammati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to become dark, to be exhausted, faint; to be in misery or anxiety Ja.iv.284 (Com.: atikilamati).</p><p>Sk. nitāmyati, ni + <em>tam</em> as in tama</p>

nitiṇṇa|niti.n.na|nitinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niti.n.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nitthiṇṇa">nitthiṇṇa</a></p>

nittaddana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(better: <em>nitthaddhana</em>) neuter paralysing DN.i.11 (jīvhā˚ = mantena jivhāya thaddhakaraṇa DN-a.i.96 variant reading (gloss) nibandhana).</p><p>Sk. *niṣṭambhana, abstr. fr. ni + thaddha = making rigid</p>

nittaṇha|nitta.nha|nittanha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitta.nha</span></p><p>free from craving.</p>

nittaṇha|nitta.nha|nittanha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitta.nha</span></p><p>adjective free from thirst or desire, desireless Pv-a.230 (= nirāsa) f. abstr. <em>nitthaṇhatā</em> Ne.38.</p><p>BSk. niṣṭṛṣṇa (Divy.210 etc.), nis + taṇhā</p>

nitteja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>without energy Vism.596.</li><li>“put out, abashed, put to shame, in <em>-ṃ karoti</em> to make blush or put to shame Ja.ii.94 (lajjāpeti +).</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. nistejas only in meaning 1; nis + teja</p>

nitthanana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>groaning, moaning DN-a.i.291 (variant reading BB. ˚ṭhuna). As <strong>nitthunana</strong> Vism.504.</p><p>nis + thanana, abstr. to thaneti</p><p>See also: nitthunana</p>

nitthananta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nitthunanta">nitthunanta</a></p>

nitthanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nitthanati &amp; Nitthunati</p><p>to moan, groan:</p><ol><li><em>-thanati</em> Ja.i.463; Ja.ii.362; Ja.iv.446; Ja.v.296; DN-a.i.291</li><li><em>-thunati</em> Vin.ii.222; Ja.v.295, Ja.v.389; Vism.311; Vv-a.224. Cp nitthuna.</li></ol><p>Sk. nisstanati “moan out,” nis + thaneti &amp; thunati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nittharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.</i></p><p>, ~ā, feminine crosses over; gets out of, escapes; expiates.</p>

nittharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cross over, get out of, leave behind, get over DN.i.73 (kantāraṃ). pp <em><a href="bword://nitti%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">nittiṇṇa</a></em> q.v. Caus. <strong>nitthāreti</strong> to bring through, help over Cnd.630 (nittāreti).</p><p>Sk. nistarati, nis + tarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nittiṇṇa">nittiṇṇa</a>, nitthāreti</p>

nittharaṇa|nitthara.na|nittharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthara.na</span></p><ol><li>getting across; escape from; finishing.</li><li>carrying out; accomplishing, dealing with.</li></ol>

nittharaṇa|nitthara.na|nittharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthara.na</span></p><p>Nittharaṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter getting across, ferrying over, traversing, overcoming SN.i.193 (oghassa); AN.ii.200 (id.); Iti.111 (id.) MN.i.134; Ja.i.48 (loka˚); Dāvs ii.29 (id.); Vism.32; Sdhp.334 (bhava˚), Sdhp.619 (tiloka˚).</p><p>Sk. nistaraṇa, nis + taraṇa, cp. nittharati</p><p>Nittharaṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter “strewing or being strewn down,” putting down, carrying, bearing SN.iv.177 (bhārassa, of a load, cp. nikkhepa); Vv-a.131 (so read for niddharaṇa, in kuṭumba-bhārassa nsamatthā = able to carry the burden of a household).</p><p>Sk. nistaraṇa, ni + tharaṇa</p>

nitthariṃsu|nitthari.msu|nittharimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthari.msu</span></p><p>of nittharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></p>

nitthiṇṇa|nitthi.n.na|nitthinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthi.n.na</span></p><p>who has crossed; who has got out of, escaped</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></p>

nitthiṇṇa-ogha|nitthi.n.na-ogha|nitthinna-ogha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthi.n.na-ogha</span></p><p>nitthiṇṇa-ogha</p><p>pp mfn. who has crossed the torrent or flood.</p>

nitthiṇṇogha|nitthi.n.nogha|nitthinnogha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthi.n.nogha</span></p><p>who has crossed the torrent or flood.</p>

nitthuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.</i></p><p>groaning</p>

nitthuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(of thunati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) moan, groan DN-a.i.291 (as variant reading BB for nitthanana)-</li><li>(of thunati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) blame, censure, curse Pv-a.76 (˚ṃ karoti to revile or curse).</li></ol><p>Sk. *nis-stanana &amp; nistava to thunati</p>

nitthunanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>moaning; groaning.</p>

nitthunati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nitthunati</p><p>etc., see <em>nitthanati</em> etc.</p>

nitthunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nitthanati &amp; Nitthunati</p><p>to moan, groan:</p><ol><li><em>-thanati</em> Ja.i.463; Ja.ii.362; Ja.iv.446; Ja.v.296; DN-a.i.291</li><li><em>-thunati</em> Vin.ii.222; Ja.v.295, Ja.v.389; Vism.311; Vv-a.224. Cp nitthuna.</li></ol><p>Sk. nisstanati “moan out,” nis + thaneti &amp; thunati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nitthāra|nitthaara|nitthara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthaara</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://netthāra">netthāra</a></p>

nitthāra|nitthaara|nitthara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitthaara</span></p><p>passing over, rescue, payment, acquittance, in <em>-ṃ vattati</em> to be acquitted, to get off scot-free MN.i.442 (variant reading netth˚, which is the usual form). See <em>netthāra</em>.</p><p>Sk. nistāra; nis + tāra of tarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nittiṇa|nitti.na|nittina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitti.na</span></p><p>adjective free from grass Ja.iii.23.</p><p>Sk. niṣṭṛṇa, nis + tiṇa</p>

nittiṇṇa|nitti.n.na|nittinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitti.n.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nitthiṇṇa">nitthiṇṇa</a></p>

nittiṇṇa-ogha|nitti.n.na-ogha|nittinna-ogha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitti.n.na-ogha</span></p><p>nittiṇṇa-ogha</p><p>pp mfn.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nitthiṇṇa-ogha">nitthiṇṇa-ogha</a></p>

nittiṇṇa|nitti.n.na|nittinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitti.n.na</span></p><p>past participle got out of, having crossed or overcome DN.ii.275 (-ogha; variant reading BB nitiṇṇa) Mnd.159 (as variant reading; text has nitiṇṇa); Cnd.278 (t.). Cp nittharati.</p><p>Sk. nistīrṇa, nis + tiṇṇa</p>

nittudana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>pricking, piercing AN.i.65 (text: nittuddana), AN.iii.403 sq.</p><p>nis + tudana, abstr. fr. tudati; cp. Sk. nistodā</p>

nittāreti|nittaareti|nittareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nittaareti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nittharati">nittharati</a></p>

nitāḷeti|nitaa.leti|nitaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nitaa.leti</span></p><p>to knock down, to strike Ja.iv.347.</p><p>Sk. nitāḍayati, ni + tāḷeti</p>

nivaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>multitude, quantity, heap Dāvs iv.53; Dāvs v.14, Dāvs v.24, Dāvs v.62.</p><p>fr. ni + <em>vah</em></p>

nivapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>throws down; scatters.</p>

nivapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to heap up, sow, throw (food) MN.i.151 sq. (nivāpaṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nivutta">nivutta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ni + vapati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivutta">nivutta</a></p>

nivarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in Caus. <em><a href="bword://niv%C4%81reti">nivāreti</a></em> (q.v.), pp. <em><a href="bword://nivuta">nivuta</a></em>.</p><p>ni + varati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a>, <a href="bword://nivuta">nivuta</a></p>

nivaraṇa|nivara.na|nivarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivara.na</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

nivasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>nivasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. clothes; dresses oneself.</p><p>nivasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. passes or spends time; dwells, lives (in), inhabits.</p>

nivasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to live, dwell, inhabit, stay Vin.ii.11<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nivuttha">nivuttha</a></em>, cp. also nivāsana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; nivāsin.</p><p>ni + vasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivuttha">nivuttha</a></p>

nivatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>gone or come back, returned, turned back; turned away from, giving up; ceased</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivattati">nivattati</a></p>

nivatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>past participle returned, turning away from, , giving up, being deprived of, being without (˚- Vin.ii.109 (˚bīja) Ja.i.203 Vv-a.72</p><p>pp. of nivattati</p>

nivattabīja|nivattabiija|nivattabija
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivattabiija</span></p><p>whose seed is inoperative?</p>

nivattana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>returning, turning, fig. turning away from, giving up, “conversion” Pv-a.120 (pāpato)</li><li>a bend, curve (of a river), nook Ja.i.324; Ja.ii.117, Ja.ii.158; Ja.iv.256; Ja.v.162.</li></ol><p>fr. nivattati</p>

nivattanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nivattati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivattati">nivattati</a></p>

nivattanīya|nivattaniiya|nivattaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivattaniiya</span></p><p>adjective only neg. <em>a˚</em>; not liable to return, not returning Dhp-a.i.63.</p><p>grd. formation fr. nivattana</p>

nivattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(intrans.) returns, turns round, turns back; gives up; stops, ceases; tums away from.</p>

nivattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to turn back, to return (opp. gacchati), to turn away from, to flee, vanish, disappear Vin.i.46; DN.i.118; Ja.i.223; Ja.ii.153 Ja.iv.142; Snp.p.80; Pv.ii.9#34; Pv.iv.10#7; Snp-a.374; Pv-a.74 Pv-a.161. aor. <em><a href="bword://nivatti">nivatti</a></em> Ja.ii.3; Pv-a.141. pp. <em><a href="bword://nivatta">nivatta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus 1 <em><a href="bword://nivatteti">nivatteti</a></em> to lead back, to turn from, to make go back, to convert Ja.i.203; Vv-a.110; Pv-a.204 (pāpato from sin). Cp. upa˚, paṭi˚, vi˚<br />caus 2 <em>nivattāpeti</em> to send back, to return Pv-a.154.</p><p>Vedic nivartati, ni + vattati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivatti">nivatti</a>, <a href="bword://nivatta">nivatta</a>, <a href="bword://nivatteti">nivatteti</a></p>

nivattenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nivattati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivattati">nivattati</a></p>

nivatteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp (mf)n.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivattati">nivattati</a></p>

nivattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~entī)n.</p><ol><li><ol><li>clothed in or with; wearing (esp. an under-garment)</li><li>worn; used.</li></ol></li><li><p>(m.n.) clothing; an (undergarment)</p></li></ol>

nivattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>past participle clothed in or with (-˚ or acc.), dressed, covered SN.i.115; Ja.i.59 (su˚), Ja.i.307 (sāṭakaṃ); Pv-a.47, Pv-a.49 (dibbavattha˚), Pv-a.50.</p><p>pp. of ni + vasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nivatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>returning, return Pv-a.189 (gati˚ going &amp; coming).</p><p>fr. ni + vṛt</p>

nivattitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of nivattati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivattati">nivattati</a></p>

nivattāpeti|nivattaapeti|nivattapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivattaapeti</span></p><p>(~antī)n. Makes to return, turn round, turn back; makes give up; makes to stop, ceases; makes to turn away from.</p>

nivattāpetvā|nivattaapetvaa|nivattapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivattaapetvaa</span></p><ol><li>makes to return, turn round, turn back; makes give up; makes to stop, ceases; makes to turn away from.</li></ol>

nivedaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective relating, admonishing Ja.vi.21.</p><p>to nivedeti</p>

nivedeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to communicate, make known, tell, report, announce Ja.i.60, Ja.i.307; Pv-a.53, Pv-a.66 (attānaṃ reveal oneself); Dāvs v.42.</p><p>ni + vedeti, Caus. of <em>vid</em>.</p>

nivesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>settling (on or in); a settlement; a house, a home.</p>

nivesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>entering, stopping, settling down; house, abode Vv.8#2 (= nivesanāni kacchantarāni Vv-a.50).</li><li>= nivesana 2, in <em>diṭṭhi˚</em>; Snp.785 (= idaṃ-sacchâbhinivesa-sankhātāni diṭṭhi-nivesanāni Snp-a.522).</li></ol><p>Vedic niveśa, fr. ni + <em>viś</em></p>

nivesana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>settlement; a dwelling-place, a house, a home.</li><li>settling (on or in), attachment (to); where one settles.</li></ol>

nivesana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>entering, entrance, settling; settlement, abode, house home DN.i.205, DN.i.226; DN.ii.127; Ja.i.294; Ja.ii.160 (˚ṭṭhāna) Pv-a.22, Pv-a.81, Pv-a.112.</li><li>(fig.) (also <em>nivesanā</em> f.: Cnd.366 settling on, attachment, clinging to (in diṭṭhi˚ clinging to a view = dogmatism cp. nivissa-vādin) Snp.1055 (nandi + ; = taṇhā Cnd.366); Dhp.40 (diṭṭhi˚); Mnd.76 Mnd.110. See also <em><a href="bword://nivesa">nivesa</a></em>.</li></ol><p>Vedic niveśana, fr. nivesati, cp. niviṭṭha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivesa">nivesa</a></p>

nivesayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

nivesayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of niveseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

nivesesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of niveseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

nivesetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>of niveseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

niveseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>makes enter; directs towards; causes to settle (on); establishes, founds; gives in marriage</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivasati">nivasati</a></p>

niveseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cause to enter, to establish; to found, build, fix, settle; (fig.) to establish in, exhort to (c. loc.), plead for, entreat, admonish DN.i.206; SN.v.189; Dhp.158, Dhp.282 (attānaṃ); Iti.78 (brahmacariye); Thig.391 (manaṃ); Ja.v.99; Pv.iii.7#7 (saṃyame nivesayi); DN-a.i.273 (gāmaṃ); Pv-a.206.</p><p>Caus. of nivesati</p>

nivesita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective settled, arranged, designed, built Vv-a.82 (= sumāpita).</p><p>pp. of nivesati</p>

nivesiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of niveseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

niveṭha|nive.tha|nivetha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nive.tha</span></p><p>in pañhe dunniveṭha at Mil.90 see <em><a href="bword://nibbedha">nibbedha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbedha">nibbedha</a></p>

niveṭhana|nive.thana|nivethana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nive.thana</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

niveṭheti|nive.theti|nivetheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nive.theti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nibbe%E1%B9%ADheti">nibbeṭheti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbeṭheti">nibbeṭheti</a></p>

nivicikicchā|nivicikicchaa|nivicikiccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivicikicchaa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://nibbicikicch%C4%81">nibbicikicchā</a></em>; MN.i.260.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbicikicchā">nibbicikicchā</a></p>

nivijjha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

nivisamāna|nivisamaana|nivisamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivisamaana</span></p><p>settles down; enters; establishes oneself.</p>

nivisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>enters or penetrates into; settles (on); is intent on; is convinced, insists on; is founded.</p>

nivisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to enter, stop, settle down on (loc.), to resort to, establish oneself Vin.i.207; Ja.i.309 = Ja.iv.217 (yasmiṃ mano nivisati)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://nivi%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">niviṭṭha</a></em> ger <em>nivissa</em> (q.v.). Caus. <em><a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></em>. Nivissa-vadin</p><p>ni + visati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niviṭṭha">niviṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://niveseti">niveseti</a></p>

nivisi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of nivisati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivisati">nivisati</a></p>

nivissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nivisati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivisati">nivisati</a></p>

nivissa-vādin|nivissa-vaadin|nivissa-vadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivissa-vaadin</span></p><p>Nivissa-vādin</p><p>adjective noun “speaking in the manner of being settled or sure,” a dogmatist Snp.910, Snp.913, explained at Mnd.326 as “sassato loko idam eva saccaṃ, mogham aññan ti”; at Snp-a.560 as “jānāmi passāmi tath’ eva etan ti.”</p><p>nivissa (ger. of nivisati) + vādin</p>

nivissavāda|nivissavaada|nivissavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivissavaada</span></p><p>of nivisati speaking insistently, dogmatically</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivisati">nivisati</a></p>

nivissavādi|nivissavaadi|nivissavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivissavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivissavādin">nivissavādin</a></p><p>speaking insistently, dogmatically</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivissa">nivissa</a></p>

nivissavādin|nivissavaadin|nivissavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivissavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivissavādin">nivissavādin</a></p><p>speaking insistently, dogmatically</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivissa">nivissa</a></p>

niviṭṭha|nivi.t.tha|nivittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivi.t.tha</span></p><p>niviṭṭha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>3 pl.</p><p>niviṭṭha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. of nivisati</p><ol><li>settled; encamped.</li><li>resting or staying in; turned to, intent upon</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbiṭṭha">nibbiṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://nivisati">nivisati</a></p>

niviṭṭha|nivi.t.tha|nivittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivi.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective settled, established (in); confirmed, sure; fixed on, bent on, devoted to (loc. Snp.57 (= satta allīna etc. Cnd.364), Snp.756, Snp.774, Snp.781 (ruciyā), Snp.824 (saccesu), Snp.892; Mnd.38, Mnd.65, Mnd.162; Iti.35, Iti.77; Ja.i.89 Ja.i.259 (adhammasmiṃ); Mil.361; Vv-a.97 (˚gāma, built situated); DN-a.i.90 (su˚ &amp; dun˚ of a street = well &amp; badly built or situate). Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>pp. of nivisati</p>

nivusita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having lived, stayed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivasati">nivasati</a></p>

nivuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>hindered, obstructed; suppressed; surrounded, enveloped.</p>

nivuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective surrounded, hemmed in, obstructed, enveloped DN.i.246; SN.ii.24; SN.iv.127; Snp.348 (tamo˚), Snp.1032, Snp.1082; Iti.8; Cnd.365 (= ophuṭa, paṭicchanna, paṭikujjita); Mil.161; Snp-a.596 (= pariyonaddha).</p><p>pp. of nivarati (nivāreti) cp. nivārita</p>

nivutabrahmaloka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who is kept from the brahma world.</p>

nivutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>nivutta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. shaved, cut off.</p><p>nivutta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. thrown down; scattered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivapati">nivapati</a></p>

nivutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nivutta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(pp.) called, termed, designated Pv-a.73 (dasavassa-satāni, vassa-sahassaṃ n. hoti).</p><p>pp. of ni + <em>vac</em></p><p>Nivutta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(pp.) shorn, shaved, trimmed Snp.456 (˚kesa = apagatakesa ohāritakesamassu Snp-a.403).</p><p>Sk. *nyupta, pp. of vapati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to shear</p><p>Nivutta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(pp.) sown, thrown (of food), offered, given MN.i.152; Ja.iii.272.</p><p>Sk. *nyupta, pp. of vapati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to sow</p>

nivuttakesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of nivutta whose hair has been shaved off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivutta">nivutta</a></p>

nivuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(pp.</i></p><p>of nivasati) inhabited; dwelling, living; see *<a href="bword://san">san</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://san">san</a></p>

nivuṭa|nivu.ta|nivuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivu.ta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivissavāda">nivissavāda</a></p>

nivyaggha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective free from tigers Ja.ii.358 (variant reading nibbyaggha).</p><p>nis + vyaggha</p>

nivāpa|nivaapa|nivapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaapa</span></p><p>seed, grain; feed, fodder; a feeding place.</p>

nivāpa|nivaapa|nivapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaapa</span></p><p>food thrown (for feeding), fodder, bait; gift, portion, ration MN.i.151 sq. (Nivāpa-sutta); Ja.i.150; Ja.iii.271; Dhp-a.i.233 (share); Dhp-a.iii.303; Vv-a.63 (diguṇaṃ ˚ṃ pacitvā cooking a double portion). Cp. nevāpika.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://ti%E1%B9%87a">tiṇa</a></em> grass to eat Ja.i.150;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">puṭṭha</a></em> fed on grains Dhp.325 (= kuṇḍakâdinā sūkara-bhattena puṭṭho Dhp-a.iv.16 = Ne.129 = Thag.17;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhojana">bhojana</a></em> a meal on food given, a feeding MN.i.156).</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. nivāpa, ni + <em>vap</em>, cp. nivapati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tiṇa">tiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://puṭṭha">puṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://bhojana">bhojana</a></p>

nivāpapuṭṭha|nivaapapu.t.tha|nivapaputtha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaapapu.t.tha</span></p><p>nourished on grain.</p>

nivāraya|nivaaraya|nivaraya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaraya</span></p><p>adjective in <em>dun˚</em>; hard to check or keep back Mil.21 (+ durāvaraṇa).</p><p>grd. of nivāreti</p>

nivārayati|nivaarayati|nivarayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaarayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivāraṇa|nivaara.na|nivarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaara.na</span></p><p>warding off, keeping out, preventing; restraining; obstructing.</p>

nivāraṇa|nivaara.na|nivarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaara.na</span></p><p>adjective warding off, keeping back, preventing; refusal Snp.1034, Snp.1035, Snp.1106 (= Cnd.363 āvāraṇa rakkhaṇa gopana); Dhs-a.259; Pv-a.102 Pv-a.278; Sdhp.396.</p><p>fr. nivāreti</p>

nivāresi|nivaaresi|nivaresi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaresi</span></p><p>of nivāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivāresuṃ|nivaaresu.m|nivaresum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaresu.m</span></p><p>of nivāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivāreta|nivaareta|nivareta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaareta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivāretar">nivāretar</a></p><p>one who holds back or restrains; one who keeps out.</p>

nivāretabba|nivaaretabba|nivaretabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaretabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivāretar|nivaaretar|nivaretar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaretar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivāretar">nivāretar</a></p><p>one who holds back or restrains; one who keeps out.</p>

nivāretar|nivaaretar|nivaretar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaretar</span></p><p>one who holds back or refuses (entrance) (opp. pavesetar) DN.ii.83 = SN.iv.194; AN.v.194 (dovāriko aññātānaṃ nivāretā ñātānam pavesetā).</p><p>n. agent to nivāreti</p>

nivāreti|nivaareti|nivareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaareti</span></p><p>keeps out; holds back from, restrains; obstructs; prevents; forbids.</p>

nivāreti|nivaareti|nivareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaareti</span></p><p>to keep back, to hold back from (c. abl.), to restrain; to refuse, obstruct, forbid warn Vin.i.46; Vin.ii.220; SN.i.7 (cittaṃ nivāreyya), SN.i.14 (yato mano nivāraye); SN.iv.195 (cittaṃ); Dhp.77, Dhp.116 (pāpā cittaṃ nivāraye); Ja.i.263; Pv.iii.7#4; Vv-a.69; Pv-a.79 Pv-a.102; Dhp-a.i.41.</p><p>Caus. of nivarati</p>

nivāretvā|nivaaretvaa|nivaretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaretvaa</span></p><p>of nivāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivārita|nivaarita|nivarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaarita</span></p><p>adjective unobstructed, open Pv-a.202 (= anāvaṭa).</p><p>pp. of nivāreti</p>

nivāriyamāna|nivaariyamaana|nivariyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaariyamaana</span></p><p>of nivāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāreti">nivāreti</a></p>

nivāsa|nivaasa|nivasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasa</span></p><ol><li>living, dwelling; stay, residence; where one lives, a dwelling-place, a home.</li><li>where one is, a state of existence.</li></ol>

nivāsa|nivaasa|nivasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasa</span></p><p>stopping, dwelling, resting-place, abode; living, sheltering Ja.i.115 (˚ṃ kappeti to put up), Ja.ii.110; Pv-a.76, Pv-a.78. Usually in phrase <em>pubbe-nivāsaṃ anussarati</em> “to remember one’s former abode or place of existence (in a former life),” characterising the faculty of remembering one’s former birth DN.i.13, DN.i.15, DN.i.16 DN.i.81; SN.i.167, SN.i.175, SN.i.196; SN.ii.122, SN.ii.213; SN.v.265, SN.v.305; AN.i.25 AN.i.164; AN.ii.183; AN.iii.323, AN.iii.418 sq.; AN.iv.141 sq.; AN.v.211, AN.v.339 Also in pubbenivāsaṃ vedi Iti.100; Snp.647 = Dhp.423 p-n-paṭisaṃyuttā dhammikathā DN.ii.1; p-n-anussatiñāṇa DN.iii.110, DN.iii.220, DN.iii.275; AN.iv.177. Cp. nevāsika.</p><p>fr. nivasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nivāsana|nivaasana|nivasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasana</span></p><p>dress, clothing; an undergarment; wearing (an (under) garment)</p>

nivāsana|nivaasana|nivasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasana</span></p><p>Nivāsana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective noun dressed, clothed; dressing, clothing, undergarment (opp. pārupana) Vin.i.46; Vin.ii.228; Ja.i.182 (manāpa˚), Ja.i.421; Ja.iii.82; Pv-a.50, Pv-a.74, Pv-a.76 Pv-a.173 (pilotikakkhaṇḍa˚ dressed in rags).</p><p>fr. nivāseti</p><p>Nivāsana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter dwelling, abode Pv-a.44 (˚ṭṭhāna place of abode), Pv-a.76 (id.).</p><p>fr. nivasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nivāsayati|nivaasayati|nivasayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāsenta|nivaasenta|nivasenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasenta</span></p><p>of nivāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāsesi|nivaasesi|nivasesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasesi</span></p><p>(~entī)n. of nivāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāsesuṃ|nivaasesu.m|nivasesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasesu.m</span></p><p>of nivāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāsetabba|nivaasetabba|nivasetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāseti|nivaaseti|nivaseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaseti</span></p><p>of nivāsati puts on (a garment), wears; dresses, clothes oneself (in an (under) garment); makes put on, clothes</p><p>See also: nivāsati</p>

nivāseti|nivaaseti|nivaseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaaseti</span></p><p>to dress oneself, to put on (the undergarment), to get clothed or dressed. Freq in ster. phrase “<em>pubbaṇhasamayaṃ nivāsetvā patta-</em> cīvaram ādāya…,” describing the setting out on his round of the bhikkhu; e.g. DN.i.109, DN.i.178, DN.i.205, DN.i.226. Vin.i.46; Vin.ii.137, Vin.ii.194; DN.ii.127; Ja.i.265; Pp.56; Pv.i.10#3; Pv-a.49, Pv-a.61, Pv-a.75, Pv-a.127 (nivāsessati + pārupissati), Pv-a.147 (= pārupāmi)<br />caus 2 <em>nivāsāpeti</em> to cause or order to be dressed (with 2 acc.) Ja.i.50; Ja.iv.142; Dhp-a.i.223.</p><p>Caus. of nivasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nivāsetvā|nivaasetvaa|nivasetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasetvaa</span></p><p>of nivāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāseti">nivāseti</a></p>

nivāsi|nivaasi|nivasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivāsin">nivāsin</a></p><p>living (in), dwelling, staying; a (permanent) inhabitant.</p>

nivāsika|nivaasika|nivasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasika</span></p><p>adjective staying, living, dwelling Ja.ii.435 (= nibaddha-vasanaka C.).</p><p>fr. nivāsa</p>

nivāsin|nivaasin|nivasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://nivāsin">nivāsin</a></p><p>living (in), dwelling, staying; a (permanent) inhabitant.</p>

nivāsin|nivaasin|nivasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaasin</span></p><p>adjective noun dwelling, staying; (n.) an inhabitant Dāvs v.45.</p><p>to nivasati</p>

nivāta|nivaata|nivata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaata</span></p><p>nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg.</p><ol><li>(mfn.) sheltered from the wind; unruffled, calm; free from drafts; safe, secure.</li><li>(n.) a place sheltered from the wind; absence of wind, calm, stillness; safety, security.</li></ol><p>nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. &amp; neuter humble; modest; biddable; calm, mild.</p>

nivāta|nivaata|nivata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaata</span></p><p>Nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective with the wind gone down, i.e. without wind, sheltered from the wind, protected, safe, secure Vin.i.57, Vin.i.72; MN.i.76; AN.i.137 (kūṭāgāra); AN.i.101 (id.); Iti.92 (rahada); Thag.1 (kuṭikā); Thag.2, Thag.376 (pāsāda)<br />■ (nt.) a calm (opp. pavāta Vin.ii.79.</p><p>Sk. nivāta, ni + vāta “wind-down”</p><p>Nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>lowliness, humbleness, obedience, gentleness MN.i.125; Snp.265 (= nīcavattana Kp-a.144); Ja.vi.252; Pv.iv.7#12. Cp. Mhvs.ii.423. Freq. in cpd. <em>nivātavutti</em> (id.) AN.iii.43; Snp.326 (= nīcavutti Snp-a.333) Ja.iii.262; Mil.90, Mil.207; Vv-a.347.</p><p>identical with nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, sheltered from the wind = low</p>

nivātaka|nivaataka|nivataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaataka</span></p><p>sheltered place, a place of escape, opportunity (for hiding) Ja.i.289 = Ja.v.435; cp. Mil.205 (where reading is nimantaka, with variant reading nivātaka, see note on p. 426). See Com. on this stanza at Ja.v.437.</p><p>fr. nivāta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

nivāyāsa|nivaayaasa|nivayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nivaayaasa</span></p><p>? oozing of trees; Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of ikkāsa at Vin.ii.321. See niyyāsa.</p>

niya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>one’s own.</p>

niya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one’s own Snp.149 (˚putta = orasaputta Kp-a.248); <em>niyassakamma</em> at AN.i.99 &amp; Pv.iv.1#13 (variant reading Minayeff tiyassa) is to be read as nissayakamma (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. nija, q.v.</p>

niyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>one’s own.</p>

niyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one’s own Thig.469; Thag-a.284; Dhs-a.169, Dhs-a.337; DN-a.i.183; Vb.2; Vism.349.</p><p>= niya</p>

niyama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>restraint, constraint, training, self-control Mil.116 (yama +); Pv-a.98 (yama +).</li><li>definiteness certainty, limitation Dhp-a.iii.83 (catumagga˚, variant reading niyāma); Snp-a.124 (niyāma); Dhs-a.154; Pv-a.166 (ayaṃ n. saṃsāren’ atthi: law, necessity)<br />■ <em>aniyama</em> indefiniteness choice, generality Dhs-a.57; Vv-a.16 (yaṃ kiñci = aniyame, i.e. in a general sense), Vv-a.17 (same of ye keci); Pv-a.175 (vā saddo aniyamattho = indefinite)- <em>niyamena</em> (instr.) adv. by necessity, necessarily Pv-a.287; <em>niyamato</em> (abl.) id. Dhs-a.145, Dhs-a.304 (so read).</li><li>natural law, cosmic order; in Commentarial literature this was fivefold: utu-, bīja-, kamma-, ćitta-, dhammaDA on DN.ii.11; <em>Dial.</em> ii.8; Dhs-a.272; trs. 360.</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. niyama, ni + <em>yam</em>; often confused with niyāma</p>

niyamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>fixing, settling, definition, explanation in detail Mil.352 (lakkha-n aiming at the target); Vv-a.22 (visesattha˚); Vv-a.231, Pv-a.255 (so read for nigamana?).</p><p>Sk. niyamana, to niyameti</p>

niyameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tie down, to fix; explain in detail, exemplify Pv-a.265; Vism.666. pp. <strong>niyamita</strong> see *<a href="bword://a">a</a>*˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. niyamayati, ni + yamati</p><p>See also: niyamita, <a href="bword://a">a</a></p>

niyasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyassa">niyassa</a></p>

niyassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(a formal act of the <em>saṅgha</em>) conferring disgrace, loss of status; (or requiring effort)</p>

niyassaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i></p><p>formal niyassa act.</p>

niyassakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>formal act of the <em>saṅgha</em> conferring loss of status; disgracing, reprimanding.</p>

niyassakammakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dealt with by a formal act of the <em>saṅgha</em> conferring loss of status.</p>

niyata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>constrained, bound; restrained; disciplined; constant.</li><li>(whose outcome or course is) determined, fixed; restricted, limited, specified; established, sure; inevitable, invariable.</li></ol>

niyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective restrained, bound to, constrained to, sure (as to the future), fixed (in its consequences), certain, assured, necessary DN.ii.92 (sambodhiparāyanā), DN.ii.155; DN.iii.107; Snp.70 (= ariyamaggena niyāmappatta Snp-a.124, cp. Cnd.357); Dhp.142 (= catumagga niyamena n. Dhp-a.iii.83); Ja.i.44 (bodhiyā); Pp.13, Pp.16 Pp.63; Kv.609 sq.; Dhs.1028 sq. (micchatta˚ etc.; cp Dhs. trsl. 266, 267), Dhs.1414, Dhs.1595; Vb.17, Vb.24, Vb.63, Vb.319 Vb.324; Mil.193; Tikp.168 (˚micchādiṭṭhi); Dhp-a.iii.170; Pv-a.211. Discussed in <em>Pts. of Contr.</em> (see Index). <em><a href="bword://aniyata">aniyata</a></em> see separately.</p><p>pp. of ni + <em>yam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aniyata">aniyata</a></p>

niyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>necessity, fate, destiny DN.i.53; DN-a.i.161; Vv-a.341; Pv-a.254.</p><p>cp. Sk. niyati, ni + <em>yam</em></p>

niyatāyuka|niyataayuka|niyatayuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyataayuka</span></p><p>whose lifetime is fixed.</p>

niyoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>command, order; necessity. abl. <em>niyogā</em> “strictly speaking” Dhs.1417.</p><p>ni + yoga</p>

niyojayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyojeti">niyojeti</a></p>

niyojayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nijoyeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyojeti">niyojeti</a></p>

niyojeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>urges, directs to; incites; employs.</p>

niyojeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to urge, incite to (with loc.) Vin.ii.303; AN.iv.32; Pv.ii.1#4; Mil.229. Niyyati = Niyati</p><p>Caus. of niyuñjati</p>

niyujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be fit for, to be adapted to, to succeed, result, ensue Pv-a.49 (= upakappati).</p><p>Pass. of niyuñjati</p>

niyutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Niyutta(ka)</p><p>adjective tied to, appointed to (with loc.), commissioned, ordered Dhs-a.47; Pv-a.20 (janapade), Pv-a.124 (dānâdhikāre), Pv-a.127 (dāne).</p><p>pp. of niyuñjati</p>

niyuttaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Niyutta(ka)</p><p>adjective tied to, appointed to (with loc.), commissioned, ordered Dhs-a.47; Pv-a.20 (janapade), Pv-a.124 (dānâdhikāre), Pv-a.127 (dāne).</p><p>pp. of niyuñjati</p>

niyuñjati|niyu~njati|niyunjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyu~njati</span></p><p>yokes; ties; impels, directs; fixes the mind on.</p>

niyyamāna|niyyamaana|niyyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyamaana</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīyamāna">nīyamāna</a></p>

niyyanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nīyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīyati">nīyati</a></p>

niyyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Niyyati = Nīyati</p><p>(Pass. of nayati).</p>

niyyatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>given; yielded.</p>

niyyatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>escape Ja.i.215.</p><p>cp. Sk. niryaṇa</p>

niyyiṃsu|niyyi.msu|niyyimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyi.msu</span></p><p>of niyyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyādayati|niyyaadayati|niyyadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyādesi|niyyaadesi|niyyadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadesi</span></p><p>of niyyāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyādetabba|niyyaadetabba|niyyadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyātetabba">niyyātetabba</a></p>

niyyādeti|niyyaadeti|niyyadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyādeti|niyyaadeti|niyyadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadeti</span></p><p>Niyyādeti &amp; niyyāteti &amp; nīyādeti</p><p>(to) give (back), give into charge, give over, assign, dedicate, to present, denote SN.i.131 (niyyātayāmi); SN.iv.181 (sāmikānaṃ gāvo), SN.iv.194; Ja.i.30 Ja.i.66, Ja.i.496; Ja.ii.106, Ja.ii.133; Vv.46#8 niyyādesi = sampaṭicchāpesi adāsi Vv-a.199); Pv.iii.2#11 (niyātayiṃsu = adaṃsu Pv-a.184); Vism.115 (t); Dhp-a.i.70; Dhp-a.ii.87; Vv-a.33, Vv-a.67; Pv-a.20 (vihāraṃ nīyādetvā), Pv-a.25 (= uddissati dadāti), Pv-a.42, Pv-a.81, Pv-a.276 (at all Pv-a passages as nī˚)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81dita">niyyādita</a></em> Cp. similarly paṭiyādeti &amp; paṭiyādita.</p><p>cp. Sk. ni-or nir-yātayati, Caus. of ni(r)yatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyādita">niyyādita</a></p>

niyyādetuṃ|niyyaadetu.m|niyyadetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadetu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyādetvā|niyyaadetvaa|niyyadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyātetvā">niyyātetvā</a></p>

niyyādita|niyyaadita|niyyadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyātita">niyyātita</a></p>

niyyādita|niyyaadita|niyyadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaadita</span></p><p>assigned, presented, given, dedicated Pv-a.196 (dhana nī˚). As <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81tita">niyyātita</a></em> at Vism.115.</p><p>pp. of niyyādeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyātita">niyyātita</a></p>

niyyāma|niyyaama|niyyama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaama</span></p><p>Niyyāma(ka)</p><p>a pilot, helmsman, master mariner, guide Ja.i.107 (thala˚); Ja.iv.137, Ja.iv.138; Mil.194, Mil.378 sq.; Dāvs iv.42.</p><p>Sk. niyāmaka &amp; niryāma(ka). Cp. also P. niyāmaka</p>

niyyāmaka|niyyaamaka|niyyamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaamaka</span></p><p>Niyyāma(ka)</p><p>a pilot, helmsman, master mariner, guide Ja.i.107 (thala˚); Ja.iv.137, Ja.iv.138; Mil.194, Mil.378 sq.; Dāvs iv.42.</p><p>Sk. niyāmaka &amp; niryāma(ka). Cp. also P. niyāmaka</p>

niyyāna|niyyaana|niyyana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaana</span></p><p>going out, setting forth; (the way of) getting out, leaving (<em>saṃsāra</em>)</p>

niyyāna|niyyaana|niyyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaana</span></p><ol><li>going out, departure DN.i.9 (= niggamana DN-a.i.94).</li><li>way out, release deliverance Snp.170, Snp.172 (“magga-saccaṃ bhāvento lokamhā niyyāti” Snp-a.212); Pts.i.163, Pts.i.176; Ne.119 Cp. niyyānika<br />■ <em>aniyyāna</em> Dhp-a.ii.209.</li></ol><p>nis + yāna, cp. niyyāti</p>

niyyānika|niyyaanika|niyyanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaanika</span></p><p>going out, setting forth; being a good way, leading to good; conducive to leaving (<em>saṃsāra</em>)</p>

niyyānika|niyyaanika|niyyanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaanika</span></p><p>adjective leading out (of saṃsāra), leading to salvation, salutary, sanctifying, saving profitable DN.i.235, DN.i.237; SN.i.220; SN.v.82, SN.v.166, SN.v.255, SN.v.379 sq. Ja.i.48 (a˚), Ja.i.106; Dhs.277, Dhs.339, Dhs.505 (cp. <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> pp. 82 335); Vb.12, Vb.19, Vb.56, Vb.319, Vb.324; Ne.29, Ne.31, Ne.63, Ne.83; Dhp-a.iv.87<br />■ Also found in spelling <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABy%C4%81nika">nīyānika</a></em> e.g. AN.iii.132 (ariyā diṭṭhi n. nīyāti takkarassa sammādukkha-khayāya); DN-a.i.89 (anīyānikattā tiracchanabhūtā kathā). Niyyama(ka)</p><p>to niyyāna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīyānika">nīyānika</a></p>

niyyāsa|niyyaasa|niyyasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaasa</span></p><p>any exudation (of plants or trees), as gum, resin, juice, etc. Vism.74 (˚rukkha, one of the 8 kinds of trees), Vism.360 (paggharitan- rukkha). Cp. <em><a href="bword://niv%C4%81y%C4%81sa">nivāyāsa</a></em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. niryāsa, Halāyudha 5, 75</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nivāyāsa">nivāyāsa</a></p>

niyyāsi|niyyaasi|niyyasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaasi</span></p><p>of niyyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyāsuṃ|niyyaasu.m|niyyasum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaasu.m</span></p><p>of niyyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyāta|niyyaata|niyyata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaata</span></p><p>of niyyāti laid by; given, handed over; gone forth; gone away</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyāta|niyyaata|niyyata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaata</span></p><p>past participle = <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81dita">niyyādita</a></em> MN.i.360.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyādita">niyyādita</a></p>

niyyātana|niyyaatana|niyyatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatana</span></p><p>returning, return to (-˚) Ja.v.497 (saka-raṭṭha˚); Vism.556; DN-a.i.234.</p><p>fr. niyyāti</p>

niyyātar|niyyaatar|niyyatar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://niyyātar">niyyātar</a></p><p>one who leaves (<em>saṃsara</em>)</p>

niyyātar|niyyaatar|niyyatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatar</span></p><p>guide, leader MN.i.523 sq.</p><p>n. ag. to niyyāma</p>

niyyātayati|niyyaatayati|niyyatayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyātesi|niyyaatesi|niyyatesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatesi</span></p><p>of niyyāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyātetabba|niyyaatetabba|niyyatetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatetabba</span></p><p>of niyyāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyāteti|niyyaateti|niyyateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaateti</span></p><ol><li>hands back; restores; pays back.</li><li>hands over (ownership or responsibility); entrusts; gives (formally), dedicates.</li></ol>

niyyāteti|niyyaateti|niyyateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaateti</span></p><p>Niyyādeti &amp; niyyāteti &amp; nīyādeti</p><p>(to) give (back), give into charge, give over, assign, dedicate, to present, denote SN.i.131 (niyyātayāmi); SN.iv.181 (sāmikānaṃ gāvo), SN.iv.194; Ja.i.30 Ja.i.66, Ja.i.496; Ja.ii.106, Ja.ii.133; Vv.46#8 niyyādesi = sampaṭicchāpesi adāsi Vv-a.199); Pv.iii.2#11 (niyātayiṃsu = adaṃsu Pv-a.184); Vism.115 (t); Dhp-a.i.70; Dhp-a.ii.87; Vv-a.33, Vv-a.67; Pv-a.20 (vihāraṃ nīyādetvā), Pv-a.25 (= uddissati dadāti), Pv-a.42, Pv-a.81, Pv-a.276 (at all Pv-a passages as nī˚)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81dita">niyyādita</a></em> Cp. similarly paṭiyādeti &amp; paṭiyādita.</p><p>cp. Sk. ni-or nir-yātayati, Caus. of ni(r)yatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyādita">niyyādita</a></p>

niyyātetvā|niyyaatetvaa|niyyatetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatetvaa</span></p><p>of niyyāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyāti|niyyaati|niyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaati</span></p><p>goes out, goes forth (to); leaves; esp. leaves <em>saṃsara</em>.</p>

niyyāti|niyyaati|niyyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaati</span></p><p>to go out, get out (esp. of saṃsāra); SN.v.6 (niyyanti dhīrā lokamhā); Snp-a.212 aor. <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81si">niyyāsi</a></em> DN.i.49, DN.i.108; Ja.i.263; Snp.417; 3rd pl. <em>niyyiṃsu</em> AN.v.195; fut. <em>niyyassati</em> AN.v.194<br />■ See also niyyāna &amp; niyyānika.</p><p>Sk. niryāti, nis + yāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāsi">niyyāsi</a></p>

niyyātita|niyyaatita|niyyatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatita</span></p><p>of niyyāteti handed back, restored; handed over, entrusted; given</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāteti">niyyāteti</a></p>

niyyātuṃ|niyyaatu.m|niyyatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaatu.m</span></p><p>of niyyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyāyanta|niyyaayanta|niyyayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaayanta</span></p><p>of niyyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyāyati|niyyaayati|niyyayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyaayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

niyyūha|niyyuuha|niyyuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyuuha</span></p><p>projection, a projecting room; a pinnacle, a turret.</p>

niyyūha|niyyuuha|niyyuha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyyuuha</span></p><p>pinnacle, turret, gate MN.i.253; DN-a.i.284 (pāsāda +).</p><p>Sk. niryūha (&amp; nirvyūha?), perhaps to; <em>vah</em></p>

niyāma|niyaama|niyama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaama</span></p><p>fixed rule; a particular or specified way or manner; an invariable practice or experience; a determined or inevitable outcome, certainty; an assured state; what leads to an inevitable outcome, the way to an end.</p>

niyāma|niyaama|niyama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaama</span></p><p>way, way to an end or aim, esp. to salvation, right way (sammatta˚); method manner, practice SN.i.196; SN.iii.225 (sammatta˚); AN.i.122; Snp.371 (˚dassin = sammatta-niyāmabhūtassa maggassa dassāvin Snp-a.365); Mnd.314 (˚avakkanti); Cnd.358 (= cattāro maggā); Pts.ii.236 sq. (sammatta˚ okkamati); Pp.13, Pp.15; Vb.342<br />■ <em>niyāmena</em> (instr.) adv in this way, by way of, according to Ja.i.278; Ja.iv.139 Ja.iv.414 (suta˚ as he had heard); Dhp-a.i.79; Dhp-a.ii.9, Dhp-a.ii.21; Vv-a.4; Pv-a.260; Kvu trs. 383<br />■ <em>aniyāmena</em> (see also aniyāmena) without order, aimlessly, at random Ja.v.337.</p><p>Sk. niyama &amp; niyāma</p>

niyāmaka|niyaamaka|niyamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaamaka</span></p><p>Niyāmaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective sure of or in, founded in, or leading to, completed in DN.i.190 (dhamma-n. paṭipadā, cp. niyamatā).</p><p>either to niyama or niyāma</p><p>Niyāmaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(see <em>niyyāmaka</em>) ship’s captain Vism.137 (simile).</p>

niyāmatā|niyaamataa|niyamata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaamataa</span></p><p>the having an inevitable outcome; a state of certainty, an assured or determined state; a rule.</p>

niyāmatā|niyaamataa|niyamata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaamataa</span></p><p>state of being settled, certainty, reliance surety, being fixed in (-˚) SN.ii.25 (dhamma˚ + dhammaṭṭhitatā); AN.i.286 (id.), Ja.i.113 (saddhammassa n assurance of…); Kv.586 (accanta˚ final assurance).</p><p>abstr. to niyāma, influenced in meaning by niyama</p>

niyāmeti|niyaameti|niyameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaameti</span></p><p>to restrain, control, govern, guide Mil.378 (nāvaṃ).</p><p>Denom. fr. niyāma or niyama</p>

niyāteti|niyaateti|niyateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyaateti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81deti">niyyādeti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyādeti">niyyādeti</a></p>

niyūha|niyuuha|niyuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niyuuha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyūha">niyyūha</a></p>

niḍḍha|ni.d.dha|niddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.d.dha</span></p><p>nest, place, seat Dhp.148 (variant reading niḷa).</p><p>Vedic nīḍa resting-place ni + <em>sad</em> “sitting down”</p>

niḍḍāpetabba|ni.d.daapetabba|niddapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.d.daapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niddāpetabba">niddāpetabba</a></p>

niḍḍāyati|ni.d.daayati|niddayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.d.daayati</span></p><p>to cut out, to weed DN.i.231 (niddāyit˚); Iti.56 (as variant reading niddāta for niṇhāta, q.v.); Ja.i.215. Caus niḍḍāpeti to cause to weed, to have weeds dug up Vin.ii.180.</p><p>Sk. nirdāti, nis + dāyati, cp. Sk. nirdātar weeder</p>

niḍḍāyitabba|ni.d.daayitabba|niddayitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.d.daayitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niddāyitabba">niddāyitabba</a></p>

niṅka|ni"nka|ninka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni"nka</span></p><p>NiṅkaNika</p><p>A deva who visits the Buddha in the company of several other devas and utters a verse in praise of Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta. SN.i.65<em>f.</em></p>

niṇhāta|ni.nhaata|ninhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.nhaata</span></p><p>adjective cleansed, purified Iti.56 (˚pāpaka = sinless; with several vv. ll amongst which niddāta of niḍḍāyati = cleansed of weeds = Mnd.58 (ninhāta˚) = Cnd.514 (ninhāta, variant reading SS ninnahāta).</p><p>Sk. *niḥsnāta, nis + nahāta</p>

niṭṭha|ni.t.tha|nittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective dependent on, resting on, intent upon SN.iii.13 (accanta˚); Mnd.263 (rūpa˚).</p><p>Sk. niṣṭha, ni + ˚tha; cp. niṭṭhā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

niṭṭhapetvā|ni.t.thapetvaa|nitthapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhāpetvā">niṭṭhāpetvā</a></p>

niṭṭhapita|ni.t.thapita|nitthapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thapita</span></p><p>Niṭṭhāpita &amp; niṭṭhapita</p><p>accomplished, performed, carried out Ja.i.86, Ja.i.172 (˚ṭha˚), Ja.i.201.</p><p>pp. of niṭṭhāpeti</p>

niṭṭhita|ni.t.thita|nitthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thita</span></p><p>of niṭṭhāti brought or come to an end, finished, completed; ready</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhāti">niṭṭhāti</a></p>

niṭṭhita|ni.t.thita|nitthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thita</span></p><p>adjective brought or come to an end, finished, accomplished; (made) ready, prepared (i.e. the preparations being finished) Vin.i.35; DN.i.109 (bhattaṃ: the meal is ready); DN.ii.127 (id.); Ja.i.255 (id.); Ja.ii.48; Ja.iii.537 (finished); Vv-a.188; Pv-a.81; &amp; often at conclusion of books &amp; chapters. aniṭṭhita not completed Dhp-a.iii.172<br />■ <em>su˚</em>; well finished, nicely got up, accomplished Snp.48, Snp.240. Cp. pari˚.</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhita (niḥṣṭhita), nis + ṭhita, cp. niṭṭhāti</p>

niṭṭhubhana|ni.t.thubhana|nitthubhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thubhana</span></p><p>spitting out, spittle Ja.i.47; Pv-a.80 (= kheḷa, variant reading SS niṭṭhuvana, BB niṭhūna).</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhīvana, see <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></em> &amp; cp. Prk. niṭṭhuhana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

niṭṭhubhati|ni.t.thubhati|nitthubhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thubhati</span></p><p>spits; spits out.</p>

niṭṭhubhati|ni.t.thubhati|nitthubhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thubhati</span></p><p>Niṭṭhubhati &amp; nuṭṭhubhati</p><p><em>nuṭṭhubhati</em> at Vin.i.271; Ja.i.459; also <em>niṭṭhuhaṭi</em> to spit out, to expectorate Vin.i.271 (nuṭṭhuhitvā), Vin.iii.132 (id.); Ja.ii.105, Ja.ii.117 (nuṭṭh˚); Ja.vi.367; Dhp-a.ii.36 (niṭṭhuhitvā). pp. <em>nuṭṭhubhita</em> Sdhp.121<br />■ Cp. oṭṭhubhati</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhubhati, but in meaning = Sk. niṣṭhīvati nis + *thīv, <em>stubh</em> taking the function of <em>ṣṭhīv</em>, since <em>stubh</em> itself is represented by thavati &amp; thometi</p>

niṭṭhubhitabba|ni.t.thubhitabba|nitthubhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thubhitabba</span></p><p>of niṭṭhubhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

niṭṭhubhitvā|ni.t.thubhitvaa|nitthubhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thubhitvaa</span></p><p>of niṭṭhubhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

niṭṭhuhati|ni.t.thuhati|nitthuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thuhati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

niṭṭhurin|ni.t.thurin|nitthurin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thurin</span></p><p>adjective rough, hard, cruel, merciless Snp.952 (a˚; this reading is mentioned as variant reading by Bdhgh at Snp-a.569, &amp; the reading; <em>anuddharī</em> given; vv. ll SS anuṭṭhurī, BB anuṭṭharī, explained as anissukī Mnd.440 however has aniṭṭhurī with expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of nitthuriya as under issā at Vb.357).</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhura or niṣṭhūra, ni + thūra = thūla; cp. Prk. niṭṭhura</p>

niṭṭhuriya|ni.t.thuriya|nitthuriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thuriya</span></p><p>hardness, harshness, roughness Mnd.440; Cnd.484 (in exegesis of makkha) Vb.357.</p><p>cp. Sk. niṣṭhuratva</p>

niṭṭhā|ni.t.thaa|nittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaa</span></p><p>niṭṭhā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>completion; culminating point; end, object, aim.</li><li>death.</li></ol><p>niṭṭhā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>certain knowledge, decision, conclusion.</p>

niṭṭhā|ni.t.thaa|nittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaa</span></p><p>Niṭṭhā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine basis, foundation, familiarity with Snp.864 (expl Snp-a.551 by samiddhi, but see Mnd.263).</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhā; ni + ṭhā, abstr. of adj<br />■ suff. ˚ṭha</p><p>Niṭṭhā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine end, conclusion; perfection, height, summit; object aim Vin.i.255; SN.ii.186; AN.i.279 (object); Pts.i.161 <em>niṭṭhaṃ gacchati</em> to come to an end; fig. to reach perfection be completed in the faith MN.i.176; Ja.i.201; Mil.310; freq. in pp. <em>niṭṭhaṃ gata</em> (niṭṭhangata) one who has attained perfection (= pabbajitānaṃ arahattaṃ patta) Dhp-a.iv.70; SN.iii.99 (a˚); AN.ii.175; AN.iii.450 AN.v.119 sq.; Dhp.351; Pts.i.81, Pts.i.161.</p><p>Vedic niṣṭhā (niḥṣṭhā), nis + ṭhā from ˚ṭha</p>

niṭṭhāgata|ni.t.thaagata|nitthagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaagata</span></p><p>decided, certain, convinced.</p>

niṭṭhām gaccathi|ni.t.thaam gaccathi|nittham gaccathi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaam gaccathi</span></p><p>is decided, is certain; knows; concludes, is convinced; ( <em>~aṃ gata</em> is often written <em>niṭṭhaṅgata</em>, or occasionally there is <em>niṭṭhāgata</em>) reaches completion; achieves its aim or culminating point.</p>

niṭṭhāna|ni.t.thaana|nitthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaana</span></p><p>(from niṭṭhāti) completion, culmination; being finished.</p>

niṭṭhāna|ni.t.thaana|nitthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaana</span></p><p>being finished, carrying out, execution, performance DN.i.141; Thag-a.19 (= avasāya). Cp. san˚.</p><p>abstr. of niṭṭhāti</p>

niṭṭhānantika|ni.t.thaanantika|nitthanantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaanantika</span></p><p>defined by completion.</p>

niṭṭhāpesi|ni.t.thaapesi|nitthapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaapesi</span></p><p>caused to accomplish; caused to finish; caused to carry out.</p>

niṭṭhāpeti|ni.t.thaapeti|nitthapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaapeti</span></p><p>to carry out, perform; prepare, make ready, accomplish Ja.i.86, Ja.i.290; Ja.vi.366; Dhp-a.iii.172<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81pita">niṭṭhāpita</a></em> Cp. pari˚.</p><p>Caus. to niṭṭhāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhāpita">niṭṭhāpita</a></p>

niṭṭhāpetvā|ni.t.thaapetvaa|nitthapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaapetvaa</span></p><p>having caused to accomplish; having caused to finish; having caused to carry out.</p>

niṭṭhāpita|ni.t.thaapita|nitthapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaapita</span></p><p>Niṭṭhāpita &amp; niṭṭhapita</p><p>accomplished, performed, carried out Ja.i.86, Ja.i.172 (˚ṭha˚), Ja.i.201.</p><p>pp. of niṭṭhāpeti</p>

niṭṭhāti|ni.t.thaati|nitthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaati</span></p><p>is finished, comes to an end; is completed; reaches completion.</p>

niṭṭhāti|ni.t.thaati|nitthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaati</span></p><p>to be at an end, to be finished Ja.i.220; Ja.iv.391; Dhp-a.i.393<br />pp niṭṭhita, Caus <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81peti">niṭṭhāpeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. niṣṭiṣṭhati, nis + tiṭṭhati, the older *sthāti restored in compound</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhāpeti">niṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

niṭṭhāyati|ni.t.thaayati|nitthayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhāti">niṭṭhāti</a></p>

niṭṭhāṅgata|ni.t.thaa"ngata|nitthangata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ni.t.thaa"ngata</span></p><p>decided, certain, convinced.</p>

no
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>no<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>3 pl. us, me.</p><p>no<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pl. of us; to us; by us.</p><p>no<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pl.</p><ol><li><p>not; esp.:</p></li><li><p>and not, but not (often followed by ca or ca kho)</p></li><li><p>nor.</p></li><li><p>or not (either alone or with vā, udāhu etc)</p></li><li><p>no ce:</p><ol><li>if not (followed by a verb, or forming a sentence by itself)</li><li>may there not; I hope that … not.</li></ol></li><li><p>no! not so.</p></li></ol><p>no<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable emphatic and interrog. particle.</p>

no
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>No<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable affirm. &amp; emphatic part. = <em><a href="bword://nu">nu</a></em> (cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->): indeed, then, now Snp.457, Snp.875, Snp.1077; Ja.v.343 (api no api nu), Ja.v.435 (= nipātamattaṃ p. 437).</p><p>No<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable negative &amp; adversative particle = neither, nor, but not surely not, indeed not</p><ol><li>in neg. sentences: Snp.852, Snp.855, Snp.1040; Iti.103 (but not); Pv.ii.3#13 (but not) as answer: <em>no hi</em> etaṃ “indeed not, no indeed” Vin.i.17; DN.i.3; no hi idaṃ DN.i.105<br />■ no ca kho “but surely not” DN.i.34, DN.i.36; AN.v.195<br />■ Often emphasized by <em><a href="bword://na">na</a></em>, as <em>no na</em> not at all Ja.i.64; na no Snp.224 (= “avadhāraṇe” Kp-a.170); disjunctively <em>na hi… no</em> neither-nor Snp.813; <em>na no… na</em> neither-nor (notnor) Snp.455</li><li>in disjunctive questions: “or not”, as evaṃ hoti vā… no vā (is it so-or not) DN.i.61, DN.i.227 kacci… no (is it so—or not; Lat. ne-annon) DN.i.107 nu kho… no udāhu (is it that—or not; or rather DN.i.152</li><li><em>noce</em> (no ce = Sk. no ced) if not (opp sace) Snp.348, Snp.691, Snp.840; Ja.i.222; Ja.vi.365; Vv-a.69. Also in sense of “I hope not” Ja.v.378.</li></ol><p>Sk. no = na + u, a stronger na; cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>No<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>enclitic form, gen. dat. acc. pl. of pron. 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (we) = amhākaṃ, see <em><a href="bword://vaya%E1%B9%83">vayaṃ</a></em>; cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>Sk. naḥ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nu">nu</a>, <a href="bword://na">na</a>, <a href="bword://vayaṃ">vayaṃ</a></p>

nodeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚.</p><p>fr. <em>nud</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

nonīta|noniita|nonita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">noniita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://navanīta">navanīta</a></p>

nonīta|noniita|nonita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">noniita</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://navan%C4%ABta">navanīta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://navanīta">navanīta</a></p>

nu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i></p><ol><li>an emphatic particle (very often followed by <em>kho</em>):</li><li>indeed; surely.</li><li><ol><li>by itself.</li><li>combined with an interrogative pronoun or adverb.</li><li>in double questions (repeated, or followed by another particle)</li></ol></li></ol>

nu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable affirm<br />■ indef part. “then, now.”</p><ol><li>most freq. combined with interr pron. and followed by <em><a href="bword://kho">kho</a></em>, as kin nu kho Ja.ii.159 kacci Ja.i.279; kaccin nu (for kaccid nu) Ja.ii.133 kathan nu (kho) Vin.i.83; kattha Pv-a.22; etc.</li><li>as interr. part. (= Lat. ne, num) in enclitic position Vin.i.17; Ja.iii.52; Snp.866, Snp.871, Snp.1071; etc. As such also combined with na = <em><a href="bword://nanu">nanu</a></em> (Lat. nonne), which begins the sentence: Vin.ii.303. (nanu tvaṃ vuḍḍho vīsativasso ‘sī ti?) Pv.i.8#4; Pv-a.39, Pv-a.136 etc<br />■ Often combined with other emphatic or dubitative particles, like <em>[api nu](bword://api nu)</em> Vin.ii.303; DN.i.97; <em>nu idha</em>, contr. to <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABdha">nīdha</a></em> Vv.83#6 or with sandhi as <em>nu-v-idha</em> DN.i.108 (variant reading nu khv idha). Cp. na<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> nūna, no.</li></ol><p>Ved. nu, Idg. *nu, orig. adv. of time = now; cp. Lat. num (to nunc, now), see <em><a href="bword://n%C5%ABna">nūna</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kho">kho</a>, <a href="bword://nanu">nanu</a>, <a href="bword://api nu">api nu</a>, <a href="bword://nīdha">nīdha</a>, <a href="bword://nūna">nūna</a></p>

nuda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective expelling, casting out, dispelling; in <em>tamo˚</em>; dispelling darkness Snp.1133; Vv.35#2 (= viddhaṃsana Vv-a.161).</p><p>Sk. ˚nud &amp; ˚nuda, to nudati</p>

nudaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>or <em>Nūdaka</em> (-˚) = nuda Ja.v.401 (āsa-nūdaka).</p>

nudati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>impels, pushes; drives away, dispels; removes.</p>

nudati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to push, impel; expel, drive away, reject Dhp.28; Ja.iv.443; Dhp-a.i.259. aor. <strong>nudi</strong> Cnd.281. Cp apa˚, pa˚, vi˚<br />pp <em>nunna</em> (nuṇṇa). Nunna (nunna)</p><p>Vedic nudati; Idg. <em>*(s)neu</em> to push, cp. Sk. navate, Gr. νεύω &amp; νύσσω, Lat. nuo; Ags. neosian, Low Ger. nucken</p><p>See also: nudi</p>

nudeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nudati">nudati</a></p>

nujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nudati">nudati</a></p>

nunna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of nudeti impelled; driven away, dispelled, removed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nudeti">nudeti</a></p>

nunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Nunna (nuṇṇa)</p><p>thrust, pushed, driven away, removed Cnd.220 (ṇṇ) = khitta, cp. panuṇṇa AN.ii.41.</p><p>pp. of nudati</p>

nuṇṇa|nu.n.na|nunna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.n.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nunna">nunna</a></p>

nuṇṇa|nu.n.na|nunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.n.na</span></p><p>Nunna (nuṇṇa)</p><p>thrust, pushed, driven away, removed Cnd.220 (ṇṇ) = khitta, cp. panuṇṇa AN.ii.41.</p><p>pp. of nudati</p>

nuṭṭhubhati FIXME double|nu.t.thubhati FIXME double|nutthubhati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.t.thubhati FIXME double</span></p><p>Nuṭṭhubhati</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></em>. (aor. <em>nuṭṭhubhi</em>, e.g. Ja.ii.105).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

nuṭṭhubhati|nu.t.thubhati|nutthubhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.t.thubhati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

nuṭṭhubhati|nu.t.thubhati|nutthubhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.t.thubhati</span></p><p>Niṭṭhubhati &amp; nuṭṭhubhati</p><p><em>nuṭṭhubhati</em> at Vin.i.271; Ja.i.459; also <em>niṭṭhuhaṭi</em> to spit out, to expectorate Vin.i.271 (nuṭṭhuhitvā), Vin.iii.132 (id.); Ja.ii.105, Ja.ii.117 (nuṭṭh˚); Ja.vi.367; Dhp-a.ii.36 (niṭṭhuhitvā). pp. <em>nuṭṭhubhita</em> Sdhp.121<br />■ Cp. oṭṭhubhati</p><p>Sk. niṣṭhubhati, but in meaning = Sk. niṣṭhīvati nis + *thīv, <em>stubh</em> taking the function of <em>ṣṭhīv</em>, since <em>stubh</em> itself is represented by thavati &amp; thometi</p>

nuṭṭhubhitvā|nu.t.thubhitvaa|nutthubhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.t.thubhitvaa</span></p><p>of niṭṭhubhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

nuṭṭhuhati|nu.t.thuhati|nutthuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nu.t.thuhati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niṭṭhubhati">niṭṭhubhati</a></p>

nābhasa|naabhasa|nabhasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhasa</span></p><p>celestial, being in the sky.</p>

nābhi|naabhi|nabhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhi</span></p><ol><li>the navel.</li><li>the nave of a wheel.</li></ol>

nābhi|naabhi|nabhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhi</span></p><p>Nābhi &amp; Nābhī</p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>the navel AN.iii.240; Ja.i.238; DN-a.i.254 (where it is said that the Vessā (Vaiśyas) have sprung from the navel of Brahmā).</li><li>the nave of a wheel Vv.64#4 (pl. nabhyo &amp; nabbho SS = nābhiyo Vv-a.276) Ja.i.64; Ja.iv.277; Mil.115.</li></ol><p>Vedic nābhi, nābhī; Av. nabā; Gr. ὀμφαλός (navel); Lat. umbo &amp; umbilicus; Oir. imbliu (navel); Ags. nafu; Ohg. naba (nave), Ger. nabel = E nave &amp; navel</p>

nābhimaṇḍala|naabhima.n.dala|nabhimandala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhima.n.dala</span></p><p>the area of the navel.</p>

nābhī|naabhii|nabhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nābhi">nābhi</a></p>

nābhī|naabhii|nabhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naabhii</span></p><p>Nābhi &amp; Nābhī</p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>the navel AN.iii.240; Ja.i.238; DN-a.i.254 (where it is said that the Vessā (Vaiśyas) have sprung from the navel of Brahmā).</li><li>the nave of a wheel Vv.64#4 (pl. nabhyo &amp; nabbho SS = nābhiyo Vv-a.276) Ja.i.64; Ja.iv.277; Mil.115.</li></ol><p>Vedic nābhi, nābhī; Av. nabā; Gr. ὀμφαλός (navel); Lat. umbo &amp; umbilicus; Oir. imbliu (navel); Ags. nafu; Ohg. naba (nave), Ger. nabel = E nave &amp; navel</p>

nāda|naada|nada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naada</span></p><p>loud sound, roaring, roar Ja.i.19 (sīha˚), Ja.i.50 (koñca˚), Ja.i.150 (mahā˚). Cp. pa˚.</p><p>Sk. nāda, see <em><a href="bword://nadati">nadati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nadati">nadati</a></p>

nādayati|naadayati|nadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nadati">nadati</a></p>

nādeti|naadeti|nadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nadati">nadati</a></p>

nādhati|naadhati|nadhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadhati</span></p><p>to have need of to be in want of (c. gen.) Ja.v.90 (Com. explains by upatappati milāyati; thinking perhaps of <em>nalo</em> va chinno).</p><p>Sk. nādhate = nāthate (see <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81tha">nātha</a></em>), only in nadhamāna, cp. RV x.65, 5: nādhas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nātha">nātha</a></p>

nādi|naadi|nadi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadi</span></p><p>= nāda, loud sound, thundering (fig.) Vv.64#10.</p>

nādikā|naadikaa|nadika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadikaa</span></p><p>The Mahā Parinibbāna Sutta DN.ii.72<em>ff.</em> gives a list of the places at which the Buddha stopped during his last journey along that road—Ambalaṭṭhikā, Nāḷandā, Pāṭaligāma, Koṭigāma, Nādikā, Vesāli, Bhaṇḍagāma, Hatthigāma, Ambagāma, Jambugāma, Bhoganagara, Pāvā, and the Kakuttha River, beyond which lay the Mango grove and the Sāla grove of the Mallas.</p><p>25.743004,85.2566525town</p>

nādita|naadita|nadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nadita">nadita</a></p>

nādiya|naadiya|nadiya
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naadiya</span></p><p><em>Vitis assamica</em>, a common creeper with shiny leaves and which produces edible succulent black berries Ja.vi.536.</p>

nāga|naaga|naga
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaga</span></p><p>A class of beings classed with Garuḷas and Supaṇṇas and playing a prominent part in Buddhist folk lore. They are gifted with miraculous powers and great strength. Generally speaking, they are confused with snakes, chiefly the hooded Cobra, and their bodies are described as being those of snakes, though they can assume human form at will. The Vinaya Vin.ii.109 contains a list of four royal families of Nāgas (<em>Ahirājakulāni</em>): Virūpakkha, Erāpatha, Chabyāputta and Kaṇhāgotamaka. The enmity between the Nāgas and the Garulas is proverbial. DN.ii.258 In the Aṭāṇātiya Sutta, DN.iii.198<em>f.</em> speaking of dwellers of the Cātummahārajika world, the Nāgas are mentioned as occupying the Western Quarter, with Virūpakkha as their king.</p><p>The word Nāga is often used as an epithet of the Buddha and the Arahants.</p><p>In the accounts given of the Nāgas, there is undoubtedly great confusion between the Nāgas as supernatural beings, as snakes, elephants, and as the name of certain non Aryan tribes, but the confusion is too difficult to unravel.</p>

nāga|naaga|naga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaga</span></p><p>a</p><ol><li>a snake; a supematural serpent (usually living in water or under the earth), one of a race of mythical snakes (the enemies of the garuḷas)</li><li>an elephant.</li><li>the best or most excellent of its kind; a mighty being.</li></ol>

nāga|naaga|naga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaga</span></p><ol><li><p>a serpent or Nāga demon, playing a prominent part in Buddh. fairy-tales, gifted with miraculous powers &amp; great strength. They often act as fairies are classed with other divinities (see <em><a href="bword://devat%C4%81">devatā</a></em>), with whom they are sometimes friendly, sometimes at enmity (as with the Garuḷas) DN.i.54; SN.iii.240 sq. SN.v.47, SN.v.63; Bu. SN.i.30 (dīghāyukā mahiddhikā); Mil.23 Often with supaṇṇā (Garuḷas); Ja.i.64; Dhp-a.ii.4; Pv-a.272. Descriptions e.g. at Dhp-a.iii.231, Dhp-a.iii.242 sq.; see also compounds</p></li><li><p>an elephant, esp. a strong, stately animal (thus in combination hatthi-nāga characterising “a Nāga elephant”) &amp; freq. as symbol of strength &amp; endurance (“heroic”). Thus epithet of the Buddha &amp; of Arahants Popular etymologies of n. are based on the excellency of this animal (āguṃ na karoti = he is faultless, etc.): see Mnd.201 = Cnd.337; Thag.693; Pv-a.57</p><ol><li>the animal DN.i.49; SN.i.16; SN.ii.217, SN.ii.222; SN.iii.85; SN.v.351; AN.ii.116; AN.iii.156 sq.; Snp.543; Vv.5#5 (= hatthināga Vv-a.37); Pv.i.11#3. mahā˚ AN.iv.107, AN.iv.110</li><li>fig. hero or saint: SN.ii.277; SN.iii.83; MN.i.151, MN.i.386; Dhp.320; Snp.29, Snp.53, Snp.166, Snp.421, Snp.518. Of the Buddha: Snp.522, Snp.845 Snp.1058, Snp.1101; Mil.346 (Buddha˚).</li></ol></li><li><p>The Nāga-tree (now called “iron-wood tree,” the P. meaning “fairy tree”), noted for its hard wood &amp; great masses of red flowers (= Sk. nāgakesara, mesua ferrea Lin.): see cpds ˚rukkha, ˚puppha, ˚latā.</p></li></ol><ul><li><em>-āpalokita</em> “elephant-look” (turning the whole body) a mark of the Buddhas MN.i.337; cp. BSk. nāgâvalokita Divy.208;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://danta">danta</a></em> an ivory peg or pin, also used as a hook on a wall Vin.ii.117 (˚ka Vin.ii.114, Vin.ii.152); Ja.vi.382</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81%E1%B9%ADaka">nāṭaka</a></em> snakes as actors Dhp-a.iv.130;</li><li><em>-nāsūru</em> (f. (woman) having thighs like an elephant’s trunk Ja.v.297</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://puppha">puppha</a></em> iron-wood flower Mil.283;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bala">bala</a></em> the strength of an elephant Ja.i.265; Ja.ii.158;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhavana">bhavana</a></em> the world of snakes Mnd.448; Ja.iii.275; Dhp-a.iv.14;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C4%81%E1%B9%87avaka">māṇavaka</a></em> a young serpent Ja.iii.276; f. <em>-ikā</em> ib. 275; Dhp-a.iii.232</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C4%81j%C4%81">rājā</a></em> king of the Nāgas, i.e. serpents Ja.ii.111; Ja.iii.275; Snp.379 (Erāvaṇa, see detail Snp-a.368); Dhp-a.i.359 Dhp-a.iii.231, Dhp-a.iii.242 sq. (Ahicchatta); Dhp-a.iv.129 sq. (Paṇṇaka)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a></em> the iron-wood tree Ja.i.35 (cp. Mhvs.ii.249)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://lat%C4%81">latā</a></em> = rukkha Ja.i.80 (the Buddha’s toothpick made of its wood), Ja.i.232; Dhp-a.ii.211 (˚dantakaṭṭha toothpick)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vatta">vatta</a></em> habits of serpents Mnd.92, also adj. <em>-ika</em> ibid. Mnd.89</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vana">vana</a></em> elephant-grove Dhp.324; Dhp-a.iv.15;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vanika">vanika</a></em> el hunter MN.i.175; MN.iii.132;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://hata">hata</a></em> one who strikes the el (viz. the Buddha) Vin.ii.195.</li></ul><p>Ved. nāga; etym. of 1 perhaps fr. *snagh = Ags. snaca (snake) &amp; snaegl (snail); of 2 uncertain, perhaps a Non-Aryan word distorted by popular analogy to nāga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://devatā">devatā</a>, <a href="bword://danta">danta</a>, <a href="bword://nāṭaka">nāṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://puppha">puppha</a>, <a href="bword://bala">bala</a>, <a href="bword://bhavana">bhavana</a>, <a href="bword://māṇavaka">māṇavaka</a>, <a href="bword://rājā">rājā</a>, <a href="bword://rukkha">rukkha</a>, <a href="bword://latā">latā</a>, <a href="bword://vatta">vatta</a>, <a href="bword://vana">vana</a>, <a href="bword://vanika">vanika</a>, <a href="bword://hata">hata</a></p>

nāga<sup>1</sup>|naaga<sup>1</sup>|naga<sup>1</sup>
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaga<sup>1</sup></span></p><p>Iron-Wood Tree, Hindi <em>nagkesar</em>, <em>Mesua ferrea</em> Ja.i.35 Ja.i.80, a large evergreen tree with narrow pointed leaves, bright red and shiny when new, and which produces a large fragrant four-petaled white flower. The flower is referred to as <em>nāgamāḷika</em> Ja.vi.269 or <em>nāgapuppha</em> Mil.283 and even when dried retains their fragrance. When the iron-wood tree is in bloom on a Himalayan mountain, and when the gentle breezes are blowing, they waft the fragrance of the flowers for ten or 20 <em>yojana</em> Mil.283. According to Hinduism, the arrows of Kāma, the god of sensual love, are tipped with these flowers. The tree gets its English name from the exceptionally hard timber which is used for building.</p><p>Nāga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Cobra, Hindi <em>nag</em>, large snakes of the family Elapidae which are capable of flattening the long ribs in their necks into a hood when threatened. There are three species of cobra found in northern India. When referring to the cobra the common words for snakes, <em>āsīvisa</em> Ja.ii.274, <em><a href="bword://sappa">sappa</a></em> Ja.vi.6 and <em>nāga</em> Ja.vi.166 are used together with the comment that it has a hood, suggesting that the cobra was also known as “the hooded snake”. Snake charmers were a feature of street life in cities and towns Ja.iii.198 and they usually used cobras because of their impressive appearance, their ability to stand erect and their tendency to follow and therefore appear to dance to the moving flute played by the charmer. These charmers were believed to use special herbs and magic spells to mesmerize cobras in order to attract and then catch them. They would approach the snake, seize it by the tail, drag it backwards so that it would be stretched out full length and then use what was called a “goat’s foot stick” to pin its head down. Seizing it by the head and applying pressure to the sides would force the cobra’s mouth open so the charmer could spit the juice of certain herbs into it which would, it was believed, break its fangs. Over the next few days the cobra would be subjected to a series of procedures meant to pacify it and get it used to being handled. These procedures were called <em>cuṇṇamāna</em>, <em>tantamajjita</em> and <em>dussapoṭhima</em> and seem to have involved wrapping the cobra in cloth and then stretching, squeezing, massaging and striking it. After this the cobra was ready to be used Ja.iv.457. During their performances, charmers would make cobras dance and drape them around their necks Ja.i.370. They would feed them with frogs they had killed Ja.vi.458. The species of cobra favored by charmers has always been the nocturnal Indian Cobra, <em>Naja naja</em>, because daylight hampers its ability to strike. See <em>Kaṇhasappa</em> and <em>Sappa</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sappa">sappa</a>, Kaṇhasappa, Sappa</p>

nāgadanta|naagadanta|nagadanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagadanta</span></p><p>peg; a hook.</p>

nāgadantaka|naagadantaka|nagadantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagadantaka</span></p><p>peg; a hook.</p>

nāgadatta|naagadatta|nagadatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagadatta</span></p><p>He once lived in a forest tract in Kosala and was inclined to be indolent. A deva, noticing this, admonished him, and it is said that Nāgadatta paid heed to the warning. SN.i.200</p>

nāgahata|naagahata|nagahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagahata</span></p><p>who has struck or killed the mighty being.</p>

nāgalatā|naagalataa|nagalata
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagalataa</span></p><p>Uncertain, but perhaps the same as <em><a href="bword://tamb%C5%ABla">tambūla</a></em> Ja.i.80. The name means “snake creeper”.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tambūla">tambūla</a></p>

nāgapacāra|naagapacaara|nagapacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagapacaara</span></p><p>an attendant on a nāga.</p>

nāgapalokita|naagapalokita|nagapalokita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagapalokita</span></p><p>an elephant’s look (turning the whole body)</p>

nāgara|naagara|nagara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagara</span></p><p>citizen Ja.i.150; Ja.iv.404; Ja.v.385; Dāvs ii.85; Vv-a.31; Pv-a.19; Dhp-a.i.41.</p><p>Sk. nāgara, see <em><a href="bword://nagara">nagara</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nagara">nagara</a></p>

nāgarika|naagarika|nagarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagarika</span></p><p>adjective citizen-like, urbane, polite DN-a.i.282.</p><p>Sk. nāgarika</p>

nāgasamāla|naagasamaala|nagasamala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagasamaala</span></p><p>He was a Sākiyan and entered the Order when the Buddha visited his kinsmen at Kapilavatthu. For some time he was the Buddha’s personal attendant—e.g., when the Buddha breached the Mahāsīhanāda Sutta. MN.i.83</p><p>One day, when entering the city for alms, he saw a nautch girl gaily dressed, dancing to the accompaniment of music and contemplated her as the snare of Māra. Making this his topic of thought, he developed insight into the perishableness of life and became an arahant. Thag.267–270 Another day, while walking with the Buddha, they came to a cleft in the road, and the Buddha wished to go along one way, while Nāgasamāla wished to go along another, in spite of the Buddha’s warning that it was dangerous. In the end, he put the Buddha’s begging bowl and robe on the ground and left him. Brigands waylaid him and ill treated him, breaking his bowl and threatening to kill him. Thereupon he turned back to the Buddha and asked his forgiveness. Ud.viii.7</p>

nāgavallika|naagavallika|nagavallika
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagavallika</span></p><p>Uncertain, but perhaps the same as <em><a href="bword://tamb%C5%ABla">tambūla</a></em> Ja.vi.536. The name means “snake vine.”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tambūla">tambūla</a></p>

nāgavana|naagavana|nagavana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagavana</span></p><p>A park near Hatthigāma, belonging to Uggagahapati. It was there that he first met the Buddha and was converted. AN.iv.213</p><p>26.38202, 84.840543park</p>

nāgavanika|naagavanika|nagavanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagavanika</span></p><p>forester; an elephant tracker.</p>

nāgavatā|naagavataa|nagavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagavataa</span></p><p>the being (like) an elephant (?)</p>

nāginī|naaginii|nagini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaginii</span></p><p>female snake; a female nāga-snake.</p>

nāgita|naagita|nagita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagita</span></p><p>Nāgita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse speaks of the unique efficacy of practicing the Buddha’s path. Thag.86</p><p>Nāgita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>For some time the personal attendant of the Buddha. DN.i.151 AN.iii.31 AN.iii.341 AN.iv.341</p>

nāgāvalokita|naagaavalokita|nagavalokita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagaavalokita</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāgapalokita">nāgapalokita</a></p>

nāgī|naagii|nagi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naagii</span></p><p>female snake; a female nāga-snake.</p>

nāha|naaha|naha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaha</span></p><p>armour Ja.i.358 (sabba˚sannaddha). Cp. onāha.</p><p>cp. nayhati, naddha</p>

nāla|naala|nala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naala</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷa">nāḷa</a></p>

nāla|naala|nala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naala</span></p><p>Nāla &amp; Nāḷa</p><p>neuter a hollow stalk, esp. that of the water lily AN.iv.169; Ja.i.392 (˚pana variant reading ˚vana); Vv-a.43. See also nāḷikā &amp; nālī.</p><p>Sk. nāla, see <em>nala</em></p>

nālaka|naalaka|nalaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalaka</span></p><p>Nephew of Asita Kāladevala. When Asita realized that he would not live to see the Buddha, he sought out Nālaka and asked him to leave the world at once and become an ascetic and hold himself in readiness to profit by the Buddha’s Enlightenment. This Nālaka did. When the time came, he visited the Buddha to ask about the way of practice. Snp.838–862</p>

nālaṃ|naala.m|nalam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naala.m</span></p><p>adverb not enough, insufficient Iti.37; Ja.i.190; DN-a.i.167.</p><p>= na alaṃ</p>

nālikasaṇḍāsa|naalikasa.n.daasa|nalikasandasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalikasa.n.daasa</span></p><p>blowing pipe and tongs.</p>

nālikā|naalikaa|nalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalikaa</span></p><p>, ~a, n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷikā">nāḷikā</a></p>

nāliya|naaliya|naliya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaliya</span></p><p>, ~a, n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷikā">nāḷikā</a></p>

nāliyasāka|naaliyasaaka|naliyasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaliyasaaka</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷikāsaka">nāḷikāsaka</a></p>

nāliyā|naaliyaa|naliya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaliyaa</span></p><p>, ~a, n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷikā">nāḷikā</a></p>

nālā|naalaa|nala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalaa</span></p><p>NālāNālakagāmakāNālagā</p><p>A village in Māgadha, near the Bodhi tree at Gayā. Thig.294</p><p>near Gayā3town</p>

nālāgiri|naalaagiri|nalagiri
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalaagiri</span></p><p>NālāgiriNāḷāgiri</p><p>An elephant of the royal stalls at Rājagaha. Devadatta, after several vain attempts to kill the Buddha, obtained Ajātasattu’s consent to use Nālagiri as a means of encompassing the Buddha’s death. Nālagiri was a fierce animal, and in order to increase his fierceness, Devadatta instructed his keeper to give him twice his usual amount of toddy. Proclamation was made, by the beating of drums, that the streets of the city should be cleared as Nālāgiri would be let loose upon them. When the Buddha was informed of this and warned against going into the city for alms, he ignored the warning, and went into Rājagaha with the monks of the eighteen monasteries of the city. At the sight of Nālāgiri all the people fled in terror. Ānanda, seeing the elephant advancing towards the Buddha, went, in spite of the Buddha’s orders to the contrary, and stood in front of the Buddha, who had to make use of his supernatural power to remove him from his place. The Buddha spoke to him, suffusing him with all the love at his command, and, stretching out his right hand, he stroked the animal’s forehead. Thrilling with joy at the touch, Nālāgiri sank on his knees before the Buddha. The Buddha returned to Veḷuvana. Vin.ii.194<em>f.</em></p>

nālī|naalii|nali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naalii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷī">nāḷī</a></p>

nāma|naama|nama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naama</span></p><p>nāma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li>by name, called.</li><li>as a marker of emphasis<ol><li>to mark a word being defined or explained.</li><li>to emphasize a preceding word; with interrogatives or other particles (often expressing astonishment or reproach):</li></ol></li></ol><p>nāma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter</p><ol><li>a name, a personal name.</li><li>(as associated with and contrasted with rūpa) the name, which differentiates an individual by verbal means; sometimes defined as what is non-material in a person, the mental components; or as the four (or three) non-material khandhas.</li></ol>

nāma|naama|nama
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naama</span></p><p>name.</p><ol><li><em>Literal.</em> nom. nāmaṃ SN.i.39; Snp.808; Ja.ii.131; Mil.27; acc. nāmaṃ Pv-a.145 (likhi: he wrote her name)<br />■ nāmaṃ karoti to give a name Snp.344 Cnd.466 (n’ etaṃ nāmaṃ mātarā kataṃ on “Bhagavā”) Ja.i.203, Ja.i.262 (w. double acc.)<br />■ nāmaṃ gaṇhāti to call by name, to enumerate Ja.iv.402; Pv-a.18 (variant reading BB nāmato g.). Definitions at Vin.iv.6 (two kinds: hīna &amp; ukkatṭha˚) and at Vism.528 (= namanalakkhaṇa)</li><li><em>Specified.</em> nāma as metaphysical term is opposed to <em><a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em>, &amp; comprises the 4 immaterial factors of an individual (arūpino khandhā, viz. vedanā saññā sankhāra viññāṇa; see <em><a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></em> II. B<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->). These as the noëtic principle combined with the material principle make up the individual as it is distinguished by “name &amp; body” from other individuals. Thus nāmarūpa individuality, individual being. These two are inseparable (aññamaññûpanissitā ete dhammā, ekato va uppajjanti Mil.49). SN.i.35 (yattha n. ca rūpañ ca asesaṃ uparujjhati taṃ te dhammaṃ idh’ aññāya acchiduṃ bhavabandhanaṃ); Snp.1036, Snp.1100; Mnd.435 = Cnd.339 (nāma = cattāro arūpino khandhā); Dhp-a.iv.100 (on Dhp.367): vedanādīnaṃ catunnaṃ rūpakkhandhassa cā ti pañcannaṃ khandhānaṃ vasena pavattaṃ nāmarūpaṃ; Dhs-a.52: nāmarūpa-duke nāmakaraṇaṭṭhena nāmaṭṭhena namanaṭṭhena ca nāmaṃ ruppanaṭṭhena rūpaṃ. Cp. DN.i.223; DN.ii.32, DN.ii.34, DN.ii.56, DN.ii.62; SN.i.12 (taṇhā nrūpe), SN.i.23 (n-rūpasmiṃ asajjamāna); SN.ii.3, SN.ii.4, SN.ii.66 (nrūpassa avakkanti), SN.ii.101 sq. (id.); MN.i.53; AN.i.83, AN.i.176 AN.iii.400; AN.iv.385 (˚ārammaṇa); AN.v.51, AN.v.56; Snp.355, Snp.537 Snp.756, Snp.909; Dhp.367; Iti.35; Pts.i.193; Pts.ii.72, Pts.ii.112 sq. Vb.294; Ne.15 sq., Ne.28, Ne.69; Mil.46. Nāma + rūpa form an elementary pair DN.iii.212; Kp iv. Also in the Paṭicca-samuppāda (q.v.), where it is said to be caused (conditioned) by viññāṇa &amp; to cause saḷāyatana (the 6 senses), DN.ii.34; Vin.i.1 sq.; SN.ii.6 sq.; Snp.872 (nāmañ ca rūpañca paṭicca phassā; see in detail explained at Mnd.276). Synonymous with nāmarūpa is <em>nāmakāya</em> Snp.1074; Cnd.338; Pts.i.183; Ne.27, Ne.41, Ne.69, Ne.77. In this connection to be mentioned are var. definitions of nāma as the principle or distinguishing mark (“label” of the individual, given by Com<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, e.g. Mnd.109, Mnd.127 Kp-a.78; with which cp. Bdhgh’s speculation concerning the connotation of nāma mentioned by Mrs. Rh. D. at <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 341.</li><li><em>Use of Cases.</em> Instr. <em>nāmena</em> by name Pv-a.1 (Petavatthū ti n.); Mhvs.vii.32 (Sirīsavatthu n.)<br />■ acc. <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81ma">nāma</a></em> (the older form, cp. Sk. nāma by name SN.i.33, SN.i.235 (Anoma˚); Snp.153, Snp.177; Ja.i.59 (ko nām’ esa “who by name is this one” = what is his name), Ja.i.149 (nāmena nigrodhamigarājā n.), Ja.i.203 (kiṃsaddo nāma esa); Ja.ii.4; Ja.iii.187; Ja.vi.364 (kā nāma tvaṃ). See also evaṃnāma, kinnāma; &amp; cp. the foll.</li><li><em><a href="bword://n%C4%81ma">nāma</a></em> (acc.) as <em>adv.</em> is used as emphatic particle = just, indeed for sure, certainly Ja.i.222; Ja.ii.133, Ja.ii.160, Ja.ii.326; Ja.iii.90; Pv-a.6, Pv-a.13, Pv-a.63 etc. Therefore freq. in exclamation exhortation (“please,” certainly) Ja.vi.367; Dhp-a.iii.171; Pv-a.29 (n. detha <em>do</em> give); in combination with interr. pron. = now, then Ja.i.221 (kiṃ n.), Ja.i.266 (kathaṃ n.); Ja.iii.55 (kiṃ); Kp iv. (ekaṃ n. kiṃ); with neg. = not at all, certainly not Ja.i.222; Ja.ii.352; Ja.iii.126 etc<br />■ Often further emphasised or emphasising other part; e.g. <em><a href="bword://pi">pi</a></em> (= api) <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81ma">nāma</a></em> really, just so Vin.i.16 (seyyathā p. n.) Snp.p.15 (id.); Vv-a.22 (read nāma kāro); Pv-a.76 <em>app’</em> (= api) <em>eva n</em>. thus indeed, forsooth Vin.i.16; Iti.89 = MN.i.460; Ja.i.168; Pv.ii.2#6 (= <em>api nāma</em> Pv-a.80) <em>eva nāma</em> in truth Pv-a.2; <em>nāma tāva</em> certainly Dhp-a.i.392, etc.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-kamma</em> giving a name, naming, denotation Dhs.1306 Bdhd.83</li><li><em>-karaṇa</em> name-giving, “christening” Dhp-a.ii.87</li><li><em>-gahaṇa</em> receiving a name, “being christened” Ja.i.262 (˚divasa)</li><li><em>-gotta</em> ancestry, lineage SN.i.43 (˚ṁ na jīrati) Snp.648 Cnd.385 (mātāpettikaṁ n.)</li><li><em>-dheyya</em> assigning a name, name-giving Ja.iii.305 Ja.iv.449; Ja.v.496 Dhs.1306</li><li><em>-pada</em> ‣See <a href="bword://pada">pada</a></li><li><em>-matta</em> a mere name Mil.25</li></ul><p>Vedic nāman, cp. Gr. ο ̓́νομα (ἀν ώνυμος without name); Lat. nomen; Goth. namō; Ags. noma, Ohg namo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a>, <a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a>, <a href="bword://nāma">nāma</a>, <a href="bword://nāma">nāma</a>, <a href="bword://pi">pi</a>, <a href="bword://nāma">nāma</a>, <a href="bword://pada">pada</a></p>

