nānā atthi|naanaa atthi|nana atthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaa atthi</span></p><p>is separated; is different, is distinct.</p>

nānā bhavati|naanaa bhavati|nana bhavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaa bhavati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nānā atthi">nānā atthi</a></p>

nānā|naanaa|nana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaa</span></p><p>indeclinable variously, differently; separately; different, separate; various, of various kinds (frequently iic); (difference, diversity (?))</p>

nānā|naanaa|nana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaa</span></p><p>adverb variously differently.</p><ol><li>(abs.) AN.i.138 (on different sides, viz right ↔ left). Snp.878 (= na ekaṁ Snp-a.554 = vividhaṁ aññoññaṁ puthu na ekaṁ Mnd.285), Snp.884 sq.</li><li>more frequently in compounds, as first part of adj. or n. where it may be trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “different, divers, all kinds of” etc Before a double cons. the final ā is shortened: nānagga (for nānā + agga), nānappakāra etc. see below.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> (-rasa) all the choicest delicacies Ja.i.266 (˚bhojana, of food); Ja.vi.366; Pv-a.155 (˚dibbabhojana)</li><li><em>-ādhimuttikatā</em> diversity of dispositions DN-a.i.44 Ne.98;</li><li><em>-āvudhā</em> (pl.) various weapons Ja.i.150;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara%E1%B9%87a">karaṇa</a></em> difference, diversity Vin.i.339 (sangha˚); MN.ii.128 cp. Divy.222;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a></em> of all kinds of descent Pv.ii.9#16</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://citta">citta</a></em> of varying mind Ja.i.295 (itthiyo);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://jana">jana</a></em> all kinds of folk Snp.1102; Mnd.308 (puthu˚);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://titthiya">titthiya</a></em> of var. sects DN.iii.16 sq.;</li><li><em>-pakkāra</em> various, manifold Ja.i.52 (sakuṇā), Ja.i.127, Ja.i.278 (phalāni); DN-a.i.148 (āvudhā); Pv-a.50, Pv-a.123 Pv-a.135;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ratta">ratta</a></em> multi-coloured Snp.287; Ja.vi.230;</li><li><em>-rasā</em> (pl. all kinds of dainties Pv.ii.9#11;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> difference of opinion DN.i.236;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vidha">vidha</a></em> divers, various, motley Pv-a.53, Pv-a.96 Pv-a.113, and passim;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83v%C4%81saka">saṃvāsaka</a></em> living in a different part or living apart Vin.i.134 sq. (opp. samāna˚), Vin.i.321; Vin.ii.162. Nabhi &amp; Nabhi</li></ul><p>Ved. nānā, a redupl. nā (emphatic particle, see *<a href="bword://na">na</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) “so and so,” i.e. various, of all kinds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://karaṇa">karaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>, <a href="bword://citta">citta</a>, <a href="bword://jana">jana</a>, <a href="bword://titthiya">titthiya</a>, <a href="bword://ratta">ratta</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a>, <a href="bword://vidha">vidha</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvāsaka">saṃvāsaka</a>, <a href="bword://na">na</a></p>

nānābhava|naanaabhava|nanabhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaabhava</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nānābhāva">nānābhāva</a></p>

nānābhāva|naanaabhaava|nanabhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaabhaava</span></p><p>being apart; separation; difference, diversity.</p>

nānācaya|naanaacaya|nanacaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaacaya</span></p><p>consisting of various mixtures.</p>

nānādiṭṭhika|naanaadi.t.thika|nanaditthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaadi.t.thika</span></p><p>holding various (false) views or theories; holding different views.</p>

nānājacca|naanaajacca|nanajacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaajacca</span></p><p>belonging to various families or classes.</p>

nānākaraṇa|naanaakara.na|nanakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaakara.na</span></p><p>difference; distinction; separation.</p>

nānākhantika|naanaakhantika|nanakhantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaakhantika</span></p><p>holding various or different preferred views.</p>

nānārajja|naanaarajja|nanarajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaarajja</span></p><p>or neuter a surgical probe (?)</p>

nānārucika|naanaarucika|nanarucika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaarucika</span></p><p>having various wishes; having different aims.</p>

nānāsaṃvāsaka|naanaasa.mvaasaka|nanasamvasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaasa.mvaasaka</span></p><p>who belongs to a different community (for carrying out official acts of the <em>saṅgha</em>); who is separated from the <em>saṅgha</em> community.</p>

nānāverajjagata|naanaaverajjagata|nanaverajjagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaaverajjagata</span></p><p>gone to various different kingdoms.</p>

nānāverajjaka|naanaaverajjaka|nanaverajjaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaaverajjaka</span></p><p>from various different kingdoms.</p>

nānāvihita|naanaavihita|nanavihita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naanaavihita</span></p><p>of various kinds.</p>

nārada|naarada|narada
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naarada</span></p><p>A monk, mentioned once as staying at the Ghositārāma in Kosambī, with Musila, Savittha, and Ānanda. In the course of discussion he declares that, though aware of the nature of nibbana, he is not an arahant. SN.ii.115<em>f.</em> Elsewhere AN.iii.57<em>f.</em> he is mentioned as staying in the Kukkuṭārāma in Pāṭaliputta. At that time King Muṇḍa was grieving over the death of his wife, Bhaddā, to the neglect of everything else, and his treasurer, Piyaka, suggested that he should visit Nārada. The king agreed, and Nārada preached to him on the inevitable ness of old age, disease, death, etc. Muṇḍa was consoled, and buried the body of his wife, which he had till then preserved.</p>

nārāca|naaraaca|naraca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaraaca</span></p><p>an iron arrow; an arrow.</p>

nārāca|naaraaca|naraca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaraaca</span></p><p>an iron weapon, an arrow or javelin MN.i.429; Ja.iii.322; Mil.105, Mil.244, Mil.418 <em>-<a href="bword://valaya">valaya</a></em> an iron ring or collar (?) Mhvs.vii.20 (Com “vaṭṭita-assanārāca-pasa” = a noose formed by bending the ends of the n. into a circle).</p><p>Sk. nārāca; perhaps for *nāḍāca &amp; conn. with nālīka, a kind of arrow, to nāḷa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://valaya">valaya</a></p>

nārī|naarii|nari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naarii</span></p><p>woman; a female.</p>

nārī|naarii|nari
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naarii</span></p><p>woman, wife, female Snp.301, Snp.836; Dhp.284; Ja.i.60; Ja.iii.395; Ja.iv.396 (˚gaṇa); Vv.6#1, Vv.44#16; Pv.i.9#1 (= itthi Pv-a.44). pl. nariyo (Snp.299, Snp.304, Snp.703), nāriyo (Snp.703 variant reading BB; Pv.ii.9#52). combined with <em><a href="bword://nara">nara</a></em> as naranārī, male &amp; female (angels), e.g. Vv.53#8 Pv.ii.11#2 (see <em><a href="bword://nara">nara</a></em>). Nala &amp; Nala;</p><p>Sk. nārī to nara man, orig. “the one belonging to the man”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nara">nara</a>, <a href="bword://nara">nara</a></p>

nārīsahanāmasavhaya|naariisahanaamasavhaya|narisahanamasavhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naariisahanaamasavhaya</span></p><p>called by a feminine name.</p>

nāsa|naasa|nasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasa</span></p><p>destruction, ruin, death Ja.i.5, Ja.i.256; Sdhp.58, Sdhp.319. Usually vi˚, also adj. vināsaka Cp. panassati.</p><p>Sk. nāśa, see <em><a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nāsacchidda|naasacchidda|nasacchidda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasacchidda</span></p><p>nostril.</p>

nāsacchinna|naasacchinna|nasacchinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasacchinna</span></p><p>(one) whose nose has been cut or cut off.</p>

nāsana|naasana|nasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasana</span></p><p>destruction, abandoning, expulsion, in <em>-antika</em> (adj.) a bhikkhu who is under the penalty of expulsion Vin.i.255.</p><p>Sk. nāśana</p>

nāsanāntika|naasanaantika|nasanantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasanaantika</span></p><p>defined by loss or destruction.</p>

nāsayati|naasayati|nasayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsayi|naasayi|nasayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasayi</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsayitvāna|naasayitvaana|nasayitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasayitvaana</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsesi|naasesi|nasesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasesi</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsesuṃ|naasesu.m|nasesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasesu.m</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsetabba|naasetabba|nasetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāseti|naaseti|naseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaseti</span></p><p>of nassati brings to naught; loses, lets disappear; spoils; destroys, ruins; removes; drives away; esp. expels from the <em>saṅgha</em> (permanently or temporarily)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nassati">nassati</a></p>

nāseti|naaseti|naseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naaseti</span></p><ol><li>to destroy, spoil, ruin; to kill Ja.i.59; Ja.ii.105, Ja.ii.150; Ja.iii.279, Ja.iii.418.</li><li>to atone for a fault (with abl.) Vin.i.85, Vin.i.86, Vin.i.173 etc. Cp. vi˚.</li></ol><p>Sk. nāśayati, Caus. of nassati, q.v.</p>

nāsetva|naasetva|nasetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasetva</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsika|naasika|nasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the nose, nasal, in <em>-sota</em> the nostril or nose (orig. “sense of smell” DN.i.106; Snp.p.108.</p><p>cp. Sk. nāsikya</p>

nāsikasota|naasikasota|nasikasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasikasota</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāsikāsota">nāsikāsota</a></p>

nāsikā|naasikaa|nasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasikaa</span></p><p>nostril; a nose; a snout.</p>

nāsikāloma|naasikaaloma|nasikaloma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasikaaloma</span></p><p>nostril hair.</p>

nāsikāsota|naasikaasota|nasikasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasikaasota</span></p><p>nostril.</p>

nāsita|naasita|nasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasita</span></p><p>of nāseti removed; expelled; spoiled, ruined; destroyed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsitabba|naasitabba|nasitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāsetabba">nāsetabba</a></p>

nāsitaka|naasitaka|nasitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasitaka</span></p><p>(one) who is subject to expulsion.</p>

nāsitaka|naasitaka|nasitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasitaka</span></p><p>adjective one who is ejected Vin.iv.140 (of a bhikkhu).</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81sa">nāsa</a></em> &amp; nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāsa">nāsa</a></p>

nāsiyamāna|naasiyamaana|nasiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasiyamaana</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nāsā|naasaa|nasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasaa</span></p><p>the nose (of a human or animal); the trunk of an elephant.</p>

nāsā|naasaa|nasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasaa</span></p><ol><li>the nose, Snp.198, Snp.608.</li><li>the trunk (of an elephant) Ja.v.297 (nāga˚-uru); Sdhp.153.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pu%E1%B9%ADa">puṭa</a></em> “nose-cup”; the outside of the nose, the nostril Ja.vi.74; Vism.195 (nāsa˚), Vism.264 (nāsa˚, but Kp-a.67 nāsā˚), Vism.283 (nāsa˚).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ta">vāta</a></em> wind, i.e. breath from the nostrils Ja.iii.276.</li></ul><p>Vedic nāsā (du.); Lat. nāris, Ohg. nasa, Ags. nasu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://puṭa">puṭa</a>, <a href="bword://vāta">vāta</a></p>

nāsāpeti|naasaapeti|nasapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naasaapeti</span></p><p>of nāseti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāseti">nāseti</a></p>

nātaputta|naataputta|nataputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naataputta</span></p><p>Mahāvīra, leader of the Jains.</p>

nātaputtiya|naataputtiya|nataputtiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n). a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naataputtiya</span></p><p>follower of Mahāvīra.</p>

nātha|naatha|natha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naatha</span></p><p>protector, patron, lord; a husband; a prop, support; esp. the lord, the Buddha.</p>

nātha|naatha|natha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naatha</span></p><p>protector, refuge, help AN.v.23, AN.v.89; Dhp.160 (attā hi attano n.), Dhp.380; Snp.1131 (Nd ii.has nāga) Dhp-a.iv.117; Pv-a.1. <strong>lokanātha</strong> Saviour of the world (Ep. of the Buddha) Snp.995; Pv-a.42<br />■ <strong>anātha</strong> helpless, unprotected, poor Ja.i.6 (nāthânāthā rich poor); Pv-a.3 (˚sālā poor house) Pv-a.65 Cp. nādhati.</p><p>Ved. nātha, <em>nāth</em>, to which Goth. nipan (to support), Ohg. gināda (grace)</p><p>See also: lokanātha, anātha</p>

nāthakaraṇa|naathakara.na|nathakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naathakara.na</span></p><p>producing a support, making for protection.</p>

nāthaputta|naathaputta|nathaputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naathaputta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nātaputta">nātaputta</a></p>

nāthaputtiyā|naathaputtiyaa|nathaputtiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naathaputtiyaa</span></p><p>The followers of Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta. DN.iii.117</p>

nāvika|naavika|navika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavika</span></p><p>ferryman.</p>

nāvika|naavika|navika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavika</span></p><ol><li>a sailor, mariner Ja.ii.103; Ja.iv.142; Mil.359; Dāvs iv.43 (captain).</li><li>a ferryman Ja.ii.111; Ja.iii.230 (Avariya-pitā.).</li></ol><p>Sk. nāvika</p>

nāvikī|naavikii|naviki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavikii</span></p><p>ferry-woman.</p>

nāvindakī|naavindakii|navindaki
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavindakii</span></p><p>One of King Eḷeyya’s guards. He was a follower of Rāmaputta. AN.ii.180</p>

nāvutika|naavutika|navutika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavutika</span></p><p>ninety years old.</p>

nāvutika|naavutika|navutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavutika</span></p><p>adjective 90 years old Ja.iii.395 (˚ā itthi); Snp-a.172.</p><p>fr. navuti</p>

nāvā|naavaa|nava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavaa</span></p><p>ship; boat; trough.</p>

nāvā|naavaa|nava
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavaa</span></p><p>boat, ship Vin.iii.49 (q.v. for definition &amp; description) SN.i.106 <em>(eka-rukkhikā)</em>; SN.i.iii.155 = SN.i.v.51 = AN.iv.127 (sāmuddikā “a liner”); AN.ii.200; AN.iii.368; Snp.321, Snp.770, Snp.771; Dhp.369 (metaphor of the human body); Ja.i.239 Ja.ii.112; Ja.iii.126; Ja.iii.188; Ja.iv.2, Ja.iv.21, Ja.iv.138; Ja.v.75 (with “500 passengers), Ja.v.433; Ja.vi.160 (= nāvyā canal? or read nālaṃ?); Vv.6#1 (= pota Vv-a.42, with pop. etym. “satte netī ti nāvā ti vuccati”); Pv.iii.3#5 (= doṇi Pv-a.189) Mil.261 (100 cubits long); Dāvs iv.42; Pv-a.47, Pv-a.53 Sdhp.321. In simile Vism.690.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://tittha">tittha</a></em> a ferry Ja.iii.230;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%C3%B1cara%E1%B9%87a">sañcaraṇa</a></em> (a place for) the traffic of boats, a port Mil.359.</li></ul><p>Ved. nāuḥ &amp; nāvā, Gr.; ναϋς, Lat. navis</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tittha">tittha</a>, <a href="bword://sañcaraṇa">sañcaraṇa</a></p>

nāvāyika|naavaayika|navayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naavaayika</span></p><p>mariner, sailor, skipper Mil.365.</p><p>Sk. nāvāja = Gr. ναυηγός, cp. Lat. navigo</p>

nāyaka|naayaka|nayaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naayaka</span></p><p>guide, a leader; a chief, a lord; esp. a Buddha.</p>

nāyaka|naayaka|nayaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naayaka</span></p><p>leader, guide, lord, mostly as epithet of the Buddha (loka˚ “Lord of the World” Snp.991 (loka˚); Mhvs.vii.1 (id.); Sdhp.491 (tilokassa) bala-nāyakā gang leaders Ja.i.103.</p><p>BSk. nāyaka (cp. anāyaka without guide Avs.i.210); fr. neti; cp. naya</p>

nāyati|naayati|nayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naayati</span></p><p>of jānāti is known; is known as</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jānāti">jānāti</a></p>

nāyikā|naayikaa|nayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naayikaa</span></p><p>female guide, a female leader; a female chief, a mistress.</p>

nāḷa|naa.la|nala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.la</span></p><p>hollow stalk, esp. of the waterlily; a tube.</p>

nāḷa|naa.la|nala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.la</span></p><p>Nāla &amp; Nāḷa</p><p>neuter a hollow stalk, esp. that of the water lily AN.iv.169; Ja.i.392 (˚pana variant reading ˚vana); Vv-a.43. See also nāḷikā &amp; nālī.</p><p>Sk. nāla, see <em>nala</em></p>

nāḷandā|naa.landaa|nalanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.landaa</span></p><p>NāḷandāNālaNālakaNālikaUpatissagāma</p><p>Mentioned as a brahmin village in Māgadha, not far from Rājagaha. SN.iv.251 AN.v.120 AN.v.121 SN.v.161</p><p>Also mentioned as a town near Rājagaha. The Buddha is mentioned as having several times stayed there during his residence in Pāvārika’s mango grove, and while there he had discussions with Upāli-Gahapati and Dīghatapassī, SN.ii.110 MN.i.376<em>ff.</em> with Kevaṭṭa, DN.i.211<em>ff.</em> and also several conversations with Asibandhakaputta. SN.ii.311–323</p><p>The Buddha visited Nāḷandā during his last tour through Māgadha, and it was there that Sāriputta uttered his “lion’s roar,” affirming his faith in the Buddha, shortly before his death.DN.ii.81<em>f.</em> DN.iii.99<em>ff.</em> SN.v.159<em>ff.</em> The road from Rājagaha to Nāḷandā passed through Ambalaṭṭhikā, DN.ii.81 Vin.ii.287 and from Nāḷandā it went on to Pāṭaligāma. DN.ii.84 Between Rājagaha and Nāḷandā was situated the Bahuputta cetiya. SN.ii.220</p><p>According to the Kevaṭṭa Sutta, DN.i.211 in the Buddha’s time Nāḷandā was already an influential and prosperous town, thickly populated, though it was not till later that it became the centre of learning for which it afterwards became famous. There is a record in the Saṃyutta Nikāya SN.iv.322 of the town having been the victim of a severe famine during the Buddha’s time.</p><p>25.136951,85.4435211town</p>

nāḷi|naa.li|nali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.li</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷī">nāḷī</a></p>

nāḷijaṅgha|naa.lija"ngha|nalijangha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.lija"ngha</span></p><p>A brahmin, whom Mallikā sent to the Buddha to find out if it were true that the Buddha had said that loved ones brought morrow and tribulation. MN.ii.108</p>

nāḷikera|naa.likera|nalikera
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likera</span></p><p>Coconut Palm, also called <em>nārikera</em>, Hindi <em>nariyal</em>, <em>Coco nucifera</em> Ja.iv.159 Ja.v.384, a tall unbranched palm with large pinnate leaves. The woody flower ranges in color from creamy white to bright yellow and produces large oval fruits in which is a round nut with a white edible kernel. Coconut palms are of enormous economic importance, producing oil, copra, fiber and timber.</p>

nāḷikera|naa.likera|nalikera
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likera</span></p><p>the coconut tree Vv.44#13 Ja.iv.159; Ja.v.384; DN-a.i.83; Vv-a.162.</p><p>Sk. nārikera, nārikela, nalikera, nālikela: dialect, of uncertain etym.</p>

nāḷikerika|naa.likerika|nalikerika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likerika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the coconut tree Ja.v.417.</p>

nāḷikodana|naa.likodana|nalikodana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likodana</span></p><p>naḷikā measure of boiled rice.</p>

nāḷikā|naa.likaa|nalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likaa</span></p><p>, ~a, neuter a hollow stalk; a pipe or cylinder (esp. used to blow, or as a container, or as a small measure of capacity)</p>

nāḷikā|naa.likaa|nalika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likaa</span></p><p>stalk, shaft; a tube, pipe or cylinder for holding anything; a small measure of capacity Vin.ii.116 (sūci˚, cp. sūcighara, needle-case) DN.i.7 (= bhesajja˚ DN-a.i.89); AN.i.210; Ja.i.123 (taṇḍula a nāḷi full of rice); Ja.vi.366 (aḍḍha-n-matta); Cnd.229 Cp. pa˚.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://odana">odana</a></em> a nāḷi measure of boiled rice SN.i.82; Dhp-a.iv.17;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gabbha">gabbha</a></em> an (inner) room of tubular shape Vin.ii.152.</li></ul><p>Sk. nāḍikā &amp; nālikā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odana">odana</a>, <a href="bword://gabbha">gabbha</a></p>

nāḷikāgabbha|naa.likaagabbha|nalikagabbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likaagabbha</span></p><p>kind of (oblong) inner room.</p>

nāḷikāsaka|naa.likaasaka|nalikasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.likaasaka</span></p><p>kind of (prepared) vegetable.</p>

nāḷiyasāka|naa.liyasaaka|naliyasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.liyasaaka</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷikāsaka">nāḷikāsaka</a></p>

nāḷī|naa.lii|nali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.lii</span></p><p>hollow stalk, a tube, a pipe; a tubular container; a measure of capacity.</p>

nāḷī|naa.lii|nali
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.lii</span></p><p>(in compounds); <em><a href="bword://n%C4%81%E1%B8%B7i">nāḷi</a></em> a hollow stalk, tube, pipe; also a measure of capacity Vin.i.249; AN.iii.49; Ja.i.98 (suvaṇṇa˚), Ja.i.124 (taṇḍula˚), Ja.i.419; Ja.iii.220 (kaṇḍa˚ a quiver); Ja.iv.67; Dhp-a.ii.193 (tela˚), Dhp-a.ii.257. Cp pa˚.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADa">paṭṭa</a></em> a covering for the head, a cap Ja.vi.370, Ja.vi.444 (text ˚vaṭṭa);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://matta">matta</a></em> as much as a tube holds AN.ii.199; Pv-a.283; Dhp-a.ii.70; Ja.i.419 (of aja-laṇḍikā).</li></ul><p>Sk. nāḍī, see <em>nala</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nāḷi">nāḷi</a>, <a href="bword://paṭṭa">paṭṭa</a>, <a href="bword://matta">matta</a></p>

nāṭaka|naa.taka|nataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.taka</span></p><ol><li>(m.) a dancer, actor, player Ja.i.206; Ja.v.373; Dhp-a.iii.88; Dhp-a.iv.59, Dhp-a.iv.130; nāṭakitthi a dancing-girl, nautch-girl Dhp-a.iii.166; Vv-a.131.</li><li>(nt.) a play, pantomime Ja.i.59; Ja.v.279, also used coll. = dancing-woman Ja.i.59 (?), Ja.ii.395.</li></ol><p>Sk. nāṭaka; see <em><a href="bword://naccati">naccati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://naccati">naccati</a></p>

nāṭaputta|naa.taputta|nataputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.taputta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nātaputta">nātaputta</a></p>

nāṭaputtiya|naa.taputtiya|nataputtiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.taputtiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nātaputtiya">nātaputtiya</a></p>

nāṭasuriyā|naa.tasuriyaa|natasuriya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">naa.tasuriyaa</span></p><p>NāṭasuriyāNātapuriya</p><p>A city in Uttarakuru. DN.iii.200</p><p>5town</p>

nībhata|niibhata|nibhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niibhata</span></p><p>bought out Ja.iii.471.</p><p>cp. Sk. nirbhṛta, pp. of nis + <em>bhṛ</em>.</p>

nīca|niica|nica
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niica</span></p><p>low; lowly; inferior, base, mean; short.</p>

nīca|niica|nica
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niica</span></p><p>adjective low, inferior, humble (opp. <em><a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></em> high, fr. adv. ud˚) Vin.i.46, Vin.i.47; Vin.ii.194; DN.i.109, DN.i.179 DN.i.194; AN.v.82; Snp-a.424 (nīcaṃ karoti to degrade); passim.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://kula">kula</a></em> of low clan Ja.i.106; Snp.411<br />■ (˚ā) <em><a href="bword://kul%C4%ABna">kulīna</a></em> belonging to low caste Snp.462; <em>-<a href="bword://cittat%C4%81">cittatā</a></em> being humble-hearted Dhs.1340; Dhs-a.395; <em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%AB%E1%B9%ADhaka">pīṭhaka</a></em> a low stool Dhp-a.iv.177 <em>-<a href="bword://mano">mano</a></em> humble Snp.252 (= nīcacitto Snp-a.293); <em>-<a href="bword://seyy%C4%81">seyyā</a></em> a low bed AN.i.212 (opp. uccâsayana).</p><p>Vedic nīca, adj<br />■ formation fr. adv. ni˚, cp. Sk. nyañc downward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a>, <a href="bword://kula">kula</a>, <a href="bword://kulīna">kulīna</a>, <a href="bword://cittatā">cittatā</a>, <a href="bword://pīṭhaka">pīṭhaka</a>, <a href="bword://mano">mano</a>, <a href="bword://seyyā">seyyā</a></p>

nīcakula|niicakula|nicakula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicakula</span></p><p>family of low rank.</p>

nīcakulīna|niicakuliina|nicakulina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicakuliina</span></p><p>born in a family of low rank.</p>

nīcaniviṭṭha|niicanivi.t.tha|nicanivittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicanivi.t.tha</span></p><p>adverb intent upon lowly things; humble.</p>

nīcatara|niicatara|nicatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicatara</span></p><p>lower; shorter.</p>

nīcataraṃ|niicatara.m|nicataram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. compar.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicatara.m</span></p><p>, lower; shorter.</p>

nīcavatthuka|niicavatthuka|nicavatthuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicavatthuka</span></p><p>with a low platform, on a low site.</p>

nīcaṃ karoti|niica.m karoti|nicam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niica.m karoti</span></p><p>lowers; belittles.</p>

nīcceyya|niicceyya|nicceyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicceyya</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) lower, inferior.</li><li>(n.) lowness; inferiority.</li></ol>

nīceyya|niiceyya|niceyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiceyya</span></p><p>adjective lower inferior, rather low MN.i.329; Snp.855, Snp.918; Mnd.244, Mnd.351.</p><p>compar. of nīca (for ˚īya?), in function of ˚eyya as “of the kind of,” sort of, rather</p>

nīcākulīna|niicaakuliina|nicakulina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niicaakuliina</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīcakulīna">nīcakulīna</a></p>

nīdha|niidha|nidha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niidha</span></p><p>= nu idha, see <em><a href="bword://nu">nu</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nu">nu</a></p>

nīdhura|niidhura|nidhura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niidhura</span></p><p>? bracelet, bangle Ja.vi.64, (= valaya; variant reading BB nivara). Also given as <em>nīyura</em> (cp Prk. neura &amp; P. nūpura).</p><p>Sk.? Cp. keyura</p>

nīgha|niigha|nigha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niigha</span></p><p>affliction; destroying.</p>

nīgha|niigha|nigha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niigha</span></p><p>(in <em><a href="bword://an%C4%ABgha">anīgha</a></em>) see *<a href="bword://nigha">nigha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīgha">anīgha</a>, <a href="bword://nigha">nigha</a></p>

nīharanta|niiharanta|niharanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharanta</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīharati|niiharati|niharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharati</span></p><p>takes out, extracts; extricates; draws out; carries out; removes; drives out, expels.</p>

nīharati|niiharati|niharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharati</span></p><p>to take out, to throw out, drive out Ja.i.150, Ja.i.157; Ja.iii.52; Ja.vi.336; Cnd.199#7 (ni˚); Vv-a.222 Vv-a.256; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.254; Mil.8, Mil.219. aor. <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABhari">nīhari</a></em> DN.i.92; Ja.i.293; Ja.ii.154; Pv-a.41, Pv-a.178 (gehato taṃ n.). grd <em>nīharitabba</em> Dhp-a.i.397 (opp. pavesetabba)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABha%E1%B9%ADa">nīhaṭa</a></em> -Caus. <em>nīharāpeti</em> to have thrown out, to order to be ejected Vv-a.141.</p><p>nis + <em>hṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīhari">nīhari</a>, <a href="bword://nīhaṭa">nīhaṭa</a></p>

nīharaṇa|niihara.na|niharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihara.na</span></p><p>taking out, carrying away, removing DN-a.i.296; Pv-a.7.</p><p>fr. nīharati</p>

nīhari|niihari|nihari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihari</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīharitabba|niiharitabba|niharitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharitabba</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīharittha|niiharittha|niharittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharittha</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīharitvā|niiharitvaa|niharitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiharitvaa</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīhariṃsu|niihari.msu|niharimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihari.msu</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīhata|niihata|nihata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihata</span></p><p>See also: nīhāṭa</p>

nīhaṭa|niiha.ta|nihata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiha.ta</span></p><p>taken out, brought out; driven out; expelled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīhaṭa|niiha.ta|nihata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiha.ta</span></p><p>thrown out, removed; in f. abstr. ˚tā ejection, removal [cp. Sk. nirhṛti] Dhp-a.iii.336 (malānaṃ n. the extirpation of impurity or removal of stain).</p><p>pp. of nīharati = Sk. nirhṛta</p>

nīhāra|niihaara|nihara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaara</span></p><p>way, manner Vin.i.13; Ja.i.127; Dhp-a.iv.7. At Vin.i.13 also in nīhāra-bhatta (= nīhāraka).</p><p>cp. Sk. nirhāra</p>

nīhārabhatta|niihaarabhatta|niharabhatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaarabhatta</span></p><p>for whom food has been brought.</p>

nīhāraka|niihaaraka|niharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaaraka</span></p><p>adjective noun one who carries away Vin.i.13 (nīhāra-bhatta); SN.v.12, SN.v.320, SN.v.325 (piṇḍapāta).</p><p>fr. nīhāra, cp. nīharaṇa</p>

nīhāritabba|niihaaritabba|niharitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaaritabba</span></p><p>will take out, extract; will extricate; will draw out; will carry out; will remove; will drive out, expel.</p>

nīhārituṃ|niihaaritu.m|niharitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaaritu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīhārāpetabba|niihaaraapetabba|niharapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaaraapetabba</span></p><p>will take out, extract; will extricate; will draw out; will carry out; will remove; will drive out, expel.</p>

nīhātuṃ|niihaatu.m|nihatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niihaatu.m</span></p><p>of nīharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīharati">nīharati</a></p>

nīka|niika|nika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niika</span></p><p>kind of deer (or pig) Ja.v.406 (vv.ll. nika, ninga).</p><p>Sk. nyanku? Doubtful reading</p>

nīla|niila|nila
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niila</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) of a dark color, esp. dark blue,</li><li>(n.) a blue-green substance or dye or mineral; blue-black; dark green; blue-green.</li></ol>

nīla|niila|nila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niila</span></p><p>adjective dark-blue, blue-black blue-green. Nīla serves as a general term to designate the “coloured-black,” as opposed to the “colouredwhite” (pīta yellow), which pairs (nīla-pīta) are both set off against the “pure” colour-sensations of red (lohitaka) &amp; white (odāta), besides the distinct black or dark (see kaṇha). Therefore n. has a fluctuating connotation (cp. Mrs. Rh. D.; <em>Buddh. Psych.</em> p. 49; <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 62), its only standard combination being that with <em><a href="bword://p%C4%ABta">pīta</a></em>, e.g. in the enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of the ten kasiṇa practices (see <em><a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em>): <em>nīla pīta lohita odāta</em>; in the description of the 5 colours of the Buddha’s eye: nīla pītaka lohitaka kaṇha odāta (Cnd.235, I<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted --> under cakkhumā); which goes even so far as to be used simply in the sense of “black &amp; white,” e.g. Vv-a.320. Applied to hair (lomāni DN.ii.144; MN.ii.136. See further enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.111 &amp; under kaṇha<br />■ AN.iii.239; AN.iv.263 sq., AN.iv.305, AN.iv.349, AN.v.61; Vism.110, Vism.156, Vism.173; Thag-a.42 (mahā˚ great blue lotus); Dhs.617; Pv.ii.2#5; Pv-a.32, Pv-a.46, Pv-a.158; Sdhp.246 Sdhp.270, Sdhp.360.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></em> a black cloud Pv.iv.3#9.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://abhij%C4%81ti">abhijāti</a></em> a dark (unfortunate) birth (cp. kaṇh˚) AN.iii.383;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://uppala">uppala</a></em> blue lotus Ja.iii.394; Vv.45#4 (= kuvalaya); Dhp-a.i.384</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> the “blue” kasiṇa (q.v.) DN.iii.248; Dhs.203 (Vam 172 etc.;</li><li><em>-gīva</em> “blue neck,” a peacock Snp.221 = maṇi-daṇḍa-sadisāya gīvāya n. ti Snp-a.277);</li><li><em>-pupphī</em> Name of plant (“blue-blossom”) Ja.vi.53;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://b%C4%ABjaka">bījaka</a></em> a waterplant (“blue-seed”) Bdhgh at Vin.iii.276;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ma%E1%B9%87i">maṇi</a></em> a sapphire (“blue-stone”) Ja.ii.112; Ja.iv.140; Dhp-a.iii.254</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">vaṇṇa</a></em> blue colour, coloured blue or green Ja.iv.140 (of the ocean); Dhs.246.</li></ul><p>Vedic nīla, perhaps conn. with Lat. nites to shine, see Walde, <em>Lat. Wtb.</em> s. v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pīta">pīta</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a>, <a href="bword://abhijāti">abhijāti</a>, <a href="bword://uppala">uppala</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://bījaka">bījaka</a>, <a href="bword://maṇi">maṇi</a>, <a href="bword://vaṇṇa">vaṇṇa</a></p>

nīlabhijāti|niilabhijaati|nilabhijati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilabhijaati</span></p><p>the blue-green class of beings.</p>

nīlagiva|niilagiva|nilagiva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilagiva</span></p><p>blue-necked; a peacock.</p>

nīlagīva|niilagiiva|nilagiva
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilagiiva</span></p><p>See <em>Mayūra</em>.</p><p>See also: Mayūra</p>

nīlaka|niilaka|nilaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilaka</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) of a dark color, esp. dark-blue, blue-black; dark green; blue-green.</li><li>(n.) a dark blue, blue-black dye.</li></ol>

nīlaka|niilaka|nilaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilaka</span></p><p>adjective for nīla MN.ii.201; see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

nīlakasiṇa|niilakasi.na|nilakasina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilakasi.na</span></p><p>meditation based on (something) dark blue; a dark blue meditation object; (the jhana) which is or is brought about by meditation based on (something) dark blue.</p>

nīlakavaddhika|niilakavaddhika|nilakavaddhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; \~ā, f</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilakavaddhika</span></p><p>with dark-blue straps.</p>

nīlakā|niilakaa|nilaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilakaa</span></p><p>A type of moss which grow over rocks Th.1070.</p>

nīlamakkhikā|niilamakkhikaa|nilamakkhika
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilamakkhikaa</span></p><p>The name means “blue fly” and must refer to flies of the genera Lucilia, Pycnosoma, Thelychoeta and Pyrellia, of which there are many species in India Ja.i.165 Ja.iii.177. These flies, usually called “blue bottles” in English, have distinctive iridescent abdomens and feed on animal and human faeces, carrion and moist garbage.</p>

nīlamaṇḍūka|niilama.n.duuka|nilamanduka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilama.n.duuka</span></p><p>Indian Pond Frog, <em>Eyphlyctis hexadactylus</em>. This large frog has a flattish snout, smooth skin except on the flanks which are warty and is bright green or olive with a yellow streak extending from the nose to the vent. Pond frogs favor dense aquatic vegetation where they feed on insects, snails and sometimes smaller frogs. According to the Jātakas, the Bodhisattva was once reborn as a pond frog Ja.ii.238. The Pāḷi word <em>nīla</em> usually means blue but can also mean blue-green, green or dark-coloured.</p>

nīlanibhāsa|niilanibhaasa|nilanibhasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilanibhaasa</span></p><p>of dark-blue appearance or luster.</p>

nīlapupphī|niilapupphii|nilapupphi
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilapupphii</span></p><p>A type of plant with blue flowers Ja.vi.536.</p>

nīlavadhika|niilavadhika|nilavadhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilavadhika</span></p><p>with dark-blue straps.</p>

nīliya|niiliya|niliya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiliya</span></p><p>an (indigo) hair dye Ja.iii.138 (Com. nīliyaka).</p><p>fr. nīlī</p>

nīlī|niilii|nili
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilii</span></p><p>Indigo, Hindi <em>nil</em>, <em>Indigofera tinctoria</em> A.iii.230 Ja.vi.537, a small shrub from which a blue dye is extracted. Indigo was a major crop in northern India until the development of synthetic dyes. We read of paint made of indigo S.ii.102 S.iii.152. An extract of the leaves is also used as a medicine.</p>

nīlī|niilii|nili
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilii</span></p><p>the indigo plant or dye.</p>

nīlī|niilii|nili
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niilii</span></p><p>the indigo plant, indigo colour AN.iii.230, AN.iii.233.</p><p>Sk. nīlī</p>

nīpa|niipa|nipa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niipa</span></p><p><em>Nauclea cadamba</em>, a large straight tree with smooth brown bark, spreading branches, ovate leaves and small orange flowers Bv.11.51 Ja.i.13.</p>

nīpa|niipa|nipa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niipa</span></p><p>adjective lit. lying low, deep, Name of the tree Nauclea cadamba, a species of Asoka tree Ja.i.13 (v. 61) = Bv.ii.51; Ja.v.6 (so read for nipa).</p><p>Vedic nīpa, contr. fr. ni + āpa “low water”</p>

nīraja|niiraja|niraja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiraja</span></p><p>adjective free from passion Sdhp.370.</p><p>Sk. nīraja, nis + raja</p>

nīrasa|niirasa|nirasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niirasa</span></p><p>adjective sapless, dried up, withered, tasteless, insipid Ja.iii.111.</p><p>Sk. nīrasa, nis + rasa</p>

nīrava|niirava|nirava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niirava</span></p><p>adjective soundless, noiseless, silent DN-a.i.153 (tuṇhī +).</p><p>Sk. nīrava, nis + rava</p>

nīroga|niiroga|niroga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiroga</span></p><p>adjective free from disease, healthy, well, unhurt Ja.i.421; Ja.iii.26; Ja.iv.31; Pv-a.198 (ni˚). Cp. <em><a href="bword://n%C4%ABruja">nīruja</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. nīroga, nis + roga</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīruja">nīruja</a></p>

nīruja|niiruja|niruja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiruja</span></p><p>adjective = nīroga Sdhp.496.</p><p>Sk. nīruja, nis + rujā</p>

nīta|niita|nita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niita</span></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse asks when the lazy fool will ever escape suffering. Thag.84</p>

nīta|niita|nita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niita</span></p><p>of nayati led, brought to; brought to a state; taken away; carried off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

nīta|niita|nita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niita</span></p><p>past participle led, guided; ascertained, inferred AN.i.60 (˚attha); Ja.i.262; Ja.ii.215 (kāma˚); Ne.21 (˚attha natural meaning, i.e. the primarily inferred sense, opp neyyattha); Sdhp.366 (dun˚). Cp. vi˚.</p><p>pp. of neti</p>

nītattha|niitattha|nitattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niitattha</span></p><p>whose meaning is brought out, determined, evident, explicit.</p>

nītha|niitha|nitha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niitha</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, mentioned in a nominal list. MN.iii.69</p>

nīti|niiti|niti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiti</span></p><p>guidance, practice, conduct, esp. right conduct, propriety; statesmanship, polity Pv-a.114 (˚mangala commonsense), Pv-a.129 (˚sattha science of statecraft, or of prudent behaviour), Pv-a.130 (˚cintaka a lawgiver), Pv-a.131 (˚naya polity &amp; law), Pv-a.132 (˚kusala versed in the wisdom of life); Mil.3 (here meaning the Nyāyaphilosophy cp. Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> p. 58).</p><p>Sk. nīti, fr. nīta</p>

nīvaraṇa|niivara.na|nivarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivara.na</span></p><p>an obstacle, a hindrance (esp. five: <em>kāmacchanda</em>, <em>vyāpāda</em>, <em>thīnamiddha</em>, <em>uddhaccakukkucca</em>, <em>vicikicchā</em>); being a hindrance, which is an obstacle.</p>

nīvaraṇa|niivara.na|nivarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivara.na</span></p><p>, occasionally masculine an obstacle, hindrance, only as tt. applied to obstacles in an ethical sense &amp; usually enumerated or referred to in a set of 5 (as pañca nīvaraṇāni and p. āvaraṇāni), viz. <em>kāmacchanda, (abhijjhā-)vyāpāda, thīna-middha, uddhaccakukkucca vicikicchā</em> i.e. sensuality, ill-will, torpor of mind or body, worry, wavering (cp. <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 310) DN.i.73 (˚e, acc. pl.), DN.i.246; DN.ii.83, DN.ii.300; DN.iii.49 sq., DN.iii.101, DN.iii.234, DN.iii.278; SN.ii.23; SN.iii.149; SN.v.60, SN.v.84 sq., SN.v.93 sq., SN.v.145, SN.v.160, SN.v.226 SN.v.327, SN.v.439; MN.i.60, MN.i.144, MN.i.276; MN.iii.4, MN.iii.295; AN.i.3, AN.i.161 AN.iii.16, AN.iii.63, AN.iii.230 sq.; AN.iii.386; AN.iv.457; AN.v.16, AN.v.195, AN.v.322; Snp.17 Mnd.13; Cnd.379; Pts.i.31, Pts.i.129, Pts.i.163; Pp.68; Dhs.1059 Dhs.1136, Dhs.1495; Vb.199, Vb.244, Vb.378; Ne.11, Ne.13, Ne.94; Vism.146, Vism.189; DN-a.i.213; Sdhp.459, Sdhp.493 and passim. Other enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->ns<!-- raw HTML omitted --> are occasionally found e.g. 10 at SN.v.110 SN.v.8 at MN.i.360 sq.; MN.i.6 at Dhs.1152.</p><p>Sk. *nivāraṇa, nis + varaṇa of <em>vṛ</em>; (vṛṇoti), see <em><a href="bword://nibbuta">nibbuta</a></em> &amp; cp. nivāraṇa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbuta">nibbuta</a></p>

nīvaraṇiya|niivara.niya|nivaraniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivara.niya</span></p><p>adjective belonging to an obstacle, forming a hindrance, obstructing Dhs.584, Dhs.1164, Dhs.1488 Vb.12, Vb.30, Vb.66, Vb.130 etc.</p><p>fr. nīvaraṇa</p>

nīvāra|niivaara|nivara
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivaara</span></p><p>Wild Rice or Red Rice, <em>Oryza rufipogon</em>, probably the same as what was called <em><a href="bword://karumbhaka">karumbhaka</a></em>, <em>sayañjātasālī</em>, “self-sown rice” or <em><a href="bword://rattas%C4%81l%C4%AB">rattasālī</a></em>, “red rice” D.i.166 Ja.iii.247 Ja.v.37 Mil.252. Wild rice is an aggressive hardy aquatic grass very similar to domesticated rice except that it has narrower leaves that are a deeper green and a red kernel. Wild rice is considered a pest because it infests paddy fields, then flowers and sheds its seeds before the harvest, thereby reducing the yield. A gruel consisting of wild rice, <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81m%C4%81ka">sāmāka</a></em> and the leaves of some other herbs was given as a cure for bloody diarrhea Ja.iii.144. In the Jātakas, ascetics and animals are said to have eaten wild rice. See <em>Taṇḍula</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://karumbhaka">karumbhaka</a>, <a href="bword://rattasālī">rattasālī</a>, <a href="bword://sāmāka">sāmāka</a>, Taṇḍula</p>

nīvāra|niivaara|nivara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivaara</span></p><p>raw rice, paddy DN.i.166; AN.i.241, AN.i.295; AN.ii.206; Pp.55; Ja.iii.144 (˚yāgu).</p><p>Sk. nīvāra, unexplained</p>

nīvārabhakka|niivaarabhakka|nivarabhakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niivaarabhakka</span></p><p>eating wild rice.</p>

nīyamāna|niiyamaana|niyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyamaana</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of nīyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nīyati">nīyati</a></p>

nīyanta|niiyanta|niyanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyanta</span></p><p>of niyyānta</p><p>See also: niyyānta</p>

nīyati FIXME double|niiyati FIXME double|niyati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyati FIXME double</span></p><p>Nīyati</p><p>to be led or guided, to go, to be moved SN.i.39 (cittena nīyati loko); Dhp.175; Pv.i.11#1 (= vahīyati Pv-a.56); Ja.i.264 (ppr. nīyamāna) Pv-a.4 (id.); Dhp-a.iii.177; Sdhp.292, Sdhp.302. Also found in spelling <em><a href="bword://niyyati">niyyati</a></em> at Snp.851; Mnd.223 (= yāyati vuyhati), Mnd.395<br />■ In the sense of a Med. in imper <em>nīyāmase</em> (let us take) Pv.ii.9#1 (= nayissāma Pv-a.113).</p><p>Sk. nīyati, Pass. of neti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyati">niyyati</a></p>

nīyati|niiyati|niyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyati</span></p><p>is led (away); is taken off; is determined, is interpreted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayati">nayati</a></p>

nīyati|niiyati|niyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyati</span></p><p>Niyyati = Nīyati</p><p>(Pass. of nayati).</p>

nīyyāṇa|niiyyaa.na|niyyana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyyaa.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāna">niyyāna</a></p>

nīyādeti FIXME double|niiyaadeti FIXME double|niyadeti FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaadeti FIXME double</span></p><p>Nīyādita, Nīyādeti</p><p>see <em>niyy˚</em>.</p>

nīyādeti|niiyaadeti|niyadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaadeti</span></p><p>Niyyādeti &amp; niyyāteti &amp; nīyādeti</p><p>(to) give (back), give into charge, give over, assign, dedicate, to present, denote SN.i.131 (niyyātayāmi); SN.iv.181 (sāmikānaṃ gāvo), SN.iv.194; Ja.i.30 Ja.i.66, Ja.i.496; Ja.ii.106, Ja.ii.133; Vv.46#8 niyyādesi = sampaṭicchāpesi adāsi Vv-a.199); Pv.iii.2#11 (niyātayiṃsu = adaṃsu Pv-a.184); Vism.115 (t); Dhp-a.i.70; Dhp-a.ii.87; Vv-a.33, Vv-a.67; Pv-a.20 (vihāraṃ nīyādetvā), Pv-a.25 (= uddissati dadāti), Pv-a.42, Pv-a.81, Pv-a.276 (at all Pv-a passages as nī˚)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81dita">niyyādita</a></em> Cp. similarly paṭiyādeti &amp; paṭiyādita.</p><p>cp. Sk. ni-or nir-yātayati, Caus. of ni(r)yatati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyādita">niyyādita</a></p>

nīyādita|niiyaadita|niyadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaadita</span></p><p>Nīyādita, Nīyādeti</p><p>see <em>niyy˚</em>.</p>

nīyānika|niiyaanika|niyanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaanika</span></p><p>see <em>niyy˚</em>.</p>

nīyāti|niiyaati|niyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

nīyāti|niiyaati|niyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://niyy%C4%81ti">niyyāti</a></em>. Niyadita, Niyadeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāti">niyyāti</a></p>

nīyāyanta|niiyaayanta|niyayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">niiyaayanta</span></p><p>of niyyāyanta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niyyāyanta">niyyāyanta</a></p>

nīḷa|nii.la|nila
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nii.la</span></p><p>nest (verse 92): see <em><a href="bword://ni%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dha">niḍḍha</a></em>: cp. ˚pacchi bird cage Ja.ii.361; roga˚ Iti.37; vadharoga˚ Thag.1093.</p><p>Vedic nīḍa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://niḍḍha">niḍḍha</a></p>

nūna|nuuna|nuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nuuna</span></p><ol><li>indeed; certainly.</li><li>surely; probably, in all probability; it must be that …</li><li>is it that?; esp. <em>yam</em> + <em>nūna</em> + opt.: what if …? suppose …? why don’t …?</li></ol>

nūna|nuuna|nuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nuuna</span></p><p>(&amp; nūnaṃ Dhs-a.164) indeclinable affirmative-dubitative particle with Pot or Ind., viz.</p><ol><li>(dubit.-interrog.) is it then, now, shall I etc. (= Lat. subjunctive, hortative &amp; dubitative DN.i.155 (= Lat. num, cp. nu). Esp. freq. with rel pron. yaṃ = <em>yaṃ nūna</em> what if, shall I, let me (Lat. age) Snp.p.80 (yaṃ nūn’ âhaṃ puccheyyaṃ let me ask, I will ask); Ja.i.150, Ja.i.255; Ja.iii.393; Pv-a.5 (y. n. âhaṃ imassa avassayo bhaveyyaṃ = let me help him).</li><li>(affirm.) surely, certainly, indeed Snp.1058 (api nūna pajaheyyuṃ); AN.v.194; Ja.i.60; Ja.v.90; Pv.ii.9#24 (nuna) Mil.20; Dhs-a.164; Pv-a.95 (nuna as variant reading; text reads nanda).</li></ol><p>Ved. nūnaṃ = Gr. νύν, Lat. nunc (cp. num); Goth. nu, Ger. nun, cp. E. now See also nu</p>

nūpura|nuupura|nupura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nuupura</span></p><p>an ornament for the feet, an anklet Thig.268; DN-a.i.50.</p><p>Sk. nūpura; Non-Aryan. Cp. Prk. ṇeura &amp; nīdhura (nīyura)</p>

nūtana|nuutana|nutana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">nuutana</span></p><p>adjective “of now,” i.e. recent, fresh, new Dāvs iv.47.</p><p>Vedic nūtana, adj<br />■ formation fr. adv. nū, cp. nūna. In formation cp. Sk. śvastana (of to-morrow) Lat. crastinus etc.</p>

o
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Initial o in Pali may represent a Vedic o or a Vedic au (see ojas, ogha, etc.). Or it may be guṇa of u (see <em><a href="bword://o%E1%B8%B7%C4%81rika">oḷārika</a></em> opakammika, etc.). But it is usually a prefix representing Vedic ava. The form in o is the regular use in old Pali; there are only two or three cases where ava for metrical or other reasons, introduced. In post-canonical Pali the form in ava is the regular one. For new formations we believe there is no exception to this rule. But the old form in o has in a few cases, survived. Though o; standing alone, is derived from ava, yet compounds with o are almost invariably older than the corresponding compounds with ava (see note on ogamana).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oḷārika">oḷārika</a></p>

obandhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bind, to tie on to Vin.ii.116 (obandhitvā ger.).</p><p>o + bandhati</p>

obhagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>broken off.</p>

obhagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>broken down, broken up, broken SN.v.96 (˚vibhagga); AN.iv.435 (obhagg’obhagga); Dhp-a.i.58 (id.); Ja.i.55 (˚sarīra).</p><p>o + bhagga, pp. of <em>bhañj</em>, Sk. avabhagna</p>

obhaggavibhagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>broken (or bent) and crushed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhagga">obhagga</a></p>

obhaggobhagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>variously broken of; (or variously bent down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhagga">obhagga</a></p>

obhanditvā|obhanditvaa|obhanditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhanditvaa</span></p><p>tying on, fixing on.</p>

obharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to carry away or off, to take off<br />pp <em><a href="bword://obhata">obhata</a></em>.</p><p>ava + bharati, cp. Sk. avabharati = Lat. aufero</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhata">obhata</a></p>

obhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>having taken away or off, only in cpd. <em>-cumbaṭā</em> with the “cumbaṭa” taken off, descriptive of a woman in her habit of carrying vessels on her head (on the cumbaṭa stand) Vin.iii.140 = Vv-a.73 (Hardy: “a woman with a circlet of cloth on her head”?).</p><p>pp. of obharati</p>

obhatacumbaṭa|obhatacumba.ta|obhatacumbata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhatacumba.ta</span></p><p>(a wife) married when the cumbaṭa coiled pad has been taken off.</p>

obhañjati|obha~njati|obhanjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obha~njati</span></p><p>to fold up, bend over, crease (a garment); only Caus. ii. <em>obhañjāpeti</em> Ja.i.499 (dhovāpeti +). See also pp. <em><a href="bword://obhagga">obhagga</a></em>.</p><p>o + <em>bhañj</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhagga">obhagga</a></p>

obhoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>fold, coil.</p>

obhoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bending, winding, curve, the fold of a robe Vin.i.46 (obhoge kāyabandhanaṃ kātabbaṃ).</p><p>o + bhoga from <em>bhuj</em> to bend</p>

obhāsa|obhaasa|obhasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasa</span></p><p>obhāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine light, luster, radiance; appearance, manifestation.</p><p>obhāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine intimation.</p>

obhāsa|obhaasa|obhasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasa</span></p><p>shine, splendour, light, lustre, effulgence; appearance. In clairvoyant language also “aura (see <em>Cpd.</em> 214<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with C. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “rays emitted from the body on account of insight”)-DN.i.220 (effulgence of light); MN.iii.120, MN.iii.157; AN.ii.130, AN.ii.139; AN.iv.302; Iti.108 (obhāsakara); Pts.i.114, Pts.i.119 (paññā˚); Pts.ii.100, Pts.ii.150 sq. Pts.ii.159, Pts.ii.162; Vism.28, Vism.41; Pv-a.276 (˚ṃ pharati to emit a radiance); Sdhp.325. With <em><a href="bword://nimitta">nimitta</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://parikath%C4%81">parikathā</a></em> at Vism.23; Snp-a.497. See also <em><a href="bword://avabh%C4%81sa">avabhāsa</a></em>.</p><p>from obhāsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nimitta">nimitta</a>, <a href="bword://parikathā">parikathā</a>, <a href="bword://avabhāsa">avabhāsa</a></p>

obhāsana|obhaasana|obhasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasana</span></p><p>suggestive or lewd speech.</p>

obhāsana|obhaasana|obhasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasana</span></p><p>(nt<br />■ adj.) shining Vv-a.276 (Hardy: “speaking to someone”).</p><p>fr. obhāsa, cp. Sk. avabhāsana</p>

obhāsanta|obhaasanta|obhasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāsati">obhāsati</a></p>

obhāsate|obhaasate|obhasate
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasate</span></p><p>speaks to suggestively or lewdly or offensively; insults</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāsati">obhāsati</a></p>

obhāsati|obhaasati|obhasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasati</span></p><p>obhāsati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. speaks to suggestively or lewdly or offensively; insults</p><p>obhāsati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. shines forth, is bright; becomes manifest, appears; (perhaps also: shines through, illumines)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a>, <a href="bword://obhāsate">obhāsate</a></p>

obhāsati|obhaasati|obhasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasati</span></p><p>Obhāsati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to shine, to be splendid Pv.i.2#1 (= pabhāseti vijjoteti Pv-a.10)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://obh%C4%81seti">obhāseti</a></em> to make radiant or resplendent to illumine, to fill with light or splendour<br />pres <em><a href="bword://obh%C4%81seti">obhāseti</a></em> Pv.iii.1#15 (= joteti Pv-a.176); Mil.336; ppr <em>obhāsayanto</em> Pv.i.11#1 (= vijjotamāna Pv-a.56) &amp; <em>obhāsento</em> Pv.ii.1#10 (= jotanto ekālokaṃ karonto Pv-a.71) ger. <em><a href="bword://obh%C4%81setv%C4%81">obhāsetvā</a></em> SN.i.66; Kp v. = Snp.p.46; Kp-a.116 (ābhāya pharitvā ekobhāsaṃ karitvā)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avabh%C4%81sita">avabhāsita</a></em>.</p><p>o + bhāsati from <em>bhās</em>, cp. Sk. avabhāsati</p><p>Obhāsati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to speak to (inopportunely), to rail at, offend, abuse Vin.ii.262; Vin.iii.128.</p><p>ava + bhāsati fr. <em>bhāṣ</em>; Sk. apabhāṣati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a>, <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a>, <a href="bword://obhāsetvā">obhāsetvā</a>, <a href="bword://avabhāsita">avabhāsita</a></p>

obhāsaya|obhaasaya|obhasaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasaya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://obhāsayat">obhāsayat</a></p><p>making bright, illumining</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a></p>

obhāsayat|obhaasayat|obhasayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://obhāsayat">obhāsayat</a></p><p>making bright, illumining</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a></p>

obhāsenta|obhaasenta|obhasenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a></p>

obhāseti|obhaaseti|obhaseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaaseti</span></p><p>speaks to suggestively or lewdly or offensively; insults</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāsati">obhāsati</a></p>

obhāsetvā|obhaasetvaa|obhasetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasetvaa</span></p><p>having made radiant; having illumined</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a></p>

obhāsi|obhaasi|obhasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāsati">obhāsati</a></p>

obhāsita|obhaasita|obhasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasita</span></p><p>illumination, brightness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāseti">obhāseti</a></p>

obhāsitabba|obhaasitabba|obhasitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">obhaasitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://obhāsati">obhāsati</a></p>

ocaraka FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ocaraka</p><p>in special meaning of one who makes himself at home or familiar with, an investigator, informant scout, spy (ocarakā ti carapurisā C. on Ud.66)<br />■ Thus also in BSk. as avacaraka one who furnishes information Divy.127; an adaptation from the Pāli<br />■ Vin.iii.47 Vin.iii.52; MN.i.129 = MN.i.189 (corā ocarakā, for carā?); SN.i.79 (purisā carā (variant reading corā) ocarakā (okacarā variant reading SS) janapadaṃ ocaritvā etc.; cp. <em>K. S.</em> p. 106 n. 1) = Ud.66 (reads coiā o.).</p><p>fr. ocarati</p>

ocaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>being active in a secret or low way; spying out, investigating; an informer, spy, secret agent; a robber.</p>

ocaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avacaraka &amp; ocaraka</p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>only in cpd. kāmâvacarika as adj. to kāmâvacara, belonging to the sphere of sense experiences, Sdhp.254.</li><li>Late form of ocaraka, spy, only in C. on Thag.315 ff. quoted in <em>Brethren</em> 189, n</li><li>Occurs in BSk (Divy.127).</li></ol><p>fr. avacara</p>

ocarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>comes down.</li><li>spies out; investigates.</li></ol>

ocarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be after something, to go into, to search, reconnoitre, investigate, pry Vin.iii.52 (ger. ˚itvā) MN.i.502 (ocarati); SN.i.79 (˚itvā: so read for T. ocaritā C. explains by vīmaṃsitvā taṃ taṃ pavattiṃ ñatvā). pp. ociṇṇa.</p><p>o + carati</p>

occhindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cut off, sever Ja.ii.388 (maggaṃ occhindati &amp; occhindamāna to bar the way; variant reading BB ochijjati), Ja.ii.404.</p><p>o + chindati</p>

ocetuṃ|ocetu.m|ocetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ocetu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocināti">ocināti</a></p>

ocinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>gathers, picks; collects; accumulates; increases, strengthens</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocināti">ocināti</a></p>

ocinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ocināti (ocinati)</p><ol><li>= Sk. avacinoti, ava + <em>ci<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to gather, pluck, pick off Dhp-a.i.366; also in pp. <em><a href="bword://ocita">ocita</a></em>.</li><li>Sk. avacinoti or ˚ciketi ava + <em>ci<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, cp. apacināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to disregard, disrespect, treat with contempt; pres. <em>ocināyati</em> (for ocināti metri causa) Ja.vi.4 (= avajānāti C.).</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocita">ocita</a></p>

ocināpesi|ocinaapesi|ocinapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ocinaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocināpeti">ocināpeti</a></p>

ocināpeti|ocinaapeti|ocinapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ocinaapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocināti">ocināti</a></p>

ocināti|ocinaati|ocinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ocinaati</span></p><p>gathers, picks; collects; accumulates; increases, strengthens</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocinati">ocinati</a></p>

ocināti|ocinaati|ocinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ocinaati</span></p><p>Ocināti (ocinati)</p><ol><li>= Sk. avacinoti, ava + <em>ci<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to gather, pluck, pick off Dhp-a.i.366; also in pp. <em><a href="bword://ocita">ocita</a></em>.</li><li>Sk. avacinoti or ˚ciketi ava + <em>ci<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, cp. apacināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to disregard, disrespect, treat with contempt; pres. <em>ocināyati</em> (for ocināti metri causa) Ja.vi.4 (= avajānāti C.).</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocita">ocita</a></p>

ocita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>gathered, picked; collected; accumulated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocināti">ocināti</a></p>

ocita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>gathered, picked off Ja.iii.22; Ja.iv.135, Ja.iv.156; Sdhp.387. Ocinati (ocinati)</p><p>o + cita, pp. of ocināti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ociṇṇa|oci.n.na|ocinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oci.n.na</span></p><p>spied out; investigated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocarati">ocarati</a></p>

ociṇṇa|oci.n.na|ocinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oci.n.na</span></p><p>gone into, investigated, scouted, explored SN.i.79 = Ud.66 (reads otiṇṇa).</p><p>pp. of ocarati</p>

ocīraka|ociiraka|ociraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ociiraka</span></p><p>with bark hanging down.</p>

ocīraka|ociiraka|ociraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ociiraka</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://od%C4%ABraka">odīraka</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odīraka">odīraka</a></p>

ocīrakajāta|ociirakajaata|ocirakajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ociirakajaata</span></p><p>seeming to have its bark hanging down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocīraka">ocīraka</a></p>

odagya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>exultation, elation Mnd.3 = Cnd.446 = Dhs.9, Dhs.86, Dhs.285, Dhs.373; Dhs-a.143 (= udaggasabhāva a “topmost” condition).</p><p>der. fr. udagga</p>

odahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>placing (in); laying down; applying.</p>

odahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>putting down, applying, application MN.ii.216; heaping up, storing Dhp-a.iii.118.</li><li>putting in, fig, attention, devotion Ne.29.</li></ol><p>fr. odahati</p>

odahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>places (in); lays down; applies; directs, esp. <em>~ati sotaṃ</em>, gives ear attentively</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odheti">odheti</a></p>

odahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to put down, to put in, supply MN.i.117 (okacaraṃ, see under <em><a href="bword://oka">oka</a></em>); MN.ii.216 (agad’angāraṃ vaṇa-mukhe odaheyya); Thag.774 (migavo pāsaṃ odahi the hunter set a snare; Morris, J.P.T.S. 1884, 76 suggests change of reading to oḍḍayi, hardly justified); Ja.iii.201 (visaṃ odahi araññe), Ja.iii.272 (passaṃ o to turn one’s flanks towards, dat.); Mil.156 (kāye ojaṃ odahissāma supply the body with strength).</li><li>(fig.) to apply, in phrase <em>sotaṃ odahati</em> to listen DN.i.230 Dāvs v.68</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://ohita">ohita</a></em>.</p><p>o + dahati, fr. <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a>, <a href="bword://ohita">ohita</a></p>

odahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

odahitvā|odahitvaa|odahitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odahitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

odahiṃsu|odahi.msu|odahimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odahi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

odaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) watery; living in water.</li><li>(n.) water.</li></ol>

odaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>water; abs. only at Ja.iii.282<br />■ <em>an˚</em>; without water, dried up Thig.265 (udaka-bhasta Thag-a.212). Cp. comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <strong>sītodaka</strong>, e.g. MN.i.376. See udaka.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://antika">antika</a></em><ol><li>neighbourhood of the water, a place near the water (see *<a href="bword://antika">antika</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) Kp.viii.1, Kp.viii.3 (gambhīre odakantike which Childers, <em>Kh. trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> p. 30, interprets “a deep pit” see also Kp-a.217 sq.).</li><li>“water at the end”, i.e. final ablution (see *<a href="bword://antika">antika</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), in spec. sense the ablution following upon the sexual act Vin.iii.21; cp. <em>odak-antikatā</em> (f. abstr.) final ablution, cleansing Ja.ii.126.</li></ol></li></ul><p>comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> form of udaka</p><p>See also: sītodaka, <a href="bword://antika">antika</a>, <a href="bword://antika">antika</a>, <a href="bword://antika">antika</a></p>

odakantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>ending with water; concluding with an ablution.</p>

odana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i></p><p>boiled rice; cooked grain.</p>

odana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><p>boiled (milk-)rice, gruel Vin.ii.214 (m.); DN.i.76, DN.i.105; SN.i.82 (nāḷik˚); Dhp-a.iv.17 (id.); AN.iii.49; AN.iv.231; Snp.18; Ja.iii.425 (til˚ m.) Dhs.646, Dhs.740, Dhs.875; Pv-a.73; Vv-a.98; Sdhp.113. Comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with kummāsa (sour milk) in phrase o-k-upa-caya a heap of boiled rice and sour milk, of the body (see <em><a href="bword://k%C4%81ya">kāya</a></em> I.) also at MN.i.247.</p><p>Sk. odana, to Idg.; <em>*ud</em>, from which also udaka, q.v. for full etym.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāya">kāya</a></p>

odanakummāsūpacaya|odanakummaasuupacaya|odanakummasupacaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odanakummaasuupacaya</span></p><p>built up by, achieving growth through, boiled rice and gruel or barley porridge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odana">odana</a></p>

odanapāka|odanapaaka|odanapaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odanapaaka</span></p><p>cooking of rice</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odana">odana</a></p>

odanasurā|odanasuraa|odanasura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odanasuraa</span></p><p>fermented rice water; rice liquor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odana">odana</a></p>

odanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>cook Ja.iii.49.</p><p>fr. odana</p>

odaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective belonging to rice-gruel, made of rice-gruel Vin.iii.59 (˚ghara a ricekitchen); Vv-a.73 (˚surā rice-liquor).</p><p>fr. odana, cp. Sk. odanika</p>

odaniyaghara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>rice seller’s house; a cooked rice shop.</p>

odapattakinī|odapattakinii|odapattakini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odapattakinii</span></p><p>(a wife) married with a ritual involving a bowl of water.</p>

odapattakinī|odapattakinii|odapattakini
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odapattakinii</span></p><p>(adj.) [f. of uda + pattaka + in, i.e. having a bowl of water, epithet of bhariyā a wife, viz. the wife in the quality of providing the house with water Thus in enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of the 10 kinds of wives (&amp; women in general) at Vin.iii.140 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by udakapattaṃ āmasitvā vāseti) = Vv-a.73.</p>

odapattiyā|odapattiyaa|odapattiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odapattiyaa</span></p><p>at Cp.ii.4#8 = last. Odarika &amp; ya;</p>

odarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>gluttonous; caring only for one’s stomach</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odariya">odariya</a></p>

odarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Odarika &amp; ˚ya</p><p>adjective living for one’s belly, voracious, gluttonous Mil.357; Ja.vi.208 (˚ya); Thag.101.</p><p>fr. udara</p>

odarikatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>gluttony; concern for one’s stomach</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odarika">odarika</a></p>

odarikatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>stomach-filling MN.i.461; Vism.71.</p><p>fr. odarika</p>

odariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>gluttonous; caring only for one’s stomach</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odarika">odarika</a></p>

odarya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Odarika &amp; ˚ya</p><p>adjective living for one’s belly, voracious, gluttonous Mil.357; Ja.vi.208 (˚ya); Thag.101.</p><p>fr. udara</p>

odhasta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>placed ready, lying ready to hand.</p>

odhasta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>fallen down, scattered MN.i.124 = SN.iv.176 (˚patoda; S reads odhasata but has variant reading odhasta).</p><p>Sk. avadhvasta, pp. of ava + dhvaṃsati: see <em><a href="bword://dha%E1%B9%83sati">dhaṃsati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhaṃsati">dhaṃsati</a></p>

odhastapatoda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with the goad laid down or lying ready in hand</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhasta">odhasta</a></p>

odhesiṃ|odhesi.m|odhesim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhesi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odheti">odheti</a></p>

odheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>places (in); lays down; applies; directs, esp. <em>~ati sotaṃ</em>, gives ear attentively</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

odhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(&amp; f.?) limit; outer edge, boundary; divisions.</p>

odhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>putting down, fixing, i.e. boundary, limit, extent Dhp-a.ii.80 (jaṇṇu-mattena odhinā to the extent of the knee, i.e. kneedeep); Dhp-a.iv.204 (id.)<br />■ <em><a href="bword://odhiso">odhiso</a></em> (adv.) limited, specifically Vb.246; Ne.12; Vism.309. Opp. <em>anodhi</em> MN.iii.219 (˚jina), also in <em>anodhiso</em> (adv.) unlimited, universal general Pts.ii.130, cp. anodissaka (odissaka); also as <em>anodhikatvā</em> without limit or distinction, absolutely Kv.208, and odhisodhiso “piecemeal” Kv.103 (cp. <em>Kvu trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 76<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, 127<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://su%E1%B9%85ka">suṅka</a></em> “extent of toll”, stake Ja.vi.279 (= sunkakoṭṭhāsaṃ C.).</li></ul><p>from odahati, Sk. avadhi, fr. ava + <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhiso">odhiso</a>, <a href="bword://suṅka">suṅka</a></p>

odhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “according to limit”, i.e. all kinds of, various, in phrase yathodhikāni kāmāni Snp.60, cp Cnd.526; Ja.v.392 (id.).</p><p>fr. odhi</p>

odhiso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i></p><p>in a limited way, within bounds; directed to limited or defined objects; individually, separately; partly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhi">odhi</a></p>

odhunitvā|odhunitvaa|odhunitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhunitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhunāti">odhunāti</a></p>

odhuniṃsu|odhuni.msu|odhunimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhuni.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odhunāti">odhunāti</a></p>

odhunāti|odhunaati|odhunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhunaati</span></p><p>shakes, shakes out or down; shakes off; discards, cancels.</p>

odhunāti|odhunaati|odhunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhunaati</span></p><p>to shake off MN.i.229; SN.iii.155; AN.iii.365 (+ niddhunāti); Pv.iv.3#54 (variant reading BB ophun˚, SS otu˚) = Pv-a.256; Vin.ii.317 (Bdhgh. in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of ogumphetvā of CV. v.11, 6; p. 117); Mil.399 (+ vidhunāti).</p><p>o + dhunāti</p>

odhānīya|odhaaniiya|odhaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhaaniiya</span></p><p>place for putting something down or into a receptacle Vin.i.204 (salāk˚, vy. ll. and gloss on p. 38 as follows: sālākāṭṭhāniya A, salākātaniya C, salākadhāraya B, salāk’odhāniyan ti yattha salākaṃ odahanti taṃ D E)<br />■ Cp. samodhāneti.</p><p>fr. avadhāna, ava + <em>dhā</em>, cp. Gr. ἀποχήκη, see <em><a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

odhārita|odhaarita|odharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odhaarita</span></p><p>determined; affirmed; stated with assurance.</p>

odissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>indicating, specifying; with reference (to a specific person)</p>

odissa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb only in neg. <em><a href="bword://anodissa">anodissa</a></em> without a purpose, indefinitely (? Mil.156 (should we read anudissa?).</p><p>ger. of o + disati = Sk. diśati, cp. uddissa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anodissa">anodissa</a></p>

odissaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective only in adv. expression <em>odissaka-vasena</em> definitely, in special, specifically (opp. to anodissaka-vasena in general, universally) Ja.i.82; Ja.ii.146; Vv-a.97. See also <em>anodissaka &amp; odhiso</em>.</p><p>fr. odissa</p>

odumbara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective belonging to the Udumbara tree Vv.50#16; cp. Vv-a.213.</p><p>fr. udumbara</p>

odāta|odaata|odata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaata</span></p><p>cleansed, clean, pure; bright, white; pale.</p>

odāta|odaata|odata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaata</span></p><p>adjective</p><p>clean, white, prominently applied to the dress as a sign of distinction (white), or special purity at festivities, ablutions &amp; sacrificial functions DN.ii.18 (uṇṇā, of the Buddha), DN.iii.268; AN.iii.239; AN.iv.94, AN.iv.263, AN.iv.306, AN.iv.349; AN.v.62; Dhs.617 = (in enumeration of colours); DN-a.i.219; Vv-a.111. See also <strong>ava˚</strong>. <em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> meditation on the white (colour) Vism.174 <em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">vaṇṇa</a></em> of white colour, white MN.ii.14; Dhs.247. <em>-<a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a></em> a white dress; adj. wearing a white dress, dressed in white DN.i.7, DN.i.76, DN.i.104; Ja.iii.425 (+ alla-kesa). <em>-<a href="bword://vasana">vasana</a></em> dressed in white (of householders or laymen as opposed to the yellow dress of the bhikkhus) DN.i.211; DN.iii.118 DN.iii.124 sq., DN.iii.210; MN.i.491, MN.ii.23; AN.i.73; AN.iii.384; AN.iv.217 [cp. BSk. avadāta-vasana Divy.160]</p><p>Derivation unknown. The Sk. is avadāta, ava + dāta, pp. of hypothetical <em>dā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to clean, purify</p><p>See also: ava˚, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://vaṇṇa">vaṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a>, <a href="bword://vasana">vasana</a></p>

odātagayhā|odaatagayhaa|odatagayha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaatagayhaa</span></p><p>A class of eminent devas among those present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.260</p>

odātaka|odaataka|odataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaataka</span></p><p>clean; white; pale; a white garment.</p>

odātaka|odaataka|odataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaataka</span></p><p>adjective white, clean, dressed in white SN.ii.284 (variant reading SS odāta); Thag.965 (dhaja).</p><p>fr. odāta</p>

odātakasiṇa|odaatakasi.na|odatakasina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaatakasi.na</span></p><p>concentration on the color white; a white meditational device</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odāta">odāta</a></p>

odātanibhāsa|odaatanibhaasa|odatanibhasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaatanibhaasa</span></p><p>appearing white; having a white appearance</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odāta">odāta</a></p>

odātavasana|odaatavasana|odatavasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odaatavasana</span></p><p>wearing white clothes (the mark of a householder)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odāta">odāta</a></p>

odīraka|odiiraka|odiraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">odiiraka</span></p><p>in odīrakajāta SN.iv.193 should with variant reading be read <em><a href="bword://oc%C4%ABraka">ocīraka</a></em> “with its bark off”, stripped of its bark.</p><p>= ava + cira + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ocīraka">ocīraka</a></p>

ogacchamāna|ogacchamaana|ogacchamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogacchamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

ogacchanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

ogacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes down, descents; goes lower, sets</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oggacchati">oggacchati</a></p>

ogacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go down, sink down, recede; of sun &amp; moon: to set DN.i.240 (opp. uggacchati); AN.iv.101 (udakāni og.). See also <strong>ava˚</strong>.</p><p>ava + gacchati</p><p>See also: ava˚</p>

ogadha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>firm footing in water, firm ground.</p>

ogadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective immersed, merging into, diving or plunging into. Only in two main phrases viz.; <em>Amatogadha &amp; Nibbānogadha;</em> diving into N. Besides these only in jagat’ogadha steeped in the world SN.i.186.</p><p>Sk. avagāḍha; P. form with shortened a, fr. ava + <em>gāh</em>, see *<a href="bword://g%C4%81dha">gādha</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; gāhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gādha">gādha</a></p>

ogahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>bathing place; a ford.</p>

ogahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>submersion ducking, bathing; fig. for bathing-place Snp.214 (= manussānaṃ nahāna-tittha Snp-a.265). See also <strong>avagāhana</strong>.</p><p>o + gahana fr. gāhati; Sk. avagāhana; concerning shortening of ā cp. avagadha</p><p>See also: avagāhana</p>

ogamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going down; setting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oggamana">oggamana</a></p>

ogamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going down, setting (of sun &amp; moon), always in contrast to; <em><a href="bword://uggamana">uggamana</a></em> (rising), therefore freq.variant reading ogg DN.i.10, DN.i.68; DN-a.i.95 (= atthangamana); Vv-a.326.</p><p>o + gam + ana; Sk. avagamana. That word is rather more than a thousand years later than the Pāli one. It would be ridiculous were one to suppose that the P. could be derived from the Sk. On the other hand the Sk. cannot be derived from the P. for it was formed at a time &amp; place when &amp; where P. was unknown, just as the Pali was formed at a time &amp; place when &amp; where Sk. was unknown. The two words are quite independent. They have no connection with one another except that they are examples of a rule of word-formation common to the two languages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggamana">uggamana</a></p>

ogata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>gone down; descended.</li><li>learnt, understood</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://oggata">oggata</a>, <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

ogayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oggayha">oggayha</a>, <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogaṇa|oga.na|ogana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oga.na</span></p><p>consisting of a small company; accompanied by a small (or no) company.</p>

ogaṇa|oga.na|ogana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oga.na</span></p><p>adjective separated from the troop or crowd, standing alone, Vin.i.80; Ja.iv.432 = (gaṇaṃ ohīna C.).</p><p>Vedic ogaṇa with dial. o for ava</p>

oggacchamāna|oggacchamaana|oggacchamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oggacchamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

oggacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes down, descents; goes lower, sets</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

oggamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going down; setting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogamana">ogamana</a></p>

oggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>gone down; descended.</li><li>learnt, understood</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogata">ogata</a>, <a href="bword://ogacchati">ogacchati</a></p>

oggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>gone down, set (of the sun) Vin.iv.55 (oggate suriye = atthangate s.), Vin.iv.268 (id. = ratt’ andhakāre); Thag.477 (anoggatasmiṃ suriyasmiṃ).</p><p>pp. of avagacchati: spelling gg on acct. of contrast with uggata, cp. avagamana. Müller <em>P. Gr.</em> 43 unwarrantedly puts oggata = apagata</p>

oggayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogayha">ogayha</a>, <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>rapid flow of water, a torrent; the torrent or flood of phenomenal life, of <em>saṃsāra</em>; a torrent or flood of evil or passion etc. (often identified with the <em>āsavas</em>).</p>

ogha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(rare in the old texts) a flood of water Vv-a.48 (udak’ ogha); usually as <strong>mahogha</strong> a great flood Dhp.47; Vism.512; Vv-a.110; Dhp-a.ii.274 = Thag-a.175.</li><li>(always in sg.) the flood of ignorance and vain desires which sweep a man down, away from the security of emancipation To him who has “crossed the flood”, <em>oghatiṇṇo</em>, are ascribed all, or nearly all, the mental and moral qualifications of the Arahant. For details see Snp.173, Snp.219, Snp.471 Snp.495, Snp.1059, Snp.1064, Snp.1070, Snp.1082; AN.ii.200 sq. Less often we have details of what the flood consists of. Thus <em>kāmogha</em> the fl. of lusts AN.iii.69 (cp. Dhs.1095, where o. is one of the many names of <em><a href="bword://ta%E1%B9%87h%C4%81">taṇhā</a></em>, craving, thirst). In the popular old riddle at SN.i.3 and Thag.15, Thag.633 (included also in the Dhp. Anthology, 370) the “flood” is 15 states of mind (the 5 bonds which impede a man on his entrance upon the Aryan Path, the 5 which impede him in his progress towards the end of the Path, and 5 other bonds: lust, ill-temper, stupidity, conceit, and vain speculation). Five <em>Oghas</em> referred to at SN.i.126 are possibly these last. Snp.945 says that the flood is <em><a href="bword://gedha">gedha</a></em> greed and the <strong>avijjogha</strong> of Pp.21 may perhaps belong here. As means of crossing the flood we have the Path SN.i.193 (˚assa nittharaṇatthaṃ); SN.iv.257; SN.v.59; It iii.(˚assa nittharanatthāya); faith SN.i.214 = Snp.184 = Mil.36; mindfulness SN.v.168, SN.v.186; the island Dhp.25; and the dyke Thag.7; Snp.4 (cp. DN.ii.89).</li><li>Towards the close of the Nikāya period we find, for the first time, the use of the word in the pl., and the mention of 4 <em>Oghas</em> identical with the 4 <em>Āsavas</em> (mental Intoxicants). See DN.iii.230, DN.iii.276; SN.iv.175, SN.iv.257; SN.v.59, SN.v.292, SN.v.309; Mnd.57, Mnd.159; Cnd.178 When the <em>oghas</em> had been thus grouped and classified in the livery, as it were, of a more popular simile, the older use of the word fell off, a tendency arose to think only of 4 oghas, and of these only as a name or phase of the 4 āsavas. So the Abhidhamma books (Dhs.1151 Vb.25 sq., Vb.43, Vb.65, Vb.77, Vb.129; Comp. Phil. 171). The Netti follows this (31, 114–24). Grouped in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> āsavagantha-ogha-yoga-agati-taṇh’upādāna at Vism.211. The later history of the word has yet to be investigated. But it may be already stated that the 5<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> cent. commentators persist in the error of explaining the old word ogha used in the singular, as referring to the 4 Āsavas; and they extend the old simile in other ways. Dhammapāla of Kāñcipura twice uses the word in the sense of flood of water (Vv-a.48, Vv-a.110, see above 1).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-ātiga</em> one who has overcome the flood Snp.1096 (cp Cnd.180).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ti%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">tiṇṇa</a></em> id. SN.i.3, SN.i.142; Snp.178, Snp.823, Snp.1082 Snp.1101, Snp.1145; Dhp.370 (= cattāro oghe tiṇṇa Dhp-a.iv.109) Vv.64#28 (= catunnaṃ oghānaṃ saṃsāra-mah’oghassa taritattā o. Vv-a.284); Vv.82#7 ; Mnd.159; Cnd.179.</li></ul><p>Vedic ogha and augha; BSk. ogha, e.g. Divy.95 caturogh’ ottīrṇa, Jtm.215 mahaugha. Etym. uncertain</p><p>See also: mahogha, <a href="bword://taṇhā">taṇhā</a>, <a href="bword://gedha">gedha</a>, avijjogha, <a href="bword://tiṇṇa">tiṇṇa</a></p>

oghana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>pushing downwards, downwards pressure; a thick or dense entanglement.</p>

oghana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>watering, flooding (?) MN.i.306 (variant reading ogha).</p>

oghaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective that which can be engulfed by floods (metaph.) Dhs.584 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 308); Vb.12, Vb.25 &amp; passim; Dhs-a.49.</p><p>fr. ogha(na)</p>

oghatama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>darkness of the flood(s)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></p>

oghatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>crossing the torrent or flood</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></p>

oghatiṇṇa|oghati.n.na|oghatinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oghati.n.na</span></p><p>who has crossed the torrent, who has overcome the flood(s)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></p>

oghātiga|oghaatiga|oghatiga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oghaatiga</span></p><p>going beyond or crossing the flood(s)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></p>

ogilati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to swallow down (opp. uggilati) MN.i.393 (inf. ogilituṃ) Mil.5 (id.).</p><p>o + gilati</p>

ogilituṃ|ogilitu.m|ogilitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogilitu.m</span></p><p>swallows, swallows down.</p>

ogumpheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>winds round, weaves round; ties together.</p>

ogumpheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to string together, wind round, adorn with wreaths, cover, dress Vin.i.194 (pass. ogumphiyanti; vv.ll. ogumbhiyanti ogubbiy˚, ogummīy˚, okumpiy˚); Vin.ii.142 (ogumphetvā).</p><p>ava + Denom. of gumpha garland</p>

ogumphetvā|ogumphetvaa|ogumphetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogumphetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogumpheti">ogumpheti</a></p>

ogumphiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogumpheti">ogumpheti</a></p>

oguṇṭheti|ogu.n.theti|oguntheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogu.n.theti</span></p><p>to cover, veil over, hide SN.iv.122 (ger. oguṇṭhitvā sīsaṃ, perhaps better read as oguṇṭhitā variant reading SS. okuṇṭhitū)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ogu%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhita">oguṇṭhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>o + guṇṭheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oguṇṭhita">oguṇṭhita</a></p>

oguṇṭhita|ogu.n.thita|ogunthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogu.n.thita</span></p><p>covered; with the head covered; veiled.</p>

oguṇṭhita|ogu.n.thita|ogunthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogu.n.thita</span></p><p>covered or dressed (with) Vin.ii.207; Pv-a.86 (variant reading okuṇṭhita).</p><p>pp. of oguṇṭheti, cp. BSk. avaguṇṭhita, e.g. Jtm.30</p>

oguṇṭhitvā|ogu.n.thitvaa|ogunthitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogu.n.thitvaa</span></p><p>covering (the head); veiling.</p>

ogādha|ogaadha|ogadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaadha</span></p><p>Ogādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective immersed, entered; firm, firmly footed or grounded in (-˚), spelt <em><a href="bword://og%C4%81%E1%B8%B7ha">ogāḷha</a></em> Mil.1 (abhidhamma-vinay˚). Cp. BSk. avagādhaśrāddha of deep faith Divy.268. Cp. pariyogāḷha.</p><p>Sk. avagāḍha; ava + gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Ogādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a firm place, firm ground, only in cpd. <em>ogādhappatta</em> having gained a sure footing AN.iii.297 sq.</p><p>ava + gādha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāḷha">ogāḷha</a></p>

ogādhappatta|ogaadhappatta|ogadhappatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaadhappatta</span></p><p>having gained firm footing.</p>

ogāha-m-uttiṇṇa|ogaaha-m-utti.n.na|ogaha-m-uttinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaaha-m-utti.n.na</span></p><p>ogāha-m-uttiṇṇa</p><p>mfn. emerged from the bathing place</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāha">ogāha</a></p>

ogāha|ogaaha|ogaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaaha</span></p><p>bathing place.</p>

ogāha|ogaaha|ogaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaaha</span></p><p>diving into; only in cpd. <em>pariy˚</em>. Ogahati (ogaheti)</p><p>fr. o + <em>gah</em></p>

ogāhana|ogaahana|ogahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahana</span></p><p>plunging into (-˚) Pv-a.158.</p><p>fr. ogāhati</p>

ogāhati FIXME double|ogaahati FIXME double|ogahati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahati FIXME double</span></p><p>Ogāhati (ogāheti)</p><p>to plunge or enter into, to be absorbed in (w. acc. or loc.). Pv.ii.12#11; Vv.6#1 (= anupavisati Vv-a.42), Vv.39#2 (sālavanaṃ o = pavisati Vv-a.177). <em>ogāheti</em> Pv-a.155 (pokkharaṇiṃ) ger. <em>ogāhetvā</em> MN.iii.175 (T. ogah˚; variant reading ogāhitvā); Pv-a.287 (lokanāthassa sāsanaṃ, variant reading ˚itvā). See also <strong>ava˚</strong>.</p><p>Sk. avagāhate; ava + gāhati</p><p>See also: ava˚</p>

ogāhati|ogaahati|ogahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahati</span></p><p>plunges into; bathes in; goes deep into (+ acc. or loc).</p>

ogāhati|ogaahati|ogahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahati</span></p><p>Avagāhati &amp; ogāhati</p><p>to plunge or enter into, to be absorbed in (acc. &amp; loc.) Vism.678 (vipassanāvīthiṃ); Sdhp.370, Sdhp.383.</p><p>ava + gāhati</p>

ogāhaṃ|ogaaha.m|ogaham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaaha.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogāheti|ogaaheti|ogaheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaaheti</span></p><p>Ogāhati (ogāheti)</p><p>to plunge or enter into, to be absorbed in (w. acc. or loc.). Pv.ii.12#11; Vv.6#1 (= anupavisati Vv-a.42), Vv.39#2 (sālavanaṃ o = pavisati Vv-a.177). <em>ogāheti</em> Pv-a.155 (pokkharaṇiṃ) ger. <em>ogāhetvā</em> MN.iii.175 (T. ogah˚; variant reading ogāhitvā); Pv-a.287 (lokanāthassa sāsanaṃ, variant reading ˚itvā). See also <strong>ava˚</strong>.</p><p>Sk. avagāhate; ava + gāhati</p><p>See also: ava˚</p>

ogāhetvā|ogaahetvaa|ogahetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahetvaa</span></p><p>ogāhetvā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>absol</p><p>ogāhetvā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. absol. plunging (someone) into; immersing; inserting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a>, <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogāhi|ogaahi|ogahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogāhitvā|ogaahitvaa|ogahitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaahitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ogāḷha|ogaa.lha|ogalha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ogaa.lha</span></p><p>having plunged into; immersed in</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ogāhati">ogāhati</a></p>

ohacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>extracting; raising, removing.</p>

ohana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in cpd. bimb’ohana, see under <em><a href="bword://bimba">bimba</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bimba">bimba</a></p>

ohanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to defecate, to empty the bowels Pv-a.268 (+ osajjati).</p><p>ava + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em>, but prob. a new formation from Pass. avahīyati of <em><a href="bword://h%C4%81">hā</a></em>, taking it to <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em> instead of the latter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://han">han</a>, <a href="bword://hā">hā</a>, <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

oharati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Oharati</p><p>to take away, take down, take off SN.i.27 (ger. ohacca, variant reading ūhacca); Pv.ii.6#6 (imper ohara = ohārehi Pv-a.95); Dhp-a.iv.56 (see <em><a href="bword://oh%C4%81rin">ohārin</a></em>). See also ava˚.</p><p>caus 1 <em><a href="bword://oh%C4%81reti">ohāreti</a></em> (see avahārati);<br />caus 2 <em>oharāpeti</em> in meaning of oharati to take down, to cut or shave off (hair) Ja.vi.52 (kesamassuṃ); Dhp-a.ii.53 (cp oropeti)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avaha%E1%B9%ADa">avahaṭa</a></em>.</p><p>o + <em>hṛ</em>; take</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohārin">ohārin</a>, <a href="bword://ohāreti">ohāreti</a>, <a href="bword://avahaṭa">avahaṭa</a></p>

oharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>brings down; takes down; puts down; removes; drags down.</p>

oharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avaharati &amp; oharati</p><p>to steal Ja.i.384; Pv-a.47 (avahari vatthaṃ), Pv-a.86 (id., = apānudi)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://avaha%E1%B9%ADa">avahaṭa</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ava + <em>hṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avahaṭa">avahaṭa</a></p>

oharaṇa|ohara.na|oharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ohara.na</span></p><p>lit. “taking away”, leading astray, side-track, deviating path Ja.vi.525 (C.: gamana-magga) Cp. avaharaṇa.</p><p>fr. oharati</p>

oharetvā|oharetvaa|oharetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oharetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oharati">oharati</a></p>

ohata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>take away, removed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohaṭa">ohaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://oharati">oharati</a></p>

ohaṭa|oha.ta|ohata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oha.ta</span></p><p>take away, removed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohata">ohata</a>, <a href="bword://oharati">oharati</a></p>

ohita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>placed in; confined (in); applied, directed.</li><li>laid down (?)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://odahati">odahati</a></p>

ohita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>put down into, deposited Dhp.150.</li><li>put down, laid down, taken off, relieved of, in phrase <em>ohitabhāro</em> (arahaṃ) (a Saint) who has laid down the burden: see <em><a href="bword://arahatta">arahatta</a></em> iii. C.; cp. ˚khandhabhāra Dhp-a.iv.168.</li><li>put down in, hidden, put away in (-˚) Snp.1022 = (kos’ohita).</li><li>(fig.) put down to, applied to, in <em>ohita-sota</em> listening, attentive, intent upon (cp. sotaṃ odahati to listen) usually in phrase ohitasoto dhammaṃ suṇāti; MN.i.480; MN.iii.201; SN.v.96; AN.iv.391 Vism.300 (+ aṭṭhiṃ katvā).</li></ol><p>pp. of odahati; BSk. avahita (Jtm.210 e.g.) as well as apahita (Lal.552 e.g.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://arahatta">arahatta</a></p>

ohitabhāra|ohitabhaara|ohitabhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ohitabhaara</span></p><p>whose burden has been put down or removed.</p>

ohitasota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with ears directed; with attentive ears, attentive</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohita">ohita</a></p>

ohiyyaka
