<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ohiiyitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohīyaka">ohīyaka</a></p>

ohīḷanā|ohii.lanaa|ohilana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ohii.lanaa</span></p><p>scorning, scornfulness Vb.353 (+ ohīḷattaṃ).</p><p>ava + hīḷanā, of <em>hīḍ</em></p>

ojahāti|ojahaati|ojahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojahaati</span></p><p>to give up, leave, leave behind, renounce, ger. ohāya DN.i.115 (ñāti-sanghaṃ &amp; hirañña-suvaṇṇaṃ) MN.ii.166 (id.); Ja.v.340 (= chaḍḍetvā C.); Pv-a.93 (maṃ). Pass. <em>avahīyati &amp; ohīyati;</em>, pp. <em><a href="bword://oh%C4%ABna">ohīna</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://ohanati">ohanati</a></em>.</p><p>o + jahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ohīna">ohīna</a>, <a href="bword://ohanati">ohanati</a></p>

ojasi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Servant of Kuvera. He takes Kuvera’s messages and makes them known in Uttarakuru. DN.iii.201</p>

ojava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ojavat">ojavat</a></p><p>vigorous, strong; sappy; juicy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojā">ojā</a></p>

ojavanikā|ojavanikaa|ojavanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojavanikaa</span></p><p>the going downstream.</p>

ojavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective possessing strengthening qualities, giving strength. MN.i.480 SN.i.212 (so read for ovajaṁ; phrase ojavaṁ asecanakaṁ of Nibbāna, translated “elixir”) Thag.2, 196 (identical ojavantaṁ Thag-a.168) AN.iii.260 (an˚ of food, i.e. not nourishing Dhp-a.i.106</p><p>fr. ojā; Vedic ojasvant in diff. meaning: powerful</p>

ojavantatā|ojavantataa|ojavantata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojavantataa</span></p><p>richness in sap, strength giving (nourishing) quality. Ja.i.68 (of milk)</p><p>abstr. fr. ajavant</p>

ojavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ojavat">ojavat</a></p><p>vigorous, strong; sappy; juicy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojā">ojā</a></p>

ojaṭṭhāyi|oja.t.thaayi|ojatthayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oja.t.thaayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ojaṭṭhāyin">ojaṭṭhāyin</a></p><p>resting on or supported by its vigor or vitality</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojā">ojā</a></p>

ojaṭṭhāyin|oja.t.thaayin|ojatthayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oja.t.thaayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ojaṭṭhāyin">ojaṭṭhāyin</a></p><p>resting on or supported by its vigor or vitality</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ojā">ojā</a></p>

ojināti|ojinaati|ojinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojinaati</span></p><p>to conquer, vanquish, subdue Ja.vi.222 (ojināmase).</p><p>Sk. avajayati, ava + <em>ji</em></p>

ojā|ojaa|oja
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojaa</span></p><p>See <em>Jatu</em> and <em>Rukkha</em>.</p><p>See also: Jatu, Rukkha</p>

ojā|ojaa|oja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojaa</span></p><p>bodily strength, vigour, vitality (produced by food); nutriment, sustenance; sap, juice.</p>

ojā|ojaa|oja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojaa</span></p><p>strength, but only in meaning of strength-giving, nutritive essence (appl; <!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to food). MN.i.245 SN.ii.87 SN.v.162 (dhamm’) AN.iii.396 Ja.i.68 Dhs.646 Dhs.740, Dhs.875 Mil.156 Dhp-a.ii.154 (paṭhav˚) ‣See also definition at Vism.450 (referring to kabalinkār’āhāra. The compound form is oja, e.g. ojadāna Ja.v.243 ojaṭṭhamaka (rūpa) Vism.341</p><p>Vedic ojas nt., also BSk. oja nt. Divy.105; fr. <em>*aug</em> to increase, as in Lat. auges, augustus &amp; auxilium Goth. aukan (augment), Ags. ēacian; cp. also Gr. ἀέςω Sk. ukṣati &amp; vakṣana increase</p>

ojāpaharaṇa|ojaapahara.na|ojapaharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ojaapahara.na</span></p><p>(~ī)n. robbing of strength or vitality.</p>

oka-m-okata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>oka-m-okata</p><p>ind. from its home, the water; from a watery home</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka-m-okato">oka-m-okato</a></p>

oka-m-okato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>oka-m-okato</p><p>ind. from its home, the water; from a watery home</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka-m-okata">oka-m-okata</a></p>

oka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>house, home; resort, refuge.</p>

oka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>resting place, shelter, resort; house, dwelling; fig. (this meaning according to later commentators prevailing in anoka, liking, fondness, attachment to (worldly things) SN.iii.9 = Snp.844 (okam pahāya; oka here is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Snp-a.547 by rūpa-vatth’ ādi-viññaṇass’ okāso); SN.v.24 = AN.v.232 = Dhp.87 (okā anokam āgamma); Dhp.34 (oka-m-okata ubbhato, i.e. oka-m-okato from this &amp; that abode, from all places, thus taken as okato, whereas Bdhgh. takes it as okasya okato and interprets the first oka as contracted form of udaka, water, which happens to fit in with the sense required at this passage, but is not warranted otherwise except by Bdhgh’s quotation “okapuṇṇehi cīvarehī ti ettha udakaṃ”. This quot. is taken from Vin.i.253, which must be regarded as a corrupt passage cp. remarks of Bdhgh. on p. 387: oghapuṇṇehī ti pi pāṭho. The rest of his interpretation at Dhp-a.i.289 runs: “okaṃ okaṃ pahāya aniketa-sārī ti ettha ālayo, idha (i.e. at Dhp.34) ubhayam pi labbhati okamokato udaka-sankhātā ālayā ti attho” i.e. from the water’s abode. Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is of course problematic); Dhp.91 (okam okaṃ jahanti “they leave whatever shelter they have”, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by ālaya Dhp-a.ii.170).</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://cara">cara</a></em> (f. ˚carikā Ja.vi.416; ˚cārikā MN.i.117) living in the house (said of animals), i.e. tame (cp. same etym of “tame” = Lat. domus, domesticus). The passage MN.i.117, MN.i.118 has caused confusion by oka being taken as “water”. But from the context as well as from C. on Ja.vi.416 it is clear that here a tame animal is meant by means of which other wild ones are caught. The passage at MN.i.117 runs “<em>odaheyya</em> okacaraṃ <em>ṭhapeyya</em> okacārikaṃ” i.e. he puts down a male decoy and places a female (to entice the others), opp. “<em>ūhaneyya</em> o. <em>nāseyya</em> o.” i.e. takes away the male &amp; kills the female; <em>-(ñ)jaha</em> giving up the house (and its comfort), renouncing (the world), giving up attachment Snp.1101 (= ālayaṁjahaṁ Snp-a.598 cp. Cnd.176 with variant reading oghaṁjaha)<br />■ <em>-anoka</em> houseless, homeless, comfortless, renouncing, free from attachment ‣See separately.</p><p>Vedic okas (nt.), fr. <em>uc</em> to like, thus orig. “comfort”, hence place of comfort, sheltered place, habitation The indigenous interpretation connects oka partly with okāsa = fig. room (for rising), chance, occasion (thus Mnd.487 on Snp.966: see <em><a href="bword://anoka">anoka</a></em>; Snp-a.573 ibid.; Snp-a.547: see <em><a href="bword://anoka">anoka</a></em>; Snp-a.573 ibid.; Snp-a.547: see below) partly with udaka (as contraction): see below on Dhp.34 Geiger (<em>P. Gr.</em> § 20) considers oka to be a direct contraction of udaka (via *udaka, *utka, *ukka, *okka). The customary synomym for oka (both lit. &amp; fig.) is ālaya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cara">cara</a>, <a href="bword://anoka">anoka</a>, <a href="bword://anoka">anoka</a></p>

oka-ñ-jaha|oka-~n-jaha|oka-n-jaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oka-~n-jaha</span></p><p>oka-ñ-jaha</p><p>mfn. leaving home, abandoning a house; without resort</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

okacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>decoy deer; a tame animal</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

okacārikā|okacaarikaa|okacarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okacaarikaa</span></p><p>tame animal; a tamed and trained female deer</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

okampeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shake, to wag, only in phrase <em>sīsaṃ okampeti</em> to shake one’s head MN.i.108 MN.i.171; SN.i.118.</p><p>o + Caus. of <em>kamp</em></p>

okampetvā|okampetvaa|okampetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okampetvaa</span></p><p>shaking; wagging.</p>

okantati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okantati (okkant˚)</p><p>to cut off, cut out, cut away, carve; pres. <em>okantati</em> MN.i.129; Pv.iii.10#2 (= ava˚ Pv-a.213); ger. <em>okantitvā</em> Ja.i.154 (migaṃ o. after carving the deer); Pv-a.192 (piṭṭhi maṃsāni), &amp; <em>okacca</em> Ja.iv.210 (T. okkacca, variant reading BB ukk˚ C. explains by okkantitvā)<br />pp <em>avakanta &amp; avakantita</em>.</p><p>o + kantati, cp. also apakantati</p>

okantati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cuts, cuts off; cuts up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkantati">okkantati</a></p>

okantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakantati &amp; okantati</p><p>(okk˚) to cut off, cut out, cut away carve-(<em>ava</em>:) Ja.iv.155<br />pp <em>avakanta &amp; avakantita</em>.</p><p>cp. Sk. avakṛntati, ava + kantati, cp. also apakantati</p>

okappanā FIXME double|okappanaa FIXME double|okappana FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okappanaa FIXME double</span></p><p>Okappanā</p><p>feminine fixing one’s mind (on), settling in, putting (trust) in, confidence Dhs.12, Dhs.25, Dhs.96 Dhs.288; Ne.15, Ne.19, Ne.28; Vb.170.</p><p>o + kappanā</p>

okappanā|okappanaa|okappana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okappanaa</span></p><p>Avakappanā &amp; okappanā</p><p>feminine preparation, fixing up, esp. harnessing Ja.vi.408.</p><p>ava + kappanā</p>

okappanīya|okappaniiya|okappaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okappaniiya</span></p><p>to believe; to trust.</p>

okappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to preface, arrange, make ready, settle on, feel confident, put (trust) in Vin.iv.4; Pts.ii.19 (= saddahati ibid. 21); Mil.150, Mil.234; DN-a.i.243.</p><p>o + kappati</p>

okappeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>considers as possible, believes likely, gives credence to; trusts, has confidence (in, ace. or gen./dat.); is sure (about).</p>

okappeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to fix one’s mind on, to put one’s trust in MN.i.11; Mil.234 (okappessati).</p><p>o + kappeti</p>

okapuṇṇa|okapu.n.na|okapunna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okapu.n.na</span></p><p>soaked with water.</p>

okassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>dragging down or away; roughly, forcibly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkassa">okkassa</a></p>

okassati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okassati</p><p>to drag down, draw or pull away, distract, remove. Only in ger.; <em>okassa</em>, always comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with pasayha “removing by force” DN.ii.74 (T. okk˚); AN.iv.16 (T. okk˚, variant reading ok˚), AN.iv.65 (id.); Mil.210. Also in Caus. <em>okasseti</em> to pull out draw out Thig.116 (vaṭṭiṃ = dīpavaṭṭiṃ ākaḍḍheti Thag-a.117). [MSS. often spell <em>okk˚</em>; ].</p><p>o + kassati, see also <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em> &amp; avakaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

okassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakassati &amp; okassati</p><p>to drag down, to draw or pull away, distract, remove<br />■ AN.v.74 = Vin.ii.204 (+ vavakassati).</p><p>cp. Sk. avakarṣati, ava + kṛṣ; see also <em><a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></em> &amp; avakaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakassati">apakassati</a></p>

okasāri|okasaari|okasari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okasaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okasārin">okasārin</a></p><p>frequenting a house; keeping to a refuge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

okasārin|okasaarin|okasarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okasaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okasārin">okasārin</a></p><p>frequenting a house; keeping to a refuge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oka">oka</a></p>

okaḍḍhati|oka.d.dhati|okaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oka.d.dhati</span></p><p>drags away, drags off.</p>

okaḍḍhati|oka.d.dhati|okaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to drag away, remove Thig.444. See also ava˚. Okantati (okkant)</p><p>o + kaḍḍhati</p>

okhittapaligha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with crossbars put down (into position)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhitta">okkhitta</a></p>

okilinī|okilinii|okilini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okilinii</span></p><p>(according to commentary) discharging; exuding moisture.</p>

okilinī|okilinii|okilini
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okilinii</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://okira%E1%B9%87a">okiraṇa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okiraṇa">okiraṇa</a></p>

okirati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okirati</p><ol><li>to pour down on, pour out over MN.i.79; aor. okiri Vin.iii.107 = SN.ii.260; Pv.ii.3#8; Pv-a.82.</li><li>to cast-out, reject, throw out: see <em><a href="bword://okira%E1%B9%87a">okiraṇa</a></em>.</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://oki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">okiṇṇa</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus 2 <strong>okirāpeti</strong> to cause to pour out or to sprinkle over Vism.74 (vālikaṃ).</p><p>o + kirati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okiraṇa">okiraṇa</a>, <a href="bword://okiṇṇa">okiṇṇa</a>, okirāpeti</p>

okirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>ours out or down upon or into (loc.); scatters (upon, loc.); bestrews, covers (with, instr.).</p>

okirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakirati &amp; okirati</p><ol><li>to pour down on, to pour out over; aor. avakiri Pv-a.86; ger. ˚kiritvā Ja.v.144.</li><li>to cast out, reject, throw out; aor. avākiri Vv.30#5 = Vv.48#5 (variant reading ˚kari; Vv-a.126 explains by chaḍḍesi vināsesi)</li></ol><p>pass <em>avakirīyati</em> Pv.iii.1#10 (= chaḍḍīyati Pv-a.174); grd. ˚kiriya (see sep.). See also apakiritūna. pp <em><a href="bword://oki%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">okiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>ava + kirati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okiṇṇa">okiṇṇa</a></p>

okiraṇa|okira.na|okirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okira.na</span></p><p>casting out (see the later avakirati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), only as adj<br />■ f. <em>okirinī (okilinī</em> through dialect. variation a cast-out woman (cast-out on acct of some cutaneous disease), in double comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> okilinī okirinī (perhaps only the latter should be written) Vin.iii.107 = SN.ii.260 (in play of words with avakirati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->). Bdhgh’s allegorical expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vin.iii.273 puts okilinī = kilinnasarīrā, okiriṇī = angāraparikiṇṇa Cp. kirāta.</p><p>o + kiraṇa</p>

okiri
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okirati">okirati</a></p>

okiriṃsu|okiri.msu|okirimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okiri.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okirati">okirati</a></p>

okiriṇi|okiri.ni|okirini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okiri.ni</span></p><p>(according to commentary) spotted, spattered.</p>

okirīyati|okiriiyati|okiriyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okiriiyati</span></p><p>is bestrewn, covered (with); extends in various directions</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okirati">okirati</a></p>

okiṇṇa|oki.n.na|okinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oki.n.na</span></p><p>bestrewn, covered with; surrounded by</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okirati">okirati</a></p>

okiṇṇa|oki.n.na|okinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oki.n.na</span></p><p>strewn over, beset by, covered with, full of Ja.v.74, Ja.v.370; Pv-a.86, Pv-a.189 (= otata of Pv.iii.3#3).</p><p>pp. of okirati; BSk. avakīrṇa Divy.282; Jtm.31#92</p>

okiṇṇavikiṇṇa|oki.n.naviki.n.na|okinnavikinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oki.n.naviki.n.na</span></p><p>covered and filled with</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okiṇṇa">okiṇṇa</a></p>

okkala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ukkala">ukkala</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkala">ukkala</a></p>

okkalā|okkalaa|okkala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkalaa</span></p><p>the inhabitants of the country Ukkala (modern day Orissa).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkalā">ukkalā</a></p>

okkama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okkamat">okkamat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamamāna|okkamamaana|okkamamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkamamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>okkamana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter coming down (into); entering; entering (a womb), conception.</p><p>okkamana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter going away from; going astray; neglecting; transgression.</p>

okkamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>entering into, approaching, reaching MN.iii.6; AN.iii.108 (entering the path); also in phrase nibbānassa okkamanāya AN.iv.111 sq., cp. AN.iv.230 sq.</p><p>fr. okkamati</p>

okkamaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>connected with going astray (or going down); or to be abandoned.</p>

okkamanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okkamat">okkamat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okkamati</p><p>lit. to enter, go down into, fall into. fig. to come on, to develop, to appear in (of a subjective state). It is strange that this important word has been so much misunderstood, for the English idiom is the same. We say ʻhe went to sleep’, without meaning that he went anywhere. So we may twist it round and say that ʻsleep overcame him’, without meaning any struggle. The two phrases mean exactly the same an internal change, or development, culminating in sleep. So in Pali <em>niddā okkami</em> sleep fell upon him, Vin.i.15 <em>niddaṃ okkami</em> he fell on sleep, asleep, Dhp-a.i.9; Pv-a.47 At Iti.76 we hear that a dullness developed (dubbaṇṇiyaṃ okkami) on the body of a god, he lost his radiance. At DN.ii.12; MN.iii.119 a god, on his rebirth, entered his new mother’s womb (kucchiṃ okkami). At DN.ii.63 occurs the question ʻif consciousness were not to develop in the womb?’ (viññāṇaṃ na okkamissatha) SN.v.283 ʻabiding in the sense of bliss’ (sukha-saññaṃ okkamitvā). See also Pp.13 = Pp.28 (niyāma okk˚, ʻhe enters on the Path’). Caus. <em>okkāmeti</em> to make enter, to bring to SN.iv.312 (saggaṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://okkanta">okkanta</a></em>. See also avakkamati.</p><p>o + kamati fr. <em>kram</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkanta">okkanta</a></p>

okkamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>steps down upon; comes down into, moves into, enters (+ acc. or loc), esp. <em>niddaṃ ~ati</em>, falls asleep; enters (a womb), takes birth; comes down upon (+ acc. gen. or loc.); treads down overcomes.</p>

okkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkamati &amp; okkamati</p><p>to approach. to enter, go into or near to, to fall into, appear in, only in ger. (poetically) avakamma Ja.iii.480 (variant reading apa˚).</p><p>ava + kamati fr. <em>kram</em></p>

okkamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>moves into, enters</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkameti">okkameti</a>, <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>okkameti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. moves into, enters</p><p>okkameti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. makes go up; makes move away.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamayati">okkamayati</a>, <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a>, <a href="bword://okkāmeti">okkāmeti</a></p>

okkami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamissatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>cond. 3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okkamitar">okkamitar</a></p><p>one who moves into, enters.</p>

okkamitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://okkamitar">okkamitar</a></p><p>one who moves into, enters.</p>

okkamitvā|okkamitvaa|okkamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkamitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamiṃsu|okkami.msu|okkamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkamati">okkamati</a></p>

okkamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>having gone aside (from); having gone over, neglected.</p>

okkandika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.ii.448 is doubtful, variant reading <em><a href="bword://okkantika">okkantika</a></em>. It is used adverbially: <em>okkandikaṃ kīḷati</em> to sport (loudly or joyfully). C. explains as “migo viya okkandi-katvā kīḷati”; in the way of roaring(?) or frisking about(?), like a deer.</p><p><em>kand</em> or <em>kram?</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkantika">okkantika</a></p>

okkant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okantati (okkant˚)</p><p>to cut off, cut out, cut away, carve; pres. <em>okantati</em> MN.i.129; Pv.iii.10#2 (= ava˚ Pv-a.213); ger. <em>okantitvā</em> Ja.i.154 (migaṃ o. after carving the deer); Pv-a.192 (piṭṭhi maṃsāni), &amp; <em>okacca</em> Ja.iv.210 (T. okkacca, variant reading BB ukk˚ C. explains by okkantitvā)<br />pp <em>avakanta &amp; avakantita</em>.</p><p>o + kantati, cp. also apakantati</p>

okkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp.</i></p><p>entered; fallen into; come on.</p>

okkanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>coming on, approaching, taking place DN.ii.12; Mil.299 (middhe okkante). See also avakkanta SN.ii.174; SN.iii.46.</p><p>pp. of okkamati</p>

okkantati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cuts, cuts off; cuts up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okantati">okantati</a></p>

okkanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>coming down (into); entering (the womb), conception.</p>

okkanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>entry (lit. descent), appearance, coming to be. Usually in stock phrase <em>jāṭi sañjāti o. nibbatti</em> MN.iii.249; SN.ii.3; SN.iii.225; Cnd.257; Pp-a.184 Also in gabbh˚ entry into the womb DN-a.i.130.</p><p>fr. okkamati</p>

okkantika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective coming into existence again and again, recurring. Only as epithet of pīti, joy. The opposite is khaṇika, momentary Vism.143 = Dhs-a.115 (<em>Expositor</em> 153 trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “flooding”).</p><p>fr. okkanti</p>

okkassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>dragging down or away; roughly, forcibly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okassa">okassa</a></p>

okkassa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://okassati">okassati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okassati">okassati</a></p>

okkassayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>draws down or out.</p>

okkhipati FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okkhipati</p><p>to throw down or out, cast down, drop; fig. usually appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to the eyes = cast down, hence transferred to the other senses and used in meaning “keep under, restrain, to have control over” (cp. also avakkhāyati);<br />aor ˚khipi AN.iv.264 (indriyāni);<br />ger ˚khipitvā Vin.iv.18 (id.)<br />pp <strong>avakkhitta &amp; okkhitta;</strong> (q.v.).</p><p>ava + khipati; Sk. avakṣipati</p><p>See also: avakkhitta &amp; okkhitta;</p>

okkhipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws down, casts down; subdues, controls; disparages.</p>

okkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkhipati &amp; okkhipati</p><p>to throw down or out, cast down, drop; fig usually applied to the eyes = to cast down, hence transferred to the other senses and used in meaning of “to keep under, to restrain, to have control over” (cp. also avakkhāyati), aor. ˚khipi DN-a.i.268 (bhusaṃ, variant reading avakkhasi).</p><p>ava + khipati; cp. Sk. avakṣipati</p>

okkhipitvā|okkhipitvaa|okkhipitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhipitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhipati">okkhipati</a></p>

okkhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>besprinkled, bestrewn with (-˚) Thig.145 (candan˚ = candanânulitta Thag-a.137); Ja.v.72 (so in variant reading T. reads okkita; C. explains by okiṇṇa parikkita parivārita).</p><p>pp. of ava + ukkhati, Sk. avokṣita, fr. <em>ukṣ</em> to sprinkle</p>

okkhitta FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Okkhitta</p><p>thrown down, flung down, cast down, dropped; thrown out, rejected; only in phrase <em>okkhitta-cakkhu</em>, with down-cast eyes, i.e. turning the eyes away from any objectionable sight which might impair the morale of the bhikkhu; thus meaning “with eyes under control” Snp.63, Snp.411, Snp.972; Mnd.498; Cnd.177; Pv.iv.3#44 (variant reading ukkh˚); Vv-a.6<br />■ For further use &amp; meaning see; <em><a href="bword://avakkhitta">avakkhitta</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of okkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avakkhitta">avakkhitta</a></p>

okkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>throws down; cast down; subdued</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhipati">okkhipati</a></p>

okkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avakkhitta &amp; okkhitta</p><ol><li>[= Sk. avakṣipta] thrown down, flung down, cast down, dropped thrown out, rejected. (<em>ava</em>:) MN.i.296 (ujjhita +); DN-a.i.281 (an˚), DN-a.i.289 (pinḍa); Pv-a.174 (piṇḍa).</li><li>[= Sk. utkṣipta?] thrown off, gained, produced, got (cp. uppādita), in phrase <em>sed’ âvakkhitta</em> gained by sweat AN.ii.67; AN.iii.45.</li></ol><p>pp. of avakkhipati</p>

okkhittacakku
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with eyes cast down; with eyes under control</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhitta">okkhitta</a></p>

okkhā|okkhaa|okkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaa</span></p><p>cooking pot.</p>

okkhāsata|okkhaasata|okkhasata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaasata</span></p><p>hundred cooking pots</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhā">okkhā</a></p>

okkhāyati|okkhaayati|okkhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaayati</span></p><p>is seen or known, is visible, appears.</p>

okkhāyati|okkhaayati|okkhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaayati</span></p><p>to lie low, to be restrained (in this sense evidently confounded with avakkhipati) SN.iv.144 sq. (cakkhuṃ etc okkhāyati).</p><p>ava + khāyati, corresp. to Sk. kṣeti fr. <em>kṣi</em> to lie</p>

okkhāyika|okkhaayika|okkhayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaayika</span></p><p>apparent, visible; seeming (to be); or far removed.</p>

okkhāyika|okkhaayika|okkhayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkhaayika</span></p><p>adjective low-lying, deep remote, only in one phrase, viz. udaka-tarakā gambhīragatā okkhāyikā MN.i.80, MN.i.245.</p><p>fr. ava + khāyin fr. <em>kṣi</em>, cp. avakkhāyati; Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. suggests relation to BSk. avakhāta of <em>khan</em>, and compares Lal.319</p>

okkādhara|okkaadhara|okkadhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkaadhara</span></p><p>torch bearer.</p>

okkāka|okkaaka|okkaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkaaka</span></p><p>A king, ancestor of the Sākyas and the Kolians.</p><p>In the Ambaṭṭha Sutta DN.i.92 it is stated that Okkāka, being fond of his queen and wishing to transfer the kingdom to her son, banished from the kingdom the elder princes by another wife. These princes were named Okkāmukha, Karakaṇḍa, Hatthinika, and Sīnipura. They lived on the slopes of the Himalaya and, consorting with their sisters and their descendants, formed the Sākyan race.</p><p>Okkāka had a slave-girl called Disā, who gave birth to a black baby named, accordingly, Kaṇha. He was the ancestor of the Kaṇhāyanas, of which race the Ambaṭṭha-clan was an offshoot. Later, Kaṇha became a mighty sage and, by his magic power, won in marriage Maddarūpi, another daughter of Okkāka. DN.i.93 DN.i.96</p><p>According to the Brāhmaṇa-Dhammika Sutta, Snp.p.52<em>ff.</em> it was during the time of Okkāka that the brahmins started their practice of slaughtering animals for sacrifice. Till then there had been only three diseases in the world—desire, hunger and old age; but from this time onwards the enraged devas afflicted humans with various kinds of suffering.</p>

okkāmeti|okkaameti|okkameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okkaameti</span></p><p>makes go up; makes move away.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkameti">okkameti</a></p>

okoṭimaka|oko.timaka|okotimaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oko.timaka</span></p><p>dwarfish.</p>

okoṭimaka|oko.timaka|okotimaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oko.timaka</span></p><p>adjective lit. “having the top lowered”, with the head squashed in or down, i.e. of compressed &amp; bulging out stature; misshapen, deformed, of ugly shape (Mrs. Rh. D trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s<!-- raw HTML omitted --> hunchback at SN.i.94, pot-bellied at SN.i.237; Warren <em>Buddhism</em> p. 426 trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> decrepit). It occurs only in one stock phrase, viz. <em>dubbaṇṇa dud-das(s)ika okoṭimaka</em> “of bad complexion, of ugly appearance and dwarfed” at Vin.ii.90 = SN.i.94 = AN.i.107 = AN.ii.85 = AN.iii.285 sq. = Pp.51. The same also at MN.iii.169; SN.i.237; SN.ii.279; Ud.76.</p><p>o + koṭi + mant + ka. Ava in BSk., in formula durvarṇa durdarśana avakoṭimaka Sp. Avs.i.280. Kern (note on above passage) problematically refers it to Sk. avakūṭara = vairūpya (Pāṇini v.2, 30). The Commentary on SN.i.237 explains by mahodara (fat-bellied as well as lakuṇṭaka (dwarf); Pp-a.227 explains by lakuṇṭaka only</p>

okāra|okaara|okara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaara</span></p><p>degradation, demeaning.</p>

okāra|okaara|okara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaara</span></p><p>only in stock phrase kāmānaṃ ādīnavo okāro sankileso DN.i.110, DN.i.148 (= lāmaka-bhāva DN-a.i.277); MN.i.115 MN.i.379, MN.i.405 sq.; MN.ii.145; AN.iv.186; Ne.42 (variant reading vokāra) Dhp-a.i.6, Dhp-a.i.67. The exact meaning is uncertain. Etymologically it would be degradation. But Bdhgh. prefers folly, vanity, and this suits the context better.</p><p>o + kāra fr. karoti, BSk. okāra, e.g. Mvu.iii.357</p>

okāsa FIXME double|okaasa FIXME double|okasa FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa FIXME double</span></p><p>Okāsa</p><ol><li>lit. “visibility”, (visible) space as geometrical term, open space, atmosphere, air as space DN.i.34 (ananto okāso); Vism.184 (with disā pariccheda), Vism.243 (id.); Pv-a.14 (okāsaṃ pharitvā permeating the atmosphere). This meaning is more pronounced in <em><a href="bword://%C4%81k%C4%81sa">ākāsa</a></em>.</li><li>“visibility”, i.e. appearance, as adj looking like, appearing. This meaning closely resembles &amp; often passes over into meaning 3, e.g. katokāsa kamma when the k. makes its appearance = when its chance or opportunity arises Pv-a.63; okāsaṃ deti to give one’s appearance, i.e. to let any one see, to be seen by (dat. Pv-a.19.</li><li>occasion, chance, opportunity, permission consent, leave AN.i.253; AN.iv.449; Ja.iv.413 (vātassa o. natthi the wind has no access); Snp-a.547<br />■ In this meaning freq. in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with foll. verbs:<ol><li>okāsaṃ karoti to give permission, to admit, allow; to give a chance or opportunity, freq. with pañhassa veyyā-karaṇāya (to ask a question), e.g. DN.i.51, DN.i.205; MN.ii.142; SN.iv.57. Vin.i.114, Vin.i.170; Mnd.487; Pv-a.222<br />caus ˚ṃ karoti Vin.ii.5, Vin.ii.6, Vin.ii.276;<br />caus 2 ˚ṃ kārāpeti Vin.i.114, Vin.i.170. katokāsa given permission (to speak), admitted in audience granted leave Snp.1031; Vv-a.65 (raññā); anokāsakata without having got permission Vin.i.114</li><li>okāsaṃ <em><a href="bword://y%C4%81cati">yācati</a></em> to ask permission MN.ii.123</li><li>okāsaṃ <em><a href="bword://deti">deti</a></em> to give permission, to consent, give room Ja.ii.3; Vv-a.138.</li><li>with <em><a href="bword://bh%C5%AB">bhū</a></em>: anokāsa-bhāva want of opportunity Sdhp.15; anokāsa-bhūta not giving (literally becoming) an opportunity Snp-a.573 Elliptically for o. detha <em>Yogāvacara’s Man.</em> 4 etc</li></ol></li></ol><ul><li><em>-ādhigama</em> finding an opportunity DN.ii.214 sq.; AN.iv.449.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> giving opportunity or permission Snp.p. 94 (˚kata allowed); Pv.iv.1#11 (˚ṁ karoti to give permission)</li><li><em>-matta</em> permission Snp.p. 94.</li><li><em>-loka</em> the visible world (manussa-loka) Vism.205 Vv-a.29</li></ul><p>ava + <em>kāś</em> to shine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a>, <a href="bword://yācati">yācati</a>, <a href="bword://deti">deti</a>, <a href="bword://bhū">bhū</a>, <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

okāsa|okaasa|okasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa</span></p><p>space, room; place, area; occasion, opportunity; possibility, chance.</p>

okāsa|okaasa|okasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa</span></p><p>Avakāsa &amp; okāsa</p><ol><li>“appearance”: akkhuddâvakāso dassanāya not little (or inferior) to behold (of appearance) DN.i.114; ariyāvakāsa appearing noble or having the app. of an Aryan Ja.v.87; katâvakāsa put into appearance Vv.22#9.</li><li>“opportunity”: kata˚ given leave DN.i.276; Snp.1030; anavakāsakārin not giving occasion Mil.383<br />■ <em>anavakāsa</em> not having a chance or opportunity (to happen), impossible; always in ster. phrase aṭṭhānaṃ etaṃ anavakāso Vin.ii.199; AN.i.26; AN.v.169; Pp.11, Pp.12; Pv-a.28.</li></ol><p>ava + <em>kāś</em> to shine, cp. Sk. avakāśa</p>

okāsati|okaasati|okasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasati</span></p><p>to be visible; Caus. <em>okāseti</em> to make visible, let appear, show SN.iv.290.</p><p>ava + <em>kāś</em></p>

okāsaṃ karoti|okaasa.m karoti|okasam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa.m karoti</span></p><p>makes a space; gives way; gives the opportunity (esp. for speech); allows, permits; consents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okāsa">okāsa</a></p>

okāsaṃ kāreti|okaasa.m kaareti|okasam kareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa.m kaareti</span></p><p>causes to give an opportunity; obtains consent; gains permission (esp. to speak)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okāsa">okāsa</a></p>

okāsaṃ kārāpeti|okaasa.m kaaraapeti|okasam karapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaasa.m kaaraapeti</span></p><p>causes to give an opportunity; obtains consent; gains permission (esp. to speak)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okāsa">okāsa</a></p>

okāseti|okaaseti|okaseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">okaaseti</span></p><p>puts down; scatters.</p>

olagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>fastened; attached.</p>

olagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>restrained, checked Thag.356</p><p>Sk. avalagna, pp. of avalagati</p>

olaggeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>fastens, ties on; attaches.</p>

olaggeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to make stick to, to put on, hold fast, restrain MN.ii.178; AN.iii.384 (vv.ll. oloketi olabheti, oketi); Thag.355.</p><p>Caus. of o + <em>lag</em></p>

olaggetvā|olaggetvaa|olaggetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">olaggetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olaggeti">olaggeti</a></p>

olaggi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>fastens, ties on; attaches.</p>

olamba
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective hanging down Vin.iii.49; Ja.iv.380 (˚vilamba).</p><p>fr. ava + <em>lamb</em></p>

olambaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>hanging down; what hangs down; something carried hanging from the arm or hand.</p>

olambaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) hanging down Vv-a.32 (˚dāma).</li><li>(n.)<ol><li>support, walking stick Ja.iv.40 (hatth˚)</li><li>plumb-line Ja.vi.392.</li></ol></li></ol><p>see <em><a href="bword://olambati">olambati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olambati">olambati</a></p>

olambanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>or neuter something hanging down to be clung to.</p>

olambanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an armchair, lit. a chair with supports Vin.ii.142.</p><p>fr. olambati</p>

olambati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>hangs down; hangs over; slips down; hangs from, hangs on to, clings to; catches hold of, leans on.</p>

olambati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Olambati &amp; avalambati</p><p>to hang down, hang on, to be supported by, rest on. The form in o is the older. Pres. avalambare Pv.ii.1#18 (= olambamānā tiṭṭhanti Pv-a.77); Pv.ii.10#2 (= olambanti Pv-a.142); olambati MN.iii.164 (+ ajjholambati); Ja.i.194; Pv-a.46<br />ger <em>avalamba</em> (for ˚bya) Pv.iii.3#5 (= olambitva Pv-a.189 &amp; <em>olambetvā</em> Ja.iii.218. See also <em><a href="bword://olubbha">olubbha</a></em>.</p><p>ava + <em>lamb</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olubbha">olubbha</a></p>

olambenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olambeti">olambeti</a></p>

olambeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes hang down, lets hang down; suspends</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olambati">olambati</a></p>

olaṅghanā|ola"nghanaa|olanghana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ola"nghanaa</span></p><p>bending down Vin.iii.121 (= heṭṭhā onamanā).</p><p>fr. <em><a href="bword://ola%E1%B9%85gheti">olaṅgheti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olaṅgheti">olaṅgheti</a></p>

olaṅgheti|ola"ngheti|olangheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ola"ngheti</span></p><p>makes jump down; lowers.</p>

olaṅgheti|ola"ngheti|olangheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ola"ngheti</span></p><p>to make jump down, in phrase <em>ullaṅgheti olaṅgheti</em> to make dance up down Ja.v.434 = Dhp-a.iv.197 (the latter has T. ullaggheti ol˚; but variant reading ullangheti ol˚).</p><p>Caus. of ava + <em>laṅgh</em></p>

olaṅghāna|ola"nghaana|olanghana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ola"nghaana</span></p><p>pulling down; lowering.</p>

oligalla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter a</i></p><p>cesspool; a sewer</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oḷigalla">oḷigalla</a></p>

oligalla
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>dirty pool near a village MN.iii.168; SN.v.361; AN.i.161; AN.iii.389; Mil.220; Vism.343.</p><p>of unknown etym.: prob. Non-Aryan, cp. BSk. oḍigalla Saddh. P. chap. vi.</p>

olikhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>scrapes; combs, brushes; (according to commentary) shaves.</p>

olikhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to scrape off, cut off, shave off (hair) AN.iii.295 (veṇiṃ olikhituṃ); Thag.169 (kese olikhissaṃ); Thag.2, Thag.88.</p><p>o + <em>likh</em>, cp. Sk. apalikhati</p>

olikhiṃ|olikhi.m|olikhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">olikhi.m</span></p><p>scraped off; combed.</p>

oliyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>hangs down; sinks down, cowers; hangs back; falls behind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oliyati">oliyati</a></p>

oliyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to stick, stick fast, adhere, cling to Iti.43; Ne.174<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ol%C4%ABna">olīna</a></em> (see avalīna).</p><p>o + līyate from <em>lī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olīna">olīna</a></p>

olokana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>looking, looking at, sight Sdhp.479 (mukhass’).</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

olokanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>window.</p>

olokanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun window Vin.ii.267 (olokanakena olokenti, adv.).</p><p>fr. oloketi</p>

olokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks at; watches; observes, examines; regards; looks to; looks down; looks to for permission or instruction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

olokayitvāna|olokayitvaana|olokayitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">olokayitvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olokayati">olokayati</a></p>

olokenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

olokesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

oloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

oloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks at; watches; observes, examines; regards; looks to; looks down; looks to for permission or instruction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olokayati">olokayati</a></p>

oloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to look at, to look down or over to, to examine, contemplate, inspect, consider Ja.i.85, Ja.i.108 (nakkhattaṃ); Pv.ii.9#64; Dhp-a.i.10, Dhp-a.i.12 Dhp-a.i.25, Dhp-a.i.26; Dhp-a.ii.96 (variant reading for T. voloketi); Dhp-a.iii.296; Pv-a.4, Pv-a.5 Pv-a.74, Pv-a.124.</p><p>BSk. avalokayati or apaloketi</p>

oloketvā|oloketvaa|oloketva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oloketvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oloketi">oloketi</a></p>

olubbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>leaned on to; held on to.</p>

olubbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>holding on to leaning on, supporting oneself by (with acc.); most frequently in phrase <em>daṇḍaṃ olubbha</em> leaning on a stick e.g. MN.i.108 (= daṇḍaṃ olambitvā C.; see MN.i.539); AN.iii.298; Thig.27 (= ālambitvā); Vv-a.105. In other connections: SN.i.118; SN.iii.129; Ja.i.265 (āvāṭa-mukha-vaṭṭiyaṃ); Ja.vi.40 (hatthe); Dhp-a.ii.57 (passaṃ; gloss olambi) Vv-a.217, Vv-a.219.</p><p>assimil. form of olumbha which in all likelihood for olambya, ger. of olambati. The form presents difficulties See also Morris, <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1887, 156</p>

olugga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>fallen down, collapsed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olujjati">olujjati</a></p>

olugga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>breaking off, falling to pieces, rotting away MN.i.80, MN.i.245 (olugga-vilugga), MN.i.450 (id.) Vism.107 (id.).</p><p>pp. of olujjati</p>

oluggavilugga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>collapsing and falling down, dilapidated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olugga">olugga</a></p>

olujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>breaks away; falls into pieces.</p>

olujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to break off, go to wreck, fall away SN.ii.218 (variant reading ull˚)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://olugga">olugga</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. avarujyate, Pass. of ava + <em>ruj</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olugga">olugga</a></p>

oluka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Spotted Owlet, Hindi <em>oolloo</em>, <em>Athene brama</em> Ja.ii.351. This small bird is grayish-brown with white spots and has large yellow eyes. It is India’s most common and familiar owl and usually lives around human habitation. The spotted owl is mainly nocturnal and eats insects, lizards and mice. See <em>Kosika</em>.</p><p>See also: Kosika</p>

olumpeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to strip off, seize, pick, pluck Vin.i.278 (bhesajjan olumpetvā, vv.ll. ulumpetvā, oḷump˚ odametvā).</p><p>o + Caus. of <em>lup</em></p>

olumpetvā|olumpetvaa|olumpetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">olumpetvaa</span></p><p>cuts off; removes.</p>

olīna|oliina|olina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliina</span></p><p>adhering, sticking or clinging to (worldliness), infatuated MN.i.200 (˚vuttika); Ja.vi.569 (anolīna-mānasa); Vb.350 (˚vuttikā); Mil.393 (an˚).</p><p>pp. of oliyate</p>

olīnavilīna|oliinaviliina|olinavilina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliinaviliina</span></p><p>hanging or dripping down and round about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olīyati">olīyati</a></p>

olīnavuttika|oliinavuttika|olinavuttika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliinavuttika</span></p><p>liable to hang back; habitually sluggish</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olīyati">olīyati</a></p>

olīnavuttitā|oliinavuttitaa|olinavuttita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliinavuttitaa</span></p><p>the habit of sluggishness; hanging back</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://olīyati">olīyati</a></p>

olīyanā|oliiyanaa|oliyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliiyanaa</span></p><p>adhering, infatuation Pts.i.157; Dhs.1156, Dhs.1236.</p><p>fr. oliyati</p>

olīyati|oliiyati|oliyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oliiyati</span></p><p>hangs down; sinks down, cowers; hangs back; falls behind</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oliyati">oliyati</a></p>

oma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>lower; inferior; deficient.</p>

oma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective lower (in position &amp; rank), inferior, low; pl. omā AN.iii.359 (in contrast with ussā superiors); Snp.860 (ussā samā omā superiors, equals, inferiors), Snp.954; Snp-a.347 (= paritta lāmaka)<br />■ More freq. in neg. form <em><a href="bword://anoma">anoma</a></em> not inferior i.e. excellent.</p><p>Vedic avama, superl. formation fr. ava</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anoma">anoma</a></p>

omadda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>crushing, oppressing.</p>

omaddakāraka|omaddakaaraka|omaddakaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omaddakaaraka</span></p><p>one who oppresses (others); a bully (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omadda">omadda</a></p>

omaddati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>presses, crushes; tramples on; presses together; rubs into (+instr.); rubs, strokes.</p>

omaddati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to rub Ja.vi.262 (sarīraṃ omaddanto); Mil.220.</li><li>to crush, oppress MN.i.87 = Cnd.199#6 (abhivaggena); Ja.ii.95.</li></ol><p>o + maddati from <em>mṛd</em>, BSk. avamardati Jtm.31#33</p>

omadditvā|omadditvaa|omadditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omadditvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omaddati">omaddati</a></p>

omaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>inferior, deficient; smaller; smallest.</p>

omaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective lower in rank, inferior; low, insignificant Mnd.306 (appaka +); Ja.ii.142; Dhp-a.i.203.</p><p>oma + ka</p>

omakacātumāsaṃ|omakacaatumaasa.m|omakacatumasam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omakacaatumaasa.m</span></p><p>for less than four months</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omaka">omaka</a></p>

omasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>piercing, striking, attacking (verbally).</p>

omasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omasati">omasati</a></p>

omasanā|omasanaa|omasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omasanaa</span></p><p>stroking in a downwards direction; lowering.</p>

omasanā|omasanaa|omasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omasanaa</span></p><p>touching, touch Vin.iii.121 (= heṭṭhā oropanā).</p><p>fr. omasati</p>

omasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>omasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. hurts; pierces; strikes; strikes in a downward direction; attacks verbally.</p><p>omasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. touches, strokes (in a downward direction).</p>

omasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(lit.) to touch Ja.v.446.</li><li>(fig.) to touch a person, to reproach, insult Vin.iv.4 sq</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://oma%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">omaṭṭha</a></em>.</p><p>o + mas = Sk. <em>mṛṣ</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omaṭṭha">omaṭṭha</a></p>

omasavāda|omasavaada|omasavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omasavaada</span></p><p>aggressive speech; hurtful speech</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omasa">omasa</a></p>

omasitvā|omasitvaa|omasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omasati">omasati</a></p>

omasiṃsu|omasi.msu|omasimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omasi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omasati">omasati</a></p>

omaṭṭha|oma.t.tha|omattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oma.t.tha</span></p><p>struck, pierced, struck downwards (from above); made with a downward stroke; (a hole or cut) made with a downward stroke or facing downwards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omasati">omasati</a></p>

omaṭṭha|oma.t.tha|omattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oma.t.tha</span></p><p>touched SN.i.13 = SN.i.53 = Thag.39.</p><p>pp. of omasati</p>

omissaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective mixed, miscellaneous, various Ja.v.37; Ja.vi.224 (˚parisā). Cp. vo˚.</p><p>o + missaka</p>

omuddhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with the head hanging down; upside down.</p>

omukka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>taken off, loosened; discarded, secondhand (?).</li><li>(one) who has loosened (his shoes).</li></ol>

omukka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective cast off, second hand Vin.i.187.</p><p>fr. + <em>muc</em></p>

omutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>released, freed, discharged, taken off Iti.56 (read omutt’assa Mārapāso for T. omukkassa m.).</p><p>pp. of omuñcati</p>

omuttayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>urinates upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omutteti">omutteti</a></p>

omutteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>urinates upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omuttayati">omuttayati</a></p>

omutteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to discharge urine, pass water MN.i.79, MN.i.127.</p><p>Sk. avamūtrayati, Denom. fr. mūtra, urine</p>

omuñcapetvā|omu~ncapetvaa|omuncapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omu~ncapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omuñcati">omuñcati</a></p>

omuñcati|omu~ncati|omuncati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omu~ncati</span></p><p>loosens; takes off.</p>

omuñcati|omu~ncati|omuncati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omu~ncati</span></p><p>to take off, loosen, release; unfasten, undo, doff DN.i.126 (veṭhanaṃ as form of salute); Ja.ii.326 Ja.vi.73 (sāṭakaṃ); Vism.338; Pv-a.63 (tacaṃ); Vv-a.75 (ābhāraṇāni)<br />caus <em>omuñcāpeti</em> to cause to take off Vin.i.273<br />pp <em><a href="bword://omutta">omutta</a></em>.</p><p>o + <em>muc</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omutta">omutta</a></p>

omuñcitvā|omu~ncitvaa|omuncitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omu~ncitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://omuñcati">omuñcati</a></p>

omāna FIXME double|omaana FIXME double|omana FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omaana FIXME double</span></p><p>Omāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>disregard, disrespect, contempt Dhp-a.ii.52 (+ atimāna) Cp. foll. &amp; see also; <em><a href="bword://avam%C4%81na">avamāna</a></em>.</p><p>fr. o + <em>man</em>, think. The Sk. avamāna is later</p><p>Omāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>at Ja.ii.443 we read <em>ucce sakuṇa omāna</em> meaning ʻOh bird, flying high’. With the present material we see no satisfactory solution of this puzzle. There is a Burmese correction which is at variance with the commentary “flying”, the variant reading BB is <em>ḍemāna</em> (fr. <em>ḍī</em>). C. explains by caramāna gacchamāna. Müller, <em>P. Gr.</em> 99 proposes to read ḍemāna for omāna.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avamāna">avamāna</a></p>

omāna|omaana|omana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omaana</span></p><p>contempt; self disgust, self despising.</p>

omāna|omaana|omana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omaana</span></p><p>Avamāna &amp; omāna</p><p>disregard, disrespect, contempt Ja.ii.386; Ja.iii.423; Ja.v.384. Cp. next.</p><p>fr. ava + <em>man</em>, think</p>

omāti|omaati|omati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">omaati</span></p><p>measures up to; is able, is capable (of).</p>

onaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>covered; having something stretched over</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onandhati">onandhati</a></p>

onaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bound, tied; put over, covered Vin.ii.150, Vin.ii.270 sq. (˚mañca, ˚pīṭha); MN.ii.64; Dhp.146 (andhakārena); Sdhp.182. See also <em><a href="bword://onayhati">onayhati</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of onandhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onayhati">onayhati</a></p>

onahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>covers; stretches over (trans.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onandhati">onandhati</a>, <a href="bword://onayhati">onayhati</a></p>

onahinayāna|onahinayaana|onahinayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onahinayaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onahati">onahati</a></p>

onahiyāna|onahiyaana|onahiyana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onahiyaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onahati">onahati</a></p>

onamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective bending down, stooping Dhp-a.ii.136 (an˚).</p><p>fr. onamati</p>

onamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; \~ā feminine</i></p><p>bending own, bowing; sinking</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇamana">oṇamana</a></p>

onamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>-unnamana</em> lowering &amp; raising, bending down &amp; up Dhp-a.i.17.</p><p>abstr. fr. onamati</p>

onamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bend down (instr.), stoop DN.ii.17 (anonamanto ppr. not bending); DN.iii.143 (id.); Vv.39#3 (onamitvā ger.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://o%E1%B9%87ata">oṇata</a></em>.</p><p>o + namati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇata">oṇata</a></p>

onameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes bow; bends down (trans.); lowers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onāmeti">onāmeti</a></p>

onandhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>covers; stretches over (trans.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onayhati">onayhati</a>, <a href="bword://onahati">onahati</a></p>

onandhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bind, fasten; to cover up Vin.ii.150 (inf onandhituṃ); Mil.261.</p><p>o + nandhati, a secondary pres. form constructed from naddha after bandhati → baddha; see also apiḷandhati</p>

onandhitvā|onandhitvaa|onandhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onandhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onandhati">onandhati</a></p>

onata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>who has bent down, bowed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇata">oṇata</a></p>

onatonata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>low (in birth) and low (in moral behavior), who begins low and stays low</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇatoṇata">oṇatoṇata</a></p>

onayhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>covers; stretches over (trans.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onandhati">onandhati</a>, <a href="bword://onahati">onahati</a></p>

onayhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tie down, to cover over, envelop, shroud Dhs-a.378 (megho ākāsaṃ o.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://onaddha">onaddha</a></em>.</p><p>ava + nayhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onaddha">onaddha</a></p>

oneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>prob. for <em><a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></em>, see <em><a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></em> &amp; pp.; <em><a href="bword://on%C4%ABta">onīta</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a>, <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a>, <a href="bword://onīta">onīta</a></p>

onojana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>giving formally</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇojana">oṇojana</a></p>

onojeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>ours water ritually (over the hands) in giving a gift; gives formally</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇojeti">oṇojeti</a></p>

onojeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://o%E1%B9%87ojeti">oṇojeti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇojeti">oṇojeti</a></p>

onāha|onaaha|onaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onaaha</span></p><p>covering; shrouding.</p>

onāha|onaaha|onaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onaaha</span></p><p>drawing over, covering, shrouding DN.i.246 (spelt onaha); Mil.300; Dhs.1157 (= megho viya ākāsaṃ kāyaṃ onayhati).</p><p>fr. ava + <em>nah</em>, cp. onaddha &amp; onayhati</p>

onāmesi|onaamesi|onamesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onaamesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onāmeti">onāmeti</a></p>

onāmeti|onaameti|onameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">onaameti</span></p><p>makes bow; bends down (trans.); lowers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onameti">onameti</a></p>

onīta|oniita|onita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oniita</span></p><p>put into water; washed; (according to commentaries) removed; put away</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oṇīta">oṇīta</a>, <a href="bword://onīyati">onīyati</a></p>

onīta|oniita|onita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oniita</span></p><p>only found in one ster. phrase, viz. <em>onīta-patta-pāṇi</em> “having removed (or removing) his hand from the bowl” a phrase causing constructional difficulties &amp; sometimes taken in glosses as “onitta˚” (fr. <em>nij</em>), i.e. having washed (bowl and hands after the meal). The C<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as onīto pattato pāṇi yeva, i.e. “the hand is taken away from the bowl”. The spelling is frequently oṇīta, probably through BB sources. See on term also Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 66<!-- raw HTML omitted -->24<!-- raw HTML omitted --> cp. apa-nīta-pātra at Mvu.iii.142. The expression is always comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with bhuttāvin “having eaten” and occurs very frequently, e.g. at Vin.ii.147: DN.i.109 (= DN-a.i.277, q.v. for the 2 explains mentioned above MN.ii.50, MN.ii.93; SN.v.384; AN.ii.63; Snp.p.111 (= pattato onītapāṇi, apanītahattha Snp-a.456); Vv-a.118; Pv-a.278.</p><p>in form = Sk. avanīta, but semantically = apanīta. Thus also BSk. apanīta, pp. of apa + <em>nī</em>, see <em><a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apaneti">apaneti</a></p>

onītapattapāṇi|oniitapattapaa.ni|onitapattapani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oniitapattapaa.ni</span></p><p>with hands and bowl rinsed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://onīta">onīta</a></p>

onīyati|oniiyati|oniyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oniiyati</span></p><p>is led down or away; is put into water, has water poured over it.</p>

opabhogga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>to be used or enjoyed.</p>

opadhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>connected with material acquisitions and attachment; (according to commentaries) forming a basis for rebirth.</p>

opadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective forming a substratum for rebirth (always with ref. to puñña, merit). Not with Morris, J.P.T.S. 1885 38 as “exceedingly great”; the correct interpretation is given by Dhpāla at Vv-a.154 as “atta-bhāva-janaka paṭisandhi-pavatti-vipāka-dāyaka”<br />■ SN.i.233 = AN.iv.292; Vv.34#21; Iti.20 (variant reading osadhika), Iti.78.</p><p>fr. upadhi. BSk. after the P., aupadhika Divy.542</p>

opaguyha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://opavayha">opavayha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opavayha">opavayha</a></p>

opakkamika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>brought about by (someone’s) action; caused by an act of violence.</p>

opakkamika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective characterising a sensation of pain: attacking suddenly, spasmodic, acute; always in connection with <em><a href="bword://%C4%81b%C4%81dha">ābādha</a></em> or <em><a href="bword://vedan%C4%81">vedanā</a></em> MN.i.92, MN.i.241; SN.iv.230 = AN.ii.87 = AN.iii.131 = AN.v.110 = Cnd.304#ic = Mil.112.</p><p>fr. upakkama</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ābādha">ābādha</a>, <a href="bword://vedanā">vedanā</a></p>

opakkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “with wings off” i.e. having one’s wings clipped, powerless AN.i.188 (˚ṃ karoti to deprive of one’s wings or strength; so read for T. opapakkhiṃ karoti).</p><p>o + pakkhin, adj. fr. pakkha wing, cp. similarly avapatta</p>

opama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.i.89 &amp; Sdhp.93 (anopama) stands for ūpama, which metri causā for; <em><a href="bword://upama">upama</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upama">upama</a></p>

opamañña|opama~n~na|opamanna
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opama~n~na</span></p><p>Opamañña<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>One of the names of Pokkharasāti. MN.ii.200</p><p>Opamañña<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Gandhabba chieftain, who was among those present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.258 He is mentioned in the Āṭānāṭiya Sutta, DN.iii.204 in a list of eminent <em>yakkha</em> generals.</p>

opamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>state of resemblance; likeness; comparison; analogy.</p>

opamma
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>likeness, simile, comparison, metaphor MN.i.378; Vin.v.164; Mil.1, Mil.70, Mil.330; Vism.117, Vism.622; Thag-a.290.</p><p>fr. upama; cp. Sk. aupamya</p>

opanayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>fit for bringing near, for taking to oneself; fit for making use of; deserving to be used.</p>

opanayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective leading to (Nibbāna) SN.iv.41 sq., SN.iv.272, SN.iv.339; SN.v.343; AN.i.158; AN.ii.198; DN.iii.5; Vism.217.</p><p>fr. upaneti, upa + <em>nī</em></p>

opapaccayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having the characteristic of being born without parents, as deva Ne.28 (upādāna).</p><p>= opapātika</p>

opapakkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>rejected, despised; or rejects, insults.</p>

opapakkhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in phrase <em>-ṃ karoti</em> at AN.i.188 read <em>opakkhiṃ</em> karoti to deprive of one’s wings, to render powerless.</p>

opapakkhiṅkaroti|opapakkhi"nkaroti|opapakkhinkaroti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opapakkhi"nkaroti</span></p><p>rejected, despised; or rejects, insults.</p>

opapātika|opapaatika|opapatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opapaatika</span></p><p>arising spontaneously; (one) being reborn without parents.</p>

opapātika|opapaatika|opapatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opapaatika</span></p><p>adjective arisen or reborn without visible cause (i.e. without parents), spontaneous rebirth (<em>Kvu trsl.</em> 283<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), apparitional rebirth (<em>Cpd.</em> 165<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, q.v.) DN.i.27 DN.i.55, DN.i.156; DN.iii.132, DN.iii.230 (˚yoni), DN.iii.265; MN.i.34, MN.i.73, MN.i.287, MN.i.401 sq., MN.i.436 sq, MN.i.465 sq.; MN.ii.52; MN.iii.22, MN.iii.80, MN.iii.247; SN.iii.206 SN.iii.240 sq., SN.iii.246 sq.; SN.iv.348; SN.v.346, SN.v.357 sq., SN.v.406; AN.i.232 AN.i.245, AN.i.269; AN.ii.5, AN.ii.89, AN.ii.186; AN.iv.12, AN.iv.226, AN.iv.399, AN.iv.423 sq.; AN.v.265 sq., AN.v.286 sq., AN.v.343 sq.; Pp.16, Pp.62, Pp.63; Vb.412 sq. Mil.267; Vism.552 sq., Vism.559; DN-a.i.165, DN-a.i.313. The C on MN.i.34 explains by “sesa-yoni-paṭikkhepa-vacanaṃ etaṃ” See also Pp-a 1, § 40.</p><p>fr. upapatti; the BSk. form is a curious distortion of the P. form, viz. aupapāduka Avs.ii.89 Divy.300, Divy.627, Divy.649</p>

opapātin|opapaatin|opapatin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opapaatin</span></p><p>adjective = opapātika, in phrase opapātiyā (for opapātiniyā?) iddhiyā at SN.v.282 (so read for T. opapāti ha?) is doubtful reading &amp; perhaps best to be omitted altogether.</p>

oparajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the state of being an uparāja, vice royalty)</p>

oparajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>viceroyalty is variant reading for <em><a href="bword://uparajja">uparajja</a></em>. Thus at MN.ii.76; AN.iii.154.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparajja">uparajja</a></p>

opasamika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn</i></p><p>bringing clam, leading to tranquility.</p>

opasamika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective leading to quiet, allaying quieting; epithet of Dhamma DN.iii.264 sq.; AN.ii.132.</p><p>fr. upa + sama + ika; cp. BSk. aupaśamika Avs.ii.107; Mvu.ii.41</p>

opasāyika|opasaayika|opasayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opasaayika</span></p><p>(one) who lies by the side of, who keeps near to.</p>

opasāyika|opasaayika|opasayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opasaayika</span></p><p>adjective being near at hand or at one’s bidding (?) MN.i.328.</p><p>fr. upasaya, upa + <em>śī</em></p>

opatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to fall or fly down (on), to fall over (w. acc.) Ja.ii.228 (lokāmisaṃ ˚anto); Ja.vi.561 (˚itvā ger.) Mil.368, Mil.396<br />pp <em><a href="bword://opatita">opatita</a></em>.</p><p>o + <em>pat</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opatita">opatita</a></p>

opatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>falling (down) Pv-a.29 (udaka; variant reading ovuḷhita, opalahita; context rcads at Pv-a.29 mahāsobbhehi opatitena udakena, but id. p. at Kp-a.213 reads mahāsobbha-sannipātehi).</p><p>pp. of opatati</p>

opatitvā|opatitvaa|opatitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opatitvaa</span></p><p>having flow down; fell down; fell on (to).</p>

opatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective with leaves fallen off, leafless (of trees) Ja.iii.495 (opatta = avapatta nippatta patita-patta C.).</p><p>o + patta, Sk. avapattra</p>

opavayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>serving for riding; an animal for riding; a king’s elephant</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opavuyha">opavuyha</a></p>

opavayha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun fit for riding, suitable as conveyance, state-elephant (of the elephant of the king) SN.v.351 = Ne.136 (variant reading opaguyha C. explains by ārohana-yogga); Ja.ii.20 (SS opavuyha); Ja.iv.91 (variant reading ˚guyha); Ja.vi.488 (T. opavuyha, variant reading opaguyha gajuttama opavayha = rāja-vāhana C.); DN-a.i.147 (ārohanayogga opavuyha, variant reading ˚guyha); Vv-a.316 (T. opaguyha to be corrected to ˚vayha).</p><p>fr. upavayha, grd. of upavahati</p>

opavuyha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>serving for riding; an animal for riding; a king’s elephant</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opavayha">opavayha</a></p>

opayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws into, heaps up (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opeti">opeti</a></p>

opeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws into, heaps up (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opayati">opayati</a></p>

opeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to deposit, receive (syn. with osāpeti) SN.i.236 (SA na… pakkhipanti) = Thig.283; Ja.v.252 (T. upeti); in which Thig.283 has <em>oseti</em> (Thag-a.216, with expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of oseti = ṭhapeti on p. 219)<br />aor opi Ja.iv.457 (ukkhipi gloss); Ja.vi.185 (= pakkhipi gloss). ger. <em>opitvā</em> (opetvā?) Ja.iv.457 (gloss khipetvā).</p><p>unless we here deal with a very old misspelling for oseti we have to consider it a secondary derivation from opiya in Caus. sense, i e. Caus. fr. upa + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>. Trenckner <em>Notes</em> 77, 78 offers an etym. of ā + vapati, thus opiya would be *āvupiya, a risky conclusion, which besides being discrepant in meaning (āvapati = to distribute) necessitates der. of opiya fr. opati (*āvapati) instead of vice versā. There is no other instance of *āva being contracted to o Trenckner then puts opiya = ūpiya in tadūpiya (“conform with this”, see *<a href="bword://ta">ta</a>*˚ I<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), which is however a direct derivation from upa = upaka, upiya, of which a superl formation is upamā (“likeness”). Trenckner’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of ūpiya as der. fr. ā + <em>vap</em> does not fit in with its meaning to make go into (c. loc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a>, <a href="bword://ta">ta</a></p>

ophunāti|ophunaati|ophunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ophunaati</span></p><p>exposes to the wind, winnows; makes clean or clear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāti">opunāti</a>, <a href="bword://opuṇāti">opuṇāti</a></p>

ophuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>filled, pervaded by; encompassed by; possessed by</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ophuṭa">ophuṭa</a></p>

ophuṭa|ophu.ta|ophuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ophu.ta</span></p><p>filled, pervaded by; encompassed by; possessed by</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ophuta">ophuta</a></p>

ophuṭa|ophu.ta|ophuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ophu.ta</span></p><p>covered, obstructed; always in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>āvuta nivuta ophuṭa</em> (oputa ovuta) DN.i.246 (T. ophuta, vv.ll. ophuṭa &amp; opuṭa); MN.iii.131 (T. ovuṭa); Mnd.24 ovuṭa, variant reading SS ophuṭa); Cnd.365 (ophuṭa, variant reading BB oputa; SS ovuta); DN-a.i.59 (oputa) Snp-a.596 (oputa = pariyo-naddha); Mil.161 (ovuta).</p><p>a difficult, but legitimate form arisen out of analogy, fusing ava-vuta (= Sk. vṛta from <em>vṛ</em>; opp. *apāvuta P. apāruta) and ava-phuṭa (Sk. sphuṭa from <em>sphuṭ</em>;). We should probably read ovuta in all instances</p>

opilavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>plunges into (intrans.), sinks.</p>

opilavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be immersed, to sink down SN.ii.224<br />caus <em><a href="bword://opil%C4%81peti">opilāpeti</a></em> (see sep.).</p><p>Sk. avaplavati, ava + <em>plu</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opilāpeti">opilāpeti</a></p>

opilāpesi|opilaapesi|opilapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opilaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opilavati">opilavati</a></p>

opilāpetabba|opilaapetabba|opilapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opilaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opilavati">opilavati</a></p>

opilāpeti|opilaapeti|opilapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opilaapeti</span></p><p>plunges into (intrans.); immerses</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opilavati">opilavati</a></p>

opilāpeti|opilaapeti|opilapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opilaapeti</span></p><p>to immerse, to dip in or down, to drop (into = loc.) Vin.i.157 = Vin.i.225 = SN.i.169 (C.: nimujjapeti, see <em>K. S.</em> 318) MN.i.207 = MN.iii.157; Dhp-a.iii.3 (˚āpetvā; so read with vv.ll. for opīḷetvā); Ja.iii.282<br />pp <em><a href="bword://opil%C4%81pita">opilāpita</a></em>.</p><p>Caus. of opilavati, cp. Sk. avaplāvayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opilāpita">opilāpita</a></p>

opilāpita|opilaapita|opilapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opilaapita</span></p><p>immersed into (loc.), gutted with water, drenched Ja.i.212, Ja.i.214.</p><p>pp. of opilāpeti</p>

opiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>having thrown or scattered into or onto.</p>

opiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>is metric for <em><a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a></em> undergoing, going into SN.i.199 = Thag.119 (nibbānaṃ hadayasmiṃ opiya; Mrs. Rh. D. trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “suffering N. in thy heart to sink”, S A. hadayasmiṃ pakkhipitvā.</p><p>upa + ger. of <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

opuni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāti">opunāti</a></p>

opunāpetabba|opunaapetabba|opunapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opunaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāpeti">opunāpeti</a></p>

opunāpeti|opunaapeti|opunapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opunaapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāti">opunāti</a></p>

opunāpetvā|opunaapetvaa|opunapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opunaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāpeti">opunāpeti</a></p>

opunāti|opunaati|opunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opunaati</span></p><p>exposes to the wind, winnows; makes clean or clear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opuṇāti">opuṇāti</a>, <a href="bword://ophunāti">ophunāti</a></p>

opunāti|opunaati|opunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opunaati</span></p><p>also as <em><a href="bword://opu%E1%B9%87%C4%81ti">opuṇāti</a></em> (Dh) to winnow, sift; fig. lay bare, expose Dhp.252 (= bhusaṃ opuṇanto viya Dhp-a.iii.375); Snp-a.312<br />caus <em>opunāpeti</em> [cp. BSk. opunāpeti Mvu.iii.178] to cause to sift AN.i.242; Ja.i.447.</p><p>o + punāti fr. <em>pū</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opuṇāti">opuṇāti</a></p>

opuppha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bud, young flower Ja.vi.497 (vv.ll. Ja.vi.498 opaṇṇa &amp; opatta).</p><p>o + puppha</p>

opuñchana|opu~nchana|opunchana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~nchana</span></p><p>or <em>Opuñjana</em> (nt.) heaping up, covering over; a heap, layer Dhp-a.iii.296.</p><p>fr. opuñjeti</p>

opuñchati|opu~nchati|opunchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~nchati</span></p><p>is uncertain reading for <em><a href="bword://opu%C3%B1jeti">opuñjeti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opuñjeti">opuñjeti</a></p>

opuñchitvā|opu~nchitvaa|opunchitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~nchitvaa</span></p><p>having wiped; wiped over (with); smeared</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opuñjitvā">opuñjitvā</a></p>

opuñchāpetvā|opu~nchaapetvaa|opunchapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~nchaapetvaa</span></p><p>having made caused someone to wipe</p><p>See also: opuñjapetvā</p>

opuñjati|opu~njati|opunjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~njati</span></p><p>heaps up, collects.</p>

opuñjeti|opu~njeti|opunjeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~njeti</span></p><p>or <em>-ati</em> to heap up, make a heap, cover over with (Morris, J.P.T.S. 1887, 153 trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “cleanse”) Vin.ii.176 (opuñjati bhattaṃ) Ja.iv.377 (opuñchetvā T., but variant reading opuñjetvā; gloss upalimpitvā); Dhp-a.iii.296 (opuñchitvā, gloss sammajjitvā). Caus. <em>opuñjāpeti</em> in same meaning “to smear” Vin.iii.16 (opuñjāpetvā; variant reading opuñchāpetvā).</p><p>o + puñjeti Denom. of puñja, heap</p>

opuñjitvā|opu~njitvaa|opunjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~njitvaa</span></p><p>opuñjitvā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>absol. having wiped; wiped over (with); smeared</p><p>opuñjitvā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>absol.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opuñchitvā">opuñchitvā</a>, <a href="bword://opuñjati">opuñjati</a></p>

opuñjāpetvā|opu~njaapetvaa|opunjapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu~njaapetvaa</span></p><p>having made caused someone to wipe</p><p>See also: opuñjapetvā</p>

opuṇāti|opu.naati|opunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opu.naati</span></p><p>exposes to the wind, winnows; makes clean or clear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opunāti">opunāti</a>, <a href="bword://ophunāti">ophunāti</a></p>

opāna|opaana|opana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaana</span></p><p>Only in phrase <em>opāna-bhūta</em> (adj.) a man who has become a welling spring as it were, for the satisfaction of all men’s wants; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “khata-pokkharaṇī viya hutvā” DN-a.i.298 = Ja.v.174<br />■ Vin.i.236; DN.i.137; MN.i.379; AN.iv.185; Vv.65#4; Pv.iv.1#60; Ja.iii.142; Ja.iv.34; Ja.v.172; Vb.247; Mil.411; Vism.18; Vv-a.286; DN-a.1177, DN-a.298.</p><p>o + pāna fr. pivati. Vedic avapāna. The P. Commentators however take o as a contracted form of udaka, e.g. Bdhgh. at DN-a.i.298 = udapāna</p>

opānabhūta|opaanabhuuta|opanabhuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaanabhuuta</span></p><p>being like a pool or well; ever generous, satisfying.</p>

opārambha|opaarambha|oparambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaarambha</span></p><p>open to criticism, censurable.</p>

opārambha|opaarambha|oparambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaarambha</span></p><p>adjective acting as a support, supporting, helpful MN.ii.113.</p><p>fr. upārambha</p>

opāta|opaata|opata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaata</span></p><p>hole or pit, a trap.</p>

opāta|opaata|opata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaata</span></p><ol><li>falling or flying down, downfall, descent Ja.vi.561.</li><li>a pitfall Ja.i.143; Dhp-a.iv.211.</li></ol><p>o + pāta fr. patati to fall, Vedic avapāta</p>

opātetabba|opaatetabba|opatetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaatetabba</span></p><p>will prompt; will interrupt.</p>

opāteti|opaateti|opateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaateti</span></p><p>makes or lets fall (speech), speaks; speaks at the same time; prompts; interrupts.</p>

opāteti|opaateti|opateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaateti</span></p><p>to make fall, to destroy (cp. atipāteti), i.e.</p><ol><li>to break, to interrupt, in kathaṃ opāteti to interrupt a conversation MN.ii.10, MN.ii.122, MN.ii.168; AN.iii.137 AN.iii.392 sq.; Snp.p.107.</li><li>to drop, to omit (a syllable Vin.iv.15.</li></ol><p>o + Caus. of <em>pat</em></p>

opāyika|opaayika|opayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaayika</span></p><p>answering a purpose; fit, proper, right; <em>~am</em>, (expressing assent:) that is fit, that’s right.</p>

opāṭesi|opaa.tesi|opatesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaa.tesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opāṭeti">opāṭeti</a></p>

opāṭeti|opaa.teti|opateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaa.teti</span></p><p>splits; tears apart.</p>

opāṭeti|opaa.teti|opateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaa.teti</span></p><p>to tear asunder, unravel, open Vin.ii.150 (chaviṃ opāṭetvā).</p><p>ava + Caus. of <em>paṭ</em>; Sk. avapāṭayati</p>

opāṭetvā|opaa.tetvaa|opatetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opaa.tetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opāṭeti">opāṭeti</a></p>

opīḷeti|opii.leti|opileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">opii.leti</span></p><p>in “bhattaṃ pacchiyaṃ opīḷetvā” at Dhp-a.ii.3 is with variant reading to be read opilāpetvā (gloss odahitvā), i.e. dropping the food into the basket.</p>

ora
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>this side; this shore, the nearer bank; this side of existence.</p>

ora
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective below, inferior, posterior. Usually as nt. <em><a href="bword://ora%E1%B9%83">oraṃ</a></em> the below, the near side, this world Snp.15; Vv-a.42 (orato abl. from this side)<br />■ Cases adverbially: acc. <em><a href="bword://ora%E1%B9%83">oraṃ</a></em> (with abl. on this side of, below, under, within MN.ii.142; Snp.804 (oraṃ vassasatā); Pv.iv.3#35 (oraṃ chahi māsehi in less th an.6 months or after 6 months; id. p. at Pv.i.10#12 has uddhaṃ); Pv-a.154 (dahato); instr. <em><a href="bword://orena">orena</a></em> Ja.v.72 abl. <em>orato</em> on this side Mil.210.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81ra">pāra</a></em> the below and the above, the lower &amp; higher worlds Snp.1 (see Snp-a.13 = Cnd.422<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b<!-- raw HTML omitted --> and cp. paroparaṃ); Mil.319 (samuddo anorapāro, boundless ocean)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pure">pure</a></em> (avarapure) below the fortress MN.i.68 (bahinagare +)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mattaka">mattaka</a></em> belonging only to this world, mundane; hence trifling, insignificant, little, evanescent Vin.ii.85, Vin.ii.203; Iti.85; DN.i.3; MN.i.449; AN.iv.22; AN.v.157, AN.v.164; Vb.247 Ne.62; Dhp-a.i.203; DN-a.i.55.</li></ul><p>compar. formation fr. ava; Vedic avara</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oraṃ">oraṃ</a>, <a href="bword://oraṃ">oraṃ</a>, <a href="bword://orena">orena</a>, <a href="bword://pāra">pāra</a>, <a href="bword://pure">pure</a>, <a href="bword://mattaka">mattaka</a></p>

orabbhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>sheep butcher.</p>

orabbhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who kills sheep, a butcher (of sheep) MN.i.343, MN.i.412; SN.ii.256; AN.i.251; AN.ii.207 = Pp.56; Pp.iii.303; Thig.242 (= urabbhaghātaka Thag-a.204) Ja.v.270; Ja.vi.111 (and their punishment in Niraya); Pp-a.244 (urabbhā vuccanti eḷakā; urabbhe hanatī ti orabbhiko).</p><p>fr. urabbha. The Sk. aurabhrika is later &amp; differs in meaning</p>

oraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā &amp; -ikā)n. inferior; unimportant, petty.</p>

oraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective inferior, posterior Vin.i.19; Vin.ii.159; MN.ii.47; Snp.692 (= paritta Snp-a.489; cp. omaka) Ja.i.381.</p><p>ora + ka</p>

oramatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oramati">oramati</a></p>

oramati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>stops, is quiet; gives up, desists; abstains (from).</p>

oramati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to stay or be on this side, i.e. to stand still, to get no further Ja.i.492 (oramituṃ na icchi), Ja.i.498 (oramāma na pārema) <em>Note.</em> This form may also be expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; taken as imper of ava + ramati (cp. avarata 2), i.e. let us desist, let us give up, (i.e. we shall not get through to the other side); <em>-<a href="bword://anoramati">anoramati</a></em> (neg.) see sep<br />■ On the whole question see also Morris, J.P.T.S. 1887, 154 sq.</p><p>Denom. fr. ora instead of orameti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anoramati">anoramati</a></p>

oramattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. lesser; insignificant; of small value or importance</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

orambhāgamanīya|orambhaagamaniiya|orambhagamaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orambhaagamaniiya</span></p><p>connected with this side of existence, with the lower spheres of existence.</p>

orambhāgiya|orambhaagiya|orambhagiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orambhaagiya</span></p><p>connected with this side of existence, with the lower spheres of existence; binding to lower states</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

orambhāgiya|orambhaagiya|orambhagiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orambhaagiya</span></p><p>adjective being a share of the lower, i e this world, belonging to the kāma world, epithet of the 5 saṃyojanāni (see also <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83yojana">saṃyojana</a></em>) DN.i.156; DN.iii.107, DN.iii.108 DN.iii.132; MN.i.432; Iti.114; Pp.22; Ne.14; Snp-a.13; DN-a.i.313<br />■ <em>Note.</em> A curious form of this word is found at Thig.166 orambhāga-manīya, with gloss (Thag-a.158) oraṃ āgamanīya. Probably the bh should be deleted.</p><p>ora + bhāga + iya; BSk. avarabhāgīya, e.g. Divy.533</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://saṃyojana">saṃyojana</a></p>

oramgāmanīya|oramgaamaniiya|oramgamaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oramgaamaniiya</span></p><p>connected with coming to this world, with returning to the lower spheres of existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

oramāpeti|oramaapeti|oramapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oramaapeti</span></p><p>to make someone desist from Ja.v.474 (manussa-maṃsā).</p><p>Caus. ii. of oramati</p>

orapāra|orapaara|orapara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orapaara</span></p><p>the nearer and farther shore.</p>

orasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n). &amp; neuter</i></p><p>belonging to one’s breast; one’s own; one’s own son, a true or legitimate son.</p>

orasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective belonging to one’s own breast, self-begotten, legitimate; innate, natural, own MN.ii.84; MN.iii.29; SN.ii.221 (bhagavato putto o. mukhato jāto); SN.iii.83; Ja.iii.272; Vv.50#22; Thag-a.236 Kp-a.248; Pv-a.62 (urejāta +).</p><p>Fr. ura, uras breast Vedic aurasa</p>

orasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>belonging to or in one’s breast.</p>

orata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>delighted, satisfied, pleased Mil.210 (cp. abhirata).</li><li>desisting, abstaining from, restraining oneself Vv-a.72 (= virato; cp. uparata).</li></ol><p>o + rata, pp. of ramati</p>

orato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>on this side (of), on the nearer side; before</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

oravita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://oravitar">oravitar</a></p><p>(one) who cries out, who caws.</p>

oravitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://oravitar">oravitar</a></p><p>(one) who cries out, who caws.</p>

oravitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>doubtful reading at AN.v.149, meaning concerned with worldly things (?). The vv.ll. are oramitā, oravikā, oramato, oravi.</p><p>ora + n. ag. of vitarati?</p>

oraṃ|ora.m|oram
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ora.m</span></p><p>on this side (of); before, within, in less than</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

orena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>on this side (of); within, in less than; to this side, over here</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ora">ora</a></p>

orima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>nearest, nearer; on this side.</p>

orima
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the lower or lowest, the one on this side, this (opp. yonder) only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>orima-tīra</em> the shore on this side, the near shore (opp. pāra˚ and pārima˚ the far side) DN.i.244; SN.iv.175 (sakkāyass’ adhivacanaṃ) = Snp-a.24; Dhs.597 Vism.512 (˚tīra-mah’ogha); Dhp-a.ii.99.</p><p>superl. formation fr. ora, equivalent to avama</p>

orodha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>(&amp; ~ā f. (?)) the royal women’s apartments; the harem; the queen; (pi.) the women of the harem.</p>

orodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>obstruction; confinement, harem, seraglio Vin.ii.290; Vin.iv.261 (rāj’ orodhā harem-lady, concubine); Ja.iv.393, Ja.iv.404.</p><p>fr. orundhati; Sk. avarodha</p>

orodheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>causes to be confined or checked; checks</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruddhati">oruddhati</a></p>

orohanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

orohati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes down, descends; goes down, descends (on to or into); alights (at)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

orohati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to descend, climb down DN.ii.21; MN.iii.131; Ja.i.50; Mil.395; Pv-a.14<br />caus <em><a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>o + rohati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></p>

orohaṇa|oroha.na|orohana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oroha.na</span></p><p>descent, in udak’orohaṇânuyoga practice of descending in to the water (i.e. bathing Pp.55; Ja.i.193; Mil.350.</p><p>abstr. fr. orahati</p>

orohitvā|orohitvaa|orohitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orohitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

orohitvāna|orohitvaana|orohitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">orohitvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

oropana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>taking down, lowering; removal; putting (the hands) downwards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropaṇa">oropaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://oropanā">oropanā</a></p>

oropanā|oropanaa|oropana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropanaa</span></p><p>taking down, lowering; removal; putting (the hands) downwards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropaṇa">oropaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://oropana">oropana</a></p>

oropayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ca.pr causes to descend; puts down; takes down, lowers (on to); removes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a>, <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

oropayitvā|oropayitvaa|oropayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropayitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropayati">oropayati</a></p>

oropaṇa|oropa.na|oropana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropa.na</span></p><p>taking down, lowering; removal; putting (the hands) downwards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropana">oropana</a>, <a href="bword://oropanā">oropanā</a></p>

oropaṇa|oropa.na|oropana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropa.na</span></p><p>taking down, removal, cutting off (hair), in <em>kes’ oropaṇa</em> hair-cutting Dhp-a.ii.53 (T. has at one place orohaṇa, variant reading oropaṇa).</p><p>abstr. fr. oropeti</p>

oropenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></p>

oropesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></p>

oropesuṃ|oropesu.m|oropesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></p>

oropeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ca.pr causes to descend; puts down; takes down, lowers (on to); removes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropayati">oropayati</a>, <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

oropeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take down, bring down, deprive of, lay aside, take away, cut off (hair) Vv-a.64 (bhattabhājanaṃ oropeti)-ger. <em>oropayitvā</em> Snp.44 (= nikkhipitvā paṭippassambhayitvā Cnd.181 apanetvā Snp-a.91); Ja.vi.211 (kesamassuṃ).</p><p>Caus. fr. orohati; BSk. avaropayati</p>

oropetvā|oropetvaa|oropetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oropetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oropeti">oropeti</a></p>

oruddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>shut in, enclosed; checked, stopped</p><p>See also: orunddhati</p>

oruddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>kept back, restrained, subdued AN.iii.393.</li><li>imprisoned Ja.iv.4. See also ava˚.</li></ol><p>fr. orundhati. In meaning equalling Sk. aparuddha as well as ava˚</p>

oruddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>encloses, shuts in; confines; restrains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orundhati">orundhati</a></p>

oruhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes down, descends; goes down, descends (on to or into); alights (at)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orohati">orohati</a></p>

oruhitvā|oruhitvaa|oruhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oruhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

oruhitvāna|oruhitvaana|oruhitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oruhitvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

orundhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>encloses, shuts in; confines; restrains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruddhati">oruddhati</a></p>

orundhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to get, attain, take for a wife<br />ger <em>orundhiya</em> Ja.iv.480<br />aor <em><a href="bword://oruddha">oruddha</a></em> Thig.445<br />pp <em><a href="bword://oruddha">oruddha</a></em>. See also avarundhati.</p><p>cp. Sk. avarundhate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruddha">oruddha</a>, <a href="bword://oruddha">oruddha</a></p>

oruyha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

oruḷha|oru.lha|orulha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oru.lha</span></p><p>who has come down, descended; alighted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://orūḷha">orūḷha</a>, <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

orūḷha|oruu.lha|orulha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oruu.lha</span></p><p>who has come down, descended; alighted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oruḷha">oruḷha</a>, <a href="bword://oruhati">oruhati</a></p>

osadha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>an herb, a medicinal herb; medicine; a drug.</p>

osadha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://osadh%C4%AB">osadhī</a></em>.</p><p>Vedic auṣadha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osadhī">osadhī</a></p>

osadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>variant reading Iti.20 for <em><a href="bword://opadhika">opadhika</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opadhika">opadhika</a></p>

osadhikā|osadhikaa|osadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhikaa</span></p><p>remedy, esp. poultice, fomentation Ja.iv.361.</p><p>fr. osadha</p>

osadhī|osadhii|osadhi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhii</span></p><p>The morning star, used in describing typical whiteness, DN.ii.111 and also great brightness and purity. It.20</p>

osadhī|osadhii|osadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhii</span></p><p>an herb, a plant, esp. a medicinal plant; an annual plant or herb.</p>

osadhī|osadhii|osadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhii</span></p><p>There is no difference in meaning between osadha and osadhī; both mean equally any medicine whether of herbs or other ingredients. Cp. e.g. AN.iv.100 (bījagāma-bhūtagāmā… osadhi-tiṇavanappatayo) Pv.ii.6#10 with Snp.296 (gāvo… yāsu jāyanti osadhā); DN.i.12, cp DN-a.i.98; Pv.iii.5#3; Pv-a.86; Ja.iv.31; Ja.vi.331 (? trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> medicinal <em>herb</em>). Figuratively, ʻbalm of salvation’ (amatosadha) Mil.247. Osadhi-tārakā, star of healing. The only thing we know about this star is its white brilliance, SN.i.65; Iti.20 = AN.v.62; Vv.9#2; Pv.ii.1#10; cp. Pv-a.71 Vism.412. Childers calls it Venus, but gives no evidence other translators render it ʻmorning star’. According to Hindu mythology the lord of medicine is the moon (oṣadhīśa), not any particular star.</p><p>Vedic avaṣa + dhī: bearer of balm, comfort, refreshment</p>

osadhītiṇavanappatayo|osadhiiti.navanappatayo|osadhitinavanappatayo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhiiti.navanappatayo</span></p><p>plants, grasses, and trees</p><p>See also: osadhītināvanaspatayo, <a href="bword://osadhī">osadhī</a></p>

osadhītiṇavanaspatayo|osadhiiti.navanaspatayo|osadhitinavanaspatayo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhiiti.navanaspatayo</span></p><p>plants, grasses, and trees</p><p>See also: osadhītināvanappatayo, <a href="bword://osadhī">osadhī</a></p>

osadhītārakā|osadhiitaarakaa|osadhitaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osadhiitaarakaa</span></p><p>the <em>osadhī</em> star</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osadhī">osadhī</a></p>

osajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lets go, releases; throws; abandons</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossajati">ossajati</a>, <a href="bword://ossajjati">ossajjati</a></p>

osajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to emit, evacuate Pv-a.268 (vaccaṃ excrement, + ohanati)<br />pp <em>osaṭṭha</em>.</p><p>o + <em>sṛj</em></p>

osajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osajjati">osajjati</a></p>

osakkanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osakkati">osakkati</a></p>

osakkati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>draws back (from), retreats; recedes; diminishes, slackens.</p>

osakkati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to draw back, move back DN.i.230; Ja.iv.348 (for apavattati C.); Ja.v.295 (an-osakkitvā) See also Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> p. 60.</p><p>o + sakkati fr. P. <em>sakk</em> = *Sk. <em>ṣvaṣk</em>, cp. Māgadhī osakkai; but sometimes confused with <em>sṛp</em>, cp P. osappati &amp; Sk. apasarpati</p>

osakkita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>having receded; diminished; slackened</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osakkati">osakkati</a></p>

osanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective given out, exhausted, weak Mil.250 (˚viriya).</p><p>o + pp. of <em>syad</em> to move on</p>

osappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to draw back, give way Ja.vi.190 (osappissati; gloss apīyati).</p><p>o + <em>sṛp</em> to creep</p>

osaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>or masculine a projection to a house or building, a verandah</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāraka">osāraka</a></p>

osaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective of the nature of a resort, fit for resorting to, over-hanging eaves, affording shelter Vin.ii.153. See also <em><a href="bword://os%C4%81raka">osāraka</a></em>.</p><p>fr. osarati, osarana &amp; osaṭa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāraka">osāraka</a></p>

osarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes down (to); approaches; enters; flows into; (+ acc. or loc).</p>

osarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to flow, to go away, to recede to, to visit MN.i.176 (gāmaṃ etc.); MN.ii.122<br />pp <em><a href="bword://osa%E1%B9%ADa">osaṭa</a></em>. See also <em><a href="bword://avasarati">avasarati</a></em>.</p><p>o + <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osaṭa">osaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://avasarati">avasarati</a></p>

osaraṇa|osara.na|osarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osara.na</span></p><p>going down (to), entering; approach; entrance.</p>

osaraṇa|osara.na|osarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osara.na</span></p><ol><li>return to, going into (acc.) visiting Ja.i.154 (gāmantaṃ ˚kāle).</li><li>withdrawal, distraction, drawing or moving away, heresy Snp.538 (ogahanāni titthāni, diṭṭhiyo ti attho Snp-a.434).</li></ol><p>fr. avasarati</p>

osaritvā|osaritvaa|osaritva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaritvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a></p>

osaṇhetabba|osa.nhetabba|osanhetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.nhetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osaṇheti">osaṇheti</a></p>

osaṇheti|osa.nheti|osanheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.nheti</span></p><p>makes smooth; smooths down.</p>

osaṇheti|osa.nheti|osanheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.nheti</span></p><p>to make smooth, to smooth out, comb or brush down (hair) Vin.ii.107 (kese); Ja.iv.219 (id.).</p><p>o + saṇheti, denom. fr. saṇha</p>

osaṭa FIXME double|osa.ta FIXME double|osata FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.ta FIXME double</span></p><p>Osaṭa</p><p>having withdrawn to (acc.), gone to or into, undergone, visited MN.i.176, MN.i.469 (padasamācāro sangha-majjhe o.); MN.ii.2 (Rājagahaṃ vass˚āvāsaṃ o.); Mil.24 (sākacchā osaṭā bahū). See also <em><a href="bword://avasa%E1%B9%ADa">avasaṭa</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of o + <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avasaṭa">avasaṭa</a></p>

osaṭa|osa.ta|osata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.ta</span></p><p>gone down to; having entered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a></p>

osaṭa|osa.ta|osata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osa.ta</span></p><p>Avasaṭa &amp; Osaṭa</p><p>withdrawn, gone away; one who has left a community &amp; gone over to another sect, a renegade Vin.iv.216, Vin.iv.217 (= titthāyatanaṃ saṃkata).</p><p>Sk. apasṛta, cp. also samavasṛta, pp. of ava + <em>sṛ</em>.</p>

oseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lays down; places, deposits.</p>

osita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>inhabited (by), accessible (to) Snp.937 (an˚). Cp. vy˚.</p><p>pp. of ava + <strong>sā</strong></p><p>See also: sā</p>

ositta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>sprinkled, besprinkled Ja.v.400. See also avasitta.</p><p>pp. of osiñcati</p>

osiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://oseti">oseti</a></p>

osiñcanta|osi~ncanta|osincanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~ncanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osiñcati">osiñcati</a></p>

osiñcati|osi~ncati|osincati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~ncati</span></p><p>sprinkles; pours upon; pours out.</p>

osiñcati|osi~ncati|osincati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~ncati</span></p><ol><li>to pour out or down over, to besprinkle Vin.ii.262; MN.i.87 (telena); Pv.i.8#5 (ppr osiñcaṃ = āsiñcanto Pv-a.41).</li><li>to scoop out, empty drain (water) Ja.v.450 (osiñciyā, pot. = osiñceyya C.). pp. <em>avasitta &amp; ositta</em>.</li></ol><p>o + siñcati</p>

osiñceyya|osi~nceyya|osinceyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">opt. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~nceyya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osiñcati">osiñcati</a></p>

osiñci|osi~nci|osinci
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~nci</span></p><p>is sprinkled (with), is moistened</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osiñcati">osiñcati</a></p>

osiñcitabba|osi~ncitabba|osincitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~ncitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osiñcati">osiñcati</a></p>

osiñciṃsu|osi~nci.msu|osincimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osi~nci.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osiñcati">osiñcati</a></p>

ossa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ussa">ussa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussa">ussa</a></p>

ossagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>relaxation, in cpd. sati-ossagga (for which more common sati-vossagga) relaxation of memory inattention, thoughtlessness Dhp-a.iii.163 (for pamāda Dhp.167). See <em><a href="bword://vossagga">vossagga</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ossajati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vossagga">vossagga</a></p>

ossajati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lets go, releases; throws; abandons</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osajjati">osajjati</a>, <a href="bword://ossajjati">ossajjati</a></p>

ossajati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avassajati &amp; ossajati</p><p>to let loose, let go, send off, give up, dismiss, release (ava) Ja.iv.425; Ja.v.487 (aor. avassaji read for avissaji).</p><p>ava + <em>sṛj</em>, perhaps ud + <em>sṛj</em> = Sk. utsṛjati, although the usual Vedic form is avasṛjati The form ossajati puzzled the BSk. writers in their sanskritisation apotsṛjati = apa + ut + <em>sṛj</em> Divy.203</p>

ossaji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossajati">ossajati</a></p>

ossajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>release, dismissal, sending off DN-a.i.130.</p><p>fr. ossajati</p>

ossajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lets go, releases; throws; abandons</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osajjati">osajjati</a>, <a href="bword://ossajati">ossajati</a></p>

ossajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to let loose, let go, send off, give up, dismiss, release DN.ii.106 (aor. ossaji); Snp.270 = SN.i.207; Thag.321; Ja.iv.260<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ossa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">ossaṭṭha</a></em>. See also <em>avassajati</em>.</p><p>o + <em>sṛj</em> send off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossaṭṭha">ossaṭṭha</a></p>

ossajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossajjati">ossajjati</a></p>

ossakk
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ossakk˚</p><p>see <em>osakk˚</em>.</p>

ossanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>sunk, low down, deficient, lacking Ja.i.336 (opp. ussanna) Hardly to be derived from ava + <em>syad</em>.</p><p>pp. of osīdati for osanna, ss after ussanna</p>

ossavana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(according to commentary) the top of the roof</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossāvana">ossāvana</a></p>

ossavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>outflow, running water MN.i.189 (variant reading ossāvana &amp; osavana). Cp.; <em><a href="bword://avassava">avassava</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ava + <em>sru</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avassava">avassava</a></p>

ossaṭṭha|ossa.t.tha|ossattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ossa.t.tha</span></p><p>let go, released; thrown; abandoned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossajjati">ossajjati</a></p>

ossaṭṭha|ossa.t.tha|ossattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ossa.t.tha</span></p><p>let loose, released, given up, thrown down DN.ii.106; SN.iii.241; Ja.i.64; Ja.iv.460 (= nissaṭṭha).</p><p>pp. of ossajati</p>

ossaṭṭhakāya|ossa.t.thakaaya|ossatthakaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ossa.t.thakaaya</span></p><p>with one’s form abandoned, abandoning one’s body</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossaṭṭha">ossaṭṭha</a></p>

ossāvana|ossaavana|ossavana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ossaavana</span></p><p>(according to commentary) the top of the roof</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ossavana">ossavana</a></p>

osādayati|osaadayati|osadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaadayati</span></p><p>causes to sink; immerses; discourages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osādeti">osādeti</a>, <a href="bword://osīdati">osīdati</a></p>

osādeti|osaadeti|osadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaadeti</span></p><p>causes to sink; immerses; discourages</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osādayati">osādayati</a>, <a href="bword://osīdati">osīdati</a></p>

osāna|osaana|osana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaana</span></p><p>stopping, cessation; end.</p>

osāna|osaana|osana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaana</span></p><p>stopping, ceasing; end, finish, conclusion SN.v.79 (read paṭikkamosāna), SN.v.177, SN.v.344; Snp.938</p><p>fr. osāpeti</p>

osāpayati|osaapayati|osapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaapayati</span></p><p>brings to an end; finishes; ascertains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāpeti">osāpeti</a></p>

osāpeti|osaapeti|osapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaapeti</span></p><p>brings to an end; finishes; ascertains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāpayati">osāpayati</a></p>

osāpeti|osaapeti|osapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaapeti</span></p><p>to put forth, bring to an end, settle, put down, fix, decide SN.i.81 (fut. osāpayissāmi; vv.ll. oyayiss˚ and obhāyiss˚ Ud.66 (T. otarissāmi? vv.ll. obhāyiss˚, otāy˚ &amp; osāy˚ C. paṭipajjissāmi karissāmi); Ja.i.25 (osāpeti, variant reading obhāseti); Mnd.412 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of osāna); Vv-a.77 (agghaṃ o to fix a price; vv.ll. ohāpeti &amp; onarāpeti) = Dhp-a.iii.108 (variant reading osāreti). Cp. <em><a href="bword://os%C4%81reti">osāreti</a></em>.</p><p>With Morris, J.P.T.S. 1887, 158 Caus. of ava + <strong>sā</strong>, Sk. avasāyayati (cp. P. avaseti, oseti), but by MSS &amp; Pāli grammarians taken as Caus. of; <em>sṛ</em>: sarāpeti contracted to sāpeti, thus ultimately the same as Sk. sārayati = P. sāreti (thus vv.ll.). Not with Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 78 and Müller <em>P. Gr.</em>.42. Caus. of ā + <em>viś</em> to sling</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a>, sā</p>

osāraka|osaaraka|osaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaaraka</span></p><p>or masculine a projection to a house or building, a verandah</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osaraka">osaraka</a></p>

osāraka|osaaraka|osaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaaraka</span></p><p>shelter, outhouse Ja.iii.446. See also <em><a href="bword://osaraka">osaraka</a></em>.</p><p>fr. osarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osaraka">osaraka</a></p>

osārayati|osaarayati|osarayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaarayati</span></p><p>osārayati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn.</p><ol><li>makes enter; puts into or on to; lowers (into).</li><li>makes re-enter, re-admits (a <em>bhikkhu</em> to the <em>saṅgha</em> after suspension)</li></ol><p>osārayati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. recites; recounts.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a>, <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a>, <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a></p>

osāraṇa|osaara.na|osarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaara.na</span></p><p>bringing before the <em>saṅgha</em>, admitting; esp. re-admitting (a suspended bhikkhu) to the <em>saṅgha</em>; re-accepting (a layman, who had been disciplined).</p>

osāraṇā|osaara.naa|osarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaara.naa</span></p><ol><li>restoration, rehabilitation reinstatement (of a bhikkhu after exclusion from the Sangha) Vin.i.322; Mil.344.</li><li>procession (?) (perhaps reading should be ussāraṇā) Dhp-a.ii.1 (T. oss˚).</li></ol><p>fr. osāreti 3</p>

osāraṇīya|osaara.niiya|osaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaara.niiya</span></p><p>(the formal act) connected with re-admittance.</p>

osārenta|osaarenta|osarenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaarenta</span></p><p>osārenta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>part. pr. mfn.</p><p>osārenta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>part. pr. mfn.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a>, <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a></p>

osāresi|osaaresi|osaresi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaaresi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a></p>

osāretabba|osaaretabba|osaretabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaaretabba</span></p><p>to be readmitted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a></p>

osāreti|osaareti|osareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaareti</span></p><p>osāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn.</p><ol><li>makes enter; puts into or on to; lowers (into).</li><li>makes re-enter, readmits (a <em>bhikkhu</em> to the <em>saṅgha</em> after suspension)</li></ol><p>osāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. recites; recounts.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osārayati">osārayati</a>, <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a>, <a href="bword://osārayati">osārayati</a></p>

osāreti|osaareti|osareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaareti</span></p><ol><li>(with variant reading osāpeti, reading osāreti is uncertain) to stow away, deposit, put in, put away (see also <em><a href="bword://opeti">opeti</a></em>) Ja.vi.52, Ja.vi.67 (pattaṃ thavikāya o.).</li><li>to bring out, expound, propound, explain Mil.13 (abhidhammapiṭikaṃ), Mil.203 (kāraṇaṃ), Mil.349 (lekhaṃ to compose a letter).</li><li>(t.t.) to restore a bhikkhu who has undergone penance Vin.i.96, Vin.i.322, Vin.i.340; Vin.iv.53 (osārehi ayyā ti vuccamāno osāreti)</li></ol><p>pass osāriyati Vin.ii.61 pp. <em><a href="bword://os%C4%81rita">osārita</a></em> (cp. <em><a href="bword://os%C4%81ra%E1%B9%87%C4%81">osāraṇā</a></em>).</p><p>Caus. of o + <em>sṛ</em>; to flow</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://opeti">opeti</a>, <a href="bword://osārita">osārita</a>, <a href="bword://osāraṇā">osāraṇā</a></p>

osāretvā|osaaretvaa|osaretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaaretvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osāreti">osāreti</a></p>

osārita|osaarita|osarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaarita</span></p><p>re-admitted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a></p>

osārita|osaarita|osarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaarita</span></p><p>restored, rehabilitated Vin.iv.138.</p><p>pp. of osāreti 3</p>

osāriyati|osaariyati|osariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osaariyati</span></p><p>is readmitted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://osarati">osarati</a></p>

osīdana|osiidana|osidana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osiidana</span></p><p>sinking Dhs-a.363.</p><p>fr. osīdati</p>

osīdati|osiidati|osidati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osiidati</span></p><p>sinks, sinks down; sinks (in spirit); slackens.</p>

osīdati|osiidati|osidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">osiidati</span></p><p>to settle down, to sink, run aground (of ships) SN.iv.314 (osīda bho sappi-tela); Mil.277 (nāvā osīdati)<br />ger <em>osīditvā</em> Ja.ii.293<br />caus 2 <strong>osīdāpeti</strong> Ja.iv.139 (nāvaṃ).</p><p>fr. o + <em>sad</em></p><p>See also: osīdāpeti</p>

otallaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective clothed in rags, poor indigent Ja.iv.380 (= lāmaka olamba-vilamba-nantakadharo C.).</p><p>of uncertain etym. perhaps *avatāryaka from ava + <em>tṛ</em>; or from uttāḷa?</p>

otappati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is heated; becomes warm.</p>

otaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otarati">otarati</a></p>

otarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>descends, comes down; alights (at, loc); goes down into (esp. into water), enters.</li><li>goes into; has a place in; fits.</li></ol>

otarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to descend, to go down to (c. acc.), to be-take oneself to. ppr. <em>otaranto</em> Vin.ii.221<br />aor otari Snp-a.486 (for avaṃsari); Dhp-a.i.19 (cankamanaṃ) Pv-a.47 (nāvāya mahāsamuddaṃ), Pv-a.75<br />inf <em>otarituṃ</em> Pp.65, Pp.75 (sangamaṃ)<br />ger <em><a href="bword://otaritv%C4%81">otaritvā</a></em> Pv-a.94 (pāsādā from the palace), Pv-a.140 (devalokato)<br />caus 2 <em>otarāpeti</em> to cause to descend, to bring down to Ja.vi.345<br />pp otiṇṇa<br />caus 1 <em><a href="bword://ot%C4%81reti">otāreti</a></em>. Opp. <em><a href="bword://uttarati">uttarati</a></em>.</p><p>o + tarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otaritvā">otaritvā</a>, <a href="bword://otāreti">otāreti</a>, <a href="bword://uttarati">uttarati</a></p>

otaraṇa|otara.na|otarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otara.na</span></p><p>descending; alighting; going down (into); entering.</p>

otaraṇa|otara.na|otarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otara.na</span></p><p>adjective going down, descending Ne.1, Ne.2, Ne.4, Ne.107.</p><p>fr. otarati</p>

otaritabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otarati">otarati</a></p>

otaritvā|otaritvaa|otaritva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otaritvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otarati">otarati</a></p>

otariṃ|otari.m|otarim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otari.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otarati">otarati</a></p>

otata FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Otata</p><p>stretched over, covered, spread over with; Dhp.162 (variant reading otthata); Mil.307 (+ vitata) Dhp-a.iii.153 (= pariyonandhitvā ṭhita). See also <em>avatata &amp; sam-otata</em>.</p><p>o + tata, pp. of <em>tan</em></p>

otata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>overspread; covered.</p>

otata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Avatata &amp; otata</p><p>stretched over, covered, spread over with Vv.64#3 (-˚); Vv-a.276 (chādita).</p><p>ava + tata, pp. of <em>tan</em></p>

otiṇṇa FIXME double|oti.n.na FIXME double|otinna FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oti.n.na FIXME double</span></p><p>Otiṇṇa</p><ol><li>(med.) gone down, descended Pv-a.104 (uddho-galaṃ na otiṇṇaṃ not gone down further than the throat).</li><li>(pass.) beset by (cp. avatāra 2), affected with, a victim of, approached by MN.i.460 = AN.ii.123 (dukkh’ otiṇṇa) = Iti.89 (as variant reading; T. has dukkhâbhikiṇṇa which is either gloss or wrong reading for dukkhâvatiṇṇa) MN.ii.10; SN.i.123 (sokâva˚), SN.i.137 (id.); Snp.306 (icchâvatiṇṇa affected with desire), Snp.939 (sallena otiṇṇo = pierced by an arrow, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Mnd.414 as “sallena viddho phuṭṭho”) Ja.v.98 (issâva˚ = issāya otiṇṇa C.).</li><li>(in special sense affected with love, enamoured, clinging to, fallen in love with Vin.iii.128 (= sāratto apekkhavā paṭibaddha-citto) AN.iii.67, AN.iii.259 (˚citta); Snp-a.322 (id.)<br />■ <em>Note.</em> otiṇṇa at SN.v.162 should with variant reading SS be deleted. See also <em><a href="bword://avati%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">avatiṇṇa</a></em>.</li></ol><p>pp. of otarati; the form ava˚ only found in poetry as-˚ e.g. issâvatiṇṇa Ja.v.98; dukkha˚, soka˚ etc. see below 2</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avatiṇṇa">avatiṇṇa</a></p>

otiṇṇa|oti.n.na|otinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oti.n.na</span></p><ol><li><p>(act.)</p><ol><li>descended; gone down into; having entered; having penetrated (intellectually)</li><li>having entered, appeared; taking place; under consideration</li></ol></li><li><p>(pass.) entered (by); overcome, overpowered (by); esp. overcome by passion, lustful.</p></li></ol>

otiṇṇa|oti.n.na|otinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">oti.n.na</span></p><p>Avatiṇṇa &amp; otiṇṇa</p><p>fallen into, affected with (-˚), as <strong>ava˚</strong>; rare late or poetical form of <em>o˚</em>; e.g. Ja.v.98 (issâ˚). See <em><a href="bword://oti%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">otiṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of otarati</p><p>See also: ava˚, <a href="bword://otiṇṇa">otiṇṇa</a></p>

otomasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i></p><p>(one) who is in the dark.</p>

ottapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ottapat">ottapat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottapati">ottapati</a></p>

ottapamāna|ottapamaana|ottapamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottapamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappati">ottappati</a></p>

ottapat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ottapat">ottapat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottapati">ottapati</a></p>

ottapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>feels ashamed or bashful; is afraid (of doing wrong); is scrupulous (to avoid, with instr., gen., and, rarely, acc.);</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappati">ottappati</a></p>

ottappa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>shame, fear (of doing wrong); scrupulousness (in avoiding wrong-doing or blame).</p>

ottappa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>fear of exile, shrinking back from doing wrong, remorse. See on term and its distinction from hiri (shame) <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 20, also Dhs-a.124, Dhs-a.126; Vism.8, Vism.9 and the definition at Snp-a.181 Ottappa generally goes with hiri as one of the 7 noble treasures (see <em><a href="bword://ariya">ariya</a></em>-dhanā). Hiri-ottappa Iti.36; Ja.i.129 hir-ottappa at MN.i.271; SN.ii.220; SN.v.1; AN.ii.78; AN.iv.99 AN.iv.151; AN.v.214; Iti.34; Ja.i.127, Ja.i.206; Vv-a.23. See also hiri<br />■ Further passages: DN.iii.212; MN.i.356; SN.ii.196 SN.ii.206, SN.ii.208; SN.v.89; AN.i.50, AN.i.83, AN.i.95; AN.iii.4 sq., AN.iii.352; AN.iv.11, AN.v.123 sq.; Pp.71; Dhs.147, Dhs.277; Ne.39<br />■ <em><a href="bword://anottappa">anottappa</a></em> (nt.) lack of conscience, unscrupulousness, disregard of morality AN.i.50, AN.i.83, AN.i.95; AN.iii.421; AN.v.146, AN.v.214; Vb.341, Vb.359, Vb.370, Vb.391; as adj. Iti.34 (ahirika +).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81ravat%C4%81">gāravatā</a></em> respect for conscience, AN.iii.331; AN.iv.29</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhana">dhana</a></em> the treasure of (moral) self-control DN.iii.163, DN.iii.251 DN.iii.282; Vv-a.113.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bala">bala</a></em> the power of a (good) conscience DN.iii.253; Pts.ii.169, Pts.ii.176; Dhs.31, Dhs.102 (trl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> power of the fear of blame).</li></ul><p>fr. tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + <em>ud</em>, would corresp. to a Sk. form *auttapya fr. ut-tapya to be regretted, tormented by remorse. The BSk. form is a wrong adaptation of the Pāli form, taking o˚ for apa˚, viz. apatrapya Mvu.iii.53 and apatrapā ibid. i.463. Müller, <em>P. Gr.</em> &amp; Fausböll Sutta Nipāta Index were both misled by the BSk. form as also recently Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ariya">ariya</a>, <a href="bword://anottappa">anottappa</a>, <a href="bword://gāravatā">gāravatā</a>, <a href="bword://dhana">dhana</a>, <a href="bword://bala">bala</a></p>

ottappamāna|ottappamaana|ottappamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottappamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappati">ottappati</a></p>

ottappat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ottappat">ottappat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottappati">ottappati</a></p>

ottappati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>feels ashamed or bashful; is afraid (of doing wrong); is scrupulous (to avoid, with instr., gen., and, rarely, acc.);</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottapati">ottapati</a></p>

ottappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to feel a sense of guilt, to be conscious or afraid of evil SN.i.154; Pts.ii.169, Pts.ii.176; Pp.20, Pp.21; Dhs.31; Mil.171. Ottappin &amp; Ottapin;</p><p>ut + tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ottappi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ottappin">ottappin</a></p><p>fearful (of doing wrong); scrupulous (in avoiding wrong doing or blame).</p>

ottappin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ottappin">ottappin</a></p><p>fearful (of doing wrong); scrupulous (in avoiding wrong doing or blame).</p>

ottappin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ottappin &amp; Ottāpin</p><p>adjective afraid of wrong, conscientious, scrupulous</p><ol><li>ottappin DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; Iti.28, Iti.119</li><li>ottāpin MN.i.43 sq.; SN.ii.159 sq., SN.ii.196 SN.ii.207; SN.iv.243 sq.; AN.ii.13 sq.; AN.iii.3 sq., AN.iii.112; AN.iv.1 sq. AN.v.123, AN.v.146. <em>Anottappin</em> bold, reckless, unscrupulous Pp.20 (+ ahirika). <em>anottāpin</em> at SN.ii.159 sq., SN.ii.195, SN.ii.206; SN.iv.240 sq.; Snp.133 (ahirika +).</li></ol><p>fr. ottappa</p>

ottappiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>fear (of wrong doing).</p>

ottharaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of sieve or filter (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottharika">ottharika</a></p>

ottharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of strainer, a filter Vin.ii.119.</p><p>fr. of tharati</p>

ottharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>scatters, spreads; covers, overspreads; overwhelms, submerges, crushes.</p>

ottharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to spread over, spread out, cover Mil.121 (opp. paṭikkamati, of water). See also <em><a href="bword://avattharati">avattharati</a></em>.</p><p>o + tharati, Sk. root <em>str</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avattharati">avattharati</a></p>

ottharaṇa|otthara.na|ottharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otthara.na</span></p><p>spreading over, veiling Mil.299 (mahik˚).</p><p>fr. ottharati</p>

ottharika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of sieve or filter (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottharaka">ottharaka</a></p>

ottharitvā|ottharitvaa|ottharitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottharitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottharati">ottharati</a></p>

ottharāpetvā|ottharaapetvaa|ottharapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottharaapetvaa</span></p><p>having caused (something) to be overwhelmed or crushed or submerged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottharati">ottharati</a></p>

ottharīyati|otthariiyati|otthariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">otthariiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ottharati">ottharati</a></p>

otthata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>scattered; covered; overwhelmed, submerged, crushed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otthaṭa">otthaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://ottharati">ottharati</a></p>

otthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Otthata = Otthaṭa</p><p>variant reading at Dhp.162 for <em><a href="bword://otata">otata</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otata">otata</a></p>

otthaṭa FIXME double|ottha.ta FIXME double|otthata FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottha.ta FIXME double</span></p><p>Otthata = Otthaṭa</p><p>variant reading at Dhp.162 for <em><a href="bword://otata">otata</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otata">otata</a></p>

otthaṭa|ottha.ta|otthata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottha.ta</span></p><p>scattered; covered; overwhelmed, submerged, crushed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://otthata">otthata</a>, <a href="bword://ottharati">ottharati</a></p>

otthaṭa|ottha.ta|otthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottha.ta</span></p><ol><li>spread over, veiled, hidden by (-˚) Mil.299 (mahik˚ suriya the sun hidden by a fog).</li><li>strewn over (with) Sdhp.246 (-˚). Otthata = Otthata</li></ol><p>pp. of ottharati</p>

ottāpa|ottaapa|ottapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ottaapa</span></p><p>shame, fear (of doing wrong).</p>

ottāpi|ottaapi|ottapi
