sampamodita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>delighted, rejoicing Sdhp.301.</p><p>saṃ + pamodita</p>

sampamūḷha|sampamuu.lha|sampamulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampamuu.lha</span></p><p>adjective confounded Snp.762.</p><p>saṃ + pamūḷha</p>

sampanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>successful, complete, perfect Vin.ii.256; sampannaveyyākaraṇa a full explanation Snp.352.</li><li>endowed with, possessed of abounding in Vin.i.17; Snp.152, Snp.727 (ceto-vimutti˚) Ja.i.421; vijjācaraṇasampanna full of wisdom and goodness DN.i.49; Snp.164; often used as first part of a compound e.g. sampannavijjācaraṇa Dhp.144; Dhp-a.iii.86 sampannasīla virtuous Iti.118; Dhp.57; sampannodaka abounding in water Ja.iv.125.</li><li>sweet, well cooked Vin.ii.196; Mil.395.</li></ol><p>pp. of sampajjati</p>

sampaphulla
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective blooming, blossoming Sdhp.245.</p><p>saṃ + pa + phulla</p>

sampareta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective surrounded, beset with Ja.ii.317; Ja.iii.360 = SN.i.143.</p><p>saṃ + pareta</p>

samparibhinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective broken up Ja.vi.113 (˚gatta).</p><p>saṃ + paribhinna</p>

samparikantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cut all round MN.iii.275. (Trenckner reads sampakantati.)</p><p>saṃ + parikantati</p>

samparikaḍḍhati|samparika.d.dhati|samparikaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparika.d.dhati</span></p><p>to pull about, drag along MN.i.228.</p><p>saṃ + parikaḍḍhati</p>

samparikiṇṇa|sampariki.n.na|samparikinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampariki.n.na</span></p><p>surrounded by Vin.iii.86; Mil.155.</p><p>saṃ + parikiṇṇa</p>

samparitāpeti|samparitaapeti|samparitapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparitaapeti</span></p><p>to make warm, heat, scourge MN.i.128, MN.i.244 = SN.iv.57.</p><p>saṃ + paritāpeti</p>

samparivajjeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to avoid, shun Sdhp.52, Sdhp.208.</p><p>saṃ + parivajjeti</p>

samparivatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective rolling about Dhp.325.</p><p>saṃ + parivatta</p>

samparivattaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective rolling about grovelling Ja.ii.142 (turning somersaults); Dhp-a.ii.5, Dhp-a.ii.12; Mil.253, Mil.357; <em>samparivattakaṃ</em> (adv.) in a rolling about manner MN.ii.138; samparivattakaṃ-samparivattakaṃ continually turning (it) Vin.i.50.</p><p>saṃ + parivattaka</p>

samparivattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to turn, to roll about; ppr. samparivattamāna Ja.i.140; pp. <em><a href="bword://samparivatta">samparivatta</a></em>. Caus. <strong>samparivatteti</strong> [cp. BSk. ˚parivartayati to wring one’s hands Divy.263] to turn over in one’s mind, to ponder over SN.v.89.</p><p>saṃ + parivattati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samparivatta">samparivatta</a>, samparivatteti</p>

samparivāreti|samparivaareti|samparivareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparivaareti</span></p><p>to surround, wait upon, attend on Ja.i.61; aor. 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. samparivāresuṃ Ja.i.164 ger. samparivārayitvā Ja.i.61; ˚etvā (do.) Ja.vi.43, Ja.vi.108 Cp. sampavāreti.</p><p>saṃ + parivāreti</p>

samparivāsita|samparivaasita|samparivasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparivaasita</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://pariv%C4%81sita">parivāsita</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parivāsita">parivāsita</a></p>

samparāya|samparaaya|samparaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparaaya</span></p><p>future state, the next world Vin.ii.162; AN.iii.154; AN.iv.284 sq.; DN.ii.240; SN.i.108; Snp.141, Snp.864, Ja.i.219; Ja.iii.195; Mil.357; Dhp-a.ii.50.</p><p>fr. saṃ + parā + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

samparāyika|samparaayika|samparayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samparaayika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the next world Vin.i.179; Vin.iii.21; DN.ii.240; DN.iii.130; AN.iii.49, AN.iii.364 AN.iv.285; MN.i.87; Iti.17, Iti.39; Ja.ii.74.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sampassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to see, behold; to look to, to consider; ppr sampassanto Vin.i.42; DN.ii.285; sampassaṃ Dhp.290.</p><p>saṃ + passati</p>

sampasāda|sampasaada|sampasada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasaada</span></p><p>serenity, pleasure DN.ii.211, DN.ii.222; AN.ii.199; MN.ii.262.</p><p>saṃ + pasāda</p>

sampasādana|sampasaadana|sampasadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasaadana</span></p><p>tranquillizing DN.i.37; Dhs.161; Mil.34; Vism.156; Dhs-a.170 (in the description of the second Jhāna); happiness, joy Bv.i.35.</p><p>saṃ + pasādana</p>

sampasādaniya|sampasaadaniya|sampasadaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasaadaniya</span></p><p>adjective leading to serenity, inspiring faith DN.iii.99 sq. (the S. Suttanta), DN.iii.116.</p><p>saṃ + pasādaniya</p>

sampasāreti|sampasaareti|sampasareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasaareti</span></p><p>to stretch out, to distract Vism.365<br />pass <em>sampasāriyati</em> AN.iv.47; Mil.297; Dhs-a.376.</p><p>saṃ + pasāreti</p>

sampasīdana|sampasiidana|sampasidana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasiidana</span></p><p>becoming tranquillized Ne.28.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sampasīdati|sampasiidati|sampasidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampasiidati</span></p><p>to be tranquillized, reassured DN.i.106; MN.i.101; DN-a.i.275.</p><p>saṃ + pasīdati</p>

sampatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to jump about, to fly along or about Ja.vi.528 (dumā dumaṃ); imper, sampatantu ib. Ja.vi.448 (itarītaraṃ); ppr. sampatanto flying to Ja.iii.491. pp. <em><a href="bword://sampatita">sampatita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + patati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampatita">sampatita</a></p>

sampati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>now Mil.87; sampatijāta, just born DN.ii.15 = MN.iii.123. Cp. <em><a href="bword://sampa%E1%B9%ADike">sampaṭike</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + paṭi; cp. Sk. samprati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampaṭike">sampaṭike</a></p>

sampatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>jumping about Ja.vi.507.</p><p>pp. of sampatati</p>

sampatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>reached, arrived, come to, present Ja.iv.142; Mil.9, Mil.66; Pv-a.12; Kp-a.142; Snp-a.295; Sdhp.56.</p><p>pp. of sampāpuṇāti</p>

sampattakajāta|sampattakajaata|sampattakajata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampattakajaata</span></p><p>merged in, given to Ud.75.</p><p>read sammattaka (?)</p>

sampatthanā|sampatthanaa|sampatthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampatthanaa</span></p><p>entreating, imploring Dhs.1059.</p><p>saṃ + patthanā</p>

sampatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><ol><li>success, attainment; happiness, bliss, fortune (opp. <em><a href="bword://vipatti">vipatti</a></em>) AN.iv.26, AN.iv.160; Vism.58 Vism.232; Ja.iv.3 (dibba˚); DN-a.i.126; <em>three</em> attainments Ja.i.105; Mil.96; Dhp-a.iii.183 (manussa˚, devaloka˚ nibbāna˚); Ne.126 (sīla˚, samādhi˚, paññā˚; cp sampadā); <em>four</em> Vb-a.439 sq. (gati˚, upadhi˚, kāla˚ payoga˚); <em>six</em> Ja.i.105; <em>nine</em> Mil.341.</li><li>excellency magnificence Snp-a.397; rūpasampatti beauty Ja.iii.187 Ja.iv.333.</li><li>honour Mhvs.22, Mhvs.48.</li><li>prosperity splendour Ja.iv.455; Mhvs.38, Mhvs.92; s. bhavaloko Pts.i.122 Cp. samāpatti &amp; sampadā.</li></ol><p>saṃ + patti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vipatti">vipatti</a></p>

sampavattar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an instigator AN.iii.133.</p><p>saṃ + pavattar</p>

sampavatteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to produce, set going AN.iii.222 (saṃvāsaṃ); Mhvs.23, Mhvs.75.</p><p>saṃ + pavatteti</p>

sampavaṅka|sampava"nka|sampavanka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampava"nka</span></p><p>adjective intimate, friend DN.ii.78; SN.i.83, SN.i.87; Pp.36.</p><p>perhaps saṃ + pari + anka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, contracted to *payyanka → *pavanka</p>

sampavaṅkatā|sampava"nkataa|sampavankata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampava"nkataa</span></p><p>connection, friendliness, intimacy SN.i.87; AN.iii.422 (pāpa˚ &amp; kalyāṇa˚); AN.iv.283 sq.; AN.v.24, AN.v.199; Dhs.1326; Pp.20, Pp.24; Dhs-a.394. Cp anu˚ Vin.ii.88.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sampavaṇṇita|sampava.n.nita|sampavannita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampava.n.nita</span></p><p>adjective described, praised Ja.vi.398.</p><p>saṃ + pa + vaṇṇita</p>

sampavedhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be shaken violently, to be highly affected Vin.i.12; DN.ii.12, DN.ii.108; MN.i.227 Thig.231; Ja.i.25; SN.iv.71<br />caus <strong>sampavedheti</strong> to shake violently DN.ii.108; MN.i.253; Mnd.316, Mnd.371 (pp. ˚pavedhita).</p><p>saṃ + pavedhati</p><p>See also: sampavedheti</p>

sampavedhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be shaken Snp.28; Mil.386.</p>

sampavāreti|sampavaareti|sampavareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampavaareti</span></p><p>to cause to accept, to offer, to regale, serve with; ger. sampavāretvā Vin.i.18; Vin.ii.128; DN.i.109; aor. sampavāresi DN.ii.97.</p><p>saṃ + pavāreti; cp. BSk. saṃpravārayati Divy.285, Divy.310, etc.; Avs.i.90; Mvu.iii.142</p>

sampavāti|sampavaati|sampavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampavaati</span></p><p>to blow, to be fragrant MN.i.212; Ja.vi.534; Vv-a.343 (= Vv.84#32).</p><p>saṃ + pavāti</p>

sampavāyana|sampavaayana|sampavayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampavaayana</span></p><p>making fragrant Vv-a.344.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sampavāyati|sampavaayati|sampavayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampavaayati</span></p><p>to make fragrant, Vv.81#6, Vv.84#32; Vv-a.344.</p><p>saṃ + pavāyati</p>

sampayoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>union, association Vin.i.10; SN.v.421; DN-a.i.96, DN-a.i.260.</p><p>saṃ + payoga</p>

sampayojeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to associate (with) Vin.ii.262; MN.ii.5.</li><li>to quarrel Vin.ii.5; SN.i.239</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://sampayutta">sampayutta</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + payojeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampayutta">sampayutta</a></p>

sampayutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>associated with, connected Dhs.1; Kv.337; Dhs-a.42. <em>-paccaya</em> the relation of association (opp. vippayutta˚) Vism.539; Vb-a.206 Tikp.6, Kp.20, Kp.53, Kp.65, Kp.152 sq.; Duka-pa.1 sq.</p><p>saṃ + payutta</p>

sampayāta|sampayaata|sampayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampayaata</span></p><p>gone forth, proceeded Dhp.237.</p><p>saṃ + payāta</p>

sampayāti|sampayaati|sampayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampayaati</span></p><p>to proceed, to go on;<br />inf sampayātave Snp.834;<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sampay%C4%81ta">sampayāta</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + payāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampayāta">sampayāta</a></p>

sampaṭicchana|sampa.ticchana|sampaticchana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.ticchana</span></p><p>acceptance, agreement Dhs-a.332; Snp-a.176 (“sādhu”); Vism.21; Sdhp.59, Sdhp.62.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sampaṭicchati|sampa.ticchati|sampaticchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.ticchati</span></p><p>to receive, accept Ja.i.69; Ja.iii.351; Mhvs.6, Mhvs.34; ovādaṃ s. to comply with an admonition Ja.iii.52; sādhū ti s. to say “well” and agree Ja.ii.31; Mil.8. Caus. ii. <em>sampaṭicchāpeti</em> Ja.vi.336.</p><p>saṃ + paṭicchati</p>

sampaṭiggaha|sampa.tiggaha|sampatiggaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tiggaha</span></p><p>summing up, agreement Kp-a.100.</p><p>saṃ + paṭiggaha</p>

sampaṭike|sampa.tike|sampatike
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tike</span></p><p>adverb now Ja.iv.432 (= sampati, idāni C.).</p><p>loc. fr. saṃ + paṭi + ka</p>

sampaṭinipajjā|sampa.tinipajjaa|sampatinipajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tinipajjaa</span></p><p>squatting down, lying down Thag-a.111.</p><p>saṃ + paṭi + nipajjā</p>

sampaṭisaṅkhā|sampa.tisa"nkhaa|sampatisankha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tisa"nkhaa</span></p><p>deliberately SN.ii.111; contracted from ger. ˚-saṃkhāya.</p>

sampaṭivedha|sampa.tivedha|sampativedha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tivedha</span></p><p>penetration Ne.27, Ne.41, Ne.42, Ne.220.</p><p>saṃ + paṭivedha</p>

sampaṭivijjhati|sampa.tivijjhati|sampativijjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampa.tivijjhati</span></p><p>to penetrate; Pass. sampaṭivijjhiyati Ne.220.</p><p>saṃ + paṭivijjhati</p>

sampha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun frivolous; nt. frivolity, foolishness only in connection with expressions of talking, as <em>samphaṃ bhāsati</em> to speak frivolously AN.ii.23; Snp.158; <em>samphaṃ giraṃ bh</em>. Ja.vi.295; <em>samphaṃ palapati</em> Tikp.167 sq. Also in compounds <em>-palāpa</em> frivolous talk DN.i.4; DN.iii.69, DN.iii.82 DN.iii.175, DN.iii.269; AN.i.269 sq., AN.i.298; AN.ii.60, AN.ii.84, AN.ii.209; AN.iii.254, AN.iii.433 AN.iv.248; AN.v.251 sq., AN.v.261 sq.; Tikp.168, Kp.281; DN-a.i.76 <em>-palāpin</em> talking frivolously DN.i.138; DN.iii.82; AN.i.298 Pp.39, Pp.58.</p><p>not clear, if &amp; how connected with Sk. śaśpa, grass. The BSk. has sambhinna-pralāpa for sampha-ppalāpa</p>

samphala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective abounding in fruits SN.i.70; SN.i.90 = Iti.45.</p><p>saṃ + phala</p>

samphassa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>contact, reaction Vin.i.3; AN.ii.117; DN.ii.62; MN.i.85; Ja.i.502; kāya-s. the touch of the skin DN.ii.75; cakkhu-, sota-, ghāna-, jivhā-, kāya-and mano-s. DN.ii.58, DN.ii.308; SN.iv.68 sq.; Vb-a.19.</p><p>saṃ + phassa</p>

samphulla
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective full-blown Ja.vi.188.</p><p>saṃ + phulla</p>

samphusanā|samphusanaa|samphusana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samphusanaa</span></p><p>touch, contact Thig.367; Dhs.2, Dhs.71.</p><p>saṃ + phusanā</p>

samphusati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to touch, to come in contact with;<br />ppr samphussaṃ Iti.68;<br />ppr med samphusamāna Snp.671; Cnd.199 (reads samphassamāna, where id. p at MN.i.85 has <em>rissamāna</em>);<br />aor <strong>samphusi</strong> DN.ii.128;<br />inf samphusituṃ Snp.835; DN.ii.355;<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samphu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">samphuṭṭha</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + phusati</p><p>See also: samphusi, <a href="bword://samphuṭṭha">samphuṭṭha</a></p>

samphusitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the state of having been brought into touch with Dhs.2, Dhs.71.</p><p>abstr. fr. samphusita</p>

samphuṭṭha|samphu.t.tha|samphuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samphu.t.tha</span></p><p>touched SN.iv.97; AN.v.103; Iti.68.</p><p>pp. of samphassati</p>

sampiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective friendly; <em>sampiyena</em> by mutual consent, in mutual love Snp.123, Snp.290.</p><p>saṃ + piya</p>

sampiyāyanā|sampiyaayanaa|sampiyayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampiyaayanaa</span></p><p>intimate relation, great fondness Ja.iii.492.</p><p>saṃ + piyāyanā</p>

sampiyāyati|sampiyaayati|sampiyayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampiyaayati</span></p><p>to receive with joy, to treat kindly, address with love Ja.iii.482; ppr. sampiyāyanto Ja.i.135; sampiyāyamāna (do.) fondling, being fond of DN.ii.223; Ja.i.191, Ja.i.297, Ja.i.361; Ja.ii.85; Dhp-a.ii.65. aor. 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. <em>sampiyāyiṃsu</em> Ja.vi.127.</p><p>saṃ + piyāyati</p>

sampiṇḍana|sampi.n.dana|sampindana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampi.n.dana</span></p><p>combining, connection, addition Vism.159 (of “ca”); Kp-a.228 (id.); Dhs-a.171.</p><p>fr. saṃ + piṇḍ˚</p>

sampiṇḍeti|sampi.n.deti|sampindeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampi.n.deti</span></p><p>to knead or ball together, combine, unite Vism.159; Kp-a.125, Kp-a.221, Kp-a.230; Dhs-a.177; pp <em><a href="bword://sampi%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Dita">sampiṇḍita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + piṇḍeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampiṇḍita">sampiṇḍita</a></p>

sampiṇḍita|sampi.n.dita|sampindita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampi.n.dita</span></p><p>brought together, restored Ja.i.230; compact, firm Ja.v.89.</p><p>pp. of sampiṇḍeti</p>

sampucchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to ask DN.i.116; ger. <em>sampuccha</em> having made an appointment with SN.i.176.</p><p>saṃ + pucchati</p>

sampupphita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in full bloom Pv.iv.12 (= niccaṃ pupphita Pv-a.275).</p><p>saṃ + pupphita</p>

sampurekkharoti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to honour MN.ii.169.</p><p>saṃ + purakkharoti</p>

sampuṇṇa|sampu.n.na|sampunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampu.n.na</span></p><p>(sampūrṇa) filled, full Snp.279; Bv.ii.119 = Ja.i.20; Mhvs.22, Mhvs.60.</p>

sampuṭa|sampu.ta|samputa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampu.ta</span></p><p>the hollow of the hand (in posture of veneration), in <em>pāṇi˚</em>; Mhvs.37, Mhvs.192 i.e. Cūḷavaṃsa (ed. Geiger) p. 15.</p><p>cp. saṃ + puṭa (lexicogr. Sk. sampuṭa “round box”) &amp; BSk. sampuṭa in meaning “añjali” at Divy.380, in phrase kṛta-kara-sampuṭah</p>

sampuṭita|sampu.tita|samputita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampu.tita</span></p><p>shrunk, shrivelled MN.i.80.</p><p>saṃ + puṭita = phuṭita, cp. BSk. sampuṭaka Mvu.ii.127</p>

sampādaka|sampaadaka|sampadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaadaka</span></p><p>one who obtains Mil.349.</p><p>fr. sampādeti</p>

sampādana|sampaadana|sampadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaadana</span></p><p>effecting, accomplishment Ne.44; preparing, obtaining Ja.i.80.</p><p>fr. sampādeti</p>

sampādeti|sampaadeti|sampadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to procure, obtain Vin.i.217; Vin.ii.214; ekavacanaṃ s. to be able to utter a single word Ja.ii.164; kathaṃ s. to be able to talk Ja.ii.165 dohaḷe s. to satisfy the longing Mhvs.22, Mhvs.51.</li><li>to strive, to try to accomplish one’s aim DN.ii.120; SN.ii.29</li></ol><p>Caus. of sampajjati</p>

sampāka|sampaaka|sampaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaaka</span></p><ol><li>what is cooked, a cooked preparation, concoction Vin.ii.259 (maṃsa˚ etc.); Vv.43#5 (kola˚); Vv-a.186.</li><li>ripeness, development Ja.vi.236.</li></ol><p>saṃ + pāka</p>

sampāleti|sampaaleti|sampaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaaleti</span></p><p>to protect Ja.iv.127.</p><p>saṃ + pāleti</p>

sampāpaka|sampaapaka|sampapaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaapaka</span></p><p>adjective causing to obtain, leading to, bringing Ja.iii.348; Ja.vi.235.</p><p>fr. sampāpeti</p>

sampāpana|sampaapana|sampapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaapana</span></p><p>reaching, getting to Mil.355, Mil.356 (tīra˚).</p><p>fr. sampāpuṇāti</p>

sampāpuṇāti|sampaapu.naati|sampapunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaapu.naati</span></p><p>to reach, attain; to come to, meet with; aor. sampāpuṇi Ja.i.67; Ja.ii.20; pp. <em><a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></em> -Caus. <em>sampāpeti</em> to bring, to make attain Vism.303.</p><p>saṃ + pāpuṇāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampatta">sampatta</a></p>

sampāruta|sampaaruta|samparuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaaruta</span></p><p>(quite) covered MN.i.281.</p><p>saṃ + pāruta</p>

sampāta|sampaata|sampata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaata</span></p><p>falling together, concurrence, collision Iti.68; kukkuṭasampāta neighbouring, closely adjoining (yasmā gāmā nikkhamitvā kukkuṭo padasā va aññaṃ gāmaṃ gacchati, ayaṃ kukkuṭasampāto ti vuccati) Vin.iv.63, Vin.iv.358; kukkuṭasampātaka lying close together (lit. like a flock of poultry) AN.i.159. Cp. the similar <em><a href="bword://sannip%C4%81ta">sannipāta</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + pāta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sannipāta">sannipāta</a></p>

sampāyati|sampaayati|sampayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampaayati</span></p><p>to be able to explain (DN-a.i.117: sampādetvā kathetuṃ sakkuṇoti), to agree, to come to terms, succeed DN.i.26; DN.ii.284; MN.i.85, MN.i.96, MN.i.472; MN.ii.157; AN.v.50; SN.iv.15 SN.iv.67; SN.v.109; Vin.ii.249 (cp. p. 364); aor. sampāyāsi MN.i.239. Cp. sampayāti.</p><p>der<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> not clear; Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>i.62 = sampādayati; but more likely = sampāyāti, i.e. sam + pa + ā + <strong>yā</strong>.</p><p>See also: yā</p>

sampīḷa|sampii.la|sampila
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampii.la</span></p><p>trouble, pain; asampīḷaṃ free from trouble Mil.351.</p><p>saṃ + pīḷa, cp. pīḷā</p>

sampīḷeti|sampii.leti|sampileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampii.leti</span></p><p>to press, to pinch, to worry Vin.iii.126; pp. <em><a href="bword://samp%C4%AB%E1%B8%B7ita">sampīḷita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + pīḷeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sampīḷita">sampīḷita</a></p>

sampīḷita|sampii.lita|sampilita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampii.lita</span></p><p>troubled; as nt., worry, trouble Mil.368.</p><p>pp. of sampīḷeti</p>

sampīṇeti|sampii.neti|sampineti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampii.neti</span></p><p>to satisfy, gladden, please; aor. 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. <em>sampesi</em> Ja.iii.253; ger. <em>sampīṇayitvā</em> Dāvs iv.11.</p><p>saṃ + pīṇeti</p>

sampūjeti|sampuujeti|sampujeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampuujeti</span></p><p>to venerate Mhvs.30, Mhvs.100.</p><p>saṃ + pūjeti</p>

sampūreti|sampuureti|sampureti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sampuureti</span></p><p>Pass. <em>pūriyati˚</em>; to be filled, ended; aor. sampūri (māso, “it was a full month since…” Ja.iv.458.</p><p>saṃ + pūreti</p>

samubbahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to carry Dāvs iii.3; Dāvs v.35; ppr. <em>samubbahanto</em> Ja.vi.21 (making display of).</p><p>saṃ + ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

samubbhūta|samubbhuuta|samubbhuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samubbhuuta</span></p><p>borne from, produced from Dāvs ii.25.</p><p>saṃ + ud + bhūta</p>

samuccaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>collection, accumulation Ja.ii.235 (the signification of the particle vā); Snp-a.266 (id.)<br />■ samuccaya-kkhandhaka the third section of Cullavagga Vin.ii.38–Vin.ii.72.</p><p>saṃ + uccaya</p>

samucchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://samu%C3%B1chaka">samuñchaka</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samuñchaka">samuñchaka</a></p>

samucchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be consolidated, to arise samucchissatha (Conditional) DN.ii.63.</p><p>derivation and meaning uncertain; Windisch, <em>Buddha’s Geburt</em>, p. 39, n.1 derives it fr. saṃ + mucchati Cp. Geiger, <em>P.Gr.</em> § 157</p>

samuccheda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cutting off, abolishing, giving up. MN.i.360 Kp-a.142; sammā s. Pts.i.101; ˚pahāna relinquishing by extirpation Vism.5 Snp-a.9 ˚maraṇa dying by extirpation (of saṁsāra) Vism.229; ˚visuddhi Pts.ii.3; ˚suññaṁ Pts.ii.180</p><p>saṃ + uccheda</p>

samucchindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to extirpate, abolish, spoil, give up DN.i.34; DN.ii.74; MN.i.101 sq., MN.i.360; Ja.iv.63. pp. <em><a href="bword://samucchinna">samucchinna</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ucchindati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samucchinna">samucchinna</a></p>

samucchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cut off, extirpated DN.i.34.</p><p>saṃ + ucchinna</p>

samucchita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>infatuated SN.i.187; SN.iv.71; Thag.1219. It is better to read <em><a href="bword://pamucchita">pamucchita</a></em> at all passages.</p><p>saṃ + mucchita</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pamucchita">pamucchita</a></p>

samucita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>suitable Vin.iv.147 (must mean something else here, perhaps “hurt,” or “frightened”) Dāvs v.55.</p><p>saṃ + ucita, pp. of <em>uc</em> to be pleased</p>

samudaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>rise, origin DN.i.17; DN.ii.33, DN.ii.308; DN.iii.227; AN.i.263 (kamma˚); Vin.i.10; Snp.p.135; Iti.16 (samuddaya metri causa) etc. <em>dukkha˚</em>; the origin of ill, the second ariya-sacca, e.g. DN.iii.136; AN.i.177 Vism.495 (where samudaya is explained in its parts as sam u + aya); Vb-a.124.</li><li>bursting forth, effulgence (pabhā˚) Ja.i.83.</li><li>produce, revenue DN.i.227.</li></ol><p>saṃ + udaya</p>

samudda
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>(large) quantity of water, e.g. the Ganges; the sea the ocean DN.i.222; MN.i.493; AN.i.243; AN.ii.48 sq. AN.iii.240; DN.iii.196, DN.iii.198; SN.i.6, SN.i.32, SN.i.67; Ja.i.230; Ja.iv.167 Ja.iv.172; Dhp.127; Mnd.353; Snp-a.30; Pv-a.47, Pv-a.104, Pv-a.133 Pv-a.271; explained by adding sāgara, SN.ii.32; four oceans SN.ii.180, SN.ii.187; Thag-a.111. Often characterized as mahā the great ocean, e.g. Vin.ii.237; AN.i.227; AN.ii.55 AN.iii.52; AN.iv.101; Snp-a.371; Dhp-a.iii.44. <em>Eight</em> qualities AN.iv.198, AN.iv.206; popular etymology Mil.85 sq. (viz “yattakaṃ udakaṃ tattakaṃ loṇaṃ,” and vice versa) the eye etc. (the senses), an ocean which engulfs all beings SN.iv.157 (samudda = mahā udakarāsi)<br />■ Cp sāmuddika.</p><ul><li><em>-akkhāyikā</em> (f.) tales about the origin of the sea cosmogony Vin.i.188; MN.i.513 sq.; DN.i.8; DN-a.i.91</li><li><em>-ṭṭhaka</em> situated in the ocean Ja.vi.158.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%ABci">vīci</a></em> a wave of the ocean Vism.63.</li></ul><p>cp. Vedic samudra, fr. saṃ + udra, water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vīci">vīci</a></p>

samuddadatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk whom Devadatta persuaded to join him in trying to bring about schism in the Order.Vin.ii.196 Vin.iii.171 He was a favourite of Thullanandā. Vin.iv.66</p>

samuddakappāsī|samuddakappaasii|samuddakappasi
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuddakappaasii</span></p><p>Bengal Cotton, <em>Gossypum neglectum</em>, a pyramidal bush with leathery furrowed and corrugated leaves, reddish stems and yellow flowers with a purple centre Ja.vi.537. The bush gives an inferior cotton but is hardy and easily grown.</p>

samuddaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>metri causa instead of samudaya Iti.16, Iti.52.</p>

samuddharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>pulling out, salvation Mil.232.</p><p>saṃ + uddharaṇa</p>

samuddharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take out or away; to lift up, carry away, save from; aor. <strong>samuddhari</strong> Ja.vi.271 <em>samuddhāsi</em> (aor. thus read instead of samuṭṭhāsi Ja.v.70.</p><p>saṃ + uddharati</p><p>See also: samuddhari</p>

samuddhaṭa|samuddha.ta|samuddhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuddha.ta</span></p><p>pulled out, eradicated Mhvs.59, Mhvs.15; Ja.vi.309; Sdhp.143.</p><p>saṃ + uddhaṭa</p>

samudeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to arise; pres. <em>samudayati</em> (variant reading samudīyati) SN.ii.78; <em><a href="bword://samudeti">samudeti</a></em> AN.iii.338; pp. <em><a href="bword://samudita">samudita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + udeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samudeti">samudeti</a>, <a href="bword://samudita">samudita</a></p>

samudikkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to behold Thag-a.147 (Tha-ap.52).</p><p>saṃ + udikkhati</p>

samudita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>arisen Dāvs v.4.</li><li>excited SN.i.136.</li><li>united Vv-a.321.</li></ol><p>saṃ + udita<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

samudācarati|samudaacarati|samudacarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaacarati</span></p><ol><li>to be current, to be in use MN.i.40 (= kāya-vacī-dvāraṃ sampatta s. MN-a.182)</li><li>to occur to, to befall, beset, assail MN.i.109, MN.i.112 MN.i.453; SN.ii.273; Iti.31; Vism.343.</li><li>to behave towards to converse with (instr.), to address Vin.i.9; DN.ii.154 DN.ii.192; AN.iii.124, AN.iii.131; AN.iv.415, AN.iv.440; AN.v.103; Ja.i.192.</li><li>to practise Ja.ii.33 (aor. ˚ācariṃsu).</li><li>to claim, to boast of Vin.iii.91</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://samud%C4%81ci%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">samudāciṇṇa</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ud + ācarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samudāciṇṇa">samudāciṇṇa</a></p>

samudācaritatta|samudaacaritatta|samudacaritatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaacaritatta</span></p><p>practice Mil.59.</p><p>abstr. fr. samudācarita, pp. of samudācarati</p>

samudāciṇṇa|samudaaci.n.na|samudacinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaaci.n.na</span></p><p>practised, indulged in Ja.ii.33; Tikp.320.</p><p>pp. of samudācarati</p>

samudācāra|samudaacaara|samudacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaacaara</span></p><p>behaviour, practice, habit, familiarity Ja.iv.22; Snp-a.6; Dhs-a.392; Pv-a.279.</p><p>saṃ + ud + ācāra</p>

samudāgacchati|samudaagacchati|samudagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaagacchati</span></p><p>to result, rise; to be got, to be at hand DN.i.116; MN.i.104<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samud%C4%81gata">samudāgata</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + udāgacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samudāgata">samudāgata</a></p>

samudāgama|samudaagama|samudagama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaagama</span></p><p>beginning Ja.i.2.</p><p>saṃ + ud + āgama</p>

samudāgata|samudaagata|samudagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaagata</span></p><p>arisen, resulted; received SN.ii.24; Snp.648 (= āgata C.).</p><p>pp. of last</p>

samudāhāra|samudaahaara|samudahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaahaara</span></p><p>talk, conversation Mil.344; <em>piya˚</em>; AN.v.24, AN.v.27, AN.v.90 AN.v.201, AN.v.339; Thag-a.226.</p><p>saṃ + udāhāra, cp. BSk. samudāhāra Divy.143</p>

samudānaya|samudaanaya|samudanaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaanaya</span></p><p>adjective to be procured or attained Ja.iii.313 (su˚).</p><p>grd. of samudāneti</p>

samudāneti|samudaaneti|samudaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaaneti</span></p><p>to collect, procure, attain get MN.i.104; Snp.295<br />pp <em>-ānīta</em>.</p><p>saṃ + ud + āneti; cp. BSk. samudānayati Divy.26, Divy.50, Divy.490; Avs.i.199</p>

samudānīta|samudaaniita|samudanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaaniita</span></p><p>collected, procured Ja.iv.177.</p><p>pp. of samudāneti, cp. BSk. samudānīta Mvu.i.231</p>

samudāvaṭa|samudaava.ta|samudavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaava.ta</span></p><p>restrained Dhs-a.75.</p><p>saṃ + ud + āvaṭa? Better read as saṃ + udāvatta</p>

samudāya|samudaaya|samudaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudaaya</span></p><p>multitude, quantity Vv-a.175; the whole Vv-a.276.</p><p>fr. saṃ + ud + ā + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

samudīraṇa|samudiira.na|samudirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudiira.na</span></p><p>moving MN.i.119; DN.i.76; Vism.365; Dhs-a.307.</p><p>saṃ + udīraṇa in meaning udīreti 1</p>

samudīrita|samudiirita|samudirita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samudiirita</span></p><p>uttered Ja.vi.17.</p><p>saṃ + udīrita</p>

samugga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>box, basket Ja.i.265, Ja.i.372, Ja.i.383; Mil.153, Mil.247; Sdhp.360 (read samuggābhaṃ) Samugga-jātaka the 436th Jātaka Ja.iii.527 sq. (called Karaṇḍaka-Jātaka ibid.; v.455).</p><p>Class. Sk. samudga</p>

samuggahīta|samuggahiita|samuggahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuggahiita</span></p><p>seized, taken up Snp.352, Snp.785, Snp.801, Snp.837, Snp.907; Mnd.76, Mnd.100, Mnd.193.</p><p>pp. of samuggaṇhāti</p>

samuggama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>rise, origin Vb-a.21 (twofold, of the khandhas).</p><p>saṃ + uggama</p>

samuggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>arisen Vv-a.280; Ja.iv.403 (text samuggagata).</p><p>saṃ + uggata</p>

samuggaṇhāti|samugga.nhaati|samugganhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samugga.nhaati</span></p><p>to seize, grasp, embrace; ger. <em>samuggahāya</em> Snp.797; Mnd.105<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samuggah%C4%ABta">samuggahīta</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + uggaṇhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samuggahīta">samuggahīta</a></p>

samugghāta|samugghaata|samugghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samugghaata</span></p><p>uprooting, abolishing, removal DN.i.135; MN.i.136; AN.ii.34; AN.iii.407; AN.v.198; SN.ii.263; SN.iii.131; SN.iv.31; Vin.i.107, Vin.i.110; Ja.iii.397.</p><p>saṃ + ugghāta; BSk. samudghāṭa Lal.36, Lal.571</p>

samugghātaka|samugghaataka|samugghataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samugghaataka</span></p><p>adjective removing Mil.278.</p><p>fr. last</p>

samugghātita|samugghaatita|samugghatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samugghaatita</span></p><p>abolished, completely removed; nt. abstr. <em>-tta</em> Mil.101.</p><p>pp. of samugghāteti, see <em><a href="bword://sam%C5%ABhanati">samūhanati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samūhanati">samūhanati</a></p>

samuggirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to throw out, eject Vv-a.199; to cry aloud Dāvs v.29.</p><p>saṃ + uggirati</p>

samujjala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective resplendent Ja.i.89, Ja.i.92 (pañcavaṇṇa-vattha˚). raṃsi-jāla˚ resplendent with the blaze of rays Vv-a.12, Vv-a.14, Vv-a.166.</p><p>saṃ + ujjala</p>

samujju
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective straightforward, perfect Snp.352; SN.iv.196 (text saṃmuju).</p><p>saṃ + uju</p>

samukkaṃsati|samukka.msati|samukkamsati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samukka.msati</span></p><p>to extol, to praise Snp.132, Snp.438; MN.i.498<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samukka%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">samukkaṭṭha</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ukkaṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samukkaṭṭha">samukkaṭṭha</a></p>

samukkaṭṭha|samukka.t.tha|samukkattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samukka.t.tha</span></p><p>exalted AN.iv.293; Thag.632.</p><p>saṃ + ukkaṭṭha</p>

samukkheṭita|samukkhe.tita|samukkhetita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samukkhe.tita</span></p><p>despised, rejected Vin.iii.95; Vin.iv.27.</p><p>saṃ + ukkheṭita</p>

samukkācanā|samukkaacanaa|samukkacana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samukkaacanaa</span></p><p>= ukkācanā Vb.352; Vism.23.</p>

samullapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>talking (with), conversation Snp-a.71.</p><p>saṃ + ullapana</p>

samullapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to talk, converse Vin.iii.187; Pv-a.237; ppr. <em>samullapanto</em> Ja.iii.49.</p><p>saṃ + ullapati</p>

samullāpa|samullaapa|samullapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samullaapa</span></p><p>conversation, talk Mil.351.</p><p>= last</p>

samunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>moistened, wet, immersed SN.iv.158; cp. the similar passage AN.ii.211 with ref. to taṇhā as a snare (pariyonaddha).</p><p>saṃ + unna</p>

samunnameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to raise, elevate, Thag.29.</p><p>saṃ + unnameti</p>

samupabbūḷha|samupabbuu.lha|samupabbulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samupabbuu.lha</span></p><p>set up; heaped, massed, in full swing (of a battle), crowded MN.i.253; DN.ii.285; SN.i.98; Mil.292; Ja.i.89.</p><p>saṃ + upa + viyūḷha</p>

samupagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to approach Mil.209.</p><p>saṃ + upagacchati</p>

samupajaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to produce; ˚janiyamāna (ppr. pass.) Ne.195.</p><p>saṃ + upa + janeti</p>

samupama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>resembling Mhvs.37, Mhvs.68; also <em>samūpama</em> Ja.i.146; Ja.v.155; Ja.vi.534.</p><p>saṃ + upama</p>

samuparūḷha|samuparuu.lha|samuparulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuparuu.lha</span></p><p>ascended Dāvs iv.42.</p><p>saṃ + uparūḷha</p>

samupasobhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adorned Mil.2.</p><p>saṃ + upasobhita</p>

samupaṭṭhahati|samupa.t.thahati|samupatthahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samupa.t.thahati</span></p><p>to serve, help; pres. <em>samupaṭṭhāti</em> Sdhp.283; aor. <em>samupaṭṭhahi</em> Mhvs.33 Mhvs.95.</p><p>saṃ + upaṭṭhahati</p>

samupeta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>endowed with, Mil.352.</p><p>saṃ + upeta</p>

samuppajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to arise, to be produced SN.iv.218; pp. <em><a href="bword://samuppanna">samuppanna</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + uppajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samuppanna">samuppanna</a></p>

samuppanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>arisen, produced, come about Snp.168, Snp.599; Dhs.1035.</p><p>saṃ + uppanna</p>

samuppatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>origin, arising SN.iv.218.</p>

samupphosita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>sprinkled Ja.vi.481.</p><p>saṃ + ud + phosita</p>

samuppilava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective jumping or bubbling up Snp.670 (˚āso nom. pl.).</p><p>fr. saṃ + uppilavati</p>

samuppāda|samuppaada|samuppada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuppaada</span></p><p>origin, arising, genesis, coming to be, production Vin.ii.96; SN.iii.16 sq.; Iti.17; AN.iii.406 (dhamma˚); Ja.vi.223 (anilūpana-samuppāda, v. read ˚-samuppāta, “swift as the wind”); Vism.521 (sammā &amp; saha uppajjati = samuppāda). Cp. paṭicca˚.</p><p>saṃ + uppāda</p>

samupādika|samupaadika|samupadika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samupaadika</span></p><p>being on a level with the water Mil.237 (Trenckner conjectures samupodika). The better reading, however, is samupp˚, sama = peace, quiet, thus “producing quiet,” calm.</p>

samupāgacchati|samupaagacchati|samupagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samupaagacchati</span></p><p>to come to; aor. samupāgami Mhvs.36, Mhvs.91; pp. <em><a href="bword://samup%C4%81gata">samupāgata</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + upāgacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samupāgata">samupāgata</a></p>

samupāgata|samupaagata|samupagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samupaagata</span></p><p>come to, arrived at Mhvs.37, Mhvs.115; Mhvs.38, Mhvs.12; Ja.vi.282; Sdhp.324.</p><p>saṃ + upāgata</p>

samussaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>accumulation, complex AN.ii.42; Iti.48; Iti.34; bhassasamuccaya, grandiloquence Snp.245</li><li>complex form, the body DN.ii.157 = SN.i.148; Vv.35#12 (= sarīra Vv-a.164); Dhp.351; Thag.202 (“confluence, i.e. of the 5 factors, translation); Thig.22, Thig.270; Dhp-a.iv.70 Thag-a.98, Thag-a.212; rūpasamussaya the same Thig.102 cp. samuccaya.</li></ol><p>saṃ + ud + <em>śri</em>, cp. BSk. samucchraya “body,” Divy.70 = Avs.i.162</p>

samusseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to raise, lift up, Pot. <em>samusseyya</em> AN.i.199 (here = to be grandiloquent)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samussita">samussita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ud + <em>śri</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samussita">samussita</a></p>

samussita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>elevated, erected Ja.iii.497.</li><li>arrogant, proud, haughty Dhp.147 (interpreted at Dhp-a.iii.109 as “compounded,” i.e. the body made up of 300 bones); AN.i.199; Snp-a.288 (˚ṃ bhassaṃ high and mighty talk).</li></ol><p>saṃ + ussita</p>

samussāhita|samussaahita|samussahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samussaahita</span></p><p>instigated Vv-a.105.</p><p>saṃ + ussāhita</p>

samussāpita|samussaapita|samussapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samussaapita</span></p><p>lifted, raised Ja.iii.408.</p><p>saṃ + ussāpita</p>

samuttarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to pass over Mil.372.</p><p>saṃ + uttarati</p>

samuttejaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective instigating, inciting, gladdening MN.i.146; AN.ii.97; AN.iv.296, AN.iv.328; AN.v.155; SN.v.162; Iti.107.</p><p>fr. samuttejeti</p>

samuttejeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to excite, gladden, to fill with enthusiasm Vin.i.18; DN.i.126. Cp. BSk. samuttejayati e.g. Divy.80.</p><p>saṃ + ud + <em>tij</em></p>

samuyyuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>energetic, devoted Vv.63#33; Vv-a.269.</p><p>saṃ + uyyuta</p>

samuñchaka|samu~nchaka|samunchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu~nchaka</span></p><p>adjective only as nt. adv. ˚ṃ gleaning, (living) by gleaning SN.i.19; Ja.iv.466 (˚ṃ carati).</p><p>saṃ + uncha + ka</p>

samuṭṭhahati|samu.t.thahati|samutthahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu.t.thahati</span></p><p>to rise up, to originate; pres. <em>samuṭṭhāti</em> Vin.v.1; aor. <em>samuṭṭhahi</em> Mhvs.28 Mhvs.16<br />pp <em><a href="bword://samu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">samuṭṭhita</a></em><br />caus <strong>samuṭṭhāpeti</strong> to raise to originate, set on foot Ja.i.144, Ja.i.191, Ja.i.318.</p><p>saṃ + uṭṭhahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samuṭṭhita">samuṭṭhita</a>, samuṭṭhāpeti</p>

samuṭṭhita|samu.t.thita|samutthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu.t.thita</span></p><p>arisen, originated, happened, occurred Ja.ii.196; Dhs.1035.</p><p>pp. of samuṭṭhahati</p>

samuṭṭhāna|samu.t.thaana|samutthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu.t.thaana</span></p><p>rising, origination, cause; as adj. (-˚) arising from AN.ii.87; Dhs.766 sq., Dhs.981, Dhs.1175; Mil.134, Mil.302, Mil.304; Ja.i.207; Ja.iv.171; Kp-a.23, Kp-a.31, Kp-a.123 Vism.366.</p><p>saṃ + uṭṭhāna</p>

samuṭṭhānika|samu.t.thaanika|samutthanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu.t.thaanika</span></p><p>adjective originating Dhs-a.263.</p><p>fr. last</p>

samuṭṭhāpaka|samu.t.thaapaka|samutthapaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samu.t.thaapaka</span></p><p>˚ikā) occasioning, causing Dhs-a.344; Vv-a.72.</p><p>fr. samuṭṭhāpeti</p>

samā|samaa|sama
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaa</span></p><ol><li>a year Dhp.106; Mhvs.7, Mhvs.78.</li><li>in agginisamā a pyre Snp.668, Snp.670.</li></ol><p>Vedic samā</p>

samācarati|samaacarati|samacarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaacarati</span></p><p>to behave, act, practise MN.ii.113.</p><p>saṃ + ācarati</p>

samācāra|samaacaara|samacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaacaara</span></p><p>conduct, behaviour DN.ii.279; DN.iii.106, DN.iii.217; MN.ii.113; AN.ii.200, AN.ii.239; AN.iv.82; Snp.279; Vin.ii.248; Vin.iii.184.</p><p>saṃ + ācāra</p>

samādahati|samaadahati|samadahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadahati</span></p><p>to put together SN.i.169. jotiṃ s. to kindle a fire Vin.iv.115; cittaṃ s. to compose the mind, concentrate MN.i.116; pres. samādheti Thig.50; pr. part. <em>samādahaṃ</em> SN.v.312; ppr. med. samādahāna SN.i.169; aor 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. samādahaṃsu DN.ii.254. Pass <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhiyati">samādhiyati</a></em> to be stayed, composed DN.i.73; MN.i.37; Mil.289; Caus. ii. <em>samādahāpeti</em> Vin.iv.115<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81hita">samāhita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ādahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samādhiyati">samādhiyati</a>, <a href="bword://samāhita">samāhita</a></p>

samādapaka|samaadapaka|samadapaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadapaka</span></p><p>instructing, arousing MN.i.145; AN.ii.97; AN.iv.296, AN.iv.328 AN.v.155; SN.v.162; Mil.373; Iti.107; Dhp-a.ii.129.</p><p>fr. samādapeti; cp. BSk. samādāpaka Divy.142</p>

samādapana|samaadapana|samadapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadapana</span></p><p>instructing, instigating MN.iii.132.</p>

samādapetar|samaadapetar|samadapetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadapetar</span></p><p>adviser, instigator MN.i.16.</p>

samādapeti|samaadapeti|samadapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadapeti</span></p><p>to cause to take, to incite, rouse Pp.39, Pp.55; Vin.i.250; Vin.iii.73; DN-a.i.293, DN-a.i.300; aor. ˚dapesi DN.ii.42, DN.ii.95 DN.ii.206; Mil.195; Snp.695; ger. ˚dapetvā DN.i.126; Vin.i.18 ger. samādetvā (sic) Mhvs.37, Mhvs.201; ppr. pass. <em>-dapiyamāna</em> DN.ii.42.</p><p>saṃ + ādapeti, cp. BSk. samādāpayati Divy.51</p>

samādhi|samaadhi|samadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadhi</span></p><ol><li><p>concentration; a concentrated, self-collected, intent state of mind and meditation which, concomitant with right living, is a necessary condition to the attainment of higher wisdom and emancipation. In the <em>Subha-suttanta</em> of the Dīgha (DN.i.209 sq.) samādhi-khandha (“section on concentration”) is the title otherwise given to the <em>cittasampadā</em>, which, in the ascending order of merit accruing from the life of a samaṇa (see <em>Sāmaññaphala-suttanta</em>, and cp. <em>Dial.</em> i.57 sq.) stands between the <em>sīla-sampadā</em> and the <em>paññā-sampadā</em>. In the <em>Ambaṭṭha-sutta</em> the corresponding terms are sīla, <em><a href="bword://cara%E1%B9%87a">caraṇa</a></em>, vijjā (DN.i.100) Thus <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhi">samādhi</a></em> would comprise</p><ol><li>the guarding of the senses (indriyesu gutta-dvāratā),</li><li>self-possession (sati-sampajañña),</li><li>contentment (santuṭṭhi),</li><li>emancipation from the 5 hindrances (nīvaraṇāni)</li><li>the 4 jhānas.</li></ol><p>In the same way we find <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhi">samādhi</a></em> grouped as one of the <em>sampadās</em> at AN.iii.12 (sīla˚ samādhi˚, paññā˚, vimutti˚), and as <em>samādhi-khandha</em> (with sīla˚ &amp; paññā˚) at DN.iii.229 (+ vimutti˚); AN.i.125 AN.ii.20; AN.iii.15; AN.v.326; Mnd.21; Cnd.p.277 (s. v. sīla) It is defined as <em>cittassa ekaggatā</em> MN.i.301; Dhs.15; Dhs-a.118; cp. <em>Cpd.</em> 89 n. 4; identified with <em><a href="bword://avikkhepa">avikkhepa</a></em> Dhs.57, and with <em><a href="bword://samatha">samatha</a></em> Dhs.54<br />■ <em>sammā˚</em>; is one the constituents of the eightfold ariya-magga, e.g. DN.iii.277 Vb-a.120 sq<br />■ See further DN.ii.123 (ariya); Vin.i.97 Vin.i.104; SN.i.28; Mnd.365; Mil.337; Vism.84 sq. (with definition), Vism.289 (+ vipassanā), Vism.380 (˚vipphārā iddhi) Vb-a.91; Dhp-a.i.427; and on term in general Heiler <em>Buddhistische Versenkung</em> 104 sq.</p></li><li><p>Description &amp; characterization of <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhi">samādhi</a></em>: Its four <em>nimittas</em> or signs are the four <em>satipaṭṭhānas</em> MN.i.301; six conditions and six hindrances AN.iii.427; other hindrances MN.iii.158. The second jhāna is born from samādhi DN.ii.186; it is a condition for attaining kusalā dhammā AN.i.115; Mil.38; conducive to insight AN.iii.19, AN.iii.24 sq., AN.iii.200; SN.iv.80; to seeing heavenly sights etc. DN.i.173; to removing mountains etc. AN.iii.311; removes the delusions of self AN.i.132 sq.; leads to Arahantship AN.ii.45 the ānantarika s. Snp.226; cetosamādhi (rapture of mind) DN.i.13; AN.ii.54; AN.iii.51; SN.iv.297; <em>citta˚</em>; id. Ne.16. <em>dhammasamādhi</em> almost identical with samatha SN.iv.350 sq<br />■ Two grades of samādhi distinguished viz. <em><a href="bword://upac%C4%81ra">upacāra</a></em> -s. (preparatory concentration) and <em><a href="bword://appan%C4%81">appanā</a></em> -s (attainment concentration) DN-a.i.217; Vism.126; <em>Cpd.</em> 54, 56 sq.; only the latter results in jhāna; to these a 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (preliminary) grade is added as <em>khaṇika˚</em>; (momentary at Vism.144<br />■ Three kinds of s. are distinguished <em><a href="bword://su%C3%B1%C3%B1ata">suññata</a></em> or empty, <em><a href="bword://appa%E1%B9%87ihita">appaṇihita</a></em> or aimless, and <em>animitta</em> or signless AN.i.299; SN.iv.360; cp. SN.iv.296; Vin.iii.93; Mil.337; Mil.333 sq.; Dhs-a.179 sq., Dhs-a.222 sq., Dhs-a.290 sq. see <em>Yogāvacara’s Manual</em> p. xxvii; samādhi (tayo samādhī) is savitakka savicāra, avitakka vicāramatta or avitakka avicāra DN.iii.219; Kv.570; cp. 413; Mil.337; Dhs-a.179 sq.; it is <em>fourfold</em> chanda-, viriya-, citta-, and vīmaṃsā-samādhi DN.ii.213; SN.v.268<br />■ Another fourfold division is that into hāna-bhāgiya, ṭhiti˚, visesa˚ nibbedha˚ DN.iii.277 (as “dhammā duppaṭivijjhā”).</p></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a></em> the faculty of concentration AN.ii.149; Dhs.15</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></em> the section on s. see above 1.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ja">ja</a></em> produced by concentration DN.i.74; DN.iii.13; Vism.158.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://parikkh%C4%81ra">parikkhāra</a></em> requisite to the attainment of samādhi: either 4 (the sammappadhānas) MN.i.301; or 7: DN.ii.216; DN.iii.252; AN.iv.40.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bala">bala</a></em> the power of concentration AN.i.94 AN.ii.252; DN.iii.213, DN.iii.253; Dhs.28.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81van%C4%81">bhāvanā</a></em> cultivation attainment of samādhi MN.i.301; AN.ii.44 sq. (four different kinds mentioned); iii.25 sq.; DN.iii.222; Vism.371</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83vattanika">saṃvattanika</a></em> conducive to concentration AN.ii.57; SN.iv.272 sq.; DN.iii.245; Dhs.1344.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sambojjha%E1%B9%85ga">sambojjhaṅga</a></em> the s. constituent of enlightment DN.iii.106, DN.iii.226, DN.iii.252 Vism.134 = Vb-a.283 (with the eleven means of cultivating it).</li></ul><p>fr. saṃ + ā + <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://caraṇa">caraṇa</a>, <a href="bword://samādhi">samādhi</a>, <a href="bword://samādhi">samādhi</a>, <a href="bword://avikkhepa">avikkhepa</a>, <a href="bword://samatha">samatha</a>, <a href="bword://samādhi">samādhi</a>, <a href="bword://upacāra">upacāra</a>, <a href="bword://appanā">appanā</a>, <a href="bword://suññata">suññata</a>, <a href="bword://appaṇihita">appaṇihita</a>, <a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a>, <a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a>, <a href="bword://ja">ja</a>, <a href="bword://parikkhāra">parikkhāra</a>, <a href="bword://bala">bala</a>, <a href="bword://bhāvanā">bhāvanā</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvattanika">saṃvattanika</a>, <a href="bword://sambojjhaṅga">sambojjhaṅga</a></p>

samādhika|samaadhika|samadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadhika</span></p><p>adjective excessive, abundant DN.ii.151; Ja.ii.383; Ja.iv.31.</p><p>sama + adhika</p>

samādhiyati|samaadhiyati|samadhiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadhiyati</span></p><p>is Passive of <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dahati">samādahati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samādahati">samādahati</a></p>

samādhāna|samaadhaana|samadhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadhaana</span></p><p>putting together, fixing; concentration Vism.84 (= sammā ādhānaṃ ṭhapanaṃ) in def<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhi">samādhi</a></em> as “samādhān’ aṭṭhena.”</p><p>saṃ + ā + <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samādhi">samādhi</a></p>

samādinna|samaadinna|samadinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadinna</span></p><p>taken up, undertaken AN.ii.193.</p><p>pp. of samādiyati</p>

samādisati|samaadisati|samadisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadisati</span></p><p>to indicate, to command DN.i.211; Mhvs.38, Mhvs.59.</p><p>saṃ + ādisati</p>

samādiyati|samaadiyati|samadiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadiyati</span></p><p>to take with oneself, to take upon oneself, to undertake DN.i.146; imper. <em>samādiya</em> Bv.ii.118 = Ja.i.20; aor. samādiyi SN.i.232; Ja.i.219; ger <strong>samādiyitvā</strong> SN.i.232; &amp; <strong>samādāya</strong> having taken up, i.e. with DN.i.71; Pp.58; DN-a.i.207; Mhvs.1, Mhvs.47; having taken upon himself, conforming to DN.i.163; DN.ii.74; Dhp.266; Snp.792, Snp.898, Snp.962; samādāya sikkhati sikkhāpadesu he adopts and trains himself in the precepts DN.i.63; SN.v.187; Iti.118; Snp.962 (cp. Mnd.478)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dinna">samādinna</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ādiyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: samādiyitvā, samādāya, <a href="bword://samādinna">samādinna</a></p>

samādāna|samaadaana|samadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaadaana</span></p><ol><li>taking, bringing; asamādānacāra (m.) going for alms without taking with one (the usual set of three robes) Vin.i.254.</li><li>taking upon oneself, undertaking acquiring MN.i.305 sq.; AN.i.229 sq.; AN.ii.52; Ja.i.157, Ja.i.219; Vin.iv.319; Kp-a.16, Kp-a.142. <em>kammasamādāna</em> acquiring for oneself of Karma DN.i.82; AN.iii.417; AN.v.33; SN.v.266 SN.v.304; Iti.58 sq., Iti.99 sq.; Vb-a.443 sq.</li><li>resolution vow Vin.ii.268; Ja.i.233; Mil.352.</li></ol>

samāgacchati|samaagacchati|samagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaagacchati</span></p><p>to meet together, to assemble Bv.ii.171; Snp.222; to associate with, to enter with, to meet, DN.ii.354; Snp.834; Ja.ii.82; to go to see Vin.i.308; to arrive, come Snp.698; aor. 1 sg. <em>-gañchiṃ</em> DN.ii.354; 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> ˚gañchi Dhp.210; Ja.ii.62; aor. 2 sg. <em>-gamā</em> Snp.834; ger. <em>-gamma</em> Bv.ii.171 = Ja.i.26; ger. <em>-gantvā</em> Vin.i.308; pp. <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81gata">samāgata</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + āgacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samāgata">samāgata</a></p>

samāgama|samaagama|samagama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaagama</span></p><p>meeting, meeting with, intercourse AN.ii.51; AN.iii.31; Mil.204; cohabitation DN.ii.268 meeting, assembly Ja.ii.107; Mil.349; Dhp-a.iii.443 (three: yamaka-pāṭihāriya˚; dev’orohaṇa˚; Gangārohaṇa˚).</p><p>saṃ + āgama</p>

samāgata|samaagata|samagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaagata</span></p><p>met, assembled Dhp.337; Snp.222.</p><p>pp. of samāgacchati</p>

samāhata|samaahata|samahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaahata</span></p><p>hit, struck Snp.153 (ayosanku˚); Mil.181, Mil.254, Mil.304. Sankusamāhata name of a purgatory MN.i.337.</p><p>saṃ + āhata</p>

samāhita|samaahita|samahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaahita</span></p><ol><li>put down, fitted Ja.iv.337.</li><li>collected (of mind), settled, composed, firm, attentive DN.i.13; SN.i.169; AN.ii.6 (˚indriya); AN.iii.312, AN.iii.343 sq. AN.v.3, AN.v.93 sq., AN.v.329 sq.; Snp.212, Snp.225, Snp.972 etc.; Dhp.362; Iti.119; Pp.35; Vin.iii.4; Mil.300; Vism.410; Mnd.501</li><li>having attained SN.i.48 (cp. <em>K.S.</em> i.321 &amp; Mil.352).</li></ol><p>pp. of samādahati</p>

samākaḍḍhati|samaaka.d.dhati|samakaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to pull along; to entice; ger. ˚iya Mhvs.37, Mhvs.145.</p><p>saṃ + ākaḍḍhati</p>

samākiṇṇa|samaaki.n.na|samakinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaki.n.na</span></p><p>covered, filled SN.i.6; Mil.342.</p><p>saṃ + ākiṇṇa</p>

samākula|samaakula|samakula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaakula</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>filled, crowded B ii.4 = Ja.i.3; Mil.331, Mil.342.</li><li>crowded together Vin.ii.117</li><li>confused, jumbled together Ja.v.302.</li></ol><p>saṃ + ākula</p>

samālapati|samaalapati|samalapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaalapati</span></p><p>to speak to, address Ja.i.478. At Ja.i.51 it seems to mean “to recover the power of speech.” Samavaya = samavaya</p><p>saṃ + ālapati</p>

samāna|samaana|samana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atthi">atthi</a></p>

samāna|samaana|samana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaana</span></p><p>Samāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective similar, equal, even, same Snp.18, Snp.309; Ja.ii.108. Cp. <em>sāmañña<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p><p>Vedic samāna, fr. sama<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Samāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>being, existing DN.i.18, DN.i.60; Ja.i.218; Pv-a.129 (= santo), Pv-a.167 (id.).</li><li>a kind of god DN.ii.260.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-āsanika</em> entitled to a seat of the same height Vin.ii.169.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gatika">gatika</a></em> identical Tikp.35.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> equanimity Snp.702.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vassika">vassika</a></em> having spent the rainy season together Vin.i.168 sq.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83v%C4%81sa">saṃvāsa</a></em> living together with equals Dhp.302 (a˚), cp. Dhp-a.iii.462.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83v%C4%81saka">saṃvāsaka</a></em> belonging to the same communion Vin.i.321.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%ABm%C4%81">sīmā</a></em> the same boundary parish Vin.i.321; ˚ma belonging to the same parish Vin.ii.300.</li></ul><p>ppr. fr. <em>as</em> to be</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gatika">gatika</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://vassika">vassika</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvāsa">saṃvāsa</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvāsaka">saṃvāsaka</a>, <a href="bword://sīmā">sīmā</a></p>

samānatta|samaanatta|samanatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaanatta</span></p><p>adjective equanimous, of even mind AN.iv.364.</p><p>samāna + attan</p>

samānattatā|samaanattataa|samanattata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaanattataa</span></p><p>equanimity, impartiality AN.ii.32 = AN.ii.248; AN.iv.219, AN.iv.364; DN.iii.152, DN.iii.190 sq., DN.iii.232.</p><p>abstr. fr. last</p>

samāneti|samaaneti|samaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaneti</span></p><ol><li>to bring together Ja.i.68.</li><li>to bring, produce Ja.i.433.</li><li>to put together, cp. Ja.i.120 Ja.i.148.</li><li>to collect, enumerate Ja.i.429.</li><li>to calculate (the time) Ja.i.120, Ja.i.148; aor. <em>samānayi</em> DN-a.i.275 pp. <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81n%C4%ABta">samānīta</a></em>.</li></ol><p>saṃ + āneti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samānīta">samānīta</a></p>

samāniyā|samaaniyaa|samaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaniyaa</span></p><p>(all) equally, in common Snp.24.</p><p>instr. fem. of samāna, used adverbially, Vedic samānyā</p>

samānā|samaanaa|samana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaanaa</span></p><p>A class of Devas present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.260</p>

samānīta|samaaniita|samanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaniita</span></p><p>brought home, settled Mil.349.</p><p>pp. of samāneti</p>

samāpajjana|samaapajjana|samapajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapajjana</span></p><p>entering upon, passing through (?) Mil.176.</p><p>fr. last</p>

samāpajjati|samaapajjati|samapajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapajjati</span></p><ol><li>to come into, enter upon, attain DN.i.215 (samādhiṃ samāpajji); Vin.iii.241 (Pot. ˚pajjeyya); <em>samāpattiṃ</em> Ja.i.77; <em>arahattamaggaṃ</em> AN.ii.42 sq.; Vin.i.32; saññāvedayitanirodhaṃ to attain the trance of cessation SN.iv.293; kayavikkayaṃ to engage in buying and selling Vin.iii.241; <em>sākacchaṃ</em> to engage in conversation DN.ii.109; tejodhātuṃ to convert one’s body into fire Vin.i.25; Vin.ii.76.</li><li>to become SN.iii.86 (aor. 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. samāpaduṃ)</li></ol><p>pp <em>samāpajjita samāpanna</em>.</p><p>saṃ + āpajjati</p>

samāpajjita|samaapajjita|samapajjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapajjita</span></p><p>attained, reached, got into DN.ii.109 (parisā ˚pubbā).</p><p>pp. of ˚āpajjati</p>

samāpanna|samaapanna|samapanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapanna</span></p><p>having attained, got to, entered, reached SN.iv.293 (saññā-nirodhaṃ); AN.ii.42 (arahatta-maggaṃ entered the Path); Dhp.264 (icchālobha˚ given to desire); Kv.572 (in special sense attaining the samāpattis).</p><p>pp. of samāpajjati</p>

samāpannaka|samaapannaka|samapannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapannaka</span></p><p>adjective possessed of the <em>samāpattis</em> DN-a.i.119.</p><p>last + ka</p>

samāpattesiya|samaapattesiya|samapattesiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapattesiya</span></p><p>adjective longing for attainment Kv.502 sq.</p><p>samāpatti + esiya, adj. to esikā</p>

samāpatti|samaapatti|samapatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapatti</span></p><p>attainment AN.iii.5; SN.ii.150 sq.; SN.iv.293 (saññā-vedayita-nirodha˚); Dhs.30 Dhs.101; a stage of meditation AN.i.94; Dhs.1331; Ja.i.343 Ja.i.473; Pv-a.61 (mahā-karuṇā˚); Mnd.100, Mnd.106, Mnd.139, Mnd.143 the Buddha acquired anekakoṭisata-sahassā s. Ja.i.77. The <em>eight</em> attainments comprise the four Jhānas, the realm of the infinity of space, realm of the infinity of consciousness, realm of nothingness, realm of neither consciousness nor unconsciousness Pts.i.8, Pts.i.20 sq. Mnd.108, Mnd.328; Bv.192 = Ja.i.28, Ja.i.54; necessary for becoming a Buddha Ja.i.14; acquired by the Buddha Ja.i.66 the <em>nine</em> attainments, the preceding and the trance of cessation of perception and sensation SN.ii.216, SN.ii.222 described MN.i.159 sq. etc.; otherwise called <em>anupubbavihārā</em> DN.ii.156; AN.iv.410, AN.iv.448 &amp; passim [cp. Divy.95 etc.]<br />■ In collocation with jhāna, vimokkha, and samādhi Vin.i.97; AN.iii.417 sq.; cp. <em>Cpd.</em> 59, 133 n. 3 <em>-bhāvanā</em> realizing the attainments Ja.i.67; <em>-kusalatā</em> success in attainment DN.iii.212; Dhs.1331 sq.</p><p>fr. saṃ + ā + <em>pad</em></p>

samāpattila|samaapattila|samapattila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapattila</span></p><p>one who has acquired Ja.i.406.</p><p>fr. last</p>

samāpaṭipatti|samaapa.tipatti|samapatipatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapa.tipatti</span></p><p>misprint for sammā˚ AN.i.69.</p>

samāpeti|samaapeti|samapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaapeti</span></p><p>to complete, conclude Mhvs.5, Mhvs.280; Mhvs.30, Mhvs.55; DN-a.i.307 (desanaṃ)<br />pp <em>samatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p><p>saṃ + āpeti</p>

samārabhati|samaarabhati|samarabhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaarabhati</span></p><p>to begin, undertake MN.i.227; Mhvs.5, Mhvs.79<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81raddha">samāraddha</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samāraddha">samāraddha</a></p>

samāraddha|samaaraddha|samaraddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaraddha</span></p><p>undertaken SN.iv.197; Dhp.293; Ja.ii.61.</p><p>pp. of samārabhati</p>

samāraka|samaaraka|samaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaraka</span></p><p>adjective including Māra Vin.i.11 = SN.v.423; DN.i.250; DN.iii.76, DN.iii.135 &amp; passim.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + māra + ka</p>

samārambha|samaarambha|samarambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaarambha</span></p><ol><li>undertaking, effort, endeavour, activity AN.ii.197 sq. (kāya˚, vacī˚, mano˚) Vin.iv.67.</li><li>injuring, killing, slaughter Snp.311; DN.i.5; DN-a.i.77; AN.ii.197; SN.v.470; Pp.58; Dhs-a.146- <strong>appasamārambha</strong> (written ˚rabbha) connected with little (or no) injury (to life) DN.i.143. Cp. ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</li></ol><p>saṃ + ārambha</p><p>See also: appasamārambha</p>

samāropana|samaaropana|samaropana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaropana</span></p><p>one of the Hāras Ne.1, Ne.2, Ne.4, Ne.108, Ne.205 sq., Ne.256 sq.</p><p>fr. samāropeti</p>

samāruhati|samaaruhati|samaruhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaruhati</span></p><p>to climb up, to ascend, enter; pres. <em>samārohati</em> Ja.vi.209 (cp. samorohatī p. 206, read samārohatī); aor. <em>samārūhi</em> Mhvs.14, Mhvs.38<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81r%C5%AB%E1%B8%B7ha">samārūḷha</a></em><br />caus <strong>samāropeti</strong> to raise, cause to enter Mil.85; to put down, enter Ne.4, Ne.206.</p><p>saṃ + āruhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samārūḷha">samārūḷha</a>, samāropeti</p>

samārūḷha|samaaruu.lha|samarulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaaruu.lha</span></p><p>ascended, entered MN.i.74.</p><p>pp. of samāruhati</p>

samāsa|samaasa|samasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaasa</span></p><ol><li>compound, combination Vism.82; Snp-a.303; Kp-a.228. Cp. vyāsa.</li><li>an abridgment Mhvs.37, Mhvs.244.</li></ol><p>fr. saṃ + <em>ās</em></p>

samāsama|samaasama|samasama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaasama</span></p><p>“exactly the same” at Ud.85 (= DN.ii.135) read sama˚.</p>

samāsana|samaasana|samasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaasana</span></p><p>sitting together with, company Snp.977.</p><p>saṃ + āsana</p>

samāsati|samaasati|samasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaasati</span></p><p>to sit together, associate; Pot. 3 sg. <em>samāsetha</em> SN.i.17, SN.i.56 sq.; Ja.ii.112; Ja.v.483, Ja.v.494; Thag.4.</p><p>saṃ + āsati</p>

samāsādeti|samaasaadeti|samasadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaasaadeti</span></p><p>to obtain, get; ger. <em>samāsajja</em> Ja.iii.218.</p><p>saṃ + āsādeti</p>

samātapa|samaatapa|samatapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaatapa</span></p><p>ardour, zeal AN.iii.346.</p><p>saṃ + ātapa</p>

samāvaya|samaavaya|samavaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaavaya</span></p><p>Samāvaya = samavāya</p><p>closely united Ja.vi.475 (in verse).</p>

samāyoga|samaayoga|samayoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaayoga</span></p><p>combination, conjunction DN-a.i.95; Sdhp.45, Sdhp.469.</p><p>saṃ + āyoga</p>

samāyuta|samaayuta|samayuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaayuta</span></p><p>combined, united Mil.274.</p><p>saṃ + āyuta</p>

samāyāti|samaayaati|samayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samaayaati</span></p><p>to come together, to be united Ja.iii.38.</p><p>saṃ + āyāti</p>

samīcī|samiicii|samici
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiicii</span></p><p>DN.ii.94: see <em>sāmīcī</em>.</p>

samīhati|samiihati|samihati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiihati</span></p><p>to move, stir; to be active; to long for, strive after Snp.1064 (cp. Cnd.651); Vv.5#1; Vv-a.35; Ja.v.388<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%ABhita">samīhita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + īhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samīhita">samīhita</a></p>

samīhita|samiihita|samihita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiihita</span></p><p>endeavour, striving after, pursuit Ja.v.388.</p><p>pp. of samīhati</p>

samīpa|samiipa|samipa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiipa</span></p><p>adjective near, close (to) Snp-a.43 (bhumma-vacana), Snp-a.174, Snp-a.437; Kp-a.111; Pv-a.47 (dvāra˚ magga) (nt.) proximity DN.i.118. Cases adverbially: acc. <em>-aṃ</em> near to Pv-a.107; loc. <em>--e</em> near (with gen.) Snp-a.23, Snp-a.256; Pv-a.10, Pv-a.17, Pv-a.67, Pv-a.120.</p><ul><li><em>-ga</em> approaching Mhvs.4, Mhvs.27; Mhvs.25, Mhvs.74.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cara">cara</a></em> being near Dhs-a.193.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81rin">cārin</a></em> being near DN.i.206; DN.ii.139</li><li><em>-ṭṭha</em> standing near Mhvs.37, Mhvs.164.</li></ul><p>cp. Epic &amp; Class. Sk. samīpa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cara">cara</a>, <a href="bword://cārin">cārin</a></p>

samīpaka|samiipaka|samipaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiipaka</span></p><p>adjective being near Mhvs.33, Mhvs.52.</p><p>samīpa + ka</p>

samīra|samiira|samira
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiira</span></p><p>air, wind Dāvs iv.40.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>īr</em></p>

samīrati|samiirati|samirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiirati</span></p><p>to be moved Vin.i.185; Dhp.81; Dhp-a.ii.149<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%ABrita">samīrita</a></em> Ja.i.393.</p><p>saṃ + īrati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samīrita">samīrita</a></p>

samīrita|samiirita|samirita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiirita</span></p><p>stirred, moved Ja.i.393.</p><p>saṃ + īrita</p>

samītar|samiitar|samitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiitar</span></p><p>one who meets, assembles; pl. samītāro Ja.v.324.</p><p>= sametar</p>

samīti|samiiti|samiti
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samiiti</span></p><p>A waggon builder of Sāvatthī. In the Aṅgaṇa Sutta, Moggallāna tells Sāriputta how he once saw Samīti shaping a felloe; by his side, Paṇḍuputta was watching, wishing that Samiti might shape the felloe without crook, twist, or blemish. When Samīti did this, Paṇḍuputta sang with joy, saying that Samīti had read his thoughts. MN.i.31<em>f.</em></p>

samūha|samuuha|samuha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuuha</span></p><p>multitude, mass, aggregation Ne.195; Pv-a.49, Pv-a.127, Pv-a.157 (= gaṇa), Pv-a.200 (id.).</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>vah, uh</em></p>

samūhanati|samuuhanati|samuhanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuuhanati</span></p><p>to remove, to abolish Vin.i.110; DN.i.135 sq. (˚hanissati); DN.ii.91 = SN.v.432; MN.i.47 MN.ii.193; SN.v.76; Ja.i.374 = Snp.360; Snp.14, Snp.369, Snp.1076 sikkhāpadaṃ Vin.iii.23; DN.ii.154; uposathāgāraṃ to discontinue using a Vihāra as an Uposathāgāra Vin.i.107; sīmaṃ to remove the boundary Vin.i.110.<br />pres also <em>samūhanti</em> SN.iii.156;<br />pot <em>samūhaneyya</em> Vin.i.110<br />imper <em>samūhantu</em> DN.ii.154; &amp; ˚ūhanatu Mil.143;<br />ger <em>samūhanitvā</em> MN.i.47; Vin.i.107; a˚ MN.iii.285;<br />inf <em>samugghātuṃ</em> Mhvs.37, Mhvs.32;<br />grd <em>samūhantabba</em> Vin.i.107<br />caus 2 <em>samugghātāpeti</em> to cause to be removed, i.e. to put to death Mil.193; <em>samūhanāpeti</em> Mil.142.<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sam%C5%ABhata">samūhata</a></em> &amp; (Caus.); <em><a href="bword://samuggh%C4%81tita">samugghātita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samūhata">samūhata</a>, <a href="bword://samugghātita">samugghātita</a></p>

samūhata|samuuhata|samuhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuuhata</span></p><p>taken out, removed DN.i.136; SN.iii.131; Thag.604; Dhp.250; Snp.14, Snp.360; Iti.83; Ja.iv.345 (Kern, wrongly, “combined”).</p><p>pp. of samūhanati</p>

samūhatatta|samuuhatatta|samuhatatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuuhatatta</span></p><p>abolition MN.iii.151.</p><p>abstr. fr. samūhata</p>

samūheti|samuuheti|samuheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuuheti</span></p><p>to gather, collect Mhvs.37, Mhvs.245.</p><p>Caus. of saṃ + <em>uh = vah</em></p>

samūlaka|samuulaka|samulaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuulaka</span></p><p>adjective including the root Thig.385; Thag-a.256.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + mūla + ka</p>

samūpasanta|samuupasanta|samupasanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">samuupasanta</span></p><p>is variant reading for su-vūpasanta (?) “calmed,” at Kp-a.21.</p><p>saṃ + upasanta</p>

san
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>San<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a dog; nom. sg <strong>sā</strong> DN.i.166 = MN.i.77; SN.i.176; SN.iii.150; Kv.336. For other forms of the same base see <em><a href="bword://suv%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">suvāṇa</a></em>.</p><p>cp. Vedic śvā, gen. śunaḥ; Av. spā, Gr. κύων; Lat. canis, Oir. cū, Goth. hunds = hound</p><p>San<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(= saṃ) acc. of <em>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>.</p><p>See also: sā, <a href="bword://suvāṇa">suvāṇa</a></p>

sanacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dancing (-party) Vin.ii.267.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + nacca</p>

sanantana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective primeval, of old; for ever, eternal DN.ii.240, DN.ii.244; SN.i.189 (cp. <em>K.S.</em> i.321: porāṇaka santānaṃ vā paṇḍitānaṃ dhamma); Dhp-a.i.51.</p><p>for sanātana (cp. purātana); Idg. *seno = Gr. ε ̔́νος old; Sk. sanaḥ in old times; Av. hana old, Lat. seneo, senex (“senile”), senatus; Goth sineigs old; Oir. sen old</p>

sanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%87ati">saṇati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://saṇati">saṇati</a></p>

sanaṅkumāra|sana"nkumaara|sanankumara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sana"nkumaara</span></p><p>A Mahā Brahmā, whose name means “ever young”. In the Nikāyas DN.i.121 MN.i.358 SN.i.153 AN.v.327 he is mentioned as the author of a famous verse, which is eleswhere attribued to the Buddha. SN.ii.284 Sanaṅkumāra is represented as a very devout follower of the Buddha.</p><p>In a sutta of the Saṃyutta, SN.i.153 he is spoken of as visiting the Buddha on the banks of the Sappinī, and it was during this visit that the above verse was spoken. Sanaṅkumāra was present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.261</p><p>In the Janavasabha Sutta, Janavasabha describes to the Buddha an occasion on which Sanaṅkumāra attended an assembly of the Devas, presided over by Sakka and the Four Regent Gods. There was suddenly a vast radiance, and the devas knew of the approach of Sanaṅkumāra. As the usual appearance of the Brahmā is not sufficiently materialized for him to be perceived by the Devas of Tāvatiṃsā, he is forced to appear as a relatively gross personality which he specially creates. As he arrives, the Devas sit in their places with clasped hands waiting for him to choose his seat. Then Sanaṅkumāra takes on the form of Pañcasikha and sits, above the assembly, cross legged, in the air. So seated, he expresses his satisfaction that Sakka and all the Tāvatiṃsā Devas should honour and follow the Buddha. His voice has all the eight characteristics of a Brahmā’s voice. DN.ii.211 He then proceeds to create thirty three shaper, of himself, each sitting on the divan of a Tāvatiṃsā Deva, and addresses the Devas, speaking of the advantages of taking refuge in the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Saṅgha. Each deva fancies that only the shape sitting on his own divan has spoken and that the others are silent. Then Sanaṅkumāra goes to the end of the Hall, and, seated on Sakka’s throne, addresses the whole assembly on the four ways of <em>iddhi</em>; on the three avenues leading to Bliss, as manifested by the Buddha; on the four <em>satipaṭṭhānas</em>, and the seven <em>samādhiparikkhārā</em>. He declares that more than twenty four lakhs of Māgadha disciples, having followed the teachings of the Buddha, have been born in the deva worlds. When Sanaṅkumāra has finished his address, Vessavaṇa wonders if there have been Buddhas in the past and will be in the future. The Brahmā reads his thoughts and says there certainly were and will be.</p>

sanda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Sanda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>thick, dense; in <em>-cchāya</em> giving dense shade SN.iv.194 Ja.i.57 Ja.i.249 DN-a.i.209</li><li>(thick) wood, forest; in <em>-vihāra</em> dwelling in the wood, life as a hermit Thag.688.</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. sāndra</p>

sandahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>applying, placing (an arrow) on the string Mil.352.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>dhā</em></p>

sandahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to put together, to connect, to fit, to arrange Ja.iv.336; Mhvs.vii.18; ppr. med <em>sandahamāna</em> Dhs-a.113; ger. <strong>sandahitvā</strong> Ja.iv.336 &amp; <em><a href="bword://sandh%C4%81ya">sandhāya</a></em> lit. after putting on Ja.iv.258 (the arrow on to the bow); fig. with reference to, concerning MN.i.503; Ja.i.203, Ja.i.274; Ja.ii.177; Pv-a.87, Pv-a.89, Pv-a.110; towards Ja.i.491; Ja.iii.295. pp. <em>sandhīyate [&amp; sandhiyyate];</em> to be put together, to be self-contained Pp.32; to be connected Snp-a.376, Snp-a.572; to reflect upon, to resent Snp.366; to be reconciled Ja.ii.114<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83hita">saṃhita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + dahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: sandahitvā, <a href="bword://sandhāya">sandhāya</a>, <a href="bword://saṃhita">saṃhita</a></p>

sandaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Paribbājaka.</p>

sandambhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>is Kern’s proposed reading for <strong>santhambhita</strong> at Ja.vi.207.</p><p>fr. Sk. sandarbhati</p><p>See also: santhambhita</p>

sandamānikā|sandamaanikaa|sandamanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandamaanikaa</span></p><p>chariot Vin.iii.49; Vin.iv.339; DN-a.i.82; Kp-a.50; Vism.255.</p><p>fr. <em>syand</em></p>

sandana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Sandana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter trappings DN.ii.188 (read sandāna?).</p><p>Sandana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>a chariot Mhvs.21, Mhvs.25; Dpvs.14, 56; Vv.642; Ja.iv.103; Ja.v.264; Ja.vi.22.</p><p>cp. Vedic syandana</p>

sandassaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>instructing MN.i.145; AN.ii.97; AN.iv.296; SN.v.162; Iti.107; Mil.373.</p><p>fr. sandassati, Caus. of sandissati</p>

sandassana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>showing Ja.i.67.</p>

sandati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to flow DN.ii.128, DN.ii.129 (aor. sandittha); Ja.i.18; Ja.vi.534 (variant reading sikandati siyandati?); Pv.ii.10#4 (= pavatteti Pv-a.143)<br />caus <em>sandāpeti</em> to cause to flow Mil.122<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sanna">sanna</a></em>. Cp. <em>vissandati &amp; vissandaka</em>.</p><p><em>syand</em>; Dhtp.149: passavane</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sanna">sanna</a></p>

sandeha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>accumulation; the human body Dhp.148.</li><li>doubt Mil.295.</li></ol><p>saṃ + deha</p>

sandesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>news, message Mhvs.18, Mhvs.13.</p><p>Sk. sandeśa</p>

sandha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>SandhaSaddha</p><p>A monk who visited the Buddha at Ñātikā in the Giñjakāvasatha, when the Buddha preached to him the Sandha Sutta. AN.v.323<em>f.</em></p><p>It is, perhaps, the same monk who is mentioned as Saddho Kaccāyano. SN.ii.153 He asks the Buddha a question on elements, and the Buddha explains it to him.</p>

sandhamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to blow, to fan Ja.i.122. - pp. <em><a href="bword://sandhanta">sandhanta</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + dhamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sandhanta">sandhanta</a></p>

sandhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>property, belongings MN.ii.180.</p><p>saṃ + dhana</p>

sandhanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>blown, smelted (of gold) AN.i.253.</p><p>pp. of sandhamati</p>

sandhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine</i></p><ol><li>union, junction Mil.330 (of 2 roads); Bdhgh on SN.ii.270 (between 2 houses).</li><li>breach, break, hole, chasm DN.ii.83 = AN.v.195; Thag.786; Ja.v.459. <em>āloka˚</em>; a window Vin.ii.172; <em>sandhiṃ chindati</em> to make a break, to break into a house DN.i.52; DN-a.i.159.</li><li>joint, piece, link Ja.ii.88; Vism.277 (the 5, of kammaṭṭhāna); Mhvs.33, Mhvs.11; Mhvs.34, Mhvs.47; applied to the joints of the body Vism.185 (the 14 mahā˚); Dhs-a.324.</li><li>connection, combination Vb-a.191 (hetuphala˚ &amp; phalahetu˚ etc.).</li><li>euphonic junction euphony, “sandhi” Snp-a.76. See pada˚.</li><li>agreement Mhvs.9, Mhvs.16.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://cheda">cheda</a></em> 1 housebreaking Ja.i.187 sq.; Ja.ii.388 2 one who has brought rebirths (= paṭisandhi) to an end Dhp.97; Dhp-a.ii.187; Dhp-a.iii.257.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://chedaka">chedaka</a></em> one who can cut a break, an underminer Ja.vi.458.</li><li><em>-bheda(ka)</em> causing discord Ja.iii.151.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> opening of a break (made by burglars) into a house Thag.786; Pv-a.4.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://samala">samala</a></em> (-sankaṭīva) refuse heap of a house-sewer (cp. <em>K.S.</em> ii.181 203) DN.ii.160; MN.i.334 = SN.ii.270.</li></ul><p>fr. saṃ + ; <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cheda">cheda</a>, <a href="bword://chedaka">chedaka</a>, <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a>, <a href="bword://samala">samala</a></p>

sandhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) , in <em>pañca˚</em>; having 5 links or pieces Vism.277.</p>

sandhita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse speaks of attaining a perception of the Buddha under a fig tree. Thag.217–218</p>

sandhovati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to clean AN.i.253.</p><p>saṃ + dhovati</p>

sandhovika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>washing; <em>kaṇṇa-sandhovikā khiḍḍā</em> ear-washing sport or gambol (of elephants, with <em>piṭṭhi˚</em>; etc.) AN.v.202. So probably for <em>saṇadhovika</em> at MN.i.229, MN.i.375. Cp. <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81%E1%B9%87adhovana">sāṇadhovana</a></em> (?).</p><p>fr. sandhovati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sāṇadhovana">sāṇadhovana</a></p>

sandhunāti|sandhunaati|sandhunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhunaati</span></p><p>to shake DN.ii.336.</p><p>saṃ + dhunāti</p>

sandhāna|sandhaana|sandhana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaana</span></p><p>A householder of Rājagaha. He was a follower of the Buddha, and it was his conversation with the Paribbājaka Nigrodha that led to the preaching of the Udumbarika Sīhanāda Sutta.</p><p>In the Aṅguttara he is mentioned in a list of eminent lay disciples. AN.iii.451</p>

sandhāna|sandhaana|sandhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaana</span></p><ol><li>uniting, conciliation, friendship DN-a.i.74; Dhs-a.113.</li><li>bond, fetter Ud.77 (read <em>sandāna?</em>).</li></ol><p>fr. saṃ + <em>dhā</em></p>

sandhāpana|sandhaapana|sandhapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaapana</span></p><p>combination Vv-a.349.</p><p>fr. sandhāpeti, Caus. of sandahati</p>

sandhāraka|sandhaaraka|sandharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaaraka</span></p><p>adjective checking, restraining Vism.205.</p><p>fr. sandhāreti</p>

sandhārana|sandhaarana|sandharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaarana</span></p><p>checking Mil.352.</p><p>fr. sandhāreti</p>

sandhāreti|sandhaareti|sandhareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaareti</span></p><ol><li>to hold, bear, carry Ja.iii.184.</li><li>to hold up, support Ja.iv.167.</li><li>to curb restrain, check Vin.ii.212; Ja.ii.26, Ja.ii.59<br />■ <em>dussandhāriya</em> difficult to keep back Ja.iii.340.</li></ol><p>saṃ + dhāreti</p>

sandhātar|sandhaatar|sandhatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaatar</span></p><p>one who puts together, a conciliator DN.i.4; DN.iii.171; MN.i.345; AN.ii.209; Pp.57.</p><p>saṃ + dhātar</p>

sandhāvati|sandhaavati|sandhavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaavati</span></p><p>to run through, to transmigrate DN.i.14; AN.ii.1; SN.iii.149; Ja.i.503; aor. <em>sandhāvissaṃ</em> Dhp.153 = Ja.i.76 (= apar’ âparaṃ anuvicariṃ Dhp-a.iii.128).</p><p>saṃ + dhāvati</p>

sandhāya|sandhaaya|sandhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhaaya</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://sandahati">sandahati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sandahati">sandahati</a></p>

sandhīyati|sandhiiyati|sandhiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhiiyati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://sandahati">sandahati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sandahati">sandahati</a></p>

sandhūpeti|sandhuupeti|sandhupeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandhuupeti</span></p><p>to fumigate SN.iii.89; Pts.ii.167. As <strong>sandhūpāyati</strong> to cause thick smoke or steam thickly, at Vin.i.225; Snp.p.15 (= samantā dhūpāyati Snp-a.154).</p><p>saṃ + dhūpeti</p><p>See also: sandhūpāyati</p>

sandiddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>smeared, indistinct, husky Vin.ii.202; DN-a.i.282.</p><p>saṃ + diddha</p>

sandissati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be seen together with, to be engaged in, or to tally, agree with, to live conformably to (loc., e.g. dhamme) DN.i.102; DN.ii.75; SN.v.177; Snp.50; DN.ii.127; Ne.23; ppr. a-saṃdissamāna invisible Dāvs iv.30; Caus. <em>saṃdasseti</em> to teach, instruct DN.i.126; DN.ii.95; Vin.i.18; to compare, verify, DN.ii.124; ppr. sandassiyamāna DN.ii.124; Ja.vi.217 (sunakhesu sandissanti, i.e. they are of no more value).</p><p>saṃ + dissati</p>

sandita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>bound, tied, Thag.290 (diṭṭhi-sandāna˚).</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>dā</em>: see <em><a href="bword://sand%C4%81na">sandāna</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sandāna">sandāna</a></p>

sandiyyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">&amp;</i></p><p><em>sandīyati</em> to be vexed, to resent SN.ii.200 sq.; Ja.vi.570 (spelt wrongly sandhīyati C. explains as “manku hoti”).</p><p>saṃ + diyyati(= dīyati) = Sk. dīyate of dyati, i.e. <em>dā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to cut: see <em><a href="bword://d%C4%81tta">dātta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dātta">dātta</a></p>

sandiṭṭha|sandi.t.tha|sandittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandi.t.tha</span></p><p>seen together, a friend Ja.i.106, Ja.i.442; Vin.iii.42; yathāsandiṭṭhaṃ, where one’s friends live DN.ii.98; SN.v.152.</p><p>pp. of sandissati</p>

sandiṭṭhi|sandi.t.thi|sanditthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandi.t.thi</span></p><p>the visible world, worldly gain DN.iii.45, DN.iii.247; MN.i.43; Snp.891; Vin.ii.89; Mnd.288, Mnd.300 <em>-parāmāsin</em> infected with worldliness MN.i.97.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>dṛś</em></p>

sandiṭṭhika|sandi.t.thika|sanditthika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandi.t.thika</span></p><p>visible; belonging to, of advantage to, this life, actual DN.i.51 DN.ii.93, DN.ii.217; DN.iii.5; MN.i.85, MN.i.474; AN.i.156 sq.; AN.ii.56, AN.ii.198; SN.i.9, SN.i.117, SN.iv.41, SN.iv.339; Snp.567, Snp.1137; Vism.215 sq<br />■ As <em>sandiṭṭhiyā</em> (f.) at Ja.vi.213</p><p>cp. BSk. sandṛṣṭika Divy.426</p>

sandosa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>pollution, defilement MN.i.17; AN.iii.106, AN.iii.358; AN.v.292; Snp.327.</p><p>saṃ + dosa</p>

sandāleti|sandaaleti|sandaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandaaleti</span></p><p>to break; ger. <em>sandālayitvāna</em> Snp.62.</p><p>saṃ + dāleti</p>

sandāna|sandaana|sandana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandaana</span></p><p>, a cord, tether, fetter DN.ii.274; Thag.290; Dhp.398; Snp.622; Ja.ii.32; Ud.77 (text sandhāna); Dhp-a.iv.161.</p><p>saṃ + dāna, fr. <em>dā</em> to bind: see <em><a href="bword://d%C4%81ma">dāma</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāma">dāma</a></p>

sandīpeti|sandiipeti|sandipeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sandiipeti</span></p><p>to kindle Ja.v.32.</p><p>saṃ + dīpeti</p>

sangharakkhita 
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Sangharakkhita</p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse speaks of one who, although in seclusion, still has uncontrolled faculties. Thag.109</p>

sanidassana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective visible DN.iii.217; Dhs.1087.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + nidassana</p>

sankassa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>During the Vajjiputta controversy, Revata Thera, on his way from Soreyya to Sahājāti, went through Sankassa.</p><p>26.21347,79.86595town</p>

sanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Sanna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>sunk Dhp.327.</p><p>pp. of sīdati</p><p>Sanna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>flown Ja.vi.203 (dadhi˚).</p><p>pp. of sandati</p>

sannaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>fastened, bound, DN.ii.350 (susannaddha); Mil.339.</li><li>put on, clothed (with Pv.iv.1#36 (˚dussa).</li><li>armed, accoutred SN.ii.284; Ja.i.179; Dhp.387; Dhp-a.iv.144; Pv-a.154 (˚dhanu-kalāpa).</li></ol><p>pp. of sannayhati</p>

sannakaddu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the tree Buchanania latifolia Abḥp 556.</p><p>lexicogr. Sk. sannakadru</p>

sannata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>bent down, low Ja.vi.58 (opp. unnata).</li><li>bent, prepared Ja.v.215 (C. suphassita).</li></ol><p>pp. of saṃ + <em>nam</em>, cp. sannāmeti</p>

sannayhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tie, bind, fasten, to arm oneself Ja.i.129; to array, arm DN.ii.175; Vin.i.342; to arrange, fit DN.i.96; Ja.i.273; aor. <strong>sannayhi</strong> DN.i.96; inf sannayhituṃ Ja.i.179; ger. <strong>sannayhitvā</strong> DN.ii.175; Ja.ii.77; &amp; <em>sannahitvā</em> Ja.i.273.</p><p>saṃ + nayhati</p><p>See also: sannayhi, sannayhitvā</p>

sanneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to mix, knead DN.i.74 (pot. sanneyya); DN.iii.29; Vin.i.47 (grd. ˚netabba); MN.i.276; SN.ii.58 sq.; Ja.vi.432<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sann%C4%ABta">sannīta</a></em>.</p><p>fr. saṃ + neti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sannīta">sannīta</a></p>

sannibha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective resembling DN.ii.17; Snp.551; Ja.i.319.</p><p>saṃ + nibha</p>

sannicaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>accumulation, hoarding AN.i.94; AN.ii.23; Dhp.92; Vin.ii.95; Vin.iv.243; Dhp-a.ii.171; AN.iv.108 Kp-a.62 (lohita).</p><p>saṃ + nicaya</p>

sannicita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>accumulated, hoarded Mil.120.</p><p>saṃ + nicita</p>

sannidhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>putting together, storing up DN.i.6; Snp.306, Snp.924; Mnd.372; <em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> storing DN.i.6; <em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81raka">kāraka</a></em> storing up, store MN.i.523; Vin.i.209; Vin.iv.87; DN.iii.235; AN.iii.109; AN.iv.370. <em>-<a href="bword://kata">kata</a></em> stored up Vin.ii.270; put by postponed Vin.i.254.</p><p>saṃ + nidhi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a>, <a href="bword://kāraka">kāraka</a>, <a href="bword://kata">kata</a></p>

sannidhāna|sannidhaana|sannidhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannidhaana</span></p><p>[saṃ + nidhāna) lit. “putting down together,” proximity Dāvs v.39.</p>

sanniggaṇhāti|sannigga.nhaati|sannigganhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannigga.nhaati</span></p><p>to restrain SN.i.238.</p><p>saṃ + niggaṇhāti</p>

sannighāta|sannighaata|sannighata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannighaata</span></p><p>concussion, knocking against each other Dhs.621.</p><p>saṃ + nighāta</p>

sannihita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>put down, placed Mil.326.</li><li>stored up Thig.409; Thag-a.267.</li></ol><p>saṃ + nihita; cp. sannidhi</p>

sannikkhepana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>elimination Vb-a.355.</p><p>saṃ + nikkhepana</p>

sannikāsa|sannikaasa|sannikasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannikaasa</span></p><p>adjective resembling, looking like Ja.iii.522; Ja.v.87 = Ja.vi.306; Ja.v.169 (C. dassana); Ja.vi.240, Ja.vi.279.</p><p>saṃ + nikāsa</p>

sannipatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to assemble, come together Ja.i.167; pp. <em>-ita</em>. Caus. <strong>sannipāteti</strong> to bring together convoke DN.ii.76; Mil.6; Caus. ii. <em>sannipātāpeti</em> to cause to be convoked or called together Ja.i.58, Ja.i.153, Ja.i.271 Ja.iii.376; Vin.i.44; Vin.iii.71.</p><p>saṃ + nipatati</p><p>See also: sannipāteti</p>

sannipatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>come together DN.i.2; DN.ii.76.</p><p>pp. of ˚nipatati</p>

sannipāta|sannipaata|sannipata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannipaata</span></p><ol><li>union, coincidence SN.iv.68 sq.; Mil.60, Mil.123 sq.; Ne.28.</li><li>assemblage assembly, congregation DN.ii.5; Mil.7.</li><li>union of the humours of the body Mil.303.</li><li>collocation Dhp.352.</li></ol><p>fr. sannipatati</p>

sannipātika|sannipaatika|sannipatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannipaatika</span></p><p>adjective resulting from the union of the humours of the body AN.ii.87; AN.v.110; SN.iv.230; Mil.135, Mil.137, Mil.302, Mil.304.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sannirata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective being (quite) happy together Ja.v.405. Sannirumbhati (rundhati)</p><p>saṃ + nirata</p>

sannirumbhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Sannirumbhati (˚rundhati)</p><p>to restrain, block, impede; ger. <em>sannirumhitvā</em> Ja.i.109, Ja.i.164 Ja.ii.6; Vv-a.217. <em>sannirumbhitvā</em> Ja.i.62; Ja.ii.341. <em>sannirujjhitvā</em> Vism.143; Pot. <em>sannirundheyya</em> MN.i.115. pp. <em>sanniruddha</em> Vism.278.</p><p>saṃ + nirumbhati</p>

sannirumhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>restraining, checking, suppression Ja.i.163; DN-a.i.193; as <em>-bhana</em> at Vb-a.355.</p><p>fr. last</p>

sannirundhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Sannirumbhati (˚rundhati)</p><p>to restrain, block, impede; ger. <em>sannirumhitvā</em> Ja.i.109, Ja.i.164 Ja.ii.6; Vv-a.217. <em>sannirumbhitvā</em> Ja.i.62; Ja.ii.341. <em>sannirujjhitvā</em> Vism.143; Pot. <em>sannirundheyya</em> MN.i.115. pp. <em>sanniruddha</em> Vism.278.</p><p>saṃ + nirumbhati</p>

sannisajjā|sannisajjaa|sannisajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannisajjaa</span></p><p>meeting-place Vin.i.188; Vin.ii.174 = Vin.iii.66; sannisajja-ṭṭhāna (n.) the same Vin.iii.287.</p><p>saṃ + nissajjā</p>

sannisinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>sitting down together DN.i.2; DN.ii.109; Vin.ii.296; Ja.i.120.</li><li>(having become settled, established Vin.ii.278 (˚gabbhā pregnant).</li></ol><p>pp. of sannisīdati</p>

sannissayatā|sannissayataa|sannissayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannissayataa</span></p><p>dependency, connection Ne.80.</p><p>saṃ + nissayatā</p>

sannissita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>based on, connected with, attached to Vism.43, Vism.118, Vism.120, Vism.554 (viññāṇa is “hadaya-vatthu˚”; cp. Vb-a.163).</p><p>saṃ + nissita, cp. BSk. sanniśrita</p>

sannisīdati|sannisiidati|sannisidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannisiidati</span></p><ol><li>(lit.) to sink down, to settle Mil.35.</li><li>(fig.) to subside, to become quiet MN.i.121; SN.iv.196; AN.ii.157</li></ol><p>caus <em>sannisādeti</em> to make quiet to calm MN.i.116; AN.ii.94<br />caus 2 <em>sannisīdāpeti</em> to cause to halt Ja.iv.258<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sannisinna">sannisinna</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + nisīdati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sannisinna">sannisinna</a></p>

sannitodaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>“pricking,” instigating, jeering DN.i.189; AN.i.187; SN.ii.282.</p><p>fr. saṃ + ni + <em>tud</em></p>

sannitāḷeti|sannitaa.leti|sannitaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannitaa.leti</span></p><p>to strike Ja.v.71.</p><p>saṃ + nitāḷeti</p>

sannivasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to live together, to associate AN.i.78; pp. <em><a href="bword://sannivuttha">sannivuttha</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + nivasati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sannivuttha">sannivuttha</a></p>

sannivaṭṭa|sanniva.t.ta|sannivatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanniva.t.ta</span></p><p>returning, return Vin.i.139 sq.</p><p>= saṃ + nivatta</p>

sannivesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>preparation, encampment, settlement Thag-a.257.</p><p>saṃ + nivesa</p>

sannivesana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>position, settlement; pāṭiekka-˚ private, separate Ja.i.92.</p><p>saṃ + nivesana</p>

sannivuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>living together (with), associating AN.iv.303 sq.</p><p>pp. of sannivasati</p>

sannivāreti|sannivaareti|sannivareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannivaareti</span></p><p>to restrain, check; to keep together MN.i.115; Thig.366.</p><p>saṃ + nivāreti</p>

sannivāsa|sannivaasa|sannivasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannivaasa</span></p><p>association, living with; community AN.i.78; AN.ii.57; DN.iii.271; Dhp.206; Ja.iv.403 <em>loka-sannivāsa</em> the society of men, all the world Ja.i.366 Ja.ii.205.</p><p>saṃ + nivāsa</p>

sanniyojeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to appoint, command Mhvs.5, Mhvs.34.</p><p>saṃ + niyojeti</p>

sanniyyātana|sanniyyaatana|sanniyyatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanniyyaatana</span></p><p>handing over, resignation DN-a.i.232.</p><p>saṃ + niyyātana</p>

sanniṭṭhāna|sanni.t.thaana|sannitthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanni.t.thaana</span></p><ol><li>conclusion, consummation, Ja.ii.166.</li><li>resolve Ja.i.19; Ja.i.69; Ja.i.187; Ja.iv.167; Vin.i.255 sq.</li><li>ascertainment, definite conclusion conviction, Ja.vi.324; Vism.43.</li></ol><p>saṃ + niṭṭhāna</p>

sannāha|sannaaha|sannaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannaaha</span></p><ol><li>dressing, fastening together Pv-a.231.</li><li>armour, mail SN.v.6; Ja.ii.443; Th. 1, 543; Ja.i.179.</li></ol><p>fr. sannay ati</p>

sannāmeti|sannaameti|sannameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sannaameti</span></p><p>to bend MN.i.365, MN.i.439, MN.i.450, MN.i.507 = SN.iv.188 (kāyaṃ sannāmeyya-i.e. to writhe) Cp. <em>Cpd.</em> 162 n. 5 (“strengthen”?).</p><p>Caus. of saṃ + <em>nam</em></p>

sannīta|sanniita|sannita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanniita</span></p><p>mixed, put together, kneaded Mhvs.29, Mhvs.11 &amp; Mhvs.12.</p><p>pp. fr. sanneti</p>

sant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>being, existing DN.i.61, DN.i.152; AN.i.176; Iti.62 sq.; Snp.98, Snp.124.</li><li>good, true SN.i.17; Dhp.151.</li></ol><h3>Cases</h3><p>■ nom. sg. m. <em>santo</em> Snp.98; Mil.32; Cnd.635 (= samāna); f. satī (q.v.); nt. <em>santaṃ</em> AN.v.8; Pv-a.192<br />■ acc. <em>santaṃ</em> DN.ii.65; &amp; <em>sataṃ</em> Ja.iv.435 (opp. asaṃ);<br />■ instr <em>satā</em> DN.ii.55;<br />■ loc. <em><a href="bword://sati">sati</a></em> DN.ii.32; AN.i.176; AN.iii.338; Snp.81; Dhp.146; Iti.85; &amp; <em>sante</em> DN.i.61;<br />■ abl. <em>santato</em> Ne.88; Dhs-a.206 sq<br />■ pl. nom. <em>santo</em> MN.i.24; SN.i.71; Snp.450; Iti.62; Dhp.151; nt. <em>santāni</em> DN.i.152;<br />■ acc. <em>sante</em> Snp.94 Snp.665;<br />■ gen. <em>sataṃ</em> MN.i.24; SN.i.17; Snp.227;<br />■ instr. <strong>sabbhi</strong> DN.ii.246; SN.i.17, SN.i.56; Mil.221 = Ja.v.49; Dhp.151;<br />■ loc <em>santesu</em><br />■ Compar. <em><a href="bword://santatara">santatara</a></em> Iti.62;<br />■ superl. <em><a href="bword://sattama">sattama</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ppr. of atthi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sati">sati</a>, sabbhi, <a href="bword://santatara">santatara</a>, <a href="bword://sattama">sattama</a></p>

santa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atthi">atthi</a></p>

santa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Santa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>calmed, tranquil, peaceful, pure DN.i.12; Vin.i.4; SN.i.5; AN.ii.18; Snp.746; Pv.iv.1#34 (= upasanta-kilesa Pv-a.230); Mil.232, Mil.409; Vism.155 (˚anga; opp. oḷārik’anga); Dhp-a.ii.13; Dhp-a.iii.83<br />■ nt peace, bliss, nibbāna SN.iv.370.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a></em> one whose senses are tranquil AN.ii.38; Snp.144; Vin.i.195; Ja.i.506;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ya">kāya</a></em> of calmed body Dhp.378; Dhp-a.iv.114.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> peaceful condition, quietude Ja.i.506;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> id. Mil.265.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://m%C4%81nasa">mānasa</a></em> of tranquil mind Vin.i.195; Ja.i.506.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81sa">vāsa</a></em> peaceful state Dhp-a.iv.114</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vutti">vutti</a></em> living a peaceful life Iti.30, Iti.121.</li></ul><p>pp. of sammati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Santa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>tired, wearied, exhausted Dhp.60; Ja.i.498; Pv.ii.9#36 (= parissama-patta Pv-a.127).</p><p>pp. of sammati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://indriya">indriya</a>, <a href="bword://kāya">kāya</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://mānasa">mānasa</a>, <a href="bword://vāsa">vāsa</a>, <a href="bword://vutti">vutti</a></p>

santacitta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha. MN.iii.70</p>

santacā|santacaa|santaca
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santacaa</span></p><p>bark Ja.v.202 <em>(sattacaṃ?)</em>.</p><p>?</p>

santajjeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to frighten, scold, menace Ja.i.479; Ja.v.94; Thag-a.65; Pv-a.123, Pv-a.195.</p><p>saṃ + tajjeti</p>

santaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Santaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>belonging to Ja.i.122; nt. property Ja.i.91, Ja.i.494; Dhp-a.i.346.</li><li>due to (gen.) Ja.iii.408; Ja.iv.37.</li><li>(being in the power of Ja.iv.260 <em>(bhaya˚)</em>.</li></ol><p>fr. sant; cp. BSk. santaka Divy.280 etc.</p><p>Santaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective limited (opp. anantika) SN.v.272.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + antaka</p>

santaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Santaneti &amp; ˚tāneti</p><p>to continue AN.iii.96 sq.; SN.iv.104; Pp.66 sq.; Snp-a.5 (see <em><a href="bword://sant%C4%81yati">santāyati</a></em>).</p><p>Caus. of saṃ + <em>tan</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santāyati">santāyati</a></p>

santappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be heated or chafed; fig. to grieve, sorrow MN.i.188; Ja.iii.153<br />pp <em>santatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> Caus. <em>-tāpeti</em> to burn, scorch, torment MN.i.128; SN.iv.56 sq<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sant%C4%81pita">santāpita</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santāpita">santāpita</a></p>

santappeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to satisfy, please DN.i.109; Vin.i.18; Ja.i.50, Ja.i.272<br />pp <em><a href="bword://santappita">santappita</a></em>.</p><p>Caus. of saṃ + tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santappita">santappita</a></p>

santappita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>satisfied, pleased Ja.ii.44; Pv.ii.8#11 (= pīṇita Pv-a.110).</p><p>pp. of santappeti</p>

santara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective inside; in compound <em>-uttara</em> inner &amp; outer Vin.iii.214; Vin.iv.281; <em>-uttarena</em> with an inner &amp; outer garment Vin.i.298; Thag-a.171 <em>-bāhira</em> within &amp; without DN.i.74; Dhp.315; Ja.i.125; DN-a.i.218; Dhp-a.iii.488.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + antara, cp. E. with-in</p>

santarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be in haste, to be agitated; ppr. <em>-amāna</em> (˚rūpa) Ja.iii.156, Ja.iii.172; Ja.vi.12, Ja.vi.451.</p><p>saṃ + tarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

santasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be frightened or terrified, to fear, to be disturbed Mil.92. ppr. <em>santasaṃ</em> Ja.vi.306 (a˚) &amp; <em>santasanto</em> Ja.iv.101 (a˚); Pot. <em>santase</em> Ja.iii.147 Ja.v.378; ger. <strong>santasitvā</strong> Ja.ii.398<br />pp <em>santasita santatta</em>.</p><p>saṃ + tasati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: santasitvā</p>

santasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>frightened Mil.92; Pv-a.260 (= suṭṭhu tasita).</p><p>pp. of santasati</p>

santatara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://sant">sant</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sant">sant</a></p>

santataṃ|santata.m|santatam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santata.m</span></p><p>adverb continually, only in compounds: <em>-kārin</em> consistent AN.ii.187; <em>-vutti</em> of consistent behaviour AN.ii.187; MN.i.339; <em>-sīla</em> steady in character MN.i.339.</p><p>satataṃ, or fr. saṃ + <em>tan</em></p>

santati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><ol><li>continuity, duration, subsistence Dhs.643; Ne.79; Mil.72, Mil.185 Vb-a.8, Vb-a.170, Vb-a.173; Vv-a.25; Vism.431, Vism.449. <em>citta˚</em> continuity of consciousness Kv.458; cp. <em>Cpd.</em> 6, 153<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 252 sq.; <em>dhamma˚</em>; continuity of states Mil.40; <em>rūpa˚</em> of form Vb-a.21; <em>saṅkhāra˚</em>; causal connection of material things Thag.716.</li><li>lineage Mil.160.</li></ol><p>fr. saṃ + <em>tan</em>, lit. stretch</p>

santatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Santatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>heated, glowing DN.ii.335; MN.i.453; SN.i.169 (divasa˚); Ja.iv.118; Mil.325; Pv-a.38 (soka˚).</p><p>pp. of santappati</p><p>Santatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>frightened, disturbed Ja.iii.77 (= santrasta C.).</p><p>pp. of santasati</p>

santavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective tranquil Dhp.378.</p><p>fr. santa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

santevāsika|santevaasika|santevasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santevaasika</span></p><p>with resident pupils, i.e. with internal impurities</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sāntevāsika">sāntevāsika</a>, <a href="bword://antevāsika">antevāsika</a></p>

santha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>See <em>Naḷa</em>.</p><p>See also: Naḷa</p>

santhamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.i.122 is to be read <em>saṇdhamati</em> “to blow.”</p>

santhambhanā|santhambhanaa|santhambhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhambhanaa</span></p><p><em>-thambhitatta</em> (nt.) stiffening, stiffness, rigidity Dhs.636; Dhs-a.324; Ja.i.10 (a-santhambhana-bhāva).</p><p>abstr. fr. santhambhati</p>

santhambhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to restrain oneself, to keep firm Snp.701 (imper. med. 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. ˚thambhassu) Pp.65; Ja.i.255; Ja.iii.95<br />caus <em>-thambheti</em> to make stiff or rigid, to numb Ja.i.10.</p><p>saṃ + thambhati</p>

santhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>appeasing Dhp.275.</li><li>satisfaction Vv.18#6.</li></ol><p>fr. <em>śam</em>, cp. Sk. śāntvana</p>

santhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>couch or mat Vin.ii.162; AN.i.277; Tha-ap.97 (tiṇa˚).</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>str</em></p>

santharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= santhara; only as <em>tiṇa˚</em>; made of grass Vin.i.24; MN.i.501; Ja.i.360; Vv-a.262.</p>

santharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to spread, strew DN.ii.84<br />pp <em><a href="bword://santhata">santhata</a></em><br />caus <em>santhāreti</em> Mhvs.29, Mhvs.12<br />caus 2 <strong>santharāpeti</strong> to cause to be spread Vin.iv.39; Mhvs.29, Mhvs.9.</p><p>saṃ + tharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santhata">santhata</a>, santharāpeti</p>

santharaṇaka|santhara.naka|santharanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhara.naka</span></p><p>adjective spreading, strewing; ˚vāta a wind which strews things about Snp-a.67.</p><p>fr. santharati</p>

santhariṃ|santhari.m|santharim
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhari.m</span></p><p>adverb by way of spreading; in <em>sabba˚</em>; so that all is spread, prepared DN.ii.84; cp. Vin.i.227, Vin.i.384.</p><p>fr. santhara</p>

santhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li><p>spread, strewn with (-˚), covered DN.ii.160; Vin.iii.32; Snp.401, Snp.668<br />■ <em>dhamani˚gatta</em> having the body strewn with veins, emaciated Vin.iii.146 = Ja.ii.283; Ja.i.346, Ja.i.350 &amp; passim (see <em><a href="bword://dhamani">dhamani</a></em>). Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. considers <em>santata</em> the right spelling.</p></li><li><p>(nt.) a rug or mat Vin.iii.224; Vv.63#5 (= tiṇa-santharaka Vv-a.262).</p></li></ol><p>pp. of santharati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhamani">dhamani</a></p>

santhatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective sleeping on a rug Mil.342, Mil.359.</p><p>fr. santhata 2</p>

santhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>acquaintance, intimacy SN.i.17; Snp.37, Snp.168, Snp.207, Snp.245; Ja.i.158; Ja.ii.27, Ja.ii.42 Ja.ii.180; Dhs.1059; Dhs-a.364; Dhp-a.i.235. nom. pl <em>santhavāni</em> Snp.844 = SN.iii.9; Ja.iv.98. <em>-jāta</em> having become acquainted, an acquaintance Mnd.198<br />■ <em>a˚vissāsin</em> intimate without being acquainted AN.iii.136.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>stu</em>, cp. santhuta</p>

santhavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>acquaintance Dhs-a.364.</p><p>fr. saṃ + thavati</p>

santhuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective acquainted, familiar Ja.i.365; Ja.iii.63 (cira˚); Ja.v.448 (so read for santhata); Sdhp.31 Neg. <em>a˚</em>; Ja.iii.63, Ja.iii.221; Ja.vi.310. Cp. <em><a href="bword://santhava">santhava</a></em>.</p><p>saṃ + thuta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santhava">santhava</a></p>

santhutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective acquainted Vism.78.</p><p>fr. santhuta</p>

santhāgāra|santhaagaara|santhagara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhaagaara</span></p><p>council hall, a mote hall DN.i.91; DN.ii.147; AN.ii.207; MN.i.228, MN.i.353, MN.i.457; MN.iii.207; DN-a.i.256; Ja.iv.72, Ja.iv.147; Vin.i.233; Vv-a.298; Dhp-a.i.347. Cp. saṇṭhāna 3.</p><p>Sk. sansthāgāra</p>

santhāna|santhaana|santhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhaana</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81na">saṇṭhāna</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://saṇṭhāna">saṇṭhāna</a></p>

santhāra|santhaara|santhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhaara</span></p><ol><li>spreading, covering, floor(ing) SN.i.170; Vin.ii.120 (3 kinds of floors: iṭṭhakā˚, silā˚ dāru˚, i.e. of tiles, flags, wood); AN.i.136 (paṇṇa˚); Ja.vi.24 (id.); Ja.i.92; Pts.i.176.</li><li>(cp. paṭi˚) friendly welcome AN.i.93 (āmisa˚ &amp; dhamma˚).</li></ol><p>saṃ + thāra</p>

santhāraka|santhaaraka|santharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santhaaraka</span></p><p>spread, cover, mat Vin.ii.113 (tiṇa˚), Vin.ii.116.</p><p>santhāra + ka cp. BSk. sanstāraka Mvu.iii.272</p>

santi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>tranquillity, peace Snp.204; DN.ii.157; AN.ii.24; Dhp.202.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> act of appeasing (the gods), pacification DN.i.12; DN-a.i.97.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pada">pada</a></em> “the place of tranquillity” tranquil state, i.e. Nibbāna AN.ii.18; Vv-a.219.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> an advocate of mental calm Snp.845 (˚vada in verse) Mnd.203.</li></ul><p>fr. <em>śam</em>, cp. Sk. ˚śānti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a>, <a href="bword://pada">pada</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a></p>

santika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>vicinity, presence; <em>santikaṃ</em> into the presence of, towards Ja.i.91, Ja.i.185; <em><a href="bword://santik%C4%81">santikā</a></em> from the presence of, from Ja.i.43, Ja.i.83, Ja.i.189; <em>santike</em> in the presence of, before, with DN.i.79, DN.i.144; Dhp.32 = Mil.408; Snp.379; Vin.i.12; SN.i.33; Ja.v.467; with acc. SN.iv.74; with abl. Mhvs.205; nibbānasantike Dhp.372; instr. <em>santikena</em> = by, along with Ja.ii.301 (if not a mistake instead of santikaṃ or santike?).</p><ul><li><em>-āvacara</em> keeping or being near DN.i.206; DN.ii.139; Ja.i.67.</li></ul><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + antika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santikā">santikā</a></p>

santikā|santikaa|santika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santikaa</span></p><p>kind of game, “spellicans” (Rh. D.); (Kern: knibbelspel) DN.i.6; Vin.ii.10; Vin.iii.180; DN-a.i.85.</p><p>unclear in origin &amp; meaning</p>

santiṭṭhati|santi.t.thati|santitthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santi.t.thati</span></p><ol><li>to stand, stand still, remain, continue AN.iv.101 (udakaṃ = stands still), AN.iv.282, AN.iv.302 sq. Pp.31; Ja.i.26.</li><li>to be established, to be put into order Vin.ii.11.</li><li>to stick to, to be fixed or settled, to be composed DN.ii.206; DN.iii.239 (citta); SN.v.321; Vin.i.9, Vin.i.15; Iti.43.</li><li>to restrain oneself Ja.i.438.</li><li>to wait for (acc.) Dhp-a.i.50</li></ol><h3>Forms</h3><p>pres <em><a href="bword://santi%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhati">santiṭṭhati</a></em> DN.ii.206; SN.iii.133; <em>saṇṭhahati</em> Ja.vi.160; &amp; <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81ti">saṇṭhāti</a></em> Pp.31; Ja.iv.469.<br />ppr <em>saṇṭhahanto</em> Vin.i.9;<br />pot <em>saṇṭhaheyya</em> Vin.ii.11; SN.v.321.<br />aor <strong>saṇṭhāsi</strong> Vin.i.15 <em>saṇṭhahiṃsu</em> (3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl.) SN.ii.224.<br />inf <em>saṇṭhātuṃ</em> Ja.i.438; Dhp-a.i.50<br />pp <em><a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhita">saṇṭhita</a></em><br />caus 2 <em>saṇṭhapeti (˚ṭhāpeti)</em>.</p><p>saṃ + tiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santiṭṭhati">santiṭṭhati</a>, <a href="bword://saṇṭhāti">saṇṭhāti</a>, saṇṭhāsi, <a href="bword://saṇṭhita">saṇṭhita</a></p>

santosa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>contentment DN-a.i.204.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>tuṣ</em></p>

santuleyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective commeasurable; neg. <em>a˚</em>; Ja.vi.283.</p><p>metric for ˚tulya, grd. of saṃ + tuleti</p>

santusita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Chief of the devas of the Tusita world. DN.i.218 AN.iv.243 SN.iv.280</p>

santusita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: Santussita</p><p>contented, pleased, happy SN.iii.45 (˚tussit’ attā); Snp.1040; Dhp.362 (= suṭṭhu tusita Dhp-a.iv.90); Mhbv.31 (ss).</p><p>pp. of santussati</p>

santussaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective content Snp.144.</p><p>fr. santussati</p>

santussati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be contented, or pleased, or happy; ppr. <em>-amāna</em> Snp.42<br />pp <em>santuṭṭha &amp; ˚tusita</em>.</p><p>saṃ + tussati</p>

santussita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Also written as: Santussita</p><p>contented, pleased, happy SN.iii.45 (˚tussit’ attā); Snp.1040; Dhp.362 (= suṭṭhu tusita Dhp-a.iv.90); Mhbv.31 (ss).</p><p>pp. of santussati</p>

santuṭṭha|santu.t.tha|santuttha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santu.t.tha</span></p><p>A disciple of the Buddha at Ñātikā. He was born after death in Akanitthābhavana, there to pass entirely away. DN.ii.92 SN.v.358<em>f.</em></p>

santuṭṭha|santu.t.tha|santuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santu.t.tha</span></p><p>pleased, happy DN.i.60, DN.i.71; MN.ii.6; AN.ii.209; AN.iv.232 sq.; AN.v.25, AN.v.67, AN.v.130, AN.v.154. mahā˚ the greatly contented one, the Arahant Dhs-a.407.</p><p>pp. of santussati</p>

santuṭṭhi|santu.t.thi|santutthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santu.t.thi</span></p><p>satisfaction, contentment DN.i.71; MN.i.13; Snp.265; Dhp.204; AN.ii.27, AN.ii.31; AN.iii.219 sq. AN.iii.432 (a˚); Dhp-a.iv.111.</p><p>saṃ + tuṭṭhi</p>

santuṭṭhitā|santu.t.thitaa|santutthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santu.t.thitaa</span></p><p>state of contentment DN.iii.115; AN.i.12; Pp.25; Vism.53; Dhs.1367 (a˚).</p><p>abstr. formation fr. last</p>

santāna|santaana|santana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaana</span></p><ol><li>spreading, ramification, tendril (valli˚) Kp-a.48.</li><li>one of the 5 celestial trees Ja.vi.239 (˚maya made of its flowers).</li><li>(also m.) continuity, succession; lineage SN.iii.143; DN-a.i.46; Dhs-a.63, Dhs-a.217, Dhs-a.297; Vism.555; Vb-a.164. Cp. <em>citta˚</em> continuity of consciousness <em>Cpd.</em> 167<!-- raw HTML omitted -->7<!-- raw HTML omitted -->.</li></ol><p>fr. saṃ + <em>tan</em></p>

santānaka|santaanaka|santanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaanaka</span></p><ol><li>(nt.) = santāna 1; Vv-a.94, Vv-a.162 (˚valli a sort of long creeper). <em>mūla˚</em>; a spreading root SN.iii.155; Ja.i.277.</li><li>= santāna 2; Vv-a.12.</li><li>(nt.) a cobweb Vin.i.48.</li><li>offspring SN.i.8.</li></ol><p>santanā + ka</p>

santāneti|santaaneti|santaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaaneti</span></p><p>Santaneti &amp; ˚tāneti</p><p>to continue AN.iii.96 sq.; SN.iv.104; Pp.66 sq.; Snp-a.5 (see <em><a href="bword://sant%C4%81yati">santāyati</a></em>).</p><p>Caus. of saṃ + <em>tan</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santāyati">santāyati</a></p>

santāpa|santaapa|santapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaapa</span></p><p>adjective noun burning; heat, fire; fig. torment, torture Snp.1123 (cp. Cnd.636); Ja.i.502; Mil.97, Mil.324; Vb-a.70 (various), Vb-a.245 (aggi˚, suriya˚); Sdhp.9, Sdhp.572.</p><p>fr. saṃ + <em>tap</em></p>

santāpeti|santaapeti|santapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaapeti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://santappati">santappati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://santappati">santappati</a></p>

santāpita|santaapita|santapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaapita</span></p><p>heated, aglow Thig.504.</p><p>pp. of santāpeti</p>

santāraṇa|santaara.na|santarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaara.na</span></p><p><em>-ī</em> (f.) conveying to the other shore SN.iv.174; MN.i.134<br />■ f. <em>santāraṇī</em> Tha-ap.234 (scil. nāvā).</p><p>fr. saṃ + tāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

santāsa|santaasa|santasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaasa</span></p><p>trembling, fear, shock AN.ii.33; SN.iii.85; Ja.i.274; Mil.146, Mil.207; Pv-a.22.</p><p>saṃ + tāsa</p>

santāsaniya|santaasaniya|santasaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaasaniya</span></p><p>adjective making frightened, inspiring terror Mil.387.</p><p>fr. saṃ + tāsana</p>

santāsin|santaasin|santasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaasin</span></p><p>adjective trembling, frightened Dhp.351.</p><p>fr. santāsa</p>

santāyati|santaayati|santayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santaayati</span></p><p>to preserve (connect?) Vism.688 (better ˚dhāyati) = Snp-a.5 (reads ˚tāneti).</p><p>saṃ + tāyati</p>

santīraṇa|santiira.na|santirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">santiira.na</span></p><p>investigation, decision; as t.t. denoting a stage in the act of sense-cognition judging an impression (see <em>Cpd.</em> 28, 40, 238) DN-a.i.194; Dhs-a.264, Dhs-a.269, Dhs-a.272; Vism.459. As <em>-ā</em> (f.) at Ne.82, Ne.191. <em>-kicca</em> function of judging Tikp.33 Vism.21, Vism.454.</p><p>saṃ + tīraṇa</p>

sanucara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>together with follower(s).</p>

sanucārika|sanucaarika|sanucarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanucaarika</span></p><p>with a female companion.</p>

sanābhika|sanaabhika|sanabhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanaabhika</span></p><p>adjective having a nave (of a wheel) DN.ii.17, DN.ii.172; AN.ii.37; at both places combined with <em>sa-nemika</em> “with a felly” (i.e. complete).</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + nābhi + ka</p>

sanāmika|sanaamika|sanamika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sanaamika</span></p><p>adjective having a name, called Bv.ii.194 = Ja.i.28.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + nāma + ika</p>

sapadi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb instantly, at once Dāvs i.62.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + adv. form<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> fr. pada</p>

sapadānaṃ|sapadaana.m|sapadanam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sapadaana.m</span></p><p>adverb “with the same steps,” i.e. without interruption, constant successive (cp. Lat stante pede &amp; Sk. adv.; <em><a href="bword://sapadi">sapadi</a></em> at once).</p><ol><li>lit. (perhaps a later use) of a <em>bird</em> at Ja.v.358 (s. sāliṃ khādanto, without a stop); of a <em>lion</em> at Mil.400 (sapadāna-bhakkha).</li><li>applied in phrase <em>sapadānaṃ carati</em> to go on uninterrupted alms-begging Vin.iv.191; SN.iii.238; Snp.413; Ja.i.66; Pv.iv.3#44; Vv-a.121; and in phrases <em>sapadāna-cārikā</em> Ja.i.89; <em>-cārika</em> (adj.) Vin.iii.15; <em>-cārin</em> MN.i.30; MN.ii.7; Snp.65; Cnd.646 Also as adj. <strong>sapadāna</strong> (piṇḍapāta) Vin.ii.214.</li></ol><p>fr. phrase sa-padānaṃ-cārikā; i.e. sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + <em>gen. pl.</em> of pada (cp. gimhāna). Weber (<em>Ind Str.</em> iii.398) suggests sapadā + naṃ, sapadā being an instr. by-form of sapadā, and naṃ an enclitic. Trenckner (<em>Miln.</em> p. 428) says sapadi + ayana. Kern (</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>ii.73 agrees on the whole, but explains padānaṃ as pad’āyanaṃ</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sapadi">sapadi</a>, sapadāna</p>

sapajāpatika|sapajaapatika|sapajapatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">sapajaapatika</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>with Pajāpati. The passage under <em><a href="bword://paj%C4%81pati">pajāpati</a></em> 1 was distorted through copyist’s default. It should read: “only in one formula, with Inda Brahmā, viz. devā sa-indakā sa-brahmakā sa-pajāpatikā DN.ii.274 (without sa-brahmakā); SN.iii.90; AN.v.325. Otherwise <em><a href="bword://sapaj%C4%81patika">sapajāpatika</a></em> in sense of foll. Also at Vb-a.497 with Brahmā.”</li><li>with one’s wife Vin.i.23; Vin.iv.62; Ja.i.345.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://pajāpati">pajāpati</a>, <a href="bword://sapajāpatika">sapajāpatika</a></p>

sapallava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective with the sprouts Vv-a.173.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + pallava</p>

saparidaṇḍā|saparida.n.daa|saparidanda
