satullapakāyikā|satullapakaayikaa|satullapakayika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">satullapakaayikaa</span></p><p>A class of Devas. The Saṃyutta contains SN.i.16–22 a whole group of suttas in which these devas are stated to have visited the Buddha, asking him several questions on different topics.</p>

satī|satii|sati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">satii</span></p><ol><li>being Ja.iii.251.</li><li>a good or chaste woman Abhp.237; <em>asatī</em> an unchaste woman Mil.122 = Ja.iii.350; Ja.v.418; Ja.vi.310.</li></ol><p>fr. sant, ppr. of <em>as</em></p>

sava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective dripping, flowing with (-˚) Pv.ii.9#11 (madhu˚, with honey).</p><p>fr. <em>sru</em>, savati</p>

savacanīya|savacaniiya|savacaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">savacaniiya</span></p><p>(the subject of a) conversation Vin.ii.5, Vin.ii.22, Vin.ii.276.</p><p>sa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + vacanīya</p>

savajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>blameworthy, faulty</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://avajja">avajja</a></p>

