tedhā|tedhaa|tedha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tedhaa</span></p><p>indeclinable in three ways; threefold.</p>

teja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejas">tejas</a></p><ol><li>(sg. nom. <em>tejo</em>; acc. <em>tejo</em>, <em>tejaṃ</em>; instr. <em>tejasā</em>, <em>tejena</em>; gen. <em>tejaso</em>, <em>tejassa</em>; loc. <em>tejasmiṃ</em>; pl. nom. <em>tejā</em>; iic <em>tejo-</em>, <em>teja-</em>)</li><li>fire, flame, heat.</li><li>fiery energy; fierceness; spiritual or moral power; authority.</li></ol>

teja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Teja &amp; Tejo</p><p>“sharpness,” heat, flame, fire, light; radiance, effulgence splendour, glory, energy, strength, power DN.ii.259 (personified as deva, among the 4 Elements paṭhavī, āpo, t., vāyo; cp. tejo-dhātu); SN.iv.215; MN.i.327; Snp.1097 (glory of the sun comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with that of the Buddha); Dhp.387 (sabbaṃ ahorattiṃ Buddho tapati tejasā); Ja.iii.53 (sīla˚); Ja.i.93 (puñña˚ the power of merit); Vb.426 (id.); Pts.i.103; Vism.350 (def.) Vv-a.116.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> fire-contemplation for the purpose of kammaṭṭhāna practice (see <em><a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em>) DN.iii.268; Dhs.203; Vism.171; Dhp-a.ii.49; Dhp-a.iii.214; Bdhd 106;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the element of flame (or fire), the 3rd of the 6 Elements, viz. paṭhavī āpo t. vāyo ākāsa viññāṇa (cp. <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 242) DN.iii.27 DN.iii.228, DN.iii.247; MN.i.188, MN.i.422; AN.i.176; AN.ii.165; Dhs.588, Dhs.648 Dhs.964; Ne.74; Vism.363.</li></ul><p>Vedic tejas (nt.) from <em>tij</em> to be sharp or to pierce = a (piercing) flame. See tejate; semantically (sharp → light) cp. Ger. strahl (ray of light) = Ags. strael (arrow)<br />■ The nt. tejo is the usual form; instr. tejasā (Dhp.387; Snp.1097) &amp; tejena (Ja.iii.53), cp. tapa &amp; tapo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a></p>

tejadhātu|tejadhaatu|tejadhatu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tejadhaatu</span></p><p>the element of fire.</p>

tejagaharaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who scorns or disparages (the element of fire)</p>

tejajiggicchaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who spurns or despises (the element of) fire.</p>

tejana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.m.</i></p><p>the shaft of an arrow; an arrow.</p>

tejana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the point or shaft of an arrow, an arrow Thag.29; Dhp.80, Dhp.145; Dhp-a.ii.147.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://tejate">tejate</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tejate">tejate</a></p>

tejas
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejas">tejas</a></p><ol><li>(sg. nom. <em>tejo</em>; acc. <em>tejo</em>, <em>tejaṃ</em>; instr. <em>tejasā</em>, <em>tejena</em>; gen. <em>tejaso</em>, <em>tejassa</em>; loc. <em>tejasmiṃ</em>; pl. nom. <em>tejā</em>; iic <em>tejo-</em>, <em>teja-</em>)</li><li>fire, flame, heat.</li><li>fiery energy; fierceness; spiritual or moral power; authority.</li></ol>

tejasi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>One of the messengers employed by Kuvera. DN.iii.201</p>

tejasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejasin">tejasin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tejassin">tejassin</a></p>

tejasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejasin">tejasin</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tejassin">tejassin</a></p>

tejassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejassin">tejassin</a></p><p>full of fiery power or energy.</p>

tejassin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejassin">tejassin</a></p><p>full of fiery power or energy.</p>

tejate
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be sharp or to make sharp, to prick, to incite, etc<br />■ See tikkha, tikhiṇa, tiṇha, titikkhati tittaka, teja, etc.</p><p>Vedic tejate from <em>tij</em> (*stij) = Lat. in-stīgo (to spur), Gr. στίζω, στικτός, Ohg. stehhan, Nhg. stecken E. stick</p>

tejavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>splendid, powerful, majestic Dhp-a.i.426.</li><li>in flames, heated, burning with (-˚) Mil.148.</li></ol><p>tejas + vant</p>

teji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejin">tejin</a></p><p>fiery; brilliant (from teja(s))</p>

tejin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tejin">tejin</a></p><p>fiery; brilliant (from teja(s))</p>

tejin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun having light or splendour, shining forth, glorious Snp.1097 (= Cnd.286 tejena samannāgata).</p><p>see <em>teja</em></p>

tejo-dhātu|tejo-dhaatu|tejo-dhatu
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tejo-dhaatu</span></p><p><strong>Tejo-dhātu:</strong> ‘fire-element, heat-element’; see: <em>dhātu</em></p>

tejo-kasina
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Tejo-kasina:</strong> ‘fire-kasina’, is one of the 10 kasina exercises; see: <em>kasina</em>.</p>

tejo
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Teja &amp; Tejo</p><p>“sharpness,” heat, flame, fire, light; radiance, effulgence splendour, glory, energy, strength, power DN.ii.259 (personified as deva, among the 4 Elements paṭhavī, āpo, t., vāyo; cp. tejo-dhātu); SN.iv.215; MN.i.327; Snp.1097 (glory of the sun comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with that of the Buddha); Dhp.387 (sabbaṃ ahorattiṃ Buddho tapati tejasā); Ja.iii.53 (sīla˚); Ja.i.93 (puñña˚ the power of merit); Vb.426 (id.); Pts.i.103; Vism.350 (def.) Vv-a.116.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> fire-contemplation for the purpose of kammaṭṭhāna practice (see <em><a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em>) DN.iii.268; Dhs.203; Vism.171; Dhp-a.ii.49; Dhp-a.iii.214; Bdhd 106;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the element of flame (or fire), the 3rd of the 6 Elements, viz. paṭhavī āpo t. vāyo ākāsa viññāṇa (cp. <em>Dhs. trsl.</em> p. 242) DN.iii.27 DN.iii.228, DN.iii.247; MN.i.188, MN.i.422; AN.i.176; AN.ii.165; Dhs.588, Dhs.648 Dhs.964; Ne.74; Vism.363.</li></ul><p>Vedic tejas (nt.) from <em>tij</em> to be sharp or to pierce = a (piercing) flame. See tejate; semantically (sharp → light) cp. Ger. strahl (ray of light) = Ags. strael (arrow)<br />■ The nt. tejo is the usual form; instr. tejasā (Dhp.387; Snp.1097) &amp; tejena (Ja.iii.53), cp. tapa &amp; tapo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a></p>

tejokasiṇa|tejokasi.na|tejokasina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tejokasi.na</span></p><p>meditation based on fire; the meditation object fire; (the jhana) which is or is brought about by meditation based on fire.</p>

tekaṭula|teka.tula|tekatula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">teka.tula</span></p><p>made of three pungent substances.</p>

tekiccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>doctor.</p>

tekiccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective curable; fig. one who can be helped or pardoned. Only in compounds <em>a˚</em>; incurable unpardonable Vv-a.322 (of a sick person); Dhp-a.i.25 (id.); Mil.322; of Devadatta w. ref. to his rebirth in Niraya Vin.ii.202 = Iti.85; MN.i.393; &amp; <em>sa˚</em>; pardonable Mil.192, Mil.221, Mil.344.</p><p>der. fr. tikiccha</p>

tekicchakārī|tekicchakaarii|tekicchakari
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tekicchakaarii</span></p><p>TekicchakārīTekicchakāni</p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verses speak of how, by recollecting the triple Gem and the four immeasurables, he overcomes lack of food and comfort. Thag.381–386</p>

tekula
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk who, with his brother Yamelu, asked from the Buddha permission to translate the Buddha’s teachings into <em>chandas</em>, which probably is Sanskrit metre. Vin.ii.139</p>

tela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>sesame oil; oil in general.</p>

tela
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>sesamum-oil (prepared from tila seeds), oil in general (tela = tilatelādika DN-a.i.93): used for drinking, anointing &amp; burning purposes Vin.i.205, Vin.i.220 Vin.i.245, etc.; AN.i.209, AN.i.278 (sappi vā t. vā); AN.ii.122≈(tattena pi telena osiñcante; punishment of pouring over with boiling oil); Ja.i.293; Ja.ii.104; Pv.iv.1#48 (tiṇena telaṃ pi na tvaṃ adāsi: frequent as gift to mendicants) Pp.55; Dhs.646, Dhs.740, Dhs.815; Pv-a.80 (kaḷebarānaṃ vasā telañ ca: fat or oil in general)<br />■ tila ˚ṃ pātukāma desire to drink tila-wine Vv-a.54; pāka-tela oil concoction Vv-a.68 = Dhp-a.iii.311; Ja.ii.397 (sata˚); Ja.iii.372 (sahassa worth a thousand); Ja.v.376 (sata˚ worth a hundred) pādabbhañjana˚ oil for rubbing the feet Vv-a.44 sāsapa˚ (mustard seed &amp; oil) Pv-a.198; sappi˚ (butter &amp; oil) Snp.295; Pv-a.278 (see <em>+ madhu</em>) as var. objects of grocery trade (dhañña).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://ko%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81g%C4%81ra">koṭṭhāgāra</a></em> oil store Dhp-a.i.220;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%ADa">ghaṭa</a></em> oil jar DN-a.i.144;</li><li><em>-cāṭī</em> an oil tank Dhp-a.i.220;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C5%ABpita">dhūpita</a></em> spiced or flavoured with oil (of a cake) Vv.43#5;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://n%C4%81%E1%B8%B7i">nāḷi</a></em> a reed used for keeping oil in, an oil tube Vism.99; Dhp-a.ii.193 (+ udakatumba);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pajjota">pajjota</a></em> an oil lamp Vin.i.16; DN.i.85 = AN.i.56 = Snp.p.15;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pad%C4%ABpa">padīpa</a></em> an oil lamp Vin.i.15; SN.iii.126; SN.v.319; Vv-a.198;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81ka">pāka</a></em> an oil decoction mixed with spirits, oil-wine Vin.i.205;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pilotik%C4%81">pilotikā</a></em> (pl. rags soaked in oil Dhp-a.i.221;</li><li><em>-makkhana</em> anointing (the body) with oil Mil.11;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mi%C3%B1jaka">miñjaka</a></em> an oil-cake Pv-a.51</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://va%E1%B9%87ijj%C4%81">vaṇijjā</a></em> oil trade Pv-a.47;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://homa">homa</a></em> an oblation of oil DN.i.9.</li></ul><p>from tila</p><p>See also: \+ madhu, <a href="bword://koṭṭhāgāra">koṭṭhāgāra</a>, <a href="bword://ghaṭa">ghaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://dhūpita">dhūpita</a>, <a href="bword://nāḷi">nāḷi</a>, <a href="bword://pajjota">pajjota</a>, <a href="bword://padīpa">padīpa</a>, <a href="bword://pāka">pāka</a>, <a href="bword://pilotikā">pilotikā</a>, <a href="bword://miñjaka">miñjaka</a>, <a href="bword://vaṇijjā">vaṇijjā</a>, <a href="bword://homa">homa</a></p>

telaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>medicine snuffed through the nose, or inserted in the nose.</p>

telaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= tela Vin.i.204 (“a small quantity of oil”); Vin.ii.107 (sittha-t. oil of beeswax).</p>

telapāka|telapaaka|telapaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">telapaaka</span></p><p>decoction of oil.</p>

teliya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective oily Ja.iii.522.</p>

temana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>wetting, moistening Vism.338; Vv-a.20 (aggimhi tāpanaṃ udake vā temanaṃ) Dhp-a.iii.420.</p><p>from temeti</p>

temayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://temeti">temeti</a></p>

temetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://temeti">temeti</a></p>

temeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes wet; moistens.</p>

temeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to make wet, to moisten Vin.i.47 (temetabba); Vin.ii.209 (temetvā); Dhp-a.i.220, Dhp-a.i.394 (id.) Ja.i.88≈ Kp-a.164; Ja.ii.325 (temento); Pv-a.46 (sutemitvā for temetvā).</p><p>cp. Divy.285 tīmayati; Caus. of <em>tim</em> to moisten. There is an ancient confusion between the roots <em>tim</em> tamas, etc. (to be dark), <em>tim</em>, temeti (to be wet), and <em>stim</em> to be motionless. Cp. tintiṇāyati, tinta, tibba (= tamas), timira</p>

temetvā|temetvaa|temetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">temetvaa</span></p><p>of temeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://temeti">temeti</a></p>

temperature
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Temperature:</strong> utu. - For materiality produced by temperature, see: <em>samutthāna</em></p>

temāsa|temaasa|temasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">temaasa</span></p><p>period of three months; three months.</p>

temīyati|temiiyati|temiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">temiiyati</span></p><p>of temeti is made wet</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://temeti">temeti</a></p>

tena hi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>therefore; now then; well then.</p>

tena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><ol><li>in that direction; there.</li><li>because of that, for that reason, therefore</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://taṃ">taṃ</a></p>

tena upāyena|tena upaayena|tena upayena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tena upaayena</span></p><p>in this way, thus; by this means</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāya">upāya</a></p>

tendencies
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Latent tendencies:</strong> <em>anusaya</em></p>

tepaññasa|tepa~n~nasa|tepannasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tepa~n~nasa</span></p><p>num.</p><p>See also: tepaññāsaṃ</p>

tepaññasaṃ|tepa~n~nasa.m|tepannasam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tepa~n~nasa.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tepaññasaṃ">tepaññasaṃ</a></p><p>num. fifty three.</p>

terasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>num., mfn.</p><ol><li>(num.) thirteen.</li><li>(mfn.) containing (a poem of) 13 verses (Theragāthā)</li></ol>

terasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see under <em><a href="bword://tayo">tayo</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tayo">tayo</a></p>

terasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>group of 13</p>

terasama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā ~ī)n. the 13th.</p>

terovassika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>more than a year old; (according to commentary: rained on for a long time)</p>

terovassika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective lasting over or beyond a year (or season), a year old, dried up or decayed SN.iv.161 (thero vassiko in text) = SN.iv.185 (of wood) MN.i.58 (of bones).</p><p>tiro + vassa + ika</p>

terror
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Terror:</strong> awareness of: one of the insight-knowledges; see: <em>visuddhi</em> VI. 3.</p>

tesaṭṭhi|tesa.t.thi|tesatthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tesa.t.thi</span></p><p>num. sixty three.</p>

tettiṃsa|tetti.msa|tettimsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tetti.msa</span></p><p>num. thirty three.</p>

tettiṃsa|tetti.msa|tettimsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tetti.msa</span></p><p>number thirty-three Ja.i.273; Dhp-a.i.267 sq. See also under tayo &amp; tāvatiṃsa.</p><p>tayo + tiṃsa</p>

tevijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>knowing the three Vedas; possessed of the three knowledges.</p>

tevijja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em>Vijjā</em>.</p>

tevijjaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>tevijjaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. who knows the three Vedas.</p><p>tevijjaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. connected with the three Vedas; belonging to those who know the three Vedas.</p>

tevijjiriṇa|tevijjiri.na|tevijjirina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tevijjiri.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tevijjīriṇa">tevijjīriṇa</a></p>

tevijjīriṇa|tevijjiiri.na|tevijjirina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tevijjiiri.na</span></p><p>desert that is the three Vedas.</p>

tevācika|tevaacika|tevacika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tevaacika</span></p><p>using or involving three utterances or proclamations.</p>

tevīsa|teviisa|tevisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">teviisa</span></p><p>num. &amp; mfn.</p><ol><li>(num.) twenty three.</li><li>(mfn.) the 23rd.</li></ol>

tevīsati|teviisati|tevisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">teviisati</span></p><p>num. twenty three.</p>

tevīsatima|teviisatima|tevisatima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">teviisatima</span></p><p>the 23rd.</p>

teḷasa|te.lasa|telasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">te.lasa</span></p><p>num., mfn. of terasa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://terasa">terasa</a></p>

thabbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>is to be read for <em>-tthambha</em> in para˚ Ja.iv.313.</p>

thaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>stiff, rigid; hard; paralyzed, senseless; stiff or puffed-up with pride or anger.</p>

thaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>lit. hard, rigid firm Ja.i.293 (opp. muduka); Vism.351 (˚lakkhaṇa) Pv-a.139 (= ujjhangala).</li><li>fig.<ol><li><p>hardened, obdurate callous, selfish DN.i.118 (māna˚); DN.iii.45 (+ atimānin); AN.ii.26 = Iti.113 (kuha th. lapa); Snp.104 (see *<a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>*˚); Ja.i.88 (māna˚), Ja.ii.136; Sdhp.90</p></li><li><p>slow Mil.103 (opp. lahuka; cp. BSk. dhandha, on which Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>ii.90)<br />■ See thambha &amp; thūṇa.</p></li></ol></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://maccharin">maccharin</a></em> obdurate &amp; selfish, or very selfish Dhp-a.iii.313; Vv-a.69; Pv-a.45;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://hadaya">hadaya</a></em> hard-hearted Ja.iii.68.</li></ul><p>pp. of thambeti, Sk. stabhnāti to make firm, prop, hold up; cp. Av. stawra firm, Gr. ἀστεμφής, σταφυλή; Goth. stafs, Ags. staef = E. staff; Ohg. stab See also khambha &amp; chambheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gotta">gotta</a>, <a href="bword://maccharin">maccharin</a>, <a href="bword://hadaya">hadaya</a></p>

thaddhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>making stiff; making solid.</p>

thakana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>covering, lid; closing up Dhp-a.iv.85 (saṃvara +).</p><p>see next</p>

thakayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thakenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of thaketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thakesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg</i></p><p>of thaketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thaketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thaketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>covers, covers up; closes.</p>

thaketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cover, cover up, close (usually of doors windows) Vin.ii.134 (kaṇṇagūthakehi kaṇṇā <em>thakitā</em> honti: the ears were closed up), Vin.ii.148 (kavaṭā na <em>thakīyanti</em>, pass.), Vin.ii.209 (vātapāna); Vin.iv.54; Ja.iv.4 (sabbe apihitā dvārā; api-dhā = Gr. ἐπι χη˚, cp. Hom. Od. 9, 243 ἠλίβατον πέτρην ἑπέχηκε χύρησιν the Cyclops covered the door with a polished rock), Ja.v.214; Dhp-a.iv.180 (ṭhakesi, variant reading ṭhapesi); Vv-a.222; Pv-a.216 (dvārā Dāvs iv.33; Dāvs v.25 (chiddaṃ mālāguḷena th.).</p><p>Sk. sthagayati, Caus. to sthagati, from <em>*steg</em> to cover; cp. Gr. στέγω cover, τέγη roof; Lat. tego tegula (E. = tile), toga; Oir. tech house; Ohg. decchu cover, dah roof. On P. form cp. Trenckner, <em>Notes</em>, p. 62</p>

thaketvā|thaketvaa|thaketva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaketvaa</span></p><p>of thaketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thakita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>of thaketi closed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thakīyati|thakiiyati|thakiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thakiiyati</span></p><p>of thaketi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaketi">thaketi</a></p>

thala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>dry land; firm ground; high ground; a plateau.</p>

thala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Thala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter dry ground, viz. high, raised (opp. low) or solid, firm (opp. water) SN.iv.179. As plateau opp. to ninna (low lying place) at Snp.30 (Snp-a.42 = ukkūla) Dhp.98; Iti.66 = SN.i.100 (megho thalaṃ ninnañ ca pūreti) Pv-a.29 (= unnatapadesa). As dry land, terra firma opp. to jala at Dhp.34; Ja.i.107, Ja.i.222; Pv.iv.1#21; Pv-a.260 As firm, even ground or safe place at DN.i.234; Snp.946. Cp. Ja.iii.53; Ja.iv.142; Vism.185.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://gocara">gocara</a></em> living on land Ja.ii.159;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ja">ja</a></em> sprung from land (opp. vārija Dhp.34 or udakarūha Vv.35#6 = water-plant) referring to plants AN.i.35; Ja.i.51; Vv.35#6 (= yodhikādikā Vv-a.162); Mil.281;</li><li><em>-ṭṭha</em> standing on firm ground AN.ii.241;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://patha">patha</a></em> a road by land (opp. jala˚ by water Ja.i.121; Ja.iii.188.</li></ul><p>Vedic sthala, to <em>sthā</em>, orig. standing place; cp. Gr. στέλλω, στόλος; Ags. steall (place); also P thaṇḍila</p><p>Thala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter the haft of a sword, the scabbard Ja.iii.221 (reading uncertain).</p><p>prob. dialect. variant of tharu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gocara">gocara</a>, <a href="bword://ja">ja</a>, <a href="bword://patha">patha</a></p>

thalaṭṭha|thala.t.tha|thalattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thala.t.tha</span></p><p>being or living on dry land; being on high ground.</p>

thamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>delays (?); is disturbed (?)</p>

thamba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>tuft or clump of grass.</p>

thambenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of thambheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thambheti">thambheti</a></p>

thambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>thambha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine</p><p>thambha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine</p><ol><li>a pillar, a post; a column; a baluster.</li><li>hardness, rigidity; arrogance.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://thamba">thamba</a></p>

thambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a pillar, a post Vin.i.276; DN.i.50 (majjhimaṃ ˚ṃ nissāya), DN.ii.85 (id.); Snp.214; Vv.78#2 (veḷuriya˚, of the pillars of a Vimāna); Pv.iii.3#1 (id.); Dhp-a.iv.203; Vv-a.188 (+ tulā-gopānasī); Pv-a.186.</li><li>(fig.) in all meanings of thaddha, applied to selfishness, obduracy, hypocrisy &amp; deceit; viz. immobility, hardness, stupor, obstinacy (cp. Ger. “verstockt”): thambho ti thaddha-bhāvo Snp-a.288, Snp-a.333; th. thambhanā thambhittaṃ kakkhaliyaṃ phāruliyaṃ ujucittatā (an˚?) amudutā Vb.350<br />■ Often combined w. <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81na">māna</a></em> (= arrogance), freq. in set sāṭheyyaṃ th. sārambho māno, etc. AN.i.100, AN.i.299 = Nd ii.under rāga = Mil.289; cp. MN.i.15<br />■ AN.iii.430 (+ māna), AN.iv.350, AN.iv.465 (+ sāṭheyya); Snp.245 (+ mada), Snp.326, Snp.437 (as one of Māra’s combatants: makkho th. te aṭṭhamo) Ja.i.202.</li><li>a clump of grass MN.i.324; cp. thambhaka.</li></ol><p>see etym. under thaddha; occasionally spelt thamba, viz. AN.i.100; MN.i.324; Pv-a.186, Pv-a.187</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://māna">māna</a></p>

thambhanā|thambhanaa|thambhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thambhanaa</span></p><p>firmness, rigidity, immobility Dhs.636 = Dhs.718; Vb.350.</p><p>abstr. to thambha</p>

thambhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">&amp;</i></p><p>thambheti, see *<a href="bword://upa">upa</a>*˚, paṭi˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

thambhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thambheti">thambheti</a></p>

thambhenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of thambheti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thambheti">thambheti</a></p>

thambheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>establishes, supports; makes stiff or rigid.</p>

thambhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thambhin">thambhin</a></p><p>hard; arrogant.</p>

thambhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thambhin">thambhin</a></p><p>hard; arrogant.</p>

thambhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective obstinate Thag.952.</p>

thambhitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= thambha 2, viz. hardness, rigidity, obduracy, obstinacy Vb.350 <em>Note.</em> Quite a late development of the term, caused by a misinterpretation of chambhitatta, is “fluctuation unsteadiness, inflation” at Dhs.965 (in def. of vāyodhātu: chambhittattaṃ [?] thambhitattaṃ. See on this Dhs. trsl. p. 242), &amp; at Vb.168 (in def. of vicikicchā variant reading chambhitatta), and at Asl.338 (of vayo). None of these meanings originally belong to the term thambha.</p><p>abstr. to thambha</p>

thamhhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(= thambha 3) a clump of grass Vv-a.276 (= gumba).</p>

thana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>the breast, esp. the breast of a woman.</li><li>the (teat of the) udder of a cow.</li></ol>

thana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>the breast of a woman DN.ii.266; Ja.v.205; Ja.vi.483 Sdhp.360.</li><li>the udder of a cow MN.i.343 = Pp.56; Dhp-a.ii.67.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> the nipple Ja.iv.37.</li><li><em>-sita-dāraka</em> <a href="bword://sita">sita</a>*] a child at the breast, a suckling Mil.364 = Mil.408.</li></ul><p>Vedic stana; cp. Gr. στηνιον = στ ̈ηχος (Hesychius)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a>, <a href="bword://sita">sita</a></p>

thanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the breast.</p>

thanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>little breast, the breast of a girl Thig.265 (= Thag-a.212).</p>

thanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>roars, thunders.</p>

thanaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thanayat">thanayat</a></p><p>of thanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thanayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of thanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thanayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thanayat">thanayat</a></p><p>of thanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thanayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thanayitvā|thanayitvaa|thanayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thanayitvaa</span></p><p>of thanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thaneti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thanati">thanati</a></p>

thaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to roar, to thunder DN.ii.262; SN.i.100, SN.i.154 (megho thanayaṃ), SN.i.154 (thaneti devo) Iti.66 (megho thanayitvā)<br />pp thanita. See also gajjati &amp; thunati.</p><p>Vedic stanayati &amp; stanati to thunder; cp. Gr. στένω, στενάζω to moan, groan, στονος; Lat. tono; Ags stunian; Ger. stöhnen</p>

thanin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective having breasts, -breasted; in <em>timbaru˚</em>; Snp.110; Ja.vi.457<br />■ <em>pucimanda˚</em>; Ja.vi.269.</p>

thanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>thundering thunder Ja.i.470; Thag.1108; Mil.377.</p><p>pp. of thaneti cp. Vedic (s)tanayitnu thunder = Lat. tonitrus, Ohg. donar, etc.</p>

thapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>master carpenter; an officer; an equerry.</p>

thapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a builder, master carpenter MN.i.396 = SN.iv.223; MN.iii.144.</li><li>officer, overseer SN.v.348.</li></ol><p>Vedic sthapati, to <em>sthā</em> + pati</p>

thapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of ṭhapeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ṭhapeti">ṭhapeti</a></p>

thapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of ṭhapeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ṭhapeti">ṭhapeti</a></p>

thapita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ṭhapita">ṭhapita</a></p>

tharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>only in compounds ā˚, ava˚, etc.</p><p>Sk. stṛṇoti</p>

tharaṇa|thara.na|tharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thara.na</span></p><p>strewing, spreading. In compounds like assa˚, bhumma˚, ratha˚, hattha˚, etc. the reading ass-attharaṇa, etc. should be preferred (= ā <em>stṛ</em>;). See attharaṇa and compounds</p><p>Sk. staraṇa to <em>stṛ</em>.</p>

tharu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the hilt of a sword; a sword.</p>

tharu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the hilt or handle of a sword or other weapons, a sword AN.iii.152; Ja.iii.221 (= sword); Mil.178; Dhp-a.ii.249 (˚mūla); Dhp-a.iv.66 (asi˚)<br />■ tharusmiṃ sikkhati to learn the use of a sword Vin.ii.10; Mil.66.</p><ul><li><em>-ggaha</em> one who carries a sword-(handle) Mil.331 (dhanuggaha + ; not in corresponding list of occupations at DN.i.51);</li><li><em>-sippā</em> training in swordsmanship Ud.31.</li></ul><p>Sk. tsaru</p>

thassanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 pl.</i></p><p>of tiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tiṭṭhati">tiṭṭhati</a></p>

thassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>of tiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tiṭṭhati">tiṭṭhati</a></p>

thatvā|thatvaa|thatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thatvaa</span></p><p>of tiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tiṭṭhati">tiṭṭhati</a></p>

thava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>praise, praising, eulogy Ne.161, Ne.188, Ne.192.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://thavati">thavati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thavati">thavati</a></p>

thavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>praises.</p>

thavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to praise, extol;<br />inf thutuṃ Snp.217 (= thometuṃ Snp-a.272).<br />caus <em>thaveti</em> [Sk. stavayati]<br />pp <em>thavita</em> Mil.361 See thuta, thuti, thoma, thometi.</p><p>Sk. stauti, Av. staviti, cp. Gr. στεϋται</p>

thavikā|thavikaa|thavika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thavikaa</span></p><p>bag; a purse.</p>

thavikā|thavikaa|thavika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thavikaa</span></p><p>knapsack, bag, purse; esp. used for the carrying of the bhikkhu’s strainer Vin.i.209 (parissāvanāni pi thavikāyo pl pūretvā), Vin.i.224 (patte + pariss˚ + th.); Ja.i.55 (pattaṃ thavikāya pakkhipitvā); Ja.vi.67 (pattaṃ thavikāya osāretvā); Vv-a.40 (patta-thavikato parissāvanaṃ nīharitvā). Also for carrying money: <em>sahassathavikā</em> a purse of 1,000 pieces Ja.i.54, Ja.i.195, Ja.i.506; Vv-a.33; Anvs 35. See also Vin.ii.152, Vin.ii.217; Vism.91.</p><p>derivation uncertain</p>

thañña|tha~n~na|thanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tha~n~na</span></p><p>mother’s milk.</p>

thañña|tha~n~na|thanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tha~n~na</span></p><p>mother’s milk Vin.ii.255 = Vin.ii.289 (˚ṃ pāyeti); AN.iv.276; Ja.iii.165; Ja.vi.3 (madhura˚) Thig.496.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://thana">thana</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thana">thana</a></p>

thaṇḍila|tha.n.dila|thandila
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tha.n.dila</span></p><p>bare, esp. hard, stony ground Pv.iv.7#5 (= kharakaṭhāna bhūmippadesa Pv-a.265).</p><ul><li><em>-sāyikā</em> (f.) the act of lying on the bare ground (as a penance) [BSk. sthaṇḍila-śāyikā] SN.iv.118; Dhp.141</li></ul><p>(= Dhp-a.iii.77: bhūmisayana); <em>-<a href="bword://seyy%C4%81">seyyā</a></em> (f.) a bed on bare ground DN.i.167≈(variant reading BB. taṇḍila˚) Mil.351 cp. Sk. sthaṇḍilaśayyā.</p><p>Vedic sthaṇḍila a levelled piece of ground prepared for a sacrifice. Cognate with sthala, level ground</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://seyyā">seyyā</a></p>

thaṇḍilaseyyā|tha.n.dilaseyyaa|thandilaseyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tha.n.dilaseyyaa</span></p><p>bed on the bare ground; sleeping on the bare ground.</p>

thaṇḍilasāyikā|tha.n.dilasaayikaa|thandilasayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tha.n.dilasaayikaa</span></p><p>the act of sleeping on the bare ground.</p>

thena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>thief.</p>

thena
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>thief adj. stealing: athenena not stealing, not stealthily, openly DN.i.4; DN-a.i.72. f. athenī AN.iii.38. Cp. kumbhatthena Vin.ii.256 (see k.).</p><p>Vedic stena &amp; stāyu, besides which tāyu, the latter prob. original, cp. Gr.; τϋτάω to deprive; Oir. tāid thief, to a root meaning “conceal”</p>

thenaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>thief Ja.vi.115.</p><p>= prec.</p>

theneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to steal, to conceal Ja.iv.114; Dhp-a.i.80.</p><p>Denom. fr. thena</p>

thera
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Name of a monk in Rājagaha. He lived in solitude, the virtues of which state he extolled. Hearing this, the Buddha sent for him and taught him how the solitary life could be perfected in detail. SN.ii.282<em>f.</em></p>

thera
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(~ā ~ī)n. &amp; m. &amp; ~ī, feminine</p><ol><li><p>(m.fn.) thick; solid; firm.</p></li><li><p>(mfn.)</p><ol><li>old, elderly; elder, senior; venerable.</li><li>elder, senior in the <em>saṅgha</em> (ordained for 10 years or more)</li></ol></li><li><p>(m.) an elder in the <em>saṅgha</em>; a venerable <em>bhikkhu</em> (of whatever seniority)</p></li><li><p>therī, f.</p></li></ol>

thera
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">t.t.</i></p><p>only used with ref. to the bhikkhus of Gotama Buddha’s community<br />(adj.) senior Vin.i.47, Vin.i.290 (th. bhikkhū opp. navā bh.), Vin.i.159 (th bhikkhu a senior bh. opp. to navaka bh. a novice), Vin.i.187, Vin.ii.16, Vin.ii.212. Therânutherā bhikkhū seniors &amp; those next to them in age dating not from birth, but from admission to the Order). Three grades are distinguished thera bh., majjhima bh., nava bh., at DN.i.78<br />■ See also AN.ii.23, AN.ii.147, AN.ii.168; AN.v.201, AN.v.348; DN.iii.123 sq., DN.iii.218; Dhp.260, Dhp.261. In <em>Sangha-thera</em>, used of Bhikkhus not senior in the Order, the word thera means distinguished Vin.ii.212, Vin.ii.303. In <em>Mahāthera</em> the meaning, as applied to the 80 bhikkhus so called, must also have some similar meaning Dpvs.iv.5 <em>Psalms of the Brethren</em> xxxvi. Ja.v.456. At AN.ii.22 it is said that a bhikkhu, however junior, may be called thera on account of his wisdom It is added that four characteristics make a man a thera-high character, knowing the essential doctrines by heart, practising the four Jhānas, and being conscious of having attained freedom through the destruction of the mental intoxications. It is already clear that at a very early date, before the Anguttara reached its extant shape, a secondary meaning of thera was tending to supplant that of senior-that is, not the senior of the whole Order, but the senior of such a part of the Sangha as live in the same locality, or are carrying out the same function<br />■ <em>Note.</em> thera in thero vassiko at SN.iv.161 is to be read tero-vassiko.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81th%C4%81">gāthā</a></em> hymns of senior bhikkhus, Name of a canonical book, incorporated in the Khuddaka-Nikāya. Theratara very senior, opp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to navatara, novice DN.ii.154</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> the doctrine of the Theras, the original Buddhist doctrine MN.i.164; Dpvs.iv.6, Dpvs.iv.13.</li></ul><p>Vedic sthavira. Derivation uncertain. It may come from <em>sthā</em> in sense of standing over, lasting (one year or more), cp. thāvara old age, then “old = venerable”; (in meaning to be compared w. Lat. senior, etc from num. <em>sem</em> “one” = one year old, i.e. lasting over one and many more years). Cp. also vetus = Gr. ε ̓́τος year, E. wether, one year old ram, as cpd. w. veteran old man. Or it may come from <em>sthā</em> in der. *stheṷā in sthūra (sthūla: see etym. under thūla) thus, “strong venerable”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāthā">gāthā</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a></p>

theradhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with an elder in charge.</p>

theragāthā|theragaathaa|theragatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theragaathaa</span></p><p>theragāthā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine a verse of an elder; a verse from the Theragāthā.</p><p>theragāthā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine pl. the verses of the elders; one of the collections making up the Khuddakanikāya of the Pāḷi Canon.</p>

theraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>thick.</p>

theraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective strong (?), of clothes: therakāni vatthāni DN.ii.354 (vv.ll. thevakāni, dhorakāni, corakāni). Theri &amp; Therika</p>

theranutherā|theranutheraa|theranuthera
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theranutheraa</span></p><p>elders and those next in seniority.</p>

therapāli|therapaali|therapali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therapaali</span></p><p>of therapāḷi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://therapāḷi">therapāḷi</a></p>

therapāḷi|therapaa.li|therapali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therapaa.li</span></p><p>the fine, the list, of elders.</p>

theratara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.; compar.</i></p><p>more senior.</p>

theravāda|theravaada|theravada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theravaada</span></p><p>the statements of elders; what the elders say.</p>

theravāda|theravaada|theravada
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theravaada</span></p><p><strong>Theravāda:</strong> ‘Doctrine of the Elders’, is a name of the oldest form of the Buddha’s teachings, handed down to us in the Pāli language. According to tradition, its name is derived from the fact of having been fixed by 500 Noble Elders of the Order, soon after the death of the Master.</p><p>Theravāda is the only one of the old schools of Buddhism that has survived among those which Mahāyānists have called ‘Hinayāna’. It is sometimes called Southern Buddhism or Pāli Buddhism. It is found today in Sri Lanka, Burma, Thailand, Cambodia, Laos and Chittagong East Bengal. - Cf. Guide, p. 60. - App..</p>

therika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of the elders; belonging to the elders.</p>

therikā|therikaa|therika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therikaa</span></p><p>A bhikkhunī whose Therīgāthā verse says she may sleep happily now the defilements are stilled. Thig.1</p>

therikā|therikaa|therika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therikaa</span></p><p>female elder; a venerable bhikkhu.</p>

therikā|therikaa|therika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therikaa</span></p><p>Therī &amp; Therikā</p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>an old woman (cp. sthavirikā Mvu.iii.283) Pv.ii.11#6 (= thāvarijiṇṇā Pv-a.149).</li><li>a female thera (see compounds), as therikā at Thig.1; Dpvs.xviii.11.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81th%C4%81">gāthā</a></em> hymns of the therīs, following on the Theragāthā (q.v.).</li></ul><p>see <em><a href="bword://thera">thera</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāthā">gāthā</a>, <a href="bword://thera">thera</a></p>

theriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://therika">therika</a></p>

therovassika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://terovassika">terovassika</a></p>

therī|therii|theri
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therii</span></p><p>female elder in the <em>saṅgha</em>; a venerable <em>bhikkhunī</em> (of whatever seniority)</p>

therī|therii|theri
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">therii</span></p><p>Therī &amp; Therikā</p><p>feminine</p><ol><li>an old woman (cp. sthavirikā Mvu.iii.283) Pv.ii.11#6 (= thāvarijiṇṇā Pv-a.149).</li><li>a female thera (see compounds), as therikā at Thig.1; Dpvs.xviii.11.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://g%C4%81th%C4%81">gāthā</a></em> hymns of the therīs, following on the Theragāthā (q.v.).</li></ul><p>see <em><a href="bword://thera">thera</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gāthā">gāthā</a>, <a href="bword://thera">thera</a></p>

therīgāthā|theriigaathaa|therigatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theriigaathaa</span></p><p>the verses of the female elders; one of the collections making up the Khuddakanikāya of the Pāḷi Canon.</p>

theta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>firm; trustworthy; sure, certain.</p>

theta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective firm, reliable, trustworthy, true DN.i.4 (DN-a.i.73: theto ti thiro; ṭhita-katho ti attho); MN.i.179; SN.iv.384; AN.ii.209 = Pp.57; Cnd.623<br />■ abl. <em>thetato</em> in truth SN.iii.112<br />■ <em>attheta</em> Ja.iv.57 (= athira).</p><p>Sk. from tiṭṭhita, Müller <em>P. Gr.</em> 7 = sthātṛ</p>

theva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>drop.</p>

theva
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m.?) a</i></p><p>drop; stagnant water. In Vin. only in phrase cīvaraṃ… na acchinne theve pakkamitabbaṃ Vin.i.50, Vin.i.53 = Vin.ii.227, Vin.ii.230; Ja.vi.530 (madhu-ttheva a drop of honey).</p><p>see etym. under thīna, with which cp. in meaning from same root Gr. στοιβή &amp; Lat. stīria, both drop. Cp. also thika. Not with Trenckner (Notes p. 70 fr. <em>stip</em>.</p>

thevaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>thevaka.</p>

thevati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shine, glitter, shimmer (like a drop) Ja.vi.529 (= virocati p. Ja.vi.530).</p><p>fr. theva; orig. “to be congealed or thick”</p>

thevikī|thevikii|theviki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thevikii</span></p><p>water bag; a water skin.</p>

theyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>theft; stealing; thievishness.</p>

theyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>theft Vin.i.96; AN.i.129; Snp.119 (theyyā adinnaṃ ādiyati); Snp.242, Snp.967 (˚ṃ na kareyya) Vv.15#8 (: theyyaṃ vuccati thenabhāvo Vv-a.72); Mil.264, Mil.265; Vism.43 (˚paribhoga); DN-a.i.71; Sdhp.55, Sdhp.61.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://citta">citta</a></em> intending to steal Vin.iii.58;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%83v%C4%81saka">saṃvāsaka</a></em> one who lives clandestinely with the bhikkhus (always foll by titthiyapakkantaka) Vin.i.86, Vin.i.135, Vin.i.168, Vin.i.320; Vin.v.222; Mil.310;</li><li><em>-saṅkhātaṃ</em> (adv.) by means of theft, stealthily DN.iii.65 sq., DN.iii.133; AN.iii.209; AN.iv.370 sq.; AN.v.264.</li></ul><p>Vedic steya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://citta">citta</a>, <a href="bword://saṃvāsaka">saṃvāsaka</a></p>

theyyacitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(n.) the intention to steal; a thievish thought.</li><li>(mfn.) having an intention to steal; having a thievish thought.</li></ol>

theyyasattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>dishonest caravan.</p>

theyyasaṃvāsaka|theyyasa.mvaasaka|theyyasamvasaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theyyasa.mvaasaka</span></p><p>one who lives dishonestly in the <em>saṅgha</em> (claiming status to which he has no right)</p>

theyyasaṅkhātaṃ|theyyasa"nkhaata.m|theyyasankhatam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">theyyasa"nkhaata.m</span></p><p>adverb with dishonest intent, with intent to steal.</p>

thika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective dropping forming drops: madhutthika Ja.iii.493; Ja.vi.529 (= madhuṃ paggharantiyo madhutthevasadisā p. 530 “dropping honey.”</p><p>cp. Sk. styāyate to congeal, form a (solid) mass; see cognates under thīna &amp; cp. theva</p>

thinking
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Thinking:</strong> understanding based on: <em>cintāmayapaññā</em> see: <em>paññā.</em></p>

thira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>firm, hard; strong; fixed; durable, lasting.</p>

thira
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective solid, hard, firm; strenuous, powerful Ja.i.220 Ja.iv.106 (= daḷha); Mil.194 (thir-âthira-bhāva strength or weakness); Vv-a.212 (id.), Vv-a.35 (= thāmavant); Sdhp.321.</p><p>Vedic sthira, hard, solid; from <em>sthā</em> or Idg. ster (der. of <em>stā</em>) to stand out = to be stiff; cp. Gr.στερεός; Lat. sterilis (sterile = hardened, cp. Sk. starī) Ohg. storrēn, Nhg. starr &amp; starren, E. stare; also Lat strenuus</p>

thiratā|thirataa|thirata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thirataa</span></p><p>steadfastness, stability Dhp-a.iv.176 (thiratāya thavarā; so read for ṭhira˚).</p><p>fr. thira</p>

thiti-bhāgiya-sīla|thiti-bhaagiya-siila|thiti-bhagiya-sila
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiti-bhaagiya-siila</span></p><p><strong>Thiti-bhāgiya-sīla, Thiti-bhāgiya-samādhi, Thiti-bhāgiya-paññā:</strong> ‘static morality, static concentration, static understanding’; see: <em>hāna-bhāgiya-sīla</em></p><p><strong>thought, Thought-conception:</strong> s. <em>vitakka.</em></p>

thiṇṇa|thi.n.na|thinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thi.n.na</span></p><p>of tharati, only in compounds parivi˚, vi˚.</p>

thoka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>, pl. &amp; neuter (mfn.) little, small; being a small amount; pl. few; (n.) a little; a small amount; repeated (and as cpd thokathoka),</p>

thoka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective little, small, short, insignificant; nt. a trifle. AN.iv.10; Ja.vi.366; Pv-a.12 (kāla): nt. <em>thokaṃ</em> as adv. = a little Ja.i.220 Ja.ii.103, Ja.ii.159; Ja.v.198; Pv-a.13, Pv-a.38, Pv-a.43<br />■ <em>thokaṃ thokaṃ</em> a little each time, gradually, little by little Dhp.121, Dhp.239; Mil.9; Snp-a.18; Pv-a.168.</p><p>for etymology see under <em><a href="bword://th%C4%ABna">thīna</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thīna">thīna</a></p>

thokaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. small; few.</p>

thokaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = thoka; fem. thokikā Dhp.310.</p>

thokatara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.; compar.</i></p><p>smaller; less; fewer.</p>

thokathoka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>adverb of thoka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thoka">thoka</a></p>

thokathokaṃ|thokathoka.m|thokathokam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thokathoka.m</span></p><p>adverb just a little; a little each time; little by little; gradually.</p>

thokaṃ thokaṃ|thoka.m thoka.m|thokam thokam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thoka.m thoka.m</span></p><p>adverb</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thokathokaṃ">thokathokaṃ</a></p>

thoma
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>praise.</p><p>Vedic stoma a hymn of praise</p>

thomana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><strong>thomanā</strong> (f.) praising, praise, laudation Ja.i.220 (= pasaṃsa); Pp.53; Pv-a.27.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://thavati">thavati</a></em></p><p>See also: thomanā, <a href="bword://thavati">thavati</a></p>

thomayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>of thometi</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thometi">thometi</a></p>

thomayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thometi">thometi</a></p>

thometi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>praises; extols.</p>

thometi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to praise, extol, celebrate (often with vaṇṇeti) DN.i.240; Snp.679, Snp.1046 Cnd.291; Ja.vi.337; Snp-a.272 (= thutuṃ); Vv-a.102; Pv-a.196<br />pp <em><a href="bword://thomita">thomita</a></em> Ja.i.9.</p><p>denom. fr. thoma; cp. thavati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thomita">thomita</a></p>

thomita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>praised; (praised?)</p>

thought
<p>[Nyana]</p><p></p><p><strong>Thought:</strong> Right: <em>sammā</em> - <em>sankappa</em> see: <em>sacca</em>, <em>magga</em>.</p>

thulla FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Thūla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Thulla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(the latter usual in compounds) adjective compact, massive; coarse gross; big, strong, clumsy; common, low, unrefined rough</p><ol><li>DN.i.223; Snp.146 (aṇuka˚), Snp.633 (id.); Dhp.31, Dhp.265 Dhp.409; Ja.i.196</li><li>Dhs.617; Kp-a.246; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.74 (of a cloak); Vv-a.103; Sdhp.101, Sdhp.346<br />■ thullāni gajjati to speak rough words Ja.i.226 (= pharusavacanāni vadati).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> (adj.) heavy-limbed Ja.i.420;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></em> a grave offence Vin.i.133, Vin.i.167, Vin.i.216; Vin.ii.110, Vin.ii.170 etc.; Vism.22</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kacch%C4%81">kacchā</a></em> thick scurf Vin.i.202;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kum%C4%81r%C4%AB">kumārī</a></em> (Vin.v.129); <em><a href="bword://kum%C4%81rik%C4%81">kumārikā</a></em> a stout, fat girl Ja.iii.147; Ja.iv.220 (Com. pañcakāmaguṇika-rāgena thūlatāya thullak˚ ti vuccati) Vism.17.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phusitaka">phusitaka</a></em> (deva) (the rain-god, probably with reference to the big drops of the rain cp. DN-a.i.45; SN.iii.141; SN.v.396; AN.i.243; AN.ii.140 (a) AN.v.114 sq.; Dhp-a.iii.243;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vajja">vajja</a></em> a grave sin Vin.ii.87 (a) MN.ii.250;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a></em> a coarse garment Ja.v.383;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sar%C4%ABra">sarīra</a></em> (adj.) fat, corpulent Ja.i.420; Ja.iv.220 (opp. kisa thin);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81%E1%B9%ADaka">sāṭaka</a></em> coarse cloth Dhp-a.i.393(a).</li></ul><p>Vedic sthūla (or sthūra); cp. Lith. storas (thick) Lat. taurus, Goth. stiur, Ags. steor (bull = strong, bulky) Ohg. stūri (strong). From <em>sthā</em>: see <em><a href="bword://th%C4%ABna">thīna</a></em>, cp. thūṇā To ūl: ull cp. cūḷa: culla</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a>, <a href="bword://kacchā">kacchā</a>, <a href="bword://kumārī">kumārī</a>, <a href="bword://kumārikā">kumārikā</a>, <a href="bword://phusitaka">phusitaka</a>, <a href="bword://vajja">vajja</a>, <a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a>, <a href="bword://sarīra">sarīra</a>, <a href="bword://sāṭaka">sāṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://thīna">thīna</a></p>

thulla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thūla">thūla</a></p>

thulla
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://th%C5%ABla">thūla</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thūla">thūla</a></p>

thullaccaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. a</i></p><p>particular classification of (Vinaya) offense.</p>

thullaccayāpatti|thullaccayaapatti|thullaccayapatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullaccayaapatti</span></p><p>thullaccaya offense.</p>

thullakacchu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>an itching skin disease.</p>

thullakoṭṭhika|thullako.t.thika|thullakotthika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullako.t.thika</span></p><p>ThullakoṭṭhikaThullakoṭṭhita</p><p>A township in the Kuru country. It was the birthplace of Raṭṭhapāla, and it was there that the Buddha stayed during a tour among the Kurus. MN.ii.54 SN.ii.118</p><p>Kuru3town</p>

thullakumārī|thullakumaarii|thullakumari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullakumaarii</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thūlakumārī">thūlakumārī</a></p>

thullakumārīka|thullakumaariika|thullakumarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullakumaariika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thūlakumārī">thūlakumārī</a></p>

thullanandā|thullanandaa|thullananda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullanandaa</span></p><p>A nun, one of four sisters who all joined the Order, the others being Nandā, Nandavatī and Sundarinandā.</p><p>Thulla-Nandā appears to have had charge of a large company of nuns, all of whom followed her in various malpractices. Vin.iv.211 Vin.iv.239 Vin.iv.240 Vin.iv.280</p><p>Thulla-Nandā was well-versed in the Doctrine and was a clever preacher. Pasenadi, king of Kosala, is mentioned as having come on two occasions to hear her preach, and was so pleased with her eloquence that he allowed her to persuade him to give her the costly upper garments he was wearing. Vin.iv.254–256</p><p>She was greedy for possessions, and was later accused of misappropriating gifts intended for other nuns Vin.iv.245–246 Vin.iv.258</p><p>She was fond of the company of men, and frequented streets and cross-roads unattended that she might not be hindered in her intrigues with men. Vin.iv.270 Vin.iv.273</p><p>She seems to have regarded with sympathy women who succumbed to temptation and to have tried to shield them from discovery. Vin.iv.216 Vin.iv.225 Vin.iv.230<em>f.</em></p><p>She bribed dancers and singers to sing her praises. She could brook no rival, and especially disliked Bhaddā, whom she deliberately annoyed on more than one occasion. Vin.iv.283 Vin.iv.285 Vin.iv.287 Vin.iv.290 Vin.iv.292</p><p>She was fractious and would wish for something, but when that was procured for her, would say it was something else she really wanted. Vin.iv.248 Vin.iv.250</p><p>She was evidently an admirer of Ānanda, and was greatly offended on hearing that Mahā Kassapa had called Ānanda “boy,” and gave vent to her displeasure at what she considered Kassapa’s presumption. But we are told that soon after that she left the Order. SN.ii.219<em>ff.</em></p><p>She befriended Ariṭṭha when he was cast out of the Order. Vin.iv.218</p>

thullaphusitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>See also: thuḷaphusitaka</p>

thullatissā|thullatissaa|thullatissa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thullatissaa</span></p><p>A nun. She was present when Mahā-Kassapa, in the company of Ānanda, visited the nuns and preached to them. She expressed resentment that Kassapa should dare to preach in the presence of Ānanda. It was, she said, “as if the needle-pedlar should try to sell a needle to the needle-maker.” Ānanda afterwards asked Kassapa to ignore Tissā’s outburst, but she later left the Order. SN.ii.215<em>ff.</em></p>

thullavajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://disaṅgamika">disaṅgamika</a></p>

thuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thunat">thunat</a></p><p>moans; groans.</p>

thunat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thunat">thunat</a></p><p>moans; groans.</p>

thunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thavati">thavati</a></p>

thunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to moan, groan, roar SN.v.148 (thunaṃ ppr.; variant reading thanaṃ); Vv.52#1 (of beings in Niraya otherwise ghosenti), variant readingSS thananti (better?).</li><li>to proclaim; shout, praise (confused with thavati Snp.884.</li></ol><p>see <em><a href="bword://thaneti">thaneti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thaneti">thaneti</a></p>

thusa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i></p><p>the husk or grain of rice.</p>

thusa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>husk of grain, chaff AN.i.242 (together w. other qualities of corn); Ja.iv.8; Vism.346. <em>athusa</em> DN.iii.199.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a></em> a fire of husks Ne.23;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a></em> gruel (= sabbasambhārehi kataṃ sovīrakaṃ Pp-a.232) DN.i.166; AN.i.295 = Pp.55;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pacchi">pacchi</a></em> a bird stuffed with chaff, a straw-bird Ja.i.242;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pi%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">piṇḍa</a></em> a lump of husks Vin.ii.151</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C4%81si">rāsi</a></em> a heap of h. Dhp-a.i.309;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://homa">homa</a></em> an oblation of h DN.i.9 (= DN-a.i.93; variant reading BB kana, for kaṇa; cp. kaṇahoma DN.i.9).</li></ul><p>Vedic tuṣa (m.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aggi">aggi</a>, <a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a>, <a href="bword://pacchi">pacchi</a>, <a href="bword://piṇḍa">piṇḍa</a>, <a href="bword://rāsi">rāsi</a>, <a href="bword://homa">homa</a></p>

thusahoma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an offering of husks.</p>

thusodaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>sour gruel.</p>

thuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>praised Dhs-a.198; Ja.iv.101 (sada˚ = sadā thuto niccapasattho); Mil.278 (vaṇṇita th. pasattha).</p><p>cp. pp. of thavati</p>

thuti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>praise Ja.iv.443 (thutiṃ karoti); Vv-a.158.</p><p>cp. thavati</p>

thāla|thaala|thala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaala</span></p><p>plate, dish, vessel DN.i.74; Ja.i.69; Mil.282. <em>Kaṃsa˚</em>; a gong Mil.62 Vism.283 (in simile). See also <em><a href="bword://th%C4%81l%C4%AB">thālī</a></em>.</p><p>from thala orig. a flat dish</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thālī">thālī</a></p>

thālaka|thaalaka|thalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalaka</span></p><p>bowl or dish.</p>

thālaka|thaalaka|thalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalaka</span></p><p>small bowl, beaker Pv.ii.1#8 (thālakassa pānīyaṃ), Pv.ii.1#19 (id.); Ne.79 (for holding oil: dīpakapallika Com.).</p><p>thāla + ka</p>

thālidhovana|thaalidhovana|thalidhovana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalidhovana</span></p><p>the water used for washing a pot.</p>

thālikā|thaalikaa|thalika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalikaa</span></p><p>= thālaka Vin.i.203, Vin.i.240. See āḷhaka˚.</p>

thālipāka|thaalipaaka|thalipaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalipaaka</span></p><p>pot of food; a dish of food used as an oblation.</p>

thālī|thaalii|thali
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaalii</span></p><p>(thāli˚ in compounds) an earthen pot, kettle, large dish; in <em>-<a href="bword://dhovana">dhovana</a></em> washing of the dish AN.i.161 (+ sarāva-dhovana); <em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81ka">pāka</a></em> an offering of barley or rice cooked in milk Vin.iii.15; DN.i.97 (= DN-a.i.267) SN.ii.242; SN.v.384; AN.i.166; Ja.i.186; Mil.249.</p><p>Sk. sthālī, cp. thāla</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhovana">dhovana</a>, <a href="bword://pāka">pāka</a></p>

thāma|thaama|thama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaama</span></p><p>strength, power; ability; firmness.</p>

thāma|thaama|thama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaama</span></p><p>(&amp; <em>thāmo</em> nt. in instr. thāmasā MN.i.498; SN.ii.278 = Thag.1165; SN.iii.110, see below) “standing power, power of resistance, steadfastness, strength, firmness vigour, instr. thāmena (Mil.4; Pv-a.193); thāmasā (see above); thāmunā (Ja.vi.22). Often combined with <em><a href="bword://bala">bala</a></em> Ja.i.63; Snp.68; with <em>bala + java</em> Pv-a.4; with <em>bala viriya</em> Cnd.289, Cnd.651; with <em><a href="bword://java">java</a></em> Ja.i.62; Vv-a.104; with <em><a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a></em> Ja.i.67<br />■ DN.iii.113; SN.i.78; SN.ii.28; SN.v.227; AN.i.50 AN.ii.187 sq.; AN.iv.192. Ja.i.8, Ja.i.265 (˚sampanna); Ja.ii.158 (id.); Dhs.13, Dhs.22; Vism.233 (˚mahatta); Dhp-a.iv.18; Pv-a.259<br />■ Instr. used as adv.: <em>thāmena</em> hard, very much Pv-a.193; <em>thāmasā</em> obstinately, perseveringly MN.i.257.</p><ul><li><em>-gatadiṭṭhika</em> (adj.) one in whom heresy has become strong Ja.i.83 = Ja.vi.220.</li></ul><p>Vedic sthāman sthāmas nt.; <em>sthā</em> cp. Gr. στήμων, Lat. stamen (standing structure); Goth. stoma foundation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bala">bala</a>, <a href="bword://java">java</a>, <a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a></p>

thāmagata|thaamagata|thamagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaamagata</span></p><p>grown strong; become fixed.</p>

thāmaka|thaamaka|thamaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaamaka</span></p><p>adjective having strength Snp.1144 (dubbala˚ with failing strength); Mnd.12 (appa˚ + dubbala).</p>

thāmava|thaamava|thamava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaamava</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thāmavat">thāmavat</a></p><p>strong; firm.</p>

thāmavant|thaamavant|thamavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaamavant</span></p><p>adjective strong, steadfast, powerful, persevering SN.v.197, SN.v.225; AN.ii.250; AN.iv.110, AN.iv.234, AN.iv.291 AN.v.24; Cnd.131; Vv.5#1 (= thira balavā Vv-a.35).</p><p>thāma + vant</p>

thāmavat|thaamavat|thamavat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaamavat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://thāmavat">thāmavat</a></p><p>strong; firm.</p>

thāra|thaara|thara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaara</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://vi">vi</a>*˚, san˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vi">vi</a></p>

thāsotu|thaasotu|thasotu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaasotu</span></p><p>Thāsotu˚</p><p>in thāsotujana savana at Thag-a.61 according to Morris, <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1884, 81 it is to be read ṭhānaso tu jana˚.</p>

thāti|thaati|thati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tiṭṭhati">tiṭṭhati</a></p>

thāvara|thaavara|thavara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaavara</span></p><p>(~ī)n. standing still, still, unmoving; inanimate; stable, firm, long-lasting; permanent, invariable.</p>

thāvara|thaavara|thavara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaavara</span></p><p>Thāvara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective “standing still,” immovable (opp to tasa) firm, strong (Ep. of an Arahant: Kp-a.245; Dhp-a.iv.176. Always in connection with <em><a href="bword://tasa">tasa</a></em>, contrasting or comprising the movable creation (animal world) &amp; the immovable (vegetable world), e.g. Snp.394 (“sabbesu bhūtesu nidhāya daṇḍaṃ ye thāvarā ye ca tasanti loke”); Iti.32 (tasaṃ vā thāvaraṃ vā). See tasa for ref.</p><p>Vedic sthāvara, from <em>sthā</em>, cp. sthavira, Gr. σταυρός post, Lat. re-stauro, Goth. stana judgment &amp; stojan to judge</p><p>Thāvara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter old age Pv-a.149 (thāvari-jiṇṇa in expl. of therī, otherwise jarā-jiṇṇa Should we read thāvira-jiṇṇa?).</p><p>from thavira = thera, old</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tasa">tasa</a></p>

thāvareyya|thaavareyya|thavareyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaavareyya</span></p><p>stability; continuance; being firm, long lasting.</p>

thāvareyya|thaavareyya|thavareyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaavareyya</span></p><p>the rank of a Thera. AN.i.38; AN.ii.23. This has nothing to do with seniority It is quite clear from the context that Thera is to be taken here in the secondary sense explained under Thera. He was a bhikkhu so eminently useful to the community that his fellow bhikkhus called him Thera.</p><p>from thāvara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

thāvariya|thaavariya|thavariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thaavariya</span></p><p>immobility, firmness, security, solidity, an undisturbed state; always in <em>janapada˚</em>; an appeased country, as one of the blessings of the reign of a Cakkavattin. Explained at DN-a.i.250 as “janapadesu dhuvabhāvaṃ thāvarabhāvaṃ vā patto na sakkā kenaci cāletuṃ.” DN.i.88; DN.ii.16, DN.ii.146, DN.ii.169; SN.i.100; Snp.p.106; Iti.15.</p><p>fr. thāvara</p>

thī|thii|thi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thii</span></p><p>female; a woman.</p>

thī|thii|thi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thii</span></p><p>[Vedic strī, on which see Walde, <em>Lat. Wtb.</em> under sero. This form thī is the normal correspondent to Vedic strī; the other, more usual (&amp; dial.) form is itthi a woman Ja.i.295, Ja.i.300; Ja.v.296 (thī-pura), Ja.v.397; Ja.vi.238.</p>

thīna-middha|thiina-middha|thina-middha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiina-middha</span></p><p><strong>Thīna-middha:</strong> ‘lethargy and Laziness’ or ‘lethargy and laziness’, constitute the 3rd of the 5 hindrances <em>nīvarana</em>. They may or may not, be associated with greedy consciousness see: Tab. 23. 25, 27, 29 and II.</p>

thīna|thiina|thina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiina</span></p><p>slothful; lethargic.</p>

thīna|thiina|thina
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiina</span></p><p>stiffness, obduracy, stolidity indifference (cp. thaddha &amp; tandī, closely related in meaning). Together with; <em><a href="bword://middha">middha</a></em> it is one of the 5 hindrances (nīvaraṇāni) to Arahantship (see below) Def. as cittassa akammaññatā, unwieldiness or impliability of mind (= immobility) at Cnd.290 = Dhs.1156 Dhs.1236 = Ne.86; as citta-gelaññaṃ morbid state of mind (“psychosis”) at DN-a.i.211<br />■ Snp.942 (niddaṃ tandiṃ sahe thīnaṃ pamādena na saṃvase), Snp.1106; Vb.352 (= Cnd.290 as expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of līnatta); Vism.262 (˚sineha where p. 361 reads patthinna˚).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://middha">middha</a></em> sloth &amp; drowsiness, stolidity &amp; torpor; two of the 5 nīvaraṇāni (<em>Dhs. trsl.</em> pp. 120, 310) Vin.ii.200 (vigata˚); DN.i.71, DN.i.246; DN.iii.49, DN.iii.234, DN.iii.269, DN.iii.278; SN.i.99 SN.iii.106; SN.v.277 sq.; AN.iii.69 sq.; AN.iii.421; Snp.437 (pañcamī senā Mārassa); Iti.27, Iti.120; Pts.i.31, Pts.i.45, Pts.i.162; Pts.ii.12, Pts.ii.169 Pts.ii.179, Pts.ii.228; Pp.68; Dhs.1154, Dhs.1486; Vism.469; Sdhp.459.</li></ul><p>Sk. styāna; orig. pp. of styāyate to become hard, to congeal; <em>stei̯ā</em> (cp. also thira) = Gr. στέας grease, tale; Lat. stīpo to compress; also Sk. stimita (motionless) = P. timi; stīma (slow), Mhg. stīm; Goth etc. stains = E. stone; Gr. στϊφος (heap); Lat. stīpes (pale); Ohg. stīf = E. stiff</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://middha">middha</a>, <a href="bword://middha">middha</a></p>

thīnamiddha|thiinamiddha|thinamiddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiinamiddha</span></p><ol><li>(n.) lethargy and drowsiness (the third of the nivaraṇas)</li><li>(mfn.) drowsy and lethargic.</li></ol>

thīyanā|thiiyanaa|thiyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiiyanaa</span></p><p><em>thīyitatta</em> (nt.) = thīna, in exegesis at Cnd.290≈(see <em><a href="bword://th%C4%ABna">thīna</a></em>) Vb.352.</p><p>abstr. formations from thīna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thīna">thīna</a></p>

thīyati|thiiyati|thiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thiiyati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://pati%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%AByati">patiṭṭhīyati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://patiṭṭhīyati">patiṭṭhīyati</a></p>

thūla|thuula|thula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuula</span></p><p>coarse, gross; fat; big; strong.</p>

thūla|thuula|thula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuula</span></p><p>Thūla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->a<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Thulla<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(the latter usual in compounds) adjective compact, massive; coarse gross; big, strong, clumsy; common, low, unrefined rough</p><ol><li>DN.i.223; Snp.146 (aṇuka˚), Snp.633 (id.); Dhp.31, Dhp.265 Dhp.409; Ja.i.196</li><li>Dhs.617; Kp-a.246; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.74 (of a cloak); Vv-a.103; Sdhp.101, Sdhp.346<br />■ thullāni gajjati to speak rough words Ja.i.226 (= pharusavacanāni vadati).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> (adj.) heavy-limbed Ja.i.420;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a></em> a grave offence Vin.i.133, Vin.i.167, Vin.i.216; Vin.ii.110, Vin.ii.170 etc.; Vism.22</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kacch%C4%81">kacchā</a></em> thick scurf Vin.i.202;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kum%C4%81r%C4%AB">kumārī</a></em> (Vin.v.129); <em><a href="bword://kum%C4%81rik%C4%81">kumārikā</a></em> a stout, fat girl Ja.iii.147; Ja.iv.220 (Com. pañcakāmaguṇika-rāgena thūlatāya thullak˚ ti vuccati) Vism.17.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phusitaka">phusitaka</a></em> (deva) (the rain-god, probably with reference to the big drops of the rain cp. DN-a.i.45; SN.iii.141; SN.v.396; AN.i.243; AN.ii.140 (a) AN.v.114 sq.; Dhp-a.iii.243;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vajja">vajja</a></em> a grave sin Vin.ii.87 (a) MN.ii.250;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a></em> a coarse garment Ja.v.383;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sar%C4%ABra">sarīra</a></em> (adj.) fat, corpulent Ja.i.420; Ja.iv.220 (opp. kisa thin);</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81%E1%B9%ADaka">sāṭaka</a></em> coarse cloth Dhp-a.i.393(a).</li></ul><p>Vedic sthūla (or sthūra); cp. Lith. storas (thick) Lat. taurus, Goth. stiur, Ags. steor (bull = strong, bulky) Ohg. stūri (strong). From <em>sthā</em>: see <em><a href="bword://th%C4%ABna">thīna</a></em>, cp. thūṇā To ūl: ull cp. cūḷa: culla</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://accaya">accaya</a>, <a href="bword://kacchā">kacchā</a>, <a href="bword://kumārī">kumārī</a>, <a href="bword://kumārikā">kumārikā</a>, <a href="bword://phusitaka">phusitaka</a>, <a href="bword://vajja">vajja</a>, <a href="bword://vattha">vattha</a>, <a href="bword://sarīra">sarīra</a>, <a href="bword://sāṭaka">sāṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://thīna">thīna</a></p>

thūlakumārī|thuulakumaarii|thulakumari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuulakumaarii</span></p><p>grown-up but unmarried girl (of loose morals or promiscuous)</p>

thūlaphusitaka|thuulaphusitaka|thulaphusitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuulaphusitaka</span></p><p>composed of, raining, large drops.</p>

thūlatā|thuulataa|thulata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuulataa</span></p><p>coarseness, roughness, vileness Ja.iv.220.</p><p>abstr. to thūla</p>

thūlavajja|thuulavajja|thulavajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuulavajja</span></p><p>serious offense; being a serious offense.</p>

thūlū|thuuluu|thulu
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuuluu</span></p><p>ThūlūBumūBūmūThulūKhulūKhūlūKhuhū</p><p>The name of a <em>gotta</em>. The village of Uttarakā belonged to them, and when the Buddha was staying there with Sunakkhatta, the latter was greatly taken up with the practices of Korakkhattiya. DN.ii.6</p><p>4region</p>

thūpa|thuupa|thupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupa</span></p><ol><li>a heap; a mound.</li><li>a mound (of earth or sand) to honor or commemorate someone; a stūpa, a domed structure often containing relics; a relic-shrine.</li></ol>

thūpa|thuupa|thupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupa</span></p><p>stupa or tope, a bell-shaped pile of earth, a mound, tumulus, cairn; dome, esp. a monument erected over the ashes of an Arahant (otherwise called dhātugabbha = dāgaba), or on spots consecrated as scenes of his acts. In general as tomb: Vin.iv.308; Ja.iii.156 (mattika˚) = Pv.i.8#4; in special as tope: DN.ii.142, DN.ii.161, DN.ii.164 sq.; AN.i.77; MN.ii.244; Ja.v.39 (rajata˚) Vv-a.156 (Kassapassa bhagavato dvādasayojanikaṃ kanaka˚); Ud.8; Pv.iii.10#5. Four people are <em>thūpārahā</em>, worthy of a tope, viz. a Tathāgata, a Tathāgatasāvaka a Paccekabuddha, a Cakkavattin DN.ii.143; AN.ii.245<br />■ At Dpvs.vi.65 th. is to be corrected into dhūpaṃ.</p><p>Vedic stūpa, crown of the head, top, gable; cp. Gr. στύπος (handle, stalk). Oicel. stūfr (stump), to <em>*steud</em> as in tudati</p>

thūpacīvara|thuupaciivara|thupacivara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupaciivara</span></p><p>cloth from a mound.</p>

thūpaka|thuupaka|thupaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupaka</span></p><p>heap, a mound; a <em>stūpa</em>.</p>

thūpika|thuupika|thupika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupika</span></p><p>adjective having domed roofs (“house-tops” Ja.vi.116 (of a Vimāna = dvādasayojanika maṇimayakañcanathūpika; cp. p. Ja.vi.117: pañcaṭhūpaṃ vimānaṃ explained as pañcahi kūṭāgārehi samannāgataṃ).</p><p>from thūpa. The <em>ika</em> applies to the whole compound</p>

thūpikata|thuupikata|thupikata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">thuupikata</span></p><p>adjective “made a heap,” heaped of an alms-bowl: so full that its contents bulge out over the top Vin.iv.191.</p><p>thūpa + kata</p>

thūpīkata|thuupiikata|thupikata
