<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalacchaadi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tālacchāyā">tālacchāyā</a></p>

tālacchāti|taalacchaati|talacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalacchaati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tālacchāyā">tālacchāyā</a></p>

tālacchāyā|taalacchaayaa|talacchaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalacchaayaa</span></p><p>the shade cover of a palm tree?; a measure of length.</p>

tālakuttha|taalakuttha|talakuttha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalakuttha</span></p><p>A type of plant Ap.346.</p>

tālapakka|taalapakka|talapakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalapakka</span></p><p>ripe palm fruit.</p>

tālapatta|taalapatta|talapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalapatta</span></p><p>palm leaf.</p>

tālapattikā|taalapattikaa|talapattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalapattikaa</span></p><p>palm leaf.</p>

tālaputa|taalaputa|talaputa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalaputa</span></p><p>TālaputaTālapuṭa</p><p>A stage-manager of Rājagaha. One day he visited the Buddha and asked if it was true that players who delight large audiences are reborn among the gods of laughter. Three times the Buddha refused to answer, but in the end allowed himself to be persuaded, and told Tālapuṭa that those who induce sensual states in others will be reborn in purgatory. Tālapuṭa wept to think that older actors should so have deceived him in telling him of their theories, and, having heard the Buddha preach, entered the Order and soon became an arahant SN.iv.306<em>ff.</em> Thag.1091–1145</p>

tālataruna|taalataruna|talataruna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m.)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalataruna</span></p><p>of tāla a young shoot of the palm</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāla">tāla</a></p>

tālavatthu|taalavatthu|talavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalavatthu</span></p><p>(according to commentaries) (like) the site of a (previous) palm-tree; or like a palm-tree with its top tuft of leaves cut off; i.e. with no possibility of (re) growth.</p>

tālavatthukata|taalavatthukata|talavatthukata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalavatthukata</span></p><p>destroyed utterly (with no possibility of regrowth)</p>

tālavaṇṭa|taalava.n.ta|talavanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.a a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalava.n.ta</span></p><p>fan (made of palm leaf)</p>

tālavaṇṭaka|taalava.n.taka|talavantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalava.n.taka</span></p><p>arranged in a shape of a fan.</p>

tālisa|taalisa|talisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalisa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tālīsa">tālīsa</a></p>

tālissa|taalissa|talissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalissa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tālīsa">tālīsa</a></p>

tālu|taalu|talu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalu</span></p><p>the palate.</p>

tālu|taalu|talu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalu</span></p><p>the palate Snp.716; Ja.i.419; Vism.264 (˚matthaka top of p.); Pv-a.260.</p><p>Sk. tālu, see <em><a href="bword://tala">tala</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tala">tala</a></p>

tālāvatthu|taalaavatthu|talavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taalaavatthu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tālavatthu">tālavatthu</a></p>

tālīsa|taaliisa|talisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaliisa</span></p><p>fragrant shrub (flacourtia cataphracta?); a perfume prepared from it.</p>

tālīsa|taaliisa|talisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaliisa</span></p><p>(also <em>tālissa</em> Ja.iv.286, <em>tālīsaka</em> Mil.338) the shrub Flacourtia cataphracta &amp; a powder or ointment obtained from it Vin.i.203 (+ tagara); Ja.iv.286 (id.); Mil.338.</p><p>cp. Sk. tālī, tālīśa &amp; talāśā</p><p>Tālīsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(No. 40) is short for <em>cattālīsa</em>, e.g. Tha-ap.103, Tha-ap.234 and passim.</p>

tāma|taama|tama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taama</span></p><p>desire, longing, greed in tāmatamadasangha-suppahīna Thag.310, an epithet of frogs which perhaps (with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>ii.88) is to be read as tāma-tamata-suppahita; “horribly greedy” (Kern gruwelijk vraatzuchtig).</p><p>Sk. tāma</p>

tāpa|taapa|tapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapa</span></p><p>heat; glow; heating; pain.</p>

tāpana|taapana|tapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapana</span></p><p>burning; distressing.</p>

tāpana|taapana|tapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapana</span></p><p>burning, scorching, roasting; fig. tormenting, torture, self-mortification Vv-a.20 (aggimhi t. udake vā temanaṃ). Cp. ā˚; upa˚ pari˚.</p><p>from tāpeti</p>

tāpasa|taapasa|tapasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapasa</span></p><p>one who practises tapas, an ascetic (brahmin). Eight kinds are enumerated at DN-a.i.270 &amp; Snp-a.295<br />■ Ja.ii.101, Ja.ii.102; Ja.v.201; Pv-a.153 <em>-pabbajjā</em> the life of an a. Ja.iii.119; Dhp-a.iv.29; DN-a.i.270-f. <strong>tāpasī</strong> a female ascetic Mhvs.vii.11, Mhvs.vii.12.</p><p>from tapa &amp; tapas</p><p>See also: tāpasī</p>

tāpayati|taapayati|tapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapayati</span></p><p>(seme as tāpeti)</p>

tāpenta|taapenta|tapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapenta</span></p><p>scorching; tormenting; heating.</p>

tāpeti|taapeti|tapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapeti</span></p><p>of tapati pains; torments</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tapati">tapati</a></p>

tāpeti|taapeti|tapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapeti</span></p><p>to burn out, scorch, torment, fig. root out, quench Snp.451 (attānaṃ); Ja.v.267 (janapadaṃ); Vv-a.114 (kilesaṃ t. in expl. of tapassin). Cp. pari˚.</p><p>Sk. tāpayati, Caus. to tapati</p>

tāpetvā|taapetvaa|tapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taapetvaa</span></p><p>of tāpeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāpeti">tāpeti</a></p>

tāragaṇa|taaraga.na|taragana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaraga.na</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tārāgaṇa">tārāgaṇa</a></p>

tārakarūpa|taarakaruupa|tarakarupa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarakaruupa</span></p><p>the appearance of a star; a star.</p>

tārakā|taarakaa|taraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarakaa</span></p><ol><li>a star, a planet: osadhī viya tārakā like the morning-star (Venus) Vv.9#2 = Pv.ii.1#10<br />■ Ja.i.108; tāraka-rūpa the light (or sparkling of the stars DN.iii.85, DN.iii.90; SN.iii.156 = Iti.19; SN.v.44; Vv-a.79; Dhs.617.</li><li>fig. sparkling, glitter, twinkle akkhi˚ the pupil of the eye MN.i.80; udaka˚ sparkling of the water ibid.</li></ol><p>Sk. tārakā</p>

tāraya|taaraya|taraya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaraya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tārayat">tārayat</a></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayanta|taarayanta|tarayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayanta</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayat|taarayat|tarayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tārayat">tārayat</a></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayati|taarayati|tarayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayati</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayetum|taarayetum|tarayetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayetum</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayi|taarayi|tarayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayi</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārayituṃ|taarayitu.m|tarayitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarayitu.m</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tāresi|taaresi|taresi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaresi</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tāreti|taareti|tareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taareti</span></p><p>tāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. hurries; urges forward.</p><p>tāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>caus. pr. 3 sg. carries or leads over or across; rescues, saves (from)</p>

tāreti|taareti|tareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taareti</span></p><p>Tāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to make cross, to help over, to bring through, save, help, assist Snp.319 (pare tārayetuṃ), Snp.321 (so tāraye tattha bahū pi aññe); Iti.123 (tiṇṇo tarayataṃ varo: “one who is through is the best of those who can help through”); Ja.i.28 (verse 203). aor atārayi Snp.539, Snp.540 &amp; ṭāresi Snp.545.</p><p>Caus. of tarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>Tāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to make haste Thag.293.</p><p>Caus. of tarati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

tāretuṃ|taaretu.m|taretum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaretu.m</span></p><p>of tāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāreti">tāreti</a></p>

tārituṃ|taaritu.m|taritum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaritu.m</span></p><p>of tarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tarati">tarati</a></p>

tārukkha|taarukkha|tarukkha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taarukkha</span></p><p>One of the most eminent great brahmins, contemporary of the Buddha.</p><p>He was present at the assemblies of the brahmins in Icchānaṅgala and in Manasākaṭa.</p><p>He was the teacher of Bhāradvāja, companion of Vāseṭṭha. Snp.p.115<em>f.</em> DN.i.235 MN.ii.202</p>

tārā|taaraa|tara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaraa</span></p><p>star, a planet Snp.687 (tārāsabha the lord, lit. “the bull” of the stars, i.e. the Moon).</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87a">gaṇa</a></em> (tāra˚) the host of stars Pv.ii.9#67 (cando va t<br />■ gaṇe atirocati). <em>-maṇivitāna</em> “star-jewel-awning” canopy of jewelled stars Vism.76.</p><p>Sk. tārā = Gr. ἀστήρ, α ̓́στον (= Lat. astrum, in E. disaster), Lat. stella, Goth. staírnō, Ohg. sterro (:E. star), perhaps loan word from Semitic sources</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gaṇa">gaṇa</a></p>

tārāgaṇa|taaraaga.na|taragana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaraaga.na</span></p><p>multitude of stars.</p>

tārāsabha|taaraasabha|tarasabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaraasabha</span></p><p>the lord of the stars; the moon.</p>

tāsa|taasa|tasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taasa</span></p><p>fear, anxiety, alarm.</p>

tāsa|taasa|tasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taasa</span></p><p>terror, trembling, fear, fright, anxiety SN.iii.57; Ja.i.342; Ja.iii.177, Ja.iii.202; Mil.24. Cp. san˚.</p><p>see *<a href="bword://tasati">tasati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tasati">tasati</a></p>

tāsaniya|taasaniya|tasaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taasaniya</span></p><p>adjective to be dreaded, dreadful, fearful Mil.149.</p>

tāsayati|taasayati|tasayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taasayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāseti">tāseti</a></p>

tāseti|taaseti|taseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taaseti</span></p><p>of tasati makes tremble; frightens, scares</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tasati">tasati</a></p>

tāta|taata|tata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taata</span></p><ol><li>a father.</li><li>voc. sg. tāta, pl. tātā, a term of friendly address to one or more persons.</li></ol>

tāta|taata|tata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taata</span></p><p>father; usually in <em>voc. sg.</em> tāta (and pl. tātā) used as term of affectionate, friendly or respectful address to one or more persons, both younger &amp; older than the speaker, superior or inferior. As father (perhaps = tātā, see next) at Thig.423, Thig.424 (+ ammā). tāta (sg.) in addr. one: Ja.iii.54; Ja.iv.281 (amma tāta mammy &amp; daddy) Dhp-a.ii.48 (= father), Dhp-a.iii.196 (id.); Pv-a.41 (= father), Pv-a.73 (a son), Pv-a.74 (a minister); Ja.i.179 (id.); Mil.15, Mil.16, Mil.17 (a bhikkhu or thera) in addr. several Vin.i.249; Ja.ii.133; Pv-a.50. tātā (pl.) Ja.i.166; Ja.i.263; Ja.iv.138.</p><p>Vedic tāta, Gr. τάτα &amp; τέττα, Lat. tata, Ger. tate, E. dad(dy); onomat.</p>

tātar|taatar|tatar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taatar</span></p><p>protector, saviour, helper DN-a.i.229. For meaning “father see <em><a href="bword://t%C4%81ta">tāta</a></em> &amp; cp. pitā = tāyitā at Ja.i.412.</p><p>from Vedic <em>trā</em>, n. ag. to trāyati to protect</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāta">tāta</a></p>

tāva|taava|tava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taava</span></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li><p>to such an extent; so far; so….(sometimes written as a cpd)</p></li><li><p>so long as; as far as; esp. as correlative of yava(ṃ) (and yāvatā);</p><ol><li>yāva … tāva …; a as long as … so long …</li><li>yāva na … tāva (na) …, as long as not, until … so long …</li><li>na tāva …yāva na …, na tāva … yāva …, not … as long as (not), not … until …</li><li>tāva … yāva (na) …, so long … as long as, until …</li><li>with yāvakīvaṃ: so long.</li></ol></li><li><p>at once, now; first; just; still, yet; indeed, truly; esp. with imperat.; na tāva not; frequently tāva-d-eva, at that time; just then; at once; yet.</p></li></ol>

tāva|taava|tava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taava</span></p><p>adverb so much, so long; usually correl. with <em><a href="bword://y%C4%81va">yāva</a></em> how long, how much; in all meanings to be understood out of elliptical application of this correlation Thus</p><h3>I</h3><p><em>yāva-tāva</em> as long as: yāva dve janā avasiṭṭhā ahesuṃ tāva aññamaññaṃ ghātayiṃsu Ja.i.254; yāva dukkhā nirayā idha tattha pi tāva ciraṃ vasitabbaṃ Snp.678. Neg. na tāva-yāva na not until MN.i.428; SN.v.261; AN.i.141≈(na t. kālaṃ karoti yāva na taṃ pāpakammaṃ byantihoti he does not die until his evil kamma is exhausted).</p><h3>II <em>Elliptical:</em></h3><ol><li><p>temporal: so long as, for the time (tāvakālikaṃ = yāvak˚tāvak˚; see below).</p></li><li><p>comparative: (such-) as, like so, such, just so, rather, in such a degree, even; tāvabahuṃ suvaṇṇaṃ so much gold Vin.i.209; t<br />■ mahanto so much Ja.i.207; t. madhuraphala with such sweet fruit Ja.ii.105; asītiyā tāva kimi-kulānaṃ sādhāraṇa (of the body) or rather, i.e. Vism.235; vatthāni t. devapātubhūtāni Pv-a.44; paṭhamaṃ t. (even) at once, right away Pv-a.113, Pv-a.132; gilānāya t. ayaṃ etissā rūpasobhā even in sickness she is so beautiful Vv-a.76; parittakassa kusalakammassa t. = quidem Pv-a.51; paṃsukūlikangaṃ t. in the first place Vism.62.</p></li><li><p>concessive</p><ol><li>(absol.) as far as it goes, considering, because: yadi evaṃ pitā tāva purisabhāve na rodati, mātu nāma hadayaṃ mudukaṃ “even if the father as man does not weep, surely,” &amp; c., Pv-a.63</li><li>with imper. in expr like gaccha tāva go as long as you like (to go) (= gaccha tāva yāva gaccheyyāsi), i.e. if you like, cp. Ger. geh’immer; passa tāva just look = Lat. licet. Therefore sometimes = please or simply an emphatic imper. as “do go,” etc. Ja.ii.5 (ete t. aguṇā hontu let them be faulty), Ja.ii.133 (ehi t.), Ja.ii.352 (tiṭṭha t. leave off please), Ja.iii.53 (pāto va t. hotu only let it be to-morrow, i.e. wait till t-m.); Ja.iv.2 taṃ t. me detha give me this though) Vv-a.289 (vīmaṃsatha t. just think); Pv-a.4 (t. ayyo āgametu yāvâyaṃ puriso pānīyaṃ pivissati may your honour wait till this man shall have drunk the water), Pv-a.13 (therā t. gacchantu). With prohibitive: mā tāva ito agā please do not go from here Pv.ii.3#22.</li></ol></li><li><p>hortative with 1st pers. fut. equal to imperative-subjunctive or injunctive, cp. 3 (b): let me, well, now, then (cp Lat. age in dic age, etc.). Ja.i.62 (puttaṃ t. passissāmi please let me see the son), Ja.i.263 (vīmaṃsissāmi t. let me think), Ja.i.265 (nahāyissāmi t. just let me bathe).</p></li></ol><h3>III. <em>In other combinations:</em></h3><p><em>tāva-na</em> although, yet not even: ajjā pi t. me balaṃ na passasi not even to-day have you yet seen my full strength Ja.i.207; t. mahādhanassāmī na me dātuṃ piyaṃ ahu although lord of wealth yet I did not like to give Pv.ii.7#6. <em>na-tāva</em> (or tāva in neg. sentence) not yet, not even, not so much as (= Lat. ne-quidem) Pv.ii.11#2 (na ca tāva khīyati does not even diminish a bit); Pv-a.117 (attano kenaci anabhibhavanīyataṃ eva tāva: that he is not to be overpowered, even by anyone). <em>tāva-d-eva</em> just now instantly, on the spot, at once Snp.30; Ja.i.61, Ja.i.151 Ja.iv.2; Pv.ii.8#9 (= tadā eva Pv-a.109); Pv-a.23, Pv-a.46, Pv-a.74 Pv-a.88, etc. <strong>tāvade</strong> (= tāva-d-eva) for all times Pv.iv.3#38 (= Pv-a.255).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81lika">kālika</a></em> (adj.) “as long as the time lasts,” i.e. for the time being, temporary, pro tempore Vin.ii.174; Vin.iii.66 Vin.iv.286; Ja.i.121, Ja.i.393; Vism.95; Thag-a.288; Pv-a.87 (= na sassata).</li></ul><p>Sk. tāvat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://yāva">yāva</a>, tāvade, <a href="bword://kālika">kālika</a></p>

tāvadeva|taavadeva|tavadeva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavadeva</span></p><p>indeclinable at that time; just then; at once.</p>

tāvakālika|taavakaalika|tavakalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavakaalika</span></p><p>so long.</p>

tāvakālikaṃ|taavakaalika.m|tavakalikam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavakaalika.m</span></p><p>adverb temporarily.</p>

tāvata|taavata|tavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavata</span></p><p>lit. “so-much-ness,” i.e. relative extent or sphere, relatively Vism.481, Vism.482.</p><p>abstr. fr. tāva</p>

tāvataka|taavataka|tavataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavataka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. just so much; just so long.</p>

tāvataka|taavataka|tavataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavataka</span></p><p>adjective just so much or just so long (viz. as the situation requires), with (or ellipt. without a corresp. yāvataka Vin.i.83 (yāvatake-t. as many as) DN.ii.18 (yāvatakv’ assa kāyo tāvatakv’ assa vyāmo as tall as is his body so far can he stretch his arms: the 19th sign of a Mahāpurisa); instr. as adv. <em>tāvatakena</em> after a little time Mil.107; Dhp-a.iii.61<br />■ See also tattaka (contracted of tāvataka).</p><p>der. fr. tāva</p>

tāvataka vā|taavataka vaa|tavataka va
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavataka vaa</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. however many (with yāvataka—yāvataka va tāvataka vā)</p>

tāvatakena|taavatakena|tavatakena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavatakena</span></p><p>with just so much; merely with that.</p>

tāvatihaṃ|taavatiha.m|tavatiham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavatiha.m</span></p><p>indeclinable for a period of so many days.</p>

tāvatika|taavatika|tavatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavatika</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāvataka">tāvataka</a></p>

tāvatimsa|taavatimsa|tavatimsa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavatimsa</span></p><p><strong>Tāvatimsa:</strong> ‘the Thirty-thrce Gods’, a class of divine beings in the sense-sphere; see: <em>deva</em> I.</p>

tāvatiṃsa|taavati.msa|tavatimsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavati.msa</span></p><p>, n., &amp; masculine plural</p><ol><li>(mfn.) numbering 33; belonging to the group of 33 (gods); (m.pl.) the 33 gods; those reborn in the world of the 33 gods.</li><li>(n.) the world of the 33 gods.</li></ol>

tāvatiṃsa|taavati.msa|tavatimsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavati.msa</span></p><p>No. 33, only in compounds denoting the 33 gods, whose chief is Sakka, while the numeral 33 is always <em><a href="bword://tetti%E1%B9%83sa">tettiṃsa</a></em>. This number occurs already in the Vedas with ref. to the gods &amp; is also found in Zend-Avesta (see Haug, <em>Language &amp; Writings</em> etc., pp. 275, 276). The early Buddhists, though they took over the number 33, rejected the superstitious beliefs in the magical influence and mystic meaning of that &amp; other simple numbers. And they altered the tradition. The king of the gods had been Indra, of disreputable character from the Buddhist point of view. Him they deposed, and invented a new god named Sakka, the opposite in every way to Indra (see for details; <em>Dial.</em> ii.294–⁠298). Good Buddhists, after death in this world, are reborn in heaven (sagga), by which is meant the realm of the Thirty-three (DN.ii.209). There they are welcomed by the Thirty-three with a song of triumph (DN.ii.209, DN.ii.211, DN.ii.221, DN.ii.227) The Thirty-three are represented as being quite good Buddhists. Sakka their new chief and Brahmā address them in discourses suitable only for followers of the new movement (DN.ii.213, DN.ii.221). See further Vin.i.12; MN.i.252; MN.ii.78; MN.iii.100; AN.iii.287; AN.iv.396 = Vv-a.18 (cp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with the people of Jambudīpa); Vv-a.59, Vv-a.331, Vism.225, etc<br />■ See also tidasa.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://devaloka">devaloka</a></em> the god-world of the 33; freq. e.g. Ja.i.202 Vism.399; Dhp-a.iii.8;</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhavana">bhavana</a></em> the realm of the 33 gods Ja.i.202; Vism.207 sq., Vism.390, Vism.416, and passim.</li></ul><p>tayo + tiṃsa. Cp. Vedic trayastriṃśat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tettiṃsa">tettiṃsa</a>, <a href="bword://devaloka">devaloka</a>, <a href="bword://bhavana">bhavana</a></p>

tāvatiṃsā|taavati.msaa|tavatimsa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavati.msaa</span></p><p>TāvatiṃsāTidasāTidiva</p><p>The second of the six deva-worlds, the first being the Cātummahārājika world. Tāvatiṃsā stands at the top of Mount Sineru. Sakka is king of Tāvatiṃsā.</p><p>Tāvatiṃsā was also known as Tidasā and Tidiva. SN.i.234</p><p>5heaven</p>

tāvattaka|taavattaka|tavattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavattaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāvataka">tāvataka</a></p>

tāvatā|taavataa|tavata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavataa</span></p><p>by (only) so much; for so long; to such an extent; as correlative of yāvata (and yāva):</p>

tāvatā|taavataa|tavata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taavataa</span></p><p>adverb</p><ol><li>so long (corr. to yāva) Dpvs.iv.17.</li><li>on that account, thus DN.i.104 (variant reading ettāvatā); Dhp.266.</li></ol><p>from tāva</p>

tāyana|taayana|tayana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taayana</span></p><p>A <em>devaputta</em>. He visited the Buddha at Sāvatthī and uttered certain verses in the hearing of the monks, telling of the necessity of following the recluse’s calling with energy and sincerity. After his departure the Buddha asked the monks to learn the verses for their own good. SN.i.49</p>

tāyati|taayati|tayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taayati</span></p><p>protects; guards.</p>

tāyati|taayati|tayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taayati</span></p><p>to shelter protect, preserve, guard; bring up, nourish SN.iv.246 (rūpa-balaṃ, bhoga˚, ñāti˚, putta˚); Ja.iv.387; Snp.579 (paralokato na pitā tāyate puttaṃ ñātī vā pana ñātake) Pv-a.7 (khettaṃ tāyati bījaṃ).</p><p>Sk. trāyate &amp; trāte, connected with; <em>*ter</em> in tarati, orig. to see through, to save, cp. tāṇa, etc.</p>

tāyitar|taayitar|tayitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taayitar</span></p><p>one who protects, shelters or guards Ja.i.412 (in expl. of tāṇa, q.v.).</p><p>n. ag. from tāyati</p>

tāḷa|taa.la|tala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.la</span></p><p>tāḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a key</p><p>tāḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine beating; striking the hands together, clapping; a percussion instrument.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāla">tāla</a></p>

tāḷa|taa.la|tala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.la</span></p><p>Tāḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>beating, striking, the thing beaten or struck i.e. a musical instrument which is beaten, an instr. of percussion, as a cymbal, gong, or tambourine (for tāḷa gong cp. thāla):</p><ol><li>gong, etc. Ja.i.3; Ja.vi.60; Thag.893; DN-a.i.85; Dhs-a.319 (kaṃsa˚)</li><li>music in general Dhp-a.iv.67.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-āvacara</em> musical time or measure, music, a musician DN.ii.159 (variant reading tāla˚); Ja.i.60 (l); Ja.iv.41; Vv-a.257 (˚parivuta, of an angel).</li></ul><p>taḍ cp. Sk. tāla a blow, or musical time; tālīyaka cymbal</p><p>Tāḷa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter [Sk. tālaka = tāḍa Avs.ii.56, tāḍaka Divy.577] a key (orig. a “knocker”?) Vin.ii.148 (3 kinds loha˚, kaṭṭha˚, visāṇa˚); Bdhd 1.</p><ul><li><em>-cchiggala</em> a key-hole SN.iv.290; SN.v.453; Vism.500</li><li><em>-cchidda</em> id. Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.148, Vin.ii.153 (all tāla˚); Vin.iii.118; Dhp-a.iii.8 (l).</li></ul>

tāḷacchidda|taa.lacchidda|talacchidda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.lacchidda</span></p><p>keyhole</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāla">tāla</a></p>

tāḷacchiggala|taa.lacchiggala|talacchiggala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.lacchiggala</span></p><p>keyhole</p><p>See also: tālacchiggala</p>

tāḷayati|taa.layati|talayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.layati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāḷeti">tāḷeti</a></p>

tāḷeti|taa.leti|taleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.leti</span></p><p>strikes; beats; strikes a musical instrument.</p>

tāḷeti|taa.leti|taleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.leti</span></p><p>to strike a blow, flog, beat, esp. freq. in phrase <em>kasāhi tāḷeti</em> to flog with whips, etc. (in list of punishments, see <em><a href="bword://kas%C4%81">kasā</a></em>) MN.i.87; AN.ii.122; Cnd.604; Pv-a.4, etc<br />ppr pass. <em>taḍḍamāna</em> (for *tāḍyamāna) Ja.vi.60 (so read for taddamāna; Com poṭhīyamāna)<br />pp <strong>tāḷita</strong> Ja.vi.60 (turiya˚); Vv.62#1 (id.); Sdhp.80. Cp. abhi˚.</p><p>Sk. tāḍayati, <em>taḍ</em> perhaps = <em>tud</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasā">kasā</a>, tāḷita</p>

tāḷetvā|taa.letvaa|taletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.letvaa</span></p><p>of tāḷeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tāḷeti">tāḷeti</a></p>

tāḷāvacara|taa.laavacara|talavacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.laavacara</span></p><p>means of making music; instruments.</p>

tāḷī|taa.lii|tali
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.lii</span></p><p>strike, a blow, in <em>urattāḷiṃ karoti</em> to strike one’s chest (as a sign of grief) Pv-a.39, etc. (see <em><a href="bword://ura">ura</a></em>).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ura">ura</a></p>

tāḷīsa|taa.liisa|talisa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.liisa</span></p><p><em>Flacourtia cataphracta</em> sometimes <em>tāḷissa</em> Ja.iv.286, a small erect tree, its trunk covered with thorns when young, and with a blue-green flower. When young the trunk is covered with very long thorns while older trees have smooth pale bark. The tree is cultivated for its pleasant-tasting plum. The Buddha recommended a medicine made from this tree, probably from the plum Vin.i.203.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Tāḷīsa</em></p><p>Bark: Given together (with the roots of other plants) to women as prenatal and post-natal treatment to purify the blood; for biliousness. Fruit: For biliousness and liver complaints.</p>

tāṇa|taa.na|tana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.na</span></p><p>protection; refuge; shelter.</p>

tāṇa|taa.na|tana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.na</span></p><p>shelter, protection refuge, esp. as tt. of shelter &amp; peace offered by the Dhamma. Mostly in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>leṇa &amp; saraṇa;</em> (see <em>dīpa &amp; abhaya;</em>), in var. contexts, esp. with ref to Nibbāna (see Cnd. s.v.): DN.i.95 (˚ṃ, etc. gavesin seeking refuge); AN.i.155; SN.iv.315 (maṃtāṇa, etc. adj protected by me, in my shelter)<br />■ SN.i.2, SN.i.54, SN.i.55, SN.i.107 (˚ṃ karoti); SN.iv.372 (˚gāmī maggo); AN.iv.184; Snp.668 (˚ṃ upeti); Dhp.288; Ja.i.412 (= protector, explained by tāyitā parittāyitā patiṭṭhā); Sdhp.224, Sdhp.289. Cp tātar &amp; tāyati.</p><p>from Vedic root <em>trā</em>, variation of <em>*ter</em> in tarati. Orig. bringing or seeing through</p><p>See also: dīpa &amp; abhaya;</p>

tāṇatā|taa.nataa|tanata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">taa.nataa</span></p><p>protection, sheltering Dhp.288.</p><p>abstr. of tāṇa</p>

tīha|tiiha|tiha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiha</span></p><p>period of three days.</p>

tīhaṃ|tiiha.m|tiham
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiha.m</span></p><p>adverb a period of three days, for 3 days; usually as cpd. <em>dvīhatīhaṃ</em> 2 or 3 days (see <em><a href="bword://dv%C4%ABha">dvīha</a></em> Ja.ii.103, etc.</p><p>tri + aha</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dvīha">dvīha</a></p>

tīra|tiira|tira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiira</span></p><p>shore; riverbank.</p>

tīra|tiira|tira
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiira</span></p><p>shore, bank Vin.i.1; DN.i.222, DN.i.244; AN.ii.29, AN.ii.50; Dhp.85; Snp.672; Ja.i.212, Ja.i.222, Ja.i.279; Ja.ii.111, Ja.ii.159; Dhs.597; Vb.71 sq.; Vism.512 (orima˚); Pv-a.142, Pv-a.152<br />■ tīra-dassin finding the shore SN.iii.164; AN.iii.368<br />■ a-tīra-dassanī (f.) not seeing the shore (nāvā a ship) Ja.v.75.</p><p>Vedic tiras from <em>*ter</em>, tarati; orig. the opposite bank, the farther side (of a river or ocean), cp. tittha</p>

tīradassi|tiiradassi|tiradassi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiradassi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tīradassin">tīradassin</a></p><p>who sees or finds the shore.</p>

tīradassin|tiiradassin|tiradassin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiradassin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://tīradassin">tīradassin</a></p><p>who sees or finds the shore.</p>

tīrana-pariññā|tiirana-pari~n~naa|tirana-parinna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiirana-pari~n~naa</span></p><p><strong>Tīrana-pariññā:</strong> ‘full understanding by investigating’; see: <em>pariññā.</em></p>

tīrayati|tiirayati|tirayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiirayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tīreti">tīreti</a></p>

tīraṇa|tiira.na|tirana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiira.na</span></p><p>measurement, judgment, recognition, Cnd.413 (variant reading tir˚); Ne.54 (+ vipassanā), Ne.82 (≈ñāṇa), Ne.191; Vism.162<br />■ tīraṇa is one of the 3 pariññās, viz. t˚, pahāna˚, ñāta-pariññā. See under pariññā.</p><p>from tīreti 2</p>

tīreti|tiireti|tireti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiireti</span></p><p>carries through, accomplishes; settles, brings to a conclusion.</p>

tīreti|tiireti|tireti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiireti</span></p><ol><li>to bring through, to finish, to execute (business), to accomplish: karaṇīyaṃ Mil.7; Pv-a.203; kiccaṃ Pv-a.278.</li><li>to measure, judge, recognize, always in formula tūleti tīreti vibhāveti (Nd ii.tul˚ tir˚, etc.) as interpretation of jānāti; pp tīrita (Nd ii.tirita) Pts.ii.200; Nd ii.under ñāta &amp; No. 413.</li></ol><p>Caus. of tarati</p>

tīretvā|tiiretvaa|tiretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiretvaa</span></p><p>settles, brings to a conclusion.</p>

tīriya|tiiriya|tiriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiiriya</span></p><p>adjective dwelling on the banks of… Vin.ii.287.</p><p>from tīra</p>

tīvarā|tiivaraa|tivara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(pl.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tiivaraa</span></p><p>Name of a people in the time of Buddha Kakusandha SN.ii.191.</p>

tūla|tuula|tula
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuula</span></p><p>Down from either trees, creepers or grasses Vin.ii.149. It was used to stuff quilt blankets, pillows and mattresses.</p>

tūla|tuula|tula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuula</span></p><p>tuft of down; pappus, esp. of cotton; Cotton tuft.</p>

tūla|tuula|tula
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuula</span></p><p>tuft of grass, cotton Vin.ii.150 (3 kinds: rukkha˚, latā˚ poṭaki˚); Snp.591 = Ja.iv.127 (vāto tūlaṃ va dhaṃsaye) DN-a.i.87.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://picu">picu</a></em> cotton-wool Vism.282, Vism.285, Vism.404; Dhp-a.iii.202 Kp-a.173.</li><li><em>-puṇṇikā</em> (“stuffed with tuft of cotton” a kind of shoe Vin.i.186.</li></ul><p>Sk. tūla, to <em>*teu</em>, Sk. tavīti, to swell or be bushy, cp. Gr. τύλη swelling; Ags. pol peg</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://picu">picu</a></p>

tūlapicu|tuulapicu|tulapicu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulapicu</span></p><p>cotton; a cotton tuft.</p>

tūlapuṇṇika|tuulapu.n.nika|tulapunnika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulapu.n.nika</span></p><p>stuffed with cotton (of a kind of shoe)</p>

tūlasannibha|tuulasannibha|tulasannibha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulasannibha</span></p><p>cotton-like.</p>

tūlikā|tuulikaa|tulika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulikaa</span></p><p>cotton mattress.</p>

tūlikā|tuulikaa|tulika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulikaa</span></p><p>mattress (consisting of layers of grass or wool: tiṇṇaṃ tūlānaṃ aññatara-puṇṇa-tūlikā DN-a.i.87) Vin.i.192; Vin.ii.150; DN.i.7; AN.i.181.</p><p>der. fr. tūla</p>

tūlinī|tuulinii|tulini
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulinii</span></p><p>A type of tree M.i.128.</p>

tūlinī|tuulinii|tulini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulinii</span></p><p>like cotton.</p>

tūlinī|tuulinii|tulini
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuulinii</span></p><p>the silk-cotton tree MN.i.128.</p><p>Sk. tūlinī</p>

tūri|tuuri|turi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuuri</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://turī">turī</a></p>

tūriya|tuuriya|turiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuuriya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://turiya">turiya</a></p>

tūṇira|tuu.nira|tunira
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuu.nira</span></p><p>= tūṇī, Vism.251.</p>

tūṇī|tuu.nii|tuni
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">tuu.nii</span></p><p>quiver (lit. “carrier”) Ja.ii.403 (dhanuṃ tūṇiñ ca nikkhippa); Ja.v.47.</p><p>Sk. *tūṇa &amp; tūṇī, to; <em>*tI̊n</em>: see under <em><a href="bword://tul%C4%81">tulā</a></em>; cp. Lat. tollo. On ṇ → l. cp. cikkaṇa &amp; cikkhala, guṇa → guḷa, kiṇi → kili, etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://tulā">tulā</a></p>

u
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the sound or syllable u, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh at Vism.495 as expressing origin (= ud).</p>

ubbadhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to kill, destroy Snp.4 (praet. udabbadhi = ucchindanto vadheti Snp-a.18).</p><p>ud + vadhati</p>

ubbaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) only in cpd. dur˚ hard to pull out, difficult to remove Thag.124, Thag.495 = Thag.1053.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>vṛh</em>, i.e. to ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ubbahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to pull out, take away, destroy Snp.583 (udabbahe pot. = ubbaheyya dhāreyya Snp-a.460); Thag.158; Ja.ii.223 (udabbahe = udabbaheyya C.); Ja.iv.462 (ubbahe); Ja.vi.587 (= hareyya C.).</p><p>ud + <em>bṛh</em> or <em>vṛh</em>, see also <em><a href="bword://uddharati">uddharati</a></em></p><p>Ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to carry away, take away, lift (the corn after cutting); only in Caus. ii. <em>ubbahāpeti</em> to have the corn harvested Vin.ii.180 = AN.i.241<br />■ Here belong uddhaṭa and uddharaṇa. Cp. also pavāḷha.</p><p>ud + <em><a href="bword://vahati">vahati</a></em>, although possibly same as ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, in meaning of uddharati, which has taken up meanings of *udbharati, as well as of *udbṛhati and *udvahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uddharati">uddharati</a>, <a href="bword://vahati">vahati</a></p>

ubbandhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to hang up, strangle Vin.iii.73 (rajjuyā); Ja.i.504 (id.); Ja.iii.345; Thig.80; Vism.501; Vv-a.139, Vv-a.207 (ubbandhitu-kāmā in the intention of hanging herself).</p><p>ud + bandhati</p>

ubbandhitvā|ubbandhitvaa|ubbandhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbandhitvaa</span></p><p>having hung up; having strangled.</p>

ubbarī|ubbarii|ubbari
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbarii</span></p><p>fertile soil, sown field; fig. woman, wife Ja.vi.473 (= orodha C.).</p><p>Sk. urvarā, Av. urvara plant</p>

ubbasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ubbisati">ubbisati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbisati">ubbisati</a></p>

ubbattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go upwards, to rise, swell Ja.vi.486 (sāgaro ubbatti). See also next.</p><p>ud + <em>vṛt</em></p>

ubbatteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to tear out Ja.i.199; Mil.101 (sadevake loke ubbattiyante); Dhp-a.i.5 (hadayamaṃsaṃ), Dhp-a.i.75 (rukkhaṃ).</li><li>to cause to swell or rise Ja.iii.361 (gangāsotaṃ); Ja.iv.161 (samuddaṃ).</li><li>(intrs.) to go out of direction, or in the wrong direction Vism.327 (neva ubbaṭṭati na vivaṭṭati; variant reading uppaṭṭati); Dhp-a.iii.155.</li></ol><p>Caus. of ud + <em>vṛt</em>, of which doublet is ubbaṭṭeti; cp. also ubbaṭuma</p>

ubbaṭuma|ubba.tuma|ubbatuma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.tuma</span></p><p>off the road.</p>

ubbaṭuma|ubba.tuma|ubbatuma
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.tuma</span></p><p>adjective going out of its direction, going wrong (or upset?), in phrase; <em>ubbaṭumaṃ rathaṃ karoti</em> to put a cart out of its direction AN.iv.191, AN.iv.193.</p><p>ud + *vṛti (of <em>vṛt</em>) + ma (for mā → mant); cp. Sk. udvṛtta &amp; vṛtimant</p>

ubbaṭṭayati|ubba.t.tayati|ubbattayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.t.tayati</span></p><p>massages, rubs (with unguents)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbaṭṭeti">ubbaṭṭeti</a></p>

ubbaṭṭeti|ubba.t.teti|ubbatteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.t.teti</span></p><p>massages, rubs (with unguents)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbaṭṭayati">ubbaṭṭayati</a></p>

ubbaṭṭeti|ubba.t.teti|ubbatteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.t.teti</span></p><p>to anoint, give perfumes (to a guest), to shampoo Ja.i.87 (gandhacuṇṇena), Ja.i.238 (id.); Ja.v.89, Ja.v.438.</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>vṛt</em>, as doublet of ubbatteti, cp. BSk. udvartayati Divy.12, Divy.36</p>

ubbaṭṭhaka|ubba.t.thaka|ubbatthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubba.t.thaka</span></p><p>misprint in Pp Index as well as at Pp-a.233 for <em><a href="bword://ubbha%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhaka">ubbhaṭṭhaka</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhaṭṭhaka">ubbhaṭṭhaka</a></p>

ubbedha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>height, only as measure, contrasted with <em><a href="bword://%C4%81y%C4%81ma">āyāma</a></em> length, &amp; <em><a href="bword://vitth%C4%81ra">vitthāra</a></em> width Ja.i.29 (Ja.v.219; asīti-hatth˚), Ja.i.203 (yojana-sahass˚); Vv-a.33 (yojana˚), Vv-a.66 (asīti-hatth˚), Vv-a.158 (hattha-sat˚), Vv-a.188 (soḷasa-yojan˚), Vv-a.221, Vv-a.339; Pv-a.113. See also pabbedha.</p><p>ud + vedha of <em>vyadh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyāma">āyāma</a>, <a href="bword://vitthāra">vitthāra</a></p>

ubbedhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be moved, to shake (intrs.), quiver, quake Ja.vi.437 (= kampati C.).</p><p>ud + vedhati = Sk. vyathate</p>

ubbedhena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>intsr.</p><ol><li>in height.</li><li>in depth.</li></ol>

ubbega
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>agitation; alarm; anxiety; shrinking from, shying away from.</p>

ubbega
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>excitement, fright, anguish DN.iii.148; later, also transport, rapture, in cpd (˚pīti); Vism.143; Dhs-a.124; Pp-a 226.</p><p>Sk. udvega, fr. ud + <em>vij</em></p>

ubbega-uttāsabhayāpanūdana|ubbega-uttaasabhayaapanuudana|ubbega-uttasabhayapanudana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbega-uttaasabhayaapanuudana</span></p><p>ubbega-uttāsabhayāpanūdana</p><p>mfn. driving away alarm and fear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbega">ubbega</a></p>

ubbegin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective full of anguish or fear Ja.iii.313 (= ubbegavant C.).</p><p>fr. ubbega</p>

ubbejanīya|ubbejaniiya|ubbejaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbejaniiya</span></p><p>adjective agitating, causing anxiety Ja.i.323, Ja.i.504.</p><p>fr. ubbejeti</p>

ubbejeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p><p>one who frightens or intimidates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p>

ubbejetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p><p>one who frightens or intimidates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p>

ubbejetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ubbejitar &amp; Ubbejetar</p><p>a terrifier, a terror to AN.ii.109 (˚etar); AN.iv.189 (id.); Pp.47, Pp.48 (= ghaṭṭetvā vijjhītvā ubbegappattaṃ karotī ti Pp-a 226).</p><p>n. ag. fr. ubbejeti</p>

ubbejeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to set into agitation, terrify, frighten Mil.388 (˚jayitabba grd.); Pp-a 226.</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>vij</em></p>

ubbejita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ubbejitar">ubbejitar</a></p><p>one who frightens or intimidates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p>

ubbejitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ubbejitar">ubbejitar</a></p><p>one who frightens or intimidates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbejetar">ubbejetar</a></p>

ubbejitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ubbejitar &amp; Ubbejetar</p><p>a terrifier, a terror to AN.ii.109 (˚etar); AN.iv.189 (id.); Pp.47, Pp.48 (= ghaṭṭetvā vijjhītvā ubbegappattaṃ karotī ti Pp-a 226).</p><p>n. ag. fr. ubbejeti</p>

ubbeṭhana|ubbe.thana|ubbethana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbe.thana</span></p><p>an envelope, wrap Ja.vi.508.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>veṣṭ</em>.</p>

ubbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ubbhaṃ &amp; Ubbha˚</p><p>(indecl.) up, over, above, on top Ja.v.269 (ubbhaṃ yojanaṃ uggata); in compounds like <em>ubbhakkhakaṃ</em> above the collar bone Vin.iv.213; <em>ubbhajānumaṇḍalaṃ</em> above the knee Vin.iv.213; <em>ubbhamukha</em> upwards SN.iii.238; Mil.122.</p><p>a doublet of uddhaṃ, see <em><a href="bword://uddha%E1%B9%83">uddhaṃ</a></em> iii.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uddhaṃ">uddhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhajānumaṇḍala|ubbhajaanuma.n.dala|ubbhajanumandala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhajaanuma.n.dala</span></p><p>above the kneecap; what is above the kneecap</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhaṃ">ubbhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhajānumaṇḍalaṃ|ubbhajaanuma.n.dala.m|ubbhajanumandalam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhajaanuma.n.dala.m</span></p><p>above the kneecap</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhajānumaṇḍala">ubbhajānumaṇḍala</a></p>

ubbhajānumaṇḍalikā|ubbhajaanuma.n.dalikaa|ubbhajanumandalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhajaanuma.n.dalikaa</span></p><p>allowing contact above the kneecap</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhajānumaṇḍala">ubbhajānumaṇḍala</a></p>

ubbhakkhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(mf)n.</i></p><p>what is above the collarbone</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhaṃ">ubbhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>facing upwards; turned upwards, upstream</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhaṃ">ubbhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>lifted up; taken out; removed.</p>

ubbhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>drawn out, pulled out, brought out, thrown out or up withdrawn Vin.i.256 (kaṭhina, cp. uddhāra &amp; ubbhāra), Vin.iii.196 (id.); DN.i.77 (cp uddharati); MN.i.383 (ubbhatehi akkhihi); Dhp.34 (okamokata u. = *okamokataḥ u.); Ja.i.268; Pv-a.163.</p><p>pp. of uddharati with bbh for ddh as in ubbhaṃ for uddhaṃ; cp. ubbahati and see also the doublet uddhaṭa</p>

ubbhataka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A mote-hall built by the Mallas of Pāvā. When it was finished they invited the Buddha to be its first occupant. The Buddha went with the monks and spent the night in the hall. It was on this occasion that Sāriputta recited the Saṅgīti Sutta. DN.iii.207<em>ff.</em></p><p>Pāvā3building</p>

ubbhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>birth, origination, production Pgdp.91 (dānassa phal˚). Cp. BSk. udbhāvanā Divy.184 (guṇ˚) Divy.492 (id.).</p><p>ud + bhava</p>

ubbhaṃ|ubbha.m|ubbham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.m</span></p><p>upwards; in height; above.</p>

ubbhaṃ|ubbha.m|ubbham
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.m</span></p><p>Ubbhaṃ &amp; Ubbha˚</p><p>(indecl.) up, over, above, on top Ja.v.269 (ubbhaṃ yojanaṃ uggata); in compounds like <em>ubbhakkhakaṃ</em> above the collar bone Vin.iv.213; <em>ubbhajānumaṇḍalaṃ</em> above the knee Vin.iv.213; <em>ubbhamukha</em> upwards SN.iii.238; Mil.122.</p><p>a doublet of uddhaṃ, see <em><a href="bword://uddha%E1%B9%83">uddhaṃ</a></em> iii.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uddhaṃ">uddhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhaṇḍita|ubbha.n.dita|ubbhandita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.n.dita</span></p><p>loaded, burdened.</p>

ubbhaṇḍita|ubbha.n.dita|ubbhandita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.n.dita</span></p><p>bundled up, fixed up, wrapped up, full Vin.i.287.</p><p>pp. of ubbhaṇḍeti, ud + <em>*bhaṇḍ</em>, cp. bhāṇḍa</p>

ubbhaṭṭhaka|ubbha.t.thaka|ubbhatthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.t.thaka</span></p><p>standing upright</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhaṃ">ubbhaṃ</a></p>

ubbhaṭṭhaka|ubbha.t.thaka|ubbhatthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbha.t.thaka</span></p><p>adjective standing erect or upright DN.i.167; MN.i.78, MN.i.92, MN.i.282, MN.i.308, MN.i.343; AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Pp.55 (ubb˚; = uddhaṃ ṭhitaka Pp-a 233).</p><p>ubbha + ṭha + ka of <em>sthā</em>, prob. contracted fr. ubbhaṭṭhitaka</p>

ubbhida
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ubbhida<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. springing up, welling up.</p><p>ubbhida<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a kind of salt.</p>

ubbhida
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ubbhida<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter kitchen salt Vin.i.202, cp. <em>Vin Texts</em> ii.48.</p><p>Sk. udbhida</p><p>Ubbhida<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective breaking or bursting forth, in cpd. <em>-odaka</em> “whose waters well up”, or “spring water DN.i.74; MN.i.276; DN-a.i.218.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>bhid</em></p>

ubbhidodaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with water swelling up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhida">ubbhida</a></p>

ubbhijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhindati">ubbhindati</a></p>

ubbhijjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to burst upwards, to spring up out of the ground, to well up; to sprout DN.i.74 = MN.iii.93 = MN.iii.26; Ja.i.18 (verse 104); Dhp.339 (ger. ubbhijja uppajitvā Dhp-a.iv.49); DN-a.i.218<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ubbhinna">ubbhinna</a></em>.</p><p>ud + <em>bhid</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhinna">ubbhinna</a></p>

ubbhijjitvā|ubbhijjitvaa|ubbhijjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhijjitvaa</span></p><p>having burst forth, shot up; having welled up.</p>

ubbhindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>breaks through (trans.); breaks through, bursts through (intrans.); springs up.</p>

ubbhinditvā|ubbhinditvaa|ubbhinditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhinditvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbhindati">ubbhindati</a></p>

ubbhinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>springing up, welling up Dhp.i.218.</p><p>pp. of ubbhijjati</p>

ubbhujati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bend up, to lift up (forcibly), ger. <em>-itvā</em> in meaning of “forcibly” Vin.ii.222; Vin.iii.40.</p><p>ud + <em>bhuj</em></p>

ubbhuji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>having pulled up (one’s clothes).</p>

ubbhujitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp (mf)n. impers.</i></p><p>will pull up (one’s clothing)</p>

ubbhujitvā|ubbhujitvaa|ubbhujitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhujitvaa</span></p><p>having pulled up (one’s clothes).</p>

ubbhujitvāna|ubbhujitvaana|ubbhujitvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhujitvaana</span></p><p>having pulled up (one’s clothes).</p>

ubbhāra|ubbhaara|ubbhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhaara</span></p><p>removal, taking away (of the <em>kaṭhina</em>); ending (of the <em>kaṭhina</em> period and its special allowances).</p>

ubbhāra|ubbhaara|ubbhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbhaara</span></p><p>= uddhāra (suspension, withdrawal, removal) Vin.i.255, Vin.i.300; Vin.v.136, Vin.v.175; cp. <em>Vin Texts</em> i.19; ii.157.</p>

ubbigga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>agitated, shuddering; anxious; alarmed; shying away from</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijjati">ubbijjati</a></p>

ubbigga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>agitated, flurried, anxious Vin.ii.184; SN.i.53; Thag.408; Ja.i.486; Ja.iii.313; Mil.23, Mil.236, Mil.340 (an˚); Vism.54 (satat˚); Dhp-a.ii.27 Thag-a.267; Sdhp.8, Sdhp.77.</p><p>Sk. udvigna, pp. of ud + <em>vij</em></p>

ubbijati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is agitated, shudders with alarm; is afraid of (usually + gen.); shrinks from, shies away from</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijjati">ubbijjati</a>, <a href="bword://ubbijjeti">ubbijjeti</a></p>

ubbiji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijati">ubbijati</a></p>

ubbijjanā|ubbijjanaa|ubbijjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbijjanaa</span></p><p>agitation, uneasiness DN-a.i.111. Cp. ubbega.</p><p>abstr. fr. ubbijjati</p>

ubbijjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is agitated, shudders with alarm; is afraid of (usually + gen.); shrinks from, shies away from</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijati">ubbijati</a>, <a href="bword://ubbijjeti">ubbijjeti</a></p>

ubbijjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be agitated, frightened or afraid Vin.i.74 (u. uttasati palāyati); Vin.iii.145 (id.); SN.i.228 (aor. ubbijji); Mil.149 (tasati +), Mil.286 (+ saṃviji) Vism.58<br />caus <em><a href="bword://ubbejeti">ubbejeti</a></em> (q.v.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ubbigga">ubbigga</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Pass. of ud + <em>vij</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbejeti">ubbejeti</a>, <a href="bword://ubbigga">ubbigga</a></p>

ubbijjeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is agitated, shudders with alarm; is afraid of (usually + gen.); shrinks from, shies away from</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijati">ubbijati</a>, <a href="bword://ubbijjati">ubbijjati</a></p>

ubbijji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbijjati">ubbijjati</a></p>

ubbilla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>exhilaration, excitement.</p>

ubbilla
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>elation, elated state of mind MN.iii.159; <em>-bhāva</em> id. DN-a.i.122; Sdhp.167. See next.</p><p>either a secondary formation fr. ubbilāvita, or representing uppilava (uppilāva) for upplava, ud + <em>plu</em> as discussed under ubbilāvita. The BSk. word udvilya Lal.351, 357, or audvilya Divy.82 is an artificial reconstruction from the Pāli, after the equation of Sk dvādaśa → dial. P. bārasa, whereas the original Sk. dv is in regular P. represented by dd, as in dvīpa → dīpa *udvāpa → uddāpa. Müller’s construction ubbilla → *udvela rests on the same grounds, see P. Gr. 12.</p>

ubbilāpa|ubbilaapa|ubbilapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbilaapa</span></p><p>(variant reading uppilāva, which is prob. the correct reading) joyous state of mind, elation Ud.37. See next.</p>

ubbilāvita|ubbilaavita|ubbilavita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbilaavita</span></p><p>happy, elated, buoyant, ltt. frisky; only in compounds <em>-atta</em> rejoicing, exultancy, elation of mind DN.i.3, DN.i.37; Ja.iii.466; Mil.183; DN-a.i.53, DN-a.i.122; and <em>-ākāra</em> id. Dhp-a.i.237. At Vism.158 “cetaso ubbilāvitaṃ stands for ubbilāvitattaṃ, with variant reading BB uppilāvitaṃ. Cp Ja.v.114 (ubbilāvita-cittatā).</p><p>according to the very plausible expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> given by Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 137 sq. for uppilāpita, pp. of uppilāpeti = uplāpeti d. under uppilavati ud + <em>plu</em>; with ll for l after cases like Sk. ālīyate → P allīyati, ālāpa → allāpa etc., and bb for pp as in vanibbaka = Sk. vanīpaka (*vanipp˚)</p>

ubbinaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><p>contrary to the Vinaya; what is not the Vinaya.</p>

ubbinaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective being outside the Vinaya, ex-or un-Vinaya, wrong Vinaya Vin.ii.307; Dpvs.v.19.</p><p>ud + vinaya</p>

ubbirī|ubbirii|ubbiri
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbirii</span></p><p>Ubbirī&gt;</p><p>A bhikkhunī whose verses in the Therīgāthā tell of her lamenting the death of her daughter, Jīva, and reflecting that thousands of her daughters must likewise have died in previous lives. Thus she became enlightened. Thig.51–53</p>

ubbisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“to be out home”, to live away from home Ja.ii.76<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://ubb%C4%81s%C4%AByati">ubbāsīyati</a></em><br />pp <em>ubbisita</em> (˚kāle) ibid.</p><p>better reading variant reading ubbasati, ud + <em>vas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāsīyati">ubbāsīyati</a></p>

ubbādhanā|ubbaadhanaa|ubbadhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaadhanaa</span></p><p>oppression, harassment; paining.</p>

ubbāhana|ubbaahana|ubbahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahana</span></p><p>carrying, lifting, in ˚samattha fit for carrying, i.e. a beast of burden, of an elephant Ja.vi.448.</p><p>fr. ubbahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ubbāhapetabba|ubbaahapetabba|ubbahapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahapetabba</span></p><p>causing to carry.</p>

ubbāhapeti|ubbaahapeti|ubbahapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahapeti</span></p><p>causes to carry; has (the harvest) gathered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāhāpeti">ubbāhāpeti</a></p>

ubbāhapetvā|ubbaahapetvaa|ubbahapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāhapeti">ubbāhapeti</a></p>

ubbāheti|ubbaaheti|ubbaheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaaheti</span></p><p>lifts up; picks out.</p>

ubbāheti|ubbaaheti|ubbaheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaaheti</span></p><p>to oppress vex, hinder, incommodate Ja.v.417 sq.</p><p>hardly to be decided whether fr. ud + <em>vāh</em> (to press, urge), or <em>bṛh</em> or <em>bādh</em>; cp. uddharati 2</p>

ubbāhetvā|ubbaahetvaa|ubbahetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāheti">ubbāheti</a></p>

ubbāhikā|ubbaahikaa|ubbahika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahikaa</span></p><p>the reference (of a dispute) to a committee of selected <em>bhikkhus</em>.</p>

ubbāhikā|ubbaahikaa|ubbahika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahikaa</span></p><p>method of deciding on the expulsion of a bhikkhu, always in instr. <em>ubbāhikāya</em> “by means of a referendum”, the settlement of a dispute being laid in the hands of certain chosen brethren (see <em>Vin Texts</em> iii.49 sq. Vin.ii.95, Vin.ii.97, Vin.ii.305; Vin.v.139, Vin.v.197; AN.v.71; Mhvs.4, Mhvs.46.</p><p>orig. f. of ubbāhika, adj. fr. ubbāheti in abstr. use</p>

ubbāhāpetabba|ubbaahaapetabba|ubbahapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahaapetabba</span></p><p>causing to carry.</p>

ubbāhāpeti|ubbaahaapeti|ubbahapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahaapeti</span></p><p>causes to carry; has (the harvest) gathered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāhapeti">ubbāhapeti</a></p>

ubbāhāpetvā|ubbaahaapetvaa|ubbahapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaahaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbāhāpeti">ubbāhāpeti</a></p>

ubbāsīyati|ubbaasiiyati|ubbasiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaasiiyati</span></p><p>“to be dis-inhabited”, i.e. to be abandoned by the inhabitants Mhvs.6, Mhvs.22 (= chaḍḍīyati C.)<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://ubbisati">ubbisati</a></em>.</p><p>Pass. of ubbāseti, ud + <em>vas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbisati">ubbisati</a></p>

ubbāḷha|ubbaa.lha|ubbalha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbaa.lha</span></p><p>oppressed, troubled, harassed, annoyed vexed Vin.i.148, Vin.i.353; Vin.ii.119; Vin.iv.308; Ja.i.300; Vism.182 (kuṇapa-gandhena); Dhp-a.i.343.</p><p>adj. pp. of ud + bāhati = <em>vāh</em> or more likely of ud + <em>bādh</em></p>

ubbūḷhavant|ubbuu.lhavant|ubbulhavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubbuu.lhavant</span></p><p>see <em>uruḷhavant</em>.</p>

ubha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>*Ubha</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></em>; cp. ubhato &amp; ubhaya.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></p>

ubhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>both ends, both extremes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></p>

ubhato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>in both ways, in both respects; on both sides (of, acc.)</p>

ubhato
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb both, twofold, in both (or two) ways, on both sides usually ˚-, as <em>-bhāgavimutta</em> one who is emancipated in two ways DN.ii.71; <em>Dialogues</em> ii.70, <em>n.</em> 1; MN.i.477 (cp. MN.i.385 ˚vimaṭṭha); SN.i.191; AN.i.73; AN.iv.10, AN.iv.77; Pp.14 Pp.73; Ne.190; <em>-byañjanaka</em> (vyañj˚) having the characteristics of both sexes, hermaphrodite Vin.i.89, Vin.i.136, Vin.i.168 Vin.iii.28; Vin.v.222; <em>-saṅgha</em> twofold Sangha, viz. bhikkhu˚ bhikkhunī Vin.ii.255; Vin.iv.52, Vin.iv.242, Vin.iv.287; Mhvs.32#34. See further Vin.ii.287 (˚vinaye); DN.i.7 (˚lohitaka, cp. DN-a.i.87); MN.i.57 (˚mukha tied up at both ends), MN.i.129 (˚daṇḍakakakaca a saw with teeth on both sides), MN.i.393 (koṭiko pañho SN.iv.323 (id.).</p><p>abl. of *ubha, to which ubhaya &amp; ubho</p>

ubhato-vibhaṅga|ubhato-vibha"nga|ubhato-vibhanga
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhato-vibha"nga</span></p><p>Ubhato-vibhaṅga</p><p>A collective term, comprehending the Bhikkhu-vibhaṅga and the Bhikkhunī-vibhaṅga of the Vinaya Pītaka. Sometimes the word seems to be used as <em>varia lectio</em> for Ubhato-Vinaya. Vin.ii.287</p>

ubhatobhāgavimaṭṭha|ubhatobhaagavima.t.tha|ubhatobhagavimattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatobhaagavima.t.tha</span></p><p>smooth on both sides</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatobhāge|ubhatobhaage|ubhatobhage
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatobhaage</span></p><p>on both sides, in both respects</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatodaṇḍak|ubhatoda.n.dak|ubhatodandak
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatoda.n.dak</span></p><p>with a handle at both ends</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatodhāra|ubhatodhaara|ubhatodhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatodhaara</span></p><p>double edged, two edged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatokoṭika|ubhatoko.tika|ubhatokotika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatoko.tika</span></p><p>double edged, two pronged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatokāja|ubhatokaaja|ubhatokaja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatokaaja</span></p><p>carrying pole with a load at each end</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatolohitakūpadhāna|ubhatolohitakuupadhaana|ubhatolohitakupadhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatolohitakuupadhaana</span></p><p>having a red cushion at each end</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatomukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with an opening or spout on both sides; with a mouth or face on both sides; facing two ways</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatopakkhaṃ|ubhatopakkha.m|ubhatopakkham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatopakkha.m</span></p><p>in both ways, in two ways</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatopassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>both sides</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatosaṅgha|ubhatosa"ngha|ubhatosangha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatosa"ngha</span></p><p>both saṅghas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatovaṇṭikamālā|ubhatova.n.tikamaalaa|ubhatovantikamala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatova.n.tikamaalaa</span></p><p>garland with a stalk on both sides</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatovyañjana|ubhatovya~njana|ubhatovyanjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatovya~njana</span></p><p>having the sexual characteristics of both sexes; hermaphrodite</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhatovyañjanaka|ubhatovya~njanaka|ubhatovyanjanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhatovya~njanaka</span></p><p>having the sexual characteristics of both sexes; hermaphrodite</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhato">ubhato</a></p>

ubhaya-m-antare
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ubhaya-m-antare</p><p>ind. between the two; within the two</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhaya-m-antarena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ubhaya-m-antarena</p><p>ind. between the two; within the two</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) pl.: both; sg.: of both kinds; two fold.</li><li>(n.) both (things); the pair; two things.</li></ol>

ubhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective both, twofold Snp.547, Snp.628, Snp.712, Snp.1106, Snp.1107, Snp.801 (˚ante); Mnd.109 (˚ante) Ja.i.52; Pv-a.11, Pv-a.24, Pv-a.35, Pv-a.51<br />■ nt. <em>-ṃ</em> as adv. in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with ca c’ûbhayaṃ following after 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part of comprehension) “and both” for both-and; and also, alike, as well Dhp.404 (gahaṭṭhehi anāgārehi c’ûbhayaṃ with householders and houseless alike); Pv.i.6#9<br />■ <em>Note.</em> The form ubhayo at Pv.ii.3#10 is to be regarded as fem. pl. of ubho (= duve Pv-a.86).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%83sa">aṃsa</a></em> lit. both shoulders or both parts, i.e. completely thoroughly, all round (˚-) in ˚bhāvita thoroughly trained DN.i.154 (cp. DN-a.i.312 ubhaya-koṭṭhāsāya bhāvito).</li></ul><p>*ubha + ya, see <em><a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṃsa">aṃsa</a>, <a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></p>

ubhayacchikāṇa|ubhayacchikaa.na|ubhayacchikana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhayacchikaa.na</span></p><p>blind in both eyes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhayantarena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>between the two</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhayato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl. adv.</i></p><p>as to both; on both sides</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhayattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>in both places; in both respects.</p>

ubhayattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjverb in both places, in both cases Vin.i.107; AN.iii.64; Dhp.15–Dhp.17; Dhp-a.i.29 (˚ettha), Dhp-a.i.30; Pv-a.130.</p><p>Sk. ubhayatra, fr. ubhaya</p>

ubhayaṃ|ubhaya.m|ubhayam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhaya.m</span></p><p>also, as well (usually following ca)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhayaṃsa|ubhaya.msa|ubhayamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhaya.msa</span></p><p>both parts, both sides</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubhayaṃsabhāvita|ubhaya.msabhaavita|ubhayamsabhavita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhaya.msabhaavita</span></p><p>developed in both parts; practiced for both aspects or objects; (ubhayaṃsa)</p>

ubhayena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i></p><p>on both sides; in both ways</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubhaya">ubhaya</a></p>

ubho
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. pl.</i></p><p>both.</p>

ubho
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective both; nom. acc. ubho SN.i.87; AN.iii.48 = Iti.16; Iti.43 = Snp.661 = Dhp.306; Snp.220 Snp.543, Snp.597; Dhp.74, Dhp.256, Dhp.269; Dhp.412; Mnd.109; Pv.i.7#6 Ja.i.223; Ja.ii.3; Pv-a.13, Pv-a.82 (tā ubho)<br />■ <em>ubhantaṃ</em> both ends, both sides Snp.1042 (see Cnd.169; Snp-a.588 explains by ubho ante)<br />■ gen. <em>ubhinnaṃ</em> SN.i.162; SN.ii.222; Ja.ii.3 instr. <em>ubhohi</em> (hatthehi) Vin.ii.256; Ja.iv.142; loc. <em>ubhosu</em> Snp.778 (antesu); Ja.i.264 (passesu; Pv-a.94 (hatthesu). <em>Note.</em> The form <em>ubhayo</em> at Pv.ii.3#10 is to be regarded as a nom. fem. (= duve Pv-a.86).</p><p>Sk. ubhau, an old remnant of a dual form in Pāli; cp. Gr. α ̓́μφω both, Lat. ambo, Lith. abū, Goth. bai Ohg. beide = E. both. To prep. adv. *amb, *ambi; see <em>abhi</em> &amp; cp. also vīsati</p>

ubhosaṅga|ubhosa"nga|ubhosanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ubhosa"nga</span></p><p>both attachments, attachment to both</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubho">ubho</a></p>

ucca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>high, lofty, tall; of high rank, elevated, noble; loud.</p>

ucca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective high (opp. avaca low) DN.i.194; MN.ii.213; AN.v.82 (˚ṭhāniyaṃ nīce ṭhāne ṭhapeti puts on a low place which ought to be placed high); Pv.iv.7#4 (uccaṃ paggayha lifting high up = uccataraṃ katvā Pv-a.265); Pp.52 Pp.58; DN-a.i.135; Pv-a.176.</p><ul><li><em>-āvaca</em> high and low, various, manifold Vin.i.70, Vin.i.203; Ja.iv.115, Ja.iv.363 (= mahaggha-samaggha C. p. 366); Snp.703, Snp.714, Snp.792, Snp.959; Dhp.83; Mnd.93, Mnd.467; Vv.12#1 (vividha Vv-a.60); Vv.31#1.</li><li><em>-kulīnatā</em> high birth AN.iii.48 (cp. uccā˚).</li></ul><p>For udya, adj. formation from prep. ud above, up</p>

uccaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>high, tall.</p>

uccaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective high Vin.ii.149 (āsandikā a kind of high chair).</p><p>fr. ucca</p>

uccasadda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>making a loud noise, noisy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>height Ja.iii.318.</p><p>fr. ucca = Sk. uccatvaṃ</p>

uccattana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>height</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccattena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>in height</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccavaca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>high and low; great and small; mixed, various</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccavacaṃ|uccavaca.m|uccavacam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccavaca.m</span></p><p>up and down; in various ways; in a changing manner</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccavaca">uccavaca</a></p>

uccavatthuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>having a raised site, a raised foundation or platform</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>accumulating; collection; heap.</p>

uccaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>heaping up, heap, pile, accumulation Dhp.115, Dhp.191, Dhp.192; Vv.47#11 Vv.82#7 (= cetiya Vv-a.321); Dhp-a.iii.5, Dhp-a.iii.9; Dhs-a.41 (pāpassa). <em>-<em>siluccaya</em></em> a mountain Thag.692; Ja.i.29 (verse 209), Ja.vi.272, Ja.vi.278; Dāvs V.63.</p><p>fr. ud + <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em>, see <em><a href="bword://cin%C4%81ti">cināti</a></em>; Sk. uccaya</p><p>See also: siluccaya, <a href="bword://ci">ci</a>, <a href="bword://cināti">cināti</a></p>

uccaṅgamāya|ucca"ngamaaya|uccangamaya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucca"ngamaaya</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha found in a list of Pacceka Buddhas. MN.iii.70</p>

uccaṭhāniya|ucca.thaaniya|uccathaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucca.thaaniya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uccaṭṭhāniya">uccaṭṭhāniya</a></p><p>(one) of high rank</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

uccaṭṭhāniya|ucca.t.thaaniya|uccatthaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucca.t.thaaniya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uccaṭṭhāniya">uccaṭṭhāniya</a></p><p>(one) of high rank</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucca">ucca</a></p>

ucchaṅga|uccha"nga|ucchanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccha"nga</span></p><p>the lap.</p>

ucchaṅga|uccha"nga|ucchanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccha"nga</span></p><p>the hip, the lap Vin.i.225; MN.i.366; AN.i.130 (˚pañña); Ja.i.5, Ja.i.308; Ja.ii.412; Ja.iii.22; Ja.iv.38 Ja.iv.151; Pp.31; Vism.279; Dhp-a.ii.72.</p><p>Sk. utsanga, ts → cch like Sk. utsahate → BSk. ucchahate see <em><a href="bword://ussahati">ussahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussahati">ussahati</a></p>

ucchaṅgapañña|uccha"ngapa~n~na|ucchangapanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccha"ngapa~n~na</span></p><p>“lap brained”, scatterbrained; who does not retain what he has heard</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchaṅga">ucchaṅga</a></p>

ucchecchāmi|ucchecchaami|ucchecchami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchecchaami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

uccheda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>cutting off or out; destruction, putting an end to; annihilation.</p>

uccheda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>breaking up, disintegration, perishing (of the soul) Vin.iii.2 (either after this life, or after kāmadeva life, or after brahmadeva life) DN.i.34, DN.i.55; SN.iv.323; Mnd.324; Mil.413; Ne.95, Ne.112, Ne.160; DN-a.i.120.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhi">diṭṭhi</a></em> the doctrine of the annihilation (of the soul) as opp. to sassata-or atta-diṭṭhi (the continuance of the soul after death) SN.ii.20; SN.iii.99, SN.iii.110 sq; Pts.i.150, Pts.i.158 Mnd.248 (opp. sassati˚); Dhs.1316; Ne.40, Ne.127; Snp-a.523 (opp. atta˚).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81da">vāda</a></em> (adj.) one who professes the doctrine of annihilation (ucchedadiṭṭhi) Vin.i.235; Vin.iii.2; DN.i.34, DN.i.55; SN.ii.18; SN.iv.401; AN.iv.174, AN.iv.182 sq.; Mnd.282; Pp.38.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81din">vādin</a></em> = ˚vāda Ne.111; Ja.v.244.</li></ul><p>fr. ud + <em>chid, chind</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></em> &amp; cp. cheda</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://diṭṭhi">diṭṭhi</a>, <a href="bword://vāda">vāda</a>, <a href="bword://vādin">vādin</a>, <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

ucchedadiṭṭhi|ucchedadi.t.thi|ucchedaditthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchedadi.t.thi</span></p><p>the theory of annihilation (of a self or person)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccheda">uccheda</a></p>

ucchedana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective cutting off, destroying; f. <em>-anī</em> Ja.v.16 (surā).</p><p>fr. ud + <em>chid</em></p>

ucchedavāda|ucchedavaada|ucchedavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchedavaada</span></p><p>professing the doctrine of annihilation.</p>

ucchedin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective an adherent of the ucchedavāda Ja.v.241.</p>

ucchejjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

ucchepaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>connected with throwing away; involving what is thrown away.</p>

ucchepaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>leavings of food MN.ii.7 (variant reading uccepaka with cc for cch as ucciṭṭha: ucchiṭṭha). The passage is to be read ucchepake va te ratā. A diff. connotation would be implied by taking ucchepaka = uñchā, as Neumann does (Majjhima trsl.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> ii.682).</p><p>= ucchiṭṭhaka in sense of ucchiṭṭhabhatta</p>

ucchijja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

ucchijjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

ucchindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cuts off, cuts out; extirpates, destroys.</p>

ucchindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to break up, destroy, annihilate SN.v.432 (bhavataṇhaṃ), AN.iv.17 (fut. ucchecchāmi to be read with variant reading for T. ucchejjissāmi); Snp.2 (pret. udacchida), Snp.208 (ger. ucchijja); Ja.v.383; Dhp.285. Pass. <em><a href="bword://ucchijjati">ucchijjati</a></em> to be destroyed or annihilated, to cease to exist SN.iv.309; Ja.v.242, Ja.v.467; Mil.192; Pv-a.63, Pv-a.130 (= na pavattati), Pv-a.253 (= natthi)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ucchinna">ucchinna</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + <em>chid</em>, see <em><a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchijjati">ucchijjati</a>, <a href="bword://ucchinna">ucchinna</a>, <a href="bword://chindati">chindati</a></p>

ucchinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>cut off, destroyed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

ucchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>broken up, destroyed SN.iii.10; AN.v.32; Snp.746. Cp. sam˚.</p><p>pp. of ucchindati</p>

ucchiṭṭha|ucchi.t.tha|ucchittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchi.t.tha</span></p><p>left, left over, rejected, thrown out; impure, vile Vin.ii.115 (˚odakaṃ); Vin.iv.266 (id.); Ja.ii.83 (bhattaṃ ucchiṭṭhaṃ akatvā), Ja.ii.126 (˚nadī impure; also itthi outcast), Ja.ii.363; Ja.iv.386 (˚ṃ piṇḍaṃ), Ja.iv.388; Ja.vi.508; Mil.315; Dhp-a.i.52; Dhp-a.ii.85; Dhp-a.iii.208; Pv-a.80 (= chaḍḍita), Pv-a.173 (˚bhattaṃ). At Ja.iv.433 read ucch˚ for <em>ucciṭṭha<br />■ an˚</em> not touched or thrown away (of food) Ja.iii.257; Dhp-a.ii.3<br />■ See also <em>uttiṭṭha &amp; ucchepaka</em>.</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>śiṣ</em></p>

ucchiṭṭhaka|ucchi.t.thaka|ucchitthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchi.t.thaka</span></p><p>(fr. ucchiṭṭha) = ucchiṭṭha Ja.iv.386; Ja.vi.63, Ja.vi.509.</p>

ucchiṭṭhodaka|ucchi.t.thodaka|ucchitthodaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchi.t.thodaka</span></p><p>water which has been spat out.</p>

ucchu
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Sugar Cane, Hindi <em>ikshu</em>, a tall perennial grass with sharp-edged leaves and a purplish segmented stem from which a sweet juice can be extracted. Many recent hybrid varieties of sugar cane grow in India today. <em>Carakasaṃhitā</em> mentions two types of sugar cane, the <em>Suśrutasaṃhitā</em> mentions 12 while only one type is specifically named in the Tipiṭaka, <em>ucchagaṇṭhikā</em>. Ja.i.339. One type grown in northern India since ancient times and was probably known to the Buddha’s contemporaries is <em>Saccharum barberi</em>.</p><p>To make sugar, cane stems were crushed in a mill and the juice reduced by boiling Ja.i.339. We read of the creaking sound made by the sugar mill Bv.2.168. It was said of a particularly oppressive king that he “crushed the people like sugar cane in a press” Ja.ii.240. The ancient Indians crystallized sugar, used it as syrup, powdered and made it into solid sticks or balls by mixing it with flour and or ash Ja.iv.17 Vin.i.210 Vin.iii.59 Vin.iv.111. There is a reference to a caravan transporting 500 jars of sugar Vin.i.224. Sometimes the juice was drunk freshly squeezed from the cane Vin.i.246 and an alcoholic beverage was made from it Vin.iv.109. Sometimes molasses (<em><a href="bword://ph%C4%81%E1%B9%87ita">phāṇita</a></em>) was made Ja.ii.339 Vin.i.198. This was eaten straight or diluted with water and drunk Ja.iii.372, or mixed with hot water it was given as a medicine for wind disorders S.i.174. Rice porridge with ghee and sweetened with sugar was a popular food Ja.iv.39, and there was a type of confectionary called suger cake (<em>suguḷa</em>, Ja.vi.524). Baskets were made of cane Ja.iv.363 and temporary shelters were sometimes made out of it Vv.62.5. Ripe sugar cane could be struck by a disease called <em><a href="bword://ma%C3%B1ji%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhik%C4%81">mañjiṭṭhikā</a></em> A.iv.278. Sugar cane was usually propagated from a segment of stem but also from seeds A.i.32.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://phāṇita">phāṇita</a>, <a href="bword://mañjiṭṭhikā">mañjiṭṭhikā</a></p>

ucchu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>sugarcane; the stem of the sugarcane.</p>

ucchu
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>sugar-cane Vin.iv.35; AN.iii.76; AN.iv.279; Mil.46; Dhp-a.iv.199 (˚ūnaṃ yanta sugar-cane mill), Pv-a.257, Pv-a.260; Vv-a.124.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> (ucch˚) top of s. c. Vism.172. <em>-<a href="bword://kha%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Dik%C4%81">khaṇḍikā</a></em> a bit of sugar-cane Vv.33#26. <em>-<a href="bword://kh%C4%81dana">khādana</a></em> eating s. c. Vism.70 <em>-<a href="bword://khetta">khetta</a></em> sugar-cane field Ja.i.339; Vv-a.256. <em>-<a href="bword://ga%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhik%C4%81">gaṇṭhikā</a></em> a kind of sugar-cane, Batatas Paniculata Ja.i.339; Ja.vi.114 (so read for ˚ghaṭika). <em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81la">pāla</a></em> watchman of s<br />■ c. Vv-a.256 <em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%AB%E1%B8%B7ana">pīḷana</a></em>, cane-pressing, Asl.274. <em>-<a href="bword://pu%E1%B9%ADa">puṭa</a></em> sugar-cane basket Ja.iv.363. <em>-<a href="bword://b%C4%ABja">bīja</a></em> seed of s<br />■ c. AN.i.32; AN.v.213. <em>-yantra</em> a sugar-mill Ja.i.339. <em>-<a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a></em> s<br />■ c. juice Vin.i.246; Vism.489; Vv-a.180 <em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ta">vāta</a></em>, Asl.274. <em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81l%C4%81">sālā</a></em>, Asl.274.</p><p>Sk. cp. Vedic Np. Ikṣvāku fr. ikṣu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://khaṇḍikā">khaṇḍikā</a>, <a href="bword://khādana">khādana</a>, <a href="bword://khetta">khetta</a>, <a href="bword://gaṇṭhikā">gaṇṭhikā</a>, <a href="bword://pāla">pāla</a>, <a href="bword://pīḷana">pīḷana</a>, <a href="bword://puṭa">puṭa</a>, <a href="bword://bīja">bīja</a>, <a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a>, <a href="bword://vāta">vāta</a>, <a href="bword://sālā">sālā</a></p>

ucchugaṇṭhikā|ucchuga.n.thikaa|ucchuganthika
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchuga.n.thikaa</span></p><p>A type of sugar cane Ja.i.339 Ja.vi.114. See <em>Ucchu</em>.</p><p>See also: Ucchu</p>

ucchādana|ucchaadana|ucchadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchaadana</span></p><p>cleaning or rubbing (the body with oil or perfumes); rubbing away.</p>

ucchādana|ucchaadana|ucchadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchaadana</span></p><p>rubbing the limbs, anointing the body with perfumes, shampooing DN.i.7, DN.i.76; at the latter passage in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>anicc˚-dhamma</em>, of the body, meaning “erosion, decay” and comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://parimaddana">parimaddana</a></em> abrasion (see <em>about</em> detail of meaning <em>Dial.</em> i.87); thus in same formula at MN.i.500; SN.iv.83; Ja.i.146 &amp; passim; AN.i.62; AN.ii.70 (+ nahāpana); AN.iv.54, AN.iv.386; Iti.111; Thig.89 (nahāpan˚); Mil.241 (˚parimaddana), Mil.315 (+ nahāpana); DN-a.i.88.</p><p>ut + <em>sād</em>, Caus. of <em>sad</em>, sīdati, cp. ussada</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parimaddana">parimaddana</a></p>

ucchādeti|ucchaadeti|ucchadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ucchaadeti</span></p><p>to rub the body with perfumes Ja.vi.298; Mil.241 (+ parimaddati nahāpeti); DN-a.i.88.</p><p>fr. ut + <em>sād</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ucch%C4%81dana">ucchādana</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchādana">ucchādana</a></p>

uccinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>gathers, collects; picks out, selects; sorts out, sets in order</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccināti">uccināti</a></p>

uccinitvā|uccinitvaa|uccinitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccinitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccināti">uccināti</a></p>

uccināti|uccinaati|uccinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccinaati</span></p><p>gathers, collects; picks out, selects; sorts out, sets in order</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccinati">uccinati</a></p>

uccināti|uccinaati|uccinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccinaati</span></p><p>to select, choose, search, gather, pick out or up Vin.i.73; Vin.ii.285 (aor. uccini); Ja.iv.9; Pv.iii.2#4 (nantake = gavesana-vasena gahetvāna Pv-a.185); Dpvs.iv.2.</p><p>ud + cināti</p>

uccā|uccaa|ucca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaa</span></p><p>on high; above; upwards.</p>

uccā|uccaa|ucca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaa</span></p><p>˚- adverb high (lit. &amp; fig.), raised, in foll. compounds.</p><ul><li><em>-kaṇerukā</em> a tall female elephant MN.i.178.</li><li><em>-kāḷārikā</em> id. MN.i.178 (variant reading ˚kaḷārikā to be preferred).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kula">kula</a></em> a high noble family Pv.iii.1#16 (= uccā khattiya-kul-âdino Pv-a.176).</li><li><em>-kulīnatā</em> birth in a high-class family, high rank MN.iii.37; Vv-a.32.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> a loud noise DN.i.143, DN.i.178; AN.iii.30.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sayana">sayana</a></em> a high bed (+ mahāsayana) Vin.i.192; DN.i.5, DN.i.7; cp. DN-a.i.78.</li></ul><p>cp. Sk. uccā, instr. sg. of uccaṃ, cp. paścā behind, as well as uccaiḥ instr. pl<br />■ In BSk. we find ucca˚ (uccakulīna Avs.iii.117) as well as uccaṃ (uccaṃgama Divy.476). It is in all cases restricted to compounds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kula">kula</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://sayana">sayana</a></p>

uccākaṇerukā|uccaaka.nerukaa|uccakaneruka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaka.nerukaa</span></p><p>type of female elephant (with defective tusks?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccākula|uccaakula|uccakula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaakula</span></p><p>family of high rank, a noble family</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccākulika|uccaakulika|uccakulika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaakulika</span></p><p>belonging to a family of high rank</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccākulīna|uccaakuliina|uccakulina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaakuliina</span></p><p>born in a family of high rank</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccākāḷārikā|uccaakaa.laarikaa|uccakalarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaakaa.laarikaa</span></p><p>type of female elephant (with projecting or wide-spreading tusks)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccāliṅga|uccaali"nga|uccalinga
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaali"nga</span></p><p>A type of arthropod or caterpillar. It is mentioned together with the scorpion, the centipede and the spider, as an example of a multi-legged animal Ja.ii.146. The <em>uccāliṇga</em> is also said to be one of the things that can cause an erection in males Vin.iii.38 Vin.iii.112.</p><p>The <em>Kāma Sūtra</em> mentions a procedure men used to enlarge their penis. The soft but irritating bristles of a particular caterpillar were rubbed on the organ and the resulting swelling would become permanent. There may be some conection between this creature and the <em>uccāliṅga</em>.</p>

uccāliṅga|uccaali"nga|uccalinga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaali"nga</span></p><p>maw-worm Vin.iii.38, Vin.iii.112; Ja.ii.146.</p><p>etym.?</p>

uccāliṅgapāṇaka|uccaali"ngapaa.naka|uccalingapanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine id. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaali"ngapaa.naka</span></p><p>kind of hairy caterpillar or centipede.</p>

uccāliṅgapāṇakadaṭṭha|uccaali"ngapaa.nakada.t.tha|uccalingapanakadattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaali"ngapaa.nakada.t.tha</span></p><p>the bite of an <em>uccāliṅga</em></p><p>See also: uccālingāpāṇaka</p>

uccāra|uccaara|uccara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaara</span></p><p>dung; feces; excrement.</p>

uccāra|uccaara|uccara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaara</span></p><p>discharge, excrement, faeces Vin.iii.36 (˚ṃ gacchati to go to stool); Vin.iv.265, Vin.iv.266 (uccāro nāma gūtho vuccati); Dhp-a.ii.56 (˚karaṇa defecation); uccārapassāva faeces &amp; urine DN.i.70; MN.i.83; Ja.i.5; Ja.ii.19.</p><p>Ud + <em>car</em></p>

uccāranā|uccaaranaa|uccarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaranaa</span></p><p>lifting up, raising Vin.iii.121.</p><p>fr. uccāreti</p>

uccārapassāva|uccaarapassaava|uccarapassava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaarapassaava</span></p><p>feces and urine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccāra">uccāra</a></p>

uccārapassāvakamma|uccaarapassaavakamma|uccarapassavakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaarapassaavakamma</span></p><p>defecating and passing urine</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccārapassāva">uccārapassāva</a></p>

uccāraṇa|uccaara.na|uccarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaara.na</span></p><p>lifting up, raising.</p>

uccāresi|uccaaresi|uccaresi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaresi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccāreti">uccāreti</a></p>

uccāreti|uccaareti|uccareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaareti</span></p><p>lifts up, raises.</p>

uccāreti|uccaareti|uccareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaareti</span></p><p>to lift up, raise aloft Vin.iii.81; Vin.iv.147 = Dhp-a.iii.50; MN.i.135<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ucc%C4%81rita">uccārita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + cāreti, Caus. of <em>car</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccārita">uccārita</a></p>

uccāretvā|uccaaretvaa|uccaretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaretvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccāreti">uccāreti</a></p>

uccārita|uccaarita|uccarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaarita</span></p><ol><li>uttered, let out Pv-a.280 (akkharāni).</li><li>lifted, raised Thag-a.255.</li></ol><p>pp. of uccāreti</p>

uccāritakata|uccaaritakata|uccaritakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaritakata</span></p><p>made (formally) lifted up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccāreti">uccāreti</a></p>

uccāsadda|uccaasadda|uccasadda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaasadda</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) making a loud noise, noisy.</li><li>(m.) a loud noise</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

uccāsoṇḍā|uccaaso.n.daa|uccasonda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uccaaso.n.daa</span></p><p>an elevated trunk, a trunk raised high (fig.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccā">uccā</a></p>

ud
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ud-</p><p>prefix in verbal &amp; nominal comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> One half of all the words beginning with u˚ are comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->ns.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with ud˚, which in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> appears modified according to the rules of assimilation as prevailing in Pāli</p><h3>I. <em>Original meaning</em></h3><p>“out in an upward direction”, out of, forth; like ummujjati to rise up out of (water), ujjalati to blaze up high; udeti to come out of &amp; go up; ukkaṇṭha stretching one’s neck out high (cp. Ger. “empor”); uggilati to “swallow up” i.e. spit out</p><p>The opposites of ud-are represented by either <em>ava</em> or <em>o˚</em>; (see under II. &amp; IV. &amp; cp. ucc-âvaca uddhambhāgiya: orambhāgiya), ni (see below) or vi (as udaya: vi-aya or vaya)</p><h3>II.</h3><p>Hence develop 2 clearly defined meanings, viz.</p><ol><li>out, out of, away from-˚aṇha (“day-out”); ˚agga (“top-out”); ˚āgacchati; ˚ikkhati look out for, expect; ˚kantati tear out; ˚khitta thrown off; ˚khipati pick out; ˚gacchati come out; ˚gamaṇa rising (opp. o˚); ˚gajjati shout out; ˚gilati (opp. o˚); ˚ghoseti shout out; ˚cināti pick out; ˚chiṭṭha thrown out; ˚jagghati laugh at, cp. Ger. aus-lachen ˚tatta smelted out; ˚tāna stretched out; ˚dāleti tear out; ˚dhaṭa lifted out, drawn out; ˚disati point out to; ˚drīyati pull out; ˚pajjati to be produced; ˚patti &amp; ˚pāda coming out, origin, birth ˚paṭipatiyā out of reach; ˚paḷāseti sound out; ˚phāsulika “ribs out”; etc. etc</li><li>up (high) or high up, upwards on to (cp. ucca high, uttara higher)-: ˚kujja erect (opp. ava˚); ˚kūla sloping up (opp. vi˚); ˚khipati throw-up, ˚gaṇhāti take up; ˚chindati cut up; ˚javati go up-stream, ˚javana id. (opp. o˚); uñña pride; ˚thāna “standing up” ˚ṭhita got up; ˚tarati come out, go up (opp. o˚); ˚nata raised up, high (opp. o˚); ˚nama e-levation ˚nāmin raised (opp. ni˚); ˚patati fly up; etc. etc</li></ol><h3>III. More specialised meanings</h3><p>(from elliptical or figurative use) are:</p><ol><li>ud˚ = without, “ex-”, e.g. unnangala “outplough” = without a plough; uppabbajita an ex-bhikkhu.</li><li>ud˚ = off, i.e. out of the way, wrong, e.g. uppatha a wrong road, ummagga id</li><li>ud˚ = out of the ordinary, i.e. exceedingly, e.g. ujjangala extremely dusty uppanduka very pale; uppoṭheti to beat hard</li></ol><h3>IV. Dialectical variations &amp; combinations</h3><ol><li>Owing to semantic affinity we often find an interchange between; <em>ud˚</em>; and <strong>ava˚</strong>; (cp. E. break up = break down, grind up or down, tie up or down), according to different points of view. This wavering between the two prefixes was favoured by the fact that o always had shown an unstable tendency &amp; had often been substituted for or replaced by ū, which in its place was reduced to u before a double consonant, thus doing away with the diff. between; <em>ū &amp; u;</em> or <em>o &amp; u</em>. For comparison see the foll.: ukkamati &amp; okk˚ uññā: avañña; uddiyati: odd˚; uḍḍeyya oḍḍ˚; uppīḷeti opīḷ˚; etc., &amp; cp. abbhokirati → abbhukkirati</li><li>the most freq. comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->ns.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> that ud˚ enters into are those with the intensifying prefixes <em>abhi˚</em>; and <em>sam˚</em>; see e.g. abhi ud (= abbhud˚) + gacchati, ˚jalati; ˚ṭhāti; ˚namati etc. sam + ud + eti; ˚kamati; ˚chindati; ˚tejeti; ˚pajjati etc.</li></ol><p>Vedic ud-; Goth. ūt = Ohg. ūz = E. out, Oir. ud-; cp. Lat. ūsque “from-unto” &amp; Gr.; υ ̔́στερος = Sk. uttara</p><p>See also: ava˚</p>

uda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><p>and, and also; or.</p>

uda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable disjunctive part. “or” either singly, as at Snp.455, Snp.955, Snp.1090; Ja.v.478 (variant reading udāhu); Mnd.445 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “padasandhi” with same formula as iti, q.v.); Pv.ii.12#16 (kāyena uda cetasā); or comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with other synonymous particles, as <em>uda vā</em> at Snp.193, Snp.842, Snp.1075; Iti.82 = Iti.117 (caraṃ vā yadi vā tiṭṭhaṃ nisinno uda vā sayaṃ walking or standing, sitting or lying down); Kp-a.191<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://ud%C4%81hu">udāhu</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. uta &amp; u, with Lat. aut (or), Gr.; α ̔ϋτι (again), αὐτάρ (but, or), Goth. auk = Ger. auch to pron base ava˚ yonder, cp. ava ii.</p><p>Uda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(˚-) water, wave. In compounds sometimes the older form udan˚ is preserved (like udañjala, udaññavant), but generally it has been substituted by the later uda˚ (see under <em><a href="bword://udakaccha">udakaccha</a></em>, udakanti, udakumbha, udapatta udapāna, udabindu).</p><p>Vedic udan (nt.), also later uda (but only ˚-), commonly udaka, q.v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udāhu">udāhu</a>, <a href="bword://udakaccha">udakaccha</a></p>

uda va
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>or, or else</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uda">uda</a></p>

udabbadhī|udabbadhii|udabbadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udabbadhii</span></p><p>tore to pieces; destroyed.</p>

udabbahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>brings, procures; picks out.</p>

udabbahe
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. Pot. of ubbahati to draw out, tear out, remove Thag.158; Snp.583 (= ubbaheyya dhāreyya (?) Snp-a.460); Ja.ii.223 (udabbaheyya C.); Ja.vi.587 (= hareyya C.); aor. <em>udabbahi</em> Vin.iv.5.</p><p>ud + <em>bṛh<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, see also <em><a href="bword://abbahati">abbahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbahati">abbahati</a></p>

udabbahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>udabbahi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg. plucked out, rooted up.</p><p>udabbahi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg. having gathered; having lifted up, carried; having picked out.</p>

udabbahiṃ|udabbahi.m|udabbahim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udabbahi.m</span></p><p>plucked out, rooted up.</p>

udabbhadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor.</i></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. of <em><a href="bword://ubbadhati">ubbadhati</a></em> to destroy, kill Snp.4 (= ucchindanto vadhati Snp-a.18).</p><p>ud + <em>vadh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ubbadhati">ubbadhati</a></p>

udabindu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.) a</i></p><p>drop of water.</p>

udabindu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>drop of water MN.i.78; Snp.812; Dhp.121, Dhp.122, Dhp.336; Iti.84 (variant reading udaka˚); Mnd.135; Snp-a.114; Dhp-a.ii.51.</p><p>uda + bindu</p>

udabindunipāta|udabindunipaata|udabindunipata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udabindunipaata</span></p><p>the falling of a drop of water.</p>

udacchidā|udacchidaa|udacchida
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udacchidaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ucchindati">ucchindati</a></p>

udacchidā|udacchidaa|udacchida
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udacchidaa</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. praet. of ucchindati to break up Snp.2, Snp.3 (˚ā metri causa).</p>

udadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>‘what holds water, water-receptacle’, river; sea, ocean.</p>

udadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the sea, ocean SN.i.67; Iti.86; Snp.720; Ja.v.326; Ja.vi.526; Thag-a.289; Vv-a.155 (“udakaṃ ettha dhīyatī ti udadhi”); Sdhp.322, Sdhp.577.</p><p>uda + <em><a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a></em>, lit. water-container</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhi">dhi</a></p>

udagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>excited, exultant; joyful.</p>

udagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective topmost, high, lofty Thag.110; fig. elated, exalted, exultant joyful, happy DN.i.110 (˚citta); Snp.689 (+ sumana), Snp.1028 (id.); Pv.iv.1#55 (attamana +); Pv.iv.5#8 (haṭṭha +); Mil.248; Dhp-a.ii.42 (haṭṭha-pahaṭṭha udagg-udagga in high glee &amp; jubilant); Vism.346 (id.); Sdhp.323. See also der. <em><a href="bword://odagya">odagya</a></em>.</p><p>ud + agga, lit. “out-top”, cp. Sk. udagra</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odagya">odagya</a></p>

udaggatā|udaggataa|udaggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udaggataa</span></p><p>exaltation, jubilation, glee Sdhp.298.</p><p>abstr. fr. udagga</p>

udaggi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Udaggi˚</p><p>in udaggihuttaṃ the fire prepared (for sacrifice) Ja.v.396 (uda-aggihuttaṃ C. wrongly), lit. “the sacrifice (being) out”</p><p>= ud + aggi + hutta, cp. Vedic agnihotra</p>

udaggudagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>very joyful</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udagga">udagga</a></p>

udahāraka|udahaaraka|udaharaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udahaaraka</span></p><p>water-carrier Ja.ii.80.</p><p>uda + hāraka</p>

udahāriya|udahaariya|udahariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udahaariya</span></p><p>adjective going for water Vv.50#9.</p><p>fr. udahāra fetching of water, uda + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

udaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>water.</p>

udaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>water Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.213; DN.ii.15 (˚assa dhārā gushes or showers of w.); Dhp.80 Dhp.145; Ja.i.212; Pv.i.5#7; Pp.31, Pp.32; Mil.318; Vv-a.20 (udake temanaṃ aggimhe tāpanaṃ); Dhp-a.i.289; Dhp-a.iii.176, Dhp-a.iii.256; Pv-a.39, Pv-a.70<br />■ Syn. ambu, ela, jala etc. The comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> form (-˚) is either <em>ūdaka</em> (āsanûdaka-dāyin Ja.iv.435) or <em>-odaka</em> (pādodaka water for the feet Pv-a.78) <em><a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a></em> occurs also in abs. form (q.v.), cp. also <em><a href="bword://oka">oka</a></em> Bdgh.’s kaṃ = udakaṃ, tena dāritan: kandaran ti is a false etymology; DN-a.i.209.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87ava">aṇṇava</a></em> water-flood MN.i.134.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81yatika">āyatika</a></em> a water-pipe Vin.ii.123.</li><li><em>-āḷhaka</em> a certain measure of water, an āḷhaka of w. SN.v.400; AN.ii.55 = AN.iii.337; Vv-a.155.</li><li><em>-ūpama</em> resembling water, like water AN.iv.11 (puggala).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://og%C4%81hana">ogāhana</a></em> plunging into water Ja.iii.235.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a></em> a water flood Vv-a.48.</li><li><em>-orohaka</em> descending into water, bathing; Name of a class of ascetics, lit. “bather” MN.i.281; SN.iv.312; AN.v.263.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://oroha%E1%B9%87a">orohaṇa</a></em> plunging into water, taking a bath, bathing DN.i.167; SN.i.182; AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Ja.iv.299; Pp.55</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kalaha">kalaha</a></em> the “water dispute” Dhp-a.iii.256.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87ava">aṇṇava</a></em> water-flood MN.i.134.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kicca">kicca</a></em> libation of water, lit. water-performance; cleansing, washing DN.ii.15.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%AB%E1%B8%B7%C4%81">kīḷā</a></em> sporting in the w. Ja.vi.420.</li><li><em>-gahaṇasāṭaka</em> bathing-gown Ja.v.477</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%ADa">ghaṭa</a></em> a water pitcher Pv-a.66.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81%E1%B9%ADi">cāṭi</a></em> a water jar Dhp-a.i.52.</li><li><em>-ṭṭhāna</em> a stand for water Vin.ii.120.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://tumba">tumba</a></em> a water vessel Ja.ii.441; DN-a.i.202; Dhp-a.ii.193.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://telaka">telaka</a></em> an oily preparation mixed with water Vin.ii.107.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dantapo%E1%B9%87a">dantapoṇa</a></em> water for rinsing the mouth &amp; tooth-cleaner Vin.iii.51; Vin.iv.90, Vin.iv.92, Vin.iv.233; Ja.iv.69.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://daha">daha</a></em> a lake (of water DN.i.45.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://do%E1%B9%87ik%C4%81">doṇikā</a></em> a water-tub or trough Vin.ii.220.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81r%C4%81">dhārā</a></em> a shower of water Pts.i.125; Ja.iv.351.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://niddhamana">niddhamana</a></em> a water spout or drain Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.123; Dhp-a.ii.37.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nibb%C4%81hana">nibbāhana</a></em> an aquaduct Mil.295.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADiggaha">paṭiggaha</a></em> receiving or accepting water Vin.ii.213.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://patta">patta</a></em> a waterbowl Vin.ii.107; DN.i.80; SN.iii.105.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pu%C3%B1chan%C4%AB">puñchanī</a></em> a towel Vin.ii.122</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://posita">posita</a></em> fed or nourished by water Vv-a.173.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://phusita">phusita</a></em> a drop of water SN.ii.135.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bindu">bindu</a></em> a drop of w. Iti.84 (variant reading for udabindu); Pv-a.99.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bubbula">bubbula</a></em> a w. bubble AN.iv.137 Vism.109, Vism.479 (in comp.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhasta">bhasta</a></em> devoid of water Thag-a.212 (for anodaka Thig.265).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://ma%E1%B9%87ika">maṇika</a></em> a water-pot Vin.i.227; MN.i.354; AN.iii.27; Mil.28; Dhp-a.i.79.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mallaka">mallaka</a></em> a cup for w. AN.i.250.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rakkhasa">rakkhasa</a></em> a water-sprite Dhp-a.iii.74</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://rahada">rahada</a></em> a lake (of w.) DN.i.74, DN.i.84; AN.i.9; AN.ii.105; AN.iii.25; Snp.467; Pp.47.</li><li><em>-rūha</em> a water plant Vv.35#6.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://lekh%C4%81">lekhā</a></em> writing on w. AN.i.283 = Pp.32 (in simile ˚ûpama like writing on w.; cp. Pp-a.215).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ra">vāra</a></em> “waterturn”, i.e. fetching water Dhp-a.i.49.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81raka">vāraka</a></em> bucket SN.ii.118</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ha">vāha</a></em> a flow of water, flowing w. Ja.vi.162.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81haka">vāhaka</a></em> rise or swelling (lit. carrying or pulling along (of water) overflowing, flood AN.i.178.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81hana">vāhana</a></em> pulling up water Vin.ii.122 (˚rajju).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> sound of water Dhs.621</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sar%C4%81vaka">sarāvaka</a></em> a saucer for w. Vin.ii.120.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81%E1%B9%ADaka">sāṭaka</a></em> = sāṭikā Ja.ii.13.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81%E1%B9%ADik%C4%81">sāṭikā</a></em> “water-cloak”, a bathing-mantle Vin.i.292; Vin.ii.272; Vin.iv.279 (= yāya nivatthā nhāyati C.); Dhp-a.ii.61 (T. ˚sāṭaka).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://suddhika">suddhika</a></em> ablution with water (after passing urine) Vin.iv.262 (= mutta-karaṇassa dhovanā C.).</li></ul><p>Vedic udaka, uda + ka (see *<a href="bword://uda">uda</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), of Idg. *ṷed, *ud, fuller form *eṷed (as in Sk. odatī, odman flood odana gruel, q.v.); cp. Sk. unatti, undati to water, udra = Av. udra = Ags. otor = E. otter (“water-animal”) Gr. υ ̔́δωρ water (“hydro”), υ ̔́δρα hydra (“water-animal”) Lat. unda wave; Goth. watō = Ohg. wazzar = E. water Obulg. voda water, vydra otter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://odaka">odaka</a>, <a href="bword://oka">oka</a>, <a href="bword://aṇṇava">aṇṇava</a>, <a href="bword://āyatika">āyatika</a>, <a href="bword://ogāhana">ogāhana</a>, <a href="bword://ogha">ogha</a>, <a href="bword://orohaṇa">orohaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://kalaha">kalaha</a>, <a href="bword://aṇṇava">aṇṇava</a>, <a href="bword://kicca">kicca</a>, <a href="bword://kīḷā">kīḷā</a>, <a href="bword://ghaṭa">ghaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://cāṭi">cāṭi</a>, <a href="bword://tumba">tumba</a>, <a href="bword://telaka">telaka</a>, <a href="bword://dantapoṇa">dantapoṇa</a>, <a href="bword://daha">daha</a>, <a href="bword://doṇikā">doṇikā</a>, <a href="bword://dhārā">dhārā</a>, <a href="bword://niddhamana">niddhamana</a>, <a href="bword://nibbāhana">nibbāhana</a>, <a href="bword://paṭiggaha">paṭiggaha</a>, <a href="bword://patta">patta</a>, <a href="bword://puñchanī">puñchanī</a>, <a href="bword://posita">posita</a>, <a href="bword://phusita">phusita</a>, <a href="bword://bindu">bindu</a>, <a href="bword://bubbula">bubbula</a>, <a href="bword://bhasta">bhasta</a>, <a href="bword://maṇika">maṇika</a>, <a href="bword://mallaka">mallaka</a>, <a href="bword://rakkhasa">rakkhasa</a>, <a href="bword://rahada">rahada</a>, <a href="bword://lekhā">lekhā</a>, <a href="bword://vāra">vāra</a>, <a href="bword://vāraka">vāraka</a>, <a href="bword://vāha">vāha</a>, <a href="bword://vāhaka">vāhaka</a>, <a href="bword://vāhana">vāhana</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://sarāvaka">sarāvaka</a>, <a href="bword://sāṭaka">sāṭaka</a>, <a href="bword://sāṭikā">sāṭikā</a>, <a href="bword://suddhika">suddhika</a>, <a href="bword://uda">uda</a></p>

udakacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf(n).</i></p><p>one) who moves or lives in water; an aquatic creature</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>watery soil, swamp Ja.v.137.</p><p>uda + kaccha</p>

udakacikkhalla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>watery mud; water and mud</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakadoṇikā|udakado.nikaa|udakadonika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakado.nikaa</span></p><p>water bucket, a water-trough</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakahāra|udakahaara|udakahara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakahaara</span></p><p>(~ī). (one) who fetches or carries water; fetching water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakakaṭāha|udakaka.taaha|udakakataha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakaka.taaha</span></p><p>water-jar</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakakicca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>an obligatory or ritual ablution</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakakkhandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>tub of water; a basin for water (round the root of a tree); a a mass, a great quantity of water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakakoṭṭhaka|udakako.t.thaka|udakakotthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakako.t.thaka</span></p><p>tub of water; a basin for water (round the root of a tree); a bathroom, a room containing water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakakāka|udakakaaka|udakakaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakakaaka</span></p><p>Both the Great Cormorant, <em>Phalacrocorax carbo</em>, and the Little Cormorant <em>Phalacrocorax niger</em>. The Pāḷi name means “water crow” and the two birds are called <em>pan kowwa</em> in Hindi which has the same meaning Ja.iii.441. Both these cormorants are black with a small patch of white on the throat. The main difference between them is their size, the first being about as big as a domestic duck and the second about half that size. Also, the great cormorant has a yellow beak while the little cormorant’s is black. Both birds are commonly seen singly or in small flocks on large lakes where they hunt fish by diving and chasing them underwater. According to the Jātaka, the Bodhisattva was once reborn as a cormorant Ja.iii.149.</p>

udakamātikā|udakamaatikaa|udakamatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakamaatikaa</span></p><p>water channel; feed pipe</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakaniddhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>drain, conduit; outlet</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakanisseka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>sprinkling of water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakapuñchana|udakapu~nchana|udakapunchana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakapu~nchana</span></p><p>~ī, feminine an instrument for wiping off water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakasappa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Checkered Keelback, sometimes also <em>udakāsīva</em>, Hindi <em>panika sanp</em>, <em>Xenochrophis piscator</em> Ja.ii.238 Ja.iii.275, a snake with an oval head, slit-like nostrils and five rows of black spots on its yellowish brown-colored body. It grows up to 1.48 meters long, the tail making up to one fourth of its total length. The checkered keelback is India’s most common freshwater snake and frequents lakes, ponds, river banks and paddy fields where it feeds almost exclusively on frogs and fish. It is also an aggressive snake, striking rapidly, with great determination and holding on to its prey tenaciously.</p>

udakasuddhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>believing in or seeking purification through water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakasuddhikā|udakasuddhikaa|udakasuddhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakasuddhikaa</span></p><p>douche</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakasāṭi|udakasaa.ti|udakasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">udakasaa.ti</span></p><p>bathing cloth</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

udakasīvisa|udakasiivisa|udakasivisa
