uggahita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>picked up, taken (improperly); taken up, grasped; comprehended; learnt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggahīta">uggahīta</a>, <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>taken up, taken, acquired Vin.i.212; Ja.iii.168 (˚sippa, adj.), Ja.iii.325; Ja.iv.220; Ja.vi.76; Vism.241. The metric form is <em>uggahīta</em> at Snp.795, Snp.833, Snp.1098 Mnd.175 = Cnd.152 (= gahita parāmaṭṭha).</p><p>pp. of uggaṇhāti</p>

uggahitapaṭiggahitaka|uggahitapa.tiggahitaka|uggahitapatiggahitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggahitapa.tiggahitaka</span></p><p>received formally after being picked up or taken (by oneself</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggahita">uggahita</a></p>

uggahāpeti|uggahaapeti|uggahapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggahaapeti</span></p><p>causes to pick up; makes take or accept; makes learn, teaches</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggahāpetvā|uggahaapetvaa|uggahapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggahaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggahāpeti">uggahāpeti</a></p>

uggahāyati|uggahaayati|uggahayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggahaayati</span></p><p>to take hold of, to take up Snp.791 (= gaṇhāti Mnd.91)<br />ger uggahāya Snp.837.</p><p>poetic form of uggaheti (see uggaṇhati), but according to Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. representing Ved. udgṛbhāyati</p>

uggahīta|uggahiita|uggahita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggahiita</span></p><p>picked up, taken (improperly); taken up, grasped; comprehended; learnt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggahita">uggahita</a>, <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shout out Mnd.172.</p><p>ud + gajjati</p>

uggama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>rising up Sdhp.594.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>gam</em>; Sk. udgama</p>

uggamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>rising, coming up.</p>

uggamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(˚na) neuter going up, rising; rise (of sun &amp; stars) DN.i.10, DN.i.240; SN.ii.268 (suriy˚); Ja.iv.321 (an˚), Ja.iv.388; Pv.ii.9#41 (suriy˚); DN-a.i.95 (= udayana); Dhp-a.i.165 (aruṇ˚); Dhp-a.ii.6 (id.); Vv-a.326 (oggaman˚) Pv-a.109 (aruṇ˚). Cp. ugga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; uggama.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>gam</em></p>

uggamissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggacchati">uggacchati</a></p>

uggantvā|uggantvaa|uggantva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggantvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggacchati">uggacchati</a></p>

uggaputta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>member of the ugga caste</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugga">ugga</a></p>

uggata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>having ascended; risen; issued (from); vomited; high, tall; exalted, eminent.</p>

uggata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>come out, risen; high, lofty, exalted Ja.iv.213 (suriya), Ja.iv.296 (˚atta), Ja.iv.490; Ja.v.244; Pv.iv.1#4 (˚atta one who has risen = uggata-sabhāva samiddha Pv-a.220); Vv-a.217 (˚mānasa); DN-a.i.248; Pv-a.68 (˚phāsuka with ribs come out or showing, i.e. emaciated for upphāsulika). Cp. acc˚.</p><p>pp. of uggacchati</p>

uggatasarīra|uggatasariira|uggatasarira
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggatasariira</span></p><p>A great brahmin. Having made preparations for a great sacrifice, in which numerous animals were to be slaughtered, he visited the Buddha at Jetavana to consult him as to the efficacy of the sacrifice. Three times he told the Buddha that he had heard that the laying down of the fire and the setting up of the sacrificial post bore great fruit. Three times the Buddha agreed that it was so, and Uggatasarīra was about to conclude that the Buddha approved of his sacrifice, when Ānanda intervened and suggested that the Buddha should be asked to explain his meaning and to give his advice as to the efficacy of the sacrifice. The Buddha thereupon declared that there were three fires to be cast off: greed, hatred, and delusion; and three fires that should be honoured: <em>āhuneyyaggi</em>, <em>gahapataggi</em> and <em>dakkhiṇeyyaggi</em>. The <em>āhuneyyaggi</em> was represented by the parents; the <em>gahapata</em>, by wife, children, servants and retainers; the <em>dakkhiṇeyya</em>, by holy men and recluses.</p><p>At the end of the discourse, Uggatasarīra became a convert to the Buddha’s faith and set free the animals destined for the sacrifice. AN.iv.41–46</p>

uggateja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of terrible fiery power; of fierce heat; of powerful energy.</p>

uggatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in all Pv. readings is to be read <em>uttatta˚</em>; thus at Pv.iii.3#2; Pv-a.10, Pv-a.188.</p>

uggatthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Ja.vi.590 means a kind of ornament or trinket, it should prob. be read <em>ugghaṭṭana</em> [fr. ghaṭṭeti lit. “tinkling”, i.e. a bangle.</p>

uggavādi|uggavaadi|uggavadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggavaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uggavādin">uggavādin</a></p><p>speaking powerfully, debating fiercely.</p>

uggavādin|uggavaadin|uggavadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggavaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uggavādin">uggavādin</a></p><p>speaking powerfully, debating fiercely.</p>

uggayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggañchi|ugga~nchi|ugganchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugga~nchi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggacchati">uggacchati</a></p>

