uggaṇhāpeti|ugga.nhaapeti|ugganhapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugga.nhaapeti</span></p><p>causes to pick up; makes take or accept; makes learn, teaches</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggaṇhāpetvā|ugga.nhaapetvaa|ugganhapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugga.nhaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāpeti">uggaṇhāpeti</a></p>

uggaṇhāti|ugga.nhaati|ugganhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugga.nhaati</span></p><p>picks up, takes; picks up (improperly), takes (what has not been formally offered, cf. <em>patiggaṇhāti</em>); takes up, accepts; grasps; takes hold of mentally, grasps, comprehends; learns.</p>

uggaṇhāti|ugga.nhaati|ugganhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugga.nhaati</span></p><p>to take up, acquire, learn [cp. BSk. udgṛhṇāti in same sense, e.g. Divy.18 Divy.77 etc.] Snp.912 (uggahaṇanta = uggahaṇanti = uggaṇhanti Snp-a.561); imper. <em>uggaṇha</em> Ja.ii.30 (sippaṃ); <em>uggaṇhāhi</em> Mil.10 (mantāni); ger. <em><a href="bword://uggayha">uggayha</a></em> Snp.832 Snp.845; Mnd.173<br />caus <em>uggaheti</em> in same meaning Sdhp.520; aor. <em>uggahesi</em> Pv.iii.5#4 (nakkhatta-yogaṃ = akari Pv-a.198); ger. <em><a href="bword://uggahetv%C4%81">uggahetvā</a></em> Ja.v.282, Vv-a.98 (vipassanākammaṭṭhānaṃ); infin. <em>uggahetuṃ</em> Vv-a.138 (sippaṃ to study a craft)<br />caus 2 <em><a href="bword://ugga%E1%B9%87h%C4%81peti">uggaṇhāpeti</a></em> to instruct Ja.v.217; Ja.vi.353<br />pp <em><a href="bword://uggahita">uggahita</a></em> (q.v.). See also <em><a href="bword://uggah%C4%81yati">uggahāyati</a></em><br />■ A peculiar ppr. med. is <em><a href="bword://ugg%C4%81ham%C4%81na">uggāhamāna</a></em> going or wanting to learn DN-a.i.32 (cp. <em><a href="bword://ugg%C4%81haka">uggāhaka</a></em>).</p><p>ud + <em>gṛh</em>, see gaṇhāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggayha">uggayha</a>, <a href="bword://uggahetvā">uggahetvā</a>, <a href="bword://uggaṇhāpeti">uggaṇhāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://uggahita">uggahita</a>, <a href="bword://uggahāyati">uggahāyati</a>, <a href="bword://uggāhamāna">uggāhamāna</a>, <a href="bword://uggāhaka">uggāhaka</a></p>

ugghara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uggharat">uggharat</a></p><p>(or uggharanta) dripping; oozing.</p>

uggharanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(or ugghara(t)) dripping; oozing.</p>

uggharat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uggharat">uggharat</a></p><p>(or uggharanta) dripping; oozing.</p>

uggharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to ooze Thag.394 = Dhp-a.iii.117.</p><p>ud + <em>kṣar</em></p>

ugghaṃsetabba|uggha.msetabba|ugghamsetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.msetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghaṃseti">ugghaṃseti</a></p>

ugghaṃseti|uggha.mseti|ugghamseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.mseti</span></p><p>rubs; scratches.</p>

ugghaṃseti|uggha.mseti|ugghamseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.mseti</span></p><p>to rub Vin.ii.106. - pp. <em>ugghaṭṭha</em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + <em>ghṛṣ</em>, see *<a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%83sati">ghaṃsati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ghaṃsati">ghaṃsati</a></p>

ugghaṭeti|uggha.teti|ugghateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.teti</span></p><p>opens, unlocks; unveils, reveals</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghāṭeti">ugghāṭeti</a></p>

ugghaṭeti|uggha.teti|ugghateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.teti</span></p><p>to open, reveal (? so Hardy in Index to Nett) Ne.9; <em>ugghaṭiyati &amp; ugghaṭanā</em> ibid. Ugghatta (Ugghattha?)</p><p>ud + ghaṭati</p>

ugghaṭita|uggha.tita|ugghatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.tita</span></p><p>adjective striving, exerting oneself; keen, eager in cpd <em>-ññū</em> of quick understanding AN.ii.135; Pp.41; Ne.7–Ne.9, Ne.125; DN-a.i.291.</p><p>pp. of ud + ghaṭati; cp. BSk. udghaṭaka skilled Divy.3, Divy.26 and phrase at Mvu.iii.260 udghaṭitajña</p>

ugghaṭṭa|uggha.t.ta|ugghatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.t.ta</span></p><p>rubbed; scratched</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghaṭṭha">ugghaṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://ugghaṃseti">ugghaṃseti</a></p>

ugghaṭṭa|uggha.t.ta|ugghatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.t.ta</span></p><p>Ugghaṭṭa (Ugghaṭṭha?)</p><p>knocked crushed, rubbed against, only in phrase <em>ughaṭṭa-pāda</em> foot-sore Snp.980 (= maggakkamaṇena ghaṭṭa-pādatala etc Snp-a.582); Ja.iv.20 (ṭṭh; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by uṇha-vālukāya ghaṭṭapāda); Ja.v.69 (= raj okiṇṇa-pāda C. not to the point).</p><p>should be pp. of ugghaṃsati = Sk. udghṛṣṭa, see *<a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%83sati">ghaṃsati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, but taken by Bdhgh. either as pp. of or an adj. der. fr. ghaṭṭ, see <em><a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADeti">ghaṭṭeti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ghaṃsati">ghaṃsati</a>, <a href="bword://ghaṭṭeti">ghaṭṭeti</a></p>

ugghaṭṭapāda|uggha.t.tapaada|ugghattapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.t.tapaada</span></p><p>with rubbed feet, footsore</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghaṭṭa">ugghaṭṭa</a></p>

ugghaṭṭha|uggha.t.tha|ugghattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.t.tha</span></p><p>rubbed; scratched</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghaṭṭa">ugghaṭṭa</a>, <a href="bword://ugghaṃseti">ugghaṃseti</a></p>

ugghaṭṭha?|uggha.t.tha?|ugghattha?
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggha.t.tha?</span></p><p>Ugghaṭṭa (Ugghaṭṭha?)</p><p>knocked crushed, rubbed against, only in phrase <em>ughaṭṭa-pāda</em> foot-sore Snp.980 (= maggakkamaṇena ghaṭṭa-pādatala etc Snp-a.582); Ja.iv.20 (ṭṭh; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by uṇha-vālukāya ghaṭṭapāda); Ja.v.69 (= raj okiṇṇa-pāda C. not to the point).</p><p>should be pp. of ugghaṃsati = Sk. udghṛṣṭa, see *<a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%83sati">ghaṃsati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, but taken by Bdhgh. either as pp. of or an adj. der. fr. ghaṭṭ, see <em><a href="bword://gha%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADeti">ghaṭṭeti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ghaṃsati">ghaṃsati</a>, <a href="bword://ghaṭṭeti">ghaṭṭeti</a></p>

ugghosanā|ugghosanaa|ugghosana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghosanaa</span></p><p>proclamation DN-a.i.310.</p><p>abstr. fr. ugghoseti, cp. ghosanā</p>

ugghoseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to shout out, announce, proclaim Ja.i.75; Dhp-a.ii.94; Pv-a.127.</p><p>ud + ghoseti</p>

ugghāta|ugghaata|ugghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaata</span></p><p>shaking, jolting; jolt, jerk Vin.ii.276 (yān˚); Ja.vi.253 (an˚); Dhp-a.iii.283 (yān˚).</p><p>ud + ghāta</p>

ugghāti|ugghaati|ugghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaati</span></p><ol><li>shaking, shock Vv-a.36.</li><li>striking, conquering; victory, comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://nigh%C4%81ti">nighāti</a></em> Snp.828; Mnd.167; Snp-a.541; Ne.110 (T. reads ugghāta˚).</li></ol><p>fr. ud + ghāta</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nighāti">nighāti</a></p>

ugghātita|ugghaatita|ugghatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaatita</span></p><p>elated; stirred up, overexcited; puffed-up, swollen (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghāṭita">ugghāṭita</a></p>

ugghātita|ugghaatita|ugghatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaatita</span></p><p>struck, killed AN.iii.68.</p><p>pp. of ugghāteti, denom. fr. udghāta</p>

ugghāṭana|ugghaa.tana|ugghatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tana</span></p><p>opening.</p>

ugghāṭana|ugghaa.tana|ugghatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tana</span></p><p>? that which can be removed, in <em>-kiṭikā</em> a curtain to be drawn aside Vin.ii.153 (cp. Vin Texts iii.174, 176). Ch s. v. gives “rope &amp; bucket of a well” as meaning (kavāṭaṃ anugghāṭeti). Cp. <em>ugghaṭanā</em>.</p><p>fr. ugghāṭeti</p>

ugghāṭanakiṭika|ugghaa.tanaki.tika|ugghatanakitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tanaki.tika</span></p><p>or ~ā feminine a screen that can be opened; a shutter</p><p>See also: ugghātana</p>

ugghāṭeti|ugghaa.teti|ugghateti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.teti</span></p><p>opens, unlocks; unveils, reveals</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghaṭeti">ugghaṭeti</a></p>

ugghāṭeti|ugghaa.teti|ugghateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.teti</span></p><p>to remove, take away, unfasten, abolish, put an end to Vin.ii.148 (tālāni), Vin.ii.208 (ghaṭikaṃ); Vin.iv.37; Ja.ii.31; Ja.vi.68; Mil.140 (bhava-paṭisandhiṃ), Mil.371; Vism.374<br />caus 2 <em>ugghāṭāpeti</em> to have opened Ja.v.381.</p><p>for ugghaṭṭeti, ud + ghaṭṭ but BSk. udghāṭayati Divy.130</p>

ugghāṭetvā|ugghaa.tetvaa|ugghatetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghāṭeti">ugghāṭeti</a></p>

ugghāṭita|ugghaa.tita|ugghatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tita</span></p><p>elated; stirred up, overexcited; puffed-up, swollen (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ugghātita">ugghātita</a></p>

ugghāṭita|ugghaa.tita|ugghatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tita</span></p><p>opened Mil.55; Dhp-a.i.134.</p><p>pp. of ugghāṭeti</p>

ugghāṭitaññu|ugghaa.tita~n~nu|ugghatitannu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ugghaa.tita~n~nu</span></p><p>understanding what is revealed; understanding what is (merely) unveiled, taught briefly; of very swift understanding</p><p>See also: ugghāteti</p>

uggilati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>spits out, vomits; gets rid of.</p>

uggilati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= uggirati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, i.e. to spit out (opp. ogilati) MN.i.393; SN.iv.323; Ja.iii.529; Mil.5; Pv-a.283.</p>

uggira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>or neuter raising.</p>

uggiranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggirati">uggirati</a></p>

uggirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lifts, raises (a weapon).</p>

uggirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uggirati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to vomit up (“swallow up”) to spit out Ud.14 (uggiritvāna); DN-a.i.41 (uggāraṃ uggiranto). Cp. BSk. prodgīrṇa cast out Divy.589.</p><p>Sk. udgirati, ud + <em>gṛ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>; but BSk. udgirati in meaning to sing, chant, utter, formation fr. <em>gṛ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> instead of <em>gṛ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, pres. gṛṇāti; in giraṃ udgirati Jtm.31#26<br />■ The by-form uggirati is uggilati with interchange of I̊ and ṛ roots *gr̥ &amp; *gI̊, see <em><a href="bword://gala">gala</a></em> &amp; gilati</p><p>Uggirati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to lift up, carry Vin.iv.147 = Dhp-a.iii.50 (talasattikaṃ expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by uccāreti) Ja.i.150 (āvudhāni); Ja.vi.460, Ja.vi.472. Cp. sam˚.</p><p>cp. Sk. udgurate, ud + <em>gur</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gala">gala</a></p>

uggirita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggirati">uggirati</a></p>

uggiriṃsu|uggiri.msu|uggirimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggiri.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggirati">uggirati</a></p>

uggāhaka|uggaahaka|uggahaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggaahaka</span></p><p>adjective noun one who is eager to learn Ja.v.148 [cp. Mvu.iii.373 <em>ogrāhaka</em> in same context].</p><p>fr. ud + <em>gṛh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ugga%E1%B9%87h%C4%81ti">uggaṇhāti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggāhamāna|uggaahamaana|uggahamana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggaahamaana</span></p><p>UggāhamānaSamaṇamaṇḍikāputtaSamaṇamuṇḍikāputta</p><p>A Paribbājaka. Once when he was staying near Sāvatthī in Mallikā’s pleasance at the Samayappavādaka hall, the carpenter, Pañcakaṅga, on his way to see the Buddha, visited him and had a conversation with him, which conversation Pañcakaṅga later reported to the Buddha. MN.ii.22<em>f.</em></p>

uggāhamāna|uggaahamaana|uggahamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggaahamaana</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ugga%E1%B9%87h%C4%81ti">uggaṇhāti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uggaṇhāti">uggaṇhāti</a></p>

uggāra|uggaara|uggara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggaara</span></p><p>spitting out, vomiting, ejection Vism.54; DN-a.i.41; Kp-a.61.</p><p>ud + <em>gṛ</em>; or <em>*gḷ</em> to swallow, see <em><a href="bword://gala">gala</a></em> &amp; gilati; lit. to swallow up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gala">gala</a></p>

uggīva|uggiiva|uggiva
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uggiiva</span></p><p>neckband to hold a basket hanging down Ja.vi.562 (uggīvañ c’âpi aṃsato = aṃsakūṭe pacchi-lagganakaṃ C.).</p><p>ud + gīva</p>

uhuṅkara|uhu"nkara|uhunkara
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uhu"nkara</span></p><p>Perhaps a generic word for owls or a the name of a particular type of owl J.vi.538. See <em>Kosika</em>.</p><p>See also: Kosika</p>

uhuṅkara|uhu"nkara|uhunkara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uhu"nkara</span></p><p>an owl (lit. “uhu”-maker) Ja.vi.538 (= ulūka C.).</p><p>onomat. uhu + kara, see under <em><a href="bword://ul%C5%ABka">ulūka</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulūka">ulūka</a></p>

ujjagghanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. feminine</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjagghati">ujjagghati</a></p>

ujjagghati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>laughs at; laughs loudly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjaggheti">ujjaggheti</a></p>

ujjagghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to laugh at, deride, mock, make fun of Vin.iii.128; Thig.74 (spelt jjh = hasati Thag-a.78); AN.iii.91 (ujjh˚, variant reading ujj˚) = Pp.67 (= pāṇiṃ paharitvā mahāhasitaṃ hasati Pp-a.249).</p><p>ud + jagghati</p>

ujjaggheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>laughs at; laughs loudly</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjagghati">ujjagghati</a></p>

ujjagghi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>loud laughter.</p>

ujjagghikā|ujjagghikaa|ujjagghika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjagghikaa</span></p><p>(from ujjagghati) loud laughter.</p>

ujjagghiyamāna|ujjagghiyamaana|ujjagghiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjagghiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjagghati">ujjagghati</a></p>

ujjaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ujjahat">ujjahat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjahati">ujjahati</a></p>

ujjahat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ujjahat">ujjahat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjahati">ujjahati</a></p>

ujjahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>abandons, gives up.</p>

ujjahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to give up, let go; imper. ujjaha SN.i.188; Thig.19; Snp.342.</p><p>ud + jahati</p>

ujjala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective blazing, flashing; bright, beautiful Ja.i.220; Dāvs ii.63.</p><p>ud + <em>jval</em>, see <em><a href="bword://jalati">jalati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://jalati">jalati</a></p>

ujjalati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to blaze up, shine forth Vin.i.31; Vv-a.161 (+ jotati)<br />caus <em>ujjāleti</em> to make shine, to kindle Vin.i.31; Mil.259; Vism.428; Thag-a.69 (Tha-ap.14, read dīpāṃ ujjālayiṃ); Vv-a.51 (padīpaṃ).</p><p>ud + jalati, <em>jval</em></p>

ujjalayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cause to blaze up; kindles; lights</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjaleti">ujjaleti</a></p>

ujjaletabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjaleti">ujjaleti</a></p>

ujjaleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cause to blaze up; kindles; lights</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjalayati">ujjalayati</a></p>

ujjaliyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is kindled; is lit</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjalayati">ujjalayati</a></p>

ujjaliṃsu|ujjali.msu|ujjalimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjali.msu</span></p><p>were shining; were blazing up.</p>

ujjava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>moving up (of the yarn? a spinning term).</p>

ujjava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “running up”, in cpd. ujjav-ujjava a certain term in the art of spinning or weaving Vin.iv.300, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by “yattakaṃ patthena (patthana?) añcitaṃ hoti tasmi takkamhi vedhite”.</p><p>ud + java</p>

ujjavajjava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>each moving up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjava">ujjava</a></p>

ujjavanikā|ujjavanikaa|ujjavanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n). &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjavanikaa</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) going upstream.</li><li>(f.) the going upstream.</li></ol>

ujjavanikāya|ujjavanikaaya|ujjavanikaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjavanikaaya</span></p><p>fem. of ujjavanaka used as adv. up-stream, lit “running up” Vin.ii.290 Vin.iv.65 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of uddhaṃgāmin, opp. ojavanikāya).</p><p>ud + javanaka, q.v.</p>

ujjavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go up-stream Vin.ii.301.</p><p>ud + javati</p>

ujjavi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>went upstream.</p>

ujjaviṃsu|ujjavi.msu|ujjavimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjavi.msu</span></p><p>went upstream.</p>

ujjaya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Ujjaya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, mentioned in the list in the Isigili Sutta. MN.iii.70</p><p>Ujjaya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk whose verse is in the Theragāthā. Thag.67</p><p>Ujjaya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A brahmin. He once went to the Buddha and asked him if he thought well of sacrifices. The Buddha replied that he was opposed to sacrifices which involved the slaughter of animals, but sacrifices not necessitating butchery, such as, for instance, a long-established charity, an oblation for the welfare of the family, had his approval. AN.ii.42</p><p>The same Nikāya AN.iv.285<em>f.</em> records another visit of Ujjaya wherein he tells the Buddha that he wishes to observe a period of retreat, and asks for a teaching which will bring welfare both in this world and in the next.</p>

ujjaṅgala|ujja"ngala|ujjangala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujja"ngala</span></p><p>hard, barren soil; a very sandy and deserted place DN.ii.146 (˚nagaraka, trsl. “town in the midst of a jungle”, cp. <em>Dial.</em> ii.161); Ja.i.391; Vv.85#5 (= ukkaṃsena jangala i.e. exceedingly dusty or sandy dry); Pv.ii.9#70 (spelt ujjhangala, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by ativiya-thaddhabhūmibhāga at Pv-a.139); Vism.107. Also in BSk. ujjangala e.g. Mvu.ii.207.</p><p>ud + jangala</p>

ujjaṅgalanagaraka|ujja"ngalanagaraka|ujjangalanagaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujja"ngalanagaraka</span></p><p>little wasteland town, a little rough-country town.</p>

ujjenī|ujjenii|ujjeni
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjenii</span></p><p>The capital of Avanti. In the Buddha’s time, Caṇḍapajjota Vin.i.276 was king of Ujjenī and there was friendly intercourse between that city and Māgadha, whose king was Seniya Bimbisāra. After Bimbisāra’s death, however, Pajjota seems to have contemplated a war against Ajātasathu. MN.iii.7</p><p>The road taken by Bāvarī’s disciples ran through Ujjenī. Snp.1011</p><p>Ujjenī was also the birthplace of Isidāsī. Thig.405</p><p>We find Māhissatī mentioned just before Ujjenī among the places passed by Bāvarī’s pupils on their way to Sāvatthī. Snp.1011</p><p>23.182778, 75.7772221town</p>

ujjhaggati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ujjagghati">ujjagghati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjagghati">ujjagghati</a></p>

ujjhaggikā|ujjhaggikaa|ujjhaggika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaggikaa</span></p><p>loud laughter Vin.ii.213, cp. Vin.iv.187.</p><p>fr. ujjagghati, spelling varies</p>

ujjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ujjhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. abandons, leaves off; lets go, discharges; casts out, removes.</p><p>ujjhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to forsake, leave, give up Ja.vi.138; Dāvs ii.86.</li><li>to sweep or brush away Ja.vi.296</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://ujjhita">ujjhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. ujjhati, <em>ujjh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhita">ujjhita</a></p>

ujjhatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>complaining; indignation.</p>

ujjhatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>irritation, discontent AN.iv.223, AN.iv.467 (variant reading ujj˚) cp. <em><a href="bword://ujjh%C4%81na">ujjhāna</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ud + jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, corresponding to a Sk. *ud-dhyāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāna">ujjhāna</a></p>

ujjhattibala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>whose strength is his indignation.</p>

ujjhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>left, abandoned; cast out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhati">ujjhati</a></p>

ujjhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>destitute, forsaken; thrown out, cast away MN.i.296 (+ avakkhitta); Thag.315 (itthi); Thag.2, Thag.386 (cp. Thag-a.256 vātakkhitto viya yo koci dahano) Dhp.58 (= chaḍḍita of sweepings Dhp-a.i.445); Ja.iii.499 Ja.v.302; Ja.vi.51. Uncha &amp; Uncha</p><p>pp. of ujjhati</p>

ujjhitvā|ujjhitvaa|ujjhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhati">ujjhati</a></p>

ujjhāna|ujjhaana|ujjhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaana</span></p><ol><li>taking offence, captiousness Dhp.253 (= paresaṃ randha-gavesitāya Dhp-a.iii.377); Mil.352 (an˚-bahula).</li><li>complaining, wailing Ja.iv.287.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-saññin, -saññika</em> irritable SN.i.23; Thag.958; Vin.ii.214 cp. Vin.iv.194.; Dpvs.ii.6; Dhp-a.iii.376 (˚saññitā irritability).</li></ul><p>ud + jhāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> or jhāna<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->?</p>

ujjhānasaññi|ujjhaanasa~n~ni|ujjhanasanni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaanasa~n~ni</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ujjhānasaññin">ujjhānasaññin</a></p><p>whose ideas are (based on) complaining or faultfinding; conscious of cause for indignation.</p>

ujjhānasaññika|ujjhaanasa~n~nika|ujjhanasannika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaanasa~n~nika</span></p><p>whose ideas are (based on) complaining or faultfinding; conscious of cause for indignation.</p>

ujjhānasaññikā|ujjhaanasa~n~nikaa|ujjhanasannika
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaanasa~n~nikaa</span></p><p>The name given to a group of devas who once visited the Buddha at Jetavana late at night. They charged the Buddha with inconsistency, but later, begging his forgiveness, they were pardoned by him. SN.i.23–25</p>

ujjhānasaññin|ujjhaanasa~n~nin|ujjhanasannin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaanasa~n~nin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ujjhānasaññin">ujjhānasaññin</a></p><p>whose ideas are (based on) complaining or faultfinding; conscious of cause for indignation.</p>

ujjhāpana|ujjhaapana|ujjhapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapana</span></p><p>complaining; making indignant or contemptuous; causing indignation or ill-will.</p>

ujjhāpana|ujjhaapana|ujjhapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapana</span></p><p>stirring up, provoking Ja.v.91 (devat˚), Ja.v.94 (˚kamma).</p><p>fr. ud + jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> or jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to burn, to which jhāpeti to bring to ruin etc.? cp. ujjhāna</p>

ujjhāpanaka|ujjhaapanaka|ujjhapanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapanaka</span></p><p>complaining; causing indignation or ill-will.</p>

ujjhāpanaka|ujjhaapanaka|ujjhapanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapanaka</span></p><p>adjective one who stirs up another to discontent Vin.iv.38.</p><p>fr. ujjhāpana</p>

ujjhāpayati|ujjhaapayati|ujjhapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapayati</span></p><p>makes a complaint (to, gen. or acc.); makes someone (acc.) contemptuous or indignant (against, acc.); creates ill-will (against, acc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāpeti">ujjhāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhāpenta|ujjhaapenta|ujjhapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāpeti">ujjhāpeti</a></p>

ujjhāpesi|ujjhaapesi|ujjhapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāpeti">ujjhāpeti</a></p>

ujjhāpetabba|ujjhaapetabba|ujjhapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp (mf)n. impers;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhāpeti|ujjhaapeti|ujjhapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapeti</span></p><p>makes a complaint (to, gen. or acc.); makes someone (acc.) contemptuous or indignant (against, acc.); creates ill-will (against, acc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāpayati">ujjhāpayati</a>, <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhāpeti|ujjhaapeti|ujjhapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapeti</span></p><p>to harass, vex, irritate MN.i.126; SN.i.209 (“give occasion for offence”); Vin.iv.38 (cp. p. 356); Ja.v.286; Pv-a.266.</p><p>Caus. of ujjhāyati</p>

ujjhāpita|ujjhaapita|ujjhapita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp (mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaapita</span></p><p>complaining</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhāyanta|ujjhaayanta|ujjhayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaayanta</span></p><p>(~ī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāyati">ujjhāyati</a></p>

ujjhāyati|ujjhaayati|ujjhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaayati</span></p><p>complains (about, acc. or gen.); is indignant (at or against, acc. or gen.).</p>

ujjhāyati|ujjhaayati|ujjhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujjhaayati</span></p><p>to be irritated, to be annoyed or offended, to get angry, grumble; often in phrase <em>ujjhāyati khīyati vipāceti</em> expressing great annoyance Vin.i.53, Vin.i.62, Vin.i.73; Vin.ii.207; Vin.iv.226; SN.i.232 passim<br />■ SN.i.232 (mā ujjhāyittha); Ja.ii.15; Dhp-a.ii.20<br />aor ujjhāyi Ja.i.475; Dhp-a.ii.88;<br />inf ujjhātuṃ Ja.ii.355.<br />caus <em><a href="bword://ujjh%C4%81peti">ujjhāpeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> or perhaps more likely jhāyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to burn, fig. to be consumed. According to Müller P. G pp. 12 &amp; 42 = Sk. ava-; <em>dhyā</em>, but that is doubtful phonetically as well as semantically</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjhāpeti">ujjhāpeti</a></p>

ujjota
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>light, lustre Ja.i.183 (˚kara); Mil.321.</p><p>ud + * <em>jot</em> of jotati, Sk. uddyotate</p>

ujjotita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>illumined Dāvs v.53.</p><p>pp. of ujjoteti, ud + joteti</p>

ujju FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ujju &amp; Ujjuka</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://uju">uju</a></em> &amp; <em>ujuka</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ujju
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>going in a straight direction; straight, direct; upright; honest, right; straightforward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ujju
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uju &amp; Ujju</p><p>adjective straight direct; straightforward, honest, upright DN.iii.150 (T. ujja), DN.iii.352 (do.), DN.iii.422, DN.iii.550; Vv.18#7 (= sabba-jimha-vanka-kuṭilabhāv’âpagama-hetutāya u. Vv-a.96); Pp.59; Vb.244 (ujuṃ kāyaṃ paṇidhāya); Vism.219 (uju avanka akuṭila) DN-a.i.210 (id.), Kp-a.236; Dhp-a.i.288 (cittaṃ ujuṃ akuṭilaṃ nibbisevanaṃ karoti); Vv-a.281 (˚koṭi-vanka); Pv-a.123 (an˚).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85gin">aṅgin</a></em> (ujjangin) having straight limbs, neg. an˚ not having straight limbs, i.e. pliable, skilful, nimble, graceful Ja.v.40 (= kañcana-sannibha-sarīra C.); Ja.vi.500 (T anuccangin = anindita-agarahitangin C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> walking straight, of upright life MN.i.46; AN.iii.285 sq. (˚citta), AN.v.290 sq.; Snp.350 (ujju˚), Snp.477 (id.); Dhp.108 (ujju˚, see Dhp-a.ii.234 for interpretation).</li><li><em>-gāmin</em>, neg. an˚ going crooked, a snake Ja.iv.330.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cittat%C4%81">cittatā</a></em> straightness, unwieldiness of heart Vb.350.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhit%C4%81">diṭṭhitā</a></em> the fact of having a straightforward view or theory (of life) Mil.257.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADipanna">paṭipanna</a></em> living uprightly DN.i.192; SN.iv.304; SN.v.343; Vism.219</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://magga">magga</a></em> the straight road DN.i.235; Vin.v.149; Iti.104; Ja.i.344 Ja.vi.252; Dhp-a.ii.192.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> straightness, uprightness Snp-a.292, Snp-a.317; Pv-a.51.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C5%ABta">bhūta</a></em> straight, upright SN.i.100, SN.i.170 SN.ii.279; SN.v.384, SN.v.404; AN.ii.57; AN.iv.292; Ja.i.94; Ja.v.293 (an˚) Vv.34#23 (see Vv-a.155); Pv.i.10#10 (= citta-jimha-vankaKutīla-bhāva-karānaṃ kilesānaṃ abhāvena ujubhāvappatta Pv-a.51).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85gin">aṅgin</a></em> (ujjangin) having straight limbs, neg. an˚ not having straight limbs, i.e. pliable, skilful, nimble, graceful Ja.v.40 (= kañcana-sannibha-sarīra C.); Ja.vi.500 (T anuccangin = anindita-agarahitangin C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ta">vāta</a></em> a soft wind Mil.283.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vipaccan%C4%ABka">vipaccanīka</a></em> in direct opposition DN.i.1; MN.i.402; DN-a.i.38.</li></ul><p>Vedic ṛju, also ṛjyati, irajyate to stretch out: cp. Gr. ὀρέγω to stretch; Lat. rego to govern; Goth ufrakjan to straighten up; Ohg. recchen = Ger. recken E. reach; Oir. rēn span. See also P. ajjava</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅgin">aṅgin</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://cittatā">cittatā</a>, <a href="bword://diṭṭhitā">diṭṭhitā</a>, <a href="bword://paṭipanna">paṭipanna</a>, <a href="bword://magga">magga</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://bhūta">bhūta</a>, <a href="bword://aṅgin">aṅgin</a>, <a href="bword://vāta">vāta</a>, <a href="bword://vipaccanīka">vipaccanīka</a></p>

ujjuka FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ujju &amp; Ujjuka</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://uju">uju</a></em> &amp; <em>ujuka</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ujjuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>straight, direct; straightforward; upright, honest, right; right-minded</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujuka">ujuka</a></p>

ujjuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ujuka &amp; Ujjuka</p><p>adjective straight, direct, upright MN.i.124; SN.i.33 (ujuko so maggo, the road to nibbāna), SN.i.260 (citta); SN.iv.298; SN.v.143, SN.v.165; Ja.i.163; Ja.v.297 (opp khujja); Dhp-a.i.18 (˚magga); Sdhp.321. <em>-<a href="bword://anujjuka">anujjuka</a></em> crooked not straight SN.iv.299; Ja.iii.318.</p><p>uju + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anujjuka">anujjuka</a></p>

uju
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>going in a straight direction; straight, direct; upright; honest, right; straightforward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujju">ujju</a></p>

uju
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uju &amp; Ujju</p><p>adjective straight direct; straightforward, honest, upright DN.iii.150 (T. ujja), DN.iii.352 (do.), DN.iii.422, DN.iii.550; Vv.18#7 (= sabba-jimha-vanka-kuṭilabhāv’âpagama-hetutāya u. Vv-a.96); Pp.59; Vb.244 (ujuṃ kāyaṃ paṇidhāya); Vism.219 (uju avanka akuṭila) DN-a.i.210 (id.), Kp-a.236; Dhp-a.i.288 (cittaṃ ujuṃ akuṭilaṃ nibbisevanaṃ karoti); Vv-a.281 (˚koṭi-vanka); Pv-a.123 (an˚).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85gin">aṅgin</a></em> (ujjangin) having straight limbs, neg. an˚ not having straight limbs, i.e. pliable, skilful, nimble, graceful Ja.v.40 (= kañcana-sannibha-sarīra C.); Ja.vi.500 (T anuccangin = anindita-agarahitangin C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> walking straight, of upright life MN.i.46; AN.iii.285 sq. (˚citta), AN.v.290 sq.; Snp.350 (ujju˚), Snp.477 (id.); Dhp.108 (ujju˚, see Dhp-a.ii.234 for interpretation).</li><li><em>-gāmin</em>, neg. an˚ going crooked, a snake Ja.iv.330.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://cittat%C4%81">cittatā</a></em> straightness, unwieldiness of heart Vb.350.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://di%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhit%C4%81">diṭṭhitā</a></em> the fact of having a straightforward view or theory (of life) Mil.257.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADipanna">paṭipanna</a></em> living uprightly DN.i.192; SN.iv.304; SN.v.343; Vism.219</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://magga">magga</a></em> the straight road DN.i.235; Vin.v.149; Iti.104; Ja.i.344 Ja.vi.252; Dhp-a.ii.192.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> straightness, uprightness Snp-a.292, Snp-a.317; Pv-a.51.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C5%ABta">bhūta</a></em> straight, upright SN.i.100, SN.i.170 SN.ii.279; SN.v.384, SN.v.404; AN.ii.57; AN.iv.292; Ja.i.94; Ja.v.293 (an˚) Vv.34#23 (see Vv-a.155); Pv.i.10#10 (= citta-jimha-vankaKutīla-bhāva-karānaṃ kilesānaṃ abhāvena ujubhāvappatta Pv-a.51).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85gin">aṅgin</a></em> (ujjangin) having straight limbs, neg. an˚ not having straight limbs, i.e. pliable, skilful, nimble, graceful Ja.v.40 (= kañcana-sannibha-sarīra C.); Ja.vi.500 (T anuccangin = anindita-agarahitangin C.).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ta">vāta</a></em> a soft wind Mil.283.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vipaccan%C4%ABka">vipaccanīka</a></em> in direct opposition DN.i.1; MN.i.402; DN-a.i.38.</li></ul><p>Vedic ṛju, also ṛjyati, irajyate to stretch out: cp. Gr. ὀρέγω to stretch; Lat. rego to govern; Goth ufrakjan to straighten up; Ohg. recchen = Ger. recken E. reach; Oir. rēn span. See also P. ajjava</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅgin">aṅgin</a>, <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://cittatā">cittatā</a>, <a href="bword://diṭṭhitā">diṭṭhitā</a>, <a href="bword://paṭipanna">paṭipanna</a>, <a href="bword://magga">magga</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://bhūta">bhūta</a>, <a href="bword://aṅgin">aṅgin</a>, <a href="bword://vāta">vāta</a>, <a href="bword://vipaccanīka">vipaccanīka</a></p>

ujuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>straight, direct; straightforward; upright, honest, right; right-minded</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ujjuka">ujjuka</a></p>

ujuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ujuka &amp; Ujjuka</p><p>adjective straight, direct, upright MN.i.124; SN.i.33 (ujuko so maggo, the road to nibbāna), SN.i.260 (citta); SN.iv.298; SN.v.143, SN.v.165; Ja.i.163; Ja.v.297 (opp khujja); Dhp-a.i.18 (˚magga); Sdhp.321. <em>-<a href="bword://anujjuka">anujjuka</a></em> crooked not straight SN.iv.299; Ja.iii.318.</p><p>uju + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anujjuka">anujjuka</a></p>

ujukatā|ujukataa|ujukata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujukataa</span></p><p>straightness, rectitude Dhs.50, Dhs.51 (kāyassa, cittassa); Vism.436 sq.</p><p>abstr. fr. ujuka</p>

ujutā|ujutaa|ujuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujutaa</span></p><p>straight(forward)ness, rectitude Dhs.50, Dhs.51.</p><p>abstr. of uju</p>

ujuvipaccanīkavāda|ujuvipaccaniikavaada|ujuvipaccanikavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ujuvipaccaniikavaada</span></p><p>peaking in direct opposition; professing directly opposed views</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ujuṃ|uju.m|ujum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uju.m</span></p><p>in a straight line, straight on; straight; in the right manner</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uju">uju</a></p>

ukka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ukka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine a fire brand</p><p>ukka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine neuter a house.</p><p>ukka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine re-opening (of a (legal) question already settled).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkā">ukkā</a></p>

ukkacelā|ukkacelaa|ukkacela
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkacelaa</span></p><p>UkkacelāUkkaceḷāUkkavelāUkkaveḷā</p><p>A village in the Vajji country, on the banks of the Ganges, on the road from Rājagaha to Vesāli and near the latter.</p><p>Once while Sāriputta was staying there, the Paribbājaka Sāmaṇḍaka visited him and talked to him about Nibbāna. SN.iv.261–262 Some time later, after the death of Sāriputta and Moggallāna within a fortnight of each other, the Buddha came to Ukkacela on his way to Vesāli and at a gathering of the monks uttered high praise of the two chief disciples and spoke of the loss the Order had sustained by their death. SN.v.163<em>f.</em></p><p>The Cūḷagopālaka Sutta was also preached at Ukkacela. MN.i.225</p><p>25.723207,85.0396662town</p>

ukkala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in phrase ukkala-vassa-bhañña SN.iii.73 = AN.ii.31 = Kv.141 is translated as “the folk of Ukkala, Lenten speakers of old” (see <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 95 with n. 2). Another interpretation is ukkalāvassa˚, i.e. ukkalā + avassa, one who speaks of, or like, a porter (ukkala = Sk utkala porter, one who carries a load) and bondsman MN.iii.78 reads Okkalā (variant reading Ukkalā)-Vassa-Bhaññā all as N. pr.</p><p>*avaśya˚</p>

ukkalissati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to become depraved, to revoke(?) Mil.143.</p><p>= ukkilissati? ud + kilissati</p>

ukkalā|ukkalaa|ukkala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkalaa</span></p><p>A district identified with modern Orissa. The merchants Tapussa and Bhalluka were on the way from Ukkalā, when they visited the Buddha shortly after his Awakening, near Uruvelā, and offered food to him, becoming the first lay disciples, taking refuge only in the Buddha and Dhamma (since the Sangha was not yet established). Vin.i.4</p><p>The men of Ukkalā, together with those of Vassa and Bhañña, are represented as being deniers of cause and effect, deniers of reality (<em>ahetuvādā, akiriyavādā, natthikavādā</em>). AN.ii.31 SN.iii.72 MN.iii.78</p><p>???1region</p>

ukkalā|ukkalaa|ukkala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkalaa</span></p><p>the inhabitans of the country Ukkala (modern day Orissa).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkalā">okkalā</a></p>

ukkalāpa FIXME double|ukkalaapa FIXME double|ukkalapa FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkalaapa FIXME double</span></p><p>Uklāpa (ukkalāpa)</p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>deserted Ja.ii.275 (ukkalāpa T.; vv.ll. uklāpa ullāpa).</li><li>dirtied, soiled Vin.ii.154, Vin.ii.208, Vin.ii.222; Vism.128; Dhp-a.iii.168 (ukkalāpa).</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. ut-kalāpayati to let go</p>

ukkalāpa|ukkalaapa|ukkalapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkalaapa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ukl%C4%81pa">uklāpa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uklāpa">uklāpa</a></p>

ukkamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>stepping away from Vism.374.</p><p>fr. ukkamati</p>

ukkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(or <em>okk˚</em>; which is variant reading at all passages quoted) to step aside, step out from (w. abl.), depart from AN.iii.301 (maggā); Ja.iii.531; Ja.iv.101 (maggā); Ud.13 (id.); DN-a.i.185 (id.). Caus. <em><a href="bword://ukk%C4%81meti">ukkāmeti</a></em>; Caus. ii. <em>ukkamāpeti</em> Ja.ii.3.</p><p>ud + kamati from <em>kram</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkāmeti">ukkāmeti</a></p>

ukkamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>having gone out of, gone away (from); gone over, omitted, neglected.</p>

ukkantati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cut out, tear out, skin Vin.i.217 (˚itva); Ja.i.164; Ja.iv.210 (variant reading for okk˚); Ja.v.10 (ger ukkacca); Pv.iii.9#4 (ukkantvā, variant reading BB ukkacca); Pv-a.210 (variant reading SS ni˚), Pv-a.211 (= chinditvā).</p><p>ud + kantati</p>

ukkantikaṃ|ukkantika.m|ukkantikam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkantika.m</span></p><p>(nt. adv.), in jhān˚ &amp; kasiṇ˚, after the method of stepping away from or skipping Vism.374.</p>

ukkantitvā|ukkantitvaa|ukkantitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkantitvaa</span></p><p>cutting out or off; cutting up; butchering.</p>

ukkapiṇḍaka|ukkapi.n.daka|ukkapindaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkapi.n.daka</span></p><p>Uncertain. The name means something like “finding food in a house”. Perhaps it is a general term for animals considered pests or vermin. Once some monks put food out to dry and it was eaten by these creatures Vin.i.211.</p>

ukkapiṇḍaka|ukkapi.n.daka|ukkapindaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkapi.n.daka</span></p><p>‘finding food in a house ‘, perhaps a house-lizard, or vermin generally</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukka">ukka</a></p>

ukkapiṇḍaka|ukkapi.n.daka|ukkapindaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkapi.n.daka</span></p><p>only in pl.; vermin, Vin.i.211 = Vin.i.239. See comment at <em>Vin. Texts</em> ii.70.</p><p>etymology unknown</p>

ukkaḍḍhati|ukka.d.dhati|ukkaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.d.dhati</span></p><p>puts off postpones (the beginning of the rainy season residence).</p>

ukkaṃsa|ukka.msa|ukkamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msa</span></p><p>exaltation, excellence, superiority (opp. avakkaṃsa) DN.i.54 (ukkaṃs-âvakkaṃsa hāyana-vaḍḍhana DN-a.i.165); MN.i.518; Vism.563 (id.) Vv-a.146 (˚gata excellent), Vv-a.335 (instr. ukkaṃsena par excellence exceedingly); Pv-a.228 (˚vasena, with ref. to devatās; variant reading SS okk˚).</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kṛṣ</em> see ukkassati</p>

ukkaṃsaka|ukka.msaka|ukkamsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msaka</span></p><p>adjective raising, exalting (oneself), extolling MN.i.19 (att˚; opp. para-vambhin); Ja.ii.152. Cp <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81mukka%E1%B9%83sika">sāmukkaṃsika</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ukkaṃsa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sāmukkaṃsika">sāmukkaṃsika</a></p>

ukkaṃsanā|ukka.msanaa|ukkamsana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msanaa</span></p><p>raising, extolling, exaltation, in att˚ self-exaltation, self-praise MN.i.402 (opp para-vambhanā); Cnd.505 (id.).</p><p>abstr. of ukkaṃsati</p>

ukkaṃsati|ukka.msati|ukkamsati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msati</span></p><p>draws up, raises; extols, praises.</p>

ukkaṃsati|ukka.msati|ukkamsati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msati</span></p><p>to exalt, praise MN.i.498; Ja.iv.108<br />pp <em>ukkaṭṭha<br />■ ukkaṃseti</em> in same meaning MN.i.402 sq. (attānaṃ u. paraṃ vambheti); AN.ii.27; Cnd.141.</p><p>ud + <em>kṛṣ</em>, karṣati, lit. draw or up, raise</p>

ukkaṃsavakaṃsa|ukka.msavaka.msa|ukkamsavakamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msavaka.msa</span></p><p>excess and deficiency; superiority and inferiority; maximum and minimum.</p>

ukkaṃsayati|ukka.msayati|ukkamsayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.msayati</span></p><p>exalts; praises</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkaṃseti">ukkaṃseti</a>, <a href="bword://ukkaṃsati">ukkaṃsati</a></p>

ukkaṃseti|ukka.mseti|ukkamseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.mseti</span></p><p>exalts; praises</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkaṃsayati">ukkaṃsayati</a>, <a href="bword://ukkaṃsati">ukkaṃsati</a></p>

ukkaṇḍaka|ukka.n.daka|ukkandaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (?) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.daka</span></p><p>disease (of animals)</p><p>See also: ukkaṇtaka or ukkaṇṇaka</p>

ukkaṇṇa|ukka.n.na|ukkanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.na</span></p><p>adjective having the ears erect (?) Ja.vi.559.</p><p>ud + kaṇṇa</p>

ukkaṇṇaka|ukka.n.naka|ukkannaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.naka</span></p><p>See <em>Vitacchikā</em>.</p><p>See also: Vitacchikā</p>

ukkaṇṇaka|ukka.n.naka|ukkannaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (?) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.naka</span></p><p>disease (of animals)</p><p>See also: ukkaṇṭaka or ukkaṇḍaka</p>

ukkaṇṇaka|ukka.n.naka|ukkannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.naka</span></p><p>adjective a certain disease (? mange) of jackals SN.ii.230, SN.ii.271; S. A. ʻthe fur falls off from the whole body’.</p><p>ut + kaṇṇa + ka lit. “with ears out” or is it ukkandaka?</p>

ukkaṇṭaka|ukka.n.taka|ukkantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (?) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.taka</span></p><p>disease (of animals)</p><p>See also: ukkaṇḍaka or ukkaṇṇaka</p>

ukkaṇṭhana|ukka.n.thana|ukkanthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thana</span></p><p>(from ukkaṇṭhati) fretting; discontent.</p>

ukkaṇṭhanā|ukka.n.thanaa|ukkanthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thanaa</span></p><p>emotion, commotion DN.ii.239.</p><p>fr. ukkaṇṭhati</p>

ukkaṇṭhati|ukka.n.thati|ukkanthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thati</span></p><p>earns (for); sorrows over; frets; loses heart; is discontented (with, instr. or loc), is unhappy (esp. of a bhikkhu, longing to return to a secular life).</p>

ukkaṇṭhati|ukka.n.thati|ukkanthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thati</span></p><p>to long for, to be dissatisfied</p><p>to fret Ja.i.386 (˚māna); Ja.iii.143 (˚itvā); Ja.iv.3, Ja.iv.160; Ja.v.10 (anukkhaṇṭhanto); Dhs-a.407; Pv-a.162 (mā ukkaṇṭhi variant reading ukkaṇhi, so read for T. mā khuṇḍali)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ukka%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%ADhita">ukkaṇṭhita</a></em> (q.v.). Cp. pari˚.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kaṇṭh</em> in secondary meaning of kaṇṭha neck, lit. to stretch one’s neck for anything; i.e. long for, be hungry after, etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkaṇṭhita">ukkaṇṭhita</a></p>

ukkaṇṭhi|ukka.n.thi|ukkanthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thi</span></p><p>longing, dissatisfaction Thag-a.239 (= arati).</p><p>fr. ukkanṭḥ˚</p>

ukkaṇṭhikā|ukka.n.thikaa|ukkanthika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thikaa</span></p><p>= ukkaṇṭhi, i.e. longing, state of distress, pain Ja.iii.643.</p><p>abstr. fr. ukkaṇṭhita</p>

ukkaṇṭhita|ukka.n.thita|ukkanthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thita</span></p><p>fretting; discontented; discontent; unhappiness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkaṇṭhati">ukkaṇṭhati</a></p>

ukkaṇṭhita|ukka.n.thita|ukkanthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thita</span></p><p>dissatisfied, regretting, longing, fretting Ja.i.196; Ja.ii.92, Ja.ii.115; Ja.iii.185; Mil.281; Dhp-a.iv.66, Dhp-a.iv.225; Pv-a.13 (an˚), Pv-a.55, Pv-a.187.</p><p>pp. of ukkaṇṭhati</p>

ukkaṇṭhā|ukka.n.thaa|ukkantha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.n.thaa</span></p><p>longing, desire; distress, regret Ne.88; Pv-a.55 (spelt kkh), Pv-a.60, Pv-a.145, Pv-a.152.</p><p>fr. ukkanṭḥ˚</p>

ukkaṭṭha|ukka.t.tha|ukkattha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.tha</span></p><p>A town in Kosala, near the Himālaya. It has been given, free from all taxes (as <em>brahmadeyya</em>), to Pokkharasāti by the king of Kosala, in recognition of the former’s skill. It was thickly populated and had much grassland, woodland and corn. DN.i.87 The Icchānaṅgala wood was in the neighbourhood, and when the Buddha was staying in the wood Pokkharasāti first sent his pupil Ambaṭṭha and then went himself to visit the Buddha.</p><p>There was a road which connected Ukkaṭṭha with Setavyā. AN.ii.37</p><p>It was in the Subhagavana at Ukkaṭṭha that the Mūlapariyāya Sutta MN.i.1<em>ff.</em> was preached.</p><p>In the Brahmanimantika Sutta MN.i.326 SN.i.142 the Buddha says that it was while he was residing at Subhagavana that be became aware of the erroneous views of Baka-brahma and went to the Brahma-world to teach Baka the truth.</p><p>27.593501,82.817232town</p>

ukkaṭṭha|ukka.t.tha|ukkattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.tha</span></p><p>raised, exalted; highest, superior; big, bigger, biggest</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkaṃsati">ukkaṃsati</a></p>

ukkaṭṭha|ukka.t.tha|ukkattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>exalted, high, prominent, glorious, excellent, most freq. opp. to <em><a href="bword://h%C4%ABna">hīna</a></em> in phrase hīna-m-ukkaṭṭha-majjhime Vin.iv.7; Ja.i.20 (verse 129), Ja.i.22 (verse 143); Ja.iii.218 (= uttama C.). In other comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vism.64 (u. majjhima mudu referring to the 3 grades of the Dhutangas); Snp-a.160 (dvipadā sabbasattānaṃ ukkaṭṭhā); Vv-a.105 (superl. ukkaṭṭhatama with ref. to Gotama as the most exalted of the 7 Rishis) Sdhp.506 (opp. lāmaka).</li><li>large, comprehensive great, in ukkaṭṭho patto a bowl of great capacity (as diff. from majjhima &amp; omaka p.) Vin.iii.243 (= uk. nāma patto aḍḍhālhak’ odanaṃ gaṇhāti catu-bhāgaṃ khādanaṃ vā tadūpiyaṃ vā byañjanaṃ).</li><li>detailed, exhaustive specialised Vism.37 (ati-ukkaṭṭha-desanā); also in phrase ˚vasena in detail Snp-a.181.</li><li>arrogant, insolent Ja.v.16.</li><li>used as nom at Ja.i.387 in meaning “battle conflict”<br />■ <em>an˚</em>; Vism.64 (˚cīvara).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://niddesa">niddesa</a></em> exhaustive exposition, special designation, term par excellence Dhs-a.70; Vv-a.231; Pv-a.7.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pariccheda">pariccheda</a></em> comprehensive connotation Snp-a.229, Snp-a.231, Snp-a.376.</li></ul><p>pp. of ukkaṃsati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://hīna">hīna</a>, <a href="bword://niddesa">niddesa</a>, <a href="bword://pariccheda">pariccheda</a></p>

ukkaṭṭhatā|ukka.t.thataa|ukkatthata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.thataa</span></p><p>superiority, eminence, exalted state Ja.iv.303 (opp. hīnatā).</p><p>abstr. fr. ukkaṭṭha</p>

ukkaṭṭhita|ukka.t.thita|ukkatthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.thita</span></p><p>boiled up; seething.</p>

ukkaṭṭhita|ukka.t.thita|ukkatthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukka.t.thita</span></p><p>boiled up, boiling, seething AN.iii.231 &amp; AN.iii.234 (udapatto agginā santatto ukkaṭṭhito, variant reading ukkuṭṭhito); Ja.iv.118 (variant reading pakkudhita = pakkuṭhita, as gloss).</p><p>for ukkaṭhita, ud + pp. of <em>kvath</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ka%E1%B9%ADhati">kaṭhati</a></em> &amp; kuthati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaṭhati">kaṭhati</a></p>

ukkhali
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(˚lī) feminine a pot in which to boil rice (&amp; other food) Ja.i.68, Ja.i.235; Ja.v.389, Ja.v.471; Pp.33; Vism.346 (˚mukhavaṭṭi), Vism.356 (˚kapāla in comp.); Dhp-a.i.136; Dhp-a.ii.5; Dhp-a.iii.371; Dhp-a.iv.130; Pp-a.231; Vv-a.100. Cp. next.</p><p>der. fr. Vedic ukha &amp; ukhā pot, boiler; related to Lat. aulla (fr. *auxla); Goth. auhns oven</p>

ukkhalikā|ukkhalikaa|ukkhalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhalikaa</span></p><p>cooking pot.</p>

ukkhalikā|ukkhalikaa|ukkhalika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhalikaa</span></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://ukkhali">ukkhali</a></em>. Thig.23 (= bhatta-pacanabhājanaṃ Thag-a.29); Dhp-a.iv.98 (˚kāla); Dhs-a.376.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhali">ukkhali</a></p>

ukkhaliparimajjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>dishcloth, a cleaning rag.</p>

ukkhepa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><ol><li>(m.) throwing up; lifting up, raising; a throw.</li><li>(n.) suspension (from the <em>saṅgha</em>).</li></ol>

ukkhepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun (adj.) throwing away Dhp-a.iv.59 (˚dāya a throw-away donation, tip)<br />■ (m.) lifting up, raising Ja.i.394 (cel˚); Ja.vi.508; DN-a.i.273; <em>dur˚</em> hard to lift or raise Sdhp.347.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kṣip</em></p>

ukkhepaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn) &amp; masculine</i></p><ol><li>(mfn.) (one) who suspends (from the <em>saṅgha</em>).</li><li>(m.) throwing up; raising.</li></ol>

ukkhepaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective throwing (up); ˚ṃ (acc.) in the manner of throwing Vin.ii.214 = Vin.iv.195 (piṇḍ˚).</p><p>fr. ukkhepa</p>

ukkhepakatavaccha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A monk whose verse is recorded in the Theragāthā. Thag.65</p>

ukkhepana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>concerning suspension (from the <em>saṅgha</em>), leading to suspension.</p>

ukkhepana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>suspension Ja.iii.487.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kṣip</em></p>

ukkhepaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>concerning suspension; (a formal act of the <em>saṅgha</em>) leading to suspension.</p>

ukkhepaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective referring to the suspension (of a bhikkhu), <em>-kamma</em> act or resolution of suspension Vin.i.49, Vin.i.53, Vin.i.98, Vin.i.143, Vin.i.168; Vin.ii.27, Vin.ii.226, Vin.ii.230, Vin.ii.298: AN.i.99. Uklapa (ukkalapa)</p><p>ukkhepana + iya, cp. BSk. utkṣepanīyaṃ karma Divy.329</p>

ukkhepaniyakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>(formal) act of suspension</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhepaniya">ukkhepaniya</a></p>

ukkhepaniyakammakata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn</i></p><p>dealt by a (formal) act of suspension; suspended</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhepaniyakamma">ukkhepaniyakamma</a></p>

ukkhepanā|ukkhepanaa|ukkhepana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhepanaa</span></p><p>throwing up, provocation, sneering Vb.352 = Vism.23, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at p. 29.</p><p>= last</p>

ukkheṭita|ukkhe.tita|ukkhetita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhe.tita</span></p><p>frightened away.</p>

ukkheṭita|ukkhe.tita|ukkhetita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhe.tita</span></p><p>spit out, thrown off, in phrase moho (rāgo etc) catto vanto mutto pahino paṭinissaṭṭho u. Vin.iii.97 = Vin.iv.27.</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>kheṭ</em>; or <em>*khel</em>, see <em><a href="bword://khe%E1%B8%B7a">kheḷa</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kheḷa">kheḷa</a></p>

ukkhipana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>pushing upwards Ja.i.163.</li><li>throwing up, sneering Vism.29 (vācāya).</li></ol><p>fr. ud + <em>kṣip</em></p>

ukkhipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>throws up; raises; lifts up, picks up, holds up; raises up, extols.</li><li>throws away; rejects, dismisses; esp. suspends (a <em>bhikkhu</em> from the <em>saṅgha</em>).</li></ol>

ukkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>To hold up, to take up Ja.i.213; Ja.iv.391: Ja.vi.350; Vism.4 (satthaṃ); Pv-a.265. A t. t. of canon law, to suspend (a bhikkhu for breach of rules) Vin.iv.309; Pp.33. <em>-ukkhipiyati</em> to be suspended Vin.ii.61. Caus. ii. <em>ukkhipāpeti</em> to cause to be supported Ja.i.52; Ja.ii.15, Ja.ii.38; Ja.iii.285, Ja.iii.436<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ukkhitta">ukkhitta</a></em>, ger. ukkhipitvā as adv. “upright” Vism.126.</p><p>ut + khipati, <em>kṣip</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhitta">ukkhitta</a></p>

ukkhipi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhipitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhipitvā|ukkhipitvaa|ukkhipitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhipitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhipiyamāna|ukkhipiyamaana|ukkhipiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhipiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipiyati">ukkhipiyati</a></p>

ukkhipiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>is raised; is thrown up or about.</li><li>is suspended</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipiyyati">ukkhipiyyati</a>, <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhipiyiṃsu|ukkhipiyi.msu|ukkhipiyimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhipiyi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipiyati">ukkhipiyati</a></p>

ukkhipiyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>is raised; is thrown up or about.</li><li>is suspended</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipiyati">ukkhipiyati</a>, <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhipiṃsu|ukkhipi.msu|ukkhipimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhipi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>besmeared, besprinkled Ja.iv.331 (ruhir˚, so read for ˚rakkhita). Cp. <em><a href="bword://okkhita">okkhita</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of <em>ukṣ</em> sprinkle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://okkhita">okkhita</a></p>

ukkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>lifted, picked up, raised; thrown up; tossed about, blown away.</li><li>dismissed, suspended.</li><li>lifted up (robe)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhipati">ukkhipati</a></p>

ukkhitta-ojjagghikā|ukkhitta-ojjagghikaa|ukkhitta-ojjagghika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhitta-ojjagghikaa</span></p><p>ukkhitta-ojjagghikā</p><p>feminine raised (robe) and loud laughter</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhittaka">ukkhittaka</a></p>

ukkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>taken up, lifted up, t.t. of the canon law “suspended” Vin.iv.218; Ja.iii.487.</p><ul><li><em>-āsika</em> with drawn sword MN.i.377; SN.iv.173; Ja.i.393; Dhs-a.329; Vism.230 (vadhaka), Vism.479.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://paligha">paligha</a></em> having the obstacles removed MN.i.139; AN.iii.84; Dhp.398 = Snp.622 (= avijjā-palighassa ukkhittatāya u. Snp-a.467; Dhp-a.iv.161).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sira">sira</a></em> with uplifted head Vism.162.</li></ul><p>pp. of ukkhipati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paligha">paligha</a>, <a href="bword://sira">sira</a></p>

ukkhittaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>who has been suspended (from the <em>saṅgha</em>).</li><li>( <em>~ā</em> . sc. <em>saṅghāti</em> or <em>sāṭikā</em> ?), raised, lifted up (robe).</li></ol>

ukkhittaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun a bhikkbu who has been suspended Vin.i.97, Vin.i.121; Vin.ii.61, Vin.ii.173, Vin.ii.213.</p><p>fr. ukkhitta</p>

ukkhittapaligha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>“with crossbar raised”, with obstacles removed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhitta">ukkhitta</a></p>

ukkhittasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>(one) with a drawn sword; with sword raised</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhitta">ukkhitta</a></p>

ukkhā|ukkhaa|ukkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhaa</span></p><p>cooking pot.</p>

ukkhā|ukkhaa|ukkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhaa</span></p><p>? in ukkhasataṃ dānaṃ, given at various times of the day (meaning ἑκατόμβη?) SN.ii.264 (variant reading ukkā). Or is it to be read ukhāsataṃ d. i.e. consisting of 100 pots (of rice = mahā danaṃ?). S A: paṇītabhojana-bharitānaṃ mahā-ukkhalinaṃ sataṃ dānaṃ. Cp. ukhā cooking vessel Thag-a.71 (Tha-ap.38) Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>under ukkhā trsl. “zeker muntstuck”, i.e. kind of gift.</p><p>can it be compared with Vedic ukṣan?</p>

ukkhāsata|ukkhaasata|ukkhasata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkhaasata</span></p><p>hundred cooking pots</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkhā">ukkhā</a></p>

ukkiledeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take the dirt out, to clean out DN-a.i.255 (dosaṃ); Snp-a.274 (rāgaṃ variant reading BB. uggileti).</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>klid</em>, see <em><a href="bword://kilijjati">kilijjati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kilijjati">kilijjati</a></p>

ukkiratiparikha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with a ditch dug (round).</p>

ukkiṇṇa|ukki.n.na|ukkinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukki.n.na</span></p><p>dug up or out DN.i.105; Ja.iv.106; Mil.330; DN-a.i.274 (= khāta).</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>kṛ</em>; dig<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ukkoṭa|ukko.ta|ukkota
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.ta</span></p><p>disturbance, re-opening (of a (legal) question already settled).</p>

ukkoṭana|ukko.tana|ukkotana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tana</span></p><p>disturbance, re-opening (of a (legal) question already settled).</p>

ukkoṭana|ukko.tana|ukkotana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tana</span></p><p>crookedness, perverting justice taking bribes to get people into unlawful possessions (Bdhgh.) DN.i.5; DN.iii.176; SN.v.473; AN.ii.209, AN.v.206; DN-a.i.79 = Pp-a.240 (“assāmike sāmike kātuṃ lañcagahaṇaṃ”).</p><p>fr. ud + * <em>kuṭ</em>; to be crooked or to deceive, cp. kujja &amp; kuṭila crooked</p>

ukkoṭanaka|ukko.tanaka|ukkotanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tanaka</span></p><p>consisting in re-opening (a matter already settled).</p>

ukkoṭanaka|ukko.tanaka|ukkotanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tanaka</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the perversion of justice Vin.ii.94.</p><p>fr. ukkoṭana</p>

ukkoṭanavañcananikatisāciyoga|ukko.tanava~ncananikatisaaciyoga|ukkotanavancananikatisaciyoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tanava~ncananikatisaaciyoga</span></p><p>the crooked practice of corruption, deception and fraud</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkoṭana">ukkoṭana</a></p>

ukkoṭenta|ukko.tenta|ukkotenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkoṭeti">ukkoṭeti</a></p>

ukkoṭesuṃ|ukko.tesu.m|ukkotesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.tesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkoṭeti">ukkoṭeti</a></p>

ukkoṭeti|ukko.teti|ukkoteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.teti</span></p><p>disturbs, reopens (a (legal) question already settled).</p>

ukkoṭeti|ukko.teti|ukkoteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.teti</span></p><p>to disturb what is settled, to open up again a legal question that has been adjudged Vin.ii.94, Vin.ii.303; Vin.iv.126; Ja.ii.387; DN-a.i.5.</p><p>denom. fr. *ukkoṭ-ana</p>

ukkoṭitita|ukko.titita|ukkotitita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukko.titita</span></p><p>re-opened</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkoṭeti">ukkoṭeti</a></p>

ukkujitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkujjati">ukkujjati</a></p>

ukkujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>‘with the (hollow side of the) curve upward, concave’; set upright, being the right way up.</p>

ukkujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective set up, upright, opp. either nikkujja or avakujja AN.i.131; SN.v.89 (ukkujj’âvakujja) Pp.32 (= uparimukho ṭhapito C. 214).</p><p>ud + kujja</p>

ukkujjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>raising up, setting up again Vin.ii.126 (patt˚).</p><p>fr. ukkujjati</p>

ukkujjanā|ukkujjanaa|ukkujjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkujjanaa</span></p><p>setting upright; turning the right way up.</p>

ukkujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>holds or sets upright; holds or sets the right way up; esp. turns (the bowl) the right way up (to accept food again from a layman whose alms had been refused)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkujjeti">ukkujjeti</a></p>

ukkujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(˚eti) to bend up, turn up, set upright Vin.i.181; Vin.ii.126 (pattaṃ), Vin.ii.269 (bhikkhuṃ) mostly in phrase nikkujjitaṃ ukkujjeyya “(like) one might raise up one who has fallen” DN.i.85, DN.i.110; DN.ii.132, DN.ii.152 Snp.p.15 (= uparimukhaṃ karoti DN-a.i.228 = Snp-a.155).</p><p>Denom. fr. ukkujja</p>

ukkujjavakujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the setting up and upsetting; the rise and fall</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkujja">ukkujja</a></p>

ukkujjeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>holds or sets upright; holds or sets the right way up; esp. turns (the bowl) the right way up (to accept food again from a layman whose alms had been refused)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkujjati">ukkujjati</a></p>

ukkujjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>set upright; the right way up.</p>

ukkujjitvā|ukkujjitvaa|ukkujjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkujjitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkujjati">ukkujjati</a></p>

ukkusa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>an osprey Ja.iv.291 (˚rāja), Ja.iv.392.</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://ukku%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhi">ukkuṭṭhi</a></em> &amp; cp. BSk. utkrośa watchman (?) Divy.453</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkuṭṭhi">ukkuṭṭhi</a></p>

ukkusā|ukkusaa|ukkusa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkusaa</span></p><p>A type of raptor Ja.iv.291 Ja.iv.397, possibly another name for the <em><a href="bword://kurara">kurara</a></em>. According to the Jātaka, the Bodhisattva was once reborn as one of these birds Ja.iv.392.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kurara">kurara</a></p>

ukkuṭika|ukku.tika|ukkutika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.tika</span></p><p>one who squats; who practices the ascetic exercise of squatting.</p>

ukkuṭika|ukku.tika|ukkutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.tika</span></p><p>special manner of squatting. The soles of the feet are firmly on the ground, the man sinks down, the heels slightly rising as he does so, until the thighs rest on the calves, and the hams are about six inches or more from the ground. Then with elbows on knees he balances himself. Few Europeans can adopt this posture, &amp; none (save miners) can maintain it with comfort, as the calf muscles upset the balance. Indians find it easy, &amp; when the palms of the hands are also held together upwards, it indicates submission. See; <em>Dial.</em> i.231 n. 4<br />■ Vin.i.45 (˚ṃ nisīdati); Vin.iii.228; AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Pp.55; Vism.62, Vism.104 Vism.105 (quot. fr. Papañca Sūdanī) Vism.426; Dhp-a.i.201, Dhp-a.i.217 Dhp-a.ii.61 (as posture of humility); Dhp-a.iii.195; Dhp-a.iv.223.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://padh%C4%81na">padhāna</a></em> [in BSk. distorted to utkuṭuka-prahāṇa Divy.339 = Dhp.141] exertion when squatting (an ascetic habit DN.i.167; MN.i.78, MN.i.515; AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Ja.i.493; Ja.iii.235 Ja.iv.299; Dhp.141 (= ukkuṭika-bhāvena āraddha-viriyo Dhp-a.iii.78).</li></ul><p>fr. ud + <em>*kuṭ = *kuñc</em>, as in kuṭila &amp; kuñcita; lit. “bending up”. The BSk. form is ukkuṭuka, e.g. Av SN.i.315</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://padhāna">padhāna</a></p>

ukkuṭikappadhāna|ukku.tikappadhaana|ukkutikappadhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.tikappadhaana</span></p><p>the squatting exercise; exertion in (the ascetic practice of) squatting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkuṭika">ukkuṭika</a></p>

ukkuṭikaṃ|ukku.tika.m|ukkutikam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.tika.m</span></p><p>in a squatting position.</p>

ukkuṭikā|ukku.tikaa|ukkutika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.tikaa</span></p><p>prob, the squatting position, but according to commentary the practice of walking on tiptoe or on one’s heels.</p>

ukkuṭṭhi|ukku.t.thi|ukkutthi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.t.thi</span></p><p>crying out, shout; outcry.</p>

ukkuṭṭhi|ukku.t.thi|ukkutthi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukku.t.thi</span></p><p>shouting out, acclamation Ja.ii.367; Ja.vi.41 Bv.i.35; Mil.21; Vism.245; Dhp-a.ii.43; Vv-a.132 (˚sadda).</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kruś</em>, cp. <em>*kruñc</em> as in P. kuñca &amp; Sk. krośati</p>

ukkā|ukkaa|ukka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaa</span></p><p>fire brand; a furnace, a crucible</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukka">ukka</a></p>

ukkā|ukkaa|ukka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaa</span></p><ol><li>firebrand, glow of fire, torch DN.i.49, DN.i.108; SN.ii.264; Thig.488 (˚ûpama) Ja.i.34 (dhamm-okkā); Ja.ii.401; Ja.iv.291; Ja.v.322; Vism.428 Thag-a.287; DN-a.i.148; Dhp-a.i.42, Dhp-a.i.205; Pv-a.154. Esp as tiṇ˚ firebrand of dry grass MN.i.128, MN.i.365; Cnd.40#Ie Dhp-a.i.126; Sdhp.573.</li><li>a furnace or forge of a smith AN.i.210, AN.i.257; Ja.vi.437; see also below ˚mukha.</li><li>a meteor: see below ˚pāta.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81ra">dhāra</a></em> a torch-bearer Snp.335; Iti.108; Mil.1.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81ta">pāta</a></em> “falling of a firebrand”, a meteor DN.i.10 (= ākāsato ukkānaṃ patanaṃ DN-a.i.95); Ja.i.374; Ja.vi.476; Mil.178</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> the opening or receiver of a furnace, a goldsmith’s smelting pot AN.i.257; Ja.vi.217 (= kammār’uddhana C.), Ja.vi.574; Snp.686; Dhp-a.ii.250.</li></ul><p>Vedic ulkā &amp; ulkuṣī, cp. Gr.; α ̓́φλας (= λαμπρ ̈ως torch Hesychius), vελξάνος (= Volcanus); Lat. Volcanus Oir. Olcān, Idg. <em>*ṷI̊q</em> to be fiery</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhāra">dhāra</a>, <a href="bword://pāta">pāta</a>, <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></p>

ukkācanā|ukkaacanaa|ukkacana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaacanaa</span></p><p>enlightening, clearing up, instruction Vb.352 (in def. of lapanā, variant reading ˚kāpanā). <em>Note</em> Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. compares Vism p.115 &amp; Sk. uddīpana in same sense. Def. at Vism.27 (= uddīpanā).</p><p>fr. ukkāceti, ud + <em>*kāc</em>, see <em><a href="bword://ukk%C4%81cita">ukkācita</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkācita">ukkācita</a></p>

ukkāceti|ukkaaceti|ukkaceti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaaceti</span></p><p>to bale out water, to empty by means of buckets Ja.ii.70 (variant reading ussiñcati).</p><p>according to Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 112 a denom. fr. kāca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> a carrying pole, although the idea of a bucket is somewhat removed from that of a pole</p>

ukkācita|ukkaacita|ukkacita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp (mf)n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaacita</span></p><p>boasting; empty talk.</p>

ukkācita|ukkaacita|ukkacita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaacita</span></p><p>enlightened, made bright (fig.) or cleaned, cleared up AN.i.72, AN.i.286 (˚vinīta parisā enlightened &amp; trained).</p><p>pp. either to * <em>kāc</em> to shine or to kāceti denom. fr. kāca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ukkācitavinīta|ukkaacitaviniita|ukkacitavinita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaacitaviniita</span></p><p>trained in empty talk</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkācita">ukkācita</a></p>

ukkādhāra|ukkaadhaara|ukkadhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaadhaara</span></p><p>holding up the torch; a torch bearer</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkā">ukkā</a></p>

ukkāmeti|ukkaameti|ukkameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaameti</span></p><p>to cause to step aside Ja.vi.11.</p><p>Caus. of ukkamati</p>

ukkāmukha|ukkaamukha|ukkamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaamukha</span></p><p>the mouth, opening of a crucible</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkā">ukkā</a></p>

ukkāpata|ukkaapata|ukkapata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaapata</span></p><p>the fall of a meteor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkā">ukkā</a></p>

ukkāra|ukkaara|ukkara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaara</span></p><p>dung, excrement Ja.iv.485, otherwise only in cpd. <em>ukkāra-bhūmi</em> dung-hill Ja.i.5, Ja.i.146 (so read for ukkar˚), Ja.ii.40; Ja.iii.16, Ja.iii.75, Ja.iii.377; Ja.iv.72, Ja.iv.305, Vism.196 (˚ûpama kuṇapa); Dhp-a.iii.208. Cp. <em><a href="bword://ucc%C4%81ra">uccāra</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>kṛ</em>; “do out”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uccāra">uccāra</a></p>

ukkāsati|ukkaasati|ukkasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasati</span></p><p>coughs; clears one’s throat.</p>

ukkāsati|ukkaasati|ukkasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasati</span></p><p>to “ahem”! to cough, to clear one’s throat Vin.ii.222; Vin.iv.16; MN.ii.4; AN.v.65; aor. ukkāsi Ja.i.161, Ja.i.217<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ukk%C4%81sita">ukkāsita</a></em>.</p><p>ud + kāsati of <em>kas</em> to cough</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkāsita">ukkāsita</a></p>

ukkāsi|ukkaasi|ukkasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkāsati">ukkāsati</a></p>

ukkāsikā|ukkaasikaa|ukkasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine (?) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasikaa</span></p><p>pad of cloth (for rubbing the body).</p>

ukkāsikā|ukkaasikaa|ukkasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(f.?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasikaa</span></p><p>at Vin.ii.106 is not clear. Vin Texts iii.68 leave it untranslated. Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is vattavaṭṭi (patta˚? a leaf? Cp. SN.iii.141), prob. = vaṭṭi (Sk varti a kind of pad). See details given by Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 113, who trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “rubber, a kind of pad or roll of cotton with which the delicate bather could rub himself without too much friction”.</p><p>doubtful</p>

ukkāsita|ukkaasita|ukkasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasita</span></p><p>coughed, clearing one’s throat, coughed out, hawking DN.i.89; Bv.i.52 (+ khipita) <em>-sadda</em> the noise of clearing the throat DN.i.50; Ja.i.119; Dhp-a.i.250 (+ khipita˚).</p><p>pp. of ukkāsati</p>

ukkāsitabba|ukkaasitabba|ukkasitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp (mf)n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkāsati">ukkāsati</a></p>

ukkāsitasadda|ukkaasitasadda|ukkasitasadda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasitasadda</span></p><p>the sound of coughing.</p>

ukkāsitvā|ukkaasitvaa|ukkasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkaasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ukkāsati">ukkāsati</a></p>

ukkūla|ukkuula|ukkula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkuula</span></p><p>adjective sloping up, steep, high (opp. vikkūla) AN.i.35 sq.; Vism.153 (nadi); Snp-a.42. Cp. utkūlanikūla-sama Lal.340.</p><p>ud + kūla</p>

ukkūlavikūla|ukkuulavikuula|ukkulavikula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ukkuulavikuula</span></p><p>rising and descending; high and low; uneven.</p>

uklāpa|uklaapa|uklapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uklaapa</span></p><p>full of rubbish, dirty, dusty.</p>

uklāpa|uklaapa|uklapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uklaapa</span></p><p>Uklāpa (ukkalāpa)</p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>deserted Ja.ii.275 (ukkalāpa T.; vv.ll. uklāpa ullāpa).</li><li>dirtied, soiled Vin.ii.154, Vin.ii.208, Vin.ii.222; Vism.128; Dhp-a.iii.168 (ukkalāpa).</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. ut-kalāpayati to let go</p>

ulati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>is a commentator’s invention; said to be = <em><a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></em> to go Vism.60 (in definition of paṃsu-kūla; paṃsu viya kucchita-bhāvaṃ ulatī ti paṃsu-kūlaṃ).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gacchati">gacchati</a></p>

ullahaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective only in acc. nt. ullahakaṃ used adverbially, in cpd. <em>dant˚</em>; after the manner of rubbing the teeth, by means of grinding the teeth MN.iii.167. Seems to be a α ̔́πας λεγομένον.</p><p>?</p>

ullapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>flattering; deceptive.</p>

ullapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i></p><p><em>-ā</em> (f.) calling out, enticing, laying claim to Vin.iii.101; Thig.357; Mil.127; Thag-a.243<br />■ <strong>ullapanā</strong> = uddhaṃ katvā lapanā Vism.27.</p><p>fr. ullapati</p><p>See also: ullapanā</p>

ullapanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullapati">ullapati</a></p>

ullapati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>speaks out, utters aloud; talks up, speaks in exaggerated or deceptive terms; flatters, coaxes; boasts.</p>

ullapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to call out, to talk to, lay claim to Vin.i.97; Vin.iii.105; Pp.67 (= katheti Pp-a.249).</p><p>ud + lapati</p>

ullapitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullapati">ullapati</a></p>

ullapitvā|ullapitvaa|ullapitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ullapitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullapati">ullapati</a></p>

ullapiyamāna|ullapiyamaana|ullapiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass.part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ullapiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullapati">ullapati</a></p>

ullaṅghanā|ulla"nghanaa|ullanghana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"nghanaa</span></p><p>making jump up, raising.</p>

ullaṅghanā|ulla"nghanaa|ullanghana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"nghanaa</span></p><p>jumping up, lifting up, raising Vin.iii.121; Ja.iv.5 (˚samattha?).</p><p>abstr. fr. ud + <em>laṅgh</em></p>

ullaṅghati|ulla"nghati|ullanghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"nghati</span></p><p>to leap up Ja.iii.222 (udakato ˚itvā)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://ulla%E1%B9%85gheti">ullaṅgheti</a></em> to make jump up (always with olangheti, i.e. to make dance up &amp; down Vin.iii.121; Ja.v.434; Dhp-a.iv.197<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ulla%E1%B9%85ghita">ullaṅghita</a></em> (q v.).</p><p>ud + <em>laṅgh</em>, cp. BSk. prollanghya transgressing (= pra + ullangh˚) Divy.596</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullaṅgheti">ullaṅgheti</a>, <a href="bword://ullaṅghita">ullaṅghita</a></p>

ullaṅghayati|ulla"nghayati|ullanghayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"nghayati</span></p><p>makes jump up; raises</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullaṅgheti">ullaṅgheti</a></p>

ullaṅgheti|ulla"ngheti|ullangheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"ngheti</span></p><p>makes jump up; raises</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullaṅghayati">ullaṅghayati</a></p>

ullaṅghita|ulla"nghita|ullanghita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulla"nghita</span></p><p>being jumped on, set on C. on SN.i.40 (see <em>K. S.</em> i.318) (for uḍḍita = taṇhāya ullanghita).</p><p>pp. of ullangheti</p>

ullikhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>combing, scratching Vv-a.349; Thag-a.267.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>likh</em></p>

ullikhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i></p><p>marking, scoring.</p>

ullikhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp neuter</i></p><p>making, scoring; combing.</p>

ullikhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>scratched, combed Vin.i.254; Ja.ii.92 (aḍḍhullikhitehi kesehi); Ud.22 (id. with upaḍḍh for aḍḍh˚); Vv-a.197.</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>likh</em></p>

ullikhāpeti|ullikhaapeti|ullikhapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ullikhaapeti</span></p><p>has one’s hair combed.</p>

ullitavalitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>twice plastered; plastered inside and out</p><p>See also: ullita</p>

ullitavalittaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id;</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullitavalitta">ullitavalitta</a></p>

ullitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>anointed, smeared (upwards); plastered (inside).</p>

ullitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>smeared; only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>ullittāvalitta</em> smeared up &amp; down, i.e. smeared all round Vin.ii.117; MN.ii.8; AN.i.101, AN.i.137; AN.iv.231; Thag.737.</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>lip</em></p>

ulliṅgeti|ulli"ngeti|ullingeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulli"ngeti</span></p><p>to exhibit, show as a characteristic Vism.492.</p><p>Denom. of ud + linga</p>

ulloka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>an awning; a ceiling of cloth.</li><li>a cloth stretched over a bedstead (below the mattress).</li></ol>

ulloka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>doubtful in its meaning; occurs at Vin.i.48 = Vin.ii.209 as ullokā paṭhamaṃ ohāreti, trsl. <em>Vin Texts</em> by “a cloth to remove cobwebs”, but better by Andersen <em>Pāli Reader</em> as “as soon as it is seen”; at Vin.ii.151 the translators give “a cloth placed under the bedstead to keep the stuffing from coming out”. See on term Morris J.P.T.S. 1885, 31<br />■ In cpd ulloka-paduma at Ja.vi.432 it may mean “bright lotus” (lit. to be looked at) See ulloketi.</p><p>ud + lok˚</p>

ullokaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective looking on (to), looking out; in phrase <em>mukh˚</em>; looking into a person’s face; i.e. cheerful winning; or “of bright face”, with a winning smile DN.i.60; DN-a.i.59, DN-a.i.168; Pv-a.219 (˚ika for ˚aka).</p><p>fr. ulloketi</p>

ullokaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ullokayat">ullokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullokayati">ullokayati</a></p>

ullokayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ullokayat">ullokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullokayati">ullokayati</a></p>

ullokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks up, looks up at; looks to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulloketi">ulloketi</a></p>

ullokenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~entī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulloketi">ulloketi</a></p>

ullokesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulloketi">ulloketi</a></p>

ulloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulloketi">ulloketi</a></p>

ulloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks up, looks up at; looks to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullokayati">ullokayati</a></p>

ulloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to look on to, look for, await Ja.i.232 (ākāsaṃ), Ja.i.253; Ja.ii.221, Ja.ii.434; DN-a.i.153, DN-a.i.168; Vv-a.316<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ullokita">ullokita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + lok˚, cp. loka, āloka &amp; viloka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullokita">ullokita</a></p>

ulloketvā|ulloketvaa|ulloketva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulloketvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulloketi">ulloketi</a></p>

ullokita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>looked at, looked on Ja.i.253; DN-a.i.193.</p><p>pp. of ulloketi</p>

ullola
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a wave Ja.iii.228; Ja.vi.394.</li><li>commotion, unrest Ja.iv.306, Ja.iv.476.</li></ol><p>fr. ud + <em>lul</em></p>

ullolanā|ullolanaa|ullolana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ullolanaa</span></p><p>wavering, loitering (in expectation of something), greed Thag-a.243.</p><p>fr. ulloleti</p>

ulloleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to stroll or hang about, to wait for, expect Thag-a.243<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ullulita">ullulita</a></em>.</p><p>denom. fr. ullola</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullulita">ullulita</a></p>

ullopana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://ullumpana">ullumpana</a></em> Dhp-a.i.309 (T. faulty; see remarks ad locum).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullumpana">ullumpana</a></p>

ullulita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>waved, shaken (by the wind); waving Ja.vi.536.</p><p>pp. of ulloleti</p>

ullumpana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>saving, helping; in phrase <em>-sabhāva-saṇṭhita</em> of a helping disposition, full of mercy DN-a.i.177; Pv-a.35. Same as <em><a href="bword://ullopana">ullopana</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>fr. ullumpati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ullopana">ullopana</a></p>

ullumpati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>draws out or up; rescues, saves.</p>

ullumpati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to take up, to help (with acc.), to save Vin.ii.277; DN.i.249.</p><p>ud + <em>lup</em>, cp. BSk. ullumpati Mahāvy § 268</p>

ullāpa|ullaapa|ullapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ullaapa</span></p><p>is variant reading for <em><a href="bword://ukl%C4%81pa">uklāpa</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uklāpa">uklāpa</a></p>

ulāra|ulaara|ulara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ulaara</span></p><p>lofty, great; excellent, best; noble, illustrious; eminent</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ulāra">ulāra</a></p>

ulūka|uluuka|uluka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uluuka</span></p><p>Brown Fish Owl, sometimes also <em>ulūkasakuṇa</em>, Hindi <em>ullu</em>, <em>Ketupa zeylonensis</em> Ja.v.120, a large brown bird with a lighter breast covered with vertical black streaks, prominent tufts or “horns” on its head and large yellow staring eyes. The fish owl favors thick jungle near water and feeds on crabs, fish, frogs, mammals and birds, and like all owls they are silent and stealth creatures A.v.289. The owl was considered the king of birds Ja.ii.242 Mil.403 and they have serious or sour faces. Some ascetics made coats out of owl’s wings or feathers A.i.241 D.i.167, and sandals were made out of their skins Vin.i.186. Owls could be observed sitting on the branch of a tree waiting for prey and giving the appearance that they were “meditating, contemplating, ruminating, speculating” M.i.335. When owls appear during the day they are mobbed by crows Ja.ii.208. See <em>Kosika</em>.</p><p>See also: Kosika</p>

ulūka|uluuka|uluka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uluuka</span></p><p>an owl Vin.i.186 (˚camma, sandals of owl’s skin); Vin.iii.34; AN.v.289 sq.; Ja.ii.208, Ja.ii.352 (as king of the birds); Mil.403; Dhp-a.i.50 (kāka˚ crows &amp; owls).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pakkha">pakkha</a></em> owls’ wings (used as dress) Vin.i.305; DN.i.167.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pakkhika">pakkhika</a></em> dress of owls’ wings, or owl feathers AN.i.241, AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Pp.55 (= ulūka-pattāni ganthetvā kata-nivāsanaṃ Pp-a.233).</li></ul><p>Sk. ulūka; cp. Lat. ulucus &amp; ulula owl, ululāre to howl, Ger. uhu; onomat. <em>*ul</em>, as in Gr. ὀλολύζω, Sk. ululi, Lith. ulůti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pakkha">pakkha</a>, <a href="bword://pakkhika">pakkhika</a></p>

umhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to laugh out loud Ja.ii.131 (= hasitaṃ karoti); Ja.iii.44; Ja.iv.197; Ja.v.299 (˚amāna hasamāna C.). Caus. <em>umhāpeti</em> Ja.v.297.</p><p>Sk. *ut-smayate, ud + <em>smi</em></p>

ummaddeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>massages, rubs (with unguents).</p>

ummaddeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rub something on (acc.) Vin.ii.107 = Vin.ii.266 (mukhaṃ).</p><p>ud + maddeti, Caus. of <em>mṛd</em></p>

ummaddita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummaddeti">ummaddeti</a></p>

ummaddāpenta|ummaddaapenta|ummaddapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaddaapenta</span></p><p>(~entī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummaddāpeti">ummaddāpeti</a></p>

ummaddāpesuṃ|ummaddaapesu.m|ummaddapesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaddaapesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummaddāpeti">ummaddāpeti</a></p>

ummaddāpeti|ummaddaapeti|ummaddapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaddaapeti</span></p><p>causes (someone) to massage or rub (with unguents)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummaddeti">ummaddeti</a></p>

ummagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ummagga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a deviating way, a different way; a wrong way.</p><p>ummagga<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine emergence on the surface; (emergence of) an idea, conception; initial thought</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummaṅga">ummaṅga</a></p>

ummagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>an underground watercourse, a conduit, main MN.i.171; AN.ii.189; Ja.vi.426, Ja.vi.432; Snp-a.50 (“ummaggo paññā pavuccati”) Dhp-a.i.252 (˚cora); Dhp-a.ii.37 (v l. umanga); Dhp-a.iv.104; Pv-a.44 (read with variant reading SS kummagga).</li><li>a side track, a wrong way, devious way SN.i.193 (variant reading ˚manga) = Thag.1242; SN.iv.195; AN.iv.191.</li></ol><p>ud + magga, lit. “off-track”</p>

ummasanā|ummasanaa|ummasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummasanaa</span></p><p>stroking upwards; lifting.</p>

ummasanā|ummasanaa|ummasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummasanaa</span></p><p>lifting up Vin.iii.121 (= uddhaṃ uccāraṇā).</p><p>abstr. fr. ummasati</p>

ummasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>touches; strokes in an upward direction; lifts.</p>

ummasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to touch, take hold of, lift up Vin.iii.121. Cp. next.</p><p>ud + masati of <em>mṛś</em>.</p>

ummatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>distracted; insane.</p>

ummatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective out of one’s mind, mad SN.v.447 (+ viceta); Ja.v.386; Mil.122; Sdhp.88; Pv-a.40 (˚puggala read with variant reading SS for dummati puggala). Cp. next &amp; <em><a href="bword://umm%C4%81da">ummāda</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em> like mad, madly, insane Pv.i.8#1; Pv.ii.6#2 (where Ja.iii.156 has santaramāna).</li></ul><p>ud + matta of <em>mad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummāda">ummāda</a>, <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a></p>

ummattaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. distracted; insane.</p>

ummattaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://ummatta">ummatta</a></em>; Vin.i.123, Vin.i.321; Vin.ii.60, Vin.ii.80; Vin.iii.27, Vin.iii.33; AN.iv.248; Vism.260 (reason for); Mil.277; Pv-a.38, Pv-a.39, Pv-a.93 (˚vesa appearance of a madman), Pv-a.95. f. <em>ummattikā</em> Vin.iv.259, Vin.iv.265; Thag-a.111.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummatta">ummatta</a></p>

ummaṅga|umma"nga|ummanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">umma"nga</span></p><p>“out luck”, i.e. unlucky; or “one who has gone off the right path” Vin.v.144.</p><p>ud + manga (?) or for ummagga, q.v. for vv.ll.</p>

ummaṇga|umma.nga|ummanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">umma.nga</span></p><p>emergence on the surface; (emergence of) an idea, conception; initial thought</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummagga">ummagga</a></p>

ummi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ummi &amp; Ummī</p><p>(f.) a wave Thag.681; Mil.346.</p><p>for the usual ūmi, cp. similar double forms of bhummi → bhūmi</p>

ummihati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>urinates (upon).</p>

ummihati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to urinate Vin.i.78 (ūhanati +).</p><p>ud + <em>mih</em></p>

ummisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to open one’s eyes Ja.iii.96 (opp. nimisati; variant reading ummisati for ˚mīḷ˚?).</p><p>ud + misati</p>

ummujjamāna|ummujjamaana|ummujjamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummujjati">ummujjati</a></p>

ummujjamānaka|ummujjamaanaka|ummujjamanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjamaanaka</span></p><p>(one) who emerges, is raising his head, popping his head up.</p>

ummujjamānaka|ummujjamaanaka|ummujjamanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjamaanaka</span></p><p>adjective emerging AN.ii.182.</p><p>ummujjamāna, ppr. med, of ummujjati, + ka</p>

ummujjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(emergence of) an idea; conception.</p>

ummujjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>emerging Vism.175 (+ nimmujjana); DN-a.i.115.</p><p>fr. ummujjati</p>

ummujjanimujjā|ummujjanimujjaa|ummujjanimujja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjanimujjaa</span></p><p>immersing oneself and emerging; diving in and emerging.</p>

ummujjanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummujjati">ummujjati</a></p>

ummujjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ummujjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. emerges (from); emerges on the surface (esp. of water); raises one’s head (out of).</p><p>ummujjati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. has an idea, conceives (a thought); considers.</p>

ummujjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to emerge, rise up (out of water) Vin.i.180; SN.iv.312; AN.iv.11 sq; Ja.ii.149, Ja.ii.284; Ja.iii.507 Ja.iv.139; Pp.71; Mil.118; DN-a.i.37, DN-a.i.127; Pv-a.113.</p><p>ud + <em>majj</em></p>

ummujjitvā|ummujjitvaa|ummujjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummujjati">ummujjati</a></p>

ummujjā|ummujjaa|ummujja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummujjaa</span></p><p>emerging, jumping out of (water), only in phrase <em>ummujja-nimujjaṃ karoti</em> to emerge &amp; dive DN.i.78; MN.i.69; AN.i.170; Ja.iv.139; Ne.110; Vism.395 (= Pts.ii.208).</p><p>fr. ummujjati</p>

ummuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>firebrand, a piece of burning charcoal to kindle a fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummukka">ummukka</a></p>

ummuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>fire brand Vin.iv.265; SN.iv.92 (T. ummukka meaning “loosened”?); Ja.ii.69 variant reading ˚kk), Ja.ii.404 (kk); Ja.iii.356.</p><p>Sk. ulmuka perhaps to Lat. adoleo, cp. also alāta firebrand; see Walde, Lat. Wtb. s. v. adoleo</p>

ummukka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>ummukka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter a firebrand, a piece of burning charcoal to kindle a fire</p><p>ummukka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. loosened, taken off.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummuka">ummuka</a></p>

ummā|ummaa|umma
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaa</span></p><p>flax, only in cpd. <em>-puppha</em> the (azure) flower of flax MN.ii.13 = AN.v.61 (variant reading dammā˚ ummāta˚); DN.ii.260; Thag.1068; Dhs-a.13. Also (m.) Name of a gem Mil.118.</p><p>cp. Sk. umā</p>

ummāda|ummaada|ummada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaada</span></p><p>distraction of mind, madness, insanity.</p>

ummāda|ummaada|ummada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaada</span></p><p>madness, distraction, mental aberration SN.i.126 (˚ṃ pāpuṇeyya citta-vikkhepaṃ vā); AN.ii.80 AN.iii.119; AN.v.169; Pp.69; Pv-a.6 (˚patta frantic, out of mind), Pv-a.94 (˚vāta), Pv-a.162 (˚patta).</p><p>ud + māda</p>

ummādana|ummaadana|ummadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaadana</span></p><p>causing madness, causing intoxication.</p>

ummādananta|ummaadananta|ummadananta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaadananta</span></p><p>ending in causing intoxication, with intoxication as its end</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummādana">ummādana</a></p>

ummādanā|ummaadanaa|ummadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaadanaa</span></p><p>(or ˚aṃ nt.) maddening Snp.399 (+ mohanaṃ = paraloke ummādanaṃ ihaloke mohanaṃ Snp-a.377); Thag-a.2, Thag-a.357 (cp. Thag-a.243).</p><p>abstr. fr. ummāda</p>

ummāpuppha|ummaapuppha|ummapuppha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaapuppha</span></p><p>See <em>Khoma</em>.</p><p>See also: Khoma</p>

ummāpuppha|ummaapuppha|ummapuppha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaapuppha</span></p><p>the flax flower.</p>

ummāpupphā|ummaapupphaa|ummapuppha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaapupphaa</span></p><p>A class of devas present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.260</p>

ummāra|ummaara|ummara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaara</span></p><p>the threshold (of a house door town gate); windowsill.</p>

ummāra|ummaara|ummara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummaara</span></p><ol><li>a threshold Vin.iv.160 (= indakhīla); Thig.410; Ja.i.62; Ja.iii.101; Vism.425; Dhp-a.i.350.</li><li>a curb-stone Ja.vi.11.</li><li>as <em>uttar˚</em>; (the upper threshold) the lintel Ja.i.111; Dhp-a.ii.5 (variant reading upari˚).</li><li>window-sash or sill Ja.i.347; Ja.iv.356.</li></ol><p>according to Müller P. Gr. = Sk. udumbara (?)</p>

ummī|ummii|ummi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummii</span></p><p>a wave, a billow; a ripple.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūmi">ūmi</a>, <a href="bword://ūmī">ūmī</a></p>

ummī|ummii|ummi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummii</span></p><p>Ummi &amp; Ummī</p><p>(f.) a wave Thag.681; Mil.346.</p><p>for the usual ūmi, cp. similar double forms of bhummi → bhūmi</p>

ummīlayati|ummiilayati|ummilayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummiilayati</span></p><p>causes (the eyes) to open; opens (the eyes) wide</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummīleti">ummīleti</a></p>

ummīleti|ummiileti|ummileti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummiileti</span></p><p>causes (the eyes) to open; opens (the eyes) wide</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummīlayati">ummīlayati</a></p>

ummīleti|ummiileti|ummileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummiileti</span></p><p>to open one’s eyes Ja.i.439; Ja.ii.195; Ja.iv.457; Ja.vi.185; Mil.179, Mil.357 Mil.394; Vism.185, Vism.186; Dhp-a.ii.28 (opp. ni˚); Vv-a.205, Vv-a.314.</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>mīl</em>; opp. ni(m)mīleti</p>

ummūla|ummuula|ummula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummuula</span></p><p>pulled up by the roots; with roots upward.</p>

ummūla|ummuula|ummula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummuula</span></p><p>adjective “roots-out”, with roots showing, laying bare the roots Ja.i.249 (˚ṃ karoti); Sdhp.452.</p><p>ud + mūla</p>

ummūlaka|ummuulaka|ummulaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummuulaka</span></p><p>adjective uprooting, laying bare the roots Ja.i.303 (vāta).</p><p>= ummūla</p>

ummūlaṃ karoti|ummuula.m karoti|ummulam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummuula.m karoti</span></p><p>uproots; makes with its roots upward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ummūla">ummūla</a></p>

ummūleti|ummuuleti|ummuleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ummuuleti</span></p><p>to uproot, to root out Ja.i.329.</p><p>Caus. fr. ummūla</p>

undura
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Rat, sometimes <em><a href="bword://undura">undura</a></em>, Hindi <em>indur</em>. The rat is a small mammal with an elongated body, a moderately pointed snout, approximately equal-length legs and a long sparsely-haired tail. The two most common species of rat in India are the House Rat, <em>Rattus rattus</em>, and the Brown or Norwegian Rat, <em>Rattus norvegicus</em> Ja.iii.123 Vin.i.209. Both rats live in houses and barns but also in fields, jungles and along the banks of waterways. They chewed holes in clothes Vin.i.109 and sometimes overran monasteries Vin.i.208.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://undura">undura</a></p>

undura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>rat or mouse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://undūra">undūra</a></p>

undura
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>rat Vin.i.209; Vin.ii.148, Vin.ii.152; Vin.iii.151; Ja.i.120; Mil.23, Mil.363. Spelt undūra at Vism.62.</p><p>etym?</p>

undurakhāyita|undurakhaayita|undurakhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">undurakhaayita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://undurakkhāyita">undurakkhāyita</a></p><p>gnawed by mice or rats</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://undura">undura</a></p>

undurakkhāyita|undurakkhaayita|undurakkhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">undurakkhaayita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://undurakkhāyita">undurakkhāyita</a></p><p>gnawed by mice or rats</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://undura">undura</a></p>

undūra|unduura|undura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unduura</span></p><p>rat or mouse</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://undura">undura</a></p>

unna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in phrase pīti-vegen;’unna “bubbling up with the excitement of joy” overflowing with joy Mhvs.19, Mhvs.29 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by uggatacitta i.e. lofty, exalted C.)<br />■ It may however be better more in keeping with Pāli word-formation as well as with meaning &amp; interpretation to explain the word as ud na, taking ˚na as abs. (base)-form of; <em>nam</em>, thus lit. “bent up”, i.e. raised, high, in meaning of unnata. Cp. the exactly similar formation, use &amp; meaning of ninna ninnata. Thus unna / ninna would correspond to unnata / ninnata.</p><p>pp. of <em>ud</em>, unatti &amp; undati, see <em><a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udaka">udaka</a></p>

unnadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to resound, shout out, roar Ja.i.110; Ja.ii.90; Ja.iii.271, Ja.iii.325; Mil.18; aor. <strong>unnadi</strong> Ja.i.74; Mil.13<br />caus <em><a href="bword://unn%C4%81deti">unnādeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + nadati</p><p>See also: unnadi, <a href="bword://unnādeti">unnādeti</a></p>

unnahanā|unnahanaa|unnahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnahanaa</span></p><p>flattering, tying or pushing oneself on to somebody, begging Vism.27.</p><p>ud + <em>nah</em>, see <em><a href="bword://nayhati">nayhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nayhati">nayhati</a></p>

unnaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A perfume made from the extract of a particular plant Ja.vi.537.</p>

unnaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>species of perfume Ja.vi.537 (gloss kuṭantaja).</p><p>etym.?</p>

unnala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>boisterous, insolent; uncontrolled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnaḷa">unnaḷa</a></p>

unnala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Unnala &amp; Unnaḷa</p><p>adjective showing off, insolent, arrogant, proud, haughty in phrase <em>uddhata unnaḷa capala</em> MN.i.32; SN.i.61 = SN.i.204 (translated as “muddled in mind, puffed up, vain”, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as uggata-nala uddhaṭa-tuccha-māna K. S. 318); AN.i.70, AN.i.266 AN.ii.26; AN.iii.199, AN.iii.355, AN.iii.391; Iti.113 (+ asamāhita); Dhp.292 (+ pamatta; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “māna-naḷaṃ ukkhipitvā caraṇena unnala” Dhp-a.iii.452); Thag.634; Pp.35 (= uggatanaḷo tuccha-mānaṃ ukkhipitvā ti attho Pp-a 217).</p><p>Bdhgh. has ud + nala; but it is either a dissimilated form for *ullala (n → l change freq. cp. P. nangala → lāngala; nalāṭa → lalaṭa) from ud + <em>lal</em> to sport, thus meaning “sporting, sporty, wild” etc.; or (still more likely) with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. a dial. form of unnata P. uṇṇata, although the P. Commentators never thought of that. Cp. with this the BSk. unnata in same stock phrase uddhata unnata capala Mvu.i.305, and the Marathic Prk. mula = Sk. mṛta, Pischel, <em>Gr.</em> § 244 To these may be added P. celakedu → cetakedu Ja.vi.538</p>

unnama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>rising ground, elevation, plateau Kp.vii.7 = Pv.i.5#7 (thala unnata-padesa Pv-a.29); Mil.349; DN-a.i.154.</p><p>fr ud + <em>nam</em>; cp. also uṇṇama in fig. meaning</p>

unnamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>bends upwards; rises.</li><li>raises oneself; exalts oneself; is elated</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇamati">uṇṇamati</a></p>

unnamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to rise up, ascend Mil.117 (oṇamati +); Vism.306<br />caus unnāmeti (q.v.)<br />pp <em>unnara &amp; uṇṇata;</em> (q.v.). Unnala &amp; Unnala;</p><p>ud + namati, see <em><a href="bword://u%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87amati">uṇṇamati</a></em> in fig. meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇamati">uṇṇamati</a></p>

unnameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>makes bend upwards; raises</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇameti">uṇṇameti</a>, <a href="bword://unnamati">unnamati</a></p>

unnata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>high, lofty; risen; raised.</li><li>elated; haughty</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇata">uṇṇata</a>, <a href="bword://unnamati">unnamati</a></p>

unnata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>raised, high, lofty, in high situation (opp. oṇata) Pv.iv.6#6 (= sāmin Pv-a.262); Ja.i.71; Ja.ii.369; Ja.vi.487; Mil.146, Mil.387; DN-a.i.45 See also unnaḷa</p><p>pp. of unnamati. Besides this form we find <em><a href="bword://u%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87ata">uṇṇata</a></em> in fig. special meaning, q.v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇata">uṇṇata</a></p>

unnatavanata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>raised and depressed; prominent and low; elated and cast down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnata">unnata</a></p>

unnati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>elation; raising oneself; pride</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇati">uṇṇati</a></p>

unnati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(f)</i></p><p>rising, lifting up, elevation Mil.387 (˚avanati)</p><p>fr. unnamati; cp. uṇṇati</p>

unnatunnata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>high (in birth) and high (in moral behavior), who begins high and stays high</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uṇṇatuṇṇata">uṇṇatuṇṇata</a>, <a href="bword://unnata">unnata</a></p>

unnatunnata-onata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>unnatunnata-onata</p><p>mfn. elated and cast down</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnatunnata">unnatunnata</a></p>

unnaḷa|unna.la|unnala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unna.la</span></p><p>boisterous, insolent; uncontrolled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnala">unnala</a></p>

unnaḷa|unna.la|unnala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unna.la</span></p><p>Unnala &amp; Unnaḷa</p><p>adjective showing off, insolent, arrogant, proud, haughty in phrase <em>uddhata unnaḷa capala</em> MN.i.32; SN.i.61 = SN.i.204 (translated as “muddled in mind, puffed up, vain”, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as uggata-nala uddhaṭa-tuccha-māna K. S. 318); AN.i.70, AN.i.266 AN.ii.26; AN.iii.199, AN.iii.355, AN.iii.391; Iti.113 (+ asamāhita); Dhp.292 (+ pamatta; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as “māna-naḷaṃ ukkhipitvā caraṇena unnala” Dhp-a.iii.452); Thag.634; Pp.35 (= uggatanaḷo tuccha-mānaṃ ukkhipitvā ti attho Pp-a 217).</p><p>Bdhgh. has ud + nala; but it is either a dissimilated form for *ullala (n → l change freq. cp. P. nangala → lāngala; nalāṭa → lalaṭa) from ud + <em>lal</em> to sport, thus meaning “sporting, sporty, wild” etc.; or (still more likely) with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. a dial. form of unnata P. uṇṇata, although the P. Commentators never thought of that. Cp. with this the BSk. unnata in same stock phrase uddhata unnata capala Mvu.i.305, and the Marathic Prk. mula = Sk. mṛta, Pischel, <em>Gr.</em> § 244 To these may be added P. celakedu → cetakedu Ja.vi.538</p>

unnaṅgala|unna"ngala|unnangala
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unna"ngala</span></p><p>adjective in phrase <em>-ṃ karoti</em>, according to Morris J<em>P T S</em> 1887, 120 “to make an up-ploughing, to turn up etc.”, but more aptly with C. on Ja.vi.328 to make “out-plough” (<em>not</em> “up-plough”) in sense of out-of-work i.e. to make the people put their ploughs (or work in general) away and prepare for a festival; to take a holiday A typical “Jātaka”-phrase; Ja.i.228; Ja.ii.296, Ja.ii.367; Ja.iii.129, Ja.iii.414; Ja.iv.355; Ja.vi.328; Dhp-a.iii.10.</p><p>ud + nangala, on meaning of ud in this case see <em>ud</em></p>

unnāda|unnaada|unnada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaada</span></p><p>shout, shouting Ja.ii.405.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>nad</em></p>

unnādeti|unnaadeti|unnadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaadeti</span></p><p>to make resound Ja.i.408 (paṭhaviṃ), Ja.ii.34.</p><p>Caus. of unnadati</p>

unnādi|unnaadi|unnadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnādin">unnādin</a></p><p>crying out; clamoring; making noise.</p>

unnādin|unnaadin|unnadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnādin">unnādin</a></p><p>crying out; clamoring; making noise.</p>

unnādin|unnaadin|unnadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaadin</span></p><p>adjective shouting out; resounding, noisy, loud, tumultuous Vin.iii.336; DN.i.95, DN.i.143, DN.i.178; Ja.ii.216.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>nad</em></p>

unnāmaninnāmi|unnaamaninnaami|unnamaninnami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaamaninnaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnāmaninnāmin">unnāmaninnāmin</a></p><p>rising and sinking; uneven</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnāmininnāmin">unnāmininnāmin</a></p>

unnāmaninnāmin|unnaamaninnaamin|unnamaninnamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaamaninnaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnāmaninnāmin">unnāmaninnāmin</a></p><p>rising and sinking; uneven</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnāmininnāmin">unnāmininnāmin</a></p>

unnāmeti|unnaameti|unnameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaameti</span></p><p>(unn˚) to raise Dhs-a.5; written <em>uṇṇameti</em> (with a for ā before mutes &amp; liquids at Snp.206 (inf. uṃṇametave).</p><p>Caus. of unnamati</p>

unnāmin|unnaamin|unnamin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaamin</span></p><p>adjective raising or rising; in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://ninn%C4%81min">ninnāmin</a></em> raised &amp; bent, high &amp; low AN.iv.237 (of cultivated land).</p><p>ud + <em>nam</em> in Caus. form</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ninnāmin">ninnāmin</a></p>

unnāmininnāmi|unnaamininnaami|unnamininnami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaamininnaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnāmininnāmin">unnāmininnāmin</a></p><p>rising and sinking; uneven</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnāmaninnāmin">unnāmaninnāmin</a></p>

unnāmininnāmin|unnaamininnaamin|unnamininnamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">unnaamininnaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://unnāmininnāmin">unnāmininnāmin</a></p><p>rising and sinking; uneven</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://unnāmaninnāmin">unnāmaninnāmin</a></p>

upa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p><a href="bword://upa">upa</a>*, e.g. devūpaṭṭhāna, lokûpaga, puññûpatthambhita-Meanings:</p><ol><li>(Rest): on upon, up-˚kiṇṇa covered over; ˚jīvati live on (cp. anu˚); ˚tthambhita propped up, sup-ported; ˚cita heaped up, ac-cumulated ˚dhāreti hold or take up; ˚nata bent on; ˚nissaya foundation; ˚nissita depending on etc</li><li>(Aim): (out up to (the speaker or hearer); cp. the meanings developed out of this as “higher, above” in upara, upari, upama Lat. superus, supremus E.g. ˚kaḍḍhati drag on to; ˚kappati come to, accrue; ˚kappana ad-ministering; ˚kāra service to; ˚kkhata administered; ˚gacchati go to, ap-proach (cp. upâtigacchati); ˚disati ad-vise; ˚dhāvati run up to ˚nadati to sound out; ˚nikkhamati come out up to; ˚nisevita gone on to or after; ˚neti bring on to; etc</li><li>(Nearness): close by, close to, near, “ad-”; e.g. ˚kaṇṇaka close to the ear; ˚cāra ap-plication; ˚ṭṭhāna at-tending ṭṭhita ap-proached; ˚tiṭṭhati stand by, look after; ˚dduta urged; ˚nāmeti place close to; ˚nibandhati tie close to ˚nisīdati sit close to or down by</li><li>(Intensive use) quite, altogether, “up”; e.g. ˚antika quite near; ˚chindati cut up</li><li>(Diminutive use as in Lat. subabsurdus Gr. ὑπόλευκος whitish; Oir. fo-dord; Cymr. go-durdd murmur): nearly, about, somewhat, a little, secondary by-, miniature, made after the style of, e.g. ˚aḍḍha about half; ˚kacchaka like a little hollow; ˚kaṇḍakin (= ˚paṇḍukin? whitish); ˚deva a minor god; ˚nibha somewhat similar to; ˚nila bluish; upapurohita minor priest uparajja viceroyalty; upalohitaka, uparopa; ˚vana a little forest. etc. <em>Note.</em> The nearest semantie affinity of upa is ā˚.</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

upabbajati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes towards, approaches; visits; becomes involved with.</p>

upabbajati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go to, resort to, visit Thag.1052; Ja.iv.270, Ja.iv.295; Ja.v.495 (= upagacchati C.); Ja.vi.43.</p><p>upa + <em>vraj</em></p>

upabbūḷha|upabbuu.lha|upabbulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upabbuu.lha</span></p><p>see sam˚.</p>

upabhoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>enjoyment, profit Vin.iv.267; Ja.ii.431; Ja.iv.219 (variant reading paribhoga); Ja.vi.361; Mil.201, Mil.403; Pv-a.49, Pv-a.220 (˚paribhoga); Dhp-a.iv.7 (id.); Sdhp.268, Sdhp.341, Sdhp.547.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>bhuj</em> cp. upabhuñjati</p>

upabhogaparibhoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>various objects of enjoyment, goods and possessions.</p>

upabhogga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective to be enjoyed, enjoyable Mil.201.</p><p>Sk. upabhogya, grd. of upabhuñjati</p>

upabhogin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective enjoying Mil.267.</p><p>fr. upabhuñjati</p>

upabhunjaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who eats or enjoys Vism.555.</p><p>fr. next</p>

upabhunjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to enjoy Ja.iii.495; Ja.v.350 (inf. ˚bhottuṃ)<br />grd <em><a href="bword://upabhogga">upabhogga</a></em><br />pp <em>upabhattu</em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>bhuj</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upabhogga">upabhogga</a></p>

upabhutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>enjoyed Dāvs iii.65.</p><p>pp. of upabhunjati</p>

upabrūhaṇa|upabruuha.na|upabruhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upabruuha.na</span></p><p>expansion, increase, augmentation Vism.145; Dhs-a.117.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>bṛh<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, cp. BSk. upabṛṃhita Jtm.31#95</p>

upacarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>approaches; serves, attends; treats carefully, honors; uses figuratively.</p>

upacarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to deal with, handle, use Ja.vi.180<br />pp <em>upaciṇṇa &amp; upacarita;</em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + carati</p>

upacarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>practised, served, enacted, performed Mil.359, Mil.360.</p><p>pp. of upacarati</p>

upacaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>accumulation, quantity, heap; growth, increase.</p>

upacaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>heaping up, gathering, accumulation, heap. As t.t. with ref. to kamma “conservation”, with ref. to body &amp; form “integration” (See discussion &amp; defin. at; <em>Cpd.</em> 253; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 195). DN.i.76 (= odana = kummās’ûpacayo, see under <em><a href="bword://k%C4%81ya">kāya</a></em>) Dhs.582, Dhs.642 (rūpassa u. = āyatanānaṃ ācayo), Dhs.864 Vb.147, Vb.151 sq.; Kv.520; Ne.113; Vism.449; DN-a.i.220; Pv-a.198 (but variant reading paccayassa preferable).</p><p>fr. upa + <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em>, cp. caya &amp; ācaya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāya">kāya</a>, <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

upacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Upacca = uppacca</p><p>(q.v.) “flying up” (= uppatitvā Pv-a.103) at Thig.248 (= Thag-a.205, where variant reading and gloss upecca &amp; upacca, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by upanetvā), as well as at Pv.ii.7#17 (= Pv-a.103 where read upaccha; &amp; gloss upacca &amp; upecca).</p>

upaccagaṃ|upaccaga.m|upaccagam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaccaga.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upātigā">upātigā</a></p>

upaccaguṃ|upaccagu.m|upaccagum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaccagu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upātigā">upātigā</a></p>

upaccagā|upaccagaa|upaccaga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaccagaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upātigā">upātigā</a></p>

upaccagā|upaccagaa|upaccaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaccagaa</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. pret. of upâtigacchati (q.v.) to escape, pass, go by; to overcome Snp.333 (mā upaccagā = mā atikkami Snp-a.339) = Thig.5 (= mā atikkami Thag-a.12); Snp.636, Snp.641, Snp.827 (= accagā atikkanta Mnd.167); Dhp.315, Dhp.412, Dhp.417 (= atikkanta Dhp-a.iv.225); Bv.ii.43<br />■ pl. <em>upaccaguṃ</em> SN.i.35; AN.iii.311.</p><p>upa + ati + agā of <em>gam</em></p>

upaccagū|upaccaguu|upaccagu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaccaguu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upātigā">upātigā</a></p>

upaccati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>? in phrase “akkhīni upacciṃsu” at Ja.vi.187 is probably faulty for <em>apaciyiṃsu</em> aor. of apaciyyati, Pass of apacināti (cp. upaciyyati → upacināti) “the eyes failed” lost power, went bad; cp. apacaya falling off, diminution If not this reading we should suggest <em>upacchijjiṃsu</em> from upacchindati “were destroyed”, which however is not quite the sense wanted.</p>

upaccheda
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>breaking or cutting off, destruction, stoppage, interruption MN.i.245, MN.i.327 (pāṇ murder); Ja.i.67; Mil.134 (paveṇ˚ break of tradition Pv-a.82 (kulavaṃs˚); Dhp-a.i.152 (āhār ˚ṃ karoti to prevent fr. taking food); DN-a.i.136, DN-a.i.159.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>chid</em></p>

upacchedaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun destroying, breaking off, stopping, interrupting Ja.i.418 (vacan˚); Ja.iv.357; DN-a.i.69 (jīvit’ indriy˚); Vv-a.72 (id.).</p><p>fr. upaccheda</p>

upacchijjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacchindati">upacchindati</a></p>

upacchindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>cuts off; interrupts; destroys; stops.</p>

upacchindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to break up or off, to destroy, interrupt, to stop Snp.972 (pot. ˚chinde); Ja.iv.127; Mnd.502; Thag-a.267; Pv-a.31 (kulavaṃso upacchijji aor. pass.); Vism.164, Vism.676 (bhavangaṃ).</p><p>upa + chindati</p>

upacchinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>cut off; interrupted; destroyed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacchindati">upacchindati</a></p>

upacchinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>cut off, interrupted Ja.i.477; Mil.306.</p><p>pp. of upacchindati</p>

upacchubhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws (something, acc.) to (acc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacchumbhati">upacchumbhati</a></p>

upacchubhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to throw at MN.i.364 (vv.ll. ˚chumbh˚, ˚cubh˚).</p><p>upa + chubhati from <em>kṣubh</em> or <em>chubh</em>, see <em><a href="bword://chuddha">chuddha</a></em>, khobha, nicchubhati, nicchodeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://chuddha">chuddha</a></p>

upacchumbhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws (something, acc.) to (acc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacchubhati">upacchubhati</a></p>

upacikā|upacikaa|upacika
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacikaa</span></p><p>Sometimes called white ants, termites are small soft-bodied insects of the order Isoptera that eat wood and live in large colonies M.i.300 Vin.i.284 Vin.ii.113 Vin.ii.149 Vin.iii.151 Vin.iv.41. The Pāḷi name means “mound builder”. Snakes and iguanas are often described as living in holes in termite mounds. Winged termites, called <em><a href="bword://makkhik%C4%81">makkhikā</a></em>, sometimes swarm, particularly after rain Ja.i.488. At such times lizards and other animals gorge themselves on them, even people used to eat them with vinegar and sugar Ja.i.480-81. Termites move from one place to another only after having built a tunnel in which to conceal themselves Mil.392. The Buddha said that a diligent layman’s wealth will grow like a termite’s mound D.iii.188.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://makkhikā">makkhikā</a></p>

upacikā|upacikaa|upacika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacikaa</span></p><p>termite; white ant.</p>

upacikā|upacikaa|upacika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacikaa</span></p><p>the termite or white ant Vin.ii.113 Vin.ii.148, Vin.ii.152; Vin.iii.151; MN.i.306; Ja.iii.320; Ja.iv.331; Mil.363 Mil.392; Vism.62, Dhp-a.ii.25; Dhp-a.iii.15.</p><p>connected with Sk. upadīkā, although the relation is not quite clear. Attempts at explains by Trencker <em>Notes</em> 62 (*utpādikā → upatikā → upacikā) &amp; Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 102 (upacikā = Vedic upajīka, this fr. upajihikā for ˚dihikā, vv.ll. upadehihā &amp; upadīkā). It may however be a direct der. from upa + ; <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em>, thus meaning “making heaps, a builder”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

upacinitvā|upacinitvaa|upacinitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacinitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacināti">upacināti</a></p>

upacināti|upacinaati|upacinati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacinaati</span></p><p>heaps up, collects, accumulates; increases, strengthens.</p>

upacināti|upacinaati|upacinati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacinaati</span></p><ol><li>to collect, heap up, accumulate (puññaṃ or pāpaṃ) Vv-a.254; Pv-a.8, Pv-a.241.</li><li>to concentrate pay attention Thag.199 (C. upacetuṃ for ocetuṃ T.); Ja.v.339 (= oloketi)<br />pass <em>upaciyyati</em> Thag.807</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://upacita">upacita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacita">upacita</a>, <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

upacita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>heaped up, accumulated; strengthened; increased</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacināti">upacināti</a></p>

upacita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>heaped up, accumulated, collected, produced (usually of puñña merit, &amp; kamma karma) Snp.697; Kp-a.132; Snp-a.492; Vv-a.7, Vv-a.271, Vv-a.342; Pv-a.30, Pv-a.150.</li><li>built up, conserved (of the body Mil.232; DN-a.i.220.</li></ol><p>pp. of upacināti</p>

upacitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>storing up, accumulation Dhs.431.</p><p>abstr. fr. upacita</p>

upaciṇṇa|upaci.n.na|upacinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaci.n.na</span></p><p>used, frequented, known (as value) Ja.vi.180.</p><p>pp. of upacarati</p>

upacāla|upacaala|upacala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacaala</span></p><p>Son of Upacālā and nephew of Sāriputta and Khadiravaniya-Revata. He was ordained by Revata. Thag.43 He is mentioned in the Aṅguttara Nikāya AN.v.133 in a list of very eminent disciples, together with Cāla, Kakkaṭa, Kaḷimbha, Nikata and Kaṭissaha. They lived in the Kūṭāgārasālā in Vesāli, but when the Licchavis went there to visit the Buddha, they moved to the Gosiṅgasālavana in search of quiet.</p>

upacālā|upacaalaa|upacala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacaalaa</span></p><p>When Upacālā was taking her siesta in Andhavana, Māra tried to arouse in her sensual desires, but she vanquished him and became an arahant. This account is found in both the Therīgāthā and the Saṃyutta, but the sources ascribe different verses to the nuns Cālā, Upacālā, and Sisūpacālā. Thig.189–195 SN.i.133<em>f.</em></p>

upacāra|upacaara|upacara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacaara</span></p><p>approach, way or means of approach; access; vicinity, neighborhood; access; surrounding space; precincts; environs.</p>

upacāra|upacaara|upacara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacaara</span></p><ol><li>approach, access Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.152; Vin.iv.304; Ja.i.83, Ja.i.172; Dhs-a.328 (phal˚).</li><li>habit practice, conduct Vin.ii.20 (dassan˚); Snp-a.140 (id.); Ja.iii.280.</li><li>way, means application, use of (esp. of spells etc.) Ja.iii.280 (mantassa); Ja.vi.180; Mil.153, Mil.154 (dur˚ an evil spell); Vv-a.127 (gram. t.t. kāraṇ˚).</li><li>entrance, access, i.e. immediate vicinity or neighbourhood of (-˚) Ja.iv.182 (nagar˚); usually as <em>gām˚</em>; Vin.i.109 Vin.iii.46; Vin.iv.230; Kp-a.77; Snp-a.83, Snp-a.179.</li><li>attention attendance Vin.iv.272; Ja.vi.180; Mil.154.</li><li>civility polite behaviour Ja.ii.56; Ja.vi.102.</li><li>On upacāra as philos, t.t. and its relation to <em><a href="bword://appan%C4%81">appanā</a></em> see <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 53 54; <em>Cpd.</em> 55; <em>Mystic</em> p. xi. Thus used of <em><a href="bword://sam%C4%81dhi">samādhi</a></em> (neighbourhood-, or access-concentration, distinguishing it from appanā-samādhi) at Vism.85, Vism.126, Vism.144 and passim.</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>car</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appanā">appanā</a>, <a href="bword://samādhi">samādhi</a></p>

upacāraka|upacaaraka|upacaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upacaaraka</span></p><p>attending upon, serving.</p>

upacīyate|upaciiyate|upaciyate
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaciiyate</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacīyati">upacīyati</a>, upaciyyati, <a href="bword://upacināti">upacināti</a></p>

upacīyati|upaciiyati|upaciyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaciiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacīyate">upacīyate</a>, upaciyyati, <a href="bword://upacināti">upacināti</a></p>

upacīyyati|upaciiyyati|upaciyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaciiyyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upacīyate">upacīyate</a>, <a href="bword://upacīyati">upacīyati</a>, <a href="bword://upacināti">upacināti</a></p>

upadahanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>places upon; lays down, imposes; gives in addition, hands over; places under oneself, lies down upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadheti">upadheti</a></p>

upadahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to put down, supply, furnish, put on; give, cause, make Vin.iv.149; DN.ii.135 (vippaṭisāraṃ); AN.i.203 (dukkhaṃ); Mil.109, Mil.139, Mil.164, Mil.286 Mil.383. grd. pass. <em>-dahātabba</em> to be given or caused Vin.ii.250 = AN.iii.197 (vippaṭisāra). Cp. upadhi.</p><p>upa + dahati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

upadahita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>laid upon; handed over; supplied</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadahitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadahiṃsu|upadahi.msu|upadahimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadahi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadahātabba|upadahaatabba|upadahatabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadahaatabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadasseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(upa + dasseti, Caus. of <em>drś</em>, cp. also upadaṃseti] to make manifest, to show Mil.276, Mil.316, Mil.347.</p>

upadaṃsayati|upada.msayati|upadamsayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msayati</span></p><p>shows, exhibits; makes appear; presents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃseti">upadaṃseti</a></p>

upadaṃsayi|upada.msayi|upadamsayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msayi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsayati">upadaṃsayati</a></p>

upadaṃseta|upada.mseta|upadamseta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.mseta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upadaṃsetar">upadaṃsetar</a></p><p>one who shows; exhibits</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsitar">upadaṃsitar</a></p>

upadaṃsetar|upada.msetar|upadamsetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upadaṃsetar">upadaṃsetar</a></p><p>one who shows; exhibits</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsitar">upadaṃsitar</a></p>

upadaṃseti|upada.mseti|upadamseti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.mseti</span></p><p>shows, exhibits; makes appear; presents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsayati">upadaṃsayati</a></p>

upadaṃseti|upada.mseti|upadamseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.mseti</span></p><p>to cause to appear, to manifest MN.ii.120; SN.i.64, SN.i.65 (of gods, to become resplendent, to show divers colour-tones); AN.ii.84 = AN.iii.139 = AN.iii.264 = Pp.49 (to show pleasure); Thag.335, to bring forth (a goad, and so incite, urge on); Vin.iv.309.</p><p>= upadasseti with ˚aṃs˚ for ˚ass˚ like dhanseti = Sk. dharṣayati, haṃsa = harṣa etc. only in poetical passages</p>

upadaṃsetuṃ|upada.msetu.m|upadamsetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msetu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃseti">upadaṃseti</a></p>

upadaṃsita|upada.msita|upadamsita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upadaṃsitar">upadaṃsitar</a></p><p>one who shows; exhibits</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsetar">upadaṃsetar</a></p>

upadaṃsitar|upada.msitar|upadamsitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msitar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upadaṃsitar">upadaṃsitar</a></p><p>one who shows; exhibits</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadaṃsetar">upadaṃsetar</a></p>

upadaṃsitar|upada.msitar|upadamsitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upada.msitar</span></p><p>one who shows Pp.49 (where upadhaṃsita is to be corrected to upad˚, as already pointed out by Morris <em>J P T S.</em> 1887, 126. The word seems to be a crux to commentators, philologists and translators, like upadaṃseti. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. keeps to the reading upah˚, tries to connect it with Sk. dharṣati &amp; trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “one who confirms”. The Pp A leaves the word unexplained).</p><p>n. ag. fr. upadaṃseti</p>

upaddava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>sudden or grievous accident, calamity, misfortune; violence, oppression.</p>

upaddava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>lit. rushing on; accident, misfortune, distress, oppression SN.ii.210; AN.i.101; Snp.51; Dhp.338 (an˚); Dhp-a.i.16; Sdhp.267, Sdhp.398.</p><p>upa + dava<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of <em>dru</em></p>

upaddavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to annoy, trouble DN-a.i.213<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upadduta">upadduta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>dru</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadduta">upadduta</a></p>

upadduta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>(some times written as upaddūta) oppressed; tyrannized over; assailed; visited by calamities.</p>

upadduta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>overrun, oppressed, annoyed, overcome, distressed Vin.ii.170; Vin.iii.144, Vin.iii.283; SN.ii.210 SN.iv.29; Ja.i.26, Ja.i.61, Ja.i.339; Ja.ii.102; Ja.iv.324, Ja.iv.494; Pv.ii.10#8 Vism.24 (= apakata); Mil.279; Vv-a.311 (aṭṭita +) Pv-a.61. <em>an˚</em>; unmolested Pv-a.195; anupaddutatta state of not being molested Vv-a.95. Upadhamsitar &amp; Upadhamseti;</p><p>pp. of upaddavati</p>

upaddūta|upadduuta|upadduta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadduuta</span></p><p>oppressed; tyrannized over; assailed; visited by calamities</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadduta">upadduta</a></p>

upadesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>pointing out, indication, instruction, advice Pv-a.26 (tadupadesena read for tadupād˚; Kp-a.208 differs at id. p.); Kp-a.100; Sdhp.227.</p><p>fr. upadisati</p>

upadeva
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>secondary, lesser, minor god Pv-a.136.</p><p>upa + deva, on use of upa in this meaning see <em><a href="bword://upa">upa</a></em> 5</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

upadhaṃseti|upadha.mseti|upadhamseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadha.mseti</span></p><p>Upadhaṃsitar &amp; Upadhaṃseti</p><p>at Pp.49 is to be read <em>upad˚</em>; (q.v.).</p>

upadhaṃsitar|upadha.msitar|upadhamsitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadha.msitar</span></p><p>Upadhaṃsitar &amp; Upadhaṃseti</p><p>at Pp.49 is to be read <em>upad˚</em>; (q.v.).</p>

upadheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>places upon; lays down, imposes; gives in addition, hands over; places under oneself, lies down upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upadheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>cushion Ja.vi.490 (for upatheyya, q.v.).</p><p>cp. upadhāna</p>

upadhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>worldly possessions or belongings, acquisitions (according to commentaries including the body); attachment to such possessions (forming a basis for rebirth).</p>

upadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>putting down or under, foundation basis, ground, substratum (of rebirth) SN.i.117, SN.i.124 SN.i.134, SN.i.186; AN.ii.24 (˚sankhaya); AN.iii.382 (id.); AN.iv.150 (˚kkhaya); Iti.21, Iti.69; Snp.364, Snp.728 (upadhī-nidānā dukkha = vaṭṭa-dukkhaṃ Snp-a.505), Snp.789, Snp.992; Mnd.27, Mnd.141; Cnd.157; Vb.338; Ne.29; Dhp-a.iv.33</li><li>clinging to rebirth (as impeding spiritual progress), attachment (almost syn. with kilesa or taṇhā, cp. nirupadhi &amp; anupadhi) S A. = pañcakkhandhā, SN.ii.108. At MN.i.162 (cp. Snp.33 = SN.i.6 = SN.i.107) wife and children, flocks and herds silver and gold are called <em>upadhayo. upadhi</em> is the root of sorrow MN.i.454; SN.ii.108; Snp.728 = Snp.1051 Thag.152 and the rejection of all upadhis is Nibbāna DN.ii.36. (cp. SN.i.136; SN.iii.133; SN.v.226; AN.i.80; MN.i.107 = MN.ii.93; Vin.i.5, Vin.i.36 = Ja.i.83 = Mvst.ii.444; Iti.46 Iti.62); DN.iii.112 calls that which has <em><a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></em> ignoble (non-Aryan). At SN.i.117 = Divy.224 upadhi is called a bond (saṃgo). Cp. opadhika<br />■ The upadhis were later systematized into a set of 10, which are given at Cnd.157 as follows: 5 taṇh’ upadhis (taṇhā, diṭṭhi, kilesa kamma, duccarita), āhār-upadhi, paṭigh˚, catasso upādinnā dhātuyo u. (viz. kāma, diṭṭhi, sīlabbata, attavāda; see DN.iii.230, cha ajjhattikāni āyatanāni u., cha viññāṇa-kāyā u. Another modified classification see at <em>Brethren</em> p.398 Upadhika (Upadhika).</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>dhā</em>, cp. upadahati &amp; BSk. upadhi Divy.50, Divy.224, Divy.534</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhi">upadhi</a></p>

upadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Upadhika (Upadhīka)</p><p>adjective (-˚) having a substratum, showing attachment to rebirth, only in compounds <em>an˚</em> free from clinging Vin.i.36; Snp.1057, &amp; <em>nir˚</em>; id. SN.i.141.</p><p>fr. upadhi</p>

upadhāna|upadhaana|upadhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaana</span></p><p>adj. nt. “putting under”, i.e.</p><ol><li>a pillow, cushion DN.i.7; SN.ii.267 = Mil.366 (kaḷingar˚); SN.iii.145; AN.i.137, AN.i.181; AN.iii.50; Ja.iv.201; Ja.v.506 (tamb˚ = ratt˚ C.).</li><li>imposing, giving, causing Dhp.291 (dukkh˚)</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>dhā</em>, cp. upadahati</p>

upadhāneti|upadhaaneti|upadhaneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaaneti</span></p><p>to suppose, think, reflect Dhp-a.i.239 (should be corrected to <em><a href="bword://upadh%C4%81reti">upadhāreti</a></em>).</p><p>f. upa + <em>dhā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhāreti">upadhāreti</a></p>

upadhārayati|upadhaarayati|upadharayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaarayati</span></p><p>considers, reflects on; holds in the mind, pays attention to; holds, considers as; looks for, investigates, finds out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhāreti">upadhāreti</a></p>

upadhāraṇa|upadhaara.na|upadharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine part</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaara.na</span></p><p>of a vinā, perhaps the head or finger-holds.</p>

upadhāraṇā|upadhaara.naa|upadharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaara.naa</span></p><p>[fr. upa + <em>dhṛ</em>;) “receptacle”, milk-pail DN.ii.192; AN.iv.393; Ja.vi.503. See kaṃs˚. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>i. 142 proposes corruption fr. kaṃs’ûpadohana, which latter however does not occur in Pali.</p><p>Upadhāraṇā</p><p>(f) calculation Vv-a.7.</p><p>cp. upadhāraṇa</p>

upadhāreti|upadhaareti|upadhareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaareti</span></p><p>considers, reflects on; holds in the mind, pays attention to; holds, considers as; looks for, investigates, finds out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhārayati">upadhārayati</a></p>

upadhāreti|upadhaareti|upadhareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaareti</span></p><ol><li>“to hold or take up” (cp. semantically Lat. teneo = E. tenet) to reason out, conclude, reflect, surmise, know as such such, realise Ja.i.338; Dhp-a.i.28, Dhp-a.i.41; Dhp-a.ii.15, Dhp-a.ii.20, Dhp-a.ii.37, Dhp-a.ii.96 Dhp-a.iv.197 (an˚); Vv-a.48, Vv-a.200 (an˚), Vv-a.234, Vv-a.260 (an˚), Vv-a.324; Pv-a.119 (for jānāti).</li><li>to look out for (acc.) Ja.iii.65; Ja.vi.2.</li></ol><p>Caus. of upa + <em>dhṛ</em>; cp. dhāreti 3</p>

upadhārita|upadhaarita|upadharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaarita</span></p><p>considered; kept in mind; understood</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhāreti">upadhāreti</a></p>

upadhārita|upadhaarita|upadharita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaarita</span></p><p>considered, reflected upon Dhp.i.28; sûpadh˚ Mil.10; dûpadh˚ Vin.iv.275.</p><p>pp. of upadhāreti</p>

upadhāvati|upadhaavati|upadhavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaavati</span></p><p>runs up to, rushes up; hurries towards (for help); runs after.</p>

upadhāvati|upadhaavati|upadhavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaavati</span></p><p>to run up to or after, fall upon, surround Vin.ii.207; Vin.iv.260 (pp. ˚dhāvita); SN.i.185; SN.ii.26 (aparantaṃ); Thag.1209; Mil.209; Vv-a.256; Pv-a.154, Pv-a.168, Pv-a.173 (for padhāvitā).</p><p>upa + dhāvati 1</p>

upadhāvitvā|upadhaavitvaa|upadhavitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaavitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadhāvati">upadhāvati</a></p>

upadhāya|upadhaaya|upadhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhaaya</span></p><p>places upon; lays down, imposes; gives in addition, hands over; places under oneself, lies down upon</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadheti">upadheti</a></p>

upadhīka|upadhiika|upadhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadhiika</span></p><p>Upadhika (Upadhīka)</p><p>adjective (-˚) having a substratum, showing attachment to rebirth, only in compounds <em>an˚</em> free from clinging Vin.i.36; Snp.1057, &amp; <em>nir˚</em>; id. SN.i.141.</p><p>fr. upadhi</p>

upadisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg</i></p><p>points out (to); indicates; explains, teaches.</p>

upadisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to point out, show, advise, specify Ja.v.457 (sippaṃ); Mil.21 (dhamma-maggaṃ)<br />pp upadiṭṭha (q.v.).</p><p>upa + disati</p>

upadissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is seen, appears.</p>

upadissati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be seen (open), to be shown up, to be found out or discovered Snp.140 (pres. upadissare = ˚nti Snp-a.192).</p><p>upa + dissati</p>

upadiṭṭha|upadi.t.tha|upadittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadi.t.tha</span></p><p>pointed out, put forth, specified Mil.144 (pañha).</p><p>pp. of upadisati</p>

upadussati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>bears ill-will, is resentful.</p>

upadussiṃ|upadussi.m|upadussim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadussi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadussati">upadussati</a></p>

upadāyaka|upadaayaka|upadayaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upadaayaka</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) giving, bestowing. Sdhp.319</p><p>fr. upa + <em>dā</em></p>

upaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(always as ˚ûpaga) adjective</p><ol><li>going to, getting to, reaching, in phrases kāy˚, SN.ii.24; ākās’ānañc’ āyatan˚ etc. Pts.i.84; kāy˚ SN.ii.24; brahmalok˚ Pv.ii.13#19 yathākamm˚ DN.i.82.</li><li>coming into, experiencing, having as vikappan˚ according to option Vin.iv.283; phal˚ bearing fruit, &amp; pupph˚ having flowers, in flower Pv-a.275.</li><li>attached to, belonging to, being at Ja.i.51 (hatth˚) Vv-a.12 (id. + pādûpaga).</li><li>in phrase <em>gayh˚</em>; lit. “accessible to the grip”, acquisition of property, theft Ja.iv.219 (T. gayhûpaka); Mil.325; Dhp-a.ii.29; Pv-a.4.</li></ol><p>upa + ga</p>

upagacchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>approaches, reaches, arrives at; enters any state, enters upon; participates in, undergoes; makes choice of, holds to; admits, regards (as).</p>

upagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to come to, go to, approach, flow to (of water) DN.ii.12; Pv-a.12 (vasanaṭṭhānaṃ), Pv-a.29, Pv-a.32 (vāsaṃ) Pv-a.132;<br />ger <em>-gantvā</em> Pv-a.70 (attano santikaṃ), &amp; <em>-gamma</em> SN.ii.17, SN.ii.20.</li><li>to undergo, go (in) to, to begin, undertake Snp.152 (diṭṭhiṃ anupagamma); Ja.i.106 (vassaṃ); Pv-a.42 (id.); Ja.i.200; niddaṃ upagacchati to drop off into sleep Pv-a.43 (aor. upagacchi MSS. ˚gañchi), Pv-a.105, Pv-a.128</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://upagata">upagata</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + gacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagata">upagata</a></p>

upagacchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagacchisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagacchiṃ|upagacchi.m|upagacchim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagacchi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagacchiṃsu|upagacchi.msu|upagacchimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagacchi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagacchu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagacchuṃ">upagacchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagacchuṃ|upagacchu.m|upagacchum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagacchu.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagacchuṃ">upagacchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>approach, arrival; allowing, consenting to; entering upon; holding to.</p>

upagamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>approaching, going or coming to, undergoing, undertaking Vin.ii.97 (+ ajjhupag˚) Ne.27; Vism.600; Pv-a.42 (vass˚).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>gam</em></p>

upagamanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective going to, one who goes to (with acc.) Pv-a.168 (= ˚upaga).</p><p>fr. upagamana</p>

upagamiṃsu|upagami.msu|upagamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upaganta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagantar">upagantar</a></p><p>one who approaches; one who enters upon.</p>

upagantabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagantar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagantar">upagantar</a></p><p>one who approaches; one who enters upon.</p>

upagantuṃ|upagantu.m|upagantum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagantu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagantvā|upagantvaa|upagantva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagantvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>having approached, having arrived, having reached; having gone to, entered upon; undergoing; holding to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>gone to, come, approached (intrs.) Snp.708 (āsan˚ = nisinna Snp-a.495) Pv-a.77 (santikaṃ), Pv-a.78, Pv-a.79 (petalokaṃ), Pv-a.123.</li><li>undergoing coming or come under, overpowered, suffering Nd ii.under asita (= ajjhupagata in same conn. at AN.v.187) Pv.i.11#10 (khuppipās˚); Pv-a.60 (= abhibhūta).</li></ol><p>pp. of upagacchati</p>

upagañchi|upaga~nchi|upaganchi
