<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga~nchi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagañchu|upaga~nchu|upaganchu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga~nchu</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagañchuṃ">upagañchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagañchuṃ|upaga~nchu.m|upaganchum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga~nchu.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upagañchuṃ">upagañchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upagacchati">upagacchati</a></p>

upagaḷita|upaga.lita|upagalita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga.lita</span></p><p>flowing out, spat or slobbered out Ja.v.471 (˚khelo; variant reading paggharita).</p><p>pp. of upagaḷati</p>

upagaṇhanā|upaga.nhanaa|upaganhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga.nhanaa</span></p><p>taking up, keeping up. meditating Mil.37.</p><p>abstr. of upa + <em>gṛh</em></p>

upagaṇhāti|upaga.nhaati|upaganhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaga.nhaati</span></p><p>to take up (for meditation) Mil.38.</p><p>upa + gaṇhāti</p>

upagghāta|upagghaata|upagghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagghaata</span></p><p>scented, smelled, kissed Ja.vi.543 (C. sīsamhi upasinghita).</p><p>pp. of next</p>

upagghāyati|upagghaayati|upagghayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagghaayati</span></p><p>to smell at, in sense of “to kiss” Ja.v.328 (see <em>inf. upagghātuṃ</em>).</p><p>upa + <em>ghrā</em>, see *<a href="bword://gh%C4%81yati">ghāyati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: inf. upagghātuṃ, <a href="bword://ghāyati">ghāyati</a></p>

upaghaṭṭita|upagha.t.tita|upaghattita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagha.t.tita</span></p><p>knocked or knocking against Ja.i.26 (verse 179).</p><p>pp. of upaghaṭṭeti</p>

upaghāta|upaghaata|upaghata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaata</span></p><p>hurt; injury; damage.</p>

upaghāta|upaghaata|upaghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaata</span></p><p>hurting, injuring, injury MN.iii.237; SN.ii.218; SN.iv.323 sq.; AN.iii.173; Thag.583; Mil.274, Mil.307, Mil.347; DN-a.i.273. <em>an˚</em>; not hurting others, kindness Dhp.185.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>(g)han</em>, cp. ghāta</p>

upaghātana|upaghaatana|upaghatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaatana</span></p><p>hurting Dhp-a.iii.237 (an˚).</p><p>fr. upaghāta</p>

upaghāti|upaghaati|upaghati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaati</span></p><p>hurting; damage.</p>

upaghātika|upaghaatika|upaghatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaatika</span></p><p>hurting, injury; damage.</p>

upaghātika|upaghaatika|upaghatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaatika</span></p><p>adjective injuring, offending Vin.ii.13.</p><p>fr. upaghāta</p>

upaghātin|upaghaatin|upaghatin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaghaatin</span></p><p>adjective hurting, injuring Ja.iii.523.</p><p>fr. upaghāta</p>

upagā|upagaa|upaga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagaa</span></p><p>approached; arrived at;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāgā">upāgā</a></p>

upagāmin|upagaamin|upagamin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upagaamin</span></p><p>adjective going to, undergoing, experiencing AN.ii.6 (jāti jar˚).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>gam</em>, cp. ˚upaga</p>

upagūhati|upaguuhati|upaguhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaguuhati</span></p><p>to embrace Ja.i.346, Ja.i.349; Ja.ii.424; Ja.iii.437; Ja.v.157, Ja.v.328, Ja.v.384<br />ger <em>upaguyha</em> Ja.vi.300.</p><p>upa + gūhati</p>

upahacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>coming into contact with, reaching; harming; making impure or ill disposed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahanati">upahanati</a></p>

upahacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>˚-</p><ol><li>spoiling, impairing, defiling Ja.v.267 (manaṃ)</li><li>reducing, cutting short only in phrase <em>upahacca-parinibbāyin</em> “coming to extinction after reducing the time of rebirths (or after having almost reached the destruction of life”) SN.v.70, SN.v.201 sq. AN.i.233 sq.; AN.iv.380; Pp.17 (upagantvā kālakiriyaṃ āyukkhayassa āsane ṭhatvā ti attho Pp-a.199); Ne.190</li></ol><p>■ The term is not quite clear; there seems to have existed very early confusion with upapacca → upapajja → uppajja, as indicated by BSk. upapadya-parinirvāyin, and by remarks of C. on Kv.268, as quoted at <em>Kvu trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 158, 159.</p><p>ger. of upahanti</p>

upahaccaparinibbāyi|upahaccaparinibbaayi|upahaccaparinibbayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahaccaparinibbaayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upahaccaparinibbāyin">upahaccaparinibbāyin</a></p><p>who realizes nibbāna in the latter half of his existence in one of the suddhāvāsas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahacca">upahacca</a></p>

upahaccaparinibbāyin|upahaccaparinibbaayin|upahaccaparinibbayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahaccaparinibbaayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upahaccaparinibbāyin">upahaccaparinibbāyin</a></p><p>who realizes nibbāna in the latter half of his existence in one of the suddhāvāsas</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahacca">upahacca</a></p>

upahanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>hits, comes into contact with, reaches; hurts, damages, impairs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahanti">upahanti</a></p>

upahanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>hits, comes into contact with, reaches; hurts, damages, impairs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahanati">upahanati</a></p>

upahanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(&amp; <em>-hanati</em> Ja.i.454) to impair, injure; to reduce, cut short; to destroy, only in ger. <em><a href="bword://upahacca">upahacca</a></em>; pp. <em><a href="bword://upahata">upahata</a></em> &amp; Pass.; <em><a href="bword://upaha%C3%B1%C3%B1ati">upahaññati</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahacca">upahacca</a>, <a href="bword://upahata">upahata</a>, <a href="bword://upahaññati">upahaññati</a>, <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

upahantvāna|upahantvaana|upahantvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahantvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahanati">upahanati</a></p>

upaharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>brings, brings near; offers, presents.</p>

upaharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to bring, offer, present AN.ii.87; AN.iii.33; Dhp.i.301, Dhp.i.302; Ja.v.477.</p><p>upa + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upaharaṇa|upahara.na|upaharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahara.na</span></p><ol><li>presentation; luxury Ja.i.231.</li><li>taking, seizing Ja.vi.198.</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>harmed, injured, impaired; upset, spoiled; made impure or ll-disposed.</p>

upahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>injured, spoilt; destroyed DN.i.86 (phrase khata + upahata); SN.i.238 (na sûpahata “not easily put out” trsl.); SN.ii.227; AN.i.161; Dhp.134; Ja.vi.515; Mil.223, Mil.302; Dhp-a.ii.33 (an˚).</p><p>The formula at DN.i.86 (khata + upahata) is doubtful as to its exact meaning According to Bdhgh it means “one who has destroyed his foundation of salvation, i.e. one who cannot be saved. Thus at DN-a.i.237: “bhinna-patiṭṭho jāto, i.e. without a basis. Cp. remarks under khata. The translation at <em>Dial.</em> i.95 gives it as “deeply affected and touched in heart”: doubtful. The phrase <em>upahaccaparinibbāyin</em> may receive light from <em><a href="bword://upahata">upahata</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of upahanti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahata">upahata</a></p>

upahatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upahattar">upahattar</a></p><p>one who brings, who presents.</p>

upahattar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upahattar">upahattar</a></p><p>one who brings, who presents.</p>

upahattar
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>bringer (of) MN.i.447 sq.</p><p>Sk. *upahartṛ, n. ag. of upa + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upahaññati|upaha~n~nati|upahannati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaha~n~nati</span></p><p>is harmed, is damaged; suffers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upahanati">upahanati</a></p>

upahaññati|upaha~n~nati|upahannati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaha~n~nati</span></p><p>to be spoilt or injured Snp.584; Ja.iv.14; Mil.26.</p><p>Pass. of upahanti</p>

upahiṃsati|upahi.msati|upahimsati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahi.msati</span></p><p>hurts, injures.</p>

upahiṃsati|upahi.msati|upahimsati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahi.msati</span></p><p>to injure, hurt Vin.ii.203; Ja.iv.156.</p><p>upa + <em>hiṃs</em></p>

upahāra|upahaara|upahara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahaara</span></p><p>bringing; offering; present, presentation; service.</p>

upahāra|upahaara|upahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upahaara</span></p><p>bringing forward, present, offering, gift Vin.iii.136 (āhār˚) AN.ii.87; AN.iii.33; AN.v.66 (mett˚); Ja.i.47; Ja.iv.455; Ja.vi.117; DN-a.i.97.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upajjha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see next.</p>

upajjhā|upajjhaa|upajjha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaa</span></p><p>the state of having a preceptor; pupillage; being under instruction.</p>

upajjhāya|upajjhaaya|upajjhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaaya</span></p><ol><li>a companion, adviser; teacher.</li><li>the preceptor (of a bhikkhu); an instructor; the sponsor for a bhikkhu’s upasampadā.</li></ol>

upajjhāya|upajjhaaya|upajjhaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaaya</span></p><p>spiritual teacher or preceptor, master. Often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://%C4%81cariya">ācariya</a></em> e.g. Vin.i.119; Mnd.350; the ācariya being only the deputy or substitute of the upajjhāya. Vin.i.45, Vin.i.53, Vin.i.62, Vin.i.120; Vin.iv.130; SN.i.185; AN.ii.66, AN.ii.78; AN.iii.69; Snp-a.346; Dhp-a.ii.93; Pv-a.55, Pv-a.60, Pv-a.230<br />■ A short form of upajjhāya is <em><a href="bword://upajjha">upajjha</a></em>, found in the Vinaya, e.g. at Vin.i.94; Vin.iii.35 with f. <em>upajjhā</em> Vin.iv.326.</p><p>Vedic upādhyāya, upa + adhi + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, līt. “one who is gone close up to”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ācariya">ācariya</a>, <a href="bword://upajjha">upajjha</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

upajjhāyamatta|upajjhaayamatta|upajjhayamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaayamatta</span></p><p>(one) having the status of preceptor; like a preceptor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajjhāya">upajjhāya</a></p>

upajjhāyamūlaka|upajjhaayamuulaka|upajjhayamulaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaayamuulaka</span></p><p>deriving from the preceptor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajjhāya">upajjhāya</a></p>

upajjhāyā|upajjhaayaa|upajjhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajjhaayaa</span></p><p>the preceptress (of a <em>bhikkhunī</em>); an instructor.</p>

upajānāti|upajaanaati|upajanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajaanaati</span></p><p>finds out (for oneself), ascertains; invents; knows by personal experience (+ acc. or gen.); (first) teaches.</p>

upajānāti|upajaanaati|upajanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajaanaati</span></p><p>to learn, acquire or have knowledge of (w. gen. or instr.), to know Vin.i.272 (saṃyamassa); Vin.ii.181 (gharāvās’atthena); AN.i.50 (dvinnaṃ dhammānaṃ upaññāsin)<br />fut <em>upaññissati (&amp; upaññassati;</em> Snp.716) Snp.701, Snp.716 (= upaññayissati kathayissati Snp-a.498); Ja.v.215<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upa%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81ta">upaññāta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + jānāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaññāta">upaññāta</a></p>

upajāyati|upajaayati|upajayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajaayati</span></p><p>is produced (in addition); appears; is (re)born.</p>

upajīvamāna|upajiivamaana|upajivamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajiivamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajīvati">upajīvati</a></p>

upajīvati|upajiivati|upajivati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajiivati</span></p><p>lives upon, supports oneself by, makes a living from (+ acc.; occasionally + instr.); is dependent on, serves; makes use of.</p>

upajīvati|upajiivati|upajivati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajiivati</span></p><p>to live on (w. acc.), to depend on, to live by somebody, to be supported by (acc.) DN.i.228; SN.i.217; Snp.612 sq.; Thag.943; Ja.iii.309, Ja.iii.338 Ja.iv.271 (= anujīvati); Pv.ii.9#50 (Ankuraṃ u. ti taṃ nissāya jīvanti Pv-a.134); Mil.231.</p><p>upa + jīvati</p>

upajīvika|upajiivika|upajivika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajiivika</span></p><p>adjective Sdhp.501 (see next).</p><p>= upajīvin</p>

upajīvin|upajiivin|upajivin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajiivin</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective noun living on, subsisting by AN.ii.135 (phal˚); Snp.217 (para-datt˚), Ja.i.227 (vohār˚); Ja.iv.380; Pp.51; Mil.160 (Satth˚); Vv-a.141 (sipp˚). f. upajīvinī in rūp˚ (itthi) a woman earning her living by her beauty (i.e. a courtesan) Mil.122; Pv-a.46; cp. kiliṭṭha-kamm˚ gaṇikā Pv-a.195.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>jīv</em></p>

upajūta|upajuuta|upajuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upajuuta</span></p><p>stake at game Ja.vi.192.</p><p>upa + jūta</p>

upaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Upaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>An Ājivaka whom the Buddha met on his way between Gayā and the Bodhi Tree, after he set out from Isipatana for the preaching of the First Sermon. Upaka questioned the Buddha on his attainments, and when the Buddha told him what he had accomplished he asked the Buddha if he were “Anantajina.” When the Buddha acknowledged it, Upaka shook his bead saying, “It may be so, friend,” and went along by another road Vin.i.8 MN.i.170–171 The Saṃyutta Nikāya SN.i.35 SN.i.60 records a visit paid to the Buddha by Upaka and six other beings born in Avihā.</p><p>Upaka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Maṇḍikāputta</p><p>He once visited the Buddha at Gijjhakūṭa and stated before him his view that whoever starts abusive talk of another, without being able to make good his case, is blameworthy. The Buddha agrees and says that Upaka himself has been guilty of this offence. Upaka protests against being caught in a big noose of words, like a fish caught as soon as he pops up his head. The Buddha explains that it is necessary for him to teach with endless variations of words and similes. Upaka is pleased with the Buddha’s talk and reports the conversation to Ajātasattu. The king shows his anger at the man’s presumption in having remonstrated with the Buddha. AN.ii.181<em>f.</em></p><p>He was apparently of low caste, and Ajātasattu addresses him as “salt-worker’s boy” (<em>loṇakārakadāraka</em>). AN.ii.182</p>

upaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) found only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>kulūpaka</em> where second k stands for g. through assimilation with first k. Only with ref. to a bhikkhu = one who frequents a certain family (for the purpose of getting alms), a family friend, associate Vin.i.192, Vin.i.208; Vin.iii.84; SN.ii.200 sq.; AN.iii.258 sq.; Cnd.385#1; Pv.iii.8#5; Pv-a.266<br />■ f. <em>kulūpikā</em> (bhikkhunī) Vin.ii.268; Vin.iv.66<br />■ Sporadic in gayhūpaka (for ˚ûpaga) at Ja.iv.219.</p><p>for ˚upaga</p>

upakaccha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>˚- only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>-antare</em> lit. “in between the hips or loins or arm-pits” in 3 phrases (cp. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>ii.140 s. v.), viz. upakacchantare <em><a href="bword://katv%C4%81">katvā</a></em> taking (it) between the legs Ja.i.63, Ja.i.425 <strong>khipitvā</strong> throwing (it) into the armpits Ja.v.211 &amp; <em><a href="bword://%E1%B9%ADhapetv%C4%81">ṭhapetvā</a></em> id. Ja.v.46.</p><p>upa + kacchā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://katvā">katvā</a>, khipitvā, <a href="bword://ṭhapetvā">ṭhapetvā</a></p>

upakacchaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i></p><p>(the region of) the armpit.</p>

upakacchaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>[= upa + kaccha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + ka] like an enclosure adj. in the form of a hollow or a shelter Ja.i.158</li><li>[= upa + kacchā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + ka] like the armpit, a hollow usually the armpit, but occasionally it seems to be applied to the hip or waist Vin.iii.39; Vin.iv.260 (pudendum muliebre); Mil.293; Ja.v.437 (= kaccha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->).</li></ol><p>upa + kacchā + ka, cp. Sk. upakakṣa in diff. meaning</p>

upakannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective by the ear, being at or on the ear of somebody, only in loc. as adv. <em><a href="bword://upaka%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87ake">upakaṇṇake</a></em> secretly Vin.i.237; Vin.ii.99; Vin.iv.20, Vin.iv.271; SN.i.86; AN.iii.57; Snp-a.186; and in cpd. <em>-jappin</em> one who whispers into the ear (of another), spreader of reports AN.iii.136. Cp. kaṇṇajappaka &amp; kaṇṇajappana.</p><p>upa + kaṇṇa + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇake">upakaṇṇake</a></p>

upakappana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>profit Pv-a.29 (dān˚), Pv-a.49 (an˚).</p><p>fr. upakappati</p>

upakappanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective profitable Ja.i.398; Dhp-a.ii.133.</p><p>fr. upakappana</p>

upakappati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is fit for, is ready at hand; serves, is a benefit (to, gen./dat.).</p>

upakappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>intrs.) to be beneficial to (w. dat.), to serve, to accrue SN.i.85; Pv.i.4#4 (= nippajjati Pv-a.19); Pv.i.5#7 (petānaṃ); Pv.i.10#4 (= viniyujjati Pv-a.49) Ja.v.350; Pv-a.8, Pv-a.29 (petānaṃ), Pv-a.27 (id.), Pv-a.241; Sdhp.501, Sdhp.504.</p><p>upa + kappati</p>

upakappeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>prepares, makes ready; brings near, fetches</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakappati">upakappati</a></p>

upakaraṇa|upakara.na|upakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakara.na</span></p><p>useful object; means, instrument, implement; requisite.</p>

upakaraṇa|upakara.na|upakarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakara.na</span></p><p>help, service, support; means of existence, livelihood DN.ii.340; AN.ii.86; Ja.i.7; Pv-a.60 (commodities), Pv-a.133 (˚manussa, adj. suitable, fit) Sdhp.69. In general any instrument or means of achieving a purpose, viz. apparatus of a ship Ja.iv.165; <em>tunnavaya˚</em> a weaver’s outfit Ja.ii.364; <em>dabb˚</em>; fit to be used as wood Vism.120; <em>dān˚</em>; materials for a gift Pv-a.105 (so read &amp; cp. upakkhaṭa); <em>nahān˚</em>; bathing requisites Vv-a.248 vitt˚ luxuries AN.v.264 sq., AN.v.283, AN.v.290 sq.; Pv-a.71.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>kṛ</em>.</p>

upakaroti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to do a service, serve, help, support Thig.89 (aor. upakāsiṃ = anugaṇhiṃ santappesiṃ Thag-a.88)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upakkha%E1%B9%ADa">upakkhaṭa</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + karoti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkhaṭa">upakkhaṭa</a></p>

upakaḍḍhati|upaka.d.dhati|upakaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>draws towards; drags away to, drags near.</p>

upakaḍḍhati|upaka.d.dhati|upakaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to drag or pull on to (w. dat.), or down to DN.i.180 (+ apakaḍḍhati); DN.iii.127 (id.); MN.i.365; SN.i.49; SN.ii.99; Dhp.311 (nirayāya = niraye nibbattapeti Dhp-a.iii.484).</p><p>upa + kaḍḍhati, cp. upakaṭṭha</p>

upakaṇḍakin|upaka.n.dakin|upakandakin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.dakin</span></p><p>(Pv.ii.1#13) see under <em>uppaṇḍukin</em>.</p>

upakaṇṇa|upaka.n.na|upakanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.na</span></p><p>˚- lit. (spot) near the ear, only in oblique cases or in der. ˚ka (q.v.) Thag.200 (upakannamhi close to the ear, under the ear).</p><p>upa + kaṇṇa</p>

upakaṇṇakajappi|upaka.n.nakajappi|upakannakajappi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.nakajappi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakajappin">upakaṇṇakajappin</a></p><p>whisper in the ear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakaṃ">upakaṇṇakaṃ</a></p>

upakaṇṇakajappin|upaka.n.nakajappin|upakannakajappin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.nakajappin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakajappin">upakaṇṇakajappin</a></p><p>whisper in the ear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakaṃ">upakaṇṇakaṃ</a></p>

upakaṇṇakamhi|upaka.n.nakamhi|upakannakamhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.nakamhi</span></p><p>close to the ear; in the ear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇake">upakaṇṇake</a></p>

upakaṇṇakaṃ|upaka.n.naka.m|upakannakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.naka.m</span></p><p>close to the ear; in the ear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakamhi">upakaṇṇakamhi</a>, <a href="bword://upakaṇṇake">upakaṇṇake</a></p>

upakaṇṇake|upaka.n.nake|upakannake
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.nake</span></p><p>close to the ear; in the ear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṇṇakamhi">upakaṇṇakamhi</a></p>

upakaṇṭha|upaka.n.tha|upakantha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.n.tha</span></p><p>at Dāvs v.41 is to be corrected to <em><a href="bword://upaka%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">upakaṭṭha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṭṭha">upakaṭṭha</a></p>

upakaṭṭha|upaka.t.tha|upakattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.t.tha</span></p><p>drawn near, near; drawing near (esp. of time)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaḍḍhati">upakaḍḍhati</a></p>

upakaṭṭha|upaka.t.tha|upakattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaka.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective approaehing, near Ja.iv.213 (yāva upakaṭṭha-majjhantikā till nearly noon). Usually in foll. two phrases: <em>upakaṭṭhe kāle</em> when the time was near, i.e. at the approach of meal time Vin.iv.175; Vv-a.6, Vv-a.294; and <em>upakaṭṭhāya vassūpanāyikāya</em> as Lent was approaching Vin.i.253; Pv-a.42; Vv-a.44. Cp. vūpakaṭṭha<br />■ loc. <em>upakaṭṭhe</em> as adv. or prep. “near, in the neighbourhood of” Cnd.639 (= santike); Dāvs v.41 (so read for upakaṇṭhe).</p><p>pp. of upa + <em>karś</em> to draw up or near to</p>

upakhandha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>lit. upper (side of the) trunk, back, shoulder Ja.iv.210 (= khandha C.).</p><p>upa + khandha</p>

upakiliṭṭha|upakili.t.tha|upakilittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakili.t.tha</span></p><p>stained, soiled; defiled; spoiled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkiliṭṭha">upakkiliṭṭha</a></p>

upakirituṃ|upakiritu.m|upakiritum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakiritu.m</span></p><p>to bestrew; to scatter (upon).</p>

upakiriyā|upakiriyaa|upakiriya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakiriyaa</span></p><p>implement, ornament Ja.v.408.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>kr</em></p>

upakiṇṇa|upaki.n.na|upakinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaki.n.na</span></p><p>strewn over with (-˚), covered Vv.35#1 (rucak˚, so read for rājak˚; explained by okiṇṇa Vv-a.160).</p><p>pp. of upakirati</p>

upakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>(as if) boiled or inflamed; (red and) swollen</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppakka">uppakka</a></p>

upakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://uppakka">uppakka</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppakka">uppakka</a></p>

upakkama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>approach, beginning; setting about, undertaking, enterprise; act, action; act of violence, assault; means, expedient; treatment.</p>

upakkama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li><p>lit.</p><ol><li>going to, nearing, approach (-˚) Vv-a.72</li><li>attack Vin.ii.195; Mil.157; DN-a.i.69, DN-a.i.71</li></ol></li><li><p>applied</p><ol><li>in general: doing acting, undertaking, act SN.i.152 = Snp.p.126</li><li>in special: ways, means, i.e. either good of helpful means expedient, remedy Snp.575; Mil.151, Mil.152; or bad or unfair means, treachery, plotting Thag.143; Ja.iv.115 (punishment); Mil.135, Mil.176.</li></ol></li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>kram</em></p>

upakkamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>going near to, attacking Ja.iv.12.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>kram</em></p>

upakkamat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">par. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upakkamat">upakkamat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkamati">upakkamati</a></p>

upakkamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes near, approaches (for a purpose); sets about, begins; applies oneself, exerts oneself (esp. sexually), masturbates; sets upon, assaults.</p>

upakkamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go on to, i e.</p><ol><li>to attack MN.i.86 = Ud.71</li><li>to undertake Vin.iii.110, Vin.iii.111</li><li>to begin Vin.iv.316; DN-a.i.318.</li></ol><p>upa + kamati of <em>kram</em></p>

upakkami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkamati">upakkamati</a></p>

upakkamitvā|upakkamitvaa|upakkamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkamitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkamati">upakkamati</a></p>

upakkamiṃsu|upakkami.msu|upakkamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkamati">upakkamati</a></p>

upakkanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>attacked by (-˚) Mil.112.</li><li>attacking, intriguing or plotting against (loc. DN-a.i.140.</li></ol><p>pp. of upakkamati</p>

upakkhalana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>stumbling, tripping Vism.500.</p><p>fr. prec.</p>

upakkhalati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>trips; stumbles.</p>

upakkhalati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to stumble, trip DN.ii.250; MN.ii.209; AN.iii.101; Ja.iii.433.</p><p>upa + khalati</p>

upakkhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Upakkhaṭa &amp; ˚ta</p><p>done as a favour or service, given, prepared, administered DN.i.127 (= sajjita DN-a.i.294); Pv.ii.8#4 (= sajjita Pv-a.107); Ja.vi.139; Mil.156.</p><p>pp. of upakaroti</p>

upakkhaṭa|upakkha.ta|upakkhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkha.ta</span></p><p>prepared; arranged.</p>

upakkhaṭa|upakkha.ta|upakkhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkha.ta</span></p><p>Upakkhaṭa &amp; ˚ta</p><p>done as a favour or service, given, prepared, administered DN.i.127 (= sajjita DN-a.i.294); Pv.ii.8#4 (= sajjita Pv-a.107); Ja.vi.139; Mil.156.</p><p>pp. of upakaroti</p>

upakkhittaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Pts.ii.196 see upakk˚.</p>

upakkilesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>stain, soiling; blot, blemish, defilement; impurity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaklesa">upaklesa</a></p>

upakkilesa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>anything that spoils or obstructs, a minor stain, impurity, defilement, depravity, Vin.ii.295 (cp. Snp-a.487 &amp; Vv-a.134 &amp; see <em><a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></em>); MN.i.36, MN.i.91; DN.iii.42 sq., DN.iii.49 sq., DN.iii.201; SN.v.92 sq. (pañca cittassa upakkilesā), SN.v.108, SN.v.115; AN.i.10 (āgantuka), AN.i.207 (cittassa), AN.i.253 (oḷārika etc.); AN.ii.53 (candima-suriyānaṃ samaṇa-brāhmaṇānaṃ), AN.ii.67; AN.iii.16 (jātarūpassa, cittassa), AN.iii.386 sq.; AN.iv.177 (vigatā); AN.v.195; Pts.i.164 (eighteen); Pp.60; Dhs.1059 Dhs.1136; Ne.86 sq., Ne.94, Ne.114 sq.; Sdhp.216, Sdhp.225 (as upaklesa). Ten stains at Vism.633.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>kliś</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://abbha">abbha</a></p>

upakkiliṭṭha|upakkili.t.tha|upakkilittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkili.t.tha</span></p><p>stained, soiled; defiled; spoiled</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakiliṭṭha">upakiliṭṭha</a></p>

upakkiliṭṭha|upakkili.t.tha|upakkilittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkili.t.tha</span></p><p>soiled, stained, depraved, impure SN.i.179; AN.i.207 (citta) Vism.13.</p><p>pp. of upa + <em>klid</em> or <em>kliś</em>, cp. kilesa &amp; next</p>

upakkitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>one who is bought; a purchased (slave)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkītaka">upakkītaka</a>, <a href="bword://upakītaka">upakītaka</a></p>

upakkitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>buyer, hawker, dealer comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with bhataka Dhp-a.i.119 = Ud.23 (C. explains by “yo kahāpaṇâdīhi kiñci kināti so upakkitako ti vuccati”) Pts.ii.196 (? T. upakkhittaka).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>krī</em> to buy</p>

upakkosa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>censure, reproach Ja.vi.489.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>kruś</em></p>

upakkosati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>blames; criticizes; reproaches; complains.</p>

upakkosati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to scold, reprove, blame DN.i.161; Ja.iii.436, Ja.iii.523; Ja.iv.81, Ja.iv.317, Ja.iv.409. Upakkhata &amp; ta;</p><p>upa + kosati</p>

upakkuṭṭha|upakku.t.tha|upakkuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakku.t.tha</span></p><p>blamed, reproached, censured, faulty DN.i.113 (an˚); Snp.p.115 (id.); Ja.iii.523; DN-a.i.211.</p><p>pp. of upakkosati</p>

upakkītaka|upakkiitaka|upakkitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakkiitaka</span></p><p>one who is bought; a purchased (slave)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkitaka">upakkitaka</a>, <a href="bword://upakītaka">upakītaka</a></p>

upaklesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>stain, soiling; blot, blemish, defilement; impurity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkilesa">upakkilesa</a></p>

upakāla|upakaala|upakala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaala</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha mentioned in a list of Pacceka Buddhas. MN.iii.70</p>

upakāra|upakaara|upakara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaara</span></p><p>who is a help; helping; useful; beneficial.</p>

upakāra|upakaara|upakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaara</span></p><p>service, help, benefit, obligation, favour DN.iii.187 sq.; Vv-a.68; Pv-a.8 Pv-a.18 (˚āya hoti is good for); Sdhp.283, Sdhp.447, Sdhp.530. <strong>bahūpakāra</strong> (adj.) of great help, very serviceable or helpful SN.iv.295; Pv-a.114. upakāraṃ karoti to do a favour, to oblige Pv-a.42, Pv-a.88, Pv-a.159 (kata); katûpakāra one to whom a service has been rendered Pv-a.116.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81vaha">āvaha</a></em> useful, serviceable, doing good Pv-a.86.</li></ul><p>fr. upa + <em>kṛ</em>; cp. upakaraṇa</p><p>See also: bahūpakāra, <a href="bword://āvaha">āvaha</a></p>

upakāraka|upakaaraka|upakaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaaraka</span></p><p>adjective serviceable, helping, effective Ja.v.99; Vism.534<br />■ f. <strong>upakārikā</strong></p><ol><li>benefactress helper Ja.iii.437.</li><li>fortification (strengthening of the defence) on a city wall DN.i.105, see DN-a.i.274 &amp; cp parikkhāra; MN.i.86 (= Cnd.199#6).</li><li>(philosophy) cause (that which is an aid in the persistence or happening of any given thing) <em>Tikapaṭṭhāna</em> i.11</li></ol><p>fr. upakāra</p><p>See also: upakārikā</p>

upakārin|upakaarin|upakarin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaarin</span></p><p>adjective noun a benefactor Ja.iii.11; DN-a.i.187; Sdhp.540 Sdhp.546.</p><p>fr. upakāra; cp. ASk. upakārin Jtm.31#42</p>

upakārī|upakaarii|upakari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaarii</span></p><p>subsidiary fortification added to a rampart; a low bastion.</p>

upakāsiṃ|upakaasi.m|upakasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakaasi.m</span></p><p>assisted, helped; served; fostered, took care of.</p>

upakītaka|upakiitaka|upakitaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakiitaka</span></p><p>one who is bought; a purchased (slave)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkītaka">upakkītaka</a>, <a href="bword://upakkītaka">upakkītaka</a></p>

upakūjati|upakuujati|upakujati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuujati</span></p><p>to sing to (of birds) Ja.iv.296 (kūjantaṃ u. = replies w. song to the singing)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upak%C5%ABjita">upakūjita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + kūjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakūjita">upakūjita</a></p>

upakūjita|upakuujita|upakujita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuujita</span></p><p>(-˚) resounding, filled with the hum or song of (birds) Ja.iv.359; Pv-a.154.</p><p>pp. of upakūjati</p>

upakūla|upakuula|upakula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuula</span></p><p>embankment, a river’s bank, riverside Ja.vi.26 (rukkh’ûpakūlaje the trees sprung up at its bank).</p><p>upa + kūla</p>

upakūlita|upakuulita|upakulita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuulita</span></p><p>burnt, scorched; shriveled</p><p>See also: upakuḷita</p>

upakūlita|upakuulita|upakulita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuulita</span></p><p>used of the nose in old age Thig.258 (jarāya <em>paṭisedhikā viya</em> says the commentary Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 74 trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> obstructed; Mrs Rh. D. in <em>“Sisters”</em> takes it for upakūḷita and trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->s.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> seared and shrivelled. So also Ed. Müller</p><blockquote><p>J.R A S.</p></blockquote><ol start="1919"><li>538 This is probably right; but Oldenberg, Pischel and Hardy all read <em><a href="bword://upak%C5%ABlita">upakūlita</a></em>.</li></ol><p>derivation uncertain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakūlita">upakūlita</a></p>

upakūsita|upakuusita|upakusita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuusita</span></p><p>at Ja.ii.134 is perhaps faulty for ˚kūḷita, which is suggested by C. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> “kukkule jhāmo” and also by variant reading ˚kuṭhita (for kuṭṭhita boiled, sweltering, hot). The variant (gloss) <em>-kūjita</em> may have the same origin, viz ˚kūḷita, was however interpreted (variant reading BB.) by ˚kupita (meaning “shaken, disturbed by fire”).</p>

upakūḷita|upakuu.lita|upakulita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuu.lita</span></p><p>burnt, scorched; shriveled</p><p>See also: upakulita</p>

upakūḷita|upakuu.lita|upakulita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upakuu.lita</span></p><p>singed, boiled, roasted Ja.i.405 (“half-roasted” = aḍḍhajjhāmaka C.) See also <em><a href="bword://upak%C5%ABsita">upakūsita</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of <em>kūḍ</em>, a variant of <em>kuth</em>, kvathati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakūsita">upakūsita</a></p>

upala
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>stone Dāvs iii.87.</p><p>Lit. Sk. upala, etym. uncertain</p>

upalabbhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is found, exists; is perceived; is ascertained</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalabbhiyati">upalabbhiyati</a></p>

upalabbhiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upalabbhiyati">upalabbhiyati</a></p><p>is found, exists; is perceived; is ascertained; (upalabbhati)</p>

upalabhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to receive, get, obtain to find, make out Mil.124 (kāraṇaṃ); usually in Pass. <em><a href="bword://upalabbhati">upalabbhati</a></em> to be found or got, to be known; to exist MN.i.138 (an˚); SN.i.135; SN.iv.384; Snp.858; Pv.ii.11#1 (= paccanubhavīyati Pv-a.146); Kv.1, 2; Mil.25; Pv-a.87.</p><p>upa + <em>labh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalabbhati">upalabbhati</a></p>

upalabhiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upalabbhiyati">upalabbhiyati</a></p><p>is found, exists; is perceived; is ascertained; (upalabbhati)</p>

upaladdha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>acquired, got, found Ja.vi.211 (˚bāla; variant reading paluddha˚); Sdhp.4, Sdhp.386.</p><p>pp. of upalabhati</p>

upaladdhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>acquisition; knowledge Mil.268; Vv-a.279.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>labh</em></p>

upalakkhaṇā|upalakkha.naa|upalakkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalakkha.naa</span></p><p><em>-aṃ</em> (nt.) discrimination SN.iii.261 (an˚); Dhs.16, Dhs.20, Dhs.292, Dhs.1057; Pp.25; Vv-a.240.</p><p>upa + lakkhaṇa</p>

upalakkheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to distinguish, discriminate Vism.172.</p><p>upa + <em>lakṣay</em></p>

upalepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>defilement Ja.iv.435.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>lip</em></p>

upalikkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr.</i></p><ol start="3"><li>sg. scratches, cuts, wounds.</li></ol>

upalikkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to scratch, scrape, wound AN.iii.94 sq. (= vijjhati C.).</p><p>upa + <em>likh</em></p>

upalimpati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>upalimpati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. anoints; smears; defiles.</p><p>upalimpati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pass. pr. 3 sg.</p><ol><li>is smeared, is defiled (by, instr.).</li><li>is attached to, sticks, adheres (to, loc.)</li></ol><p>See also: upalippat, <a href="bword://upalimpati">upalimpati</a></p>

upalimpati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to smear, defile DN.ii.18; Vin.iii.312; Ja.i.178; Ja.iv.435; Mil.154<br />pass <em><a href="bword://upalippati">upalippati</a></em>, pp <em><a href="bword://upalitta">upalitta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>lip</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalippati">upalippati</a>, <a href="bword://upalitta">upalitta</a></p>

upalippati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>(also <em>upalimpati (?)</em>)</p><ol><li>is smeared, is defiled (by, instr.).</li><li>is attached to, sticks, adheres (to, loc.)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalimpati">upalimpati</a></p>

upalippati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be defiled; to stick to, hang on to Snp.547, Snp.812; Ja.iii.66 (= allīyati C.); Mil.250, Mil.337.</p><p>Pass. of upalimpati</p>

upalitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>attached to; sticking to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalimpati">upalimpati</a></p>

upalitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>smeared with (-˚), stained, tainted Thig.467 (cp. Thag-a.284; T. reads apalitta) Pp.56. Usually neg. <em>an˚</em>; free from taint, undefiled MN.i.319, MN.i.386; Mil.318; metri causa <em><a href="bword://an%C5%ABpalitta">anūpalitta</a></em> SN.i.141 SN.ii.284; Snp.211, Snp.392, Snp.468, Snp.790, Snp.845; Dhp.353 (cp. Dhp-a.iv.7).</p><p>pp. of upalimpati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anūpalitta">anūpalitta</a></p>

upallaviṃ|upallavi.m|upallavim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upallavi.m</span></p><p>I swam, I floated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaplaviṃ">upaplaviṃ</a></p>

upallaviṃ|upallavi.m|upallavim
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upallavi.m</span></p><p>Snp.1145 see <em><a href="bword://upaplavati">upaplavati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaplavati">upaplavati</a></p>

upalohitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective reddish Ja.iii.21 (= rattavaṇṇa C.).</p><p>upa + lohita + ka, see <em><a href="bword://upa">upa</a></em> 5</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

upalāpana|upalaapana|upalapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapana</span></p><p>persuading (to friendship), winning over (with gifts).</p>

upalāpana|upalaapana|upalapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapana</span></p><p>talking over or down, persuasion; diplomacy, humbug DN.ii.76; Mil.115, Mil.117.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>lap</em></p>

upalāpenta|upalaapenta|upalapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalāpeti">upalāpeti</a></p>

upalāpesuṃ|upalaapesu.m|upalapesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalāpeti">upalāpeti</a></p>

upalāpetabba|upalaapetabba|upalapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalāpeti">upalāpeti</a></p>

upalāpeti|upalaapeti|upalapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapeti</span></p><p>treats with kindness, makes friendly overtures; wins over; persuades to friendship.</p>

upalāpeti|upalaapeti|upalapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapeti</span></p><p>to persuade, coax, prevail upon, talk over, cajole Vin.i.119; Vin.iii.21; Ja.ii.266; Ja.iii.265 Ja.iv.215; Pv-a.36, Pv-a.46, Pv-a.276.</p><p>Caus. of upa + <em>lap</em></p>

upalāpetvā|upalaapetvaa|upalapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalāpeti">upalāpeti</a></p>

upalāḷeti|upalaa.leti|upalaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaa.leti</span></p><ol><li>to caress, coax, fondle, win over Ja.ii.267; Vism.300; Sdhp.375.</li><li>to boast of, exult in Ja.ii.151</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://upal%C4%81%E1%B8%B7ita">upalāḷita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Caus. of upa + <em>lal</em>; cp. BSk. upalāḍayati Divy.114, Divy.503</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upalāḷita">upalāḷita</a></p>

upalāḷita|upalaa.lita|upalalita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upalaa.lita</span></p><p>caressed, coaxed Sdhp.301.</p><p>pp. of upalāḷeti</p>

upama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “coming quite or nearly up to” i.e. like, similar, equal DN.i.239 (andha-veṇ˚); MN.i.432 (taruṇ˚ a young looking fellow); AN.iv.11 udak˚ puggala a man like water); Pv.i.1#1 (khett˚ like a well cultivated field; = sadisa Pv-a.7); Pv-a.2, Pv-a.8 etc<br />■ <em>Note.</em> ūpama metri causa see ū˚ and cp. opamma &amp; upamā.</p><p>compar<br />■ superl. formation fr. upa, cp. Lat. summus fr. *(s)ub-mo</p>

upamañña|upama~n~na|upamanna
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upama~n~na</span></p><p>The family to which Pokkharasāti belonged. He was, therefore, called Opamañña. MN.ii.200</p>

upameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to measure one thing by another, to compare Ja.vi.252; Vism.314 (˚metvā, read ˚netvā?).</p><p>upa + <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

upameyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective to be compared, that which is to be likened or compared, the 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part of a comparison Vv-a.13.</p><p>grd. of upa + <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

upamā|upamaa|upama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upamaa</span></p><p>comparison, similarity, likeness; object of comparison; simile; exemplification; parable.</p>

upamā|upamaa|upama
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upamaa</span></p><p>likeness, simile, parable, example (cp. formula introducing u. SN.ii.114; MN.i.148); Snp.705 (cp. Dhp.129, Dhp.130), Snp.1137 (= upanidhā sadisaṃ paṭibhāgo Cnd.158); Iti.114; Vism.341 Vism.478, Vism.512, Vism.582 sq., Vism.591 sq.; Pv-a.29, Pv-a.112 (dhen˚); Snp-a.329, Snp-a.384; Sdhp.29, Sdhp.44, Sdhp.259.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://vacana">vacana</a></em> expression of comparison (usually applied to part. evaṃ) Snp-a.13, Snp-a.472; Kp-a.185, Kp-a.195, Kp-a.208, Kp-a.212; Pv-a.25.</li></ul><p>f. of upama in abstract meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vacana">vacana</a></p>

upamāna|upamaana|upamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upamaana</span></p><p>comparison, the 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part of the comparison Ja.v.341; Vv-a.13.</p><p>fr. upa + <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

upamānita|upamaanita|upamanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upamaanita</span></p><p>measured out, likened, like, comparable Thig.382 (= sadisa Thag-a.255).</p><p>pp. of caus. upa + <em><a href="bword://m%C4%81">mā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://mā">mā</a></p>

upanaccantī|upanaccantii|upanaccanti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaccantii</span></p><p>dancing; dancing before.</p>

upanaccati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to perform a dance DN.ii.268.</p><p>upa + naccati</p>

upanadati
