upanijjhāya|upanijjhaaya|upanijjhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaaya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyat">upanijjhāyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyati">upanijjhāyati</a></p>

upanijjhāyana|upanijjhaayana|upanijjhayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayana</span></p><p>meditation, reflection Mil.127; Vism.418.</p><p>for ˚nijjhāna</p>

upanijjhāyat|upanijjhaayat|upanijjhayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyat">upanijjhāyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyati">upanijjhāyati</a></p>

upanijjhāyati|upanijjhaayati|upanijjhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayati</span></p><p>observes closely; looks at or thinks of lustfully; thinks of, reflects on.</p>

upanijjhāyati|upanijjhaayati|upanijjhayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayati</span></p><p>to meditate upon, consider, look at, reflect on Vin.i.193 (“covet”); Vin.ii.269; Vin.iii.118; DN.i.20; AN.iv.55; Mil.124; Vism.418<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upanijjh%C4%81yita">upanijjhāyita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + nijjhāyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyita">upanijjhāyita</a></p>

upanijjhāyi|upanijjhaayi|upanijjhayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyati">upanijjhāyati</a></p>

upanijjhāyita|upanijjhaayita|upanijjhayita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayita</span></p><p>observed, thought of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyati">upanijjhāyati</a></p>

upanijjhāyita|upanijjhaayita|upanijjhayita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayita</span></p><p>considered, looked at, thought over or about Snp.p.147 (= diṭṭha, ālokita Snp-a.508).</p><p>pp. of ˚nijjhāyati</p>

upanijjhāyitabba|upanijjhaayitabba|upanijjhayitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanijjhaayitabba</span></p><p>observed, thought of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanijjhāyati">upanijjhāyati</a></p>

upanikkhamanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhamati">upanikkhamati</a></p>

upanikkhamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>goes out; comes out.</p>

upanikkhamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go out, to come out (up to somebody) Thig.37; Thig.169; Ja.iii.244; Pv.i.10#1 (aor. ˚nikkhami; imper. ˚nikkhamassu).</p><p>upa + nikkhamati</p>

upanikkhepa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“putting near”, depositing.</p><ol><li>appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to the course of memory, association of ideas Mil.78, Mil.80; cp. ˚nikkhepana SN.ii.276</li><li>deposit, pledge Ja.vi.192, Ja.vi.193 (= upajūta).</li></ol><p>fr. upa + nis + <em>kṣip</em></p>

upanikkhepana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>putting down (near); placing beside (for comparison).</p>

upanikkhipana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>placing near.</p>

upanikkhipana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>putting down (near somebody), putting in the way, trap Vin.iii.77.</p><p>fr. ˚nikkhipati</p>

upanikkhipati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>places, puts down (near, gen.); places beside (for comparison); adds to; deposits.</p>

upanikkhipati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to deposit near, to lay up Vin.i.312; SN.ii.136 sq.; Mil.78, Mil.80; Ne.21, Ne.22; DN-a.i.125<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upanikkhitta">upanikkhitta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + n˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhitta">upanikkhitta</a></p>

upanikkhipi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhamati">upanikkhamati</a></p>

upanikkhipitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhamati">upanikkhamati</a></p>

upanikkhipitvā|upanikkhipitvaa|upanikkhipitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanikkhipitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhamati">upanikkhamati</a></p>

upanikkhitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>positioned, placed; deposited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanikkhamati">upanikkhamati</a></p>

upanikkhitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>laid down (secretly), placed by or on top SN.v.457; Ja.vi.390; Mil.80<br />■ m. a spy Ja.vi.394 (˚purisa).</p><p>upa + n˚</p>

upanikkhittaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>spy Ja.vi.409 (˚manussa), Ja.vi.431 (id.), Ja.vi.450 (id.).</p><p>= prec.</p>

upanipajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lies down beside or near (+acc.).</p>

upanipajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to lie down close to or on top of (acc.) Vism.269; Ja.v.231.</p><p>upa + ni + <em>pad</em></p>

upanipanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>lying down or near</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanipajjati">upanipajjati</a></p>

upanisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>based on, living near.</p>

upanisevana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective going close after, following Ja.v.399 [f. ˚ī.).</p><p>fr. upanisevati</p>

upanisevati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>frequents; keeps close to, clings to.</p>

upanisevati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to pursue, follow, go up after, cling to (acc) MN.i.306<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upanisevita">upanisevita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + n˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanisevita">upanisevita</a></p>

upanisevita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>gone on to, furnished with, sticking or clinging to, full of Ja.v.302 (kakka˚).</p><p>pp. of upanisevati</p>

upanisinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>seated near, seated beside</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanisīdati">upanisīdati</a></p>

upanissaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>basis, reliance, support, foundation, assurance, certainty; esp. sufficing condition or qualification for Arahantship (see long article in Childers s. v.); no 9 in the 24 paccayas, Tikapatthāṇa, Tikapaṭṭhāna i.1, a term only found in the Paṭṭhāna, the Jātaka &amp; later exegetical literature Ja.i.78, Ja.i.508; Ja.iv.96; Ja.vi.70; Ne.80 Vism.19 (˚gocara), Vism.535 (˚paccaya); Dhs-a.315 (id.); Dhp-a.ii.33; Vv-a.98; Pv-a.38 (sotāpatti-phalassa), Pv-a.55 (˚sampatti) Sdhp.265, Sdhp.320.</p><p>upa + ni˚</p>

upanissayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to depend or rely on (acc.) Mil.240 (attānaṃ)<br />ger <em>-nissāya</em> (q.v.)<br />pp ˚nissita (q.v.).</p><p>upa + ni˚</p>

upanissita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>clinging to, dependent (upon).</p>

upanissita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>dependent or relying on Snp.877; Mnd.283, Mil.245.</p><p>upa + ni˚</p>

upanissāya|upanissaaya|upanissaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanissaaya</span></p><p>going near to; depending on; near (+acc. or gen.).</p>

upanissāya|upanissaaya|upanissaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanissaaya</span></p><p>adverb near, close by (with acc.); depending on, by means of (acc) MN.ii.3; SN.ii.269; Snp.867 (taṃ), Snp.901 (tāpa˚), Snp.978, Pv-a.9 (Rājagahaṃ), Pv-a.67 (id.) Vv-a.63 (Rājagaha-seṭṭhiṃ “with”). Cp. BSk. upaniśritya also a ger. formation, in same meaning, e.g. at Divy.54 Divy.207, Divy.505.</p><p>ger. of upanissayati, cp. nissayati in same use &amp; meaning</p>

upanisā|upanisaa|upanisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisaa</span></p><ol><li>sitting near (a teacher) to listen, attention; secret knowledge.</li><li>cause, basis; condition, prerequisite.</li></ol>

upanisā|upanisaa|upanisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisaa</span></p><ol><li>cause, means DN.ii.217, DN.ii.259; MN.iii.71 (samādhiṃ sa-upanisaṃ); SN.ii.30–SN.ii.32 (SN-a. = kāraṇa, paccaya); SN.v.25; AN.i.198; AN.iii.20, AN.iii.200 sq. AN.iii.360; AN.iv.99, AN.iv.336, AN.iv.351; AN.v.4 sq., AN.v.313 sq.; Snp.322 (= upanissaya Snp-a.331); Snp.p.140 (= kāraṇa, payojana Snp-a.503) Dhp.75 (cp. Dhp-a.ii.102 aññā nibbānagāminī paṭipadā).</li><li>likeness, counterfeit [= Sk. upaniṣad = aupamye Pāṇini i.4, 79] Ja.vi.470 (= paṭirūpaka C.).</li></ol><p>if = Vedic upaniṣad, it would be fr. upa + ni + <em>sad</em>, but if, <em>as</em> is more likely, a contracted form of upanissaya, it would be fr. upa + ni + <em>śri</em>. The history of this word has yet to be written, cp. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. &amp; Divy.530 svopaniṣad</p>

upanisīdapetvā|upanisiidapetvaa|upanisidapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisiidapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanisīdati">upanisīdati</a></p>

upanisīdati|upanisiidati|upanisidati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisiidati</span></p><p>sits close to, sits beside (+acc.).</p>

upanisīdati|upanisiidati|upanisidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisiidati</span></p><p>to sit close to or down by DN.i.95; AN.iv.10; Ja.ii.347; Pv.iv.1#63 (ger. ˚sajja = ˚sīditvā Pv-a.242); Vism.269.</p><p>upa + nisīdati of <em>sad</em></p>

upanisīditvā|upanisiiditvaa|upanisiditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanisiiditvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanisīdati">upanisīdati</a></p>

upanivattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes back, returns (to).</p>

upanivattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to return Snp.712; Ja.iv.417; Ja.v.126.</p><p>upa + n˚</p>

upanti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb near, before, in presence of Ja.iv.337.</p><p>upa + anti</p>

upantika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective nt. acc. ˚ṃ near Ja.iv.337; Ja.v.58 (with gen.); Ja.vi.418 (so read for ˚ā); loc. ˚e near or quite near Pv.ii.9#15 (= samīpe gehassa Pv-a.120).</p><p>upa + antika</p>

upanāha|upanaaha|upanaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaaha</span></p><p>nursing a grudge, resentment.</p>

upanāha|upanaaha|upanaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaaha</span></p><p>ill-will, grudge, enmity MN.i.15; AN.i.91, AN.i.95, AN.i.299; AN.iv.148, AN.iv.349, AN.iv.456; AN.v.39, AN.v.41 sq., AN.v.209 AN.v.310; Pp.18 = Vb.357 (pubbakālaṃ kodho aparakālaṃ upanāho Mil.289.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>nah</em>, see <em><a href="bword://upanayhati">upanayhati</a></em>, same in BSk.; e.g. at Mvu.ii.56.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanayhati">upanayhati</a></p>

upanāhi|upanaahi|upanahi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaahi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upanāhin">upanāhin</a></p><p>prone to nurse a grudge, of resentful nature.</p>

upanāhin|upanaahin|upanahin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaahin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upanāhin">upanāhin</a></p><p>prone to nurse a grudge, of resentful nature.</p>

upanāhin|upanaahin|upanahin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaahin</span></p><p>adjective noun one who bears ill-will, grudging, grumbling, finding fault Vin.ii.89; MN.i.95; DN.iii.45; SN.ii.206; SN.iv.241; AN.iii.260, AN.iii.334; AN.v.123, AN.v.156; Snp.116; Thag.502; Ja.iii.260 (kodhana +); Pp.18; Vb.357<br />■ Opp. <em>an˚</em>; not being angry (loc.) DN.iii.47; SN.ii.207; SN.iv.244; AN.v.124 sq.; Ja.iv.463.</p><p>fr. upanāha</p>

upanāmayati|upanaamayati|upanamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamayati</span></p><p>makes bends towards, makes touch; brings near, hands (to); holds out, offers, presents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmeti">upanāmeti</a>, <a href="bword://upanamati">upanamati</a></p>

upanāmayi|upanaamayi|upanamayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamayi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmayati">upanāmayati</a></p>

upanāmayuṃ|upanaamayu.m|upanamayum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamayu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmayati">upanāmayati</a></p>

upanāmesi|upanaamesi|upanamesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmeti">upanāmeti</a></p>

upanāmesuṃ|upanaamesu.m|upanamesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmeti">upanāmeti</a></p>

upanāmetabba|upanaametabba|upanametabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaametabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmeti">upanāmeti</a></p>

upanāmeti|upanaameti|upanameti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaameti</span></p><p>makes bends towards, makes touch; brings near, hands (to); holds out, offers, presents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmayati">upanāmayati</a>, <a href="bword://upanamati">upanamati</a></p>

upanāmeti|upanaameti|upanameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaameti</span></p><ol><li>to bend over to, to place against or close to, to approach, bring near DN.ii.134; SN.i.207; Thag.1055; Snp.p.48 (= attano kāyaṃ Bhagavato upanāmeti); Ja.i.62; Ja.v.215; Snp-a.151.</li><li>to offer, to present Ja.iv.386; Ja.ii.5; Mil.210, Mil.373; Pv-a.274<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upan%C4%81mita">upanāmita</a></em> (q.v.). [cp. BSk. upanāmayati to hand over Divy.13, Divy.14, Divy.22].</li></ol><p>Caus. of upanamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanāmita">upanāmita</a></p>

upanāmita|upanaamita|upanamita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamita</span></p><p>brought near, handed (to); made to touch</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanamati">upanamati</a></p>

upanāmita|upanaamita|upanamita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaamita</span></p><p>brought up to, placed against DN.ii.134.</p><p>pp. of upanāmeti</p>

upanāyika|upanaayika|upanayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upanaayika</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective</p><ol><li>referring to, belonging to in cpd. <em>att˚</em>; ref. to oneself Vin.iii.91; Vism.27.</li><li>beginning, in phrase <em>vass’ūpanāyikā</em> (f.) the approach of the rainy season, period for entering on Lent (cp. BSk. varṣopanāyikā Divy.18, Divy.489 &amp; see also <em><a href="bword://upaka%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">upakaṭṭha</a></em> and vassa) Vin.i.253; AN.i.51 (divided into 2 parts first &amp; second, or purimikā &amp; pacchimikā); Ja.iii.332; DN-a.i.8; Dhp-a.i.203; Dhp-a.iii.438; Vv-a.44; Pv-a.42.</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>nī</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakaṭṭha">upakaṭṭha</a></p>

upanīhātuṃ|upaniihaatu.m|upanihatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniihaatu.m</span></p><p>to take out, to produce.</p>

upanīla|upaniila|upanila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniila</span></p><p>adjective somewhat dark-blue Ja.v.168.</p><p>upa + nīla</p>

upanīta|upaniita|upanita
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniita</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, mentioned in the Isigili Sutta. MN.iii.70</p>

upanīta|upaniita|upanita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniita</span></p><p>led, drawn towards; brought to, offered; used; used up; initiated; alluded to, cited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaneti">upaneti</a></p>

upanīta|upaniita|upanita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniita</span></p><ol><li>brought up to or into (mostly -˚) Thig.498; Snp.677 (niraye), Snp.774 (dukkha˚), Snp.898 (bhava˚); Ja.iii.45 (thūṇa˚); Ja.iv.271 (dukkh˚); Mnd.38; Dhp.237 (˚yaya = atikkantavayo Dhp-a.iii.337, advanced in age); Pv.iv.1#10 (dukkha˚ made to suffer). <em>an˚</em>; Snp.846.</li><li>offered, presented Ja.i.88; Pv-a.274, Pv-a.286.</li><li>brought to conclusion, brought to an end (of life) Ja.v.375 (= maraṇa-santikaṃ u. C.).</li><li>bringing up (for trial), charging MN.i.251 (vacanapatha, cp. upanīya). Upaniya (iyya, eyya)</li></ol><p>pp. of upaneti</p>

upanītavaya|upaniitavaya|upanitavaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniitavaya</span></p><p>with one; s youth gone, advanced in age</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanīta">upanīta</a></p>

upanīya|upaniiya|upaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaneti">upaneti</a></p>

upanīya|upaniiya|upaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiya</span></p><p>Upanīya (˚īyya, ˚eyya)</p><p>“bringing up” (for trial), charging, accusing DN.i.107 (vadati, cp. DN-a.i.276); AN.i.172 (˚vācā); cp. upanīta 3.</p><p>ger. of upaneti</p>

upanīyamāna|upaniiyamaana|upaniyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaneti">upaneti</a></p>

upanīyati|upaniiyati|upaniyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaneti">upaneti</a></p>

upanīyya|upaniiyya|upaniyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiyya</span></p><p>Upanīya (˚īyya, ˚eyya)</p><p>“bringing up” (for trial), charging, accusing DN.i.107 (vadati, cp. DN-a.i.276); AN.i.172 (˚vācā); cp. upanīta 3.</p><p>ger. of upaneti</p>

upanīyyati|upaniiyyati|upaniyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaniiyyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaneti">upaneti</a></p>

upapacciyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://uppaccati">uppaccati</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppaccati">uppaccati</a></p>

upapajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapajjat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upapajjat">upapajjat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapajjatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><ol><li>enters upon, enters (a state); appears; comes into existence (in); is reborn (in).</li><li>is fit for, is suitable.</li></ol>

upapajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to get to, be reborn in (acc.) to originate, rise Vin.iii.20 (nirayaṃ); AN.iii.415; AN.v.292 sq.; Snp.584; Iti.13 (nirayaṃ), Iti.14 (sugatiṃ; variant reading upp˚), Iti.67 (saggaṃ lokaṃ; variant reading upp˚); Iti.43 = Dhp.307 (nirayaṃ); Dhp.126, Dhp.140; Pv.i.10#7 (variant reading BB. udapajjatha = uppajja Pv-a.50); Pp.16, Pp.51, Pp.60; Ne.37, Ne.99, cp. Kv.611 sq. pp. <em>upapannā</em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://upap%C4%81deti">upapādeti</a></em> &amp; pp.; <em><a href="bword://upap%C4%81dita">upapādita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>doubtful whether a legitimate form as upa + <em>pad</em> or a diaeretic form of uppajjati = ud + <em>pad</em>. In this case all passages ought to go under the latter. Trenckner however (<em>Notes</em> 77) defends upa˚ &amp; considers in many cases upp˚ a substitution for upa. The diaeresis may be due to metre, as nearly all forms are found in poetry. The variant reading upp˚ is apparently frequent; but it is almost impossible to distinguish between upap˚ and upp˚ in the Sinhalese writing, and either the scribe or the reader may mistake one for the other</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapādeti">upapādeti</a>, <a href="bword://upapādita">upapādita</a></p>

upapajjaṃ|upapajja.m|upapajjam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapajja.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapajjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upapajjitar">upapajjitar</a></p><p>(from upapajjati) one who comes into existence (in), is reborn (in).</p>

upapajjitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upapajjitar">upapajjitar</a></p><p>(from upapajjati) one who comes into existence (in), is reborn (in).</p>

upapajjiṃsu|upapajji.msu|upapajjimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapajji.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>entered; come into existence, reborn (in).</li><li>possessed of, furnished with; fit, suitable</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>(-˚) possessed of, having attained, being furnished with Snp.68 (thāma-bala), Snp.212, Snp.322, Snp.1077 (ñāṇa˚, cp. Cnd.266#b and uppanna-ñāṇa).</li><li>reborn, come to existence in (with acc.) SN.i.35 (Avihaṃ, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. not quite to the point as “nipphattivasena upagata”, i.e. gone to A, on account of their perfection. Should we read uppanna?) AN.v.68.</li></ol><p>pp. of upapajjati</p>

upaparikkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaparikkhat">upaparikkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaparikkhati">upaparikkhati</a></p>

upaparikkhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaparikkhat">upaparikkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaparikkhati">upaparikkhati</a></p>

upaparikkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">act.pr.</i></p><p>a_ investigates; examines.</p>

upaparikkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to investigate, ascertain, test, examine MN.i.133, MN.i.292, MN.i.443; SN.ii.216; SN.iii.42, SN.iii.140; SN.iv.174; Ja.i.489 Ja.ii.400; Ja.v.235; Mil.91, Mil.293; Dāvs v.27; Sdhp.539; Pv-a.60 (paññāya u. = ñatvā), Pv-a.140 (= viceyya).</p><p>upa + pari + <em>īkṣ</em>; cp. BSk. upaparīkṣate Divy.5, Divy.230</p>

upaparikkhaṇa|upaparikkha.na|upaparikkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaparikkha.na</span></p><p>= upaparikkhā Vv-a.232.</p>

upaparikkhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaparikkhati">upaparikkhati</a></p>

upaparikkhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective investigating, reflecting, testing SN.iii.61; AN.iv.221 sq., AN.iv.296, AN.iv.328. Cp BSk. upaparīkṣaka Divy.212.</p><p>fr. upaparikkhati</p>

upaparikkhitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaparikkhati">upaparikkhati</a></p>

upaparikkhā|upaparikkhaa|upaparikkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaparikkhaa</span></p><p>investigation, examination Vin.iii.314; MN.ii.175 (attha˚); AN.iii.381 sq.; AN.iv.221; AN.v.126; Dhs.16, Dhs.20 Dhs.292; Pp.25; Ne.8, Ne.42; DN-a.i.171.</p><p>fr. upaparikkhati, cp. BSk. upaparīkṣā Divy.3 etc.</p>

upapatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>coming into existence, rebirth.</p>

upapatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>birth, rebirth, (lit. attainment) MN.i.82; SN.iii.53; SN.iv.398; AN.v.289 sq.; Snp.139, Snp.643, Snp.836; Dhp.419 (sattānaṃ); in var specifications as: deva˚ rebirth among gods Pv-a.6, Pv-a.81 devaloka˚ AN.i.115; kāma˚ existence in the sensuous universe DN.iii.218; Iti.94; arūpa˚ in the formless spheres Vb.172, Vb.267, Vb.296; rūpa˚, in the world of form Vb.171 sq., Vb.263 sq.; Vb.299; niraya˚ in Purgatory Pv-a.53.</li><li>occasion, opportunity (lit. “coming to”); object for in dāna˚ objects suitable for gifts AN.iv.239 (where 8 enumerated, see <em><a href="bword://d%C4%81na">dāna</a></em>).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em> a god by birth (or rebirth) Vv-a.18; also given as uppatti-deva, e.g. at Kp-a.123. See detail under <em><a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em>.</li></ul><p>fr. upa + <em>pad</em>, cp. uppatti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dāna">dāna</a>, <a href="bword://deva">deva</a>, <a href="bword://deva">deva</a></p>

upapattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>(-˚) adjective belonging to a birth or rebirth; in peta˚ born as a Peta Pv-a.119<br />■ Cp <em><a href="bword://upap%C4%81tika">upapātika</a></em>.</p><p>fr. upapatti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapātika">upapātika</a></p>

upapattipaṭilābhiya|upapattipa.tilaabhiya|upapattipatilabhiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapattipa.tilaabhiya</span></p><p>leading to the obtaining of rebirth, or a new existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapatti">upapatti</a></p>

upapisana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(&amp; ~ī f.) grinding; something ground, powder</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapiṃsana">upapiṃsana</a></p>

upapisana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>grinding, powder, in <em>añjan˚</em>; powdered ointment (for the eyes) Vin.i.203; Vin.ii.112.</p><p>upa + <em>piṣ</em></p>

upapiṃsana|upapi.msana|upapimsana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapi.msana</span></p><p>(&amp; ~ī f.) grinding; something ground, powder</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapisana">upapisana</a></p>

upaplavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to swim or float to (acc.), in uncertain reading as aor. <em>upaplaviṃ</em> at Snp.1145 (dīpā dīpaṃ upaplaviṃ floated from land to land vv.ll. at Snp-a.606 uppalaviṃ &amp; upallaviṃ; all MSS. of Cnd.p.54 &amp; no. 160 write upallaviṃ). Perhaps we should better read; <em>uppalaviṃ</em> (or upallaviṃ) as diaeretic form for *upplaviṃ, aor. of <em><a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a></em> (or uplavati), q. v Expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Cnd.160 by samupallaviṃ.</p><p>upa + plavati, cp. uppilavati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a></p>

upaplaviṃ|upaplavi.m|upaplavim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaplavi.m</span></p><p>I swam, I floated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upallaviṃ">upallaviṃ</a></p>

upapphusati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to touch; aor. upapphusi Ja.v.417, Ja.v.420.</p><p>upa + phusati, of <em>spṛś</em></p>

upapurohita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>minor or assistant priest Ja.iv.304.</p><p>upa + purohita, see <em><a href="bword://upa">upa</a></em> 5</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

upapādeti|upapaadeti|upapadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaadeti</span></p><p>to execute, perform Ja.v.346.</p><p>Caus. of upapajjati</p>

upapādita|upapaadita|upapadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaadita</span></p><p>accomplished Ja.ii.236.</p><p>pp. of upapādeti, Caus. of upapajjati</p>

upapādiṃ|upapaadi.m|upapadim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaadi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></p>

upapāramī|upapaaramii|upaparami
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaaramii</span></p><p>minor perfection Bv.i.77 (opp. paramattha-pāramī); Dhp-a.i.84.</p><p>upa + pāramī, cp. upa 5</p>

upapāta|upapaata|upapata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaata</span></p><p>= upapatti rebirth Vin.iii.4; SN.iv.59 (cut˚); Pp.50.</p><p>but der. fr. <em>pat</em> (cp. uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = ud + <em>pat</em> but uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = ud + <em>pad</em>) with the meaning of the casual &amp; unusual</p>

upapātika|upapaatika|upapatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapaatika</span></p><p>adjective = opapātika i.e. rebirth without parents, as a deva DA on DN.iii.107 Thag-a.207.</p><p>fr. upapāta but evidently mixed with uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> and uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, cp. upapajjati, upapatti &amp; BSk upapāduka Av. SN.ii.94, SN.ii.95; Divy.523</p>

upapīla|upapiila|upapila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upapiila</span></p><p>at DN.i.135 read uppīḷa (q.v.).</p>

uparacita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>formed Thag-a.211; Sdhp.616.</p><p>pp. of upa + <em>rac</em></p>

uparajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>(from uparāja) the state of being an uparāja, viceroyalty.</p>

uparajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>viceroyalty AN.iii.154 (variant reading opa˚); Ja.i.511; Ja.iv.176; DN-a.i.134.</p><p>upa + rajja, cp. uparaja</p>

uparamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cease, desist, to be quiet Ja.iii.489; Ja.v.391 (variant reading for upāramati, also in C.); Mil.152.</p><p>upa + <em>ram</em></p>

uparamā|uparamaa|uparama
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparamaa</span></p><p>cessation Mil.41, Mil.44 (an˚).</p><p>cp. lit. Sk. uparama, to uparamati</p>

uparata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>ceased, stopped; quiet; dead; having given up, abstaining (from).</p>

uparata
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>having ceased, desisting from (-˚), restraining oneself (cp. orata) Vin.i.245 (ratt-ûparata abstaining from food at night = ratti-bhojanato uparata DN-a.i.77); DN.i.5 (id.); MN.i.319 (bhaya˚); Snp.914 (virata etc. Mnd.337); Mil.96, Mil.307; Dhs-a.403 (vihiṃs˚).</p><p>pp. of uparamati</p>

uparati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>ceasing, resting; cessation MN.i.10; SN.iv.104; Mil.274.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>ram</em></p>

uparava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>noise Ja.ii.2.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>ru</em></p>

upari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><ol><li>(adv.) up, above, upwards; upstream; further, in addition; afterwards, later (in a text).</li><li>(prep, or postp.)over, above; upon, on (+ gen. or loc.; occasionally + abl.; often written as cpd.).</li></ol>

upari
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indeclinable over, above (prep. &amp; prefix)</p><ol><li>(adv. on top, above (opp. adho below) Vin.iv.46 (opp. heṭṭhā) Ja.vi.432; Kp-a.248 (= uddhaṃ; opp. adho); Snp-a.392 (abtimukho u. gacchati explaining paccuggacchati of Snp.442); Pv-a.11 (heṭṭhā manussa-saṇṭhānaṃ upari sūkara-s˚), Pv-a.47 (upari chattaṃ dhāriyamāna), Pv-a.145 (sabbattha upari upon everything).</li><li>(prep. w. gen) with ref. either to <em>space</em> = on top of, on, upon, as in kassa upari sāpo patissati on whom shall the curse fall? Dhp-a.i.41; attano u. patati falls upon himself Pv-a.45; etissā upari kodho anger on her, i.e. against her Vv-a.68; or to <em>time</em> = on top of, after, later, as in catunnaṃ māsānaṃ upari after 4 months Pv-a.52 (= uddhaṃ catūhi māsehi of Pv.i.10#12) sattannaṃ vassa-satānaṃ upari after 700 years Pv-a.144.</li><li>(adv. in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, meaning “upper, higher, on the upper or top side”, or “on top of”, if the phrase is in loc. case See below.</li></ol><ul><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://cara">cara</a></em> walking in the air, suspended, flying Ja.iii.454</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81s%C4%81da">pāsāda</a></em> the upper story of a palace, loc. on the terrace DN.i.112 (loc.); Pv-a.105, Pv-a.279.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://pi%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhi">piṭṭhi</a></em> top side, platform Vin.ii.207 (loc).</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://bhaddaka">bhaddaka</a></em> Name of a tree [either Sk bhadraka Pinus Deodara, or bhadra Nauclea Cadamba after Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v.] Ja.vi.269.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81ga">bhāga</a></em> the upper part used in instr., loc or aor. in sense of “above, over beyond” Ja.iv.232 (instr.).</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> higher state or condition MN.i.45 (opp. adh˚).</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> face upwards DN-a.i.228; Pp-a.214.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://vasana">vasana</a></em> upper garment Pv-a.49.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ta">vāta</a></em> higher than the wind, loc. on the wind Ja.ii.11; or in <em>˚passe</em> (loc.) on the upper (wind-) side Dhp-a.ii.17.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://cara">cara</a></em> walking in the air, suspended, flying Ja.iii.454</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://veh%C4%81sa">vehāsa</a></em> high in the air (˚-), in <em>˚kuṭī</em> a lofty or open air chamber, or a room in the upper story of the Vihāra Vin.iv.46 (what the C. means by expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> majjhimassa purisassa asīsa-ghaṭṭā “not knocking against the head of a middle-(sized) man” is not quite clear).</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://sacca">sacca</a></em> higher truth Pv-a.66 (so read for upari sacca).</p></li></ul><p>Vedic upari, der. fr. upa, Idg. *uper(i); Gr. υπερ, Lat. s-uper; Goth. ufar, Ohg. ubir = Ger. über E. over; Oir. for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cara">cara</a>, <a href="bword://pāsāda">pāsāda</a>, <a href="bword://piṭṭhi">piṭṭhi</a>, <a href="bword://bhaddaka">bhaddaka</a>, <a href="bword://bhāga">bhāga</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a>, <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a>, <a href="bword://vasana">vasana</a>, <a href="bword://vāta">vāta</a>, <a href="bword://cara">cara</a>, <a href="bword://vehāsa">vehāsa</a>, <a href="bword://sacca">sacca</a></p>

uparibhaddaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of tree Ja.vi.269.</p>

uparibhāvaṅgamanīya|uparibhaava"ngamaniiya|uparibhavangamaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparibhaava"ngamaniiya</span></p><p>tending to go up, leading towards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upari">upari</a></p>

uparima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>being above, upper, higher; later, following.</p>

uparima
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective uppermost, above, overhead DN.iii.189 (disā); Ne.88. Cp. upariṭṭhima.</p><p>upari + ma, superl. formation</p>

uparipāsāda|uparipaasaada|uparipasada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparipaasaada</span></p><p>the upper terrace, the flat roof of a mansion</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upari">upari</a></p>

uparito
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i></p><ol><li>(adv.) upwards, above, over, on top; from above.</li><li>(prep, or postp.) over, above (+ gen. or abl.).</li></ol>

uparivehāsakutī|uparivehaasakutii|uparivehasakuti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparivehaasakutii</span></p><p>hut with its top story open to the air (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upari">upari</a></p>

upariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adverb above, on top, in compd. <em>heṭṭh˚</em>; below and above Vism.1.</p><p>fr. upari</p>

upariṭṭha|upari.t.tha|uparittha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upari.t.tha</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha. MN.iii.69</p>

upariṭṭha|upari.t.tha|uparittha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upari.t.tha</span></p><p>adjective highest, topmost, most excellent Thag.910. Cp. next.</p><p>superl. formation fr. upari in analogy to seṭṭha</p>

upariṭṭhima|upari.t.thima|uparitthima
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upari.t.thima</span></p><p>adjective = upariṭṭha uparima Dhs.1016, Dhs.1300, Dhs.1401; Pp.16, Pp.17 (sañyojanāni = uddhaṃbhāgiya-sañyojanāni Pp-a.198).</p><p>double-superl. formation after analogy of seṭṭha, pacchima &amp; heṭṭhima: heṭṭhā</p>

uparocati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to please (intrs.) Ja.vi.64.</p><p>upa + <em>ruc</em></p>

uparodati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to lament Ja.vi.551 (fut ˚rucchati)</li><li>to sing in a whining tone Ja.v.304.</li></ol><p>upa + <em>rud</em></p>

uparodha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>obstacle; breaking up, destruction, end Ja.iii.210, Ja.iii.252; Pv.iv.1#5; Mil.245, Mil.313.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>rudh</em></p>

uparodhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>stopping; obstructing.</p>

uparodhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>breaking up, destruction Snp.732, Snp.761.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>rudh</em></p>

uparodhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>stops, obstructs; besieges; molests, troubles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparuddhati">uparuddhati</a>, <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparodheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>obstructs, injures, destroys</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparodhati">uparodhati</a></p>

uparodheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to cause to break up; to hinder, stop; destroy Vin.iii.73.</p><p>Caus of uparundhati</p>

uparopa
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>A type of plant, sometimes <em>uparopaha</em> Ja.ii.345 Ja.iv.359 Vin.ii.154.</p>

uparopa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>plant.</p>

uparopa
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“little plant”, sapling Vin.ii.154. See also next.</p><p>upa + ropa, cp. upa 5</p>

uparopaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>= uparopa, sapling Ja.ii.345; Ja.iv.359.</p>

uparuddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>stopped; obstructed; besieged</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparuddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>stopped, ceased Mil.151 (˚jīvita).</p><p>pp. of uparujjhati</p>

uparuddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>stops, obstructs; besieges; molests, troubles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparodhati">uparodhati</a>, <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparujjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparujjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be stopped, broken, annihilated, destroyed DN.i.223; Thag.145; Iti.106; Snp.724, Snp.1036, Snp.1110; Cnd.159 (= nirujjhati vūpasammati atthangacchati); Mil.151; Sdhp.280. pp. <em><a href="bword://uparuddha">uparuddha</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. uparudhyate, Pass. of uparundhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparuddha">uparuddha</a></p>

uparundhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>stops, obstructs; besieges; molests, troubles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparodhati">uparodhati</a>, <a href="bword://uparuddhati">uparuddhati</a></p>

uparundhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to break up, hinder, stop, keep in check MN.i.243; Ja.i.358; Thag.143, Thag.1117; Snp.118 Snp.916 (pot. uparundhe, but uparuddhe Mnd.346 = uparuddheyya etc.); Mil.151, Mil.245, Mil.313<br />ger <em>uparundhiya</em> Thag.525; Snp.751; aor. <strong>uparundhi</strong> Ja.iv.133; Pv-a.271<br />pass <em><a href="bword://uparujjhati">uparujjhati</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>rudh</em></p><p>See also: uparundhi, <a href="bword://uparujjhati">uparujjhati</a></p>

uparundhiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparundhiṃ|uparundhi.m|uparundhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparundhi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uparundhati">uparundhati</a></p>

uparājā|uparaajaa|uparaja
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparaajaa</span></p><p>secondary or deputy king, a viceroy Ja.i.504; Ja.ii.316; Dhp-a.i.392.</p><p>upa + rājā; see <em><a href="bword://upa">upa</a></em> 5</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

uparūpari|uparuupari|uparupari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparuupari</span></p><p>more and more; higher and higher; over, on top of; right above</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upari">upari</a></p>

uparūḷha|uparuu.lha|uparulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uparuu.lha</span></p><p>grown again, recovered Ja.iv.408 (cakkhu).</p><p>upa + rūḷha, pp. of <em>ruh</em></p>

upasagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>misfortune; trouble.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upassagga">upassagga</a></p>

upasagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>attack, trouble, danger Vin.i.33; AN.i.101; Thig.353; Dhp.139 (where spelt upassaga, cp. Dhp-a.iii.70); Mil.418.</li><li>(technical term in grammar) prefix, preposition Ja.ii.67 (saṃ), Ja.ii.126 (apa); Ja.iii.121 (ni pa); DN-a.i.245 (adhi); Kp-a.101 (sa˚ and an˚); Pv-a.88 (atthe nipāto a particle put in metri causa, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of handa) Dhs-a.163, Dhs-a.405.</li></ol><p>Sk upasarga, of upa + <em>sṛj</em></p>

upasama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>becoming quite, alleviation, stopping; calmness, peace, tranquility.</p>

upasama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>calm, quiet, appeasement, allaying, assuagement, tranquillizing Vin.i.10; SN.iv.331 = SN.v.421 (in freq. phrase upasamāya abhiññāya sambodhāya nibbānāya saṃvattati; see <em><a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> iii.7); DN.i.50; DN.iii.130 sq., DN.iii.136 sq., DN.iii.229 (as one of the 4 objects of adhiṭṭhāna, viz. paññā˚ sacca˚ cāga˚ upasama˚); MN.i.67; MN.iii.246; SN.i.30, SN.i.34 (sīlena), SN.i.46 citta-v-ûpasama), SN.i.48 SN.i.55; SN.ii.223, SN.ii.277; SN.iii.86 (sankhārānaṃ… v-ūpasamo) DN.ii.157; SN.i.158 (see <em><a href="bword://v%C5%ABpasama">vūpasama</a></em> and sankhāra); (ariyaṃ maggaṃ dukkh˚-gāminaṃ); SN.iv.62, SN.iv.331; SN.v.65 (avūpasama), SN.v.179 SN.v.234 (˚gāmin), SN.v.378 sq.; AN.i.3 (avūpasama), AN.i.30, AN.i.42; AN.ii.14 (vitakk˚); AN.iii.325 sq.; AN.v.216, AN.v.238 sq.; Snp.257, Snp.724, Snp.735 Snp.737; Iti.18 (dukkh˚) Iti.83; Dhp.205; Mnd.351; Ja.i.97; Pts.i.95; Mil.170, Mil.248; Vism.197 (˚ânussati); Sdhp.587 Cp. vi˚ (vū˚).</p><p>Sk. upaśama, upa + <em>śam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a>, <a href="bword://vūpasama">vūpasama</a></p>

upasamaddhiṭṭhāna|upasamaddhi.t.thaana|upasamaddhitthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasamaddhi.t.thaana</span></p><ol><li>(n.) fixing the mind on tranquillity; resolution for calmness.</li><li>(mfn.)fixing the mind on tranquility; resolving for calmness</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasama">upasama</a></p>

upasamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>becomes calm or quite; ceases; is allayed, is extinguished</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasammati">upasammati</a></p>

upasamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to appease, calm, allay, assuage Snp.919; Thag.50 (pot. upasame = upasameyya nibbāpeyya Mnd.352)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upasanta">upasanta</a></em> q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>śam</em> in trs. meaning for usual sammati in intrs. meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasanta">upasanta</a></p>

upasammati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>becomes calm or quite; ceases; is allayed, is extinguished</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasamati">upasamati</a></p>

upasammati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to grow calm, to cease, to be settled or composed, to be appeased SN.i.62, SN.i.221; Dhp.100 sq.</p><p>Sk. upasamyati, upa + <em>śam</em> in intrs. function</p>

upasampadā|upasampadaa|upasampada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampadaa</span></p><ol><li>attainment.</li><li>full ordination, admission into the <em>saṅgha</em>.</li></ol>

upasampadā|upasampadaa|upasampada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampadaa</span></p><ol><li>taking, acquiring; obtaining, taking upon oneself, undertaking DN.ii.49; MN.i.93; AN.iii.65; Dhp.183 (cp. Dhp-a.iii.236) Ne.44 (kusalassa).</li><li>(in special sense) taking up the bhikkhuship, higher ordination, admission to the privileges of recognized bhikkhus [cp. BSk. upasampad ˚padā Divy.21, Divy.281 etc.] Vin.i.12, Vin.i.20, Vin.i.95, Vin.i.146 and passim; Vin.iii.15; Vin.iv.52; DN.i.176, DN.i.177, DN.i.202; SN.i.161; AN.iv.276 sq. &amp; passim; Dhp-a.ii.61 (pabbajjā +); Pv-a.54 (laddh one who has received ordination), Pv-a.179 (id.).</li></ol><p>fr. upa + saṃ + <em>pad</em></p>

upasampajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><ol><li>reaching, attaining; having entered upon.</li><li>being ordained; after ordination</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampajjati">upasampajjati</a></p>

upasampajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>obtains; is ordained, is received into the <em>saṅgha</em>; obtains the <em>upasampadā</em> ordination.</p>

upasampajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to attain, enter on, acquire, take upon oneself usually in ger. <em><a href="bword://upasampajja">upasampajja</a></em> MN.i.89; SN.iii.8; AN.iv.13; AN.v.69; Dhs.160 (see Dhs-a.167) DN-a.i.313; Snp-a.158<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upasampanna">upasampanna</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + sampajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampajja">upasampajja</a>, <a href="bword://upasampanna">upasampanna</a></p>

upasampajjituṃ|upasampajjitu.m|upasampajjitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampajjitu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampajjati">upasampajjati</a></p>

upasampanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>possessed of; ordained; who is a member of the <em>saṅgha</em>.</p>

upasampanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>obtained, got, received; in special sense of having attained the recognition of bhikkhuship ordained [cp. BSk. upasampanna Divy.281] SN.i.161; AN.v.70; Vin.iii.24; Vin.iv.52, Vin.iv.130; Mil.13.</p><p>pp. of upasampajjati</p>

upasamphassati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to embrace Ja.v.297.</p><p>upa + sam + <em>spṛś</em></p>

upasampādayati|upasampaadayati|upasampadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadayati</span></p><p>ordains, receives into the <em>saṅgha</em> (with the <em>upasampadā</em> ordination)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādenta|upasampaadenta|upasampadenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadenta</span></p><p>(~entī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādesi|upasampaadesi|upasampadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādesuṃ|upasampaadesu.m|upasampadesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādeta|upasampaadeta|upasampadeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasampādetar">upasampādetar</a></p><p>(from <em>upasampādeti</em>) one who ordains.</p>

upasampādetabba|upasampaadetabba|upasampadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādetar|upasampaadetar|upasampadetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasampādetar">upasampādetar</a></p><p>(from <em>upasampādeti</em>) one who ordains.</p>

upasampādeti|upasampaadeti|upasampadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadeti</span></p><p>ordains, receives into the <em>saṅgha</em> (with the <em>upasampadā</em> ordination)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādayati">upasampādayati</a></p>

upasampādeti|upasampaadeti|upasampadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to attain to, obtain, produce Dhs-a.167 (= nipphādeti).</li><li>to admit to bhikkhuship, to ordain Vin.iv.130, Vin.iv.226, Vin.iv.317 (= vutṭhāpeti); grd. <em>-etabba</em> Vin.i.64 sq.; Vin.iv.48; AN.v.72.</li></ol><p>Denom. fr. upasampadā</p>

upasampādetvā|upasampaadetvaa|upasampadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasampādita|upasampaadita|upasampadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasampaadita</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) given ordination, ordained (with the <em>upasampadā</em> ordination).</li><li>(n.) ordaining</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasampādeti">upasampādeti</a></p>

upasamā|upasamaa|upasama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasamaa</span></p><p>Upasamā&gt;</p><p>A bhikkhunī whose verse is found in the Therīgāthā. Thig.10</p>

upasamāna|upasamaana|upasamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasamaana</span></p><p>= upasama Thag.421; Sdhp.335 (dukkh˚).</p>

upasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>calmed, pacified, at peace; calm, tranquil; ceased, extinguished</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasammati">upasammati</a></p>

upasanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>calmed, composed, tranquil, at peace MN.i.125; SN.i.83, SN.i.162; AN.iii.394; Snp.848, Snp.919, Snp.1087, Snp.1099; Mnd.210, Mnd.352, Mnd.434; Cnd.161; Dhp.201, Dhp.378; Mil.394; Dhp-a.iii.260; Dhp-a.iv.114; Pv-a.132 (= santa).</p><p>pp. of upa + <em>śam</em>, cp. upasammati</p>

upasantapadissa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>of calm appearance, looking at peace</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasanta">upasanta</a></p>

upasavyāna|upasavyaana|upasavyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasavyaana</span></p><p>? “a robe worn over the left shoulder” (Hardy, Index to ed.) Vv-a.166 (variant reading upavasavya).</p><p>?</p>

upasaṃharanta|upasa.mharanta|upasamharanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mharanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṃharati">upasaṃharati</a></p>

upasaṃharati|upasa.mharati|upasamharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mharati</span></p><p>draws together, collects; puts together with, puts in; brings, brings near to, provides; brings about; brings into contact with; brings to bear on, focuses on; brings together (for comparison), sees as similar.</p>

upasaṃharati|upasa.mharati|upasamharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mharati</span></p><ol><li>to collect, bring together, heap up, gather Mil.132.</li><li>to dispose arrange, concentrate, collect, focus Vin.iv.220 (kāyaṃ) MN.i.436 (cittaṃ), MN.i.468 (cittaṃ tathattāya); SN.v.213 sq. (id.) Dhs-a.309 (cakkhuṃ).</li><li>to take hold of, take care of, provide, serve, look after Mil.232.</li></ol><p>upa + saṃ + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upasaṃharaṇa|upasa.mhara.na|upasamharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mhara.na</span></p><p>drawing together, bringing up to, comparison Vism.232 sq.; Ja.v.186.</p><p>fr. upasaṃharati</p>

upasaṃharitvā|upasa.mharitvaa|upasamharitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mharitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṃharati">upasaṃharati</a></p>

upasaṃhariṃsu|upasa.mhari.msu|upasamharimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mhari.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṃharati">upasaṃharati</a></p>

upasaṃhasi|upasa.mhasi|upasamhasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mhasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṃharati">upasaṃharati</a></p>

upasaṃhita|upasa.mhita|upasamhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mhita</span></p><p>adjective accompanied by, furnished or connected with (-˚) DN.i.152; MN.i.37 MN.i.119 (chand˚); SN.ii.220 (kusal˚); SN.iv.60 (kām˚), SN.iv.79 (id.) Snp.341 (rāg˚), Snp.1132 (giraṃ vaṇṇ˚ = vaṇṇena upetaṃ Cnd) Thag.970; Ja.i.6; Ja.ii.134, Ja.ii.172; Ja.v.361.</p><p>pp. of upa + saṃ + <em>dhā</em></p>

upasaṃhāra|upasa.mhaara|upasamhara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mhaara</span></p><p>taking hold of, taking up, possession, in <em>devat˚</em>; being seized or possessed by a god Mil.298.</p><p>fr. upa + saṃ + <em>hṛ</em>.</p>

upasaṃvasati|upasa.mvasati|upasamvasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.mvasati</span></p><p>to live with somebody, to associate with (acc.) Ja.i.152.</p><p>upa + saṃ + <em>vas</em></p>

upasaṃyāti|upasa.myaati|upasamyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.myaati</span></p><p>comes in a body towards.</p>

upasaṅgayha|upasa"ngayha|upasangayha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"ngayha</span></p><p>taking hold of, clasping.</p>

upasaṅkama|upasa"nkama|upasankama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkama</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamat">upasaṅkamat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamana|upasa"nkamana|upasankamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamana</span></p><p>going to see, visiting; approaching.</p>

upasaṅkamana|upasa"nkamana|upasankamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamana</span></p><p>going near, approach MN.ii.176; SN.v.67 = Iti.107; Pv-a.232.</p><p>fr. upasankamati</p>

upasaṅkamanta|upasa"nkamanta|upasankamanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamat|upasa"nkamat|upasankamat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamat">upasaṅkamat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamati|upasa"nkamati|upasankamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamati</span></p><p>approaches; goes to, goes to see, visits.</p>

upasaṅkamati|upasa"nkamati|upasankamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamati</span></p><ol><li>to go up to (with acc.), to approach come near; freq. in stock phrase “yena (Pokkharasādissa parivesanā) ten’ upasankami, upasankamitvā paññatte āsane nisīdi”, e.g. Vin.i.270; DN.i.109; DN.ii.1, and passim<br />aor ˚sankami Pv.ii.2#10; Snp-a.130, Snp-a.140; Kp-a.116; Pv-a.88; ger. ˚sankamitvā Snp-a.140; Pv-a.6, Pv-a.12 Pv-a.19, Pv-a.20, Pv-a.88; ˚sankamma Snp.166, Snp.418, Snp.460, Snp.980, Snp.986; inf ˚sankamituṃ Pv-a.79</li><li>to attend on (as a physician), to treat Mil.169, Mil.233, Mil.353; DN-a.i.7.</li></ol><p>upa + saṃ + <em>kram</em>, cp. BSk. upasankramati Av. SN.i.209</p>

upasaṅkami|upasa"nkami|upasankami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamita|upasa"nkamita|upasankamita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamitar">upasaṅkamitar</a></p><p>(from <em>upasaṅkamati</em>) one who approaches, goes to; one who goes to see; visits.</p>

upasaṅkamitabba|upasa"nkamitabba|upasankamitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamitar|upasa"nkamitar|upasankamitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamitar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamitar">upasaṅkamitar</a></p><p>(from <em>upasaṅkamati</em>) one who approaches, goes to; one who goes to see; visits.</p>

upasaṅkamituṃ|upasa"nkamitu.m|upasankamitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamitu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamitvā|upasa"nkamitvaa|upasankamitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamiṃsu|upasa"nkami.msu|upasankamimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkami.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkamma|upasa"nkamma|upasankamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkamma</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkanta|upasa"nkanta|upasankanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkanta</span></p><p>who has approached, gone to, visited; having approached, gone to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṅkamati">upasaṅkamati</a></p>

upasaṅkheyya|upasa"nkheyya|upasankheyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkheyya</span></p><p>to be reckoned (as).</p>

upasaṅkheyya|upasa"nkheyya|upasankheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa"nkheyya</span></p><p>adjective to be prepared, produced or contracted Snp.849 (= ˚sankhātabba Snp-a.549; cp. Mnd.213).</p><p>grd of upa + sankharoti</p>

upasaṇṭhapanā|upasa.n.thapanaa|upasanthapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.n.thapanaa</span></p><p>stopping, causing to cease, settling Pp.18 (see also <em>an</em>˚;).</p><p>fr. upa + sanṭḥapeti</p>

upasaṭṭha|upasa.t.tha|upasattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasa.t.tha</span></p><p>troubled, afflicted, visited by troubles; trouble, affliction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upassaṭṭha">upassaṭṭha</a></p>

upasecana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>sprinkling over, i.e. sauce Thag.842; Ja.ii.422; Ja.iii.144; Ja.iv.371 (maṃs˚); Ja.vi.24. See also nandi˚ &amp; maṃsa˚.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>sic</em></p>

upasena
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>UpasenaVaṅgantaputta</p><p>When his ordination was but one year old, he ordained another bhikkhu, to increase the number of holy ones, and went with him to wait upon the Buddha. The Buddha roundly rebuked him for this hasty procedure. Vin.i.59 Later the Buddha declared Upasena to be the best of those who were altogether charming (<em>samantapāsādikānaṃ</em>). AN.i.24 He visited the Buddha when the Buddha had enjoined on himself a period of solitude for a fortnight; the monks had agreed that anyone who went to see the Buddha would be guilty of a <em>pācittiya</em> offence, but the Buddha, desiring to talk to him, asked one of Upasena’s followers if he liked rag-robes. “No, Sir, but I wear them out of regard for my teacher,” was the reply. Vin.iii.230<em>ff.</em></p><p>In the Theragāthā are found several verses ascribed to Upasena as having been spoken by him in answer to a question by his student, regarding what was to be done during the dissensions of the Kosambī monks Thag.577–586 The Udāna states Ud.p.45<em>f.</em> that once when he was taking his siesta he reviewed the happiness he enjoyed and the glories of the life he led under the guidance of the Buddha. The Buddha, noticing this, proclaimed his approval.</p><p>One day, while Upasena was sitting after his meal in the shadow of the Sappasondika-pabbhāra, fanned by the gentle breeze, mending his outer robe, two young snakes were sporting in the tendrils overhanging the cave. One fell on his shoulder and bit him, and the venom spread rapidly throughout his body; he called to Sāriputta and other monks who were near, and requested that he might be taken outside on a couch, there to die. This was done, and his body “was scattered there and then like a handful of chaff.”. SN.iv.40<em>f.</em></p>

upaseniyā|upaseniyaa|upaseniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaseniyaa</span></p><p>(a girl) who likes to be always near (her mother), a pet, darling, fondling Ja.vi.64 (= mātaraṃ upagantvā sayanika C.).</p><p>Sk. upa + either śayanika of śayana, or sayaniya of <em>śī</em></p>

upasevamāna|upasevamaana|upasevamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasevamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasevati">upasevati</a></p>

upasevanā|upasevanaa|upasevana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasevanaa</span></p><p>practice, performance; devotion (to); association (with); use.</p>

upasevanā|upasevanaa|upasevana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasevanaa</span></p><p>serving, pursuing, following, service, honouring, pursuit SN.iii.53 = Mnd.25 Cnd.570 (nand˚ pleasure-seeking); Iti.68 (bāl˚ &amp; dhīr˚) Snp.249 (utu˚ observance of the seasons); Mil.351.</p><p>abstr. fr. upasevati</p>

upasevati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>frequents, stays at; associates with, consorts with; makes use of (frequently).</p>

upasevati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to practice, frequent, pursue Mil.355.</li><li>to serve, honour, Snp.318 (˚amāna). pp. <em><a href="bword://upasevita">upasevita</a></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><p>upa + <em>sev</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasevita">upasevita</a></p>

upasevin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (-˚) pursuing, following, going after AN.iii.136 (vyatta˚); Mil.264 (rāj˚); Dhp-a.iii.482 (para-dār˚).</p><p>fr. upasevati</p>

upasevita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>visited, frequented Pv-a.147 (for sevita).</p><p>pp. of upasevati</p>

upasiṃsaka|upasi.msaka|upasimsaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasi.msaka</span></p><p>adjective striving after, longing or wishing for Mil.393 (āhār˚ Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 75 proposes reading upasinghaka).</p><p>fr. upa + siṃsati = <em>śaṃs</em>, cp. āsiṃsaka</p>

upasiṅghaka|upasi"nghaka|upasinghaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasi"nghaka</span></p><p>adjective sniffing after Ja.ii.339; Ja.iii.144; Mil.393 (? see <em><a href="bword://upasi%E1%B9%83saka">upasiṃsaka</a></em>).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>siṅgh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasiṃsaka">upasiṃsaka</a></p>

upasiṅghati|upasi"nghati|upasinghati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasi"nghati</span></p><p>smells, sniffs; kisses.</p>

upasiṅghati|upasi"nghati|upasinghati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasi"nghati</span></p><ol><li>to sniff at SN.i.204 (padumaṃ); SN.i.455; Ja.ii.339, Ja.ii.408; Ja.vi.336.</li><li>to sniff up Vin.i.279</li></ol><p>caus <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yati">āyati</a></em> to touch gently Kp-a.136<br />caus 2 <em><a href="bword://apeti">apeti</a></em> to touch lightly, to stroke Ja.iv.407.</p><p>upa + <em>siṅgh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyati">āyati</a>, <a href="bword://apeti">apeti</a></p>

upasiṅghita|upasi"nghita|upasinghita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasi"nghita</span></p><p>scented, smelled at (loc.) Ja.vi.543 (sisaṃhi, C. for upagghata).</p><p>pp. of upasinghati</p>

upasobhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks beautiful or splendid; shines, is brilliant.</p>

upasobhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to appear beautiful, to shine forth Thag.1080<br />caus <em>-sobheti</em> to make beautiful, embellish, adorn Vv.52#6; Ja.v.132; Pv-a.153<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upasobhita">upasobhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>śubh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasobhita">upasobhita</a></p>

upasobhayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>making beautiful, adorning.</p>

upasobhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>made beautiful, adorned; beautiful</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasobhati">upasobhati</a></p>

upasobhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>embellished, beautified, adorned Pv-a.153, Pv-a.187; Sdhp.593.</p><p>pp. of upasobheti</p>

upassagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>misfortune; trouble.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasagga">upasagga</a></p>

upassagga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://upasagga">upasagga</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasagga">upasagga</a></p>

upassaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i></p><p>support; shelter, refuge; dwelling, place to stay; recourse, resorting to.</p>

upassaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>abode, resting home, dwelling, asylum SN.i.32, SN.i.33; Vv.68#4 Mil.160. Esp. freq. as <em>bhikkhuni˚</em>; or <em>bhikkhun˚</em>; a nunnery Vin.ii.259; Vin.iv.265, Vin.iv.292; SN.ii.215; Ja.i.147, Ja.i.428; Mil.124.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>śri</em>, cp. assaya &amp; missaya</p>

upassaṭṭha|upassa.t.tha|upassattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upassa.t.tha</span></p><p>troubled, afflicted, visited by troubles; trouble, affliction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasaṭṭha">upasaṭṭha</a></p>

upassaṭṭha|upassa.t.tha|upassattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upassa.t.tha</span></p><p>“thrown upon”, overcome, visited, afflicted, ruined, oppressed SN.iv.29; AN.iii.226 (udak˚); Ja.i.61; Ja.ii.239.</p><p>Sk. upasṛṣṭa, pp. of upa + <em>sṛj</em></p>

upassuti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>listening to, attention SN.ii.75; SN.iv.91; Ja.v.100; Mil.92.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>śru</em></p>

upassutika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective one who listens, an eavesdropper Ja.v.81.</p><p>fr. upassuti</p>

upassutiṃ|upassuti.m|upassutim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upassuti.m</span></p><p>within earshot; so as to listen or overhear.</p>

upassāsa|upassaasa|upassasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upassaasa</span></p><p>breathing Ja.i.160.</p><p>upa + assāsa; upa + ā + <em>śvas</em></p>

upasumbhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>throws to.</p>

upasussati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>dries up.</p>

upasussati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to dry up MN.i.481; Snp.433; Ja.i.71.</p><p>upa + sussati</p>

upasussayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upasussati">upasussati</a></p>

upasāmayati|upasaamayati|upasamayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasaamayati</span></p><p>calms, makes settle.</p>

upasīdarī|upasiidarii|upasidari
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasiidarii</span></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, mentioned in the Isigili Sutta. MN.iii.70</p>

upasīva|upasiiva|upasiva
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upasiiva</span></p><p>One of the disciples of Bāvarī. Snp.1007 The questions he asked the Buddha, when he visited him in the company of his colleagues, are recorded in the Upasīva-māṇavaa-pucchā. Snp.1069–1076</p>

upatappati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to be vexed or tormented Ja.v.90; Dhs-a.42.</p><p>upa + tappati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->)</p>

upatheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>cushion Ja.vi.490, Ja.vi.513.</p><p>for upadheyya, see Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 62<!-- raw HTML omitted -->16<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

upatissa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha, found in a nominal list. MN.iii.69</p>

upatiṭṭhanta|upati.t.thanta|upatitthanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upati.t.thanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatiṭṭhati">upatiṭṭhati</a></p>

upatiṭṭhati|upati.t.thati|upatitthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upati.t.thati</span></p><p>stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: upaṭṭhahati, upaṭṭhaheti, upaṭṭhāti</p>

upatiṭṭhati|upati.t.thati|upatitthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upati.t.thati</span></p><p>lit. “to stand by”, to look after, to worship Pv.iii.1#18; Ja.ii.73 (ādiccaṃ = namassamāno tiṭṭhati C.); Mil.231 (ger ˚tiṭṭhitvā); Ja.v.173 (˚tiṭṭhate). pp. <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">upaṭṭhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + <em>sthā</em>, cp. upaṭṭhahati, ˚ṭṭhāti etc.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhita">upaṭṭhita</a></p>

upatiṭṭhitvā|upati.t.thitvaa|upatitthitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upati.t.thitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>smeared, spread over MN.i.343; Ja.i.399.</p><p>upa + akta, pp. of <em>añj</em></p>

upatthaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>supported; sustained.</li><li>stiff, proud; haughty.</li></ol>

upatthaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>stiff Vin.iii.37 (angāni).</li><li>supported or held up by, resting on, founded on, relying on Thag.1058, Thag.1194 Thag.2, Thag.72 (yobbanena); Ja.i.47 (Ja.v.267: mettābalena); Ja.v.121 Ja.v.301; Kv.251 (cakkhu dhamm˚ “when it is the medium of an idea”); Ne.117; Mil.110 (kāruñña-bal˚).</li></ol><p>upa + thaddha, pp. of upatthambhati</p>

upatthambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>a support, prop, stay Mil.355, Mil.415, Mil.417; Sdhp.565.</li><li>relief, ease Vin.iii.112.</li><li>encouragement Ja.v.270; Dhp-a.i.279.</li></ol><p>fr. upa + <em>stambh</em></p>

upatthambhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adj. nt. holding up, supporting, sustaining Dhs-a.153.</p><p>fr. upatthambha</p>

upatthambhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://upatthambha">upatthambha</a></em> Mil.36; Ja.i.447; DN-a.i.124; Thag-a.258; Vism.279.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatthambha">upatthambha</a></p>

upatthambheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to make firm, shore up, support, prop up Ja.i.127 (ppr ˚ayamāna), Ja.i.447; DN-a.i.113; Dhp-a.iii.73 (˚ayamāna ppr.). pp. <em><a href="bword://upatthambhita">upatthambhita</a></em>.</p><p>upa + thambheti, Caus. of thambhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatthambhita">upatthambhita</a></p>

upatthambhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>propped up, supported, sustained Ja.i.107; Mil.36; DN-a.i.234; Pv-a.117 (puñña-phal˚), Pv-a.148 (utu-āhārehi u.).</p><p>pp. of upatthambheti</p>

upatthara
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>(floor) covering, carpet, rug DN.i.103 (rath˚); Ja.ii.126 (pabbat˚); Ja.ii.534.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>stṛ</em>.</p>

upatāpa|upataapa|upatapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upataapa</span></p><p>vexation, trouble Vism.166.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>tap</em></p>

upatāpana|upataapana|upatapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upataapana</span></p><p>vexation, tormenting, torture Ja.iv.13; Thag-a.243.</p><p>upa + tāpana</p>

upatāpeti|upataapeti|upatapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upataapeti</span></p><p>to cause pain, to vex, torment, harass Ja.ii.178, Ja.ii.224; Ja.iv.11; Dhs-a.42 (vibādhati +).</p><p>upa + tāpeti</p>

upatāpika|upataapika|upatapika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upataapika</span></p><p>adjective causing pain, molesting Ja.ii.224.</p><p>fr. upatāpa</p>

upavadamāna|upavadamaana|upavadamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavadamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavadati">upavadati</a></p>

upavadati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>speaks ill of, abuses, criticizes.</p>

upavadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to tell (secretly) against, to tell tales; to insult, blame DN.i.90; SN.iii.125 (attā sīlato na upav.); AN.ii.121 (id.); AN.v.88; Ja.ii.196; Pv-a.13.</p><p>upa + <em>vad</em></p>

upavadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>criticized</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavadati">upavadati</a></p>

upavajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p></p><p>upavajja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>fpp mfn.</p><p>upavajja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>fpp mfn. to be criticized, blameworthy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upabbajati">upabbajati</a>, <a href="bword://upavadati">upavadati</a></p>

upavajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective blameworthy SN.iv.59, SN.iv.60; AN.ii.242. <em>an˚</em>; blameless, without fault SN.iv.57 sq AN.iv.82; Mil.391.</p><p>grd. of upavadati</p>

upavajjakula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>family which can be approached</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavajja">upavajja</a></p>

upavajjatā|upavajjataa|upavajjata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavajjataa</span></p><p>blameworthiness SN.iv.59 (an˚).</p><p>abstr. fr. upavajja</p>

upavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>kind of wood, miniature wood, park Ja.iv.431; Ja.v.249; Mil.1; Vv-a.170 (= vana), Vv-a.344; Thag-a.201; Pv-a.102 (ārām˚), Pv-a.177 (mahā˚).</p><p>upa + vana, see <em><a href="bword://upa">upa</a></em> 5</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upa">upa</a></p>

upavasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>lives in a state of abstinence, observes (a fast or (the duties of) an uposatha day).</p>

upavasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><ol><li>to dwell in or at Ja.iii.113; DN-a.i.139.</li><li>to live (trs.); to observe, keep (a holy day); only in phrase <em>uposathaṃ upavasati</em> to observe the fast day SN.i.208; AN.i.142, AN.i.144, AN.i.205; Snp.402 (ger upavassa); Ja.iii.444; Snp-a.199; Pv-a.209</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://upavuttha">upavuttha</a></em> (q.v.). See also uposatha.</p><p>upa + vasiti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavuttha">upavuttha</a></p>

upavasitvā|upavasitvaa|upavasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

upavassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

upavassaṃ|upavassa.m|upavassam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavassa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

upavatta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>UpavattaUpavattana</p><p>The <em>sāla</em>-grove of the Mallas of Kusināra, on the further side of the Hiraññavatī. This was the last resting-place of the Buddha on his last tour, and here he passed away, lying on a bed placed between two sāla trees. DN.ii.137<em>ff.</em></p><p>Here Subhadda visited the Buddha in the earlier part of the last night of his life, was converted and gained admission into the Order, afterwards winning arahantship. It was here, too, that the Buddha asked the monks if they had any doubts they wished to hear solved regarding the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Saṅgha, magga and paṭipadā, or any questions they wished to ask, AN.ii.79 and here he gave his last admonition to the monks SN.i.157 Ud.37<em>f.</em> Ānanda tried to persuade him to die in a place of greater importance, and the Buddha, in order to disabuse his mind, preached to him the Mahā Sudassana Sutta. DN.ii.169<em>f.</em></p><p>26.73012,83.898632park</p>

upavattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>comes near, falls to; is found, is.</p>

upavattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to come to pass, to take place Ja.vi.58.</p><p>upa + <em>vṛt</em></p>

upavaṇṇeti|upava.n.neti|upavanneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upava.n.neti</span></p><p>to describe fully Sdhp.487.</p><p>upa + vaṇṇeti</p>

upavhayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavhayati">upavhayati</a></p>

upavhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>calls (to); invites.</p>

upavhyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to invoke, call upon DN.ii.259; SN.i.168.</p><p>upa + ā + <em>hū</em>, cp. avhayati for *āhvayati</p>

upavicarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>ranges over (mentally), considers, occupies oneself with.</p>

upavicāra|upavicaara|upavicara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavicaara</span></p><p>ranging over (mentally); preoccupation.</p>

upavicāra|upavicaara|upavicara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavicaara</span></p><p>applying (one’s mind) to, discrimination DN.iii.245 (domanass˚); MN.iii.239; SN.iv.232 (somanass˚ etc.); AN.iii.363 sq.; AN.v.134; Pts.i.17; Dhp.8, Dhp.85, Dhp.284; Vb.381.</p><p>upa + vicāra; cp. BSk. upavicāra Divy.19, trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> on p. 704 in Notes by “perplexed by doubts” (?)</p>

upavijañña|upavija~n~na|upavijanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavija~n~na</span></p><p>close to giving birth, in advanced stage of pregnancy.</p>

upavijaññā|upavija~n~naa|upavijanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavija~n~naa</span></p><p>(adj.) about to bring forth a child, nearing childbirth MN.i.384; Thig.218; Ud.13; Dāvs iii.38 Thag-a.197.</p><p>grd. formation of upa + vi + <em>jan</em>, cp. Sk. vijanya</p>

upavisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to come near, to approach a person Ja.iv.408; Ja.v.377; aor. <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81visi">upāvisi</a></em> Snp.415, Snp.418 (āsajja upāvisi = samīpaṃ gantvā nisīdi Snp-a.384).</p><p>upa + visati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāvisi">upāvisi</a></p>

upavisiṃ|upavisi.m|upavisim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavisi.m</span></p><p>(I am) approaching; entering.</p>

upaviṭṭha|upavi.t.tha|upavittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavi.t.tha</span></p><p>seated.</p>

upavuttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(act.) having fasted; having observed.</li><li>(pass.) observed, kept</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

upavuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>celebrated, kept (of a fastday) AN.i.211 (uposatha); Snp.403 (uposatha). Cp. <em><a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></em>.</p><p>pp. of upavasati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

upavutthuposatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>who has observed the uposatha day</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavuttha">upavuttha</a></p>

upavāda|upavaada|upavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaada</span></p><p>criticism, blame; abuse.</p>

upavāda|upavaada|upavada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaada</span></p><p>insulting, railing; blaming, finding fault Mnd.386; Pv-a.269; <em>an˚</em>; (adj.) not grumbling or abusing Dhp.185 (anûpa˚ metri causa).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>vad</em></p>

upavādaka|upavaadaka|upavadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaadaka</span></p><p>one who criticizes or abuse.</p>

upavādaka|upavaadaka|upavadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaadaka</span></p><p>adjective blaming, finding fault, speaking evil of (gen.), generally in phrase <em>ariyānaṃ u</em> insulting the gentle Vin.iii.5; AN.i.256; AN.iii.19; AN.iv.178 AN.v.68; Iti.58, Iti.99<br />■ <em>an˚</em>; Pts.i.115; Pp.60.</p><p>fr. upavāda</p>

upavādin|upavaadin|upavadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaadin</span></p><p>adjective = upavādaka; in <em>ariy˚</em>; SN.i.225; SN.ii.124; SN.v.266; Pv.iv.3#39. an˚ MN.i.360.</p><p>fr. upavāda</p>

upavāhana|upavaahana|upavahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaahana</span></p><p>carrying away, washing away Snp.391 (sanghāṭi-raj-ûpa˚ = paṃsu-malādino sanghāṭirajassa dhovanaṃ Snp-a.375).</p><p>upa + vāhana</p>

upavāna|upavaana|upavana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaana</span></p><p>A monk who was occasionally the attendant of the Buddha, and who features in a number of discourses. Once when the Buddha was attacked by cramp, Upavāna, with the help of his lay-friend Devahita, obtained hot water and suitable medicines, with which the ailment was healed; the Buddha, thereupon, expressed his gratitude. SN.i.174<em>f.</em></p><p>When the Buddha lay on his death-bed at Kusināra, Upavāna was by his side fanning him; the Buddha, seeing that he obstructed the vision of the devas who had come to pay their last homage to the Teacher, asked Upavāna to move away. DN.ii.138<em>f.</em></p><p>Two occasions are mentioned on which Upavāna consulted the Buddha on matters of doctrine, once regarding the arising of suffering SN.ii.41–2 and once on the immediate and practical use of the Dhamma. SN.iv.41 There is also recorded a visit of Upavāna to Sāriputta when they were both staying in the Ghositārāma at Kosambī. Sāriputta asks him about the bojjhangas as being conducive to a happy life and Upavāna explains. SN.v.76 On another occasion Upavāna is the enquirer, and he asks Sāriputta about the “end-maker”; Sāriputta explains that the “end-maker” is the one who knows and sees things as they really are. AN.ii.163</p><p>When an unpleasant interview took place between Sāriputta and Lāludāyī and no one was found to support Sāriputta, the matter is reported to the Buddha, who declares that Ānanda should have taken Sāriputta’s side. Soon afterwards Ānanda seeks Upavāna and tells him that he was too timid to interfere, and if the Buddha referred to the matter again, would Upavāna undertake to answer? In the evening the Buddha engages Upavāna in conversation and asks him to explain the five qualities which make a monk esteemed and loved by his colleagues. At the end of the discourse the Buddha applauds Upavāna. AN.iii.195<em>f.</em></p>

upavāsa|upavaasa|upavasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaasa</span></p><p>tenant; dependant.</p>

upavāsa|upavaasa|upavasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaasa</span></p><p>keeping a prescribed day, fasting, self-denial, abstaining from enjoyments <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a>*; used extensively in BSk. in meaning of uposatha, e.g. at Avs.i.338, Avs.i.339; Divy.398 in phrase aṣṭânga-samanvāgataṃ upavāsaṃ upavasati] AN.v.40 (? uncertain; vv.ll. upāsaka, ovāpavāssa, yopavāsa); Ja.vi.508; Snp-a.199 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of uposatha).</p><p>fr. upa + <em>vas</em>, see <em><a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a>, <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

upavāsita|upavaasita|upavasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaasita</span></p><p>adjective (upa + vāsita] perfumed Pv-a.164 (for gandha-samerita).</p>

upavāyanta|upavaayanta|upavayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaayanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavāyati">upavāyati</a></p>

upavāyati|upavaayati|upavayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaayati</span></p><p>blows, blows upon.</p>

upavāyati|upavaayati|upavayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavaayati</span></p><p>to blow on or towards somebody MN.i.424; AN.iv.46; Thag.544; Pv.iii.6#6; Mil.97.</p><p>upa + vāyati</p>

upavīna|upaviina|upavina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaviina</span></p><p>the neck of a lute SN.iv.197; Mil.53.</p><p>upa + vīṇā</p>

upavīta|upaviita|upavita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaviita</span></p><p>covered (?) at Vv-a.8 in phrase “vettalatâdīhi upavītaṃ āsanaṃ” should prob. be read <em><a href="bword://upan%C4%ABta">upanīta</a></em> (vv. ll uparivīta &amp; upajita); or could it be pp. of upavīyati (woven with)?</p><p>?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upanīta">upanīta</a></p>

upavīyati|upaviiyati|upaviyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaviiyati</span></p><p>te be woven Ja.vi.26.</p><p>Pass. of upa + <em>vā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to weave</p>

upavīṇa|upavii.na|upavina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upavii.na</span></p><p>the head or neck of a <em>vīṇa</em>.</p>

upaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(m.) going near to, involvement.</li><li>(mfn.) going to be; being involved.</li></ol>

upaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>approach, undertaking, taking up; clinging to, attachment, only as adj. (-˚) in <em>an˚</em>; (anûpaya metri causā) not going near, aloof, unattached SN.i.141, SN.i.181; SN.ii.284; Snp.786, Snp.787, Snp.897 (cp. Snp-a.558); and in <em>rūpūpaya</em> (vv.ll. rūpupaya &amp; rūpupāya “clinging to form” (etc.) SN.iii.53 = Mnd.25 = Cnd.570 (+ rup’ārammaṇa).</p><p>fr. upa + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em>, cp. upāya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

upayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayāti">upayāti</a></p>

upayoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>connection, combination; employment, application Ja.vi.432 (nagare upayogaṃ netvā for use in the town? variant reading upabhogaṃ). Usually in cpd <em>-vacana</em> as tt. g. meaning either combined or condensed expression, ellipsis Snp-a.386; Kp-a.236; Pv-a.73, Pv-a.135 or the <em>acc.</em> case, which is frequently substituted for the foll. cases: sāmi-vacana Snp-a.127; Pv-a.102; bhumma Snp-a.140; Kp-a.116; karaṇa˚ Snp-a.148; sampadāna˚ Ja.v.214; Snp-a.317; itthambhūta˚ Snp-a.441; nissakka˚ Ja.v.498.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>yuj</em></p>

upayupādāna|upayupaadaana|upayupadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; \~ā m. plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayupaadaana</span></p><p>involvement and holding on to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayūpādāna">upayūpādāna</a>, <a href="bword://upaya">upaya</a></p>

upayuñjati|upayu~njati|upayunjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayu~njati</span></p><p>to combine, connect with; to use, apply; ppr. med. <em>upayujjamāna</em> Vv-a.245 (preferably be read as ˚bhuñjamāna, with reference to enjoying drink &amp; food).</p><p>upa + <em>yuj</em></p>

upayācati|upayaacati|upayacati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaacati</span></p><p>to beg, entreat, pray to Ja.vi.150 (divyaṃ).</p><p>upa + yācati</p>

upayācitaka|upayaacitaka|upayacitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaacitaka</span></p><p>begging, asking, praying, propitiation Ja.vi.150 (= devatānaṃ āyācana).</p><p>of adj. upa + yācita + ka; pp. of yācati</p>

upayāna|upayaana|upayana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaana</span></p><p>approach, arrival.</p>

upayāna|upayaana|upayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaana</span></p><p>nearing, approach, arrival DN.i.10; DN-a.i.94.</p><p>fr. upa + <strong>yā</strong>, cp. BSk. upayāna Jtm.31#63</p><p>See also: yā</p>

upayānaka|upayaanaka|upayanaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaanaka</span></p><p>An aquatic animal, perhaps a type of crab Ja.vi.530.</p>

upayānaka|upayaanaka|upayanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaanaka</span></p><p>crab Ja.vi.530.</p><p>fr. upayāna</p>

upayāpetabba|upayaapetabba|upayapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayāpeti">upayāpeti</a></p>

upayāpeti|upayaapeti|upayapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayāti">upayāti</a></p>

upayāsi|upayaasi|upayasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayāti">upayāti</a></p>

upayāti|upayaati|upayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaati</span></p><p>comes near, approaches, reaches; enters (a state or condition); performs (a sacrifice); flows; flows into; flows at the full.</p>

upayāti|upayaati|upayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayaati</span></p><p>to go to, to approach SN.i.76; SN.ii.118 (also Caus. <em>-yāpeti</em>); Dpvs.vi.69; Sdhp.579.</p><p>upa + yāti of <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: yā</p>

upayūpādāna|upayuupaadaana|upayupadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp; \~ā m. plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upayuupaadaana</span></p><p>involvement and holding on to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upayupādāna">upayupādāna</a>, <a href="bword://upaya">upaya</a></p>

upaññassaṃ|upa~n~nassa.m|upannassam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~nassa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajānāti">upajānāti</a></p>

upaññissaṃ|upa~n~nissa.m|upannissam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~nissa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajānāti">upajānāti</a></p>

upaññāsiṃ|upa~n~naasi.m|upannasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~naasi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajānāti">upajānāti</a></p>

upaññāta|upa~n~naata|upannata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~naata</span></p><p>found out (for oneself); invented; known; instituted; (first) taught</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajānāti">upajānāti</a></p>

upaññāta|upa~n~naata|upannata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~naata</span></p><p>found out, learnt, known Vin.i.40; Ja.v.325, Ja.v.368; AN.i.61.</p><p>pp. of upajānāti</p>

upaññātta|upa~n~naatta|upannatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa~n~naatta</span></p><p>made known; instituted; (first) taught</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upajānāti">upajānāti</a></p>

upaḍayhati|upa.dayhati|upadayhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.dayhati</span></p><p>to be burnt up Mil.277.</p><p>upa + ḍayhati</p>

upaḍḍha|upa.d.dha|upaddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; m. neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.d.dha</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) half; the half of.</li><li>(m. n.) a half.</li></ol>

upaḍḍha|upa.d.dha|upaddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.d.dha</span></p><p>adjective noun half Vin.i.281 (˚kāsina); Vin.ii.200 (˚āsana); Ja.iii.11 (˚rajja); Vism.320 (˚gāma); Dhp-a.i.15 Dhp-a.i.205 (˚uposathakamma); Dhp-a.ii.85; Kp-a.239 (˚gāthā); Snp-a.298; Vv-a.38, Vv-a.61, Vv-a.120; Pv-a.209, Pv-a.276.</p><p>upa + aḍḍha, used abs. whereas aḍḍha only in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, cp. also BSk. upārdha Divy.86, Divy.144 Divy.514; Avs.i.211, Avs.i.240</p>

upaḍḍhullikhita|upa.d.dhullikhita|upaddhullikhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.d.dhullikhita</span></p><p>half combed, half dressed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaḍḍha">upaḍḍha</a></p>

upaḷāseti|upa.laaseti|upalaseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.laaseti</span></p><p>to sound forth, to (make) sound (a bugle) DN.ii.337 (for uppaḷāseti? q.v.).</p><p>upa + Caus. of <em>las</em></p>

upaḷāsetvā|upa.laasetvaa|upalasetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.laasetvaa</span></p><p>making sound, playing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaḷāsitvā">upaḷāsitvā</a></p>

upaḷāsitvā|upa.laasitvaa|upalasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.laasitvaa</span></p><p>making sound, playing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaḷāsetvā">upaḷāsetvā</a></p>

upaṭṭhahanta|upa.t.thahanta|upatthahanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a></p>

upaṭṭhahati|upa.t.thahati|upatthahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahati</span></p><p>stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: upatiṭṭhati, upaṭṭhaheti, upaṭṭhāti, upaṭṭheti</p>

upaṭṭhahati|upa.t.thahati|upatthahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahati</span></p><p>Upaṭṭhahati &amp; ˚ṭṭhāti</p><ol><li>(trs.) to stand near or at hand (with acc.), to wait on attend on, serve, minister, to care for, look after, nurse (in sickness) Vin.i.50, Vin.i.302; Vin.iv.326; MN.iii.25; SN.i.167; AN.iii.94; AN.v.72; Snp.82 = Snp.481<br />imper ˚ṭṭhahassu; Ja.i.67<br />ppr ˚ṭṭhahamāna, Ja.i.262<br />ppr ˚ṭṭhahanto Ja.iv.131; Ja.v.396 Dpvs.ii.16; Pv-a.19, Pv-a.20<br />aor <strong>upaṭṭhahi</strong> Pv-a.14, Pv-a.42 Pv-a.82<br />inf <em>upaṭṭhātuṃ</em> AN.v.72; Pv-a.20<br />ger <strong>upaṭṭhahitvā</strong> Pv-a.76<br />grd <em>upaṭṭhātabba</em> Vin.i.302; Pv-a.20<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">upaṭṭhita</a></em> (q.v.).</li><li>(intrs.) to stand out or forth, to appear, to arise, occur, to be present MN.i.104 sq.; AN.iv.32; Ja.iv.203 (mante anupaṭṭhahante since the spell did not occur to him); Ja.v.207; Mil.64; Thag-a.258.<br />aor <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81si">upaṭṭhāsi</a></em> Ja.i.61; Ja.iv.3; Pv-a.42<br />caus 1 <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADheti">upaṭṭheti</a></em>;<br />caus 2 <strong>upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti;</strong> (q.v.)<br />pass <em>upaṭṭhīyati</em> Ja.iv.131 (ppr. ˚ṭṭhiyamāna), &amp; <em>upaṭṭhahīyati</em> AN.iii.94 (ppr. ˚ṭṭhahiyamāna).</li></ol><p>upa + <em>sthā</em>, cp. upatiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: upaṭṭhahi, upaṭṭhahitvā, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhita">upaṭṭhita</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāsi">upaṭṭhāsi</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a>, upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti;</p>

upaṭṭhaheti|upa.t.thaheti|upatthaheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaheti</span></p><p>stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatiṭṭhati">upatiṭṭhati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāti">upaṭṭhāti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhahiyamāna|upa.t.thahiyamaana|upatthahiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhahiṃ|upa.t.thahi.m|upatthahim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a></p>

upaṭṭhahiṃsū|upa.t.thahi.msuu|upatthahimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thahi.msuu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a></p>

upaṭṭhapana|upa.t.thapana|upatthapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thapana</span></p><p>producing, bringing about; arranging attendance; having (someone) served or cared for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpana">upaṭṭhāpana</a></p>

upaṭṭhapayati|upa.t.thapayati|upatthapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thapayati</span></p><p>brings near, provides; procures, fetches; makes serve or attend, employs; causes to appear, brings about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapeti">upaṭṭhapeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhapeti|upa.t.thapeti|upatthapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thapeti</span></p><p>brings near, provides; procures, fetches; makes serve or attend, employs; causes to appear, brings about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapayati">upaṭṭhapayati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhapeti|upa.t.thapeti|upatthapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thapeti</span></p><p>Upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti</p><ol><li>to provide, procure, get ready, put forth, give Vin.ii.210; DN.ii.19; MN.i.429; Ja.i.266; Ja.iv.2; Ja.v.218; Pp.59, Pp.68; Mil.15, Mil.257, Mil.366 (pānīyaṃ paribhojanīyaṃ), Mil.397; DN-a.i.270; Sdhp.356.</li><li>to cause to be present Vin.i.45; SN.i.170; Pv.iv.1#70.</li><li>to cause to be waited on or to be nursed AN.v.72 (gilānaṃ upaṭṭhātuṃ vā upaṭṭhāpetuṃ vā).</li><li>to keep (a servant) for hire Vin.ii.267.</li><li>to ordain Vin.i.62, Vin.i.83. Upatthahati &amp; tthati;</li></ol><p>Caus. ii. of upaṭṭhahati</p>

upaṭṭhenta|upa.t.thenta|upatthenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thenta</span></p><p>(~entī)n. stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatiṭṭhati">upatiṭṭhati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhaheti">upaṭṭhaheti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāti">upaṭṭhāti</a></p>

upaṭṭheti|upa.t.theti|upattheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.theti</span></p><p>stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatiṭṭhati">upatiṭṭhati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhaheti">upaṭṭhaheti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāti">upaṭṭhāti</a></p>

upaṭṭheti|upa.t.theti|upattheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.theti</span></p><p>to make serve or attend; sakkaccaṃ u. (with acc.) to bestow respect (upon) Vin.iv.275. fut. <em>-essati</em> Vin.iv.291. to place, fix (parimukhaṃ satiṃ upaṭṭhapetvā) Vb.244.</p><p>Caus. of upaṭṭhahati</p>

upaṭṭhita|upa.t.thita|upatthita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thita</span></p><ol><li>(act.) come near, approached; arrived, present, at hand; serving, attending; dedicated.</li><li>(pass.) served, attended</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhita|upa.t.thita|upatthita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thita</span></p><ol><li>furnished provided, served got ready, honoured with Snp.295 (˚asmiṃ yaññasmiṃ) Ja.v.173 (annena pānena); Pv.i.5#2 (= sajjita paṭiyatta Pv-a.25); Pv.ii.98 (= payirupāsita Pv-a.116); Pv-a.132.</li><li>come, come about, appeared, arrived; present, existing Snp.130 (bhattakāle upaṭṭhite when mealtime has come) Snp.898; Dhp.235; Mil.274; Pv-a.124 (dānakāle ˚e).</li><li>standing up (ready), keeping in readiness MN.i.77; AN.ii.206; Snp.708 (= ṭhito C.); Pv.ii.9#53 (ready for service serving, waiting upon cp. Pv-a.135.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://sati">sati</a></em> with ready attention, one whose attention is fixed concentrated Vin.i.63; DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; SN.iv.186; AN.iii.251; Pp.25.</li></ul><p>pp. of upaṭṭhahati or upatiṭṭhāti, cp. BSk. upasthita Divy.281, Divy.342</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sati">sati</a></p>

upaṭṭhābba|upa.t.thaabba|upatthabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāka|upa.t.thaaka|upatthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaaka</span></p><p>(~ī)n. &amp; m. feminine</p><ol><li>(mfn.) serving, attending, supporting.</li><li>(m.f.) one who serves or supports; a personal attendant; a nurse.</li></ol>

upaṭṭhāka|upa.t.thaaka|upatthaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaaka</span></p><p>servitor, personal attendant, servant, “famulus” Ānanda was the last u. of Gotama Buddha (see DN.i.206 Thag.1041 f.; Thag-a in <em>Brethren</em> loc. cit.; Vin.i.179 (Sāgato u.), Vin.i.194; Vin.ii.186; Vin.iii.66; Vin.iv.47; DN.i.150 (Nāgita) SN.iii.113; AN.i.121; AN.iii.31, AN.iii.189; Ja.i.15, Ja.i.100 (a merchant’s), Ja.ii.416; Pp.28; Dhp-a.ii.93; Vv-a.149; Pv-a.211<br />■ <em>agg˚</em> main follower, chief attendant DN.ii.6; <em>gilān˚</em>; an attendant in sickness, nurse Vin.i.303; AN.i.26; <em>saṅgh˚</em>; one who looks after the community of Bhikkhus Vin.i.216; AN.i.26 AN.iii.39<br />■ <em>dupaṭṭhāka &amp; supaṭṭhāka;</em> a bad (&amp; good) attendant Vin.i.302.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kula">kula</a></em> a family entertaining (or ministering to) a thera or a bhikkhu, a family devoted to the service of (gen. Vin.i.83 (Sāriputtassa), Vin.i.213; Vin.iii.62, Vin.iii.66, Vin.iii.67; Vin.iv.283, Vin.iv.286; Vv-a.120.</li></ul><p>fr. upa + <em>sthā</em>, cp. BSk. upasthāka Mvu.i.251, and upasthāyaka Divy.426; Avs.i.214; Avs.ii.85 112.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kula">kula</a></p>

upaṭṭhāna|upa.t.thaana|upatthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter .</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaana</span></p><p>going near, keeping close to; presence, appearance; serving, waiting on, attendance; worshiping.</p>

upaṭṭhāna|upa.t.thaana|upatthana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaana</span></p><ol><li>attendance, waiting on, looking after, service, care, ministering AN.i.151, AN.i.225; Snp.138; Ja.i.226, Ja.i.237, Ja.i.291; Ja.ii.101; Ja.iv.138; Ja.vi.351; Pts.i.107; Pts.ii.7 sq., Pts.ii.28, Pts.ii.230; Pv-a.104, Pv-a.145 (paccekabuddhassa), Pv-a.176; Vv-a.75 (ther˚); Sdhp.560.</li><li>worship (divine) service DN.iii.188 sq. (˚ṃ gacchati); Pv-a.122. <em>Buddh˚</em>; attendance on a Buddha Pv-a.93; Thag-a.18.</li><li>a state room Ja.iii.257.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://sambh%C4%81ra">sambhāra</a></em> means of catering, provisions Pv-a.20.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81l%C4%81">sālā</a></em> hall for attendance, assembly room, chapel [cp. BSk upasthāna-śālā Divy.207] Vin.i.49, Vin.i.139; Vin.ii.153, Vin.ii.208; Vin.iii.70 (at Vesālī); Vin.iv.15, Vin.iv.42; DN.ii.119 (at Vesālī); SN.ii.280 SN.v.321; AN.ii.51, AN.ii.197; AN.iii.298; Dhp-a.i.37, Dhp-a.i.38; Dhp-a.iii.413.</li></ul><p>fr. upa + <em>sthā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sambhāra">sambhāra</a>, <a href="bword://sālā">sālā</a></p>

upaṭṭhānasālā|upa.t.thaanasaalaa|upatthanasala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaanasaalaa</span></p><p>audience hall, assembly hall</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāna">upaṭṭhāna</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpana|upa.t.thaapana|upatthapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapana</span></p><p>producing, bringing about; arranging attendance; having (someone) served or cared for</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapana">upaṭṭhapana</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpana|upa.t.thaapana|upatthapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapana</span></p><p>attendance, service Vin.iv.291.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>sthā</em></p>

upaṭṭhāpaya|upa.t.thaapaya|upatthapaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapaya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpayat">upaṭṭhāpayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpayati">upaṭṭhāpayati</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpayat|upa.t.thaapayat|upatthapayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpayat">upaṭṭhāpayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpayati">upaṭṭhāpayati</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpayati|upa.t.thaapayati|upatthapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapayati</span></p><p>brings near, provides; procures, fetches; makes serve or attend, employs; causes to appear, brings about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapeti">upaṭṭhapeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpesi|upa.t.thaapesi|upatthapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpeta|upa.t.thaapeta|upatthapeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpetar">upaṭṭhāpetar</a></p><p>one who causes (someone) to serve; attend.</p>

upaṭṭhāpetabba|upa.t.thaapetabba|upatthapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpetar|upa.t.thaapetar|upatthapetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpetar">upaṭṭhāpetar</a></p><p>one who causes (someone) to serve; attend.</p>

upaṭṭhāpeti|upa.t.thaapeti|upatthapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapeti</span></p><p>brings near, provides; procures, fetches; makes serve or attend, employs; causes to appear, brings about</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapeti">upaṭṭhapeti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhapayati">upaṭṭhapayati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpeti|upa.t.thaapeti|upatthapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapeti</span></p><p>Upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti</p><ol><li>to provide, procure, get ready, put forth, give Vin.ii.210; DN.ii.19; MN.i.429; Ja.i.266; Ja.iv.2; Ja.v.218; Pp.59, Pp.68; Mil.15, Mil.257, Mil.366 (pānīyaṃ paribhojanīyaṃ), Mil.397; DN-a.i.270; Sdhp.356.</li><li>to cause to be present Vin.i.45; SN.i.170; Pv.iv.1#70.</li><li>to cause to be waited on or to be nursed AN.v.72 (gilānaṃ upaṭṭhātuṃ vā upaṭṭhāpetuṃ vā).</li><li>to keep (a servant) for hire Vin.ii.267.</li><li>to ordain Vin.i.62, Vin.i.83. Upatthahati &amp; tthati;</li></ol><p>Caus. ii. of upaṭṭhahati</p>

upaṭṭhāpetvā|upa.t.thaapetvaa|upatthapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpetvāna|upa.t.thaapetvaana|upatthapetvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapetvaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāpittha|upa.t.thaapittha|upatthapittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaapittha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāpeti">upaṭṭhāpeti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāsi|upa.t.thaasi|upatthasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāti">upaṭṭhāti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāsiṃ|upa.t.thaasi.m|upatthasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaasi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāti">upaṭṭhāti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāti|upa.t.thaati|upatthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaati</span></p><p>stands near, stands by; places oneself by; presents oneself; is present, appears; stands near in order to serve, serves, attends (+ acc., occasionally + dat./gen.); tends; worships</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upatiṭṭhati">upatiṭṭhati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhahati">upaṭṭhahati</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhaheti">upaṭṭhaheti</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭhāti|upa.t.thaati|upatthati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaati</span></p><p>Upaṭṭhahati &amp; ˚ṭṭhāti</p><ol><li>(trs.) to stand near or at hand (with acc.), to wait on attend on, serve, minister, to care for, look after, nurse (in sickness) Vin.i.50, Vin.i.302; Vin.iv.326; MN.iii.25; SN.i.167; AN.iii.94; AN.v.72; Snp.82 = Snp.481<br />imper ˚ṭṭhahassu; Ja.i.67<br />ppr ˚ṭṭhahamāna, Ja.i.262<br />ppr ˚ṭṭhahanto Ja.iv.131; Ja.v.396 Dpvs.ii.16; Pv-a.19, Pv-a.20<br />aor <strong>upaṭṭhahi</strong> Pv-a.14, Pv-a.42 Pv-a.82<br />inf <em>upaṭṭhātuṃ</em> AN.v.72; Pv-a.20<br />ger <strong>upaṭṭhahitvā</strong> Pv-a.76<br />grd <em>upaṭṭhātabba</em> Vin.i.302; Pv-a.20<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhita">upaṭṭhita</a></em> (q.v.).</li><li>(intrs.) to stand out or forth, to appear, to arise, occur, to be present MN.i.104 sq.; AN.iv.32; Ja.iv.203 (mante anupaṭṭhahante since the spell did not occur to him); Ja.v.207; Mil.64; Thag-a.258.<br />aor <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81si">upaṭṭhāsi</a></em> Ja.i.61; Ja.iv.3; Pv-a.42<br />caus 1 <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADheti">upaṭṭheti</a></em>;<br />caus 2 <strong>upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti;</strong> (q.v.)<br />pass <em>upaṭṭhīyati</em> Ja.iv.131 (ppr. ˚ṭṭhiyamāna), &amp; <em>upaṭṭhahīyati</em> AN.iii.94 (ppr. ˚ṭṭhahiyamāna).</li></ol><p>upa + <em>sthā</em>, cp. upatiṭṭhati</p><p>See also: upaṭṭhahi, upaṭṭhahitvā, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhita">upaṭṭhita</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭhāsi">upaṭṭhāsi</a>, <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a>, upaṭṭhapeti &amp; ˚ṭṭhāpeti;</p>

upaṭṭhātuṃ|upa.t.thaatu.m|upatthatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.thaatu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaṭṭheti">upaṭṭheti</a></p>

upaṭṭita|upa.t.tita|upattita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upa.t.tita</span></p><p>pained, terrified; overcome, overwhelmed Ja.vi.82 (visavegena). Upatthapeti &amp; tthapeti;</p><p>upa + aṭṭita, from <em>ard</em>, see <em><a href="bword://a%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADita">aṭṭita</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṭṭita">aṭṭita</a></p>

upecca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upeti">upeti</a></p>

upekhā|upekhaa|upekha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upekhaa</span></p><p>Upekkhā &amp; Upekhā</p><p>feminine “looking on”, hedonic neutrality or indifference, zero point between joy &amp; sorrow (Cpd. 66) disinterestedness, neutral feeling, equanimity. Sometimes equivalent to adukkham-asukha-vedanā “feeling which is neither pain nor pleasure”. See detailed discussion of term at <em>Cpd.</em> 229–⁠232, &amp; cp.; <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 39<br />■ Ten kinds of upekkhā are enumerated at Dhs-a.172 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 48; Hardy, <em>Man. Buddhism</em> 505)<br />■ DN.i.38 (˚sati-parisuddhi purity of mindfulness which comes of disinterestedness cp. Vin.iii.4; Dhs.165 &amp; Dhs trsln<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 50), DN.i.251, DN.ii.279 (twofold); DN.iii.50, DN.iii.78, DN.iii.106, DN.iii.224 sq., DN.iii.239, DN.iii.245 (six ˚upavicāras), DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; MN.i.79, MN.i.364; MN.iii.219; SN.iv.71 SN.iv.114 sq., SN.v.209 sq. (˚indriya); AN.i.42; AN.i.81 (˚sukha), AN.i.256 (˚nimitta); AN.iii.185, AN.iii.291 (˚cetovimutti); AN.iv.47 sq., AN.iv.70 sq. AN.iv.300, AN.iv.443; AN.v.301, AN.v.360; Snp.67, Snp.73, Snp.972, Snp.1107, (˚satisaṃsuddha); Mnd.501 = Cnd.166; Pts.i.8, Pts.i.36, Pts.i.60, Pts.i.167 Pts.i.177; Pp.59 (˚sati); Ne.25, Ne.97 (˚dhātu), Ne.121 sq.; Vb.12, Vb.15 (˚indriya), Vb.54 (id.), Vb.69, Vb.85 (˚dhātu), Vb.228, Vb.324, Vb.326 (˚sambojjhanga), Vb.381 (˚upavicāra); Dhs.150, Dhs.153, Dhs.165 Dhs.262, Dhs.556, Dhs.1001, Dhs.1278, Dhs.1582; Vism.134 (˚sambojjhanga 5 conditions of), Vism.148 (˚ânubrūhanā), Vism.160 (def. &amp; tenfold), Vism.317 (˚bhāvanā), Vism.319 (˚brahmavihāra), Vism.325 (˚vihārin), Vism.461; Snp-a.128; Sdhp.461.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>īkṣ</em>, cp. BSk. upekṣā Divy.483; Jtm.211. On spelling upekhā for upekkhā see Müller P. Gr. 16</p>

upekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>disinterested, unaffected.</p>

upekkhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>disinterested, unaffected, uninvolved.</p>

upekkhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective disinterested, resigned, stoical Vin.iii.4; DN.i.37, DN.i.183; DN.iii.113, DN.iii.222, DN.iii.245, DN.iii.269, DN.iii.281; SN.v.295 sq., SN.v.318; AN.iii.169 sq., AN.iii.279; AN.v.30; Snp.515 Snp.855, Snp.912; Iti.81; Mnd.241, Mnd.330; Pp.50, Pp.59; Dhs.163; Dhs-a.172.</p><p>fr. upekkhā</p>

upekkhanā|upekkhanaa|upekkhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upekkhanaa</span></p><p>is commentator’s paraphrase for upekkhā (q.v.) Mnd.501 = Cnd.166 Vb.230.</p><p>abstr. fr. upa + <em>īkṣ</em></p>

upekkhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>looks on (without involvement or reaction or action); is disinterested, unaffected.</p>

upekkhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to look on, to be disinterested or indifferent Snp.911; Mnd.328; Ja.vi.294.</p><p>upa + <em>īkṣ</em></p>

upekkhavant
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = upekkhaka Ja.v.403. Upekkha &amp; Upekha</p>

upekkhindriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the faculty of disinterestedness, lack of reaction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upekkhā">upekkhā</a></p>

upekkhā|upekkhaa|upekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upekkhaa</span></p><p>disinterestedness, unaffectedness (one of the <em>brahmavihāras</em> q.v.); lack of involvement or reaction.</p>

upekkhā|upekkhaa|upekkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upekkhaa</span></p><p>Upekkhā &amp; Upekhā</p><p>feminine “looking on”, hedonic neutrality or indifference, zero point between joy &amp; sorrow (Cpd. 66) disinterestedness, neutral feeling, equanimity. Sometimes equivalent to adukkham-asukha-vedanā “feeling which is neither pain nor pleasure”. See detailed discussion of term at <em>Cpd.</em> 229–⁠232, &amp; cp.; <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 39<br />■ Ten kinds of upekkhā are enumerated at Dhs-a.172 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 48; Hardy, <em>Man. Buddhism</em> 505)<br />■ DN.i.38 (˚sati-parisuddhi purity of mindfulness which comes of disinterestedness cp. Vin.iii.4; Dhs.165 &amp; Dhs trsln<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 50), DN.i.251, DN.ii.279 (twofold); DN.iii.50, DN.iii.78, DN.iii.106, DN.iii.224 sq., DN.iii.239, DN.iii.245 (six ˚upavicāras), DN.iii.252, DN.iii.282; MN.i.79, MN.i.364; MN.iii.219; SN.iv.71 SN.iv.114 sq., SN.v.209 sq. (˚indriya); AN.i.42; AN.i.81 (˚sukha), AN.i.256 (˚nimitta); AN.iii.185, AN.iii.291 (˚cetovimutti); AN.iv.47 sq., AN.iv.70 sq. AN.iv.300, AN.iv.443; AN.v.301, AN.v.360; Snp.67, Snp.73, Snp.972, Snp.1107, (˚satisaṃsuddha); Mnd.501 = Cnd.166; Pts.i.8, Pts.i.36, Pts.i.60, Pts.i.167 Pts.i.177; Pp.59 (˚sati); Ne.25, Ne.97 (˚dhātu), Ne.121 sq.; Vb.12, Vb.15 (˚indriya), Vb.54 (id.), Vb.69, Vb.85 (˚dhātu), Vb.228, Vb.324, Vb.326 (˚sambojjhanga), Vb.381 (˚upavicāra); Dhs.150, Dhs.153, Dhs.165 Dhs.262, Dhs.556, Dhs.1001, Dhs.1278, Dhs.1582; Vism.134 (˚sambojjhanga 5 conditions of), Vism.148 (˚ânubrūhanā), Vism.160 (def. &amp; tenfold), Vism.317 (˚bhāvanā), Vism.319 (˚brahmavihāra), Vism.325 (˚vihārin), Vism.461; Snp-a.128; Sdhp.461.</p><p>fr. upa + <em>īkṣ</em>, cp. BSk. upekṣā Divy.483; Jtm.211. On spelling upekhā for upekkhā see Müller P. Gr. 16</p>

upekkhāsahagata|upekkhaasahagata|upekkhasahagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upekkhaasahagata</span></p><p>associated with disinterestedness, accompanied by disinterestedness</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upekkhā">upekkhā</a></p>

upenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upeti">upeti</a></p>

upessaṃ|upessa.m|upessam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upessa.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upeti">upeti</a></p>

upeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><ol><li>.(act.) who has approached, reached; having accepted.</li><li>(pass.)<ol><li>reached, accepted.</li><li>accompanied by, furnished with, possessed of</li></ol></li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upeti">upeti</a></p>

upeta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>furnished with, endowed with, possessed of Snp.402, Snp.463, Snp.700, Snp.722; Dhp.10, Dhp.280; Cnd s.v. Thag.789; Pv.i.7#6 (bal˚); Pv.ii.7#12 (phal˚, variant reading preferable ˚upaga), Pv.iv.1#12 (ariyaṃ aṭṭhangavaraṃ upetan = aṭṭhahi angehi upetaṃ yuttaṃ Pv-a.243); Vism.18 (+ sam˚, upagata samupagata etc); Pv-a.7<br />■ <em>Note.</em> The BSk. usually has samanvāgata for upeta (see aṭṭhanga).</p><p>pp. of upeti</p>

upetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upeti">upeti</a></p>

upeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>approaches, comes or goes to, arrives at, reaches; enters into (a state), undergoes.</p>

upeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to go to (with acc.), come to, approach, undergo, attain DN.i.55 (paṭhavi-kāyaṃ an-upeti does not go into an earthly body), DN.i.180; MN.i.486 (na upeti, as answer: “does not meet the question”); SN.iii.93; Iti.89; Snp.209, (na sankhaṃ “cannot be reckoned as”), Snp.749, Snp.911 Snp.1074; Snp.728 (dukkhaṃ), Snp.897; Snp.404 (deve); Mnd.63; Cnd.167; Dhp.151, Dhp.306, Dhp.342; Snp.318; Ja.iv.309 (maraṇaṃ upeti to die), Ja.iv.312 (id.), Ja.iv.463 (id.); Ja.v.212 (variant reading opeti, q.v.) Thag.17 (gabbhaṃ); Pv.ii.3#34 (saggaṃ upehi ṭhānaṃ); Pv.iv.3#52 (saraṇaṃ buddhaṃ dhammaṃ); Ne.66.<br />fut <em>upessaṃ</em> Snp.29; 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. upehisi Dhp.238, Dhp.348<br />ger <em><a href="bword://upecca">upecca</a></em> Vv.33#7; SN.i.209 = Ne.131; Vv-a.146 (realising = upagantvā cetetvā vā); Pv-a.103 (gloss for uppacca flying up); see also <em><a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a></em> &amp; uppacca<br />pp <em><a href="bword://upeta">upeta</a></em>.</p><p>upa + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upecca">upecca</a>, <a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a>, <a href="bword://upeta">upeta</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

upiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>undergoing, going into, metri causa as ūpiya (-˚) and opiya, viz. hadayasmiṃ opiya SN.i.199 = Thag.119; senûpiya Ja.v.96 (variant reading senopiya; C. sayanûpagata). In <em>tadūpiya</em> the 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> part upiya represents an adj. upaka fr. upa (see <em><a href="bword://ta">ta</a></em> I. a), thus found at Mil.9.</p><p>ger. of upeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ta">ta</a></p>

uplavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>swims (up); floats, floats around; leaps up, jumps; is frisky, excited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppalavati">uppalavati</a>, <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a>, <a href="bword://upplavati">upplavati</a></p>

uplavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uppilavati &amp; Uplavati</p><ol><li>to emerge (out of water), to rise, float SN.iv.313 (uplava imper.); Mil.80, Mil.379; Vv-a.47 (uplavitvā, variant reading uppalavitvā); DN-a.i.256 (variant reading upari lavati).</li><li>to jump up frisk about, to be elated or buoyant Ja.ii.97 (cp. Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 139); Mil.370<br />■ See also upaplavati uplāpeti &amp; ubbillāvita etc.</li></ol><p>Sk. utplavati, ud + <em>plu</em>, cp. utplutya jumping up, rising Sp. Avs.i.209</p>

uplāpeti|uplaapeti|uplapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uplaapeti</span></p><p>to immerse MN.i.135 (vv.ll. upal˚ &amp; opil˚); Ja.iv.162 (fig. put into the shade, overpower; variant reading upal˚). See also <em>opilāpeti ubbillāvita</em>.</p><p>Sk. avaplāvayati, Caus. of ava + <em>plu</em>, with substitution of ud for ava; see also <em><a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a></p>

upocita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>heaped up, abounding, comfortable Ja.iv.471.</p><p>pp. of upa + ava + <em><a href="bword://ci">ci</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ci">ci</a></p>

uposatha
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>The name of the Elephant Treasure of the Cakkavatti Mahāsudassana. He was “all white, sevenfold firm (<em>sattappatiṭṭha</em>), wonderful in power, flying through the sky.”. DN.ii.174 MN.iii.173<em>f.</em></p>

uposatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><ol><li>(for non-buddhists) a fastday, a day of purification; (for Buddhists) a day on which lay-followers undertake to observe eight abstinence and on which they listen to teaching on the dhamma (usually the day preceding the nights of the new moon and the full moon, and the night midway between the two, i.e. the eighth, fourteenth and/or fifteenth days of the lunar fortnight; sometimes also the fifth day); the observance of this day; the abstinence undertaken on this day.</li><li>the day on which the <em>saṅgha</em> assembles to confess any faults and to recite the <em>pātimokkha</em> q.v. (usually the fourteenth or fifteenth day of the lunar fortnight); the ceremony or (formal) act on this day.</li></ol>

uposatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>At the time of the rise of Buddhism the word had come to mean the day preceding four stages of the moon’s waxing and waning, viz. 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, 8<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, 15<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 23<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d<!-- raw HTML omitted --> nights of the lunar month that is to say, a weekly sacred day, a Sabbath. These days were utilized by the pre-Buddhistic reforming communities for the expounding of their views, Vin.i.101. The Buddhists adopted this practice and on the 15<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> day of the half-month held a chapter of the Order to expound their dhamma, <em>ib.</em> 102 They also utilized one or other of these Up. days for the recitation of the Pāṭimokkha (pāṭimokkhuddesa), <em>ibid.</em> On Up. days laymen take upon themselves the Up. vows that is to say, the eight Sīlas, during the day. See Sīla The day in the middle of the month is called cātudassiko or paṇṇarasiko according as the month is shorter or longer The reckoning is not by the month (māsa), but by the half-month (pakkha), so the twenty-third day is simply aṭṭhamī, the same as the eighth day. There is an accasional Up. called sāmaggi-uposatho, “reconciliation-Up.”, which is held when a quarrel among the fraternity has been made up, the gen. confession forming as it were a seal to the reconciliation (Vin.v.123; Mah. 42)<br />■ Vin.i.111 Vin.i.112, Vin.i.175, Vin.i.177; Vin.ii.5, Vin.ii.32, Vin.ii.204, Vin.ii.276; Vin.iii.164, Vin.iii.169; DN.iii.60, DN.iii.61, DN.iii.145, DN.iii.147; AN.i.205 sq. (3 uposathas: gopālaka˚ nigaṇṭha˚, ariya˚), AN.i.208 (dhamm˚), AN.i.211 (devatā˚); AN.iv.248 (aṭṭhanga-samannāgata), AN.iv.258 sq. (id.), AN.iv.276, AN.iv.388 (navah angehi upavuttha); AN.v.83; Snp.153 (pannaraso u); Vb.422; Vism.227 (˚sutta = AN.i.206 sq.); Sdhp.439; DN-a.i.139; Snp-a.199; Vv-a.71, Vv-a.109; Pv-a.66, Pv-a.201<br />■ The hall or chapel in the monastery in which the Pāṭimokkha is recited is called <em>uposathaggaṃ</em> (Vin.iii.66), or <em>-āgāraṃ</em> (Vin.i.107; Dhp-a.ii.49). The Up. service is called <em>-kamma</em> (Vin.i.102; Vin.v.142; Ja.i.232; Ja.iii.342, Ja.iii.444; Dhp-a.i.205) <em>uposathaṃ karoti</em> to hold the Up. service (Vin.i.107 Vin.i.175, Vin.i.177; Ja.i.425). Keeping the Sabbath (by laymen is called <em>uposathaṃ upavasati</em> (AN.i.142, AN.i.144, AN.i.205, AN.i.208 AN.iv.248; see <em><a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></em>), or uposathavāsaṃ vasati (verse 177) The ceremony of a layman taking upon himself the eight sīlas is called uposathaṃ samādiyati (see sīlaṃ &amp; samādiyati); uposatha-sīla observance of the Up. (Vv-a.71) The Up. day or Sabbath is also called uposatha-divasa (Ja.iii.52).</p><p>Vedic upavasatha, the eve of the Soma sacrifice, day of preparation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upavasati">upavasati</a></p>

uposathagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>an area in front of the <em>uposatha</em> hall (from where the uposatha ceremony can be heard)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathagāra|uposathagaara|uposathagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uposathagaara</span></p><p>hall for the <em>uposatha</em> ceremony (of the <em>saṅgha</em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>the (formal) act (of the <em>saṅgha</em>) on an <em>uposatha</em> day</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathapamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uposathappamukha">uposathappamukha</a></p><p>hall for the <em>uposatha</em> ceremony (of the <em>saṅgha</em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathappamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uposathappamukha">uposathappamukha</a></p><p>hall for the <em>uposatha</em> ceremony (of the <em>saṅgha</em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathapucchaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>questioning about the (day of the) <em>uposatha</em> ceremony</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathaṅga|uposatha"nga|uposathanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uposatha"nga</span></p><p>one of the eight abstinence (observed by the lay-followers on an <em>uposatha</em> day)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uposathika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><ol><li>(a meal) connected with the <em>uposatha</em> day.</li><li>fasting; observing the uposatha abstinence; keeping the <em>uposatha</em> day.</li></ol>

uposathika
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>belonging to the Uposatha in phrase anuposathikaṃ (adv.) on every U., i.e. every fortnight Vin.iv.315.</li><li>observing the Sabbath fasting (cp. BSk. uposadhika Mvu.ii.9); Vin.i.58; Vin.iv.75, Vin.iv.78; Ja.iii.52; Vism.66 (bhatta); Dhp-a.i.205.</li></ol><p>fr. uposatha</p>

uposathin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = uposathika, fasting Mhvs.17, Mhvs.6.</p><p>fr. upusatha</p>

uposathūpavāsa|uposathuupavaasa|uposathupavasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uposathuupavaasa</span></p><p>observance of the duties of the <em>uposatha</em> day</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uposatha">uposatha</a></p>

uppabbajati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to leave the Order Dhp-a.i.68; Pv-a.55<br />pp <em>-pabbajita</em><br />caus <strong>uppabbājeti</strong> to turn out of the Order Ja.iv.219; Dhp-a.iv.195<br />caus 2 <em>uppabbajāpeti</em> to induce some one to leave the Order Ja.iv.304.</p><p>ud + pabbajati</p><p>See also: uppabbājeti</p>

uppabbajjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who has left the community of bhikkhus, an ex-bhikkhu Vv-a.319; Dhp-a.i.311.</p><p>ud + pabbajita</p>

uppacca FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uppacca</p><p>flying up Thig.248 (see under <em>upacca</em>); SN.i.209 (variant reading BB. upecca, C. uppatitvā pi sakuṇo viya) = Pv.ii.7#17 (= uppatitvā Pv-a.103) = Dhp-a.iv.21 (gloss uppatitvā) = Ne.131 (upecca).</p><p>ger. of uppatati</p>

uppacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Upacca = uppacca</p><p>(q.v.) “flying up” (= uppatitvā Pv-a.103) at Thig.248 (= Thag-a.205, where variant reading and gloss upecca &amp; upacca, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by upanetvā), as well as at Pv.ii.7#17 (= Pv-a.103 where read upaccha; &amp; gloss upacca &amp; upecca).</p>

uppaccati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in ppr. <em>uppacciyamāna</em> (so read for upapacciyamāna, as suggested by variant reading BB. uppajj˚) “being boiled out”, i.e. dried or shrivelled up (cp. uppakka 1) Ja.iv.327. Not with Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 129 “being tormented”, nor with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>under upapacc˚ as ppr. to <em>pṛc</em> (*upapṛcyamāna) “dicht opgesloten”, a meaning foreign to this root.</p><p>ud + paccati, Pass. of <em>pac</em></p>

uppajjamāna|uppajjamaana|uppajjamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppajjamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun coming into existence; birth, rebirth Pv-a.9 (˚vasena), Pv-a.33 (id.).</p><p>fr. uppajjati</p>

uppajjanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective (belonging to) coming into existence, i.e. arising suddenly or without apparent cause, in <em>-bhaṇḍa</em> a treasure trove Ja.iii.150.</p><p>fr. uppajjana</p>

uppajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>arises, comes into being; appears, is produced; becomes available.</p>

uppajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to come out, to arise, to be produced, to be born or reborn, to come into existence DN.i.180; Snp.584; Pv.ii.1#11 (= nibbattati Pv-a.71); Pv-a.8 (nibbattati +), Pv-a.9, Pv-a.20, Pv-a.129 (= pātubhavati); DN-a.i.165.<br />pass <em>uppajjiyati</em> Vin.i.50<br />ppr uppajjanto Pv-a.5 Pv-a.21;<br />fut ˚pajjissati Pv-a.5 (bhummadevesu, corresp. with niraye nibbattissati ibid.), Pv-a.67 (niraye);<br />aor uppajji Pv-a.21, Pv-a.50, Pv-a.66; &amp; udapādi (q.v.) Vin.iii.4; Ja.i.81;<br />ger ˚pajjitvā DN.ii.157 = SN.i.6, SN.i.158 = SN.ii.193 = Ja.i.392 = Thag.1159; &amp; uppajja Ja.iv.24<br />caus <em><a href="bword://upp%C4%81deti">uppādeti</a></em> (q.v.).<br />pp <em><a href="bword://uppanna">uppanna</a></em> (q.v.).<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://upapanna">upapanna</a></em>.</p><p>ud + pajjati of <em>pad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a>, <a href="bword://uppanna">uppanna</a>, <a href="bword://upapajjati">upapajjati</a>, <a href="bword://upapanna">upapanna</a></p>

uppajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppajjitar
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>one who produces or is reborn in (with acc.) DN.i.143 (saggaṃ etc.).</p><p>n. ag. fr. uppajjati</p>

uppajjitvā|uppajjitvaa|uppajjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppajjitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppajjiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is brought into being, is produced; is made available</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppakitaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>indexed at Ud.iii.2 wrongly for <em><a href="bword://upakkitaka">upakkitaka</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakkitaka">upakkitaka</a></p>

uppakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>(as if) boiled or inflamed; (red and) swollen</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upakka">upakka</a></p>

uppakka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>“boiled out”, scorched, seared, dried or shrivelled up; in phrase itthiṃ uppakkaṃ okiliniṃ okiriniṃ Vin.iii.107 = SN.ii.260; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh. Vin.iii.273 as “kharena agginā pakkasarīra”.</li><li>“boiled up”, swollen (of eyes through crying) Ja.vi.10.</li></ol><p>fr. ud + <em>pac</em>, cp. Sk. pakva &amp; see also <em><a href="bword://uppaccati">uppaccati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppaccati">uppaccati</a></p>

uppala
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p></p><p>Blue Water Lily, sometimes also <em><a href="bword://ind%C4%ABvara">indīvara</a></em>, <em>Nymphaea stellata</em> Vv.45.5. The rhizome of this plant grows in the mud of ponds and swamps and its rounded leaves float on the surface. The large flowers have numerous spear-shaped petals spiraling out from the bright yellow stamen and are a beautiful deep blue but sometimes crimson, white or pink. Their perfume was said to last for half a month Ja.vi.536 and a starry night sky is compared to their color J.v.93. The eyes of a beautiful woman were compared with a water lily bud Thi.382. Inhaling the perfume of the water lily was believed to have a curative effect Vin.i.279. Very similar to the blue water lily is the Small White Indian Water Lily, <em>N. esculenta</em>, with its white or sometimes yellow flower Vv.47.1. Water lilies are often incorrectly called and confused with the lotus. See <em>Sogandhika</em>.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Uppala</em></p><p>Rhizome: Powdered and given for dyspepsia, diarrhea, piles, infusion in emollient, diuretic. Leaf: For erysipelas. Flowers: Decoction in narcotic.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://indīvara">indīvara</a>, Sogandhika</p>

uppala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Uppala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A Pacceka Buddha mentioned in the Isigili Sutta. MN.iii.70</p><p>Uppala<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A hell realm. SN.i.152 Snp.p.126</p><p>5hell</p>

uppala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><ol><li>the blue lotus (Nymphaea caerulea); any lotus.</li><li>the name of a hell or place in Avīci (where one suffers for an uppala of years).</li></ol>

uppala
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>the (blue) lotus; a waterlily. The 7 kinds of lotuses, mentioned at Ja.v.37 are: nīla-ratta-set-uppala, ratta-seta-paduma, seta-kumuda kalla-hāra<br />■ DN.i.75; DN.ii.19; Vin.iii.33 (˚gandha); Ja.ii.443; Dhp.55; Vv.32#2; Vv.35#4 ; Pv.ii.1#20; Pv.iii.10#5; Dhp-a.i.384 (nīl˚); Dhp-a.iii.394 (id.); Thag-a.254, Thag-a.255; Vv-a.132, Vv-a.161. What is meant by <em>uppala-patta</em> (lotus-leaf?) at Vin.iv.261?</p><p>Sk. utpala, uncertain etym.</p>

uppalagandhapaccatthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>an enemy who is an “uppalagandha” robber (?)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppala">uppala</a></p>

uppalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the name of a hell or place in Avīci (where one suffers for an uppala(ka) of years).</p>

uppalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>“lotus-like”, Name of a hell (cp. BSk. utpala at Divy.67 etc.) AN.v.173. See also puṇḍarika.</p><p>uppala + ka</p>

uppalasikharopama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>like the bud of the blue lotus</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppala">uppala</a></p>

uppalavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>swims (up); floats, floats around; leaps up, jumps; is frisky, excited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a>, <a href="bword://upplavati">upplavati</a>, <a href="bword://uplavati">uplavati</a></p>

uppalavaṇṇā |uppalava.n.naa |uppalavanna 
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppalava.n.naa </span></p><p>Uppalavaṇṇā</p><p>One of the two chief women disciples of the Buddha, and the chief of the women possessed of psychic power. AN.i.25 The Therīgāthā Thig.234–235 SN.i.131<em>f.</em> contains several verses attributed to her.</p><p>Once Uppalavaṇṇā came across, in Andhavana, some meat left behind, obviously for her, by some kind-hearted thief.; having cooked the meat, she took it to the Buddha at Veḷuvana. Finding him away on his alms-rounds, she left the meat with Udāyi, who was looking after the <em>vihāra</em>, to be given to the Buddha, but Udāyi insisted on Uppalavaṇṇā giving him her inner robe as a reward for his services. Vin.iii.208<em>f.</em></p><p>Mention is made of a pupil of Uppalavaṇṇā, who followed the Buddha for seven years, learning the Vinaya. Vin.ii.261</p><p>The Buddha declares that Khemā and Uppalavaṇṇā are the measure of his women disciples, and that the believing nun, if she would aspire perfectly, should aspire to be like them. AN.i.88 AN.ii.164 SN.ii.236</p>

uppalin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective noun having lotuses rich in l., only in f. <em><a href="bword://uppalin%C4%AB">uppalinī</a></em> a lotus-pond DN.i.75; DN.ii.38; SN.i.138; AN.iii.26; Vv.32#2; DN-a.i.219.</p><p>fr. uppala</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppalinī">uppalinī</a></p>

uppalinī|uppalinii|uppalini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppalinii</span></p><p>multitude or clump of lotuses; a lotus pond.</p>

uppanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>arisen, come into being; appeared; produced, available</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>born, reborn, arisen, produced, DN.i.192 (lokaṃ u. born into the world); Vin.iii.4; Snp.55 (˚ñāṇa; see Cnd.168), Snp.998; Ja.i.99; Pv.ii.2#2 (pettivisayaṃ) Dhs.1035, Dhs.1416; Vb.12, Vb.17, Vb.50, Vb.319; Vb.327; Dhp-a.iii.301; Pv-a.21 (petesu), Pv-a.33, Pv-a.144, Pv-a.155<br />■ <em><a href="bword://anuppanna">anuppanna</a></em> not arisen MN.ii.11; not of good class DN.i.97 (see DN-a.i.267).</p><p>pp. of uppajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuppanna">anuppanna</a></p>

uppata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppatat">uppatat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppatanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppatat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppatat">uppatat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppatati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>flies upwards; jumps up, leaps up; rises.</p>

uppatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to fly or rise up into the air; to spring upwards, jump up; 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sq. pret. <em><a href="bword://udapatta">udapatta</a></em> [Sk *udapaptat] Ja.iii.484 (so read for ˚patto, &amp; change si to pi); ger. <em><a href="bword://uppatitv%C4%81">uppatitvā</a></em> Ja.iii.484; Ja.iv.213; Pv-a.103, Pv-a.215; and <em><a href="bword://uppacca">uppacca</a></em> (q.v.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://uppatita">uppatita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + patati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udapatta">udapatta</a>, <a href="bword://uppatitvā">uppatitvā</a>, <a href="bword://uppacca">uppacca</a>, <a href="bword://uppatita">uppatita</a></p>

uppatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>what is not the path; a path away from the right road; the wrong road, the wrong way.</p>

uppatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i></p><p>wrong road or course DN.i.10 (˚gamana, of planets); SN.i.38, SN.i.43; Ja.v.453; Ja.vi.235); Dhp-a.iii.356 (˚cāra).</p><p>Sk. utpatha, ud + patha</p>

uppatita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>springing up; sprung into being, arisen</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppatita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>jumped up, arisen, come about Snp.1 (= uddhamukhaṃ patitaṃ gataṃ Snp-a.4), Snp.591; Dhp.222 (= uppanna Dhp-a.iii.301); Thag.371.</p><p>pp. of uppatati</p>

uppatitvā|uppatitvaa|uppatitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppatitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppatati">uppatati</a></p>

uppatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>arising, coming into being, birth.</p>

uppatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i></p><p>coming forth, product, genesis, origin, rebirth, occasion AN.ii.133 (˚paṭilābhikāni sanyojanāni); Vb.137 (˚bhava), Vb.411; cp. <em>Compendium</em>, 262 f. (khaṇa); Mil.127 (˚divasa); Vism.571 sq. (˚bhava 9 fold: kāma˚ etc.); Snp-a.46, Snp-a.159, Snp-a.241, Snp-a.254, Snp-a.312, Snp-a.445; Pv-a.144, Pv-a.215. On uppatti deva see <em><a href="bword://deva">deva</a></em> and upapatti. See also aṭṭhuppatti, dānuppatti.</p><p>Vedic utpatti, ud + <em>pad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://deva">deva</a></p>

uppaḷāseti|uppa.laaseti|uppalaseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.laaseti</span></p><p>to sound out or forth, to make sound Mil.21 (dhamma-sankhaṃ). Reading at DN.ii.337 is <em><a href="bword://upa%E1%B8%B7%C4%81seti">upaḷāseti</a></em> in same meaning.</p><p>ud + pra + <em>las</em>, cp. Sk. samullāsayati in same meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upaḷāseti">upaḷāseti</a></p>

uppaṇḍanā|uppa.n.danaa|uppandana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.danaa</span></p><p>ridiculing, mocking Mil.357; Vism.29; Pp-a 250 (˚kathā).</p><p>abstr. fr. ut + <em>paṇd</em> or unknown etym.</p>

uppaṇḍesuṃ|uppa.n.desu.m|uppandesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.desu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppaṇḍeti">uppaṇḍeti</a></p>

uppaṇḍeti|uppa.n.deti|uppandeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.deti</span></p><p>mocks; derides.</p>

uppaṇḍeti|uppa.n.deti|uppandeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.deti</span></p><p>to ridicule, mock, to deride, make fun of Vin.i.216, Vin.i.272, Vin.i.293; Vin.iv.278; AN.iii.91 = Pp.67 (ūhasati ullapati +); Ja.v.288 Ja.v.300; Dhp-a.ii.29; Dhp-a.iii.41; Pv-a.175 (avamaññati +). <em>Note.</em> The BSk. utprāsayati at Divy.17 represents the P uppaṇḍeti &amp; must somehow be a corruption of the latter (vv.ll. at Divy.17 are utprāśayati, utprāṇayati &amp; utprāśrayati).</p><p>ut + <em>paṇḍ</em>, of uncertain origin</p>

uppaṇḍita|uppa.n.dita|uppandita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.dita</span></p><p>mocked; derided</p><p>See also: uppandeti</p>

uppaṇḍiyamāna|uppa.n.diyamaana|uppandiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.diyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppaṇḍeti">uppaṇḍeti</a></p>

uppaṇḍu|uppa.n.du|uppandu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.du</span></p><p>very yellowish; very pale.</p>

uppaṇḍuppaṇḍukajāta|uppa.n.duppa.n.dukajaata|uppanduppandukajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.duppa.n.dukajaata</span></p><p>extremely yellow; extremely pale</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppaṇḍu">uppaṇḍu</a></p>

uppaṇḍuppaṇḍukajāta|uppa.n.duppa.n.dukajaata|uppanduppandukajata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.n.duppa.n.dukajaata</span></p><p>adjective “having become very pale” (?), or “somewhat pale” (?), with dubbaṇṇa in Kp-a.234, and in a stock phrase of three different settings, viz.</p><ol><li>kiso lūkho dubbaṇṇo upp˚ dhamani-santhata-gatto Vin.i.276 Vin.iii.19, Vin.iii.110; MN.ii.121; distorted to BSk. bhīto utp˚. kṛśāluko durbalako mlānako at Divy.334</li><li>kiso upp˚. Ja.vi.71; Dhp-a.iv.66</li><li>upp˚ dhamanisanth˚ Ja.i.346; Ja.ii.92 Ja.v.95; Dhp-a.i.367. Besides in a doubtful passage at Pv.ii.1#12 (upakaṇḍakin, variant reading uppaṇḍ˚ BB.), expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Pv-a.72 “upakaṇḍakajāta”, vv.ll. uppaṇḍaka˚ and uppaṇḍupaṇḍuka˚.</li></ol><p>redupl. intens. formation; ud + paṇḍu + ka + jāta; paṇḍu yellowish. The word is evidently a corruption of something else, perhaps upapaṇṇḍuka upa in meaning of “somewhat like”, cp. upanīla upanibha etc. and reading at Pv.ii.1#13 upakaṇḍakin. The latter may itself be a corruption, but is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Pv-a.72 by upakaṇḍaka-jāta “shrivelled up all over, nothing but pieces (?)”. The trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is thus doubtful; the BSk. is the P. form retranslated into utpāṇḍuka Divy.334, Divy.463, and translated “very pale”</p>

uppaṭipāṭikasutta|uppa.tipaa.tikasutta|uppatipatikasutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.tipaa.tikasutta</span></p><p>“different order sutta” (title).</p>

uppaṭipāṭiyā|uppa.tipaa.tiyaa|uppatipatiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppa.tipaa.tiyaa</span></p><p>lit. “out of reach”, i.e. in a distance Ja.i.89; or impossible Vism.96 (ekapañho pi u. āgato nâhosi not one question was impossible to be understood). As tt. g. “with reference to the preceding”, supra Vism.272; Snp-a.124, Snp-a.128; Dhs-a.135 (T. ˚paṭipāṭika).</p><p>abl. of uppaṭipāṭi, ud + paṭipāṭi</p>

upphāleti|upphaaleti|upphaleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upphaaleti</span></p><p>to cut, rip or split open Vin.i.276 (udara-cchaviṃ upphāletvā; variant reading uppāṭetvā, perhaps preferable).</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>phal</em></p>

upphāletvā|upphaaletvaa|upphaletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upphaaletvaa</span></p><p>splitting, opening up.</p>

upphāsulika|upphaasulika|upphasulika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upphaasulika</span></p><p>adjective “with ribs out”, i.e. with ribs showing emaciated, thin, “skinny” Pv.ii.1#1 (= uggata-phāsuka Pv-a.68); Pv.iv.10#1 (MSS. uppā˚); Thag-a.133 (spelt uppā˚).</p><p>ud + phāsulikā for phāsukikā = phāsuka a rib</p>

uppilavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>swims (up); floats, floats around; leaps up, jumps; is frisky, excited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppalavati">uppalavati</a>, <a href="bword://upplavati">upplavati</a>, <a href="bword://uplavati">uplavati</a></p>

uppilavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uppilavati &amp; Uplavati</p><ol><li>to emerge (out of water), to rise, float SN.iv.313 (uplava imper.); Mil.80, Mil.379; Vv-a.47 (uplavitvā, variant reading uppalavitvā); DN-a.i.256 (variant reading upari lavati).</li><li>to jump up frisk about, to be elated or buoyant Ja.ii.97 (cp. Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 139); Mil.370<br />■ See also upaplavati uplāpeti &amp; ubbillāvita etc.</li></ol><p>Sk. utplavati, ud + <em>plu</em>, cp. utplutya jumping up, rising Sp. Avs.i.209</p>

uppilāvitatta|uppilaavitatta|uppilavitatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppilaavitatta</span></p><p>elation; exhilaration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a></p>

upplavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>at Dhp-a.i.309 remains to be explained, T. faulty.</p>

upplavati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>swims (up); floats, floats around; leaps up, jumps; is frisky, excited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppilavati">uppilavati</a>, <a href="bword://upplavati">upplavati</a>, <a href="bword://uplavati">uplavati</a></p>

uppoṭheti|uppo.theti|uppotheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppo.theti</span></p><p>to beat Pv-a.4.</p><p>ud + poṭheti</p>

uppāda|uppaada|uppada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaada</span></p><p>uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine an unusual or startling event, taken as a portent; theinterpretation of portents</p><p>uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine coming into being, appearance; production.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppāta">uppāta</a></p>

uppāda|uppaada|uppada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaada</span></p><p>Uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>flying up, jump; a sudden &amp; unusual event, portent, omen DN.i.9 (variant reading uppāta) Vism.30 (T. uppāta, variant reading uppāda) Snp.360; Ja.i.374; Ja.vi.475); Mil.178.</p><p>Sk. utpāta, ud + <em>pat</em></p><p>Uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>coming into existence, appearance, birth Vin.i.185; DN.i.185; SN.iii.39 (+ vaya), SN.iv.14; SN.v.30; AN.i.152 (+ vaya), AN.i.286, AN.i.296; AN.ii.248 (taṇh˚), AN.iii.123 (citt˚ state of consciousness); AN.iv.65 (id.); Dhp.182 Dhp.194; Ja.i.59, Ja.i.107 (sat˚); Vb.303 (citt˚), Vb.375 (taṇh˚) Pv-a.10; Thag-a.282<br />■ <em>anuppāda</em> either “not coming into existence” DN.iii.270, MN.i.60; AN.i.286, AN.i.296; AN.ii.214, AN.ii.249 AN.iii.84 sq.; Pts.i.59, Pts.i.66; Dhs.1367; or “not ripe” DN.i.12.</p><p>Sk. utpāda, ud + <em>pad</em></p>

uppādaka|uppaadaka|uppadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadaka</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) producing, generating Pv-a.13 (dukkh˚). f. <em>-ikā</em> Dhp-a.iv.109 (jhān’).</p><p>fr. uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

uppādana|uppaadana|uppadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadana</span></p><p>making, generating, causing Pv-a.71 (anubal˚ read for anubalappadāna?) Pv-a.114.</p><p>fr. uppada<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

uppādaya|uppaadaya|uppadaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadaya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppādayat">uppādayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a></p>

uppādayat|uppaadayat|uppadayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppādayat">uppādayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a></p>

uppādayati|uppaadayati|uppadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadayati</span></p><p>causes to issue forth or appear; produces, generates, brings into being</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a>, <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppādesi|uppaadesi|uppadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a></p>

uppādeta|uppaadeta|uppadeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppādetar">uppādetar</a></p><p>one who brings into being, who produces.</p>

uppādetabba|uppaadetabba|uppadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppādetar|uppaadetar|uppadetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://uppādetar">uppādetar</a></p><p>one who brings into being, who produces.</p>

uppādetar|uppaadetar|uppadetar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadetar</span></p><p>one who produces, causes or brings into existence, creator, producer MN.i.79; SN.i.191; SN.iii.66; SN.v.351; Mil.217.</p><p>n. ag. fr. uppādeti</p>

uppādeti|uppaadeti|uppadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadeti</span></p><p>causes to issue forth or appear; produces, generates, brings into being</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādayati">uppādayati</a>, <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppādeti|uppaadeti|uppadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to give rise to, to produce, put forth, show, evince, make DN.i.135 MN.i.162, MN.i.185; Pp.25; Pv-a.4, Pv-a.16, Pv-a.19, Pv-a.59; Sdhp.539 <em>cittaṃ u</em>. to give a (temporary) thought to (with loc. Ja.i.81; Mil.85; Dhp-a.ii.89; Pv-a.3.</li><li>to get, obtain find Ja.iv.2; Mil.140; Dhp-a.i.90; Pv-a.121.</li><li>in <em>lohitaṃ u</em>. to draw (blood) Mil.214.</li></ol><p>Caus. of uppajjati, ud + <em>pad</em></p>

uppādetvā|uppaadetvaa|uppadetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppādeti">uppādeti</a></p>

uppādin|uppaadin|uppadin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadin</span></p><p>adjective having an origin, arising, bound to arise Dhs.1037, Dhs.1416; Vb.17, Vb.50, Vb.74, Vb.92 and passim; Dhs-a.45.</p><p>fr. uppāda<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

uppādita|uppaadita|uppadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaadita</span></p><p>produced; generated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppajjati">uppajjati</a></p>

uppāta|uppaata|uppata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaata</span></p><p>an unusual or startling event, taken as a portent; the interpretation of portents</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uppāda">uppāda</a></p>

uppāṭaka|uppaa.taka|uppataka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.taka</span></p><p>Fleas are small rust-colored wingless insects of the order Siphonaptera. They feed on blood and have enlarged muscular hind legs adapted for leaping. Fleas can infest human habitation, hiding in bedding and clothing. In one place we read of a blanket being covered with fleas S.i.170. In the famous Sasa Jātaka the hare shook the fleas from its fur before leaping into the fire Ja.iii.55.</p>

uppāṭaka|uppaa.taka|uppataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.taka</span></p><p>an insect.</p>

uppāṭaka|uppaa.taka|uppataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.taka</span></p><p>an insect, vermin SN.i.170 (santhāro ˚ehi sañchanno “a siesta-couch covered by vermin swarm” translated p. 215 &amp; note).</p><p>fr. ud + <em>paṭ</em>; in meaning of “biting, stinging”</p>

uppāṭana|uppaa.tana|uppatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.tana</span></p><p>pulling out, uprooting, destroying, skinning Ja.i.454; Ja.ii.283; Ja.vi.238; Mil.166; Pv-a.46 (kes˚); Sdhp.140 (camm˚). Cp. sam˚.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>paṭ</em>.</p>

uppāṭanaka|uppaa.tanaka|uppatanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.tanaka</span></p><p>adjective pulling up, tearing out, uprooting Ja.i.303 (˚vāta); Ja.iv.333 (id.).</p><p>fr. uppāṭana</p>

uppāṭesi|uppaa.tesi|uppatesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.tesi</span></p><p>tore asunder; rooted out; skinned.</p>

uppāṭeti|uppaa.teti|uppateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.teti</span></p><p>to split, tear asunder; root out, remove, destroy Vin.ii.151 (chaviṃ to skin); MN.ii.110 (attānaṃ); Thig.396 (ger. uppāṭiyā = ˚pāṭetvā Thag-a.259); Ja.i.281 (bījāni), Ja.iv.162, Ja.iv.382; Ja.vi.109 (= luñcati); Mil.86; Dhp-a.iii.206. Caus. <em>uppāṭāpeti</em> in pp. <em>uppāṭāpita</em> caused to be torn off Dhp-a.iii.208. See also <em><a href="bword://upph%C4%81leti">upphāleti</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. utpāṭayati, Caus. uf ud + <em>paṭ</em>; to split, cp. also BSk. utpāṭayati nidhānaṇ to dig out a treasure Avs.i.294</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upphāleti">upphāleti</a></p>

uppāṭetvā|uppaa.tetvaa|uppatetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.tetvaa</span></p><p>having torn asunder; having rooted out; having skinned.</p>

uppāṭiya|uppaa.tiya|uppatiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppaa.tiya</span></p><p>having torn asunder; having rooted out; having skinned.</p>

uppīḷa|uppii.la|uppila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppii.la</span></p><p>adjective oppressing or oppressed: <em>an˚</em>; free from oppression, not hurt or destroyed DN.i.135 (opp sa-uppīḷa; T. upapīḷa but variant reading upp˚); Ja.iii.443; Ja.v.378; Pv-a.161.</p><p>ud + <em>pīḍ</em></p>

uppīḷeti|uppii.leti|uppileti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppii.leti</span></p><ol><li>to press (down) on to, to hold (tight to (with acc.), to cover up or close MN.i.539 (piṭṭhi-pāṇiṃ hanukena); Ja.i.483 (hatthena akkhīni); Ja.ii.245 (hatthikumbhe mukhaṃ); Ja.v.293 (aggalaṃ); Thag-a.188.</li><li>to stampede Vv-a.83 (paṭhaviṃ).</li></ol><p>ud + <em>pīḍ</em> for ava + <em>pīḍ</em>, cp. uplāpeti = opilāpeti, &amp; opīḷeti</p>

uppīḷita|uppii.lita|uppilita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uppii.lita</span></p><p>pressed Ja.vi.3.</p><p>pp. of uppīḷeti</p>

upādahati|upaadahati|upadahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadahati</span></p><p>places upon; lays upon, imposes</p><p>See also: upadaheti</p>

upādaheti|upaadaheti|upadaheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaheti</span></p><p>places upon; lays upon, imposes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upadahati">upadahati</a></p>

upādhi|upaadhi|upadhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadhi</span></p><ol><li>cushion Ja.vi.253.</li><li>supplement, ornament (?), in <em>-ratha</em> “the chariot with the outfit”, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as the royal chariot with the golden slipper Ja.vi.22.</li></ol><p>fr. upa + ā + <em>dhā</em></p>

upādhiya|upaadhiya|upadhiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadhiya</span></p><p>being furnished with a cushion Ja.vi.252 (adj.).</p><p>fr. upāhi</p>

upādi|upaadi|upadi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadi</span></p><p>Upādi˚</p><p>= upādāna, but in more concrete meaning of “stuff of life”, substratum of being, khandha; only in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>-sesa</em> (adj.) having some fuel of life (= khandhas or substratum) left, i.e. still dependent (on existence), not free, materially determined SN.v.129, SN.v.181; AN.iii.143; Iti.40; Vism.509. More frequently neg. <em>an-upādi-sesa</em> (nibbāna, nibbānadhātu or parinibbāna, cp. similarly BSk. anupādi-vimukti Mvu.i.69) completely emancipated, free, without any (material substratum Vin.ii.239 (nibbāna-dhātu); DN.iii.135; MN.i.148 (parinibbāna); AN.ii.120; AN.iv.75 sq., AN.iv.202, AN.iv.313; Ja.i.28, Ja.i.55; Snp.876; Iti.39, Iti.121 (nibbāna-dhātu); Pts.i.101; Vism.509; Dhp-a.iv.108 (nibbāna); Vv-a.164, Vv-a.165. Opp. <strong>saupādisesa</strong> AN.iv.75 sq., AN.iv.378 sq.; Snp.354 (opp. nibbāyi) Vism.509; Ne.92. See further ref. under nibbāna parinibbāna.</p><p>the comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->-from of upādāna, derived fr. upādā in analogy to nouns in ˚a &amp; ˚ā which change their a to i in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em>kṛ &amp; bhū;</em> otherwise a n. formation fr <em>dā</em> analogous to ˚dhi fr. <em>dhā</em> in upadhi</p><p>See also: saupādisesa</p>

upādinna|upaadinna|upadinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadinna</span></p><ol><li>taken hold of, taken for one’s own; grasped; used.</li><li>evolved, derived, esp. evolved by the influence of previous kamma (usually explained by commentaries as meaning (1) animate.</li><li>not holding onto, not grasping</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiṇṇa">upādiṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādisesa|upaadisesa|upadisesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadisesa</span></p><p>remnant of material support or fuel; a residue of grasping.</p>

upādiya|upaadiya|upadiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upādiyat">upādiyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiyamāna|upaadiyamaana|upadiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part.pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiyat|upaadiyat|upadiyat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://upādiyat">upādiyat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiyati|upaadiyati|upadiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyati</span></p><p>appropriates to oneself, takes as one’s own, adopts; lays hold of, grasps; uses; takes as material source, derives, evolves (from).</p>

upādiyati|upaadiyati|upadiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyati</span></p><p>to take hold of, to grasp, cling to, show attachment (to the world), cp. upādāna DN.ii.292; MN.i.56, MN.i.67; SN.ii.14; SN.iii.73, SN.iii.94, SN.iii.135; SN.iv.168 (na kiñci loke u. = parinibbāyati); Snp.752, Snp.1103 Snp.1104; Mnd.444 (= ādeti); Cnd.164. ppr. <em>upādiyaṃ</em> SN.iv.24 = SN.iv.65 (an˚)<br />ppr med. <em>upādiyamāna</em> SN.iii.73; Snp-a.409, &amp; <em>upādiyāna</em> (˚ādiyāno) Snp.470; Dhp.20. ger. <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81d%C4%81ya">upādāya</a></em> in lit. meaning “taking up” Ja.i.30; Mil.184, Mil.338, Mil.341; for specialised meaning &amp; use as prep see separately as also; <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81d%C4%81">upādā</a></em> and <em>upādiyitvā</em> Vv-a.209; DN-a.i.109 (an˚); Dhp-a.iv.194 (an˚)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81di%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">upādiṇṇa</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>upa + ā + <em>dā</em>, see *<a href="bword://%C4%81diyati">ādiyati</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādāya">upādāya</a>, <a href="bword://upādā">upādā</a>, <a href="bword://upādiṇṇa">upādiṇṇa</a>, <a href="bword://ādiyati">ādiyati</a></p>

upādiyitvā|upaadiyitvaa|upadiyitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiyiṃ|upaadiyi.m|upadiyim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadiyi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiṇṇa|upaadi.n.na|upadinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadi.n.na</span></p><ol><li>taken hold of, taken for one’s own; grasped; used.</li><li>evolved, derived, esp. evolved by the influence of previous kamma (usually explained by commentaries as meaning 1.); animate.</li><li>not holding on to, not grasping</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādinna">upādinna</a>, <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādiṇṇa|upaadi.n.na|upadinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadi.n.na</span></p><p>grasped at, laid hold of; or “the issue of grasping”, i.e. material, derived, secondary (cp. upādā), see def. at <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 201, 324<br />■ Dhs.585, Dhs.877, Dhs.1211, Dhs.1534 Vb.2 sq., Vb.326, Vb.433; Vism.349, Vism.451; <em>an˚</em>; Vin.iii.113; Dhs.585, Dhs.991, Dhs.1212, Dhs.1535.</p><p>for ˚ādinna with substitution of ṇṇ for nn owing to wrong derivation as pp. from ādiyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> instead of ādiyati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

upādiṇṇaka|upaadi.n.naka|upadinnaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadi.n.naka</span></p><p>(adj,) = <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81di%E1%B9%87%E1%B9%87a">upādiṇṇa</a></em> Dhs-a.311, Dhs-a.315, Dhs-a.378; Vism.398.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiṇṇa">upādiṇṇa</a></p>

upādā|upaadaa|upada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaa</span></p><p>adverb lit. “taking up”, i.e. subsisting on something else, not original, secondary derived (of rūpa form) Dhs.877, Dhs.960, Dhs.1210; Vism.275 Vism.444 (24 fold); Dhs-a.215, Dhs-a.299, Dhs-a.333, cp. <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 127 197<br />■ Usually (and this is the earlier use of upādā as neg. <em><a href="bword://anup%C4%81d%C4%81">anupādā</a></em> (for anupādāya) in meaning “not taking up any more (fuel, so as to keep the fire of rebirth alive)”, not clinging to love of the world, or the kilesas q.v., having no more tendency to becoming; in phrases <em>a. parinibbānaṃ</em> “unsupported emancipation” MN.i.148; SN.iv.48; SN.v.29; Dhp-a.i.286 etc.; <em>a. vimokkho</em> mental release AN.v.64 (A A: catuhi upādānehi agahetvā cittassa vimokkho; arahattass’etaṃ nāmaṃ); Vin.v.164; Pts.ii.45 sq.; <em>a. vimutto</em> DN.i.17 (= kinci dhammaṃ anupādiyitvā vimutto DN-a.i.109); cp. MN.iii.227 (paritassanā).</p><p>shortened ger. of upādiyati for the usual upādāya in specialised meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādā">anupādā</a></p>

upādāna|upaadaana|upadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaana</span></p><ol><li>taking as one’s own, laying hold of, grasping.</li><li>material support or cause, fuel; (it is often difficult to determine which meaning is intended; both reinforce each other: previous grasping produces fuel, which is itself then grasped).</li></ol>

upādāna|upaadaana|upadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaana</span></p><p>nt.</p><ol><li>(lit.) that (material) substratum by means of which an active process is kept alive or going), fuel, supply, provision; adj. (-˚) supported by, drawing one’s existence from SN.i.69; SN.ii.85 (aggikkhandho ˚assa pariyādānā by means of taking up fuel); SN.v.284 (vāt˚); Ja.iii.342 sa-upādāna (adj.) provided with fuel SN.iv.399; anupādāna without fuel Dhp-a.ii.163.</li><li>(appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) “drawing upon”, grasping, holding on, grip attachment; adj. (-˚) finding one’s support by or in clinging to, taking up, nourished by. See on term <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 323 &amp; <em>Cpd.</em> 171. They are classified as 4 upādānāni or four Graspings viz. kām˚, diṭṭh˚, sīlabbat˚, attavād or the graspings arising from sense-desires, speculation belief in rites, belief in the soul-theory DN.ii.58; DN.iii.230; MN.i.51, MN.i.66; SN.ii.3; SN.v.59; Dhs.1213; Pts.i.129; Pts.ii.46 Pts.ii.47; Vb.375; Ne.48; Vism.569<br />■ For upādāna in var. connections see the foll. passages: DN.i.25; DN.ii.31, DN.ii.33 DN.ii.56; DN.iii.278; MN.i.66, MN.i.136 (attavād˚) MN.i.266; SN.ii.14, SN.ii.17, SN.ii.30 SN.ii.85; SN.iii.10, SN.iii.13 sq., SN.iii.101, SN.iii.135, SN.iii.167, SN.iii.191; SN.iv.32, SN.iv.87 sq. SN.iv.102 (tannissitaṃ viññāṇaṃ tadupādānaṃ), SN.iv.390, SN.iv.400 (taṇhā); AN.iv.69; AN.v.111 (upāy˚); Snp.170, Snp.358, Snp.546; Pts.i.51 sq., Pts.i.193; Pts.ii.45 sq, Pts.ii.113; Vb.18, Vb.30, Vb.67, Vb.79, Vb.119 Vb.132; Dhs.1059, Dhs.1136, Dhs.1213, Dhs.1536 sq.; Ne.28 sq., Ne.41 sq., Ne.114 sq.; Dhp-a.iv.194<br />■ <em>sa˚</em>; full of attachment (to life) MN.i.65; Vin.iii.111; SN.iv.102; <em>an˚</em>; unattached, not showing attachment to existence SN.iv.399; Vin.iii.111 Thag.840; Mil.32; DN-a.i.98.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-kkhandha</em>, usually as pañc’ upādāna-kkhandhā the factors of the “fivefold clinging to existence” [cp. BSk pañc’ u˚-skandhāḥ Avs.ii.168#2 &amp; note] DN.ii.35, DN.ii.301 sq.; DN.iii.223, DN.iii.286; MN.i.61, MN.i.144, MN.i.185; MN.iii.15, MN.iii.30, MN.iii.114, MN.iii.295; Pts.ii.109 sq.; Vb.101; Vism.505 (khandha-pañcaka) See for detail khandha ii.B 2.</li><li><em>-kkhaya</em> extinction or disappearance of attachment SN.ii.54; AN.iii.376 sq.; Snp.475, Snp.743; Iti.75.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nid%C4%81na">nidāna</a></em> the ground of upādāna; adj founded on or caused by attachment Pts.ii.111; Vb.135 sq</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://nirodha">nirodha</a></em> destruction of “grasping” Vin.i.1 (in formula of paṭicca-samuppāda); SN.ii.7; SN.iii.14; AN.i.177.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://paccaya">paccaya</a></em> = ˚nidāna SN.ii.5; SN.iii.94; Snp.507, Snp.742.</li></ul><p>fr. upa + ā + <em>dā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nidāna">nidāna</a>, <a href="bword://nirodha">nirodha</a>, <a href="bword://paccaya">paccaya</a></p>

upādānakkhandha|upaadaanakkhandha|upadanakkhandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaanakkhandha</span></p><p>one of the five groups of the elements of existence connected with grasping (as cause or object) (and being material support or fuel)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādāna">upādāna</a></p>

upādāniya|upaadaaniya|upadaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaaniya</span></p><p>(from upādāna) likely to be taken as one’s own, tending to produce grasping; serving as a support or fuel.</p>

upādāniya|upaadaaniya|upadaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaaniya</span></p><p>adjective belonging to or connected with upādāna, sensual, (inclined to) grasping; material (of rūpa), derived. See on term <em>Dhs trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> 203, 322<br />■ SN.ii.84; SN.iii.47; SN.iv.89, SN.iv.108; Dhs.584, Dhs.1219, Dhs.1538; Vb.12 sq., Vb.30, Vb.56, Vb.119, Vb.125, Vb.319, Vb.326.</p><p>fr. upādāna, for *upādānika → ˚aka</p>

upādāya|upaadaaya|upadaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaaya</span></p><ol><li>taking for oneself taking as one’s own, adopting; making use of, having as material support or cause; being evolved or deriving (from, gen.).</li><li>(postp., + acc. or gen.), with, out of because of; with reference to, in respect of; relative to, in comparison with.</li><li>including; beginning from</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://upādiyati">upādiyati</a></p>

upādāya|upaadaaya|upadaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaadaaya</span></p><p>adverb</p><ol><li><p>(as prep. with acc.) lit. “taking it up” (as such &amp; such), i.e.</p><ol><li>out of, as, for; in phrase anukampaṃ upādāya out of pity or mercy DN.i.204; Pv-a.61, Pv-a.141, Pv-a.164</li><li>compared with, alongside of, with reference to, according to DN.i.205 (kālañ ca samayañ ca acc. to time &amp; convenience); Dhp-a.i.391; Vv-a.65 (paṃsucuṇṇaṃ); Pv-a.268 (manussalokaṃ). The same use of upādāya is found in BSk., e.g. at Divy.25, Divy.359, Divy.413; Avs.i.255.</li></ol></li><li><p>(ic same meaning application as upādā, i.e. in neg. form first &amp; then in positive abstraction from the latter) as philosophical term “hanging on to”, i.e. derived, secondary (with rūpa) Vb.12, Vb.67 etc.; Mnd.266. Usually as <em><a href="bword://anup%C4%81d%C4%81ya">anupādāya</a></em> “not clinging to”, without any (further) clinging (to rebirth) emancipated, unconditioned, free [cp. BSk. paritt-anupādāya free from the world Divy.655], freq. in phrase a nibbuta completely emancipated SN.ii.279; AN.i.162; AN.iv.290; besides in foll. pass.: Vin.i.14 (a. cittaṃ vimuccati) Vin.i.182 (id.); SN.ii.187 sq.; SN.iv.20, SN.iv.107; SN.v.317; Dhp.89 = SN.v.24 (ādānapaṭi-nisagge a. ye ratā); Dhp.414; Snp.363; Iti.94 (+ aparitassato).</p></li></ol><p>ger. of upādiyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupādāya">anupādāya</a></p>

upāgacchati|upaagacchati|upagacchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">upaagacchati</span></p><p>to come to, arrive at, reach, obtain, usually aor. upāgañchi Cp.i.10#10, pl. upāgañchuṃ Snp.1126; or upāgami Snp.426, Snp.685, pl. upāgamuṃ Snp.302, Snp.1126. Besides in pres. imper. upāgaccha Pv-a.64 (so read for upagaccha)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81gata">upāgata</a></em>.</p><p>upa + ā + <em>gam</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāgata">upāgata</a></p>

upāgacchiṃ|upaagacchi.m|upagacchim
