ussajitvā|ussajitvaa|ussajitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussajitvaa</span></p><p>having let go, let loose; having quit, abandoned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussajjitvā">ussajjitvā</a></p>

ussajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to dismiss, set free, take off, hurl AN.iv.191.</p><p>ud + <em>sṛj</em>, cp. BSk. protsṛjati Divy.587</p>

ussajjitvā|ussajjitvaa|ussajjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussajjitvaa</span></p><p>having let go, let loose; having quit, abandoned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussajitvā">ussajitvā</a></p>

ussakkati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>moves up, rises; moves forward, advance; progress</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussukkati">ussukkati</a></p>

ussakkati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ussakkati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to creep out or up to, to rise AN.iii.241 sq.; Mil.260.</p><p>ud + sakkati, see <em><a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></em></p><p>Ussakkati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to endeavour Vism.437; Vv-a.95 (Caus. ii. ussakkāpesi), Vv-a.214.</p><p>by-form of ussukkati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://sakkati">sakkati</a></p>

ussakkitvā|ussakkitvaa|ussakkitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussakkitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussakkati">ussakkati</a></p>

ussanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>having risen, thickened; raised, elevated; piled up, accumulated; plentiful, abundant, prevalent; over plentiful, abundant to excess; predominant; obtruding</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussīdati">ussīdati</a></p>

ussanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>overflowing, heaped up, crowded; extensive, abundant, preponderant, excessive, full of (˚-) Vin.i.285 (cīvaraṃ u. overstocked; Vin.ii.270 (āmisaṃ too abundant); Vin.iii.286 Thig.444 (= upacita Thag-a.271); Ja.i.48, Ja.i.145 ˚kusalamūla); Dhp-a.i.26 (id.); (lobho etc.) Asl.267; Mil.223 (id.); Ja.i.336 (kāla, fulfilled); Ja.iii.418; Ja.iv.140; Pv.iii.5#1 (˚puñña, cp. Pv-a.197); Pv-a.71 (˚pabhā thick glow). Cp <em><a href="bword://accussanna">accussanna</a></em>.</li><li>anointed Vv-a.237.</li><li>spread out wide Dhp-a.ii.67 (mahāpaṭhavī u.), Dhp-a.ii.72 (id.).</li></ol><p>pp. of ud + <em>syad</em>, cp. abhisanna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://accussanna">accussanna</a></p>

ussannatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i></p><p>accumulation, piling up; predominance, greater amount</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussanna">ussanna</a></p>

ussannatā|ussannataa|ussannata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussannataa</span></p><p>accumulation, piling up; predominance, greater amount</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussanna">ussanna</a></p>

ussannatā|ussannataa|ussannata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussannataa</span></p><p>accumulation, fulness, plenty Kv.467 (where <em>Kvu trsl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> p. 275 gives ussadattā); Vv-a.18, Vv-a.19.</p><p>abstr. fr. ussanna</p>

ussarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to run out, run away Ja.i.434 (imper. ussaratha); Ja.v.437<br />pp <em><a href="bword://ussa%E1%B9%ADa">ussaṭa</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81reti">ussāreti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + sarati of <em>sṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussaṭa">ussaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://ussāreti">ussāreti</a></p>

ussava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>celebration, festival, holiday.</p>

ussava
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>feast, making merry, holiday Vin.iii.249; Ja.i.475; Ja.ii.13, Ja.ii.248; Vv-a.7, Vv-a.109 (˚divasa).</p><p>Sk. utsava</p>

ussaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>in <em>-vādika</em> Vin.iv.224 is a variant of <em>usuyya˚</em>; “using envious language, quarrelsome”<br />■ Another ussaya <a href="bword://samussaya">samussaya</a>* only.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://samussaya">samussaya</a></p>

ussayavādikā|ussayavaadikaa|ussayavadika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussayavaadikaa</span></p><p>contentious or violent in speech; argumentative, seeking litigation.</p>

ussayāpeti|ussayaapeti|ussayapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussayaapeti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://udassaye">udassaye</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://udassaye">udassaye</a></p>

ussaṅkha|ussa"nkha|ussankha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkha</span></p><p>adjective with ankles midway (?) in <em>-pāda</em> the 7<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of the characteristics of a Mahāpurisa DN.ii.17; DN.iii.143, DN.iii.154; DA explains: the ankles are not over the heels, but midway in the length of the foot.</p><p>ud + sankha</p>

ussaṅkhapāda|ussa"nkhapaada|ussankhapada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkhapaada</span></p><p>having feet with high arches (one of the 32 characteristics of a mahāpurisa).</p>

ussaṅki|ussa"nki|ussanki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nki</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ussaṅkin">ussaṅkin</a></p><p>fearful, apprehensive; distrustful.</p>

ussaṅkin|ussa"nkin|ussankin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ussaṅkin">ussaṅkin</a></p><p>fearful, apprehensive; distrustful.</p>

ussaṅkin|ussa"nkin|ussankin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkin</span></p><p>adjective distrustful, fearful, anxious Vin.ii.192.</p><p>fr. ud + <em>śaṅk</em></p>

ussaṅkita|ussa"nkita|ussankita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkita</span></p><p>adjective = ussankin AN.iii.128; Dhp-a.iii.485 (+ pari˚; cp. ā˚).</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>śaṅk</em></p>

ussaṅkitaparisaṅkita|ussa"nkitaparisa"nkita|ussankitaparisankita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa"nkitaparisa"nkita</span></p><p>doubted and distrusted.</p>

ussaṭa|ussa.ta|ussata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa.ta</span></p><p>moving away, slipping away; come from, emerged; possibly also: great, high.</p>

ussaṭa|ussa.ta|ussata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussa.ta</span></p><p>run away MN.ii.65.</p><p>pp. of ud + sarati of <em>sṛ</em>; cp. saṭa for *sūta</p>

usseneti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>forms an association (with); is involved</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussineti">ussineti</a></p>

usseneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to draw on to oneself, to be friendly SN.iii.89 (variant reading ussi˚) AN.ii.214 sq. (opp. paṭisseneti); Pts.ii.167 (ussi˚); Kv.i.93 (reading ussineti + visineti). See also paṭiseneti.</p><p>denom. fr. ussena = ussayana, ud + <em>śri</em> (?)</p>

usseti
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to erect, raise, stand up Ja.iv.302; aor. <em>ussesi</em> Ja.vi.203<br />caus <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81peti">ussāpeti</a></em>; pp. <em>ussita &amp; ussāpita;</em> (q.v.).</p><p>ud + <em>śri</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

usseḷeti|usse.leti|usseleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usse.leti</span></p><p>(prob.) whistles.</p>

usseḷheti|usse.lheti|usselheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usse.lheti</span></p><p>? Vin.ii.10 (for ussoḷh˚?); cp. <em>ussoḷhikāya</em>.</p>

ussineti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>forms an association (with); is involved</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usseneti">usseneti</a></p>

ussita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>raised; erected; grown; high, mighty.</p>

ussita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>erected, high SN.v.228; Thag.424 (pannaddhaja); Ja.v.386; Vv.84#15; Vv-a.339. Cp. sam˚.</p><p>Sk. ucchrita, pp. of ud + <em>sri</em>, see <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81peti">ussāpeti</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

ussiñcana|ussi~ncana|ussincana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussi~ncana</span></p><p>drying, baling out, raising water, exhausting Ja.i.417.</p><p>fr. ussiñcati</p>

ussiñcati|ussi~ncati|ussincati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussi~ncati</span></p><p>to bale out, exhaust Ja.i.450, Ja.ii.70; Ja.iv.16; Mil.261.</p><p>ud + <em>sic</em></p>

ussota
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective nt. <em>ussotaṃ</em> as adv. “up-stream” Mil.117.</p><p>ud + sota</p>

ussoḷhi|usso.lhi|ussolhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usso.lhi</span></p><p>persevering exertion; great energy.</p>

ussoḷhi|usso.lhi|ussolhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usso.lhi</span></p><p>exertion MN.i.103; SN.ii.132; SN.v.440; AN.ii.93, AN.ii.195; AN.iii.307; AN.iv.320; AN.v.93 sq. Often comḅ<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81ha">ussāha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>a by-form of ussāha fr. ud + <em>sah</em>, pp. *soḍha dialectical</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāha">ussāha</a></p>

ussoḷhika|usso.lhika|ussolhika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usso.lhika</span></p><p>great energy.</p>

ussoḷhikā|usso.lhikaa|ussolhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usso.lhikaa</span></p><p>belonging to exertion, only in instr. as adv. <em>ussoḷhikāya</em> “in the way of exertion” i.e. ardently, keenly, eagerly SN.i.170 (naccati).</p><p>adj. of ussoḷhi</p>

ussuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>busy, zealously active; striving, eager (for); repining (for).</p>

ussuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>endeavouring, zealous, eager, active SN.i.15 (an˚ inactive) AN.iv.266; Snp.298.</li><li>greedy, longing for Dhp.199 (an˚).</li></ol><p>Sk. utsuka, also BSk. e.g. Jtm.31#68</p>

ussukin
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective greedy, longing; only neg. an˚ Pp.23.</p><p>fr. ussuka</p>

ussukita
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://ussukin">ussukin</a></em>; only neg. <em>an˚</em>; free from greed Vv-a.74.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussukin">ussukin</a></p>

ussukka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>zealous activity, exertion, effort; concern; eagerness.</p>

ussukka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i></p><p>zeal, energy, endeavour hard work, eagerness Vin.i.50; SN.iv.288, SN.iv.291, SN.iv.302; Cnd. s.v. Ne.29; Vv-a.147; Pv-a.5, Pv-a.135; Vism.90 (āpajjati), Vism.644 (˚ppahānaṃ)<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://appossukka">appossukka</a></em>.</p><p>*utsukya fr. ussuka; cp. BSk. utsukya Divy.601 and autsukya Avs.i.85</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appossukka">appossukka</a></p>

ussukkati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>moves up, rises; moves forward, advance; progress</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussakkati">ussakkati</a></p>

ussukkati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to endeavour DN.i.230. - Caus. ii. <strong>ussukkāpeti</strong> to practice eagerly, to indulge in to perform Vv-a.95, Vv-a.98, Vv-a.243. See also <em><a href="bword://ussakkati">ussakkati</a></em>.</p><p>denom. fr. ussukka</p><p>See also: ussukkāpeti, <a href="bword://ussakkati">ussakkati</a></p>

ussukkatā|ussukkataa|ussukkata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussukkataa</span></p><p>= <em><a href="bword://ussukka">ussukka</a></em> Ay. 195.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussukka">ussukka</a></p>

ussussati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr.</i></p><p>3 g. dries up; shrivels up.</p>

ussussati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>to dry up (intrs.) SN.i.126; SN.iii.149 (mahāsamuddo u.); Snp.985; Ja.vi.195.</p><p>ud + sussati of <em>śuṣ</em></p>

ussuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective defiled, lustful (cp. āsava), only neg. <em>an˚</em>; free from defilement Dhp.400. Ussuya, Ussuyaka</p><p>pp. of ud + <em>sru</em>, cp. avassuta</p>

ussuyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Ussuyā, Ussuyaka</p><p>uss.</p>

ussuyā|ussuyaa|ussuya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussuyaa</span></p><p>Ussuyā, Ussuyaka</p><p>uss.</p>

ussāda|ussaada|ussada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaada</span></p><p>raising oneself; rising up (on to); running aground.</p>

ussāda|ussaada|ussada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaada</span></p><p>throwing up on DN-a.i.122.</p><p>fr. ussādeti</p>

ussādana|ussaadana|ussadana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadana</span></p><ol><li>overflowing, piling up, abundance MN.iii.230 (opp. apasādana).</li><li>(probably confused with ussāraṇa) tumult, uproar, confusion AN.iii.91, AN.iii.92 (variant reading ussāraṇa) = Pp.66 (= hatthiassarathâdīnaṃ c’eva balakāyassa ca uccāsadda-mahāsaddo Pp-a.249).</li></ol><p>to ussādeti, cp. ussādita</p>

ussādanā|ussaadanaa|ussadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine \~a, neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadanaa</span></p><ol><li>raising, exalting; praise.</li><li>noise, tumult.</li></ol>

ussādeta|ussaadeta|ussadeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ussādetar">ussādetar</a></p><p>one who exalts, who praises.</p>

ussādetabba|ussaadetabba|ussadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadetabba</span></p><p>to be praise</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussīdati">ussīdati</a></p>

ussādetar|ussaadetar|ussadetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ussādetar">ussādetar</a></p><p>one who exalts, who praises.</p>

ussādeti|ussaadeti|ussadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadeti</span></p><ol><li>raises, lifts (on to); washes ashore; collects, piles up (into a mound).</li><li>raises, exalts; praises</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussīdati">ussīdati</a></p>

ussādeti|ussaadeti|ussadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to dismiss DN.iii.128 [for ussāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->]</li><li>to raise, cause to rise up on, haul up, pile up MN.i.135; MN.iii.230; AN.iv.198, AN.iv.201; Mil.187 Mil.250</li></ol><p>pass <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81diyati">ussādiyati</a></em> (q.v.)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81dita">ussādita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>denom. fr. ussada 1</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādiyati">ussādiyati</a>, <a href="bword://ussādita">ussādita</a></p>

ussādita|ussaadita|ussadita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadita</span></p><p>lifted (on to), beached; piled up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a></p>

ussādita|ussaadita|ussadita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadita</span></p><p>[See ussāpita &amp; ussārita under ussāpeti &amp; ussāreti. There exists in Pāli as well as in BSk. a confusion of different roots to express the notion of raising, rising, lifting unfolding, viz.; <em>sṛ, syad, śri, sad, chad</em>. (See ussada ucchādana, ussādeti, ussāpeti, ussāreti)].</p><p>fr. ussādeti, BSk. ucchrāyita Divy.76, Divy.77, Divy.466</p>

ussādiyati|ussaadiyati|ussadiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadiyati</span></p><ol><li>is too plentiful.</li><li>is removed</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a></p>

ussādiyati|ussaadiyati|ussadiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadiyati</span></p><p>to be in abundance, to be over Vin.ii.167.</p><p>Pass. med. of ussādeti, cp. ussada 4</p>

ussādiyittha|ussaadiyittha|ussadiyittha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadiyittha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a></p>

ussādiyiṃsu|ussaadiyi.msu|ussadiyimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaadiyi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādeti">ussādeti</a></p>

ussāha|ussaaha|ussaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaaha</span></p><p>perseverance, continuous exertion; fortitude; resolution.</p>

ussāha|ussaaha|ussaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaaha</span></p><p>strength, power, energy; endeavour, good-will MN.ii.174; SN.v.440; AN.i.147; AN.ii.93, AN.ii.195; AN.iii.75, AN.iii.307; AN.iv.320; AN.v.93 sq.; Mil.323 Mil.329 (dhiti +) Vism.330; Sdhp.49, Sdhp.223, Sdhp.535, Sdhp.619; Snp-a.50; Dhp-a.iii.394; Pv-a.31, Pv-a.106, Pv-a.166; Vv-a.32, Vv-a.48<br />■ In exegetical literature often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with the quâsi synonym <em><a href="bword://usso%E1%B8%B7hi">ussoḷhi</a></em> e.g. at Cnd. s.v.; Dhs.13, Dhs.22, Dhs.289, Dhs.571.</p><p>Sk. utsāha &amp; utsaha, see <em><a href="bword://ussahati">ussahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussoḷhi">ussoḷhi</a>, <a href="bword://ussahati">ussahati</a></p>

ussāhana|ussaahana|ussahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaahana</span></p><p>exertion, resolution.</p>

ussāhana|ussaahana|ussahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaahana</span></p><p>= ussāha Ne.8.</p><p>fr. ussahati cp. BSk. utsahana Divy.490</p>

ussāhita|ussaahita|ussahita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaahita</span></p><p>moved to action; encouraged; instigated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussahati">ussahati</a></p>

ussāhita|ussaahita|ussahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaahita</span></p><p>determined, incited, encouraged, urged Ja.i.329; Vv-a.109; Pv-a.201 Cp. sam˚.</p><p>pp. of ussāheti, Caus. of ussahati</p>

ussāpana|ussaapana|ussapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapana</span></p><p>raising; erecting.</p>

ussāpana|ussaapana|ussapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapana</span></p><p>lifting up, raising, erecting, unfolding (of a flag or banner) AN.iv.41; Cnd.503 (dhamma-dhajassa).</p><p>fr. ussāpeti</p>

ussāpayati|ussaapayati|ussapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapayati</span></p><p>raises; erects; lifts up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

ussāpenta|ussaapenta|ussapenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

ussāpesi|ussaapesi|ussapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

ussāpeti|ussaapeti|ussapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapeti</span></p><p>raises; erects; lifts up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpayati">ussāpayati</a></p>

ussāpeti|ussaapeti|ussapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapeti</span></p><p>to lift up, erect, raise, exalt Vin.ii.195; AN.iv.43; Ja.ii.219; Ja.iv.16; Ja.v.95 (chattaṃ); Pv-a.75 (id.); Mil.21; Dhp-a.i.3; Dhp-a.iii.118 (kaṭṭhāni)<br />pp <em>ussāpita &amp; ussita;</em> (q.v.). See also <em><a href="bword://usseti">usseti</a></em>.</p><p>Caus. of ud + <em>śri</em>, cp. BSk. ucchrāpayati Av. SN.i.384, SN.i.386, SN.i.387; SN.ii.2</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usseti">usseti</a></p>

ussāpetvā|ussaapetvaa|ussapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāpeti">ussāpeti</a></p>

ussāpita|ussaapita|ussapita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaapita</span></p><p>lifted, raised, unfurled Mil.328 (dhamma-dhaja); Ja.ii.219.</p><p>pp. of ussāpeti, cp. ussādita</p>

ussārayati|ussaarayati|ussarayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaarayati</span></p><ol><li>expels; sends away, dismisses.</li><li>raises, lifts; moves</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāreti">ussāreti</a></p>

ussāraṇa|ussaara.na|ussarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaara.na</span></p><p>procession, going or running about, tumult Dhp-a.ii.7 (so read for ossāraṇā). Cp. <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81dana">ussādana</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ussāreti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussādana">ussādana</a></p>

ussāreti|ussaareti|ussareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaareti</span></p><ol><li>expels; sends away, dismisses.</li><li>raises, lifts; moves</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussārayati">ussārayati</a></p>

ussāreti|ussaareti|ussareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaareti</span></p><p>Ussāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to cause to move back, to cause to go away or to recede Vin.i.32, Vin.i.46 (here a student, when folding up his master’s robe, has to make the corners move back a hand’s breadth each time. Then the crease or fold will change and not tend to wear through), Vin.i.276; Vin.ii.237 (here the reading ussādeti may be preferred); Ja.i.419; Ja.iv.349; Ja.v.347<br />caus 2 <em>ussārāpeti</em> Ja.ii.290.</p><p>Caus. of ussarati</p><p>Ussāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to cause to raise aloft (of a flag), to lift Ja.v.319 (= ussāpeti)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://uss%C4%81rita">ussārita</a></em>.</p><p>= ussādeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussārita">ussārita</a></p>

ussāretvā|ussaaretvaa|ussaretva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaaretvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāreti">ussāreti</a></p>

ussārita|ussaarita|ussarita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaarita</span></p><p>raised, lifted; moved</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāreti">ussāreti</a></p>

ussārita|ussaarita|ussarita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaarita</span></p><p>lifted out or up Vism.63 (samuddavīcīhi thale ussārita; variant reading ussādita).</p><p>pp. of ussāreti<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ussāsa|ussaasa|ussasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaasa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://niruss%C4%81sa">nirussāsa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirussāsa">nirussāsa</a></p>

ussāva|ussaava|ussava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaava</span></p><p>dew; hoarfrost.</p>

ussāva|ussaava|ussava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaava</span></p><p>Ussāva<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>hoarfrost, dew DN.ii.19; Ja.iv.120; Ja.v.417; <em>-bindu</em> a dew drop AN.iv.137; Pv.iv.1#5; Snp-a.458; in comparisons: Vism.231, Vism.633.</p><p>either = Sk. avaśyāya, or to ud + <em>sru</em></p><p>Ussāva<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>outflow, taint, stain (cp. āsava) Dhp-a.iv.165 (taṇhā˚; variant reading ussada, to ussada 6).</p><p>fr. ud + <em>sru</em></p>

ussāvabindu|ussaavabindu|ussavabindu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaavabindu</span></p><p>dewdrop, a drop of dew.</p>

ussāvana|ussaavana|ussavana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaavana</span></p><p>proclaiming, declaring aloud.</p>

ussāvana|ussaavana|ussavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaavana</span></p><p>proclamation (of a building as legal store house); in <em>-antika</em> within the proclaimed limit Vin.i.239.</p><p>= ussāpana</p>

ussāvanantikā|ussaavanantikaa|ussavanantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussaavanantikaa</span></p><p>(a place for storage, <em>kappiyabhūmi</em> or <em>kappiyakuṭi</em>) authorized by a declaration (while it is being built), whose erection is coterminous with a declaration</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussāvana">ussāvana</a></p>

ussīdati|ussiidati|ussidati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussiidati</span></p><p>raises oneself; rises up (on to); runs aground.</p>

ussīsaka|ussiisaka|ussisaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussiisaka</span></p><p>the head of a bed, a pillow for the head Ja.i.266; Ja.ii.410, Ja.ii.443; Ja.iv.154; Ja.v.99; Ja.vi.32 Ja.vi.37, Ja.vi.56; Dhp-a.i.184 (˚passe, opp. pāda-passe).</p><p>ud + sīsa + ka</p>

ussūra|ussuura|ussura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussuura</span></p><p>the time when the sun is high; the afternoon.</p>

ussūra|ussuura|ussura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussuura</span></p><p>adjective “sun-out”, the sun being out; i.e. after sunrise or after noon, adverbially in <em>-bhatta</em> eating after mid-day, unpunctual meals AN.iii.260, and <em>-seyyā</em> sleep after sunrise, sleeping late DN.iii.184; Dhp-a.ii.227. Besides as loc. adv. <em>ussūre</em> the sun having been up (for a long time), i.e. at evening Vin.i.293; Vin.iv.77; Ja.ii.286, also in ati-ussūre too long after sunrise Vv-a.65; Dhp-a.iii.305.</p><p>ut + sūra</p>

ussūrabhatta|ussuurabhatta|ussurabhatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussuurabhatta</span></p><p>whose meal is late in the day</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussūra">ussūra</a></p>

ussūraseyyā|ussuuraseyyaa|ussuraseyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.ā</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">ussuuraseyyaa</span></p><p>lying late in bed, lying in bed when the sun is up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ussūra">ussūra</a></p>

usu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine feminine</i></p><p>an arrow.</p>

usu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; feminine</i></p><p>an arrow Vin.iii.106 (˚loma); DN.i.9; MN.i.86; MN.iii.133; SN.i.127; AN.ii.117; AN.iii.162; Ja.iv.416 Ja.vi.79, Ja.vi.248, Ja.vi.454; Mil.331, Mil.339; Snp-a.466; Pv-a.155.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra">kāra</a></em> an arrow-maker, fletcher MN.ii.105; Dhp.80, Dhp.145 Thag.29; Ja.ii.275; Ja.vi.66; Dhp-a.i.288.</li></ul><p>Sk. iṣu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kāra">kāra</a></p>

usukāra|usukaara|usukara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usukaara</span></p><p>arrow maker, a fletcher</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usu">usu</a></p>

usuloma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>with arrows for hair</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usu">usu</a></p>

usumā|usumaa|usuma
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usumaa</span></p><p>heat Ja.i.31 (= uṇha Ja.iii.55), Ja.i.243; Ja.ii.433; Vism.172 (usuma-vaṭṭi-sadisa); DN-a.i.186; Dhp-a.i.225; Dhp-a.ii.20.</p><p>the diaeretic form of Sk. uṣman, of which the direct equivalent is P. usmā (q.v.)</p>

usuyyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i></p><p>(-ikā)n. discontented, envious</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usūyaka">usūyaka</a></p>

usuyyaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective envious, jealous Vin.ii.190; Snp.318, Snp.325; Ja.ii.192 (variant reading asuyy˚); Ja.v.114<br />■ <em>Note.</em> The long vowel form <em><a href="bword://us%C5%AByaka">usūyaka</a></em> occurs in cpd. <em><a href="bword://abbhus%C5%AByaka">abbhusūyaka</a></em> (q.v.). Spelling ussuyikā occurs at Vv.33#21 (see Vv-a.147). Usuyyati &amp; Usuyati;</p><p>fr. usuyyā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usūyaka">usūyaka</a>, <a href="bword://abbhusūyaka">abbhusūyaka</a></p>

usuyyanā|usuyyanaa|usuyyana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuyyanaa</span></p><p><em>Usuyyitatta</em> (nt.) are exegetical abstr. formations of <em>usuyyā</em> (q.v.). Dhs.1121; Pp.19. Usuyya &amp; Usuya</p>

usuyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i></p><p>is discontented, resentful (at the merits or achievements of another); is jealous, is envious (of usually gen./dat.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usūyati">usūyati</a></p>

usuyyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Usuyyati &amp; Usūyati</p><p>to be jealous or envious, to envy (with acc.) Vin.i.242; Ja.iii.27 (ppr. an-usuyyaṃ); Pv.ii.3#20 (maṃ usūyasi = mayhaṃ issaṃ karosi Pv-a.87).</p><p>Sk. asūyati; fr. usuyā envy</p>

usuyyā|usuyyaa|usuyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuyyaa</span></p><p>discontent (at the merits or achievements of another); envy, jealousy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usūyā">usūyā</a></p>

usuyyā|usuyyaa|usuyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuyyaa</span></p><p>Usuyyā &amp; Usūyā</p><p>feminine envy, jealousy, detraction SN.i.127 (ū); Snp.245 (u); Ja.ii.193 (ū); Ja.iii.99 (ū; variant reading ussuyyā); Mil.402 (ū); Dhs.1121 (u); Vv-a.71 (u) Snp-a.332 (u).</p><p>Sk. asūyā</p>

usā|usaa|usa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usaa</span></p><p>(a certain) food Ja.vi.80.</p><p>doubtful</p>

usīra|usiira|usira
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usiira</span></p><p>See <em>Bīraṇa</em>.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Usīra</em></p><p>Rhizome: Chewed for cough, cold, as a bitter tonic, emetic, antispasmodic, carminative, promotes flow of bronchial secretion, useful in asthma, diarrhea and dysentery, oil used as nerve stimulant, sedative and analgesic. Stem: For cough and cold. Whole plant: Sedative, analgesic, depressant for blood pressure, respiration, rubbed on body to relieve aches.</p><p>See also: Bīraṇa</p>

usīra|usiira|usira
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usiira</span></p><p>fragrant root of andropogon muricatus.</p>

usīra|usiira|usira
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usiira</span></p><p>the fragrant root of Andropogon Muricatum (cp. bīraṇa) Vin.i.201; Vin.ii.130 (˚mayā vijanī) SN.ii.88 (˚nāḷi); AN.ii.199 (id.); Dhp.337; Ja.v.39; Thag.402 (˚attho).</p><p>Sk. uśīra</p>

usīraddhaja|usiiraddhaja|usiraddhaja
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usiiraddhaja</span></p><p>A mountain range forming the northern boundary of Majjhimadesa. Vin.i.197</p><p>???3hill</p>

usīranāḷa|usiiranaa.la|usiranala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usiiranaa.la</span></p><p>~i, feminine an usīra stalk or fiber</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usīra">usīra</a></p>

usūyaka|usuuyaka|usuyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuuyaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. discontented, envious</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usuyyaka">usuyyaka</a></p>

usūyati|usuuyati|usuyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuuyati</span></p><p>is discontented, resentful (at the merits or achievements of another); is jealous, is envious (of usually gen./dat.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usuyyati">usuyyati</a></p>

usūyati|usuuyati|usuyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuuyati</span></p><p>Usuyyati &amp; Usūyati</p><p>to be jealous or envious, to envy (with acc.) Vin.i.242; Ja.iii.27 (ppr. an-usuyyaṃ); Pv.ii.3#20 (maṃ usūyasi = mayhaṃ issaṃ karosi Pv-a.87).</p><p>Sk. asūyati; fr. usuyā envy</p>

usūyā|usuuyaa|usuya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuuyaa</span></p><p>discontent (at the merits or achievements of another); envy, jealousy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usuyyā">usuyyā</a></p>

usūyā|usuuyaa|usuya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">usuuyaa</span></p><p>Usuyyā &amp; Usūyā</p><p>feminine envy, jealousy, detraction SN.i.127 (ū); Snp.245 (u); Ja.ii.193 (ū); Ja.iii.99 (ū; variant reading ussuyyā); Mil.402 (ū); Dhs.1121 (u); Vv-a.71 (u) Snp-a.332 (u).</p><p>Sk. asūyā</p>

utaṇḍa|uta.n.da|utanda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uta.n.da</span></p><p>Also written as: Uttaṇḍa</p><p>see <em><a href="bword://udda%E1%B9%87%E1%B8%8Da">uddaṇḍa</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uddaṇḍa">uddaṇḍa</a></p>

utrasta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i></p><p>trembling; fearful; frightened</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttasati">uttasati</a></p>

utrasta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>frightened, terrified, alarmed Vin.ii.184; SN.i.53, SN.i.54 (an˚); Snp.986; Mil.23; Dhp-a.ii.6 (˚mānasa); Pv-a.243 (˚citta), Pv-a.250 (˚sabhāva).</p><p>pp. of uttasati, also cp. uttasta</p>

utrāsa|utraasa|utrasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">utraasa</span></p><p>trembling; fear; dread</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttāsa">uttāsa</a></p>

utrāsa|utraasa|utrasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">utraasa</span></p><p>terror Ja.ii.8 (citt˚).</p><p>= uttāsa</p>

utrāsi|utraasi|utrasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">utraasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://utrāsin">utrāsin</a></p><p>(from uttāsa) fearful, very afraid.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttāsin">uttāsin</a></p>

utrāsin|utraasin|utrasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">utraasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://utrāsin">utrāsin</a></p><p>(from uttāsa) fearful, very afraid.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttāsin">uttāsin</a></p>

utrāsin|utraasin|utrasin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">utraasin</span></p><p>adjective terrified, frightened, fearful, anxious SN.i.99, SN.i.219<br />■ Usually neg. <em>an˚</em> in phrase abhīru anutrāsin apalāyin without fear, steadfast &amp; not running away SN.i.99; Thag.864; Cnd.13; Ja.iv.296; Ja.v.4; Mil.339. See also apalāyin.</p><p>fr. *Sk. uttrāsa = P. uttāsa</p>

utta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>speaking badly or spoken of badly i.e. of bad repute AN.ii.117, AN.ii.143; AN.iii.163; Kp.viii.2 Kp-a.218.</p><p>pp. of <em>vac</em>, Sk. ukta; for which the usual form is <em><a href="bword://vutta">vutta</a></em> only as <em>dur˚</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vutta">vutta</a></p>

uttama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>uppermost; highest; best; principal; most excellent; most important; utmost; last.</p>

uttama
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>adjective “ut-most”, highest, greatest, best Snp.1054 (dhammaṃ uttamaṃ the highest ideal = Nibbāna for which seṭṭhan Snp.1064; cp. Cnd.317); Dhp.56; Mnd.211; Cnd.502 (in paraphrase of mahā comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with pavara) Kp-a.124; Dhp-a.i.430: Pv-a.1, Pv-a.50<br />■ dum-uttama a splendid tree Vv.39#3; nar˚ the best of men Snp.1021 (narāsabha of Snp.996); pur˚ the most magnificent town Snp.1012; puris˚ the noblest man Thag.629, Thag.1084; nt. <em>uttamaṃ</em> the highest ideal, i.e. Arahantship Ja.i.96.</p><ul><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%85ga">aṅga</a></em> the best or most important limb or part of the body, viz.</p><ol><li>the head Vin.ii.256 = MN.i.32 = AN.iv.278 (in phrase uttamange sirasmiṃ); Ja.ii.163; also in cpd <em>˚bhūta</em> the hair of the head Thig.253 (= kesa-kalāpa Thag-a.209, Thag-a.210) &amp; <em>˚ruha</em> id. Ja.i.138 = Ja.vi.96 (= kesā C.)</li><li>the eye Ja.iv.403.</li><li>the penis Ja.v.197.</li></ol></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://attha">attha</a></em> the highest gain or good (i.e. Arahantship Snp-a.332) Snp.324; Dhp.386, Dhp.403; Dhp-a.iv.142; Thag-a.160.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://adhama">adhama</a></em> most contemptible Ja.v.394, Ja.v.437.</p></li><li><p><em>-guṇā</em> (pl.) loftiest virtues Ja.i.96.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://purisa">purisa</a></em> Iti.97 &amp; <em>-<a href="bword://porisa">porisa</a></em> the greatest man (= mahāpurisa) Dhp.97 (see Dhp-a.ii.188).</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> the highest condition, state or place Dhp-a.ii.188 (˚ṃ patto = puris’ uttamo).</p></li></ul><p>superl. of ud˚, to which compar. is uttara. See etym. under ud˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://aṅga">aṅga</a>, <a href="bword://attha">attha</a>, <a href="bword://adhama">adhama</a>, <a href="bword://purisa">purisa</a>, <a href="bword://porisa">porisa</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a></p>

uttamakulīna|uttamakuliina|uttamakulina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttamakuliina</span></p><p>excellent, most noble family</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></p>

uttamaporisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>most excellent man</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></p>

uttamapurisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i></p><p>most excellent man</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></p>

uttamattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i></p><p>the supreme goal; the highest good</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></p>

uttamatā|uttamataa|uttamata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttamataa</span></p><p>highest amount, climax, limit DN-a.i.169 (for paramatā).</p><p>abstr. fr. uttama</p>

uttamaṅga|uttama"nga|uttamanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttama"nga</span></p><p>the highest or chief part of the body: the head</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttama">uttama</a></p>

uttamā|uttamaa|uttama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttamaa</span></p><p>Uttamā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A bhikkhunī whose verses in the Therīgāthā tell of her repeated struggles before realizing awakening. Thig.42–44</p><p>Uttamā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A bhikkhunī whose verses in the Therīgāthā tell of how, as a daughter of the Buddha, she has developed the seven awakening-factors and attained Nibbāna. Thig.45–47</p>

uttanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>breathing with difficulty, fainting.</p>

uttanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>frightened, faint Vin.iii.84. See <em>uttasta &amp; utrasta</em>.</p><p>= utrasta, is reading correct?</p>

uttara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk whose verses are recorded in the Theragāthā. Thag.121–122</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A monk whose verse are recorded in the Theragāthā. Thag.161–162</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A <em>devaputta</em> who visits the Buddha at the Añjanavana in Sāketa. He utters a stanza, and the Buddha, in another stanza, amplifies what he has said. SN.i.54</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>At the time of the Vajjian heresy that prompted the Second Council, he was the attendant of the Elder Revata and had been twenty years in the Order. The Vajjians of Vesāli went to him and, after much persuasion, succeeded in getting him to accept one robe from them.</p><p>In return for this he agreed to say before the Saṅgha that the Pācīnaka Bhikkhus held the true Doctrine and that the Pātheyyaka monks did not. Thereafter Uttara went to Revata, but Revata, on hearing what he had done, instantly dismissed him from attendance upon him. When the Vesāli monks were informed of the occurrence, they took the nissaya from Uttara and became his pupils. Vin.ii.302–303</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->5<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A brahmin youth, pupil of Pārāsariya. He once visited the Buddha at Kajaligalā in the Mukheluvana and the Buddha preached to him the Indriya-bhāvanā Sutta. MN.iii.298<em>ff.</em></p><p>Perhaps it is this same student that is mentioned in the Pāyāsi Sutta. When Pāyāsi Rājañña was converted by Kumāra Kassapa, he instituted almsgiving to all and sundry, but the gifts he gave consisted of such things as gruel and scraps of food and coarse robes. Uttara, who was one of his retainers, spoke sarcastically of Pāyāsi’s generosity, and on being challenged by Pāyāsi to show what should be done, Uttara gave gladly and with his own hands excellent foods and garments. As a result, after death, while Pāyāsi was born only in the empty Serisakavimāna of the Cātummahārājika world, Uttara was born in Tāvatiṃsā. DN.ii.354–357</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->6<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A pupil of Brahmāyu. He was sent by his teacher from Mithilā to Videha, to find out if the Buddha bore the marks of the Super man. Having made sure of the presence of all the thirty-two marks on the Buddha’s person, he dogged the Buddha’s footsteps for seven months, in order to observe his carriage in his every posture. At the end of that period, he returned to Brahmāyu and reported what he had seen. MN.ii.134<em>ff.</em></p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->7<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>One of the chief disciples of Vessabhu Buddha. DN.ii.4</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->8<!-- raw HTML omitted -->Uttaraka</p><p>A township of the Koliyans. Once, when the Buddha was staying there, he was visited by the headman Pāṭaliya. SN.iv.340</p><p>27.35469,83.564753town</p>

uttara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i></p><p>northern.</p>

uttara
<p>[PTS]</p><p></p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>higher, high, superior, upper, only in compounds, Ja.ii.420 (musal˚ with the club on top of him? Cy not clear, perhaps to uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->); see also below.</li><li>northern (with disā region or point of compass) DN.i.153; MN.i.123; SN.i.224; Pv-a.75. uttarāmukha (for uttaraṃmukha) turning north, facing north Snp.1010.</li><li>subsequent, following, second (˚-) Ja.i.63 (˚āsāḷha-nakkhatta).</li><li>over, beyond (-˚): aṭṭh’utara-sata eight over a hundred i.e. 108; Dhp-a.i.388</li></ol><p>■ <em>sa-uttara</em> having something above or higher, having a superior i.e. inferior DN.i.80 (citta), DN.ii.299; MN.i.59; SN.v.265; Vb.324 (paññā); Dhs.1292, Dhs.1596; Dhs-a.50<br />■ <em><a href="bword://anuttara">anuttara</a></em> without a superior, unrivalled, unparalleled DN.i.40; SN.i.124; SN.ii.278; SN.iii.84; Snp.179. See also under <em><a href="bword://anuttara">anuttara</a></em>.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://atthara%E1%B9%87a">attharaṇa</a></em> upper cover Ja.vi.253.</li><li><em>-ābhimukha</em> facing North DN.ii.15.</li><li><em>-āsaṅga</em> an upper robe Vin.i.289; Vin.ii.126; SN.i.81; SN.iv.290; AN.i.67, AN.i.145; AN.ii.146; Dhp-a.i.218; Pv-a.73; Vv-a.33 = Vv-a.51.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://itara">itara</a></em> something higher, superior DN.i.45, DN.i.156, DN.i.174; SN.i.81; Ja.i.364; Dhp-a.ii.60; Dhp-a.iv.4</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://o%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">oṭṭha</a></em> the upper lip (opp. adhar˚) Ja.ii.420; Ja.iii.26; Ja.iv.184.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://chada">chada</a></em> a cover, coverlet, awning (sa˚ a carpet with awnings or canopy above it) DN.i.7; AN.i.181; AN.iii.50</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://chadana">chadana</a></em> = ˚chada DN.ii.187; Dhp-a.i.87.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dv%C4%81ra">dvāra</a></em> the northern gate Ja.vi.364.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> the higher norm of the world (lok˚), higher righteousness DN.ii.188 (paṭividdha-lok’uttara-dhammatāya uttama-bhāvaṃ patta).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://atthara%E1%B9%87a">attharaṇa</a></em> upper cover Ja.vi.253.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pubba">pubba</a></em> north-eastern Ja.vi.518.</li><li><em>-sse</em> (variant reading ˚suve) on the day after tomorrow AN.i.240.</li></ul><p>compar. of ud˚, q.v. for etym.; the superl. is uttama</p><p>Uttara<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective crossing over, to be crossed, in <em>dur˚</em>; difficult to cross or to get out of SN.i.197 (not duruttamo); Mil.158; and in cpd. <em>-setu</em> one who is going to cross a bridge Mil.194 (cp. uttara-setu).</p><p>fr. uttarati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuttara">anuttara</a>, <a href="bword://anuttara">anuttara</a>, <a href="bword://attharaṇa">attharaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://itara">itara</a>, <a href="bword://oṭṭha">oṭṭha</a>, <a href="bword://chada">chada</a>, <a href="bword://chadana">chadana</a>, <a href="bword://dvāra">dvāra</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://attharaṇa">attharaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://pubba">pubba</a></p>

uttarakuru
<p>[DPPN]</p><p></p><p>A mythical region. A detailed description of it is given in the Āṭānāṭiya Sutta. DN.iii.199<em>ff.</em> The men who live there own no property nor have they wives of their own; they do not have to work for their living. The corn ripens by itself and sweet-scented rice is found boiling on hot oven-stoves. The inhabitants go about riding on cows, on men and women, on maids and youths. Their king rides on an elephant, on a horse, on celestial cars and in state palanquins. Their cities are built in the air, and among those mentioned are Āṭānāṭā, Kusināṭā, Nātapuriyā, Parakusināṭā, Kapīvanta, Janogha, Navanavatiya, Ambara-Ambaravatiya and Āḷakamandā, the last being the chief city.</p><p>The king of Uttarakuru is Kuvera, also called Vessavaṇa.</p><p>The country is to the north of Jambudīpa. It is one of the four Mahādīpā—the others being Aparagoyāna, Pubbavideha and Jambudīpa—each being surrounded by five hundred minor islands. AN.i.227 AN.v.59 These four make up a Cakkavāla, with Mount Meru in their midst, a flat-world system. A cakkavattī’s rule extends over all these four continents. DN.ii.173</p><p>The trees in Uttarakuru bear perpetual fruit and foliage, and it also possesses a Kapparukkha which lasts for a whole kappa. AN.i.264</p><p>The Buddha is said to have travelled with his psychic powers to Uttarakuru for alms. Vin.i.27–28</p><p>5region</p>

uttarakurukā|uttarakurukaa|uttarakuruka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttarakurukaa</span></p><p>The inhabitants of Uttarakuru. AN.iv.396</p>

uttarakā|uttarakaa|uttaraka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttarakaa</span></p><p>Uttarakā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A village of the Thūlūs. The Buddha once stayed there and Sunakkhatta was in his company. At that time Korakkhattiya was also staying there. DN.iii.6</p><p>4town</p>

uttaramātā|uttaramaataa|uttaramata
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttaramaataa</span></p><p>A Yakkhinī, mother of Punabbasu and Uttarā. Once as she passed Jetavana at sunset looking for food, with her daughter on her hip and holding her son by his finger, she saw the assembly, intently listening to the Buddha’s sermon. She, too, hoping to get some benefit, listened quietly and with great earnestness, hushing her children to quietness. The Buddha preached in such a manner that both she and her son could understand, and at the end of the sermon they both became Sotāpannā. She immediately got rid of her sad Yakkha-state and obtained heavenly bliss, and took up her residence in a tree near the Buddha’s Fragrant Chamber. Little Uttarā was too young to realise the Truth. SN.i.210</p>

uttaranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttarati">uttarati</a></p>

uttarapāla|uttarapaala|uttarapala
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uttarapaala</span></p><p>A monk whose Theragāthā verse tell of how he overcame his desire and confusion and attained arahantship. Thag.252–254</p>

uttarapāsaka|uttarapaasaka|uttarapasaka
