<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagami.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgamma|aagamma|agamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamma</span></p><p>coming, having come; attained; owing to, with reference to, by means of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgamma|aagamma|agamma
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamma</span></p><p>adverb With reference to (c. acc.), owing to, relating to; by means of, thanks to In meaning nearly synonymous with ārabbha, sandhāya &amp; paṭicca (see K. S. 318 s. v.) DN.i.229; Iti.71; Ja.i.50 Ja.vi.424; Kp.viii.14 (= nissāya Kp-a.229); Pv-a.5, Pv-a.21 etc.</p><p>orig. ger. of āgacchati, q.v. under i.2 for form &amp; under; ii.3 for meaning. BSk. āgamya in meaning after the Pāli form, e.g. Divy.95, Divy.405 (with gen.); Avs.i.85, Avs.i.210 etc.; Mvu.i.243, Mvu.i.313</p>

āgamuṃ|aagamu.m|agamum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgamā|aagamaa|agama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgamāsi|aagamaasi|agamasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamaasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgamāsiṃ|aagamaasi.m|agamasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagamaasi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āganta|aaganta|aganta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaganta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgantar">āgantar</a></p><p>one who comes, who returns; (as periphrastic future) will come, will return.</p>

āgantabba|aagantabba|agantabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantabba</span></p><p>to come.</p>

āgantar|aagantar|agantar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgantar">āgantar</a></p><p>one who comes, who returns; (as periphrastic future) will come, will return.</p>

āgantar|aagantar|agantar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantar</span></p><p>one who is coming or going to come AN.i.63; AN.ii.159; Iti.4, Iti.95 (nom. āgantā only one MS, all others āgantvā). <em>an˚</em>; AN.i.64; AN.ii.160.</p><p>N. ag. fr. āgacchati</p>

āgantu|aagantu|agantu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantu</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>occasional, incidental Ja.vi.358.</li><li>an occasional arrival, a new comer, stranger Ja.vi.529 (= āgantuka-jana C.); Thag-a.16.</li></ol><p>Sk. āgantu</p>

āgantuka|aagantuka|agantuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. m. &amp; \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantuka</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) added, accessory; incidental adventitious, external.</li><li>(mfn. and m.f) arriving, coming; a newcomer, a stranger; a traveller, esp. a visiting <em>bhikkhu</em> or bhikkhum.</li></ol>

āgantuka|aagantuka|agantuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantuka</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>coming, arriving, new comer, guest stranger, esp. a newly arrived bhikkhu; a visitor (opp gamika one who goes away) Vin.i.132, Vin.i.167; Vin.ii.170; Vin.iii.65, Vin.iii.181; Vin.iv.24, AN.i.10; AN.iii.41, AN.iii.366; Ja.vi.333; Ud.25; Dhp-a.ii.54, Dhp-a.ii.74; Vv-a.24; Pv-a.54.</li><li>adventitions incidental (= āgantu<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) Mil.304 (of megha &amp; roga).</li><li>accessory, superimposed, added Vism.195.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://bhatta">bhatta</a></em> food given to a guest, meal for a visitor Vin.i.292 (opp. gamika˚); Vin.ii.16.</li></ul><p>āgantu + ka; cp. BSk. āgantuka in same meaning as P. viz. āgantukā bhikṣavaḥ Avs.i.87 Avs.i.286; Divy.50</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhatta">bhatta</a></p>

āgantuṃ|aagantu.m|agantum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgantvā|aagantvaa|agantva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagantvaa</span></p><p>coming; having come, reached; returning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgata|aagata|agata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagata</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) come, arrived, come back; present; having reached, attained; come down, handed down, learnt.</li><li>(n.) arrival; coming</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgata|aagata|agata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagata</span></p><ol><li>come, arrived Mil.18 (˚kāraṇa the reason of his coming); Vv-a.78 (˚ṭṭhāna); Pv-a.81 (kiṃ āgat’attha why have you come here) come by, got, attained (˚-) AN.ii.110 = Pp.48 (˚visa); Mhvs.xiv.28 (˚phala = anāgānuphala) <em>-āgat’āgatā</em> (pl.) people coming &amp; going, passers by, all comers Pv-a.39, Pv-a.78, Pv-a.129; Vv-a.190 (Ep. of sangha). <em>-sv’āgata</em> “wel-come”, greeted hailed; nt. welcome, hail Thig.337; Pv.iv.3#15, opp <em>durāgata</em> not liked, unwelcome, AN.ii.117, AN.ii.143, AN.ii.153; AN.iii.163; Thig.337</li><li>come down, handed down (by memory, said of texts) DN.i.88; Dhp-a.ii.35; Kp-a.229; Vv-a.30; āgatāgamo, one to whom the āgama, or the āgamas, have been handed down, Vin.i.127, Vin.i.337; Vin.ii.8; Vin.iv.158; AN.ii.147; Mil.19, Mil.21</li><li><em><a href="bword://an%C4%81gata">anāgata</a></em> not come yet i.e. future; usually in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with atīta (past) &amp; paccuppanna (present): see; <em><a href="bword://at%C4%ABta">atīta</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81gata">anāgata</a></em>.</li></ol><p>pp. of āgacchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāgata">anāgata</a>, <a href="bword://atīta">atīta</a>, <a href="bword://anāgata">anāgata</a></p>

āgatavisa|aagatavisa|agatavisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagatavisa</span></p><p>with quickly rising poison</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgata">āgata</a></p>

āgati|aagati|agati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagati</span></p><p>arrival, coming; return, rebirth.</p>

āgati|aagati|agati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagati</span></p><p>coming, coming back, return SN.iii.53; Ja.ii.172. Usually opp. to <em><a href="bword://gati">gati</a></em> going away. Used in special sense of rebirth and re-death in the course of saṃsāra Thus in āgati gati cuti upapatti DN.i.162; AN.iii.54 sq. AN.iii.60 sq., AN.iii.74; cp. also SN.ii.67; Pv.ii.9#22 (gatiṃ āgatiṃ vā).</p><p>ā + gati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gati">gati</a></p>

āgatāgama|aagataagama|agatagama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagataagama</span></p><p>who has mastered the tradition; learned</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgata">āgata</a></p>

āgatāgata|aagataagata|agatagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagataagata</span></p><p>whenever he (she, it) comes; whoever comes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgata">āgata</a></p>

āgañchi|aaga~nchi|aganchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaga~nchi</span></p><p>āgañchi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg.</p><p>āgañchi<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a>, <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgañchiṃ|aaga~nchi.m|aganchim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaga~nchi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgañchiṃsu|aaga~nchi.msu|aganchimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaga~nchi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgañchu|aaga~nchu|aganchu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaga~nchu</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgañchuṃ">āgañchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āgañchuṃ|aaga~nchu.m|aganchum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaga~nchu.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgañchuṃ">āgañchuṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgacchati">āgacchati</a></p>

āghāta|aaghaata|aghata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaata</span></p><p>ill-will, hatred, resentment.</p>

āghāta|aaghaata|aghata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaata</span></p><p>anger, ill-will hatred, malice DN.i.3, DN.i.31; DN.iii.72 sq.; SN.i.179; Ja.i.113; Dhs.1060, Dhs.1231; Vb.167, Vb.362, Vb.389; Mil.136; Vism.306; DN-a.i.52; Vv-a.67; Pv-a.178. <em>-<a href="bword://an%C4%81gh%C4%81ta">anāghāta</a></em> freedom from ill will Vin.ii.249; AN.v.80.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADivinaya">paṭivinaya</a></em> repression of ill-will; the usual enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of ā-˚ paṭivinayā comprises <em>nine</em>, for which see DN.iii.262, DN.iii.289; Vin.v.137; AN.iv.408; besides this there are sets of <em>five</em> at AN.iii.185 sq.; Snp-a.10, Snp-a.11, and one of <em>ten</em> at Vin.v.138.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></em> occasion of ill-will; closely connected with ˚paṭivinaya &amp; like that enumerated in sets of <em>nine</em> (Vin.v.137; AN.iv.408; Pts.i.130; Ja.iii.291, Ja.iii.404; Ja.v.149; Vb.389; Ne.23; Snp-a.12), and of <em>ten</em> (Vin.v.138; AN.v.150; Pts.i.130; Vb.391).</li></ul><p>Sk. āghāta only in lit. meaning of striking, killing, but cp. BSk. āghāta in meaning “hurtfulness” at Mvu.i.79; Avs.ii.129; cp. ghāta &amp; ghāteti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāghāta">anāghāta</a>, <a href="bword://paṭivinaya">paṭivinaya</a>, <a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></p>

āghātana|aaghaatana|aghatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaatana</span></p><ol><li>slaughter house; place of execution.</li><li>death.</li></ol>

āghātana|aaghaatana|aghatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaatana</span></p><ol><li>slaying, striking, destroying, killing Thag.418, Thag.711; death DN.i.31 (= maraṇa DN-a.i.119).</li><li>shambles, slaughter-house Vin.i.182 (gav˚); AN.iv.138; Ja.vi.113.</li><li>place of execution Vin.iii.151; Ja.i.326 Ja.i.439; Ja.iii.59; Mil.110; Dhp-a.iv.52; Pv-a.4, Pv-a.5.</li></ol><p>ā + ghāta(na), cp. āghata which has changed its meaning</p>

āghātapaṭivinaya|aaghaatapa.tivinaya|aghatapativinaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaatapa.tivinaya</span></p><p>repression of ill-will</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āghāta">āghāta</a></p>

āghātavatthu|aaghaatavatthu|aghatavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaatavatthu</span></p><p>occasion of ill-will; reason for resentment</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āghāta">āghāta</a></p>

āghāteti|aaghaateti|aghateti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaghaateti</span></p><p>only in phrase <em>cittaṃ a</em>. (with loc.) to incite one’s heart to hatred against, to obdurate one’s heart. Sdhp.126 = SN.i.151 = AN.v.172.</p><p>Denom. fr. āghāta, in form = ā + ghāteti, but diff. in meaning</p>

āgilāyati|aagilaayati|agilayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagilaayati</span></p><p>aches; is faint, feels tired.</p>

āgilāyati|aagilaayati|agilayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagilaayati</span></p><p>to be wearied, exhausted or tired, to ache, to become weak or faint Vin.ii.200; DN.iii.209; MN.i.354; SN.iv.184; Kp-a.66 (hadavaṃ ā.). Cp. <em>āyamati</em>.</p><p>ā + gilāyati; Sk. glāyati, cp. gilāna</p>

āgu|aagu|agu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagu</span></p><p>āgu<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>3 plural</p><p>āgu<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter offense, wrong-doing.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āguṃ">āguṃ</a>, <a href="bword://āgū">āgū</a>, <a href="bword://āgā">āgā</a></p>

āgu|aagu|agu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagu</span></p><p>guilt, offence, SN.i.123; AN.iii.346; Snp.522 = Cnd.337 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of nāga as āguṃ na karotī ti nāgo); Mnd.201. <em>Note.</em> A reconstructed <em>āgasa</em> is found at Sdhp.294 in cpd. akatāgasa not having committed sin.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81rin">cārin</a></em> one who does evil, DN.ii.339; MN.ii.88; MN.iii.163; SN.ii.100, SN.ii.128; AN.ii.240; Mil.110.</li></ul><p>for Vedic āgas nt.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cārin">cārin</a></p>

āgucāri|aagucaari|agucari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagucaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgucārin">āgucārin</a></p><p>doing wrong; criminal</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgu">āgu</a></p>

āgucārin|aagucaarin|agucarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagucaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgucārin">āgucārin</a></p><p>doing wrong; criminal</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgu">āgu</a></p>

āguṃ|aagu.m|agum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āgu">āgu</a>, <a href="bword://āgū">āgū</a>, <a href="bword://āgā">āgā</a></p>

āgā|aagaa|aga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaa</span></p><p>āgā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(?) comes to; returns.</p><p>āgā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>aor. 3 sg.; comes to; returns.</p><p>āgā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>2 sg.; comes to; returns.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āga">āga</a>, <a href="bword://āga">āga</a></p>

āgāmi|aagaami|agami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgāmin">āgāmin</a></p><p>approaching; coming, future; returning; being reborn.</p>

āgāmin|aagaamin|agamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āgāmin">āgāmin</a></p><p>approaching; coming, future; returning; being reborn.</p>

āgāmin|aagaamin|agamin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaamin</span></p><p>adjective noun returning, one who returns, esp. one who returns to another form of life in saṃsāra (cp. āgati), one who is liable to rebirth AN.i.63; AN.ii.159; Iti.95. See <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81g%C4%81min">anāgāmin</a></em>.</p><p>ā + gāmin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāgāmin">anāgāmin</a></p>

āgāmitā|aagaamitaa|agamita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaamitaa</span></p><p>found only in neg. form <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81g%C4%81mit%C4%81">anāgāmitā</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāgāmitā">anāgāmitā</a></p>

āgāra|aagaara|agara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaara</span></p><p>(-˚) see <em><a href="bword://ag%C4%81ra">agāra</a></em>. Agaraka &amp; ika;</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāra">agāra</a></p>

āgāraka|aagaaraka|agaraka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaaraka</span></p><p>small house; a cottage; a hut.</p>

āgāraka|aagaaraka|agaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaaraka</span></p><p>Āgāraka &amp; ˚ika</p><p>adjective noun (-˚) belonging to the house, viz.</p><ol><li>having control over the house, keeping, surveying, in compounds; <em>koṭṭh˚</em> possessor or keeper of a storehouse Vin.i.209; <em>bandhan˚</em> prison-keeper AN.ii.207; <em>bhaṇḍ˚</em>; keeper of wares, treasurer Pv-a.2 (see also bhaṇḍ˚)</li><li>being in the house, sharing (the house), companion SN.iii.190 (paṃsv˚ playmate).</li></ol><p>cp. BSk. āgārika Divy.275, &amp; agārika</p>

āgārika|aagaarika|agarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaarika</span></p><p>āgārikaāgāriya</p><p>mfn. &amp; m. feminine belonging to a house; living in a house; a householder; a housewife; a lay person</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agāriya">agāriya</a></p>

āgārika|aagaarika|agarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaarika</span></p><p>Āgāraka &amp; ˚ika</p><p>adjective noun (-˚) belonging to the house, viz.</p><ol><li>having control over the house, keeping, surveying, in compounds; <em>koṭṭh˚</em> possessor or keeper of a storehouse Vin.i.209; <em>bandhan˚</em> prison-keeper AN.ii.207; <em>bhaṇḍ˚</em>; keeper of wares, treasurer Pv-a.2 (see also bhaṇḍ˚)</li><li>being in the house, sharing (the house), companion SN.iii.190 (paṃsv˚ playmate).</li></ol><p>cp. BSk. āgārika Divy.275, &amp; agārika</p>

āgāḷha|aagaa.lha|agalha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaa.lha</span></p><p>strong, severe; harsh, hard.</p>

āgāḷha|aagaa.lha|agalha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aagaa.lha</span></p><p>adjective (ā + gāḷha 1; cp. Sk. samāgāḍhaṃ] strong, hard, harsh, rough (of speech), usually in instr. as adv <em>āgāḷhena</em> roughly, harshly aN.i.283, aN.i.295; Pp.32 (so to be read for agāḷhena, although Pp-a.215 has a˚, but explains by atigāḷhena vacanena); instr. f. <em>āgāḷhāya</em> Vin.v.122 (ceteyya; Bdhgh. on p. 230 reads āgaḷāya and explains by daḷhabhāvāya). See also Ne.77 (āgāḷhā paṭipadā a rough path), Ne.95 (id.; variant reading agāḷhā).</p>

āgū|aaguu|agu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaguu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āguṃ">āguṃ</a>, <a href="bword://āgu">āgu</a>, <a href="bword://āgā">āgā</a></p>

āha|aaha|aha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha</span></p><p>perf. 3 sg. (perf. expressing present, past and indefinite time) says, speaks; tells; said, spoke; speaks about; calls, regards as; (to introduce objections) someone might say, it is said; 3 sg. āha (and āhu).</p>

āha|aaha|aha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha</span></p><p>perfect in meaning of pret. &amp; pres. “he says or he said” he spoke, also spoke to somebody (w. acc.), as at Ja.i.197 (cullalohitaṃ āha). Usually in 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> person, very rarely used of 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> person, as at Snp.839, Snp.840 (= kathesi bhaṇasi Nd.188, Nd.191).</p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ha">āha</a></em> Vin.ii.191; Snp.790 (= bhaṇati Mnd.87), Snp.888; Ja.i.280; Ja.iii.53 and freq. passim; 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl <em><a href="bword://%C4%81hu">āhu</a></em> Snp.87, Snp.181; Dhp.345; Ja.i.59; Snp-a.377, and <em>āhaṃsu</em> Ja.i.222; Ja.iii.278 and freq.</p><p>Vedic āha, orig. perfect of <em>ah</em> to speak, meaning “he began to speak”, thus in meaning of pres. “he says”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āha">āha</a>, <a href="bword://āhu">āhu</a></p>

āhacca|aahacca|ahacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahacca</span></p><p>having hit, struck; beaten; came into contact with, reached.</p>

āhacca|aahacca|ahacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahacca</span></p><p>Āhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>ger. of <em><a href="bword://%C4%81hanati">āhanati</a></em>.</p><p>Āhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>(cp. āharati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) to be removed, removable, in <em>-pādaka-pīṭha &amp; ˚mañca;</em> a collapsible bed or chair, i e whose legs or feet can be put on &amp; taken away at pleasure (by drawing out a pin) Vin.ii.149 (cp. <em>Vin Texts</em> iii.164 n. 5); Vin.iv.40, Vin.iv.46 (def. as “ange vijjhitvā ṭhito hoti” it stands by means of a perforated limb), Vin.iv.168, Vin.iv.169.</li><li>(cp. āharati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) reciting, repeating, or to be quoted, recitation (of the Scriptures); by authority or by tradition MN.iii.139; Dhs-a.9, &amp; in compounds; <em>-pada</em> a text quoted from Scripture), tradition Mil.148 (˚ena by reference to the text of the Scriptures); <em>-vacana</em> a saying of the Scriptures, a traditional or proverbial saying Ne.21 (in def. of suttaṃ).</li></ol><p>grd. of āharati, corresponding to a Sk. *āhṛtya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhanati">āhanati</a></p>

āhacca tiṭṭhati|aahacca ti.t.thati|ahacca titthati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahacca ti.t.thati</span></p><p>reaches; touches, strikes.</p>

āhaccapādaka|aahaccapaadaka|ahaccapadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m(fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaccapaadaka</span></p><p>with removable or insertable legs.</p>

āhanana|aahanana|ahanana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahanana</span></p><p>beating, striking, coming into touch, “impinging” Vism.142 (+ pariyāhanana, in def of vitakka) = Dhs-a.114 (cp. <em>Expos.</em> 151); Vism.515 (id.).</p><p>fr. ā + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

āhanati|aahanati|ahanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahanati</span></p><p>to beat, strike, press against, touch ppr. <em>āhananto</em> Mil.21 (dhamma-bheriṃ); Dāvs iv.50. ger. <em>āhacca</em> touching MN.i.493; Ja.i.330; Ja.vi.2, Ja.vi.200; Snp.716 = uppīḷetva Snp-a.498; Vism.420<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81hata">āhata</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>1st sg. fut. <em>āhañhi</em> Vin.i.8; DN.ii.72, where probably to be read as āhañh (= āhañhaṃ). See Geiger, <em>P.Gr.</em> § 153, 2.</p><p>ā + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhata">āhata</a>, <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

āharahattha|aaharahattha|aharahattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharahattha</span></p><p>or mfn. (giving a) hold for the hands.</p>

āharanta|aaharanta|aharanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharati|aaharati|aharati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharati</span></p><ol><li>brings, fetches, conveys; takes, collects; uses.</li><li><em>satthaṃ ~ati</em>, <em>~eti</em>, takes the knife, kills oneself</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhareti">āhareti</a></p>

āharati|aaharati|aharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharati</span></p><ol><li>to take, take up, take hold of, take out, take away MN.i.429 (sallaṃ); SN.i.121; SN.iii.123; Ja.i.40 (ger. āharitvā “with”), Ja.i.293 (te hattaṃ); Cnd.540#c (puttamaṃsaṃ, read āhāreyya?); Pv.ii.3#10; DN-a.i.186, DN-a.i.188 DN-a.i.2.</li><li>to bring, bring down, fetch DN.ii.245; Ja.iv.159 (nāvaṃ variant reading āhāhitvā); Ja.v.466; Vv-a.63 (bhattaṃ); Pv-a.75.</li><li>to get, acquire, bring upon oneself Ja.v.433 (padosaṃ) Dhp-a.ii.89.</li><li>to bring on to, put into (w. loc.); fig &amp; intrs. to hold on to, put oneself to, touch, resort to MN.i.395 (kaṭhalaṃ mukhe ā.; also inf. āhattuṃ); Thag.1156 (pāpacitte ā.; Mrs. Rh. D. <em>Brethren</em> ver. 1156, not as “accost” p. 419, <em>n.</em>).</li><li>to assault, strike, offend (for pāhari?) Thag.1173.</li><li>(fig.) to take up, fall or go back on (w. acc.), recite, quote, repeat (usually with desanaṃ dasseti of an instructive story or sermon or homily) Ja.iii.383 (desanaṃ), Ja.iii.401; Ja.v.462 (vatthuṃ āharitvā dassesi told a story for example); Snp-a.376; Pv-a.38, Pv-a.39 (atītaṃ), Pv-a.42, Pv-a.66, Pv-a.99 (dhamma-desanaṃ). See also <em><a href="bword://payirud%C4%81harati">payirudāharati</a></em>. pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ha%E1%B9%ADa">āhaṭa</a></em> (q.v.)</li></ol><p>caus 2 <em>āhārapeti</em> to cause to be brought or fetched; to wish to take, to call or ask for Ja.iii.88, Ja.iii.342; Ja.v.466; Pv-a.215.</p><p>ā + <em>hṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://payirudāharati">payirudāharati</a>, <a href="bword://āhaṭa">āhaṭa</a></p>

āharaṇa|aahara.na|aharana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahara.na</span></p><p>adjective noun to be taken; taking away; only in phrase acorāharaṇo nidhi a treasure not to be taken by thieves Mil.320; Kp.viii.9; Kp-a.224; Sdhp.589.</p><p>fr. āharati</p>

āharaṇaka|aahara.naka|aharanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahara.naka</span></p><p>one who has to take or bring, a messenger Ja.ii.199; Ja.iii.328.</p><p>āharaṇa + ka</p>

āharesi|aaharesi|aharesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharesi</span></p><p>spoken; declared, related; quoted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhareti|aahareti|ahareti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahareti</span></p><ol><li>brings, fetches, conveys; takes, collects; uses.</li><li><em>satthaṃ ~ati</em>, <em>~eti</em>, takes the knife, kills oneself</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhari|aahari|ahari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahari</span></p><p>spoken; declared, related; quoted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharima|aaharima|aharima
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharima</span></p><ol><li>added, accessory.</li><li>which has been added to; dependent on accessories.</li></ol>

āharima|aaharima|aharima
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharima</span></p><p>adjective “fetching”, fascinating, captivating, charming Vin.iv.299; Thig.299; Thag-a.227; Vv-a.14, Vv-a.15, Vv-a.77.</p><p>fr. āharati</p>

āharissami|aaharissami|aharissami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharissami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharissati|aaharissati|aharissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharissati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharit|aaharit|aharit
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharit</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āharitr">āharitr</a></p><p>(from āharati) one who takes, uses; who eats.</p>

āharita|aaharita|aharita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharita</span></p><p>brought, taken, used</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharitr|aaharitr|aharitr
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharitr</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āharitr">āharitr</a></p><p>(from āharati) one who takes, uses; who eats.</p>

āharituṃ|aaharitu.m|aharitum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharitu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharitvā|aaharitvaa|aharitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhariya|aahariya|ahariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahariya</span></p><p>one who is to bring something Ja.iii.328. Ahavana &amp; Ahavaniya;</p><p>grd. of āharati</p>

āhariyati|aahariyati|ahariyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass.pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahariyati</span></p><ol><li>is brought or taken; is offered; is related.</li><li>takes for oneself uses; eats</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhariyyati">āhariyyati</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhariyitha|aahariyitha|ahariyitha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahariyitha</span></p><p>(they) brought, offered</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhariyyati|aahariyyati|ahariyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass.pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahariyyati</span></p><ol><li>is brought or taken; is offered; is related.</li><li>takes for oneself uses; eats</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhariyati">āhariyati</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhariṃsu|aahari.msu|aharimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahari.msu</span></p><p>see āharati)</p>

āharāpayati|aaharaapayati|aharapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharaapayati</span></p><p>causes to be taken or fetched; sends for; demands</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharāpeti">āharāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharāpeti|aaharaapeti|aharapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharaapeti</span></p><p>causes to be taken or fetched; sends for; demands</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharāpayati">āharāpayati</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āharāpetvā|aaharaapetvaa|aharapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaharaapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharāpeti">āharāpeti</a></p>

āhata|aahata|ahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahata</span></p><p>brought; conveyed; related, cited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhaṭa">āhaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhata|aahata|ahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahata</span></p><p>struck, beaten, stamped; afflicted, affected with (-˚) Vin.iv.236 = DN.iii.238 (kupito anattamano āhata-citto); Vin.i.75, Vin.i.76; SN.i.170 (tilak˚, so read for tilakā-hata, affected with freckles, C. kāḷa-setādi vaṇṇehi tilakehi āhatagatta, <em>K. S.</em> p. 318); Ja.iii.456; Sdhp.187, Sdhp.401.</p><p>pp. of āhanati</p>

āhataka|aahataka|ahataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahataka</span></p><p>servant.</p>

āhataka|aahataka|ahataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahataka</span></p><p>“one who is beaten”, a slave, a worker (of low grade) Vin.iv.224 (in def. of kammakāra, as bhaṭaka + ā).</p><p>fr. āhata</p>

āhatta|aahatta|ahatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āhattar">āhattar</a></p><p>one who brings.</p>

āhattar|aahattar|ahattar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahattar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āhattar">āhattar</a></p><p>one who brings.</p>

āhattuṃ|aahattu.m|ahattum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahattu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhatvā|aahatvaa|ahatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahatvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhavana|aahavana|ahavana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahavana</span></p><p>Āhavana &amp; Āhavanīya</p><p>see under <em>āhuneyya</em>.</p>

āhavanīya|aahavaniiya|ahavaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahavaniiya</span></p><p>Āhavana &amp; Āhavanīya</p><p>see under <em>āhuneyya</em>.</p>

āhañchaṃ|aaha~ncha.m|ahancham
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha~ncha.m</span></p><p>will hit, will strike; will beat; will come into contact with, will reach.</p>

āhañhi|aaha~nhi|ahanhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha~nhi</span></p><p>will hit, will strike; will beat; will come into contact with, will reach.</p>

āhañhiṃ|aaha~nhi.m|ahanhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha~nhi.m</span></p><p>will hit, will strike; will beat; will come into contact with, will reach.</p>

āhaṃsu|aaha.msu|ahamsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āha">āha</a></p>

āhaṭa|aaha.ta|ahata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha.ta</span></p><p>brought; conveyed; related, cited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhata">āhata</a>, <a href="bword://āharati">āharati</a></p>

āhaṭa|aaha.ta|ahata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaha.ta</span></p><p>brought, carried, obtained Vin.i.121; Vin.iii.53; DN.ii.180 (spelt āhata); Ja.iii.512 (gloss ānīta) Dāvs i.58.</p><p>pp. of āharati</p>

āhika|aahika|ahika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahika</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective only in pañcāhika every five days (cp. pañcāhaṃ &amp; sattāhaṃ) MN.iii.157.</p><p>der. fr. aha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

āhita|aahita|ahita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahita</span></p><p>fixed, established; kindled, provided with fuel.</p>

āhita|aahita|ahita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahita</span></p><p>put up, heaped; provided with fuel (of a fire), blazing Snp.18 (gini = ābhato jalito vā Snp-a.28). See sam˚.</p><p>pp. of ā + <em>dhā</em></p>

āhiṇḍanta|aahi.n.danta|ahindanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahi.n.danta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhiṇḍati">āhiṇḍati</a></p>

āhiṇḍati|aahi.n.dati|ahindati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahi.n.dati</span></p><p>goes about; wanders.</p>

āhiṇḍati|aahi.n.dati|ahindati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahi.n.dati</span></p><p>to wander about, to roam, to be on an errand, to be engaged in (w. acc.) Vin.i.203 (senāsana-cārikaṃ), Vin.i.217; Vin.ii.132 (na sakkoti vinā daṇḍena āhiṇḍituṃ); Vin.iv.62; Ja.i.48 Ja.i.108, Ja.i.239; Cnd.540#b; Pv.iii.2#29 (= vicarati Pv-a.185) Vism.38, Vism.284 (aṭaviṃ); Vv-a.238 (tattha tattha); Pv-a.143.</p><p>ā + <em>hiṇḍ</em>, cp. BSk. āhiṇḍate Divy.165 etc.</p>

āhiṇḍituṃ|aahi.n.ditu.m|ahinditum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahi.n.ditu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhiṇḍati">āhiṇḍati</a></p>

āhu|aahu|ahu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahu</span></p><p>āhu<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>perf. 3 sg. (perf. expressing present, past and indefinite time) says, speaks; tells; said, spoke; speaks about; calls, regards as; (to introduce objections) someone might say, it is said; 3 sg. āha.</p><p>āhu<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>3 plural</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āha">āha</a>, <a href="bword://āhu">āhu</a></p>

āhu|aahu|ahu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahu</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. of āha (q.v.).</p>

āhuna|aahuna|ahuna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuna</span></p><p>offering; honoring.</p>

āhuna|aahuna|ahuna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuna</span></p><p>= āhuti, in āhuna-pāhuna giving oblations and sacrificing. Vv-a.155 by itself at Vism.219</p>

āhundarika|aahundarika|ahundarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahundarika</span></p><p>dark, obscure, impenetrable (?).</p>

āhundarika|aahundarika|ahundarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahundarika</span></p><p>adjective according to Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 73 “crowded up, blocked up, impassable Vin.i.79; Vin.iv.297; Vism.413 (˚ṃ andha-tamaṃ).</p><p>doubtful or āhuṇḍ˚?</p>

āhuneyya|aahuneyya|ahuneyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuneyya</span></p><p>to be offered to or in; worthy of offerings.</p>

āhuneyya|aahuneyya|ahuneyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuneyya</span></p><p>adjective sacrificial, worthy of offerings or of sacrifice, venerable, adorable, worshipful DN.iii.5, DN.iii.217 (aggi); AN.ii.56, AN.ii.70 (sāhuneyyaka), AN.ii.145 sq. (id.); AN.iv.13, AN.iv.41 (aggi); Iti.88 (+ pāhuneyya); Vv.64#33 (cp. Vv-a.285). See def. at Vism.219 where expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by “āhavanīya” and “āhavanaṃ arahati” deserving of offerings.</p><p>a grd. form. fr. ā + <em>hu</em>, cp. āhuti</p>

āhuneyyaggi|aahuneyyaggi|ahuneyyaggi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuneyyaggi</span></p><p>the oblation of fire</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhuneyya">āhuneyya</a></p>

āhuta|aahuta|ahuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuta</span></p><p>produced, coming from.</p>

āhuti|aahuti|ahuti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuti</span></p><p>offering; sacrifice.</p>

āhuti|aahuti|ahuti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahuti</span></p><p>oblation, sacrifice; veneration, adoration MN.iii.167; SN.i.141; Thag.566 (<em>-īnaṃ</em> paṭiggaho recipient of sacrificial gifts); Ja.i.15; Ja.v.70 (id.) Vv.64#33 (paramâhutiṃ gato deserving the highest adoration); Snp.249, Snp.458; Kv.530; Snp-a.175; Vv-a.285.</p><p>Vedic āhuti, ā + <em>hu</em></p>

āhāra|aahaara|ahara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaara</span></p><p>taking; food, nutriment, sustenance; fuel; support.</p>

āhāra|aahaara|ahara
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaara</span></p><p><strong>Āhāra:</strong> ‘nutriment’, ‘food’, is used in the concrete sense as material food and as such it belongs to derived materiality see: <em>khandha</em> Summary I. In the figurative sense, as ‘foundation’ or sustaining condition, it is one of the 24 conditions <em>paccaya</em> and is used to denote 4 kinds of nutriment, which are material and mental: 1: material food <em>kabalinkārāhāra</em>, 2: sensorial and mental contact <em>phassa</em>, 3: mental intention <em>mano</em> - <em>sañcetanā,</em> 4: consciousness <em>viññāna</em>.</p><p>1: Material food feeds the 8-fold materiality having nutrient essence as its 8th factor i.e. the solid, liquid, heat, motion, color, odour, the tastable and nutrient essence; see: <em>rūpa-kalāpa</em>. 2: Sensorial and mental contact is a condition for the 3 kinds of feeling pleasant, painful and indifferent; see: <em>paticcasamuppāda</em> 6. 3: Mental intention = kamma feeds rebirth; see: <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em> 2. 4: Consciousness feeds mind and materiality, <em>nāma-rūpa</em> at the moment of conception Vis.M XI.</p><blockquote><p>Literature on the 4 Nutriments: M. 9 &amp; Com. tr. in ’ R. Und. ’, M. 38; see: S. XII, 11, 63, 64 - The Four Nutriments of Life, Selected texts &amp; Com. WHEEL 105</p></blockquote>

āhāra|aahaara|ahara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaara</span></p><p>feeding, support, food, nutriment (lit &amp; fig.). The term is used comprehensively and the usual enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> comprises four kinds of nutriment, viz.</p><ol><li>kabaḷinkāra āhāro (bodily nutriment, either oḷāriko gross, solid, or sukhumo fine)</li><li>phassāhāro Name of contact,</li><li>manosañcetanā˚. Name of volition (= cetanā SN-a on SN.ii.11 f.),</li><li>viññāṇ˚ of consciousness. Thus at MN.i.261; DN.iii.228, DN.iii.276; Dhs.71–Dhs.73 Vism.341.</li></ol><p>Another definition of Dhammapāla’s refers it to the fourfold tasting as asita (eaten), pīta (drunk), khāyita (chewed), sāyita (tasted) food Pv-a.25. A synonym with mūla, hetu, etc. for cause, Yamaka, i.3; Yam. A (<em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1910–12) 54. See on term also <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 30<br />■ Vin.i.84; DN.i.166; SN.i.172; SN.ii.11, SN.ii.13, SN.ii.98 sq. (the 4 kinds in detail); SN.iii.54 (sa˚); SN.v.64, SN.v.391; AN.iii.51 (sukhass˚), AN.iii.79, AN.iii.142 sq., AN.iii.192 sq.; AN.iv.49, AN.iv.108; AN.v.52 (the four), AN.v.108, AN.v.113 (avijjāya etc.), AN.iii.116 (bhavataṇhāya), AN.iii.269 sq (nerayikānaṃ etc.); Snp.78, Snp.165, Snp.707, Snp.747; Mnd.25; Pts.i.22 (the four), Pts.i.122 (id.), Pts.i.55, Pts.i.76 sq; Kv.508; Pp.21, Pp.55; Vb.2, Vb.13, Vb.72, Vb.89, Vb.320, Vb.383, Vb.401 sq. (the four) Dhs.58, Dhs.121, Dhs.358, Dhs.646; Ne.31, Ne.114, Ne.124; Dhs-a.153 Dhs-a.401; Dhp-a.i.183 (˚ṃ pacchindati to bring up food, to vomit); Dhp-a.ii.87; Vv-a.118; Pv-a.14, Pv-a.35, Pv-a.112, Pv-a.148 (utu physical nutriment); Sdhp.100, Sdhp.395, Sdhp.406; AN.v.136 gives ten āhāra opposed to ten paripanthā. <em>-an˚</em>; without food unfed MN.i.487 (aggi); SN.iii.126; SN.v.105; Snp.985.</p><ul><li><em>-ūpahāra</em> consumption of food, feeding, eating Vin.iii.136.</li><li><em>-<em>ṭhitika</em></em> subsisting or living on food DN.iii.211, DN.iii.273; AN.v.50, AN.v.55; Pts.i.5, Pts.i.122.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pariggaha">pariggaha</a></em> taking up or acquirement of food Mil.244 or is it “restraint or abstinence in food”? Same comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Mil.313.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://maya">maya</a></em> “food-like” feeding stuff, food Ja.iii.523.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://lolat%C4%81">lolatā</a></em> greed after food Snp-a.35.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a></em> origin of nutriment SN.iii.59.</li></ul><p>fr. ā + <em>hṛ</em>; lit. taking up or on to oneself</p><p>See also: ṭhitika, <a href="bword://pariggaha">pariggaha</a>, <a href="bword://maya">maya</a>, <a href="bword://lolatā">lolatā</a>, <a href="bword://samudaya">samudaya</a></p>

āhāra-samutthāna-rūpa|aahaara-samutthaana-ruupa|ahara-samutthana-rupa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaara-samutthaana-ruupa</span></p><p><strong>Āhāra-samutthāna-rūpa:</strong> ‘Food-produced materiality’; see: <em>samutthāna</em></p>

āhāratthaṃ|aahaarattha.m|aharattham
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaarattha.m</span></p><p>the state of being food. In the idiom āhārattaṇ pharati; Vin.i.199, of medicine, ʻto penetrate into food-ness’, to come under the category of food Mil.152, of poison, to turn into food. [According to Oldenberg (Vin.i.381) his MSS read about equally ˚attaṃ and ˚atthaṃ. Trenckner prints ˚atthaṃ, and records no variant (see p. 425)].</p><p>āhāra + tta</p>

āhāraya|aahaaraya|aharaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aahaaraya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āhārayat">āhārayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āhārayati">āhārayati</a></p>

