ākaḍḍhati|aaka.d.dhati|akaddhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>drags, pulls; pulls near, draws (towards oneself), attracts; supplies (an omitted, understood word); draws out, brings about; bends (a bow); draws (a line), scratches.</p>

ākaḍḍhati|aaka.d.dhati|akaddhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka.d.dhati</span></p><p>to pull along, pull to (oneself), drag or draw out, pull up Vin.ii.325 (bdhgh. for apakassati see under <em><a href="bword://apak%C4%81sati">apakāsati</a></em>); Vin.iv.219; Ja.i.172, Ja.i.192, Ja.i.417; Mil.102, Mil.135; Thag-a.117 (˚eti); Vv-a.226; Pv-a.68. Pass. <em>ākaḍḍhiyati</em> Ja.ii.122 (˚amāna-locana with eyes drawn away or attracted); Mil.102; Vism.163; Vv-a.207 (˚amāna-hadaya with torn heart)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ka%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dhita">ākaḍḍhita</a></em>.</p><p>ā + kaḍḍhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://apakāsati">apakāsati</a>, <a href="bword://ākaḍḍhita">ākaḍḍhita</a></p>

ākaḍḍhita|aaka.d.dhita|akaddhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka.d.dhita</span></p><p>pulled out, dragged along; upset, overthrown Ja.iii.256 (= akkhitta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->).</p><p>pp. of akaḍḍheti</p>

ākaḍḍhitva|aaka.d.dhitva|akaddhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka.d.dhitva</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaḍḍhati">ākaḍḍhati</a></p>

ākaḍḍhiyamāna|aaka.d.dhiyamaana|akaddhiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka.d.dhiyamaana</span></p><p>dragging, pulling; pulling near, drawing (towards oneself).</p>

ākaṅkha|aaka"nkha|akankha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhat">ākaṅkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhamāna|aaka"nkhamaana|akankhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhanta|aaka"nkhanta|akankhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhat|aaka"nkhat|akankhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhat">ākaṅkhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhati|aaka"nkhati|akankhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhati</span></p><p>wishes; desires, longs for; wants to, is willing (to); intends.</p>

ākaṅkhati|aaka"nkhati|akankhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhati</span></p><p>to wish for, think of, desire; intend, plan, design Vin.ii.244 (˚amāna); DN.i.78, DN.i.176; SN.i.46; Snp.569 (˚amāna); Snp.p.102 (= icchati Snp-a.436); Dhp-a.i.29; Snp-a.229; Vv-a.149; Pv-a.229.</p><p>ā + <em>kāṅkṣ</em>, cp. <em><a href="bword://ka%E1%B9%85khati">kaṅkhati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kaṅkhati">kaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhita|aaka"nkhita|akankhita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhita</span></p><p>wished for; desired</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhiya|aaka"nkhiya|akankhiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhiṃ|aaka"nkhi.m|akankhim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākaṅkhati">ākaṅkhati</a></p>

ākaṅkhā|aaka"nkhaa|akankha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaka"nkhaa</span></p><p>longing, wish; as adj. at Thag.1030.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>kāṅkṣ</em></p>

ākhu|aakhu|akhu
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakhu</span></p><p>See Mūsika.</p>

ākhu|aakhu|akhu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakhu</span></p><p>mouse or rat Pgdp.10.</p><p>Vedic ākhu, fr. ā + <em>khan</em>, lit. the digger in, i.e. a mole; but given as rat or mouse by Halāyudha</p>

ākhīṇa|aakhii.na|akhina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakhii.na</span></p><p>scattered, unrestrained; rough; impure.</p>

ākhīṇavyappatha|aakhii.navyappatha|akhinavyappatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakhii.navyappatha</span></p><p>of rough speech (<em>ākhīṇa</em>)</p>

ākilāyati|aakilaayati|akilayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakilaayati</span></p><p>variant reading at Kp-a.66 for āgilāyati.</p>

ākiranta|aakiranta|akiranta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakiranta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākirati">ākirati</a></p>

ākirati|aakirati|akirati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakirati</span></p><p>sprinkles, scatters over; pours (into, loc), heaps up (in); dispenses.</p>

ākirati|aakirati|akirati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakirati</span></p><p>to strew over, scatter, sprinkle, disperse, fill, heap Snp.665; Dhp.313; Pv.ii.4#9 (dānaṃ vipulaṃ ākiri vippakirati Pv-a.92); Mil.175, Mil.238, Mil.323 (imper. ākirāhi) Snp.383<br />pp ākiṇṇa.</p><p>ā + kirati</p>

ākiritatta|aakiritatta|akiritatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakiritatta</span></p><p>the fact or state of being filled or heaped with Mil.173 (sakataṃ dhaññassa ā).</p><p>ākirita + tta; abstr. fr. ākirita, pp. of ākirati Caus.</p>

ākiritvā|aakiritvaa|akiritva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakiritvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākirati">ākirati</a></p>

ākiñcanasambhava|aaki~ncanasambhava|akincanasambhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~ncanasambhava</span></p><p>the origin of the state of nothingness.</p>

ākiñcañña-ceto-vimutti|aaki~nca~n~na-ceto-vimutti|akincanna-ceto-vimutti
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~nca~n~na-ceto-vimutti</span></p><p><strong>Ākiñcañña-ceto-vimutti:</strong> Possessionless or desireless mental release. The mental liberation coming from relinquishment of all acquisitions. The end stage of realizing <em>Dukkha</em> see: <em>ceto-vimutti</em></p>

ākiñcañña|aaki~nca~n~na|akincanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~nca~n~na</span></p><ol><li><p>(n.)</p><ol><li>the state of having nothing; complete absence of possessions.</li><li>the state of nothingness</li></ol></li><li><p>(mfn.) associated with nothingness or with having nothing.</p></li></ol>

ākiñcañña|aaki~nca~n~na|akincanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~nca~n~na</span></p><p>state of having nothing, absence of (any) possessions; nothingness (the latter as philosophical t. t.; cp. below ˚āyatana &amp; see Dhs trsl.74)<br />■ Snp.976, Snp.1070, Snp.1115 (˚sambhava, cp. Cnd.116) Thig.341 (= akiñcanabhāva Thag-a.240; trsl. “cherish no wordly wishes whatsoëer”); Cnd.115, see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81k%C4%81sa">ākāsa</a></em> Mil.342.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a></em> realm or sphere of nothingness (cp. ākāsa˚ DN.i.35, DN.i.184; DN.ii.156; DN.iii.224, DN.iii.253, DN.iii.262 sq.; MN.i.41, MN.i.165 MN.ii.254, MN.ii.263; MN.iii.28, MN.iii.44, SN.iv.217; AN.i.268; AN.iv.40, AN.iv.401; Pts.i.36; Ne.26, Ne.39; Vism.333. See also jhāna &amp; vimokkha.</li></ul><p>abstr. fr. akiñcana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a>, <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a></p>

ākiñcaññāyatana|aaki~nca~n~naayatana|akincannayatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~nca~n~naayatana</span></p><p>the sphere or stage of (the awareness of) nothingness, i.e.</p><ol><li>the third of the arūpa states of existence; and.</li><li>the state of awareness of nothingness, the third arūpajhāna or one of the vimokkhas</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiñcañña">ākiñcañña</a></p>

ākiñcaññāyatana|aaki~nca~n~naayatana|akincannayatana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~nca~n~naayatana</span></p><p><strong>Ākiñcaññāyatana:</strong> The sphere of nothingness; see: <em>jhāna</em> 7.</p>

ākiñcāyatanūpagādevā|aaki~ncaayatanuupagaadevaa|akincayatanupagadeva
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki~ncaayatanuupagaadevaa</span></p><p>A class of devas born in the Ākiñcāyatana, the third Arūpa world. MN.iii.103 Their life term is sixty thousand kappas. AN.i.267</p>

ākiṇṇa|aaki.n.na|akinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.na</span></p><p>poured (into), heaped up (in); overspread, filled, crowded; surrounded, being in a crowd; disordered, unrestrained, impure</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākirati">ākirati</a></p>

ākiṇṇa|aaki.n.na|akinna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.na</span></p><ol><li>strewn over, beset with, crowded, full of, dense, rich in (˚-) Vin.iii.130 (˚loma with dense hair); SN.i.204 (˚kammanta “in motley tasks engaged”), SN.iv.37 (gāmanto ā. bhikkhūhi etc.); AN.iii.104 (˚vihāro), AN.iv.4; AN.v.15 (an˚ C. for appakiṇṇa); Snp.408 (˚varalakkhaṇa = <em>vipula-</em> varalakkh˚ Snp-a.383); Pv.ii.12#4 (nānā-dijagaṇ = āyutta Pv-a.157); Pp.31; Pv-a.32 (= parikiṇṇa) Sdhp.595<br />■ Freq. in idiomatic phrase describing a flourishing city “iddha phīta bahujana ākiṇṇa-manussa” e.g. DN.i.211; DN.ii.147 (˚yakkha for ˚manussa; full of yakkhas i.e. under their protection); AN.iii.215; cp. Mil.2 (˚jana-manussa).</li><li>(uncertain whether to be taken as above (1) or as equal to avakiṇṇa fr. avakirati 2) dejected, base, vile, ruthless SN.i.205 = Ja.iii.309 = Ja.iii.539 = Snp-a.383 At <em>K. S.</em> 261, Mrs. Rh. D. translates “ruthless” &amp; quotes C. as implying twofold exegesis of (a) impure, and (b) hard, ruthless. It is interesting to notice that Bdhgh explains the same verse differently at Snp-a.383, viz. by; <em>vipula˚</em>; as above under Snp.408, &amp; takes ākiṇṇaludda as vipulaludda, i.e. beset with cruelty, very or intensely cruel, thus referring it to ākiṇṇa 1.</li></ol><p>pp. of ākirati</p>

ākiṇṇakammanta|aaki.n.nakammanta|akinnakammanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.nakammanta</span></p><p>rough in action; acting impurely</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiṇṇa">ākiṇṇa</a></p>

ākiṇṇaloma|aaki.n.naloma|akinnaloma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.naloma</span></p><p>matted, thick hair</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiṇṇa">ākiṇṇa</a></p>

ākiṇṇaludda|aaki.n.naludda|akinnaludda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.naludda</span></p><p>rough and fierce; very fierce</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiṇṇa">ākiṇṇa</a></p>

ākiṇṇamanussa|aaki.n.namanussa|akinnamanussa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.namanussa</span></p><p>full of people; crowded with people</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiṇṇa">ākiṇṇa</a></p>

ākiṇṇavihāra|aaki.n.navihaara|akinnavihara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaki.n.navihaara</span></p><p>living in crowds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākiṇṇa">ākiṇṇa</a></p>

ākoṭaka|aako.taka|akotaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.taka</span></p><p>A deva who visited the Buddha at Veḷuvana accompanied by Asama, Sahali, Ninka, Vetambarī and Mānava-Gāmiya. Ākoṭaka spoke before the Buddha in praise of various teachers of other schools: Pakuddha-Kātiyāna (sic), Nigaṇṭha, Makkhali, Pūraṇa. Vetambari made rejoinder to Ākoṭaka, speaking disparagingly of the teachers he had mentioned. SN.i.65.</p>

ākoṭana|aako.tana|akotana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tana</span></p><p>Ākoṭana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter beating on, knocking MN.i.385; Mil.63, Mil.306; Dhs-a.144.</p><p>fr. ākoṭeti</p><p>Ākoṭana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective beating, driving, inciting, urging Ja.vi.253 (f. ākoṭanī of paññā, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by “nivāraṇapatoda-laṭṭhi viya paññā koṭinī hoti” p. 254).</p><p>= ākoṭana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ākoṭayati|aako.tayati|akotayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tayati</span></p><p>beats, strikes; knocks (at); pounds; hammers (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭesi|aako.tesi|akotesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭesuṃ|aako.tesu.m|akotesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭetabba|aako.tetabba|akotetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭeti|aako.teti|akoteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.teti</span></p><p>beats, strikes; knocks (at); pounds; hammers (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭayati">ākoṭayati</a></p>

ākoṭeti|aako.teti|akoteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.teti</span></p><ol><li>to beat down, pound, stamp Ja.i.264.</li><li>to beat, knock, thrash Vin.ii.217; Ja.ii.274; Pv-a.55 (aññamaññaṃ); Sdhp.159.</li><li>Esp. with ref. to knocking at the door, in phrases <em>aggaḷaṃ ākoṭeti</em> to beat on the bolt DN.i.89; AN.iv.359; AN.v.65; DN-a.i.252 (cp. aggaḷa) dvāraṃ ā. Ja.v.217; Dhp-a.ii.145; or simply ākoṭeti Vv.81#17 (ākoṭayitvāna = appoṭetvā Vv-a.316).</li><li>(intrs. to knock against anything Ja.i.239</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ko%E1%B9%ADita">ākoṭita</a></em> (q.v.) Caus. ii. <em>ākoṭapeti</em> Ja.iii.361.</p><p>a + koṭṭeti, Sk. kuṭṭayati; BSk. ākoṭayati e.g. Divy.117 dvāraṃ trir ā˚, Cowell “break” (?); Avs. Index p 222 s. v.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭita">ākoṭita</a></p>

ākoṭetvā|aako.tetvaa|akotetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭita|aako.tita|akotita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tita</span></p><p>beaten; knocked (at); pounded; hammered (in)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākoṭita|aako.tita|akotita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.tita</span></p><ol><li>beaten, touched, knocked against Ja.i.303; Mil.62 (of a gong).</li><li>pressed beaten down (tight), flattened, in phrase <em>ākoṭita-paccākoṭita</em> flattened &amp; pressed all round (of the cīvara) SN.ii.281; Dhp-a.i.37.</li></ol><p>pp. of ākoṭeti</p>

ākoṭitapaccākoṭita|aako.titapaccaako.tita|akotitapaccakotita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.titapaccaako.tita</span></p><p>beaten and pounded; pounded this way and that</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭita">ākoṭita</a></p>

ākoṭāpeti|aako.taapeti|akotapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aako.taapeti</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākoṭeti">ākoṭeti</a></p>

ākucca|aakucca|akucca
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakucca</span></p><p>A type of lizard Ja.vi.538.</p>

ākucca|aakucca|akucca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakucca</span></p><p>(or <em>-ā</em>?) an iguana Ja.vi.538 (C. godhā; gloss amatt’ākuccā).</p><p>etym. unknown, prob. non-Aryan</p>

ākula|aakula|akula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakula</span></p><p>adjective entangled, confused, upset, twisted, bewildered Ja.i.123 (salākaggaṃ ˚ṃ karoti to upset or disturb); Vv.84#9 (andha˚); Pv-a.287 (an˚ clear). Often reduplicated as <em>ākulākula</em> thoroughly confused Mil.117, Mil.220; Pv-a.56; <em>ākula-pākula</em> Ud.5 (so read for akkula-pakkula); <em>ākula-samākula</em> Ja.vi.270. On phrase <em>tantākula-</em> jātā gulā-guṇṭhika-jātā see <em><a href="bword://gu%E1%B8%B7%C4%81">guḷā</a></em>.</p><p>ā + <em>*kul</em> of which Sk<br />■ P. kula, to Idg *qṷel to turn round, cp. also cakka &amp; carati; lit. meaning “revolving quickly”, &amp; so “confused”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://guḷā">guḷā</a></p>

ākulaka|aakulaka|akulaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakulaka</span></p><p>adjective entangled DN.ii.55 (tant˚ for the usual tantākula, as given under guḷā).</p><p>fr. ākula</p>

ākulanīya|aakulaniiya|akulaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakulaniiya</span></p><p>adjective in <em>an˚</em>; not to be confounded or upset Pv-a.118.</p><p>grd. of ā + *kulāyati, Denom. of kula</p>

ākulī|aakulii|akuli
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakulii</span></p><p>(-puppha) at Kp-a.60 (milāta˚) read (according to Index p. 870) as milāta-bakula-puppha. Vism.260 (id. p. however reads ākulī-puppha “tangle-flower” (?), cp. Ud.5 gāthā 7 bakkula, which is preferably to be read as pākula.</p>

ākurati|aakurati|akurati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakurati</span></p><p>to be hoarse Mil.152 (kaṇṭho ākurati).</p><p>onomat. to sound-root <em>*kur = *kor</em> as in Lat. cornix, corvus etc. See gala note 2 B and cp. kukkuṭa kokila, khaṭa etc., all words expressing a rasping noise in the throat. The attempts at etym. by Trenckner (Mil p.425 as Denom. of ākula) &amp; Morris (J.P.T.S. 1886 154 as contr. Denom. of ankura “intumescence”, thus meaning “to swell”) are hardly correct</p>

ākāra|aakaara|akara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaara</span></p><ol><li>form, appearance; external characteristic, sign, gesture; expression; deceptive appearance, pretense.</li><li>mode; sort; aspect; way, manner.</li><li>cause, reason.</li><li>dividing up, detailing; definition.</li></ol>

ākāra|aakaara|akara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaara</span></p><p>“the (way of) making”, i.e.</p><ol><li>state, condition Ja.i.237 (avasan˚ condition of inhabitability); Ja.ii.154 (patan˚ state of falling, labile equilibrium) cp. paṇṇ˚</li><li>property, quality, attribute DN.i.76 (anāvila sabb˚-sampanna endowed with all good qualities, of a jewel); DN.ii.157 (˚varûpeta); Ja.ii.352 (sabb˚ paripuṇṇa altogether perfect in qualities)</li><li>sign, appearance form, DN.i.175; Ja.i.266 (chātak˚ sign of hunger); Mil.24 (˚ena by the sign of…); Vv-a.27 (therassa ā. form of the Th.); Pv-a.90, Pv-a.283 (rañño ā. the king’s person) Sdhp.363</li><li>way, mode, manner, <em>sa-ākāra</em> in all their modes DN.i.13 = DN.i.82 = DN.iii.111; Ja.i.266 (āgaman˚ the mode of his coming). Esp. in instr. sg. &amp; pl. with; <em>num.</em> or <em>pron.</em> (in this way, in two ways etc.): chah’ākārehi in a sixfold manner Cnd.680 (cp. kāraṇehi in same sense) Ne.73, Ne.74 (dvādasah’ākārehi); Vism.613 (navah’ākārehi indriyāni tikkhāni bhavanti); Pv-a.64 (yen’ākārena āgato ten’ākārena gato as he came so he went), Pv-a.99 (id.).</li><li>reason, ground, account DN.i.138, DN.i.139; Ne.4, Ne.8 sq. Ne.38; Dhp-a.i.14; Kp-a.100 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of evaṃ). In this meaning freq. with <em>dass</em> (dasseti, dassana, nidassana etc. in commentary style “what is meant by”, the (statement of) reason why or of, notion, idea Pv-a.26 (dātabb˚dassana), Pv-a.27 (thoman˚-dassana), Pv-a.75 (kāruññ ˚ṃ dassesi), Pv-a.121 (pucchan˚-nidassanaṃ what has been asked); Snp-a.135 (˚nidassana).</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://parivitakka">parivitakka</a></em> study of conditions, careful consideration examination of reasons SN.ii.115; SN.iv.138; AN.ii.191 Cnd.151.</li></ul><p>a + karoti, <em>kṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parivitakka">parivitakka</a></p>

ākāraka|aakaaraka|akaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaaraka</span></p><p>appearance; reason, manner (cp. ākāra<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) Ja.i.269 (ākārakena = kāraṇena C.).</p><p>ākāra + ka</p>

ākārava|aakaarava|akarava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaarava</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ākāravat">ākāravat</a></p><p>having a form; having a cause; grounded</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāra">ākāra</a></p>

ākāravant|aakaaravant|akaravant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaaravant</span></p><p>adjective having a reason, reasonable, founded MN.i.401 (saddhā).</p><p>fr. ākāra</p>

ākāravat|aakaaravat|akaravat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaaravat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ākāravat">ākāravat</a></p><p>having a form; having a cause; grounded</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāra">ākāra</a></p>

ākāsa dhātu|aakaasa dhaatu|akasa dhatu
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa dhaatu</span></p><p><strong>[[ākāsa dhātu]]Ākāsa dhātu:</strong> ‘space element’; see above and <em>dhātu</em></p>

ākāsa-kasina|aakaasa-kasina|akasa-kasina
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa-kasina</span></p><p><strong>Ākāsa-kasina:</strong> ‘space-kasina exercise’; see: <em>kasina</em></p>

ākāsa|aakaasa|akasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa</span></p><ol><li>the ether; air, sky; space; open space.</li><li>a game, playing in the air.</li></ol>

ākāsa|aakaasa|akasa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa</span></p><p><strong>Ākāsa:</strong> ‘space’, is, according to Com., of two kinds: 1. limited space <em>paricchinnākāsa</em> or <em>paricchedākāsa,</em> 2. endless space <em>anantākāsa,</em> i.e. cosmic space.</p><blockquote><ol><li><p>Limited space, under the name of <em>ākāsa</em> - <em>dhātu</em> space element, belongs to derived materiality see: <em>khandha</em> Summary I; Dhs. 638 and to a sixfold classification of elements see: <em>dhātu</em> M. 112, 115, 140. It is also an object of kasina meditation. It is defined as follows: The space element has the characteristic of delimiting matter or form. Its function is to indicate the boundaries of matter. It is manifested as the confines and container of matter or form; or its manifestation consists in being untouched by the 4 great elements, and in holes and openings. Its proximate cause is the matter delimited. It is on account of the space element that one can say of material things delimited that ‘this is above. below, around that’ Vis.M XIV, 63.</p></li><li><p>Endless space is in Atthasālini called <em>ajatākāsa</em> unentangled’, i.e. unobstructed or empty space. It is the object of the first formless absorption see: <em>jhāna</em>, the sphere of Infinite space <em>ākāsānañcāyatana</em>. According to Abhidhamma philosophy, endless space has no objective reality being purely conceptual, which is indicated by the fact that it is not included in the triad of the advantageous <em>kusalatika,</em> which comprises the entire reality. Later Buddhist schools have regarded it as one of several unconditioned or uncreated states <em>asankhata</em> <em>dharma</em> - a view that is rejected in Kath. see: Guide. p. 70. Theravāda Buddhism recognizes only <em>Nibbāna</em> as an unconditioned element <em>asankhata</em> <em>dhātu</em> see: Dhs. 1084.</p></li></ol></blockquote>

ākāsa|aakaasa|akasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa</span></p><p>Ākāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>air, sky, atmosphere; space. On the concept see <em>Cpd.</em> 5, 16, 226. On a fanciful etym. of ākāsa (fr. ā + kassati of <em>kṛṣ</em>) at Dhs-a.325 see <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 178. DN.i.55 (˚ṃ indriyāni sankamanti the sense-faculties pass into space); DN.iii.224, DN.iii.253, DN.iii.262, DN.iii.265; SN.iii.207; SN.iv.218 SN.v.49, SN.v.264; Ja.i.253; Ja.ii.353; Ja.iii.52, Ja.iii.188; Ja.iv.154; Ja.vi.126; Snp.944, Snp.1065; Mnd.428; Pv.ii.1#18; Snp-a.110, Snp-a.152; Pv-a.93; Sdhp.42, Sdhp.464. <em>-ākāsena</em> gacchati to go through the air Pv-a.75 (āgacch˚), Pv-a.103, Pv-a.105, Pv-a.162; ˚ena carati id. Ja.ii.103; ˚e gacchati id. Pv-a.65 (cando)<br />■ Formula <em>“ananto ākāso”</em> freq.; e.g. at DN.i.183; AN.ii.184; AN.iv.40, AN.iv.410 sq.; AN.v.345.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://anta">anta</a></em> “the end of the sky”, the sky, the air (on ˚anta see *<a href="bword://anta">anta</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 4) Ja.vi.89. <em>-ānañca</em> (or <em>ānañca</em>) the infinity ef space, in cpd. ˚āyatana the sphere or plane of the infinity of space, the “space-infinity-plane”, the sphere of unbounded space. The consciousness of this sphere forms the first one of the 4 (or 6) higher attainments or recognitions of the mind, standing beyond the fourth jhāna viz. 1 ākās˚, 2 viññāṇ’ānañc-āyatana 3 ākiñcaññ˚ 4 n’eva saññānâsaññ˚, 5 nirodha, 6 phala<br />■ DN.i.34 DN.i.183; DN.ii.70, DN.ii.112, DN.ii.156; DN.iii.224, DN.iii.262 sq.; MN.i.41, MN.i.159; MN.iii.27, MN.iii.44; SN.v.119; Pts.i.36; Dhs.205, Dhs.501, Dhs.579, Dhs.1418 Ne.26, Ne.39; Vism.326, Vism.340, Vism.453; DN-a.i.120 (see Nd ii.under ākāsa; Dhs.265 sq.; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 71). As classed with jhāna see also Cnd.672 (sādhu-vihārin). <em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> one of the kasiṇ’āyatanas (see under <em><a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em>) DN.iii.268; AN.i.41. <em>-<em>gaṅgā</em></em> Name of the celestial river Ja.i.95; Ja.iii.344. <em>-<a href="bword://gamana">gamana</a></em> going through the air (as a trick of elephants) Mil.201. <em>-cārika</em> walking through the air Ja.ii.103. <em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81rin">cārin</a></em> = ˚cārika Vv-a.6 <em>-ṭṭha</em> living in the sky (of devatā) Bv.i.29; Mil.181 Mil.285; Kp-a.120; Snp-a.476. <em>-<a href="bword://tala">tala</a></em> upper story, terrace on the top of a palace Snp-a.87. <em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the element of space DN.iii.247; MN.i.423; MN.iii.31; AN.i.176; AN.iii.34; Dhs.638.</p><p>Sk. ākāśa fr. ā + <em>kāś</em>, lit. shining forth, i.e. the illuminated space</p><p>Ākāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(nt.?) a game, playing chess ʻin the air’ <em>(sans voir)</em> Vin.ii.10 = DN.i.6 (= aṭṭhapada-dasapadesu viya ākāse yeva kiḷanaṃ DN-a.i.85).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://anta">anta</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, gaṅgā, <a href="bword://gamana">gamana</a>, <a href="bword://cārin">cārin</a>, <a href="bword://tala">tala</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a></p>

ākāsagata|aakaasagata|akasagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasagata</span></p><p>in the air; belonging to the sky or space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsaṅgata">ākāsaṅgata</a>, <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ākāsagotta|aakaasagotta|akasagotta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasagotta</span></p><p>A physician of Rājagaha who lanced the fistula of a monk. Meeting the Buddha, he told him of the lancing, trying to make fun of it. The Buddha, having made inquiries, declared the performance of such an operation a thullaccaya offence. Vin.i.215–216</p>

ākāsaka|aakaasaka|akasaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasaka</span></p><p>adjective being in or belonging to the air or sky Ja.vi.124.</p><p>ākāsa + ka</p>

ākāsakasiṇa|aakaasakasi.na|akasakasina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasakasi.na</span></p><p>concentration on space, meditation based on space; the meditation object space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ākāsati|aakaasati|akasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasati</span></p><p>to shine Ja.vi.89.</p><p>fr. ākāsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ākāsaṅgata|aakaasa"ngata|akasangata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa"ngata</span></p><p>in the air; belonging to the sky or space</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsagata">ākāsagata</a>, <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ākāsaṭṭha|aakaasa.t.tha|akasattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasa.t.tha</span></p><p>living in the sky; being in the sky; being in the air</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ākāsānañcāyatana|aakaasaana~ncaayatana|akasanancayatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasaana~ncaayatana</span></p><p>the sphere or stage of the infinity of space, i.e.</p><ol><li>the first of the arūpa states of existence; and.</li><li>the state of the consciousness of the infinity of space, the first arūpajhāna or one of the vimokkhas</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ākāsānañcāyatana|aakaasaana~ncaayatana|akasanancayatana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasaana~ncaayatana</span></p><p><strong>Ākāsānañcāyatana:</strong> ‘Sphere of Infinite space’, is identical with the 1st formless absorption; see: <em>jhāna</em> 5.</p>

ākāsānañcāyatanūpagādevā|aakaasaana~ncaayatanuupagaadevaa|akasanancayatanupagadeva
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aakaasaana~ncaayatanuupagaadevaa</span></p><p>A class of devas born in the Realm of Infinite Space. MN.iii.103 They belong to the Formless world and their life term is twenty thousand kappas. AN.i.267</p>

ālaggeti|aalaggeti|alaggeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaggeti</span></p><p>to (make) hang on to (loc.), to stick on, fasten to Vin.ii.110 (pattaṃ veḷagge ālaggetvā).</p><p>ā + Caus. of <em>lag</em></p>

ālaggetvā|aalaggetvaa|alaggetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaggetvaa</span></p><p>fastening, hanging (on, loc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālaggetvāna">ālaggetvāna</a></p>

ālaggetvāna|aalaggetvaana|alaggetvana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaggetvaana</span></p><p>fastening, hanging (on, loc.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālaggetvā">ālaggetvā</a></p>

ālaka-manda|aalaka-manda|alaka-manda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaka-manda</span></p><p>Ālaka-manda</p><p>at Vin.ii.152 is of uncertain reading and meaning (“open to view”? or “not having pegs” āḷaka?) vv.ll. āḷakamanta &amp; ālakamandāra; Bdhgh on p. 321 explains āḷakamandā ti ekangaṇā manussâbhikiṇṇā i.e. full of a crowd of people, Ch. quotes ālakamandā as “the city of Kuvera” (cp. Sk. alakā).</p><p>ālaya˚?</p>

ālakamanda|aalakamanda|alakamanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalakamanda</span></p><p>open, unenclosed; or crowded (like Āḷakamandā);</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āḷakamanda">āḷakamanda</a></p>

ālamba|aalamba|alamba
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalamba</span></p><p>anything to hang on, support SN.i.53 (an˚ without support); Snp.173 (id. + appatiṭṭha); Ja.iii.396; Mil.343; Sdhp.245, Sdhp.463.</p><p>Sk. ālamba, ā + <em>lamb</em></p>

ālambana|aalambana|alambana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambana</span></p><p>something to hold on to or lean on, support; a staff; a support on a carriage</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālambaṇa">ālambaṇa</a></p>

ālambana|aalambana|alambana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambana</span></p><p>adjective noun (adj.) hanging down from, hanging up Ja.iii.396; Ja.iv.457; Snp-a.214<br />■ (nt.) support, balustrade (or screen?) Vin.ii.117 Vin.ii.152 (˚bāha) Mil.126. Alambara &amp; Alambara;</p><p>fr. ā + <em>lamb</em>, cp. ālamba</p>

ālambanabāhā|aalambanabaahaa|alambanabaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambanabaahaa</span></p><p>rail, a banister</p><p>See also: ālamabana</p>

ālambara|aalambara|alambara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambara</span></p><p>drum</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āḷambara">āḷambara</a></p>

ālambara|aalambara|alambara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambara</span></p><p>Ālambara &amp; Āḷambara</p><p>neuter a drum Vin.i.15 (l); Ja.ii.344 (ḷ); Ja.v.390 (l); Vv.54#18 (ḷ).</p><p>Sk. āḍambara</p>

ālambati|aalambati|alambati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambati</span></p><p>to hang on to or up, to take hold of, to fasten to Vin.i.28, Ja.i.57; Ja.vi.192; Vv.84#48; Thag-a.34<br />■ <em>ālambeti</em> id. Vv-a.32.</p><p>ā + <em>lamb</em></p>

ālambaṇa|aalamba.na|alambana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalamba.na</span></p><p>something to hold on to or lean on, support; a staff; a support on a carriage</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālambana">ālambana</a></p>

ālambitvā|aalambitvaa|alambitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalambitvaa</span></p><p>held on to; clung to.</p>

ālapana|aalapana|alapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalapana</span></p><p>address, addressing, speaking to; (the sense of) the vocative case.</p>

ālapana|aalapana|alapana
