ālinda|aalinda|alinda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalinda</span></p><p>Ālinda &amp; Āḷinda</p><p>a terrace or verandah before the house-door Vin.i.248; Vin.ii.153; DN.i.89; MN.ii.119; SN.iv.290 (ḷ); AN.v.65 (ḷ); Ja.vi.429; DN-a.i.252; Dhp-a.i.26; Dhp-a.iv.196; Snp-a.55 (˚ka-vāsin; variant reading alindaka); Mhvs.35, Mhvs.3. As <em>ālindaka</em> at Ja.iii.283.</p><p>Sk. alinda</p>

ālippati|aalippati|alippati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalippati</span></p><p>Pass. of <em><a href="bword://%C4%81limpeti">ālimpeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālimpeti">ālimpeti</a></p>

ālitta|aalitta|alitta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalitta</span></p><p>smeared, anointed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālimpati">ālimpati</a></p>

ālitta|aalitta|alitta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalitta</span></p><p>besmeared, stained Thag.737.</p><p>pp. of ālimpati; Sk. ālipta</p>

āliṅga|aali"nga|alinga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"nga</span></p><p>small drum Ja.v.156 (suvaṇṇ˚-tala).</p><p>ā + ling</p>

āliṅgati|aali"ngati|alingati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"ngati</span></p><p>embraces, encircles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āliṅgeti">āliṅgeti</a></p>

āliṅgati|aali"ngati|alingati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"ngati</span></p><p>to embrace, enfold DN.i.230; DN.iii.73; Ja.i.281; Ja.iv.21, Ja.iv.316, Ja.iv.438; Ja.v.8; Mil.7; Dhp-a.i.101: Vv-a.260.</p><p>ā + <em>liṅg</em></p>

āliṅgeti|aali"ngeti|alingeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"ngeti</span></p><p>embraces, encircles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āliṅgati">āliṅgati</a></p>

āliṅgetvā|aali"ngetvaa|alingetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"ngetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āliṅgeti">āliṅgeti</a></p>

āliṅgitvā|aali"ngitvaa|alingitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aali"ngitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āliṅgati">āliṅgati</a></p>

āloka-kasina|aaloka-kasina|aloka-kasina
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloka-kasina</span></p><p><strong>Āloka-kasina:</strong> L ight-kasina-meditation on bright light or the white color; see: <em>kasina</em></p>

āloka|aaloka|aloka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloka</span></p><ol><li>light, daylight; luster, brightness.</li><li>insight, vision; the light of knowledge, awakening.</li></ol>

āloka|aaloka|aloka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloka</span></p><p>seeing, sight (obj. &amp; subj.), i.e.</p><ol><li>sight, view, look SN.iv.128 = Snp.763; AN.iii.236 (āloke nikkhitta laid before one’s eye). <em>anāloka</em> without sight, blind Mil.296 (andha +).</li><li>light AN.i.164 (tamo vigato ā. uppanno) = Iti.100 (vihato); AN.ii.139 (four lights, i.e. canda˚, suriya˚, agg˚, paññ˚, of the moon, sun, fire &amp; wisdom); Ja.ii.34; Dhs.617 (opp. andhakāra); Vv-a.51 (dīp˚).</li><li>(clear) sight, power of observation, intuition, in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with vijjā knowledge DN.ii.33 = SN.ii.7 = SN.ii.105, cp. Pts.ii.150 sq. (obhāsaṭṭhena, SN-a on SN.ii.7).</li><li>splendour Vv-a.53; Vv-a.71.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kara">kara</a></em> making light, bringing light, n. light-bringer Iti.108.</li><li><em>-karaṇa</em> making light, illumining Iti.108</li><li><em>-da</em> giving light or insight Thag.3</li><li><em>-dassana</em> seeing light, i.e. perceiving Thag.422</li><li><em>-pharaṇa</em> diffusing light or diffusion of light Vb.334 Netti.89</li><li><em>-bahula</em> good in sight figuratively full of foresight AN.iii.432</li><li><em>-bhūta</em> light Ja.vi.459</li><li><em>-saññā</em> consciousness or faculty of sight or perception DN.iii.223 AN.ii.45 AN.iii.93</li><li><em>-saññin</em> conscious of sight i.e. susceptible to sight or insight DN.iii.49 MN.iii.3 AN.ii.211 AN.iii.92 AN.iii.323 AN.iv.437 AN.v.207 Pp.69</li><li><em>-sandhi</em> “break for the light”, a slit to look through, an opening a crack or casement Vin.i.48 = Vin.ii.209 = Vin.ii.218; Vin.ii.172 Vin.iii.65; Vin.iv.47 Ja.iv.310 Pv-a.24</li></ul><p>ā + <em>lok</em>, Sk. āloka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kara">kara</a></p>

āloka-saññā|aaloka-sa~n~naa|aloka-sanna
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloka-sa~n~naa</span></p><p><strong>Āloka-saññā:</strong> Perception of light. The recurring canonical passage reads: Here the Bhikkhu contemplates the perception of light. He fixes his-mind to the experience of the daylight; as at day-time so at night, and as at night, so in the day. In this way, with a clear and unclouded mind, he develops the stage of mind that is full of brightness. It is one of the methods of overcoming drowsiness, recommended by the Buddha to Mahā-Moggallāna A. VII, 58. According to D. 33, it is conducive to the development of ‘knowledge and vision’ see: <em>visuddhi VI + VII,</em> and it is said to be helpful to the attainment of the ‘divine eye’ see: <em>abhiññā 5.</em></p>

ālokada|aalokada|alokada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokada</span></p><p>giving light</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloka">āloka</a></p>

ālokakaraṇa|aalokakara.na|alokakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokakara.na</span></p><p>light-giving, illuminitating</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloka">āloka</a></p>

ālokana|aalokana|alokana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokana</span></p><p>looking at, regarding DN-a.i.194.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>lok</em></p>

ālokapharaṇa|aalokaphara.na|alokapharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokaphara.na</span></p><p>pervasion or pervading with light or insight</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloka">āloka</a></p>

ālokasandhi|aalokasandhi|alokasandhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokasandhi</span></p><p>window; an opening to let the light in; shutters</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloka">āloka</a></p>

ālokaya|aalokaya|alokaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokaya</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ālokayat">ālokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālokayati">ālokayati</a></p>

ālokayat|aalokayat|alokayat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokayat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ālokayat">ālokayat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālokayati">ālokayati</a></p>

ālokayati|aalokayati|alokayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokayati</span></p><p>looks at; looks forward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloketi">āloketi</a></p>

āloketabba|aaloketabba|aloketabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloketabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloketi">āloketi</a></p>

āloketar|aaloketar|aloketar
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloketar</span></p><p>one who looks forward or before, a beholder DN-a.i.194 (opp. viloketar).</p><p>n. ag. to āloketi</p>

āloketi|aaloketi|aloketi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloketi</span></p><p>looks at; looks forward</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālokayati">ālokayati</a></p>

āloketi|aaloketi|aloketi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaloketi</span></p><p>to look before, look at, regard, see DN-a.i.193, DN-a.i.194<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81lokita">ālokita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. ālokayati, ā + <em>lok</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālokita">ālokita</a></p>

ālokita|aalokita|alokita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokita</span></p><p>looking at, looking forwards</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloketi">āloketi</a></p>

ālokita|aalokita|alokita
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokita</span></p><p>looking before, looking at, looking forward (opp. vilokitaṃ looking behind or backward), always in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> <em>ālokita-vilokita</em> in ster. phrase at DN.i.70 = e.g. AN.ii.104, AN.ii.106, AN.ii.210; Pp.44, Pp.45, Pp.50 Vism.19; Vv-a.6; DN-a.i.193 (ālokitaṃ purato pekkhanaṃ vil˚ anudisā p.).</p><p>pp. of āloketi</p>

ālokitabba|aalokitabba|alokitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalokitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloketi">āloketi</a></p>

ālopa|aalopa|alopa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalopa</span></p><ol><li>a portion, a bit; a mouthful.</li><li>plunder, pillage.</li></ol>

ālopa|aalopa|alopa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalopa</span></p><p>piece (cut off), a bit (of food) morsel, esp. bits of food gathered by bhikkhus DN.i.5 = AN.v.206; AN.iii.176; AN.ii.209; AN.iii.304; AN.iv.318; Thag.1055; Iti.18; Pv.ii.1#7; Pp.58; Mil.231, Mil.406; Vism.106; DN-a.i.80 (= vilopa-karaṇaṃ).</p><p>ā + <em>lup</em>, cp. ālumpati; BSk. ālopa, e.g. Avs.i.173, Avs.i.341; Divy.290, Divy.481</p>

ālopati|aalopati|alopati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalopati</span></p><p>robs; polunders.</p>

ālopati|aalopati|alopati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalopati</span></p><p>to break in, plunder, violate Thag.743.</p><p>ālopeti? ā + lopeti, Caus. of ālumpati</p>

ālopika|aalopika|alopika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalopika</span></p><p>adjective getting or having, or consisting of pieces (of food) AN.i.295; AN.ii.206; Pp.55.</p><p>ālopa + ika</p>

āloḷa|aalo.la|alola
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.la</span></p><p>confusion, uproar, agitation Dhp-a.i.38.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>luḷ</em>, cp. āluḷati &amp; āloḷeti</p>

āloḷayati|aalo.layati|alolayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.layati</span></p><p>cause. pr. 3 sg. stirs up, disturbs; mixes, confuses, confounds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloḷeti">āloḷeti</a></p>

āloḷeti|aalo.leti|aloleti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.leti</span></p><p>cause. pr. 3 sg. stirs up, disturbs; mixes, confuses, confounds</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloḷayati">āloḷayati</a></p>

āloḷeti|aalo.leti|aloleti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.leti</span></p><p>to confuse, mix, shake together, jumble SN.i.175; Ja.ii.272, Ja.ii.363; Ja.iv.333; Ja.vi.331 Vism.105.</p><p>Caus. of āluḷati, cp. āluḷeti</p>

āloḷetvā|aalo.letvaa|aloletva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.letvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āloḷeti">āloḷeti</a></p>

āloḷī|aalo.lii|aloli
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalo.lii</span></p><p>that which is stirred up, mud, in cpd. sītāloḷī mud or loam from the furrow adhering to the plough Vin.i.206.</p><p>a + <em>luḷ</em></p>

ālu|aalu|alu
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalu</span></p><p>A type of plant with a tuberous root Ja.iv.371 Ja.iv.373 Ja.vi.578.</p>

ālu|aalu|alu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalu</span></p><p>bulbous plant, Radix; Globosa Esculenta or Amorphophallus (Kern), Arum Campanulatum (Hardy) Ja.iv.371 = Ja.vi.578; Ja.iv.373.</p><p>Sk. ālu &amp; ˚ka; cognate with Lat. ālum &amp; alium, see Walde Lat. Wtb. under alium</p>

āluka|aaluka|aluka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaluka</span></p><p>This is used generally to mean a tuber. It may have also been a name for <em>Dioscorea alata</em> Ap.17, a stout climber commonly cultivated for its large edible tuber. See <em>Rukkha</em>.</p><p>See also: Rukkha</p>

āluka|aaluka|aluka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaluka</span></p><p>Āluka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>= ālu Ja.iv.46 (C. for ālupa).</p><p>Āluka<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective susceptiblé of, longing for, affected with (-˚) Vin.i.288 (sīt˚); DN-a.i.198 (id.); Ja.ii.278 (taṇh˚ greedy).</p><p>etym.?</p>

ālumpakāra|aalumpakaara|alumpakara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalumpakaara</span></p><p>breaking off, falling off (?) or forming into bits(?) Dhp-a.ii.55 (˚gūtha).</p><p>reading not sure, to ālumpati or ālopa</p>

ālumpati|aalumpati|alumpati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalumpati</span></p><p>tears out; pulls out.</p>

ālumpati|aalumpati|alumpati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalumpati</span></p><p>to pull out, break off MN.i.324.</p><p>ā + <em>lup</em> or <em>lump</em>, cp. ālopa</p>

ālupa|aalupa|alupa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalupa</span></p><p>= āluka the edible root of Amorphophallus Campanulatus Ja.iv.46 (= āluka-kaṇḍa C.).</p><p>the form <em>āluva</em> occurs at Tha-ap.237.</p><p>etym.? Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. suggests ālu-a → āluva → ālupa</p>

āluppakārakaṃ|aaluppakaaraka.m|aluppakarakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaluppakaaraka.m</span></p><p>making it into lumps; tearing of bits.</p>

āluḷa|aalu.la|alula
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalu.la</span></p><p>adjective being in motion, confusion or agitation, disturbed, agitated Ja.vi.431.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>lul</em></p>

āluḷati|aalu.lati|alulati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalu.lati</span></p><p>to move here &amp; there, ppr. med.; <em>āluḷamāna</em> agitated, whirling about Dhp-a.iv.47 (T. ālūl˚; variant reading āḷul˚) confuse Dhs-a.375 Caus. <strong>āluḷeti</strong> to set in motion, agitate, confound Ja.ii.9 Ja.ii.33<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81lu%E1%B8%B7ita">āluḷita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>lul</em>; Sk. ālolati, cp. also P. āloḷeti</p><p>See also: āluḷeti, <a href="bword://āluḷita">āluḷita</a></p>

āluḷita|aalu.lita|alulita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalu.lita</span></p><p>agitated, confused Ja.ii.101; Mil.397 (+ khalita).</p><p>pp. of āluḷeti</p>

ālāna|aalaana|alana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaana</span></p><p>Ālāna &amp; Āḷāna</p><p>neuter a peg, stake, post, esp one to which an elephant is tied Ja.i.415; Ja.iv.308; Dhp-a.i.126 (ḷ) where all MSS. have āḷāhana, perhaps correctly.</p><p>for ānāhana with substitution of l for n (cp. apilandhana for apinandh˚ and contraction of ˚āhana to ˚āna originally meaning “tying to” then the thing to which anything is tied</p>

ālārika|aalaarika|alarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaarika</span></p><p>cook</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālāriya">ālāriya</a></p>

ālāriya|aalaariya|alariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aalaariya</span></p><p>cook</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālārika">ālārika</a></p>

āma|aama|ama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama</span></p><p>āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>ind. (particle of assent or approval or recollection, usually followed by voc. and/or affirming clause): yes, indeed, certainly.</p><p>āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. raw, uncooked; unbaked; unripe, immature; undigested.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmo">āmo</a></p>

āma|aama|ama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama</span></p><p>Āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable affirmative part. “yes, indeed, certainly” DN.i.192 sq. (as variant reading BB.; T. has āmo); Ja.i.115 Ja.i.226 (in C. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of T. amā-jāta which is to be read for āmajāta); Ja.ii.92; Ja.v.448; Mil.11, Mil.19, Mil.253; Dhp-a.i.10, Dhp-a.i.34 Dhp-a.ii.39, Dhp-a.ii.44; Vv-a.69; Pv-a.12, Pv-a.22, Pv-a.56, Pv-a.61, Pv-a.75, Pv-a.93 etc.</p><p>a specific Pāli formation representing either amma (q.v.) or a gradation of pron. base amu˚ “that (see <em><a href="bword://asu">asu</a></em>), thus deictic-emphatic exclam<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> Cp. also BSk āma e.g. Avs.i.36</p><p>Āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective raw, viz.</p><ol><li>unbaked (of an earthen vessel), unfinished Snp.443.</li><li>uncooked (of flesh), nt. raw flesh, only in foll. compounds: <em>-gandha</em> “smell of raw flesh”, verminous odour; a smell attributed in particular to rotting corpses (cp similarly BSk. āmagandha Mvu.iii.214) DN.ii.242 sq. AN.i.280; Snp.241, Snp.242 (= vissagandha kuṇapagandha Snp-a.286), Snp.248, Snp.251; Dhs.625; and <em>-giddha</em> greedy after flesh (used as bait) Ja.vi.416 (= āmasankhāta āmisa C.).</li></ol><p>Vedic āma = Gr. ὠμός, connected with Lat. amārus. The more common P. form is <em><a href="bword://%C4%81maka">āmaka</a></em> (q.v.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://asu">asu</a>, <a href="bword://āmaka">āmaka</a></p>

āmaddana|aamaddana|amaddana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamaddana</span></p><p>crushing Vv-a.311.</p><p>ā + maddana of <em>mṛd</em></p>

āmagandha|aamagandha|amagandha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamagandha</span></p><p>the smell of raw flesh, carrion; stench, foulness; (fig.) moral foulness, corruption</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āma">āma</a></p>

āmaka|aamaka|amaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamaka</span></p><p>adjective raw, uncooked DN.i.5 = Pp.58 (˚maṃsa raw flesh); MN.i.80 (titta-kalābu āmaka-cchinno).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://dha%C3%B1%C3%B1a">dhañña</a></em> “raw” grain, corn in its natural, unprepared state DN.i.5 = Pp.58 (see DN-a.i.78 for definition); Vin.iv.264; Vin.v.135.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81ka">sāka</a></em> raw vegetables Vism.70.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sus%C4%81na">susāna</a></em> “cemetery of raw flesh” charnelgrove (cp. āmagandha under ama<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), i.e. fetid smelling cremation ground Ja.i.264, Ja.i.489 Ja.iv.45 sq.; Ja.vi.10; Dhp-a.i.176; Vv-a.76; Pv-a.196.</li></ul><p>= āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhañña">dhañña</a>, <a href="bword://sāka">sāka</a>, <a href="bword://susāna">susāna</a></p>

āmakachinna|aamakachinna|amakachinna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamakachinna</span></p><p>cut while unripe.</p>

āmakamatta|aamakamatta|amakamatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamakamatta</span></p><p>or neuter unbaked, still unbaked; or an unbaked pot or vessel.</p>

āmalaka|aamalaka|amalaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalaka</span></p><p><em>Emblica officinalis</em>, Hindi <em>amal</em> Ja.iv.363 Vin.i.277 Vin.ii.149, a medium-sized tree with greenish-gray bark, yellow flowers and a pale-green sour fruit with a large hard fluted seed. Together with <em><a href="bword://har%C4%ABtaka">harītaka</a></em> and <em><a href="bword://vibh%C4%ABtaka">vibhītaka</a></em> this fruit is one of the <em>triphala</em> or “three fruits”, long credited in traditional Indian medicine with powerful curative properties. It contains high concentrations of gallic acid and is rich in vitamin A, C and iron and either fresh or dried is taken for diarrhea and dyspepsia. It is also used in tonics or eaten dried, pickled or made into a tasty marmalade. Wood chips from the tree are used to clarify muddy water and wood cutters and honey gatherers working in the forest often take the fruit to suppress their thirst.</p><p>Medical uses for <em>Āmalaka</em></p><p>Bark: applied to sores, pimples, with the bark of<em>Dillenia pentagyna</em> for tubercular fistula; for cholera, dysentery, diarrhea. Leaf: For gravel, diarrhea and sores. Fruit: Refrigerant, diuretic, laxative, for indigestion, with Swertia and fenugreek for gonorrhea. Raw fruit: aperient, dried and used in haemorrhagia, diarrhea, as a liver tonic, for scurvy, the juice as an eye drop. Seeds: For asthma and stomach disorders.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://harītaka">harītaka</a>, <a href="bword://vibhītaka">vibhītaka</a></p>

āmalaka|aamalaka|amalaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalaka</span></p><p>the fruit of the āmalaka plant, the Indian gooseberry (phyllanthus emblica)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmalakī">āmalakī</a></p>

āmalaka|aamalaka|amalaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalaka</span></p><p>emblic myrobalan, Phyllanthus Emblica Vin.i.201, Vin.i.278; Vin.ii.149 (˚vaṇṭika pīthu); SN.i.150; AN.v.170; Snp.p.125 (˚matti); Ja.iv.363; Ja.v.380 (as variant reading for T. āmala); Mil.11; Dhp-a.i.319; Vv-a.7.</p><p>cp. Sk. āmalaka</p>

āmalakapatta|aamalakapatta|amalakapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalakapatta</span></p><p>leaf of the āmalaka plant.</p>

āmalakī|aamalakii|amalaki
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalakii</span></p><p>the Indian gooseberry plant (phyllanthus emblica)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmalaka">āmalaka</a></p>

āmalakī|aamalakii|amalaki
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalakii</span></p><p>āmalaka Vin.i.30; MN.i.456 (˚vana).</p>

āmalakīvana|aamalakiivana|amalakivana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalakiivana</span></p><p>ĀmalakīvanaĀmalakivana</p><p>A grove at Cātumā. The Buddha once stayed there, and it was on that occasion that the Cātumā Sutta was preached. MN.i.456</p><p>Cātumā3park</p>

āmalavaṇṭaka|aamalava.n.taka|amalavantaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalava.n.taka</span></p><p>the name or description of a type of chair</p><p>See also: āmalakavaṭṭika, āmalakavaṇṭika, <a href="bword://āmalaka">āmalaka</a></p>

āmalavaṇṭika|aamalava.n.tika|amalavantika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalava.n.tika</span></p><p>the name or description of a type of chair</p><p>See also: āmalakavaṭṭika, āmalakavaṇṭaka, <a href="bword://āmalaka">āmalaka</a></p>

āmalavaṭṭika|aamalava.t.tika|amalavattika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamalava.t.tika</span></p><p>the name or description of a type of chair</p><p>See also: āmalakavaṇṭika, āmalakavaṇṭaka, <a href="bword://āmalaka">āmalaka</a></p>

āmanta|aamanta|amanta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamanta</span></p><p>adjective adverb asking or asked, invited; only as <em>an˚</em>; without being asked, unasked, uninvited Vin.i.254 (˚cāra); AN.iii.259 (id.).</p><p>either ger. of āmanteti (q.v.) or root der. fr. ā + <em>mant</em>, cp. āmantaṇā</p>

āmantana|aamantana|amantana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantana</span></p><p>addressing; speaking to; invitation; inviting.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmantana">āmantana</a></p>

āmantana|aamantana|amantana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantana</span></p><p><em>-nā</em> (f., also <em>-ṇā</em>) addressing, calling; invitation, greeting Snp.40 (ep. Cnd.128) <em>-vacana</em> the address-form of speech i.e. the vocative case (cp. Sk. āmantritaṃ id.) Snp-a.435; Kp-a.167.</p><p>from āmanteti</p>

āmantanaka|aamantanaka|amantanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantanaka</span></p><p>adjective noun addressing, speaking to, conversing; f. <em>-ikā</em> interlocutor, companion, favourite queen Vv.18#8 (= allāpa-sallāpa-yoggā kīḷanakāle vā tena (i.e. Sakkena) āmantetabbā Vv-a.96).</p><p>fr. āmantana</p>

āmantayati|aamantayati|amantayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantayati</span></p><p>summons; addresses, speaks to; informs, consults; takes leave of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmanteti">āmanteti</a></p>

āmantayi|aamantayi|amantayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantayi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmantayati">āmantayati</a></p>

āmantaṇa|aamanta.na|amantana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamanta.na</span></p><p>addressing; speaking to; invitation; inviting.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmantana">āmantana</a></p>

āmantaṇīya|aamanta.niiya|amantaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamanta.niiya</span></p><p>adjective to be addressed Ja.iv.371.</p><p>grd. of āmanteti</p>

āmantesi|aamantesi|amantesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmanteti">āmanteti</a></p>

āmantesuṃ|aamantesu.m|amantesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmanteti">āmanteti</a></p>

āmanteti|aamanteti|amanteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamanteti</span></p><p>summons; addresses, speaks to; informs, consults; takes leave of</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmantayati">āmantayati</a></p>

āmanteti|aamanteti|amanteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamanteti</span></p><p>to call, address, speak to, invite, consult Ja.vi.265; DN-a.i.297; Snp-a.487 (ālapati &amp; avhayati); Pv-a.75, Pv-a.80, Pv-a.127<br />aor <em><a href="bword://%C4%81mantesi">āmantesi</a></em> DN.ii.16; Snp.p.78 (= ālapi Snp-a.394) &amp; in poetry; <em>āmantayi</em> Snp.997; Pv.ii.2#7; Pv.ii.3#7 (perhaps better with variant reading SS samantayi)<br />ger <em>āmanta</em> (= Sk. *āmantrya) Ja.iii.209 Ja.iii.315 (= āmantayitvā’ C.), Ja.iii.329; Ja.iv.111; Ja.v.233; Ja.vi.511. pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81mantita">āmantita</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus 2 <em>āmantāpeti</em> to invite to come, to cause to be called, to send for DN.i.134 (variant reading āmanteti); Mil.149.</p><p>denom. of ā + *mantra</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmantesi">āmantesi</a>, <a href="bword://āmantita">āmantita</a></p>

āmantetvā|aamantetvaa|amantetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmanteti">āmanteti</a></p>

āmantita|aamantita|amantita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantita</span></p><p>summoned; invited; addressed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmanteti">āmanteti</a></p>

āmantita|aamantita|amantita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantita</span></p><p>addressed, called, invited Pv.ii.3#13 (= nimantita Pv-a.86).</p><p>pp. of āmanteti</p>

āmantāpetvā|aamantaapetvaa|amantapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamantaapetvaa</span></p><p>making summon; making speak to, address; making take leave of.</p>

āmasabrahmacāri|aamasabrahmacaari|amasabrahmacari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasabrahmacaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āmasabrahmacārin">āmasabrahmacārin</a></p><p>fellow religious student belonging to the same house or community.</p>

āmasabrahmacārin|aamasabrahmacaarin|amasabrahmacarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasabrahmacaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āmasabrahmacārin">āmasabrahmacārin</a></p><p>fellow religious student belonging to the same house or community.</p>

āmasana|aamasana|amasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasana</span></p><p>touching; touch; considering.</p>

āmasana|aamasana|amasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasana</span></p><p>touching, handling; touch Vin.iv.214. Cp.iii.11#8; Mil.127, Mil.306; DN-a.i.78.</p><p>fr. āmasati</p>

āmasati|aamasati|amasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasati</span></p><p>touches, lays hold on; touches on, refers to; considers.</p>

āmasati|aamasati|amasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasati</span></p><p>to touch (upon), to handle, to lay hold on Vin.ii.221; Vin.iii.48 (kumbhiṃ); Ja.iii.319 (id.); AN.v.263, AN.v.266; Ja.iv.67; Pts.ii.209; Mil.306; Snp-a.400; Dhs-a.302; Vv-a.17<br />aor <em><a href="bword://%C4%81masi">āmasi</a></em> Ja.ii.360; ger <em>āmasitvā</em> Vin.iii.140 (udakapattaṃ) Ja.ii.330; grd. <em>āmassa</em> Ja.ii.360 (an˚) and <em>āmasitabba</em> id. (Comm.)<br />pp <em>āmaṭṭha &amp; āmasita;</em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + masati fr. <em>mṛś</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmasi">āmasi</a></p>

āmasi|aamasi|amasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmasati">āmasati</a></p>

āmasita|aamasita|amasita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasita</span></p><p>touched, taken hold of, occupied Vv-a.113 (an˚ khetta virgin land).</p><p>pp. of āmasati</p>

āmasitvā|aamasitvaa|amasitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamasitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmasati">āmasati</a></p>

āmata|aamata|amata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamata</span></p><p>almost dead, near to death.</p>

āmata|aamata|amata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamata</span></p><p>in anāmata at Ja.ii.56 is métric for <em><a href="bword://amata">amata</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://amata">amata</a></p>

āmattikā|aamattikaa|amattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamattikaa</span></p><p>earthenware, crockery; in <em>-āpaṇa</em> a crockery shop, chandler’s shop Vin.iv.243.</p><p>ā + mattikā</p>

āmattikāpaṇa|aamattikaapa.na|amattikapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamattikaapa.na</span></p><p>pot-seller’s stall, a shop or stall for pots.</p>

āmavasesa|aamavasesa|amavasesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamavasesa</span></p><p>remnants of undigested food</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmavasesaka">āmavasesaka</a>, <a href="bword://āma">āma</a></p>

āmavasesaka|aamavasesaka|amavasesaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamavasesaka</span></p><p>remnants of undigested food</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmavasesa">āmavasesa</a>, <a href="bword://āma">āma</a></p>

āmaya|aamaya|amaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamaya</span></p><p>affliction, illness, misery; only as <em>an˚</em>; (adj.) not afflicted, not decaying, healthy well (cp. BSk. nirāmaya Aśvaghoṣa ii.9) Vin.i.294; Vv.15#10 (= aroga Vv-a.74); Vv.17#7 ; Vv.36#8 ; Ja.iii.260, Ja.iii.528; Ja.iv.427; Ja.vi.23. Positive only very late, e.g. Sdhp.397.</p><p>etym.? cp. Sk. āmaya</p>

āmaṇḍa|aama.n.da|amanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.n.da</span></p><p>the fruit of the <em>āmaṇḍa</em> plant.</p>

āmaṇḍalikā|aama.n.dalikaa|amandalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.n.dalikaa</span></p><p>(rough) circle; a group</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmaṇḍaliyā">āmaṇḍaliyā</a></p>

āmaṇḍaliya|aama.n.daliya|amandaliya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.n.daliya</span></p><p>formation resembling a circle, in phrase <em>-ṃ karoti</em> to form a ring (of people or a circle, to stand closely together MN.i.225 (cp. Sk āmaṇḍalikaroti).</p><p>ā + maṇḍala + iya</p>

āmaṇḍaliyā|aama.n.daliyaa|amandaliya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.n.daliyaa</span></p><p>(rough) circle; a group</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmaṇḍalikā">āmaṇḍalikā</a></p>

āmaṭṭha|aama.t.tha|amattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.t.tha</span></p><p>touched; touched upon; considered; touching</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmasati">āmasati</a></p>

āmaṭṭha|aama.t.tha|amattha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aama.t.tha</span></p><p>touched, handled Ja.i.98 (an˚); DN-a.i.107 (= parāmaṭṭha); Sdhp.333.</p><p>Sk. āmṛṣṭa, pp. of āmasati; cp. āmasita</p>

āmeṇḍita|aame.n.dita|amendita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aame.n.dita</span></p><p>(or <em>Āmeḍita</em>) -(nt.) sympathy in ˚ṃ karoti to show sympathy (? so Morris J.P.T.S. 1887, 106) DN-a.i.228; Snp-a.155 (variant reading at DA āmeḍita).</p><p>Sk. āmreḍita fram ā + <em>mreḍ</em>, dialectical</p>

āmilāka|aamilaaka|amilaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamilaaka</span></p><p>? a woollen cover into which a floral pattern is woven DN-a.i.87.</p><p>etym.?</p>

āmisa|aamisa|amisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisa</span></p><ol><li>flesh; food; bait, lure, reward.</li><li>object of enjoyment; material goods, worldly possessions.</li></ol>

āmisa|aamisa|amisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisa</span></p><ol><li>originally raw meat; hence prevailing notion of “raw unprepared, uncultivated”; thus <em>-khāra</em> raw lye Vin.i.206.</li><li>“fleshy, of the flesh” (as opposed to mind or spirit), hence material, physical; generally in opposition to <em><a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> (see <em><a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> B 1a. and also next no.) thus at MN.i.12 (˚dāyāda); Iti.101 (id.); AN.i.91 = Iti.98 (˚dāna material gifts opp. to spiritual ones); Dhs.1344 (˚paṭisanthāra hospitality towards bodily needs, cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 350).</li><li>food, esp. palatable food (cp. E. sweetmeat); food for enjoyment, dainties Vin.ii.269 sq.; Ja.ii.6; Mil.413 (lok˚); DN-a.i.83 (˚sannidhi),</li><li>bait SN.i.67 SN.iv.158; Ja.iv.57, Ja.iv.219; Ja.vi.416; DN-a.i.270.</li><li>gain, reward, money, douceur, gratuity, “tip” Pv-a.36, Pv-a.46; esp in phrase <em>-kiñcikkha-hetu</em> for the sake of some (little gain SN.ii.234; AN.i.128; AN.v.265, AN.v.283 sq., AN.v.293 sq.; Pp.29; Pv.ii.8#3 (= kiñci āmisaṃ patthento Pv-a.107); Mil.93; Vv-a.241 (= bhogahetu).</li><li>enjoyment Pv.ii.8#2 (= kāmāmise-laggacitto Pv-a.107).</li><li>greed, desire, lust Vin.i.303 (˚antara out of greed, selfish, opp. mettacitto); AN.iii.144 (id.), AN.iii.184 (id.); AN.i.73 (˚garū parisā); Ja.v.91 (˚cakkhu); Pts.ii.238 (mār˚). See also compounds with nir˚ and sa˚.</li></ol><p>der. fr. āma raw, q.v. for etym<br />■ Vedic āmis (m.); later Sk. āmiṣa (nt.), both in lit. &amp; fig. meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></p>

āmisakhāra|aamisakhaara|amisakhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisakhaara</span></p><p>lye, an alkaline fluid (allowed as a treatment for constipation)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

āmisakiñcikkha|aamisaki~ncikkha|amisakincikkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisaki~ncikkha</span></p><p>some material gain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

āmisantara|aamisantara|amisantara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisantara</span></p><p>wishing for material gain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

āmisapaṭisanthāra|aamisapa.tisanthaara|amisapatisanthara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisapa.tisanthaara</span></p><p>hospitable giving of material goods</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

āmisasanthāra|aamisasanthaara|amisasanthara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamisasanthaara</span></p><p>the laying out, distribution, of material goods</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmisa">āmisa</a></p>

āmo|aamo|amo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamo</span></p><p>(particle of assent or approval or recollection, usually followed by voc. and/or affirming clause): yes, indeed, certainly.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āma">āma</a></p>

āmo|aamo|amo
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamo</span></p><p>= āma DN.i.192, DN.i.3.</p>

āmoda|aamoda|amoda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamoda</span></p><p>that which pleases; fragrance, perfume Dāvs v.51.</p><p>Sk. āmoda, fr. ā + <em>mud</em></p>

āmodamāna|aamodamaana|amodamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodamaana</span></p><p>glad; joyful; rejoicing.</p>

āmodamāna|aamodamaana|amodamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodamaana</span></p><p>adjective rejoicing, glad SN.i.100 (variant reading anu˚) = Iti.66; Vv.64#8 (= pamodamāna Vv-a.278); Ja.v.45.</p><p>ppr. med. of āmodeti</p>

āmodanā|aamodanaa|amodana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodanaa</span></p><p>rejoicing Dhs.86, Dhs.285.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>mud</em></p>

āmodayati|aamodayati|amodayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodayati</span></p><p>gladdens, cheers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmodeti">āmodeti</a></p>

āmodeti|aamodeti|amodeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodeti</span></p><p>gladdens, cheers</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmodayati">āmodayati</a></p>

āmodeti|aamodeti|amodeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodeti</span></p><p>to please, gladden, satisfy Thag.649 (cittaṃ); Ja.v.34<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81modita">āmodita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. āmodayati, Caus. of ā + <em>mud</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmodita">āmodita</a></p>

āmodita|aamodita|amodita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamodita</span></p><p>pleased, satisfied, glad Ja.i.17 (verse 80); Ja.v.45 (˚pamodita highly pleased); Mil.346.</p><p>pp. fr. āmodeti</p>

āmutta|aamutta|amutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamutta</span></p><p>having put on, clothed in, dressed with, adorned with (always ˚-) DN.i.104 (˚mālābharaṇa); Vin.ii.156 = Vv.20#8 (˚maṇi-kuṇḍala); SN.i.211; Ja.iv.460; Ja.v.155; Ja.vi.492; Vv.72#1 (= paṭimukka); Vv.80#2 (˚hatthābharaṇa); Pv.ii.9#51 (˚maṇikuṇḍala); Ja.iv.183; Vv-a.182.</p><p>Sk. āmukta, pp. of ā + <em>muc</em>, cp. also BSk. āmukta jewel Divy.2, Divy.3 etc., a meaning which might also be seen in the later Pāli passages, e.g. at Pv-a.134. Semantically cp. ābharaṇa</p>

āmuttamaṇikuṇḍala|aamuttama.niku.n.dala|amuttamanikundala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamuttama.niku.n.dala</span></p><p>with jeweled earrings put on, wearing jeweled earrings.</p>

āmuttamālābharaṇa|aamuttamaalaabhara.na|amuttamalabharana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamuttamaalaabhara.na</span></p><p>wearing garlands and ornaments.</p>

āmuñcati|aamu~ncati|amuncati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamu~ncati</span></p><p>to put on, take up; to be attached to, cling to Dhs-a.305<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81mutta">āmutta</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>mtic</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āmutta">āmutta</a></p>

āmāsaya|aamaasaya|amasaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamaasaya</span></p><p>receptacle of undigested food, i.e. the stomach Vism.260 Kp-a.59. Opp. pakkāsaya.</p><p>āma<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> + āsaya, cp. Sk. āmāśaya &amp; āmāśraya</p>

āmāya|aamaaya|amaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aamaaya</span></p><p>adjective “born in the house” (cp semantically Gr. ἰχαγενής → indigenous), inborn, being by birth, in cpd. <em>-dāsa</em> (dāsī) a born slave, a slave by birth Ja.vi.117 (= gehadāsiyā kucchismiṃ jātadasī C.), Ja.vi.285 (dāsassa dāsiyā kucchimhi jātadāsā).</p><p>to be considered either a der. from amā (see <em><a href="bword://am%C4%81j%C4%81ta">amājāta</a></em> in same meaning) or to be spelt amāya which metri causa may be written ā˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://amājāta">amājāta</a></p>

ānadati|aanadati|anadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanadati</span></p><p>to trumpet (of elephants) Ja.iv.233.</p><p>ā + nadati</p>

ānaka|aanaka|anaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaka</span></p><p>kind of kettledrum, beaten only at one end SN.ii.266; Ja.ii.344; Dpvs.xvi.14.</p><p>Sk. <em>ānaka</em>, cp. Morris J.P.T.S. 1893, 10</p>

ānana|aanana|anana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanana</span></p><p>the mouth; adj. (-˚) having a mouth Sdhp.103; Pgdp.63 (vikaṭ˚).</p><p>Vedic āna, later Sk. ānana from <em>an</em> to breathe</p>

ānanda|aananda|ananda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aananda</span></p><p>One of the principal disciples of the Buddha. He was a first cousin of the Buddha and was deeply attached to him. Ānanda entered the Order in the second year of the Buddha’s ministry, together with other Sākiyan princes, such as Bhaddiya, Anuruddha, Bhagu, Kimbila and Devadatta, and was ordained by the Buddha himself, Vin.ii.182 his upajjhāya being Belatthasīsa.Vin.i.202 Vin.iv.86 Soon after, he heard a discourse by Puṇṇa Mantāniputta and became a Sotāpanna SN.iii.105 Ānanda remained close to the Buddha for the following 25 years, and participates in numerous discourses. He was one of the principal reciters at the First Council after the Buddha passed away, and was responsible for the recitation of the Suttas.</p>

ānanda|aananda|ananda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aananda</span></p><p>joy, pleasure, bliss.</p>

ānanda|aananda|ananda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aananda</span></p><p>joy, pleasure, bliss, delight DN.i.3; Snp.679, Snp.687; Ja.i.207 (˚maccha leviathan); Ja.vi.589 (˚bheri festive drum) DN-a.i.53 (= pītiyā etaṃ adhivacanaṃ).</p><p>Vedic ānanda, fr. ā + <em>nand</em>, cp. BSk. ānandī joy Divy.37</p>

ānandacetiya|aanandacetiya|anandacetiya
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandacetiya</span></p><p>ĀnandacetiyaĀnande Cetiye</p><p>A shrine in Bhoganagara dedicated to a <em>yakkha</em> called Ānanda. There the Buddha stayed during his last sojourn, and mention is made of a sermon he preached there to the monks on the Four Great Authorities. DN.ii.123–126 AN.ii.167 From there he went to Pāvā.</p><p>26.584229,84.3097693shrine</p>

ānandajanana|aanandajanana|anandajanana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandajanana</span></p><p>(~ī)n. producing joy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānanda">ānanda</a></p>

ānandajāta|aanandajaata|anandajata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandajaata</span></p><p>full of joy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānanda">ānanda</a></p>

ānandakumāra|aanandakumaara|anandakumara
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandakumaara</span></p><p>A shipwright, who, with three hundred others, was sent by Mahosadha to the Upper Ganges to secure timber wherewith to build three hundred ships in preparation for Mahosadha’s visit to the capital of Pañcala in order to erect buildings for King Vedeha. AN.vi.427</p>

ānandamaccha|aanandamaccha|anandamaccha
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandamaccha</span></p><p>A large and probably fantastic marine creature. See <em>Timi</em>.</p><p>See also: Timi</p>

ānandati|aanandati|anandati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandati</span></p><p>to be pleased or delighted Ja.vi.589 (aor. ānandi in T. reading ānandi vittā, expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by C. as nandittha was pleased; we should however read <em>ānandi-cittā</em> with gladdened heart). See also <em>ānandiya</em>.</p><p>ā + nandati</p>

ānandi|aanandi|anandi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānandin">ānandin</a></p><p>joyful; happy.</p>

ānandin|aanandin|anandin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānandin">ānandin</a></p><p>joyful; happy.</p>

ānandin|aanandin|anandin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandin</span></p><p>adjective joyful, friendly Thag.555; Ja.iv.226.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>nand</em></p>

ānandiya|aanandiya|anandiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandiya</span></p><p>adjective enjoyable, nt. joy, feast Ja.vi.589 (˚ṃ acarati to celebrate the feast = ānandachaṇa C.).</p><p>grd. of ānandati</p>

ānandī|aanandii|anandi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanandii</span></p><p>joy, happiness in cpd. ānandi-citta Ja.vi.589 (so read probably for ānandi vitta see <em>ānandati</em>).</p><p>ā + nandī, cp. ānanda</p>

ānantarika-kamma|aanantarika-kamma|anantarika-kamma
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantarika-kamma</span></p><p><strong>Ānantarika-kamma:</strong> the 5 heinous ‘actions with immediate destiny’ are: Killing father, killing mother, killing an Arahat, wounding a Buddha so he bleeds, creating schism in the Bhikkhu-Sangha. In A. V., 129 it is said:</p><p>There are 5 hateful and incurable humans destined to the lower world and to hell, namely: the parricide, etc. About the 5th see A. X., 35, 38. With regard to the first crime, it is said in D. 2 that if King Ajātasattu had not killed his father, he would have reached entrance into the path of Stream-entry see also: Appendix.</p>

ānantarika|aanantarika|anantarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantarika</span></p><p>ānantarika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. &amp; neuter</p><ol><li>(mfn.) immediately preceding or following, next; nearest.</li><li>(mfn.) immediate; bringing immediate results; (of an action) producing consequences in the immediately following existence; (of a person) suffering immediate retribution, suffering consequences in the immediately following existence; (n.) an action producing consequences in the immediately following existence</li></ol><p>ānantarika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter immediacy, immediate sequence or succession; the state bringing immediate results</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānantariya">ānantariya</a>, <a href="bword://ānantariya">ānantariya</a></p>

ānantarika|aanantarika|anantarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantarika</span></p><p>Ānantarika &amp; ˚ya</p><p>without an interval, immediately following, successive Vin.i.321; Vin.ii.212; Pp.13; Dhs.1291.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> “conduct that finds retribution without delay (<em>Kvu trsl.</em> 275 n. 2) Vin.ii.193; Ja.i.45; Kv.480; Mil.25 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 267); Vism.177 (as prohibiting practice of kammaṭṭhāna).</li></ul><p>fr. an + antara + ika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

ānantarikakamma|aanantarikakamma|anantarikakamma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantarikakamma</span></p><p>an action bringing retribution in the immediately following existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānantarika">ānantarika</a></p>

ānantariya|aanantariya|anantariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantariya</span></p><p>ānantariya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. &amp; neuter</p><ol><li>(mfn.) immediately preceding or following, next; nearest.</li><li>(mfn.) immediate; bringing immediate results; (of an action) producing consequences in the immediately following existence; (of a person) suffering immediate retribution, suffering consequences in the immediately following existence; (n.) an action producing consequences in the immediately following existence</li></ol><p>ānantariya<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter immediacy, immediate sequence or succession; the state bringing immediate results</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānantarika">ānantarika</a>, <a href="bword://ānantarika">ānantarika</a></p>

ānantariya|aanantariya|anantariya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantariya</span></p><p><strong>Ānantariya:</strong> Immediacy, is a name for that concentration of mind which is associated with the profound and shocking insight <em>vipassanā</em> that is present in any one of the 4 kinds of supra-mundane path consciousness see: <em>ariya-puggala</em>, and which therefore is the cause of the immediately following fruition <em>phala</em> consciousness. According to the Abhidhamma, this path -moment of the <em>Stream-enterer sotāpanna</em> &amp; the other Nobles is generated by the insight into the impermanence, misery and impersonality of all existence, reaching a certain threshold at that very moment and thus instantly transforming and ennobling the individual nature forever. It is mentioned under the name of <em>ānantarika</em> - <em>samādhi</em> in the Ratana Sutta Sn. v. 22 and in Pts.M. 1, ñānakathā.</p>

ānantariya|aanantariya|anantariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanantariya</span></p><p>Ānantarika &amp; ˚ya</p><p>without an interval, immediately following, successive Vin.i.321; Vin.ii.212; Pp.13; Dhs.1291.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></em> “conduct that finds retribution without delay (<em>Kvu trsl.</em> 275 n. 2) Vin.ii.193; Ja.i.45; Kv.480; Mil.25 (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 267); Vism.177 (as prohibiting practice of kammaṭṭhāna).</li></ul><p>fr. an + antara + ika</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kamma">kamma</a></p>

ānapāna|aanapaana|anapana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanapaana</span></p><p>breathing in and out.</p>

ānapānasati|aanapaanasati|anapanasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanapaanasati</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānapānassati">ānapānassati</a></p><p>intentness of mind on one’s breathing in and out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānapāna">ānapāna</a></p>

ānapānassati|aanapaanassati|anapanassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanapaanassati</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānapānassati">ānapānassati</a></p><p>intentness of mind on one’s breathing in and out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānapāna">ānapāna</a></p>

ānaya|aanaya|anaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaya</span></p><p>adjective to be brought, in <em>suvānaya</em> easy to bring SN.i.124 = Ja.i.80.</p><p>ā + naya</p>

ānayati|aanayati|anayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanayati</span></p><p>leads towards or near; brings, fetches; brings home, brings home (as a wife); brings back; supplies, understands (a meaning or word)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānayati|aanayati|anayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanayati</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81neti">āneti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānayissami|aanayissami|anayissami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanayissami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānayati">ānayati</a></p>

ānañca|aana~nca|ananca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aana~nca</span></p><p>see *<a href="bword://%C4%81k%C4%81sa">ākāsa</a>*˚; and <em>viññāṇa˚</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a></p>

ānañja|aana~nja|ananja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aana~nja</span></p><p>see <em>ānejja</em>.</p>

ānaṇya|aana.nya|ananya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aana.nya</span></p><p>release or freedom from debt or obligation.</p>

ānaṇya|aana.nya|ananya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aana.nya</span></p><p>freedom from debt DN.i.73; AN.iii.354 (Ep. of Nibbāna, cp. <em><a href="bword://ana%E1%B9%87a">anaṇa</a></em>); Mnd.160; Vism.44; DN-a.i.3.</p><p>Sk. ānṛṇya, so also BSk. e.g. Jtm.31#18; from a + ṛṇa, P. iṇa but also aṇa in composition, thus an-aṇa as base of ānaṇya</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anaṇa">anaṇa</a></p>

ānejja|aanejja|anejja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanejja</span></p><p>immovability; imperturbability.</p>

ānejja|aanejja|anejja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanejja</span></p><p>and <strong>Ānañja</strong> immovability, imperturbability, impassibility. The word is <em>n.</em> but occurs as <em>adj.</em> at Vin.iii.109 (ānañja samādhi, with which cp. BSk. ānijyā śāntiḥ at Avs.i.199<br />■ The term usually occurs in cpd ānejja-ppatta (adj.) immovable lit. having attained impassibility expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Bdhgh. at Vin.iii.267 (on Pār. Vin.i.1, 6 as <em>acala, niccala</em>, i.e. motionless. This cpd. is indicated below by (p.) after the reference<br />■ The various spellings of the word are as follows:</p><ol><li><em>ānejja</em> DN.i.76 (variant reading ānañja-p.) AN.ii.184 (p.); AN.iii.93 (p.), AN.iii.100 (p.), AN.iii.377 sq. (p.); Cnd.471 (variant reading aneja, ānañja) = Vb.137 (āneñja) Cnd.569#a (variant reading ānañja), 601 (variant reading anejja &amp; aneñja); Pp.60 (p.); DN-a.i.219 (variant reading BB āneñja).</li><li><em>ānañja</em> Vin.iii.4 (p.) (variant reading ānañca˚, anañja˚, ānañja˚; Bdhgh. ānejja p. 267), Vin.iii.109; Ud.27 (samādhi, adj. variant reading ānañca); Dhp-a.iv.46. See also below cpd. ˚kāraṇa<br />■ A peculiarity of Trenckner a spelling is <em>āṇañja</em> at MN.ii.229 (variant reading aṇañja aneñja, āneñja), MN.ii.253, MN.ii.254.</li><li><em><a href="bword://%C4%81ne%C3%B1ja">āneñja</a></em> SN.ii.82. (variant reading āṇañje, or is it āṇeñja?); DN.iii.217 (˚âbhisankhāra of imperturbable character, remaining static, cp. <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 358); Mnd.90 (id.), Mnd.206, Mnd.442; Pts.ii.206; Vb.135, Vb.340 Vism.377 (p.), Vism.386 (sixteen˚ fold), Vism.571; Ne.87, Ne.99. See also iñjati.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://k%C4%81ra%E1%B9%87a">kāraṇa</a></em> trick of immovability, i.e. pretending to be dead (done by an elephant, but see differently Morris <em>J P T S.</em> 1886, 154) Ja.i.415; Ja.ii.325 (variant reading āṇañja, āneñca ānañca); Ja.iv.308; Ja.v.273, Ja.v.310.</li></ul><p>abstr. fr. an + *añja or *ejja = *ijja. The Sanskritised equivalent would be *iñjya or *iñgya of <em>iṅg</em> to stir, move, with a peculiar substitution of <em>*aṅg</em> in Pāli, referring it to a base with ṛ (probably Sk. <em>ṛj</em> ṛñjati) in analogy to a form like Sk. ṛṇa = Pāli aṇa iṇa, both a &amp; i representing Sk. ṛ. The form; <em><a href="bword://a%C3%B1ja">añja</a></em> would thus correspond to a Sk. *añjya (*añgya). The third P form <em>ān-eñja</em> is a direct (later, and probably re-instituted formation from Sk. iñjya, which in an interesting way became in BSk. re-sanskritised to <em>āñijya</em> (which on the other hand may represent āñejja &amp; thus give the latter the feature of a later, but more specifically Pāli form) The editions of P. Texts show a great variance of spelling based on MSS. vacillation, in part also due to confusion of derivation</p><p>See also: Ānañja, <a href="bword://āneñja">āneñja</a>, <a href="bword://kāraṇa">kāraṇa</a>, <a href="bword://añja">añja</a></p>

ānejjakārana|aanejjakaarana|anejjakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanejjakaarana</span></p><p>the feat of immovability (or a trained elephant)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānejja">ānejja</a></p>

ānejjapatta|aanejjapatta|anejjapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanejjapatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānejjappatta">ānejjappatta</a></p><p>who or which has reached immovability or imperturbability</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānejja">ānejja</a></p>

ānejjappatta|aanejjappatta|anejjappatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanejjappatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānejjappatta">ānejjappatta</a></p><p>who or which has reached immovability or imperturbability</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānejja">ānejja</a></p>

ānesi|aanesi|anesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānessami|aanessami|anessami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanessami</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānessati|aanessati|anessati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanessati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānesuṃ|aanesu.m|anesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

āneti|aaneti|aneti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaneti</span></p><p>leads towards or near; brings, fetches; brings home, brings home (as a wife); brings back; supplies, understands (a meaning or word)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānayati">ānayati</a></p>

āneti|aaneti|aneti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaneti</span></p><p>to bring, to bring towards, to fetch, procure, convey, bring back Snp.110; Pv-a.54, Pv-a.92.<br />pot 1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->st<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. <em>ānema</em> (or imper. 2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->nd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl <em>ānetha</em> MN.i.371.<br />fut <em>ānayissati</em> SN.i.124; Pv.ii.6#5; Ja.iii.173; Ja.v.154 (variant reading) <em>ānessati</em> Ja.v.154.<br />inf <em>ānayituṃ</em> Pv.ii.6#10,<br />ger <em>ānetvā</em> Pv-a.42, Pv-a.74.<br />aor <em>ānesi</em> Pv-a.3, &amp; <em>ānayi</em> Pv.i.7#7 (sapatiṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81n%C4%ABta">ānīta</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ Med. pass. <em>ānīyati &amp; āniyyati;</em> DN.ii.245 (āniyyataṃ imper. shall be brought); MN.i.371 (ppr. ānīyamāna)<br />caus 2 <em>ānāpeti</em> to cause to be fetched Ja.iii.391; Ja.v.225. Apa &amp; Apo;</p><p>ā + neti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānīta">ānīta</a></p>

ānetuṃ|aanetu.m|anetum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanetu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānetvā|aanetvaa|anetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

āneñja|aane~nja|anenja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~nja</span></p><p>(&amp; mfn.?)</p><ol><li>(n.) immovability; imperturbability.</li><li>(mfn., or n. in apposition?) unmoved; not to be stirred; imperturbable (often applied both to determinative actions leading to existence in arūpāvacara spheres, and to arūpa jhāna stages)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://aneñja">aneñja</a></p>

āneñja|aane~nja|anenja
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~nja</span></p><p><strong>Āneñja:</strong> Static imperturbability, denotes the mental state of being(s) in the formless sphere <em>arūpāvacara</em> <em>avacara</em>, see: <em>sankhāra</em> cf. M. 106.</p>

āneñjabhisaṅkhāra|aane~njabhisa"nkhaara|anenjabhisankhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~njabhisa"nkhaara</span></p><p>an accumulation of motive force, impetus, for <em>arūpa</em> states</p><p>See also: ānenja</p>

āneñjakāraṇa|aane~njakaara.na|anenjakarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~njakaara.na</span></p><p>the feat of immovability (or a trained elephant)</p><p>See also: ānenja</p>

āneñjapatta|aane~njapatta|anenjapatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~njapatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āneñjappatta">āneñjappatta</a></p><p>who or which has reached immovability or imperturbability</p><p>See also: ānenja</p>

āneñjappatta|aane~njappatta|anenjappatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~njappatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āneñjappatta">āneñjappatta</a></p><p>who or which has reached immovability or imperturbability</p><p>See also: ānenja</p>

āneñjatā|aane~njataa|anenjata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aane~njataa</span></p><p>steadfastness Vism.330, Vism.386.</p><p>fr. āneñja</p>

ānisada|aanisada|anisada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanisada</span></p><p>the buttocks, the bottom.</p>

ānisada|aanisada|anisada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanisada</span></p><p>“sit down”, bottom, behind MN.i.80 = MN.i.245; Ja.iii.435 (gloss asata) Vism.251 = Kp-a.45 (˚ttaca), Kp-a.252 (˚maṃsa).</p><p>a + <em>sad</em></p>

ānisaṃsa|aanisa.msa|anisamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanisa.msa</span></p><p>profit, advantage; benefit, blessing (in, loc.)</p>

ānisaṃsa|aanisa.msa|anisamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanisa.msa</span></p><p>praise i.e. that which is commendable, profit, merit, advantage good result, blessing in or from (c. loc.). There are <em>five</em> ānisaṃsā sīlavato sīla-sampadāya or blessings which accrue to the virtuous enumerated at DN.ii.86 viz. <strong>bhogakkhandha</strong> great wealth, <em><a href="bword://kittisadda">kittisadda</a></em> good report <em><a href="bword://vis%C4%81rada">visārada</a></em> self-confidence, <em>asammūlho kālaṃ karoti</em> an untroubled death, <em>saggaṃ lokaṃ uppajjati</em> a happy state after death<br />■ DN.i.110, DN.i.143; DN.iii.132 (four), DN.iii.236 (five) MN.i.204; SN.i.46, SN.i.52; SN.iii.8, SN.iii.93 (mahā˚); SN.v.69 (seven), SN.v.73, SN.v.129, SN.v.133, SN.v.237 (seven), SN.v.267, SN.v.276; AN.i.58 (karaṇīye kariyamāne); AN.ii.26, AN.ii.185, AN.ii.239, AN.ii.243 (sikkhā˚); AN.iii.41 (dāne), AN.iii.248 (dhammasavane), AN.ii.250 (yāguyā), AN.ii.251 (upaṭṭhita-satissa), AN.ii.253 sq. (sīlavato sīlasampadāya etc., as above), AN.ii.267 (sucarite), AN.ii.441; AN.iv.150 (mettāya ceto-vimuttiyā), AN.iv.361 (dhammasavane), AN.iv.439 sq. (nekkhamme avitakke nippītike), AN.iv.442, AN.iv.443 sq. (ākās’ānañcāyatane); AN.vi.106 (mahā˚), AN.vi.311; Iti.28, Iti.29, Iti.40 (sikkhā˚); Snp.256 (phala˚), Snp.784, Snp.952; Ja.i.9 Ja.i.94; Ja.v.491 (variant reading anu˚); Mnd.73, Mnd.104, Mnd.441; Kv.400; Mil.198; Vv-a.6, Vv-a.113; Pv-a.9 (dāna˚), Pv-a.12, Pv-a.64 (= phala), Pv-a.208, Pv-a.221 (= guṇa); Sdhp.263<br />■ <em>Eleven</em> ānisaṃsas of <em>mettā</em> (cp. Pts.ii.130) are given in detail at Vism.311 Vism.314; on another <em>eight</em> see pp. Vism.644 sq.</p><p>ā + ni + saṃsa, BSk. distorted to anuśaṃsa</p><p>See also: bhogakkhandha, <a href="bword://kittisadda">kittisadda</a>, <a href="bword://visārada">visārada</a></p>

āniyyati|aaniyyati|aniyyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaniyyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānubhi|aanubhi|anubhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanubhi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anubhavati">anubhavati</a></p>

ānubhāva|aanubhaava|anubhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanubhaava</span></p><p>majesty, splendor; authority; power, efficacy; magic or supernatural power; instr. <em>~ena</em> often expresses: by means of, owing to.</p>

ānubhāva|aanubhaava|anubhava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanubhaava</span></p><p>greatness, magnificence majesty, splendour Ja.i.69 (mahanto); Ja.ii.102 (of a jewel), Ja.v.491; Dhp-a.ii.58.</p><p>the dissociated composition form of anubhāva, q.v. for details. Only in later language</p>

ānulomika|aanulomika|anulomika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanulomika</span></p><p>in regular order; conformable to, suitable.</p>

ānupubba|aanupubba|anupubba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubba</span></p><p>order, regular order, succession.</p>

ānupubba|aanupubba|anupubba
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubba</span></p><p>rule, regularity, order Thag.727 (cp. Mhvs.ii.224 ānupubbā).</p><p>abstr. fr. anupubba</p>

ānupubbatā|aanupubbataa|anupubbata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubbataa</span></p><p>(or <em>-ta</em> nt.?) succession; only in tt. g. padânu-pubbatā word sequence, in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of iti Mnd.140; Cnd.137 (variant reading ˚ka).</p><p>fr. last</p>

ānupubbikathā|aanupubbikathaa|anupubbikatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubbikathaa</span></p><p>an exposition or narrative in a regular order; an account of previous events; a progressive or gradual sermon; instruction step by step.</p>

ānupubbikathā|aanupubbikathaa|anupubbikatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubbikathaa</span></p><p>regulated exposition, graduated sermon DN.i.110; DN.ii.41 sq. MN.i.379; Ja.i.8; Mil.228; DN-a.i.277, DN-a.i.308; Dhp-a.iv.199.</p><p>for anupubbi˚ representing its isolated composition form, cp. ānubhāva &amp; see also anupubbi˚</p>

ānupubbī-kathā|aanupubbii-kathaa|anupubbi-katha
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupubbii-kathaa</span></p><p><strong>Ānupubbī-kathā:</strong> ‘gradual instruction’, progressive teaching; given by the Buddha when it was necessary to first prepare the listener’s mind before speaking to him on the advanced teaching of the Four Noble Truths. The stock passage e.g. D. 3; D 14; M. 56 runs as follows:<br />Then the Blessed One gave him a gradual instruction - that is to say, he spoke on generosity <em>dāna</em>, on morality <em>sīla</em> and on the heavens <em>sagga</em>, he explained the danger, the loss and the depravity of sensual pleasures, and the advantage of renunciation. When the Blessed One perceived that the listener’s mind was prepared, pliant, free from obstacles, elevated and lucid; then he explained to him that exalted teaching particular to the Buddhas <em>Buddhānam</em> <em>sāmukkamsikā,</em> that is: Suffering, its cause, its ceasing, and the path.</p>

ānupuṭṭha|aanupu.t.tha|anuputtha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanupu.t.tha</span></p><p>metri causa for <em><a href="bword://anupu%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">anupuṭṭha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anupuṭṭha">anupuṭṭha</a></p>

ānutrāsi|aanutraasi|anutrasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanutraasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānutrāsin">ānutrāsin</a></p><p>not fearful; not afraid</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://utrāsin">utrāsin</a></p>

ānutrāsin|aanutraasin|anutrasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanutraasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānutrāsin">ānutrāsin</a></p><p>not fearful; not afraid</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://utrāsin">utrāsin</a></p>

ānuttariya|aanuttariya|anuttariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanuttariya</span></p><p>incomparable; preeminence; excellence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuttariya">anuttariya</a></p>

ānuttariya|aanuttariya|anuttariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanuttariya</span></p><p>incomparableness, excellency, supreme ideal DN.iii.102 sq.; AN.v.37.</p><p>see also <em><a href="bword://anuttariya">anuttariya</a></em> which as-˚ probably represents ānutt˚</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anuttariya">anuttariya</a></p>

ānuttāsi|aanuttaasi|anuttasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanuttaasi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānuttāsin">ānuttāsin</a></p><p>not fearful; not afraid</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttāsin">uttāsin</a></p>

ānuttāsin|aanuttaasin|anuttasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanuttaasin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ānuttāsin">ānuttāsin</a></p><p>not fearful; not afraid</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttāsin">uttāsin</a></p>

ānāmeti|aanaameti|anameti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaameti</span></p><p>to make bend, to bend, to bring toward or under Ja.v.154 (doubtful reading fut ānāmayissasi, variant reading ānayissati, C. ānessasi = lead to).</p><p>ā + nāmeti, Caus. of namati, which is usually spelt nameti</p>

ānāpayati|aanaapayati|anapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapayati</span></p><p>causes to be brought; has fetched</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānāpeti">ānāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānāpesi|aanaapesi|anapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānāpeti">ānāpeti</a></p>

ānāpeti|aanaapeti|anapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapeti</span></p><p>(sometimes confused with, and written as, āṇāpeti), causes to be brought; has fetched</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānāpayati">ānāpayati</a>, <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānāpeti|aanaapeti|anapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapeti</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81neti">āneti</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānāpāna|aanaapaana|anapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapaana</span></p><p>in haled &amp; exhaled breath, inspiration &amp; respiration SN.v.132 SN.v.311 sq.; Ja.i.58; Pts.i.162 (˚kathā); usually in cpd. <em>-sati</em> concentration by in-breathing &amp; out-breathing (cp.; <em>Man of Mystic</em> 70) MN.i.425 (cp. DN.ii.291); MN.iii.82 Vin.iii.70; AN.i.30; Iti.80; Pts.i.166, Pts.i.172, Pts.i.185 (˚samādhi); Cnd.466 B (id.); Mil.332; Vism.111, Vism.197, Vism.266 sq.; Snp-a.165. See detail under sati.</p><p>āna + apāna, compounds of <em>an</em> to breathe</p>

ānāpāna-sati|aanaapaana-sati|anapana-sati
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aanaapaana-sati</span></p><p><strong>Ānāpāna-sati:</strong> Awareness or mindfulness on &amp; by in-and-out-breathing, is one of the most important trainings for reaching mental concentration and the 4 absorptions <em>jhāna</em>. In the Satipatthāna Sutta M. 10, D. 22 and elsewhere, 4 methods of practice are given, which may also serve as basis for insight meditation. The speech on Awareness by Breathing’ Ānāpānasati Sutta, M. 118 and other texts have 16 methods of practice, which divide into 4 groups of four. The first three apply to both calm <em>samatha</em> and insight-meditation, while the fourth refers to pure insight praxis only. With attentive mind he breathes in, with attentive mind he breathes out.</p><blockquote><p>I. First Tetrad:<br />1: When making a long inhalation he understands: I make a long inhalation; when making a long exhalation he understands: I make a long exhalation.</p><p>2: When making a short inhalation he understands: I make a short inhalation; when making a short exhalation he understands: I make a short exhalation.</p><p>3: Clearly perceiving the entire body I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; clearly perceiving the entire breath-body I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>4: Calming all bodily activity I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; calming all bodily activity I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>II. Second Tetrad:<br />5: Experiencing joy <em>pīti</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; Experiencing joy I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>6: Experiencing a pleasurable happiness I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; Experiencing a pleasurable happiness I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>7: Experiencing the mental construction <em>citta-sankhāra</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself, Experiencing the mental construction I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>8: Calming the mental construction I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; calming the mental construction I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>III. Third Tetrad:<br />9: Experiencing the mind &amp; mood <em>citta</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; clearly perceiving the mind &amp; mood I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>10: Gladdening the mind I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; gladdening the mind I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>11: Concentrating the mind I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; concentrating the mind I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>12: Releasing the mind I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; releasing the mind I will breathe out, thus he trains himself</p><p>IV. Fourth Tetrad:<br />13: Reflecting on impermanence <em>anicca</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; reflecting on impermanence I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>14: Reflecting on disillusion <em>virāga</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; reflecting on disillusion I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>15: Reflecting on ceasing <em>nirodha</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; reflecting on ceasing I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p><p>16: Reflecting on relinquishment <em>patinissagga</em> I will breathe in, thus he trains himself; reflecting on relinquishment I will breathe out, thus he trains himself.</p></blockquote><p>In M 118 it is further shown how these 16 exercises bring about the 4 foundations of awareness <em>satipatthāna</em>, namely: 1-4 contemplation of the body, 5-8 contemplation of feeling, 9-12 contemplation of mind, 13-16 contemplation of mental states. Then it is shown how these 4 foundations of awareness or mindfulness bring about the 7 factors of enlightenment <em>bojjhanga</em>, and then how these again produce release of mind <em>ceto-vimutti</em> and release through understanding <em>paññā-vimutti</em>.</p><blockquote><p>Literature: Ānāpānasati Samyutta see: LIV. - Pts.M. <em>Ānāpānakathā</em> - Full explanation of practice in Vis.M VIII, 145ff. - For a comprehensive anthology of canonical and commentarial texts, see: <em>Mindfulness of Breathing,</em> by Nānamoli Thera Kandy: BPS, 1964-98.</p></blockquote>

ānīta|aaniita|anita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaniita</span></p><p>led near; brought; fetched, brought home</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānīta|aaniita|anita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaniita</span></p><p>fetched, brought (here), brought back adduced Ja.i.291; Ja.iii.127; Ja.iv.1.</p><p>pp. of ānetī</p>

ānīyamāna|aaniiyamaana|aniyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaniiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

ānīyati|aaniiyati|aniyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaniiyati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āneti">āneti</a></p>

āpa|aapa|apa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa</span></p><p>and f.plural water, the element water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpo">āpo</a></p>

āpa|aapa|apa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa</span></p><p>Āpa &amp; Āpo</p><p>neuter water; philosophically t. t. for cohesion, representative of one of the 4 great elements (cp. mahābhūta), viz. <em>paṭhavī, āpo, tejo vāyo</em>: see <em>Cpd.</em> 268 &amp; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 201, also below ˚dhātu. DN.ii.259; MN.i.327; SN.ii.103; SN.iii.54, SN.iii.207; AN.iv.312, AN.iv.375; Snp.307, Snp.391 (˚ṃ), Snp.392 (loc. āpe), Snp.437 (id.); Ja.iv.8 (paṭhavi-āpa-teja˚); Dhs.652; Mil.363 (gen. āpassa, with paṭhavī etc.); Sdhp.100.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> the water-device, i.e. meditation by (the element of) water (cp. <em>Mystic</em> 75 n.) DN.iii.268; Ja.i.313; Dhs.203 Vism.170; Dhp-a.i.312; Dhp-a.iii.214. <em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the fluid element the essential element in water, i.e. element of cohesion (see <em>Cpd.</em> 155 n. 2; <em>Mystic</em> 9 n. 2; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 201, 242; DN.iii.228, DN.iii.247; MN.i.187, MN.i.422: Dhs.652; Ne.74. See also <em>dhātu<br />■ rasa</em> the taste of water AN.i.32; Snp-a.6 <em>-<a href="bword://sama">sama</a></em> resembling water MN.i.423.</p><p>Vedic ap &amp; āp, f. sg. apā, pl. āpaḥ, later Sk. also āpaḥ nt<br />■ Idg.; <em>*ap &amp; *ab;</em>, primarily to Lith. ùpé water, Old Prussian ape river, Gr. ̓*Λπία Name of the Peloponnesus; further (as <em>*ab</em>) to Lat. amnis river Sk. abda cloud, &amp; perhaps ambu water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a>, <a href="bword://sama">sama</a></p>

āpabhinandi|aapabhinandi|apabhinandi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapabhinandi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpabhinandin">āpabhinandin</a></p><p>finding pleasure in (the element of) water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

āpabhinandin|aapabhinandin|apabhinandin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapabhinandin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpabhinandin">āpabhinandin</a></p><p>finding pleasure in (the element of) water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

āpadatthā|aapadatthaa|apadattha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapadatthaa</span></p><p>(for use) in case of misfortune or accident</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpadatthāya">āpadatthāya</a>, <a href="bword://āpadā">āpadā</a></p>

āpadatthāya|aapadatthaaya|apadatthaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapadatthaaya</span></p><p>(for use) in case of misfortune or accident</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpadatthā">āpadatthā</a>, <a href="bword://āpadā">āpadā</a></p>

āpadā|aapadaa|apada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapadaa</span></p><p>misfortune; distress; calamity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpadī">āpadī</a></p>

āpadā|aapadaa|apada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapadaa</span></p><p>accident, misfortune distress, DN.iii.190; AN.ii.68 (loc. pl. āpadāsu), AN.ii.187; AN.iii.45; AN.iv.31; Thag.371; Ja.iv.163 (āpadatthā, a difficult form; vv.ll. T. aparattā, āpadatvā, C. aparatthā; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by āpadāya); Ja.v.340 (loc. āpade), Ja.v.368; Pv-a.130 (quot.) Sdhp.312, Sdhp.554. <em>Note.</em> For the contracted form in loc. pl āpāsu (= *āpatsu) see <em>*āpā</em>.</p><p>Sk. āpad, fr. ā + <em>pad</em>, cp. āpajjati &amp; BSk. āpad, e.g. in āpadgata Jtm.31#33</p>

āpadī|aapadii|apadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapadii</span></p><p>misfortune; distress; calamity</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpadā">āpadā</a></p>

āpagaharaka|aapagaharaka|apagaharaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapagaharaka</span></p><p>who scorns or disparages (the element of) water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

āpagā|aapagaa|apaga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapagaa</span></p><p>river, a stream.</p>

āpagā|aapagaa|apaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapagaa</span></p><p>river Thag.309; Snp.319; Ja.v.454; Dāvs i.32; Vv-a.41.</p><p>āpa + ga of <em>gam</em></p>

āpajigucchaka|aapajigucchaka|apajigucchaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajigucchaka</span></p><p>who spurns or despises (the element of) water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

āpajja|aapajja|apajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajja</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjamāna|aapajjamaana|apajjamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjanta|aapajjanta|apajjanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjati|aapajjati|apajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjati</span></p><p>comes to, reaches; enters upon, falls into, is reduced to (a state or condition), is changed into; gets into trouble; commits an offense (with āpatti or used absolutely), transgresses; happens, occurs.</p>

āpajjati|aapajjati|apajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjati</span></p><p>to get into, to meet with (acc.); to undergo; to make, produce, exhibit Vin.ii.126 (saṃvaraṃ); DN.i.222 (pariyeṭṭhiṃ); Iti.113 (vuddhiṃ) Ja.i.73; Pp.20, Pp.33 (diṭṭh’ânugatiṃ); Pv-a.29 (ppr. āpajjanto); Dhp-a.ii.71-pot. <em>āpajjeyya</em> DN.i.119 (musāvādaṃ)<br />aor <em>āpajji</em> Ja.v.349; Pv-a.124 (sankocaṃ); <em><a href="bword://%C4%81p%C4%81di">āpādi</a></em> SN.i.37; AN.ii.34; Iti.85; Ja.ii.293; 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. āpādu DN.ii.273<br />ger <em>āpajjitva</em> Pv-a.22 (saṃvegaṃ), Pv-a.151. pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81panna">āpanna</a></em> (q.v.)<br />caus <em><a href="bword://%C4%81p%C4%81deti">āpādeti</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ <em>Note.</em> The reading <em>āpajja</em> in āpajja naṃ Iti.86 is uncertain (vv. ll āsajja &amp; ālajja). The id. p. at Vin.ii.203 (CV. vii.4, 8 has āsajjanaṃ, for which Bdhgh, on p. 325 has āpajjanaṃ Cp. pariyāpajjati.</p><p>Sk. āpadyate, ā + <em>pad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpādi">āpādi</a>, <a href="bword://āpanna">āpanna</a>, <a href="bword://āpādeti">āpādeti</a></p>

āpajji|aapajji|apajji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajji</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjissatha|aapajjissatha|apajjissatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjissatha</span></p><p>cond. 3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjita|aapajjita|apajjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjita</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpajjitar">āpajjitar</a></p><p>(from āpajjati) one who enters upon; one who commits (an offense).</p>

āpajjitabba|aapajjitabba|apajjitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjitar|aapajjitar|apajjitar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjitar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpajjitar">āpajjitar</a></p><p>(from āpajjati) one who enters upon; one who commits (an offense).</p>

āpajjitvā|aapajjitvaa|apajjitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajjitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpajjiṃsu|aapajji.msu|apajjimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapajji.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpakā|aapakaa|apaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapakaa</span></p><p>river Ja.v.452; Ja.vi.518.</p><p>= āpagā</p>

āpanna|aapanna|apanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. (act.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapanna</span></p><p>having entered upon, reached (a state or condition); in trouble; who has committed an offense.</p>

āpanna|aapanna|apanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapanna</span></p><ol><li>entered upon, fallen into, possessed of, having done Vin.i.164 (āpattiṃ ā.); Vin.iii.90; DN.i.4 (dayāpanna merciful); Cnd.32 (taṇhāya).</li><li>unfortunate miserable Ja.i.19 (verse 124). Cp. pari˚. *Apa</li></ol><p>pp. of āpajjati</p>

āpatacchika|aapatacchika|apatacchika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapatacchika</span></p><p>at Ja.vi.17 is C. reading for apatacchika in <em>khārāpat˚</em>; (q.v.).</p>

āpatati|aapatati|apatati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapatati</span></p><p>to fall on to, to rush on to Ja.v.349 (= upadhāvati C.); Ja.vi.451 (= āgacchati C.); Mil.371.</p><p>ā + patati</p>

āpatha|aapatha|apatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapatha</span></p><p>in micchāpatha, dvedhāpatha as classified in Vb Ind. p. 441 should be grouped under <em><a href="bword://patha">patha</a></em> as micchā˚ dvedhā˚.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://patha">patha</a></p>

āpathaka|aapathaka|apathaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapathaka</span></p><p>in ˚jjhāyin Cnd.342#2 is read <em>āpādaka˚</em>; at Mnd.226, and āpātaka˚ at Vism.26.</p>

āpatti|aapatti|apatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapatti</span></p><p>the entering into a state or condition; a fault, transgression, esp. an offense committed by a bhikkhu, ’ committing (an offense).</p>

āpatti|aapatti|apatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapatti</span></p><p>an ecclesiastical offence (cp.; <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 362 n. 1), Vin.i.103 (˚khandha), Vin.i.164 (˚ṃ paṭikaroti), Vin.i.322 (˚ṃ passati), Vin.i.354 (avasesā &amp; anavasesā); Vin.ii.2 sq. (˚ṃ ropeti), Vin.ii.59, Vin.ii.60 (˚pariyanta), Vin.ii.88 (˚adhikaraṇa), Vin.ii.259 (˚ṃ paṭikaroti); Vin.iv.344; DN.iii.212 (˚kusalatā); AN.i.84 (id.), AN.i.87; AN.ii.240 (˚bhaya); Dhs.1330 sq. (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 346). <em>anāpatti</em> Vin.iii.35.</p><ul><li><em>-vuṭṭhānatā</em> forgiveness of an offence Vin.ii.250 (put before anāpatti).</li></ul><p>Sk. āpatti, fr. ā + <em>pad</em>, cp. apajjati &amp; BSk. āpatti, e. g, Divy.330</p>

āpattibahula|aapattibahula|apattibahula
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattibahula</span></p><p>frequently or habitually committing offenses</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattigāmi|aapattigaami|apattigami
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattigaami</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpattigāmin">āpattigāmin</a></p><p>guilty of an offense (not yet dealt with)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattigāmin|aapattigaamin|apattigamin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattigaamin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpattigāmin">āpattigāmin</a></p><p>guilty of an offense (not yet dealt with)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattika|aapattika|apattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattika</span></p><p>adjective guilty of an offence MN.i.443; Vin.iv.224. <em>an˚</em>; Vin.i.127.</p><p>āpatti + ka, cp. BSk. āpattika Divy.303</p>

āpattikkhanda|aapattikkhanda|apattikkhanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattikkhanda</span></p><p>group or category of offense:</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattikusalatā|aapattikusalataa|apattikusalata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattikusalataa</span></p><p>skill in discerning what is an offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattilesa|aapattilesa|apattilesa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattilesa</span></p><p>an indirect, misleading statement of an offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattivuṭṭhāna|aapattivu.t.thaana|apattivutthana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattivu.t.thaana</span></p><p>removal of an offense; rehabilitation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpattādhikaraṇa|aapattaadhikara.na|apattadhikarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapattaadhikara.na</span></p><p>legal case or dispute concerning an offense</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpatti">āpatti</a></p>

āpaṇa|aapa.na|apana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa.na</span></p><p>A city in the Aṅguttarāpa country, probably its capital. The Buddha once visited the city with 1,250 monks and the whole company was entertained by the Jaṭila Keṇiya. Vin.i.245<em>ff.</em> From Āpaṇa the Buddha went on to Kusinārā. Vin.i.247 In the Saṃyutta Nikāya, SN.v.225 Āpaṇa is spoken of as a township of the Aṅgas and the Buddha is mentioned as having stayed there with Sāriputta.</p><p>Several suttas were preached at Āpaṇa, among them the Potaliya Sutta, MN.i.359<em>ff.</em> Laṭukikopama Sutta (to Udāyi), MN.i.447<em>ff.</em> Sela Sutta, MN.ii.146<em>ff.</em> Snp.p.102<em>ff.</em> and the Saddhā or Āpaṇa Sutta. SN.225–227</p><p>On the occasion of the Buddha’s visit to Āpaṇa, during which he converted Sela and Keṇiya, he seems to have stayed at Āpaṇa for over a week and ordained three hundred monks in the company of Sela. Snp.p.112</p><p>25.465594,87.1325682town</p>

āpaṇa|aapa.na|apana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.) a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa.na</span></p><p>stall (of goods for sale); a market; a shop.</p>

āpaṇa|aapa.na|apana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa.na</span></p><p>bazaar, shop Vin.i.140; Ja.i.55; Ja.v.445; Pv.ii.3#22; Mil.2, Mil.341; Snp-a.440; Dhp-a.i.317; Dhp-a.ii.89; Vv-a.157; Pv-a.88, Pv-a.333 (phal˚ fruit shop), Pv-a.215.</p><p>Sk. āpaṇa, ā + <em>paṇ</em></p>

āpaṇika|aapa.nika|apanika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa.nika</span></p><p>merchant, a shopkeeper.</p>

āpaṇika|aapa.nika|apanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapa.nika</span></p><p>shopkeeper, tradesman Ja.i.124; Mil.344; Vv-a.157; Dhp-a.ii.89.</p><p>fr. āpaṇa</p>

āpeti|aapeti|apeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapeti</span></p><p>to cause to reach or obtain Ja.vi.46. Cp. vy˚.</p><p>Caus. of <em>āp</em>, see <em><a href="bword://appoti">appoti</a></em> &amp; pāpuṇāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appoti">appoti</a></p>

āphusati|aaphusati|aphusati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaphusati</span></p><p>to feel, realise, attain to, reach; aor. <em>āphusi</em> Vv.16#9 (= adhigacchi Vv-a.84).</p><p>ā + phusati</p>

āpiyati|aapiyati|apiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapiyati</span></p><p>to be in motion (in etym. of āpo) Vism.364.</p><p>fr. <em>ṛ</em>; cp. appāyati &amp; appeti</p>

āpo-dhātu|aapo-dhaatu|apo-dhatu
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapo-dhaatu</span></p><p><strong>Āpo-dhātu:</strong> Water-element, macroscopic fluidity, microscopic cohesion; see: <em>dhātu</em></p>

āpo|aapo|apo
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine n.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapo</span></p><p>and f.plural water, the element water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpa">āpa</a></p>

āpo|aapo|apo
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapo</span></p><p>Āpa &amp; Āpo</p><p>neuter water; philosophically t. t. for cohesion, representative of one of the 4 great elements (cp. mahābhūta), viz. <em>paṭhavī, āpo, tejo vāyo</em>: see <em>Cpd.</em> 268 &amp; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 201, also below ˚dhātu. DN.ii.259; MN.i.327; SN.ii.103; SN.iii.54, SN.iii.207; AN.iv.312, AN.iv.375; Snp.307, Snp.391 (˚ṃ), Snp.392 (loc. āpe), Snp.437 (id.); Ja.iv.8 (paṭhavi-āpa-teja˚); Dhs.652; Mil.363 (gen. āpassa, with paṭhavī etc.); Sdhp.100.</p><p><em>-<a href="bword://kasi%E1%B9%87a">kasiṇa</a></em> the water-device, i.e. meditation by (the element of) water (cp. <em>Mystic</em> 75 n.) DN.iii.268; Ja.i.313; Dhs.203 Vism.170; Dhp-a.i.312; Dhp-a.iii.214. <em>-<a href="bword://dh%C4%81tu">dhātu</a></em> the fluid element the essential element in water, i.e. element of cohesion (see <em>Cpd.</em> 155 n. 2; <em>Mystic</em> 9 n. 2; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 201, 242; DN.iii.228, DN.iii.247; MN.i.187, MN.i.422: Dhs.652; Ne.74. See also <em>dhātu<br />■ rasa</em> the taste of water AN.i.32; Snp-a.6 <em>-<a href="bword://sama">sama</a></em> resembling water MN.i.423.</p><p>Vedic ap &amp; āp, f. sg. apā, pl. āpaḥ, later Sk. also āpaḥ nt<br />■ Idg.; <em>*ap &amp; *ab;</em>, primarily to Lith. ùpé water, Old Prussian ape river, Gr. ̓*Λπία Name of the Peloponnesus; further (as <em>*ab</em>) to Lat. amnis river Sk. abda cloud, &amp; perhaps ambu water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kasiṇa">kasiṇa</a>, <a href="bword://dhātu">dhātu</a>, <a href="bword://sama">sama</a></p>

āpokasiṇa|aapokasi.na|apokasina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapokasi.na</span></p><p>meditation based on water; the meditation-object water</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpo">āpo</a></p>

āpuccha|aapuccha|apuccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapuccha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpucchat">āpucchat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchat|aapucchat|apucchat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpucchat">āpucchat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchati|aapucchati|apucchati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchati</span></p><p>asks; asks permission, asks leave (for); takes leave (of); bids farewell (to).</p>

āpucchati|aapucchati|apucchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchati</span></p><p>to enquire after, look for, ask, esp. to ask permission or leave; aor. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81pucchi">āpucchi</a></em> Ja.i.140; Pv-a.110; grd. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81pucchitabba">āpucchitabba</a></em> Dhp-a.i.6; ger. <em>āpucchitvā</em> Vin.iv.267 (apaloketvā +); Mil.29; Pv-a.111; <em>āpucchitūna</em> (cp. Geiger § 211) Thig.426; <em>āpuccha</em> Thig.416 &amp; <em><a href="bword://%C4%81pucch%C4%81">āpucchā</a></em> [= āpṛcchya, cp. Vedic ācyā for ācya], only in neg. form <em>an˚</em>; without asking Vin.ii.211, Vin.ii.219; Vin.iv.165, Vin.iv.226 (= anapaloketvā); Dhp-a.i.81<br />pp <em>āpucchita</em> Vin.iv.272.</p><p>ā + pucchati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchi">āpucchi</a>, <a href="bword://āpucchitabba">āpucchitabba</a>, <a href="bword://āpucchā">āpucchā</a></p>

āpucchi|aapucchi|apucchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchita|aapucchita|apucchita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. (act.) (mfn.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchita</span></p><p>having taken leave; having asked for permission; (n.) asking permission, taking leave</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchitabba|aapucchitabba|apucchitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchitvā|aapucchitvaa|apucchitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchitūna|aapucchituuna|apucchituna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchituuna</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchiṃsu|aapucchi.msu|apucchimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpucchā|aapucchaa|apuccha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapucchaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpucchati">āpucchati</a></p>

āpā|aapaa|apa
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaa</span></p><p>A class of deities who were present at the preaching of the Mahāsamaya Sutta. DN.ii.259</p>

āpā|aapaa|apa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaa</span></p><p>*Āpā &amp; *Āvā</p><p>(f.) misery, misfortune Ja.ii.317 (loc. pl. āpāsu, variant reading avāsu, C. āpadāsu); Ja.iii.12 (BB āvāsu); Ja.v.82 (avāgata gone into misery, variant reading apagata, C apagata parihīna), Ja.v.445 (loc. āvāsu, variant reading avāsu, C. āpadāsu), Ja.v.448 (āvāsu kiccesu; variant reading apassu, read āpāsu). <em>Note.</em> Since *āpā only occurs in loc. pl., the form āpāsu is to be regarded as a direct contraction of Sk. <em>āpatsu</em>.</p><p>for āpadā, q.v.</p>

āpādaka|aapaadaka|apadaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadaka</span></p><p>(-ikā)n. (from āpādeti) bringing up, taking care of nurturing; (f.) a nurse, a foster-mother.</p>

āpādaka|aapaadaka|apadaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadaka</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) producing, leading to (-˚) Vv-a.4 (abhiññ˚ catuttha-jjhāna).</li><li>(n.) one who takes care of a child, a protector, guardian AN.i.62 = AN.i.132 = Iti.110 (+ posaka)<br />■ f. <strong>āpādikā</strong> a nurse, foster-mother Vin.ii.289 (+ posikā).</li></ol><p>fr. ā + <em>pad</em></p><p>See also: āpādikā</p>

āpādayati|aapaadayati|apadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadayati</span></p><p>causes to enter upon or reach (a state); brings to; brings up, nurtures</p><p>See also: āpadeti, <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpādenta|aapaadenta|apadenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpādeti">āpādeti</a></p>

āpādesi|aapaadesi|apadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpādeti">āpādeti</a></p>

āpādeta|aapaadeta|apadeta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadeta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpādetar">āpādetar</a></p><p>(from āpādeti) one who brings up, who nurtures.</p>

āpādetar|aapaadetar|apadetar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadetar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpādetar">āpādetar</a></p><p>(from āpādeti) one who brings up, who nurtures.</p>

āpādeti|aapaadeti|apadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadeti</span></p><p>causes to enter upon or reach (a state); brings to; brings up, nurtures</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpādayati">āpādayati</a>, <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpādeti|aapaadeti|apadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadeti</span></p><p>to produce, make out, bring, bring into MN.i.78; MN.iii.248; SN.iv.110 (addhānaṃ to live one’s life, cp. addhānaṃ āpādi Ja.ii.293 = jīvit’addhānaṃ āpādi āyuṃ vindi C.); Snp-a.466<br />■ Cp. pari˚</p><p>Caus. of āpajjati</p>

āpādi|aapaadi|apadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpādi|aapaadi|apadi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadi</span></p><p>of <em><a href="bword://%C4%81pajjati">āpajjati</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpādu|aapaadu|apadu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpāduṃ|aapaadu.m|apadum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpajjati">āpajjati</a></p>

āpādā|aapaadaa|apada
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaadaa</span></p><p>nursing woman, in <em>an˚</em>; not nursing, unmarried Ja.iv.178.</p><p>short for āpādikā</p>

āpāna|aapaana|apana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaana</span></p><p>drinking; drinking party, banquet; banqueting-hall, drinking-hall Ja.i.52 (˚maṇḍala); Ja.v.292 (˚bhūmi); Vism.399 (id.); Dhp-a.i.213 (id., rañño).</p><p>fr. ā + <em>pā</em></p>

āpānaka|aapaanaka|apanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaanaka</span></p><p>adjective drinking, one who is in the habit of drinking DN.i.167.</p><p>āpāna + ka</p>

āpānīya|aapaaniiya|apaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaaniiya</span></p><p>adjective drinkable, fit for drinking or drinking with, in <em>-kaṃsa</em> drinking-bowl, goblet MN.i.316; SN.ii.110.</p><p>fr. āpāna, ā + <em>pā</em></p>

āpānīyakaṃsa|aapaaniiyaka.msa|apaniyakamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaaniiyaka.msa</span></p><p>metal drinking bowl or cup full of drink.</p>

āpātaparipātaṃ|aapaataparipaata.m|apataparipatam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaataparipaata.m</span></p><p>using at and into; flying towards and around and into.</p>

āpātha|aapaatha|apatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaatha</span></p><p>or neuter ‘area over which one moves’, region, sphere, range; field of perception.</p>

āpātha|aapaatha|apatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaatha</span></p><p>sphere, range, focus, field (of consciousness or perception; cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 199), appearance AN.ii.67; Ja.i.336 Vb.321; Mil.298; Vism.21, Vism.548; DN-a.i.228; Dhs-a.308 Dhs-a.333; Vv-a.232 (˚kāla); Dhp-a.iv.85; Sdhp.356. Usually in phrase <em>āpāthaṃ gacchati</em> to come into focus, to become clear, to appear MN.i.190; SN.iv.160, or <em>-ṃ āgacchati</em> Vin.i.184; AN.iii.377 sq.; AN.iv.404; Vism.125. Cp. ˚gata below.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://gata">gata</a></em> come into the sphere of, appearing, visible MN.i.174 = Nd ii.jhāna (an˚ unapproached); Pv-a.23 (āpāthaṃ gata)</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://gatatta">gatatta</a></em> abstr. fr. last: appearance Vism.617.</li></ul><p>etym.? Trenckner, Mil p.428 says: “I suspect ā. to be corrupted from āpāta (cp. āpatati), under an impression that it is allied to patha; but it is scarcely ever written so”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://gata">gata</a>, <a href="bword://gatatta">gatatta</a></p>

āpāthagata|aapaathagata|apathagata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaathagata</span></p><p>come within range, perceivable; within the sphere of perception</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āpātha">āpātha</a></p>

āpāthaka|aapaathaka|apathaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaathaka</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the (perceptual) sphere of, visible, in <em>-nisādin</em> lying down visible DN.iii.44, DN.iii.47. Cp. āpathaka.</p><p>fr. āpātha</p>

āpāthakanisādi|aapaathakanisaadi|apathakanisadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaathakanisaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpāthakanisādin">āpāthakanisādin</a></p><p>sitting within the view (of men), in public.</p>

āpāthakanisādin|aapaathakanisaadin|apathakanisadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaathakanisaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āpāthakanisādin">āpāthakanisādin</a></p><p>sitting within the view (of men), in public.</p>

āpāyika|aapaayika|apayika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaayika</span></p><p>belonging to a state of misery; (one) who is born in or doomed to existence in a state of misery, in a hell.</p>

āpāyika|aapaayika|apayika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaayika</span></p><p>adjective noun one suffering in an apāya or state of misery after death Vin.ii.202 = Iti.85 (variant reading ap˚) Vin.ii.205; DN.i.103; AN.i.265; Iti.42; Vism.16; Pv-a.60.</p><p>fr. apāya</p>

āpāṇa|aapaa.na|apana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaa.na</span></p><p>life, lit. breathing, only in cpd. <em>-koṭi</em> the end of life Mil.397; Dāvs iii.93; adj. <em>-<a href="bword://ko%E1%B9%ADika">koṭika</a></em> MN.ii.120; Vism.10.</p><p>ā + pāṇa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://koṭika">koṭika</a></p>

āpāṇakoṭikaṃ|aapaa.nako.tika.m|apanakotikam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapaa.nako.tika.m</span></p><p>until the end of one’s life, all one’s life.</p>

āpūrati|aapuurati|apurati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapuurati</span></p><p>becomes full; increases.</p>

āpūrati|aapuurati|apurati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aapuurati</span></p><p>to be filled, to become full, to increase Ja.iii.154 (cando ā. = pūrati C.); Ja.iv.26, Ja.iv.99, Ja.iv.100.</p><p>a + pūrati</p>

ārabbha|aarabbha|arabbha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabbha</span></p><p>undertaking; making a starting point of; taking as subject-matter; with reference to; concerning (+ ace. or gen.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabbha|aarabbha|arabbha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabbha</span></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li>beginning, undertaking etc., in cpd. <em>-vatthu</em> occasion for making an effort, concern, duty, obligation DN.iii.256 = AN.iv.334 (eight such occasions enumerated).</li><li>(prep. with acc.) lit. beginning with, taking (into consideration), referring to, concerning, with reference to, about DN.i.180; AN.ii.27; Iti.103 (senāsanaṃ ā.); Snp.972 (upekhaṃ; variant reading ārambha C. uppādetvā); Pv.4.1 (pubbe pete ā.); Dhp-a.i.3; Dhp-a.ii.37; Pv-a.3 (seṭṭhiputta-petaṃ ā.), Pv-a.16, and passim.</li></ol><p>ger. of ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in abs. function; cp. Sk. ārabhya meaning since, from</p>

ārabbhati|aarabbhati|arabbhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabbhati</span></p><p>takes hold of; begins, undertakes, embarks upon; starts, initiates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārambhati">ārambhati</a>, <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabha|aarabha|arabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārabhat">ārabhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabhamāna|aarabhamaana|arabhamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabhat|aarabhat|arabhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārabhat">ārabhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabhati|aarabhati|arabhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhati</span></p><p>ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. takes hold of; begins, undertakes, embarks upon; starts, initiates</p><p>ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. kills, slaughters; sacrifices.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārambhati">ārambhati</a>, <a href="bword://ārabbhati">ārabbhati</a></p>

ārabhati|aarabhati|arabhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhati</span></p><p>Ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to kill, destroy MN.i.371 (pāṇaṃ).</p><p>not with Morris J.P.T.S. 1889, 202 fr. <em>rabh</em> and identical with ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, but with Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v identical with Sk. ālabhate, ā + <em>labh</em> meaning to seize the sacrificial animal in order to kill it; cp. nirārambha</p><p>Ārabhati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; Ārabbhati;</p><p>to begin, start, undertake, attempt SN.i.156 (ārabbhatha “bestir yourselves”) = Mil.245 Thag.256 (bh.); Pp.64 (bh.); viriyaṃ ārabhati to make an effort, to exert oneself (cp. ārambha) AN.iv.334. aor. <strong>ārabhi</strong> Dhp-a.ii.38 &amp; <em>ārabbhi</em> Pv-a.35<br />ger <em><a href="bword://%C4%81rabbha">ārabbha</a></em>, see sep<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81raddha">āraddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + rabhati, Sk. ārabhati &amp; ārambhati, ā + ; <em>rabh</em></p><p>See also: ārabhi, <a href="bword://ārabbha">ārabbha</a>, <a href="bword://āraddha">āraddha</a></p>

ārabhissati|aarabhissati|arabhissati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhissati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabhiyamāna|aarabhiyamaana|arabhiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhiyamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

ārabhiṃsu|aarabhi.msu|arabhimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarabhi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

āracayāracayā|aaracayaaracayaa|aracayaracaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaracayaaracayaa</span></p><p>arranging again and again; pulling this way and that.</p>

āracayāracayā|aaracayaaracayaa|aracayaracaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaracayaaracayaa</span></p><p>by means of hammering, slashing or beating (like beating a hide) Snp.673 (gloss ārajayārajayā fr. ā + <em>*rañj</em> or <em>*raj</em>)<br />■ Snp-a.481 explains the passage as follows: ārajayārajayā; i.e. yathā manussā allacammaṃ bhūmiyaṃ pattharitvā khīlehi ākoṭenti, evaṃ ākoṭetvā pharasūhi phāḷetvā ekam ekaṃ koṭiṃ chinditvā vihananti, chinnachinnakoṭi punappuna samuṭṭhāti; āracayāracayā ti pi pāṭho, āviñjitvā (variant reading BB āvijjhitvā) āviñjitvā ti attho<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ra%C3%B1jita">ārañjita</a></em>.</p><p>ā + racayā a ger. or abl. form. fr. ā + <em>*rac</em>, in usual Sk. meaning “to produce”, but here as a sound-root for slashing noise, in reduplication for sake of intensification. Altogether problematic</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārañjita">ārañjita</a></p>

āraddha|aaraddha|araddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraddha</span></p><p>āraddha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. (pass.) taken hold of, undertaken, begun, initiated</p><p>āraddha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. accomplished, achieved; pleased; satisfied.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a></p>

āraddha|aaraddha|araddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraddha</span></p><p>adjective begun, started, bent on, undertaking, holding on to, resolved, firm AN.i.148 (āraddhaṃ me viriyaṃ Iti.30; Pv-a.73 (ṭhapetuṃ began to place), Pv-a.212 (gantuṃ). Cp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81r%C4%81dhaka">ārādhaka</a></em> 1.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://citta">citta</a></em> concentrated of mind, decided, settled DN.i.176; MN.i.414; SN.ii.21; Snp.p.102; Snp-a.436. Cp. ārādheti 1.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a></em> (adj.) strenuous, energetic, resolute Vin.i.182; DN.iii.252, DN.iii.268, DN.iii.282, DN.iii.285; AN.i.24; Snp.68, Snp.344; Iti.71 (opp. hīna-viriya); Cnd.131; Pts.i.171; Thag-a.95. Cp <strong>viriyārambha</strong>; f. abstr. <em>˚viriyatā</em> MN.i.19.</li></ul><p>pp. of ā + <em>rabh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādhaka">ārādhaka</a>, <a href="bword://citta">citta</a>, <a href="bword://viriya">viriya</a>, viriyārambha</p>

āraddhā|aaraddhaa|araddha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraddhaa</span></p><p>having begun or started.</p>

āragga|aaragga|aragga
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaragga</span></p><p>the point of an awl, the head of certain arrows having the shape of an awl, or an arrow of that kind (see Halayudha p. 151) AN.i.65; Snp.625, Snp.631; Dhp.401 Dhp.407; Vism.306; Dhp-a.ii.51; Dhp-a.iv.181.</p><p>ārā + agga; Sk. ārāgra of ārā an awl, a prick</p>

āraha|aaraha|araha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraha</span></p><p>adjective metri causa for <em><a href="bword://araha">araha</a></em> deserving Ja.vi.164.</p><p>Āraha</p><p>neuter only in pl. <em>gihīnaṃ ārahāni</em>, things proper to laymen, DN.iii.163.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://araha">araha</a></p>

ārajayārajayā|aarajayaarajayaa|arajayarajaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarajayaarajayaa</span></p><p>scoring again and again.</p>

āraka|aaraka|araka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraka</span></p><p>distant, far from.</p>

ārakatta|aarakatta|arakatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter abstr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakatta</span></p><p>, id. the state of being far from.</p>

ārakatta|aarakatta|arakatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakatta</span></p><p>warding off, keeping away, holding aloof, being far from (c. gen.); occurring only in pop. etym. of arahant at AN.iv.145; Dhp-a.iv.228; DN-a.i.146 = Vv-a.105, Vv-a.106 = Pv-a.7; cp. Dhs-a.349.</p><p>*ārakāt + tvaṃ</p>

ārakkha|aarakkha|arakkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine \~ā, feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkha</span></p><p>guard, protection; preservation; keeping fast.</p>

ārakkha|aarakkha|arakkha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkha</span></p><p>watch, guard, protection, care DN.ii.59; DN.iii.289; SN.iv.97, SN.iv.175, SN.iv.195; AN.ii.120; AN.iii.38; AN.iv.266, AN.iv.270, AN.iv.281 (˚sampadā), AN.iv.322 (id.), AN.iv.400; AN.v.29 sq.; Ja.i.203; Ja.ii.326; Ja.iv.29 (˚purisa); Ja.v.212 (˚ṭṭhāna, i.e. harem), Ja.v.374 (˚parivāra); Pp.21 (an˚), Pp.24; Mil.154; Vism.19 (˚gocara preventive behaviour, cautiousness); Snp-a.476 (˚devatā); Kp-a.120 (id.), Kp-a.169; Dhp-a.ii.146; Pv-a.195 Sdhp.357, Sdhp.365.</p><p>ā + rakkha</p>

ārakkhasārathi|aarakkhasaarathi|arakkhasarathi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkhasaarathi</span></p><p>guarding charioteer</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārakkha">ārakkha</a></p>

ārakkhat|aarakkhat|arakkhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkhat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārakkhat">ārakkhat</a></p><p>guarding, keeping watch over, preserving.</p>

ārakkheyya|aarakkheyya|arakkheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkheyya</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://arakkheyya">arakkheyya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://arakkheyya">arakkheyya</a></p>

ārakkhika|aarakkhika|arakkhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkhika</span></p><p>guard, watchman Ja.iv.29.</p><p>fr. ārakkha</p>

ārakkhādhikaraṇaṃ|aarakkhaadhikara.na.m|arakkhadhikaranam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakkhaadhikara.na.m</span></p><p>as a consequence of guarding or holding fast</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārakkha">ārakkha</a></p>

ārakā|aarakaa|araka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarakaa</span></p><p>adverb far off, far from, away from, also used as prep. c abl. and as adj. pl. keeping away from, removed, far Vin.ii.239 = AN.iv.202 (sanghamhā); DN.i.99, DN.i.102 (adj.) DN.i.167; MN.i.280 (adj.) SN.ii.99; SN.iv.43 sq.; AN.i.281; Iti.91; Ja.i.272; Ja.iii.525; Ja.v.451; Mil.243; Vv-a.72, Vv-a.73 (adj + viratā).</p><p>Sk. ārāt &amp; ārakāt, abl. form. fr. *āraka, see *<a href="bword://%C4%81r%C4%81">ārā</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārā">ārā</a></p>

āramati|aaramati|aramati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaramati</span></p><p>ceases, stops; leaves off; keeps away (from).</p>

ārambha|aarambha|arambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarambha</span></p><p>wrongly directed exertion or action.</p>

ārambha|aarambha|arambha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarambha</span></p><ol><li>attempt, effort, inception of energy (cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 15 &amp; <em>K. S.</em> p. 318 giving Commentary defined as kicca, karaṇīya, attha, i.e. 1. undertaking duty, 2. object. SN.i.76 (mah˚); SN.v.66, SN.v.104 sq. (˚dhātu) SN.iii.338 (identical), SN.iii.166 (˚ja; Text arabbhaja, variant reading ārambhaja to be preferred) = Pp.64 Mil.244 Netti.41 Dhs-a.145</li><li><em>-viriyārambha</em> (cp. āraddha-viriya) zeal, resolution, energy Vin.ii.197 SN.iv.175 AN.i.12 AN.i.16</li><li>support, ground object, thing Ne.70 sq., Ne.107; <em>an˚</em>; unsupported, independent Snp.743 (= nibbāna Snp-a.507). Cp. also nirambha upārambha, sārambha.</li></ol><p>Sk. ārambha in meaning “beginning”, fr ā + <em>rabh</em> (rambh) cp. ārabhati</p>

ārambhati|aarambhati|arambhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarambhati</span></p><p>takes hold of; begins, undertakes, embarks upon; starts, initiates</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārabhati">ārabhati</a>, <a href="bword://ārabbhati">ārabbhati</a></p>

ārammana|aarammana|arammana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarammana</span></p><p><strong>Ārammana:</strong> Object. There are six: visible object, sound, odour, taste, body-contact, mental-object. The mental-object <em>dhammārammana</em> may be physical or mental, past, present or future, real or imaginary. The 5 sense-objects belong to the materiality-group <em>rūpa</em> - <em>khandha</em> see: <em>khandha</em>. They form the external foundations for the sense-perceptions, and without them no sense-perception or sense-consciousness seeing, hearing, etc. can ever arise. Cf. <em>āyatana</em> <em>paccaya.</em></p>

ārammanupanissaya|aarammanupanissaya|arammanupanissaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarammanupanissaya</span></p><p><strong>Ārammanupanissaya:</strong> Decisive Support Conditional Relation. see: <em>paccaya</em></p>

ārammanādhipati|aarammanaadhipati|arammanadhipati
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarammanaadhipati</span></p><p><strong>Ārammanādhipati,</strong> Predominance Conditional Relation. see: <em>paccaya</em></p>

ārammaṇa|aaramma.na|arammana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaramma.na</span></p><p>basis, starting point (for producing or initiating activity), footing; basis of meditation; object, object of consideration, sense-object.</p>

ārammaṇa|aaramma.na|arammana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaramma.na</span></p><p>primary meaning “foundation”, from this applied in the foll. senses:</p><ol><li>support, help, footing, expedient, anything to be depended upon as a means of achieving what is desired, i.e. basis of operation, chance Snp.1069 (= ālambana, nissaya, upanissaya Cnd.132); Pv.i.4#1 (yaṃ kiñc’ ārammaṇaṃ katvā) ārammaṇaṃ labhati (+ otāraṃ labhati) to get the chance SN.ii.268; SN.iv.185</li><li>condition, ground, cause, means esp. a cause of desire or clinging to life, pl. <em>-ā</em> causes of rebirth (interpreted by taṇhā at Mnd.429), lust Snp.474 (= paccayā Snp-a.410), Snp.945 (= Mnd.429); Kp-a.23; Dhp-a.i.288 (sappāy˚); Pv-a.279</li><li>a basis for the working of the mind &amp; intellect; i.e. sense-object, object of thought or consciousness, the outward constituent in the relation of subject &amp; object, object in general. In this meaning of “relation” it is closely connected with <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a></em> (see *<a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), so that it sometimes takes its place, it is also similar to <em><a href="bword://visaya">visaya</a></em>. <em>Cpd.</em> 3 distinguishes a 5 fold object viz. citta, cetasika, pasāda- &amp; sukhuma-rūpa, paññatti nibbāna. See on term especially; <em>Cpd.</em> 3, 14; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> xli.&amp; 209; A 1 sq.; AN.iv.385; Snp.506; Pts.i.57 sq. Pts.i.84 (four ā.); Pts.ii.97, Pts.ii.118, Pts.ii.143; Dhs.1 (dhamm˚ object of ideation), Dhs.180, Dhs.584, Dhs.1186 et passim; Vb.12, Vb.79, Vb.92 Vb.319, Vb.332 (four); Ne.191 (six); Vism.87 sq., Vism.375 (˚sankantika), Vism.430 sq. (in var. sets with ref. to var. objects) Vism.533; Dhs-a.48, Dhs-a.127; Vv-a.11, Vv-a.38<br />■ rūpārammaṇa lit dependence on form, i.e. object of sight, visible form especially striking appearance, visibility, sight DN.iii.228; SN.iii.53; AN.i.82; Ja.i.304; Ja.ii.439, Ja.ii.442; Pv-a.265. ārammaṇaṃ karoti to make it an object (of intellection or intention), to make it one’s concern (cp. Pv.i.4#1, above 1)-ārammaṇa <em>-<a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></em> clever in the objects (of meditation SN.iii.266; ā˚ <em>-<a href="bword://paccayat%C4%81">paccayatā</a></em> relation of presentation (i.e. of subj. &amp; obj.) Ne.80</li><li>(-˚) (adj.) being supported by, depending on, centred in, concentrated upon Pv-a.8 (nissay˚), Pv-a.98 (ek˚); Vv-a.119 (buddh˚ pīti rapture centred in the Buddha).</li></ol><p>cp. Sk. ālambana, <em>lamb</em>, but in meaning confounded with <em>rambh</em> (see rabhati)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a>, <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a>, <a href="bword://visaya">visaya</a>, <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a>, <a href="bword://paccayatā">paccayatā</a></p>

ārata|aarata|arata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarata</span></p><p>leaving off, keeping away from, abstaining Ja.iv.372 (= virata); Cnd.591 (+ virata paṭivirata).</p><p>Sk. ārata, pp. of ā + <em>ram</em>, cp. ārati</p>

ārati|aarati|arati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarati</span></p><p>abstinence; keeping away from; aversion.</p>

ārati|aarati|arati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarati</span></p><p>leaving off, abstinence Vv.63#9 (= paṭivirati Vv-a.263); in exegetical style occurring in typ. comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with virati paṭivirati veramaṇī, e.g. at Cnd.462; Dhs.299.</p><p>Sk. ārati, ā + <em>ram</em></p>

āratta|aaratta|aratta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaratta</span></p><p>? time, period (orig. affected, tinted with), only in cpd. <em>vassāratta</em> the rainy season, lent Ja.iv.444; Dāvs ii.74.</p><p>Sk. cp. ārakta, pp. of ā + <em>raj</em></p>

ārañjita|aara~njita|aranjita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~njita</span></p><p>slashed or scored; a mark, a furrow.</p>

ārañjita|aara~njita|aranjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~njita</span></p><p>furrowed, cut open, dug up, slashed torn (perhaps also “beaten”) MN.i.178 (hatthipadaṃ dantehi ārañjitaṃ an elephant-track bearing the marks of tusks, i.e. occasional slashes or furrows).</p><p>in form = Sk. *ārañjita, ā + pp. of rañjayati, Caus. of <em>rañj</em> or <em>raj</em>, but in meaning different. Perhaps to <em>rac</em> (as *racita) to furnish with, prepare, or better still to be regarded as an idiomatic Pāli form of soundroot <em>*rac</em> (see āracayā˚) mixed with <em>rañj</em>, of which we find another example in the double spelling of āracayā (&amp; ārajayā) q.v.</p>

ārañña|aara~n~na|aranna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~na</span></p><p>being in a forest, in the wild; forest-born, wild.</p>

āraññaka|aara~n~naka|arannaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~naka</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) belonging to the forest or the wild; living in the wild; wild; who has undertaken the <em>dhutaṅga</em> of living in the wild.</li><li>(m.) one who lives in the forest; a <em>bhikkhu</em> living in the wild, who has undertaken the <em>dhutaṅga</em> of living in the wild</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āraññika">āraññika</a></p>

āraññaka|aara~n~naka|arannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~naka</span></p><p>adjective belonging to solitude or the forest, sequestered; living in the forest, fond of seclusion, living as hermits (bhikkhū). Freq. spelt <em><a href="bword://ara%C3%B1%C3%B1aka">araññaka</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ Vin.i.92 (bhikkhū); Vin.ii.32, Vin.ii.197, Vin.ii.217 (bh.), Vin.ii.265 (bh.); MN.i.214; AN.iii.100 sq., AN.iii.219; AN.iv.21; AN.v.66; Ja.iii.174 (variant reading BB. a˚); Mil.342; Dhp-a.ii.94 (vihāra).</p><p>fr. arañña + ka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://araññaka">araññaka</a></p>

āraññakatta|aara~n~nakatta|arannakatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~nakatta</span></p><p>the habit of sequestration or living in solitude MN.i.214; MN.iii.40; AN.i.38.</p><p>abstr. fr. āraññaka, see also <em><a href="bword://ara%C3%B1%C3%B1akatta">araññakatta</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://araññakatta">araññakatta</a></p>

āraññakaṅga|aara~n~naka"nga|arannakanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~naka"nga</span></p><p>the practice of living in the wild (one of the <em>dhutaṅgas</em>):</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āraññaka">āraññaka</a></p>

āraññika|aara~n~nika|arannika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~nika</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) belonging to the forest or the wild; living in the wild; wild; who has undertaken the <em>dhutaṅga</em> of living in the wild.</li><li>(m.) one who lives in the forest; a <em>bhikkhu</em> living in the wild, who has undertaken the <em>dhutaṅga</em> of living in the wild</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āraññaka">āraññaka</a></p>

āraññika|aara~n~nika|arannika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~nika</span></p><p>adjective = āraññaka Vin.iii.15; AN.i.24; Pp.69; Vism.61, Vism.71 (where defined); Mil.341.</p>

āraññikanga|aara~n~nikanga|arannikanga
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~nikanga</span></p><p><strong>Āraññikanga:</strong> The training of living in the forest, is one of the ascetic purification-exercises <em>dhutānga</em>.</p>

āraññikaṅga|aara~n~nika"nga|arannikanga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~nika"nga</span></p><p>the practice of living in the wild (one of the <em>dhutaṅgas</em>):</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āraññaka">āraññaka</a></p>

āraññāka|aara~n~naaka|arannaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aara~n~naaka</span></p><p>Araññaka &amp; Āraññāka</p><p>(adj.) belonging to solitude or to the forest, living in the forest, fond of solitude, living as hermits (bhikkhū) MN.i.214 (ā˚), MN.i.469, MN.iii.89; SN.ii.187, SN.ii.202 (variant reading ā˚), SN.ii.208 sq.; SN.ii.281; AN.iii.343 AN.iii.391; AN.iv.291, AN.iv.344, AN.iv.435; AN.v.10. See also āraññaka.</p><p>arañña + ka</p>

āriya|aariya|ariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aariya</span></p><p>in anāriya at Snp.815 is metric for anariya (q.v.).</p>

ārocenta|aarocenta|arocenta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocenta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocesi|aarocesi|arocesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocesiṃ|aarocesi.m|arocesim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocesi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocesuṃ|aarocesu.m|arocesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocetabba|aarocetabba|arocetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

āroceti|aaroceti|aroceti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroceti</span></p><p>announces; informs; tells (something, acc., to someone, dat./gen., occasionally acc.).</p>

āroceti|aaroceti|aroceti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroceti</span></p><p>to relate, to tell, announce, speak to address DN.i.109, DN.i.224; Pv.ii.8#9 (aor, ārocayi); Pv-a.4, Pv-a.13 (aññamaññaṃ anārocetvā not speaking to each other), Pv-a.81, Pv-a.274 &amp; freq. passim<br />pp ārocita; Caus. ii. ārocāpeti (q.v.).</p><p>ā + roceti, Caus. of <em>ruc</em>; cp. BSk. ārocayati Sp. Avs.i.9 etc.</p>

ārocetvā|aarocetvaa|arocetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocimha|aarocimha|arocimha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocimha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocita|aarocita|arocita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocita</span></p><p>announced, declared; told</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroceti">āroceti</a></p>

ārocita|aarocita|arocita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocita</span></p><p>announced, called Vin.ii.213 (kāla).</p><p>pp. of āroceti</p>

ārocāpana|aarocaapana|arocapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapana</span></p><p>announcement Dhp-a.ii.167.</p><p>fr. ārocāpeti, Caus. of āroceti</p>

ārocāpesi|aarocaapesi|arocapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapesi</span></p><p>caused to tell; caused to announce.</p>

ārocāpesum|aarocaapesum|arocapesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapesum</span></p><p>they tell; they announce.</p>

ārocāpetabba|aarocaapetabba|arocapetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp. mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapetabba</span></p><p>will announce; will inform; will tell.</p>

ārocāpeti|aarocaapeti|arocapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapeti</span></p><p>to make some one announce, to let somebody know, usually in phrase <em>kālaṃ ā</em>. Snp.p.111; Ja.i.115, Ja.i.125; Dhp-a.ii.89; Pv-a.141.</p><p>Caus. ii. of āroceti</p>

ārocāpetvā|aarocaapetvaa|arocapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarocaapetvaa</span></p><p>told; announced; informed.</p>

ārodana|aarodana|arodana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarodana</span></p><p>weeping; lamenting.</p>

ārodana|aarodana|arodana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarodana</span></p><p>crying, lamenting AN.iii.268 sq.; Ja.i.34; Dhp-a.i.184; Dhp-a.ii.100.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>rud</em>, cp. āruṇṇa</p>

ārogatā|aarogataa|arogata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarogataa</span></p><p>freedom from illness, health Mil.341.</p><p>abstr. fr. a + roga + tā</p>

ārogiya|aarogiya|arogiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarogiya</span></p><p>freedom from disease; health, well-being</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārogya">ārogya</a></p>

ārogya|aarogya|arogya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarogya</span></p><p>freedom from disease; health, well-being</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārogiya">ārogiya</a></p>

ārogya|aarogya|arogya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarogya</span></p><p>absence of illness, health DN.i.11; DN.iii.220 (˚mada), DN.iii.235 (˚sampadā); MN.i.451 (T. ārūgya, variant reading ārogya), MN.i.508, MN.i.509; SN.ii.109; AN.i.146 (˚mada); AN.ii.143; AN.iii.72; AN.v.135 sq.; Snp.749, Snp.257 = Dhp.204 = Ja.iii.196; Mnd.160; Vism.77 (˚mada pride of health); Pv-a.129, Pv-a.198; Sdhp.234.</p><p>abstr. fr. aroga, i.e. ā (= a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) + roga + ya</p>

āroha|aaroha|aroha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroha</span></p><p>(-˚)</p><ol><li>climbing up, growth, increase, extent, in cpd. <em>-pariṇāha</em> length &amp; circumference SN.ii.206; AN.i.288; AN.ii.250; AN.iv.397; AN.v.19; Ja.iii.192; Ja.v.299; Ja.vi.20; Vb.345 (˚māna + pariṇāha-māna); Snp-a.382.</li><li>one who has climbed up, mounted on, a rider usually in cpd. <em>ass˚ &amp; hatth˚;</em> horse-rider &amp; elephantrider SN.iv.310; AN.ii.166 = AN.iii.162 (T. ārūha); AN.iv.107; Dhs-a.305.</li><li>outfit, possession (or increase, as 1? Snp.420 (vaṇṇ˚).</li></ol><p>fr. ā + <em>ruh</em></p>

ārohana|aarohana|arohana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohana</span></p><p>climbing, ascending, mounting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohaṇa">ārohaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://āruhana">āruhana</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhana">ārūhana</a></p>

ārohanta|aarohanta|arohanta
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohanta</span></p><p>One of the chief ministers at Sāvatthī. He joined the Order of monks and his wife became a nun. They had their meals together and she waited on him, fetching him water and fanning him. He forbade her to wait on him as it was improper and, angered by his words, she poured the water over his head and struck him with the fan. For this she was rebuked by the Buddha. Vin.iv.263</p>

ārohanta|aarohanta|arohanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

ārohanīya|aarohaniiya|arohaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohaniiya</span></p><p>fit to be mounted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohaṇīya">ārohaṇīya</a>, <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

ārohati|aarohati|arohati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohati</span></p><p>mounts, climbs, goes up; gets on to or into, steps on to; climbs on or into</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

ārohaṇa|aaroha.na|arohana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroha.na</span></p><p>climbing, ascending, mounting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohana">ārohana</a>, <a href="bword://āruhana">āruhana</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhana">ārūhana</a></p>

ārohaṇa|aaroha.na|arohana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroha.na</span></p><p>climbing, ascending; ascent Ja.i.70; Ja.vi.488; Mil.352; Vism.244; Pv-a.74. Alaka-manda</p><p>fr. ā + <em>ruh</em></p>

ārohaṇīya|aaroha.niiya|arohaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaroha.niiya</span></p><p>fit to be mounted</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohanīya">ārohanīya</a>, <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

ārohitvā|aarohitvaa|arohitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aarohitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

āropamānaka|aaropamaanaka|aropamanaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropamaanaka</span></p><p>raising, erecting; (or being impaled upon).</p>

āropana|aaropana|aropana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropana</span></p><p>“putting on to”, impaling Mil.197 (sūl˚), Mil.290 (id.).</p><p>fr. āropeti</p>

āropanā|aaropanaa|aropana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropanaa</span></p><p>(&amp; ~a n.?) raising, putting on or into; impaling (on a stake); stringing (of a bow); ascribing, indictment.</p>

āropayati|aaropayati|aropayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropayati</span></p><ol><li>makes climb, makes go on to or into; puts on to or into, raises; draws (water); impales (on a stake); strings (a bow); plants.</li><li>lays bare; refutes; imputes; ascribes</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropeti">āropeti</a>, <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

āropesuṃ|aaropesu.m|aropesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropeti">āropeti</a></p>

āropetabba|aaropetabba|aropetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

āropeti|aaropeti|aropeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropeti</span></p><ol><li>makes climb, makes go on to or into; puts on to or into, raises; draws (water); impales (on a stake); strings (a bow); plants.</li><li>lays bare; refutes; imputes; ascribes</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropayati">āropayati</a>, <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

āropeti|aaropeti|aropeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropeti</span></p><ol><li>to make ascend, to lead up to (w. acc.) Pv-a.76 (pāsādaṃ), Pv-a.160 (id.)</li><li>to put on, take up to (w. acc. or loc.) Pv.ii.9#2 (yakkhaṃ yānaṃ āropayitvāna); Pv-a.62 (sarīraṃ citakaṃ ā.), Pv-a.100 (bhaṇḍaṃ sakaṭesu ā.).</li><li>to put on, commit to the care of entrust, give over to (w. loc.) Ja.i.227; Pv-a.154 (rajjaṃ amaccesu ā.).</li><li>to bring about, get ready, make Pv-a.73, Pv-a.257 (sangahaṃ ā. make a collection); Snp-a.51, Snp-a.142.</li><li>to exhibit, tell, show, give SN.i.160 (ovādaṃ); Mil.176 (dosaṃ); Dhp-a.ii.75 (id.)</li><li>vādaṃ āropeti to refute a person, to get the better of (gen.) Vin.i.60; MN.ii.122; SN.i.160</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ropita">āropita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>Caus. of āruhati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropita">āropita</a></p>

āropetvā|aaropetvaa|aropetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropeti">āropeti</a></p>

āropita|aaropita|aropita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropita</span></p><p>made to clim; raised; put on or into; refuted, laid bare</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

āropita|aaropita|aropita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaropita</span></p><ol><li>produced, come forward, set up Pv-a.2.</li><li>effected, made SN.iii.12; Pv-a.92 Pv-a.257.</li><li>put on (to a stake), impaled Pv-a.220 (= āvuta).</li></ol><p>pp. of āropeti</p>

āruha|aaruha|aruha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruhat">āruhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruhana|aaruhana|aruhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhana</span></p><p>climbing, ascending, mounting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohaṇa">ārohaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://ārohana">ārohana</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhana">ārūhana</a></p>

āruhanta|aaruhanta|aruhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruhat|aaruhat|aruhat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruhat">āruhat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruhati|aaruhati|aruhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhati</span></p><p>mounts, climbs, goes up; gets on to or into, steps on to; climbs on or into</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

āruhati|aaruhati|aruhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhati</span></p><p>to climb, ascend, go up or on to Snp.1014 (aor. āruhaṃ); Sdhp.188; ger. āruhitvā Snp.321 āruyha Ja.vi.452; Snp.139 (variant reading abhiruyha); Iti.71. Caus. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ropeti">āropeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>ruh</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āropeti">āropeti</a></p>

āruhi|aaruhi|aruhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruhitvā|aaruhitvaa|aruhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruppa|aaruppa|aruppa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">n.m. &amp; mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppa</span></p><ol><li>(n.m.) formless, immaterial state or existence (esp. referring to the arūpajhānas).</li><li>(mfn.) formless, immaterial.</li></ol>

āruppa|aaruppa|aruppa
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppa</span></p><p><strong>Āruppa:</strong> Formless mental absorption see: <em>jhāna</em></p>

āruppa|aaruppa|aruppa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppa</span></p><p>adjective formless, incorporeal; nt. formless existence DN.iii.275; MN.i.410 cp. MN.i.472.; MN.iii.163; SN.i.131 (˚ṭṭhāyin); SN.ii.123; AN.iv.316; Iti.61; Snp.754; Ja.i.406; Dhs.1385 (cp. trsl. 57); Vism.338; DN-a.i.224; Snp-a.488, Snp-a.508; Sdhp.5, Sdhp.10; the four Vism.iii.326 sq.</p><p>fr. arūpa as ā (= a<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->)-*rūpya</p>

āruppavasī|aaruppavasii|aruppavasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppavasii</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruppavasīn">āruppavasīn</a></p><p>being or living in a formless or immaterial existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruppa">āruppa</a></p>

āruppavasīn|aaruppavasiin|aruppavasin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. id.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppavasiin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruppavasīn">āruppavasīn</a></p><p>being or living in a formless or immaterial existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruppa">āruppa</a></p>

āruppaṭṭhayi|aaruppa.t.thayi|aruppatthayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppa.t.thayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruppaṭṭhayin">āruppaṭṭhayin</a></p><p>being or living in a formless or immaterial existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruppa">āruppa</a></p>

āruppaṭṭhayin|aaruppa.t.thayin|aruppatthayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruppa.t.thayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āruppaṭṭhayin">āruppaṭṭhayin</a></p><p>being or living in a formless or immaterial existence</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruppa">āruppa</a></p>

āruyha|aaruyha|aruyha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruyha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruyhati">āruyhati</a></p>

āruyhati|aaruyhati|aruyhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruyhati</span></p><p>mounts, climbs, goes up; gets on to or into, steps on to; climbs on or into</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

āruyhi|aaruyhi|aruyhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruyhi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruḷha|aaru.lha|arulha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaru.lha</span></p><p>(act.) who has climbed, mounted; gone on to, gone into; brought to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārūḷha">ārūḷha</a>, <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a></p>

āruṇṇa|aaru.n.na|arunna
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaru.n.na</span></p><p>weeping, crying, lamenting Mil.357.</p><p>orig. pp of ā + <em>rud</em></p>

ārā|aaraa|ara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraa</span></p><p>far off, at a distance; far from ((+abl.).</p>

ārā|aaraa|ara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraa</span></p><p>Ārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>feminine an awl; see cp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ragga">āragga</a></em>. Perhaps a der. of ārā is āḷakā (q.v.).</p><p>Sk. ārâ; <em>*ēl</em> “pointed”, as in Ohg. āla = Ger. ahle, Ags. āēl = E awl; Oicel. alr</p><p>Ārā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable far from, remote (from) (adv. as well as prep. with abl.) Snp.156 (pamādamhā), Snp.736; Dhp.253 (āsavakkhayā; Dhp-a.iii.377 explains by dūragata); Ja.ii.449 (jhānabhūmiyā; = dūre ṭhita C.), Ja.v.78 (saṃyame; = dūrato C.). See also <em><a href="bword://%C4%81rak%C4%81">ārakā</a></em>.</p><ul><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81ra">cāra</a></em> [in this comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>s. v. unecessarily expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as ārā = ārya; cp. similar phrases under ārakā] a life remote (from evil) AN.iv.389.</p></li><li><p><em>-<a href="bword://c%C4%81rin">cārin</a></em> living far from evil leading a virtuous life DN.i.4; MN.i.179; MN.iii.33; AN.iii.216, AN.iii.348; AN.iv.249; AN.v.138, AN.v.205; DN-a.i.72 (= abrahmacariyato dūra-cārin).</p></li></ul><p>Vedic ārād, abl. as adv.; orig. a root der. fr. *ara remoteness, as in Sk. araṇa foreign &amp; araṇya solitude q.v. under araṇa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --> and arañña</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āragga">āragga</a>, <a href="bword://ārakā">ārakā</a>, <a href="bword://cāra">cāra</a>, <a href="bword://cārin">cārin</a></p>

ārācāri|aaraacaari|aracari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraacaari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārācārin">ārācārin</a></p><p>living remote; living far from (unwholesome)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārā">ārā</a></p>

ārācārin|aaraacaarin|aracarin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraacaarin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārācārin">ārācārin</a></p><p>living remote; living far from (unwholesome)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārā">ārā</a></p>

ārādhaka|aaraadhaka|aradhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhaka</span></p><p>(~ikā)n. who conciliates, wins approval; accomplishing, fulfilling; successful.</p>

ārādhaka|aaraadhaka|aradhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhaka</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>[perhaps for *āraddhaka because of analogy to āraddha of ā + <em>rabh</em> ] successful accomplishing or accomplished, undertaking, eager Vin.i.70 (an˚ one who fails); MN.i.491; MN.ii.197 = AN.i.69; Mil.243; SN.v.19; AN.v.329 (in correlation with āraddhaviriya).</li><li>pleasing, propitiating Mil.227; Vv-a.220 (˚ikā f.).</li></ol><p>fr. ā + <em>rādh</em></p>

ārādhana|aaraadhana|aradhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhana</span></p><p>conciliating, winning over; accomplishment; success.</p>

ārādhana|aaraadhana|aradhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhana</span></p><p>Ārādhana &amp; Ārādhanā</p><p>neuter &amp; feminine satisfying, accomplishing; satisfaction, accomplishment DN.ii.287 (opp. virādhanā failure); MN.i.479 MN.ii.199; AN.v.211 sq.; Ja.iv.427.</p><p>either fr. ā + <em>rādh</em> or ā + <em>rabh</em>, cp. ārādhaka</p>

ārādhanā|aaraadhanaa|aradhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhanaa</span></p><p>Ārādhana &amp; Ārādhanā</p><p>neuter &amp; feminine satisfying, accomplishing; satisfaction, accomplishment DN.ii.287 (opp. virādhanā failure); MN.i.479 MN.ii.199; AN.v.211 sq.; Ja.iv.427.</p><p>either fr. ā + <em>rādh</em> or ā + <em>rabh</em>, cp. ārādhaka</p>

ārādhanīya|aaraadhaniiya|aradhaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhaniiya</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) to be fulfilled, to be achieved.</li><li>(n.) what is to be accomplished; success, approval</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādheti">ārādheti</a></p>

ārādhanīya|aaraadhaniiya|aradhaniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhaniiya</span></p><p>adjective to be attained, to be won; successful Vin.i.70 (an˚); Ja.ii.233 (dur˚).</p><p>grd. fr. ārādheti</p>

ārādhapekha|aaraadhapekha|aradhapekha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhapekha</span></p><p>looking for favor, wishing to gratify.</p>

ārādhayati|aaraadhayati|aradhayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhayati</span></p><p>wins the favor or approval of, conciliates, propitiates; honors, satisfies; accomplishes, fulfills, carries to a successful end; achieves, gains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādheti">ārādheti</a></p>

ārādhayitvā|aaraadhayitvaa|aradhayitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhayitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādhayati">ārādhayati</a></p>

ārādhayiṃsu|aaraadhayi.msu|aradhayimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhayi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādhayati">ārādhayati</a></p>

ārādhesi|aaraadhesi|aradhesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādheti">ārādheti</a></p>

ārādheti|aaraadheti|aradheti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadheti</span></p><p>wins the favor or approval of, conciliates, propitiates; honors, satisfies; accomplishes, fulfills, carries to a successful end; achieves, gains</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādhayati">ārādhayati</a></p>

ārādheti|aaraadheti|aradheti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadheti</span></p><ol><li>to please, win favour, propitiate, convince Ja.i.337 (dārake), Ja.i.421, Ja.i.452; Ja.ii.72 (manusse); Ja.iv.274 (for ābhirādheti T.); Vism.73 (ārādhayanto Nāthassa vana-vāsena mānasaṃ); Dhp-a.ii.71; Dāvs iii.93 (ārādhayi sabbajanaṃ); Mil.352. In older literature only in phrase <em>cittaṃ ārādheti</em> to please one’s heart, to gladden, win over, propitiate DN.i.118 sq., DN.i.175 (but cp āraddha-citta to ārabhati); MN.i.85, MN.i.341; SN.ii.107; SN.v.109; Ja.ii.372; Mil.25.</li><li>to attain, accomplish, fulfill, succeed SN.v.23 (maggaṃ), SN.v.82, SN.v.180, SN.v.294; It iii. (variant reading ārām˚); Snp.488 = Snp.509. Cp. ārādhaka 1</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81r%C4%81dhita">ārādhita</a></em> (q.v.)<br />■ See also parābhetvā.</p><p>Caus. of ā + <em>rādh</em>, in meaning 2 confused with ārabhati. In BSk. strangely distorted to ārāgayati; freq in Divy as well as Avs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādhita">ārādhita</a></p>

ārādhetvā|aaraadhetvaa|aradhetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārādheti">ārādheti</a></p>

ārādhita|aaraadhita|aradhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraadhita</span></p><p>pleased Sdhp.510.</p><p>pp. of ārādheti; Sk. ārādhita, but BSk. ārāgita, e.g. Divy.131, Divy.233</p>

ārāgga|aaraagga|aragga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraagga</span></p><p>the point of an awl.</p>

ārāma|aaraama|arama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraama</span></p><p>pleasure-grove, a garden, a park, esp. one given for the benefit of <em>bhikkhus</em> (and containing shelters or dwellings).</p>

ārāma|aaraama|arama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraama</span></p><ol><li>pleasure, fondness of (-˚), delight, always as adj. (-˚) delighting in, enjoying finding pleasure in (usually comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with rata, e.g. dhammārāma dhammarata finding delight in the Dh.) SN.i.235 SN.iv.389 sq. (bhav˚, upādān˚); AN.i.35, AN.i.37, AN.i.130; AN.ii.28 (bhāvan˚); Iti.82 (dhamm˚); Snp.327 (id.; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Snp-a.333 as rati and “dhamme ārāmo assā ti”); Pp.53 (samagg˚) Vb.351.</li><li>a pleasure-ground, park, garden (lit sport, sporting); classified at Vin.iii.49 as pupph˚ and phal˚ a park with flowers or with fruit (i.e. orchard) def. at Dhp-a.iii.246 as Veḷuvana-Jīvak’ ambavan’ ādayo i.e. the park of Veḷuvana, or the park belonging to Jīvaka or mango-groves in general. Therefore:<ol><li>(in general) a park, resort for pastime etc. Vin.ii.109; DN.i.106; Dhp.188; Vv.79#5 (amb˚ garden of mangoes); Vv-a.305 (id.); Pv.ii.7#8 (pl. ārāmāni = ārām’ ûpavanāni Pv-a.102)</li><li>(in special) a private park, given to the Buddha or the Sangha for the benefit of the bhikkhus where they meet &amp; hold discussions about sacred secular matters; a place of recreation and meditation, a meeting place for religious gatherings. Amongst the many ārāmas given to the bhikkhus the most renowned is that of Anāthapiṇḍika (Jetavana; see Ja.i.92, Ja.i.94, DN.i.178; Vin.iv.69; others more frequently mentioned are e.g. the park of Ambapālī (Vin.i.233); of Mallikā (DN.i.178) etc<br />■ Vin.i.39, Vin.i.140, Vin.i.283, Vin.i.291; Vin.ii.170; Vin.iii.6, Vin.iii.45, Vin.iii.162 Vin.iv.85; AN.ii.176; Dpvs.v.18.</li></ol></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://p%C4%81la">pāla</a></em> keeper of a park or orchard, gardener Vin.ii.109; Vv-a.288.</li><li><em>-ropa, -ropana</em> planter, planting of pleasuregroves SN.i.33; Pv-a.151.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></em> the site of an Ārāma Vin.i.140; Vin.ii.170; Vin.iii.50, Vin.iii.90.</li></ul><p>Sk. ārāma, ā + <em>ram</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pāla">pāla</a>, <a href="bword://vatthu">vatthu</a></p>

ārāmacetiya|aaraamacetiya|aramacetiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamacetiya</span></p><p>a shrine in a park; a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāmacetya">ārāmacetya</a>, ārama</p>

ārāmacetya|aaraamacetya|aramacetya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamacetya</span></p><p>a shrine in a park; a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāmacetiya">ārāmacetiya</a>, <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmadaṇḍa|aaraamada.n.da|aramadanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamada.n.da</span></p><p>A brahmin. Once when Mahā Kaccāna was staying at Vāraṇā on the banks of the Kaddamadaha, Ārāmadaṇḍa came to see him and asked him why nobles quarrelled with nobles, brahmins with brahmins, and householders with householders. “Because of their bondage and servitude to sensual lusts,” answered Mahā Kaccāna; and for the same reason recluses quarrelled with recluses. “Is there anybody in the world who has passed beyond this bondage?” “Yes,” said Mahā Kaccāna, “in Sāvatthī lives the Exalted One,” and he proceeded to describe the Buddha’s virtues. Ārāmadaṇḍa stood up with clasped hands and, turning in the direction of Sāvatthī, he uttered his adoration of the Buddha. Thenceforward he became a disciple of Mahā Kaccāna. A.i.65–67.</p>

ārāmakinī|aaraamakinii|aramakini
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamakinii</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81r%C4%81mika">ārāmika</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāmika">ārāmika</a></p>

ārāmanisādi|aaraamanisaadi|aramanisadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamanisaadi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārāmanisādin">ārāmanisādin</a></p><p>living near a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmanisādin|aaraamanisaadin|aramanisadin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamanisaadin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ārāmanisādin">ārāmanisādin</a></p><p>living near a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmapesaka|aaraamapesaka|aramapesaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamapesaka</span></p><p>superintendent of the park-servants</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmapāla|aaraamapaala|aramapala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamapaala</span></p><p>keeper or guardian of a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmaropa|aaraamaropa|aramaropa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamaropa</span></p><p>planter of gardens or parks</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmarāmaṇeyyaka|aaraamaraama.neyyaka|aramaramaneyyaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamaraama.neyyaka</span></p><p>the loveliness of parks; a beautiful park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmatā|aaraamataa|aramata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamataa</span></p><p>pleasure, satisfaction AN.ii.28; AN.iii.116; Vb.381; Mil.233.</p><p>abstr. fr. ārāma 1</p>

ārāmavatthu|aaraamavatthu|aramavatthu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamavatthu</span></p><p>the site or ground for a park</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārāma">ārāma</a></p>

ārāmika|aaraamika|aramika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamika</span></p><p>an attendant or servant (to the <em>bhikkhus</em>) in a park:</p>

ārāmika|aaraamika|aramika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamika</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>(to ārāma 1) finding delight in, fond of (c. gen.) (or servant in general?) Mil.6 (sanghassa trsl. at the service of the order).</li><li>(to ārāma 2) belonging to an Ārāma, one who shares the congregation, an attendant of the Ārāma Vin.i.207 sq. Vin.ii.177 (&amp; ˚pesaka), Vin.ii.211; Vin.iii.24; Vin.iv.40; Vin.v.204; AN.ii.78 (˚samaṇuddesa); AN.iii.109 (id.), AN.iii.275 (˚pesaka); Ja.i.38 (˚kicca Vism.74 (˚samaṇuddesa)<br />■ f. <em>ārāmakiṇī</em> a female attendant or visitor of an Ārāma Vin.i.208.</li></ol><p>fr. ārāma</p>

ārāmikagāma|aaraamikagaama|aramikagama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamikagaama</span></p><p>ĀrāmikagāmaPilindagāma</p><p>The name given to the village in which lived the five hundred park-keepers who were given by Bimbisāra to the Elder Pilindavaccha. It was near Rājagaha and was also called Pilindagāma. Pilindavaccha depended for his alms on the residents of this village. Vin.i.207–208 Vin.iii.249</p><p>near Rajagaha3town</p>

ārāmikini|aaraamikini|aramikini
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraamikini</span></p><p>female servant in an ārāma, or the wife of an ārāmika.</p>

ārāva|aaraava|arava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaraava</span></p><p>cry, sound, noise Dāvs iv.46.</p><p>cp. Sk. ārāva, fr. ā + <em>ru</em></p>

ārūgya|aaruugya|arugya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruugya</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81rogya">ārogya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārogya">ārogya</a></p>

ārūha|aaruuha|aruha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruuha</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81roha">āroha</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āroha">āroha</a></p>

ārūhana|aaruuhana|aruhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruuhana</span></p><p>climbing, ascending, mounting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārohaṇa">ārohaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://ārohana">ārohana</a>, <a href="bword://āruhana">āruhana</a></p>

ārūhanta|aaruuhanta|aruhanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruuhanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

ārūhati|aaruuhati|aruhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruuhati</span></p><p>mounts, climbs, goes up; gets on to or into, steps on to; climbs on or into</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruhati">āruhati</a>, <a href="bword://ārohati">ārohati</a></p>

ārūhitvā|aaruuhitvaa|aruhitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruuhitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

ārūḷha|aaruu.lha|arulha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruu.lha</span></p><p>(act.) who has climbed, mounted; gone on to, gone into; brought to</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āruḷha">āruḷha</a>, <a href="bword://ārūhati">ārūhati</a></p>

ārūḷha|aaruu.lha|arulha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaruu.lha</span></p><ol><li>ascended, mounted, gone up, gone on to Ja.iv.137; Ja.vi.452 (T. āruḷha); Vism.135 (nekkhamma-paṭipadaṃ an˚); Vv-a.64 (magga˚); Pv-a.47 (˚nāva), Pv-a.56 (hatthi˚).</li><li>come about, effected, made done Pv-a.2, Pv-a.144 (cp. BSk. pratijñām ārūḍha having taken a vow Divy.26).</li><li>(of an ornament) put on (to), arrayed Ja.vi.153, Ja.vi.488.</li></ol><p>pp. of āruhati</p>

āsa|aasa|asa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa</span></p><p>Āsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>contracted form of aṃsa in cpd. <strong>koṭṭhāsa</strong> part., portion etc.: see *<a href="bword://a%E1%B9%83sa">aṃsa</a>*<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->. Can we compare BSk. āsapātrī (see next).</p><p>Āsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>food, only in cpd. <strong>pātarāsa</strong> morning food, breakfast Snp.387 (pāto asitabbo ti pātar-āso piṇḍapātass’ etaṃ nāmaṃ Snp-a.374); Dhp-a.iv.211; see further ref under pātar; and <em>pacchā-āsa</em> aftermath SN.i.74. Can we compare BSk. āsa-pātrī (vessel) Divy.246? Der. fr. āsa is <em>āsaka</em> with abstr. ending āsakattaṃ “cating”, food, in nānā˚ various food or na + anāsak˚) Snp.249. See also nirāsa, which may be taken either as nir + *āśa or nir + *āsā.</p><p>Sk. āśa</p><p>Āsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>the adj. form of āsā (f.), wish, hope. See under <em><a href="bword://%C4%81s%C4%81">āsā</a></em>.</p><p>Āsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->4<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>archaic 3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sg. perf. of atthi to be, only in cpd. <em><a href="bword://itih%C4%81sa">itihāsa</a></em> = iti ha āsa “thus it has been”.</p><p>See also: koṭṭhāsa, <a href="bword://aṃsa">aṃsa</a>, pātarāsa, <a href="bword://āsā">āsā</a>, <a href="bword://itihāsa">itihāsa</a></p>

āsabha|aasabha|asabha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasabha</span></p><p>(~ī)n. coming from or produced from a bull; bull-like; belonging to a leader; lordly.</p>

āsabha|aasabha|asabha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasabha</span></p><p>(in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) a bull peculiar to a bull, bull-like, fig. a man of strong &amp; eminent qualities, a hero or great man, a leader, thus in tār˚ Snp.687; nar˚ Snp.684, Snp.696; āsabha-camma bull’s hide Ja.vi.453 (variant reading usabha˚).</p><ul><li><em>-ṭṭhāna</em> (as āsabhaṇṭhāna) “bull’s place”, first place distinguished position, leadership MN.i.69; SN.ii.27; AN.ii.8 (C. seṭṭha-ṭṭhāna uttama-ṭṭhāna); AN.iii.9; AN.v.33 sq.; DN-a.i.31; Kp-a.104.</li></ul><p>the guṇa-and comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> form of usabha, corresponding to Sk. ārṣabha → ṛṣabha, see <em><a href="bword://usabha">usabha</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://usabha">usabha</a></p>

āsabhin|aasabhin|asabhin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasabhin</span></p><p>adjective bull-like, becoming to a bull, lordly, majestic, imposing, bold; only in phrase ˚ṃ vācaṃ bhāsati “speak the lordly word” DN.ii.15, DN.ii.82; MN.iii.123j Ja.i.53; DN-a.i.91; cp. Dāvs i.28 (nicchārayi vācaṃ āsabhiṃ).</p><p>fr. āsabha</p>

āsada|aasada|asada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasada</span></p><p>approach; attack, attacking.</p>

āsada|aasada|asada
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasada</span></p><ol><li>approach, dealing with, business with (acc.), concern, affair, means of acting or getting Vin.ii.195 = Ja.v.336 (mā kuñjara nāgam āsado); MN.i.326 (metaṃ āsado = mā etaṃ āsado do not meddle with this, lit., be not this any affair); Ja.i.414 (cakkaṃ āsado you have to do with the wheel interpreted as adj. in meaning patto = finding, getting), Ja.vi.528 (interpreted as ankusa a hook, i.e. means of getting something).</li><li>(as adj.) in phrase <em><a href="bword://dur%C4%81sada">durāsada</a></em> hard to sit on, i.e. hard to get at, unapproachable, difficult to attack or manage or conquer Snp.p.107 (cp. Snp-a.451); Ja.vi.272; Vv.50#16 (= anupagamanīyato kenaci pi anāsādanīyato ca durāsado Vv-a.213); Mil.21; Dpvs.v.21; Dpvs.vi.38; Sdhp.384.</li></ol><p>ā + <em>sad</em>; cp. āsajja &amp; āsādeti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://durāsada">durāsada</a></p>

āsado|aasado|asado
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasado</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsadā|aasadaa|asada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasadaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsajja|aasajja|asajja
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasajja</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsajja|aasajja|asajja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasajja</span></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li>sitting on, going to, approaching; allocated, belonging to; sometimes merely as prep. acc “near” (cp. āsanna) Snp.418 (āsajja naṃ upāvisi he came up near to him), Snp.448 (kāko va selaṃ ā. nibbijjāpema Gotamaṃ); Ja.ii.95; Ja.vi.194; Mil.271.</li><li>put on to (lit. sitting or sticking on), hitting, striking SN.i.127 (khaṇuṃ va urasā ā. nibbijjapetha Gotamā “ye’ve thrust as ‘t were your breast against a stake. Disgusted, come ye hence from Gotama” <em>trsl.</em> p. 159; C. explains by paharitvā which comes near the usual paraphrase ghaṭṭetvā)</li><li>knocking against or “giving one a setting-to”, insulting ,offending, assailing DN.i.107 (ā. ā. avocāsi = ghaṭṭetvā DN-a.i.276) AN.iii.373 (tādisaṁ bhikkhuṁ ā.) Ja.v.267 (isiṁ ā. Gotamaṁ; Commentary p. 272 āsādetvā); Pv.iv.7#10 (isiṁ ā. āsādetvã Pv-a.266)</li><li>“sitting on”, i.e. attending constantly to, persevering, energetically, with energy or emphasis, willingly, spontaneously MN.i.250; DN.iii.258; AN.iv.236 (dānaṃ deti); Vv.10#6 (dānaṃ adāsiṃ; cp. Vv-a.55 samāgantvā). See āsada, āsādeti, āsīdeti, āsajjana.</li></ol><p>ger. of āsādeti, Caus. of āsīdati, ā + <em>sad</em>; Sk. āsādya</p>

āsajjana|aasajjana|asajjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasajjana</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āsajjanaṃ">āsajjanaṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsajjana|aasajjana|asajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasajjana</span></p><p>“knocking against”, setting on, insult, offence Vin.ii.203 (˚ṃ Tathāgataṃ an insult to the T.; quoted as such at Vv-a.55 where two meanings of ā. are given, corresponding to āsajja 1 &amp; 3, viz. samāgama &amp; ghaṭṭana, the latter in this quot.) = Iti.86 (so to be read with variant reading; T. has āpajja naṃ); SN.i.114 (apuññaṃ pasavi Māro āsajjanaṃ Tathāgataṃ; trsl. “in seeking the T. to assail”); Ja.v.208.</p><p>fr. āsajja in meaning of no. 3</p>

āsajjanaṃ|aasajjana.m|asajjanam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasajjana.m</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āsajjanaṃ">āsajjanaṃ</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsaka|aasaka|asaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaka</span></p><p>adjective belonging to food, having food, only in neg. <em>an˚</em>; fasting SN.iv.118; Dhp.141 (f. ā fasting bhatta-paṭikkhepa Dhp-a.iii.77); Ja.v.17; Ja.vi.63.</p><p>of āsa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

āsakatta|aasakatta|asakatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasakatta</span></p><p>having food, feeding, in an˚ fasting Snp.249 (= abhojana Snp-a.292).</p><p>abstr. fr. āsaka</p>

āsamāna|aasamaana|asamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasamaana</span></p><p>adjective wishing, desiring, hoping, expecting Vv.84#6 (kiṃ ā = kiṃ paccāsiṃ santo Vv-a.336); Pv.iv.1#24 (= āsiṃsamāna patthayamana Pv-a.226).</p><p>ppr. of āsaṃsati or āsiṃsati, for the usual earlier āsasāna</p>

āsana|aasana|asana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasana</span></p><p>sitting; a place for sitting.</p>

āsana|aasana|asana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasana</span></p><p>sitting, sitting down; a seat, throne MN.i.469; Vin.i.272 (= pallankassa okāsa); SN.i.46 (ek sitting alone, a solitary seat); AN.iii.389 (an˚ without a seat); Snp.338, Snp.718, Snp.810, Snp.981; Mnd.131; Ja.iv.435 (āsān ûdaka-dāyin giving seat &amp; drink); Ja.v.403 (identical); Ja.vi.413 Dhp-a.ii.31 (dhamm˚ the preacher’s seat or throne) Snp-a.401 Pv-a.16 Pv-a.23 Pv-a.141</p><ul><li><em>-ābhihara</em> gift or distinction of the seat Ja.i.81.</li><li><em>-ūpagata</em> endowed with a seat, sitting down Snp.708 (= nisinna Snp-a.495).</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%C3%B1%C3%B1%C4%81paka">paññāpaka</a></em> one who appoints seats Vin.ii.305.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%ADikkhitta">paṭikkhitta</a></em> one who rejects all seats, or objects to sitting down DN.i.167; AN.i.296; AN.ii.206; Pp.55.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://s%C4%81l%C4%81">sālā</a></em> a hall with seating accommodation Vism.69; Dhp-a.ii.65; Dhp-a.iv.46.</li></ul><p>from āsati</p><p>Āsana<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>(?) eating Vism.116 (visam˚, cp. visam-āsita Mil.302). See, however, <em>māsana</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://paññāpaka">paññāpaka</a>, <a href="bword://paṭikkhitta">paṭikkhitta</a>, <a href="bword://sālā">sālā</a></p>

āsanaka|aasanaka|asanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanaka</span></p><p>small seat Vv.1#5.</p><p>āsana + ka</p>

āsanantarikā|aasanantarikaa|asanantarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanantarikaa</span></p><p>an interval or seat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsana">āsana</a></p>

āsanapariyanta|aasanapariyanta|asanapariyanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanapariyanta</span></p><p>the last seat</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsana">āsana</a></p>

āsandi|aasandi|asandi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasandi</span></p><p>large or long chair (generally made of basket-work)</p>

āsandi|aasandi|asandi
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasandi</span></p><p>an extra long chair, a deck-chair Vin.i.192; Vin.ii.142, Vin.ii.163, Vin.ii.169, Vin.ii.170; DN.i.7 (= pamāṇâtikkant’ āsanaṃ DN-a.i.86), DN.i.55 = MN.i.515 = SN.iii.307 (used as a bier) AN.i.181; Ja.i.108. See note at <em>Dial.</em> i.11.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>sad</em></p>

āsandika|aasandika|asandika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasandika</span></p><p>bench; a chair.</p>

āsandikā|aasandikaa|asandika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasandikaa</span></p><p>fr. āsandi] a small chair or tabouret Vin.ii.149; Kp-a.44.</p>

āsandipañcama|aasandipa~ncama|asandipancama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasandipa~ncama</span></p><p>with a chair as the fifth’, four carrying a chair (as a bier)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsandi">āsandi</a></p>

āsanika|aasanika|asanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanika</span></p><p>adjective having a seat; in <em>ek˚</em>; sitting by oneself Vism.69.</p><p>fr. āsana</p>

āsanna|aasanna|asanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanna</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) near, near at hand; close together; <em>~aṃ</em>, adv., near.</li><li>(n.)<ol><li>nearness, vicinity; loc. <em>~e</em>, adv., near, nearby; in the vicinity (of)</li></ol></li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsanna|aasanna|asanna
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasanna</span></p><p>adjective near (cp. āsajja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), opp. <em><a href="bword://d%C5%ABra">dūra</a></em> Ja.ii.154; Dhp-a.ii.91; Pv-a.42, Pv-a.243.</p><p>pp. of ā + <em>sad</em>, see <em>āsīdati</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dūra">dūra</a></p>

āsappanā|aasappanaa|asappana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasappanaa</span></p><p>(fr.) lit. “creeping on to”, doubt, mistrust, always comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://parisappan%C4%81">parisappanā</a></em> Nd<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --> 1; Dhs.1004 (trsl. “evasion”, cp. <em>Dhs trsl.</em> p 116), Dhs.1118, Dhs.1235; DN-a.i.69.</p><p>fr. + <em>sṛp</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://parisappanā">parisappanā</a></p>

āsasāna|aasasaana|asasana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasasaana</span></p><p>or aor.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsiṃsati">āsiṃsati</a></p>

āsasāna|aasasaana|asasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasasaana</span></p><p>hoping wishing, desiring, longing for Snp.369 (an˚; Snp-a.365 however reads āsayāna), Snp.1090; Thag.528; Ja.iv.18 (= āsiṃsanto C.), Ja.iv.381; Ja.v.391 (= āsiṃsanto C.). See anāsasāna āsaṃsati, āsamāna &amp; āsayāna.</p><p>either grd. for *āsaṃsāna or contracted form of ppr. med. of āsaṃsati (= āsiṃsati) for *asaṃsamāna</p>

āsati|aasati|asati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasati</span></p><p>to sit DN-a.i.208; h. sg. āsi SN.i.130. - pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81s%C4%ABna">āsīna</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>from <em>as</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīna">āsīna</a></p>

āsatta|aasatta|asatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasatta</span></p><p>fixed or fastened to; attached to; intent on (+ loc).</p>

āsatta|aasatta|asatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasatta</span></p><p>Āsatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>lit. hanging on, in phrase kaṇṭhe āsatto kuṇapo a corpse hanging round one’s neck MN.i.120; Ja.i.5</li><li>fig. attached to, clinging to Ja.i.377 (+ satta lagga); Thag-a.259 (an˚).</li></ol><p>pp. of ā + <em>sañj</em></p><p>Āsatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>accursed, cursed Ja.v.446 (an˚).</p><p>pp. of ā + <em>śap</em></p>

āsatti|aasatti|asatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasatti</span></p><p>attachment; clinging to.</p>

āsatti|aasatti|asatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasatti</span></p><p>attachment, hanging on (w. loc.), dependence, clinging Vin.ii.156 = AN.i.138; SN.i.212; Snp.777 (bhavesu); Mnd.51, Mnd.221; Ne.12, Ne.128<br />■ Cp <em><a href="bword://nir%C4%81sattin">nirāsattin</a></em>.</p><p>ā + <em>sañj</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirāsattin">nirāsattin</a></p>

āsava|aasava|asava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasava</span></p><ol><li>‘inflowing’, influence (the concerns, attitudes, predispositions, listed as kāma, bhava, avijjā, and sometimes, ditthi, which form an obstacle to the realization of the truth.</li><li>affliction, pain (so commentary).</li><li>discharge (from a awound); of things, and so, in themselves and through the actions they motivate, bring about further existence; their ending or destruction is in fact arahatship).</li></ol>

āsava|aasava|asava
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasava</span></p><p><strong>Āsava:</strong> lit: fermentations, taints, corruptions, intoxicant biases. There is a list of four as in D. 16, Pts.M., Vibh.:<br />1: The mental fermentation of sense-desire <em>kāmāsava</em>, Ex: ‘All is pleasant’<br />2: The mental fermentation of desiring existence <em>bhavāsava</em>, Ex: ‘Being is good’<br />3: The mental fermentation of wrong views <em>ditthāsava,</em> Ex: ‘My opinion is best’<br />4: The mental fermentation of ignorance <em>avijjāsava.</em> Ex: ‘Suffering exists not’<br />A list of three, omitting the fermentation of views, is possibly older and is more frequent in the Suttas, e.g. in M. 2, M. 9, D. 33; A. III, 59, 67; A. VI, 63. In Vibh. Khuddakavatthu Vibh. both the 3-fold and 4-fold division are mentioned. The fourfold division also occurs under the name of floods <em>ogha</em> and yokes <em>yoga</em>.</p><p>Through the path of Stream-Entry, the fermentation of views is destroyed;<br />Through the path of Non-Returning, the fermentation of sense-desire;<br />Through the path of Arahatship, the fermentations of existence and ignorance.<br />M. 2 shows how to overcome the fermentations, namely, through insight, sense-control, avoidance, wise use of the necessities of life. For a commentarial exposition, see Atthasālini Tr. I, p. 63f: II, pp. 475ff.</p><p><em>khīnāsava</em> = one whose fermentations are eliminated, or one who is fermentation-free, is a name for the Arahat or Noble One. The state of Arahatship is frequently called <em>āsavakkhaya</em> the destruction of the fermentations. Suttas concluding with the attainment of Arahatship by the listeners, often end with the words: During this utterance, the minds of the Bhikkhus were freed from the mental fermentations through absence of clinging <em>anupādāya āsavehi cittāni vimuccimsū’ti.</em></p>

āsava|aasava|asava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasava</span></p><p>that which; flows (out or on to) outflow &amp; influx.</p><ol><li>spirit, the intoxicating extract or secretion of a tree or flower, O. C in Vin.iv.110 (four kinds); B. on DN.iii.182 (five kinds Dhs-a.48; Kp-a.26; Ja.iv.222; Ja.vi.9.</li><li>discharge from a sore, AN.i.124, AN.i.127 = Pp.30.</li><li>in psychology t.t. for certain specified ideas which intoxicate the mind (bemuddle it, befoozle it, so that it cannot rise to higher things). Freedom from the “Āsavas” constitutes Arahantship, &amp; the fight for the extinction of these āsavas forms one of the main duties of man. On the difficulty of translating the term see Cpd.227. See also discussion of term āsava (= āsavantī ti āsavā) at Dhs-a.48 (cp <em>Expositor</em> pp. 63 sq). See also <em>Cpd.</em> 227 sq., &amp; especially; <em>Dhs trsl.</em> 291 sq<br />■ The 4 āsavas are <em>kām˚, bhav˚ diṭṭh˚, avijj˚</em>; i.e. sensuality, rebirth (lust of life), speculation and ignorance<br />■ They are mentioned as such at DN.ii.81, DN.ii.84, DN.ii.91, DN.ii.94, DN.ii.98, DN.ii.123, DN.ii.126; AN.i.165 sq., AN.i.196, AN.ii.211; AN.iii.93, AN.ii.414; AN.iv.79; Pts.i.94, Pts.i.117; Dhs.1099, Dhs.1448 Cnd.134; Ne.31, Ne.114 sq<br />■ The set of 3, which is probably older (kāma˚, bhava˚, avijjā˚) occurs at MN.i.55; AN.i.165; AN.iii.414; SN.iv.256; SN.v.56, SN.v.189; Iti.49; Vb.364 For other connections see Vin.i.14 (anupādāya āsavehi cittani vimucciṃsu), Vin.i.17, Vin.i.20, Vin.i.182; Vin.ii.202; Vin.iii.5 (˚samudaya ˚nirodha etc.); DN.i.83, DN.i.167; DN.iii.78, DN.iii.108, DN.iii.130, DN.iii.220, DN.iii.223 DN.iii.230, DN.iii.240, DN.iii.283; MN.i.7 sq., MN.i.23, MN.i.35, MN.i.76, MN.i.219, MN.i.279, MN.i.445 (˚ṭhāniya); MN.ii.22; MN.iii.72, MN.iii.277; SN.ii.187 sq. (˚ehi cittaṃ vimucci); SN.iii.45 (id.); SN.iv.107 (id.), SN.iv.20; SN.v.8, SN.v.28, SN.v.410; AN.i.85 sq. (vaḍḍhanti), AN.i.98, AN.i.165 (˚samudaya, ˚nirodha etc.) AN.i.187; AN.ii.154 (˚ehi cittaṃ vimuttaṃ), AN.ii.196; AN.iii.21, AN.iii.93 (˚samudaya, ˚nirodha etc.), AN.iii.245, AN.iii.387 sq., AN.iii.410, AN.iii.414; AN.iv.13, AN.iv.146 (˚pariyādāna end of the ā.), AN.iv.161 (˚vighāta-pariḷāha); AN.v.70 AN.v.237; Thig.4, Thig.99, Thig.101 (pahāsi āsave sabbe); Snp.162 Snp.374, Snp.535 (pl. āsavāni), Snp.546, Snp.749, Snp.915, Snp.1100; Dhp.93 Dhp.253, Dhp.292; Mnd.331 (pubb˚); Vb.42, Vb.64, Vb.426; Pp.11 Pp.13, Pp.27, Pp.30 sq.; Mil.419; Dhs-a.48; Thag-a.94, Thag-a.173; Kp-a.26; DN-a.i.224; Sdhp.1; Pgdp.65 (piyāsava-surā, meaning?).</li></ol><p>Referring specially to the <em>extinction</em> <em>(khaya)</em> of the āsavas &amp; to Arahantship following as a result are the foll. passages: 1 <em>āsavānaṃ khaya</em> DN.i.156; SN.ii.29 SN.ii.214; SN.iii.57, SN.iii.96 sq, SN.iii.152 sq; SN.iv.105, SN.iv.175; SN.v.92, SN.v.203, SN.v.220, SN.v.271, SN.v.284; AN.i.107 sq., AN.i.123 sq., AN.i.232 sq., AN.i.273, AN.i.291, AN.ii.6, AN.ii.36, AN.ii.44 sq., AN.ii.149 sq., AN.ii.214; AN.iii.69, AN.iii.114, AN.iii.131, AN.iii.202, AN.iii.306, AN.iii.319 sq.; AN.iv.83 sq., AN.iv.119, AN.iv.140 sq., AN.iv.314 sq.; AN.v.10 sq., AN.v.36, AN.v.69, AN.v.94 sq, AN.v.105, AN.v.132, AN.v.174 sq., AN.v.343 sq.; Iti.49; Pp.27, Pp.62; Vb.334, Vb.344; Vism.9; DN-a.i.224; cp. ˚parikkhaya AN.v.343 sq. See also arahatta formula C 2 <em><a href="bword://kh%C4%AB%E1%B9%87%C4%81sava">khīṇāsava</a></em> (adj. one whose Āsavas are destroyed (see <em><a href="bword://kh%C4%AB%E1%B9%87a">khīṇa</a></em>) SN.i.13, SN.i.48 SN.i.53, SN.i.146; SN.ii.83, SN.ii.239; SN.iii.199, SN.iii.128, SN.iii.178; SN.iv.217; AN.i.77 AN.i.109, AN.i.241, AN.i.266; AN.iv.120, AN.iv.224, AN.iv.370 sq.; AN.v.40, AN.v.253 sq.; Pts.ii.173; cp. parikkhīṇā āsavā AN.iv.418, AN.iv.434, AN.iv.451 sq. āsavakhīṇa Snp.370 3 <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81sava">anāsava</a></em> (adj.) one who is free from the āsavas, an Arahant Vin.ii.148 = Vin.ii.164; DN.iii.112; SN.i.130; SN.ii.214, SN.ii.222; SN.iii.83; SN.iv.128; AN.i.81, AN.i.107 sq., AN.i.123 sq., AN.i.273, AN.i.291; AN.ii.6, AN.ii.36, AN.ii.87, AN.ii.146; AN.iii.19, AN.iii.29, AN.iii.114, AN.iii.166; AN.iv.98, AN.iv.140 sq., AN.iv.314 sq., AN.iv.400; AN.v.10 sq., AN.v.36, AN.v.242 AN.v.340; Snp.1105, Snp.1133; Dhp.94, Dhp.126, Dhp.386; Thag.100; Iti.75; Cnd.44; Pv.ii.6#15; Pp.27; Vb.426; Dhs.1101, Dhs.1451; Vv-a.9. Cp. <em><a href="bword://nir%C4%81sava">nirāsava</a></em> Thag-a.148<br />■ Opp. <em><a href="bword://s%C4%81sava">sāsava</a></em> SN.iii.47 SN.v.232; AN.i.81 AN.v.242; Dhs.990; Ne.10; Vism.13, Vism.438.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>sru</em>, would corresp. to a Sk. <em>*āsrava</em>, cp. Sk. āsrāva. The BSk. āśrava is a (wrong) sankritisation of the Pāli āsava, cp. Divy.391 &amp; kṣīnāśrava</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khīṇāsava">khīṇāsava</a>, <a href="bword://khīṇa">khīṇa</a>, <a href="bword://anāsava">anāsava</a>, <a href="bword://nirāsava">nirāsava</a>, <a href="bword://sāsava">sāsava</a></p>

āsavakhīna|aasavakhiina|asavakhina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasavakhiina</span></p><p>with <em>āsavas</em> destroyed; free from the <em>āsavas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsava">āsava</a></p>

āsavakkhaya|aasavakkhaya|asavakkhaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">m.a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasavakkhaya</span></p><p>ending, destruction of the <em>āsavas</em>, arahatship</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsava">āsava</a></p>

āsavakkhaya|aasavakkhaya|asavakkhaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasavakkhaya</span></p><p><strong>Āsavakkhaya:</strong> see above.</p>

āsavati|aasavati|asavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasavati</span></p><p>to flow towards, come to, occur, happen Ne.116.</p><p>ā + <em>sru</em>, cp. Sk. āsravati; its doublet is <em><a href="bword://assavati">assavati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://assavati">assavati</a></p>

āsavaṭṭhaniya|aasava.t.thaniya|asavatthaniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasava.t.thaniya</span></p><p>being a basis or cause of afflictions; being a basis or cause of the <em>āsavas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsava">āsava</a></p>

āsaya|aasaya|asaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaya</span></p><p>resting-place; home, lair, nest; receptacle; the stomach.</p>

āsaya|aasaya|asaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaya</span></p><ol><li>abode, haunt, receptacle; dependence on, refuge, support, condition SN.i.38; Vin.iii.151; Ja.ii.99; Mil.257; Vv-a.60; Pv-a.210; jal˚ river Vv-a.47; Pgdp.80; adj. depending on, living in (-˚) Mil.317; Mnd.362 (bil˚, dak˚ etc.) See also āmāsaya, pakkāsaya.</li><li>(fig.) inclination, intention, will, hope; often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> &amp; compared with <em><a href="bword://anusaya">anusaya</a></em> (inclination, hankering, disposition), e.g. at Pts.i.133; Pts.ii.158; Vb.340; Vism.140 (˚posana); Pv-a.197. Snp-a.182 (˚vipatti), Snp-a.314 (˚suddhi), Kp-a.103 (˚sampatti) Cp. nirāsaya.</li><li>outflow, excretion Pv.iii.5#3 (gabbh = gabbha-mala Pv-a.198); Vism.344.</li></ol><p>ā + <em>śī</em>, cp. in similar meaning &amp; derivation anusaya. The semantically related Sk. āśraya from ā + <em>śri</em> is in P. represented by assaya. Cp. also BSk. āśayataḥ intentionally in earnest Divy.281; Avs.ii.161</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anusaya">anusaya</a></p>

āsayati|aasayati|asayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasayati</span></p><p>to wish, desire, hope, intend Ja.iv.291 (grd. āsāyana, gloss esamāna). See <em><a href="bword://%C4%81saya">āsaya</a></em>.</p><p>ā + <em>śī</em>; lit. “lie on”, cp. Ger. anliegen &amp; Sk. āśaya = Ger. Angelegenheit</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsaya">āsaya</a></p>

āsaṃsa|aasa.msa|asamsa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa.msa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsiṃsati">āsiṃsati</a></p>

āsaṃsa|aasa.msa|asamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa.msa</span></p><p>adjective hoping, expecting something, longing for AN.i.108 = Pp.27 (expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> by Pp-a.208 as “so hi arahattaṃ āsaṃsati patthetī ti āsaṃso”) Snp-a.321, Snp-a.336. Cp. nir˚.</p><p>of *āśaṃsā, see next</p>

āsaṃsati|aasa.msati|asamsati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa.msati</span></p><p>to expect, hope for, wish Pp-a.208 (= pattheti). See also <em>āsamāna</em>.</p><p>for the usual āsiṃsati, ā + <em>śaṃs</em></p>

āsaṃsuka|aasa.msuka|asamsuka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa.msuka</span></p><p>full of expectation; hoping.</p>

āsaṃsā|aasa.msaa|asamsa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa.msaa</span></p><p>wish, desire, expectation, hope Ja.iv.92<br />■ Cp. <em><a href="bword://nir%C4%81sa%E1%B9%83sa">nirāsaṃsa</a></em>.</p><p>from ā + śaṃs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nirāsaṃsa">nirāsaṃsa</a></p>

āsaṅga|aasa"nga|asanga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nga</span></p><ol><li>adhering, clinging to, attachment, pursuit Ja.iv.11.</li><li>that which hangs on (the body), clothing garment, dress; adj. dressed or clothed in (-˚); usually in cpd. <em><a href="bword://uttar%C4%81sa%E1%B9%85ga">uttarāsaṅga</a></em> a loose (hanging) outer robe e.g. Vin.i.289; SN.iv.290; Pv-a.73; Vv-a.33 (suddh˚), Vv-a.51 (id.).</li></ol><p>ā + sanga fr. <em>sañj</em> to hang on, cp. Sk. <em>āsaṅga</em> &amp; āsakti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://uttarāsaṅga">uttarāsaṅga</a></p>

āsaṅgin|aasa"ngin|asangin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"ngin</span></p><p>adjective hanging on, attached to Ja.iv.11.</p><p>fr. āsanga</p>

āsaṅkamāna|aasa"nkamaana|asankamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsaṅkati">āsaṅkati</a></p>

āsaṅkati|aasa"nkati|asankati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkati</span></p><p>suspects; distrusts; fears.</p>

āsaṅkati|aasa"nkati|asankati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkati</span></p><p>to be doubtful or afraid, to suspect, distrust, Ja.i.151 (pret. āsankittha), Ja.i.163 (aor. āsanki); Ja.ii.203; Snp-a.298<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sa%E1%B9%85kita">āsaṅkita</a></em> (q v.),</p><p>ā + <em>śaṅk</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsaṅkita">āsaṅkita</a></p>

āsaṅkin|aasa"nkin|asankin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkin</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective fearing, anxious, apprehensive Snp.255 (bhedā˚); Ja.iii.192 (id.).</p><p>fr. āsankā</p>

āsaṅkita|aasa"nkita|asankita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkita</span></p><p>adjective suspected, in fear, afraid, apprehensive, doubtful (obj. &amp; subj.) Mil.173, Mil.372 (˚parisankita full of apprehension and suspicion); Dhp-a.i.223; Vv-a.110<br />■ Cp. <em>ussaṅkita &amp; parisaṅkita</em>.</p><p>pp. of āsankati</p>

āsaṅkā|aasa"nkaa|asanka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasa"nkaa</span></p><p>fear, apprehension, doubt, suspicion Ja.i.338; Ja.ii.383; Ja.iii.533; Ja.vi.350, Ja.vi.370; Dhp-a.iii.485; Vv-a.110<br />■ Cp. <em>sāsaṅka &amp; nirāsaṅka</em>.</p><p>Sk. āśankā fr. ā + <em>śaṅk</em></p>

āseva|aaseva|aseva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaseva</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āsevat">āsevat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevati">āsevati</a></p>

āsevamāna|aasevamaana|asevamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevati">āsevati</a></p>

āsevana-paccaya|aasevana-paccaya|asevana-paccaya
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevana-paccaya</span></p><p><strong>Āsevana-paccaya:</strong> repetition, is one of the 24 conditional relations <em>paccaya</em>.</p>

āsevana|aasevana|asevana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter &amp;</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevana</span></p><p><em><a href="bword://%C4%81sevan%C4%81">āsevanā</a></em> (f.)</p><ol><li>practice, pursuit, indulgence in Vin.ii.117; Pv-a.45.</li><li>succession repetition Dhs.1367; Kv.510 (cp. <em>trsl.</em> 294, 362); Vism.538.</li></ol><p>fr. āsevati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevanā">āsevanā</a></p>

āsevanta|aasevanta|asevanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevanta</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevati">āsevati</a></p>

āsevanā|aasevanaa|asevana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine \~a, neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevanaa</span></p><p>assiduous practice; habitual cultivation.</p>

āsevat|aasevat|asevat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āsevat">āsevat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevati">āsevati</a></p>

āsevati|aasevati|asevati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevati</span></p><p>attends to, practises, cultivates; indulges in.</p>

āsevati|aasevati|asevati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevati</span></p><p>to frequent, visit; to practise, pursue, indulge, enjoy AN.i.10; Snp.73 (cp. Cnd.94); Pts.ii.93 (maggaṃ)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sevita">āsevita</a></em>.</p><p>ā + <em>sev</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevita">āsevita</a></p>

āsevita|aasevita|asevita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevita</span></p><p>practiced; cultivated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsevati">āsevati</a></p>

āsevita|aasevita|asevita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasevita</span></p><p>frequented, indulged, practised, enjoyed Ja.i.21 (Ja.v.141; āsevita-nisevita); Ja.ii.60; Sdhp.93, Sdhp.237.</p><p>pp. of āsevati</p>

āsevitabba|aasevitabba|asevitabba
