<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasu.m</span></p><p>3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->rd<!-- raw HTML omitted --> pl. aor. of <em><a href="bword://atthi">atthi</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atthi">atthi</a></p>

āsā|aasaa|asa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa</span></p><p>wish, hope, expectation.</p>

āsā|aasaa|asa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa</span></p><p>expectation, hope, wish, longing, desire; adj. <em>āsa</em> (-˚) longing for, anticipating, desirous of Vin.i.255 (˚avacchedika hope-destroying), Vin.i.259; DN.ii.206; DN.iii.88; MN.iii.138 (āsaṃ karoti); AN.i.86 (dve āsā), AN.i.107 (vigat-āso one whose longings have gone); Snp.474 Snp.634, Snp.794, Snp.864; Ja.i.267, Ja.i.285; Ja.v.401; Ja.vi.452 (˚chinna chinnāsa C.); Mnd.99, Mnd.261, Mnd.213 sq; Vv.37#13 (perhaps better to be read with variant reading SS ahaṃ, cp. Vv-a.172); Pp.27 (vigat˚ = arahattāsāya vigatattā vigatāso Pp-a.208) Dhs.1059 (+ āsiṃsanā etc.), Dhs.1136; Pv-a.22 (chinn˚ disappointed), Pv-a.29 (˚âbhibhūta), Pv-a.105; Dāvs v.13; Sdhp.78 Sdhp.111, Sdhp.498, Sdhp.609.</p><p>cp. Sk. āśaḥ f.</p>

āsādana|aasaadana|asadana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadana</span></p><p>insult, offensiveness.</p>

āsādanapekkha|aasaadanapekkha|asadanapekkha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadanapekkha</span></p><p>looking to insult, aiming to be offensive</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādana">āsādana</a></p>

āsādayati|aasaadayati|asadayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadayati</span></p><p>meets, encounters, reaches; approaches with (physical or verbal) violence; strikes against, hits; offends, insults</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a>, <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsādesi|aasaadesi|asadesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsādetabba|aasaadetabba|asadetabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadetabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsādeti|aasaadeti|asadeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadeti</span></p><p>meets, encounters, reaches; approaches with (physical or verbal) violence; strikes against, hits; offends, insults</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādayati">āsādayati</a>, <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsādeti|aasaadeti|asadeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadeti</span></p><ol><li>to lay hand on, to touch, strike; fig. to offend, assail insult MN.i.371; Ja.i.481; Ja.v.197; aor. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81s%C4%81desi">āsādesi</a></em> Thag.280 (mā ā. Tathāgate); ger. <em>āsādetvā</em> Ja.v.272; Mil.100, Mil.205 (˚ayitvā); Pv-a.266 (isiṃ), <em>āsādiya</em> Ja.v.154 (āsādiya metri causa; isiṃ, cp. āsajja<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted -->), &amp; <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sajja">āsajja</a></em> (q.v.); infin. <em>āsāduṃ</em> Ja.v.154 &amp; <em>āsādituṃ</em> ibid.; grd. <em>āsādanīya</em> Mil.205; Vv-a.213 (an˚).</li><li>to come near to (c. acc.), approach get Ja.iii.206 (khuracakkaṃ). Asalha &amp; Asalhi</li></ol><p>Caus. of āsīdati, ā + <em>sad</em>; cp. āsajja &amp; āsanna</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādesi">āsādesi</a>, <a href="bword://āsajja">āsajja</a></p>

āsādi|aasaadi|asadi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">2 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsādimhase|aasaadimhase|asadimhase
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 pl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadimhase</span></p><p>(or of caus.)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsādiya|aasaadiya|asadiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaadiya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsādeti">āsādeti</a></p>

āsāsati|aasaasati|asasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaasati</span></p><p>to pray for, expect, hope; confounded with <em>śaṃs</em> in āsaṃsati &amp; āsiṃsati (q.v.) &amp; their derivations<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81si%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">āsiṭṭha</a></em> (q.v.). Asi &amp; Asim</p><p>cp. Sk. āśāsati &amp; āśāsti, ā + ; <em>śās</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsiṭṭha">āsiṭṭha</a></p>

āsāvacchedika|aasaavacchedika|asavacchedika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaavacchedika</span></p><p>depending on the disappointment of expectation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsā">āsā</a></p>

āsāvati|aasaavati|asavati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaavati</span></p><p>Name of a creeper (growing at the celestial grove Cittalatā) Ja.iii.250, Ja.iii.251.</p>

āsāḷhi|aasaa.lhi|asalhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa.lhi</span></p><p>the full-moon day in the month āsāḷha; (iic) the month āsāḷha.</p>

āsāḷhā|aasaa.lhaa|asalha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa.lhaa</span></p><p>Āsāḷhā &amp; Āsāḷhī</p><p>feminine Name of a month (JuneJuly) and of a Nakkhatta; only in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as Āsaḷha˚ Āsaḷhi˚, viz.; <em>-nakkhatta</em> Ja.i.50; Snp-a.208; <em>-puṇṇamā</em> Ja.i.63; Dhp-a.i.87; Snp-a.199; Vv-a.66; Pv-a.137; <em>-māsa</em> Snp-a.378 (= vassûpanāyikāya purimabhāge A.); Vv-a.307 (= gimhānaṃ pacchimo māso).</p><p>Sk. āṣāḍha</p>

āsāḷhī|aasaa.lhii|asalhi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa.lhii</span></p><p>Āsāḷhā &amp; Āsāḷhī</p><p>feminine Name of a month (JuneJuly) and of a Nakkhatta; only in comp<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as Āsaḷha˚ Āsaḷhi˚, viz.; <em>-nakkhatta</em> Ja.i.50; Snp-a.208; <em>-puṇṇamā</em> Ja.i.63; Dhp-a.i.87; Snp-a.199; Vv-a.66; Pv-a.137; <em>-māsa</em> Snp-a.378 (= vassûpanāyikāya purimabhāge A.); Vv-a.307 (= gimhānaṃ pacchimo māso).</p><p>Sk. āṣāḍha</p>

āsāṭikā|aasaa.tikaa|asatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa.tikaa</span></p><p>the egg of a fly or other insect; a nit; vermin.</p>

āsāṭikā|aasaa.tikaa|asatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasaa.tikaa</span></p><p>fly’s egg, a nit MN.i.220 sq.; AN.v.347 sq., AN.v.351, AN.v.359; Ne.59; Ja.iii.176.</p><p>cp. Mārāṭhi āsāḍī</p>

āsīdati|aasiidati|asidati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiidati</span></p><p>approaches; meets with, encounters; approaches with (physical or verbal) violence; strikes against, hits; offends, insults.</p>

āsīdati|aasiidati|asidati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiidati</span></p><ol><li>to come together, lit. to sit by DN.i.248 (variant reading BB ādisitvā for āsīditvā, to be preferred?).</li><li>to come or go near, to approach (w. acc.), to get (to) AN.iii.69 (āsīvisaṃ), AN.iii.373 (na sādhurūpaṃ āsīde, should perhaps be read without the na); Ja.iv.56.</li><li>to knock against, insult, offend attack Ja.v.267 (Pot. āsīde = pharusa-vacanehe kāyakammena vā gbaṭṭento upagaccheyya C.)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sanna">āsanna</a></em> (q.v.). See also āsajja, āsajjana, āsada &amp; Caus. āsādeti.</p><p>cp. Sk. āsīdati, ā + <em>sad</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsanna">āsanna</a></p>

āsīditvā|aasiiditvaa|asiditva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiiditvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsīdati">āsīdati</a></p>

āsīna|aasiina|asina
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiina</span></p><p>encounters; approaches with (physical or verbal)</p>

āsīna|aasiina|asina
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiina</span></p><p>adjective sitting SN.i.195 = Cnd.136; Snp.1105, Snp.1136; Dhp.227, Dhp.386; Ja.i.390; Ja.iii.95; Ja.v.340; Ja.vi.297; Dāvs ii.17.</p><p>pp. of <em>ās</em>, see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81sati">āsati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsati">āsati</a></p>

āsīsanā|aasiisanaa|asisana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiisanaa</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81si%E1%B9%83san%C4%81">āsiṃsanā</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āsiṃsanā">āsiṃsanā</a></p>

āsītika|aasiitika|asitika
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiitika</span></p><p>Possibly Oriya <em>arsi</em>, <em>Entada scandens</em> M.i.81, a huge woody climber with cream-colored flowers and large pods. The central stem of this climber can attain a girth of 1.5 meters and has pronounced flanged segments sometimes with cork-screw-like projections. The Buddha said that when he was practising austerities before his enlightenment he became so thin that his limbs looked like a <em>āsītika</em> stem M.i.245.</p>

āsītika|aasiitika|asitika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiitika</span></p><p>āsītika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. of eighty years; eighty years old.</p><p>āsītika<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine a name of a creeper.</p>

āsītika|aasiitika|asitika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiitika</span></p><p>adjective 80 years old MN.ii.124; Ja.iii.395; Snp-a.172.</p><p>fr. asīta</p><p>Āsītika</p><p>masculine a certain plant MN.i.80 = MN.i.245 (˚pabba).</p><p>etym.? Cp. BSk. āsītakī Lal.319</p>

āsītikapabba|aasiitikapabba|asitikapabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiitikapabba</span></p><p>knot or joint of the <em>āsītika</em> creeper.</p>

āsīvisa|aasiivisa|asivisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiivisa</span></p><p>snake Vin.iv.108; SN.iv.172; AN.ii.110; AN.iii.69; Ja.i.245; Ja.ii.274; Ja.iv.30, Ja.iv.496; Ja.v.82, Ja.v.267; Pp.48; Vism.470 (in comp.); Dhp-a.i.139; Dhp-a.ii.8, Dhp-a.ii.38; Snp-a.334, Snp-a.458, Snp-a.465; Vv-a.308.</p><p>Derivation uncertain. The BSk. āsīviṣa (e.g. Jtm.31#61) is a Sanskritisation of the Pali. To suppose this to come from ahi + visa (snake’s poison) would give a wrong meaning, and leave unexplained the change from ahi to āsi.</p>

āsīyati|aasiiyati|asiyati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasiiyati</span></p><p>to have one’s home, one’s abode or support in (loc.), to live in, thrive by means of, to depend on Mil.75 (kaddame jāyati udake āsīyati i.e. the lotus is born in the mud and is supported or thrives by means of the water).</p><p>etym. doubtful; Trenckner Mil p.422 = ā + <em>śyā</em> to freeze or dry up, but taken by him in meaning to thaw, to warm oneself; Müller, <em>P. Gr.</em> 40 same with meaning “cool oneself”; Morris’ J<em>P. T. S.</em> 1884, 72 as ā + <em>śrā</em> or <em>śrī</em> to become ripe, come to perfection, evidently at fault because of <em>śrā</em> etc. not found in Sk. More likely as a Pass. formation to be referred to ā + <em>śī</em> as in āsaya, i.e. to abide etc.</p>

āsạṃsuka|aasạ.msuka|asamsuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aasạ.msuka</span></p><p>adjective full of expectation, longing, hankering after, Thig.273 (= āsiṃsanaka Thag-a.217 trsl. “cadging”).</p><p>fr. āsaṃsā</p>

ātapa|aatapa|atapa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatapa</span></p><p>sunlight; heat of the sun; heat light.</p>

ātapa|aatapa|atapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatapa</span></p><ol><li>sun-heat Snp.52; Ja.i.336; Dhs.617; Dpvs.i.57; Vv-a.54; Pv-a.58.</li><li>glow, heat (in general Pv.i.7#4; Sdhp.396.</li><li>(fig.) (cp. tapa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) ardour, zeal exertion Pv-a.98 (viriyā-tapa; perhaps better to be read ˚ātāpa q.v.). Cp. ātappa.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://v%C4%81ra%E1%B9%87a">vāraṇa</a></em> “warding off the sun-heat”, i.e. a parasol sun-shade Dāvs i.28; Dāvs v.35.</li></ul><p>ā + tapa</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vāraṇa">vāraṇa</a></p>

ātapati|aatapati|atapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatapati</span></p><p>to burn Ja.iii.447.</p><p>ā + <em>tap</em></p>

ātapatā|aatapataa|atapata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatapataa</span></p><p>glowing or burning state, heat Sdhp.122.</p><p>abstr. of ātapa</p>

ātappa|aatappa|atappa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatappa</span></p><p>heat; zeal; ascetic energy; exertion.</p>

ātappa|aatappa|atappa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatappa</span></p><p>ardour, zeal, exertion DN.i.13; DN.iii.30 sq., DN.iii.104 sq., DN.iii.238 sq.; MN.iii.210; SN.ii.132 SN.ii.196 sq.; AN.i.153; AN.iii.249; AN.iv.460 sq.; AN.v.17 sq.; Snp.1062 (= ussāha ussoḷhi thāma etc. Cnd.122); Ja.iii.447; Mnd.378; Vb.194 (= vāyāma); DN-a.i.104.</p><p>Sk. *ātāpya, fr. ātāpa</p>

ātata|aatata|atata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatata</span></p><p>generic name for drums covered with leather on one side Dpvs.xiv.14; Vv-a.37 (q.v. for enum<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of musical instruments), Vv-a.96.</p><p>fr. ā + tan, pp. tata; lit. stretched, covered over</p>

ātatta|aatatta|atatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatatta</span></p><p>heated, burnt. scorched, dry Ja.v.69 (˚rūpa = sukkha-sarīra C.).</p><p>ā + tatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->, pp. of ā-tapati</p>

ātaṅka|aata"nka|atanka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aata"nka</span></p><p>illness, sickness, disease MN.i.437; SN.iii.1; Snp.966 (˚phassa, cp. Mnd.486) Freq. in cpd. <em><a href="bword://app%C4%81ta%E1%B9%85ka">appātaṅka</a></em> freedom from illness, health (cp. appābādha) DN.i.204; DN.iii.166; AN.iii.65, AN.iii.103; Mil.14<br />■ f. abstr. <em>appātaṅkatā</em> MN.i.124.</p><p>etym. uncertain; Sk. ātanka</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://appātaṅka">appātaṅka</a></p>

ātaṅkaphassa|aata"nkaphassa|atankaphassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aata"nkaphassa</span></p><p>contact with disease; the touch, the sensation of sickness.</p>

ātaṅkin|aata"nkin|atankin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aata"nkin</span></p><p>adjective sick, ill Ja.v.84 (= gilāna C.).</p><p>fr. ātanka</p>

āthabbana|aathabbana|athabbana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aathabbana</span></p><p>or neuter (what belongs to) the Atharva-veda; a spell or a charm from the Atharva-veda</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āthabbana">āthabbana</a>, <a href="bword://athabbaṇa">athabbaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://athabbana">athabbana</a></p>

āthabbaṇa|aathabba.na|athabbana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aathabba.na</span></p><p>A branch of knowledge, dabbling in which is forbidden to monks. Probably the Atharva Veda, which includes sorcery and magic spells. Snp.927</p>

āthabbaṇa|aathabba.na|athabbana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aathabba.na</span></p><p>or neuter (what belongs to) the Atharva-veda; a spell or a charm from the Atharva-veda</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āthabbana">āthabbana</a>, <a href="bword://athabbaṇa">athabbaṇa</a>, <a href="bword://athabbana">athabbana</a></p>

āthabbaṇa|aathabba.na|athabbana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aathabba.na</span></p><p>the Atharva Veda as a code of magic working formulas, witchcraft, sorcery Snp.927 (variant reading ath˚, see interpreted at Mnd.381; expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> as āthabbaṇika-manta-ppayoga at Snp-a.564).</p><p>= athabbaṇa, q.v.</p>

āthabbaṇika|aathabba.nika|athabbanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aathabba.nika</span></p><p>adjective noun one conversant with magic, wonder-worker, medicine-man Mnd.381; Snp-a.564.</p><p>fr. athabbana</p>

ātitheyya|aatitheyya|atitheyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatitheyya</span></p><p>gift of hospitality, a gift to a guest.</p>

ātitheyya|aatitheyya|atitheyya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatitheyya</span></p><p>great theft (?) AN.i.93; AN.iv.63 sq. (variant reading ati˚ which is perhaps to be prcferred).</p><p>fr. ati + theyya</p>

ātta|aatta|atta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatta</span></p><p>taken; assumed; grasped.</p>

āttanuyogi|aattanuyogi|attanuyogi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aattanuyogi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āttanuyogin">āttanuyogin</a></p><p>intent on what one has taken up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atta">atta</a></p>

āttanuyogin|aattanuyogin|attanuyogin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aattanuyogin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āttanuyogin">āttanuyogin</a></p><p>intent on what one has taken up</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atta">atta</a></p>

āttañjaha|aatta~njaha|attanjaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatta~njaha</span></p><p>abandoning what has been grasped</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://atta">atta</a></p>

āttañjahadaṇḍa|aatta~njahada.n.da|attanjahadanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatta~njahada.n.da</span></p><p>āttañjahadaṇḍa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. who has taken a stick in hand, who has embraced violence</p><p>āttañjahadaṇḍa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>mfn. a raised stick; violence</p><p>See also: attañjaha, attañjaha</p>

ātu|aatu|atu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (?)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatu</span></p><p>father (?).</p>

ātu|aatu|atu
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatu</span></p><p>father MN.i.449 (cp. Trenckner’s note on p. MN.i.567: the text no doubt purports to make the woman speak a sort of patois).</p><p>dialectical</p>

ātuma|aatuma|atuma
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatuma</span></p><p>A monk whose verse is in the Theragāthā. Thag.72</p>

ātuma|aatuma|atuma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatuma</span></p><p>the self; oneself</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ātuman">ātuman</a></p>

ātuman|aatuman|atuman
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatuman</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ātuman">ātuman</a></p><p>the self; oneself</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ātuma">ātuma</a></p>

ātuman|aatuman|atuman
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatuman</span></p><p>self. nom. sg. <em>ātumo</em> Pv.iv.5#2 (= sabhāvo Pv-a.259), <em>ātumā</em> Mnd.69 (ātumā vuccati attā), Mnd.296 (id.) &amp; <em>ātumāno</em> Mnd.351; acc. <em>ātumānaṃ</em> Snp.782 (= attānaṃ Snp-a.521), Snp.888, Snp.918; loc. <em>ātume</em> Pv.ii.13#11 (= attani C.).</p><p>Vedic ātman, diaeretic form for the usual contracted attan; only found in poetry. Cp. also the shortened form tuman</p>

ātumā|aatumaa|atuma
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatumaa</span></p><p>A town that lay between Kusinārā and Sāvatthī. Once the Buddha, with a large company of bhikkhus, visited the town. At that time there dwelt in it a monk who had been ordained late in life and had formerly been a barber. He had two sons, handsome, elegant and well versed in the barber’s art. When the monk heard of the Buddha’s coming, he sent his sons from house to house to collect salt and oil and rice and meal. The young men, using all their powers of persuasion, collected a large quantity of each of these things, and when the Buddha arrived in Ātumā and went to stay in the Bhūsāgāra, they made ready rice-gruel and offered it to him. The Buddha, however, would not accept it as the monk, who had had the food collected, had been guilty of an unlawful act in that one monk had begged for others.</p><p>It was on this occasion that it was declared to be a <em>dukkaṭa</em> offence for a monk, who had formerly been a barber, to carry about with him a barber’s equipment. Vin.i.249–250</p><p>In the Mahā Parinibbāna Sutta DN.ii.131–132 the Buddha tells Pukkusa of another occasion on which he was staying in the Bhūsāgāra in Ātumā. There was a thunderstorm and two peasants and four oxen were struck by lightning. A large number of people having gathered at the place, one of them asked the Buddha if he were aware of the accident. But the Buddha had been in a state of concentration and had neither seen nor heard anything of it. Such was the state of calm of his mind.</p><p>27.024878,82.9632572town</p>

ātura|aatura|atura
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatura</span></p><p>sick, ill; suffering, afflicted; wretched.</p>

ātura|aatura|atura
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aatura</span></p><p>adjective ill, sick, diseased; miserable, affected SN.iii.1 (˚kāya); AN.i.250; Snp.331; Vv.83#14 (˚rūpa = abhitunna-kāya Vv-a.328); Ja.i.197 (˚anna “food of the miserable”, i.e. last meal of one going to be killed; C. explains as maraṇabhojana), Ja.i.211 (˚citta); Ja.ii.420 (˚anna, as above); Ja.iii.201, Ja.v.90, Ja.v.433; Ja.vi.248; Mil.139, Mil.168; Dhp-a.i.31 (˚rūpa) Pv-a.160, Pv-a.161; Vv-a.77; Sdhp.507. Used by Commentators as syn. of <em>aṭṭo</em>, e.g. at Ja.iv.293; Snp-a.489. <em>-anātura</em> healthy, well, in good condition SN.iii.1; Dhp.198.</p><p>Sk. ātura, cp. BSk. ātura, e.g. Jtm.31#70</p>

āturīyati|aaturiiyati|aturiyati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaturiiyati</span></p><p>becomes sick; is pained.</p>

ātāpa|aataapa|atapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapa</span></p><p>glow, heat; fig. ardour, keen endeavour, or perhaps better “torturing mortification” Mil.313 (cittassa ātāpo paritāpo); Pv-a.98 (viriya˚) Cp. <em>ātappa &amp; ātāpana</em>.</p><p>ā + tāpa fr. <em>tap</em>; cp. tāpeti</p>

ātāpana|aataapana|atapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapana</span></p><p>tormenting, torture, mortification MN.i.78; AN.i.296 (˚paritāpana); AN.ii.207 (id.); Pp.55 (id.); Vism.3 (id.).</p><p>ā + tāpana</p>

ātāpanaparitāpanānuyoga|aataapanaparitaapanaanuyoga|atapanaparitapananuyoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapanaparitaapanaanuyoga</span></p><p>the practice of self-mortification and torture.</p>

ātāpeti|aataapeti|atapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapeti</span></p><p>heats; consumes by heat; tortures, inflicts pain on.</p>

ātāpeti|aataapeti|atapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapeti</span></p><p>to burn, scorch; fig. to torment, inflict pain, torture MN.i.341 (+ paritāpeti); SN.iv.337; Mil.314, Mil.315.</p><p>ā + tāpeti</p>

ātāpetvā|aataapetvaa|atapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ātāpeti">ātāpeti</a></p>

ātāpi|aataapi|atapi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ātāpin">ātāpin</a></p><p>zealous, exerting oneself; zeal; exertion.</p>

ātāpin|aataapin|atapin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ātāpin">ātāpin</a></p><p>zealous, exerting oneself; zeal; exertion.</p>

ātāpin|aataapin|atapin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapin</span></p><p>adjective ardent, zealous, strenuous, active DN.iii.58, DN.iii.76 sq., DN.iii.141 (+ sampajāna), DN.iii.221, DN.iii.276; MN.i.22 MN.i.56, MN.i.116, MN.i.207, MN.i.349; MN.ii.11; MN.iii.89, MN.iii.128, MN.iii.156; SN.i.113, SN.i.117 sq., SN.i.140, SN.i.165; SN.ii.21, SN.ii.136 sq.; SN.iii.73 sq.; SN.iv.37, SN.iv.48, SN.iv.54 SN.iv.218; SN.v.165, SN.v.187, SN.v.213; AN.ii.13 sq.; AN.iii.38, AN.iii.100 sq.; AN.iv.29, AN.iv.177 sq., AN.iv.266 sq., AN.iv.300, AN.iv.457 sq.; AN.v.343 sq.; Snp.926 Mnd.378; Iti.41, Iti.42; Vb.193 sq.; Mil.34, Mil.366; Vism.3 (= viriyavā); Dhp-a.i.120; Snp-a.157, Snp-a.503<br />■ Freq. in the formula of Arahantship “eko vūpakaṭṭho appamatto ātāpī pahitatto”: see <em><a href="bword://arahant">arahant</a></em> II. B. See also satipaṭṭhāna. Opp. <em>anātāpin</em> SN.ii.195 sq.; AN.ii.13; Iti.27 (+ anottappin).</p><p>fr. ātāpa, cp. BSk. ātāpin Avs.i.233; Avs.ii.194 = Divy.37; Divy.618</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://arahant">arahant</a></p>

ātāpita|aataapita|atapita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapita</span></p><p>heated</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ātāpeti">ātāpeti</a></p>

ātāpiya|aataapiya|atapiya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aataapiya</span></p><p>energetic; zealous; exerting oneself.</p>

āva|aava|ava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava</span></p><p>(misery, misfortune) see <em>avā</em>.</p>

āvaha|aavaha|avaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaha</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) bringing, going, causing Pv.ii.9#24 (sukh˚); Vv.22#11 (id); Dāvs ii.37; Pv-a.86 (upakār˚), Pv-a.116 (anatth˚); Sdhp.15, Sdhp.98, Sdhp.206.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>vah</em></p>

āvahana|aavahana|avahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahana</span></p><p>bringing, bringing near; causing.</p>

āvahana|aavahana|avahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahana</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) bringing, causing Thag.519; Snp.256.</p><p>= āvaha</p>

āvahanaka|aavahanaka|avahanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahanaka</span></p><p>adjective noun one who brings Vv-a.114 (sukhassa).</p><p>= āvahana</p>

āvahati|aavahati|avahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahati</span></p><p>carries towards, carries along; brings; produces.</p>

āvahati|aavahati|avahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahati</span></p><p>to bring, cause, entail, give SN.i.42 = Snp.181, Snp.182 (āvahāti sukhaṃ metri causā); Ja.iii.169; Ja.v.80; Snp.823; Mnd.302; Pv-a.6<br />pass āvuyhati Vv-a.237 (ppr. <em>-amāna</em>).</p><p>ā + vahati</p>

āvahāpeyyum|aavahaapeyyum|avahapeyyum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. opt. 3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavahaapeyyum</span></p><p>may they bring through; may they lead through</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvahati">āvahati</a></p>

āvajati|aavajati|avajati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajati</span></p><ol><li>to go into, to or towards Ja.iii.434; Ja.iv.49, Ja.iv.107.</li><li>to return, come back Ja.v.24, Ja.v.479.</li></ol><p>ā + vajati, <em>vraj</em></p>

āvajjana|aavajjana|avajjana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjana</span></p><p><strong>Āvajjana:</strong> Directing of the mind towards the object, is the first stage in the process of consciousness see: <em>viññāna-kicca</em>. If an object of the 5 physical senses is observed, it is called ‘five-door directing’ <em>= pañca</em> <em>dvārāvajjana,</em> in the case of a mental object, an idea or mental state then it is called ‘mind-door directing’ <em>mano</em> - <em>dvārāvajjana.</em></p>

āvajjana|aavajjana|avajjana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjana</span></p><p>turning to, paying attention, apprehending; adverting the mind<br />■ See discussion of term at <em>Cpd.</em> 85, 227 (the C. derive āvajjana fr. āvaṭṭeti to turn towards, this confusion being due to close resemblance of jj and ṭṭ in writing); also <em>Kvu trsl.</em> 221 n. 4 (on Kv.380 which has āvaṭṭanā), 282 n. 2 (on Kv.491 āvaṭṭanā)<br />■ Pts.ii.5, Pts.ii.120; Ja.ii.243; Vb.320; Mil.102 sq.; Vism.432; DN-a.i.271.</p><p>fr. āvajjati, cp. BSk. āvarjana in diff. meaning</p>

āvajjati|aavajjati|avajjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjati</span></p><ol><li>tilts, tips up (to pour out); tips over.</li><li>turns (one’s mind) to, gives one’s attention to; ponders over, thinks about</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjeti">āvajjeti</a>, <a href="bword://āvajjayati">āvajjayati</a></p>

āvajjati|aavajjati|avajjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjati</span></p><ol><li>to reflect upon, notice, take in advert to, catch (a sound), listen Ja.i.81; Ja.ii.423; Ja.v.3; Mil.106.</li><li>to remove, upset (a vessel), pour out Vin.i.286 (kumbhiṃ); Ja.ii.102 (gloss āsiñcati)</li></ol><p>caus <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vajjeti">āvajjeti</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>not with Senart Mvu.377 = ava + <em>dhyā</em>, but = Sk. āvṛṇakti ā + <em>vṛj</em>, with pres. act. āvajjeti Sk. āvarjayati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjeti">āvajjeti</a></p>

āvajjayati|aavajjayati|avajjayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjayati</span></p><ol><li>tilts, tips up (to pour out); tips over.</li><li>turns (one’s mind) to, gives one’s attention to; ponders over, thinks about</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjati">āvajjati</a>, <a href="bword://āvajjeti">āvajjeti</a></p>

āvajjesi|aavajjesi|avajjesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjeti">āvajjeti</a></p>

āvajjeti|aavajjeti|avajjeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjeti</span></p><ol><li>tilts, tips up (to pour out); tips over.</li><li>turns (one’s mind) to, gives one’s attention to; ponders over, thinks about</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjati">āvajjati</a>, <a href="bword://āvajjayati">āvajjayati</a></p>

āvajjeti|aavajjeti|avajjeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjeti</span></p><ol><li>to turn over, incline, bend MN.iii.96; Ja.iv.56 (so read for āvijjhanto); DN-a.i.10 (kāyaṃ).</li><li>to incline (the mind); observe, reflect, muse, think heed, listen for. According to <em>Cpd.</em> 227 often paraphrased in C. by pariṇāmeti<br />■ Ja.i.69, Ja.i.74, Ja.i.81, Ja.i.89, Ja.i.108, Ja.i.200; Mil.297; Dhp-a.ii.96; Pv-a.181 (= manasikaroti).</li><li>to cause to yield AN.iii.27 (perhaps better āvaṭṭ˚). pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vajjita">āvajjita</a></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><p>Caus. of āvajjati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvajjita">āvajjita</a></p>

āvajjita|aavajjita|avajjita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjita</span></p><p>bent, turned to, inclined; noticed, observed Mil.297; Vism.432 (citta); Sdhp.433.</p><p>pp. of āvajjeti cp. BSk. āvarjita, e.g. Divy.171; Itin 221</p>

āvajjitatta|aavajjitatta|avajjitatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavajjitatta</span></p><p>inclination of mind, observation, paying attention Pts.ii.27 sq.</p><p>abstr. fr. āvajjita</p>

āvalī|aavalii|avali
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavalii</span></p><p>row, range Ja.v.69; DN-a.i.140.</p><p>cp. Sk. āvalī &amp; see valī</p>

āvamati|aavamati|avamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavamati</span></p><p>swallows back; swallows what one has vomited or ejected.</p>

āvapana|aavapana|avapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavapana</span></p><p>sowing, dispersing, offering,depositing, scattering Ja.i.321.</p><p>fr. āvapati</p>

āvapati|aavapati|avapati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavapati</span></p><p>to give away, to offer, to deposit as a pledge Mil.279.</p><p>a + <em>vap</em></p>

āvara|aavara|avara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara</span></p><p>adjective obstructing, keeping off from Ja.v.325 (so to be read in ariya-magg-âvara).</p><p>fr. ā + <em>vṛ</em>.</p>

āvarati|aavarati|avarati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavarati</span></p><p>covers; shuts; obstructs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuṇati">āvuṇati</a>, <a href="bword://āvuṇoti">āvuṇoti</a></p>

āvarati|aavarati|avarati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavarati</span></p><p>to shut out from (abl.), hold back from, refuse, withhold, obstruct MN.i.380 (dvāraṃ) Snp.922 (pot. ˚aye, cp. Mnd.368); DN-a.i.235 (dvāraṃ) Dpvs.i.38<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81va%E1%B9%ADa">āvaṭa</a></em> and <em>āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>vṛ</em>; cp. āvuṇāti</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭa">āvaṭa</a></p>

āvaraṇa|aavara.na|avarana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.na</span></p><p>(&amp; m) covering, enclosing; shutting off; an obstruction, hindrance, prohibition; barricade, wall, dam.</p>

āvaraṇa|aavara.na|avarana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.na</span></p><p>adjective noun shutting off, barring out withstanding; nt. hindrance, obstruction, bar Vin.i.84 (˚ṃ karoti to prohibit, hinder); Vin.ii.262 (id.); DN.i.246 (syn. of pañca nīvaraṇāni); SN.v.93 sq.; AN.iii.63; Ja.i.78 (an˚); Ja.v.412 (nadiṃ ˚ena bandhāpeti to obstruct or dam off the river); Snp.66 (pahāya pañc’ āvaraṇāni cetaso, cp Cnd.379), Snp.1005 (an˚-dassāviṇ); Pts.i.131 sq.; Pts.ii.158 (an˚) Pp.13; Dhs.1059, Dhs.1136; Vb.341, Vb.342; Mil.21 (dur hard to withstand or oppose)<br />■ dant˚ “screen of the teeth”, lip Ja.iv.188; Ja.vi.590.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>vṛ</em>; cp. āvarati; BSk. āvaraṇa in pañc’ āvaraṇāni Divy.378</p>

āvaraṇatā|aavara.nataa|avaranata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.nataa</span></p><p>keeping away from, withholding from AN.iii.436.</p><p>abstr. fr. āvaraṇa</p>

āvaraṇaṃ karoti|aavara.na.m karoti|avaranam karoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.na.m karoti</span></p><p>? obstructs; prohibits</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaraṇa">āvaraṇa</a></p>

āvaraṇīya|aavara.niiya|avaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.niiya</span></p><p>(from āvaraṇa) belonging to or causing obstruction; obstructive.</p>

āvaraṇīya|aavara.niiya|avaraniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavara.niiya</span></p><p>adjective , MN.i.273; <em>an˚</em>; not to be obstructed, impossible to obstruct MN.iii.3; Mil.157.</p><p>grd. fr. āvarati</p>

āvasa|aavasa|avasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasa</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvasat">āvasat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvasanta|aavasanta|avasanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvasat|aavasat|avasat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvasat">āvasat</a></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvasatha|aavasatha|avasatha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasatha</span></p><p>hose, dwelling; lodging; a public rest-house; hostel.</p>

āvasatha|aavasatha|avasatha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasatha</span></p><p>dwelling-place, habitation; abode, house, dwelling Vin.i.226 (˚âgāra restinghouse); Vin.iv.304 (= kavāṭabaddha); SN.i.94, SN.i.229; SN.iv.329; Snp.287, Snp.672; Ja.iv.396; Ja.vi.425; Pp.51; Mil.279.</p><p>Sk. āvasatha, fr. ā + <em>vas</em></p>

āvasathacīvara|aavasathaciivara|avasathacivara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasathaciivara</span></p><p>household robe (allowed to menstruating <em>bhikkhunīs</em>)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasatha">āvasatha</a></p>

āvasathagāra|aavasathagaara|avasathagara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasathagaara</span></p><p>dwelling; rest-house</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasatha">āvasatha</a></p>

āvasathapiṇḍa|aavasathapi.n.da|avasathapinda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasathapi.n.da</span></p><p>meal at a public rest-house</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasatha">āvasatha</a></p>

āvasati|aavasati|avasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasati</span></p><p>lives in, lives on; inhabits; dwells.</p>

āvasati|aavasati|avasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasati</span></p><p>to live at or in, to inhabit, reside, stay MN.ii.72; SN.i.42; Snp.43, Snp.805, Snp.1134; Mnd.123, Mnd.127; Cnd.133; Ja.vi.317<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vuttha">āvuttha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>vas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuttha">āvuttha</a></p>

āvasika|aavasika|avasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasika</span></p><p>(from āvasati) living in or on.</p>

āvasita|aavasita|avasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasita</span></p><p>living in or on</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvasiṃ|aavasi.m|avasim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">1 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasi.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvasiṃsu|aavasi.msu|avasimsu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavasi.msu</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvatta|aavatta|avatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavatta</span></p><p>āvatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine (n.) turning; turning round; turning back; whirlpool, eddy</p><p>āvatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn.</p><ol><li>turned, converted; enticed, seduced.</li><li>having returned; turned back (esp. to a layman’s life)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭa">āvaṭṭa</a>, <a href="bword://āvattati">āvattati</a></p>

āvatta|aavatta|avatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavatta</span></p><p>Āvatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>adjective gone away to, fallen back to, in phrase <em>hīnāy’āvatta</em> (see same phrase under āvattati MN.i.460; SN.ii.50; Ja.i.206.</p><p>pp. of āvattati</p><p>Āvatta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>neuter winding, turn, bent Ja.i.70 (in a river); Ne.81 (variant reading āvaṭṭa?), Ne.105 (˚hārasampāta).</p><p>Sk. āvarta, of ā + <em>vṛt</em>, cp. āvaṭṭa</p>

āvattaka|aavattaka|avattaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattaka</span></p><p>adjective turning, in <em>dakkhiṇ˚</em>; turning to the right, dextrorsal DN.ii.18; cp. dakkhiṇâvatta at DN-a.i.259.</p><p>āvatta + ka</p>

āvattamāna|aavattamaana|avattamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvattati">āvattati</a></p>

āvattana|aavattana|avattana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattana</span></p><p>adjective noun turning; turn, return Ne.113; Mil.251.</p><p>Sk. āvartana</p>

āvattanin|aavattanin|avattanin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattanin</span></p><p>adjective turning round or back Thag.16 (cp. <em>āvaṭṭanin</em>).</p><p>fr. āvattana</p>

āvattati|aavattati|avattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattati</span></p><ol><li>(intrans.) turns, turns to; turns round, rolls; turns back, returns (esp. to a layman’s life); winds, curls.</li><li>is enticed, is seduced</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭati">āvaṭṭati</a></p>

āvattati|aavattati|avattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattati</span></p><p>to turn round, come to, go back, go away to, turn to; only in phrase <em>hīnāya āvattati</em> to turn to “the low”, i.e. to give up orders &amp; return to the world Vin.i.17; MN.i.460; SN.ii.231; SN.iv.191; Snp.p.92 (= osakkati Snp-a.423); Ud.21; Pp.66; Mil.246. pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vatta">āvatta</a></em> (q.v.). Cp. āvaṭṭati.</p><p>ā + vattati, of <em>vṛt</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvatta">āvatta</a></p>

āvatteti|aavatteti|avatteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg. (trans.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavatteti</span></p><p>turns, turns back; diverts, directs; converts; seduces</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvattati">āvattati</a></p>

āvatthika|aavatthika|avatthika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavatthika</span></p><p>adjective befitting, original, inherent (one of the 4 kinds of nomenclature) Vism.210 = Kp-a.107.</p><p>ā + vatthika</p>

āvattin|aavattin|avattin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattin</span></p><p>adjective noun returning, coming back, one who returns, in spec. meaning of one who comes back in transmigration, syn. with āgāmin (an˚), only in neg. <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81vattin">anāvattin</a></em> not returning, a non-returner, with <em>-dhamma</em> not liable to return at DN.i.156; DN.iii.132; SN.v.346, SN.v.357, SN.v.376, SN.v.406; MN.i.91; DN-a.i.313.</p><p>fr. āvatta, cp. āvaṭṭin in diff. meaning</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāvattin">anāvattin</a></p>

āvattitvā|aavattitvaa|avattitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavattitvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvattati">āvattati</a></p>

āvaṭa|aava.ta|avata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.ta</span></p><p>covered, encompassed; obstructed; shut, shut off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuṭa">āvuṭa</a>, <a href="bword://āvuta">āvuta</a>, <a href="bword://āvarati">āvarati</a></p>

āvaṭa|aava.ta|avata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.ta</span></p><p>covered, veiled, shut off against, prohibited DN.i.97, DN.i.246; MN.i.381 (˚dvāra); Ja.vi.267. <em>-<a href="bword://an%C4%81va%E1%B9%ADa">anāvaṭa</a></em> uncovered, unveiled, exposed, open DN.i.137 (˚dvāra); DN.iii.191 (˚dvāratā); SN.i.55; Ja.v.213; Pv.iii.6#4; Mil.283. Cp. <em>āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> &amp; vy˚.</p><p>Sk. āvṛta, pp. of ā + <em>vṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāvaṭa">anāvaṭa</a></p>

āvaṭṭa|aava.t.ta|avatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.ta</span></p><p>āvaṭṭa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>masculine (n.) turning; turning round; turning back; whirlpool, eddy</p><p>āvaṭṭa<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn.</p><ol><li>turned, converted; enticed, seduced.</li><li>having returned; turned back (esp. to a layman’s life)</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvatta">āvatta</a>, <a href="bword://āvaṭṭati">āvaṭṭati</a></p>

āvaṭṭa|aava.t.ta|avatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.ta</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>turning round, winding, twisting MN.i.382; SN.i.32 (dvi-r-ā˚ turning twice) Ja.ii.217; Snp-a.439 (˚gangā).</li><li>turned, brought round, changed, enticed MN.i.381; Dhp-a.ii.153.</li><li>an eddy whirlpool, vortex MN.i.461 = AN.ii.123 (˚bhaya); Mil.122 Mil.196, Mil.377.</li><li>circumference Ja.v.337; Dāvs v.24; Dhp-a.iii.184.</li></ol><p>Sk. āvarta, ā + <em>vṛt</em></p>

āvaṭṭaggāha|aava.t.taggaaha|avattaggaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.taggaaha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭaggāha">āvaṭṭaggāha</a></p><p>(or mfn.) seizure, possession by a whirlpool; (or: seized by a whirlpool)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭa">āvaṭṭa</a></p>

āvaṭṭagāha|aava.t.tagaaha|avattagaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tagaaha</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭaggāha">āvaṭṭaggāha</a></p><p>(or mfn.) seizure, possession by a whirlpool; (or: seized by a whirlpool)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭa">āvaṭṭa</a></p>

āvaṭṭamāna|aava.t.tamaana|avattamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭati">āvaṭṭati</a></p>

āvaṭṭana|aava.t.tana|avattana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tana</span></p><p>turning, twisting; enticement, snare, temptation Ja.iii.494; Dhp-a.ii.153.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>vṛt</em>, cp. āvaṭṭa 2 and āvaṭṭanin</p>

āvaṭṭanin|aava.t.tanin|avattanin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tanin</span></p><p>adjective turning (away or towards), changing, tempting, enticing MN.i.375, MN.i.381; AN.ii.190; Ja.ii.330 = Ja.iv.471; DN-a.i.250<br />■ Cp. etymologically the same, but semantically diff. <em>āvattanin</em>.</p><p>fr. āvaṭṭana</p>

āvaṭṭanā|aava.t.tanaa|avattana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tanaa</span></p><p>turning to (of the mind), adverting apprehending Kv.380, Kv.491.</p><p>most likely for āvajjana. q.v. &amp; see also Kvu trsl. 221, 282</p>

āvaṭṭanī|aava.t.tanii|avattani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">(m)f(n.) &amp; feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tanii</span></p><p>turning, seducing; a seductress; a seductive sorcery.</p>

āvaṭṭati|aava.t.tati|avattati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tati</span></p><ol><li>(intrans.) turns, turns to; turns round, rolls; turns back, returns (esp. to a layman’s life); winds, curls.</li><li>is enticed, is seduced</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvattati">āvattati</a></p>

āvaṭṭati|aava.t.tati|avattati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tati</span></p><p>in phrase ā. vivaṭṭati to turn forward &amp; backward Vism.504.</p><p>= āvattati</p>

āvaṭṭeti|aava.t.teti|avatteti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg. (trans.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.teti</span></p><p>turns, turns back; diverts, directs; converts; seduces</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭati">āvaṭṭati</a></p>

āvaṭṭeti|aava.t.teti|avatteti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.teti</span></p><p>to turn round, entice, change convert, bring or win over MN.i.375, MN.i.381, MN.i.383, MN.i.505; AN.iii.27; DN-a.i.272.</p><p>ā + vatteti, Caus. of <em>vṛt</em>, cp. BSk. āvartayati to employ spells Divy.438</p>

āvaṭṭin|aava.t.tin|avattin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.tin</span></p><p>adjective noun only at MN.i.91 in neg. <em>an˚</em>; not enticed by (loc.), i.e. kāmesu. Cp. <em>āvattin</em>.</p><p>fr. āvaṭṭa instead of āvaṭṭana</p>

āvaṭṭitvā|aava.t.titvaa|avattitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aava.t.titvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭṭati">āvaṭṭati</a></p>

āvedha|aavedha|avedha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavedha</span></p><p>piercing, hole, wound Ja.ii.276 (variant reading aveddha; C. = viddha-ṭṭhāne vaṇa).</p><p>cp. Sk. āviddha, ā + pp. of <em>vyadh</em></p>

āvedhika|aavedhika|avedhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavedhika</span></p><p>adj. f. scil. pannā piercing, penetrating; or ravelling, turning rolling up or round (cp. āvijjhati which is derived from ā + <em>vyadh</em>, but takes its meaning from āveṭheti), discrimination thinking over Ja.ii.9 (+ nibbedhikā, variant reading for both ṭh).</p><p>ā + vedhaka of āvedha, <em>vyadh</em>, but confused with āveṭh˚ of ā + <em>veṣṭ</em>; cp. āveṭhana &amp; nibbedhaka</p>

āvelita|aavelita|avelita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavelita</span></p><p>(ḷ?) turned round wound, curved Ja.vi.354 (˚singika with curved horns variant reading āvellita).</p><p>pp. of ā + <em>vell</em>, cp. āveḷa &amp; BSk. āviddha curved, crooked Avs.i.87, Lal.207</p>

āvelā|aavelaa|avela
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavelaa</span></p><p>garland worn on the head or ears, a chaplet.</p>

āveni|aaveni|aveni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaveni</span></p><p>or mfn. separately, apart; or not united, separate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇi">āveṇi</a>, <a href="bword://āveṇiṃ">āveṇiṃ</a></p>

āvenika|aavenika|avenika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavenika</span></p><p>not united, not mingled together (as is a plait of hair or the confluence of rivers); separate; specific, particular</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇika">āveṇika</a>, <a href="bword://āveṇiya">āveṇiya</a>, <a href="bword://āveniya">āveniya</a></p>

āveniya|aaveniya|aveniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaveniya</span></p><p>not united, not mingled together (as is a plait of hair or the confluence of rivers); separate; specific, particular</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇika">āveṇika</a>, <a href="bword://āveṇiya">āveṇiya</a>, <a href="bword://āvenika">āvenika</a></p>

āvesana|aavesana|avesana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavesana</span></p><ol><li>a workshop.</li><li>making enter, putting in.</li></ol>

āvesana|aavesana|avesana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavesana</span></p><p>entrance; workshop; living-place, house Vin.ii.117 (˚vitthaka, meaning?); MN.ii.53; Pv.ii.9#15.</p><p>fr. āvisati</p>

āvesanavitthaka|aavesanavitthaka|avesanavitthaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavesanavitthaka</span></p><p>small bowl for keeping sewing utensils</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvesana">āvesana</a></p>

āveḷa|aave.la|avela
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.la</span></p><p>adjective &amp; ˚ā feminine</p><ol><li>turning round swinging round; diffusion, radiation; protuberance, with reference to the rays of the Buddha at Ja.i.12, Ja.i.95, Ja.i.501.</li><li>(f.) a garland or other ornament slung round &amp; worn over the head Vv.36#2 (kañcan˚; = āveḷa-pilandhana Vv-a.167). See <em>āveḷin</em>.</li></ol><p>not with Müller; <em>P.Gr.</em> 10, 30, 37 = Sk. āpīḍa, but fr. ā + <em>veṣṭh</em> to wind or turn round which in P. is represented by āveṭheti as well as āvijjhati ḷ then standing for either ḍh (ṭh) or dh (āvedha, q.v.) There may have been an analogy influence through <em>vell</em> to move to and fro, cp. āveḷita. Müller refers to āveḷā rightly the late dial. (Prk.) āmela</p>

āveḷin|aave.lin|avelin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.lin</span></p><p>adjective wearing garlands or other headornaments, usually in f. ˚inī Ja.v.409 (= kaṇṇālankārehi yuttā C.); Vv.30#2 (voc. āvelinī, but at id. p. Vv.48#2 āveline) Vv.32#3 ; Vv-a.125 (on Vv.30#2 explains as ratana-maya-pupph’ āveḷavatī).</p><p>fr. āveḷā</p>

āveṇi|aave.ni|aveni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.ni</span></p><p>or mfn. separately, apart; or not united, separate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇiṃ">āveṇiṃ</a>, <a href="bword://āveni">āveni</a></p>

āveṇi|aave.ni|aveni
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.ni</span></p><p>adjective (-˚) special peculiar, separate Vin.ii.204 (˚uposatha etc.); Ja.i.490 (˚sangha-kammāni).</p><p>according to Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 75 fr. ā + vinā “Sine quā non”, but very doubtful</p>

āveṇibhāva|aave.nibhaava|avenibhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.nibhaava</span></p><p>separateness, distinction; schism</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇi">āveṇi</a></p>

āveṇika|aave.nika|avenika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.nika</span></p><p>not united, not mingled together (as is a plait of hair or the confluence of rivers); separate; specific, particular</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇiya">āveṇiya</a>, <a href="bword://āvenika">āvenika</a>, <a href="bword://āveniya">āveniya</a></p>

āveṇika|aave.nika|avenika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.nika</span></p><p>adjective special, extraordinary, exceptional SN.iv.239; AN.v.74 sq.; Vism.268; Vv-a.112 (˚bhāva peculiarity, specialty), Kp-a.23, Kp-a.35.</p><p>fr. āveṇi; cp. BSk. āveṇika Avs.i.14, Avs.i.108; Divy.2, Divy.182, Divy.268, Divy.302</p>

āveṇiya|aave.niya|aveniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.niya</span></p><p>not united, not mingled together (as is a plait of hair or the confluence of rivers); separate; specific, particular</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇika">āveṇika</a>, <a href="bword://āvenika">āvenika</a>, <a href="bword://āveniya">āveniya</a></p>

āveṇiya|aave.niya|aveniya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.niya</span></p><p>adjective = <em><a href="bword://%C4%81ve%E1%B9%87ika">āveṇika</a></em> Vin.i.71; Ja.iv.358; Ja.vi.128.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇika">āveṇika</a></p>

āveṇiṃ|aave.ni.m|avenim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.ni.m</span></p><p>or mfn. separately, apart; or not united, separate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āveṇi">āveṇi</a>, <a href="bword://āveni">āveni</a></p>

āveṭhana|aave.thana|avethana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.thana</span></p><p>rolling up, winding up or round, fig. explanation Mil.28 (+ nibbeṭhana, lit rolling up and rolling down, ravelling &amp; unravelling), Mil.231 (˚viniveṭhana).</p><p>ā + veṭhana, <em>veṣṭ</em>.</p>

āveṭhita|aave.thita|avethita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aave.thita</span></p><p>turned round, slung round or over Ja.iv.383 sq. (variant reading āvedhita &amp; āveḷita, C. explains by parivattita).</p><p>pp. of āveṭheti, ā + <em>veṣṭ</em>; cp. āvedhikā</p>

āvi-akāsi|aavi-akaasi|avi-akasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi-akaasi</span></p><p>āvi-akāsi</p><p>aor. 3 sg.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikaroti">āvikaroti</a></p>

āvi|aavi|avi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi</span></p><p>before the eyes; openly, manifestly; in public</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviṃ">āviṃ</a>, <a href="bword://āvī">āvī</a></p>

āvi|aavi|avi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi</span></p><p>adverb clear, manifest, evident; openly, before one’s eyes, in full view. Only in phrase <em>āvi vā raho</em> openly or secret AN.v.350, AN.v.353; Pv.ii.7#16 = Dhp-a.iv.21 (āvī variant reading), expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Pv-a.103 by pakāsanaṃ paresaṃ pākaṭavasana Otherwise in foll. compounds (with <em>kar &amp; bhū</em>) <em>-kamma</em> making clear, evidence, explanation Vin.ii.88 Vin.iii.24; Pp.19, Pp.23; <em>-karoti</em> to make clear, show, explain DN.iii.121; Snp.84, Snp.85, Snp.349; Ja.v.457; Pp.57; Vv-a.79 Vv-a.150; <em>-bhavati</em> (˚bhoti) to become visible or evident, to be explained, to get clear Ja.i.136; Vism.287 (fnt. āvibhavissati); Dhp-a.ii.51, Dhp-a.ii.82; <em><a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> appearance, manifestation DN.i.78; AN.iii.17; Ja.ii.50, Ja.ii.111; Vism.390 sq. (revelation opp. tirobhāva). Cp. pātur. Avijjhati (avinjati, avinchati)</p><p>Sk. āviḥ, to Gr. ἀίω to hear, Lat. audio (fr. *auizdiō) to hear</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a></p>

āvibhāva|aavibhaava|avibhava
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavibhaava</span></p><p>the becoming visible; being made clear, evident; manifestation</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvi">āvi</a></p>

āviddha|aaviddha|aviddha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaviddha</span></p><p>whirling or spinning round, revolving; swung round, set into whirling motion Ja.iv.6 (cakkaṃ = kumbhakāra-cakkam iva bhamati C.); Ja.v.291. What does an-āviddha at Pv-a.135 mean?</p><p>pp. of āvijjhati 2, cp. BSk. āviddha in meaning curved, crooked Av. SN.i.87 Lal.207</p>

āvijjhana|aavijjhana|avijjhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavijjhana</span></p><p>(so for āviñchana &amp; āviñjana) adjective noun</p><ol><li>(āvijjhati 2) swinging round, hanging loose, spinning in; <em>āvijjhana-rajju</em> a loose, rope, esp. in mythology the swinging or whirling rope by which Sakka holds the world’s wheel or axis, in the latter sense at Dhp-a.ii.143 (T. āviñch˚ (variant reading āvijj˚)) = Dhp-a.iii.97, Dhp-a.iii.98 (where āviñjanaṭṭhāna for ˚rajju). Otherwise a rope used in connection with the opening &amp; shutting of a door (pulling rope? Vin.ii.120, Vin.ii.148; Ja.v.298, Ja.v.299 (T. āviñj˚, variant reading āvicch˚ āvij˚).</li><li>(cp. āvijjhati 3) going to, approach, contact with Dhs-a.312 (˚rasa, T. āviñj˚, variant reading āviñch˚; or is it “encompassing”? = āvijjhati 1?); Vism.444 (āviñjanarasa).</li><li>(cp. āvijjhati 5) pulling, drawing along Vin.iii.121 (= ākaḍḍhanā nāma).</li></ol><p>fr. āvijjhati, lit. piercing through, i.e. revolving axis</p>

āvijjhanaka|aavijjhanaka|avijjhanaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavijjhanaka</span></p><p>whirling round, that which spins round, the whirling-round wheel (or pole) of the world (cp. the potter’s wheel), the worldaxis Dhp-a.ii.146 (T. āviñch˚).</p><p>fr. āvijjhati in meaning 2</p>

āvijjhati|aavijjhati|avijjhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavijjhati</span></p><p>swings, whirls round; makes revolve; stirs.</p>

āvijjhati|aavijjhati|avijjhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavijjhati</span></p><p>Āvijjhati (āviñjati, āviñchati)</p><ol><li>to encircle, encompass, comprise, go round, usually in ger. <em>āvijjhitvā</em> (w. acc.) used as prep round about, near Ja.i.153 (khettaṃ), Ja.i.170 (pokkharaṇiṃ) DN-a.i.245 (nagaraṃ bahi avijjhitvā round the outer circle of the town). Ordinarily = go round (acc.) at Ja.iv.59 (chārika-puñjaṃ).</li><li>[as in lit. Sk.] to swing round, brandish, twirl, whirl round Vin.iii.127 (daṇḍaṃ āviñji) MN.iii.141 (matthena āviñjati to churn); Ja.i.313; Ja.v.291 (cakkaṃ, of a potter’s wheel); Snp-a.481 (T. āviñj˚, variant reading āvijjh˚; see āracaya˚); Dhp-a.ii.277 (āviñchamāna T.; variant reading āsiñciy˚, āvajiy˚, āgañch˚).</li><li>to resort to, go to, approach, incline to SN.iv.199 (T. āviñch˚; variant reading avicch˚ āviñj˚); Ne.13.</li><li>to arrange, set in order Ja.ii.406.</li><li>to pull (?) AN.iv.86 (kaṇṇasotani āvijjeyyāsi, variant reading āvijj˚ āviñj˚, āvicc˚, āviñch˚; cp. Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 āviñjati “to pull”)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81viddha">āviddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + vijjhati of <em>vyadh</em> to pierce; thus recognised by Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 72 against Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 (to <em>piñj</em>) &amp; Hardy Nett; <em>Ind.</em> = vicchāy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviddha">āviddha</a></p>

āvikaroti|aavikaroti|avikaroti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikaroti</span></p><p>makes manifest; reveals, discloses; displays.</p>

āvikata|aavikata|avikata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikata</span></p><p>revealed, disclosed; made manifest</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikaroti">āvikaroti</a></p>

āvikatta|aavikatta|avikatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikatta</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvikattar">āvikattar</a></p><p>one who makes manifest, who reveals, discloses</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvi">āvi</a></p>

āvikattar|aavikattar|avikattar
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikattar</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvikattar">āvikattar</a></p><p>one who makes manifest, who reveals, discloses</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvi">āvi</a></p>

āvikatvā|aavikatvaa|avikatva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikatvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikaroti">āvikaroti</a></p>

āvikātabba|aavikaatabba|avikatabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikaatabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikaroti">āvikaroti</a></p>

āvikātum|aavikaatum|avikatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavikaatum</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvikaroti">āvikaroti</a></p>

āvila|aavila|avila
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavila</span></p><p>turbid, not clear; disturbed; polluted.</p>

āvila|aavila|avila
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavila</span></p><p>adjective stirred up, agitated, disturbed, stained soiled, dirty AN.i.9; AN.iii.233; Ja.v.16, Ja.v.90 (ābila); Mnd.488 (+ luḷita), Mnd.489; Thag-a.251; DN-a.i.226. More frequent as <em><a href="bword://an%C4%81vila">anāvila</a></em> undisturbed, clean, pure, serene DN.i.76; SN.iii.83; SN.iv.118; AN.i.9; AN.iii.236; Snp.160; Dhp.82, Dhp.413; Ja.iii.157; Mil.35; Vv-a.29, Vv-a.30; Thag-a.251.</p><p>is it a haplological contraction from ā + vi + <em>lul</em> to roll about?</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāvila">anāvila</a></p>

āvilati|aavilati|avilati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavilati</span></p><p>to whirl round, to be agitated, to be in motion Mil.259 (+ luḷati).</p><p>fr. āvila or is it a direct contraction of ā + vi + lulati?</p>

āvilatta|aavilatta|avilatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavilatta</span></p><p>confusion, disturbance, agitation Snp.967; Mnd.488.</p><p>abstr. fr. āvila</p>

āvisati|aavisati|avisati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavisati</span></p><p>approaches; enters; takes possession of.</p>

āvisati|aavisati|avisati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavisati</span></p><p>to approach, to enter Vin.iv.334; Snp.936 (aor. āvisi); Ja.iv.410, Ja.iv.496; Vism.42.</p><p>ā + <em>vīś</em></p>

āvisi|aavisi|avisi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavisi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvisati">āvisati</a></p>

āviñchana|aavi~nchana|avinchana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~nchana</span></p><p>pulling; drawing towards; attracting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñjana">āviñjana</a></p>

āviñchati|aavi~nchati|avinchati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~nchati</span></p><p>Āvijjhati (āviñjati, āviñchati)</p><ol><li>to encircle, encompass, comprise, go round, usually in ger. <em>āvijjhitvā</em> (w. acc.) used as prep round about, near Ja.i.153 (khettaṃ), Ja.i.170 (pokkharaṇiṃ) DN-a.i.245 (nagaraṃ bahi avijjhitvā round the outer circle of the town). Ordinarily = go round (acc.) at Ja.iv.59 (chārika-puñjaṃ).</li><li>[as in lit. Sk.] to swing round, brandish, twirl, whirl round Vin.iii.127 (daṇḍaṃ āviñji) MN.iii.141 (matthena āviñjati to churn); Ja.i.313; Ja.v.291 (cakkaṃ, of a potter’s wheel); Snp-a.481 (T. āviñj˚, variant reading āvijjh˚; see āracaya˚); Dhp-a.ii.277 (āviñchamāna T.; variant reading āsiñciy˚, āvajiy˚, āgañch˚).</li><li>to resort to, go to, approach, incline to SN.iv.199 (T. āviñch˚; variant reading avicch˚ āviñj˚); Ne.13.</li><li>to arrange, set in order Ja.ii.406.</li><li>to pull (?) AN.iv.86 (kaṇṇasotani āvijjeyyāsi, variant reading āvijj˚ āviñj˚, āvicc˚, āviñch˚; cp. Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 āviñjati “to pull”)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81viddha">āviddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + vijjhati of <em>vyadh</em> to pierce; thus recognised by Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 72 against Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 (to <em>piñj</em>) &amp; Hardy Nett; <em>Ind.</em> = vicchāy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviddha">āviddha</a></p>

āviñchi|aavi~nchi|avinchi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~nchi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñjati">āviñjati</a></p>

āviñjana|aavi~njana|avinjana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~njana</span></p><p>pulling; drawing towards; attracting</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñchana">āviñchana</a></p>

āviñjanacchidda|aavi~njanacchidda|avinjanacchidda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~njanacchidda</span></p><p>the pulling-hole (for pulling a door closed with one’s finger or a rope)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñjana">āviñjana</a></p>

āviñjanarajju|aavi~njanarajju|avinjanarajju
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~njanarajju</span></p><p>the pullingrope, the rope for pulling the door or door-bolt closed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñjana">āviñjana</a></p>

āviñjati|aavi~njati|avinjati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~njati</span></p><p>pulls; draws to oneself; attracts.</p>

āviñjati|aavi~njati|avinjati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~njati</span></p><p>Āvijjhati (āviñjati, āviñchati)</p><ol><li>to encircle, encompass, comprise, go round, usually in ger. <em>āvijjhitvā</em> (w. acc.) used as prep round about, near Ja.i.153 (khettaṃ), Ja.i.170 (pokkharaṇiṃ) DN-a.i.245 (nagaraṃ bahi avijjhitvā round the outer circle of the town). Ordinarily = go round (acc.) at Ja.iv.59 (chārika-puñjaṃ).</li><li>[as in lit. Sk.] to swing round, brandish, twirl, whirl round Vin.iii.127 (daṇḍaṃ āviñji) MN.iii.141 (matthena āviñjati to churn); Ja.i.313; Ja.v.291 (cakkaṃ, of a potter’s wheel); Snp-a.481 (T. āviñj˚, variant reading āvijjh˚; see āracaya˚); Dhp-a.ii.277 (āviñchamāna T.; variant reading āsiñciy˚, āvajiy˚, āgañch˚).</li><li>to resort to, go to, approach, incline to SN.iv.199 (T. āviñch˚; variant reading avicch˚ āviñj˚); Ne.13.</li><li>to arrange, set in order Ja.ii.406.</li><li>to pull (?) AN.iv.86 (kaṇṇasotani āvijjeyyāsi, variant reading āvijj˚ āviñj˚, āvicc˚, āviñch˚; cp. Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 āviñjati “to pull”)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81viddha">āviddha</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + vijjhati of <em>vyadh</em> to pierce; thus recognised by Morris J.P.T.S. 1884, 72 against Trenckner, <em>Notes</em> 59 (to <em>piñj</em>) &amp; Hardy Nett; <em>Ind.</em> = vicchāy</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviddha">āviddha</a></p>

āviñji|aavi~nji|avinji
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi~nji</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āviñjati">āviñjati</a></p>

āviṃ|aavi.m|avim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavi.m</span></p><p>before the eyes; openly, manifestly; in public</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvi">āvi</a>, <a href="bword://āvī">āvī</a></p>

āvudha|aavudha|avudha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavudha</span></p><p>weapon; weaponry.</p>

āvudha|aavudha|avudha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavudha</span></p><p>an instrument to fight with, a weapon, stick etc. DN.iii.219; MN.ii.100; AN.iv.107, AN.iv.110; Snp.1008; Ja.i.150; Ja.ii.110; Ja.iii.467; Ja.iv.160, Ja.iv.283, Ja.iv.437; Nd ii. on Snp.72; Mil.8, Mil.339; Dhp-a.ii.2; Dhp-a.iv.207; Snp-a.225, Snp-a.466 (˚jīvika = issattha). See also <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yudha">āyudha</a></em>.</p><p>Vedic āyudha, fr. ā + <em>yudh</em> to fight</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyudha">āyudha</a></p>

āvudhalakkhaṇa|aavudhalakkha.na|avudhalakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavudhalakkha.na</span></p><p>signs of omen on weapons</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvudha">āvudha</a></p>

āvunati|aavunati|avunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavunati</span></p><p>strings; threads; inserts; impales</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuṇati">āvuṇati</a></p>

āvusavāda|aavusavaada|avusavada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavusavaada</span></p><p>the addressing as āvuso, the title āvuso</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvusovāda">āvusovāda</a>, <a href="bword://āvuso">āvuso</a></p>

āvuso|aavuso|avuso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuso</span></p><p>used</p><ol><li>by and to non-Buddhists.</li><li>by <em>bhikkhus</em> (and <em>bhikkhunīs</em>)<ol><li>to lay-followers.</li><li>to each other (but not used by or to the Buddha, and later used only by a senior <em>bhikkhu</em> to a junior.</li></ol></li></ol>

āvuso|aavuso|avuso
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuso</span></p><p>(voc. pl. m.) friend, a form of polite address “friend, brother Sir”, usually in conversation between bhikkhus. The grammatical construction is with the pl. of the verb, like bhavaṃ and bhavanto<br />■ Vin.ii.302; DN.i.151, DN.i.157; DN.ii.8; Snp-a.227; Dhp-a.i.9; Dhp-a.ii.93; Pv-a.12, Pv-a.13, Pv-a.38, Pv-a.208.</p><p>a contracted form of āyusmanto pl. of āyusman, of which the regular Pāli form is āyasmant with v for y as frequently in Pāli, e.g. āvudha for āyudha</p>

āvusovāda|aavusovaada|avusovada
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavusovaada</span></p><p>the addressing as āvuso, the title āvuso</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvusavāda">āvusavāda</a>, <a href="bword://āvuso">āvuso</a></p>

āvuta|aavuta|avuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuta</span></p><p>āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. covered, encompassed; obstructed; shut, shut off</p><p>āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. threaded through; inserted</p><p>See also: āvutā, <a href="bword://āvuṭa">āvuṭa</a>, <a href="bword://āvarati">āvarati</a>, <a href="bword://āvuṇati">āvuṇati</a></p>

āvuta|aavuta|avuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuta</span></p><ol><li>strung upon, tied on, fixed on to DN.i.76 (suttaṃ); DN.ii.13 (id.) AN.i.286 (tantāvutaṃ web); Ja.iii.52 (valliyā); Ja.vi.346 (suttakena); DN-a.i.94 (˚sutta).</li><li>impaled, stuck on (sūle on the pale) Ja.i.430; Ja.iii.35; Ja.v.497; Ja.vi.105; Pv-a.217, Pv-a.220.</li></ol><p>pp. of āvuṇāti in meaning of Sk. āvayati, the corresponding Sk. form being ā + uta = ota</p><p>Āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = Āvaṭa</p><p>(see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vu%E1%B9%87%C4%81ti">āvuṇāti</a></em> &amp; āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->) covered, obstructed, hindered Iti.8 (mohena); also in phrase <em>āvuta nivuta ophuta</em> etc. Mnd.24 (ṭ) = Cnd.365 = DN-a.i.59.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuṇāti">āvuṇāti</a></p>

āvuttha|aavuttha|avuttha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuttha</span></p><p>lived in, inhabited</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvasati">āvasati</a></p>

āvuttha|aavuttha|avuttha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuttha</span></p><p>inhabited DN.ii.50 (an˚); SN.i.33.</p><p>pp. of āvasati</p>

āvuyhamāna|aavuyhamaana|avuyhamana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavuyhamaana</span></p><p>ppr. of āvuyhati (Pass. of āvahati), being conveyed or brought Vv-a.237 (reading uncertain).</p>

āvuṇati|aavu.nati|avunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavu.nati</span></p><p>strings; threads; inserts; impales</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvunati">āvunati</a></p>

āvuṇoti|aavu.noti|avunoti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavu.noti</span></p><p>covers; shuts; obstructs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuṇati">āvuṇati</a>, <a href="bword://āvarati">āvarati</a></p>

āvuṇāti|aavu.naati|avunati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavu.naati</span></p><p>covers; shuts; obstructs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvarati">āvarati</a>, <a href="bword://āvuṇoti">āvuṇoti</a></p>

āvuṇāti|aavu.naati|avunati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavu.naati</span></p><p>to weave, thus a confusion of the two roots, the latter being merged into the former to string upon, to fix on to (c. loc.), to impale Ja.i.430 Ja.iii.35; Ja.v.145; Ja.vi.105<br />caus 2 <em>āvuṇāpeti</em> Ja.iii.218 (sūle)<br />pp <em>āvuta<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.), whereas the other pp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81va%E1%B9%ADa">āvaṭa</a></em> is the true derivative of ā + <em>vṛ</em>.</p><p>in form = *avṛṇoti, ā + <em>vṛ</em>; cp. āvarati, but in meaning = *āvayati, ā + <em><a href="bword://v%C4%81">vā</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvaṭa">āvaṭa</a>, <a href="bword://vā">vā</a></p>

āvuṭa|aavu.ta|avuta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavu.ta</span></p><p>covered, encompassed; obstructed; shut, shut off</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvuta">āvuta</a>, āvata, <a href="bword://āvarati">āvarati</a></p>

āvā|aavaa|ava
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaa</span></p><p>*Āpā &amp; *Āvā</p><p>(f.) misery, misfortune Ja.ii.317 (loc. pl. āpāsu, variant reading avāsu, C. āpadāsu); Ja.iii.12 (BB āvāsu); Ja.v.82 (avāgata gone into misery, variant reading apagata, C apagata parihīna), Ja.v.445 (loc. āvāsu, variant reading avāsu, C. āpadāsu), Ja.v.448 (āvāsu kiccesu; variant reading apassu, read āpāsu). <em>Note.</em> Since *āpā only occurs in loc. pl., the form āpāsu is to be regarded as a direct contraction of Sk. <em>āpatsu</em>.</p><p>for āpadā, q.v.</p>

āvāha|aavaaha|avaha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaaha</span></p><p>bringing home (a bride); marriage (of a son).</p>

āvāha|aavaaha|avaha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaaha</span></p><p>taking in marriage, lit. carrying away to oneself, marriage DN.i.99; Ja.vi.363; Snp-a.273, Snp-a.448; Dhp-a.iv.7 Often in cpd. <em>ā˚ vivāha(ka)</em> lit. leading to (one’s home &amp; leading away (from the bride’s home), wedding feast DN.iii.183 (˚ka); Ja.i.452; Vv-a.109, Vv-a.157. (variant reading ˚ka).</p><p>ā + <em>vah</em></p>

āvāhana|aavaahana|avahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaahana</span></p><p>arranging a marriage.</p>

āvāhana|aavaahana|avahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaahana</span></p><ol><li>= āvāha, i.e. marriage, taking a wife DN.i.11 (= āvāha-karaṇa DN-a.i.96).</li><li>“getting up, bringing together”, i.e. a mass, a group or formation, in senā˚ a contingent of an army Ja.iv.91.</li></ol><p>ā + vshana, of <em>vah</em></p>

āvāhavivāhaka|aavaahavivaahaka|avahavivahaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (fn).</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaahavivaahaka</span></p><p>one who has a son or daughter to give in marriage</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāha">āvāha</a></p>

āvāpa|aavaapa|avapa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaapa</span></p><p>potter’s furnace Dhp-a.i.177 (read for āvāsa?), Dhp-a.i.178.</p><p>if correct, fr. ā + <em>vā<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> to blow with caus. p<br />■ Cp. J<em>R A S.</em> 1898, 750 sp.</p>

āvāpaka|aavaapaka|avapaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaapaka</span></p><p>vessel, a container.</p>

āvāra|aavaara|avara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaara</span></p><p>warding off, protection, guard Ja.vi.432 (yanta-yutta˚, does it mean “cover, shield”?). For cpd. khandh’āvāra see <em><a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. āvāra, fr. ā + <em>vṛ</em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://khandha">khandha</a></p>

āvāretabba|aavaaretabba|avaretabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaaretabba</span></p><p>to obstruct; to shut off.</p>

āvāreti|aavaareti|avareti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaareti</span></p><p>to ward off, hold back, bar, SN.iv.298; Ne.99.</p><p>Sk. āvārayati, ā + Caus. of <em>vṛ</em>.</p>

āvāsa|aavaasa|avasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasa</span></p><p>living, sojourn; dwelling place, inhabited place; residence, esp. a residence or dwelling for <em>bhikkhus</em>.</p>

āvāsa|aavaasa|avasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasa</span></p><p>sojourn, stay, dwelling, living; dwelling-place, residence Vin.i.92; DN.iii.234; SN.iv.91; AN.ii.68, AN.ii.168; AN.iii.46, AN.iii.262; Snp.406; Dhp.73 (cp. Dhp-a.ii.77); Mnd.128; Ja.vi.105; Dhs.1122; Pp.15, Pp.19, Pp.57 Kp-a.40; Dhp-a.i.177 (āvāsaṃ ālimpeti: read āvāpaṃ); Pv-a.13, Pv-a.14, Pv-a.36; Vv-a.113; Sdhp.247. <em>-<a href="bword://an%C4%81v%C4%81sa">anāvāsa</a></em> (n. &amp; adj. uninhabited, without a home; an uninhabited place AN.iv.345; Ja.ii.77; Pv.ii.3#33; Pv-a.80 (= anāgāra); Vv-a.46.</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kappa">kappa</a></em> the practice of (holding Uposatha in different residence (within the same boundary) Vin.ii.294, Vin.ii.300, Vin.ii.306 Dpvs.iv.47, cp. Dpvs.v.18.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://palibodha">palibodha</a></em> the obstruction of having a home (in set of 10 Palibodhas) Kp-a.39; cp Vism.90 sq.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sapp%C4%81yat%C4%81">sappāyatā</a></em> suitability of residence Vism.127.</li></ul><p>Sk. āvāsa; ā + <em>vas</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anāvāsa">anāvāsa</a>, <a href="bword://kappa">kappa</a>, <a href="bword://palibodha">palibodha</a>, <a href="bword://sappāyatā">sappāyatā</a></p>

āvāsakappa|aavaasakappa|avasakappa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasakappa</span></p><p>residence practice (the practice of several residences holding separate uposathas within the same boundary)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsamacchari|aavaasamacchari|avasamacchari
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasamacchari</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvāsamaccharin">āvāsamaccharin</a></p><p>selfish about dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsamaccharin|aavaasamaccharin|avasamaccharin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasamaccharin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvāsamaccharin">āvāsamaccharin</a></p><p>selfish about dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsamacchariya|aavaasamacchariya|avasamacchariya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasamacchariya</span></p><p>selfish, meanness about dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsapaligedhi|aavaasapaligedhi|avasapaligedhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasapaligedhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvāsapaligedhin">āvāsapaligedhin</a></p><p>greedy, selfish about dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsapaligedhin|aavaasapaligedhin|avasapaligedhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasapaligedhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āvāsapaligedhin">āvāsapaligedhin</a></p><p>greedy, selfish about dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsaparamparā|aavaasaparamparaa|avasaparampara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasaparamparaa</span></p><p>succession of residences or dwellings</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsaparamparāṃ|aavaasaparamparaa.m|avasaparamparam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasaparamparaa.m</span></p><p>to all the dwellings in succession</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvāsa">āvāsa</a></p>

āvāsika|aavaasika|avasika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasika</span></p><ol><li>(mfn.) resident; in (regular or permanent) residence (usually of a bhikkhu).</li><li>(m.) a resident <em>bhikkhu</em> (usually staying permanently in the vihāra and/or acting as a caretaker).</li></ol>

āvāsika|aavaasika|avasika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaasika</span></p><p>adjective living in, residing at home, being in (constant or fixed) residence, usually appl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> to bhikkhus (opp. āgantuka) Vin.i.128 sq.; Vin.ii.15, Vin.ii.170; Vin.iii.65; Vin.v.203 sq.; MN.i.473; AN.i.236; AN.iii.261 sq., AN.iii.366; Ja.iv.310; Pv.iv.8#4 (= nibaddha-vasanaka Pv-a.267).</p><p>āvāsa + ika</p>

āvāṭa|aavaa.ta|avata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavaa.ta</span></p><p>hole dug in the ground, a pit, a well DN.i.142 (yaññ˚); Ja.i.99, Ja.i.264; Ja.ii.406; Ja.iii.286; Ja.iv.46 (caturassa); Ja.vi.10; Dhp-a.i.223; Vv-a.63; Pv-a.225.</p><p>etym.?</p>

āvī|aavii|avi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aavii</span></p><p>before the eyes; openly, manifestly; in public</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āvi">āvi</a>, <a href="bword://āviṃ">āviṃ</a></p>

āya|aaya|aya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaya</span></p><p>coming in, arrival, source; inlet, entrance; income, revenue; profit.</p>

āya|aaya|aya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aaya</span></p><ol><li>coming in, entrance MN.iii.93.</li><li>tax Ja.v.113.</li><li>income, earning, profit, gain (opp vaya loss) AN.iv.282 = AN.iv.323; Snp.978; Ja.i.228; Kp-a.38 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of kāya), Kp-a.82 (in etym. of āyatana); Pv-a.130.</li><li>(āyā f.?) a lucky dice (“the incomer”) Ja.vi.281.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://kammika">kammika</a></em> a treasurer Dhp-a.i.184.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></em> clever in earnings Ne.20.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kosalla">kosalla</a></em> proficiency in money making DN.iii.220 (one of the three kosallas); Vb.325.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://paricc%C4%81ga">pariccāga</a></em> expediture of one’s income Pv-a.8.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a></em> (lit. entrance, inflow, going in DN.i.74 (= āgamana-magga DN-a.i.78); MN.ii.15; AN.ii.166; (fig.) revenue income, money Snp-a.173.</li></ul><p>Sk. āya; ā + <em><a href="bword://i">i</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://kammika">kammika</a>, <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a>, <a href="bword://kosalla">kosalla</a>, <a href="bword://pariccāga">pariccāga</a>, <a href="bword://mukha">mukha</a>, <a href="bword://i">i</a></p>

āyakosalla|aayakosalla|ayakosalla
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayakosalla</span></p><p>knowledge about, proficiency in, gain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āya">āya</a></p>

āyakusala|aayakusala|ayakusala
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayakusala</span></p><p>knowledgeable about income or gain</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āya">āya</a></p>

āyamati|aayamati|ayamati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayamati</span></p><p>stretches, stretches out (trans. and intrans.); expands.</p>

āyamati|aayamati|ayamati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayamati</span></p><p>to stretch, extend, stretch out, draw out Mil.176, usually in ster. phrase <em>piṭṭhi me āgilāyati tam ahaṃ āyamissāmi</em> “my back feels weak, I will stretch it” Vin.ii.200; DN.iii.209; MN.i.354; SN.iv.184; Ja.i.491. Besides this in commentaries e.g. Ja.iii.489 (mukhaṃ āyamituṃ).</p><p>ā + <em>yam</em></p>

āyamukha|aayamukha|ayamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayamukha</span></p><p>way of entrance; an inlet (for water); a source (of gain or revenue)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āya">āya</a></p>

āyanā|aayanaa|ayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayanaa</span></p><p>at Dhs-a.259 and Vism.26 is a grammarian’s construction, abstracted from f. abstr. words ending in <em>-āyanā</em>, e.g. kankhā → kankhāyanā, of which the correct expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is a derivation fr. caus<br />■ formation kankhāyati → kankhāy + a + nā. What the idea of Bdhgh. was in propounding his expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> is hard to say, perhaps he related it to i and understood it to be the same as <em>āyāna</em>.</p><p>?</p>

āyasa|aayasa|ayasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasa</span></p><p>made of iron</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ayasa">ayasa</a></p>

āyasa|aayasa|ayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasa</span></p><p>adjective made of iron SN.ii.182; AN.iii.58; Dhp.345; Ja.iv.416; Ja.v.81; Vv.84#5 (an˚ cp. the rather strange expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.335).</p><p>Sk. āyasa, of ayas iron</p>

āyasakka|aayasakka|ayasakka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasakka</span></p><p>disgrace, ill-repute, dishonor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyasakya">āyasakya</a></p>

āyasakya|aayasakya|ayasakya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasakya</span></p><p>disgrace, ill-repute, dishonor</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyasakka">āyasakka</a></p>

āyasakya|aayasakya|ayasakya
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasakya</span></p><p>dishonour, disgrace, bad repute AN.iv.96; Ja.v.17; Vv-a.110; usually in phrase <em>-ṃ pāpuṇāti</em> to fall into disgrace Thag.292; Ja.ii.33 = Ja.ii.271; Ja.iii.514.</p><p>Bdhgh on AN.iv.96 explains it as ayasaka + ya with guṇa of the initial, cp. ārogya</p>

āyasampanna|aayasampanna|ayasampanna
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasampanna</span></p><p>provided with an inlet</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āya">āya</a></p>

āyasma|aayasma|ayasma
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasma</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyasmat">āyasmat</a></p><p>long-lived, venerable; a venerable person (an honorific and respectful title of a bhikkhu).</p>

āyasmant|aayasmant|ayasmant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasmant</span></p><p>adjective lit. old, i.e. venerable; used, either as adj. or absolute as a respectful appellation of a bhikkhu of some standing (cp. the semantically identical <em><a href="bword://thera">thera</a></em>). It occurs usually in nom. <em>āyasmā</em> and is expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> in Nd by typical formula “piya-vacanaṃ garu˚, sagārava-sappaṭissâdhivacanaṃ”, e.g. Mnd.140, Mnd.445; Cnd.130 on var. Snp loci (e.g. Snp.814, Snp.1032, Snp.1040, Snp.1061, Snp.1096)<br />■ Freq. in all texts, of later passages see Snp-a.158; Pv-a.53, Pv-a.54, Pv-a.63 Pv-a.78<br />■ See also <em><a href="bword://%C4%81vuso">āvuso</a></em>.</p><p>Sk. āyuṣmant, the P. form showing assimilation of u to a</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thera">thera</a>, <a href="bword://āvuso">āvuso</a></p>

āyasmat|aayasmat|ayasmat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayasmat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyasmat">āyasmat</a></p><p>long-lived, venerable; a venerable person (an honorific and respectful title of a bhikkhu).</p>

āyata|aayata|ayata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayata</span></p><p>stretched, stretched out; extended, long; long-drawn-out</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyamati">āyamati</a></p>

āyata|aayata|ayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayata</span></p><ol><li>(adj.) outstretched, extended, long, in length (with numeral) DN.iii.73 (ñātikkhaya, prolonged or heavy?); MN.i.178 (dīghato ā˚; tiriyañ ca vitthata); Ja.i.77, Ja.i.273 (tettiṃs’-angul’āyato khaggo); Ja.iii.438; Vv.84#15 (˚aṃsa; cp. expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at Vv-a.339) Snp-a.447; Dhs-a.48; Pv-a.152 (dāṭhā fangs; lomā hair), Pv-a.185 (˚vaṭṭa); Sdhp.257.</li><li>(n.) a bow Ja.iii.438.</li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://agga">agga</a></em> having its point (end) stretched forward, i.e. in the future (see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yati">āyati</a></em>) Iti.15, Iti.52.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pa%E1%B9%87hin">paṇhin</a></em> having long eye-lashes (one of the signs of a Mahāpurisa) DN.ii.17 DN.iii.143.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pamha">pamha</a></em> a long eye-lash Thig.384 (= dīghapakhuma Thag-a.250).</li></ul><p>Sk. āyata, pp. of ā + <em>yam</em>, cp. āyamati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://agga">agga</a>, <a href="bword://āyati">āyati</a>, <a href="bword://paṇhin">paṇhin</a>, <a href="bword://pamha">pamha</a></p>

āyatagga|aayatagga|ayatagga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatagga</span></p><p>or neuter extensive and best; or best for the future, the most excellent thing for the future; or best of sources</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyataka">āyataka</a></p>

āyataka|aayataka|ayataka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayataka</span></p><p>long, prolonged; drawn-out (of sound).</p>

āyataka|aayataka|ayataka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayataka</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>long. extended, prolonged, kept up, lasting Vin.ii.108 (gītassara); AN.iii.251 (id.) Ja.i.362.</li><li>sudden, abrupt, instr. <em>-ena</em> abruptly Vin.ii.237.</li></ol><p>= āyata</p>

āyatakena|aayatakena|ayatakena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatakena</span></p><p>suddenly, abruptly, of a sudden</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyataka">āyataka</a></p>

āyatana|aayatana|ayatana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatana</span></p><ol><li>resting-place, abode; source; region, sphere, field, area.</li><li>transcendental sphere, plane of experience; transcendental state of mind in meditation.</li><li>sphere of perception, the sense-organs (including mind) and their objects.</li></ol>

āyatana|aayatana|ayatana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatana</span></p><p>*Āyatana:<br />* 1: Spheres, is a name for the four formless absorptions; see: <em>jhāna</em> 5-8.<br />2: The 12 sources or bases on which depend the mental processes, consist of five physical sense-organs and consciousness, being the six internal <em>ajjhattika</em> sources; and the six objects, the so-called external <em>bāhira</em> sources - namely:</p><blockquote><ul><li><p>eye, or visual organ and visible object</p></li><li><p>ear, or auditory organ and sound, or audible object</p></li><li><p>nose, or olfactory organ and odour, or olfactive object</p></li><li><p>tongue, or gustatory organ and taste, or gustative object</p></li><li><p>body, or tactile organ and body-contact, or tactile object</p></li><li><p>mind-base, or consciousness and idea or mental-object<br /><em>manāyatana dhammāyatana</em></p></li></ul></blockquote><p>By the visual organ <em>cakkhāyatana</em> is meant the sensitive part of the eye <em>cakkhu</em> - <em>pasāda</em> (retina) built up of the four elements… responding to sense-stimuli <em>sa</em> - <em>ppatigha.</em> Vibh. II. Similar is the explanation of the four remaining physical sense-organs.</p><p>The source-of-mind <em>manāyatana</em> is a collective term for all consciousness whatsoever, and should therefore not be confounded with the mental-element <em>mano-dhātu</em> see: <em>dhātu</em> II, 16, which latter performs only the functions of directing <em>āvajjana</em> attention to the sense-object, and of receiving <em>sampaticchana</em> the data of the sense-object. On the functions of the mind, see: <em>viññāna-kicca</em></p><p>The visible object <em>rūpāyatana</em> is described in Vibh. II as that phenomenon which is built up of the four primary elements and appears as color and form. What is seen by-visual perception, i.e. by visual-consciousness <em>cakkhu-viññāna</em> are only colors and different intensities of light, but not three dimensional bodily things, which are interpretations.</p><p>The thinkable mental-object-source <em>dhammāyatana</em> is identical with ‘mental-object-element’ <em>dhamma-dhātu</em>, <em>dhātu</em> II-17 and <em>dhammārammana</em> see: <em>ārammana</em>. It may be physical or mental, past, present or future, real or imaginary.</p><p>The 5 physical sense-organs are also called abilities <em>indriya</em>, and of these abilities it is said in M. 43: Each of the five abilities owns a different domain, and none of them partakes of the domain of another one;… they have mind as their support… are conditioned by mental vitality (neural metabolic activity).</p><p>The 12 sense-source are fully discussed in Vis.M XV. In Yam III see: Guide, p 98f. are<br />the 12 terms are subjected to a logical investigation The six internal bases form the 5th link of dependent origination <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em> 5.</p>

āyatana|aayatana|ayatana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatana</span></p><ol><li>stretch, extent, reach, compass, region; sphere, locus, place, spot; position, occasion (corresponding to Bdhgh’s definition at DN-a.i.124 as “samosaraṇa”) DN.iii.241, DN.iii.279 (vimutti˚); SN.ii.41, SN.ii.269; SN.iv.217 SN.v.119 sq., SN.v.318. sq.; AN.iii.141 (ariya˚); AN.v.61 (abhibh˚ q.v.) Snp.406 (rajass˚ “haunt of passion” = rāgādi-rajassa uppatti-deso Snp-a.381); Ja.i.80 (raj˚). Freq. in phrase <em>araññ˚</em>; a lonely spot, a spot in the forest Ja.i.173; Vv-a.301; Pv-a.42, Pv-a.54.</li><li>exertion, doing, working, practice, performance (comprising Bdhgh’s definition at DN-a.i.124 as paññatti), usually -˚, viz. <em>kamm˚</em>; Mnd.505; Vb.324 Vb.353; <em>kasiṇ˚</em>; AN.v.46 sq., AN.v.60; Pts.i.28; <em>titth˚</em>; AN.i.173 AN.i.175; Vb.145, Vb.367; <em>sipp˚</em>; (art, craft) DN.i.51; Cnd.505 Vb.324, Vb.353; cp. <em>an˚</em>; non-exertion, indolence, sluggishness Ja.v.121.</li><li>sphere of perception or sense in general, object of thought, sense-organ &amp; object; relation order<br />■ <em>Cpd.</em> p. 183 says rightly: “āyatana cannot be rendered by a single English word to cover both sense-organs (the mind being regarded as 6<!-- raw HTML omitted -->th<!-- raw HTML omitted --> sense) and sense objects”<br />■ These āyatanāni (relations, functions reciprocalities) are thus divided into two groups, inner (ajjhattikāni) and outer (bāhirāni), and comprise the foll.:<ol><li><em>ajjhatt˚</em>: 1. <em><a href="bword://cakkhu">cakkhu</a></em> eye, 2. <em><a href="bword://sota">sota</a></em> ear, 3. <em><a href="bword://gh%C4%81na">ghāna</a></em> nose, 4. <em><a href="bword://jivh%C4%81">jivhā</a></em> tongue, 5. <em><a href="bword://k%C4%81ya">kāya</a></em> body, 6. <em><a href="bword://mano">mano</a></em> mind</li><li><em>bāh˚</em>: 1. <em><a href="bword://r%C5%ABpa">rūpa</a></em> visible object, 2. <em><a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a></em> sound, 3 <em><a href="bword://gandha">gandha</a></em> odour, 4. <em><a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a></em> taste, 5. <em><a href="bword://pho%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADhabba">phoṭṭhabba</a></em> tangible object, 6. <em><a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a></em> cognizable object<br />■ For details as regards connotation &amp; application see Dhs trsl. introduction li sq. <em>Cpd.</em> 90 n. 2; 254 sq<br />■ Approximately covering this meaning (3) is Bdhgh’s definition of āyatana at DN-a.i.124 as sañjāti and as kāraṇa (origin &amp; cause i.e. mutually occasioning &amp; conditioning relations or adaptations). See also Cnd under rūpa for further classifications.<br />■ For the above mentioned <em>12</em> āyatanāni see the foll. passages: DN.ii.302 sq.; DN.iii.102, DN.iii.243; AN.iii.400 AN.v.52; Snp.373 (cp. Snp-a.366); Pts.i.7, Pts.i.22, Pts.i.101, Pts.i.137; Pts.ii.181, Pts.ii.225, Pts.ii.230; Dhs.1335; Vb.401 sq.; Ne.57, Ne.82 Vism.481; Thag-a.49, Thag-a.285.<br />■ Of these <em>6</em> are mentioned at SN.i.113, SN.ii.3; SN.iv.100, SN.iv.174 sq.; Iti.114; Vb.135 sq., Vb.294 Ne.13, Ne.28, Ne.30; Vism.565 sq. Other sets of <em>10</em> at Ne.69; of <em>4</em> at DN.ii.112, DN.ii.156; of <em>2</em> at DN.ii.69<br />■ Here also belongs ākās’ ānañc’ āyatana, ākiñcaññ˚ etc. (see under <em><a href="bword://%C4%81k%C4%81sa">ākāsa</a></em> etc. and s. v.), e.g. at DN.i.34 sq., DN.i.183; AN.iv.451 sq.; Vb.172, Vb.189, Vb.262 sq.; Vism.324 sq<br />■ Unclassified passages: MN.i.61; MN.ii.233; MN.iii.32, MN.iii.216, MN.iii.273; SN.i.196; SN.ii.6 SN.ii.8, SN.ii.24, SN.ii.72 sq.; SN.iii.228; SN.iv.98; SN.v.426; AN.i.113, AN.i.163, AN.i.225 AN.iii.17, AN.iii.27, AN.iii.82, AN.iii.426; AN.iv.146, AN.iv.426; AN.v.30, AN.v.321, AN.v.351, AN.v.359 Mnd.109, Mnd.133, Mnd.171, Mnd.340; Ja.i.381 (paripuṇṇa˚); Vb.412 sq. (id.).</li></ol></li></ol><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://upp%C4%81da">uppāda</a></em> birth of the āyatanas (see above 3) Vin.i.185</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a></em> skilled in the ā. MN.iii.63.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kusalat%C4%81">kusalatā</a></em> skill in the spheres (of sense) DN.iii.212; Dhs.1335.</li><li><em>-ṭṭha</em> founded in the sense-organs Pts.i.132; Pts.ii.121.</li></ul><p>Sk. āyatana, not found in the Vedas; but freq. in BSk. From ā + <em>yam</em>, cp. āyata. The pl. is <em>āyatanā</em> at SN.iv.70<br />■ For full definition of term as seen by the Pāli Commentators see Bdhgh’s expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at DN-a.i.124, DN-a.i.125, with which cp. the popular etym. at Kp-a.82 “āyassa vā tananato āyatassa vā saṃsāradukkhassa nayanato āyatanāni” and at Vism.527 “āye tanoti āyatañ ca nayatī ti ā.”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://cakkhu">cakkhu</a>, <a href="bword://sota">sota</a>, <a href="bword://ghāna">ghāna</a>, <a href="bword://jivhā">jivhā</a>, <a href="bword://kāya">kāya</a>, <a href="bword://mano">mano</a>, <a href="bword://rūpa">rūpa</a>, <a href="bword://sadda">sadda</a>, <a href="bword://gandha">gandha</a>, <a href="bword://rasa">rasa</a>, <a href="bword://phoṭṭhabba">phoṭṭhabba</a>, <a href="bword://dhamma">dhamma</a>, <a href="bword://ākāsa">ākāsa</a>, <a href="bword://uppāda">uppāda</a>, <a href="bword://kusala">kusala</a>, <a href="bword://kusalatā">kusalatā</a></p>

āyatanaso|aayatanaso|ayatanaso
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatanaso</span></p><ol><li>as the fields of perception.</li><li>in the right sphere; suitably</li></ol><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a></p>

āyatanika|aayatanika|ayatanika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatanika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to the sphere of (some special sense, see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yatana">āyatana</a></em> 3) SN.iv.126 (phass niraya &amp; sagga).</p><p>fr. āyatana</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyatana">āyatana</a></p>

āyatapamha|aayatapamha|ayatapamha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatapamha</span></p><p>having long eyelashes</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyata">āyata</a></p>

āyatapaṇhi|aayatapa.nhi|ayatapanhi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatapa.nhi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyatapaṇhin">āyatapaṇhin</a></p><p>having projecting heels (one of the 32 characteristics of a mahāpurisa)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyata">āyata</a></p>

āyatapaṇhin|aayatapa.nhin|ayatapanhin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatapa.nhin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyatapaṇhin">āyatapaṇhin</a></p><p>having projecting heels (one of the 32 characteristics of a mahāpurisa)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyata">āyata</a></p>

āyati|aayati|ayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayati</span></p><p>future; next.</p>

āyati|aayati|ayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayati</span></p><p>“stretching forth”, extension, length (of time), future. Only (?) in acc. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81yati%E1%B9%83">āyatiṃ</a></em> (adv.) in future Vin.ii.89, Vin.ii.185; Vin.iii.3; Snp.49; Iti.115 (T. reads āyati but cp. p. 94 where T. āyatiṃ, variant reading āyati) Ja.i.89; Ja.v.431; DN-a.i.236.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>yam</em>, cp. Sk. āyati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyatiṃ">āyatiṃ</a></p>

āyatijātijarāmaraṇiya|aayatijaatijaraamara.niya|ayatijatijaramaraniya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatijaatijaraamara.niya</span></p><p>leading to birth, old age and death in the future</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyati">āyati</a></p>

āyatika|aayatika|ayatika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatika</span></p><p>next; future.</p>

āyatika|aayatika|ayatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatika</span></p><p>adjective future SN.i.142.</p><p>fr. last</p>

āyatikā|aayatikaa|ayatika
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatikaa</span></p><p>tube, waterpipe Vin.ii.123.</p><p>of āyataka</p>

āyatiṃ|aayati.m|ayatim
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">acc. adv.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayati.m</span></p><p>in the future, for the future</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyati">āyati</a></p>

āyatta|aayatta|ayatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayatta</span></p><ol><li>striving, active, ready, exerted Ja.v.395 (˚mana = ussukkamana C.).</li><li>striven after, pursued Ja.i.341.</li><li>dependent on Vism.310 (assāsa-passāsa˚); Ne.194; Sdhp.477, Sdhp.605.</li></ol><p>Sk. āyatta, pp. of ā + <em>yat</em></p>

āyoga|aayoga|ayoga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayoga</span></p><ol><li>binding; a bandage.</li><li>occupation, exercise, exertion; practice.</li></ol>

āyoga|aayoga|ayoga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayoga</span></p><ol><li>binding, bandage Vin.ii.135; Vv.33#41; Vv-a.142 (˚paṭṭa).</li><li>yoke Dhs.1061 (avijj˚), Dhs.1162.</li><li>ornament, decoration Mnd.226; Ja.iii.447 (˚vatta, for variant reading ˚vanta?).</li><li>occupation, devotion to, pursuit, exertion DN.i.187; Dhp.185 (= payoga-karaṇa Dhp-a.iii.238).</li><li>(t. t.) obligation guarantee(?) Snp-a.179<br />■ Cp. sam˚.</li></ol><p>Sk. āyoga, of ā + <em>yuj</em>; cp. āyutta</p>

āyu|aayu|ayu
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter (&amp; m.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayu</span></p><p>life; vital power; duration of life, life-time; long life.</p>

āyu|aayu|ayu
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayu</span></p><p>life, vitality, duration of life, longevity DN.iii.68, DN.iii.69, DN.iii.73, DN.iii.77; SN.iii.143 (usmā ca); SN.iv.294; AN.i.155; AN.ii.63, AN.ii.66 (addh˚); AN.iii.47, AN.iv.76, AN.iv.139; Snp.694, Snp.1019; Iti.89; Ja.i.197 (dīgh˚); Vv.55#5 (cp. Vv-a.247 with its definition of divine life as comprising 30 600 000 years); Vism.229 (length of man’s āyu = 100 years); Dhs.19, Dhs.82, Dhs.295, Dhs.644, Dhs.716; Sdhp.234, Sdhp.239, Sdhp.258<br />■ Long or divine life, dibbaṃ āyu is one of the 10 attributes of ādhipateyya or majesty (see <em><a href="bword://%E1%B9%ADh%C4%81na">ṭhāna</a></em>) thus at Vin.i.294; DN.iii.146; SN.iv.275 sq.; AN.i.115; AN.iii.33; AN.iv.242, AN.iv.396; Pv.ii.9#59 (= jīvitaṃ Pv-a.136).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://%C5%ABh%C4%81">ūhā</a></em> see <em>āyūhā</em>.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://kappa">kappa</a></em> duration of life Mil.141; Dhp-a.i.250.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://khaya">khaya</a></em> decay of life (cp. jīvita-kkhaya) DN.i.17 (cp. DN-a.i.110); DN-a.iii.29.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pam%C4%81%E1%B9%87a">pamāṇa</a></em> span or measure of life time DN.ii.3; AN.i.213, AN.i.267; AN.ii.126 sq.; AN.iv.138 AN.iv.252 sq., AN.iv.261; AN.v.172; Pp.16; Vb.422 sq.; Snp-a.476</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://pariyanta">pariyanta</a></em> end of life Iti.99; Vism.422.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85khaya">saṅkhaya</a></em> exhaustion of life or lifetime Dpvs.v.102.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://sa%E1%B9%85kh%C4%81ra">saṅkhāra</a></em> (usually pl. ˚ā) constituent of life, conditions or properties resulting in life, vital principle DN.ii.106; MN.i.295 sq.; SN.ii.266; AN.iv.311 sq.; Ud.64; Ja.iv.215; Mil.285; Vism.292; Dhp-a.i.129; Pv-a.210. Cp. BSk. āyuḥ-saṃskāra Divy.203.</li></ul><p>Vedic āyus; Av. āyu, gradation form of same root as Gr. αἰών “aeon”, αἰέν always; Lat. aevum, Goth aiws. Ohg. ēwa, io always; Ger. ewig eternal; Ags. āē eternity, ā always (cp. ever and aye)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ṭhāna">ṭhāna</a>, <a href="bword://ūhā">ūhā</a>, <a href="bword://kappa">kappa</a>, <a href="bword://khaya">khaya</a>, <a href="bword://pamāṇa">pamāṇa</a>, <a href="bword://pariyanta">pariyanta</a>, <a href="bword://saṅkhaya">saṅkhaya</a>, <a href="bword://saṅkhāra">saṅkhāra</a></p>

āyuda|aayuda|ayuda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuda</span></p><p>giving life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyudha|aayudha|ayudha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayudha</span></p><p>is the Vedic form of the common Pāli form āvudha weapon, and occurs only spuriously at DN.i.9 (variant reading āvudha).</p>

āyudhalakkhaṇa|aayudhalakkha.na|ayudhalakkhana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayudhalakkha.na</span></p><p>sings of omen on weapons.</p>

āyudāyi|aayudaayi|ayudayi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayudaayi</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyudāyin">āyudāyin</a></p><p>giving life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyudāyin|aayudaayin|ayudayin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayudaayin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyudāyin">āyudāyin</a></p><p>giving life</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyuhati|aayuhati|ayuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuhati</span></p><p>thrusts forward, pushes forward (intrans.); struggles, strains, exerts oneself (esp. in water); pursues, works at</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyūhati">āyūhati</a></p>

āyuka|aayuka|ayuka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuka</span></p><p>(-˚) adjective -being of life; having a life or age AN.iv.396 (niyat˚); Vv-a.196 (yāvatāyukā dibbasampatti divine bliss lasting for a lifetime). Esp. freq in comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with <em><a href="bword://d%C4%ABgha">dīgha</a></em> (long) and <em><a href="bword://appa">appa</a></em> (short) as dīghāyuka AN.iv.240; Pv-a.27; appāyuka AN.iv.247; Pv-a.103 both at Vism.422. In phrase vīsati-vassasahass’āyukesu manussesu at the time when men lived 20 000 years DN.ii.5–DN.ii.12 (see Table at <em>Dial.</em> ii.6); Dhp-a.ii.9; Pv-a.135 dasa-vassasahass’āyukesu manussesu (10 000 years) Pv-a.73; cattāḷīsa˚ Dhp-a.i.103; catusaṭṭhi-kapp’āyukā subhakiṇhā Vism.422.</p><p>fr. āyu</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://dīgha">dīgha</a>, <a href="bword://appa">appa</a></p>

āyukin|aayukin|ayukin
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayukin</span></p><p>adjective = āyuka; in appāyukin short lived Vv.41#6.</p><p>fr. āyu</p>

āyusaṅkhāra|aayusa"nkhaara|ayusankhara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayusa"nkhaara</span></p><p>constituent of life, impetus for life, vital principle</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyusmant|aayusmant|ayusmant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayusmant</span></p><p>adjective having life or vitality Pv-a.63 (āyusmāviññāṇa feeling or sense of vitality; is reading correct?).</p><p>Sk. āyuṣmant; see also the regular P. form āyasmant</p>

āyussa|aayussa|ayussa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayussa</span></p><p>giving long life.</p>

āyussa|aayussa|ayussa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayussa</span></p><p>adjective connected with life, bringing (long) life AN.iii.145 dhamma).</p><p>Sk. *āyuṣya</p>

āyuta|aayuta|ayuta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuta</span></p><p>adjective</p><ol><li>connected with, endowed, furnished with Thag.753 (dve pannaras’āyuta due to twice fifteen); Snp.301 (nārī-varagaṇ˚ = ˚saṃyutta Snp-a.320); Pv.ii.12#4 (nānā-saragaṇ˚ ˚yutta Pv-a.157).</li><li>seized, conquered, in <em>dur˚</em>; hard to conquer, invincible Ja.vi.271 (= paccatthikehi durāsada C.).</li></ol><p>Sk. ayuta, pp. of ā + <em>yu</em>, yuvati</p>

āyutta|aayutta|ayutta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayutta</span></p><p>engaged in, intent upon, devoted to (+ loc. or dat.).</p>

āyutta|aayutta|ayutta
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayutta</span></p><ol><li>yoked, to connected with, full of Pv.i.10#14 (tejas’āyuta T., but Pv-a.52 reads ˚āyutta and explains as samāyutta); Pv-a.157 (ākiṇṇa of Pv.ii.12#4).</li><li>intent upon, devoted to SN.i.67.</li></ol><p>Sk. āyukta; pp. of ā + <em>yuj</em></p>

āyuttaka|aayuttaka|ayuttaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuttaka</span></p><p>adjective noun one who is devoted to or entrusted with, a trustee, agent, superintendent, overseer Ja.i.230 (˚vesa); Ja.iv.492; Dhp-a.i.101, Dhp-a.i.103, Dhp-a.i.180.</p><p>āyutta + ka</p>

āyuva|aayuva|ayuva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuva</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyuvat">āyuvat</a></p><p>long lived</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyuvant|aayuvant|ayuvant
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuvant</span></p><p>adjective advanced in years, old, of age Thag.234.</p><p>fr. āyu</p>

āyuvat|aayuvat|ayuvat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuvat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://āyuvat">āyuvat</a></p><p>long lived</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyu">āyu</a></p>

āyācaka|aayaacaka|ayacaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacaka</span></p><p>adjective noun one who begs or prays, petitioner Mil.129.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>yāc</em></p>

āyācamāna|aayaacamaana|ayacamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacamaana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyācati">āyācati</a></p>

āyācana|aayaacana|ayacana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter \~ā, feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacana</span></p><p>prayer, entreaty, vow.</p>

āyācana|aayaacana|ayacana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacana</span></p><ol><li>asking, adhortation, addressing (t. t. g. in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of imperative) Snp-a.43, Snp-a.176 Snp-a.412.</li><li>a vow, prayer AN.i.88; AN.iii.47; Ja.i.169 = Ja.v.472.</li></ol><p>fr. āyācati</p>

āyācanta|aayaacanta|ayacanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyācati">āyācati</a></p>

āyācati|aayaacati|ayacati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacati</span></p><p>prays; begs, implores; requests, asks (someone, acc. or gen., for something, acc. or dat.).</p>

āyācati|aayaacati|ayacati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacati</span></p><ol><li>to request, beg, implore, pray to (acc.) Vin.iii.127; DN.i.240; Pv-a.160.</li><li>to make a vow, to vow, promise AN.i.88; Ja.i.169 = Ja.v.472; Ja.i.260; Ja.ii.117</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81y%C4%81cita">āyācita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + <em>yāc</em>, cp. Buddh. Sk. āyācate Divy.1.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyācita">āyācita</a></p>

āyācita|aayaacita|ayacita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaacita</span></p><p>vowed, promised Ja.i.169 (˚bhattajātaka N.).</p><p>pp. of āyācati</p>

āyāga|aayaaga|ayaga
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaaga</span></p><p>offering, gift; sacrificial fee; the recipient of an offering.</p>

āyāga|aayaaga|ayaga
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaaga</span></p><p>sacrificial fee, gift; (m.) recipient of a sacrifice or gift (deyyadhamma) Snp.486 (= deyyadhammānaṃ adhiṭṭhāna-bhūta Snp-a.412); Thag.566; Ja.vi.205 (˚vatthu worthy objact of sacrificial fees).</p><p>ā + yāga of <em>yaj</em></p>

āyāma|aayaama|ayama
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaama</span></p><p>exertion; energy.</p>

āyāma|aayaama|ayama
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaama</span></p><ol><li>(lit.) stretching, stretching out, extension Vin.i.349 = Ja.iii.488 (mukh˚).</li><li>(appl.) usually as linear measure: extension, length (often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with and contrasted to <em><a href="bword://vitth%C4%81ra">vitthāra</a></em> breadth or width &amp; <em><a href="bword://ubbedha">ubbedha</a></em> height), as n. (esp. in abl. āyāmato instr. āyāmena in length) or as adj. (-˚): Ja.i.7, Ja.i.49 (˚ato tīṇi yojanasatāni, vitthārato aḍḍhatiyāni); Ja.iii.389; Mil.17 (ratanaṃ soḷasahatthaṃ āyāmena aṭṭhahatthaṃ vitthārena), Mil.282 (ratanaṃ catuhatth’āyāmaṃ); Vism.205 (+ vitth˚) Khb 133 (+ vitthāra &amp; parikkhepa); Vv-a.188 (soḷasayojan˚), Vv-a.199 (˚vitthārehi), Vv-a.221 (˚ato + vitth˚); Pv-a.77 (+ vitth˚), Pv-a.113 (id. + ubbedha); Dhp-a.i.17 (saṭṭhi-yojan˚).</li></ol><p>fr. ā + <em>yam</em>, see <em>āyamati</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://vitthāra">vitthāra</a>, <a href="bword://ubbedha">ubbedha</a></p>

āyāmato|aayaamato|ayamato
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">abl.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaamato</span></p><p>in length.</p>

āyāmena|aayaamena|ayamena
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">instr.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaamena</span></p><p>in length.</p>

āyāna|aayaana|ayana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaana</span></p><p>coming, arrival: see <em>āyanā</em>.</p><p>fr. ā + <strong>yā</strong> to go</p><p>See also: yā</p>

āyānta|aayaanta|ayanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">part. pr. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyāti">āyāti</a></p>

āyāsa|aayaasa|ayasa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaasa</span></p><p>exertion; trouble; weariness, distress.</p>

āyāsa|aayaasa|ayasa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaasa</span></p><p>trouble, sorrow, only neg. <em>an˚</em>; (adj.) peaceful, free from trouble AN.iv.98; Thag.1008.</p><p>cp. Sk. āyāsa, etym.?</p>

āyāsita|aayaasita|ayasita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaasita</span></p><p>trouble; d distressed.</p>

āyāta|aayaata|ayata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaata</span></p><p>gone to, undertaken Sdhp.407.</p><p>pp. of āyāt.; cp. BSk. āyāta in same meaning at Jtm.210</p>

āyāti|aayaati|ayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaati</span></p><p>comes, comes near, approaches; goes to; reaches (a state or condition).</p>

āyāti|aayaati|ayati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayaati</span></p><p>to come on or here, to come near, approach, get into SN.i.240; Snp.669; Snp.p.116 (= gacchati Snp-a.463); Ja.iv.410; Pv.ii.12#12 (= āgacchati Pv-a.158); Dhp-a.i.93 (imper. āyāma let us go)<br />pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81y%C4%81ta">āyāta</a></em>.</p><p>ā + yāti of <strong>yā</strong></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyāta">āyāta</a>, yā</p>

āyūha|aayuuha|ayuha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuha</span></p><p>thrusting; striving; exertion.</p>

āyūhaka|aayuuhaka|ayuhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhaka</span></p><p>adjective keen, eager, active Mil.207 (+ viriyavā).</p><p>fr. āyūhati</p>

āyūhana|aayuuhana|ayuhana
<p>[Nyana]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhana</span></p><p><strong>Āyūhana:</strong> K ammic accumulation, is a name used in the commentarial literature for the advantageous and disadvantageous intentional activities kamma or kammic-constructions <em>sankhāra</em> (see: <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em> 2), being the causes of future rebirth. Accumulation, is a name for the past kammic-constructions, and signifies those intentions <em>cetanā,</em> which arised at the performance of a kamma, first while thinking ‘I will give food’, and then while actually giving the food or any other object. The intention, however, at the time when one is handing the food over to the recipient is called kamma-making or kamma-becoming <em>kamma-bhava</em> see. Vis.M XVII, IX, X. Or, the intentions during the first six impulse-moments <em>javana</em> depending on one and the same state of directing <em>āvajjana</em> (see. <em>viññāna-kicca</em> ), these are called the kammic-constructions, whilst the 7th impulse moment is called the kamma-making <em>kamma-bhava</em>. Or, each moment of intention is called kamma-making and the accumulation connected with it, kamma-construction. Vis.M XVII. Cf. <em>paticca</em> - <em>samuppāda</em> 2, 10 - App.</p>

āyūhana|aayuuhana|ayuhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhana</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>endeavouring, striving, Pts.i.10 sq., Pts.i.32, Pts.i.52; Pts.ii.218; Vism.103, Vism.212, Vism.462, Vism.579 f. <em>āyūhanī</em> Dhs.1059 (“she who toils” trsl.) = Vb.361 Nd ii.taṇhā</li><li>(has āyūhanā).</li><li>furtherance, pursuit DN-a.i.64 (bhavassa).</li></ol><p>fr. āyūhati</p>

āyūhati|aayuuhati|ayuhati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhati</span></p><p>thrusts forward, pushes forward (intrans.); struggles, strains, exerts oneself (esp. in water); pursues, works at</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyuhati">āyuhati</a></p>

āyūhati|aayuuhati|ayuhati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhati</span></p><p>lit. to push on or forward aim at, go for, i.e.</p><ol><li>to endeavour, strain, exert oneself SN.i.1 (ppr. anāyūhaṃ unstriving), SN.i.48; Ja.vi.35 (viriyaṃ karoti C.), Ja.vi.283 (= vāyamati C.)</li><li>to be keen on (w. acc.), to cultivate, pursue, do Snp.210 (karoti Snp-a.258); Mil.108 (kammaṃ ūyūhitvā), Mil.214 (kammaṃ āyūhi), Mil.326 (maggaṃ)</li></ol><p>pp <em><a href="bword://%C4%81y%C5%ABhita">āyūhita</a></em> (q.v.).</p><p>ā + y + ūhati with euphonic y, fr. Vedic ūhati, <em>ūh<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em>, a gradation of <em>vah</em> (see etym. under vahati). Kern’s etym. on</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>99 = āyodhati is to be doubted, more acceptable is Morris’ expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> at J.P.T.S. 1885, 58 sq. although contradictory in part.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āyūhita">āyūhita</a></p>

āyūhita|aayuuhita|ayuhita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhita</span></p><p>busy, eager, active Mil.181.</p><p>*Sk. ā + ūhita, pp. of <em>ūh</em></p>

āyūhā|aayuuhaa|ayuha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">f.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhaa</span></p><p>life, lifetime, only in <em>-pariyosāna</em> at the end of (his) life Pv-a.136, Pv-a.162; Vv-a.319.</p><p>āyu + ūhā</p>

āyūhāpeti|aayuuhaapeti|ayuhapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aayuuhaapeti</span></p><p>to cause somebody to toil or strive after Dhs-a.364.</p><p>Caus. ii. fr. āyūhati</p>

āññassati|aa~n~nassati|annassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fut. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~nassati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññeyya|aa~n~neyya|anneyya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~neyya</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññāsi|aa~n~naasi|annasi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naasi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññāta|aa~n~naata|annata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naata</span></p><p>known, understood</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññātabba|aa~n~naatabba|annatabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn. &amp; n. impers.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naatabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññātaññassāmītindriya|aa~n~naata~n~nassaamiitindriya|annatannassamitindriya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naata~n~nassaamiitindriya</span></p><p>the faculty of resolving to know something unknown</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāta">āññāta</a></p>

āññāti|aa~n~naati|annati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naati</span></p><p>understands; perceives</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ājānāti">ājānāti</a></p>

āññātuṃ|aa~n~naatu.m|annatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">inf.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naatu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āññāya|aa~n~naaya|annaya
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa~n~naaya</span></p><p>abosl.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āḷahana|aa.lahana|alahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lahana</span></p><p>place of cremation; funeral pyre</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āḷāhana">āḷāhana</a></p>

āḷaka|aa.laka|alaka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laka</span></p><p>A type of tree Ap.16.</p>

āḷaka|aa.laka|alaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laka</span></p><p>(or <em>-ā</em> f.)</p><ol><li>a thorn, sting, dart, spike used either as arrow-straightener Mil.418; Dhp-a.i.288 or (perhaps also for piece of bone, fishbone) in making up a comb Vv-a.349 (˚sandhāpana = comb; how Hardy got the meaning of “alum” in Ind. to Vv-a is incomprehensible).</li><li>a peg, spike, stake or post (to tie an elephant to, cp. <em><a href="bword://%C4%81l%C4%81na">ālāna</a></em>). Cp.ii.1#3. Alamba = alambara</li></ol><p>Dimin of aḷa (?) or of ārā i.(?). See Morris J.P.T.S. 1886, 158</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālāna">ālāna</a></p>

āḷakamanda|aa.lakamanda|alakamanda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lakamanda</span></p><p>open, unenclosed; or crowded (like Āḷakamandā);</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālakamanda">ālakamanda</a></p>

āḷakamandā|aa.lakamandaa|alakamanda
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lakamandaa</span></p><p>A city of the gods, mighty, prosperous and full of devas. DN.ii.147 DN.ii.170</p><p>It was one of the chief cities of Uttarakuru, and a royal residence of Kuvera. DN.iii.201</p><p>In the Culla Vagga Vin.ii.152 the word is used as an adjective (<em>vihārā āḷakamandā honti</em>) to mean crowded with people.</p><p>5town</p>

āḷamba|aa.lamba|alamba
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lamba</span></p><p>Āḷamba = āḷambara</p><p>Vv.18#9 = Vv.50#24. See <em>ālambara</em>.</p>

āḷambara FIXME double|aa.lambara FIXME double|alambara FIXME double
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lambara FIXME double</span></p><p>Āḷamba = āḷambara</p><p>Vv.18#9 = Vv.50#24. See <em>ālambara</em>.</p>

āḷambara|aa.lambara|alambara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lambara</span></p><p>drum</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālambara">ālambara</a></p>

āḷambara|aa.lambara|alambara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lambara</span></p><p>Ālambara &amp; Āḷambara</p><p>neuter a drum Vin.i.15 (l); Ja.ii.344 (ḷ); Ja.v.390 (l); Vv.54#18 (ḷ).</p><p>Sk. āḍambara</p>

āḷavaka|aa.lavaka|alavaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lavaka</span></p><p>The <em>yakkha</em> of Āḷavī. The Buddha once stayed at his dweling, at which the <em>yakkha</em> became angry and threatened the Buddha. He was tamed by the Buddha and asked a series of questions Snp.p.31–33 SN.i.213<em>ff.</em></p><p>Āḷavaka’s name appears in the Āṭānāṭīya Sutta, among the Yakkhas to whom followers of the Buddha should appeal for protection in time of need. DN.iii.205</p>

āḷavaka|aa.lavaka|alavaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lavaka</span></p><p>Āḷavaka &amp; ˚ika</p><p>(adj. n.) dwelling in forests, a forest-dweller SN.ii.235. As Np. at Vism.208.</p><p>= āṭavika</p>

āḷavakā|aa.lavakaa|alavaka
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lavakaa</span></p><p>ĀḷavakāĀḷavikā</p><p>A name given to the monks of Āḷavī.</p><p>The Āḷavaka-bhikkhū are mentioned several times in the Vinaya Vin.ii.172<em>ff.</em> Vin.iii.85 Vin.iv.34–35 in connection with offences relating to repairing and reconstruction of buildings, and rules are laid down by the Buddha restricting these monks in their activities.</p>

āḷavika|aa.lavika|alavika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lavika</span></p><p>Āḷavaka &amp; ˚ika</p><p>(adj. n.) dwelling in forests, a forest-dweller SN.ii.235. As Np. at Vism.208.</p><p>= āṭavika</p>

āḷavī|aa.lavii|alavi
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lavii</span></p><p>Āḷavī<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>A town between Sāvatthī and Rājagaha. The Buddha goes from Sāvatthī to Kīṭāgiri, thence to Āḷavī, and finally, to Rājagaha. The Buddha, on several occasions, stayed at Āḷavī at the Aggālava shrine, which was near the town. The town later became famous as the residence of Āḷavaka Yakkha and of Hatthaka Āḷavaka. The Therī, Selā was born in Āḷavī and was therefore known as Aḷavikā. There was evidently a large community of monks at Āḷavī, some of whom seem to have chiefly occupied themselves with building vihāras for themselves.</p><p>26.926968,80.1909262town</p>

āḷavīgotama|aa.laviigotama|alavigotama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laviigotama</span></p><p>ĀḷavīgotamaĀḷavīGotama</p><p>A thera, who is mentioned in the Sutta Nipāta Snp.1146 in a verse spoken by the Buddha to Piṅgiya when the Buddha appeared in a ray of light at Bāvarī’s hermitage.</p>

āḷha|aa.lha|alha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lha</span></p><p>= <em>āḷhaka</em>; only at AN.iii.52 (udak˚), where perhaps better with variant reading to be read as <em>āḷhaka</em>. The id p. at AN.ii.55 has ālhaka only.</p>

āḷhaka|aa.lhaka|alhaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lhaka</span></p><p>fluid and dry measure.</p>

āḷhaka|aa.lhaka|alhaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine &amp; neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lhaka</span></p><p>certain measure of capacity, originally for grain; in older texts usually applied to a liquid measure (udaka˚). Its size is given by Bdhgh. at Snp-a.476 as follows: “cattāro patthā āḷhakāni doṇaṃ etc.”-udakāḷhaka SN.v.400; AN.ii.55 = AN.iii.337; Vv-a.155<br />■ In other connections at Ja.i.419 (aḍḍh˚); Ja.iii.541 (mitaṃ āḷhakena dhañña-māpaka-kammaṃ kataṃ C.); Mil.229 (patt˚); Dhp-a.iii.367 (aḍḍh˚).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://th%C4%81lik%C4%81">thālikā</a></em> a bowl of the capacity of an āḷhaka Vin.i.240; AN.iii.369; Dhp-a.iii.370 (variant reading bhatta-thālikā).</li></ul><p>Sk. āḍhaka, fr. *āḍha probably meaning “grain”</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://thālikā">thālikā</a></p>

āḷhakathālikā|aa.lhakathaalikaa|alhakathalika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lhakathaalikaa</span></p><p>cooking pot holding an āḷhaka of rice</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āḷhaka">āḷhaka</a></p>

āḷhika|aa.lhika|alhika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lhika</span></p><p>Āḷhiya &amp; āḷhika</p><p>(adj.) rich, happy, fortunate; only in neg. anāḷhiya poor, unlucky, miserable MN.i.450; MN.ii.178 (+ daḷidda) AN.iii.352 sq. (so read with variant reading BB. ˚āḷhika for T ˚āḷika; comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with daḷidda; variant reading SS. anaddhika); Ja.v.96, Ja.v.97 (+ daḷidda; C. na āḷhika).</p><p>fr. *āḷha, Sk. āḍhya, orig. possessing grain, rich in grain, i.e. wealth; semantically cp dhañña<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

āḷhiya|aa.lhiya|alhiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lhiya</span></p><p>Āḷhiya &amp; āḷhika</p><p>(adj.) rich, happy, fortunate; only in neg. anāḷhiya poor, unlucky, miserable MN.i.450; MN.ii.178 (+ daḷidda) AN.iii.352 sq. (so read with variant reading BB. ˚āḷhika for T ˚āḷika; comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with daḷidda; variant reading SS. anaddhika); Ja.v.96, Ja.v.97 (+ daḷidda; C. na āḷhika).</p><p>fr. *āḷha, Sk. āḍhya, orig. possessing grain, rich in grain, i.e. wealth; semantically cp dhañña<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

āḷi|aa.li|ali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.li</span></p><p>an embankment, a dike</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āli">āli</a></p>

āḷika|aa.lika|alika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.lika</span></p><p>at AN.iii.352, AN.iii.384 (an˚) is preferably to be read <em>āḷhika</em>, see <em>āḷhaka</em>.</p>

āḷinda|aa.linda|alinda
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.linda</span></p><p>terrace or verandah (esp. in front of the door of a house or dwelling); a surrounding platform or ledge</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālinda">ālinda</a></p>

āḷinda|aa.linda|alinda
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.linda</span></p><p>Ālinda &amp; Āḷinda</p><p>a terrace or verandah before the house-door Vin.i.248; Vin.ii.153; DN.i.89; MN.ii.119; SN.iv.290 (ḷ); AN.v.65 (ḷ); Ja.vi.429; DN-a.i.252; Dhp-a.i.26; Dhp-a.iv.196; Snp-a.55 (˚ka-vāsin; variant reading alindaka); Mhvs.35, Mhvs.3. As <em>ālindaka</em> at Ja.iii.283.</p><p>Sk. alinda</p>

āḷādvāraka|aa.laadvaaraka|aladvaraka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laadvaaraka</span></p><p>adjective at Ja.v.81, Ja.v.82 is corrupt &amp; should with variant reading perhaps better be read; <em>advāraka</em> without doors. Cp Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>29 (ālāraka?). Ja.v.81 has āḷāraka only.</p>

āḷāhana|aa.laahana|alahana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laahana</span></p><p>place of cremation; funeral pyre</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āḷahana">āḷahana</a></p>

āḷāhana|aa.laahana|alahana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laahana</span></p><p>place of cremation, cemetery DN.i.55; Ja.i.287 (here meaning the funereal fire) Ja.i.402; Ja.iii.505; Pv.ii.12#2; Vism.76; Mil.350; DN-a.i.166; Dhp-a.i.26; Dhp-a.iii.276; Pv-a.92, Pv-a.161, Pv-a.163 (= sarīrassa daḍḍha-ṭṭhāna)<br />■ <em>Note.</em> For āḷāhana in meaning “peg, stake” see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81l%C4%81na">ālāna</a></em>.</p><p>fr. ā + <em>ḍah</em> or <em>dah</em>, see <em><a href="bword://dahati">dahati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ālāna">ālāna</a>, <a href="bword://dahati">dahati</a></p>

āḷāna|aa.laana|alana
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laana</span></p><p>Ālāna &amp; Āḷāna</p><p>neuter a peg, stake, post, esp one to which an elephant is tied Ja.i.415; Ja.iv.308; Dhp-a.i.126 (ḷ) where all MSS. have āḷāhana, perhaps correctly.</p><p>for ānāhana with substitution of l for n (cp. apilandhana for apinandh˚ and contraction of ˚āhana to ˚āna originally meaning “tying to” then the thing to which anything is tied</p>

āḷāra|aa.laara|alara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laara</span></p><p>adjective thick, massed, dense or crooked, arched (?), only in cpd. <em>-pamha</em> with thick eyelashes Vv.64#11 (= gopakhuma Vv-a.279) Pv.iii.3#5 (= vellita-dīgha-nīla-pamukha). Cp. <em>alāra</em>. Alarika &amp; iya;</p><p>= aḷāra or uḷāra or = Sk. arāla?</p>

āḷārakālāma|aa.laarakaalaama|alarakalama
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laarakaalaama</span></p><p>ĀḷārakālāmaĀḷāraKālāma</p><p>One of the two teachers to whom Gotama, after his renunciation, first attached himself, the other being Uddaka Rāmaputta. In the Ariyaparivesāna Sutta MN.i.163–165; also M.i.240<em>ff.</em> MN.ii.94<em>ff.</em> MN.ii.212<em>ff.</em> the Buddha describes his visit to Āḷāra. Gotama quickly mastered his doctrine and was able to repeat it by heart; but feeling sure that Āḷāra not only knew the doctrine but had realised it, he approached him and questioned him about it. Āḷāra then proclaimed the Ākiñcaññāyatana, and Gotama, putting forth energy and concentration greater than Āḷāra’s, made himself master of that state. Āḷāra recognised his pupil’s eminence and treated him as an equal, but Gotama, not having succeeded in his quest, took leave of Āḷāra to go elsewhere. When, after having practised austerities for six years, the Buddha attained Enlightenment and granted Sahampati’s request to preach the doctrine, it was of Āḷāra he thought first as being the fittest to hear the teaching. But Āḷāra had died seven days earlier. Vin.i.7</p><p>The Mahā Parinibbāna Sutta DN.ii.130 mentions a Mallian, Pukkusa, who says he had been Āḷāra’s disciple, but who, when he hears the Buddha’s sermon, confesses faith in the Buddha. Pukkusa describes Āḷāra to the Buddha as one who practised great concentration. Once Āḷāra was sitting in the open air and neither saw nor heard five hundred passing carts though he was awake and conscious.</p>

āḷārika|aa.laarika|alarika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laarika</span></p><p>Āḷārika &amp; ˚iya</p><p>adjective noun a cook DN.i.51 (= bhattakāraka DN-a.i.157); Ja.v.296 (bhattakāraka C.); Ja.v.307; Ja.vi.276 (˚iya, C. ˚ika = sūpika) Mil.331.</p><p>Sk. ārālika, of uncertain etym.</p>

āḷāriya|aa.laariya|alariya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.laariya</span></p><p>Āḷārika &amp; ˚iya</p><p>adjective noun a cook DN.i.51 (= bhattakāraka DN-a.i.157); Ja.v.296 (bhattakāraka C.); Ja.v.307; Ja.vi.276 (˚iya, C. ˚ika = sūpika) Mil.331.</p><p>Sk. ārālika, of uncertain etym.</p>

āṅgahetuka|aa"ngahetuka|angahetuka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa"ngahetuka</span></p><p>The Large Green-billed Malkoha, <em>Phaenicophaeus tristis</em> Ja.vi.538. This pigeon-sized bird is greenish-grey with a green bill, a patch of red skin around its eyes and has a long curved greenish-black tail with a white tip. Because it prefers dense scrub, it is usually only seen when it flies from one clump of bushes to another, at which time the white tip of its tail is very noticeable. The malkoha feeds on insects and small animals and is found in the lower Himalayan hills.</p>

āṇatta|aa.natta|anatta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.natta</span></p><p>instructed; ordered; enjoined</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āṇatti|aa.natti|anatti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.natti</span></p><p>order, command; (the sense of) the imperative mood.</p>

āṇatti|aa.natti|anatti
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.natti</span></p><p>order, command, ordinance, injunction Vin.i.62; Kp-a.29; Pv-a.260; Sdhp.59, Sdhp.354.</p><p>ā + ñatti (cp. āṇāpeti), Caus. of <em>jñā</em></p>

āṇattika|aa.nattika|anattika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.nattika</span></p><p>adjective belonging to an ordinance or command, of the nature of an injunction Kp-a.29.</p><p>āṇatti + ka</p>

āṇañja|aa.na~nja|ananja
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.na~nja</span></p><p>see <em>ānejja</em>.</p>

āṇaṇya|aa.na.nya|ananya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.na.nya</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://%C4%81na%E1%B9%87ya">ānaṇya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ānaṇya">ānaṇya</a></p>

āṇi|aa.ni|ani
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.ni</span></p><p>ĀṇiĀṇakaĀṇṇaka</p><p>A <em>mutiṅga</em> (kettle-drum) belonging to the Dasārahas. As it grew old and began to split, they fixed in another peg, and this process was continued, until, at last, the original drumhead vanished, leaving only the framework of pegs. SN.ii.266</p>

āṇi|aa.ni|ani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.ni</span></p><p>the pin of a wheel-axle; a linchpin; a peg; a bolt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇī">āṇī</a></p>

āṇi|aa.ni|ani
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.ni</span></p><ol><li>the pin of a wheel-axle, a linch-pin MN.i.119; SN.ii.266, SN.ii.267; AN.ii.32; Snp.654; Ja.vi.253, Ja.vi.432; Snp-a.243; Kp-a.45, Kp-a.50.</li><li>a peg, pin, bolt, stop (at a door) MN.i.119; SN.ii.266 (drum stick); Ja.iv.30; Ja.vi.432, Ja.vi.460; Thag.744; Dhp.i.39.</li><li>(fig.) (˚-) peg-like (or secured by a peg, of a door) small, little in <em>-colaka</em> a small (piece of) rag Vin.ii.271 cp. Vin.i.205. (vaṇabandhana-colaka); <em>-dvāra</em> Thag.355; C khuddaka-dvāra, quoted at <em>Brethren</em> 200, trsl. by Mrs Rh. D. as “the towngate’s sallyport” by Neumann as “Gestöck” (fastening, enclosure) āṇi-gaṇṭhik’āhato ayopatto at Vism.108; DN-a.i.199 is apparently a sort of brush made of four or five small pieces of flexible wood.</li></ol><p>Vedic āṇi to aṇu fine, thin, flexible, in formation an <em>n</em>-enlargement of Idg. *olenā, cp. Ohg. lun, Ger. lünse Ags. lynes = E. linch, further related to Lat. ulna elbow Gr. ὠλένη, Ohg. elina, Ags. eln = E. el-bow. See Walde <em>Lāt. Wtb.</em> under ulna &amp; lacertus</p>

āṇicoḷaka|aa.nico.laka|anicolaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.nico.laka</span></p><p>plug of cloth; a menstruation cloth</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇi">āṇi</a></p>

āṇidvāra|aa.nidvaara|anidvara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.nidvaara</span></p><p>small gate or a bolted gate</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇi">āṇi</a></p>

āṇisaṅghāṭa|aa.nisa"nghaa.ta|anisanghata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.nisa"nghaa.ta</span></p><p>framework of pegs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇi">āṇi</a></p>

āṇā|aa.naa|ana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naa</span></p><p>order, command; authority.</p>

āṇā|aa.naa|ana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naa</span></p><p>order, command, authority Mil.253; DN-a.i.289; Kp-a.179, Kp-a.180, Kp-a.194; Pv-a.217 Sdhp.347, Sdhp.576. <em>rāj’āṇā</em> the king’s command or authority Ja.i.433; Ja.iii.351; Pv-a.242. <em>āṇaṃ deti</em> to give an order Ja.i.398; ˚ṃ <em><a href="bword://pavatteti">pavatteti</a></em> to issue an order Mil.189, cp āṇāpavatti Ja.iii.504; Ja.iv.145.</p><p>Sk. ājñā, ā + <em>jñā</em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://pavatteti">pavatteti</a></p>

āṇāpaka|aa.naapaka|anapaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapaka</span></p><p>one who gives an order; one who passes on an order.</p>

āṇāpaka|aa.naapaka|anapaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapaka</span></p><p>adjective noun</p><ol><li>(adj.) giving an order Vism.303</li><li>(n.) one who gives or calls out orders a town-crier, an announcer of the orders (of an authority Mil.147.</li></ol><p>fr. āṇāpeti</p>

āṇāpana|aa.naapana|anapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapana</span></p><p>ordering or being, ordered, command, order Pv-a.135.</p><p>abstr. fr. āṇāpeti</p>

āṇāpayati|aa.naapayati|anapayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapayati</span></p><p>commands, gives an order (to); orders</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇāpeti">āṇāpeti</a>, <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āṇāpesi|aa.naapesi|anapesi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapesi</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇāpeti">āṇāpeti</a></p>

āṇāpesuṃ|aa.naapesu.m|anapesum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">3 plural</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapesu.m</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇāpeti">āṇāpeti</a></p>

āṇāpeti|aa.naapeti|anapeti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">caus. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapeti</span></p><p>commands, gives an order (to); orders</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇāpayati">āṇāpayati</a>, <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āṇāpeti|aa.naapeti|anapeti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapeti</span></p><p>to give an order, to enjoin, command (with acc. of person) Ja.iii.351; Mil.147; Dhp-a.ii.82; Vv-a.68 (dāsiyo), Vv-a.69; Pv-a.4, Pv-a.39, Pv-a.81.</p><p>ā + ñāpeti, Caus. of ā + jānāti fr. <em>jñā</em>, cp. Sk. ājñāpayati</p>

āṇāpetvā|aa.naapetvaa|anapetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapetvaa</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇāpeti">āṇāpeti</a></p>

āṇāpita|aa.naapita|anapita
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.naapita</span></p><p>instructed; ordered; enjoined</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āññāti">āññāti</a></p>

āṇī|aa.nii|ani
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.nii</span></p><p>the pin of a wheel-axle; a linchpin; a peg; a bolt</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://āṇi">āṇi</a></p>

āṭa|aa.ta|ata
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.ta</span></p><p>A bird described in the commentaries as <em>dabbīmukha</em>, “spoon-mouthed” Ja.vi.539. If this is correct it must refer to the Eurasian Spoonbill, Hindi <em>dabil</em>, <em>Platalea leucorodia</em>, a large bird with a long neck and legs, pure white plumage and a distinctive spoon-shaped bill. The spoonbill is seen either individually or in flocks feeding in marshes, mudflats and estuaries.</p>

āṭa|aa.ta|ata
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.ta</span></p><p>kind of bird Ja.vi.539 (= dabbimukha C.).</p><p>etym.? Cp. Sk. āṭi Turdus Ginginianus, see Aufrecht, Halāyudha p. 148</p>

āṭaviya|aa.taviya|ataviya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.taviya</span></p><p>is to be read for <em>aṭaviyo</em> (q.v.) at Ja.vi.55.</p><p>= Sk. āṭavika</p>

āṭhapanā|aa.thapanaa|athapana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.thapanaa</span></p><p>at Pp.18 &amp; variant reading at Vb.357 is to be read; <em>aṭṭhapanā</em> (so T. at Vb.357).</p>

āṭānāṭā|aa.taanaa.taa|atanata
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">aa.taanaa.taa</span></p><p>A city in Uttarakuru, mentioned with Kusināṭā, Parakusināṭā and Nātāpuriyā. DN.iii.200</p><p>5town</p>

īdisa|iidisa|idisa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iidisa</span></p><p>(~ā ~ī)n. of such a kind, such; such and such; like this.</p>

īdisa|iidisa|idisa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iidisa</span></p><p>adjective such like, such Dhs-a.400 (f. ˚ī); Pv-a.50, (id.) Pv-a.51</p><p>Sk. īdṛs, ī + <em>dṛś</em>, lit. so-looking</p>

īdisaka|iidisaka|idisaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iidisaka</span></p><p>of such a kind; like this.</p>

īgha|iigha|igha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iigha</span></p><p>? confusion, rage badness Snp-a.590 (in expl<!-- raw HTML omitted -->n<!-- raw HTML omitted --> of anigha). Usually as <em>an˚</em> (or anigha), e.g. Ja.iii.343 (= niddukkha C.); Ja.v.343. Iti &amp; Iti</p><p>doubtful as to origin &amp; etym. since only found in cpd. anīgha &amp; abs. only in exegetical literature. If genuine, it should belong to; <em>ṛgh</em> Sk. ṛghāyati to tremble rage etc. See discussed under nigha<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

īhati|iihati|ihati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iihati</span></p><p>to endeavour, attempt, strive after Vin.iii.268 (Bdhgh. Ja.vi.518 (cp. Kern,</p><blockquote><p>Toevoegselen</p></blockquote><p>p. 112); DN-a.i.139; Vv-a.35.</p><p>Vedic <em>īh</em>, cp. Av. īžā ardour, eagerness, āzi<em>š</em> greed</p>

īhā|iihaa|iha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iihaa</span></p><p>exertion, endeavour, activity, only in adj. <em>nir-īha</em> void of activity Mil.413.</p><p>fr. <em>īh</em></p>

īrita|iirita|irita
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iirita</span></p><ol><li>set in motion, stirred, moved, shaken Vv.39#4 (vāt’erita moved by the wind); Ja.i.32 (id.); Vv.64#20 (haday’erita); Pv.ii.12#3 (malut’erita); Pv-a.156 (has erita for ī˚); Vv-a.177 (calita).</li><li>uttered, proclaimed, said Dāvs v.12.</li></ol><p>pp. of īreti, Caus. of <em>īr</em>, see <em><a href="bword://iriyati">iriyati</a></em></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://iriyati">iriyati</a></p>

īriṇa|iiri.na|irina
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iiri.na</span></p><p>barren soil, desert DN.i.248; AN.v.156 sq.; Ja.v.70 (= sukkha-kantāra C.); Ja.vi.560; Vv-a.334.</p><p>= iriṇa, q.v. &amp; cp. Sk. īriṇa</p>

īsa|iisa|isa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisa</span></p><p>lord, owner ruler Ja.iv.209 (of a black lion = kāḷa-sīha C.); Vv-a.168 f. <em>īsī</em> see mahesī a chief queen. Cp. also <strong>mahesakkha</strong>.</p><p>fr. <em>iś</em> to have power, perf. īśe = Goth. aih; cp. Sk. īśvara = P. issara, &amp; BSk. īśa, e.g. Jtm.31#81</p><p>See also: mahesakkha</p>

īsaka|iisaka|isaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaka</span></p><p>pole Ja.ii.152; Ja.vi.456 (˚agga the top of a pole).</p><p>dimin. of īsā</p>

īsakapoṇa|iisakapo.na|isakapona
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisakapo.na</span></p><p>slanting slightly, at a slight angle.</p>

īsakaṃ|iisaka.m|isakam
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind. a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaka.m</span></p><p>little, slightly, partially.</p>

īsakaṃ|iisaka.m|isakam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaka.m</span></p><p>adverb a little, slightly, easily MN.i.450; Ja.i.77; Ja.vi.456; DN-a.i.252, DN-a.i.310; Vv-a.36; Vism.136 Vism.137, Vism.231, īsakam pi even a little Vism.106; Sdhp.586.</p><p>nt. of īsaka</p>

īsikā|iisikaa|isika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisikaa</span></p><p>the inner part of a reed or stalk (used as an arrow shaft); a stalk of grass</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://isikā">isikā</a>, <a href="bword://isīkā">isīkā</a></p>

īsā|iisaa|isa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaa</span></p><p>the pole of a carriage or of a plough.</p>

īsā|iisaa|isa
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaa</span></p><p>the pole of a plough or of a carriage SN.i.104 (nangal’ īsā read with variant reading for nangala-sīsā T.), SN.i.172, SN.i.224 (˚mukha): AN.iv.191 (rath˚); Snp.77; Ja.i.203 (˚mukha); Ja.iv.209; Ud.42; Mil.27; Snp-a.146; Vv-a.269 (˚mūlaṃ = rathassa uro).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://danta">danta</a></em> having teeth (tusks) as long as a plough-pole (of an elephant) Vin.i.352; MN.i.414; Vv.20#9 = Vv.43#9 (ratha-īsā-sadisa-danto); Ja.vi.490 = Ja.vi.515.</li></ul><p>Vedic īṣā</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://danta">danta</a></p>

īsādanta|iisaadanta|isadanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaadanta</span></p><p>having tusks like carriage-poles</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īsā">īsā</a></p>

īsāka|iisaaka|isaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaaka</span></p><p>adjective having a pole (said of a carriage) Ja.vi.252.</p><p>fr. īsā</p>

īsāmukha|iisaamukha|isamukha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaamukha</span></p><p>the top of a carriage-pole</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īsā">īsā</a></p>

īsāna|iisaana|isana
<p>[DPPN]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iisaana</span></p><p>One of the chief devas.</p><p>In the Tevijja Sutta DN.i.244 he is mentioned with Indra, Soma, Varuṇa, Pajāpati and Brahmā, as being invoked by the brahmins.</p><p>He was in the battle of the devas against the Asuras and led a section of the deva host. Indra tells the devas that if, during the struggle, they felt faint-hearted, they should look at the crest of his own banner or at that of Pajāpati, Varuṇa or Īsāna, and their fear would disappear. SN.i.219</p>

īti|iiti|iti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iiti</span></p><p>calamity, affliction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ītī">ītī</a></p>

īti|iiti|iti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iiti</span></p><p>Īti &amp; Ītī</p><p>feminine ill, calamity, plague, distress, often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with &amp; substituted for upaddava cp. BSk. ītay’ opadrava (attack of plague) Divy.119.; Snp.51; Ja.i.27 (verse 189); Ja.v.401 = upaddava; Mnd.381 Cnd.48, Cnd.636 (+ upaddava = santāpa); Mil.152, Mil.274 Mil.418. <em>-<a href="bword://an%C4%ABti">anīti</a></em> sound condition, health, safety AN.iv.238; Mil.323.</p><p>Sk. īti, of doubtful origin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīti">anīti</a></p>

ītiha|iitiha|itiha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iitiha</span></p><p>doublet of itiha, only found in neg. <em>an˚</em>.</p>

ītika|iitika|itika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iitika</span></p><p>adjective connected or affected with ill or harm, only in neg. <em>an˚</em>.</p><p>fr. īti</p>

ītī|iitii|iti
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iitii</span></p><p>calamity, affliction</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://īti">īti</a></p>

ītī|iitii|iti
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">iitii</span></p><p>Īti &amp; Ītī</p><p>feminine ill, calamity, plague, distress, often comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->b.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with &amp; substituted for upaddava cp. BSk. ītay’ opadrava (attack of plague) Divy.119.; Snp.51; Ja.i.27 (verse 189); Ja.v.401 = upaddava; Mnd.381 Cnd.48, Cnd.636 (+ upaddava = santāpa); Mil.152, Mil.274 Mil.418. <em>-<a href="bword://an%C4%ABti">anīti</a></em> sound condition, health, safety AN.iv.238; Mil.323.</p><p>Sk. īti, of doubtful origin</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://anīti">anīti</a></p>

ūha|uuha|uha
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuha</span></p><p>see <em>vy</em>˚, sam˚.</p>

ūhacca|uuhacca|uhacca
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhacca</span></p><p>extracting; rooting out.</p>

ūhacca|uuhacca|uhacca
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhacca</span></p><p>Ūhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable</p><ol><li>lifting up, raising or rising Ja.iii.206.</li><li>pulling out, taking away, removing DN.ii.254 (cp. Dhp-a.ii.181); SN.i.27 (variant reading for ohacca); Snp.1119 (= uddharitvā uppāṭayitvā Cnd.171).</li></ol><p>ger. of ūharati, ud + <em>hṛ</em>; (or ava + <em>hṛ</em>; cp. ohacca &amp; oharati) for uddharati 1 &amp; 2</p><p>Ūhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>indeclinable soiling by defecation, defecating Ja.ii.71 (= vaccaṃ katvā C.).</p><p>ger. of ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> = ūhadati</p>

ūhadati|uuhadati|uhadati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhadati</span></p><p>defecates (upon); soils (with excrement).</p>

ūhadati|uuhadati|uhadati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhadati</span></p><p>to defecate Ja.ii.355; Dhp-a.ii.181 (so read with variant reading for T. ūhadayati).</p><p>for ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --> (?) or formed secondarily fr. ūhacca or ohacca?</p>

ūhana|uuhana|uhana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhana</span></p><p>reasoning, consideration, examination Mil.32 (“comprehension” trsl.; as characteristic of manasikāra); Vism.142 = Dhs-a.114 (“prescinding” trsl. as characteristic of vitakka).</p><p>fr. ūhanati?</p>

ūhanati|uuhanati|uhanati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhanati</span></p><p>ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. pushes upwards or out; throws up, throws away; removes; raises.</p><p>ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pr. 3 sg. hits at, strikes; hurts, damages, afflicts, spoils.</p>

ūhanati|uuhanati|uhanati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhanati</span></p><p>Ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>to disturb, shake up, defile, soil MN.i.243; Ja.ii.73<br />pass aor. ūhani: see <em>ūhaññati</em><br />pp <em>ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.). Cp. sam˚.</p><p>ud + han</p><p>Ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>to cut off, discharge, emit, defecate Vin.i.78; Vin.iii.227.</li><li>[prob. for ūharati, cp. ūhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted -->] to lift up, to take away MN.i.117 (opp. odahati). Cp. ohana in bimb ohana. ger. <em>ūhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.).</li></ol><p>either ud + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em> or ava + <em><a href="bword://han">han</a></em>, cp. ohanati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://han">han</a>, <a href="bword://han">han</a></p>

ūharati|uuharati|uharati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuharati</span></p><p>only in forms of ger. <em>ūhacca<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> and pp. <em>ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></em> (q.v.).</p><p>for uddharati</p>

ūhasana|uuhasana|uhasana
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhasana</span></p><p>laughing, mocking Mil.127.</p><p>fr. ūhasati</p>

ūhasati|uuhasati|uhasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhasati</span></p><p>laughs; smiles at (flirtatiously)</p>

ūhasati|uuhasati|uhasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhasati</span></p><p>to laugh at, deride, mock AN.iii.91; Ja.v.452 (+ pahasati); Pp.67 (= avahasati Pp-a.249).</p><p>either ud or ava + <em>has</em>, cp. avahasati</p>

ūhasiyaṃāna|uuhasiya.maana|uhasiyamana
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhasiya.maana</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūhasati">ūhasati</a></p>

ūhata|uuhata|uhata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhata</span></p><p>ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. soiled (with excrement)</p><p>ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. removed; raised; elevated, high</p><p>ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->3<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>pp mfn. hit; afflicted; distressed</p><p>See also: ūdahati, <a href="bword://ūhanati">ūhanati</a>, <a href="bword://ūhanati">ūhanati</a></p>

ūhata|uuhata|uhata
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhata</span></p><p>Ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><ol><li>lifted, risen, raised Vin.iii.70; Ja.v.403.</li><li>taken out, pulled out, destroyed Thag.223 = Cnd.97#4 Thag.514; Dhp.338 (= ucchinna Dhp-a.iv.48).</li><li>soiled with excrements Vin.ii.222.</li></ol><p>pp. of ud + <em>hṛ</em>; or <em>dhṛ</em>; thus for uddhaṭa as well as uddhata</p><p>Ūhata<!-- raw HTML omitted -->2<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p><p>disturbed MN.i.116.</p><p>pp. of ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ūhaññati|uuha~n~nati|uhannati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pass. pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuha~n~nati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūhanati">ūhanati</a></p>

ūhaññati|uuha~n~nati|uhannati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuha~n~nati</span></p><p>to be soiled; to be disturbed aor. ūhaññi Vin.i.48; MN.i.116; aor. also ūhani MN.i.243.</p><p>Pass. of ūhanati<!-- raw HTML omitted -->1<!-- raw HTML omitted --></p>

ūhaññi|uuha~n~ni|uhanni
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">aor. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuha~n~ni</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūhanati">ūhanati</a></p>

ūhā|uuhaa|uha
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuhaa</span></p><p>life, only in cpd. āyūha lifetime Pv-a.136, Pv-a.162 (˚pariyosāna)<br />■ As Name of a river at Mil.70. Cp. BSk. ūhā in ūhāpoha Av. SN.i.209, SN.i.235.</p><p>etym.?</p>

ūkā|uukaa|uka
<p>[Dhammika]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uukaa</span></p><p>Head Lice, insects of the order Anoplura. Head lice are a small flattened blood-sucking insect found in human hair. There are many references in the Tipiṭaka to people asking others to pick the lice or their eggs out of their hair Ja.i.453 Ja.ii.324 Ja.iii.393 Ja.v.298. One of the reasons Buddhist monks and nuns shaved their heads was probably so they could not get and therefore did not have to kill head lice. One ancient Indian system of measurement took as its basic unit the louse’s egg, <em>likkhā</em>. Seven eggs equal the length of one louse.</p>

ūkā|uukaa|uka
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine a</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uukaa</span></p><p>louse Ja.i.453; Ja.ii.324; Ja.iii.393; Ja.v.298; Mil.11; Vism.445; Dhs-a.307 Dhs-a.319; Dhp-a.iii.342; Vv-a.86.</p><p>is also used as linear measure (cp. Sk. yūkālikṣaṃ) Vb-a.343 (where 7 likkhā are said to equal 1 ūkā).</p><p>Sk. yūkā, prob. dialectical</p>

ūmi|uumi|umi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumi</span></p><p>a wave, a billow; a ripple.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūmī">ūmī</a>, <a href="bword://ummī">ummī</a></p>

ūmi|uumi|umi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumi</span></p><p>Ūmī &amp; Ūmi</p><p>feminine a wave MN.i.460 (˚bhaya); SN.iv.157; SN.v.123 (˚jāta); AN.iii.232 sq. (id.); Snp.920; Ja.ii.216; Ja.iii.262 Ja.iv.141; Mil.260 (˚jāta)<br />■ <em>Note.</em> A parallel form of <em>ūmī</em> is <em>ummī</em>.</p><p>Sk. ūrmi, fr. Idg. *ṷel (see <em><a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> i.2); cp. Gr. ἐλύω io wind, ε ̔́λις wound; Lat. volvo to roll Ags. wylm wave; Ohg. wallan; also Sk. ulva, varutra valaya, valli, vṛṇoti. See details in Walde, Lat. Wtb under volvo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a></p>

ūmighāta|uumighaata|umighata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumighaata</span></p><p>the creation of ripples, beating up a wave</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūmi">ūmi</a></p>

ūmijāta|uumijaata|umijata
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumijaata</span></p><p>covered in ripples, disturbed</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūmi">ūmi</a></p>

ūmika|uumika|umika
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumika</span></p><p>wave Mil.197 (˚vanka waterfall, cataract). Umi &amp; Umi;</p><p>f. ūmi</p>

ūmī|uumii|umi
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumii</span></p><p>a wave, a billow; a ripple.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūmi">ūmi</a>, <a href="bword://ummī">ummī</a></p>

ūmī|uumii|umi
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uumii</span></p><p>Ūmī &amp; Ūmi</p><p>feminine a wave MN.i.460 (˚bhaya); SN.iv.157; SN.v.123 (˚jāta); AN.iii.232 sq. (id.); Snp.920; Ja.ii.216; Ja.iii.262 Ja.iv.141; Mil.260 (˚jāta)<br />■ <em>Note.</em> A parallel form of <em>ūmī</em> is <em>ummī</em>.</p><p>Sk. ūrmi, fr. Idg. *ṷel (see <em><a href="bword://nibb%C4%81na">nibbāna</a></em> i.2); cp. Gr. ἐλύω io wind, ε ̔́λις wound; Lat. volvo to roll Ags. wylm wave; Ohg. wallan; also Sk. ulva, varutra valaya, valli, vṛṇoti. See details in Walde, Lat. Wtb under volvo</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://nibbāna">nibbāna</a></p>

ūna|uuna|una
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuna</span></p><p>wanting, deficient; lacking (something), incomplete, not full; fewer, less (by, instr.); less (than, abl. or iic); what is deficient; something lacking; a lack.</p>

ūna|uuna|una
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuna</span></p><p>adjective wanting, deficient less MN.ii.73; Ja.v.330; Dhp-a.i.77; Dhp-a.iv.210. Mostly adverbially with numerals = one less, but one, minus (one or two); usually with <em><a href="bword://eka">eka</a></em> (as ekūna one less, e.g. ekūna-aṭṭhasataṃ (799) Ja.i.57; ekūna-pañcasate Kp-a.91 ekūna-vīsati (19) Vism.287; eken’ūnesu pañcasu attabhāvasatesu (499) Ja.i.167; also with eka in instr. as eken’ūnapañcasatāni (deficient by one) Vin.ii.285; Kp-a.91; sometimes without eka, e.g. ūnapañcasatāni (499) Vin.iii.284 ūnavīsati (19) Vin.iv.130, Vin.iv.148. With “two” less: dvīhi ūnaṃ sahassaṃ (998) Ja.i.255<br />■ <em><a href="bword://an%C5%ABna">anūna</a></em> not deficient complete Pv-a.285 (= paripuṇṇa).</p><ul><li><em>-<a href="bword://udara">udara</a></em> (ūnudara, ūnūdara, ūnodara) an empty stomach adj. of empty stomach; ˚udara Ja.ii.293; Ja.vi.295; <em>-ūdara</em> Ja.vi.258; Mil.406; odara Snp.707; Dhp-a.i.170.</li><li><em>-<a href="bword://bh%C4%81va">bhāva</a></em> depletion, deficiency Snp-a.463 (variant reading hānabhāva).</li></ul><p>Vedic ūna; cp. Av. ūna, Gr. ε ̓ϋνις, Lat. vāpus, Goth. wans, Ags. won = E. want</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://eka">eka</a>, <a href="bword://anūna">anūna</a>, <a href="bword://udara">udara</a>, <a href="bword://bhāva">bhāva</a></p>

ūnadasavassa|uunadasavassa|unadasavassa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunadasavassa</span></p><p>(one) less than ten years’ standing</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a></p>

ūnaka|uunaka|unaka
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunaka</span></p><p>not full; smaller, less (than, abl. or iic.); less (by, instr.).</p>

ūnaka|uunaka|unaka
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunaka</span></p><p>adjective deficient, wanting, lacking Vin.iii.81, Vin.iii.254; Vin.iv.263; Snp.721; Mil.310, Mil.311, (˚satta-vassika one who is not yet 7 years old), Mil.414; Dhp-a.i.79.</p><p>ūna + ka</p>

ūnakadvatikkhatuṃ|uunakadvatikkhatu.m|unakadvatikkhatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunakadvatikkhatu.m</span></p><p>less than two or three times</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnakadvitikkhatuṃ">ūnakadvitikkhatuṃ</a>, <a href="bword://ūnaka">ūnaka</a></p>

ūnakadvitikkhatuṃ|uunakadvitikkhatu.m|unakadvitikkhatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunakadvitikkhatu.m</span></p><p>less than two or three times</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnakadvatikkhatuṃ">ūnakadvatikkhatuṃ</a>, <a href="bword://ūnaka">ūnaka</a></p>

ūnakatikkhatuṃ|uunakatikkhatu.m|unakatikkhatum
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">ind.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunakatikkhatu.m</span></p><p>less than three times</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnaka">ūnaka</a></p>

ūnatta|uunatta|unatta
<p>[PTS]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunatta</span></p><p>depletion, deficiency Vin.ii.239; Ja.v.450.</p><p>abstr. fr. ūna</p>

ūnodara|uunodara|unodara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunodara</span></p><p>with unfilled belly; whose stomach is not (completely) full</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnudara">ūnudara</a>, <a href="bword://ūnūdara">ūnūdara</a>, <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a></p>

ūnudara|uunudara|unudara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunudara</span></p><p>with unfilled belly; whose stomach is not (completely) full</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnūdara">ūnūdara</a>, <a href="bword://ūnodara">ūnodara</a>, <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a></p>

ūnūdara|uunuudara|unudara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uunuudara</span></p><p>with unfilled belly; whose stomach is not (completely) full</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūnudara">ūnudara</a>, <a href="bword://ūnodara">ūnodara</a>, <a href="bword://ūna">ūna</a></p>

ūpiya|uupiya|upiya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uupiya</span></p><p>see <em><a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a></em> &amp; opiya.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upiya">upiya</a></p>

ūpāya|uupaaya|upaya
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uupaaya</span></p><p>at Dhp-a.ii.93 stands for <em><a href="bword://up%C4%81ya">upāya</a></em>.</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://upāya">upāya</a></p>

ūrantarikā|uurantarikaa|urantarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uurantarikaa</span></p><p>the area between the thighs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūruntarikā">ūruntarikā</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūraṭṭhika|uura.t.thika|uratthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uura.t.thika</span></p><p>the thighbones</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūruṭṭhika">ūruṭṭhika</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūru|uuru|uru
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuru</span></p><p>the thigh.</p>

ūru|uuru|uru
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuru</span></p><p>the thigh Snp.610; Vin.ii.105 (in contrast with bāha); Vin.iii.106; Ja.i.277; Ja.ii.275, Ja.ii.443; Ja.iii.82; Ja.v.89, Ja.v.155; Cnd.659 (so read for uru); Vv.64#13; DN-a.i.135 = Vin.ii.190.</p><ul><li><em>-aṭṭhi(ka)</em> the thigh bone MN.i.58; MN.iii.92; Ja.i.428 (ūraṭṭhika); Kp-a.49, Kp-a.50 (ūraṭṭhi).</li><li><em>-(k)khambha</em> stiffening or rigidity of the thigh, paralysis of the leg (as symptom of fright) MN.i.237; Ja.v.23.</li></ul><p>Vedic ūru; cp. Lat. vārus bow-legged, of Idg. *ṷā, to which also Ohg. wado = Ger. wade calf of leg</p>

ūrubali|uurubali|urubali
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uurubali</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ūrubalin">ūrubalin</a></p><p>strong in the thighs, with strong thighs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūrubalin|uurubalin|urubalin
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uurubalin</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ūrubalin">ūrubalin</a></p><p>strong in the thighs, with strong thighs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūrukkhambha|uurukkhambha|urukkhambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uurukkhambha</span></p><p>paralysis of the thighs (through fear)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūrutthambha">ūrutthambha</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūruntarikā|uuruntarikaa|uruntarika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">feminine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuruntarikaa</span></p><p>the area between the thighs</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūrantarikā">ūrantarikā</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūrutthambha|uurutthambha|urutthambha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uurutthambha</span></p><p>paralysis of the thighs (through fear)</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūrukkhambha">ūrukkhambha</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūruṭṭhika|uuru.t.thika|urutthika
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uuru.t.thika</span></p><p>the thighbones</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ūraṭṭhika">ūraṭṭhika</a>, <a href="bword://ūru">ūru</a></p>

ūsa|uusa|usa
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine (&amp; n.)</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uusa</span></p><p>saline substance; saline soil.</p>

ūsa|uusa|usa
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uusa</span></p><p>salt-ground; saline substance, always comb<!-- raw HTML omitted -->d.<!-- raw HTML omitted --> with khāra SN.iii.131 (˚gandha); AN.i.209.</p><p>Sk. ūṣa</p>

ūsara|uusara|usara
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">mfn. &amp; neuter</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uusara</span></p><p>saline; having saline soil; saline soil.</p>

ūsara|uusara|usara
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uusara</span></p><p>adjective saline SN.iv.315; AN.iv.237; Dhs-a.243<br />■ nt. ˚ṃ a spot with saline soil Pv-a.139 (gloss for ujjhangala).</p><p>Sk. ūṣara, fr. ūṣa</p>

ūtagītaṃ|uutagiita.m|utagitam
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">uutagiita.m</span></p><p>at Ja.i.290 in phrase “jimaṃ ūtagītaṃ gāyanto” read “imaṃ jūtagītaṃ g.”</p>

ḍaha|.daha|daha
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">masculine</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.daha</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍāha">ḍāha</a></p>

ḍahanta|.dahanta|dahanta
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg. mf</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahanta</span></p><p>(~antī)n. of ḍahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍahati">ḍahati</a></p>

ḍahat|.dahat|dahat
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg. mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahat</span></p><p>Also written as: <a href="bword://ḍahat">ḍahat</a></p><p>of ḍahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍahati">ḍahati</a></p>

ḍahati|.dahati|dahati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahati</span></p><p>burns, consumes; torments.</p>

ḍahati|.dahati|dahati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahati</span></p><p>(&amp; dahati) to burn (trs.) consume, torment MN.i.365; MN.ii.73 AN.v.110; Ja.ii.44 (aor. 3 sg. med. adaḍḍha = Sk. adagdha) Dhp.31, Dhp.71, Dhp.140; Mil.45, Mil.112 (cauterize). Pp. <em><a href="bword://da%E1%B8%8D%E1%B8%8Dha">daḍḍha</a></em> -Pass. <em><a href="bword://%E1%B8%8Dayhati">ḍayhati</a></em> SN.i.188 (kāmarāgena ḍayhāmi cittam me pariḍayhati); ib. (mahārāga: mā ḍayhittho punappunaṃ) MN.ii.73; SN.iii.150 (mahāpaṭhavī ḍayhati vinassati na bhavati) esp. in ppr. ḍayhamāna consumed with or by, burning, glowing Dhp.371; Iti.23 (˚ena kāyena cetasā Pv.i.11#10, Pv.i.12#2; Pv.ii.2#3) (of a corpse being cremated) Pv-a.63, Pv-a.152 (vippaṭisārena: consumed by remorse) See also similes <em>J.P.T.S.</em> 1907, 90. Cp. uḍ˚.</p><p>Sk. dahati, pp. dagdha, cp. dāha, nidāgha (summer heat); Gr. τέφρα ashes, Lat. favilla (glowing) cinders, Goth. dags, Ger. tag. E. day = hot time</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://daḍḍha">daḍḍha</a>, <a href="bword://ḍayhati">ḍayhati</a></p>

ḍahetvā|.dahetvaa|dahetva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahetvaa</span></p><p>of ḍahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍahati">ḍahati</a></p>

ḍahitvā|.dahitvaa|dahitva
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">absol.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dahitvaa</span></p><p>of ḍahati</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍahati">ḍahati</a></p>

ḍasati|.dasati|dasati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dasati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍaṃsati">ḍaṃsati</a></p>

ḍasati|.dasati|dasati
<p>[PTS]</p><p> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dasati</span></p><p>(&amp; ḍaṃsati) to bite (esp. of flies snakes, scorpions, etc.),<br />pres ḍasati MN.i.519;<br />pot ḍaseyya MN.i.133; AN.iii.101 = AN.iv.320 (where ḍaṃs˚) ḍaṃseyya AN.iii.306;<br />ppr ḍasamāna Ja.i.265 (gīvāya) fut. ḍaṃsayissāmi Ja.vi.193 (variant reading ḍass˚);<br />aor aḍaṃsi Vv.80#8 (= Sk. adānkṣīt), <em>ḍaṃsi</em> Pv-a.62 &amp; <em>ḍasi</em> Ja.i.502; Dhp-a.ii.258;<br />inf ḍasituṃ Ja.i.265;<br />ger ḍasitvā Ja.i.222 Ja.ii.102; Ja.iii.52, Ja.iii.538; Dhp-a.i.358<br />pp <em><a href="bword://da%E1%B9%AD%E1%B9%ADha">daṭṭha</a></em>; cp. also dāṭhā &amp; saṇḍāsa.</p><p>cp. Sk. da ati &amp; daṃśati, Gr.; δάκνω, Ohg. zanga, Ags. tonge, E. tong</p><p>See also: <a href="bword://daṭṭha">daṭṭha</a></p>

ḍasitabba|.dasitabba|dasitabba
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">fpp mfn.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dasitabba</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍaṃsitabba">ḍaṃsitabba</a></p>

ḍassati|.dassati|dassati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dassati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍaṃsati">ḍaṃsati</a></p>

ḍayati|.dayati|dayati
<p>[NCPED]</p><p><i style="color: #448C19;">pr. 3 sg.</i> | <span style="color: #0B4A72;">.dayati</span></p><p>See also: <a href="bword://ḍeti">ḍeti</a></p>

ḍayhamāna|.dayhamaana|dayhamana
